window.STATECRAFT_ANALYSIS = {"CA-AB1170":{"summary":"AB 1170 is California's annual code maintenance bill. It makes nonsubstantive technical changes across various code provisions to carry out the Legislative Counsel's recommendations for maintaining the codes. It was signed and chaptered as Chapter 67, Statutes of 2025, on July 28, 2025.","mechanics":["Implements the existing statutory duty of the Legislative Counsel to advise the Legislature on legislation needed to maintain the codes.","Makes only nonsubstantive changes, such as corrections, conforming edits and cleanup, according to the official abstract.","Affects unspecified 'various provisions of the law'. The full text was not available, so the specific code sections amended cannot be confirmed here.","Enacted as Chapter 67, Statutes of 2025, chaptered 2025-07-28."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states were listed. This is a routine housekeeping vehicle rather than a gaming policy bill, and it is unusual only in that it may touch many code sections at once without changing their legal effect.","watch":"Check the chaptered text to see whether any technical edits land in Penal Code gambling provisions, the Gambling Control Act (Bus. & Prof. Code §19800 et seq.) or horse-racing wagering statutes, so that compliance citations and internal references stay accurate.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"AB1170","text_chars":0,"generated":"2026-11-02T17:57:52.202Z"},"CA-AB831":{"summary":"AB 831 (Chapter 623, Statutes of 2025) makes it a misdemeanor to operate, conduct, or offer an online sweepstakes game in California. It also makes it a crime for payment processors, financial institutions, geolocation providers, gaming content suppliers, platform providers, and media affiliates to knowingly and willfully support or promote one. It also expands California's existing unfair-practices rules for contests and sweepstakes so they expressly reach internet websites and online apps, and it redefines simulated gambling to include examples such as lottery games, bingo, and sports wagering.","mechanics":["Unfair-practice expansion: the existing ban on electronic simulated-gambling methods tied to sweepstakes prizes now expressly covers 'any method, including an internet website or an online application.' The term 'gambling-themed games' is deleted, and 'gambling' is redefined to include examples such as lottery games, bingo, sports wagering, or any game that mimics or simulates similar gambling.","Payment-system trigger: it is an unfair practice to offer such games using a payment system that lets a person play a simulated gambling program for direct or indirect consideration, with eligibility for a prize, cash or cash equivalents, or a chance to win them. This applies in a business establishment, on the internet, or via an online app. Games that do not award cash prizes or cash equivalents are expressly not made unlawful.","New crime: it is unlawful for any person or entity to operate, conduct, or offer an online sweepstakes game (as defined in the bill) in California.","Secondary liability: any entity, financial institution, payment processor, geolocation provider, gaming content supplier, platform provider, or media affiliate that knowingly and willfully supports, directly or indirectly, the operation, conduct, or promotion of an online sweepstakes game in the state is also liable.","Penalties: misdemeanor punishable by a fine of $1,000 to $25,000, up to one year in county jail, or both. The bill creates a state-mandated local program with no reimbursement required.","Carve-outs: lawful games and methods of gambling enterprises licensed under the Gambling Control Act, and California State Lottery operations, are exempt. So are limited, occasional promotional sweepstakes by for-profit businesses that are incidental to substantial bona fide sales of consumer products or services and are not a vehicle for ongoing gambling."],"issue_tags":["sweeps","advertising"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"The closest analogue on the list is AR HB 1861, which makes operating an illegal online casino or sports betting a felony within a broader interactive gaming framework. AB 831 is narrower in target (online sweepstakes games) and lighter in penalty (misdemeanor), but it reaches further down the supply chain by naming payment processors, geolocation vendors, content suppliers, platforms, and media affiliates. Unlike GA HB 686, HI HB 2570, and HI HB 1308, which would legalize and tax sports wagering, AB 831 authorizes nothing; it is purely prohibitive. KY HB 904, by contrast, regulates rather than bans an adjacent contest product (fantasy contests).","watch":"Watch the 'knowingly and willfully' secondary-liability clause and the express inclusion of sports wagering in the simulated-gambling definition, because vendors, payment partners, and media affiliates that also serve sweepstakes-model or prize-based sports products face misdemeanor exposure in California, where sports betting remains unlicensed and the exemption covers only Gambling Control Act licensees and the State Lottery.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"AB831","text_chars":0,"generated":"2026-11-02T17:58:03.046Z"},"IA-SSB1240":{"summary":"SSB 1240 (renumbered SF 660) appropriates a total of $22 million from Iowa's sports wagering receipts fund (Code section 8.57I) for FY 2025-2026: $14 million to the Department of Education for an education support personnel pay supplement distributed to school districts by enrollment, and $8 million to the Department of Public Safety for the public safety equipment fund. It does not change sports wagering tax rates, licensing, or operator obligations.","mechanics":["Sec. 1: $14,000,000 from the sports wagering receipts fund to the Department of Education for FY 2025-2026 to supplement compensation of education support personnel, defined as regular and part-time non-salaried school district employees.","Each district's share equals $14,000,000 multiplied by its FY 2024-25 budget enrollment divided by the statewide total budget enrollment. Districts must report support-personnel data to the department on or before July 1, 2025 to qualify.","The funds are treated as miscellaneous income under section 257.2, are excluded from district cost computations under chapter 257, and are paid on the foundation aid schedule under section 257.16.","Sec. 2: $8,000,000 from the same fund to the Department of Public Safety for deposit in the public safety equipment fund (section 80.48).","Sec. 3: the education appropriation takes effect upon enactment. The public safety appropriation follows the normal effective date.","Per the explanation, the fund is fed by the internet fantasy sports contest tax (Code 99E.6) and the sports wagering net receipts tax (Code 99F.11(5))."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. The bill is a routine appropriation of existing sports wagering and fantasy sports tax revenue. It is notable mainly because it earmarks wagering receipts for non-gaming purposes (school support staff pay and police equipment) rather than for problem-gambling or regulatory programs, and it leaves the underlying tax structure untouched.","watch":"Operators face no direct new obligations here, but growing legislative reliance on sports wagering receipts for recurring education and public safety spending could build pressure to raise the 99F.11 net receipts tax rate in future sessions.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":5691,"generated":"2026-11-02T17:58:06.975Z"},"GA-HB686":{"summary":"HB 686 (House Higher Education Committee substitute, LC 56 0648S) would legalize online-only sports betting in Georgia, with the Georgia Lottery Corporation acting as both regulator and \"master sports betting licensee.\" It caps the market at 16 Type 1 licenses, most tied to Georgia pro teams and sports venues, and imposes a 27.5% privilege tax on adjusted gross income, with proceeds going to a dedicated trust fund for the constitutionally specified purposes. The bill has a contingent effective date tied to Georgia constitutional provisions, and it died after being reported favorably by substitute on 2026-03-06.","mechanics":["Market structure (50-27-133): no more than 16 Type 1 licenses. Five are reserved for qualifying Georgia pro teams (baseball, football, men's and women's basketball, soccer). One each goes to a 30-year PGA-tour tournament body, a 30-year invitational golf facility, a NASCAR track owner, and the Lottery Corporation. The remaining seven go to online sports betting services providers through public procurement. Each eligible entity may contract with only one platform provider, and entities must give notice of intent within 60 days or be permanently disqualified.","Tax (50-27-150): 27.5% annual privilege tax on adjusted gross income, paid monthly by the services provider. AGI is wagers minus federal excise tax minus winnings paid, including the cash value of merchandise. Negative AGI may be carried forward. The tax is in lieu of other state and local taxes and fees on sports betting operations or proceeds, and it is deposited quarterly into the Georgia Sports Betting Proceeds Trust Fund.","Fees and terms (50-27-130, -131): Type 1 licenses carry a $100,000 application fee and a $1M annual fee. Services provider licenses are $10,000/$100,000, and supplier licenses are $2,000/$20,000. Licenses run five years. Operators must hold a reserve of at least $500,000 or their outstanding wager liabilities, whichever is greater. Operators licensed elsewhere may receive temporary licenses for up to one year.","Launch timing: Lottery rules are due within 90 days, exempt from the Georgia APA. Applications for all 16 licenses open on the same date, and timely applicants get a common go-live date no later than July 31, 2027. No Type 1 licensee may launch until at least one procurement-based provider is licensed.","Wagering scope: bettors must be 21 or older and physically present in Georgia. Wagers covered include in-play, parlay, props and exchange wagering. Proposition bets on collegiate sporting events are banned (50-27-135). Youth events with mostly under-18 participants and Special Olympics are excluded. Official league data is defined for settling tier 2 wagers, but the operative mandate falls in the truncated portion of the text.","Regulatory limits and carve-outs: the corporation may not set hold or payout percentages (50-27-123(b)). Leagues and Type 1 eligible entities may sign revenue-share agreements without corporation approval (50-27-131(d)). Institutional investors are exempt from qualification requirements. The corporation must set responsible gaming rules covering self-exclusion (SSN required), bet limits for problem gamblers, 1-800-GAMBLER notices and advertising guidelines aimed at minors."],"issue_tags":["legalize","licensing","tax","integrity","advertising","prop"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Like HI HB 2570 and HI HB 1308, HB 686 creates a full licensing regime from scratch. Hawaii places regulation in DBEDT or the Department of Law Enforcement and taxes through a general excise tax on operator licenses. Georgia instead uses its lottery corporation as regulator and operator, with a team- and venue-tethered cap of 16 licenses. Georgia's 27.5% GGR rate sits well above Colorado's 10% rate (whose deductions CO HB 25-1311 narrows) and is in the range of higher-tax states like Louisiana after LA HB 639. Unlike CO SB 131, Georgia leaves deposit and bet limits to rulemaking rather than fixing statutory caps.","watch":"Watch three things: the scarcity of the seven procurement-based skins and the one-platform-per-Type-1-entity rule; the 27.5% rate, where the only promotional relief is the merchandise-as-winnings language in the AGI definition; and the truncated official league data provisions for tier 2 wagers, which could impose data-purchase costs.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"LC 56 0648S/hs","text_chars":89148,"generated":"2026-11-02T17:58:14.467Z"},"IA-SF605":{"summary":"SF 605 amends Iowa Code section 99F.18 to declare that all winnings from sports wagering authorized under chapter 99F are Iowa earned income subject to state and federal income tax, with any state tax withheld remitted to the Department of Revenue on the winner's behalf. It also amends section 422.16(2)(d) to require state income tax withholding on sports wagering winnings whenever federal withholding is required on the same winnings under the Internal Revenue Code. The Act takes effect January 1, 2026.","mechanics":["Sec. 1 splits 99F.18 into two subsections: subsection 1 keeps the existing rule for slot machine winnings, and new subsection 2 extends the same earned-income treatment and remittance duty to sports wagering winnings under chapter 99F.","Sec. 2 adds a state withholding trigger for chapter 99F sports wagering that is tied to the federal trigger. State tax must be withheld whenever the Internal Revenue Code requires federal withholding on the same winnings, rather than at a fixed dollar threshold.","Existing state withholding thresholds are unchanged: winnings over $600 under chapters 99B and 99G, over $1,000 under chapter 99D, and over $1,200 for slot machines under chapter 99F.","Operators must remit amounts withheld under 422.16(2) to the Iowa Department of Revenue on behalf of the winner.","Sec. 3 makes the Act effective January 1, 2026. It was signed by the Governor on March 28, 2025."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike the operator-side tax measures listed, SF 605 addresses only bettor-level income tax withholding and does not touch the operator tax rate or how it is calculated. Those operator-side measures are LA HB 639, which changes the online sports wagering tax, and CO HB 25-1311, which limits deductions from net sports betting proceeds. It also differs from the comprehensive authorization bills, GA HB 686 and HI HB 1308/HB 2570, which create licensing and tax frameworks from scratch. What is unusual is that Iowa ties state withholding to the federal trigger instead of setting its own dollar threshold, so the state obligation will move automatically with any change to federal withholding rules.","watch":"Operators should update payout and withholding systems before January 1, 2026 so that Iowa withholding is triggered in lockstep with federal withholding on sports wagering winnings. They should also watch for Department of Revenue guidance on the withholding rate, remittance timing, and how federal threshold changes flow through.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Enrolled with Governor's Action","text_chars":2977,"generated":"2026-11-02T17:58:31.006Z"},"LA-SB339":{"summary":"Act 494 gives Louisiana's gaming regulators (the Gaming Control Board and State Police gaming enforcement division, the Racing Commission, the Office of Charitable Gaming, and the Lottery Corporation via the gaming enforcement division) express statutory authority to obtain state and FBI fingerprint-based criminal history record information on applicants, licensees, permittees, and employees. Under the Gaming Control Board's Title 27 jurisdiction, which expressly includes the Louisiana Sports Wagering Act and Fantasy Sports Contests Act, every license or permit applicant and every person required to be found suitable must now submit a full set of fingerprints for a national check. The Act takes effect August 1, 2026.","mechanics":["New R.S. 27:15(B)(1)(b) requires every applicant for a license or permit, and any person required to be found suitable under Title 27, to submit fingerprints to the gaming enforcement division, which forwards them to the FBI. The board and the division are the designated recipients of the results.","New R.S. 27:20.1 defines 'criminal history record information' to include state arrest, prosecution, and conviction records, including those expunged or dismissed under Code of Criminal Procedure Articles 893 and 894, plus national fingerprint-based records. The definition of 'permit' covers any permit, license, or authorization, or associated application, issued under Title 27.","Use of fingerprints is limited to eligibility determinations under the suitability standards in R.S. 27:28, 234, 235, 306, 427, and 604. The records are confidential under R.S. 27:21 and may be used exclusively to evaluate eligibility or disqualification. They are also available to the board and the Louisiana Department of Justice.","The bureau's costs under R.S. 15:587, including the costs of the FBI check, may be imposed on the individual. Under amended R.S. 47:9065(B), the bureau may charge the Lottery Corporation or the applicant a reasonable processing fee.","Parallel sections are added for the Racing Commission (R.S. 4:147.3, tied to R.S. 4:150 suitability) and the Office of Charitable Gaming (R.S. 4:705.1). R.S. 15:587(C) is rewritten to route state and FBI results to each agency.","Under new R.S. 47:9065(C), the gaming enforcement division may share criminal history results with the Lottery Corporation to determine eligibility of vendors, retailers, and employees."],"issue_tags":["licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. On its own, the Act is unusual in two respects. It is a cross-agency housekeeping statute that recodifies express FBI fingerprint-check authority for every Louisiana gaming regulator at once. It also explicitly sweeps expunged and Article 893/894 dismissed records into what regulators may review, rather than changing any substantive licensing standard, tax, or operating rule.","watch":"Operators should watch how the board applies the new mandatory fingerprinting of 'any person required to be found suitable', which could extend to key persons, owners, vendors, and employees of sports wagering and fantasy licensees, along with the review of expunged or dismissed records and the pass-through of check fees to individuals.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"SB339 Act 494","text_chars":19808,"generated":"2026-11-02T17:59:03.076Z"},"IA-SSB1152":{"summary":"Iowa SSB 1152, a Department of Revenue bill later renumbered SF 605, amends Code section 99F.18 to declare that all winnings from sports wagering authorized under chapter 99F are Iowa earned income subject to state and federal income tax. It amends section 422.16(2)(d) to require Iowa income tax withholding on sports wagering winnings whenever federal withholding is required on the same winnings under the Internal Revenue Code. Amounts withheld must be remitted to the Department of Revenue on the winner's behalf.","mechanics":["Sec. 1 adds new subsection 2 to Iowa Code 99F.18. It states that all sports wagering winnings under chapter 99F are Iowa earned income subject to state and federal income tax laws.","Sec. 1 also requires that amounts deducted from sports wagering winnings under 422.16(2) be remitted to the Department of Revenue on behalf of the winner, mirroring the existing slot machine rule in 99F.18(1).","Sec. 2 amends 422.16(2)(d) to tie the state withholding trigger for sports wagering to the federal trigger. Under IRC rules this is generally winnings over $5,000 that are at least 300 times the wager.","The existing withholding thresholds stay the same: $600 for chapter 99B and 99G (lottery) winnings, $1,000 for chapter 99D (pari-mutuel) winnings, and $1,200 for chapter 99F slot machines.","The bill sets no state withholding rate; the rate is governed by existing 422.16.","Sec. 3 makes the Act effective January 1, 2026."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike CO HB 25-1311 and LA HB 639, which change operator-level taxes on net sports betting proceeds or online wagering, Iowa SSB 1152 targets only patron-level income tax withholding and leaves operators' gaming tax untouched. It is also far narrower than the framework bills in GA HB 686, HI HB 1308 and HI HB 2570, which authorize, license and tax sports wagering wholesale. Its distinguishing feature is that it ties the state withholding trigger to federal rules rather than setting a fixed dollar threshold, as Iowa does for slots, lottery and pari-mutuel winnings.","watch":"Operators should prepare payment and withholding systems so that, from January 1, 2026, Iowa withholding fires automatically on every sports wagering payout that triggers federal withholding (including after any future federal threshold changes), with remittance to the Department of Revenue handled as for slot winnings.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":3540,"generated":"2026-11-02T17:59:53.596Z"},"IA-HSB91":{"summary":"Iowa HSB 91, a Department of Revenue proposal later renumbered HF 794, amends Iowa Code section 99F.18 to declare that all winnings from sports wagering authorized under chapter 99F are Iowa earned income subject to state and federal income tax. It also amends section 422.16(2)(d) to require Iowa income tax withholding on sports wagering winnings whenever federal withholding is required on the same winnings under the Internal Revenue Code. The Act takes effect January 1, 2026.","mechanics":["Sec. 1 adds new subsection 2 to section 99F.18 (Tax on winnings). All winnings from chapter 99F sports wagering are Iowa earned income, and amounts deducted for state tax under section 422.16(2) must be remitted to the Department of Revenue on the winner's behalf.","Sec. 2 amends section 422.16(2)(d) to tie the state withholding trigger for sports wagering to the federal trigger: withhold state tax whenever federal income tax withholding is required on the same winnings under the IRC.","Existing dollar thresholds for other games are unchanged:\n- more than $600 for chapter 99B and 99G (lottery and other) winnings;\n- more than $1,000 for chapter 99D pari-mutuel winnings;\n- more than $1,200 for chapter 99F slot machine winnings.","Covered parties are sports wagering licensees and operators under chapter 99F that pay out winnings subject to federal withholding.","Sec. 3 sets the effective date as January 1, 2026."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike CO HB 25-1311 and LA HB 639, which change operator-level taxes, HSB 91 targets bettor income tax withholding. CO HB 25-1311 changes deductions from net sports betting proceeds, and LA HB 639 changes online sports wagering tax rates and dedications. HSB 91 leaves the operator tax untouched and instead imposes a payout-side compliance duty on operators. Also unlike GA HB 686 and HI HB 2570/HB 1308, which would create new sports betting regimes with licensing and gross revenue or excise taxes, Iowa's bill is a narrow administrative fix by the revenue department within an existing legal market. Its most unusual feature is that it sets no fixed dollar threshold for state withholding and instead conforms automatically to the federal withholding trigger.","watch":"Operators should map their payout and withholding systems to the federal IRC withholding rules before January 1, 2026, since the bill sets no fixed dollar threshold. Any future federal change to sports wagering withholding (for example, the trigger amount or the 300-to-1 odds test) would automatically change Iowa withholding obligations.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":3537,"generated":"2026-11-02T17:59:54.128Z"},"HI-HB2570":{"summary":"HB 2570 HD1 (the \"Representative Daniel Holt Act\") adds a new HRS chapter legalizing mobile-only sports wagering in Hawaii, regulated and licensed by the Department of Business, Economic Development, and Tourism, with at least six operator licenses and a universal launch date. It taxes operators through a new privilege tax under amended HRS §237-13(9), earmarks revenue to UH Manoa athletics, a Native Hawaiian organization, an AFL-CIO affiliate, the general fund, problem gambling, and education, and creates escalating criminal penalties for unlicensed wagering. It also expressly exempts licensed sports wagering and defined fantasy sports contests from the gambling offenses in HRS ch. 712 part III.","mechanics":["Licensing (§-5, §-6): DBEDT must issue a minimum of six sports wagering operator licenses, open only to a \"qualified gaming entity\"; $500,000 initial/renewal fee plus cost-based processing fees; five-year term; temporary licenses valid up to three years on payment of the initial fee; universal start date no later than 180 days after the Act's effective date, with equal launch for entities applying within 30 days of the application window. Supplier licenses cost $10,000 for five years, and DBEDT may accept approvals from other jurisdictions with similar standards.","Scope and wagers (§-1, §-10): mobile or digital platform only, with no kiosks or terminals at physical locations; bettors must be 21 or older and physically in Hawaii unless an interstate or international agreement under §-9 applies. Pro, collegiate, amateur, Olympic, motorsports and e-sports are covered. Youth events are excluded, as are games involving Hawaii-based colleges, including those teams' individual games within tournaments. Layoff wagers are permitted, and accounts may be funded by card, bank transfer, online payment systems, or bonuses and promotions.","Tax (§-12, §237-13(9)): operators pay the §237-13(9) privilege tax on adjusted gross sports wagering receipts using accrual accounting, in lieu of all other taxes on sports wagering. Adjusted gross receipts are gross receipts less winnings and applicable taxes, including federal excise tax, with no promotional deduction. The rate text in §237-13(9) is not in the excerpt provided. §-12(a) allocates 2% to UH Manoa athletics, 2% to a Native Hawaiian organization, 1% to the Hawaii Employees Lifeline Program (an AFL-CIO affiliate) and 10% to the general fund. §-12(b) separately directs 7% of all taxes to a new DOH problem gambling special fund (§-13) and 7% to education.","Mandatory rulemaking (§-2(f)) covers: geolocation, age and identity verification; integrity and AML; data privacy and cybersecurity; a statewide voluntary exclusion program; patron-set limits on wagers, deposits and time; and advertising rules barring targeting of minors and self-excluded persons. Rules must also prohibit bets on injuries, penalties, player discipline rulings, replay reviews and any other bet type harmful to an athlete or patron. Rules are to be consistent with other jurisdictions where commercially reasonable.","Operator duties (§-7, §-8, §-11): operators need department-approved house rules. They must run suspicious-activity monitoring software, report suspicious betting above an operator-set, department-approved threshold, and display 1-800-GAMBLER in all advertising. They must keep daily records of gross and adjusted gross receipts and honor the exclusion list, which is distributed at least quarterly.","Enforcement (§-4, §-14, §-15): civil fines of up to $5,000 per violation or $10,000 per occurrence, applicable to non-licensees. Unlicensed sports wagering is a misdemeanor with a minimum $10,000 fine for a first offense, a class C felony with a minimum $50,000 fine for a second, and a class B felony with a minimum $100,000 fine for a third or later. Control persons for background checks are 15% owners with control, parents and subsidiaries, and key decision-makers."],"issue_tags":["legalize","licensing","tax","advertising","integrity","prop"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Like GA HB 686, this is a full legalization-and-tax framework, but Hawaii is mobile-only, bans retail kiosks entirely and sets a floor of six licenses rather than a cap. Unlike KS SB 356, which only authorizes regulations for an existing Kansas market, or CO SB 131, which imposes specific statutory deposit caps, Hawaii leaves responsible-gaming limits and bans on certain prop and bet types to DBEDT rulemaking. On tax base, Hawaii's adjusted gross receipts deduct only winnings and taxes, with no promotional-credit deduction, which goes further than the deduction narrowing at issue in CO HB 25-1311. Its escalating felony penalties for unlicensed operators parallel AR HB 1861's approach.","watch":"Watch the actual §237-13(9) rate, which is absent from this excerpt, and the internally inconsistent revenue allocations in §-12(a) and (b), along with the undefined \"qualified gaming entity\" eligibility standard and the rule-delegated bans on injury, penalty and replay bets that could narrow the prop menu as the bill moves through JHA and CPC.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"HB2570_HD1","text_chars":73237,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:00:03.005Z"},"IA-HSB330":{"summary":"HSB 330 (renumbered HF 1041) rewrites how Iowa spends money in the sports wagering receipts fund (Code 8.57I), which is fed by the sports wagering net receipts tax (99F.11(5)) and the internet fantasy sports contest tax (99E.6). Starting FY 2025-26 it sets a standing priority list of appropriations. It also makes three $10 million one-time FY 2024-25 appropriations from the fund. It does not change tax rates, licensing or any operator obligations.","mechanics":["Sec. 2 replaces 8.57I(4)-(5) for FY 2025-26 onward with an ordered list paid from each year's deposits: first $1.75M to HHS for behavioral health and problem gambling prevention, treatment and recovery; next $750,000 to the Economic Development Authority, split equally among regional sports authority districts (15E.321); next $1.5M to the Department of Revenue's length of service award program grant fund (100B.52).","Under Sec. 2, the remainder is split 50% to the public safety equipment fund (80.48) and 50% to a new local emergency services equipment grant account run by the Department of Management. Eligible grantees are the 88 least populous counties and small cities as defined in 15.352, and DOM must adopt rules under chapter 17A.","Under Sec. 6, the $1.5M length of service award program allocation takes effect only if HF 1002 or SF 627 (or successor legislation) is enacted.","Sec. 4 appropriates from the fund for FY 2024-25: $10M to the community attraction and tourism fund, $10M to the Destination Iowa fund and $10M to the levee improvement fund. Sec. 5 makes this effective on enactment.","According to the explanation, Secs. 1 and 3 strike three things: the authority to use fund moneys for cash flow, the project reporting requirements, and the requirement that county endowment grantees consider racetrack-operating charities when using sports wagering money (no such transfers occur under current law)."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"No other states' bills are listed for comparison. What is unusual is that the bill deals only with where existing sports wagering and fantasy sports tax revenue goes, not with the rate or base. It also earmarks most of the residual revenue for public safety and rural emergency equipment rather than the general fund or education.","watch":"The bill leaves rates untouched, but operators should watch HF 1041 for amendments, because tying sports wagering receipts to public safety and rural emergency grants creates constituencies that may later push for higher net receipts tax rates or a broader tax base to grow the 50/50 residual pool.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":12702,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:00:03.679Z"},"AZ-HB2592":{"summary":"HB 2592 is an omnibus tax-base broadening bill that repeals selected Arizona tax exemptions. For gaming, Section 1 amends A.R.S. 5-111(C) so that the state's pari-mutuel percentage, which previously did not apply to wagering on simulcasts of out-of-state races, now also applies to those pools. The rest of the visible text repeals transaction privilege tax retail exemptions for warranty and service contracts and for sales of stocks and bonds, with conforming renumbering across Titles 28 and 42.","mechanics":["Sec. 1, A.R.S. 5-111(C): the state's take of 2% of the first $1,000,000 of daily pari-mutuel pools and 5% of the amount above $1,000,000 is extended to pools on simulcasts of out-of-state races, repealing the existing carve-out.","The existing 5-111 exemptions are retained in the shown text: county fair racing meetings (D), charity racing days (E), unclaimed tickets from unclaimed property law (F), and permittee pari-mutuel receipts and purse monies from Title 42, Chapter 5 TPT (G).","Sec. 5, A.R.S. 42-5061(A): repeals the retail TPT exclusions for sales of warranty or service contracts (former para. 3) and for the sale of stocks and bonds (former para. 7), and renumbers the remaining paragraphs.","Secs. 2-4 (28-2154.01, 42-5008.01, 42-5009) make conforming cross-reference changes to reflect the renumbering.","The title also lists amendments to 42-5071, 42-5072, 42-5075, 42-6017 and 43-1111, but those sections fall outside the supplied text, which is truncated at 42-5061(A)(34).","No effective date or enforcement provision appears in the supplied text. Status: second reading in the House on 2025-01-28."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"Unlike LA HB 639 (online sports wagering tax changes) and CO HB 25-1311 (limits on operator deductions from net sports betting proceeds), HB 2592 does not touch Arizona's event wagering tax. Its gaming hook is narrower: removing a pari-mutuel simulcast exemption for horse racing. It also differs from the IA withholding bills (SF 605, SSB 1152, HSB 91), which target bettor winnings rather than operator or permittee liability. What is unusual is that a gaming tax increase is folded into a general exemption-repeal bill instead of a standalone gaming measure.","watch":"Watch whether this exemption-repeal approach widens in amendment to reach event wagering deductions or other Title 5 carve-outs, and how much extending the state's 2% to 5% take to out-of-state simulcast pools would raise costs for racetrack permittees and the advance-deposit or simulcast wagering clients.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced Version","text_chars":150205,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:00:10.748Z"},"LA-SB325":{"summary":"Act 491 amends R.S. 27:27.2 to require the Louisiana Gaming Control Board to adopt rules creating a separate exclusion list for retail sports books and mobile wagering, covering people already on the general gaming exclusion list, people who threaten violence or harm against anyone involved in a sporting event where the threat relates to sports gaming, and anyone the board finds threatens the state's interests or effective sports wagering regulation. It makes clear that both the board and the division can place people on exclusion lists, bars listing based on race, color, creed, national origin, sex or disability, and re-letters the existing notice, petition, penalty, sanction and hearing provisions. Effective August 1, 2026.","mechanics":["New R.S. 27:27.2(B): the board must adopt rules for a list of persons to be excluded or ejected from retail sports books and barred from the play or operation of mobile wagering under a license or contract issued under Title 27.","Three listing categories under (B): (1) persons excluded under Subsection A; (2) persons who, before, during or after a sporting event, threaten violence or harm against any person involved in the event, where the threat is related to sports gaming; (3) persons the board determines pose a threat to the state's interests, the purposes of Title 27, or the strict and effective regulation of sports wagering.","New (C): the board and the division may both place persons on the list, but not because of race, color, creed, national origin, sex or disability (as defined in R.S. 51:2232).","Carried-over provisions, re-lettered: notice by personal service or certified mail (D); a petition for removal, where the petitioner bears the burden of proof (E); entering or attempting to enter after listing is punishable by up to 6 months' imprisonment and/or a fine of up to $500 (F); and a hearing within 30 days of the petition under R.S. 27:25 (H).","Operator exposure under (G): the board may sanction licensees, permittees or the casino operator for willful failure to exclude a listed person, and may seek suspension or revocation for a pattern of willful failure.","Effective August 1, 2026, as Act No. 491. Video draw poker licensees remain exempt under (I)."],"issue_tags":["integrity","licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states were listed. The unusual feature is that it ties exclusion from sports wagering, including mobile accounts, to threats of violence against athletes, officials or others involved in a sporting event when the threat is linked to betting. That targets bettor harassment rather than the traditional casino exclusion grounds such as cheating or criminal history. The catch-all in (B)(3) gives the board broad discretion to exclude anyone it deems a threat to effective sports wagering regulation.","watch":"Watch the board's implementing rules for how mobile operators must identify, block and eject listed persons, and how 'willful failure to exclude' under Section 27.2(G) will be applied to online accounts, because that failure is a basis for sanctions and, if it forms a pattern, license suspension or revocation.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"SB325 Act 491","text_chars":6776,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:00:13.297Z"},"LA-HB513":{"summary":"Act 810 rewrites Louisiana's college NIL statute (R.S. 17:3701 et seq.) and, in new R.S. 17:3705, extends NIL rights to high school athletes for the first time. The high school provisions ban NIL compensation tied to a list of restricted categories that includes gaming, gambling, or wagering, expressly including sports wagering. The college provisions update agent rules, raise disclosure obligations to a $600 threshold, broaden institutional liability shields, and require management-board NIL policies.","mechanics":["New R.S. 17:3705(E)(2)(d) bars interscholastic athletes (students in grades 9-12 at public or nonpublic high schools) from earning NIL compensation in connection with \"gaming, gambling, or wagering, including but not limited to sports wagering.\" The same list covers alcohol, cannabis, adult entertainment, tobacco and vapor products, weapons, and performance-enhancing drugs.","Under 3705(B), NIL agreements signed by high school athletes under 18 are valid only with written parental or guardian consent. Under 3705(G), athletes must disclose proposed agreements to the school before signing, and under 3705(J) an aggrieved athlete may sue for injunctive relief.","High schools and athletic associations may not bar NIL earnings or representation, or declare an athlete ineligible for engaging in them (3705(C)-(D)). Schools may still restrict NIL activity during team or academic activities, block deals that conflict with existing school agreements, and control use of school marks (3705(F)).","Under R.S. 17:3703(I), college athletes must disclose NIL contracts worth $600 or more to their institution, with reporting rules aligned to the applicable athletic governing commission.","R.S. 17:3703(D) requires athlete agents to be registered under R.S. 4:422 and comply with the federal SPARTA. Section 3703(L) shields institutions, affiliated nonprofits, and staff from liability over third-party NIL deals, except for gross negligence or intentional misconduct.","Section 3703(N) requires management boards to adopt NIL policies covering compensation, institutional involvement, use of marks, representation, education, enforcement, and a compliance contact. Institutions cannot implement the Chapter until their board acts."],"issue_tags":["advertising"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"Like HI HB 2384 and KY SB 3, LA HB 513 adapts state NIL law to the post-settlement institutional-agreement era, with board-adopted policies and disclosure requirements. Unlike either of those, it extends NIL rights to high school athletes and expressly bars minors' NIL deals involving sports wagering. KY HB 904 is a gaming-regulation bill on fantasy contests and is not comparable in substance. The only overlap is that both touch on who may be marketed to or associated with wagering products.","watch":"Operators and their marketing affiliates should screen influencer and endorsement programs to exclude Louisiana high school athletes under R.S. 17:3705(E)(2)(d). Note that this Act imposes no comparable gaming ban on college athletes, so any restrictions there come from LGCB advertising rules and institutional policies.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"HB513 Act 810","text_chars":16928,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:00:18.886Z"},"HI-HB2384":{"summary":"HB 2384 HD2 adds a new subpart to HRS chapter 304A creating a name, image, and likeness (NIL) framework for University of Hawaii student-athletes. It affirms their right to earn NIL compensation, requires the university to adopt NIL, disclosure, agent, education, and Title IX policies, and requires annual aggregate reporting to the legislature. Its only gaming link is that the university may bar student-athletes from NIL deals involving gambling or sports wagering.","mechanics":["New §304A-(c)(3) lets the university prohibit NIL agreements involving \"gambling, sports wagering, or illegal drugs,\" alongside adult entertainment, firearms, tobacco/vaping, and any industry that conflicts with the university's mission or financial interests.","Student-athletes must disclose third-party NIL deals worth $600 or more, or an alternative threshold set by NCAA rules or federal law. They may rescind any institutional or third-party NIL agreement within at least ten business days of signing.","The university, conferences, and the NCAA may not unduly restrict NIL activity, except to comply with law, post-House settlement governing-body rules, or reasonable, neutral policies on conflicts of interest, time commitments, or prohibited industries.","Annual reports to the legislature begin with the 2028 session and cover state-funded institutional NIL spending by team and gender, compliance measures, and violations. Underlying records are exempt from chapter 92F disclosure.","Section 3 makes a general-fund appropriation for FY2026-27, with the amount left blank, including for direct institutional NIL payments.","Section 5 sets a placeholder effective date of July 1, 3000, a sunset of June 30, 2030, and a planned legislative review in the 2029 session."],"issue_tags":["advertising"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"Like KY SB 3 and LA HB 513, this is a student-athlete NIL bill updating state law after the House v. NCAA settlement. Hawaii's version is limited to the University of Hawaii, whereas LA HB 513 also reaches interscholastic athletes. Unlike KY HB 904, which directly regulates gaming (fantasy contest operators, geolocation), HB 2384 touches wagering only through a permissive prohibited-industries list for NIL endorsements. Its defective 3000 effective date and blank appropriation signal it is still a vehicle for conference negotiation.","watch":"Watch whether the permissive gambling and sports-wagering NIL exclusion in §304A-(c)(3) is hardened into a mandatory ban, or broadened, as the bill moves through EDU and WAM/CPN, since that would limit operator sponsorship and endorsement deals with UH athletes if Hawaii ever authorizes wagering.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"HB2384_HD2","text_chars":13295,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:00:21.330Z"},"HI-HB1308":{"summary":"HB 1308 HD3 SD2 would legalize mobile-only sports wagering in Hawaii under a new HRS chapter regulated by the Department of Law Enforcement, with at least four operator licenses limited to qualified gaming entities and a separate supplier license. It taxes licensees at 10% of adjusted gross sports wagering receipts through a new general excise tax paragraph, section 237-13(9), in lieu of all other taxes on sports wagering, and earmarks part of that revenue for problem gambling and education. It also creates misdemeanor and felony penalties for unlicensed sports wagering and amends the chapter 712 gambling definitions (the supplied text cuts off in Section 3).","mechanics":["Licensing: the Department of Law Enforcement must issue a minimum of four operator licenses (§-5), open only to a 'qualified gaming entity', meaning one already offering online sports wagering in Hawaii or in at least three US jurisdictions. The fee is $250,000 per five-year term plus cost-based processing fees. Supplier licenses cost $10,000 per five-year term. Three-year temporary licenses are available on payment of the initial fee. A universal start date must be set no later than 180 days after the effective date.","Tax: 10% of adjusted gross sports wagering receipts under new §237-13(9), which by its terms applies to both licensed operators and suppliers, on the accrual method. The receipts base is gross wagers less winnings paid, with no express deduction for promotional credits. Of collections, 7% goes to a DOH-run problem gambling special fund and 7% to education programs (§-11, §-12).","Wagering scope: mobile or digital platforms only, with no kiosks or retail terminals. Bettors must be 21 or older and physically in Hawaii unless the department signs an interstate or international agreement (§-9). Covered events include professional, collegiate (with no in-state college carve-out), amateur, Olympic, motorsports and e-sports. Youth events are excluded. Props, in-play, parlays, pools and exchange wagering are expressly included, and fantasy contests are excluded.","Operator duties (§-8, §-10): integrity monitoring for volume and odds irregularities, with reporting to the department; suspicious-bet reporting above an operator-set, department-approved threshold; department-approved house rules; '1-800-GAMBLER' in all advertising; daily receipts records; and an employee betting ban. Layoff wagers between operators are allowed. Accounts may be funded by card, bonuses or promotions, bank transfer or e-wallet.","Responsible gaming: a department-run voluntary self-exclusion program of up to lifetime duration. Operators must make reasonable efforts to stop direct marketing to excluded persons, may share the exclusion list with out-of-state affiliates, and receive the list at least quarterly.","Enforcement: civil fines up to $5,000 per violation or $10,000 per occurrence, applicable to non-licensees (§-13). Unlicensed wagering is a misdemeanor with a fine of at least $10,000 on a first offense, a class C felony with at least $50,000 on a second, and a class B felony with at least $100,000 on a third or later offense (§-14)."],"issue_tags":["legalize","licensing","tax","integrity","advertising"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Like GA HB 686, this is a ground-up legalization act, but Hawaii's version is mobile-only with no retail or tribal/casino tether. It sets a license floor rather than a cap, and access is gated by prior multi-state operating experience. Its 10% rate matches the Colorado rate referenced in CO HB 25-1311, but Hawaii's base already allows deduction only of winnings paid, avoiding the promotional-deduction fight in Colorado. It is well below the rate-hike direction in LA HB 639. Unlike AR HB 1861, which focuses on criminalizing illegal online operators, Hawaii pairs legalization with an escalating felony ladder. It also lacks the deposit-frequency and other consumer-protection limits in CO SB 131.","watch":"Watch whether the bill survives conference after the discharge of House conferees, and whether the drafting that extends the 10% tax to suppliers and the generic 'subject to federal income tax' winnings deduction are fixed. Also watch the department's rulemaking on temporary licenses, the tight 180-day universal start date and the operator-set suspicious-bet threshold.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"HB1308_SD2","text_chars":67585,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:01:22.790Z"},"CO-SB163":{"summary":"Colorado SB 163 is a regulatory clean-up and enforcement bill for the Limited Gaming Act of 1991 and sports betting law. It lets the Limited Gaming Control Commission delegate more licensing duties to the Division of Gaming, authorizes division investigators to inspect and impound premises suspected of unlicensed gaming or sports betting, and extends the voluntary self-exclusion program to sports betting. It also moves game pre-approval from the commission to the division and clarifies the fingerprint-based background check process.","mechanics":["The commission may delegate to the Division of Gaming licensing duties found anywhere in the gaming act, not only the specific duties currently listed.","Division investigators and their supervisors may inspect, examine, investigate, hold, or impound any premises in Colorado where they suspect unlicensed gaming or unlicensed sports betting.","The division's voluntary self-exclusion program, which currently covers gaming, is expanded to cover sports betting.","A designee of the director is a peace officer while performing their duties, with primary authority that includes enforcing all state laws.","Licensees need prior approval from the division, rather than the commission, before offering certain games.","The bill clarifies the definition of 'race meet', adds other gaming definitions, and clarifies how the commission and the Colorado Bureau of Investigation run fingerprint-based background checks on license applicants. Full text was unavailable, so the specific statutory sections and effective date could not be confirmed."],"issue_tags":["licensing","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike the authorization and tax bills on the list (GA HB 686, HI HB 2570 and HB 1308, LA HB 639, IA SF 613 and SSB 1157), CO SB 163 does not touch tax rates or market structure; it is administrative and enforcement housekeeping for an existing market. Its enforcement against unlicensed operators parallels AR HB 1861, which makes operating an illegal online casino or sportsbook a felony, though Colorado uses administrative inspection and impoundment rather than new criminal penalties. Its shift of authority from commission to agency resembles Kentucky's HB 566 corporation clean-up and Kansas SB 356's grant of rulemaking authority to the racing and gaming commission.","watch":"Operators should watch for division rulemaking that sets up the sports-betting self-exclusion program, including how exclusion lists are shared with operators, compliance obligations, and liability for serving excluded patrons, along with any faster or stricter game-approval process now that the division, not the commission, grants approval.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Signed Act","text_chars":0,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:00:42.906Z"},"KY-HB757":{"summary":"HB 757 is Kentucky's 2026 omnibus revenue bill, enacted with an emergency clause as Acts Ch. 161. It updates Kentucky's Internal Revenue Code conformity date to December 31, 2025, decouples from several new federal individual deductions, simplifies the marketplace-provider sales tax nexus test, sunsets the state's three tax increment financing participation programs, and makes technical changes to tax interest and TVA payment reporting. In the portion of the text provided, it does not amend Kentucky's sports wagering licensing or tax provisions.","mechanics":["Section 1 (KRS 139.450): the marketplace-provider collection obligation is triggered only by more than $100,000 in sales. The 200-transaction alternative threshold is deleted, and services provided to Kentucky purchasers are expressly covered.","Section 6 (KRS 141.010(21)): for taxable years beginning on or after January 1, 2026, the Internal Revenue Code is adopted as in effect on December 31, 2025.","Section 7 (KRS 141.019(2)): Kentucky still disallows federal itemized loss deductions under 26 U.S.C. 165 except wagering losses allowed under Section 165(d). Because of the updated conformity date, that carve-out now follows whatever 165(d) says as of December 31, 2025.","Section 7 also decouples from the new federal deductions for qualified tips (sec. 224), overtime (sec. 225) and passenger vehicle loan interest (sec. 163(h)(4)), and from sec. 174A R&E expensing. It limits mortgage interest to acquisition debt on one principal residence for 2026 and later.","Section 5 (KRS 154.30-030): all three state TIF participation programs sunset on the section's effective date. No new applications or amendments to existing agreements are allowed, and previously approved projects are grandfathered.","Section 2 (KRS 131.183): obsolete 2008 transition language is deleted, keeping prime plus 2% on underpayments and prime minus 2% on overpayments. Section 4 redirects TVA regional development agency reports to the Department for Local Government, which must report to the LRC by December 1."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"Iowa's SF 605, SSB 1152 and HSB 91 address state withholding on sports wagering winnings, and Colorado HB 25-1311 and Louisiana HB 639 change operator-level deductions and tax rates. Kentucky HB 757 does none of these, at least in the text provided. Its only gaming touchpoint is the existing individual income tax carve-out for wagering losses under IRC 165(d), now tied to a 2025 federal code. That makes it closer in character to the general tax omnibus bills such as Florida HB 7031 or Arizona SB 1274 than to Georgia HB 686 or Hawaii HB 2570, which are full sports-betting authorization bills.","watch":"The supplied text is cut off partway through Section 8, so check the remaining sections for any changes to Kentucky's wagering excise tax or withholding rules, and confirm whether rolling conformity to the December 31, 2025 IRC imports the federal change limiting 165(d) wagering-loss deductions for Kentucky bettors.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Acts Chapter 161","text_chars":303767,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:01:02.533Z"},"KY-HB904":{"summary":"HB 904 (Acts Ch. 184) creates a licensing regime for paid fantasy contests under the Kentucky Horse Racing and Gaming Corporation, adds fixed-odds wagering on live horse racing to the KRS 138.552 excise tax, and requires licensed online gaming, sports wagering and fantasy operators to block and suspend accounts of people on a child support arrearage registry. It also adds definitions of \"event contract\" and \"prediction market\" to KRS 230.210, and lets the Lottery Corporation and the Corporation issue regulations without the Governor's signature. The supplied text is truncated partway through Section 14, so any operative provisions using the new prediction-market definitions are not visible here.","mechanics":["Fixed-odds horse racing tax (Section 13, amending KRS 138.552): fixed-odds wagers on live horse racing, and parlays containing one, are taxed at the same rates as sports wagering, 9.75% at the track and 14.25% online. These wagers are carved out of adjusted gross sports wagering revenue. The proceeds go to a new purse stabilization fund (Section 12), which must be held at least 1.5 years and can be spent only to supplement live-meet purses, capped at 10% of the fund.","Fantasy contest licensing (Sections 4-11): providers must be licensed and use geolocation, and players must be 18 or older. Fees (Section 10) are: $7,500 initial and $5,000 renewal for providers already licensed as sports wagering service providers; $15,000 and $10,000 for others; $5,000 and $1,000 for providers under $50,000 in adjusted gross fantasy receipts. Existing operators may keep operating if they apply within 90 days of the regulations, which are due within 180 days. Annual CPA audit and compliance testing are required, deferred 24 months for new licensees (Section 7).","Child support registry (Section 3): the Attorney General compiles a registry of people with arrearages of $500 or more per Title IV-D case, and the Corporation passes it to operators monthly. Operators must screen applicants before opening an account and suspend matching existing accounts within 5 business days, with reinstatement within 5 business days of clearance. Knowing violations can lead to license suspension or revocation, and there is a good-faith safe harbor from private suits. Charitable gaming is exempt.","Integrity (Sections 1, 5, 8, 9): new definitions of prohibited and restricted participants (players, coaches, officials, their family members, and owners of more than 5% of a team), plus a fantasy-tampering offense. Providers must notify the Corporation of match fixing, governing-body rule breaches and illegal activity. The Corporation must investigate allegations and refer credible ones to law enforcement and sports governing bodies.","Definitions (Section 1): adds \"event contract\" (keyed to CEA 7 U.S.C. 1a(19)(i)), \"prediction market\" (any platform for event contracts or speculative positions on future events, in or out of state, including a CFTC designated contract market) and \"fixed-odds wagering.\" It removes the horse racing exclusion from the chapter 230 definition of \"sporting event\" but keeps it in the tax-specific definition.","Responsible gaming and rulemaking (Sections 2, 4, 11): fantasy self-exclusion is folded into the existing program, winnings of self-excluded players are forfeited to problem-gaming charities, and a help statement is required on all marketing. Regulations no longer need the Governor's signature (new KRS Chapter 13A section), and the Corporation may not restrict fantasy contests merely by labeling them sports wagering."],"issue_tags":["tax","licensing","integrity","prediction","advertising","legalize"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike LA HB 639, which raises online sports wagering taxes, and CO HB 25-1311, which narrows operators' deductions, KY HB 904 leaves Kentucky's 9.75%/14.25% rates unchanged and instead applies them to a new product, fixed-odds horse racing, with that revenue reserved for purses. Its prediction-market treatment is definitional (at least in the visible text), whereas IA SF 2085 imposes a permit and a tax on event-contract markets. Its child support arrearage screening and suspension mandate for online operators has no counterpart among the listed bills, such as CO SB 131's deposit limits or the HI sports wagering bills (HB 1308, HB 2570).","watch":"Operators should build monthly child-support registry screening with 5-business-day suspension and reinstatement (knowing violations put the license at risk). They should also check how the new prediction-market and event-contract definitions are used in the untruncated remainder of the Act, since the CFTC-DCM language suggests the Corporation may assert jurisdiction over competing event-contract platforms.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Acts Chapter 184","text_chars":271437,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:01:08.543Z"},"LA-SR178":{"summary":"Louisiana Senate Resolution 178 urges and requests the Louisiana Department of Health to study how to modernize the state's problem gambling prevention and education efforts, with a focus on youth, college-age populations and young adults. It is a non-binding simple resolution: it creates no regulatory obligations, but it asks LDH to deliver a written report with findings and recommendations by February 1, 2027.","mechanics":["LDH is urged to study age-appropriate educational resources and curriculum covering gambling-related harms, financial literacy, decision-making, online gaming environments and responsible behavior.","LDH is urged to examine voluntary digital tools and mobile resources for prevention, self-assessment, awareness, early intervention and connection to behavioral health and recovery services.","LDH is to consult with the Department of Education, the Board of Regents, colleges and universities, behavioral health providers, community organizations, researchers and other stakeholders.","A written report is due to the Senate and the David R. Poynter Legislative Research Library under R.S. 24:771 and 772 no later than February 1, 2027.","There are no taxes, fees, operator mandates or enforcement provisions; the whereas clauses cite expanded legal sports wagering and youth exposure through advertising, social media and mobile apps."],"issue_tags":["integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike the Hawaii sports wagering bills (HB 2570 and HB 1308), SR 178 does not legalize anything or impose any tax. Those bills would create licensing regimes and a Problem Gambling fund, while this resolution only commissions a study. It is also far softer than Colorado SB 131, which imposes direct operator limits such as a cap of 6 deposits per gaming day. SR 178 addresses problem gambling purely through public-health education and voluntary tools, and it places no obligations on licensees.","watch":"Watch the February 1, 2027 LDH report for recommendations that could later become legislation, such as youth-focused advertising restrictions, mandated operator funding for prevention programs or required integration with self-assessment and referral tools.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"SR178 Enrolled","text_chars":3394,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:01:24.978Z"},"LA-SB135":{"summary":"SB 135 amends R.S. 27:625(G)(2) and (8) to change how the state treasurer distributes Louisiana's tax on sports wagering net gaming proceeds collected under Subsection B between two dedicated funds. As engrossed, both provisions credit 25% of Subsection B collections, capped at $20 million, to the Louisiana Early Childhood Education Fund (R.S. 17:407.30) and to the Supporting Programs, Opportunities, Resources, and Teams (SPORT) Fund (R.S. 17:1792). It does not change the tax rate or any operator obligation.","mechanics":["Amends R.S. 27:625(G)(2): 25% of Subsection B collections, 'not to exceed twenty million dollars,' goes to the Louisiana Early Childhood Education Fund. The digest describes this as tied to net mobile sports wagering proceeds.","Amends R.S. 27:625(G)(8): adds a $20 million cap on the 25% share of Subsection B collections credited to the SPORT Fund, which was previously uncapped per the digest.","Distributions apply each fiscal year only after the Subsection D requirements are met.","Senate Finance amendments dropped an earlier version that guaranteed the Early Childhood fund the greater of 25% or $20 million, removing that floor.","Effective July 1, 2027, moved from July 1, 2026 by committee amendment. Passed the Senate and on 2026-05-18 was referred to the House Committee on Administration of Criminal Justice."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. This bill is unusual because it only reallocates already-collected sports wagering tax revenue between two education and youth funds, with no change to rates, licensing or conduct rules. The digest also conflicts with the bill text: it says the $20 million cap on the Early Childhood fund is removed, yet the visible engrossed text keeps the cap. The lost strikethrough formatting makes the final effect on that fund uncertain.","watch":"The bill does not touch operator tax rates as engrossed, but in the House it is a vehicle on R.S. 27:625, so watch for amendments that raise the Subsection B rate or change the deductions behind net gaming proceeds to fund these capped dedications.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"SB135 Engrossed","text_chars":4442,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:01:34.878Z"},"IA-SF660":{"summary":"SF 660 does not change how sports wagering is taxed, licensed or conducted in Iowa; it spends money from the sports wagering receipts fund (Iowa Code 8.57I). It adds two ongoing claims on the fund and four one-time FY2025-26 appropriations. It also creates a new Iowa major events and tourism grant program (new chapter 15G) and repeals the sports tourism marketing fund (15F.403), moving that fund's balance into the new program.","mechanics":["Sec. 1 amends 8.57I(3) to make a standing annual appropriation of $8 million from the sports wagering receipts fund to the public safety equipment fund (80.48), starting FY2025-26, exempt from the reporting rules in subsections 4 and 5.","Sec. 2 adds 8.57I(3A): from FY2026-27, the fund must top up the length of service award program grant fund (100B.52) to a $1.5 million balance each July 1. This takes effect only if HF 1002 is enacted (Sec. 8) and is repealed July 1, 2030.","Secs. 3-6 make one-time FY2025-26 appropriations from the fund: $14 million for education support personnel pay, split among school districts by budget enrollment and effective on enactment (Sec. 7); $5 million for the special education division; $4 million to the film production incentive fund, contingent on SF 657; and $4 million to the new major events and tourism fund.","Division II creates chapter 15G. The economic development authority board may give grants to Iowa nonprofit economic development and tourism entities for bid, hosting, sanctioning and operating costs of athletic contests, conventions and music or art festivals. Recipients must put up a 50% match, administration is capped at 5% of the fund, and funding decisions are exempt from chapter 17A.","Division III narrows chapter 15F to an infrastructure-only program and repeals the sports tourism marketing fund (15F.403), transferring its remaining balance to the 15G fund (Sec. 23). Entities cannot receive assistance under both programs."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. What stands out is that the title says the bill relates to sports wagering, yet it contains nothing on operators, tax rates, licensing or wagering conduct. Its only link to wagering is using the 8.57I receipts fund as a funding source. The notable step is turning a fund previously spent 'as directed by the general assembly' into a source of permanent and formula-driven claims: the $8 million yearly public safety appropriation and the uncapped length of service award top-up.","watch":"The new standing claims on the sports wagering receipts fund (the $8 million yearly public safety appropriation and the open-ended length of service award top-up) make the state more dependent on sports wagering revenue, which could add pressure in future sessions to raise the wagering tax or fees if receipts fall short.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Enrolled with Governor's Action","text_chars":24603,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:01:47.043Z"},"LA-HB639":{"summary":"Act 298 raises Louisiana's tax on net gaming proceeds from online sports wagering (website or mobile app) from 15% to 21.5% under R.S. 27:625(B), leaving retail wagering through a sports wagering mechanism untouched. It redirects 25% of online sports wagering tax collections to a new Supporting Programs, Opportunities, Resources, and Teams (SPORTS) Fund for Division I university athletic departments and 3% to the Louisiana Postsecondary Inclusive Education Fund, with the remainder going to the state general fund. Despite the digest title, the enrolled text contains no video poker provisions.","mechanics":["R.S. 27:625(B): online sports wagering tax rises from 15% to 21.5% of net gaming proceeds; electronic wagering through an in-person sports wagering mechanism is expressly excluded.","R.S. 27:625(G)(8): 25% of Subsection B collections go to the new SPORTS Fund (R.S. 17:1792), after Subsection D obligations are met.","R.S. 27:625(G)(9)-(10): 3% goes to the Louisiana Postsecondary Inclusive Education Fund (R.S. 17:3138.11), and any remainder goes to the state general fund.","R.S. 17:1792: Board of Regents distributes SPORTS Fund money equally among public universities in FBS and FCS conferences. Permitted uses are scholarships, insurance, medical coverage, facility enhancements, litigation settlement fees and Alston awards, and the money may not supplant other awards.","The text states no separate effective date or change to deductions, licensing or enforcement. The fiscal note estimates about +$34.7 million in revenue."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Colorado HB 25-1311 raises effective tax by restricting operators' deductions against the existing 10% rate, whereas Louisiana HB 639 simply raises the headline online rate by 6.5 points and leaves the deduction base alone. Iowa SF 613 and SSB 1157 combine tax rate and license fee changes with gambling enforcement and dedicate revenue to tourism and major events; Louisiana instead earmarks a fixed share to college athletics, including Alston awards and litigation settlement costs. Hawaii HB 1308 and HB 2570 and Georgia HB 686 would legalize and tax sports betting from scratch, while Iowa SF 605 deals only with winnings withholding.","watch":"Operators should watch the split between online and retail rates and the definition of \"sports wagering mechanism\" in 27:625(B), and should expect the college-athletics earmark to create a constituency for further online rate increases.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"HB639 Act 298","text_chars":4429,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:01:59.013Z"},"CO-SB131":{"summary":"SB 26-131 layers consumer-protection limits onto Colorado's existing sports betting regime. It bans solicitation push notifications, caps deposits at six per gaming day, prohibits credit card funding, and restricts advertising aimed at under-21 audiences. It also requires annual transactional data submissions, tightens integrity reporting to sports governing bodies, and lets leagues petition to restrict any type of wager on their events.","mechanics":["Online operators may not send push notifications, including texts shown when the app is not open, that solicit bets or deposits (44-30-1501(8.7), 44-30-1506(8)(b)). Deposits are capped at six per individual per operator-defined 24-hour gaming day (1506(8)(a)(II)).","Accepting credit card deposits, directly or indirectly (including via accounts funded by a credit card), is unlawful under 44-30-1511(1)(d). Violations carry a monetary penalty of up to $25,000, in addition to or instead of suspension or revocation (44-30-524(1)(b)(III)).","New 44-30-1506.5 bars operators and their marketing affiliates (paid promoters, excluding broadcasters) from targeting under-21s or creating content clearly meant for them. It also bars advertising on any media whose audience is reasonably expected to be majority under-21, with an exception for public venues where attendee demographics cannot be determined.","Integrity reports under 1506(5)(a)(II)-(III) must now be made 'as soon as practicable' and sent simultaneously to the relevant sports governing body. The division and operators must use 'reasonable efforts' to cooperate with league investigations, replacing the earlier 'good and sufficient reason' standard. Leagues may petition the commission to restrict, limit, or exclude any type of wager on their events, no longer only single-player-outcome bets (1505(7)).","Starting February 1, 2028, and every February 1 after, online operators must submit all transactional data and metrics for the prior year, redacted of PII and exempt from CORA. The division publishes a compiled report by January 1, 2029 and every three years after (44-30-1507.5).","The act takes effect August 12, 2026 (assuming May 13 adjournment), unless a referendum petition is filed, and applies to conduct and agreements on or after that date. It also appropriates $124,623 and 0.5 FTE to the Department of Law, and requires the sports betting fund's water plan transfer to be no less than the prior year's (1509(2)(e))."],"issue_tags":["advertising","integrity","prop"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike the authorization bills listed (GA HB 686, HI HB 2570 and HB 1308), which create licensing and tax frameworks for new markets, Colorado's SB 131 imposes operational restrictions on a mature legal market. It also differs from tax-focused measures such as LA HB 639 and enforcement-against-illegal-operators bills such as AR HB 1861, because it targets licensed operators' marketing, payment, and engagement practices. Its combination of a hard deposit-frequency cap, a solicitation push-notification ban, and a credit card prohibition with a specific $25,000 penalty is unusual among the listed bills, as is mandatory annual data disclosure feeding a public triennial report.","watch":"Operators should watch how the commission interprets several open provisions: the scope of 'relating to the solicitation of bets or deposits' for push notifications, 'indirectly' funded credit card deposits (such as e-wallets or PayPal backed by cards), affiliate liability under the majority-under-21 audience test, and the breadth of 'all transactional data and metrics,' since these will drive compliance costs from August 12, 2026 onward.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Signed Act","text_chars":11492,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:02:00.054Z"},"IA-SSB1157":{"summary":"SSB 1157 (renumbered SF 613) raises Iowa's sports wagering tax on net receipts from 6.75% to 9%. It replaces the graduated casino adjusted gross receipts tax with flat rates and doubles the casino annual license fee. It also shifts DCI gaming enforcement funding from licensee regulatory fees to an off-the-top credit of AGR tax, and redirects sports wagering receipts to public safety equipment ($8M a year) and a new $15M Iowa Major Events and Tourism Fund.","mechanics":["Sports wagering tax under Iowa Code 99F.11(5)(a) rises from 6.75% to 9% of sports wagering net receipts (Sec. 15); Division I takes effect January 1, 2026 (Sec. 16).","The casino AGR tax drops the 5% (first $1M) and 10% (next $2M) tiers and the variable racetrack rates; it becomes a flat 22% for excursion boats and gambling structures and 24.125% for racetrack enclosures, whether or not they hold a table games license (Secs. 11-12).","The annual license fee for excursion gambling boats and gambling structures doubles from $5 to $10 per person of capacity (Sec. 6).","The DCI enforcement-cost component of the 99D.14 and 99F.10 regulatory fees is struck (Secs. 3-10). From FY2026-27, AGR tax is instead credited to the gaming enforcement revolving fund up to the amount appropriated to DPS, before the remainder goes to the rebuild Iowa infrastructure fund (Sec. 14). Internet fantasy sports background-check fees under 99E.4(4) are unchanged.","Beginning FY2026-27, $8M a year is appropriated from the sports wagering receipts fund to the public safety equipment fund (Sec. 1). A one-time $15M for FY2025-26 goes to the new Iowa Major Events and Tourism Fund under new chapter 15G (Sec. 21).","The new 15G program gives grants to Iowa nonprofits for bid, hosting and sanctioning costs of major events, with a 50% match requirement and a 5% administrative cap (Secs. 17-20). The sports tourism marketing fund (15F.403) is repealed and its balance transferred to the new fund (Secs. 31-32)."],"issue_tags":["tax","licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Like LA HB 639, which raises Louisiana's online sports wagering tax and dedicates gaming revenue, SSB 1157 combines a sports betting rate increase with earmarks of the proceeds. Its 9% rate on net receipts remains low by national standards. CO HB 25-1311 takes a different route to more revenue: it keeps Colorado's 10% rate but narrows the deductions operators can take in calculating net proceeds, whereas Iowa raises the headline rate and leaves the net receipts base untouched. Unlike GA HB 686 and HI HB 1308/HB 2570, which would legalize and build sports wagering regimes from scratch, Iowa's bill adjusts an existing market and pairs the sports tax change with a flattening of land-based casino AGR taxes that eliminates the low-revenue tiers.","watch":"Operators should watch whether the 9% rate survives in SF 613 or is raised further, and how a January 1, 2026 effective date applies to a tax imposed on receipts 'each fiscal year', which could mean mid-year proration or partial-year compliance issues. Casino-affiliated licensees should also watch the loss of the 5%/10% AGR tiers and the doubled per-capacity license fee.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":40137,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:02:17.919Z"},"CO-HB1168":{"summary":"HB1168 is a supplemental appropriation bill adjusting the current fiscal year budget of the Colorado Department of Revenue. Only the official abstract is available, so the specific line items, amounts, and any changes affecting the Division of Gaming, which sits within the Department of Revenue and regulates limited gaming and sports betting, cannot be confirmed from the text provided.","mechanics":["Makes a supplemental (mid-year) appropriation to the Colorado Department of Revenue, per the official abstract.","Specific dollar amounts, funding sources, and divisions affected are not available because the full text was not provided.","The Department of Revenue houses the Division of Gaming, so gaming-related cash funds or spending authority may be adjusted, but this cannot be verified from the abstract.","Signed by the Governor on 2026-03-12; supplemental appropriations typically take effect on signature for the current fiscal year."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states were listed. Supplemental appropriation bills like this are routine budget housekeeping. Based on the abstract alone, it does not appear to change sports-betting policy, tax rates, or licensing rules.","watch":"Pull the chaptered text and check any Division of Gaming or Sports Betting Fund line items for changes in spending authority, staffing, or fund transfers that could signal shifts in regulatory or enforcement capacity.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":0,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:42:15.128Z"},"CA-AB1840":{"summary":"AB 1840 adds Government Code Section 87451 to bar public officials, designated employees, and lobbyists from knowingly trading prediction market contracts on elections, administrative actions, legislative actions, or governmental decisions in their official duties while holding, or reasonably expecting to obtain through those duties, material nonpublic information about the transaction. It also amends Section 86205 so that a violation of 87451 is a prohibited lobbyist act. Violations carry Political Reform Act penalties, including misdemeanor liability.","mechanics":["Covered persons (Sec. 87451(b)(1)): elected or appointed state or local public officials, Section 87200 filers, designated employees under Section 82019, and lobbyists under Section 82039.","Covered transactions (Sec. 87451(b)(2)): purchase, sale, or exchange of a prediction market contract on administrative actions or elections (both as defined in Sec. 82002), on governmental decisions made in the covered person's official duties, or on actions before the Legislature (Sec. 87102.5(b)(1)).","Prediction market contract (Sec. 87451(b)(4)): any contract, derivative, financial instrument, or investment issued, listed, or offered by a business entity that relates to the outcome, occurrence, or nonoccurrence of a future event; the definition is not limited to CFTC-registered platforms.","Knowledge standard: the person must knowingly trade while possessing material nonpublic information, or while it is reasonably foreseeable they may obtain it in their official duties. MNPI uses a 'reasonable investor' significance test.","Enforcement: misdemeanor under the Political Reform Act. The bill amends an initiative statute, so it needs a 2/3 vote of each house. It is fiscal and contains a state-mandated local program with no reimbursement (Sec. 3).","Status: introduced February 11, 2026; the second committee hearing was canceled at the author's request on April 9, 2026."],"issue_tags":["prediction","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"AB 1840 is closest to NY S09825, which also restricts public officers and legislative staff from trading on information gained in official duties. The New York bill also reaches mobile sports wagering platforms, while AB 1840 covers only prediction market contracts on political and governmental events and also reaches lobbyists and local officials. Unlike the platform-facing regimes in IL HB5059 and NY S09414 (ORACLE Acts), MN HF 4437/SF 4511, NC H1171, and VT H0913, it imposes no obligations or bans on operators and does not treat prediction markets as gambling (contrast IL HB5142). It also contains no tax (contrast NJ A5336/S4447) and no age limits (contrast CT HB05038).","watch":"Operators listing election or policy event contracts should watch whether amendments add platform-side duties such as KYC screening of covered persons, reporting, or account restrictions, and should note that the broad 'prediction market contract' definition could later be borrowed for operator-facing regulation; the canceled hearing suggests the bill may be stalled this session.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":6436,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:42:18.601Z"},"AK-HB145":{"summary":"HB 145 creates a new AS 05.18 authorizing mobile-only sports wagering in Alaska, administered by the Commissioner of Revenue, with up to 10 licenses at $100,000 per year and a 20 percent tax on adjusted gross revenue. Applicants must already hold a mobile sports wagering license (directly or through an affiliate or partner) in at least three other states. The bill also excludes AS 05.18 activity from the criminal definition of gambling and authorizes interstate wagering agreements.","mechanics":["Licensing (AS 05.18.010): cap of 10 licenses. The license fee is $100,000 for the initial license and for each annual renewal, plus an optional processing fee. Licenses are non-transferable and require licensure in at least three other states. If applicants exceed slots, the commissioner awards licenses on past experience, projected AGR and projected tax revenue. Applicants must disclose holders of 15 percent or more and submit fingerprint-based or recent out-of-state criminal history checks.","Tax (AS 05.18.090): 20 percent of adjusted gross revenue, defined as wagers less winnings, voided bets and federal excise taxes. Promotional credits are not deductible. Returns and electronic payment are due by the 15th of each month. Negative AGR may be carried forward for up to three years with no carryback or refund unless the license is surrendered. Licensees are exempt from municipal wagering taxes.","Permitted wagers (AS 05.18.060 and .070): single-game bets, parlays, teasers, props, in-play bets, futures, pools and exchange wagering. Wagering events include collegiate and esports events but exclude events where most participants are under 18, other than the Olympics. Bettors must be 21 or older and located in Alaska or in a jurisdiction with an interstate agreement. Layoff wagers between licensees are allowed.","Operator duties (AS 05.18.030 and .050): integrity monitoring software and reporting of suspicious activity, including under 31 C.F.R. 1021.320 SAR rules. Operators must verify identity, advertise to no one under 21, obtain approval of house rules, and display 1-800-GAMBLER. Accounts may be funded by credit card, bonuses and promotions, ACH or e-wallets.","Enforcement (AS 05.18.110): penalties of up to $10,000 per violation, plus suspension or revocation and power to require termination of employees. Section 1 also requires ABC Board liquor license suspension for repeat AS 05.18 violations. Fantasy contests are excluded from the definition.","Effective dates: January 1, 2026, with the regulations section effective immediately. Operations cannot begin until at least three licenses are issued, and the public must receive 14 days' notice."],"issue_tags":["legalize","licensing","tax","integrity","advertising","prop"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Like GA HB 686 and the Hawaii bills (HB 1308, HB 2570, SB1569), HB 145 would legalize sports wagering from scratch, but it is mobile-only and housed in the Department of Revenue rather than a gaming commission. The Hawaii bills use a general excise tax on operator licenses, whereas Alaska uses a flat 20 percent AGR tax. HB 145's $100,000 annual fee is far below the $15 million license fee proposed in IL HB2878 and HB5034. Its explicit municipal tax exemption parallels IL HB4171's preemption of local sports wagering taxes. Unlike CT HB05229, it contains only a minimal under-21 advertising restriction and no AI or withdrawal rules.","watch":"Operators should watch whether the 20 percent rate, the lack of a promotional-credit deduction, the three-state licensure prerequisite and the 10-license cap with discretionary selection survive committee review in Labor and Commerce and in Finance. They should also watch whether the commissioner's regulations, which may apply retroactively under Section 6(b), add burdens beyond the statute.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":26203,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:42:21.835Z"},"CO-SB169":{"summary":"SB169 is Colorado's annual Revisor's Bill, which makes nonsubstantive technical corrections across the Colorado Revised Statutes and amends or repeals obsolete, imperfect, or inoperative language while preserving existing legislative intent. Only the official abstract is available, so it is not possible to confirm whether any gaming or sports-betting provisions (e.g., C.R.S. Title 44, Article 30, Part 15) are touched.","mechanics":["Scope: nonsubstantive revision of the Colorado Revised Statutes as amended, per the official abstract.","Function: amends or repeals obsolete, imperfect, and inoperative law; by its terms it is not meant to change the intent, effect, or meaning of existing law.","Status: signed by the Governor on 2026-06-02.","The full chaptered text was unavailable, so specific sections amended, effective dates, and any gaming-related cleanups cannot be identified from the material provided."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. Revisor's bills like this one are routine housekeeping measures and are unusual only in that they can sweep in technical edits across any title, including gaming statutes, without a substantive policy debate.","watch":"Once the chaptered text is available, check whether any cross-references, definitions, or repealed provisions in Colorado's sports-betting or limited-gaming statutes were altered, since even technical edits can affect compliance citations or licensing language.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":0,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:42:22.768Z"},"AK-SB194":{"summary":"SB 194 legalizes statewide mobile-only sports wagering in Alaska under a new AS 05.18, administered by the Department of Revenue. It allows up to 10 one-year licenses at $100,000 each and taxes adjusted gross revenue at 20 percent. It also carves licensed wagering out of the criminal gambling definition in AS 11.66.280(3) and adds fingerprint-based background checks for licensees.","mechanics":["Licensing (AS 05.18.010): applicants must hold, or have a subsidiary, partner or joint operating agreement with someone holding, a mobile sports wagering license in at least three other states, and must show their platform has been certified by an independent lab in another US jurisdiction. The fee is $100,000 per year plus a discretionary processing fee. Licenses are non-transferable and capped at 10; if oversubscribed, the commissioner picks based on experience, projected AGR and projected tax revenue.","Tax (AS 05.18.090): 20 percent of adjusted gross revenue, defined as handle less winnings, voided bets and federal excise tax, with no deduction for promotional credits. Returns and payment are due electronically by the 15th of each month. Negative AGR may be carried forward for up to three years, with no carryback or refund unless the licensee surrenders its license. Licensees are exempt from municipal wagering taxes.","Permitted wagers (AS 05.18.060 and .190): explicitly includes proposition, in-play, parlays, teasers, pools, exchange wagering and futures. Wagering events cover collegiate, amateur, professional, esports and motor racing, but exclude events where most participants are under 18 (Olympics excepted). Fantasy contests are excluded from the definition.","Player and conduct rules: bettors must be 21 or older and physically in Alaska or in a jurisdiction with an interstate agreement. Licensees may not advertise to under-21s, must display 1-800-GAMBLER, must get house rules approved, must report suspicious activity including SAR-type disclosures under 31 C.F.R. 1021.320, and may accept funding by card, bank transfer, online payment systems and bonuses.","Enforcement: civil penalties of up to $10,000 per violation, plus suspension, revocation, restriction, cease-and-desist and closure orders. Application information stays confidential for 25 years.","Timing: the Act takes effect January 1, 2026, with the regulations section effective immediately. Operations cannot start until at least three licenses are issued and the Department gives 14 days' public notice."],"issue_tags":["legalize","tax","licensing","prop","advertising","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Like GA HB 686 and the Hawaii bills (HB 1308, HB 2570, SB1569), SB 194 is a ground-up legalization bill. Unlike the Hawaii bills, which place regulation in the Department of Law Enforcement or DBEDT and use a general excise tax on operator licenses, Alaska gives the Department of Revenue sole regulatory authority and imposes a flat 20 percent AGR tax. Its $100,000 annual fee is modest compared with the $15 million master license fee proposed in IL HB2878 and HB5034. Its express exemption from municipal wagering taxes mirrors the home-rule preemption in IL HB4171.","watch":"Watch the three-other-states licensure prerequisite and the 10-license cap with selection based partly on projected tax revenue, both of which favour incumbent national operators, as well as the AGR definition that does not allow a deduction for promotional play at the 20 percent rate.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":26196,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:42:26.801Z"},"DC-B26-0034":{"summary":"This DC emergency act approves six modifications to Contract No. DCAM-22-CS-RFP-0026 with Chiaramonte Construction Company for renovations to the Public Safety Communications Center building and authorizes payment of up to $13,693,879.06. It contains nothing about sports betting, gaming, wagering or any related regulatory subject.","mechanics":["Sec. 2 approves Modification Nos. 1 through 6 under section 451 of the Home Rule Act (D.C. Official Code § 1-204.51), notwithstanding section 202 of the Procurement Practices Reform Act of 2010.","Sec. 2 authorizes payment in a not-to-exceed amount of $13,693,879.06 for goods and services received and to be received.","Sec. 2 defines the scope as construction services for the Public Safety Communications Center building renovations.","Sec. 3 adopts the CFO's fiscal impact statement.","Sec. 4 makes it an emergency act effective on Mayoral approval and lasting no more than 90 days. It was published as A26-0009 and expires May 21, 2025."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills is comparable. Those bills address sports-wagering authorization and tax (AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, HI SB1569), license fees and definitions (IL HB2878, IL HB5142), advertising and consumer protections (CT HB05229) or broader gaming reform (FL H0189). This DC act is a routine procurement approval for a public-safety building contract and does not mention wagering at all. It was likely matched on keywords or by error.","watch":"Nothing for a sports-betting operator to watch here: this is an expired, gaming-unrelated construction contract approval and can be removed from the tracking list.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":2407,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:42:28.658Z"},"CT-HB5038":{"summary":"Governor's Bill 5038 imposes consumer-protection conduct rules on prediction market platforms serving Connecticut residents: a 21+ age floor with age and geolocation verification, mandatory voluntary self-exclusion and spend-limit tools, and advertising restrictions aimed at under-21 audiences and college campuses, enforced by Attorney General civil penalties. It does not license, tax or ban prediction markets. It expressly carves out sports wagering, online casino gaming and internet games regulated under chapter 229b, and includes a federal-preemption savings clause. A separate section directs DCP, consulting the AG and the Mohegan and Mashantucket Pequot tribes, to study prediction markets' effects, including on master wagering licensee revenue, and report by February 1, 2027.","mechanics":["Definitions (Sec. 1(a)): a 'prediction market' is any system letting state residents open speculative positions on future-event outcomes in a bid-ask format, regardless of mechanism. It excludes chapter 229b sports wagering, online casino gaming and internet games, and bid-asks tracking security, commodity or interest-rate prices or broad economic measures.","Age and access (Sec. 1(b)-(d)): no registration or positions for anyone under 21. Platforms must verify age 21+ and physical presence in the state before an account opens, and must offer self-exclusion from account creation or trading plus spend limits. On discovering an underage user, the platform must suspend the account, close all positions, pay out funds and bar the user until age 21.","Advertising (Sec. 2): every ad must state the 21+ requirement, and direct or targeted messages need a one-click unsubscribe link. Barred: ads aimed exclusively or primarily at under-21s or college campuses, targeting of self-excluded consumers, imagery or celebrity endorsements appealing to under-21s, misleading content, and placement in media or audiences that are majority or presumed majority under 21.","Enforcement (Sec. 3): AG civil penalty up to $10,000 per violation, rising to $50,000 per violation for a persistent course of conduct. DCP is to adopt implementing regulations (Sec. 4).","Effective dates: Secs. 1-5 take effect July 1, 2027. The Sec. 6 study takes effect July 1, 2026, with its report due February 1, 2027. Sec. 5 provides that any provision a court finds preempted by conflicting federal law yields to federal law."],"issue_tags":["prediction","advertising","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"CT HB05038 closely tracks the age-21, registration and verification framework in IL HB5059 and NY S09414 (both ORACLE Acts), but strips it down to a minors-and-advertising regime with AG penalties and no licensing. It contrasts with MN HF 4437/SF 4511 and NC H1171, which would prohibit prediction market wagering outright, and with IL HB5142, which would fold sports-event prediction markets into the Sports Wagering Act; Connecticut instead expressly excludes chapter 229b sports wagering from its definition. Unlike NJ A5336/S4447, it imposes no tax, and its advertising restrictions resemble NY S 10092's minors-focused approach. Distinctively, the Sec. 6 study must measure prediction markets' impact on tribal master wagering licensee revenue and state payments.","watch":"Operators should watch whether the Sec. 6 study (consulting the Mohegan and Mashantucket Pequot tribes, report due February 1, 2027) becomes the basis for later legislation treating sports-event contracts as unlicensed sports wagering, and whether DCP regulations or preemption litigation over CFTC-regulated event contracts reshape the bill's light-touch, non-licensing approach before its July 1, 2027 effective date.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":8885,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:42:32.496Z"},"CT-HB5229":{"summary":"Public Act 26-53 tightens Connecticut's consumer-protection and advertising rules for online gaming and sports wagering licensees. It newly bars gaming advertising in college athletic facilities and on college-run digital channels, and requires geolocation controls for fantasy-only and online lottery/keno platforms. It also orders a Department of Consumer Protection study of prediction markets (report due February 1, 2027) and revises lottery game testing and certification procedures.","mechanics":["Sec. 1 (eff. July 1, 2026) amends CGS 12-863(c). Master wagering licensees, online gaming operators, service providers and sports wagering retailers must set up a toll-free number for help resolving platform problems; a prerecorded message routing users to online help is allowed. The number must be displayed conspicuously on each website and app, alongside the existing problem-gambling line, self-exclusion link, time-on-site display, break-in-play tool and balance display.","Sec. 2 (eff. July 1, 2026) amends 12-863(e). A new subdivision (9) bars gaming advertising in athletic facilities on any public or private college campus in Connecticut, and on any website, social media, online service or app run by or for such an institution. The only exception is an announcement, signage or display directed to a general audience.","Sec. 2 also bars ads pertaining exclusively to keno, online lottery or fantasy contests from TV programs aimed primarily at under-18s, judged by a nationally recognized content rating system, and from ad breaks during those programs. The existing ban on targeting self-excluded individuals now expressly covers Internet games and retail sports wagering.","Sec. 3 (eff. from passage) directs the DCP Commissioner to study prediction market platforms, consulting the Attorney General, the Mohegan Tribe, the Mashantucket Pequot Tribe and the Connecticut Lottery Corporation. Topics include under-21 use and advertising, problem gambling, the most common positions taken, and effects on master wagering licensee revenue and state payments. The report is due February 1, 2027.","Sec. 4 (eff. from passage) covers platforms offering exclusively fantasy contests or online lottery draw/keno sales. Their geolocation systems must defeat or block proxies and VPNs, check location at each entry fee or purchase, block transactions when location cannot be determined and show a pop-up, and flag geographically inconsistent account access.","Sec. 5 amends CGS 12-806a(c). Lottery draw games sold in at least 20 states and tested by a lab licensed in at least 20 states are exempt from Connecticut lab certification. DCP may publish technical standards, with updates effective 30 days after posting. Revocation authority and the 'without notice' language are removed; suspension is now allowed where continued operation 'could reasonably cause substantial detriment to the public interest.'"],"issue_tags":["advertising","prediction","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike IL HB5059 (ORACLE Act), which would directly bar under-21s from prediction markets and impose platform duties, Connecticut stops at a study. That study expressly measures prediction markets' effect on master wagering licensee revenue and state payments, which signals possible protective legislation in 2027. Bills such as AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686 and HI HB 1308/SB1569 would create new sports wagering markets. HB05229 instead fine-tunes an existing regime's responsible-gaming, marketing and geolocation rules. Its campus-advertising ban is a targeted conduct rule rather than the broad market overhaul in FL H0189.","watch":"Operators should audit college sponsorships, in-venue signage and university digital placements against the new Sec. 2(9) campus advertising ban, and have the new customer-assistance toll-free number live and displayed by July 1, 2026. They should also track the February 2027 prediction-market report, since its revenue-impact findings could drive competitive or restrictive legislation.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":16323,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:42:41.970Z"},"DC-B26-0340":{"summary":"This is D.C.'s omnibus FY2026 Budget Support emergency act, and nearly all of it deals with non-gaming budget matters. Two subtitles in Title II are gaming-relevant. Subtitle G (Secs. 2061-2064) carves bingo, sports wagering and games of skill out of the D.C. gaming-contract statute (D.C. Code § 16-1702) and reworks game-of-skill machine licensing between the Office of Lottery and Gaming (OLG) and the Alcoholic Beverage and Cannabis Administration (ABCA); Subtitle H (Secs. 2071-2073) creates a new 'commercial bingo' category limited to on-premises alcohol licensees.","mechanics":["Sec. 2062 adds § 16-1702(b)-(d) so that subsection (a) does not apply to: commercial bingo under § 36-601.01 et seq.; sports wagering under § 36-621.01 et seq. as of May 3, 2019; and games of skill under § 36-641.01 et seq. as of April 27, 2021. This is a retroactive carve-out from the gaming-contract statute.","Games of skill (Secs. 2063-2064): a game of skill retailer must hold a retailer's license plus a game of skill machine endorsement under § 25-113.01(e). ABCA, not OLG, processes and issues retailer's licenses (new § 36-641.08(a-1)). OLG must promptly notify ABCA of any citation, revocation or other enforcement action (new § 36-641.16(f)). A § 25-508 fee is raised from $200 to $500/year.","Commercial bingo is defined as bingo played for consideration, with numbers drawn and called aloud by a person physically present with the players (new § 36-601.01(c)(4A)). Bingo is defined as a 75-ball game on 5x5 cards. The definition excludes charitable bingo under § 36-601.23(b)-(d), bingo played on federal gambling devices (15 U.S.C. 1171(a)), and online or class II facsimile play.","Only on-premises alcohol licensees in the listed classes (C/R, D/R, C/T, D/T, C/H, D/H, C/X, D/X, C/RB, D/RB, C/N, D/N) may offer commercial bingo. They need both an ABC Board endorsement ($300/year) and an OLG license, and OLG may not issue any commercial bingo license before October 1, 2025.","Sec. 2073(b) renames § 36-601.12 the 'Lottery, Gambling, and Gaming Fund' and excludes from it funds generated by the tax under §§ 47-2002(a)(9) and 47-2202(a)(6). The rate of that tax is not shown in the supplied text.","The CFO must adopt rules on the conduct of bingo and raffles to ensure integrity and fair play (rewritten § 36-601.24). The supplied text is truncated at this point, so the remaining rule requirements are not visible."],"issue_tags":["licensing","legalize","tax"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. What stands out is that a budget support act retroactively exempts legal sports wagering (back to May 3, 2019) and games of skill (back to April 27, 2021) from D.C.'s gaming-contract statute. Effectively, this is a legislative fix that shores up the enforceability of wagering transactions. The act also creates a narrowly fenced commercial bingo product: in person, live-called, alcohol-licensee only, and expressly excluding online or electronic play.","watch":"Operators should confirm how the retroactive § 16-1702(c) carve-out for sports wagering affects litigation risk over pre-enactment wagers, and should watch whether the truncated bingo provisions or the separate §§ 47-2002(a)(9)/47-2202(a)(6) tax are later extended toward electronic or online formats.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":226903,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:42:48.611Z"},"DC-B26-0620":{"summary":"This emergency act approves, under section 451 of the Home Rule Act, the Amended and Restated Lease Agreement between the Washington Convention and Sports Authority (Events DC) and Washington Nationals Stadium, LLC for Nationals Ballpark, as submitted on March 2, 2026, and authorizes Events DC to enter into it notwithstanding the Procurement Practices Reform Act of 2010 and the 1939 surplus-property sale statute. The enrolled text contains no provisions on sports wagering, gaming, taxation of wagers, or advertising. Any betting-related terms would sit in the underlying lease, which is not reproduced here.","mechanics":["Sec. 2: Council approval of the Amended and Restated Lease Agreement submitted by Events DC on March 2, 2026, with waivers of the Procurement Practices Reform Act of 2010 (D.C. Code § 2-351.01 et seq.) and the 1939 real-estate sale act (D.C. Code § 10-801 et seq.).","Sec. 2: Approval is given under Home Rule Act § 451 (D.C. Code § 1-204.51), which requires Council approval of multiyear contracts.","Sec. 3: Adopts the Budget Director's fiscal impact statement. No figures are included in the text.","Sec. 4: Emergency act effective on Mayoral approval and limited to 90 days. Published as A26-0313 at 73 DCR 006836, it expires July 23, 2026.","The text contains no wagering definitions, rates, licensing terms or enforcement provisions."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No other states' bills are listed for comparison. This act is unusual only in that it is a bare contract-approval vehicle. Any operative terms, including any concession, naming or sponsorship rights that could cover an on-site sportsbook, live in the separately submitted lease document rather than in the act. It neither amends nor references the District's sports wagering statute.","watch":"Obtain the March 2, 2026 lease itself and check whether it reallocates sportsbook concession, exclusivity, sponsorship or revenue-sharing rights at Nationals Park, and track the companion temporary and permanent bills that will replace this act after it expires on July 23, 2026.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":2258,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:42:53.307Z"},"DC-PR26-0566":{"summary":"This DC Council resolution confirms the Mayor's appointment of Kristi C. Whitfield (Ward 5) to the Board of Library Trustees for a term ending January 5, 2029. It has no connection to sports betting, gaming, or wagering.","mechanics":["Sec. 2 confirms Kristi C. Whitfield as a member of the Board of Library Trustees, established under D.C. Official Code § 39-104 (Act of June 3, 1896, 29 Stat. 244).","The term ends January 5, 2029.","Sec. 3 directs the Council to send a copy of the resolution to the nominee and the Mayor.","Sec. 4 makes it effective immediately. It was adopted March 31, 2026 as Resolution R26-0363 and published in DC Register Vol. 73, p. 006077."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed, and this is a routine personnel confirmation, not a gaming measure. It contains no wagering, tax, licensing, or advertising provisions, and it does not even mention gaming in passing. It is best treated as a false positive in a gaming-legislation tracker.","watch":"Nothing to watch for sports-betting operators, because the resolution only fills a library board seat and does not touch the DC Lottery, OLG, or any gaming regulator.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":1022,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:42:53.985Z"},"DC-B26-0450":{"summary":"This is the District's omnibus FY2026 budget support emergency act. Most of it is unrelated to gaming, but two subtitles matter. Subtitle G retroactively exempts licensed sports wagering (as of May 3, 2019) and games of skill (as of April 27, 2021) from D.C. Code § 16-1702(a), and tightens alcohol-license integration for game-of-skill machines. Subtitle H creates a new commercial bingo regime limited to on-premises alcohol licensees, who need both an ABCA endorsement and an Office of Lottery and Gaming (OLG) license.","mechanics":["Sec. 2062 designates the existing text of D.C. Code § 16-1702 as subsection (a) and adds carve-outs: (a) does not apply to commercial bingo, does not apply to sports wagering under § 36-621.01 et seq. as of May 3, 2019, and does not apply to games of skill under § 36-641.01 et seq. as of April 27, 2021.","Game of skill (Secs. 2063-2064): the § 25-508 fee rises from $200 to $500/year, and § 25-113.01(e)(1) drops the 'on-site sales and consumption permit' holder category. A retailer must hold a retailer's license plus a game of skill machine endorsement, ABCA takes over processing and issuing retailer's licenses, and OLG must notify ABCA of citations, revocations or other enforcement against a licensed establishment.","Commercial bingo definition (Sec. 2073, new § 36-601.01(c)(4A)): bingo played for consideration, with a 75-ball draw announced by a caller physically present with the players. It excludes charitable bingo under § 36-601.23(b)-(d), bingo on gambling devices as defined in 15 U.S.C. 1171(a), and online bingo or Class II facsimile machines as defined in 25 U.S.C. 2703(7).","Commercial bingo licensing: only on-premises retailers in classes C/R, D/R, C/T, D/T, C/H, D/H, C/X, D/X, C/RB, D/RB, C/N and D/N may offer it. Each needs an ABC Board commercial bingo endorsement ($300/year) plus an OLG license, and OLG may not issue licenses before October 1, 2025. The CFO must adopt integrity rules for bingo and raffles.","Funds: the lottery fund at § 36-601.12 is renamed the 'Lottery, Gambling, and Gaming Fund', and revenue from the taxes imposed by §§ 47-2002(a)(9) and 47-2202(a)(6) is excluded from it. The supplied text is truncated, so the substance of those tax provisions is not visible.","Duration: this is a congressional-review emergency act, published at 72 DCR 890, that expires February 22, 2026. Durable effect depends on the companion permanent FY2026 Budget Support Act (D.C. Act 26-148)."],"issue_tags":["legalize","licensing","tax","integrity"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. The unusual feature is that a budget support vehicle carries a retroactive statutory carve-out shielding licensed sports wagering, dating back to the May 3, 2019 effective date of DC's sports wagering law, from the general gaming provision in § 16-1702. It does so alongside a narrowly drawn, in-person-only commercial bingo authorization that expressly bars electronic, online and Class II-style formats.","watch":"Confirm that the retroactive § 16-1702 sports-wagering carve-out, which is significant for defending loss-recovery or contract-voidness claims against licensed operators, is carried into permanent law before this emergency act lapses on February 22, 2026, and review the truncated commercial bingo tax provisions (§§ 47-2002(a)(9), 47-2202(a)(6)) for any spillover onto other gaming revenue.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":242625,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:42:54.514Z"},"DC-B26-0265":{"summary":"The District's omnibus FY2026 budget support act, effective December 6, 2025, includes two gaming subtitles among dozens of unrelated provisions. Subtitle G (Sec. 2062) retroactively exempts sports wagering (as of May 3, 2019) and games of skill (as of April 27, 2021) from D.C. Code § 16-1702(a), and moves game-of-skill retailer licensing to ABCA. Subtitle H creates a regulated commercial bingo category limited to on-premises alcohol licensees, who must hold both an ABC Board endorsement and an Office of Lottery and Gaming license.","mechanics":["Sec. 2062 adds § 16-1702(b)-(d): subsection (a) does not apply to commercial bingo under § 36-601.01 et seq., to sports wagering under Subchapter II of Chapter 6 of Title 36 as of May 3, 2019, or to games of skill under Subchapter III as of April 27, 2021.","Game of skill (Secs. 2063-2064): a retailer needs a retailer's license plus a game of skill machine endorsement under § 25-113.01(e). The fee in § 25-508 rises from $200 to $500/year. ABCA, not OLG, processes and issues retailer's licenses, and OLG must promptly notify ABCA of any citation, revocation or other enforcement against a licensed establishment.","Commercial bingo is defined (§ 36-601.01(c)(4A)) as bingo for consideration with numbers drawn and called by a person physically present with the players. It excludes charitable bingo, bingo on federally defined gambling devices, and online play or machines that would be a facsimile of IGRA class II gaming.","Only on-premises retailers in classes C/R, D/R, C/T, D/T, C/H, D/H, C/X, D/X, C/RB, D/RB, C/N and D/N may offer commercial bingo. They need a $300/year commercial bingo endorsement from the ABC Board and an OLG license, and no OLG license may issue before October 1, 2025.","The Lottery fund is renamed the 'Lottery, Gambling, and Gaming Fund'. Revenue from the taxes at §§ 47-2002(a)(9) and 47-2202(a)(6) is carved out of the deposit rule, though the visible text does not show what those taxes are.","The CFO must adopt rules on bingo and raffle integrity and fair play, covering application requirements, fees, and device and technology configurations. The enrolled text supplied is truncated mid-section, so the remaining rulemaking items and any later gaming provisions are not visible."],"issue_tags":["licensing","legalize","integrity","tax"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. On its own, the act is unusual because it uses a budget vehicle to declare, retroactive to the 2019 launch of DC sports wagering, that § 16-1702(a) does not apply to authorized sports wagering. It also creates a narrowly drawn, in-person-only commercial bingo category that expressly bars electronic, online and class II-style formats, and it splits gaming licensing between ABCA and OLG.","watch":"Confirm the scope and retroactive effect of the new § 16-1702(c) carve-out for sports wagering, which appears aimed at foreclosing challenges under the District's general gaming statute to bets placed since May 3, 2019. Also track the CFO bingo rules, the as-yet-unseen tax provisions at §§ 47-2002(a)(9) and 47-2202(a)(6), and any expansion of the ABCA licensing role that could reach sports-wagering retail partners.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":226277,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:42:54.838Z"},"DE-HB314":{"summary":"HB314 creates a new crime of student athlete harassment (new 28 Del. C. § 908) covering threats and sports-related bullying or cyber-bullying of intercollegiate student athletes tied to their athletic performance, and gives convicted offenders' victims a private right of action with $75,000 minimum statutory damages per violation. It also amends the lottery exclusion statute (29 Del. C. § 4835) so that people convicted under § 908 are mandatorily placed on the exclusion list for video lottery, table, Internet lottery and sports lottery games. It further lets the Lottery Director emergency-exclude from sports lottery play anyone charged with, or found to have threatened, such harassment, and directs the Director and the Division of Gaming Enforcement to set up a reporting channel for athletes and coaches.","mechanics":["New § 908(b) offense: intentionally communicating a threat to a student athlete related to performance in an intercollegiate sport, judged on both a subjective intent and a reasonable-person test, or engaging in sports-related bullying or cyber-bullying, which must be repeated, intended to harm, and cause emotional distress.","Penalties under § 908(c): a first offense is a class A misdemeanor and a second or subsequent offense is a class E felony, with exclusive Superior Court jurisdiction; 'student athlete' and 'intercollegiate sport' are defined by cross-reference to 24 Del. C. § 5402.","Private right of action under § 908(e) against a person convicted of the offense, with at least $75,000 in statutory damages per intentional violation plus actual damages, punitive damages, equitable relief, costs and attorney fees.","Electronic communications providers are exempt when acting in the normal course of business, but social network operators serving Delaware must disclose log files and communications on a warrant, a court order based on specific and articulable facts, or user consent; providers get immunity for compliance and may move to quash unduly burdensome orders.","Amended § 4835(c)(3) lets the Director issue an emergency order excluding a person from sports lottery play if the person has been charged under § 908 or a similar out-of-state statute, or has threatened a student athlete over performance in an intercollegiate sport that is the subject of a sports lottery game; new § 4835(j) mandates placing anyone convicted under § 908 or a similar statute on the exclusion list.","New § 4835(k) requires the Director and the Division of Gaming Enforcement to establish and publicize a method for student athletes and intercollegiate coaches to report violations or threats."],"issue_tags":["integrity"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"Unlike MD HB518, which pairs college athlete protections with problem-gambling and voluntary exclusion regulations, Delaware's bill is built around a new criminal offense and a civil damages remedy, with gaming exclusion as the enforcement hook. It resembles NY A 7903 and S07482 in designating people convicted of crimes against athletes as barred bettors, but NY places the duty to block accounts on account wagering licensees, while Delaware runs it through the State Lottery Director's exclusion list, because Delaware sports betting operates as a state lottery. It does not touch prop-bet restrictions like LA SB354 or NC H828, and it has nothing in common with the NIL bills listed (GA HB383, HI HB 2384, TX HB126).","watch":"Watch how the mandatory § 4835(j) exclusion list, which also reaches convictions under 'similar' statutes in other states, and the emergency exclusions based only on charges or threats will be passed to and enforced by Delaware's sports lottery vendors and casino licensees, including duties to screen accounts and to respond to the new reporting channel; note also that the bill's last recorded action, a failed motion to release on 2026-03-18, suggests it is stalled.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Draft","text_chars":11021,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:43:11.015Z"},"GA-HB383":{"summary":"HB383 creates a new Article 32A in Title 20 of the Georgia Code that lets high school student athletes, including eighth graders and home-study participants, receive name, image, or likeness (NIL) compensation, subject to Commissioner of Labor consent under the child-labor provisions of Code Section 39-2-18. Among other limits, it bars NIL deals that can reasonably be perceived as endorsing or promoting casinos and gambling, including sports betting, the lottery, and betting tied to video, online, or mobile games. It also bars pay-for-enrollment and school or booster NIL payments, makes contracts expire automatically after graduation or withdrawal, and stops athletic associations from penalizing NIL activity.","mechanics":["Section 20-2-2105.3(e)(7) prohibits a student athlete from receiving NIL compensation for any conduct that can reasonably be perceived as endorsing or promoting casinos and gambling, including sports betting, the lottery, and betting in connection with video games, online games, and mobile devices. The same list covers alcohol, tobacco, vapor, cannabis, firearms, and any product restricted to people under 18.","Revised Section 39-2-18(b)(4) requires the Commissioner of Labor, before giving the written consent needed for a minor's NIL compensation, to determine that the minor's NIL will not be used for any purpose barred by 20-2-2105.3(e). This builds the gambling ban into the state labor consent process.","Section 20-2-2105.3(b)-(d) bars NIL tied to attending a particular school or college, and bars payments from schools, boosters, or their agents. It also bans use of school marks or references to a school or association in NIL activity, and bars endorsing an NIL payor or its third-party partners during games and competitions (tryouts, practices, and training sessions are exempt).","Under Section 20-2-2105.6, NIL contracts cannot survive past the earlier of 10 days after graduation or 30 days after enrollment ends, and any noncompliant contract terms are void.","Under Sections 20-2-2105.7 and 20-2-2105.8, athletic associations may not penalize athletes or schools for permitted NIL activity. Publicly funded schools may not take part in associations whose governing documents do not comply, and athletes may hire certified agents or attorneys, with agents required to comply with the federal SPARTA.","Under Section 4, the Act takes effect on the Governor's approval (or on becoming law without it) and applies to contracts entered into or renewed on or after that date."],"issue_tags":["advertising"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"Unlike CT HB05229, which restricts gaming advertising on college campuses directly through gaming licensees, HB383 places the gambling restriction on the minor's ability to accept NIL compensation rather than on operators. It is also unlike MD HB518, which pairs problem-gambling regulations with college athlete protections inside the gaming code. Its closest analogues are LA HB 513 (interscholastic and intercollegiate NIL) and DE HB314 (student athlete protection), while HI HB 2384 and SB3263 address only university-level NIL. None of the listed summaries indicate that those bills contain an express sports-betting endorsement ban like Georgia's 20-2-2105.3(e)(7).","watch":"Operators should watch the broad 'reasonably can be perceived as endorsing or promoting' standard and the 'third-party partner' language, because sponsorships, affiliate marketing, or social-casino and sweepstakes-style promotions that touch high school athletes (including eighth graders) could be swept in even though the bill imposes no direct penalty on operators.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Comm Sub","text_chars":12556,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:43:14.498Z"},"FL-H189":{"summary":"CS/CS/CS/HB 189 is an omnibus Florida gambling-enforcement bill that tightens Florida Gaming Control Commission ethics and revolving-door rules, raises many gambling offenses from misdemeanors to felonies, and adds licensure consequences for gambling-house and slot-machine convictions. According to its title, it also expressly criminalizes Internet gambling and Internet sports wagering (amended s. 849.08), imposes requirements on fantasy sports contests (new s. 849.0932), prohibits certain gambling advertising (new s. 849.48), and preempts local ordinances (new s. 849.49). The House bill was laid on the table on 2026-03-11 in favor of CS/CS/SB 1580, so the Senate bill is now the live vehicle.","mechanics":["Integrity: amended s. 838.12(3) makes it a third-degree felony to bet on any professional or amateur contest knowing the result is prearranged or fixed. Section 1 expands the Commission's annual report to cover complaints, seized property and forfeitures.","Revolving door: ss. 16.713 and 16.715 bar commissioners and employees from working for, owning, or contracting with any entity that 'conducts or facilitates an activity regulated, enforced, or investigated by the commission, including fantasy sports contests and other betting activities.' The bar runs for 2 years before service, during service, and for 2 years after.","Licensure: new s. 849.023 lets DBPR, the Commission (chs. 550, 551, s. 849.086) and OFR (ch. 560) summarily suspend or revoke a license after a conviction under s. 849.01, 849.03 or 849.15, regardless of adjudication. The same applies if a 'controlling person' (officer, director, or more than 5% equity holder) is convicted. The licensee faces a fine of up to $75,000 but can keep the license by removing the controlling person.","Criminal penalties: s. 849.01 (keeping a gambling house) moves from a second-degree misdemeanor to a third-degree felony. Sections 849.02 and 849.03 add escalating penalties, reaching a second-degree felony for repeat offenses. New s. 849.021 makes it a felony for a government employee to knowingly license, aid or conceal a gambling house.","Pari-mutuel ownership: s. 550.054(12) requires Commission pre-approval for ownership changes of 5% or more unless the owner was previously approved, and changes under 5% must be reported within 20 days. Escheated harness and thoroughbred permits can no longer be reissued.","Amusement machines: under s. 546.10(8), federally chartered veterans' organizations with liquor licenses may seek a binding declaratory statement on a machine, which the Commission must issue within 60 days. The option is unavailable if the machine or premises is under criminal investigation."],"issue_tags":["integrity","licensing","advertising","legalize"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686 and HI HB 1308/HB 2570/SB1569, which authorize, license and tax mobile sports wagering, FL H0189 goes the other way: it is a prohibition and enforcement bill that criminalizes Internet sports wagering outside existing exceptions. Its fantasy-contest provisions (s. 849.0932) sit in the criminal code with Attorney General and state attorney enforcement, unlike IL HB2342's Gaming Board licensing model for fantasy operators. Its advertising ban (s. 849.48) targets illegal gambling generally, rather than regulating licensed-operator marketing as CT HB05229 does.","watch":"Track CS/CS/SB 1580, the live vehicle, for the final text of the s. 849.08 Internet sports wagering prohibition, the s. 849.0932 fantasy contest requirements and the s. 849.48 advertising ban, which are truncated in this excerpt; check how the exceptions are drawn, since offshore, DFS and pick'em operators and their vendors and advertisers could face felony exposure.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Comm Sub","text_chars":127509,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:43:17.897Z"},"FL-S1580":{"summary":"CS/CS/SB 1580 is a broad Florida illegal-gambling enforcement package. It sharply increases criminal penalties for keeping or facilitating gambling houses, creates new offenses (including betting on a fixed contest), and adds licensure consequences for businesses whose controlling persons commit gambling crimes. According to its title, it also criminalizes Internet gambling, Internet sports wagering and advertising of illegal gambling, preempts local gaming ordinances, and restructures Florida Gaming Control Commission governance and ethics rules; it died in returning messages on 2026-03-13.","mechanics":["s. 849.01: keeping a gambling house, now expressly defined, moves from a second-degree misdemeanor to a third-degree felony for a first offense and a second-degree felony for repeat offenses (or where great bodily harm results). New s. 849.01(3) makes it a third-degree felony, with a mandatory $50,000 fine per violation, to knowingly or in reckless disregard benefit financially from, or participate in a 'course of conduct' providing any service, product or material benefit in furtherance of, a gambling house. Keeping a gambling house is also added as a felony-murder predicate in s. 782.04(3)(s).","s. 838.12(3) (new): staking, betting or wagering on any professional or amateur game, contest, match, race or sport with knowledge that the outcome is prearranged or predetermined is a third-degree felony.","s. 849.023 (new): violations of ss. 849.01, 849.03 or 849.15 are deemed an immediate and serious danger to public health, safety and welfare. DBPR, the Gaming Control Commission or OFR may summarily suspend licenses (alcohol, lodging, pari-mutuel, slot, cardroom, money services) of violators or of entities whose 'controlling person' commits them; a controlling person includes officers, directors and holders of more than 5% equity. Convictions, including withheld adjudication and nolo pleas, can lead to revocation, avoidable by removing the controlling person, and licensees face fines up to $75,000.","Per the bill title (operative text not reproduced in the excerpt provided), the bill also: amends s. 849.08 to define and criminalize playing, operating, conducting or promoting Internet gambling or Internet sports wagering; creates s. 849.48 criminalizing advertising of illegal gambling, with exceptions; creates s. 849.49 preempting local ordinances on certain gaming; and creates s. 849.155 on bulk slot-machine trafficking and a Limited Slot Machine Surrender Program offering immunity.","s. 849.021 (new): a government employee who knowingly certifies, licenses, approves, aids, facilitates or conceals a gambling house commits a third-degree felony, and a second-degree felony on a repeat offense.","Commission and industry changes: the full commission, rather than the chair, appoints the inspector general. Revolving-door restrictions for former staff are expanded to 2 years, covering employment with any regulated person or any party to a commission proceeding, subject to case-by-case waivers. Veterans' organizations may seek binding declaratory statements on amusement machines, decided within 60/90 days. Slot licensees may relocate gaming areas within 1,320 feet in the same county under s. 551.114(4)(b)."],"issue_tags":["integrity","advertising","licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike the legalization-and-tax bills listed (AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, HI HB 1308/HB 2570/SB1569), FL S1580 does not authorize or tax any new wagering; it is a criminal enforcement bill aimed at unlicensed gambling, and, per its title, Internet sports wagering outside Florida's compact-based framework. CT HB05229 restricts advertising by licensed operators, whereas FL's advertising provision (per its title) targets promotion of illegal gambling. FL's match-fixing wagering offense in s. 838.12(3) is an integrity measure absent from the listed IL bills. Unlike IL HB5142 and HB5059, nothing in the reproduced FL text addresses prediction markets.","watch":"Although the bill died, a reintroduction would carry the s. 849.01(3) 'reckless disregard' facilitation offense, with a $50,000 per-violation fine, and the reported Internet sports wagering and advertising crimes, which could expose payment processors, suppliers, affiliates and media partners of any operator not authorized under Florida law; operators with Florida-licensed affiliates should also note that s. 849.023 lets regulators summarily suspend licenses based on the conduct of 5%-plus owners.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Comm Sub","text_chars":137816,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:43:21.481Z"},"GA-HR753":{"summary":"HR 753, as substituted by the House Rules Committee, is an omnibus study-committee resolution. Sections 1 and 2 create committees on rural internal medicine access and on paternity legitimation, and Section 3 creates the House Study Committee on Gaming in the State of Georgia. That committee is charged with studying the economic, fiscal, workforce and social impacts of legalizing destination resort casinos, pari-mutuel and fixed-odds horse racing, and digital, retail and on-premise sports betting, and with proposing legislation before it is abolished on December 1, 2025.","mechanics":["Section 3(2): seven members, namely the chairs of the House Ways and Means, Regulated Industries, and Economic Development Committees plus four House members appointed by the Speaker, one of whom the Speaker designates as chair.","Section 3(4): the committee must hold at least four public hearings at various locations around the state and at least one meeting to finalize findings.","Section 3(6): unlike the other two committees, which may file a report, the gaming committee is directed that it 'shall adopt specific findings or recommendations that include proposed legislation' and must file a report before abolishment.","Scope per the whereas clauses: revenue and tax potential, job quality, social harms, gaming dollars lost to other states (Nevada, Mississippi, Alabama, North Carolina and Massachusetts are named), and horse-breeding industry benefits from pari-mutuel racing.","Funding comes from House appropriations, with member allowances under O.C.G.A. 28-1-8 capped at three days unless additional days are authorized; all committees are abolished December 1, 2025.","The resolution creates no legal authorization, tax rate or licensing regime. Any legalization would still require voter approval of a constitutional amendment and enabling legislation."],"issue_tags":["legalize","tax"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. This is an unusual vehicle: a gaming study committee bundled into a resolution whose title concerns rural internal medicine. Unlike earlier Georgia legalization measures that went straight to constitutional amendments or enabling statutes, it sets up a pre-session fact-finding process that is required to produce draft legislation.","watch":"Operators should engage with the four or more statewide public hearings and the committee's mandatory proposed legislation due before December 1, 2025, since that report will likely shape the tax rate, mobile versus retail licensing structure, and any bundling of sports betting with casinos and horse racing in Georgia's 2026 session.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Comm Sub","text_chars":13513,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:43:26.355Z"},"GA-HR450":{"summary":"HR 450 is a proposed amendment to Article I, Section II, Paragraph VIII of the Georgia Constitution that would let the General Assembly authorize and regulate sports betting by general law, subject to voter ratification. It earmarks sports betting tax proceeds to a new Georgia Sports Betting Proceeds Trust Fund for problem-gambling programs and lottery-style education programs such as pre-K and HOPE scholarships. It also allows the Georgia Lottery Corporation to participate in sports betting. The resolution failed on House third reading on 2026-03-06.","mechanics":["Rewritten subparagraph (a) keeps a constitutional ban, enforced by penal laws, on 'all forms of sports betting, pari-mutuel betting, and casino gambling' except as the Paragraph provides; only sports betting is carved out.","New subparagraph (d) authorizes the General Assembly to provide by law for operating and regulating sports betting. Tax proceeds go to the Georgia Sports Betting Proceeds Trust Fund. Other regulatory proceeds first cover regulatory operating expenses and reserves without appropriation, and the net remainder goes to the Trust Fund.","Subparagraph (e)(1) splits Trust Fund appropriations: 15% of the first $150 million each fiscal year goes to problem-gambling prevention and treatment, and 85% of the first $150 million plus everything above $150 million goes to voluntary pre-K and the existing lottery education purposes in subparagraph (c). Under (e)(2), the education share is exempt from the lapse rule in Art. III, Sec. IX, Para. IV(c).","Subparagraph (f) lets the Georgia Lottery Corporation be authorized by law to participate in sports betting. Its participation proceeds follow the lottery education rules in (c), while any tax imposed on its participation follows the Trust Fund rules in (e).","The amendment contains no tax rate, licensing structure, license cap, or mobile-versus-retail rules; all of that is left to implementing legislation.","Section 2 sets the ballot question: authorize sports betting 'to provide additional funding for pre-kindergarten and HOPE scholarships?' Ratification follows Art. X, Sec. I, Para. II."],"issue_tags":["legalize","prop","tax"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Alaska's HB145 and SB194 and Hawaii's HB 1308, HB 2570 and SB1569 are statutory frameworks that directly set licensing, regulators, and taxes for operators. HR 450 is only a constitutional enabling amendment with no operator-facing terms. Its distinctive features are that it hard-codes revenue earmarks into the constitution, including a problem-gambling carve-out capped at 15% of the first $150 million, and that it gives the state lottery corporation an express constitutional role. It also writes explicit prohibitions on pari-mutuel and casino gambling into the constitution. Illinois bills such as HB3159 and HB5143 instead adjust taxes within an already operating sports wagering regime.","watch":"The resolution lost on House third reading, so watch for reconsideration or a successor vehicle, and for whether companion enabling legislation uses subparagraph (f) to channel the market through the Georgia Lottery Corporation, which could shape whether private operators participate and on what terms.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":10621,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:43:27.321Z"},"HI-SB1569":{"summary":"SB1569 SD1 would legalize mobile-only sports wagering in Hawaii under a new HRS chapter administered by the Department of Business, Economic Development, and Tourism (DBEDT), with at least four operator licenses restricted to \"qualified gaming entities\" already live in three or more U.S. states. It taxes adjusted gross sports wagering receipts at 10% through a new paragraph (9) of the general excise tax statute, section 237-13. It also carves sports wagering and fantasy sports contests out of the gambling definitions in section 712-1220. The SD1 carries a placeholder effective date of July 1, 2050 and blank criminal fine amounts and appropriation figures, signalling the bill is still a vehicle for negotiation.","mechanics":["Licensing: DBEDT must issue a minimum of four operator licenses (section -5), open only to entities offering online sports wagering in at least three U.S. jurisdictions. The fee is $250,000 for a five-year term plus cost-based processing fees. A three-year temporary license is available on payment of the initial fee, and a universal start date must be set no later than 180 days after enactment.","Supplier licenses (section -6) cost $10,000 for five years, with temporary licenses available. Suppliers are oddly defined as providers of geolocation and age verification services.","Tax: 10% of adjusted gross sports wagering receipts under new HRS 237-13(9), on the accrual method, stated to be in lieu of all other taxes on sports wagering operations. Adjusted gross receipts are gross cash wagers less winnings paid. There is no promotional-credit deduction, and bonuses count as an account-funding method.","Product scope: mobile/digital only, with kiosks and retail terminals barred (section -10(c)). Bettors must be 21+ and physically in Hawaii. Collegiate, amateur, Olympic, motorsport and e-sports events are permitted, and wagering on events where most participants are under 18 is excluded. Exchange wagering, in-play and prop bets are expressly allowed, as are layoff bets between operators and interstate wagering agreements.","Operator duties (section -8): integrity monitoring software, with suspicious activity reported to DBEDT; 1-800-GAMBLER in all advertising; daily receipts records; DBEDT-approved house rules. There is also a voluntary self-exclusion program with lifetime option and at least quarterly list sharing, and an employee betting ban.","Enforcement: civil fines up to $5,000 per violation or $10,000 per occurrence, which can be imposed on unlicensed persons too. Unlicensed sports wagering escalates from a misdemeanor to a class C and then class B felony, with fine amounts left blank. A problem gambling special fund administered by the Department of Health is funded only by appropriation."],"issue_tags":["legalize","licensing","tax","integrity","advertising"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Like AK HB145 and SB194, SB1569 is a mobile-only legalization bill in a state with no existing casino industry, so licenses go to out-of-state online operators rather than being tethered to land-based venues. Its 10% GGR rate is low compared with the steep rate and license-fee proposals in Illinois, for example IL HB2878 and HB5034 setting a $15 million master license fee. Unlike CT HB05229 or IL HB5059, it contains no detailed advertising, AI-targeting or prediction-market provisions beyond the 1-800-GAMBLER requirement. Unlike GA HR450 and SR131, it proceeds by statute without a constitutional amendment.","watch":"Watch whether the 2050 placeholder effective date, the blank fine amounts and the 10% rate are reset in the 2026 session. Also watch whether the 'minimum of four' license language and the three-jurisdiction qualification gate stay intact, along with the absence of a promotional-play deduction, since these will determine market access and effective tax burden.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":48389,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:43:36.829Z"},"GA-SR131":{"summary":"SR131 is a proposed amendment to the Georgia Constitution (Art. I, Sec. II, Para. VIII) that would let the General Assembly authorize and regulate sports betting and casino gambling by statute, administered by a gaming commission it must create. The amendment fixes the tax rate and dedicates the proceeds to a new Georgia Gaming Proceeds Fund, with large set-asides for county governments and problem-gambling programs. It takes effect only if passed by the legislature and ratified by voters on the stated ballot question.","mechanics":["New subpara. (e)(1)-(2): the General Assembly may provide by law for sports betting and casino gambling and must create a gaming commission with regulatory and taxing powers. Revised subpara. (a) expressly lists sports betting, pari-mutuel betting and casino gambling as prohibited except as provided, and nothing in (e) authorizes pari-mutuel betting.","Subpara. (e)(3) on casinos: at least eight casino gaming licenses, each for a single facility. No person or entity may hold more than one license or own an interest in more than one licensee. No off-site casino gambling is permitted, which would effectively bar online casino gaming. The text is silent on whether sports betting must be in-person or may be mobile.","Subpara. (e)(4) tax: the rate is fixed at 20 percent of gross income, less operating expenses allowed by law, winnings paid, and reserves permitted by law. The proceeds go to the Georgia Gaming Proceeds Fund. Other regulatory proceeds, such as fees, first fund the commission's operating expenses and reserves, and any remainder goes to the Fund.","Subpara. (e)(5) allocation: the first $2 billion is split evenly among all counties. After that, the first $500 million of each fiscal year's additional proceeds goes evenly to counties until at least $5 billion more has been paid out. At least 5 percent of the remaining proceeds must fund problem-gambling prevention and treatment. The Fund sits outside the general fund and lottery allocation rules, and under (e)(6) appropriations do not lapse.","Section 2: the measure goes to voters under Art. X, Sec. I, Para. II, with the question whether the General Assembly may provide by law for sports betting and casino gambling."],"issue_tags":["legalize","tax","licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike the statutory mobile-wagering frameworks in AK HB145/SB194 and HI HB1308, HB2570 and SB1569, SR131 creates no operative licensing regime itself; it is a constitutional enabling amendment that still needs voter ratification and later implementing legislation. It is also unusual in locking a tax rate (20 percent) and a revenue distribution formula into the constitution. Illinois bills such as HB2878 (a $15 million master license fee) and HB3159 (a 2 percent add-on for fire and EMS) adjust an existing statute and can be changed by ordinary legislation. Its express ban on off-site casino gambling also runs opposite to iGaming authorizations such as IL HB3080 and HB4797.","watch":"Watch three things: the constitutionally fixed 20 percent rate and its unusual net base, since the operating expenses 'allowed by law' will be defined in implementing legislation; the 'no off-site casino gambling' clause, which forecloses iGaming; and whether enabling legislation permits statewide mobile sports betting given the one-license-per-entity ownership limit, which is drafted for casino licenses but could be extended.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":7287,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:43:39.416Z"},"IA-HF2089":{"summary":"HF2089 amends Iowa Code section 99F.6(4)(a)(2) to require casino licensees, when making their existing mandatory charitable distributions (at least 3% of gambling-game adjusted gross receipts and 0.75% of sports wagering net receipts), to direct at least 75% of those moneys pro rata to Iowa counties based on where contributing players reside. It imposes new player-residency recordkeeping, an annual public report to the Racing and Gaming Commission, and civil penalties and license discipline for noncompliance.","mechanics":["Does not change the existing 3% AGR and 0.75% sports wagering net receipts charitable distribution floors; new subparagraph division (b) governs how at least 75% of the required distribution is allocated by county.","County share equals gross receipts from players residing in that county divided by gross receipts from players in all Iowa counties; receipts from nonresidents or players whose county cannot be verified are spread proportionally on the same formula.","Licensees must collect and maintain records of the county of residence of all players or participants and the receipts attributable to each, covering both gambling games and sports wagering.","Annual report to the commission, posted publicly, covering receipts by county, nonresident and unverified receipts, total distributions, and per-county distributions.","Enforcement: civil penalty up to 5% of the total required distribution, deposited in the general fund; the commission may order redistribution; willful or repeated failure may lead to additional discipline including license suspension.","Applies to receipts collected in fiscal years beginning on or after the Act's effective date. The 25% remainder stays distributable as otherwise allowed by law."],"issue_tags":["tax","licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states were listed. What is unusual is that it ties a licensee's charitable giving to player geography, effectively mandating player-level residency tracking. That is straightforward for online sports wagering accounts with KYC data but hard for anonymous retail casino and slot play. The penalty is measured against the whole required distribution, not just the misallocated portion.","watch":"Watch the player-level county-of-residence tracking requirement, which is hard to meet for anonymous retail gaming. Its interaction with existing operating-agreement distribution terms is also unclear, since the bill's 75% rule is not expressly limited by the \"unless an operating agreement otherwise provides\" clause. Licensees also face penalty exposure of up to 5% of total required distributions plus possible license suspension.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":11222,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:43:40.068Z"},"HI-SB3263":{"summary":"SB3263 CD1 creates a University of Hawaii name, image, and likeness framework under HRS chapter 304A. It affirms student-athletes' right to earn NIL compensation, requires UH to adopt NIL policies, sets up third-party deal disclosure and annual legislative reporting, and establishes a NIL endowment fund seeded with tuition-fund, general-fund, and matching private money. The only gaming hook is §304A-C(c)(3), which lets UH bar student-athletes from NIL deals involving gambling or sports wagering.","mechanics":["§304A-C(c) permits, but does not require, UH to prohibit NIL agreements involving adult entertainment, firearms, gambling or sports wagering, illegal drugs, tobacco or vaping, or any industry conflicting with the university's mission or financial interests.","§304A-C(d) gives student-athletes at least ten business days after signing any institutional or third-party NIL agreement to rescind it.","§304A-D(a) requires student-athletes to disclose third-party NIL agreements worth $600 or more, or an alternative NCAA or federal threshold UH designates. Disclosed records are confidential and exempt from chapter 92F. Annual aggregate reports to the legislature begin with the 2028 session.","§304A-G endowment: $4M transferred from the UH tuition and fees special fund, $2.5M general-fund appropriation for FY2026-27 (Section 5, effective July 1, 2026), and $5M in university matching gifts. The corpus has a stated $10M minimum, and interest may be spent once the corpus has held $7.5M for three years.","The Act takes effect on approval and sunsets June 30, 2030, with legislative review planned for the 2029 session. Unexpended fund balances lapse to their source."],"issue_tags":["advertising","integrity"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"Unlike MD HB518 (college athlete protections inside a problem-gambling bill), CT HB05229 (restricting gaming advertising on college campuses) and TN SB1662 (funding athletic programs from a fee on sports wagering vendors), Hawaii's bill is a pure NIL statute. It touches wagering only through a discretionary university power to exclude gambling sponsors. It is closer to the NIL bills in WI AB1034, TX HB126, NE LB370 and NH HB312, but unusual in pairing a post-House-settlement framework with a state-seeded NIL endowment and a hard 2030 sunset. Because Hawaii has no legal sports betting, the gambling-sponsorship carve-out has limited immediate commercial effect.","watch":"Watch whether UH exercises its §304A-C(c)(3) discretion to adopt a blanket ban on gambling and sports-wagering NIL deals, since that policy could become a template for other states and would bar operator endorsements with UH athletes if Hawaii ever legalizes sports betting.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":18376,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:43:40.123Z"},"IL-HB1075":{"summary":"Illinois's Fiscal Year 2026 Budget Implementation Act (Public Act 104-0002) makes the statutory changes needed to carry out the FY2026 budget across many state programs, including agency powers, fund transfers, fund dissolutions and grant program tweaks. In the portion of the text provided, gaming appears only as a source of transfers into the Audit Expense Fund. The text supplied is truncated partway through State Finance Act Section 6z-27, so later articles cannot be assessed here.","mechanics":["Section 5-30 amends 30 ILCS 105/6z-27 to order transfers into the Audit Expense Fund within 30 days after July 1, 2025. The schedule shown includes Sports Wagering Fund $14,696, State Gaming Fund $216,475, Horse Racing Fund $233,685, Illinois Gaming Law Enforcement Fund $1,411 and State Lottery Fund $106,169. The text as provided mixes stricken and new schedules, so treat these figures as indicative.","Section 5-5 gives the Department of Central Management Services authority to procure 'site readiness work' (abatement, remediation, demolition, title and environmental reports) for surplus state real property, under new 20 ILCS 405/405-217.","Section 5-10 raises the SBIR/STTR Phase I matching grant cap from $50,000 to $75,000. It also adds Phase II matching grants of up to $250,000, subject to appropriation, with a lifetime limit of 2 awards.","Section 5-25 reallocates the $4-per-car-year insurer assessment from July 1, 2025: 10% to the State Police Law Enforcement Administration Fund, 10% to the State Police Vehicle Fund and 80% to the Law Enforcement Training Fund.","Section 5-10 dissolves the Small Business Environmental Assistance Fund into the Clean Air Act Permit Fund on July 1, 2025, and Section 5-30 repeals its State Finance Act listing effective January 1, 2026."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No other states' bills are listed for comparison. On its own, this is a general budget implementation omnibus. In the text provided, wagering-related funds appear only as line items in the routine Audit Expense Fund sweep, not as changes to sports wagering tax rates, licensing or operations. Those changes, if any, would sit in separate FY2026 revenue legislation not shown here.","watch":"Because the supplied text cuts off mid-Section 6z-27, review the remaining articles of P.A. 104-0002 for any further transfers from, or diversions of, the Sports Wagering Fund or State Gaming Fund, or other amendments touching the Sports Wagering Act or Illinois Gambling Act.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":979334,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:43:50.410Z"},"IL-HB111":{"summary":"This is Governor Pritzker's June 16, 2026 veto message returning Illinois HB 111, the FY 2027 appropriations act, with item and reduction vetoes totaling $500,418,129,782, and approving all other appropriations. The vetoes correct lines the House and Senate sponsors (Rep. Gabel and Sen. Sims) identified as erroneous or duplicative. Nearly the entire total comes from one typographical line, Article 52, Section 285, enacted at $500,250,000,000. The message contains no sports-betting or gaming provisions on its face.","mechanics":["Authority: Article IV, Section 9(d) of the Illinois Constitution of 1970 (item and reduction veto).","Total vetoed: $500,418,129,782. Of that, $500,250,000,000 is a single typographical error at Art. 52, Sec. 285, p. 430, lines 4-7.","Item vetoes hit lines in Articles 37, 41, 52, 93 and 118. Examples are Art. 93, Secs. 190, 195 and 200 ($20M, $4.5M and $30M) and Art. 52, Secs. 490 and 495 ($17.5M and $17.81M).","Reductions include Art. 15, Secs. 476 and 1295 (each cut from $17M to $1.7M), Art. 52, Sec. 140 (cut from $60M to $45M) and Art. 93, Sec. 40 (cut from $35M to $30M).","The message identifies lines only by article, section, page and line, without naming agencies, funds or purposes, so it cannot be determined from this text whether any vetoed line touches the Gaming Board or wagering-funded programs.","All other appropriations in HB 111 were approved, and the enacted act is PA 104-0464."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No other states' bills are listed for comparison. What is unusual is the headline veto figure of roughly $500.4 billion, which is almost entirely one typographical line rather than a real policy cut. The vetoes were coordinated with the bill's own sponsors as technical clean-up, not adversarial line-item cuts.","watch":"Check the underlying enacted text of PA 104-0464 to see whether any vetoed or reduced lines, especially in Articles 52 and 93, correspond to Illinois Gaming Board operations or sports-wagering-funded appropriations, such as the Capital Projects Fund or problem gambling programs, since this veto message itself does not identify them.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Veto Message","text_chars":3255,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:43:50.958Z"},"IL-HB1704":{"summary":"HB1704 amends Section 6z-27 of the Illinois State Finance Act to update the annual audit-cost sweep, directing the Comptroller and Treasurer to transfer specified amounts from roughly 250 state funds into the Audit Expense Fund within 30 days after July 1, 2025 (changed from 2024). It is a routine shell-style finance bill; gaming-related funds, including the Sports Wagering Fund, appear only as line items among the transferring funds.","mechanics":["Changes the transfer trigger date from July 1, 2024 to July 1, 2025; transfers occur within 30 days or as soon as practical.","Gaming-related line items include Sports Wagering Fund $14,696; State Gaming Fund $1,997 and $216,475; State Lottery Fund $311,489 and $106,169; Horse Racing Fund $233,685; Illinois Gaming Law Enforcement Fund $1,411.","General Revenue Fund is listed twice at $17,653,153 each, and Education Assistance Fund is listed at $2,193,017 and $2,347,928; several funds appear twice in the list.","Federal and State trust funds are excluded unless trustees direct transfers; any shortfall in a listed fund is backfilled from the General Revenue Fund.","Auditor General must return any excess over actual audit costs to the originating fund on or around September 30; the Act is effective immediately upon becoming law."],"issue_tags":["sweeps"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. The bill is unusual only because it is a recurring Illinois audit-cost transfer vehicle. It touches sports betting only through a $14,696 draw on the Sports Wagering Fund and other modest gaming-fund draws, and it contains no substantive gaming policy. Its last action was a Rule 19(a) re-referral to the Rules Committee on 2025-04-11, which suggests it is likely dormant or held as a shell for later amendment.","watch":"Because this is a re-referred finance shell bill, watch for a floor or committee amendment that repurposes it to carry larger sweeps from the Sports Wagering Fund or State Gaming Fund, or changes to wagering taxes, late in session.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":25672,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:43:52.193Z"},"IL-HB1451":{"summary":"HB1451 amends the Illinois Substance Use Disorder Act to fold gambling disorder more fully into the Department of Human Services' prevention, treatment, licensing and grant authority, largely by adding 'or gambling disorders' throughout the Act. It expands the Sec. 5-20 gambling disorder program to include a toll-free hotline and website, stigma-reduction awareness and suicide-prevention efforts. It also requires each master sports wagering licensee to display a DHS-determined help statement on its portal, website, or computer or mobile app.","mechanics":["Sec. 1-10 adds definitions. 'Gambling disorder' means persistent and recurring maladaptive gambling behavior that disrupts personal, family, or vocational pursuits. 'Gambling' means risking money or other items of value in games of chance, expressly including video gaming and sports betting. 'Gaming' is defined separately as playing video games.","Sec. 5-20(a)-(b): subject to appropriation, DHS (or a contractor) must run a toll-free hotline and website in place of the '800' number, promote awareness of gambling disorder impacts and stigma, and use screening, crisis intervention, treatment and training to reduce gambling-related suicide attempts.","Sec. 5-20(c): DHS determines a statement on obtaining help with a gambling disorder. Licensed gambling establishment owners must post it, and master sports wagering licensees must include it on their portal, website, or app. The provision cross-references Sec. 25.95 of the Sports Wagering Act, among other gaming statutes, for DHS-supplied signage.","Sec. 5-20(d)-(e): DHS may produce prevention literature and curricula, advise State and local officials, and support prevention projects. It may also award grants for local prevention, recognition, response and treatment programs to local health departments, correctional institutions, hospitals, universities, community-based organizations and faith-based organizations, funded by appropriations or outside grants.","Sec. 15-5: makes it unlawful to provide treatment for gambling disorders without a DHS license, extending existing substance use treatment licensure to gambling treatment providers.","Sec. 10-10 and 10-15: the Advisory Council gains duties on gambling disorder prevention, including preventing 'gambling behaviors while gaming by minors', and its public members include representatives of gambling disorder treatment programs. No new tax, fee or operator funding obligation and no effective date appear in the provided text, which is truncated."],"issue_tags":["advertising"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike the Hawaii sports wagering bills (HI HB 1308, HB 2570, SB1569), which create problem gambling programs as part of a full legalization, licensing and tax package, IL HB1451 is a standalone public-health bill inside an existing legal market. It imposes no tax or fee earmark. It also lacks the direct operator conduct rules in CT HB05229 (withdrawal timing, customer service lines, AI-targeting ban) and Indiana HB1078's voluntary exclusion and HB1092's child-support withholding mechanisms. Its only direct operator obligation is displaying a DHS-determined help statement.","watch":"Watch the content, placement and formatting DHS sets for the required help statement on master sports wagering licensees' websites and apps under Sec. 5-20(c), and whether later amendments add an operator-funded assessment to pay for the expanded, appropriation-dependent programs.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":86527,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:44:01.321Z"},"IL-HB1833":{"summary":"HB1833 creates the Metropolitan Mobility Authority Act, which merges the Chicago Transit Authority, the RTA's Commuter Rail and Suburban Bus Divisions, and the RTA itself into a single Metropolitan Mobility Authority covering Cook, DuPage, Kane, Lake, McHenry and Will counties, and abolishes the Service Boards. It also creates an Equitable Transit-Supportive Development Act and fund, an IDOT Office of Public Transportation Support, and makes the Authority's Chair a nonvoting member of the Tollway board. The text provided contains no sports betting, gaming or wagering provisions.","mechanics":["Sec. 3.02: on the effective date, CTA, Metra, Pace and the RTA are consolidated into the Authority. The Authority takes over all of their assets, contracts and ordinances, and existing RTA directors serve as temporary Directors for up to 270 days.","Sec. 2.04: the voting board is 3 Governor appointees, 5 Chicago Mayor appointees, 5 Cook County Board President appointees, and 1 appointee from each collar county. Nonvoting seats include IDOT, the Tollway Chair, labor, business, disability and the Citizen Advisory Board chair. Directors are paid $25,000 a year under Sec. 2.09.","Sec. 3.01: a Transition Committee may be paid up to $150,000 per Director per year. Deadlines run from the effective date: board seated within 1 year, Integration Management Office within 2 years, permanent CEO and final transition plan within 3 years, and full transfer of functions within 4 years.","Sec. 3.03: all employees transfer with their existing collective bargaining agreements and pension obligations. Implementation disputes go to 21-day mediation and then binding three-member arbitration.","Sec. 4.02(12): the Authority may set fines by ordinance of up to $5,000 per offense, effective 10 days after newspaper publication."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. This is a transit governance and consolidation bill, and the portion of text provided does not touch wagering, gaming taxes or gaming licensing. The text supplied is truncated (it ends in Sec. 4.04), so any funding provisions later in the bill could not be reviewed.","watch":"Since the excerpt is truncated, review the full text and any amendments to see whether new transit funding sources, such as gaming or sports-wagering tax revenue or diverted wagering receipts, are added to close the region's transit funding gap.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":813134,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:44:08.286Z"},"IL-HB2513":{"summary":"HB2513 amends the Illinois Lottery Law to create a dedicated Lyme Disease Innovation instant scratch-off game (new 20 ILCS 1605/21.16.5), with net revenue deposited into the existing Lyme Disease Awareness Fund. It also amends 20 ILCS 805/805-72 to state that the DNR's Lyme Disease Innovation Program is funded partly by that game's revenue. It makes conforming edits to the lottery proceeds-distribution (Sec. 9.3), State Lottery Fund (Sec. 20), and Joint Special Instant Scratch-off (Sec. 21.4) provisions.","mechanics":["New Sec. 21.16.5(a): the game commences January 1, 2026, or as soon as reasonably practical, and must be discontinued December 31, 2035. It is to be offered 'upon the discontinuation of the first game that is discontinued' within the Sec. 21.4 joint game, and the Lottery must consult DNR on design and promotion.","Sec. 21.16.5(c) defines 'net revenue' as ticket sales less prizes and the Department's actual administrative expenses 'solely related' to this game. This is narrower than the Sec. 21.4(d) definition, which also deducts retailer payments and estimated administrative expenses.","Sec. 21.16.5(d) bars the Department from unreasonably diminishing marketing of other instant scratch-off games while Lyme tickets are sold, and Sec. 21.16.5(e) gives the Department discretionary rulemaking authority.","Sec. 21.4(a) adds Sec. 21.16.5 to the list of special causes benefiting from the joint special instant game. That game is capped at 10 causes under Sec. 21.4(c) and sunsets January 1, 2027.","The Sec. 9.3(a) and Sec. 20(n) amendments carve Lyme game proceeds out of the general distribution waterfall that otherwise sends net lottery proceeds to the Common School Fund. Existing references to Sports Wagering Act Sec. 25-70 are left unchanged.","Sec. 805-72(c) funds the Program and its contracted not-for-profit operator and the U of I Prairie Research Institute tick surveillance work from the Fund. All of this remains subject to appropriation under Sec. 805-72(e)."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"core","comparison":"No other states' bills are listed for comparison. What is unusual is the drafting tension: the bill both adds the Lyme cause to the Sec. 21.4 joint scratch-off game and creates a standalone Lyme game. The standalone game nominally starts January 1, 2026 but is conditioned on another joint-game cause being discontinued. Its net revenue definition also differs from the joint game's.","watch":"This is a state-run lottery charitable-game bill with no effect on sports wagering licensing, taxes, or operations, so the only point to monitor is whether amendments to Secs. 9.3 or 20 begin touching the Sports Wagering Act Sec. 25-70 lottery sports wagering carve-outs.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":16279,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:44:08.968Z"},"IL-HB2342":{"summary":"HB2342 creates the Fantasy Sports Consumer Protection Act, making paid-entry online fantasy contests lawful in Illinois only when offered by an operator licensed by the Illinois Gaming Board. It imposes a monthly tax of 10% to 15% on adjusted gross fantasy contest receipts, sets license fees, and adds age, geolocation, AML, integrity and self-exclusion requirements. It also amends the Sports Wagering Act and Criminal Code so fantasy contests are not sports wagering or gambling, but the bill was re-referred to Rules under Rule 19(a) on 2025-03-21 and appears stalled.","mechanics":["Definition (Sec. 5): a fantasy contest is an online skill contest with an entry fee and prizes disclosed in advance, built from rosters of real athletes or regulated esports players and scored on individual statistics. No winning outcome may rest entirely on a single team's score or spread or solely on one athlete's single performance in one event. The definition expressly covers single-participant contests against an operator-set target score.","Licensing (Secs. 15, 25): a Board license is required, with fingerprint background checks of owners, officers and directors and disclosure of 10%+ owners. Incumbents pay the lesser of 8% of prior-year AGFCR or $1,000,000. New entrants pay $10,000, plus $990,000 once they reach 10,000 active paying Illinois participants. Licenses run 4 years, and renewal costs 1% of AGFCR for the prior 4 years.","Transition: operators already active under a bona fide attorney opinion letter may keep operating if they apply within 90 days after rules are adopted (Sec. 25(b)). Rules are due within 180 days, with emergency rulemaking authorized (Sec. 10(b), Sec. 100).","Tax (Sec. 40): beginning July 1, 2025, a privilege tax of at least 10% and no more than 15% of AGFCR, with the rate set by Board rule. It is calculated on the accrual method, filed and paid monthly, and deposited in the Common School Fund. Fees are split 85% to the State Gaming Fund and 15% to DHS compulsive gambling programs.","Conduct rules (Secs. 10, 20, 45): participants must be 21+ and physically in Illinois, verified by geolocation. Operators must meet BSA/AML standards, verify identity, detect fraud and collusion, and report integrity breaches and match fixing. Problem-gambling messaging is required on platforms and all advertising, and self-excluded players' winnings must be forfeited to problem-gaming programs.","Limits on the Board (Sec. 20(e)-(f)): the Board may not regulate individual contest rules, statistical makeup, or operator platforms, and rule changes alone cannot force license surrender. Operators must also make biennial supplier diversity reports (Sec. 50) and undergo independent CPA audits and annual lab testing (Sec. 30)."],"issue_tags":["licensing","tax","legalize","integrity","prop","advertising"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"KY HB 904 likewise places fantasy contest operators under a state gaming regulator (the Kentucky Horse Racing and Gaming Corporation) with geolocation and consumer-protection procedures. Its summary does not indicate a gross-receipts tax comparable to HB2342's 10-15% band or the up-to-$1,000,000 incumbent license fee. FL H0189 addresses fantasy sports contests only as one piece of a broad gambling overhaul, whereas HB2342 is a standalone fantasy licensing and tax statute. HB2342 expressly authorizes player-versus-house target-score contests, which runs opposite to LA SB354's push to prohibit proposition and micro-bets in sports wagering.","watch":"Watch the rate the Board would set within the 10-15% band, the July 1, 2025 tax start date, and the 8%/$1,000,000 incumbent and $990,000 threshold-triggered license fees, along with whether the express inclusion of target-score (pick'em) contests survives any revival given Illinois's sports-wagering licensees' interest in excluding prop-like fantasy products.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":43179,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:44:15.964Z"},"IL-HB2580":{"summary":"HB2580 amends the Illinois Substance Use Disorder Act (20 ILCS 301) to fold gambling disorders more fully into DHS's statutory mission, updating the definition of gambling disorder and adding definitions of \"gambling\" (expressly including sports betting and video gaming) and \"gaming.\" It expands the Section 5-20 gambling disorder program to cover stigma awareness and suicide prevention, and makes DHS responsible for selecting the help statement that licensed gambling establishments must post and master sports wagering licensees must display on their portals, websites, and apps. It also authorizes, but does not require, DHS to advise officials, support prevention projects, and award grants for local gambling prevention and treatment programs.","mechanics":["Sec. 1-10 redefines \"gambling disorder\" as persistent and recurrent problematic gambling behavior leading to clinically significant impairment or distress, replacing \"recurring maladaptive gambling behavior.\" It defines \"gambling\" as risking money or value in games of chance, including video gaming and sports betting.","Sec. 5-20(a) replaces the \"800\" number with a toll-free hotline and website. It adds public awareness of stigma and the impact on families and communities, and adds a new item (5) requiring screening, crisis intervention, and other means to decrease gambling-related suicide attempts, subject to specific appropriation.","Sec. 5-20(c) has DHS select the gambling-help statement that each licensed gambling establishment owner must post and each master sports wagering licensee must include on its portal, website, or mobile app. It adds cross-references to Sports Wagering Act Sec. 25.95 and the Video Gaming Act for DHS-supplied signs.","New Sec. 5-20(d) lets DHS publish prevention literature and curricula, advise State and local officials on prevalence and \"the relationship between gaming and gambling disorders,\" and promote elements such as peer recovery specialists, reentry outreach, judicial training, and targeted mass media on gambling stigma.","New Sec. 5-20(e) authorizes grants to local health departments, correctional institutions, hospitals, universities, and community and faith-based groups for prevention and treatment programs. Funding comes from appropriations plus private, federal, and other sources.","Sec. 15-5 extends DHS treatment licensure to gambling disorder treatment. Other conforming edits add gambling disorders to DHS functions, Advisory Council duties, and Council membership (Sec. 10-15(s) treatment program representatives). No effective date is specified."],"issue_tags":["integrity","advertising"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"Unlike the authorization-and-tax bills such as AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, and HI HB 1308, HB2580 creates no new operator licensing, tax, or wagering rules; it is a public-health and agency-mandate bill. It is closest in spirit to the consumer-protection measures in CT HB05229 (advertising and account withdrawal limits) and IN HB1078 (voluntary exclusion), but it reaches operators only through the DHS-selected help statement on master sports wagering licensee platforms. Its explicit suicide-prevention mandate and broad grant authority are unusual among the listed bills.","watch":"Watch whether DHS uses its new authority under Sec. 5-20(c) to change the content or format of the mandatory problem-gambling statement on master sports wagering licensees' websites and apps, and whether that later becomes a hook for operator-funded assessments, since the bill currently relies only on appropriations and outside grants.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":86557,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:44:18.042Z"},"IL-HB2761":{"summary":"HB2761 adds Section 25-47 to the Illinois Sports Wagering Act so that, where a public entity that owns a sports facility does not apply for a master sports wagering license, a professional sports team that plays most of its home contests there and has the owner's written authorization may apply to the Illinois Gaming Board instead. The team is deemed a sports facility, must operate through a designee under Section 25-40(a), and pays a $1,000,000 initial fee that is later adjusted to 5% of its first-year handle. The bill was re-referred to the House Rules Committee under Rule 19(a) on 2025-03-21, which effectively stalls it.","mechanics":["Eligibility (Sec. 25-47(a)): the public entity owning the sports facility must not have applied for a master sports wagering license. The team must play the majority of its home contests at that facility and hold written authorization from the public entity.","The team is deemed a 'sports facility' for purposes of the Act. It must operate through a designee as defined in Sec. 25-40(a), and the designee is treated as the facility owner for all purposes under the Act and its rules.","Fee (Sec. 25-47(b)): the initial license fee is $1,000,000. Twelve months after sportsbook operations begin, the fee is adjusted based on 5% of handle from the first 12 months of operations.","The license term is 4 years.","Sec. 25-47(c) authorizes the Illinois Gaming Board to adopt implementing rules.","The bill contains no separate effective date or tax-rate change."],"issue_tags":["licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Most of the listed bills are either broad authorization-and-tax frameworks for new markets (AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, HI HB 1308/SB1569) or consumer-protection and integrity measures (CT HB05229, LA SB354). IL HB2761 instead makes a narrow tweak to an existing licensing category, letting a tenant team stand in for a publicly owned venue. The closest analogue is MA H481, which adjusts a specific license class (category 2 retail licenses), though for a different purpose. The handle-based fee true-up (5% of first-year handle) is unusual among the listed bills, which generally use flat fees or revenue-based taxes.","watch":"Operators should watch the 5% of first-year handle fee true-up, because handle-based charges can far exceed the $1,000,000 base and may shape the economics of designee and market-access deals with team licensees, and should also watch whether the measure revives from the Rules Committee or reappears as an amendment in a larger gaming package.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":2912,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:44:20.923Z"},"IL-HB2755":{"summary":"HB2755, enacted as Public Act 104-0006, is Illinois's FY2026 omnibus revenue act amending multiple tax statutes. The excerpt provided covers only Article 5: a new 2025 tax amnesty window under the Tax Delinquency Amnesty Act and amendments to Section 9 of the Use Tax Act on retailer returns, vendor discounts, and fund deposits. The excerpt cuts off before any wagering or gaming provisions, so the full chaptered text must be checked for gaming articles.","mechanics":["Tax amnesty (35 ILCS 745/10): a new window from October 1, 2025 through November 15, 2025 covers taxable periods ending after June 30, 2018 and before July 1, 2024. Clause (iv) refers to the window ending November 17, 2025, an internal date inconsistency.","Amnesty terms: on full payment of tax due, the Department of Revenue abates interest and penalties and forgoes civil or criminal prosecution. Taxpayers in pending criminal investigations or tax litigation are excluded, and underpayment voids the amnesty.","Amnesty proceeds that would go to the General Revenue Fund are split half to the Common School Fund and half to the GRF, with 2% going to the Tax Compliance and Administration Fund.","Use Tax Act Sec. 9 (35 ILCS 105/9): for returns due on or after January 1, 2025, the 1.75% retailer collection discount is capped at $1,000 per month in the aggregate across the Use, Retailers' Occupation, Service Occupation and Service Use Tax Acts.","Sec. 9 also restates electronic filing and payment thresholds, quarter-monthly payment rules, and monthly earmarks, including McCormick Place Expansion Project Fund deposits rising to $375M in FY2027 and $450M from FY2036."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"Like NY A 3009, a revenue omnibus that sets gaming tax rates (Part GG) and mobile sports tax problem-gambling funding (Part OO), HB2755 is a broad budget revenue vehicle rather than a standalone gaming bill, but the excerpt shows no gaming-specific part. It is unlike KS SB303, which expressly imposes a 2% excise tax on the amount wagered, and AK HB145/SB194, which create a mobile sports wagering tax. Its closest analogue is an omnibus tax bill such as MN SF2374, where any wagering impact is buried among general tax changes.","watch":"Obtain and review the full text of P.A. 104-0006 beyond this excerpt, since omnibus Illinois revenue acts often carry Sports Wagering Act or gaming tax changes in later articles. Separately confirm whether any operator liabilities collected by the Department of Revenue could qualify for the October to November 2025 amnesty window.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":1826514,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:44:31.096Z"},"IL-HB2880":{"summary":"HB2880 would delete the one-year pre-employment cooling-off period for staff of the Illinois Racing Board and the Illinois Gaming Board. Under current law, neither board may hire someone who, in the prior year, was employed by or paid by a person or entity (or its parent or affiliate) that did business with the board, a racing licensee, or a gambling licensee. The bill keeps the identical one-year bar for board members.","mechanics":["Horse Racing Act, 230 ILCS 5/6(f): removes the references to 'employee' and 'employment' from the one-year look-back, so the bar applies only to Board members within one year before gubernatorial nomination.","Gambling Act, 230 ILCS 10/5(a)(8): strikes the sentence barring Gaming Board employees from having been employed by, or paid by, any entity (or its parent or affiliate) that did business with the Board, a licensee, or a Racing Act licensee in the year before hiring.","Retained in 5(a)(8): no Gaming Board employee may be, or have a spouse, parent or child who is, an official of, or hold a financial interest in or financial relation with, any gambling operator or horse-racing organization in Illinois. Violations remain grounds for termination.","Retained for members: the one-year look-back for Gaming Board members in 5(a)(2), including the public disclosure of prior gaming affiliations within 30 days after nomination. The Racing Board conflict-of-interest, gift and Ethics Act Section 5-45 restrictions in 6(d)-(f) also remain.","The Gaming Board administers the Sports Wagering Act (5(b)(13.2)), so the change reaches staff who regulate sports wagering licensees.","No effective-date provision. The bill was re-referred to the House Rules Committee under Rule 19(a) on 2025-03-21, which effectively stalls it."],"issue_tags":["integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"The closest analog is FL S1580, which keeps Florida's employment-ineligibility rules for the Gaming Control Commission but lets an ineligible person seek a waiver based on expertise. Illinois HB2880 instead repeals the employee look-back outright, with no case-by-case review. CO SB 163 also concerns regulator structure, but it deals with delegating licensing duties from the commission to the division rather than staff ethics. MD HB 1612 moves the other way on integrity, targeting campaign contributions and independent evaluation of sports wagering.","watch":"If revived, the bill would let the Gaming Board hire recently departed operator, supplier or vendor personnel into roles overseeing sports wagering, but the surviving 'financial interest or financial relation' bar in 230 ILCS 10/5(a)(8) could still disqualify hires with deferred compensation, equity or severance ties to a licensee.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":34593,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:44:33.416Z"},"IL-HB2949":{"summary":"HB2949 is Illinois's Fiscal Year 2027 Budget Implementation Act, signed as Public Act 104-0466, which makes statutory changes needed to carry out the FY27 budget. The portion of the enrolled text provided amends only the State Employees Group Insurance Act of 1971 and contains no sports-betting or gaming provisions. The excerpt is truncated partway through Section 10(k), so later Articles were not reviewed.","mechanics":["Section 1-1 sets the short title as the Fiscal Year 2027 Budget Implementation Act, and Section 1-5 states its purpose is to implement the FY27 State budget.","Section 5-2 amends Sections 6.5, 6.10, 10, 11 and 13.1 of the State Employees Group Insurance Act (5 ILCS 375).","From July 1, 2026, Sec. 6.10 lets CMS set community college employee and employer contributions to the Community College Health Insurance Security Fund. Each year's rate may not exceed 105% of the prior year's percentage, replacing the 0.1-percentage-point annual cap that applied July 1, 2024 through June 30, 2026.","CMS may hold accounts outside the State treasury to receive transfers from three funds: the Teacher Health Insurance Security Fund, the Community College Health Insurance Security Fund and the Local Government Health Insurance Reserve Fund. Those moneys may be used only for claims payments by administrative service organizations, not for their administrative fees.","Existing TRS retiree premium rules are carried forward, including the cap of 105% of the prior year's premium for FY2008 onward and the 75%/50% fund subsidy split for managed care versus major medical coverage."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. On its own, this is an omnibus budget implementation vehicle. The provided text deals only with public-employee and retiree health insurance financing, with no wagering tax, licensing or integrity provisions.","watch":"Because Illinois has used budget and revenue omnibus bills to change wagering taxes in recent years, operators' counsel should check the full enrolled text of PA 104-0466 beyond this truncated excerpt for any Article amending the Sports Wagering Act (230 ILCS 45) or the Illinois Gambling Act, or redirecting wagering fund transfers.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":1318718,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:44:35.527Z"},"IL-HB2878":{"summary":"HB2878 amends Sections 25-10 and 25-45 of the Illinois Sports Wagering Act to restructure the standalone online master sports wagering license. Per the synopsis, it sets a $250,000 application fee and a $15,000,000 license fee, and removes the statutory cap of 3 online-only licenses and the competitive selection process. It also strips the eligibility, disclosure and diversity-outreach provisions, which would effectively open Illinois to any qualifying online operator. It takes effect immediately upon becoming law.","mechanics":["Sec. 25-45(b): nonrefundable application fee of $250,000 and initial license fee of $15,000,000, compared with the $20,000,000 fee tied to the 3-license competitive process in current subsection (a). The license is valid for 4 years and renewable for $1,000,000 under subsection (c).","Per the synopsis, it removes the provision directing the Board to issue 3 master sports wagering licenses to online sports wagering operators and the public notice and solicitation requirements, so the number of online-only licenses would no longer be capped by statute.","Sec. 25-45(d): states that a master sports wagering licensee may conduct sports wagering over the Internet or through a mobile application. Per the synopsis, the enumerated eligibility criteria (age 21, no felony or Article 28 gambling convictions, no dishonesty or moral turpitude crimes, demonstrated skill, Board standards) are removed.","Subsections (e) through (l) are blanked. These cover public opening of applications, the 90-day winning-bidder selection, the post-selection compliance check, the no-hearing-right clause, application plans as license conditions and the duty to disclose material changes, and DCEO/Board outreach to minority-, women-, veteran- and disability-owned businesses.","Sec. 25-10: a conforming change to definitions, most likely tied to the 'winning bidder' and competitive-selection concept. The struck text is not visible in the version provided.","Section 99: effective immediately upon becoming law."],"issue_tags":["licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike AK HB145 and SB194 and HI HB 1308, HB 2570 and SB1569, which build mobile sports wagering licensing regimes from scratch, HB2878 retools an existing, mature market by converting a scarce, competitively bid online-only license into what reads as an open-entry license at a fixed fee. MA H481 also adjusts an existing license category, but it targets retail category 2 licenses rather than online access. Unusually, the bill lowers rather than raises the fee and drops a statutory license cap and diversity-outreach mandates, which runs against the general trend of tightening entry.","watch":"Operators should watch whether removing the 3-license cap and the competitive process lets new online-only entrants in for $15 million, eroding the market position of tethered casino and track licensees. They should also check whether the deleted statutory eligibility and disclosure duties are replaced by Board rulemaking under remaining Sec. 25-45(d).","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":15955,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:44:35.558Z"},"IL-HB3080":{"summary":"HB3080 would create a standalone Illinois Internet Gaming Act legalizing online casino games, including slots, table games, poker, live-dealer games and tournaments, under Illinois Gaming Board regulation. Online licenses would be available only to existing casino owners licensees and racetrack organization licensees, who may partner with up to three Internet management service providers. Online gaming revenue would be taxed at 25% of adjusted gross gaming revenue, and sports wagering and skill-based fantasy contests are expressly excluded. The bill was re-referred to the House Rules Committee on 2025-03-21 under Rule 19(a), so it is effectively stalled for now.","mechanics":["Licensing (Sec. 35): an Internet gaming license is issued on request to owners and organization licensees for a $250,000 fee ($100,000 renewal) and runs with the underlying license. The Internet management service provider license costs $100,000, or $50,000 for existing licensees, with a $50,000 four-year renewal. The supplier license costs $75,000 or $50,000, and the occupational license costs $150 or $100.","Fast track for existing operators (Sec. 35(f)-(g)): temporary licenses must be issued within 30 days to holders of Sports Wagering Act licenses or equivalent out-of-state iGaming licenses. Background checks are waived for anyone previously investigated under the Sports Wagering Act.","Workforce condition (Sec. 35(i)): the Board may not license an owners or organization licensee that has cut its workforce by 25% or more since February 28, 2020. The same 25% test applies at each renewal.","Structure (Sec. 20): each licensee may offer up to 3 branded skins. Primary servers may be anywhere in the U.S. for one year, after which they must be located in Illinois.","Tax (Sec. 45): a 25% privilege tax on adjusted gross gaming revenue, which excludes free play and promotional credits. It is the exclusive tax, with local taxes preempted. Negative revenue may be carried forward up to 12 months, and proceeds go to the State Gaming Fund for distribution under Sec. 13 of the Illinois Gambling Act.","Player protection and operations: minimum age is 21. The Board must keep a statewide self-exclusion database exempt from FOIA. Platforms must offer daily, weekly and monthly deposit and spend limits (Sec. 40). Accounts may be shared with sports wagering accounts, and crypto deposits are allowed (Sec. 30). Out-of-state and multi-state compacts such as poker are permitted (Sec. 60). Advertising samples must be kept for 2 years, but no preapproval is required (Sec. 25(h)). Emergency rules are due within 90 days, and the Act takes effect immediately."],"issue_tags":["legalize","licensing","tax","advertising"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Among the listed bills, the closest analogues are MA H332 and MA H4431, which likewise propose regulating internet gaming. Most other listed bills concern sports wagering only, such as AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686 and HI HB 1308/SB1569, whereas HB3080 expressly carves out sports wagering and builds on Illinois' existing Sports Wagering Act licensees. Unlike CT HB05229, which would restrict gaming advertising and AI-based bet targeting, HB3080 takes a light-touch approach: it bars Board preapproval of ads and allows advertising from the date of temporary licensure. It also contains an unusual labor-linked eligibility bar, the 25% workforce-reduction test, and an in-state server mandate after one year.","watch":"Operators should watch the Sec. 35(i) workforce-reduction bar, which could disqualify casino or racetrack partners, along with the 3-skin cap, the one-year in-state server requirement, and whether the 25% rate and the sports-wagering-licensee fast track survive any revival of the bill from Rules Committee.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":37061,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:44:49.364Z"},"IL-HB3276":{"summary":"HB3276 adds Section 25-70a to the Illinois Sports Wagering Act, creating a Department of the Lottery sports wagering pilot program that runs through sports lottery terminals placed at any lottery retailer, including bars, video gaming locations and retail food establishments. The definition of terminal expressly includes Internet, mobile application and other digital wagering. The program sits outside Gaming Board jurisdiction, falls under the Lottery's private management agreement, and splits gross gaming revenue among the State, the central system provider, terminal owners/operators and retailers. It replaces references to the existing Section 25-70 lottery program throughout the Lottery Law and amends Board-powers Section 25-15 to carve out the new program.","mechanics":["Revenue split under 25-70a(g): gross gaming revenue (gross dollars played minus prizes) goes 30% to the State, of which the local municipality receives 5%; 20% to the central system provider; the remainder is split evenly between the terminal owner/operator and the lottery retailer. Subsection (j) separately directs net proceeds into the State Lottery Fund daily or weekly, with the remainder after provider and agent payments transferred on the 15th of each month to the Capital Projects Fund.","Placement and access: terminals may go in any lottery retail location (25-70a(d)). Terminals cannot dispense cash, only receipt tickets (25-70a(e)). The 'sports lottery terminal' definition covers Internet, mobile and digital platforms.","Age limit: retailers may not permit anyone under 21 to use a terminal or sports wagering application; a knowing violation is a business offense with a fine of up to $5,000 (25-70a(f)).","Testing: terminals must be tested and approved under Department rules by accredited independent labs and be ready for play within 90 days of the effective date. Lottery games are folded into the private management agreement and competitive bidding under Lottery Law Section 9.1.","Department authority: exclusive jurisdiction over the program, with implementation required by June 30, 2025. Rulemaking covers penalties, advertising standards, minority business ownership and hardware apportionment. Emergency rulemaking is authorized under new 5 ILCS 100/5-45.65, which sunsets one year after enactment.","Who may own terminals: the Department facilitates purchase or lease, and video gaming terminal operators, Illinois Gambling Act owners licensees and lottery retailers may all own or lease terminals (25-70a(k)). Wager types and payouts are set by Department rule. The bill is effective immediately."],"issue_tags":["legalize","licensing","advertising","tax"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Most listed bills, such as AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686 and HI HB 1308/SB1569, authorize licensed private mobile operators taxed on adjusted gross revenue under a gaming regulator. HB3276 instead builds a lottery-run retail-terminal and digital channel with a fixed revenue split to retailers, terminal operators and a central provider, outside the Gaming Board. It most resembles IN HB1078 in expanding lottery distribution, but goes much further into sports wagering. Unlike CT HB05229 or LA SB354, it imposes no new consumer-protection or bet-type limits on existing operators.","watch":"Watch whether the bill revives in Rules Committee, because it would create a lottery-controlled, Board-exempt sports wagering channel with mobile and digital reach, placed in thousands of bars and video gaming locations. That channel would compete directly with master sports wagering licensees while escaping the Act's licensing, integrity and tax regime.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":22338,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:44:51.438Z"},"IL-HB3159":{"summary":"HB3159 amends Section 25-90 of the Illinois Sports Wagering Act (230 ILCS 45/25-90) to add a new subsection (f). Beginning July 1, 2026, it imposes an additional 2% tax on adjusted gross receipts from all sports wagers placed within Illinois, on top of the existing privilege taxes. Subject to appropriation, the revenue would go from the Sports Wagering Fund to public, full-time career local government fire departments, fire districts and EMS ambulance departments.","mechanics":["New Sec. 25-90(f): 2% of adjusted gross receipts from sports wagers placed within the State, effective July 1, 2026, imposed 'for the privilege of holding a license to operate sports wagering.'","Expressly stacks 'in addition to' the taxes under subsections (a), (d) and (d-5), which as shown in the bill are the tiered privilege taxes of 20%, 25%, 30%, 35% and 40% on annual AGR across the $30M, $50M, $100M and $200M bands, for both online and retail wagering.","The new subsection does not reference the existing (a-5) 2% surcharge on wagers placed in a home rule county over 3,000,000 (Cook County), so Cook County wagers would appear to bear both surcharges, for a combined 4% on top of the tiered rate.","Proceeds are paid from the Sports Wagering Fund subject to General Assembly appropriation, and only to public, full-time career fire and EMS departments, which by its terms excludes volunteer departments.","Uses the term 'adjusted gross receipts' rather than the Act's 'adjusted gross sports wagering receipts,' mirroring the drafting of (a-5).","Status: introduced, then re-referred to House Rules Committee under Rule 19(a) on 2025-03-21, which effectively stalls it unless revived."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686 and HI HB 1308, which would create new sports wagering markets with their own tax structures, HB3159 adds a dedicated surcharge to an existing, already high-rate progressive regime. Its closest analogue is KS SB303, which would impose a 2% excise tax; however, the Kansas tax is levied on the amount wagered (handle), while the Illinois 2% is levied on adjusted gross receipts. What is distinctive about HB3159 is the earmark of statewide sports wagering tax revenue for career fire and EMS departments, layered over the existing Cook County criminal-justice surcharge.","watch":"Watch for the effective tax stacking: the bill adds 2% statewide on top of the 20–40% tiers and, for Cook County wagers, the existing (a-5) 2%. If revived from Rules, it could also become a vehicle for further amendments to Section 25-90, so check how it interacts with any later changes to that section.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":7659,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:44:53.161Z"},"IL-HB3274":{"summary":"HB3274 would create a statutory safe harbor in the Prizes and Gifts Act for 'prize and gift kiosks.' These are no-purchase-necessary sweepstakes-style devices that promote a bona fide product and can award cash prizes, and they would be lawful if they meet specified technical standards. Machines outside those standards are folded into the Criminal Code's definition of 'gambling device,' and any offense involving them becomes a Class 4 felony. The bill also bars the Illinois Gaming Board from disciplining or delaying video gaming licensees that operate compliant devices, and it prohibits municipalities from restricting any activity exempted under 720 ILCS 5/28-1(b).","mechanics":["Prizes and Gifts Act Sec. 10 defines 'prize and gift kiosk' and imposes standards. Each kiosk needs a revenue-based self-contained fill system that shuts off at the end of a predetermined cycle; time- or spin-based systems are excluded. It also needs a 'route boost plus' monitoring system that records cash in, winnings, entries, malfunctions and disconnections, and remote activations and disabling, and that cannot alter play or read patron personal or financial data. The kiosk may not connect to the Internet or a network except through a redemption vault. Free play is limited to a mail-in code.","New Sec. 33 makes it unlawful to operate a kiosk that fails these standards, sells anything other than a 'bona fide product' (excluding discount coupons, Internet access and phone cards), or offers free-play options beyond the mail-in method. New Sec. 45 requires registration with the Department of Revenue and an annual fee set by DOR. Misfiling a device as a kiosk is treated as filing a false report, with seizure available, and DOR and the Liquor Control Commission (or a public or private task force) have jurisdiction.","Criminal Code 28-2(a)(iii) adds to the 'gambling device' definition vending or electronic machines that award and record credits or are Internet- or network-connected, unless they meet the kiosk fill and monitoring standards. 28-1(c)(2) makes any gambling offense involving such a device a Class 4 felony, and 28-1(b)(13) removes the no-purchase sweepstakes exemption for these devices.","Video Gaming Act Sec. 35 (230 ILCS 40/35) says applicants and licensees do not violate the Act or IGB rules, specifically 11 Ill. Adm. Code 1800.420, and face no discipline, delay or denial for operating devices that are not gambling under 28-1(b) or 28-2(a)(iii).","New 720 ILCS 5/28-1.2 bars any municipality from imposing restrictions or prohibitions on activities lawful under 28-1(b). That subsection covers more than kiosks; it also includes video gaming, casino gaming, pari-mutuel betting and sports wagering under the Sports Wagering Act.","The bill is effective immediately on becoming law. It was re-referred to House Rules under Rule 19(a) on 2025-03-21 and is effectively stalled."],"issue_tags":["sweeps","legalize","licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Most of the listed bills that deal with unregulated or illegal gaming push toward enforcement. MD HB1226 (Illegal Online Gambling Enforcement Act), LA HB883 (illegal online gambling enforcement) and FL S1580 (illegal gaming penalties) all crack down on gray-market products. HB3274 instead creates a registration-based legal channel for land-based sweepstakes kiosks while criminalizing noncompliant machines. None of the listed bills comparably shields existing gaming licensees from regulator discipline, and none preempts municipal regulation of all exempted gambling activities. Unlike the AK, GA and HI sports wagering authorization bills, it adds no sports-betting framework or tax.","watch":"Watch the breadly worded municipal preemption in new 28-1.2: it would bar local restrictions on all activity exempt under 28-1(b), including sports wagering, while also legitimizing cash-prize sweepstakes kiosks inside video gaming locations outside the Gaming Board's licensing and tax regime.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":30726,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:44:58.383Z"},"IL-HB3778":{"summary":"HB3778 creates the Metropolitan Mobility Authority Act, consolidating the Chicago Transit Authority, Metra (Commuter Rail Division), Pace (Suburban Bus Division) and the RTA into a single Metropolitan Mobility Authority covering Cook, DuPage, Kane, Lake, McHenry and Will counties, abolishing the Service Boards and repealing the Metropolitan Transit Authority Act and RTA Act. It also creates the Equitable Transit-Supportive Development Act and Fund, an IDOT Office of Public Transportation Support, and adds the Authority Chair as a nonvoting member of the Tollway board. Nothing in the supplied text addresses sports betting, wagering or gaming.","mechanics":["Sec. 3.02: on the effective date, CTA, Metra, Pace and RTA are consolidated into the Authority, which succeeds to all their assets, contracts, ordinances and liabilities.","Sec. 2.04: the Board has 3 Governor appointees, 5 Chicago Mayor appointees, 5 Cook County Board President appointees and 1 each from the DuPage, Kane, Lake, McHenry and Will county board chairs, plus nonvoting members including the IDOT Secretary and the Tollway Chair.","Secs. 2.09 and 3.01: Directors are paid $25,000 a year, and Transition Committee members may receive up to $150,000 more per year on an hourly basis.","Sec. 3.01(c) sets the transition timetable: Board seated within 1 year, permanent CEO within 3 years, all functions transferred within 4 years.","Sec. 4.02(12) lets the Authority set ordinance fines of up to $5,000 per offense, effective 10 days after newspaper publication.","Sec. 3.03: all employees, collective bargaining agreements and pension obligations transfer to the Authority, with impasse disputes going to mediation and then binding arbitration."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. This is a transit governance consolidation bill with no gaming content in the supplied text. Gaming relevance could arise only if the conforming amendments in the unprovided portion touch gaming revenue streams or advertising on transit property, and nothing in the text shown indicates that.","watch":"Check whether the conforming amendments or later funding amendments pledge gaming or sports-wagering tax revenue to the new Authority, or whether Authority ordinances under Sec. 4.02(12) restrict gambling advertising on transit property.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":941053,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:45:05.391Z"},"IL-HB4549":{"summary":"HB4549 amends Section 6z-27 of the Illinois State Finance Act (30 ILCS 105/6z-27) to reset the annual sweep into the Audit Expense Fund. It directs the Comptroller and Treasurer to move listed sums from many state funds within 30 days after July 1, 2026 (replacing the July 1, 2025 date) to pay for Auditor General audits. Several gaming-related funds are on the list, but the bill does not touch wagering regulation, taxes or operator obligations.","mechanics":["Transfers are due within 30 days after July 1, 2026, or as soon as practical; the date change from 2025 is the only amendment visibly marked in the text.","Gaming-related transfers include the Sports Wagering Fund ($11,518), the State Gaming Fund ($176,882 and a second line of $1,997), the State Lottery Fund ($120,030 and $311,489), the Horse Racing Fund ($205,483) and the Illinois Gaming Law Enforcement Fund ($1,285).","Any shortfall in a source fund is backfilled from the General Revenue Fund.","Amounts transferred beyond actual audit costs are returned to the source fund on or after September 30 at the Auditor General's direction; federal and State trust funds are excluded unless their trustees direct a transfer.","The Act takes effect upon becoming law (Section 99). It was re-referred to the Rules Committee under Rule 19(a) on 2026-03-27, so it is effectively stalled."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. This is Illinois's routine annual audit-cost transfer, and its gaming content is limited to line items charging audit costs against the Sports Wagering, State Gaming, Lottery and Horse Racing funds. It is unusual only in that several funds appear twice with different amounts, such as the Agricultural Premium Fund and the State Gaming Fund, and the General Revenue Fund and State Pensions Fund appear twice at identical amounts ($17,653,153 and $500,000).","watch":"Nothing here imposes new costs on operators, so the main thing to watch is whether the Sports Wagering Fund ($11,518) and State Gaming Fund draws resurface in the FY2027 budget package, likely through a budget implementation bill, rather than in this stalled stand-alone bill.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":23693,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:45:27.688Z"},"IL-HB4171":{"summary":"HB4171 adds Section 25-115 to the Illinois Sports Wagering Act (230 ILCS 45/25-115) to make the regulation and licensing of sports wagering, including fees, surcharges or other charges for the privilege of conducting or participating in it, exclusive state functions. It bars home rule units from regulating, licensing or taxing sports wagering or receipts from it. The bill takes effect on becoming law, but it was re-referred to the House Rules Committee under Rule 19(a) on 2026-04-17, which effectively parks it.","mechanics":["Sec. 25-115(a): the regulation and licensing of sports wagering, including 'fees, surcharges, or other costs for the privilege of conducting or participating in sports wagering,' are declared exclusive powers and functions of the State. Home rule units may not regulate or license sports wagering.","Subsection (a) is framed as a denial of home rule power under Ill. Const. Art. VII, Sec. 6(h), the exclusive-state-exercise clause.","Sec. 25-115(b): home rule units may not impose or collect a tax on sports wagering or on receipts generated from sports wagering.","Subsection (b) is framed as a denial under Art. VII, Sec. 6(g), which requires a three-fifths vote of each house to limit home rule taxing power. That is a higher passage threshold than a simple majority.","The preemption covers both the operator side ('conducting') and the bettor side ('participating'), so per-wager surcharges on patrons are captured as well as taxes on operator receipts.","Section 99 makes the Act effective immediately. The text contains no grandfathering, refund or transition language for any existing local levies."],"issue_tags":["tax","licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"None of the listed bills deals with preempting local regulation or taxation of sports wagering. Most of them either authorize and tax wagering at the state level (AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, HI HB 1308/HB 2570/SB1569) or add state-level levies and withholding: KS SB303 imposes a 2% excise on the amount wagered, and IA SF 605 addresses income tax withholding on winnings. HB4171 runs the other way, protecting operators from an additional local layer of tax and licensing rather than setting state rates. It is notable as a stand-alone, two-subsection preemption bill.","watch":"Watch whether the bill moves out of Rules, given the Rule 19(a) re-referral and the three-fifths vote needed for the Sec. 6(g) tax limit. Also watch whether 'a tax on receipts generated from sports wagering' would be read to reach generally applicable local amusement or gross-receipts taxes applied to wagering, and note that the bill has no language addressing existing local levies already in force.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":1548,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:45:30.610Z"},"IL-HB3733":{"summary":"HB3733 targets so-called sweepstakes or 'no-chance' gaming machines in Illinois. It adds a new category to the Criminal Code's definition of 'gambling device' covering any vending or electronic machine that offers a chance-based prize entry tied to a purchase or to a free coupon or credit. It makes any offense involving such a device a Class 4 felony and removes the Video Gaming Act language that let unlicensed game devices operate if their use was not 'gambling.' The bill was re-referred to Rules under Rule 19(a) on 2026-03-27 and is effectively stalled.","mechanics":["New 720 ILCS 5/28-2(a)(iii): a 'gambling device' includes any vending or other electronic machine or device, including credit-awarding machines with a circuit, meter or switch that records credit removal. This applies if the machine offers entry into a contest, sweepstakes or other chance-based selection for a prize, and the offer is incidental to (A) a purchase or (B) the purchase or free receipt of a coupon, voucher or credit redeemable at the machine or elsewhere.","28-1(b)(13) no-purchase-necessary exemption is narrowed: it no longer protects participation accomplished through a 28-2(a)(iii) device.","New 28-1(c)(2): any gambling offense involving a 28-2(a)(iii) device is a Class 4 felony, overriding the usual Class A misdemeanor for a first offense.","The vending-machine exclusion in 28-2(a)(2) becomes subject to 'except as otherwise provided,' and new 28-2(d) states an intent to reach any mechanism using 'subterfuge or pretense' to avoid gambling-device status.","230 ILCS 40/35 (Video Gaming Act) strikes the sentence allowing use of an unlicensed game device in an activity that is not gambling under 28-1(b). Existing Class 4 felony, seizure and destruction provisions for unlicensed credit-awarding chance devices remain.","Effective immediately upon becoming law. The existing sports wagering exemption in 28-1(b)(15) is unchanged."],"issue_tags":["sweeps"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"The closest analogue is CA AB 831, which also attacks sweepstakes run on electronic video monitors, but through California's unfair-practices sweepstakes statute rather than by redefining criminal gambling devices with felony penalties. FL S1580 and FL H0189 address illegal gaming machines and criminal penalties more broadly as part of commission and gaming overhauls, while HB3733 is a narrow, device-focused fix. The other listed bills (AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, HI HB 1308) concern authorizing and taxing sports wagering, which this bill does not touch.","watch":"The phrase 'vending or other electronic machine or device' combined with the 28-2(d) anti-subterfuge intent is broad enough that a revived version could be argued to reach purchase-linked promotional sweepstakes or online sweepstakes-casino play on personal devices. Operators should watch for amendments clarifying its scope, even though the bill is currently parked in Rules.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":21190,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:45:31.614Z"},"IL-HB3473":{"summary":"HB3473 amends the Illinois Video Gaming Act and the Criminal Code of 2012 to crack down on unlicensed 'skill' and sweepstakes-style machines. It expands the definition of 'gambling device' to cover electronic and internet-connected credit-awarding machines, makes offenses involving them a Class 4 felony, and narrows the no-purchase-necessary exemption so it does not protect those devices. It also creates a Gaming Disparity Task Force, funded by a fee on large terminal operators, to study minority participation in video gaming, direct set-aside payments to a minority-owned terminal operator, and design a sunset process tied to opening video gaming in Chicago.","mechanics":["Criminal Code 28-2(a)(iii) adds to 'gambling device' any vending or other electronic machine that awards credits and has a circuit, meter, or switch that removes and records credits, or that connects to the Internet or a networked system to play or to retrieve data. Carve-outs apply for a 'redemption vault' and for certain non-revenue-based, self-terminating fill systems; the drafting of these carve-outs is garbled.","Section 28-1(b)(13), the no-purchase games of skill or chance exemption, no longer applies where play uses an (a)(iii) device. New 28-1(c)(2) makes any gambling offense involving such a device a Class 4 felony regardless of prior convictions.","Video Gaming Act Sec. 35 adds a safe harbor: applicants and licensees are not in violation of the Act or Board rule 11 Ill. Adm. Code 1800.420, and face no discipline, delay, or license denial, for operating a gaming device that complies with and is not gambling under 28-1(b) or 28-2(a)(iii).","New Sec. 76 creates a 5-member, Governor-appointed Gaming Disparity Task Force. It has data-collection and subpoena powers and must deliver an annual disparity and availability study to the General Assembly by February 1.","The Task Force is funded by a fee it sets, collected by the Gaming Board from terminal operators with at least $5,000,000 in net terminal income in any month. Part of the fee is set aside for a minority business enterprise-owned terminal operator, with the share pegged to that minority group's percentage of the Illinois population.","Sec. 76(h) requires a sunset process for 28-1(b)(13)-exempt devices. Minimum conditions include authorizing video gaming in municipalities over 2,000,000 people (Chicago), deciding all eligible applications there, and an independent audit showing a majority of licensed establishments there are served by MBE terminal operators. The Act is effective immediately."],"issue_tags":["sweeps","licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Most listed bills authorize or regulate sports wagering, such as AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686 and HI HB 1308/SB1569, or online casino, such as MA H332/S235; HB3473 addresses neither. The closest analogues are FL H0189 (gaming machines, criminal penalties, internet gambling) and FL S1580 and LA HB883 (illegal gaming enforcement). HB3473 is unusual in pairing a felony crackdown on unregulated connected machines with a race-conscious funding set-aside for a minority-owned terminal operator. It also ties the sunset of the no-purchase exemption to Chicago video gaming and MBE majority benchmarks.","watch":"The broad internet-connected 'gambling device' definition and Class 4 felony could reach networked kiosks or promotional and sweepstakes products, even though the sports wagering exemption in 28-1(b)(15) is untouched; any revival from the Rules Committee (re-referred under Rule 19(a) on 2026-04-17) would warrant a review of the carve-out drafting and of the fee on operators above $5M in monthly net terminal income.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":27898,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:45:37.081Z"},"IL-HB4774":{"summary":"HB4774 amends the Illinois Pull Tabs and Jar Games Act to let video gaming licensees (licensed establishments, truck stops and large truck stops) obtain Department of Revenue licenses to run pull tabs and jar games for charitable purposes. It also adds Section 21 to the Video Gaming Act, letting the Illinois Gaming Board license any licensed video gaming establishment, including fraternal and veterans establishments, to sell sports betting squares as a low-stakes, chance-based promotional game. Squares proceeds may be kept as operating revenue taxed like net terminal income, or directed to charity.","mechanics":["Pull tabs: adds 'video gaming licensee' as a third eligible license class under 230 ILCS 20/2(iii) at the existing $500 annual fee. Applicants must file a sworn statement of the charitable purpose, and the entire net proceeds must go to that purpose (Sec. 3(1)(B), 4(1)).","Existing pull tab limits still apply to video gaming licensees: $2 maximum per chance, $500 maximum single prize, 6,000 tickets per game, $5,000 daily prize cap, 18+ play, and play only on the licensed premises (Sec. 4(4)-(6)).","Sports betting squares (new 230 ILCS 40/21) are licensed by the Illinois Gaming Board on application. The establishment must set a price cap of $100 per square, set a mandatory payout percentage, and publicly post its rules.","Squares must be a fixed-grid pool of randomly assigned numbers. Odds, parlays, proposition bets and any online or mobile component are excluded (Sec. 21(c)).","Squares proceeds may be claimed as operational revenue, which is treated like net terminal income under Section 60 (the video gaming tax), or directed to a charity of the establishment's choice.","The Board oversees compliance and adopts implementing rules. The bill sets no license fee or effective date for the squares license."],"issue_tags":["licensing","tax","legalize","sweeps"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Most listed bills concern regulated sportsbooks: mobile wagering authorization and tax (AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, HI HB 1308/HB 2570/SB1569) or restrictions on bet types (LA SB354 bans props and micro-bets). HB4774 instead creates a parallel, non-sportsbook channel for sports-linked pools at bars and truck stops, overseen by the Gaming Board but outside the Sports Wagering Act. Its exclusion of props and parlays echoes LA SB354's skepticism of props, but here the aim is to keep squares distinct from sports wagering rather than to restrict licensed books.","watch":"Watch whether this retail sports-pool product, with its $100-per-square cap, operator-set payout and NTI-equivalent tax, becomes a lightly regulated competitor to licensed sportsbooks or blurs the line between promotional games and sports wagering under the Sports Wagering Act, and push for the Board's rules to cap pool sizes and set integrity and age requirements.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":25538,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:45:45.492Z"},"IL-HB4619":{"summary":"HB4619 amends Section 13(b) of the Illinois Gambling Act (230 ILCS 10/13) to earmark an additional amount equal to 0.50% of the adjusted gross receipts of the Waukegan riverboat (the licensee under Section 7(e-5)(3)) for the Lake County State's Attorney's Office, to fund its Gun Violence Prevention Initiative and community violence intervention partners. The money comes out of privilege tax revenue already deposited in the State Gaming Fund, so it redirects existing state revenue rather than raising the operator's tax rate. The bill was re-referred to the Rules Committee under Rule 19(a) on 2026-03-27, which in practice stalls it.","mechanics":["New earmark in Sec. 13(b): 0.50% of AGR generated by the Section 7(e-5)(3) riverboat (Waukegan) goes to the Lake County State's Attorney Office, remitted monthly and subject to appropriation.","At least 0.40% of AGR must directly fund the Lake County State's Attorney's Gun Violence Prevention Initiative; the remainder may go to community violence intervention partners.","The earmark is in addition to the existing 5% host-community share for this riverboat, split 70% Waukegan, 10% Park City, 15% North Chicago and 5% Lake County.","Privilege tax rates under subsection (a-5) (15% to 50% graduated on non-table games; 15%/20% on table games) are left unchanged, so the licensee's tax liability does not rise.","No effective date appears in the provided text, which is truncated at subsection (d-7); no sports wagering provisions are touched."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Like NY A08167/S05640, which would carve up to 2% of mobile sports tax revenue (capped at $23 million) out for youth sports, and NY S3009 Part OO, which sets the problem-gambling allocation from mobile sports tax, HB4619 reallocates existing gaming tax revenue to a dedicated purpose rather than changing the rate. It is far narrower than any of these: it targets a single casino's AGR and sends the money to a single prosecutor's office. Unlike KS SB303, which would impose a new 2% excise on all sports wagers, or NE LB421/LB438, which revise sports wagering tax distribution statewide, HB4619 touches only casino privilege tax and imposes no new operator burden.","watch":"Although the earmark leaves the Waukegan licensee's tax bill unchanged, operators should watch whether it sets a precedent for facility-specific, AGR-based local earmarks that could later be funded by rate increases, or extended to sports wagering privilege tax, if the State Gaming Fund residual shrinks.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":51289,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:45:48.922Z"},"IL-HB4797":{"summary":"HB4797 would create the Illinois Internet Gaming Act, legalizing online casino games (slots, table games, poker, live-dealer and tournaments, but not sports wagering or fantasy contests) through Illinois casino owners licensees and racetrack organization licensees, each of which may run up to 3 branded skins directly or through up to 3 licensed Internet management service providers. The Illinois Gaming Board would regulate, with fast-track and temporary licensing for entities already licensed under the Sports Wagering Act. A 25% privilege tax on adjusted gross gaming revenue would go to the State Gaming Fund. The bill was re-referred to the Rules Committee under Rule 19(a) on 2026-03-27, so it is effectively stalled for now.","mechanics":["Tax (Sec. 45): 25% privilege tax on adjusted gross gaming revenue, which excludes free play, promotional credits and non-cashable promotions. This is the exclusive tax, with no local or home-rule levies. Negative monthly revenue may be carried forward for up to 12 months. Of the revenue, 95% is distributed under Sec. 13 of the Illinois Gambling Act and 5% goes to municipalities and counties by population.","Licensing fees (Sec. 35): Internet gaming license is $250,000, with $100,000 renewal on the same term as the underlying owners or organization license. Internet management service provider license is $100,000, or $50,000 if already licensed under other State law, for 4 years with $50,000 renewal. Supplier license is $75,000 or $50,000. Occupational license is $150 or $100.","Licensing access (Sec. 35(f)-(h)): licenses issue on fee payment to holders of equivalent Sports Wagering Act or comparable out-of-state iGaming licenses. Temporary licenses must issue within 30 days, last one year and are renewable. Background checks are waived if already done under the Sports Wagering Act. The Board must set an equal launch date for all licensees.","Workforce condition (Sec. 35(i)): no license for an owners or organization licensee that has cut its workforce by 25% or more since Feb. 28, 2020. No renewal after a 25% cut since the last issuance or renewal.","Platform and patron rules (Secs. 20, 25, 30, 40): minimum age 21 with geolocation. Primary servers may be anywhere in the U.S. for one year, then must be in Illinois. Accounts may be shared with sports wagering accounts and opened remotely. Deposits are allowed by a wide range of methods, including crypto and cash at approved retail locations. Platforms must offer a statewide self-exclusion database and daily, weekly and monthly deposit and spend limits. Operators must keep 2 years of advertising samples, but no advertising preapproval is required.","Other: out-of-state and multijurisdictional wagers (e.g., poker) are allowed via reciprocal agreements (Sec. 60). Home-rule regulation is preempted (Sec. 65). Emergency rules are due within 90 days. Licensees must file annual diversity reports starting April 15, 2027. The Act is effective immediately."],"issue_tags":["legalize","tax","licensing","advertising"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Among the listed bills, only MA H332 and MA H4431 appear to address internet casino gaming directly; only titles are available for them, so detailed comparison isn't possible. Most other listed bills, such as AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686 and HI HB 1308/SB1569, would authorize sports wagering rather than iCasino. HB4797 instead explicitly excludes sports wagering and is built on Illinois's existing Sports Wagering Act licensees and shared accounts. Its advertising approach is light-touch (record-keeping only, no preapproval), in contrast to CT HB05229, which would restrict campus advertising and AI-targeted betting.","watch":"Operators should watch the 25% workforce-reduction bar on the land-based partner, the requirement to move primary servers into Illinois after one year, the 3-skin cap per licensee and the 25% rate, all of which could be negotiated if the bill revives from Rules.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":37476,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:45:54.878Z"},"IL-HB5034":{"summary":"HB5034 amends Section 25-45 of the Illinois Sports Wagering Act to replace the current cap of three competitively bid, online-only master sports wagering licenses ($20,000,000 each) with an open licensing path. Applicants would pay a $250,000 application fee and a $15,000,000 license fee. Per the synopsis, it also strips the competitive-selection machinery, the statutory eligibility criteria, the duty of disclosure and the DCEO/Board diversity outreach mandates. The bill was re-referred to the Rules Committee under Rule 19(a) on 2026-03-27, so it is effectively stalled for now.","mechanics":["Sec. 25-45(b): nonrefundable $250,000 application fee and a $15,000,000 initial license fee; the license is valid for 4 years and renewable for 4 years at $1,000,000 (Sec. 25-45(c)).","The synopsis states the bill removes the provision directing the Board to issue 3 online-only master licenses at $20,000,000 through an open and competitive selection process, so no numerical cap would remain in Section 25-45.","Sec. 25-45(d) keeps the authorization that a master sports wagering licensee may conduct sports wagering over the Internet or through a mobile application.","Removed, per the synopsis: the statutory eligibility test (age 21, no felony or Article 28 gambling conviction, no dishonesty or moral-turpitude crimes, skill and knowledge standards) and the 30-day public notice requirement.","Also removed: public opening of applications, the 90-day winning-bidder timeline, the licensee duty to disclose material changes, and the DCEO and Board outreach to minority-, women-, veteran- and disability-owned businesses (former subsections (e)-(l)).","Sec. 25-10 receives a conforming change, likely to the 'winning bidder' definition that is tied to the competitive selection. The bill is effective immediately upon becoming law."],"issue_tags":["licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Most listed bills either legalize mobile wagering from scratch, such as AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686 and HI HB 1308/SB1569, or adjust regulatory administration, such as CO SB 163 on commission delegation and MA H481 on category 2 retail licenses. HB5034 is different because it restructures an existing mature market: Illinois's online-only license tier would move from a capped, competitively bid model to an uncapped, fixed-fee model at a lower price ($15M versus $20M). Unusually, it also deletes statutory suitability criteria and diversity outreach mandates rather than adding consumer-protection or integrity rules, as CT HB05229 and LA SB354 do.","watch":"Watch whether the uncapped $15M online-only license survives revival from the Rules Committee, since it would lower the barrier for new national operators to enter Illinois without a tethered casino, track or sports-facility partner, and its deletion of the Sec. 25-45(d) eligibility criteria would shift suitability standards entirely to Board rulemaking.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":15955,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:45:56.551Z"},"IL-HB5035":{"summary":"HB5035 deletes the one-year pre-employment cooling-off period for staff of the Illinois Racing Board and the Illinois Gaming Board. Currently, neither agency can hire someone who, in the prior year, was employed by or paid by a person or entity (or its parent or affiliate) doing business with the Board, a racing licensee, or a Gambling Act licensee. The parallel one-year bar for Board members before gubernatorial nomination stays in place.","mechanics":["Section 5 amends 230 ILCS 5/6(f) (Horse Racing Act) to remove employees from the one-year look-back on employment or compensation from entities doing business with the Racing Board, a racing licensee, or a Gambling Act licensee; the bar continues to apply to Board members before nomination.","Section 10 amends 230 ILCS 10/5(a)(8) (Gambling Act) to delete the one-year pre-employment restriction for Gaming Board employees. Because the IGB administers the Sports Wagering Act under Sec. 5(b)(13.2), the change reaches sports-wagering regulatory staff.","The rest of Sec. 5(a)(8) is unchanged: it still bars hiring anyone who, or whose spouse, parent or child, is an official of, or has a financial interest in or financial relation with, an Illinois gambling operator or horse racing organization, and violations remain grounds for termination.","Other ethics rules for both Boards are untouched: the conflict-of-interest and gift bans in 230 ILCS 5/6(d)-(e), the Section 5-45 State Officials and Employees Ethics Act restrictions, and the IGB member one-year bar and disclosure duty in Sec. 5(a)(2).","The bill has no effective date clause or new enforcement provisions. It was re-referred to House Rules under Rule 19(a) on 2026-03-27, which effectively stalls it for now."],"issue_tags":["integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"The closest analogue on the list is FL S1580. It lets a person ineligible for employment with the Florida Gaming Control Commission seek a case-by-case waiver based on expertise, whereas HB5035 repeals the Illinois look-back for staff outright with no waiver process or Board review. CO SB 163 also concerns regulator structure, but it addresses how the Colorado commission may delegate licensing duties to its division, not staff eligibility. Most other listed bills, such as AK HB145, GA HB 686 and HI HB 1308, authorize or tax sports wagering and do not touch regulator hiring rules.","watch":"Operator counsel should watch whether this revives, because it would let former operator, supplier or vendor employees join IGB sports-wagering staff immediately, which could ease regulatory staffing but invite recusal and appearance-of-conflict challenges; the separate financial-interest bar in Sec. 5(a)(8) still applies.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":34592,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:46:02.842Z"},"IL-HB5059":{"summary":"HB5059 creates the ORACLE Act, an Attorney General-enforced consumer protection regime for prediction market platforms serving Illinois residents. It sets a 21+ age floor, bans athletic event, political, catastrophic, death and security markets for state users, and imposes responsible gaming, advertising, funding and anti-manipulation duties. It also bars markets that use gaming companies as market makers or revenue-share partners. The bill was re-referred to Rules under Rule 19(a) on 2026-03-27, so it is effectively stalled for now.","mechanics":["Sec. 10 bars registration or positions by anyone under 21. If an underage account is discovered, the provider must immediately suspend it, close all positions and pay out the funds. Sec. 15 requires exclusion of self-excluded persons, provider and settlement-source insiders, persons with inside information, and any AG-designated categories.","Sec. 20 prohibits athletic event markets for state users. The definition includes horse racing and prop betting but carves out tournament-champion and full-bracket positions. Political, catastrophic event, death and single-company stock-price markets are also banned. Sec. 25 requires disclosure of settlement sources and bars settlement on proprietary or confidential information.","Sec. 30 requires self-exclusion, optional deposit and spend limits with a 14-day wait to raise them, and time-on-platform tools. Only the 1-800-GAMBLER/ILGAMB hotline may be displayed. Sec. 35 bans push notifications about bonuses or markets where the user holds no open position, \"risk-free\" claims, and ads depicting or targeting under-21s. It also requires the hotline on all ads (visible for the full length of a video) and makes providers responsible for affiliate compliance. Sec. 40 bans gift certificates and credit-card funding.","Sec. 45 prohibits offering any market in which a liquidity provider or market maker, or any affiliate, parent, subsidiary, JV or agent of one, knowingly engages in gaming activities in the ordinary course of business anywhere. It also bars markets involving a contract or revenue share with such an entity.","Sec. 60 penalties: up to $10,000 per violation, or $50,000 for a persistent course of conduct. Violations of Secs. 15 or 45 carry the greater of 2x market profits or $50,000. The AG may obtain an injunction, and the court must then order the provider to cease Illinois operations, with $1,000,000 per day for operating in violation of that order. Insider trading is penalized by cross-reference to Sec. 20-70 of the Digital Assets and Consumer Protection Act.","The AG has rulemaking authority (Sec. 65). Sec. 50 voids terms of service that waive rights under the Act. No effective date or licensing process is specified, although Sec. 35 refers to a \"licensed\" platform."],"issue_tags":["prediction","advertising","integrity","prop"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"The closest listed analogue is CT HB05038, which also bars prediction market use by, and advertising to, under-21s. HB5059 goes much further by banning entire market categories, including all athletic event markets, and by restricting gaming-industry market makers. CA AB1840 approaches prediction markets only through Political Reform Act ethics rules for officials, whereas Illinois would bar political markets outright. CT HB05229 and LA SB354 regulate licensed sportsbook advertising and prop/micro-bets rather than prediction markets. Most other listed bills (e.g., GA HB 686, HI HB 1308, AK HB145) authorize and tax sports wagering, so the Illinois approach of an AG-run conduct code with no licensing or tax component stands apart.","watch":"Section 45's ban on any market involving a market maker, revenue-share partner or affiliate that engages in gaming in the ordinary course anywhere would effectively shut licensed sportsbook operators and their corporate families out of Illinois prediction market ventures, backed by penalties of 2x profits or $50,000 and possible cease-operations orders.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":19485,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:46:14.051Z"},"IL-HB5048":{"summary":"HB5048 would legalize and regulate non-networked \"prize and gift kiosks\" (sweepstakes-style devices that promote a bona fide product and award prizes, including cash, with a mail-in free-play option) under the Prizes and Gifts Act. At the same time it makes gambling offenses involving Internet-connected or credit-metering electronic devices a Class 4 felony. It also bars the Gaming Board from penalizing or denying video gaming licensees and applicants over lawful kiosk activity, and voids every municipal restriction on any activity exempted under 720 ILCS 5/28-1(b). The bill was re-referred to Rules under Rule 19(a) on 2026-03-27, so it is effectively stalled.","mechanics":["Prizes and Gifts Act Sec. 10/33: a prize and gift kiosk must promote a bona fide product, offer a No Payment or Purchase option limited to a mail-in code, and use a revenue-based self-contained fill system that stops after a predetermined cycle. It must also carry a \"route boost plus\" monitoring system that records cash in, winnings, entries, malfunctions and remote activation and disabling, but cannot alter play or read patron financial data. It may not connect to the Internet or a network except through a redemption vault.","Sec. 45: operators must register with the Department of Revenue on Form REG-1-A and register each kiosk as a Coin-Operated Amusement Device (Form RCOA-1), paying annual fees set by the Department. Misclassifying a device is treated as filing a false report, with penalties including seizure. Jurisdiction lies with the Department of Revenue and the Liquor Control Commission or its designee, including any public-private task force.","Criminal Code 28-2(a)(iii): the definition of \"gambling device\" is expanded to any vending or electronic machine that meters credits or connects to the Internet or a network to play or retrieve data, excluding redemption vaults and prize and gift kiosks. New 28-1(c)(2) makes any gambling offense involving such a device a Class 4 felony.","New municipal preemption in 28-1: no municipality may restrict or prohibit any activity lawful under 28-1(b), and existing local restrictions become void. The 28-1(b) list includes video gaming, casino gaming, the lottery, and sports wagering under the Sports Wagering Act (b)(15), not just kiosks.","Video Gaming Act 230 ILCS 40/35: lawful game devices that are not Internet-connected cannot be grounds for discipline, delay or denial. Prior denials based on lawful devices, including those the Board labeled \"sweepstakes devices,\" must be rescinded and licenses issued. Applications disclosing such devices must be processed within 90 days.","Effective immediately upon becoming law."],"issue_tags":["sweeps","licensing","legalize"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Most listed bills that touch unlicensed gaming run in the opposite direction. LA HB883 and MD HB1226 tighten enforcement against illegal online gambling, and FL S1580 revises illegal-gambling penalties. HB5048 instead carves out a registered, tax-administered safe harbor for brick-and-mortar sweepstakes kiosks, keeping only an Internet-connection felony as its enforcement hook. None of the listed bills, including the sports wagering authorizations in AK HB145, GA HB 686 or the HI bills, contains a comparable blanket preemption of local regulation for every exempted gambling activity.","watch":"Watch the municipal preemption clause in 28-1, which would void local restrictions on all 28-1(b) activities including sports wagering and video gaming, and the broad Internet-connected \"gambling device\" felony definition in 28-2(a)(iii), whose interplay with licensed mobile and retail wagering equipment should be confirmed if the bill is revived.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":29796,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:46:15.565Z"},"IL-HB5077":{"summary":"HB5077 amends the Illinois Sports Wagering Act to authorize interstate pool sports wagering and to remove the cap of three standalone online master sports wagering licenses, letting the Gaming Board issue such licenses without the $20,000,000 competitive-selection model. It also creates a new licensing, conduct and tax regime for fantasy contest operators under the Board, with a 15% privilege tax on adjusted gross fantasy contest receipts beginning July 1, 2026. Single-participant contests against the operator are excluded from the definition of a lawful fantasy contest. The bill was re-referred to Rules Committee under Rule 19(a) on 2026-03-27 and is effectively stalled.","mechanics":["Sec. 25-25(i) allows interstate pool wagering if the pool is expressly permitted in every participating jurisdiction. Illinois taxable receipts equal in-state wagers minus a pro rata share of winnings, calculated to the nearest .01%.","Sec. 25-45 lets the Board issue online master sports wagering licenses, replacing the three-license, $20M competitive process. Fees are a $250,000 nonrefundable application fee and a $15,000,000 initial license fee. Licenses run 4 years with a $1,000,000 renewal fee, and the competitive-bid subsections (e)-(l) are blanked.","Fantasy contest operators must be licensed (Sec. 25-120.3), and unlicensed offering is a Class 4 felony. Small operators (7,500 or fewer patrons in the prior 365 days) pay $500 at application and $300 on renewal. Large operators pay $7,500 and $5,000. Licenses run 2 years. Existing operators may keep operating if they apply within 90 days after implementing rules take effect, and they receive temporary permits.","The 'fantasy contest' definition (Sec. 25-120.1) excludes pool sports wagering, single-participant contests played against the operator, and free contests. Prior single-player contests do not bar licensure only if they stopped by the effective date (Sec. 25-120.3(f)).","Conduct and integrity rules (Secs. 25-120.4 to 25-120.6): participants must be 21 or older and physically in-state, verified by geolocation. Insiders and operator employees may not play. Operators must follow AML, identity verification and fraud/collusion detection requirements, report integrity issues to the Board, and obtain annual CPA audits and annual independent lab certification of their platform.","Tax and administration: a 15% privilege tax on adjusted gross fantasy contest receipts from July 1, 2026 (Sec. 25-120.7), deposited to the State Gaming Fund. Fantasy contests are declared not gambling and exempted in Criminal Code 28-1(b)(16). Self-exclusion is extended to fantasy accounts. Emergency rulemaking authority sunsets after one year. The bill is effective immediately."],"issue_tags":["licensing","tax","integrity","legalize"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"The closest analogue is KY HB 904, which also puts fantasy contest operators under the state gaming regulator with geolocation and operator-procedure requirements. KY HB 904's summary does not show HB5077's 15% receipts tax or its exclusion of operator-banked single-participant contests. FL H0189 also addresses fantasy sports contests, but within a broader gambling and enforcement overhaul rather than a licensing-and-tax regime layered onto an existing sports wagering act. Unlike LA SB354, which removes prop betting from permitted wagers, HB5077 leaves the sports wagering menu intact and instead expands market structure through interstate pools and an uncapped online license.","watch":"Watch three things: the exclusion of single-participant contests combined with the Class 4 felony for unlicensed operation, which would push pick'em-style products out of Illinois or into the Sports Wagering Act; the uncapped $15M standalone online license, which could reshape the competitive landscape; and the drafting error in Sec. 25-120.2(c)(2), which routes tax collected under Sec. 25-120.6 (the audit section) when the tax actually sits in Sec. 25-120.7.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":51680,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:46:19.463Z"},"IL-HB5142":{"summary":"HB5142 amends the definition of \"sports wagering\" in Section 25-10 of the Illinois Sports Wagering Act (230 ILCS 45/25-10) to expressly include participation in any prediction market on sports events, portions of sports events, or athlete performance statistics, covering the offering, listing, solicitation, purchase, sale, trading, exchange, or settlement of event contracts and similar instruments. It provides that such activity is sports wagering regardless of peer-to-peer structure, whether the operator is a counterparty, or how the operator labels the product. The practical effect is to bring sports event contracts within the Act's licensing and enforcement regime, though the bill adds no new penalties or licensing provisions of its own.","mechanics":["Single operative change: adds a new sentence to the \"sports wagering\" definition in 230 ILCS 45/25-10. All other definitions are restated unchanged.","Covered instruments include any \"event contract, sports-event contract, or other contract, agreement, or transaction,\" whether styled as a derivative, option, binary contract, or similar instrument, where a person risks something of value on the occurrence, nonoccurrence, or outcome of a sports event, any portion of one, or athlete performance statistics.","Covered conduct spans the full lifecycle: offering, listing, solicitation, purchase, sale, trading, exchange, and settlement.","Anti-recharacterization clause: the activity is sports wagering even if it is peer-to-peer, participants take positions against each other rather than the operator, the operator is not a counterparty, or the operator calls it an investing opportunity, exchange, marketplace, or prediction market.","No effective date, tax rate, penalty, or licensing provision is added; enforcement would flow from the existing Act and the Illinois Gaming Board's authority.","Status: introduced by Rep. Didech and re-referred to the Rules Committee under Rule 19(a) on 2026-03-27, which means it is effectively stalled for now."],"issue_tags":["prediction","licensing","legalize"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike CT HB05038 (age-21 and advertising limits for prediction market platforms) and CA AB1840 (Political Reform Act restrictions on officials and prediction market contracts), HB5142 does not regulate prediction markets as a separate product. Instead, it folds sports event contracts directly into the statutory definition of sports wagering, which subjects them to the state's existing licensing regime. LA SB354 also works through the definition of permitted sports wagers, but in the opposite direction: it removes proposition bets and bans micro-bets. HB5142's anti-recharacterization language aimed at exchange and peer-to-peer structures is unusual among the listed bills and appears designed to set up a state-law conflict with CFTC-regulated designated contract markets.","watch":"Watch whether this definitional expansion revives or is folded into a larger gaming package, because it would set up a direct federal preemption fight with CFTC-registered prediction markets offering sports contracts in Illinois and could reshape the competitive landscape for licensed sportsbooks versus exchange-based competitors.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":8226,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:46:24.708Z"},"IL-HB5139":{"summary":"HB5139 amends the Illinois Video Gaming Act and the Criminal Code of 2012 to crack down on unlicensed sweepstakes, 'skill' and similar chance-based machines while reshuffling video gaming tax revenue. It expands the criminal definition of 'gambling device' to cover vending and electronic machines offering chance-based sweepstakes entries tied to a purchase or coupon, and makes offenses involving such devices a Class 4 felony. It also shifts more of the 35% net terminal income tax to local governments starting January 1, 2027, and repeals Video Gaming Act Section 27 on political-subdivision prohibitions. The bill was re-referred to Rules under Rule 19(a) on March 27, 2026, so it is effectively stalled for now.","mechanics":["Criminal Code 28-2(a)(iii): 'gambling device' now includes any vending or other electronic machine, including one that awards credits and has a credit-removal circuit, meter or switch, that offers entry into a contest, sweepstakes or other chance-based selection process. This applies where the entry is incidental to (A) buying an item or service or (B) buying or receiving a redeemable coupon, voucher or credit. New 28-2(d) states an anti-subterfuge intent, and the vending machine exclusion is made subject to this new language.","Criminal Code 28-1(c)(2): any gambling offense involving a 28-2(a)(iii) device is a Class 4 felony, notwithstanding the general Class A misdemeanor rule. The no-purchase-necessary exemption in 28-1(b)(13) is narrowed so it no longer applies where the game runs through a gambling device or by means of the Internet.","Video Gaming Act Sec. 35: adds a safe harbor so an applicant or licensee is not in violation of the Act or 11 Ill. Adm. Code 1800.420, and faces no discipline, delay of Board consideration or license denial, for operating a gaming device that complies with, and is not gambling under, Criminal Code 28-1(b) or 28-2(a)(iii). It deletes the prior 'nothing shall be deemed to prohibit' sentence.","Video Gaming Act Sec. 60(b-5): the 35% tax on net terminal income stays. From January 1, 2027, the split moves from 83.7% Capital Projects Fund / 14.3% Local Government Video Gaming Distributive Fund / 2% State Gaming Fund to 80.84% / 17.16% / 2%.","Repeals Video Gaming Act Section 27, the provision on prohibition of video gaming by political subdivisions."],"issue_tags":["sweeps","tax","licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Most listed bills are sports-wagering authorization or tax bills (AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, HI HB 1308/SB1569, KS SB303), and none overlaps directly with this one. The closest analogues are MD HB295, which bans operating or promoting 'interactive games' and requires licensees to report ties to illegal-market actors, and FL H0189 and FL S1580, which revise gaming machine rules and illegal-gambling penalties. Unlike those, HB5139 attacks unregulated sweepstakes machines through the criminal definition of 'gambling device' and a felony classification, rather than through a standalone prohibition. It also pairs that with a video gaming tax redistribution.","watch":"Watch the narrowing of the 28-1(b)(13) no-purchase exemption for games conducted 'by means of the Internet', which could reach online sweepstakes casinos and potentially free-to-play contests or promotions run outside the Sports Wagering Act exemption in 28-1(b)(15). Also watch the felony exposure for device-based sweepstakes.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":24591,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:46:26.210Z"},"IL-HB5143":{"summary":"HB5143 amends Section 25-90 of the Illinois Sports Wagering Act (230 ILCS 45/25-90) to sunset the per-wager tax on online and mobile sports wagers in subsection (d-7), so that it runs from July 1, 2025 and ends July 1, 2026 instead of continuing indefinitely. The graduated privilege tax on adjusted gross sports wagering receipts, the 2% Cook County surcharge and the fund-distribution provisions are left unchanged. The bill was re-referred to the House Rules Committee under Rule 19(a) on March 27, 2026, so it is stalled for now.","mechanics":["Subsection (d-7) is amended to strike 'each month thereafter' and add 'ending July 1, 2026'. This terminates the per-wager tax of $0.25 per wager on the first 20,000,000 annual combined Tier 1 and Tier 2 wagers and $0.50 per wager above that threshold.","The per-wager tax applies only to wagers placed over the Internet or through a mobile application, and its revenue goes to the General Revenue Fund. The repeal therefore removes that GRF stream.","The bill leaves in place the graduated privilege tax under (d) and (d-5) on annual adjusted gross sports wagering receipts: 20% up to $30M, 25% from $30M to $50M, 30% from $50M to $100M, 35% from $100M to $200M, and 40% above $200M.","The 2% additional tax under (a-5) on wagers placed in a home rule county of over 3,000,000 inhabitants (Cook County), earmarked for criminal justice, is retained. So is the 58/42 split of excess Sports Wagering Fund moneys between the General Revenue Fund and the Capital Projects Fund.","The Section 99 effective date is immediate upon becoming law. There are no new enforcement, licensing or audit changes, and the (e) audit and quarterly compliance-report requirements are unchanged."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike most listed bills, which create or raise sports-wagering taxes, HB5143 is a tax rollback. AK HB145 and SB194 and HI HB 1308 and HB 2570 impose new revenue or excise taxes as part of legalization, and KS SB303 would add a handle-based excise tax of 2% of the amount wagered. Illinois's per-wager levy is itself a rare transaction-count tax, and this bill would make it a one-year experiment rather than a permanent fixture. None of the other listed bills proposes repealing an existing operator tax.","watch":"Operators should track whether the per-wager sunset language is revived from Rules Committee or folded into the FY2027 budget or revenue omnibus, since without it the $0.25/$0.50 per-wager tax under (d-7) continues past July 1, 2026 alongside the 20%–40% graduated rate.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":8527,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:46:27.526Z"},"IL-HB5318":{"summary":"HB5318 amends the Illinois Income Tax Act to end the '80/20' exclusion, under which unitary business group members with 80% or more of their business activity outside the United States are left out of the combined group. The exclusion would apply only to taxable years ending before January 1, 2027, pulling such affiliates into Illinois combined reports after that date. The bill also defines 'United States' for 'foreign person' purposes and adds joint and several liability for members of a combined reporting group; it is a general corporate income tax measure and does not mention wagering.","mechanics":["Sec. 1501 (per synopsis): the 80/20 exclusion of members with 80% or more foreign business activity from the unitary business group applies only to taxable years ending before January 1, 2027.","Sec. 203 addition modifications (D-17) for interest and (D-18) for intangible expenses paid to 80/20 foreign affiliates are limited to taxable years ending on or after December 31, 2004 and before January 1, 2027; the matching subtractions (DD) and (EE) are cut off at the same date. Add-backs for persons excluded under Sec. 1501(a)(27) continue.","For 'foreign person' purposes, 'United States' means the 50 states, D.C., U.S. territories and possessions, and any area where the U.S. asserts jurisdiction or exclusive rights over natural-resource exploration or exploitation (per synopsis).","New Sec. 1009 imposes joint and several liability on members of a combined reporting group (per synopsis; operative text not in the excerpt provided). Sec. 304 apportionment is also amended.","Effective immediately; status is Introduced, with Rep. La Shawn K. Ford added as co-sponsor on 2026-05-25."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills deals with corporate combined reporting or the unitary group rules. The gaming tax bills, such as AK HB145/SB194 (mobile wagering revenue tax), KS SB303 (2% excise on the amount wagered) and NY A3009/S3009 Part GG (gaming revenue tax rates), impose taxes specific to wagering. HB5318 instead changes the general corporate income tax base, which reaches gaming companies only as multinational corporate taxpayers. The closest analogues are general tax vehicles such as MN SF2374/SF5052, though the summaries given for those bills show no combined-reporting provisions.","watch":"Operators with foreign parents or affiliates that have substantial non-U.S. activity should model the effect of those affiliates entering the Illinois combined group from tax years ending in 2027, along with the new joint and several liability of group members under Sec. 1009.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":288502,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:46:36.650Z"},"IL-HB5452":{"summary":"HB5452 renames the Illinois Liquor Control Act of 1934 as the Liquor and Hemp Products Control Act and creates a Hemp Products Commission as a division of the Illinois Liquor Control Commission, along with local hemp products control commissions, to license and regulate hemp products. It sets a 21+ age limit, creates a four-tier licensing system, and covers taxation, testing, labeling, advertising and social equity for hemp products. It does not, in the text provided, address sports wagering or gaming. It was re-referred to the Rules Committee under Rule 19(a) on 2026-03-27, which in Illinois usually means it is stalled for the session.","mechanics":["Renames the Liquor Control Act of 1934 to the Liquor and Hemp Products Control Act and makes conforming changes across the statutes. The excerpt shows changes to Election Code Secs. 3-1.3 and 28-6, 20 ILCS 2505/2505-40, Criminal Identification Act Sec. 3.1, and Retailers' Occupation Tax Act Sec. 3.","Establishes the Hemp Products Commission within the Illinois Liquor Control Commission, with the State Commission's liquor powers applied to hemp products, and creates local hemp products control commissions.","Bars giving, selling, transferring or delivering any hemp product to anyone under 21.","Creates four license types: grower/cultivator, processor/manufacturer, distributor/wholesaler, and adult-use hemp products retailer. Fees are referenced in the synopsis but no figures appear in the excerpt.","Imposes a tax on hemp cannabinoid products (no rate appears in the excerpt) and sets labeling, testing, advertising, recordkeeping and social equity rules.","Effective January 1, 2027."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills is comparable, because they all deal with sports wagering, iGaming, prediction markets or gaming tax and enforcement. Examples include AK HB145, GA HB 686, MD HB1343 and KS SB303, while HB5452 is a hemp and cannabinoid regulatory bill. The only loose parallel is the 21+ access limit, which echoes CT HB05038's under-21 restriction for prediction markets but applies to a different product. What is unusual about HB5452 is its structure: it places a new product category under the existing liquor regulator rather than creating a stand-alone agency.","watch":"The excerpt does not show whether the statute-wide renaming reaches gaming laws that cross-reference the Liquor Control Act, such as the Video Gaming Act's liquor-licensed establishment provisions, so operators should check the full conforming-changes index; otherwise the bill is stalled in Rules and has no direct sports-betting impact.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":520421,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:46:45.052Z"},"IL-HB5368":{"summary":"HB5368 amends the Illinois Substance Use Disorder Act (20 ILCS 301) to fold gambling disorder more fully into the Department of Human Services' prevention, treatment, and education mandate alongside substance use disorders. It expands the Section 5-20 gambling disorder program, adding a suicide-reduction element, hotline-and-website language, and permissive authority for prevention literature, curricula, technical support, and grants to local prevention and treatment projects. It creates no new tax, fee, or licensing obligation for gaming operators, and as of 2026-03-27 it has been re-referred to Rules under Rule 19(a), so it is effectively stalled.","mechanics":["Sec. 1-10 defines \"gambling\" as betting or wagering on uncertain outcomes, including but not limited to activity regulated by the Illinois Gaming Board. It rewrites \"gambling disorder\" with reference to DSM-5, and defines \"video gaming\" as playing video games, which means consumer video games, not video gaming terminals.","Sec. 5-20(a)-(b): subject to appropriation, the DHS program covers a toll-free hotline and website for crisis counseling and referral, public awareness including stigma, in-service training, and prevalence studies. A new item (a)(5) directs screening, crisis intervention, treatment and similar means to reduce suicide attempts related to gambling disorder.","Sec. 5-20(c) keeps the existing DHS-determined help statement that licensed gambling establishments must post and that master sports wagering licensees must include on their portal, website, or app, with signs supplied under Section 25.95 of the Sports Wagering Act and parallel statutes. The bill adds no new operator duty here.","New Sec. 5-20(d)-(e) gives DHS permissive authority to publish prevention literature, develop curricula, and advise officials, including on \"the relationship between gaming and gambling disorders.\" It may also award grants to local health departments, correctional institutions, hospitals, universities, and community and faith-based organizations for prevention and treatment projects, and may seek private and federal funds.","Sec. 5-10: DHS must collaborate with the State Board of Education on substance use and gambling disorder instructional resources (new (a)(4)(H-5)). DHS may fund parent programs covering healthy video gaming, financial planning, and how to talk about gambling. The Advisory Council's remit under Sec. 10-10(g) includes preventing gambling and gaming by minors.","Sec. 15-10 keeps gambling disorder treatment as an adjunct to any treatment license level of care and to recovery home intervention licenses. The bill is effective immediately."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"Unlike MD HB518, which directs the Lottery and Gaming Control Commission to adopt problem-gambling regulations including a voluntary exclusion list, HB5368 places no new obligations on operators or the Illinois Gaming Board. Its changes run entirely through DHS public-health programming. It also contrasts with CT HB05229, which imposes direct operator conduct rules on campus advertising, withdrawals, customer service lines, and AI-targeted betting. It differs from HI HB1308 and HB 2570, which pair a problem gambling fund with a licensing and tax regime, because HB5368 identifies no dedicated revenue source and makes everything subject to appropriation.","watch":"Watch whether a revived version adds an operator-funded assessment or earmark to pay for the expanded Section 5-20 hotline, suicide-prevention, and grant programs, or changes the Section 5-20(c) help-statement content that master sports wagering licensees must display on their apps and websites.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":94967,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:46:47.179Z"},"IL-HB5443":{"summary":"HB5443 amends the Illinois Sports Wagering Act to bring paid fantasy contests under Illinois Gaming Board licensing, taxation and responsible-gaming rules. It declares licensed fantasy contests are not gambling and makes unlicensed operation a Class 4 felony. The definition excludes single-participant contests played against the operator, which pushes out house-banked pick'em products. It also redefines \"sports wagering\" to exclude fantasy contests, adds rules for interstate pool sports wagering, and appears to strike parts of the master sports wagering license competitive-selection provisions in Sec. 25-45.","mechanics":["Licensing (Sec. 25-120.3): a Board fantasy contest operator license is required. Fees are a $50,000 nonrefundable application fee, a $500,000 license fee due within 30 days of initial issuance, a 4-year term, and a $50,000 renewal fee. Knowingly offering contests without a license is a Class 4 felony, and no one other than an owner or key person may take a revenue share without a license.","Definition (Sec. 25-120.1): a fantasy contest is an online contest of skill between 2 or more participants with an entry fee and prizes announced in advance. It expressly excludes pool sports wagering, single-participant contests against the operator, contests without an entry fee, and any outcome based entirely on a single team's result or solely on a single athlete's single performance in a single event.","Incumbent operators and pick'em history (Sec. 25-120.3(b), (f)): operators already active may continue until 90 days after implementing rules take effect, and get a temporary operating permit if they apply and pay within that window. Prior single-player contests against the operator are not grounds for denial only if they stopped by the effective date.","Tax (Sec. 25-120.7): beginning July 1, 2026, a graduated privilege tax applies to adjusted gross fantasy contest receipts. Rates are 20% up to $3M, 25% from $3M to $5M, 30% from $5M to $10M, 35% from $10M to $20M, and 40% above $20M, with proceeds deposited in the State Gaming Fund.","Conduct and duties (Secs. 25-120.4 to 25-120.6): participants must be 21 or older and physically in Illinois, verified by geolocation. Licensees must apply anti-money-laundering standards, identity verification, and underage and collusion controls, and must report integrity breaches and match fixing to the Board. They also face an annual CPA audit and annual independent lab certification of their platform. Covered team and league insiders and licensee employees are barred from participating.","Other changes: fantasy contests are added to the self-exclusion program (Secs. 25-100, 25-120.8). Interstate pools must be legal in every jurisdiction where offered, with Illinois receipts calculated pro rata (Sec. 25-25(i)). Criminal Code Sec. 28-1(b)(16) exempts licensed fantasy contests from gambling, and a new Sec. 28-8(d) addresses losses on pre-effective-date contests against an operator, though the supplied text is truncated. The bill authorizes one-year emergency rulemaking and is effective immediately."],"issue_tags":["licensing","tax","integrity","legalize","prop"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"KY HB 904 is the closest analogue: like HB5443, it gives a gaming regulator (the Kentucky Horse Racing and Gaming Corporation) authority over fantasy contest operators and requires geolocation. HB5443 adds a steep 20-40% graduated receipts tax, felony penalties, and an explicit exclusion of player-versus-house contests. FL H0189 also addresses fantasy sports contests within a broad gambling overhaul. HB5443's exclusion of single-athlete, single-event outcomes and house-banked contests parallels in effect LA SB354's removal of proposition bets from permitted wagers, but works through the DFS definition rather than the sportsbook menu. Its tax is on net receipts, unlike KS SB303's 2% excise on amounts wagered.","watch":"Watch whether the definition in Sec. 25-120.1 and the Sec. 28-8(d) loss-recovery language survive as drafted, since together they would shut out house-banked pick'em products and could expose operators to civil claims to recover pre-enactment losses, while the 40% top tax tier would hit even sportsbook-affiliated DFS products.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":50956,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:46:50.364Z"},"IL-HB571":{"summary":"HB0571 is a shell bill that amends only Section 25-1 (230 ILCS 45/25-1), the short-title section of the Illinois Sports Wagering Act, with what the synopsis calls a technical change. It has no substantive effect on sports wagering regulation, taxation or licensing as introduced. Because it is sponsored by Speaker Emanuel \"Chris\" Welch and opens the Sports Wagering Act, it can serve as a vehicle for a substantive amendment later in the 104th General Assembly.","mechanics":["Single operative section (Section 5) amends 230 ILCS 45/25-1, the short title of the Sports Wagering Act (originally P.A. 101-31, eff. 6-28-19).","The change is described only as technical; the printed text contains no rates, thresholds, definitions, licensing terms or enforcement provisions.","Sponsor: Rep. Emanuel \"Chris\" Welch, the House Speaker, which is typical of leadership shell bills held for end-of-session deals.","Status: re-referred to the Rules Committee under Rule 19(a) on 2025-05-31, so it is dormant but can be revived and amended while the 2025-2026 General Assembly is in session."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike the full mobile sports wagering frameworks in AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686 and HI HB 1308, which set licensing, tax and regulatory structures, IL HB0571 contains no substantive policy. It also lacks the targeted consumer-protection provisions in CT HB05229 (campus advertising limits, withdrawal rules, AI targeting) and the child-support withholding mandate in IN HB1092. What is unusual is that it is a pure placeholder whose significance lies in its sponsor and its ability to carry later amendments into the Sports Wagering Act.","watch":"Track any floor or committee amendment to HB0571, especially near budget or session deadlines, since a Speaker-sponsored shell opening the Sports Wagering Act could be used to insert changes to the privilege tax, per-wager fees or licensing terms with little advance notice.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":764,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:46:51.841Z"},"IL-SB2510":{"summary":"SB2510 is Illinois's FY2026 capital appropriations bill, and the text provided is only Governor Pritzker's June 16, 2025 reduction-veto message, not the bill itself. Under Article IV, Section 9(d) of the Illinois Constitution, the Governor cut two line items by a combined $161,156,000 and approved all other appropriations. He said the cut amounts were duplicative new Build Illinois Bond Fund appropriations that pushed spending past the bond authorization in HB3374. The General Assembly did not override, and the reductions stood on October 29, 2025.","mechanics":["Article 129, Section 310 (p. 910, line 1) is reduced from $147,314,964 to $52,852,964, a cut of $94,462,000.","Article 181, Section 5 (p. 3305, line 13) is reduced from $103,608,422 to $36,914,422, a cut of $66,694,000.","The stated rationale is that new Article 129 appropriations were meant to replace portions of existing appropriations and reappropriations in Articles 129 and 181. Those existing amounts were never reduced, so the bill would have exceeded the Build Illinois bond authorization in HB3374.","All other appropriations in SB2510 were approved as passed.","The last recorded action, on 2025-10-29, is that the item/reduction veto stands."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. On its own, this is a routine reduction veto correcting a capital-budget drafting error. The veto message contains no reference to sports wagering, gaming taxes, licensing or gaming-funded capital funds. Whether any underlying SB2510 line items draw on gaming-derived revenue cannot be determined from this text.","watch":"Nothing in the veto message touches gaming, but operators may want to check whether any underlying SB2510 capital appropriations are funded from gaming or sports-wagering tax receipts, since pressure on capital funding can feed future proposals to raise Illinois wagering taxes.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Veto Message","text_chars":1412,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:46:59.893Z"},"IL-SB2394":{"summary":"SB2394, the First 2025 General Revisory Act (Public Act 104-0417), is Illinois' routine technical clean-up statute. Section 1(b) says it makes no substantive change; it reconciles conflicting versions of Sections amended by more than one Public Act, renumbers duplicates, fixes cross-references and deletes obsolete text. The excerpt provided does not reach any gaming or wagering statute.","mechanics":["Section 1(b) states the Act is not intended to make any substantive change in the law; it only reconciles multiple amendments and makes technical corrections.","Section 1(d): Public Acts 103-584 through 103-1059 were considered in preparing the combining revisories; source notes at the end of each Section are not part of the text.","Sections 10 and 12 tidy the Regulatory Sunset Act: they correct the professional-licensing Acts listed for repeal in 2029 and 2030 (Secs. 4.39 and 4.40) and repeal obsolete Sec. 4.35.","Section 15 renumbers three duplicate Illinois Administrative Procedure Act emergency-rulemaking sections, all numbered 5-45.55, as 5-45.58, 5-45.59 and 5-45.60, and corrects sunset dates such as 5-45.52.","Section 20 re-letters duplicate Freedom of Information Act exemption paragraphs in 5 ILCS 140/7.5, now (ooo) through (sss), without changing their content.","Section 25 makes grammatical and cross-reference fixes to the Illinois Public Labor Relations Act (Secs. 5 and 15), for example replacing 'this amendatory Act' with the specific Public Act number."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No other states' bills are listed for comparison. What is unusual is that this is a non-substantive omnibus revisory act rather than gaming legislation. Its only potential relevance to operators is codification: if the full text combines versions of the Sports Wagering Act, Illinois Gambling Act or Video Gaming Act amended by multiple 103rd General Assembly Public Acts, those sections would be restated, not changed.","watch":"Check the full enrolled text, which is truncated here, for any combining revisories of 230 ILCS chapters (Sports Wagering Act, Illinois Gambling Act, Video Gaming Act); those would set the authoritative codified wording of provisions such as the sports wagering tax and licensing sections, even though Section 1(b) disclaims any substantive change.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":4587515,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:47:01.546Z"},"IL-SB2667":{"summary":"SB2667 amends the Illinois Sports Wagering Act to fold \"awards shows\" into the definition of \"sports event,\" so licensed operators could take wagers on the Emmys, Grammys, Oscars, Tonys, and any other live competition or talent contest. It directs the Illinois Gaming Board to adopt rules permitting awards show wagering within 6 months of the effective date and authorizes emergency rulemaking to do so.","mechanics":["230 ILCS 45/25-10: new definition of \"awards show\" covers the Emmy, Grammy, Academy, and Tony Awards Shows \"or any other live competition or talent contest.\" \"Sports event\" is amended to include any awards show \"unless the context requires otherwise.\"","New 230 ILCS 45/25-66(a): the Board shall adopt rules permitting awards show wagering pursuant to Section 25-20 within 6 months after the effective date.","Section 25-66(b) and new 5 ILCS 100/5-45.65: the Board may use emergency rulemaking under IAPA Section 5-45. The emergency rulemaking section self-repeals one year after the effective date.","Existing definitions are unchanged, including covered persons, prohibited conduct, official league data, and tier 1/tier 2 wagers. They remain athlete- and sports-governing-body oriented and are not adapted to awards shows.","Effective immediately upon becoming law. Status: Senate Committee Amendment No. 1 was re-referred to Assignments on 2026-05-22, so the bill appears stalled."],"issue_tags":["legalize","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Most of the listed bills create or restructure a sports wagering market rather than expand the menu of wagerable events. These include AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, HI HB 1308/HB 2570/SB1569, and MA's internet gaming bills. The closest analog is MD HB1240, which also expands the \"sporting event\" definition, but to historical horse races. IL SB2667 is unusual in reaching non-athletic entertainment outcomes whose results are known in advance to a small group of insiders such as accounting firms and academy voters. The \"any other live competition or talent contest\" catch-all could sweep in reality TV.","watch":"Watch whether amendments or Board rules narrow the open-ended \"any other live competition or talent contest\" language and address integrity risks specific to predetermined results, since the existing covered-person and official-league-data provisions do not fit awards-show insiders or the organizations that run these shows.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":8276,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:47:02.091Z"},"IL-SB3019":{"summary":"The enrolled portion of SB3019 creates two new Illinois taxes effective January 1, 2027. Article 1, the Targeted Advertising Services Tax Act, imposes a 10% gross receipts tax on providers of programmatic targeted advertising delivered to Illinois user-consumers. Article 3, the Digital Asset Tax Act, imposes a 0.2% tax on customers receiving digital asset exchange, transfer or storage services, collected by digital asset brokers. The supplied text is truncated partway through Article 3, and no wagering or gaming provisions appear in the portion provided.","mechanics":["Sec. 1-15: 10% tax on gross receipts of targeted advertising services provided in Illinois, beginning January 1, 2027. The legal incidence falls on the provider, not the advertiser. News media entity platforms are excluded from the definition of targeted advertising services.","Sec. 1-10 and 1-20(c): only providers with more than $1,000,000 in annual Illinois targeted-ad gross receipts are covered, tested quarterly. Controlled groups under IRC 1563(a) are aggregated as a single entity.","Sec. 1-20: services are sourced to the user-consumer's location. There is a rebuttable presumption of Illinois location based on an Illinois home address, mailing address, IP address or place of primary use, and the provider bears the burden of proof.","Sec. 1-25, 1-35 and 1-50: providers must register by January 1, 2027 and file monthly returns by the 20th, with a 1.75% vendor discount capped at $1,000 per return. Failing to file, failing to keep records or filing a fraudulent return is a Class 3 felony.","Sec. 1-70, 1-900 and 1-905: home rule counties and municipalities are preempted from taxing targeted advertising services.","Sec. 3-20 and 3-15: 0.2% tax on the value of digital assets exchanged, transferred or stored for Illinois customers, beginning January 1, 2027. Remote brokers are covered once Illinois gross receipts reach $100,000 or more."],"issue_tags":["tax","advertising"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"Unlike KS SB303, which imposes a 2% excise on the amount of each sports wager, SB3019 does not tax wagering at all. It taxes the digital ad platforms that sportsbooks buy from. It also differs from CT HB05229 and CT HB05038, which regulate the content and audience of gaming and prediction-market advertising, because SB3019 is a pure revenue measure on programmatic ad sellers with no conduct rules. The Illinois approach is closer to Maryland's digital ad tax model than to any of the listed gaming bills, using a 10% flat rate and a low $1M threshold.","watch":"Watch for platforms such as Meta, Google and programmatic DSPs passing the 10% tax through to sportsbook advertising contracts starting January 1, 2027. Also check the untruncated enrolled text for any later Article touching sports wagering taxes, since none appears in the excerpt provided.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":2554556,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:47:10.406Z"},"IL-SB555":{"summary":"SB0555 is a shell bill that amends Section 25-1 (the short title section) of the Illinois Sports Wagering Act, 230 ILCS 45/25-1, making only what the synopsis calls a technical change. It has no substantive effect on sports wagering regulation, taxation or licensing as introduced. It was filed by Senate President Don Harmon on 1/24/2025 and was re-referred to Assignments under Rule 3-9(a) on 2025-04-11.","mechanics":["Single operative section (Section 5) amends 230 ILCS 45/25-1, the short title of the Sports Wagering Act (Article 25 of P.A. 101-31).","The change is technical wording in the short-title sentence. The printed text reads 'the the Sports Wagering Act', and the edit appears to concern that article. It makes no change to rates, licenses, definitions or enforcement.","No effective date, tax, fee or regulatory provision is included.","Status: introduced, then re-referred to Senate Assignments under Rule 3-9(a) after missing committee deadlines, which leaves it dormant but revivable as a vehicle."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike substantive framework bills such as AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686 and HI HB 1308/SB1569, SB0555 contains no provisions on authorization, licensing, taxation or consumer protection. It also differs from targeted measures like CT HB05229 (advertising and AI restrictions) or IN HB1092 (child-support withholding from wagering accounts). What is unusual is that it is purely a placeholder 'shell' bill: it is held by the Senate President and is designed to be amended later with substantive Sports Wagering Act changes.","watch":"Because this is a leadership-sponsored shell bill on the Sports Wagering Act, operators' counsel should monitor it for a late-session gut-and-replace amendment that could insert tax, fee, licensing or other substantive changes with little notice.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":772,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:47:16.611Z"},"IL-SB3731":{"summary":"SB3731, enacted as Public Act 104-0852, is Illinois's First 2026 General Revisory Act. It reconciles conflicting versions of sections amended by multiple Public Acts, renumbers duplicate section numbers, fixes cross-references, and deletes obsolete text. Section 1(b) states that it is not intended to make any substantive change in the law. The only gaming-related text in the portion provided is the existing Open Meetings Act exception for Illinois Gaming Board deliberations, carried forward unchanged.","mechanics":["Section 1(d): the revisory considered Public Acts 103-1059 through 104-459, and many of the combined sections contain no striking or underscoring because no new changes are made.","Section 5 renumbers duplicate Statute on Statutes sections (for example, the juvenile prostitution prior-prosecutions provision moves from 1.45 to 1.48), and Section 15 renumbers several Administrative Procedure Act emergency-rulemaking sections numbered 5-45.65.","Sections 10 and 11 combine the Regulatory Sunset Act list of Acts repealed January 1, 2031 and repeal Section 4.36.","Section 20 sets out the Open Meetings Act Section 2 text before and after P.A. 104-457 and 104-458 (effective 6-1-26).","In both versions, exception (c)(36) is retained as is: the Illinois Gaming Board may close deliberations that discuss privileged, proprietary, confidential or trade-secret information, or information exempt from disclosure under federal or State law.","Section 25 reconciles the Freedom of Information Act Section 7 exemptions, including the 7(1)(g) trade-secret and commercial-information exemption, before and after P.A. 104-300; the text provided is truncated at this point."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. What is unusual is that this is a purely technical omnibus revisory measure that disclaims substantive effect. Its gaming relevance is limited to restating existing confidentiality protections, such as the Gaming Board closed-deliberation exception and the FOIA trade-secret exemption, rather than changing tax, licensing or wagering rules.","watch":"Because the text provided is truncated, review the full enrolled act for any combined or renumbered sections of the Sports Wagering Act, Illinois Gambling Act or Video Gaming Act, and confirm that the reconciled versions accurately reflect the intervening 2025 Public Acts, including any sports-wagering tax changes, with no inadvertent drafting changes.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":4581072,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:47:16.614Z"},"IN-HB1052":{"summary":"HEA 1052 (P.L. 153-2026) is an omnibus administrative bill amending Indiana's horse racing, charity gaming, casino, alcohol and tobacco statutes, mostly effective July 1, 2026. For gaming clients, the key provisions are a new IC 4-33-10-7, which lets the Indiana Gaming Commission levy a $100,000 civil penalty on anyone who knowingly runs an online dual-currency sweepstakes game in Indiana, and amended voluntary exclusion program language in IC 4-33-4-3 that expressly covers sports wagering certificate holders under IC 4-38. It also overhauls racing commission discipline, routing appeals of steward and judge sanctions to the Office of Administrative Law Proceedings.","mechanics":["Sweepstakes (Sec. 15, new IC 4-33-10-7): 'sweepstakes game' means an Internet game, contest or promotion that meets four tests. It is accessible by phone or computer, uses a dual- or multi-currency system exchangeable for cash prizes or cash equivalents (or a chance to win them), and simulates lottery or casino-style gaming, including slots, video poker, table games, bingo or sports wagering. State lottery products and peer-to-peer skill-based poker are excluded.","Sweepstakes penalty: the Gaming Commission may levy a $100,000 civil penalty against an operator or individual who knowingly uses the Internet to conduct a sweepstakes game in Indiana or in a transaction directly involving a person located in Indiana. The text creates no criminal offense and no express supplier, payment-processor or affiliate liability.","Voluntary exclusion (Sec. 14, IC 4-33-4-3(b)): commission rules must cover the following for IC 4-38 sports wagering certificate holders as well as casino facilities. Participants agree to refrain from placing wagers with those holders, and the confidential exclusion list may be shared with them for enforcement. Holders must make all reasonable attempts to stop direct marketing to participants and may not cash participants' checks or extend them credit, though pre-enrollment debts remain collectible.","Racing integrity and licensing (Secs. 2–12): the bill adds a 'judge' definition covering licensed judges and stewards. It adds a new license-denial ground for material misrepresentation of Indiana owned, sired or bred status (IC 4-31-6-6(b)(20)). It reworks the bar on wagering by commission members, senior staff, judges and their spouses at IC 4-35 racinos (Class C infraction, or Class A misdemeanor for a repeat within 5 years).","Racing discipline (IC 4-31-13-2): stewards and judges may impose civil penalties up to $5,000, license suspensions up to 3 years, and exclusions from track premises. Appeals of these sanctions and of breath-test sanctions go to the Office of Administrative Law Proceedings under IC 4-15-10.5 and IC 4-21.5, with the commission bearing a preponderance burden.","Charity gaming (Sec. 13, IC 4-32.3-5-8): meals and social events for workers at charity gaming events are now limited by an 'unreasonable expense' test rather than a 'significant inducement' test. Remaining sections cover alcohol permits and tobacco certificates and are unrelated to gaming."],"issue_tags":["sweeps","integrity","licensing"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"CA AB 831 likewise targets online dual-currency sweepstakes. Indiana's approach is narrower: a single flat $100,000 civil penalty enforced by the Gaming Commission against the operator or individual, with no new crime and no express reach to suppliers or payment intermediaries, and with a carve-out for peer-to-peer skill-based poker. Unlike CT HB05229, which layers new consumer-protection duties such as withdrawal rules, customer service lines and AI-targeting limits onto gaming licensees, HB1052's sportsbook-facing change is limited to tying IC 4-38 certificate holders into the casino voluntary exclusion program. Unlike the stand-alone sports betting authorization bills (AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, HI HB 1308), this is an enacted cleanup bill in which gaming provisions sit alongside alcohol and tobacco amendments.","watch":"Watch how the Gaming Commission defines and enforces 'knowingly' and 'transaction directly involving a person located in Indiana' under IC 4-33-10-7, and whether it treats the $100,000 penalty as per violation. Also watch for implementing rules under IC 4-33-4-3 that could tighten sportsbook duties on marketing suppression, credit and data handling for self-excluded patrons.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":89257,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:47:27.659Z"},"IN-HB1078":{"summary":"HB 1078 amends IC 4-30 to let the Indiana lottery commission sell draw games and eInstant games over the Internet, reversing the current statutory ban on digital draw and scratch-off sales. It keeps the bans on video lottery terminals, lottery couriers, online casino-style games, and digital lottery games that simulate slot machines. It also requires geolocation and age verification rules, a retailer incentive program, and a commission-run voluntary exclusion program for digital lottery purchases.","mechanics":["New IC 4-30-3-20(c) authorizes Internet sales of draw games and eInstant games. An eInstant game is a digital game that, on reveal of numbers, symbols or other insignia, immediately shows whether the player won (IC 4-30-2-3.8).","Amended IC 4-30-3-20(b) still requires express legislative authority for the following, and the ban applies to both the lottery commission and the Indiana Gaming Commission: video lottery terminals; digital lottery games simulating slots (spinning reels, pull mechanism, spinning drum, varied pay lines); lottery courier services; and Internet sales of poker, roulette, slots or blackjack. The exemption for IC 4-38 sports wagering is retained.","IC 4-30-3-7(b), added by floor amendment, requires rules obliging the digital channel contractor to geolocate purchasers and refuse out-of-state payments. It also requires verification that buyers are 18 or older, using commercially reasonable standards, evidence more reliable than self-attestation, and secure data handling.","New IC 4-30-3-17.5 requires a retailer incentive program built with the digital contractor. The commission must hold an annual public meeting to review the impact of online sales on retailers, assess whether the program is working, and recommend changes if it is not.","New IC 4-30-3-21 requires rules for a voluntary exclusion program covering digital lottery games. Under it, the contractor must make reasonable attempts to stop direct marketing to participants and may not accept payment from or extend credit to them, though pre-existing debts remain collectible. The exclusion list is confidential but may be shared with channel operators, or with other entities on request and commission approval. The commission may set fines, fees or civil penalties by rule.","Effective July 1, 2026. The Public Policy Committee reported the bill 9-3, and it was amended on second reading January 13, 2026."],"issue_tags":["legalize","advertising"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"None of the listed bills authorizes online lottery (iLottery) sales; most authorize or regulate sports wagering, for example AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686 and HI HB 1308/SB1569. The closest is IL HB3276, which creates a lottery sports wagering pilot, whereas Indiana's bill expressly keeps lottery products separate from IC 4-38 sports wagering. Its responsible-gaming elements (self-exclusion, a halt to marketing to excluded persons, verification stronger than self-attestation) parallel consumer-protection themes in CT HB05229. Unusually, it pairs iLottery authorization with an explicit statutory bar on slot-like digital lottery games and a retailer-protection program.","watch":"Watch how the commission's rules draw the line between permitted eInstant games and the prohibited slot-simulating digital games under amended IC 4-30-3-20(b)(2), since a loose line could create a state-run, iGaming-like competitor for operators' online wallet share; also watch whether the lottery's exclusion list is coordinated with the Indiana Gaming Commission's existing exclusion programs.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Engrossed","text_chars":14227,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:47:31.485Z"},"KS-HB2007":{"summary":"HB 2007 is Kansas's annual reconciliation bill. It merges conflicting 2025 amendments made to the same statutes by separate enacted bills into a single version of each section and repeals the duplicate versions. The provisions in the supplied text cover distinctive license plates, resident-agent resignation, foreign-national contributions to constitutional-amendment campaigns, adult care home definitions, and background checks for adult care workers; none addresses sports betting or gaming.","mechanics":["Sec. 1 (K.S.A. 8-1,141): from January 1, 2025, any distinctive license plate may be personalized for double the K.S.A. 8-132(d) fee. It keeps the 250-plate minimum for issuance, the sponsor development payment of up to $5,000, and a $4,000 transfer once 100 paid orders are received for certain plates.","Sec. 2 (K.S.A. 17-7929): a resident agent may resign without naming a successor, effective 30 days after filing. The covered entity then has 60 days to designate a new agent or its organizing documents are forfeited, and service of process then goes to the secretary of state.","Sec. 3 (K.S.A. 25-4180): bars foreign-national contributions to campaigns for or against constitutional amendments. It requires donor certifications (including a $100,000 four-year threshold), allows statutory damages up to twice the prohibited amount, and imposes late-filing penalties of $10 per day capped at $300.","Secs. 4-5 (K.S.A. 39-923, 39-2009): reconcile adult care home definitions and the rules on criminal-history disqualification, waivers and fingerprinting for workers, including a fee of up to $19 for criminal history records.","The title also reconciles K.S.A. 45-229 (open-records exception review), 59-2946, 59-29b46, 72-5170 and 77-440, but those sections fall outside the supplied text, which is truncated in Sec. 5."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. What is unusual is that this is a purely technical Kansas reconciliation vehicle, with no substantive gaming policy, that lands in a sports-betting tracker probably because of its omnibus scope. The only conceivable connection is its reconciliation of K.S.A. 45-229, the statute governing legislative review of open-records exceptions, which can include confidentiality protections for gaming-related records; the supplied text does not reach that section, so no such effect can be confirmed.","watch":"Check the full enrolled text of the K.S.A. 45-229 reconciliation (not included in the supplied excerpt) to confirm that no open-records exemption protecting confidential sports-wagering or lottery-gaming operator data was altered or allowed to lapse; otherwise this bill does not affect operators.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":150338,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:47:32.783Z"},"IN-HB1092":{"summary":"HEA 1092 is mainly a family and juvenile law bill covering paternity, support payment channels, adoption petitions, child caring institution licensing and background checks, and DCS assessments. Section 1 amends Indiana's sports wagering child support intercept statute, IC 4-38-11-1. It extends the check-and-withhold duty, which previously applied only to certificate holders paying $600+ in-person sports wagering winnings, to vendors (e.g., online/mobile sports wagering operators) whenever a payout triggers a federal Form W-2G filing obligation.","mechanics":["Coverage (IC 4-38-11-1(a)-(b)): the bureau must supply child support delinquency information to vendors as well as certificate holders. Before disbursing a payout, the payer must check whether the claimant is delinquent in child support.","Certificate holders keep the existing trigger: a sports wagering payout of $600 or more claimed in person at the facility.","New vendor trigger: any payout that requires the vendor to file IRS Form W-2G or a substantially equivalent form. The trigger is tied to the federal reporting threshold, not a fixed state dollar figure.","If the claimant is delinquent (subsection (c)), the payer may keep an administrative fee of the lesser of 3% of the amount withheld or $100. The payer must then: withhold the delinquent amount; transmit it to the bureau with the obligor's name, address, SSN, case identifier, payment date and amount, and the payer's name and location; and issue a receipt in the bureau's prescribed form.","The bureau notifies the obligor and holds withheld funds for 10 business days before applying them. Withheld support and the fee take priority over all secured and unsecured claims on winnings except required federal and state tax withholding.","Effective July 1, 2026. The remaining sections (paternity orders, payment through a circuit court clerk or state-approved payment facility, adoption petition disclosures, child caring institution background checks and license denial grounds, DCS school interviews) do not involve gaming."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"None of the listed bills deal with child support intercepts on wagering payouts. AK HB145/SB194, HI HB 1308/HB 2570/SB1569, and GA HB 686 create new licensing and tax frameworks. The Illinois bills (e.g., HB2878, HB3159, HB5142) adjust licensing fees, taxes, or definitions within an existing regime. Unusually, this bill adds an operational compliance duty for online sports wagering vendors inside a child-welfare omnibus. It uses the federal W-2G trigger instead of a state dollar threshold, so the intercept duty moves with any change in federal reporting thresholds.","watch":"Online and mobile sports wagering vendors need payout-flow integration with the child support bureau's delinquency data by July 1, 2026, covering the pre-disbursement check, withholding, data transmission including SSNs, and receipt issuance on every W-2G-triggering payout, along with any bureau guidance on form, timing, and enforcement.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":29306,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:47:35.741Z"},"KS-HB2063":{"summary":"HB2063 creates a Kansas state conservation fund financed by a recurring $60 million annual transfer from the state general fund. Each July 15 the balance is split three ways: 50% to a working lands conservation fund run by the Kansas Department of Agriculture's division of conservation, 25% to a wildlife conservation fund run by the Secretary of Wildlife and Parks, and the remainder to a Kansas outdoors fund run by the parks division. The bill contains no gaming, wagering or sports-betting provisions of any kind.","mechanics":["Sec. 1(b): $60,000,000 is transferred from the state general fund to the state conservation fund on July 1, 2025 and each July 1 thereafter. The fund is exempt from K.S.A. 75-3722, 75-3725a and 75-3726a, and it may not supplant existing appropriations.","Sec. 1(c)-(e): on each July 15, 50% of the balance goes to the working lands conservation fund, 25% to the wildlife conservation fund, and the remaining balance to the Kansas outdoors fund.","Sec. 1(a)(2): moneys may not fund any fee simple land acquisition by the federal or state government. Sec. 3(g) separately says state-agency fee simple acquisitions are subject to K.S.A. 32-833 at the time of acquisition.","Secs. 2-4: each fund runs a grant program open to public entities, tribal nations, conservation districts, recreation commissions and nonprofits. Applications that capture nonstate matching funds get priority, and the legislature intends moneys to be obligated within 12 months.","Each administering agency must report to the governor and legislature by December 1 annually, covering expenditures and the disposition of applications.","Sec. 5: the act takes effect on publication in the statute book. The bill was stricken from the House calendar under Rule 1507 on 2026-02-19."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"Unlike the listed gaming bills, HB2063 does not touch wagering, licensing, taxation of gaming revenue or advertising. Several of those bills earmark gaming revenue for non-gaming purposes, such as IL HB3159 (2% of sports wagering adjusted gross receipts for fire and EMS) and IA SSB 1157 (gambling revenue for tourism programs). HB2063 instead funds conservation entirely through a fixed general fund transfer, with no gaming revenue source. Its only plausible connection to the tracked issue set is as a competing claimant on general fund dollars, and even that is not stated in the text.","watch":"The bill is effectively dead after being stricken under Rule 1507 and has no direct operator impact, but an operator lawyer should watch any revived version or floor amendment that tries to replace the $60 million general fund transfer with a diversion of Kansas sports wagering or lottery revenues.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":15698,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:47:41.650Z"},"KS-HB2146":{"summary":"HB2146 amends K.S.A. 74-8711 to double the annual cap on transfers from the Kansas lottery operating fund to two KDADS mental-health funds, the community crisis stabilization centers fund and the clubhouse model program fund, from $8,000,000 to $16,000,000 for fiscal year 2026 and each year after. The transfers come only from certified net profits on lottery tickets sold through lottery ticket vending machines. The existing sports wagering revenue allocations in subsections (f) through (h) are restated without change.","mechanics":["Sec. 1, 74-8711(e)(2): the aggregate cap on vending-machine net-profit transfers rises from $8,000,000 to $16,000,000 for FY2026 and each fiscal year thereafter. Prior caps of $9M (FY2024) and $10M (FY2025) are left in place.","74-8711(e)(1), unchanged: the lottery director certifies vending-machine net profits monthly by the 10th, split 75% to the community crisis stabilization centers fund and 25% to the clubhouse model program fund.","74-8711(d), unchanged: the state gaming revenues fund receives the greater of (1) fund moneys in excess of the (c)(1)-(c)(6) purposes or (2) a floor of 30% of monthly non-pull-tab sales and 20% of pull-tab sales, net of estimated returns. A larger (e) transfer could therefore shrink the 'excess' calculation.","74-8711(f)-(h), unchanged: from sports wagering revenues in the lottery operating fund, the first $750,000 goes to the white collar crime fund. Of the remainder, 2% goes to the problem gambling and addictions grant fund and 80% to the attracting professional sports to Kansas fund.","Sec. 3: the act takes effect on publication in the statute book. Requested on behalf of the Kansas Club House Coalition."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No other states' bills are listed for comparison. The bill is narrow: it reallocates lottery vending-machine profits to mental-health programs rather than changing any gaming tax rate or regulatory rule. Its text also restates, but does not alter, Kansas's sports wagering revenue waterfall, under which 80% of the post-$750,000 remainder goes to the stadium-attraction fund (APEX).","watch":"Although the sports wagering allocations in 74-8711(f)-(h) are untouched, any amendment while this section is open could be used to redirect sports wagering revenue (for example, away from the attracting professional sports fund or toward a higher problem-gambling share), so operators should monitor committee substitutes.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":7148,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:47:46.178Z"},"KS-HB2434":{"summary":"Substitute for HB 2434 is Kansas's omnibus supplemental and biennial appropriations bill, adjusting FY2026 amounts and appropriating FY2027 (and some FY2028) funds for state agencies, authorizing capital improvement projects, fees and fund transfers. The portion of text provided covers fee-funded licensing boards, the Legislature, the Governor and the Attorney General. None of it contains any sports-wagering or gaming provision.","mechanics":["Sec. 1 makes FY2026–FY2028 appropriations and exempts the act from K.S.A. 75-6702(a) and the appropriations from K.S.A. 46-155.","Secs. 2–15 adjust FY2026/FY2027 expenditure limits on fee funds of professional licensing boards, e.g. the Bank Commissioner fee fund rises from $13,711,453 to $13,986,695 and the Pharmacy fee fund falls from $3,607,526 to $3,205,483.","Secs. 16–24 lapse FY2026 balances and appropriate FY2027 funds for the Public Disclosure Commission, the Legislature and the Governor, including $25,124,413 for domestic violence prevention grants.","Secs. 25–26 lapse $10,000,000 from the AG's safe and secure firearm detection program, transfer $460,593 from the Kansas endowment for youth fund to the tobacco MSA compliance fund, and set FY2027 AG appropriations.","The title amends K.S.A. 2-223, 12-1775a, 12-5256, 74-50,107, 74-99b34, 76-775, 76-7,107, 79-2989, 79-3425i, 79-34,171 and 82a-955, but the amendment text is not in the excerpt provided."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"Unlike the substantive gaming bills listed, such as AK HB145/SB194 and GA HB 686 (authorizing and taxing mobile sports wagering), CT HB05229 (advertising and withdrawal rules) and IL HB5142/HB5059 (prediction markets), KS HB2434 is a general budget act with no wagering policy content in the text provided. The closest analogue on the list is DC B26-0034, another fiscal and contract measure listed only because of overlapping keyword matches. Any gaming relevance would come from agency line items in later sections, such as the Racing and Gaming Commission, the Lottery or sports-wagering-funded accounts, which are not in this excerpt.","watch":"Review the later, unprovided sections for any transfers from, or expenditure limits on, the Kansas sports wagering revenues fund, the attracting professional sports to Kansas fund, or Racing and Gaming Commission and Lottery fee funds, since budget provisos like these can quietly redirect wagering revenue or change regulator funding.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":300535,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:47:51.634Z"},"KS-SB125":{"summary":"SB 125 is Kansas's omnibus appropriations act. It makes supplemental FY2025 appropriations and FY2026–FY2027 appropriations for state agencies, authorizes capital improvement projects, fees and fund transfers, and pays small claims against the state. The Legislature overrode selected line-item vetoes on 2025-04-11. The excerpt provided (through Sec. 28) covers only professional licensing boards and claims and contains no wagering provisions. Gaming relevance turns on the title's amendment of K.S.A. 74-8711 and 79-4804, whose amended text is not in the excerpt.","mechanics":["Sec. 1(c) designates the FY2026 provisions as the omnibus appropriation act of 2025 and the omnibus reconciliation spending limit bill under K.S.A. 75-6702(a).","Secs. 2–5 pay claims against the state: inmate lost-property claims of $30.04 to $100, and $3,100 each from KDADS and KDHE to Hillside Village, LLC for delayed HCBS eligibility processing; Sec. 5(b) requires a written release before payment.","Secs. 6–28 set fee-fund expenditure caps for FY2026 and FY2027 for regulatory boards, for example the bank commissioner fee fund at $13,667,399 and $13,711,453, with official hospitality caps and budget-director-gated litigation funds.","Sec. 12(b) authorizes a barbering examination fee of up to $150 for FY2026–FY2027.","The title amends K.S.A. 74-8711 and 79-4804, statutes tied to Kansas lottery and gaming revenue funds, but the amended text is outside the provided excerpt, so any change to gaming revenue transfers cannot be confirmed here."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"Unlike the substantive sports wagering bills listed, such as AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, HI HB 1308 and IL HB3159, SB 125 neither authorizes, taxes nor regulates wagering. It is a general budget bill. The closest analogue is IA SSB 1157, which pairs appropriations with gaming tax rates and the use of gaming revenue. In SB 125, by contrast, any gaming touchpoint is limited to the statutory amendments of K.S.A. 74-8711 and 79-4804 listed in the title, plus whatever appropriations to gaming-related agencies appear later in the act.","watch":"Pull the full enrolled text of the K.S.A. 74-8711 and 79-4804 amendments and the Kansas Lottery and Racing and Gaming Commission appropriation sections, including any overridden line items, to see whether lottery or gaming revenue transfers or problem-gambling funding that could affect sports-wagering revenue allocations have been redirected.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":392639,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:47:51.963Z"},"KS-SB303":{"summary":"House Substitute for SB 303 imposes a new 2% privilege tax on the total amount of every sports wager placed with a Kansas lottery gaming facility manager. The tax is paid by the bettor but collected and remitted by the facility manager. It also narrows certain sales tax exemptions, including the lottery ticket exemption, and routes the resulting revenue into a new property tax relief fund. That fund backfills the state school district finance fund so the statewide 20-mill school levy under K.S.A. 72-5142(b) can be reduced.","mechanics":["New Sec. 3(a)-(b): a 2% privilege tax is imposed 'upon the total amount wagered' (handle, not revenue) on each sports wager placed with a lottery gaming facility manager. The bettor bears the tax, and the manager must collect the full amount from the bettor.","New Sec. 3(c): managers must remit monthly, by the 25th of the following month, with a true report on a Department of Revenue form covering all sports wagers accepted that month.","New Sec. 3(d)-(e): proceeds go to the new property tax relief fund. Refunds are paid from a new sports wagering privilege tax refund fund replenished from collections. The director of taxation gets subpoena and records-examination power, and sales tax assessment, collection, penalty and appeal provisions (K.S.A. 79-3610 to 79-3617, 75-5133) apply.","Sec. 5 amends K.S.A. 79-3606 effective July 1, 2026 to discontinue certain sales tax exemptions. Subsection (ee) appears to limit the lottery ticket exemption to multistate lottery association products where imposing sales tax is prohibited, but the strike/insert markup is not visible in the text provided, so the extent of the change should be confirmed.","New Secs. 1-2 and Sec. 4: starting after tax year 2027, the budget director certifies the new sales tax revenue, and the revenue secretary computes a reduced school mill rate (currently 20 mills for 2025-26 and 2026-27), publishing it by February 1. Certified amounts are transferred to the state school district finance fund."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"Unlike IL HB3159, which adds 2% of adjusted gross receipts earmarked for fire and EMS, Kansas levies 2% on gross handle and places the legal incidence on the bettor rather than the operator. That makes it closer in structure to Illinois' per-wager tax, which IL HB5143 would sunset on July 1, 2026. Like ME LD1304, it uses gaming revenue to fund property tax relief, but by statute rather than constitutional amendment. It sits apart from the authorization bills (AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, HI HB 1308, MS HB 4074, NE LB421/LB438) because it layers a new consumer-facing levy onto an existing market rather than setting operator tax rates.","watch":"Watch whether the tax reaches online wagers taken by platform providers operating under a lottery gaming facility manager's license, since the text names only facility managers. Also watch how a 2% handle tax collected from bettors interacts with pricing, odds, promotional and free bets, voided-wager refunds, and the manager's monthly collection and remittance liability.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Comm Sub","text_chars":159242,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:48:00.048Z"},"KS-SB315":{"summary":"Substitute for SB 315 is Kansas's omnibus appropriations bill. It makes supplemental FY2026 appropriations and lapses, sets FY2027 (and later) appropriations and expenditure limitations for state agencies, and authorizes capital improvement projects, fees and fund transfers. The portion of the text provided covers fee-funded licensing boards, the Legislature, the Governor and the Attorney General, and contains no sports-betting or gaming provisions.","mechanics":["Sec. 1: Makes FY2026–FY2028 appropriations and authorizes capital projects. It exempts the act from K.S.A. 75-6702(a) and the appropriations from K.S.A. 46-155.","Secs. 2–15: Adjust fee-fund expenditure limitations for licensing boards. Examples: State Bank Commissioner raised from $13,711,453 to $13,986,695; Board of Pharmacy cut from $3,607,526 to $3,205,483.","Secs. 16–22: Lapse FY2026 legislative-branch funds, including $2,129,006 from Legislature operations, and set FY2027 amounts, including $24,099,428 for Legislature operations.","Secs. 23–24: Lapse and reappropriate Governor's office accounts, including $25,124,413 for domestic violence prevention grants in FY2027.","Secs. 25–26: Lapse $10,000,000 from the Attorney General's safe and secure firearm detection program. They also transfer $460,593 from the Kansas endowment for youth fund to the tobacco MSA compliance fund and set AG fund appropriations.","Amends K.S.A. 2025 Supp. 2-223, 12-1775a, 12-5256, 74-50,107, 74-99b34, 76-775, 76-7,107, 79-2989, 79-3425i, 79-34,171 and 82a-955. The supplied text does not show the substance of these amendments."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"Unlike the listed substantive gaming bills, this is a general state budget act rather than gaming policy legislation. Those bills include GA HB 686 (authorizing and taxing sports betting), AK HB145/SB194 (mobile wagering tax), IL HB5143 (ending the per-wager tax) and IL HB5142 (folding prediction markets into the definition of sports wagering). Its closest analogue on the list is DC B26-0034, a fiscal and contract authorization measure with no wagering content. Any gaming relevance would come only from budget lines for gaming-related agencies or funds, and the provided excerpt does not reach those.","watch":"Review the later, unreproduced sections for any appropriations, expenditure limits or transfers touching the Kansas Racing and Gaming Commission, the Kansas Lottery, or funds fed by sports-wagering revenue, since budget-bill transfers can quietly redirect wagering revenue or change regulator funding.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":300452,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:48:01.630Z"},"KS-SB502":{"summary":"SB502 is a one-operative-section bill that lets the Kansas Racing and Gaming Commission adopt a specific package of proposed permanent sports wagering regulations under K.S.A. 2025 Supp. 77-441. The bill itself contains no substantive rules. It ratifies the adoption of regulations identified in an economic impact statement that the Division of Budget approved on February 13, 2025, and takes effect on publication in the Kansas Register.","mechanics":["Section 1 authorizes the Commission to adopt proposed permanent rules K.A.R. 112-201-1 through 112-201-23, omitting 112-201-14.","Section 1 also covers K.A.R. 112-202-1 and 112-202-2, 112-203-1 through 112-203-8, and 112-204-1 through 112-204-7.","The authority rests on K.S.A. 2025 Supp. 77-441, and the covered regulations are those described in the Division of Budget economic impact statement approved February 13, 2025.","Section 2 makes the act effective on publication in the Kansas Register rather than the statute book, so it takes effect quickly.","The bill sets no tax rates, license fees, thresholds or enforcement provisions of its own. All substance sits in the referenced K.A.R. sections, which are not reproduced in the bill."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686 and HI HB 1308/SB1569, which would create a sports wagering framework with licensing and tax terms, KS SB502 is a purely procedural legislative sign-off on regulations the Commission has already drafted. It is also unlike the Illinois bills that amend the Sports Wagering Act directly, such as HB2878 and HB5034 on license fees or HB5142 on prediction markets. SB502 changes no statutory terms and only clears the way for agency rules. Its unusual feature is the requirement that the legislature approve specific K.A.R. numbers before the agency can adopt them.","watch":"Get the text of the listed K.A.R. 112-201 through 112-204 regulations and the February 13, 2025 economic impact statement, note why 112-201-14 is left out, and be ready for those rules to bind operators as soon as the act is published in the Kansas Register.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":1351,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:48:05.864Z"},"KY-HB869":{"summary":"HB 869 (Acts Ch. 198, signed April 27, 2026, with an emergency clause) is a Kentucky fiscal and economic-development tax bill. It creates a new refundable economic development income tax credit, re-orders the KRS 141.0205 credit stack, and adds a 'heritage county' concept (county population ranking of 97 or more) that loosens thresholds and enlarges benefits across several incentive programs. The portion of the text provided contains no provisions on sports wagering, pari-mutuel wagering or gaming, though the text is truncated partway through Section 9.","mechanics":["Section 1 creates a refundable, nontransferable economic development credit against KRS 141.020, 141.040 and 141.0401 tax for taxable years beginning on or after January 1, 2026. It is capped at $4,000,000 per year for all approved companies, of which no more than $1,000,000 may go to wages in non-heritage counties. Claim data is expressly not confidential.","Section 2 amends the KRS 141.0205 ordering rules to add the new credit and a certified mixed-use development credit (Section 31) as refundable credits. It also adds an alternative jet fuel producer credit (Section 42) as a nonrefundable credit.","Sections 4-5 change the Bluegrass State Skills Corporation skills training investment credit. The per-trainee cap rises from $2,000 to $4,500 in heritage counties and is set at $3,500 elsewhere. The cap remains $500,000 per company per fiscal year and $2.5M in aggregate, the training completion window extends from one to three years, and the minimum base wage is set at 200% (heritage) or 300% of the federal minimum wage.","Sections 6-7 raise the Kentucky Product Development Initiative maximum award to $2.5M for heritage-county projects, versus $2M elsewhere.","Section 9 lowers the jobs retention eligibility thresholds for heritage-county projects to 250 full-time employees and $25M in eligible costs, versus 1,000 employees and $100M elsewhere.","An emergency clause means the act is effective on signature."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"The listed bills are gaming-specific. AK HB145/SB194, HI HB1308/HB2570/SB1569 and GA HB686 would authorize and tax sports wagering, IA SF 605 addresses withholding on sports wagering winnings, and IL HB3159 would add a 2% adjusted gross receipts levy. KY HB869 does none of these in the text provided; it is a general business-incentive tax bill built around income tax credits and county-tiered economic development benefits. Nothing comparable is listed, and its distinctive feature is the heritage-county tiering applied across multiple incentive programs.","watch":"Because the supplied text is truncated, an operator's counsel should review the remaining sections for any amendment to Kentucky's wagering excise or pari-mutuel tax provisions or fund allocations, which would take effect immediately under the emergency clause.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":377977,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:48:15.357Z"},"LA-HB1":{"summary":"HB1 is Louisiana's general appropriations act for Fiscal Year 2026-2027, effective July 1, 2026, funding executive branch agencies, schools, roads and institutions. Gaming appears only as budget units, including appropriations for the regulators and a pass-through of sports wagering revenue to local governments. No substantive gaming or sports-betting policy is changed in the text provided.","mechanics":["Gaming-related budget units listed in the table of contents: 01-254 Louisiana State Racing Commission; 04-141 Office of the Attorney General (Gaming program); 08-419 Office of State Police (Gaming Enforcement Program); 08-423 Louisiana Gaming Control Board.","Schedule 20 includes 20-924 Video Draw Poker – Local Government Aid and 20-926 Sports Wagering Local Allocation Fund, which pass gaming revenue through to local governments. Dollar amounts are not in the excerpt provided.","Section 2.A: if statutorily dedicated or self-generated revenues, which would include gaming fee and tax dedications, fall short, appropriations are reduced accordingly. Increases require approval by the commissioner of administration and the Joint Legislative Committee on the Budget.","Section 6.A bars transfers between departments or schedules unless expressly provided. Transfers within a department require approval by the commissioner of administration and the Joint Legislative Committee on the Budget.","Section 5.A: program descriptions and performance information are not enacted into law."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. The notable feature is that Louisiana runs sports wagering proceeds through a dedicated budget unit, 20-926 Sports Wagering Local Allocation Fund, alongside a separate video draw poker local aid line. That makes the annual appropriation the place where local distribution of sports-betting revenue is actually funded.","watch":"Check the full-text figures for the Gaming Control Board, the State Police Gaming Enforcement Program and the Sports Wagering Local Allocation Fund, since regulator funding levels and local revenue distributions affect enforcement capacity and political pressure on future tax or fee changes.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":269140,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:48:16.081Z"},"LA-SB354":{"summary":"SB354 amends Louisiana's sports wagering chapter (Title 27, Chapter 11) to strike \"proposition bets\" from the list of included wager types in the R.S. 27:602(23) definition of \"sports wager.\" It adds definitions of \"proposition bet\" and \"sports micro-bet\" and adds both to the R.S. 27:608(C) list of wagers that no operator may accept or pay. The effect is a flat ban on all prop bets, including player and in-game props, for every licensed operator, retail and mobile alike.","mechanics":["R.S. 27:602(23): deletes \"proposition bets\" from the non-exhaustive list of permitted sports wager types. Single-game, teaser, parlay, over-under, moneyline, pools, exchange, in-game, in-play and straight bets remain.","New R.S. 27:602(29): defines a \"proposition bet\" or \"prop bet\" as a side wager on a part of a sport or athletic event that does not concern the event's final outcome.","New R.S. 27:602(30): defines a \"sports micro-bet\" as a proposition bet wagered live, while the event is ongoing, on the outcome of a play or action in the event.","New R.S. 27:608(C)(5) and (6): no operator may accept or pay any wager on a sports micro-bet or on any proposition bet.","Effective August 1, 2026. The bill has a fiscal note flagging a possible decrease in general fund revenue. No new penalty provisions are added; enforcement runs through existing Chapter 11 limitations and Gaming Control Board authority."],"issue_tags":["prop","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike the authorization and tax bills listed (AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, HI HB 1308/HB 2570/SB1569) or the licensing-fee bills (IL HB2878/HB5034), LA SB354 narrows an existing legal market by removing an entire product category. That makes it the most restrictive market-product bill on the list. CT HB05229 targets AI-driven bet targeting and other consumer-protection rules rather than banning bet types outright. IL HB5142, which pulls sports prediction markets into the definition of sports wagering, is the only other listed bill that reshapes the definition of a sports wager, but it expands the definition rather than contracting it. An outright ban on all props, rather than only college-player props or micro-bets, is unusually broad among recent state proposals.","watch":"Watch the very broad \"does not concern the final outcome\" definition, which on its face could sweep in spreads-by-period, first-half, totals-adjacent and same-game parlay legs as well as player props, and press for amendments narrowing it to player or collegiate props or for a delayed effective date ahead of August 1, 2026.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":4045,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:48:16.143Z"},"LA-HB883":{"summary":"Act 182 of 2026 rewrites Louisiana's gambling-by-computer crime (R.S. 14:90.3) to explicitly cover dual-currency sweepstakes-style games, sharply raises operator penalties, and creates new criminal exposure for anyone who knowingly supports or facilitates illegal online gambling, including platform providers, geolocation providers, content suppliers, promoters, endorsers, media affiliates, and merchant payment processors. New R.S. 27:19.1 and 19.2 give the attorney general, and optionally the Gaming Control Board chairman, a cease-and-desist process with a public AG blacklist, subpoena power, and broad civil injunctive remedies, including ex parte takedown and payment-blocking orders. It takes effect August 1, 2026.","mechanics":["Definition (14:90.3(B)): any internet or mobile game, contest, or promotion using a dual-currency system redeemable for cash, prizes, or cash equivalents that simulates gambling is gambling by computer, which squarely targets sweepstakes casinos.","Penalties: clients face up to $500 and/or 6 months (D). Operators face up to $100,000 (raised from $20,000) and/or 5 years (E). Knowing facilitators, including licensed entities, applicants, geolocation providers, content suppliers, promoters, endorsers, and media affiliates, face up to $20,000 and/or 5 years, with fines doubled where a person under 21 is involved (F). Each wager and each promotion is a separate violation (I), and forfeiture of profits and gross receipts is mandatory (J).","Payment processors and platform providers (G, H) are barred from processing or transmitting illegal gambling transactions or information. They are deemed to have constructive knowledge of every person and URL on the AG's list. Running an ad for a listed site is prima facie evidence of support. Good-faith blocking carries tort immunity. Federally insured banks and payment card networks are excluded (R), and a 47 U.S.C. 230 savings clause applies (Q).","Cease and desist (27:19.1): the notice gives a 10-day cure period, is served under the Code of Civil Procedure, and is admissible as prima facie evidence of knowledge. The AG maintains a public listing of noticed operators, URLs, platforms, and processors. Out-of-state site operators who know or should know their content reaches Louisiana submit to state jurisdiction. The AG gets subpoena and injunction authority, and the statute calls for liberal construction.","Civil remedies (27:19.2): courts may order TROs and injunctions, site removal, freezing of bank and credit accounts, and cutting off players' access. Violation is conclusively irreparable harm. Ex parte injunctions are available against platforms and processors, with a hearing within 30 days. Violating an order costs $50,000 for the first violation and $100,000 for each subsequent day or transaction.","Carve-outs: licensed sports wagering under Title 27 Ch. 10 or Title 47 Ch. 10 (P), fantasy sports under R.S. 27:302 (O), on-premises licensed gaming (K, L), securities and commodities exchange transactions (M), and ordinary-course ISPs and service providers unless their primary purpose is gambling (N)."],"issue_tags":["sweeps","integrity","advertising","licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Most bills listed, such as AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, and HI HB 1308/SB1569, authorize and tax legal sports wagering, while Louisiana's HB883 is purely an enforcement statute aimed at the unlicensed and sweepstakes market. It is closest to FL H0189 and FL S1580, which also tighten illegal-gaming and internet-gambling penalties. HB883 goes further by imposing liability on third-party intermediaries such as payment processors, platforms, geolocation vendors, and endorsers, and by tying it to an AG blacklist with constructive-knowledge and ex parte takedown tools. Unlike IL HB5142, it does not address prediction markets, and the 14:90.3(M) exchange carve-out may be argued to shield exchange-traded event contracts.","watch":"Licensed operators and their vendors, affiliates, and geolocation and content suppliers should screen every partner against the AG's public cease-and-desist list. Subsection F expressly reaches 'licensed entities' that knowingly support or facilitate illegal online gambling, with per-promotion violations, mandatory forfeiture, and doubled fines where under-21 users are involved.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":20174,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:48:21.735Z"},"LA-SB445":{"summary":"SB445 adds two new revenue streams to the Louisiana Early Childhood Education Fund (R.S. 17:407.30): year-end unspent, non-dedicated Department of Education cash balances swept in under R.S. 39:82(J), and an annual state general fund top-up of up to $30 million beginning July 1, 2027. It also removes the \"subject to legislative appropriation\" condition on BESE awards from the fund. The bill does not amend the existing sports wagering, fantasy sports, or casino tax dedications to the fund. Those dedications are noted only in the digest as present law.","mechanics":["R.S. 39:82(J) is rewritten to direct the treasurer to deposit all year-end cash balances from legislative or Interim Emergency Board appropriations to the Department of Education into the fund. Dedicated funds and balances with a bona fide liability on the last day of the fiscal year are excluded. The rewrite replaces the existing Office of Behavioral Health retention language.","Beginning July 1, 2027, the treasurer must transfer from the general fund each year the difference between $30 million and the fund's unencumbered balance at the start of the prior fiscal year, capped at $30 million per year. These monies are to be used to match one-to-one grants approved by BESE.","The deposits in R.S. 17:407.30(B) remain subordinate to the Bond Security and Redemption Fund requirements under La. Const. Art. VII, §9(B).","Section (D)(1)(a) deletes \"Subject to legislative appropriation,\" so BESE would award fund monies annually without a separate appropriation. The one-to-one nonstate, nonfederal local match requirement for CCAP-eligible slots in Type III centers is retained.","Existing fund sources are untouched, according to the digest. These include casino gaming revenue (R.S. 27:270), sports wagering tax (R.S. 27:625), and fantasy sports tax (R.S. 27:316).","The bill takes effect on the governor's signature or on lapse of time for gubernatorial action. A fiscal note flags a general fund expenditure."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. The bill is unusual in that it shores up a fund that already receives sports wagering and fantasy sports tax dedications by adding general fund and agency-sweep money, instead of changing gaming tax rates or dedications. It also makes the fund self-appropriating, which would let BESE spend gaming-derived deposits without annual legislative appropriation.","watch":"Because the bill guarantees a $30 million general fund backstop and does not touch R.S. 27:625, watch for amendments in the Senate Finance Committee that would raise the sports wagering tax rate or its dedication to this fund as an alternative or additional revenue source.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":7366,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:48:25.567Z"},"MA-H1":{"summary":"House 1 is Governor Healey's FY2026 general appropriations bill, proposing $59.58 billion in non-surtax spending plus $1.95 billion tied to the Fair Share income surtax, and setting out consensus revenue estimates by source and fund. In the portion of text provided, gaming appears only as budget lines: Massachusetts Gaming Commission resource totals and references to the gaming-related funds among 'other major funds.' The bill was reported, in part, by H4000, the House's own budget.","mechanics":["Section 1A revenue table lists 'Other major funds' that include the Gaming Local Aid Fund and the Gaming Economic Development Fund. It shows $555.5M in total revenue for that fund group but does not break out gaming receipts separately.","The Independents resource summary for the Massachusetts Gaming Commission shows a $923K budgetary recommendation and $92.82M in federal, trust and intragovernmental service fund spending, for $93.743M total. It also projects $385.966M in budgetary non-tax revenue.","The Section 1A consensus tax table has no separate sports wagering or gaming tax line. Gaming receipts appear to flow as non-tax or departmental revenue rather than tax revenue.","House 1 tax initiatives total $386M: tax-related settlements, capping the charitable deduction, and tax enforcement and loophole closures. In the text provided, none targets wagering.","The text provided is truncated partway through the District Attorneys section. Outside sections and any later Gaming Commission line items or policy riders are not visible and cannot be assessed."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. What stands out is that the Governor's budget treats gaming purely as a revenue and appropriation matter in the visible text. The Gaming Commission is funded almost entirely off-budget through trust spending ($92.8M) and is projected to return about $386M in non-tax revenue, with no change to sports wagering tax rates or regulation visible in the provided portion.","watch":"Check the untruncated outside sections and the H4000 and Senate budget amendments for any riders that raise the sports wagering tax rate, redirect Gaming Commission trust funds or gaming fund distributions, or add advertising or responsible-gaming conditions, since budget vehicles in Massachusetts are a common route for such changes.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":188575,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:48:27.576Z"},"MA-H3064":{"summary":"H3064 creates a Bilingual Education Trust Fund to pay stipends and loan repayment for bilingual educators, and rewrites the bilingual endorsement and reciprocity rules in chapter 71A. It funds the trust partly by redirecting 5 points of the existing sports wagering excise in chapter 23N, section 14(a), and by imposing a new 15% excise on adjusted gross fantasy contest receipts. The bill was sent to a study order (H5238) on 2026-03-16, which in Massachusetts practice usually ends a bill for the session.","mechanics":["Section 5 rewrites the first sentence of G.L. c. 23N, §14(a). It keeps the headline rates of 15% on in-person and 20% on mobile/digital adjusted gross sports wagering receipts. In each case 5 percentage points go to the new Bilingual Education Trust Fund, leaving 10% (in-person) and 15% (mobile) for the Sports Wagering Fund under §17.","Section 5 also adds a new clause (iii): a 15% excise on the adjusted gross fantasy wagering receipts of anyone offering fantasy contests 'as defined in section 11M1/2'. Of that, 5% goes to the trust fund and 10% to the Sports Wagering Fund. The bill does not say which chapter section 11M1/2 belongs to (the existing fantasy contest provision is in chapter 12), and it does not define 'adjusted gross fantasy wagering receipts'.","Section 4 creates new G.L. c. 29, §2BBBBBB, the trust fund, administered by the Commissioner of Elementary and Secondary Education. Money in the fund is not subject to further appropriation and does not revert to the General Fund. The Commissioner reports to the Legislature annually by October 1.","Section 6 keeps the marijuana retail excise at 10.75% but diverts 2 points of it to the trust fund.","Section 3 adds new c. 71A, §14, funded from the trust. It provides a $10,000 annual stipend for endorsed bilingual educators, $5,000 more for staff certified as bilingual, $1,000 for holders of the state seal of biliteracy, and $1,000 a year for 5 years for graduates of Massachusetts public schools. Educators with 10 years of service in the same district get $30,000 in loan repayment. Stipends count as compensation for teacher retirement purposes.","Effective date is January 1, 2027 (Section 8)."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"No other states' bills are listed for comparison. What is unusual is that an education workforce bill carries two gaming changes. It earmarks a fixed share of the sports wagering excise to a dedicated, non-appropriated trust, which reduces what flows into the Sports Wagering Fund without raising operators' headline rates. It also writes a brand-new 15% excise on daily fantasy operators into the sports wagering statute. A similar bill was filed last session as H3947 (2023-2024).","watch":"The main risk is the new 15% excise on fantasy contest operators in proposed c. 23N, §14(a)(iii), with its undefined 'adjusted gross fantasy wagering receipts' base and unclear cross-reference; the bill is likely dead after the study order, but watch for this language resurfacing in revenue or budget vehicles.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":17584,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:48:39.720Z"},"MA-H432":{"summary":"H432 extends Massachusetts' racing and simulcasting authorizations, which expire December 15, 2025 under chapter 26 of the acts of 2023, to December 15, 2030 across a series of special acts. It keeps the Suffolk County running-horse licensee licensed for simulcast wagering through the later of December 15, 2030 or the end of its category 2 sports wagering license term. It also shields category 2 sports wagering licenses under G.L. c. 23N from any adverse effect if racing or simulcasting laws lapse.","mechanics":["Sections 1-15 replace 'December 15, 2025' with 'December 15, 2030' in the sunset clauses of c. 494 of 1978 (ss. 12A, 13, 15), c. 277 of 1986 (s. 9), c. 114 of 1991 (ss. 3-5), c. 139 of 2001 (s. 45), c. 449 of 2006 (s. 20), c. 194 of 2011 (ss. 92, 112) and c. 10 of 2015 (s. 74). Section 3 cites the amending source as 'said chapter 128', apparently a drafting error for chapter 26.","Section 16 repeals section 23 of chapter 26 of the acts of 2023. The text does not say what that section provides.","Section 17 applies to the Suffolk County running-horse licensee that held live-racing (c. 128A) and simulcast (c. 128C) licenses in 2025. That licensee stays licensed and authorized to simulcast until the later of December 15, 2030 or the expiration, including any renewal, of a c. 23N category 2 sports wagering license it holds as of that date.","Section 17 also sets the following conditions. All days from January 1, 2025 to December 31, 2030 are dark days, so no live racing occurs unless the licensee obtains a supplemental live racing license. Greyhound simulcasts and wagers are barred. Simulcasts must comply with the Interstate Horse Racing Act, 15 U.S.C. 3001 et seq.","For simulcasts from states not requiring IHA approval under 15 U.S.C. 3004(a)(1)(A), approval from the New England HBPA is required. If the HBPA approves a simulcast for one licensee, it must approve it for all eligible licensees.","Section 18 provides that expiration or repeal of c. 128A, c. 128C or related racing laws, or any resulting cessation of simulcasting, cannot suspend, terminate or otherwise adversely affect a category 2 sports wagering license. The holder is deemed authorized to simulcast for the full license term and any renewal."],"issue_tags":["licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"None of the listed bills address pari-mutuel sunset extensions or the link between racing status and sports wagering eligibility. Florida H0189 touches pari-mutuel permits as part of a broad gambling rewrite. Illinois HB2880 and HB5035 amend the Horse Racing Act only on Racing Board employee restrictions. H432 is unusual because Section 18 decouples category 2 sports wagering licensure from continued simulcasting, a protection for racing-based sportsbook licensees not found in the Alaska, Georgia, Hawaii or Illinois authorization bills.","watch":"Watch whether Section 18's deemed-authorization language for category 2 licensees survives as the bill moves with H341, since without it a lapse in racing or simulcasting authority could jeopardize a racetrack-based sports wagering license under c. 23N.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":8171,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:48:43.370Z"},"MA-H332":{"summary":"H332 would create a new Chapter 23O, the Massachusetts Internet Gaming Act, legalizing online casino games (poker, blackjack, roulette, slots, live dealer and other Commission-approved games) under the Massachusetts Gaming Commission. Licenses would go to existing Chapter 23K casino licensees and to up to four standalone operators. Revenue would be taxed at 20% of adjusted gross receipts, with a promotional-credit deduction that is phased down starting in 2028. On April 2, 2026 the bill was sent to a study order (H5320), which in Massachusetts practice means it is effectively shelved for the 2025-2026 session.","mechanics":["Licensing (§6): Category 1 licenses go to Chapter 23K casino licensees, each allowed up to two platforms and two platform providers, with one brand per platform plus an optional second poker brand. Category 2 licenses for non-casino entities are capped at 4, with one platform each, awarded on eight best-qualified factors if oversubscribed. No partnership with a casino licensee is required (§6(b)(3)).","Fees: $200,000 nonrefundable processing fee (§7). $5,000,000 initial license fee for a 5-year term, renewable for $5,000,000 (§6(g)). A $1,000,000 temporary license is available to a 'qualified gaming entity' (a casino licensee or an operator licensed for iGaming in 3+ US jurisdictions), and all applicants filing within 30 days get the same launch day (§6(c)). Transfer review fee is capped at $200,000. Operators share a $1,000,000 annual Public Health Trust Fund assessment (§15(e)).","Tax (§14): 20% excise on adjusted gross internet gaming receipts, in lieu of all other state and local taxes and fees. Promotional credits are fully deductible until Jan. 1, 2028. After that the deduction is capped at 2.5% of monthly handle, stepping down to 2.25% (July 2029), 2% (July 2030) and 1.75% (from July 1, 2031), with no carryover of excess credits. Negative AGR may be carried forward but not back.","Consumer protection: §4(c) requires rules banning deceptive ads, pop-ups or texts to self-excluded persons, and marketing targeting under-21s. §4(d) requires responsible gaming plans, no credit lines, an unsolicited-free withdrawal right, account closure, and a 1-800-GAMBLER display. Minimum age is 21. The Commission keeps a self-exclusion list and may extend Chapter 23N sports-wagering self-exclusions to iGaming.","Integrity (§§11-12): bars insiders and proxy bettors, requires monitoring systems, immediate reporting of suspicious wagering, and 3-year records of abnormal activity. The Commission must default to requesting anonymized data, and operators get notice and appeal rights before personally identifiable information is demanded.","Market structure: a single patron wallet may be shared with the operator's sports wagering account (§3). Interstate reciprocal agreements are authorized (§4(i), §13(b)). Live dealer studios may be located outside Massachusetts (§13(c)). Supervisory staff need occupational licenses ($100), and Chapter 23K/23N licensees are deemed qualified."],"issue_tags":["legalize","licensing","tax","advertising","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"The closest analogues on the list are Illinois HB3080 and HB4797, which also create an 'Internet Gaming Act'. Per their descriptions, those bills route iGaming through licensees or management services providers contracted with a licensee. MA H332 instead opens up to four Category 2 licenses to operators with no casino partner. Most other listed bills deal with sports wagering, including AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, HI HB 1308/HB 2570/SB1569 and the Illinois Sports Wagering Act amendments, rather than online casino. H332's statutory phase-down of the promotional-credit deduction to 1.75% of handle is more granular than anything described in the listed bills.","watch":"Watch three things for when this framework resurfaces after the study order: the phased cap on promotional-credit deductions (with a no-carryover rule that mistakenly refers to a 'mobile sports betting operator' in §14(b)(3)), the scarcity and best-qualified criteria for the four standalone Category 2 licenses, and Section 2's redraft of the 'illegal gaming' definition, which drops the fantasy-contest and greyhound exclusions that Section 1 had included.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":73627,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:48:44.382Z"},"MA-H4037":{"summary":"H4037 is a broad Massachusetts responsible-gaming bill. It links self-exclusion across the lottery, casino (c. 23K), sports wagering (c. 23N) and a new Attorney General fantasy-contest list. It also imposes new advertising restrictions and a 5% excise on celebrity endorsement income, requires betting and loss limits plus daily and monthly activity statements, mandates anonymized player-data sharing for research, and bars sports wagering licensees from limiting or excluding bettors except under the statute or clearly disclosed terms of service. On 2026-03-18 it was accompanied by a new draft, H5258, so the operative text may have changed.","mechanics":["Advertising (Sections 4, 11 and 13): a new c. 23K §72 bars 23K and 23N licensees from advertising, branding, sponsorships or celebrity endorsements deemed to appeal to persons under 21, and from TV, radio, internet, billboard or print ads unless at least 85% of the audience is reasonably expected to be 21 or older. A new c. 23N §4(F) addresses advertising on TV or streaming during broadcasts of sporting events. A single standardized disclaimer with the MA Gambling Helpline number is required in all licensee and contractor advertising (new c. 23K §3(z)).","Excluded persons (Sections 1, 3, 6–7 and 17–18): enrolling on any one of the casino, sports wagering, fantasy or lottery self-exclusion lists can be done together with the others. The commission must offer online and phone enrollment. Operators may not market to anyone on an excluded persons list and must use all available technology (cookies, IP addresses, digital fingerprints) to keep ads from being served to them; MGC regulations will set penalties.","Section 11 / new c. 23K §72(b): the commission must establish a 5% excise tax on income an individual celebrity earns under advertising, sponsorship or endorsement contracts with 23K or 23N licensees, with revenue going to the Public Health Trust Fund.","Section 16 / new c. 23N §12(h): operators must let bettors set per-bet, hourly, daily, weekly and monthly limits, with limit increases capped at once per 24 hours. At registration, bettors must be prompted to set spending and loss limits and to enroll in or opt out of a play-management program such as Play-My-Way. Patrons get daily electronic and monthly mailed statements of bets, wins and losses (opt-out allowed), and operators report annual spend and losses by zip code.","Section 16 / new c. 23N §12(i): a licensee may not exclude an individual or limit their wagers or potential winnings except under a section of c. 23N or a preexisting term of service clearly disclosed when the user logs into the app.","Data, records and information sharing (Sections 14, 15 and 19): operators must supply customer tracking data, which a nonprofit anonymizes (the contract was due by Dec. 31, 2025) for research by qualified researchers. Operator license applications become public records, with trade secrets withholdable at the commission's discretion. The investigations and enforcement bureau may exchange information with law enforcement, other gaming regulators and the FBI."],"issue_tags":["advertising","tax","licensing","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"New Jersey's A 4002/S3419 only require operators to publish account-limitation rules and notify patrons. H4037's §12(i) goes further, effectively banning limiting or excluding bettors except under the statute or terms of service disclosed at login. NJ AR150 merely urges a ban on targeted gambling ads to problem gamblers, whereas H4037 mandates technical suppression of ads to excluded persons. Maryland's HB518 (voluntary exclusion list) and HB1409 (prevalence studies) address similar harms, but none of the listed bills combines an 85% adult-audience ad standard, a celebrity endorsement excise and mandatory anonymized player-data sharing.","watch":"Check whether the new draft H5258 keeps the §12(i) restriction on limiting or excluding bettors, the 85% adult-audience advertising test with its restriction on ads during sports broadcasts, and the mandatory player-data handover, as these carry the largest compliance and commercial impact.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":33371,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:48:50.261Z"},"MA-H4431":{"summary":"H4431 creates a new M.G.L. chapter 23O authorizing internet casino gaming (iGaming) in Massachusetts, licensed exclusively by the Massachusetts Gaming Commission and limited to existing chapter 23K casino licensees, each of which may contract out up to 3 branded skins. It imposes a 15% tax on adjusted gross revenue, allows multi-state reciprocal agreements, sets detailed responsible-gaming and advertising rules, and separately bans online sweepstakes games with fines of $10,000 to $100,000 per violation. The bill was sent to a study order (H5269) on 2026-03-23, which in Massachusetts practice usually means it will not advance this session.","mechanics":["Eligibility and fees (§6): only chapter 23K gaming licensees qualify for an internet gaming operator license ($100,000 application fee); each licensee may issue up to 3 skins to platform providers ($50,000 nonrefundable fee per skin); supplier licenses carry a $15,000 fee (§11); licenses run 5 years with renewal fees of $100,000, $50,000 and $15,000 (§12); temporary licenses are available in the first 12 months for applicants holding a plenary iGaming license in an equivalent state (§7).","Tax (§13): 15% of adjusted gross revenue, in lieu of all other taxes and fees on internet gaming, paid monthly, with negative months carried forward; an unspecified percentage set by regulation goes to a new Player Health Program Fund (§16).","Player accounts (§14): patrons must be 21 or older and physically located in Massachusetts or a permissible jurisdiction; deposits are capped at $20,000 per 24 hours; credit-card funding is excluded, including via digital wallets and prepaid cards; a single account may be shared with the operator's mobile sports wagering product (§2 definition).","Responsible gaming and advertising (§§3(c), 17, 23-24): ban on using biometric data; commission-designated AI triggers to flag risky play; mandatory deposit, loss, wager and time limits; cool-off periods of at least 72 hours; no ads on college campuses; no 'risk-free' language; no influencers who primarily appeal to under-21s; 1-800-GAMBLER messaging; self-exclusion terms of 1, 3 or 5 years or lifetime, with an opt-in to a national list.","Sweepstakes ban (§19): operating, promoting or assisting online sweepstakes games (dual-currency casino-style, lottery or sports wagering) is unlawful, with fines of $10,000 to $100,000 per violation, possible loss of gaming license, and up to 2 years' imprisonment for repeat offenders.","Enforcement (§§20, 26): tampering with games carries up to 5 years and $50,000 (or $200,000 for entities) plus mandatory license revocation; civil penalties generally require prior written notice and a cure period, except for patterns of noncompliance, willful conduct, integrity breaches or failures to report."],"issue_tags":["legalize","tax","licensing","advertising","sweeps","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike the sports-wagering legalization bills listed (AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, HI HB 1308/SB1569), H4431 addresses online casino gaming rather than sports betting; its closest analogues are IL HB3080 and HB4797, which would create an Illinois Internet Gaming Act. It pairs iGaming authorization with an outright online sweepstakes ban carrying criminal exposure, a combination not reflected in the listed titles. Its advertising limits overlap with CT HB05229 (college-campus ad restrictions and AI-related rules), but H4431 takes the opposite approach on AI: it directs AI to detect risky play rather than barring AI-driven bet targeting.","watch":"Watch the casino-tethered three-skin cap, the $20,000 daily deposit limit and mandatory AI risk triggers that would bind sportsbooks offering combined iGaming wallets, and the §19 sweepstakes ban's 'support or assist' language and license-loss penalty, which could reach licensed operators' vendors and affiliates, should the study order give way to a redraft.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":52431,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:48:54.612Z"},"MA-H481":{"summary":"H481 would amend the definition of \"Category 2 license\" in M.G.L. c. 23N, section 3, so that a Category 2 (retail, in-person) sports wagering licensee could make a capital investment of at least $7,500,000 in a sports wagering facility instead of paying the license fee referenced in \"section 5(f).\" The bill is a single section and adds no other conditions or procedures. As of 2026-03-23 it was accompanied a study order (see H5269), which in Massachusetts practice usually means it will not advance this session.","mechanics":["SECTION 1 replaces the last clause of the c. 23N, section 3 definition of \"Category 2 license\" with a new proviso.","A Category 2 licensee may, \"in lieu of the fee in section 5(f),\" make a capital investment of \"not less than $7,500,000 in a sports wagering facility.\"","The bill does not define what counts as a qualifying capital investment, set a deadline for making it, provide for verification or clawback, or say how it interacts with renewal fees.","The fee cross-reference to \"section 5(f)\" is not explained in the text, and the bill contains no effective date or transition clause.","Only Category 2 (in-person) licensees are covered; Category 1 and Category 3 (mobile) licensees are unaffected."],"issue_tags":["licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike IL HB2878 and HB5034, which would set a $250,000 application fee and a $15,000,000 master license fee, H481 moves the other way: it lets a retail licensee swap its license fee for facility spending. It does not touch tax rates or mobile authorization, which are the focus of AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686 and the Hawaii bills (HB 1308, HB 2570, SB1569). Allowing capital investment to satisfy a statutory license fee for a retail sportsbook tier is unusual among the listed bills.","watch":"Watch whether the concept comes back out of the H5269 study with a defined meaning of \"capital investment,\" a timing and verification mechanism, and a corrected fee cross-reference, because as drafted it would create a fee-substitute option for Category 2 retail licensees that mobile-only operators could not use.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":1599,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:48:57.165Z"},"MA-H506":{"summary":"H506 would create a PLAY Trust Fund, administered by the Commissioner of Elementary and Secondary Education, to pay for youth athletics and sports fees for K-8 public school students and to fight childhood obesity. One of its revenue sources is 1 per cent of the total excise tax collected from sports wagering operators under M.G.L. c. 23N, section 14. The bill does not change the tax rate or impose any new obligation on operators; it redirects a share of existing sports wagering tax revenue.","mechanics":["Inserts new section 2KKKKKK into M.G.L. c. 29, establishing the PLAY Trust Fund as a separate fund on the Commonwealth's books, administered by the DESE commissioner.","Permitted uses: funding K-8 public school youth athletics, paying sports-related fees that students and families cannot afford, and combating childhood obesity through youth sports.","Fund sources: legislative appropriations, gifts, grants and donations, interest, and 1 per cent of the total excise tax collected from sports wagering operators under c. 23N, section 14.","Money credited to the fund is not subject to further appropriation and does not revert to the General Fund at fiscal year end.","Requires an annual report by October 1 to the House and Senate clerks, the Joint Committee on Education and both Ways and Means committees, covering sources, expenditures, anticipated remaining revenue and the number of students and families funded, to be posted publicly on DESE's website.","No effective date, operator obligations or enforcement provisions; the bill does not expressly amend the existing distribution of sports wagering revenue in c. 23N."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states were listed. What is unusual is that the bill uses sports wagering excise revenue as a fixed-percentage earmark for a youth sports and obesity program, rather than for problem-gambling or general-fund purposes, and it does so without touching the 15 and 20 per cent rates or expressly amending the c. 23N distribution scheme. It is one of a recurring type of Massachusetts bills that treat the sports wagering tax as a funding source for unrelated programs.","watch":"Operators bear no direct cost here, but watch whether this and similar earmarks of the c. 23N, section 14 tax build pressure to raise rates in order to fund multiple new programs, and whether the bill is amended to reconcile the 1 per cent diversion with the existing c. 23N revenue distribution.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":3407,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:48:57.464Z"},"MA-H5555":{"summary":"H5555 is Massachusetts' FY2027 general appropriations act, enacted as Chapter 137 of the Acts of 2026, with a total appropriation of about $63.4 billion. In the portion of the text provided, the only gaming content is two non-tax revenue projections in Section 1B. Those cover budgeted transfers from the Massachusetts Gaming Commission and the State Lottery Commission. Nothing in the provided text changes sports wagering or gaming law.","mechanics":["Section 1B projects $372,395,024 in budgeted transfers attributed to the Massachusetts Gaming Commission for FY2027, all unrestricted, with no departmental revenues.","Section 1B projects $1,321,596,996 in budgeted transfers from the State Lottery Commission, plus $30,887 in departmental revenues.","The Section 1A tax revenue table has no separate line for sports wagering or gaming taxes. Gaming money appears only within non-tax revenue: $5,190.6M in consolidated transfers and $27,588.0M in total non-tax revenue.","The act is declared an emergency law, effective immediately for the fiscal year beginning July 1, 2026.","The text supplied is truncated partway through Section 2 (around item 0339-1003). Any Gaming Commission line items or outside sections that might touch chapters 23K or 23N were not available for review."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. On its own, the bill is an ordinary state budget. Its gaming relevance in the provided text is limited to revenue projections. Sports wagering revenue is folded into the Gaming Commission's budgeted-transfer figure rather than broken out as its own tax line, which makes it hard to isolate expected sports-betting receipts.","watch":"Obtain the full enacted Chapter 137, including the Gaming Commission line items and outside sections, which were not in the text provided, to check for any amendments to chapter 23N, changes to sports wagering tax rates or fund distributions, or new Gaming Commission assessments on operators.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Bill Text","text_chars":290055,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:49:05.392Z"},"MA-S2067":{"summary":"S2067 creates a Massachusetts Bilingual Education Trust Fund (new G.L. c.29 §2BBBBBB) to pay bilingual-educator stipends and loan repayment, and funds it partly by earmarking existing sports wagering and marijuana excise revenue. For gaming clients, the key change is Section 5, which rewrites the first sentence of G.L. c.23N §14(a). It keeps the 15% in-person and 20% mobile sports wagering rates, routes 5 points of each to the new fund, and adds a new 15% excise on adjusted gross fantasy wagering receipts of fantasy contest operators.","mechanics":["Section 5 (c.23N §14(a)) sets the in-person sports wagering excise at 15% of adjusted gross sports wagering receipts: 5% goes to the Bilingual Education Trust Fund and 10% to the Sports Wagering Fund (§17).","Mobile and digital sports wagering stays at 20%: 5% goes to the Bilingual Education Trust Fund and 15% to the Sports Wagering Fund, so the Sports Wagering Fund's share falls.","Section 5 also adds a new 15% excise on the adjusted gross fantasy wagering receipts of any person or entity offering fantasy contests as defined in §11M½, split 5% to the trust fund and 10% to the Sports Wagering Fund.","Section 6 keeps the marijuana retail excise at 10.75% but diverts 2 points to the trust fund.","The fund is not subject to further appropriation and does not revert to the General Fund. It pays $10,000 annual stipends to endorsed bilingual educators, plus $5,000, $1,000 and 5-year $1,000 add-ons, and $30,000 in loan repayment after 10 years of service.","Effective date is January 1, 2027 (Section 8). The bill was reported favorably by committee on 2025-12-22 and is now in Senate Ways and Means."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. On its own, the bill is unusual because it leaves Massachusetts' sports wagering rates unchanged and instead carves an education earmark out of existing revenue. It also folds a new 15% excise on daily fantasy sports into the sports wagering excise statute, a significant expansion of the tax base tucked inside an education-funding bill.","watch":"Watch the new 15% fantasy contest excise in Section 5(iii): it would newly tax DFS operators under c.23N §14 from January 1, 2027, and its placement in a sentence aimed at 'sports wagering operators' raises scope and definitional questions; also watch whether Ways and Means uses the earmark as a vehicle to raise the headline rates.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Bill Text","text_chars":17604,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:49:12.496Z"},"MA-H5576":{"summary":"H5576 is the House-engrossed Massachusetts economic development bond bill, declared an emergency law, that authorizes capital spending through the Executive Office of Economic Development, available until June 30, 2036. The portion of the text provided (roughly pages 1 to 23 of 225) consists almost entirely of section 2 line items and local earmarks and contains no sports wagering or gaming provisions. Any gaming-related language would have to appear in the roughly 200 pages not reproduced here.","mechanics":["Section 1: sums in section 2 are made available, in addition to prior authorizations, until June 30, 2036.","7002-8079: $25M capital grant program for private businesses building or expanding commercial, industrial or manufacturing facilities, with decarbonization, climate-resilience and job-creation criteria.","7002-8080: $75M for AI technology grants, and 7002-8082: $100M for defense-sector capital and infrastructure. Either may be administered by quasi-public agencies such as MassTech or the Life Sciences Center.","7002-8085: $25M downtown and main-street grants, including at least $200,000 for security at the 2027 Boston Calling festival.","7002-8087: local economic development earmarks, including at least $1,000,000 to reimburse businesses in the Gillette Stadium 'clean zone' in Foxborough for revenue lost during the June and July 2026 World Cup games.","Status: a conference committee was appointed on 2026-07-30, so the final text may differ from the House version."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. On its own, this is a large omnibus bond and earmark bill. The provided excerpt never mentions wagering, licensing, or the Gaming Commission. The only sports-adjacent item is the World Cup clean-zone revenue reimbursement at Gillette Stadium, which concerns local merchants rather than betting.","watch":"Because Massachusetts economic development and conference-committee vehicles can pick up unrelated policy riders, review the remaining roughly 200 pages and the eventual conference report for any amendments to G.L. c. 23K or c. 23N, including tax rates, advertising limits or Gaming Commission authority.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":205308,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:49:13.222Z"},"MA-H5258":{"summary":"H5258 is a broad responsible-gaming package. It imposes advertising restrictions and a mandatory standardized problem-gambling disclaimer on Gaming Commission licensees under chapters 23K and 23N, on fantasy contest operators and on the Lottery. It creates an Attorney General self-exclusion list for fantasy contests that is cross-linked to the casino and sports wagering lists, and requires casinos and sports wagering operators to hand customer tracking data to the Commission for anonymized addiction research. It also orders a Commission study of stricter sports betting ad rules, including a whistle-to-whistle ban, and creates a special commission on problem gambling screening and treatment.","mechanics":["Advertising (new 23K §72, also applied to fantasy operators by new ch. 12 §11M3/4(f)): 23K and 23N licensees may not advertise, brand, or sign sponsorships or celebrity endorsements the Commission deems to appeal to under-21s. TV, radio, internet, billboard and print ads are barred unless at least 85% of the audience is reasonably expected to be 21+, based on reliable audience composition data, with penalties set by Commission regulation. A single standardized disclaimer with the MA Gambling Helpline number is required in all licensee and contractor advertising (23K §3(z)), and the same disclaimer applies to fantasy operators and Lottery ads.","Excluded persons: sports wagering operators, qualified gaming entities, casinos and fantasy operators may not market to anyone on an excluded list in any form, including mail, app notifications, digital ads and ads placed in third parties' advertising. They must use 'all technological means available' (cookies, IP addresses, digital fingerprints) to keep ads from reaching those people (23N §13(e)(4); 23K §45(g)). The Commission must offer online and phone self-exclusion, and one request can enroll a person on the casino, sports wagering and new AG fantasy lists together.","Data mandate: casinos (new 23K §73) and sports wagering operators (23N §4(d)(4)) must supply the Commission with customer tracking data from loyalty programs, player tracking, player cards, online transactions and any other system. The Commission must contract a nonprofit to build an anonymizing system by the earlier of June 30, 2027 or six months after the act takes effect. Anonymized data goes to a research facility for qualified researchers, and the Commission must make a good-faith effort to implement the resulting harm-minimization recommendations.","Casino cashless wagering (rewritten 23K §29): per-bet, hourly, daily, weekly and monthly bet and loss limits must be available, and players may raise limits only once per 24 hours. Players receive mailed monthly statements and daily email/app statements (opt-out allowed), are prompted to set spending limits and enroll in a play management program such as Play-My-Way, and casinos must report patron losses by zip code annually.","Other 23N changes: regulations requiring a responsible gaming module for bettors (§4(d)(5)). Adds 'agents, promoters' to §11. Lets the investigations and enforcement bureau share applicant and licensee information with law enforcement, other gaming regulators and the FBI (§16(j)). Expands the §23 research mandate to cover suicide and self-harm among gamblers.","Studies: a Commission study (new 23N §20A) of stricter sports betting ad restrictions, an unfair/deceptive marketing definition, a complaint helpline, a whistle-to-whistle ban and First Amendment issues, due December 31, 2027. Health insurer, GIC and MassHealth screening provisions are amended to include gambling questions. A special commission on problem gambling must report first by June 30, 2028."],"issue_tags":["advertising","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Massachusetts goes considerably further than the narrower New Jersey consumer-protection bills. NJ A 4002/S3419 cover account-limit disclosure, NJ A 4003 covers incentives to people using responsible gaming tools, and NJ AR150 is only a non-binding resolution urging bans on targeting problem gamblers; H5258 instead imposes hard statutory bans on marketing to excluded persons and an 85% adult-audience threshold. Its cross-linked self-exclusion lists and mandated research overlap with Maryland HB518 (Commission-run voluntary exclusion list) and MD HB1409/SB862 (prevalence studies). None of those listed bills requires operators to turn over raw player tracking data for anonymized academic research, which is the most unusual feature here.","watch":"Operators should watch the 23N §4(d)(4) customer-data turnover mandate, the open-ended 'all technological means available' duty to keep ads away from excluded persons, the 85% adult-audience test and under-21 'appeal' standard that could restrict sponsorships and celebrity endorsements, and the §20A study that could lay the groundwork for a whistle-to-whistle ad ban.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Bill Text","text_chars":32558,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:49:14.195Z"},"MA-S234":{"summary":"S234 reorganizes Massachusetts horse racing law by creating a three-member State Racing Board inside the Massachusetts Gaming Commission. It moves the racing and simulcasting statutes (currently chapters 128A and 128C) into new chapters 23K ¼ and 23K ½, and rewrites the Race Horse Development Fund and the thoroughbred and standardbred capital improvement trust funds. It also restates the definition of illegal gaming in G.L. c. 4, §7, Tenth, and lets the Commission grant simulcasting licenses to casino gaming establishments. The text supplied is cut off partway through new c. 23K ¼, §9, and the bill was replaced on 2026-01-05 by a new draft, S2820.","mechanics":["New c. 23K §7A creates a gubernatorially appointed three-member racing board. The chair must have gaming or racing regulatory or industry experience and one member must be an equine veterinarian. The board administers c. 23K ¼ and c. 23K ½ and acts as the host racing and off-track betting commission under the Interstate Horseracing Act (15 U.S.C. 3001). The Commission keeps appellate review over board decisions on pari-mutuel wagering and simulcasting (§7(a)).","Rewritten §7(b) lets the Commission grant simulcasting licenses to gaming establishments under c. 23K ½ §§8–9, after considering the impact on licensed racing facilities.","Race Horse Development Fund distributions (§60): 80% to horsemen's purse accounts, 16% to state breeding programs and 4% to health and pension benefits. Shifts in the thoroughbred/standardbred split are capped at 10 percentage points per year.","Capital improvement trust funds (§§60A–60B): thoroughbred tracks pay the daily breaks less $100,000, which goes to the council on compulsive gambling. Harness tracks pay the breaks plus 2% of exotic handle. Unspent balances sweep to the Race Horse Development Fund each December 31.","Racing meeting licensing (new c. 23K ¼): applications due October 1, a cap of 200 racing days each for running and harness racing, one licensee per county, and a $1.25M surety bond ($250,000 for fairs). Board approval is required for transfers over 10%, and 85% of employees must be Massachusetts residents.","Takeout for running horse wagering is capped at 19% on straight wagers and 26% on exotics. From that, 0.75% goes to the board, 1% to the Massachusetts Thoroughbred Breeders Association and 8.5% or 9.5% to purses. Account wagering and simulcast wagering are carved out of the on-track-only rule (§9(b))."],"issue_tags":["licensing","tax"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"None of the listed bills restructure pari-mutuel racing regulation. The closest are Illinois HB2880 and HB5035, which only loosen pre-employment restrictions on Illinois Racing Board staff, whereas S234 builds a new racing board, a licensing scheme and fund system from the ground up. Most of the listed bills, such as Alaska HB145/SB194, Georgia HB 686 and Hawaii HB 1308, authorize and tax sports wagering, which S234 does not touch except through the illegal gaming definition. Its distinctive feature is folding racing oversight into the Gaming Commission structure while giving casinos a route to simulcasting licenses.","watch":"Check whether S2820 keeps S234's illegal gaming carve-out for sports wagering 'conducted under chapter 10' (the lottery chapter) rather than chapter 23N, since that apparent drafting error could cast doubt on the exemption for licensed sports wagering, and also watch the casino simulcasting and account wagering provisions for competitive effects.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":144740,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:49:26.899Z"},"MA-S294":{"summary":"S294 would let a Massachusetts Category 2 sports wagering licensee (the retail, in-person license class under G.L. c. 23N) make a capital investment of at least $7,500,000 in a sports wagering facility instead of paying the fee referenced in \"section 5(f).\" It does this by striking the last clause of the \"Category 2 license\" definition in G.L. c. 23N, §3 and inserting the investment option in its place. On 2025-12-18 it was sent to a study order (S2885), which usually means it is dead for the 2025-2026 session.","mechanics":["Amends the definition of \"Category 2 license\" in G.L. c. 23N, §3 by replacing its last clause.","Gives Category 2 licensees an option: make a capital investment of not less than $7,500,000 in a sports wagering facility \"in lieu of the fee in section 5(f).\"","Covers only Category 2 (retail) licensees, not Category 1 casino licensees or Category 3 mobile operators.","Does not define \"capital investment,\" set a deadline for making it, provide for verification by the Gaming Commission, or include clawback or enforcement terms.","Has no effective date or transition rule for licensees that have already paid the fee.","Referred to Economic Development and Emerging Technologies; accompanied a study order on 2025-12-18 (S2885)."],"issue_tags":["licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. What is unusual is that it turns a fixed license fee into an optional in-kind investment obligation, a rare approach in sports betting licensing. The text is also thin: it cites \"section 5(f)\" for the fee without saying what that fee is, and it has no mechanism for the Gaming Commission to approve or audit the investment.","watch":"Watch whether the \"section 5(f)\" cross-reference actually reaches the Category 2 license fee, and whether any revived version adds Commission verification, timing, or clawback terms that would determine if the $7.5 million investment option is real relief or a compliance trap for retail licensees.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":1654,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:49:29.527Z"},"MA-S2820":{"summary":"S2820 overhauls Massachusetts horse racing regulation by creating a three-member state racing board within the Gaming Commission (new G.L. c. 23K s. 7A) to administer live racing, pari-mutuel wagering and simulcasting, and by recodifying the old chapters 128A and 128C as new chapters 23K ¼ (horse racing meetings) and 23K ½ (simulcasting). It restructures the Race Horse Development Fund and creates thoroughbred and standardbred capital improvement trust funds financed by breaks and wagering set-asides. It also rewrites the c. 4 s. 7 definition of illegal gaming to carve out lottery, chapter 23K casino gaming, sports wagering conducted under chapter 10, pari-mutuel racing, bingo, charitable gaming and fantasy contests.","mechanics":["Racing board (s. 7A): three full-time governor appointees with five-year terms and a 15-year cap, conflict-of-interest bars, and one member required to be an equine veterinarian. The board serves as host racing and off-track betting commission under the Interstate Horseracing Act, and the Gaming Commission hears appeals of board decisions (s. 7).","Race Horse Development Fund (s. 60): distributions split 80% to purse accounts, 16% to breeding programs and 4% to horsemen's health and pension benefits. The thoroughbred/standardbred split cannot shift more than 10 percentage points a year.","Capital improvement trust funds (ss. 60A, 60B): thoroughbred tracks pay daily breaks less $100,000, with that $100,000 going, subject to appropriation, to the Massachusetts council on compulsive gambling. Harness tracks pay breaks plus 2% of exotic handle. Unspent balances revert to the Race Horse Development Fund each December 31.","Racing licensing (c. 23K ¼ ss. 2-4): applications due October 1. Caps of 200 racing days a year each for running and harness meetings, one licensee per county except fairs, and a $1,250,000 surety bond ($250,000 for fairs). The daily fee is the greater of $300 or 0.075% of average daily handle, with a cap of $100 a day for fairs.","Pari-mutuel takeout (c. 23K ¼ s. 9): capped at 19% on straight running-horse wagers and 26% on exotics. From the takeout: 0.75% to the board, 1% to the Massachusetts Thoroughbred Breeders Association, and 8.5% (straight) or 9.5% (exotic) to purses. Simulcast and account wagering under c. 23K ½ are exempted from the track-only wagering restriction.","Other provisions: transfers of more than 10% of a licensee require board approval, with notice only for publicly held companies (s. 7). 85% of track employees must be Massachusetts residents of two years (s. 8). Compulsive gambling helpline notices must be posted (s. 3(m)). The supplied text is truncated partway through s. 9."],"issue_tags":["licensing","tax","legalize","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. The bill is unusual for permanently relocating racing law into the Gaming Commission's chapter 23K structure while placing it under a separate racing board. The Commission keeps simulcasting licensing for casinos and appellate review over the board.","watch":"Watch the rewritten illegal-gaming definition in c. 4 s. 7, which carves out only 'sports wagering conducted under chapter 10' (the lottery chapter) rather than the existing chapter 23N sports wagering framework, and could unintentionally leave chapter 23N operators outside the exclusion; also watch whether the simulcasting and account-wagering provisions of c. 23K ½, cut off in the supplied text, expand online racing wagers that compete with or complement sportsbook offerings.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Bill Text","text_chars":144772,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:49:30.530Z"},"MA-S235":{"summary":"S235 would create a new chapter 23O, the Massachusetts Internet Gaming Act, legalizing online casino games (poker, blackjack, craps, roulette, slots, live dealer and other Commission-approved games) under Massachusetts Gaming Commission regulation. It sets up two license categories, imposes a 20% excise on adjusted gross internet gaming receipts, and phases down the promotional-credit deduction starting in 2028. The bill was sent to a study order (S2996) on 2026-03-09, which in Massachusetts practice usually means it is effectively shelved for the session.","mechanics":["Licensing (s.6): Category 1 licenses go to existing chapter 23K casino licensees, with up to two platforms or skins each, run through up to two licensed platform providers, plus an optional second brand for poker. Category 2 licenses go to non-casino entities, capped at 4, with one platform each; if more than 4 apply, the Commission picks under 8 competitive factors, including projected tax revenue and history of doing business in Massachusetts.","Fees: $200,000 nonrefundable processing fee (s.7). $5,000,000 initial operator license for a 5-year term and $5,000,000 renewal (s.6(g)). $1,000,000 temporary license for 'qualified gaming entities' (casino licensees, or operators licensed for iGaming in 3+ US states), with simultaneous launch for those applying within 30 days. Transfer review fee capped at $200,000. $100 occupational license fee. A $1,000,000 annual assessment, shared among operators, goes to the Public Health Trust Fund (s.15(e)).","Tax (s.14): 20% excise on adjusted gross internet gaming receipts, in lieu of all other state and local taxes and fees. Monthly returns are due by the 15th. Negative AGR carries forward but not back. Promotional credits are fully deductible until 1/1/2028, then capped as a share of monthly handle: 2.5% through 6/30/2029, stepping down to 1.75% from 7/1/2031, with no carryover of excess credits.","Consumer protection and advertising (s.4(c)-(d), s.13): Commission must ban deceptive ads, ads targeting people under 21, and pop-ups or texts to self-excluded persons. It must require responsible-gaming programs, an annual problem gaming plan, account closure on request, withdrawals by the deposit method without solicitation, no credit lines, and display of 1-800-GAMBLER. Minimum age is 21. A self-exclusion list is created, and the Commission may extend chapter 23N sports-wagering self-exclusions to iGaming.","Integrity (s.11-12): operators must run monitoring systems and report suspicious wagering. They must keep wager-level records, including IP location and 3 years of abnormal-activity records, and provide anonymized account data to regulators by default, with a right to appeal requests for personally identifiable information. Insider and proxy betting is barred.","Interstate and structural provisions: the Commission must pursue multistate reciprocal agreements. Live dealer studios may be located outside Massachusetts. A single patron account may be shared with the operator's sports wagering product. Intermediate data routing does not determine wager location, citing UIGEA."],"issue_tags":["legalize","licensing","tax","advertising","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"The closest analogues on the list are IL HB3080 and HB4797 (Internet Gaming Act), which likewise authorize online casino gaming. MA S235 is distinctive in opening up to 4 standalone Category 2 licenses for non-casino operators with no tether required (s.6(b)(3)), and in importing a scheduled promotional-deduction phase-down into an iGaming tax. Most other listed bills are sports-wagering authorizations or amendments (AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, HI HB 1308/SB1569) or narrower consumer-protection bills. S235's advertising and withdrawal-friction rules partly overlap with CT HB05229, though CT's AI-targeting and campus-advertising restrictions have no counterpart here.","watch":"Watch whether any revived version keeps the 4-license Category 2 cap with its revenue-projection selection criteria, the 2028 promotional-deduction cap (s.14(b)(3) mistakenly refers to a 'mobile sports betting operator'), and the Section 2 rewrite of chapter 4 §7 clause Tenth, which drops fantasy contests and greyhound racing from the 'illegal gaming' exclusions.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":73626,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:49:34.613Z"},"MA-S4":{"summary":"S.4 is the Senate Ways and Means Committee's FY 2027 general appropriations bill, reported as a strike-all amendment to House Bill 5501. It recommends about $63.3 billion in total spending. The excerpt provided contains only the chair's letter, the executive summary and the reporting page, and none of it addresses sports wagering, casinos, the Gaming Commission or gaming revenue.","mechanics":["Total recommended spending is $63,295,391,957: $62,459,094,416 in direct appropriations plus $836,297,541 in retained revenue authorization.","Built on a consensus tax revenue estimate of $42.2 billion for FY 2027, excluding the Fair Share surtax, which is a 2.4 percent increase over the FY 2026 benchmark.","The committee states the budget does not raise taxes or fees and does not draw on the Stabilization Fund. It deposits an additional $51 million, bringing the fund to about $8.2 billion.","Allocates $2.7 billion in Fair Share surtax revenue to education and transportation.","Major local-aid lines are $7.66 billion for Chapter 70 school aid and $1.376 billion in Unrestricted General Government Aid (UGGA).","The excerpt includes no gaming or wagering line items or outside sections. Last action on 2026-05-19 was the rejection of Amendment #304 (Dooner), whose content is not in the text provided."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No other states' bills are listed for comparison. What sets this bill apart is that it is an omnibus state budget rather than gaming legislation. Any gaming content would sit in line items or outside sections that are not in the excerpt provided.","watch":"Review the full line-item and outside-section text, and the floor amendments such as #304, for any change to sports wagering tax rates, Gaming Commission funding or the distribution of gaming revenue, despite the committee's statement that the budget raises no taxes or fees.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":262297,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:49:40.254Z"},"MD-HB1078":{"summary":"HB1078 (cross-filed with SB 883) makes the Carroll Park Soccer Stadium and Facility in Baltimore City an eligible Maryland Stadium Authority project, caps Authority bonds for it at $216.6 million, and creates a nonlapsing Carroll Park Soccer Stadium and Facility Fund to service that debt. Starting in fiscal 2028, the Comptroller must pay the stadium's annual debt service out of state sports wagering proceeds before those proceeds go to the Blueprint for Maryland's Future Fund or the General Fund. The bill does not change what operators pay or retain.","mechanics":["Debt service is the first claim on sports wagering money: new State Government §9-1E-12(b)(2)(iii) requires the Comptroller, for FY2028 onward, to send the annual principal and interest on the stadium bonds to the Carroll Park Fund before making the Blueprint distribution and the 5% mobile General Fund carve-out. Econ. Dev. §10-646.5(d) mirrors this.","Operator retention is unchanged: Class A and B facility licensees keep 85% and mobile licensees keep 80% under §9-1E-12(b)(1). The diversion comes entirely from the state's share.","Bonding is capped at $216,600,000 outstanding (§10-628(c)(1)(xii)). Fund revenues can be pledged with a lien that attaches immediately under §10-634.","Bonds cannot be issued until the Authority and United Partnership sign a written operating agreement (§10-646.5(e)).","For each bond issue, the Authority must give the legislature's fiscal committees a comprehensive financing plan at least 45 days in advance and obtain Board of Public Works approval (§10-646.5(b)-(c)).","The Fund keeps its own interest earnings. The bill takes effect July 1, 2026."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"Unlike IL HB3159, which adds a new 2% levy on adjusted gross sports wagering receipts for fire and EMS departments, MD HB1078 imposes no new charge on operators. Instead it re-sequences how the state's existing share is distributed, so a stadium's debt service is paid ahead of education funding. It is closer in spirit to IA SSB 1157, which ties gambling revenue to tourism and sports infrastructure, and to KS HB 2466's sports authority and stadium financing. However, MD HB1078 is unusual in pledging sports wagering proceeds directly as the repayment source for up to $216.6 million in bonds for a single soccer stadium.","watch":"Operators' retention is untouched, but putting up to $216.6 million of bond debt service ahead of the Blueprint distribution creates a long-term claim on sports wagering revenue, which could fuel future pressure to raise Maryland's sports wagering tax rates to make the Blueprint whole.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":21464,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:49:45.203Z"},"MA-S302":{"summary":"S302 would substantially tighten Massachusetts' sports wagering law (G.L. c. 23N). It would remove in-play and proposition bets from authorized sports wagering, require affordability checks above set wagering thresholds, ban percentage-of-wager or deposit compensation, and raise the 20 per cent tax figure in section 14 to 51 per cent. It would also mandate anonymized player-data reporting for research and bring specified betting promotions within the c. 93A unfair and deceptive practices standard.","mechanics":["Section 1 amends c. 23N §3 so that sports wagering includes neither in-play bets nor proposition bets, leaving straight bets (and other listed types) authorized.","Section 4 amends §11 so that no person may wager more than $1,000 per day or $10,000 per month with an operator unless the operator has done an affordability assessment and found the amounts do not exceed 15% of the bettor's available bank balance.","Section 5 adds §11(l), barring operators, directors, officers, owners, employees, affiliates and subcontractors from receiving compensation based on any percentage of a customer's wagers or deposits, which reaches revenue-share affiliate deals.","Sections 6 and 7 replace the figure \"20\" in §14 (line 4) with \"51\", which on its face raises the 20% sports wagering tax rate to 51%, and double a $1,000,000 figure in §15 (line 37) to $2,000,000.","Section 2 adds \"advertising related to sports wagering during a televised sporting event\" to the enumerated list in §4. Section 10 adds c. 93A §2(d), applying the unfair and deceptive acts standard to advertising of bonus promotions, same-game parlays, odds boosts, reload bonuses and risk-free or no-sweat free bets.","Sections 8 and 9 add suicide and self-harm among gamblers to the §23 research mandate. They also require mobile operators to supply annual customer tracking data, including frequency, length, speed and time of play, amounts wagered, and whether each wager was in-game or a parlay. The data would be anonymized through a nonprofit research entity and made available to qualified researchers."],"issue_tags":["tax","prop","advertising"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. On its own terms, S302 is unusual in combining several measures in one bill: a ban on in-play and prop betting, a tax hike to 51% that would be among the highest in the country, mandatory bank-balance-based affordability checks, and a ban on revenue-share compensation. Using c. 93A to police specific promotional products, including same-game parlays and odds boosts, also goes further than typical advertising restrictions because it exposes operators to consumer protection enforcement.","watch":"Watch Senate Ways and Means, now that the committee has reported the bill favorably, for whether the 51% tax figure, the in-play and prop ban, or the affordability-check and revenue-share prohibitions survive. Any of these would sharply change mobile operator economics, affiliate arrangements and product offerings in Massachusetts.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Bill Text","text_chars":6158,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:49:47.060Z"},"MD-HB1120":{"summary":"HB1120 (Chapter 492) lets a member of the U.S. Foreign Service, or the spouse of one, who relocates to Maryland because of an assignment or detail and holds a license from another state, practise in Maryland under that out-of-state license for the length of the assignment. It does this by adding new State Government Subtitle 10-14A and inserting cross-references to § 10-14A-03 into dozens of occupational licensing statutes. The gaming hook is that the cross-reference is also added to the casino (video lottery and table games) licensing requirement in § 9-1A-06(a) and (c)(1) and to the sports wagering licensing requirement in § 9-1E-05(a).","mechanics":["New § 10-14A-03(a) sets five conditions for practising under another state's license for the duration of the assignment: (1) give the licensing authority a copy of the assignment or detail notification; (2) stay in good standing with the issuing regulator; (3) stay in good standing in all states where a license of similar scope in the same discipline is or was held; (4) have actively used the license in the 2 years before relocating; and (5) comply with the licensing authority's practice, discipline and continuing education standards.","Under § 10-14A-03(b) and § 10-14A-04, practice is limited to the discipline and scope allowed in the issuing state. The licensing authority may not require a Maryland license unless the person seeks a different discipline or scope. The individual is subject to the same disciplinary authority as a Maryland licensee.","§ 9-1E-05(a) now opens with 'Except as provided in § 10-14A-03.' The licensing requirement it qualifies covers sports wagering operators, online operators, suppliers and service providers, and individuals directly employed in the operation of sports wagering.","The same carve-out is added to § 9-1A-06(a), which covers video lottery operators, manufacturers, service providers and video lottery employees. It is also added to § 9-1A-06(c)(1), which bars a casino licensee from employing an unlicensed individual as a video lottery employee.","§ 10-14A-02 excludes occupations covered by an interstate compact and the practice of law. 'License' is defined broadly in § 10-14A-01 to include any permit, certification, registration or other authorization needed for an occupation or profession.","§ 10-14A-05 allows each licensing authority, which would include the Maryland Lottery and Gaming Control Commission, to adopt implementing regulations. The supplied text is truncated, so it does not show an effective date."],"issue_tags":["licensing"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills deals with licensing portability. The closest gaming-licensing items are Illinois HB2880/HB5035, which loosen pre-employment restrictions on Racing Board and Gaming Board staff, and Alaska HB145/SB194, which tie mobile sports wagering licensure to criminal history checks. Neither creates a reciprocity route. What stands out in HB1120 is that a general military-style license portability law is written to reach gaming and sports wagering licenses expressly, rather than exempting them as many universal-recognition statutes do. In principle, that lets an out-of-state gaming employee license substitute for a Maryland occupational license.","watch":"Operators should watch whether the Lottery and Gaming Control Commission uses its § 10-14A-05 rulemaking power to require background or suitability checks and notice procedures for Foreign Service members and spouses working under out-of-state gaming licenses, since the statute itself requires only good standing and a copy of the assignment notice before a licensee may employ them in roles that otherwise need a Maryland occupational license.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":163691,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:49:55.870Z"},"MD-HB1226":{"summary":"HB1226 creates a new State Government Article Subtitle 1F prohibiting anyone from knowingly operating, offering, promoting, or knowingly supporting (directly or indirectly) illegal online gambling in Maryland, with \"illegal\" defined as anything not expressly authorized by law and \"online gambling\" expressly including online casino games and dual-currency sweepstakes games. It gives the Attorney General's Consumer Protection Division and prosecuting attorneys cease-and-desist, injunction, and ex parte powers against operators, platform providers, payment processors, and other supporters. Violations are also treated as unfair, abusive, or deceptive trade practices under Commercial Law §13-301. Licensed online sports wagering and fantasy operators under Subtitles 1D and 1E are exempt.","mechanics":["Penalties (§9-1F-06): operating/promoting is a misdemeanor, up to 3 years and/or $50,000 first offense and $100,000 each subsequent offense; supporting carries fines up to $25,000 first and $50,000 subsequent. Each wager and each promotion is a separate violation, and courts must order forfeiture of profits and gross receipts.","Mandatory licence consequence (§9-1F-06(D)): the Lottery and Gaming Control Commission must revoke any gaming licence held by a person found to have violated §9-1F-03, with permanent ineligibility. §9-1F-12 separately lets the Commission deny, suspend, condition, or limit licences for noncompliance.","Cease-and-desist regime (§9-1F-04, -09): operators get 10 days to geoblock Maryland. Platform providers, payment processors, geolocation providers, content suppliers, promoters, celebrity endorsers, media affiliates, and licensed entities get 30 days to comply. Orders are prima facie evidence of knowledge, service by publication is allowed without locating the operator, and the AG publishes a public list of operators and URLs.","Intermediaries: financial transaction providers may not knowingly process illegal gambling transactions and are deemed to have constructive knowledge of AG-listed sites (§9-1F-07). Platform providers must obtain a certified copy of the operator's licence or Commission confirmation before hosting any online gambling advertisement (§9-1F-08(B)). Both get tort immunity for voluntary blocking (§9-1F-09).","Court relief (§9-1F-10): TROs, injunctions, account freezes, and ex parte injunctions with a hearing within 30 days. A violation is conclusively irreparable harm. Fines for violating orders are $50,000 then $100,000, per day for platforms and per transaction or act of support for supporters.","Long-arm jurisdiction over out-of-state operators, platforms, and supporters; the Division has rulemaking power; liberal construction is mandated (§9-1F-15). Effective October 1, 2026; cross-filed with SB 652."],"issue_tags":["sweeps","integrity","advertising","legalize"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike the authorization bills on the list (AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, HI HB 1308/SB1569, and the Illinois iGaming bills HB3080/HB4797), HB1226 authorizes nothing and is purely an enforcement statute aimed at offshore and sweepstakes operators. Its closest analogue is FL S1580/H0189, which also tighten illegal-gambling penalties and address Internet gambling. Maryland goes further by imposing duties and per-transaction fines on payment processors, hosting platforms, geolocation vendors, and celebrity endorsers. It does not address prediction markets, unlike CT HB05038 or IL HB5059/HB5142.","watch":"Licensed sportsbooks are exempt only for activity under Subtitles 1D/1E, so multi-product licensees, their affiliates, vendors, and media partners face mandatory permanent licence revocation under §9-1F-06(D) if found to have even indirectly supported unauthorized online casino or sweepstakes activity. Advertising partners will also now require licence verification under §9-1F-08(B) before running any online gambling ad.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Engrossed","text_chars":24629,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:49:58.509Z"},"MD-HB1240":{"summary":"HB1240 adds historical horse races to Maryland's statutory definition of \"sporting event\" and lets certain retail sports wagering facility licensees (casinos, racetracks, the Timonium fairgrounds licensee and satellite simulcast facilities) offer pari-mutuel wagering on them through Commission-approved terminals, capped at 4,000 statewide. Mobile licensees and most Class B licensees are excluded. Historical horse racing proceeds get their own split modeled on video lottery terminal (VLT) distributions, and the Act takes effect January 1, 2027 only if voters approve it at the November 2026 general election.","mechanics":["Coverage (new § 9-1E-09(f)): Class A-1/A-2 licensees may offer it at video lottery facilities; Class A-2 horse racing licensees at thoroughbred or standardbred race courses; § 11-524 licensees at the Timonium fairgrounds; and satellite simulcast facility owners or lessees. All other facility licensees and all mobile sports wagering licensees are barred.","Format (new § 9-1E-09.1): wagering must be pari-mutuel and player-versus-player, with no wagering against the house and no house retention that depends on any race outcome. Optional player-funded pools may cover minus pools. Races of any breed may be used.","Terminal and display rules: only Commission-approved terminals, with a statewide cap of 4,000. Race identity (location, date, horse and jockey or driver names) must be hidden until the wager is placed, while past-performance data current as of the race date must be shown. Results and a replay must follow the wager.","Other limits: minimum wager is 10 cents, and the minimum payout may not be less than the amount wagered. Wagering is allowed only in Commission-approved designated areas, on approved days and hours, and with written approval of all wager types and pool explanations.","Revenue (§ 9-1E-12(c)): VLT operators retain the same percentage they retain on VLT proceeds under § 9-1A-27; non-VLT licensees retain 60%, compared with 85% on ordinary retail sports wagering. The State share funds local impact grants, the agency, the Purse Dedication Account and the Small, Minority, and Women-Owned Businesses Account at VLT ratios, then 5% to the General Fund, with the remainder to the Blueprint for Maryland's Future Fund.","Referendum (Section 2): the Act goes to a November 2026 referendum under Art. XIX, § 1(e). If approved it takes effect January 1, 2027; if rejected it is void. Section 3 takes effect July 1, 2026 solely to enable the referendum. The bill is a reintroduction of HB 1048/25 and is cross-filed with SB 639."],"issue_tags":["legalize","tax","licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"None of the listed bills addresses historical horse racing. The Alaska (HB145/SB194), Georgia (HB 686, HR450, SR131) and Hawaii (HB 1308, HB 2570, SB1569) bills would authorize sports wagering from scratch, and Illinois HB5142 expands the sports wagering definition to cover prediction markets. HB1240 instead expands an existing regime to a slot-like pari-mutuel product, routed through the sports wagering statute but taxed and distributed on the VLT model. It is unusual in barring the product online, capping terminals at 4,000 and requiring voter approval as an expansion of commercial gaming.","watch":"Operators should note that the bill never defines \"historical horse race,\" that mobile and most Class B licensees are excluded, and that the Commission controls terminal allocation within the 4,000 cap and approval of every pool and wager type, all of which will shape who captures the market if the November 2026 referendum passes.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":23838,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:50:07.917Z"},"MD-HB1409":{"summary":"HB1409 raises Maryland's effective tax on sports wagering and fantasy competitions by one percentage point and earmarks that new 1% for the Problem Gambling Fund. It also requires the Maryland Department of Health to run a separate prevalence study, with five-year replications, on problem and pathological mobile gambling, and requires annual reporting on how the Fund is spent. It takes effect July 1, 2026.","mechanics":["Retention cuts in State Gov't § 9-1E-12(b)(1): Class A-1/A-2 and B-1/B-2 retail licensees drop from 85% to 84% of proceeds, and mobile licensees from 80% to 79%. This moves the effective rates from 15% to 16% retail and from 20% to 21% online.","Fantasy competition operators drop from 85% to 84% retained under § 9-1D-04(a). The Commission must send 1% of fantasy proceeds to the Problem Gambling Fund, with the rest still going to the Blueprint for Maryland's Future Fund.","New § 9-1E-12(b)(2)(ii)1 directs the Commission to distribute 1% of 'all sports wagering proceeds' to the Problem Gambling Fund (§ 9-1A-33). The existing 5% General Fund carve-out is reworded from proceeds 'attributable to mobile' to proceeds 'from online' sports wagering.","Health-General § 19-804 splits the prevalence study into in-person and mobile tracks. The initial mobile study must be completed by July 1, 2031 by an independent researcher using current diagnostic criteria, with replications at least every 5 years.","New § 9-1A-33(b)(7) requires the Secretary of Health to report to the General Assembly by November 1, 2027 and annually after that on Problem Gambling Fund spending on treatment and prevention.","Effective date is July 1, 2026. The bill is cross-filed with SB 862 and reintroduces HB 700 of 2025."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike the Hawaii bills (HB 1308, HB 2570, SB1569), which create a problem-gambling fund inside a brand-new legalization and licensing framework, HB1409 funds an existing fund by shaving operator retention in a mature market. Its earmark-via-rate-increase approach most resembles Illinois HB3159, which adds a 2% AGR levy dedicated to fire and EMS. Illinois HB1451, HB2580 and SB 118 instead expand DHS gambling-disorder education programs without touching tax rates. The dedicated mobile-gambling prevalence study is unusual among the listed bills.","watch":"Operators should watch whether the 1-point retention cut (to 79% mobile, 84% retail and fantasy) survives or is amended, and whether the 1% to the Problem Gambling Fund is read as an additional levy or as a carve-out from state proceeds. The drafting of § 9-1E-12(b)(2)(ii)1 ('1% of all sports wagering proceeds') is ambiguous on that point, and a mobile-gambling prevalence study could later be used to justify further responsible-gaming mandates or rate increases.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":6966,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:50:11.460Z"},"MD-HB1431":{"summary":"HB1431 amends State Government Article § 9-1E-15(h)(4) to require Maryland's Sports Wagering Application Review Commission (SWARC) to accept and consider applications for sports wagering licenses submitted before January 1, 2028, subject to the limitations in § 9-1E-06. In effect, it keeps the licensing window open for new sports wagering facility and mobile applicants through the end of 2027. It sits alongside SWARC's existing diversity and minority-business outreach mandates.","mechanics":["Adds new item (iii) to § 9-1E-15(h)(4): SWARC 'shall' accept and consider an application for a sports wagering license submitted before January 1, 2028.","The purpose clause covers both sports wagering facility licenses and mobile sports wagering licenses.","The new duty is expressly subject to the limitations under § 9-1E-06, the existing licensing provisions, so it does not on its face lift any statutory license-number caps.","Existing duties are retained unchanged: pursuing racial, ethnic and gender diversity in awards, and encouraging MBE and small, minority or women-owned applicants.","Takes effect July 1, 2026. The bill is a reintroduction of HB 526 from the 2024 session and is assigned to House Ways and Means, with a hearing on March 5."],"issue_tags":["licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike Illinois HB2878 and HB5034, which raise master sports wagering license fees to a $250,000 application fee plus a $15,000,000 license fee and strip provisions on the number of online licenses, HB1431 changes no fees or caps and only fixes the application window. It is likewise far narrower than the authorizing frameworks in Alaska HB145/SB194, Hawaii HB 1308/HB 2570/SB1569 and Georgia HB 686, which build licensing regimes from scratch. It is a single-sentence procedural fix to an existing mature market.","watch":"Operators should watch whether the guaranteed application window through 2027 brings in new mobile or facility competitors under the remaining § 9-1E-06 capacity, and whether amendments in Ways and Means alter those caps or attach equity-related conditions to late applicants.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":1950,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:50:11.499Z"},"MD-HB1343":{"summary":"HB1343 would create a new Subtitle 1F in the State Government Article authorizing the State Lottery and Gaming Control Commission to license online casino-style gaming, including live dealer games from in-state studios. It sets the state's revenue share and how it is distributed, adds social equity ownership rules, consumer protection and labor peace requirements, and authorizes multistate compacts. The Act is subject to a statewide referendum. Revenue distributions begin in fiscal 2028, and a Video Lottery Facility Employee Displacement Fund is created for displaced casino workers.","mechanics":["Revenue split (§9-1F-05(b)): licensees keep 80% of live dealer proceeds and 45% of all other iGaming proceeds, so the effective state take is 20% and 55%. 'Proceeds' deduct a tiered share of redeemed free play and promotional credits for the first 5 years only, falling from 35% (proceeds under $4M) to 20% (over $12M).","Eligible licensees (§9-1F-03(b)): video lottery operators; sports wagering facility licensees under §9-1E-06(a)(2)(i)2 or 3; and Maryland-headquartered applicants. The Maryland applicants need 10+ years of in-state HQ, 250+ in-state employees, a prior Commission qualification for a 5%+ VLT equity stake (2018-2025), a Maryland brand (waivable after 1 year), and a $5M commitment to a live gaming or film studio.","Social equity licensing: video lottery operators can get an extra license if partnered with a 33% social equity joint venture. Five more licenses (plus any unclaimed VLT licenses) go out in two competitive rounds, the first limited to applicants with 33% social equity ownership. All licensees need at least 5% social equity ownership or a qualifying profit-sharing plan with nonmanagement employees.","Licensing terms: $1M initial license fee, which may be paid in installments, and a 5-year term. Renewal costs 1% of average annual retained proceeds over the prior 3 years. Each licensee may contract with only one Internet gaming operator (§9-1F-02(a)(2)), and ownership cannot be transferred for 3 years.","Supplier and operator rules: no license if an affiliate knowingly takes revenue from FATF blacklist jurisdictions, state sponsors of terrorism, or online casino markets where the activity is prohibited. Annual disclosure of revenue from supplying game content to illegal interactive gaming markets is required. Clear and convincing proof of labor peace agreements and union neutrality is required (§9-1F-04(c)).","Consumer and integrity rules: minimum age 21 with periodic identity reverification; credit card ban; mandatory deposit limits, with increases delayed 24 hours; monthly certification of the problem gambling disclosure; a voluntary exclusion list; no targeting of ads to prohibited or at-risk persons and no false advertising; penalties up to $1M plus a suspension of at least 30 days for tampering with games (§9-1F-07)."],"issue_tags":["legalize","tax","licensing","advertising","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"The closest analogues on the list are Illinois HB3080 and HB4797 (Internet Gaming Act), which likewise channel iGaming through licensees and contracted management service providers. HB1343 differs by capping each license at one operator, layering heavy social equity set-asides, labor peace mandates, and a statewide referendum, and setting an unusually high 55% effective rate on non-live-dealer play. Most other listed bills, such as Alaska HB145/SB194, Georgia HB 686, and Hawaii HB 1308/SB1569, authorize sports wagering rather than online casino. Connecticut HB05229 overlaps only on consumer protections such as withdrawals and targeting. The bill is a reintroduction of Maryland HB 17 from 2025.","watch":"Operators should watch the 55% rate on non-live-dealer play, the promotional deduction that shrinks with scale and expires after 5 years, the one-operator-per-license market-access limit, and the illegal-market revenue disclosure, which could disqualify suppliers or affiliates active in grey markets.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":55015,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:50:15.919Z"},"MD-HB1578":{"summary":"Maryland Chapter 82 (HB1578) reauthorizes the State's Minority Business Enterprise (MBE) Program for five more years, moving its sunset from July 1, 2026 to July 1, 2031, and enacts new legislative findings based on the 2025 MBE disparity study. It extends the same 2031 sunset to the MBE compliance obligations that apply to cannabis licensees, offshore wind applicants, public-private partnerships, video lottery terminal (casino) licensees, and sports wagering licensees. It also tightens agency reporting deadlines and makes administrative changes to the Office of Small, Minority, and Women Business Affairs (OSMWBA).","mechanics":["State Government §9–1E–07(b)(4): sports wagering licensees' duty to comply with the MBE Program 'to the extent practicable and authorized by the U.S. Constitution' now sunsets July 1, 2031 instead of July 1, 2026. OSMWBA must set an MBE participation plan within 6 months of license issuance, covering construction, equipment, and ongoing services.","State Government §9–1A–10: video lottery terminal applicants and licensees are now subject to a duty to make a good-faith effort to meet the unit MBE goal, and a new subsection (a) directly requires licensee MBE compliance. OSMWBA monitors compliance and reports to the Commission every 6 months, and the Commission may take immediate action on noncompliance. These provisions sunset July 1, 2031.","New SFP §14–301.1 replaces the prior findings with findings based on the '2025 MBE Disparity Study', citing statistically significant disparities consistent with discrimination, to support Croson narrow-tailoring. The general MBE sunset in §14–309 moves to July 1, 2031, and the next disparity study is due September 30, 2030.","State agency annual MBE and Small Business Reserve reports are now due within 60 days of fiscal year-end instead of 90 days, with new quarterly reporting. OSMWBA's summary reports move to the 5th day of the regular legislative session.","OSMWBA must adopt regulations for a certification review process (§14–503(a)(2)). Joint ventures meeting the size criteria now qualify as a 'small business' (§14–501(d)(4)). The Secretary of Social and Economic Mobility replaces the OSMWBA Special Secretary as chair of the procurement subcabinet, and the Secretary of Labor is added.","Effective June 1, 2026; approved April 14, 2026."],"issue_tags":["licensing"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"Unlike the listed sports wagering bills, which authorize, tax, or regulate wagering directly, this is a procurement reauthorization that touches gaming only by extending MBE obligations on Maryland sports wagering and VLT licensees. Examples of those direct-regulation bills include AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, HI HB 1308/SB1569, and IL HB2878 and HB3159. The closest analogues are other licensing-condition bills such as CO SB 163, but none of the listed bills imposes race-conscious supplier-diversity goals on gaming licensees. That feature is distinctive to Maryland's 2021 sports wagering law, which this bill keeps alive through 2031.","watch":"Operators holding Maryland sports wagering or VLT licenses should plan for five more years of MBE participation plans and compliance monitoring, which for VLT licensees includes semiannual OSMWBA reports to the Commission and the risk of immediate Commission action. They should also track whether the 2025 disparity-study findings withstand post-SFFA equal-protection challenges that could undercut these license conditions.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":46074,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:50:24.186Z"},"MD-HB1612":{"summary":"As introduced, HB1612 amends Md. State Government § 9-1E-17(d) to require, rather than merely permit, any sports wagering licensee or operator that advertises in Maryland to contract with an independent evaluator licensed under § 9-1E-17(b) to evaluate and rate its sports wagering content, experts, influencers, and content partners. The mandate is contingent on the State Lottery and Gaming Control Commission licensing at least three independent evaluators, and would take effect July 1, 2026. The current title references campaign finance contributions and a study on independent evaluation, which suggests amendments in the first chamber, but the text provided is the first reader and contains neither provision.","mechanics":["Section 1 changes 'may' to 'shall' in § 9-1E-17(d), converting a voluntary independent-evaluator arrangement into a mandatory one.","Covers any sports wagering licensee or sports wagering operator that advertises in the State; there is no carve-out by size or advertising volume.","The evaluator must rate four categories: sports wagering content, sports wagering experts, sports wagering influencers, and content partners.","Evaluators must be licensed under existing § 9-1E-17(b).","Section 2 makes the Act contingent on the Commission issuing licenses to at least three independent evaluators.","Section 3 sets a July 1, 2026 effective date, subject to the Section 2 contingency; the text adds no new penalty, so enforcement would fall under existing Commission authority."],"issue_tags":["advertising","licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"The closest listed analogue is CT HB05229, which regulates operator marketing directly by restricting advertising on college campuses and barring AI-driven bet targeting. Maryland instead imposes a third-party content-rating mandate covering influencers and content partners. The AK (HB145, SB194), GA (HB 686) and HI (HB 1308, HB 2570, SB1569) bills would legalize and build out regulatory frameworks, whereas Maryland's bill tightens one compliance obligation in an existing market. The contingency tying effectiveness to a minimum of three licensed evaluators is unusual and is apparently meant to prevent a single-vendor bottleneck.","watch":"Get the amended (third reader) text, which apparently adds campaign finance contribution limits and replaces or supplements the mandate with a study, and track how many evaluators the Commission licenses, since that count triggers the mandatory-contract obligation and its cost.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"First - Sports Wagering - Independent Evaluation of Sports Wagering","text_chars":1849,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:50:27.099Z"},"MD-HB1453":{"summary":"HB1453 layers new player-protection and enforcement rules onto Maryland's casino (video lottery) and sports wagering regimes. It caps individual wagers at $100,000 per sporting event, bans credit cards for online sports wagering transactions, and raises the Commission's maximum per-violation fine tenfold. It also reallocates 1% of fantasy and online sports wagering proceeds to the Problem Gambling Fund and directs credit-extension rules and a central credit list for video lottery licensees.","mechanics":["Fines: § 9-1A-25(b) (video lottery) and § 9-1E-08(b) (sports wagering) raise the maximum penalty per violation from $5,000 to $50,000. Each day of violation remains a separate violation.","Wager cap: new § 9-1E-09(f) bars any sports wagering licensee from accepting a wager from an individual on any one sporting event in excess of $100,000.","Credit card ban: new § 9-1E-11(b)(2) bars mobile licensees from accepting a credit card as payment for any online sports wagering-related transaction.","Proceeds: the mobile licensee's 80% retention under § 9-1E-12(b)(1)(iv) is unchanged. Beginning FY2027, the 5% General Fund carve-out from mobile proceeds becomes 4% to the General Fund and 1% to the Problem Gambling Fund (§ 9-1A-33), and the wording changes from 'attributable to mobile' to 'from online' sports wagering.","Fantasy: under § 9-1D-04, fantasy operators' retention drops from 85% to 84%, with 1% of fantasy proceeds going to the Problem Gambling Fund.","Video lottery credit: new § 9-1A-24(h) requires regulations mandating 1-800-GAMBLER language on credit applications and additional review where outstanding credit payments exceed the player's net worth and net winnings. It also requires a Commission-run central list of credit and counter-check players that licensees must feed and monitor so cumulative credit does not exceed net worth. Effective July 1, 2026."],"issue_tags":["tax","licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike IL HB3159, which adds a new 2% levy on adjusted gross receipts for fire and EMS departments, HB1453 leaves Maryland's sports wagering rate unchanged and only re-splits the existing 5% carve-out. It does, however, effectively raise the fantasy take from 15% to 16%. Its consumer-protection focus (credit card ban, per-event wager cap) overlaps most with CT HB05229, which addresses withdrawals from gaming accounts, customer service and AI-targeted betting rather than deposit methods or bet limits. A hard $100,000 per-event cap on all licensees, retail included, is unusual among the listed bills, none of which impose a statutory maximum wager.","watch":"Watch the $100,000 per-event cap and the credit card ban, which will hit high-limit and VIP business and payment flows (including whether 'any online sports wagering-related transaction' reaches withdrawals and third-party funding), along with per-day exposure under the tenfold fine increase.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":9568,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:50:28.153Z"},"MD-HB291":{"summary":"HB291, a departmental bill requested by the Lottery and Gaming Control Agency (cross-filed with SB 102), amends the definition of \"proceeds\" in State Government § 9-1E-01(h) to restructure the deduction for free promotional play. It removes the existing unlimited deduction for \"free bets and promotional credits redeemed by bettors\". In its place, it allows promotional play to be excluded from proceeds, but only up to a percentage cap set by the State Lottery and Gaming Control Commission by regulation, applying after a licensee's first full fiscal year of operations.","mechanics":["Strikes current § 9-1E-01(h)(3), the uncapped deduction of free bets and promotional credits redeemed by bettors, and retains deductions for winnings paid, cash equivalents of prizes, and federal excise taxes.","New § 9-1E-01(h)(2)(i) excludes from proceeds money given away by a licensee as free promotional play and used by players to bet on a sporting event.","New § 9-1E-01(h)(2)(ii) provides that, after the first full fiscal year of operations, the exclusion may not exceed a Commission-set percentage of the licensee's prior-fiscal-year retained proceeds.","The cap base differs by license type: for facility licensees, proceeds retained under § 9-1E-12(b)(1)(ii) or (iii); for mobile licensees, online proceeds retained under § 9-1E-12(b)(1)(iv).","The bill sets no statutory percentage and delegates the figure entirely to Commission regulation.","Effective July 1, 2026. Hearing in House Ways and Means scheduled for February 5."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Most listed bills either authorize sports wagering and set a tax (AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, HI HB 1308/HB 2570/SB1569) or adjust rates and fees in existing markets (IL HB3159's added 2% AGR levy, IL HB2878/HB5034's $15 million license fee). HB291 instead changes the tax base by capping the promotional-play deduction, which raises effective tax without touching the headline rate. Unlike capped-deduction models that fix a percentage in statute, HB291 leaves the percentage entirely to the Commission's rulemaking.","watch":"Because the cap percentage will be set by Commission regulation rather than statute, operators should prepare to engage in the rulemaking. They should also scrutinize how \"first full fiscal year of operations\" and the prior-year retained-proceeds base under § 9-1E-12(b)(1) are interpreted, particularly for established licensees, which would be capped immediately from July 1, 2026.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":2869,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:50:36.767Z"},"MD-HB1587":{"summary":"Chapter 669 loosens Maryland's grandfathered electronic instant bingo regime. It amends the Criminal Law Article's slot-machine and bingo provisions so that eligible commercial and qualified-organization operators face fewer machine and ticket restrictions. It also orders the State Lottery and Gaming Control Agency to commission an independent study, due December 1, 2026, on expanding electronic instant bingo at commercial facilities and adding e-bingo, historical horse racing machines or underutilized VLTs at off-track satellite simulcast facilities.","mechanics":["Slot-machine definition, Crim. Law §12-301(3)(iv): the handheld bingo-device exclusion's cap on cards played at once is changed. The text as supplied shows the cap going from 54 to 100, though the bracketing suggests an earlier version would have repealed the cap entirely; the chaptered final should be checked.","Grandfather conditions, §12-308(a): the requirement that machines were in operation during 2007 or under a commercial license on December 31, 2007 is deleted. The machine cap now reads 'twice' the number in operation on February 28, 2008, and Chapter 603 of 2012, §3 drops the parallel cap on repair and replacement. However, the final title omits 'Maximum Number of Machines', so it should be confirmed whether the doubling survived amendment.","New §12-308(b) and (c): machines need not print a paper ticket for each instant bingo game unless the player elects to, and the Commission must adopt implementing regulations.","New §12-301.1(e)(1): the rule that unlicensed electronic gaming devices as of January 1, 2013 are illegal does not apply to electronic instant bingo machines authorized under §12-308.","Section 2 study: due December 1, 2026, prepared by an independent third-party vendor. The vendor must include the Agency and stakeholders from the e-bingo industry, simulcast facilities both at and away from VLT facilities, and gaming labor unions. Required topics include State revenue, revenue lost to neighboring states, and cannibalization of VLT facility revenue, plus the impact of sports wagering on stand-alone simulcast facilities (§2(d)(4)).","Effective July 1, 2026."],"issue_tags":["legalize","licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. What stands out is that Maryland expands a narrow, 2008-era grandfathered class of electronic bingo machines by statute, rather than through casino or VLT licensing. The bill pairs that with a study that could open stand-alone OTB and simulcast sites to historical horse racing machines or VLTs, explicitly weighing cannibalization of the State's six casinos and the effect of sports wagering.","watch":"Watch the December 1, 2026 Agency report and the Commission's implementing regulations. They will set up 2027 legislation on more machines and HHR or VLT placement at satellite simulcast sites, which could reshape retail competition for casino and sportsbook licensees and revive debate over how sports wagering affects legacy gaming outlets.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":12836,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:50:36.963Z"},"MD-HB195":{"summary":"HB195 adds to the Maryland State Lottery and Gaming Control Commission's study duties under State Government \"9-109(b)(1)(v)\" a mandate to identify trends and emerging technology that make gambling safer and more transparent, specifically cybersecurity, AI-based customer behavior monitoring for responsible gaming, and digital identity, age, facial recognition and biometric verification. Where the Commission lacks resources or expertise for these studies, or for its sports wagering technology and remote surveillance duties under \"9-1E-03(a)(2)\", it must partner with or contract with a Maryland university, a foundation, or a private entity, and the Agency Director or a designee must fully participate. The amended title passed by the chamber 43-0 refers to a Workgroup on Gambling Prevention Technology, but the first-reader text provided does not contain any workgroup provisions.","mechanics":["New \"9-109(b)(1)(v)\" requires studies identifying emerging technology, including: (1) cybersecurity such as data encryption, multifactor authentication, secure payment gateways and blockchain; (2) AI to protect customers, monitor behavioral patterns and promote responsible gaming; and (3) digital identity verification of age and identity, including facial recognition and biometrics to reduce identity fraud.","New \"9-109(c)\": if the Commission lacks resources or expertise, it shall collaborate with, or contract with, an in-state institution of higher education, a foundation, or a private entity to study best practices.","New \"9-1E-03(a)(3)-(4)\" applies the same collaborate-or-contract mechanism to the Commission's existing duty in the sports wagering subtitle to consider technology and remote surveillance (tied to \"9-1E-04\").","The Agency Director or designee must communicate with, and fully participate in, any outside study.","Existing reporting provisions are redesignated as \"9-109(d)\" without substantive change.","Effective October 1, 2026; no new operator obligations, fees, penalties or rulemaking mandates appear in the introduced text."],"issue_tags":["integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike CT HB05229, which would bar operators from using artificial intelligence to target certain bets, MD HB195 treats AI favorably, as a tool the regulator should study for behavioral monitoring and responsible gaming, and it imposes no operator mandate. It is narrower than the operational reforms in CO SB 163, which changes commission-to-division delegation, and the broad Florida rewrite in FL H0189. It is closer in spirit to the problem-gambling education bills IL HB1451 and HB2580, but it targets regulator technology rather than public outreach. Its distinctive feature is the mandatory turn to outside academic, foundation or private partners whenever the Commission lacks the expertise to do the work itself.","watch":"Watch whether the final amended version, including the Workgroup on Gambling Prevention Technology named in the title, turns these studies into recommendations for mandatory operator requirements on AI behavioral monitoring, biometric or facial-recognition identity verification, or cybersecurity standards, and which private entities are engaged to shape them.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"First - State Lottery and Gaming Control Commission - Authority to Conduct Studies on the Use of Emerging Technology - Collaboration","text_chars":7567,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:50:42.367Z"},"MD-HB46":{"summary":"HB46 would have created a Gambling Prevention Technology Pilot Program in the State Lottery and Gaming Control Commission. Under it, Bowie State University and Morgan State University campuses would get designated \"no gambling zones\", and every online sports wagering operator would have to geofence those zones and block wagering inside them. The sponsor withdrew the bill on February 23, 2026, so it is dead for this session.","mechanics":["New State Government Article § 9–1E–18. Bowie State and Morgan State must participate. The Commission, working with the universities and the Maryland Center of Excellence on Problem Gambling, identifies no-gambling-zone boundaries, develops signage, and implements cost-effective technologies, including real-time monitoring of online sports wagering activity (§ (d)).","Each online sports wagering operator must establish a geofence around each designated zone that prevents access to its platform, and must collaborate with the Commission on enforcement (§ (e)(1)).","An individual who attempts to wager inside the geofence must be blocked and shown a warning about the pilot program and options for responsible gambling assistance (§ (e)(2)).","Safe harbor: the Commission may not fine, penalize or sanction an operator for unforeseeable technological failures within the geofenced boundaries during the pilot (§ (g)).","The Commission sets the timeline and implementation steps (§ (f)) and must report to the Governor and General Assembly by January 1, 2031 on whether geofencing is effective and viable (§ (h)).","The Act would take effect July 1, 2026 and sunset automatically on June 30, 2031."],"issue_tags":["integrity","licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states were provided. Most campus-gambling measures elsewhere target marketing to students or university partnership deals. This bill is unusual because it requires operators to geofence and block wagering at specific campus locations, which goes beyond the usual statewide-border geolocation duty. It also pairs that mandate with an explicit safe harbor for unforeseeable technical failures.","watch":"The bill is withdrawn, but the concept could return, so watch for a version that drops the § (g) safe harbor, expands the zones to other campuses, or turns the real-time monitoring language into data-sharing obligations on operators.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":4665,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:50:48.060Z"},"MD-HB295":{"summary":"HB295 creates a new criminal offense (Criminal Law § 12-115) barring any person from operating, conducting, or promoting an \"interactive game\" in Maryland. The definition targets online dual- or multi-currency sweepstakes-style games that simulate casino games, lottery games, or sports wagering. It also adds licensing conditions under State Government Title 9, which the bill extends to sports wagering licensees through § 9-1E-03. Applicants and licensees must disclose revenue earned from illegal interactive gaming markets, and the Commission is barred from licensing or allowing operations by anyone whose affiliates knowingly take revenue from certain high-risk jurisdictions or from online casino gaming where it is prohibited.","mechanics":["Definition: an \"interactive game\" (§ 12-115(a)(3)) involves consideration, chance, and reward; is available online or on a mobile or similar device; uses multiple currency systems that let the player exchange currency for a prize, cash, or cash equivalent; and simulates casino games, lottery games (draw, instant, keno, bingo), or sports wagering. Games awarding only noncash prizes that are free to play or offline are excluded.","Penalties: a knowing violation is a misdemeanor punishable by up to 3 years' imprisonment and/or a fine of $10,000 to $100,000, with fines paid to the Problem Gambling Fund. A violation is \"knowing\" if the Commission issued and published a cease and desist notice to the person, or if a licensee or applicant keeps doing business with a person who received a published notice. A finding of violation triggers mandatory license denial or revocation.","Vendor reporting: Commission licensees and applicants (Subtitles 1A, 1B, 1D, 1E) must report annually any business relationship with a financial institution, payment processor, geolocation provider, gaming content supplier, platform provider, or media affiliate known to support, operate, conduct, or promote an interactive game in Maryland.","Revenue disclosure (§ 9-1A-07(g)): on application and annually, an applicant or licensee must disclose every jurisdiction where it or any affiliate directly or indirectly accepted revenue from an interactive game in a jurisdiction that prohibits such games during the prior 12 months. A material misrepresentation or omission may support denial or sanctions against the licensee and its officers and board members.","Revenue bar (§ 9-1A-08.1): the Commission may not license, or allow to commence or continue operations, any person whose affiliates (including entities under common control) knowingly accept revenue directly or indirectly derived from (1) FATF call-for-action high-risk jurisdictions, (2) US-designated state sponsors of terrorism, or (3) online casino gaming in jurisdictions where it is prohibited. Revocation requires notice and a hearing plus a public-interest finding.","The bill also changes the false-statement offense in § 9-1A-07(h) from \"an individual\" to \"a person,\" which reaches entities. Effective date is July 1, 2026."],"issue_tags":["sweeps","licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike the authorization and tax bills listed (AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, HI HB 1308/SB1569), HB295 is an enforcement bill aimed at sweepstakes casinos and at licensed operators' exposure to grey and black markets. FL S1580 and FL H0189 also tighten illegal-gambling penalties, and IL HB5142 pulls prediction markets into the definition of sports wagering. None of the listed bills, however, pairs a sweepstakes ban with affiliate-level disclosure of revenue from illegal markets and a licensing bar tied to FATF, state-sponsor-of-terrorism, and prohibited online casino jurisdictions. The vendor reporting duty, covering payment processors, geolocation, content, platform, and media partners, is also unusual and extends compliance burdens to licensees' supply chains.","watch":"Watch the affiliate-revenue bar in § 9-1A-08.1(a)(3) and the disclosure in § 9-1A-07(g): \"directly or indirectly derived\" revenue from online casino gaming in prohibited jurisdictions, measured across all affiliates under common control, could jeopardize a Maryland sports wagering license held by a multinational group with B2C or B2B activity in unregulated markets, and the annual vendor-relationship reporting could force operators to drop shared payment, geolocation, content, or media partners that also serve sweepstakes platforms.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Engrossed","text_chars":10060,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:50:54.669Z"},"MD-HB420":{"summary":"HB420 creates a new State Government Article Subtitle 14A (§§ 10-14A-01 to 10-14A-05) letting active service members and their spouses who relocate to Maryland on military orders practice under an occupational or professional license issued by another state for the duration of those orders, without obtaining a Maryland license. It then threads an 'except as provided in § 10-14A-03' carve-out through dozens of licensing statutes, including the video lottery/table games licensing requirement in § 9-1A-06(a) and (c)(1) and the sports wagering licensing requirement in § 9-1E-05(a).","mechanics":["Eligibility (§ 10-14A-03(a)): an active service member (as defined in § 9-901, including reservists on active duty orders) or spouse who relocates because of orders and holds another state's license. The applicant must submit proof of orders, a marriage certificate if a spouse, and a notarized affidavit. The applicant must also be in good standing, have no disciplinary action or pending unprofessional-conduct investigation, and have no surrender of the license while under investigation.","Timing (§ 10-14A-03(b)): if the licensing authority cannot verify the license within 30 days of receiving the application, it may issue a temporary license with the same rights as a permanent one.","Background checks (§ 10-14A-03(c)): the licensing authority may, but is not required to, run a criminal history records check before authorizing practice or issuing a temporary license.","Scope and discipline (§§ 10-14A-03(d), 10-14A-04): practice is limited to the discipline and scope allowed in the issuing state. The individual is subject to the same disciplinary authority as Maryland licensees. A Maryland license can be required only for a different discipline or scope.","Gaming carve-outs: § 9-1A-06(a) and (c)(1) (video lottery operators, manufacturers, suppliers and video lottery employees) and § 9-1E-05(a) (sports wagering operators, online operators, suppliers and individuals directly employed in sports wagering) become subject to the § 10-14A-03 exception.","Exclusion and rulemaking: Subtitle 14A does not apply where an interstate compact already provides portability (§ 10-14A-02). Each licensing authority may adopt implementing regulations (§ 10-14A-05)."],"issue_tags":["licensing"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills deal with military licensing portability. AK HB145 and SB194 move the opposite way, building criminal history record checks into mobile sports wagering licensure, whereas HB420 makes such checks discretionary for qualifying service members and spouses entering Maryland's gaming workforce. The Illinois license-structure bills (e.g., IL HB2878, HB5034) and CO SB 163's delegation of licensing duties change how gaming regulators license, but none create a cross-state exemption from gaming licensure. HB420 is unusual in that it reaches sports wagering and casino licensing only as one of many technical conforming amendments in a general occupational licensing bill.","watch":"Watch whether the Maryland Lottery and Gaming Control Agency uses its § 10-14A-05 rulemaking power to require criminal history checks and suitability review for service members and spouses working under out-of-state sports wagering or casino employee licenses, and how it treats operator-level licensees given that § 9-1E-05(a) as amended covers operators and suppliers as well as individual employees.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Engrossed","text_chars":195198,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:50:55.550Z"},"MD-HB518":{"summary":"HB518 tightens Maryland's responsible-gaming rules across the lottery, video lottery, fantasy and sports wagering programs. It requires mandatory voluntary exclusion lists with opt-out referral to the Maryland Center for Excellence on Problem Gambling, and raises the fantasy participation age to 21 with a grandfather clause. It bans player-specific proposition bets on collegiate athletes, imposes new mobile-wagering account controls including a credit card ban and mandatory deposit limits, and requires operators to share anonymized transactional data with three state universities.","mechanics":["Voluntary exclusion and referral: §§ 9-109(d), 9-1A-24(e), 9-1D-05 and 9-1E-04(b)(7)-(8) and (d) make voluntary exclusion lists mandatory for the lottery, video lottery, fantasy and sports wagering. The Commission must pass each enrollee's contact information to the Center for Excellence on Problem Gambling unless the individual affirmatively declines. Section 2 requires a 'NO CONTACT' checkbox on the application form by August 1, 2026.","Fantasy age limit: § 9-1D-02(b) bars anyone under 21 from fantasy competitions and bars operators from offering contests or services to them. Accounts opened by 18-20 year olds before July 1, 2026 are exempt.","College player props: § 9-1E-09(f) prohibits licensees from offering or accepting, and individuals from placing, player-specific proposition bets on participants in collegiate sporting events. A 'proposition bet' is defined as a wager on an action, statistic, occurrence or nonoccurrence not directly connected to the final outcome or final score.","Mobile operator duties under § 9-1E-11(b):\n- Verify age and identity at account creation, with periodic reverification.\n- Display 1-800-GAMBLER messaging at log-on and log-off.\n- Require every account holder to set a self-imposed deposit limit and lockout period.\n- Offer a temporary self-suspension tool.\n- Prohibit credit cards for all online wagering transactions.\n- Show a problem-gambling disclosure at login, certified before account opening and monthly thereafter.","Account controls under §§ 9-1E-11(f)-(h):\n- No wagering-related email during a self-suspension of 72 hours or more.\n- Controls cannot be changed while a suspension is in effect.\n- Deposit-limit increases take effect only after the later of 24 hours or expiry of the prior limit period.\n- Withdrawals remain available during a suspension.","Data sharing and effective date: new § 9-1E-18 requires licensees, by September 1 each year, to provide quarterly transactional data and metrics to Morgan State, Bowie State and the UMD School of Medicine. The data covers bans and suspensions, complaints, ages, geolocation, responsible-gaming tool use, and balances, bet amounts, bonus usage, deposits and withdrawals. It must also include counts of players flagged for erratic gambling and the related bans. Personal data under Com. Law § 14-4801 is excluded, the data is exempt from the Public Information Act, and the universities may disclose it only for research or peer-reviewed or prevalence studies. The Act takes effect July 1, 2026."],"issue_tags":["prop","integrity","advertising"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"CT HB05229 is the closest analogue: like HB518 it layers consumer-protection duties on licensees, but it focuses on campus advertising, withdrawal rules and AI bet targeting rather than prop bans, deposit limits and research data-sharing. HB518's under-21 rule for fantasy parallels the under-21 bar that IL HB5059 (ORACLE Act) would impose on prediction markets. IL HB1451 and HB2580 address gambling-disorder education through a state agency rather than operator mandates. DE HB314 also addresses student-athlete protection, but HB518 is unusual in pairing a collegiate player-prop ban with a statutory requirement to hand operator transactional data to named universities.","watch":"Operators should prepare for July 1, 2026 compliance with the credit card ban, mandatory deposit-limit setup and monthly disclosure certification, and should scrutinize the ambiguous September 1 'quarterly' data-delivery obligation and its data-confidentiality safeguards as the bill moves through the Senate.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Engrossed","text_chars":18501,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:51:05.748Z"},"MD-HB731":{"summary":"HB731 (cross-filed with SB 456) expands Maryland's statutory definition of \"fantasy competition\" to expressly cover player-versus-player pick'em contests in which participants select higher or lower projected or actual statistics for two or more real athletes, and carves those contests out of the sports wagering definitions in § 9-1E-01. It also converts the optional fantasy operator registration fee into a mandatory $50,000 annual fee and requires the Lottery and Gaming Control Commission to run a fantasy-specific voluntary exclusion program, with operator duties and prize forfeiture.","mechanics":["§ 9-1D-01(d): fantasy competition now covers contests where participants \"assemble\" teams or rosters. Prizes may be shared and may be fixed, tiered or conditional bonus payouts if the prize value and structure are disclosed in advance. Participants must compete against other participants, expressly including higher/lower pick'em on two or more athletes. The existing bars on outcomes based solely on one athlete or on real-world team scores or spreads are retained.","§ 9-1D-02(a): a compliant fantasy competition \"does not constitute betting, wagering, or gambling for any purpose.\" §§ 9-1E-01(i)(1)(vii), (i)(2)(ii) and (j)(2) exclude regulated fantasy competitions, including the individual-stat carve-out, from \"sporting event\" and \"sports wagering.\" The in-state kiosk ban in § 9-1D-02(b) remains.","§ 9-1D-03(c): the Commission \"shall\" (previously \"may\") impose an annual registration fee of $50,000 per fantasy operator, distributed to the Blueprint for Maryland's Future Fund.","New § 9-1D-05(a): each operator must maintain a program to curtail compulsive play and display a problem-gambling assistance statement on its website or app and on all marketing materials and advertisements.","New § 9-1D-05(b)-(f): the Commission must create a voluntary exclusion list and fold it into its other self-exclusion programs. Operators may not let listed individuals open accounts or play; they may allow view and download of transaction history only. Operators must report any participation by an excluded individual. Prizes won by excluded individuals are forfeited and remitted quarterly to the Problem Gambling Fund (§ 9-1A-33). The Commission may sanction operators that knowingly fail to exclude.","Effective October 1, 2026; the Commission retains general rulemaking authority under renumbered § 9-1D-06."],"issue_tags":["legalize","prop","licensing","advertising"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike IL HB2342, which builds a fuller Gaming Board regulatory regime for fantasy operators with fees, surcharges, civil penalties and a levy on adjusted gross fantasy contest receipts, HB731 keeps Maryland's light registration model and adds only a flat $50,000 fee, with no revenue-based tax. It moves in the opposite direction from IL HB5142, which widens \"sports wagering\" to capture individual athlete performance statistics (there via prediction markets). HB731 instead expressly removes stat-based pick'em from the sports wagering definition when it is offered as a compliant fantasy contest. FL H0189 also revises fantasy sports contest law within a broader gambling overhaul, but HB731 is notable for codifying peer-to-peer higher/lower pick'em as lawful fantasy rather than banning it or pulling it into the sportsbook regime.","watch":"Watch whether the pick'em language survives as written, because the requirement that participants compete against other participants (§ 9-1D-01(d)(3)) and the pre-disclosed prize-value rules would permit peer-to-peer pick'em but likely exclude house-banked fixed-odds props, creating a lower-cost, untaxed competitor channel to licensed sportsbooks; also note the drafting inconsistencies (\"mandatory exclusion list\" in the purpose paragraph and \"licensed\" in § 9-1D-05(b) versus a registration-only regime).","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":11262,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:51:07.075Z"},"MD-HB758":{"summary":"HB758 changes how Maryland's problem gambling money may be spent without touching how the money is raised. It strikes the existing language in Health-General § 19-804(a)(2) that made research and training proper expenses of the Secretary's problem gambling service grants. It also adds a new authorized use of the Problem Gambling Fund under State Government § 9-1A-33(b)(4)(v): reimbursement for peers who provide behavioral health services to people in problem gambling treatment or prevention programs.","mechanics":["Health-General § 19-804(a): repeals paragraph (2), which said that research and training designed to improve or extend problem gambling services are proper items of expense. The seven listed service categories are renumbered but otherwise unchanged.","State Government § 9-1A-33(b)(4)(v): adds a new permitted expenditure from the Problem Gambling Fund. It covers reimbursement to support peers providing behavioral health services to individuals in the treatment and prevention programs under items (iii) and (iv), including programs aimed at sports wagering and fantasy-related problem gambling.","Leaves the rest of the Fund intact: its status as a special nonlapsing fund, the existing uses for a 24-hour hotline, outreach (including to voluntary exclusion list registrants), and treatment programs, the spillover of unspent money to drug and other addiction treatment under (b)(5), and the appropriation requirement under (b)(6).","Does not change any contribution, fee or tax paid by gaming or sports wagering licensees into the Fund.","Effective October 1, 2026. The bill is in House Ways and Means, with a hearing on February 19."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Illinois HB1451, HB2580 and SB 118 amend the Substance Use Disorder Act to expand DHS public education and awareness duties on gambling disorders. HB758 instead narrows one category of spending (research and training) and adds peer-support reimbursement. Hawaii HB1308, HB2570 and SB1569 create problem gambling funding as part of new sports wagering frameworks, while HB758 only reallocates uses within Maryland's existing Fund. Unusually, HB758 removes research and training as authorized expenses at the same time that other states are expanding responsible-gaming research and education.","watch":"Watch whether committee amendments add new or higher licensee contributions to fund the expanded peer-support use, and whether dropping research and training as a grant expense affects state-funded responsible-gaming research that operators rely on or partner with.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":5401,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:51:10.698Z"},"MD-SB102":{"summary":"SB 102, a departmental bill requested by the Lottery and Gaming Control Agency (cross-filed with HB 291), changes how promotional play is treated in Maryland's sports wagering \"proceeds\" definition under State Government Article § 9–1E–01(h). It removes the current uncapped deduction for \"free bets and promotional credits redeemed by bettors.\" In its place it adds an exclusion for free promotional play, which the State Lottery and Gaming Control Commission may cap by regulation as a percentage of a licensee's prior-year retained proceeds once the licensee has completed its first full fiscal year of operations. The practical effect is to expand the taxable base for operators that rely heavily on promotional credits.","mechanics":["Strikes former § 9–1E–01(h)(3), the deduction for free bets and promotional credits redeemed by bettors. Proceeds would then equal amounts wagered less winnings paid, the cash value of prizes awarded, and federal excise taxes paid.","New § 9–1E–01(h)(2)(i) excludes from proceeds money a sports wagering licensee gives away as free promotional play and that players use to bet on a sporting event.","New § 9–1E–01(h)(2)(ii) provides that, after the first full fiscal year of operations, the exclusion may not exceed a percentage the Commission sets by regulation. The bill itself sets no figure.","The cap's base differs by licensee type. For facility licensees it is total proceeds retained in the prior fiscal year under § 9–1E–12(b)(1)(ii) or (iii). For mobile licensees it is total online proceeds retained in the prior fiscal year under § 9–1E–12(b)(1)(iv).","Effective July 1, 2026. Hearing in Senate Budget and Taxation was set for January 21."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Most of the listed bills that touch tax change rates or add new levies rather than the deduction base. Examples are IL HB3159 (an added 2% of adjusted gross receipts for fire and EMS), AK HB145/SB194 (a new tax on mobile sports wagering), and IA SSB 1157 (tax rates and license fees). None of the listed bills targets the promotional-credit deduction. SB 102 is unusual because it raises the effective tax burden without touching the rate, by narrowing the promotional deduction. It also delegates the size of the cap entirely to the Commission's rulemaking rather than fixing a number in statute.","watch":"Operators should engage early in the Commission's rulemaking on the cap percentage, because the statute leaves it uncapped. They should also seek clarity on three points: whether \"after the first full fiscal year of operations\" makes the limit apply immediately to existing licensees on July 1, 2026; what \"retained\" proceeds under § 9–1E–12(b)(1) means as the base; and how unused or forfeited promotional credits are counted.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":2880,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:51:11.381Z"},"MD-SB242":{"summary":"SB242 (Chapter 694 of 2026) creates a new State Government Article Subtitle 10-14A that lets an active service member, or the spouse of one, who relocates to Maryland on military orders practise an occupation or profession under a license issued by another state for as long as the orders last. It writes an exception to that new section into dozens of licensing statutes, including the video lottery licensing requirement (§ 9-1A-06) and the sports wagering licensing requirement (§ 9-1E-05). Per its purpose clause, it also broadens the 'eligible veteran' definition for licensing-process provisions, but that part is not in the excerpt provided.","mechanics":["Eligibility (§ 10-14A-03(a)): the applicant must submit to the licensing authority proof of orders, a marriage certificate if a spouse, and a notarized affidavit covering identity, truthfulness, understanding of Maryland scope of practice, and good standing in all states. The applicant must also be in good standing with the issuing regulator, have no disciplinary action and no pending unprofessional-conduct investigation, and not have surrendered the license while under investigation.","Processing (§ 10-14A-03(b)-(c)): if the authority cannot verify the license within 30 days of receiving the application, it may issue a temporary license with the same rights as a permanent one. The authority may, but is not required to, run a criminal history records check first.","Limits and oversight (§ 10-14A-03(d), § 10-14A-04): the individual may practise only within the discipline and scope of the out-of-state license and cannot be required to obtain a Maryland license unless going beyond that scope. Within that scope they may practise to the same extent as an in-state licensee and are subject to the same disciplinary authority.","Exclusions and terms (§§ 10-14A-01, 10-14A-02): the subtitle does not apply to occupations covered by an interstate compact. 'License' is defined broadly to include any permit, certification, registration or other authorization, and only licenses issued by another state (not another 'jurisdiction') qualify.","Gaming amendments: §§ 9-1A-06(a) and (c)(1) (video lottery operators, manufacturers, service providers and employees) and § 9-1E-05(a) (sports wagering operators, online operators, suppliers and individual sports wagering employees) now begin 'except as provided in § 10-14A-03'.","Rulemaking (§ 10-14A-05): each licensing authority, which would include the gaming regulator, may adopt implementing regulations. No effective date appears in the provided excerpt."],"issue_tags":["licensing"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills is a military-spouse licensing portability measure, and none carves an out-of-state-license exception into a gaming licensing statute. The closest overlap is AK HB145 and SB194, which tie criminal history record checks to mobile sports wagering licensure; Maryland instead makes such checks optional for qualifying service members and spouses. CO SB 163 also deals with gaming licensing administration, but it concerns delegating licensing duties from Colorado's commission to its division rather than recognising other states' licenses. What is unusual here is that a general veterans-affairs reciprocity law quietly reaches the sports wagering and video lottery licensing provisions.","watch":"Operators should watch whether the Maryland Lottery and Gaming Control Commission adopts regulations under § 10-14A-05 to apply § 10-14A-03 to sports wagering and video lottery employee licenses, and how it handles background-check and suitability standards and the 30-day temporary-license rule for service-member and spouse employees holding another state's gaming license.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":187583,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:51:25.072Z"},"MD-SB282":{"summary":"SB282 (Chapter 4 of 2026) is Maryland's operating budget for fiscal 2027 (July 1, 2026 to June 30, 2027), enacted under Article III, Section 52 of the Maryland Constitution. It sets general, special and federal fund appropriations by agency and program, attaching conditions, contingent reductions and report-based spending restrictions. The portion of the text provided covers payments to civil divisions through the Department of Social and Economic Mobility and contains no provisions on sports wagering, the lottery or casino gaming.","mechanics":["A15O00.03 appropriates $1,600,000 in special funds for Admissions and Amusement Tax distributions to local governments, a general entertainment tax line rather than a wagering-specific one.","A15O00.01 sets Disparity Grants at $203,599,585 in general funds, reduced by $26,996,721 if legislation holds the grant at the fiscal 2026 level.","Several appropriations depend on companion legislation expanding the allowable uses of the Strategic Energy Investment Fund, including $100,000,000 at the PSC (C90G00.01) and $100,000,000 plus $13,000,000 at MEA (D13A13.08).","D23A01 funds the Maryland Cannabis Administration at $29,338,189 in special funds, and D24A01.01 shifts $2,279,883 to the Cannabis Business Assistance Fund, contingent on authorizing legislation.","Many lines withhold funds until agencies submit reports to the budget committees, which then have a 45-day review period, for example the Uninsured Employers' Fund report due October 1, 2026, and the VOCA report due November 1, 2026."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. On its own, the excerpt is unusual for a gaming practice only because it is a general appropriations act. The nearest touchpoints are the admissions and amusement tax distribution and the cannabis regulatory appropriations, neither of which concerns wagering. Any Maryland Lottery and Gaming Control Agency appropriation or sports-wagering revenue line (for example, Blueprint for Maryland's Future fund transfers) would appear in later sections not included in the text provided.","watch":"Review the remainder of the chaptered budget, which is not in this excerpt, for the Maryland Lottery and Gaming Control Agency appropriation and any conditions, reporting requirements or contingencies tied to sports-wagering revenue or companion Budget Reconciliation and Financing Act legislation.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":168139,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:51:27.526Z"},"MD-SB112":{"summary":"Maryland SB112, a departmental bill requested by the Lottery and Gaming Control Agency (cross-filed with HB 295), makes it a crime to operate, conduct, or promote sweepstakes-style \"interactive games\" in the State and requires Commission licensees to report business ties to vendors known to support them. It also requires casino and sports wagering applicants and licensees to disclose annually any revenue they or their affiliates took from interactive games in jurisdictions where those games are illegal. It bars licensing, or continued operation, where the licensee or any affiliate knowingly accepts revenue from FATF call-for-action jurisdictions, U.S.-designated state sponsors of terrorism, or online casino gaming in jurisdictions that prohibit it.","mechanics":["New Crim. Law §12-115 defines \"interactive game\" as an internet or mobile game, contest, or promotion that uses multiple currency systems redeemable for prizes, cash, or cash equivalents and simulates casino games, lottery games (including keno and bingo), or sports wagering. Games awarding solely noncash prizes are excluded.","Violation is a misdemeanor punishable by up to 3 years' imprisonment and/or a fine of $10,000 to $100,000. Under §12-115(c)(2), the Commission \"shall\" deny or revoke the violator's license under Subtitles 1A, 1B, 1D, or 1E.","Applicants and licensees must report annually any business relationship with a financial institution, payment processor, geolocation provider, gaming content supplier, platform provider, or media affiliate known to support, operate, conduct, or promote an interactive game in Maryland (§12-115(b)(2)).","New §9-1A-07(g) requires a disclosure on application and annually covering the prior 12 months. It must list the jurisdictions where the applicant, licensee, or any affiliate directly or indirectly accepted revenue from interactive games in an \"illegal interactive gaming market.\" A material misrepresentation or omission can ground denial, or sanctions under §9-1A-25 on the licensee and its officers and board members. The false-statement offense in renumbered (h) is broadened from \"individual\" to \"person.\"","New §9-1A-08.1 bars issuing a license where the applicant or any affiliate, including an entity under common control, knowingly accepts revenue directly or indirectly derived from (1) FATF high-risk call-for-action jurisdictions, (2) U.S.-designated state sponsors of terrorism, or (3) online casino gaming in jurisdictions that prohibit it. The Commission must stop operations on such a finding and may revoke after notice and hearing if that serves the public interest.","§9-1E-03(a)(1) is amended to apply §9-1A-08.1 to sports wagering licensees, their employees, and their contractors. The Act takes effect July 1, 2026."],"issue_tags":["sweeps","licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Most listed bills, such as AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, and HI HB 1308/HB 2570/SB1569, authorize and tax new sports wagering markets, whereas SB112 is an enforcement and suitability measure for an existing market. It sits closer to FL H0189 and FL S1580, which revise Florida's illegal and internet gambling penalties, and to IL HB3733, which broadens Illinois' \"gambling device\" definition. None of the listed bills appears to tie licensure to an operator group's worldwide revenue sources, as SB112's affiliate revenue bar and illegal-market disclosure do. Its targeting of dual-currency sweepstakes is distinct from IL HB5142, which addresses sports-related prediction markets instead.","watch":"The affiliate-wide revenue bar in §9-1A-08.1(a)(3) and the illegal-market disclosure in §9-1A-07(g) could jeopardize Maryland licenses held by multinational operator groups with grey-market online casino revenue anywhere in the world, so watch closely how \"knowingly,\" \"indirectly derived,\" and \"common control\" are interpreted, especially since there is no de minimis threshold or cure period, alongside the vendor-relationship reporting duty.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":8013,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:51:27.569Z"},"MD-SB418":{"summary":"SB418 (Chapter 493 of 2026) lets a member of the U.S. Foreign Service, or the member's spouse, who relocates to Maryland on an assignment or detail practice an occupation or profession under a license issued by another state for the length of that assignment, without obtaining a Maryland license. It then threads a cross-reference to new State Government § 10–14A–03 through dozens of licensing statutes. These include the video lottery licensing requirement (§ 9–1A–06(a) and (c)(1)) and the sports wagering licensing requirement (§ 9–1E–05(a)), so qualifying individuals can work in those roles without a Maryland gaming license.","mechanics":["New State Government Subtitle 14A (§§ 10–14A–01 to 10–14A–05) broadly defines 'license' as any license, permit, certification, registration or other legal authorization required for an occupation or profession. 'Licensing authority' means any State agency that issues one.","Under § 10–14A–03(a), the individual must hold another state's license and do all of the following: give the licensing authority a copy of the assignment or detail notice; remain in good standing with the issuing regulator and in all states where they hold or held a similar license; have actively used the license in the 2 years before relocating; and comply with Maryland practice standards, discipline and continuing education requirements.","Practice is limited to the discipline and scope of the out-of-state license (§ 10–14A–03(b)(1)). The licensing authority may not require a Maryland license unless the person seeks to practice beyond that scope (§ 10–14A–03(b)(2)).","Authorized individuals practice to the same extent as Maryland licensees and are subject to the same disciplinary authority (§ 10–14A–04). Each licensing authority may adopt implementing regulations (§ 10–14A–05).","§ 10–14A–02 carves out the practice of law and occupations already covered by an interstate compact.","§ 9–1E–05(a) (sports wagering licensees, including online operators, suppliers and employees under item (4)) and § 9–1A–06(a) and (c)(1) (video lottery operators, manufacturers, service providers and employees) now open with 'except as provided in § 10–14A–03.' The effective date is not shown in the portion of text provided."],"issue_tags":["licensing"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills address professional licensing portability. Bills such as AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686 and HI HB 1308 create sports wagering licensing regimes from scratch, and IL HB2878/HB5034 change master sports wagering license fees. IL HB2880/HB5035 loosen employment restrictions for regulator staff rather than licensees. SB418 is unusual because a general workforce-mobility statute silently amends Maryland's existing sports wagering and video lottery licensing provisions through a cross-reference.","watch":"Watch how the Lottery and Gaming Control Commission implements § 10–14A–03 for sports wagering and casino employees, including whether it can still require background checks and suitability review. Watch also whether operators may rely on another state's gaming employee license, and whether regulations under § 10–14A–05 confine the exception to individual employees under § 9–1E–05(a)(4) rather than entity licenses.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":163720,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:51:32.340Z"},"MD-SB639":{"summary":"SB639 adds \"historical horse race\" to the definition of \"sporting event\" in Maryland's sports wagering law (State Gov't §9-1E-01(i)(1)(vii)). It lets certain retail sports wagering facility licensees offer pari-mutuel wagering on historical horse races (HHR) on Commission-approved terminals, capped at 4,000 terminals statewide, and sets a separate revenue split for those proceeds. Because it expands commercial gaming, it takes effect only if voters approve it at the November 2026 referendum, with an effective date of January 1, 2027.","mechanics":["Eligible operators under new §9-1E-09(f): Class A-1 and A-2 licensees at video lottery facilities; Class A-2 horse racing licensees at thoroughbred or standardbred race courses; §11-524 licensees at the Timonium fairgrounds; and satellite simulcast facility owners or lessees. All other facility licensees and all mobile licensees are barred.","§9-1E-09.1 requires pari-mutuel play only, with no betting against the house, in a Commission-approved designated area, on approved days and hours, using approved wager types and terminals. The Commission may not authorize more than 4,000 terminals. Minimum wager is 10 cents, and the minimum payout may not be less than the amount wagered.","Terminal rules: race identity (location, date, horses, jockeys or drivers) is hidden until after the bet is placed; past performance data current as of the race date must be shown; odds or payouts must be posted; and results plus a replay must be shown after the wager. Player-funded pools may be used to cover minus pools.","Revenue (§9-1E-12(c)): video lottery operators keep the same percentage they keep on VLT proceeds under §9-1A-27; other licensees keep 60%. From the State share, local impact grants, the Lottery and Gaming Control Agency, the Purse Dedication Account and the Small, Minority, and Women-Owned Businesses Account get the same share they receive from VLT proceeds; 5% goes to the General Fund; the remainder goes to the Blueprint for Maryland's Future Fund.","Effectiveness: referendum at the November 2026 general election under Art. XIX §1(e) of the Maryland Constitution. If approved, the Act takes effect January 1, 2027; if rejected, it is null and void. The Act takes effect July 1, 2026 solely to allow the referendum to be held."],"issue_tags":["legalize","tax","licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike the listed bills that authorize mobile sports wagering from scratch (AK HB145 and SB194, HI HB1308, HB2570 and SB1569, GA HB686), SB639 extends Maryland's existing sports wagering framework to historical horse racing, a slot-like pari-mutuel product. It also expressly bars mobile licensees from offering HHR. The closest analogues are definitional expansions such as IL HB5142, which would fold prediction markets into \"sports wagering\", but SB639 uniquely pairs the expansion with a VLT-style revenue split and a mandatory voter referendum. It is a reintroduction of SB 982 (2025) and is cross-filed with a companion bill (6lr0860).","watch":"Mobile and Class B operators are excluded, and HHR revenue tracks VLT retention rates or a 60% retention rate rather than the 80–85% sports wagering rates, so watch for amendments widening eligibility or adjusting the split, the 4,000-terminal cap, and whether the referendum makes the November 2026 ballot.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":23839,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:51:39.415Z"},"MD-SB557":{"summary":"SB557 narrows the definition of \"own\" in Maryland's video lottery subtitle (State Government \\u00a7 9-1A-01(s)), which also governs sports wagering licensees through \\u00a7 9-1E-03(a)(1). Non-voting beneficial or proprietary interests of 15% or less, held directly or through a passive trust, would no longer count as ownership, provided the applicant or licensee files a pre-transfer certification and a fingerprint-based criminal history check with the Commission. The existing 5% ownership threshold otherwise stays in place.","mechanics":["Current rule kept: \"own\" means a beneficial or proprietary interest of at least 5% in the property or business of an applicant or licensee (\\u00a7 9-1A-01(s)(1)).","New carve-out in \\u00a7 9-1A-01(s)(2): an interest of 15% or less is not \"ownership\" if it confers no voting rights. The Senate committee amendment cut this cap from 25%.","The interest must be held directly by a person, or in trust for that person's benefit, and the trustee may not exercise any voting rights or control over the applicant or licensee.","Before the interest is transferred or issued, the applicant or licensee must give the Commission written notice and certification, in the form the Commission requires, that the holder is not disqualified for a video lottery employee license under \\u00a7 9-1A-14(c)(1)(iii) and (iv).","A state and national criminal history records check with fingerprints is also required, under Title 10, Subtitle 2, Part III of the Criminal Procedure Article.","The change applies to sports wagering licensees, employees and contractors through the cross-reference in \\u00a7 9-1E-03(a)(1), which pulls in \\u00a7\\u00a7 9-1A-08 and 9-1A-12. It takes effect October 1, 2026."],"issue_tags":["licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"None of the listed bills addresses ownership-attribution thresholds for licensure suitability. Illinois HB2878 and HB5034 change master sports wagering license fees and license counts, IL HB2761 changes who may apply for a facility-based license, and Colorado SB 163 changes how licensing duties are delegated within the gaming regulator. Maryland's bill is unusual because it is a targeted capital-structure fix: it lets passive, non-voting investors take up to a 15% stake without full owner qualification, in exchange for a lighter certification and background check.","watch":"Watch three things: whether the House restores the 25% cap or adds conditions; how the Commission defines its certification form and the \"no control\" test for trustees; and the fact that the reenacted \\u00a7 9-1A-05(d)(1) still treats any ownership interest, regardless of percentage, as ownership for the one-video-lottery-facility limit and the ban on legislators holding interests, so the carve-out does not reach those restrictions.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Engrossed","text_chars":6498,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:51:43.358Z"},"MD-SB456":{"summary":"SB 456 expands Maryland's fantasy competition definition to cover peer-to-peer higher/lower 'pick'em' contests built on two or more real athletes' projected stats, and declares compliant fantasy contests outside sports wagering 'for any purpose.' It replaces the Commission's discretionary registration fee with a mandatory $50,000 annual fee directed to the Blueprint for Maryland's Future Fund. It also writes a statutory voluntary exclusion regime and responsible-gaming obligations into the fantasy subtitle.","mechanics":["Definition (§9-1D-01(d)): participants 'assemble' teams or rosters. Contests may include picking higher or lower than projected or actual performance statistics for rosters of two or more real athletes. Shared, tiered and conditional bonus prizes are allowed if disclosed in advance, but participants must compete 'against other participants.' The existing ban on outcomes based solely on one athlete or on a team score or point spread is kept.","§9-1D-02(a): a compliant fantasy competition 'does not constitute betting, wagering, or gambling for any purpose.' The ban on in-state kiosks and machines in §9-1D-02(b) is unchanged.","§9-1D-03(c): the Commission 'shall' impose an annual registration fee of $50,000, replacing the discretionary 'may' fee. Proceeds go to the Blueprint for Maryland's Future Fund (§5-206 Education Article).","New §9-1D-05 requires each operator to maintain a compulsive-play mitigation program. Operators must also post a problem-gaming help statement on their site or app and on all marketing materials and advertisements.","The Commission must create a voluntary exclusion list and fold it into its other self-exclusion programs. Excluded individuals may not open accounts or play, though existing accountholders may view and download their transaction history. Operators must report any participation by an excluded individual. Prizes won by self-excluded players are forfeited and remitted quarterly to the Problem Gambling Fund (§9-1A-33). Sanctions apply for knowing failures to exclude.","§9-1E-01(i) and (j): 'sporting event' and 'sports wagering' are amended to carve out fantasy competitions under Subtitle 1D, including for individual player stats. Effective October 1, 2026; cross-filed as HB 731."],"issue_tags":["prop","legalize","licensing","advertising"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"IL HB5142 moves in the opposite direction by pulling contracts on individual athlete performance statistics into the definition of sports wagering, whereas MD SB456 expressly carves player-stat pick'em contests out of sports wagering when offered as fantasy. IL HB2342 would have the Gaming Board levy fees and charges on adjusted gross fantasy receipts, while SB456 relies on a flat $50,000 registration fee with no receipts-based levy. FL H0189 also revises fantasy sports contest rules, but as part of a broad gambling overhaul. Maryland's bill is narrower and operator-friendly on product scope while adding exclusion and forfeiture obligations.","watch":"Watch whether the 'competes against other participants' element (§9-1D-01(d)(3)) is read to exclude house-banked pick'em formats, and whether the Budget and Taxation committee converts the flat $50,000 fee into a receipts-based tax. Also watch whether the purpose paragraph's reference to a 'mandatory exclusion list' and the voluntary list's coverage of contests 'licensed' (rather than registered) under the subtitle are cleaned up in amendments.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":11271,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:51:44.285Z"},"MD-SB652":{"summary":"SB652 creates a new State Government Article subtitle (§§ 9-1F-01 to 9-1F-16) that prohibits operating, offering, or promoting \"illegal\" online gambling in Maryland and bars anyone from knowingly supporting it, with dual-currency sweepstakes games expressly covered. It lets the Attorney General's Consumer Protection Division and local prosecutors issue cease-and-desist orders and seek ex parte injunctions against operators, payment processors, platform providers, and other supporters. Violations carry criminal and civil fines, forfeiture, mandatory license revocation, and treatment as an unfair, abusive, or deceptive trade practice under the Consumer Protection Act.","mechanics":["Definitions (§ 9-1F-01): \"illegal\" means not expressly authorized by applicable law. \"Online gambling\" covers wagers on any game or contest, including casino games and sweepstakes games, meaning dual-currency systems simulating casino, lottery, or sports wagering. Persons licensed for online sports wagering or fantasy competitions under Subtitles 1D and 1E are excluded (§ 9-1F-02).","Operator cease-and-desist (§ 9-1F-04): an order gives 10 days to geoblock Maryland users. It may be served by publication without first trying to locate the operator, it is prima facie evidence of knowledge, and the AG must publish a public list of named operators and URLs. Out-of-state operators broadcasting into Maryland submit to state jurisdiction.","Penalties (§ 9-1F-06): operating is a misdemeanor punishable by up to 3 years and/or a $50,000 fine for a first offense and $100,000 for later offenses. Knowingly supporting carries fines of $25,000, then $50,000. Each wager and each promotion is a separate violation. Forfeiture of profits and gross receipts is mandatory. The Commission must revoke any gaming license held by a violator and bar the person from future licensure.","Intermediaries (§§ 9-1F-07 to 9-1F-09): financial transaction providers and platform providers are deemed to know about every URL on the AG list. Platforms must obtain a certified license copy or Commission confirmation before hosting any online gambling ad. Orders giving 30 days to comply may go to applicants, licensees, geolocation providers, content suppliers, promoters, celebrity endorsers, and media affiliates, with good-faith blocking protected from tort liability.","Courts (§ 9-1F-10): courts may issue TROs and injunctions, including account freezes and website takedowns. Ex parte injunctions are available, with a hearing within 30 days. A violation is conclusively deemed irreparable harm. Violating an order brings fines of $50,000, then $100,000, per day or per transaction.","Other provisions: the Commission may deny, revoke, suspend, or condition licenses for noncompliance (§ 9-1F-12). The Division gets rulemaking authority (§ 9-1F-14), and the subtitle is to be construed liberally (§ 9-1F-15). The Act takes effect October 1, 2026."],"issue_tags":["sweeps","advertising","licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Florida's FL S1580 and FL H0189 also address illegal and internet gambling penalties. SB652 differs by building a consumer-protection enforcement regime run by the Attorney General rather than the gaming regulator. Its core tools are a public blocklist of operators and URLs, deemed knowledge for payment processors and hosting platforms, and ex parte takedown injunctions. Unlike IL HB5142, which pulls prediction markets into the sports wagering definition, and CT HB05038 and IL HB5059, which regulate prediction markets directly, SB652 does not mention prediction markets. Its broad \"wager\" and \"illegal\" definitions could nonetheless be read to reach any unlicensed product. The mandatory revocation and lifetime ineligibility for licensees in § 9-1F-06(D) is unusually severe compared with the advertising-focused CT HB05229.","watch":"Watch three points: the § 9-1F-02 carve-out covers only Subtitle 1D and 1E licensees, so casino licensees, B2B suppliers, and affiliates are not exempt; § 9-1F-06(D) mandatory revocation and lifetime ineligibility could follow from \"indirect\" support of unlicensed iGaming or sweepstakes; and § 9-1F-08(B) license-verification duty for platforms hosting \"online gambling\" ads could slow legitimate sportsbook advertising.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":23912,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:55:44.918Z"},"MD-SB844":{"summary":"Maryland's annual corrective bill, enacted as Chapter 153 of 2026, fixes technical errors across the Annotated Code and the Public Local Laws of Somerset County. The fixes cover omitted words, stale cross-references, renamed agencies and misnomers such as the NMLS name, obsolete death-penalty language, and mis-tabulated subsections. It states that it is not intended to affect any law other than to correct technical errors, and it authorizes the Code's publishers to correct certain errors and obsolete provisions.","mechanics":["Each amended section carries a Drafter's Note identifying the error and the session law that caused it. Examples: the omitted word 'HISTORY' in criminal history records check provisions in Business Occupations and Professions §§ 18-204, 18-303, 18-304 and 18-3A-03, -04 and -08; and 'Nationwide Mortgage Licensing System and Registry' renamed 'Nationwide Multistate Licensing System' throughout Financial Institutions §§ 1-101(q) and 11-604 through 11-622 and Commercial Law § 14-1201(l).","The purpose paragraph states the Act is not intended to affect any law other than to correct technical errors, so the changes are not meant to carry substantive weight.","The bill's list of amended sections includes State Government Article § 9-1E-12(b)(2)(ii), which falls within Maryland's sports wagering subtitle. The operative text of that change is not in the excerpt provided, so its content cannot be confirmed here.","Tax-General § 10-706(c) is also listed for amendment, but its text is likewise not in the excerpt.","Other notable fixes: obsolete 'or is to be executed' language struck from Correctional Services § 9-605(a), Education § 7-1512 subsections redesignated after the 2025 repeal of subsection (g), and Health-General § 19-706(m) repealed as obsolete."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. What stands out is that a purely technical, omnibus corrective vehicle reaches into Maryland's sports wagering subtitle (State Government § 9-1E-12). Such changes are expressly framed as non-substantive and usually draw little scrutiny from industry stakeholders.","watch":"Pull the chaptered text of State Government § 9-1E-12(b)(2)(ii), and of Tax-General § 10-706(c) if it touches wagering withholding, to confirm the change is purely clerical and does not quietly alter a cross-reference or obligation affecting sports wagering licensees.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":87168,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:55:50.697Z"},"MD-SB862":{"summary":"SB862 directs the Maryland Department of Health to run a separate prevalence study of problem and pathological mobile gambling, with the first study due by July 1, 2031. It also funds the Problem Gambling Fund by cutting the share of proceeds that sports wagering and fantasy operators keep by one percentage point and sending that 1% to the Fund. In practice this raises the effective state take on every operator class by one point, effective July 1, 2026.","mechanics":["Health-General §19-804(b),(d): the existing prevalence study is relabeled as covering in-person gambling. A new mobile gambling prevalence study is added, with the initial study due July 1, 2031 and replication studies at least every 5 years, conducted by an independent researcher.","State Gov't §9-1E-12(b)(1): operator retention falls from 85% to 84% for Class A-1/A-2 and Class B-1/B-2 facility licensees, and from 80% to 79% for mobile licensees. The effective rate therefore rises from 15% to 16% (retail) and from 20% to 21% (mobile).","§9-1E-12(b)(2)(ii): the Commission must send 1% of all sports wagering proceeds to the Problem Gambling Fund (§9-1A-33). The existing 5% General Fund carve-out is reworded from proceeds 'attributable to mobile' to proceeds 'from online' sports wagering.","§9-1D-04: fantasy competition operators' retention falls from 85% to 84%, and 1% of fantasy proceeds goes to the Problem Gambling Fund. The rest still goes to the Blueprint for Maryland's Future Fund.","New §9-1A-33(b)(7): the Secretary of Health must report to the General Assembly on Problem Gambling Fund spending by November 1, 2027 and every November 1 after that.","The Act takes effect July 1, 2026. It reintroduces SB 706 of 2025 and is cross-filed with 6lr3499."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Like IL HB3159, which adds a 2% charge on adjusted gross receipts for fire and EMS, SB862 raises the operator burden to fund a dedicated earmark. Maryland's version is smaller (1 point) and is built by lowering operator retention percentages rather than by imposing a new tax. IL HB1451 and HB2580 expand DHS problem-gambling education without touching operator economics. HI HB1308 and HB2570 create a problem-gambling fund as part of a new legalization framework, whereas Maryland is retrofitting funding onto an existing regime. The mandated mobile-specific prevalence study is unusual among the listed bills.","watch":"Watch the one-point retention cut, which raises mobile operators' effective rate to 21% and retail and fantasy operators' to 16% from July 1, 2026; watch also whether 'proceeds' and 'online' are reconciled with existing definitions, and how the mobile prevalence findings due in 2031 may be used to justify future restrictions on mobile wagering.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":6921,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:55:55.367Z"},"MD-SB883":{"summary":"SB883 authorizes the Maryland Stadium Authority to acquire, finance, and build a Carroll Park Soccer Stadium and Facility in Baltimore City, with up to $216.6 million in Authority bonds outstanding. Debt service on those bonds would come from State sports wagering proceeds: beginning in fiscal 2028, the Comptroller must pay the annual principal and interest into a new Carroll Park fund before any sports wagering money goes to the Blueprint for Maryland's Future Fund or the 5% mobile share for the General Fund.","mechanics":["Adds the Carroll Park Soccer Stadium and Facility (defined in new Econ. Dev. §10-601(p) as a Baltimore City stadium used primarily for professional soccer and as a soccer academy and school) to the Authority's facility list, its site-acquisition power (§10-618(b)(1)(ix)), and its annual tax-revenue reporting (§10-625(a)(2)(viii)).","Sets a bond cap of $216,600,000 for the stadium under §10-628(c)(1)(xii) and creates the nonlapsing Carroll Park Soccer Stadium and Facility Fund (§10-657.7), which keeps its own interest earnings and can be pledged to debt service under §10-634.","Amends State Gov't §9-1E-12(b)(2)(iii): for FY2028 and later, the Comptroller pays the fund the full amount needed for that year's bond principal and interest out of sports wagering proceeds in the State Lottery Fund, ahead of the Blueprint and General Fund distributions.","Leaves operator retention unchanged: 85% for Class A and B facility licensees and 80% for mobile licensees under §9-1E-12(b)(1).","Under new §10-646.5, the Authority must give the legislative fiscal committees a comprehensive financing plan at least 45 days before seeking Board of Public Works approval of each bond issue, and may not issue bonds until it and \"United Partnership\" sign a written operating agreement.","The Act takes effect July 1, 2026; its cross-file is HB 1078."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"Most of the listed bills create or restructure wagering markets or licensing, such as AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686 and HI HB 1308. SB883 does neither: it only redirects existing sports wagering revenue. The closest analogue is IL HB3159, which earmarks a fixed 2% of adjusted gross receipts for fire and EMS. SB883 instead takes an uncapped, first-priority annual draw equal to stadium debt service. IA SSB 1157 and KS HB 2466 also tie gaming or tax-increment policy to sports and tourism infrastructure, but SB883 is unusual in pledging wagering proceeds directly as security for stadium bonds.","watch":"Operators' retention rates are not touched, but a senior, open-ended claim on sports wagering proceeds for up to $216.6M in bonds would shrink money for the Blueprint education fund and could invite pressure to raise the wagering tax again, so watch for amendments to the §9-1E-12(b)(1) retention percentages as the bill or its cross-file HB 1078 moves.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":21455,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:55:57.202Z"},"MD-SB970":{"summary":"SB 970 amends Md. State Government § 9-1E-17(d) to change \"may\" to \"shall,\" so that any sports wagering licensee or operator that advertises in Maryland must contract with an independent evaluator licensed under § 9-1E-17(b) to evaluate and rate its sports wagering content, experts, influencers and content partners. The mandate is contingent on the State Lottery and Gaming Control Commission licensing at least three independent evaluators, and it takes effect July 1, 2026, subject to that contingency.","mechanics":["Section 1 amends § 9-1E-17(d), converting the existing permissive authorization (\"may\") into a mandatory requirement (\"shall\").","Covered parties are sports wagering licensees and sports wagering operators that advertise in the State.","The required contract scope is evaluating and rating the licensee's sports wagering content, sports wagering experts, sports wagering influencers and content partners.","Evaluators must be licensed under § 9-1E-17(b).","Section 2 makes the Act contingent on the Commission issuing licenses to at least three independent evaluators.","Section 3 sets the effective date at July 1, 2026, subject to the Section 2 contingency; the text contains no new penalty or enforcement provision."],"issue_tags":["advertising","licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike the authorization bills (AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, HI HB 1308/HB 2570/SB1569), SB 970 assumes an existing market and targets only marketing content through third-party evaluation. It is closest in spirit to CT HB05229, which restricts gaming advertising on college campuses and limits AI-targeted bets. CT HB05229 imposes direct conduct restrictions, while Maryland instead mandates a paid outside rating relationship for influencer and content-partner marketing. It is also distinct from the age and advertising limits in prediction-market bills such as CT HB05038 and IL HB5059, which address a different product category.","watch":"Operators should track how many independent evaluators the Commission actually licenses, since three triggers the mandate, along with any rules defining the evaluators' rating standards and fees and whether a poor rating will carry advertising consequences that the bill text itself does not specify.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"First - Sports Wagering - Independent Evaluation of Sports Wagering","text_chars":1822,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:56:04.130Z"},"MD-SB884":{"summary":"SB884 would create a new Subtitle 1F in Maryland's State Government Article letting the State Lottery and Gaming Control Commission license and regulate internet poker and other Commission-approved 'skill-based' games, effectively at a 30% rate on proceeds. It also authorizes the Governor to join multistate liquidity-sharing agreements. The sponsor withdrew the bill on March 13, 2026, so it is dead for this session but serves as a template for future Maryland iPoker and online-gaming efforts.","mechanics":["Eligibility (§9-1F-03(b)): licenses may go to video lottery operators, sports wagering facility or mobile sports wagering licensees, applicants with at least 100 full-time Maryland employees, and majority minority-owned applicants or joint ventures. Holders of multiple 1A/1E licenses may apply for only one license, and each licensee may contract with only one operator.","Fees and term: initial license fee is $1,000,000, or $500,000 for minority-owned applicants. The term is 5 years, renewal costs 1% of average annual retained proceeds over the prior 3 years, and the license cannot be transferred for 3 years after issuance.","Tax and distribution (§9-1F-05): the licensee retains 70% of proceeds and the State receives 30%. The State share is split 5.5% to local jurisdictions with video lottery facilities, 6% to the Purse Dedication Account, 1% to Racetrack Facility Renewal, 1.5% to the Small, Minority, and Women-Owned Businesses Account, 1% to the Problem Gambling Fund, and the remainder to the Blueprint for Maryland's Future Fund.","Bad-actor and labor rules: operator and supplier licensees are barred, and may have their license revoked, if they or their affiliates knowingly take revenue from FATF-blacklisted countries, state sponsors of terrorism, or online casino gaming in jurisdictions that prohibit it. They must file annual disclosures of revenue from illegal interactive gaming markets, and applicants must show a labor peace agreement and union neutrality by clear and convincing evidence.","Player protections (§9-1F-04, -06): minimum age is 21, with ID verification and periodic reverification. Credit cards are banned and deposit limits are mandatory. Advertising may not target excluded or at-risk individuals or be false or deceptive. The Commission must run a voluntary exclusion list, and licensees must report annually on players showing erratic gambling behavior.","Other provisions: tampering with games carries a penalty of up to $1,000,000 plus a suspension of at least 30 days. Live dealer studios must be located in Maryland. The Commission may add new skill games after notice to the Legislative Policy Committee (45-day wait) and must study sweepstakes and microtransaction platforms, reporting by December 1, 2029. The act would have taken effect July 1, 2026."],"issue_tags":["legalize","licensing","tax","advertising","integrity","sweeps"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Most listed bills address sports wagering, such as AK HB145/SB194, GA HB686 and the HI bills, whereas MD SB884 assumes mobile sports wagering already exists and builds a separate online poker and skill-game vertical. Its closest analogues are IL HB3080 and HB4797, the Internet Gaming Acts, but those authorize full internet gaming, while SB884 is limited to poker and other games the Commission deems skill-based. SB884 is unusual in combining multistate compact authority, a supplier disclosure requirement on illegal-market revenue, a labor peace mandate, and a mandated study of sweepstakes and microtransaction platforms.","watch":"If revived, the illegal-market revenue bar and annual disclosure duty (§9-1F-02(e)) would reach operators' and suppliers' affiliates everywhere, and the Commission's power to add 'skill-based' games, including live dealer formats, could expand the subtitle well beyond poker, so both deserve close scrutiny.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":40943,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:56:04.969Z"},"ME-LD1304":{"summary":"LD 1304 was a proposed constitutional amendment to Art. IX, §8 of the Maine Constitution. It would have authorized the Legislature to cap property tax increases at 2% per year on the primary residence of a permanent resident aged 65 or older who has owned the home for at least 12 months. It would also have required the State to reimburse municipalities for at least 90% of the lost revenue, using a 2% tax on lottery tickets and sports betting. The measure died in Legislative Files on May 21, 2025 under Joint Rule 310.3 and would have needed two-thirds of each chamber plus voter ratification at a November statewide election.","mechanics":["New Art. IX, §8, sub-§6 lets the Legislature limit property tax increases to 2% per property tax year on the primary residence of a Maine permanent resident aged 65 or older who has owned it for at least 12 months.","If the property is transferred to someone other than a family member, it reverts to just value at the time of transfer.","Municipal revenue lost to the cap must be reimbursed at least 90% by the State.","The reimbursement must come from a constitutionally specified 2% tax on lottery tickets and sports betting. The text does not define the base (handle, gross receipts or adjusted gross revenue) or who bears the tax; the summary calls it a tax 'imposed on the sale of' lottery tickets and sports betting.","Referendum procedure: two-thirds of each branch must concur, followed by a November statewide ballot question. The amendment takes effect on the Governor's proclamation of a majority vote.","Status: dead as of May 21, 2025 (Joint Rule 310.3)."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"Kansas SB303 (House Substitute) is the closest analogue: it also ties a new wagering levy, a 2% excise on the amount wagered, to property tax relief through a property tax relief fund, and it also taxes lottery ticket sales. Kansas did this by statute, whereas Maine would have written the 2% sports-betting and lottery tax into the Constitution itself. Unlike Georgia SR131, which uses a constitutional amendment to authorize sports betting, LD 1304 assumes an existing legal market and earmarks it for senior homeowner relief. It also differs from Illinois HB3159, which adds a 2% adjusted gross receipts levy earmarked for fire and EMS. Because the Maine text leaves the 2% base undefined, it could have been read as a handle tax similar to Kansas SB303 rather than a tax on receipts like Illinois HB3159.","watch":"Although LD 1304 is dead, operators should watch for its reintroduction, because it would constitutionally lock in an additional 2% levy with an undefined base (potentially handle rather than gross gaming revenue) on top of Maine's existing sports wagering tax, insulated from ordinary legislative repeal.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":4638,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:56:15.107Z"},"MD-SB885":{"summary":"SB885 would create a new Subtitle 1F in the State Government Article letting the State Lottery and Gaming Control Commission license internet casino gaming (iGaming) and online electronic instant bingo, regulated on the same footing as VLTs, table games, mobile sports wagering and electronic bingo. It sets licensee eligibility (VLT operators, certain combined retail/mobile sports wagering licensees, qualifying Maryland-headquartered firms, and social-equity applicants), effective tax rates of 40% (20% for live dealer), fee levels, responsible-gaming and labor-peace requirements, and a Video Lottery Facility Employee Displacement Fund. According to the purpose clause, the Act is also submitted to a statewide referendum.","mechanics":["Tax: under §9-1F-05(b)(1)(ii) licensees retain 80% of gaming proceeds from live dealer games and 60% from all other iGaming, so the effective state share is 20% and 40%. Online bingo licensees retain 60% (§9-1F-05(c)). For each licensee's first 5 years, gaming proceeds exclude a tiered share of redeemed free play and promotional credits, sliding from 35% (proceeds under $4M) down to 20% (proceeds over $12M), per §9-1F-01(f)(3).","Eligibility (§9-1F-03(b)): VLT operators; sports wagering licensees holding both a facility and a mobile license, each issued on or before Dec 31, 2026; and Maryland firms with a 10-year in-state headquarters, at least 250 in-state FTEs, a prior Commission qualification to hold 5% or more of a VLT operator (2018 to 2025), a Maryland-associated brand, and a $5M live studio or film studio commitment. Holders of multiple 1A/1E licenses get only one license, plus an extra license if they partner with social equity owners holding at least 33%. Five additional licenses go through two competitive rounds, with the first round reserved for applicants at least 33% owned by social equity applicants; all other applicants need at least 5% social equity ownership or an employee profit-sharing alternative. Commercial bingo operators may hold an online bingo license but not an iGaming license.","Fees and term: initial iGaming license fee is $1M, or $500K if live-dealer only. For major minority-owned or joint venture applicants (51%), it is $500K or $250K. The online bingo fee is $500K. Terms are 5 years, and renewal costs 1% of average annual retained proceeds over the prior 3 years. Each licensee may contract with no more than one online gaming operator. Ownership cannot be transferred for 3 years after issuance, with limited exceptions. Reciprocity waivers are available for applicants licensed in Maryland or at least three other states.","Supplier integrity (§9-1F-02(e)): operators and suppliers that knowingly accept revenue from FATF-blacklisted jurisdictions, state sponsors of terrorism, or illegal US gambling are barred or subject to revocation. They must also file annual disclosures of revenue from supplying content to illegal interactive gaming markets, and misstatements can lead to denial, suspension or revocation and penalties on officers.","Consumer protection and responsible gaming (§§9-1F-04, 9-1F-06): players must be 21 or older, with age and identity verified at signup and periodically reverified. Credit cards are banned. Mandatory deposit limits are required, and limit increases are delayed at least 24 hours. Players can set self-suspensions, and no gaming emails may be sent during suspensions of 72 hours or more. Monthly problem-gambling disclosure certifications, a voluntary exclusion list and annual reports on erratic play are required. Advertising targeted at prohibited or at-risk individuals, and false or deceptive advertising, are prohibited.","Labor and enforcement: applicants, licensees and live dealer studio operators must show by clear and convincing evidence a labor peace agreement (unless already under a CBA) that bars strikes for 5 years, plus employer neutrality on unionization (§9-1F-04(c)). Tampering penalties run up to $1M plus a suspension of at least 30 days (§9-1F-07). Revenue distributions begin in FY2028, including up to $10M in year one to the employee displacement fund, set amounts to VLT jurisdictions and racing accounts, 1% to the agency, 1% to problem gambling, 5% to counties for education, and the remainder to the Blueprint Fund."],"issue_tags":["legalize","licensing","tax","advertising","integrity","prop"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"The closest analogues on the list are Illinois' Internet Gaming Act bills, IL HB3080 and HB4797, which likewise authorize iGaming through licensees and contracted management service providers. Maryland's version is distinguished by several features: an explicit 40% headline rate with a lower 20% rate for live dealer play, a tiered promotional-credit deduction, heavy social-equity ownership set-asides, a labor peace requirement, a displacement fund for casino workers, and a referendum requirement. Most other listed bills (AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, HI HB1308/HB2570/SB1569) concern sports wagering only. CT HB05229 overlaps only on advertising and responsible-gaming controls.","watch":"Sportsbook clients should note that iGaming eligibility for sports wagering licensees is limited to those holding both a facility and a mobile license issued by December 31, 2026, with only one license per multi-license holder, and should weigh the 40% effective rate, the 5-year-only and declining promotional deduction, the single-operator-per-licensee limit, and the labor peace and neutrality obligations.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":61349,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:56:17.453Z"},"ME-LD1188":{"summary":"As printed, LD 1188 lets Maine commercial harness racetrack licensees (8 MRSA §271) sell pari-mutuel pools and common pari-mutuel pools on simulcast races at any facility licensed for sports wagering under 8 MRSA §1206. This bridges the split between Harness Racing Commission (Agriculture) and Gambling Control Unit (Public Safety) oversight. It also repeals the §275-B(3) commission-approved facility pathway, which was otherwise set to sunset January 1, 2026; note that the enacted title focuses on that §275-B(3) provision, so the final chaptered text may differ from this printed version.","mechanics":["Sec. 1 amends 8 MRSA §275-B(1) so a §271 racetrack licensee may sell pari-mutuel and simulcast common pools within its track enclosure, at its §1011 slot facility, or at any licensee's facility licensed under §1206 (sports wagering), not only its own.","The printed bill strikes §275-B(3), the commission-approved facility pathway. That pathway was limited to the same county as the track, barred in a municipality hosting an off-track betting facility, required municipal approval, and carried a 5% share of the track's §286(5) commission to the host municipality; it had a January 1, 2026 sunset.","Sec. 2 amends §275-C(1), replacing the reference to commission-approved §275-B(3) facilities with any §1206 licensed facility as a permitted location for common pari-mutuel pool sales.","Off-track betting licensees under §275-D are unchanged and still limited to their licensed facility.","It was enacted as an emergency measure with a two-thirds vote, effective on approval, so it would be in place for the 2025 racing season starting in April."],"issue_tags":["licensing","legalize"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed. What stands out is that Maine routes horse-race pari-mutuel wagering into sports-wagering retail locations by statutory cross-reference to the §1206 sports wagering license, rather than folding racing into the sports wagering regime itself. The change in title from the printed version (\"Include Harness Racing with Other Sports for Purposes of Sports Wagering\") to the enacted one suggests the committee may have narrowed or restructured it, possibly keeping or extending §275-B(3) rather than repealing it.","watch":"Confirm the final chaptered text, since the enacted title suggests amendment, and check whether §1206 sports wagering licensees must host racetrack pari-mutuel sales, how revenue and space are split, and whether Harness Racing Commission rules now reach operations inside Gambling Control Unit-licensed facilities.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"LD 1188 HP 793","text_chars":5424,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:56:17.778Z"},"ME-LD1164":{"summary":"LD 1164 creates a new chapter 39 in Title 8 legalizing internet casino gaming (card, dice and other games of chance such as blackjack, poker, craps, roulette and baccarat) in Maine and restricts operator licenses exclusively to the federally recognized Wabanaki tribes, one license each, regulated by the Gambling Control Unit in the Department of Public Safety. Commercial operators can take part only as licensed suppliers or as a tribe's single management services partner, under a director-approved contract with a cap on the partner's share of revenue. The chaptered text shows it became Public Law 2025, chapter 538 without the Governor's signature (dated January 11, 2026), not by signature as the status line suggests.","mechanics":["Eligibility and license limits (§1406): only a federally recognized Indian nation, tribe or band in Maine may hold an Internet gaming license, limited to one per tribe. The license is non-transferable except to a Maine-based entity wholly owned by the tribe. The fee is $50,000 a year plus processing costs, temporary licenses are available, and play is limited to persons physically located in the State.","Management services (§§1403(2)(F), 1406(3), 1408): each tribal licensee may contract with no more than one management services licensee. The contract must be pre-approved by the director, and the partner may receive no more than 30% of adjusted gross Internet gaming receipts, or up to 40% if the director finds the fee commercially reasonable given the partner's capital investment. The partner may not subcontract duties or compensation to anyone without a management services license. Supplier and management services licenses cost $10,000 a year each.","Definitions (§1402): adjusted gross Internet gaming receipts are gross receipts less winnings paid and federal excise tax. Internet gaming excludes chapter 31 table games and slots, pari-mutuel pools, fantasy contests, mobile sports wagering under chapter 35 and advance deposit wagering. The director must levy a tax on adjusted gross receipts (§1403(1)(C)), but no rate appears in the portion of text provided.","Suitability and enforcement (§§1404-1405): 10% ownership and control disclosure, fingerprint-based state and FBI background checks, and 30-day notice of material changes. Sanctions range from reprimand to revocation, with civil penalties up to $25,000 per violation and appeal to Superior Court.","Player protection and advertising (§§1403(2), 1412): 21+ age minimum, identity and geolocation verification, and a self-exclusion list. Rules must bar misleading advertising, require operators to disclose their tribal status, and restrict, to the extent permissible, advertising likely to reach or appeal to persons under 21. Wagers from insiders, interested parties, proxies and Gambling Control Unit staff are prohibited.","Integrity, records and child support (§§1413-1415): operators must report abnormal wagering, keep wager records for 3 years, share anonymized integrity data on request, and may use cloud hosting. Before any payout at or above the W-2G threshold, operators must check the DHHS child-support registry, withhold the debt, remit it within 7 days and may retain $10. Conforming amendments to §1210 allow fee-free cross-licensing of sports wagering and iGaming occupational licensees."],"issue_tags":["legalize","licensing","tax","advertising","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Illinois HB3080 and HB4797 would create a commercial Internet Gaming Act open to multiple licensees and management services providers, whereas Maine LD 1164 gives tribes exclusive operator status and caps each tribe at one platform partner with a regulated revenue split, mirroring Maine's existing tribal mobile sports wagering model. Indiana HB1092 imposes a child-support hold on sports wagering account withdrawals; Maine's §1415 intercept instead triggers at W-2G-level payouts and extends the obligation to the management services licensee. Connecticut HB05229 legislates specific advertising limits such as campus restrictions, while Maine delegates advertising restrictions to director rulemaking, including under-21 reach and tribal-disclosure requirements.","watch":"Operators seeking Maine iGaming market access should focus on the 30% (up to 40%) cap on management services compensation, the one-partner-per-tribe limit and the director's prior approval of contracts and material changes, along with the tax rate and effective date, which do not appear in the excerpt provided.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":63047,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:56:23.357Z"},"ME-LD1862":{"summary":"LD 1862 would have added a new 8 MRSA §1220 declaring that, notwithstanding any other law, jurisdiction over sports wagering conducted in person on the property of a facility operator rests solely with the Gambling Control Board (established under 8 MRSA §1002, within the Department of Public Safety). It is a one-section jurisdictional clarification and does not change tax rates, licensing fees, or wagering rules. The bill died on May 20, 2025, when it was placed in Legislative Files under Joint Rule 310.3.","mechanics":["Sec. 1 enacts 8 MRSA §1220, titled 'Jurisdiction,' within Maine's sports wagering chapter.","It covers only in-person sports wagering conducted on the property of a 'facility operator'. Mobile and online wagering are not addressed.","Exclusive jurisdiction is vested in the Gambling Control Board under §1002, and the grant applies 'notwithstanding any provision of law to the contrary.'","The text contains no new licensing, tax, enforcement, or effective-date provisions. Default effective-date rules for nonemergency legislation would have applied.","Sponsored by Sen. Baldacci (Penobscot) and referred to the Committee on Veterans and Legal Affairs. It died in committee files on May 20, 2025."],"issue_tags":["licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. What is unusual is that the bill does nothing except allocate regulatory authority. Its 'notwithstanding any provision of law to the contrary' clause appears designed to foreclose competing claims of jurisdiction over retail sportsbooks at facility-operator premises. The text does not say whose claims those would be.","watch":"Watch whether this exclusive-jurisdiction language is revived, because the notwithstanding clause could override other statutory or negotiated allocations of authority over retail sports wagering at facility-operator sites. That would affect which regulator licenses, audits, and disciplines operators there.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":1022,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:56:30.669Z"},"ME-LD1838":{"summary":"LD 1838 would have created a new 8 MRSA chapter 39 authorizing licensed electronic wagering terminals offering historical horse racing, electronic beano and virtual sealed tickets, regulated by the Gambling Control Unit director. Eligible operators were limited to Maine's federally recognized tribes, commercial harness tracks without casino licenses, certain charitable games-of-chance licensees, and existing off-track betting facilities. Operators would remit 25% of net terminal income for distribution to agriculture, harness racing, veterans' homes, education, problem-gambling and municipal funds. The bill died in the Legislative Files on June 13, 2025.","mechanics":["Terminal caps under §1503(4): 100 per commercial track, 25 per OTB facility, and 4 per charitable organization, each subject to municipal approval. Tribes may operate up to 100 total, either on tribal territory or in municipalities that approve.","§1501(4) defines an electronic wagering terminal as not a slot machine under §1001(39). Outcomes must come from historical race results, a central-server beano draw of no more than 75 numbers, or a predetermined sealed-ticket deal. Terminals may not pay out cash, only vouchers.","§1504 requires 25% of net terminal income to be remitted to the state. The split is 1% GCU administration, 1% problem gambling, 2% agricultural fairs, 5% harness purses, 1% harness promotion, 1% Sire Stakes, 5% Maine Milk Pool (Agricultural Development Fund from 2035), 5% Veterans' Homes, 3% career and technical education, and 1% host municipality. Late payments accrue 1.5% interest per month, and knowing nonpayment is a Class D crime with possible forfeiture.","Operator license fees under §1503(3) and (6) may not exceed the greater of $500 or $10 per terminal, plus $50 per terminal annually. Terminal distributors pay $5,000 a year plus background investigation costs, and distributor employees pay $250 initially and $50 on renewal (§1505). Leases may not give a distributor more than 30% of net terminal income.","Players must be at least 21 (§1503(8)). Civil fines under §1506 run up to $1,000 per violation, capped at $5,000 per transaction or occurrence. Required rules under §1508 include self-exclusion using the §1003 list and concurrent use of facilities and staff with pari-mutuel, sports wagering and high-stakes beano operations.","Cooperative agreements under §1503(5) may colocate terminals at a tribal or track facility or link them through an in-state shared wagering server, and their terms are confidential."],"issue_tags":["legalize","licensing","tax"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states were listed. On its own terms, the bill is unusual in pairing historical horse racing with electronic beano and virtual pull-tabs on a single terminal type. It also statutorily declares these terminals not to be slot machines, sidestepping Maine's casino framework. Its 25% contribution is earmarked entirely to dedicated funds rather than the General Fund, which reflects a coalition built around agriculture, harness racing, the Wabanaki tribes and veterans.","watch":"Although LD 1838 is dead, operators should watch for reintroduction, particularly the §1501(4) not-a-slot-machine carve-out and the expanded tribal gaming footprint in a state where tribes already hold exclusive mobile sports wagering, as well as §1508(1)(E), which would let licensees run these terminals alongside sports wagering operations.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":25188,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:56:33.849Z"},"ME-LD1892":{"summary":"LD 1892 would have created a dedicated School Construction Debt Service Fund (new 20-A MRSA §15919) with a target of at least $175 million in FY2026-27 and $200 million a year after that, financed by earmarking the first dollars of several existing state revenue streams. Those streams include lottery revenue, slot machine income, the General Fund share of sports wagering receipts, cannabis and tobacco taxes, and General Fund surplus. It would also have set a 75/25 state/local cost share for school construction and created a Maine Public School Financing Authority seeded with $100 million from the Budget Stabilization Fund. The bill died in the Senate at sine die adjournment on April 29, 2026.","mechanics":["Sec. 5 would amend 8 MRSA §1218(1)(F) so that, from FY2026-27, the first $5,000,000 of adjusted gross sports wagering receipts goes to the debt service fund rather than the General Fund. The 6.5% distribution rate itself is unchanged.","Sec. 4 would amend 8 MRSA §1036(1) to divert the first $5,000,000 of gross slot machine income to the fund. That subsection otherwise covers the 1% administrative distribution to the Gambling Control Board, so the drafting is ambiguous about which pool the $5,000,000 comes from.","Secs. 2–3 would divert the first $32,500,000 of State Lottery revenue and agents' license fees (8 MRSA §386) and the first $32,500,000 of Tri-state Lotto receipts (§415). The $65,000,000 combined total matches §15919(1)(A)(7).","The remaining fund sources are General Fund surplus ($35M in FY27, then $60M a year), cigarette tax ($40M), tobacco products tax ($10M), cannabis sales tax ($10M), cannabis excise tax ($5M), federal funds, and any other source. Any shortfall would be backfilled from lapsed General Purpose Aid balances.","Each year $20M would be reserved for projects involving district consolidation. The State would pay at least 75% of debt service and local units at least 25%, adjustable for ability to pay. All projects would have to be green-certified.","The new Financing Authority would sell bonds to fund local shares and could offer zero-interest financing, and its securities would be state tax-exempt. All provisions would take effect July 1, 2026 / FY2026-27."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. What is unusual is that the bill does not change any gaming tax rate or licensing term. It instead re-prioritizes existing lottery, slot and sports wagering distributions by taking fixed-dollar 'first money' carve-outs for school construction ahead of the General Fund, bundled with tobacco and cannabis revenue in a single earmark.","watch":"Although the bill is dead, operators should watch for reintroduction because the Sec. 5 and Sec. 4 drafting refers to the first $5 million of gross receipts or income rather than of the State's share, which could be read as a new carve-out beyond current distributions, and because fixed-dollar earmarks tend to create pressure to raise sports wagering rates if receipts fall short.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":20639,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:56:37.572Z"},"ME-LD2135":{"summary":"Enacts 37-B MRSA §513-B creating a Veterans' Housing Provider Incentive Program and Fund within the Maine Bureau of Veterans' Services to award grants to 501(c)(3) organizations supporting permanent housing for veterans. Section 2 directs the State Controller to transfer $90,000 from the Department of Public Safety, Gambling Control Unit's Sports Wagering program Other Special Revenue Funds account to the new fund by June 30, 2027. Signed as Public Law Chapter 636 on April 3, 2026.","mechanics":["Sec. 1: Creates the Veterans' Housing Provider Incentive Fund under the Bureau of Veterans' Services, accepting state, federal and private funds.","Grants limited to IRS 501(c)(3) organizations with a core program supporting permanent veteran housing; uses include housing provider incentives, risk mitigation payments and past rental debt for homeless veterans.","Bureau may adopt routine technical rules, including grantee reporting requirements; annual report to the veterans affairs committee due February 15, 2027 and each February 15 in a year grants are issued.","Sec. 2: One-time transfer of $90,000 from the Gambling Control Unit Sports Wagering program Other Special Revenue Funds account, notwithstanding any other law, on or before June 30, 2027.","Sec. 3: Allocates $90,000 in Other Special Revenue Funds for FY 2026-27 ($0 in FY 2025-26) to fund N570."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. The bill is unusual only in that it uses a 'notwithstanding' transfer to sweep a fixed $90,000 from Maine's sports wagering special revenue account for an unrelated veterans housing purpose, rather than changing wagering tax rates, licensing or statutory distribution formulas.","watch":"Although the $90,000 one-time sweep does not change operator tax rates or obligations, operators should monitor whether the Legislature increasingly treats the Sports Wagering program account as a discretionary funding source, which could create pressure to raise wagering revenue or divert funds from regulatory operations.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":3247,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:56:45.044Z"},"ME-LD811":{"summary":"LD 811 would have amended 8 MRSA §1206 to require an applicant for a Maine facility sports wagering license to obtain approval from the municipal officers of the host municipality (or county commissioners in an unincorporated place), after a local public hearing, before the Gambling Control Unit director could issue the license. Denied applicants could appeal to the director, who could override the local denial only on clear and convincing evidence that it lacked justifiable cause. The bill died in committee on March 20, 2025 under Joint Rule 310.3.","mechanics":["New 8 MRSA §1206(2-A) adds municipal approval as a licensing condition on top of the existing eligibility requirements in subsection 2. It applies to facility sports wagering licenses, which are tied to an underlying off-track betting license.","Municipal officers must hold a public hearing before approving or denying, state their reasons, and send a written decision to both the applicant and the Gambling Control Unit director.","An aggrieved applicant may appeal to the director within 15 days of receiving the written decision. The director must hold a public hearing in the host municipality and may consider all licensure requirements and the municipal findings.","The director may issue a license over a local denial only if the director finds by clear and convincing evidence that the denial was without justifiable cause.","For facilities in unincorporated places, approval comes from the county commissioners.","Amended §1206(5) makes a facility license valid only at the licensee's municipally approved physical location. The existing one-year term (for licenses on or after Sept. 1, 2024) and the voiding of the facility license if the OTB license lapses are unchanged."],"issue_tags":["licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states were listed. The bill stands out for adding a local-option, liquor-license-style veto layered onto state sports wagering licensing. It is also unusual in putting a heightened clear-and-convincing burden on applicants who appeal a local denial to the state regulator. It reaches only retail facility licenses tied to OTB licensees and leaves Maine's mobile wagering framework untouched.","watch":"Although LD 811 is dead, operators with OTB-linked retail facility plans should watch for reintroduction, because a local hearing-and-approval requirement with a clear-and-convincing appeal standard would give municipalities a practical veto over siting and could also be applied at renewal, given the one-year license term and the location-specific validity language.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"LD 811 HP 518","text_chars":4476,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:56:47.253Z"},"ME-LD2080":{"summary":"LD 2080 amends Maine's sports wagering law (8 MRSA ch. 31) to bar operators from accepting credit cards to fund wagers and adds a new §1220 listing the payment methods operators may accept. It also directs the Gambling Control Unit director's rules to require both retail facility operators and mobile operators to have methods for ensuring wagers are not made by credit card. The enacted title also references internet gaming, but the printed text supplied covers only sports wagering, which suggests an amendment that is not reflected here.","mechanics":["Sec. 1 adds 8 MRSA §1202(4-A), defining \"digital wallet\" as any digital or electronic technology that stores a person's financial information and allows purchases of goods and services.","Sec. 2 amends the rulemaking mandate in §1203(2)(C) so that rules on the use of credit and checks must provide that a person may not make a wager using a credit card.","Secs. 3 and 4 amend §1203(2)(F) and (G). Rules setting minimum design and security requirements, for facility premises (including self-serve kiosks) and for mobile apps and digital platforms, must include methods for ensuring a person does not use a credit card to make a wager.","Sec. 5 enacts §1220, which lists permitted payment methods: online and mobile payment systems supporting online money transfers; debit cards; digital wallets, but only where the wallet provider gives assurance that no credit card was used to deposit funds; prepaid cards not purchased with credit cards; bank wires; cash; and any other method the director approves.","§1220 closes with a flat rule that an operator may not accept payment for a wager by credit card.","The text sets no specific penalty or effective date. Enforcement runs through the director's existing rulemaking and disciplinary authority, and Maine's general effective date for non-emergency legislation applies."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike the authorization bills listed, such as AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686 and HI HB 1308/SB1569, Maine's LD 2080 assumes an existing regulated market and targets only how wagers are funded. It is closest in kind to CT HB05229, which regulates withdrawals from gaming accounts, and IN HB1092, which requires withholding mobile account withdrawals for delinquent child support. Both of those address money movement in betting accounts, but neither, as summarized, bans credit card funding. LD 2080 is unusual in pushing the credit-card ban through to indirect channels: it conditions digital wallets on provider \"assurance\" and prepaid cards on not having been bought with a credit card.","watch":"Watch how the director's implementing rules define the \"assurance\" digital wallet providers must give and how operators must verify prepaid card provenance, given the unconditioned \"online and mobile payment systems\" category that may itself be credit-funded, and confirm whether the enacted version extends the ban to internet gaming, as its amended title indicates.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"LD 2080 HP 1395","text_chars":4145,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:56:48.321Z"},"ME-LD2007":{"summary":"LD 2007 (Public Law 2026, c. 645, approved April 6, 2026) bans online sweepstakes games that use a dual-currency payment model to simulate casino-style gaming, including sports wagering. Anyone who operates, promotes or supports the operation or promotion of such a game commits a civil violation punishable by fines of $10,000 to $100,000 under new 8 MRSA §1352. The same conduct is also made criminal unlawful gambling under new 17-A MRSA §954(1-B). Maine gaming licensees who violate the ban face mandatory license revocation and lifetime-style ineligibility for future licenses.","mechanics":["Definition (8 MRSA §1351(6)): an 'online sweepstakes game' is any internet game, contest or promotion that meets three tests. It is accessible on a phone, computer or similar device; it uses a 'dual-currency system of payment'; and it simulates casino-style gaming such as slots, poker, table games, lottery, bingo or sports wagering.","Dual-currency system (§1351(3)): play with direct consideration (purchased or bonus coins) or indirect consideration (free or bundled coins redeemable for prizes or cash), or any design that bars direct purchase of redeemable coins while encouraging purchases to obtain them. The Gambling Control Unit may further define the term by rule, and those rules are major substantive rules requiring legislative approval.","Civil penalty (§1352(1)): fines of $10,000 to $100,000 per violation for operating, promoting or supporting the operation or promotion of an online sweepstakes game. The Unit collects the fines, which are deposited in the Gambling Addiction Prevention and Treatment Fund (5 MRSA §20006-B, amended by Sec. 1).","License consequences (§1352(2)-(3)): revocation is mandatory for any 'licensee' convicted under 17-A §954(1-B) or found in violation through the administrative process for its license. Covered licenses are those under 8 MRSA chapters 31, 33, 35 (sports wagering) and 39, and 17 MRSA chapter 62. Any violator is ineligible for any of those licenses.","Criminal liability (Sec. 3): the same conduct is made unlawful gambling under 17-A MRSA §954(1-B). The text does not set a separate offense class or a specific effective date."],"issue_tags":["sweeps","licensing","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Among the listed bills, the closest analogue is CA AB 831. It works by amending California's existing unfair-practices rules for contests and sweepstakes, whereas Maine creates a standalone prohibition chapter keyed to a statutory 'dual-currency' definition, backed by both civil fines and a criminal unlawful-gambling offense. Maine's approach contrasts with Illinois HB3274 and HB5048, which would shield prize and gift kiosk devices from Gaming Board discipline rather than restrict sweepstakes-style products. Unlike the authorization bills listed (AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, HI HB 1308), LD 2007 is purely prohibitory and ties sweepstakes enforcement directly to the licensure status of existing Maine sports wagering and casino licensees.","watch":"The 'supports the operation or promotion' language combines with mandatory revocation and permanent ineligibility for chapter 35 sports wagering licensees, so operators should audit any B2B, affiliate, marketing, payment or supplier relationships that touch dual-currency sweepstakes products. They should also monitor the Unit's major substantive rulemaking defining 'dual-currency system of payment' for reach into their own free-to-play or bonus-coin promotions.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":5477,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:56:48.361Z"},"MI-HB4346":{"summary":"This substitute bill amends section 15 of Michigan's Lawful Internet Gaming Act (MCL 432.315) to change how the existing internet gaming tax under section 14 is divided among recipients. It cuts the Michigan agriculture equine industry development fund's share from 5% to 4% and raises the state internet gaming fund's share from 65% to 66%. It also replaces the flat $3,000,000 annual cap on the equine allocation with a stepped cap of $9M, then $12M, then $15M, with any excess going to the section 16 fund. It does not change the tax rate or the obligations of operators.","mechanics":["Sec. 15(1)(a): the host city (Detroit) keeps 30% of internet gaming tax revenue, restricted to listed uses such as police, public safety, blight, youth programs, tax relief, capital improvements and roads.","Sec. 15(1)(b): the state share deposited in the internet gaming fund rises from 65% to 66%.","Sec. 15(1)(c): the equine industry development fund share falls from 5% to 4%. The current $3,000,000 cap is replaced by $9,000,000 for FY ending 9/30/2026, $12,000,000 for FY 2027, and $15,000,000 for FY 2028 and each year after. Any excess goes to the section 16 fund.","Sec. 15(2): keeps the $183,000,000 floor for the host city, made up from the fund, which counts internet gaming, casino wagering tax and lawful sports betting act allocations plus development agreement payments. The city's combined take is capped at 55% of section 14 tax receipts.","Enacting section 1 tie-bar: the bill takes effect only if HB 4347 or Senate request S01712'25 of the 103rd Legislature is enacted.","Status: passed the House. Referred to the Senate Committee on Regulatory Affairs on 2026-03-04."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Like IL HB3159, which would earmark 2% of sports wagering adjusted gross receipts for fire and EMS, this bill deals with where gaming tax money goes rather than how much is collected. Unlike IL HB3159, it reshuffles percentages within the existing tax instead of adding a new levy on operators. It also resembles the revenue-use provisions of IA SSB 1157 and the earmarking approach of KS HB2063, but it is narrowly focused on agricultural and equine industry support. One unusual feature is that the title says \"remove cap\" while the engrossed substitute in fact keeps a cap, raising it in steps from $3M to $15M.","watch":"Operator tax liability is unchanged, but counsel should track the tie-barred HB 4347 (likely the parallel lawful sports betting act change) and watch whether larger guaranteed earmarks to the equine fund and the Detroit $183M floor squeeze the state fund enough to prompt pressure for rate increases.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Engrossed","text_chars":4193,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:56:59.916Z"},"MI-HB4576":{"summary":"The Senate substitute turns HB 4576 into a multi-department FY2025-26 supplemental appropriations act totaling $633,999,000. It mostly re-appropriates one-time items, and Sec. 206 caps spending at the unexpended balances of the same-purpose FY2024-25 appropriations under 2024 PA 121, 2025 PA 15 and 2025 PA 22. Its only gaming content is a small Treasury carryforward in Sec. 114 for Michigan Gaming Control Board and Lottery operations and IT, funded partly from the internet sports betting, internet gaming and fantasy contest funds.","mechanics":["Sec. 101: gross appropriation of $633,999,000, of which $351,454,600 is general fund and $245,081,600 is other state restricted revenue; Sec. 201 puts state-source spending at $596,536,200.","Sec. 114 (Treasury): one-time lines of $1,584,900 for casino gaming control operations, $1,382,700 for gaming control IT services and projects, $1,050,100 for lottery IT and $1,950,000 for lottery operations.","Sec. 114 funding sources: $853,500 from the internet sports betting fund, $1,707,000 from the internet gaming fund, $407,100 from the fantasy contest fund and $3,000,100 from the state lottery fund.","Sec. 206: all spending is limited to unexpended FY2024-25 balances for the same purposes, so these are carryforwards rather than new money.","The non-gaming bulk includes $159.3M for Make it in Michigan (Sec. 1003 creates the Make it in Michigan competitiveness fund), $102.6M in community enhancement grants, $50M in indigent defense grants and $31.5M for IT investment.","The supplied text cuts off mid-Sec. 1003, and it contains no changes to gaming tax rates, licensing or conduct rules."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. The bill is unusual mainly because its caption describes a Department of Education budget while the Senate substitute is an omnibus carryforward bill. Its gaming relevance is limited to drawing regulator operating and IT money from the internet sports betting, iGaming and fantasy contest funds.","watch":"Watch whether the $853,500 internet sports betting fund draw and the related MGCB operations and IT lines survive conference unchanged, since they show how operator-generated restricted funds are being used to pay for regulator operations and technology.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Engrossed","text_chars":55108,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:57:00.186Z"},"MI-HB5619":{"summary":"HB 5619 is Michigan's FY2026-27 omnibus appropriations act. The portion of the text provided covers only Article 1, the Department of Agriculture and Rural Development: $132,213,600 gross ($74,222,200 GF/GP), plus standard boilerplate on reporting, contracting, E-Verify, foreign-adversary contracting bans, and DEI spending limits. The only gaming-adjacent content is Sec. 108, which funds horse-racing purses and breeder awards from the Agriculture Equine Industry Development Fund.","mechanics":["Sec. 108 (Fairs and Expositions) appropriates $9,332,200, all from the Agriculture Equine Industry Development Fund with $0 GF/GP.","Sec. 108 line items: county fairs $3,000,000; fairs and racing $201,600; purses and supplements for fairs and licensed tracks $4,073,600; standardbred breeders' awards $345,900; standardbred purses and supplements at licensed tracks $991,100; standardbred sire stakes $720,000.","Sec. 101 totals for the department are $132,213,600 gross, $15,292,000 federal, $42,392,800 state restricted, and $74,222,200 GF/GP, for the fiscal year ending September 30, 2027.","Sec. 223 requires E-Verify for contractors and bars contracts with listed foreign adversaries (China, Russia, Iran, North Korea, Cuba, the Maduro regime, Syria).","Secs. 226-227 bar use of state funds for services to non-qualified aliens and for DEI programs as defined by federal EOs 14151, 14168 and 14173.","The provided text is truncated in Article 1. Other departmental articles, which could include the Gaming Control Board or Lottery, are not shown."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. As an omnibus budget, the bill is unusual only in bundling multiple departments into one act. Its sole visible wagering link is the funding of pari-mutuel horse-racing purses and standardbred programs through a dedicated equine fund rather than the general fund.","watch":"Obtain the full engrossed text and check the later articles, especially LARA/Gaming Control Board, Treasury/Lottery and any boilerplate on internet gaming or sports betting fund transfers, for changes to regulatory funding, fee use or restricted-fund diversions, since the visible Article 1 touches gaming only through racing purse appropriations.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Engrossed","text_chars":1554946,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:57:00.806Z"},"MI-HB4706":{"summary":"HB 4706, enacted as Public Act 22 of 2025 with immediate effect on October 7, 2025, is Michigan's omnibus appropriations act for FY 2025-2026, with supplemental appropriations for FY 2024-2025, covering multiple departments and the judicial and legislative branches. The text supplied covers only Article 1 (Department of Agriculture and Rural Development) and cuts off in Sec. 701. Within that portion, the only gaming-related content is horse-racing funding in Sec. 108; no sports betting, iGaming, or Gaming Control Board provisions appear in the text provided.","mechanics":["Article 1 appropriates $145,885,000 gross to the Department of Agriculture and Rural Development for FY ending September 30, 2026, including $77,496,400 general fund/general purpose (Sec. 101).","Sec. 108 (Fairs and Expositions) appropriates $4,764,200 for horse racing and fairs. Of that, $4,514,200 comes from the agriculture equine industry development fund and $250,000 from the general fund.","Sec. 108 line items include fairs and racing ($258,600), the horse racing advisory commission ($125,000), purses and supplements for fairs and licensed tracks ($2,073,600), standardbred breeders awards ($345,900), standardbred purses at licensed tracks ($991,100), and standardbred sire stakes ($720,000).","Sec. 210 authorizes contingency appropriations that can be spent only after transfer to a line item under MCL 18.1393: up to $3,000,000 federal, $2,000,000 state restricted, $100,000 local, and $100,000 private.","General boilerplate in the 200-series sections imposes reporting duties, including a Sec. 229 report on federal policy changes affecting regulated industries, plus E-Verify contractor requirements (Sec. 228) and legislatively directed grant controls (Sec. 239).","Effective date is October 7, 2025, with immediate effect."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. What stands out is that the provided text touches gaming only through pari-mutuel horse-racing purse and breeder funding, routed through the agriculture equine industry development fund. Any articles funding the Michigan Gaming Control Board, the Lottery, or problem-gambling programs are not included in the excerpt and cannot be assessed here.","watch":"Obtain the full enrolled text and review any later articles covering the Gaming Control Board, Lottery, and Treasury for boilerplate conditions on internet sports betting fund allocations, problem-gambling funding, or regulator reporting duties, none of which appear in the truncated Article 1 excerpt.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":1547680,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:57:01.263Z"},"MI-SB95":{"summary":"SB 95 (House substitute) creates the Hospital Price Transparency Act. It bars a Michigan-licensed hospital from pursuing collection actions, meaning referral to collectors, lawsuits, arbitration enforcement or credit reporting, for debts tied to dates when it was not in material compliance with federal price transparency rules (42 USC 300gg-18(e)) or did not post a state-mandated list of at least 300 shoppable services. It gives patients a civil action with refund, penalty, fee-shifting and credit-report-removal remedies. Wagering appears only in a definitional carve-out.","mechanics":["Sec. 3(1): a hospital may not initiate or pursue a collection action for items and services provided on a date it was out of material compliance with hospital price transparency laws or lacked the required public list.","The required list must cover standard charges for at least 300 shoppable services, including the 70 CMS-specified services where provided. It must show payer-specific negotiated charges, the discounted cash price, de-identified min/max charges and billing codes, and be free, searchable, prominently posted and accessible without registration (Sec. 3(1)(a)-(e)).","Critical access hospitals are covered only beginning 6 months after the effective date (Sec. 3(2)).","Remedies (Sec. 3(4)): if a judge or jury finds noncompliance, the hospital must refund amounts paid, pay the patient a penalty equal to the total debt, dismiss related suits with prejudice, pay attorney fees and costs, and remove credit reports. Collection is stayed while the action is pending, and these remedies are exclusive (Sec. 3(5)).","Gaming reference: Sec. 2(f)(viii) excludes from the definition of 'collection agency' licensees under Michigan's horse racing law, lottery act, bingo act, Gaming Control and Revenue Act, Lawful Sports Betting Act (MCL 432.401-432.419), Fantasy Contests Consumer Protection Act and Lawful Internet Gaming Act.","The bill is tie-barred to SB 94. It passed one chamber, and the House substitute was laid over one day on 2025-12-23."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. On its own, the only gaming touchpoint is the Sec. 2(f)(viii) carve-out exempting gaming, sports betting, iGaming, fantasy, lottery, bingo and racing licensees from the 'collection agency' definition. That carve-out appears to track existing Michigan collection-agency definitional language and does not regulate wagering. The bill is otherwise a hospital billing and price-transparency measure.","watch":"There is little operator exposure, since the act reaches only debts owed to hospitals, but confirm that the Sec. 2(f)(viii) exclusion for sports betting, iGaming and fantasy licensees survives any further amendment so it is not cited as precedent for narrowing similar carve-outs in Michigan's general collection statutes.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Engrossed","text_chars":16203,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:57:15.673Z"},"MI-HB5833":{"summary":"HB5833 rewrites section 19 of Michigan's Lawful Sports Betting Act (MCL 432.419) to specify which individuals must submit fingerprints for state police and FBI criminal history record checks. It narrows the checks from applicants and licensees generally to individuals tied to sports betting supplier licenses and occupational licenses, then defines those categories by role and ownership. It also adds a bar on sharing check results with private entities and keeps fingerprint retention with ongoing arrest notification.","mechanics":["New subsection (1) requires an applicant to submit, on board forms, a photograph and 2 sets of fingerprints for each individual subject to licensure; subsection (2) requires persons applying for or holding a supplier or occupational license to consent to inspections, background checks, searches and seizures, and handwriting, fingerprint, and photograph exemplars.","Subsection (6) defines covered occupational-license individuals: sports traders and those acting as sports traders, their direct supervisors, supervisors of anyone with access to critical components of an internet sports betting platform, and heads of the fraud and risk, sports trading, infrastructure technology, accounting and finance, and daily state-payment remittance departments.","Subsection (7) covers, for suppliers, directors and principal executive, operations, or accounting officers who are not occupationally licensed. It also covers directors and principal executive, operations, or financial officers of a 'qualifying business': an entity that controls the applicant, holds more than a 5% direct or indirect ownership interest, or shares common ownership and provides a material aspect of the applicant's goods or services in Michigan.","Fingerprints go to the Department of State Police and the FBI. State police may charge the board a fee, and the board may not share criminal history record checks with a private entity (subsection (5)).","Subsections (6) and (7) of the existing text, renumbered, keep state police AFIS retention and FBI Rap Back participation, with immediate notice to the board of new arrest matches.","The board may fingerprint and check its own employees and prospective employees. The prior reference to board agents and contractors appears to be dropped. No effective date is specified in the text."],"issue_tags":["licensing","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. What stands out is the role-based enumeration of who must be fingerprinted: sports traders, platform-access supervisors, and named department heads, plus a 5% indirect-ownership look-through to parent and affiliate officers. The text also lists only supplier and occupational licenses, not sports betting operator licenses, in the record-check authority. This narrow, individual-specific drafting reads like an effort to satisfy FBI requirements for state statutory authority to run fingerprint checks, though the bill does not say so.","watch":"Operators should map which of their staff (traders, risk, IT, finance, and payment-remittance heads) and which parent and affiliate officers above the 5% threshold will be pulled into fingerprinting and continuous Rap Back monitoring, and should confirm whether the omission of operator licensees from the check authority is intentional or will be addressed elsewhere.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":5894,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:57:17.072Z"},"MI-SB878":{"summary":"SB 878, enacted as Public Act 21 of 2026 with immediate effect on July 21, 2026, is Michigan's omnibus appropriations act for fiscal year 2026-2027, with supplemental appropriations for fiscal year 2025-2026, covering multiple departments, the judicial and legislative branches, and capital outlay. The text supplied is truncated and covers only Article 1, the Department of Agriculture and Rural Development. Its only wagering-related content is a horse-racing purse and fairs line in Sec. 108.","mechanics":["Article 1 appropriates $135,935,700 gross to the Department of Agriculture and Rural Development for the fiscal year ending September 30, 2027, including $72,145,400 in general fund/general purpose money (Sec. 101).","Sec. 108 (Fairs and Expositions) appropriates $4,389,200, entirely from the agriculture equine industry development fund, with $0 from the general fund. The line items are: fairs and racing $258,600; purses and supplements for fairs and licensed tracks $2,073,600; standardbred breeders awards $345,900; standardbred purses and supplements at licensed tracks $991,100; and standardbred sire stakes $720,000.","Sec. 201 sets total state-source spending under Article 1 at $120,784,100, of which $10,400,000 goes to local units of government.","Sec. 217 authorizes contingency appropriations on top of the line items: up to $3,000,000 federal, $2,000,000 state restricted, $100,000 local and $100,000 private. These funds are usable only after transfer to a line item under MCL 18.1393.","Standard reporting, transparency and in-person work requirements apply to the department (Secs. 205-231).","Effective date: July 21, 2026, with immediate effect."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No other states' bills are listed for comparison. What is unusual is that this is a general omnibus budget, and its only visible link to wagering is the pari-mutuel horse-racing purse and breeder-award funding in Sec. 108. The supplied text contains no sports betting, internet gaming or casino provisions.","watch":"Because the supplied text is truncated at Article 1, review the full enacted act for any Michigan Gaming Control Board or Treasury articles, boilerplate conditions, or fund transfers that could affect gaming regulatory budgets, operator fees or wagering-derived restricted funds, including the agriculture equine industry development fund.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":1530228,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:57:19.579Z"},"MI-SB714":{"summary":"SB 714 adds section 9a to Michigan's Lawful Sports Betting Act (MCL 432.401–432.419) to regulate sports betting advertising, despite a catalog title referring to internet gaming. It requires Michigan Gaming Control Board pre-approval of all sports betting advertisements, bans deceptive ads and marketing to people under 21, sets an under-21 audience-composition limit, and requires responsible-gaming disclaimers. Violations carry escalating civil fines and, on a third violation, revocation of the operator's license, with attorney general enforcement.","mechanics":["Sec. 9a(1): every sports betting advertisement must be approved by the board before use. Sec. 9a(11) broadly defines 'advertisement' as paid marketing, including search, display, video, native, billboard, leaflet, mobile app ads and sponsorships likely to induce a wager, but excludes TV or internet programs that discuss betting or a specific operator's odds.","Sec. 9a(5): no advertising unless the advertiser has reliable evidence that no more than 30% of the TV, radio, website or print audience is reasonably expected to be under 21. Sec. 9a(7) bans marketing and sponsorships targeting under-21s but exempts operator, supplier or platform-provider naming rights for arenas, stadiums and other public places, and for TV channels.","Sec. 9a(4) and (6): bars deceptive, false or misleading ads and statements on products, signs or documents, and requires a problem-gambling disclaimer with the toll-free compulsive gaming helpline number. Sec. 9a(8) exempts ads that appear on live broadcasts because of their physical placement at the venue.","Sec. 9a(2)–(3): ads must comply with municipal ordinances, and municipalities may adopt ordinances setting reasonable restrictions on public sports betting signs, provided they are not unreasonably impracticable and do not conflict with the act or board rules.","Sec. 9a(9)–(10): penalties are up to $1,000 for a first violation, up to $5,000 for a second, and revocation of the sports betting operator license for a third or subsequent violation. The attorney general may sue to enforce."],"issue_tags":["advertising","licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Most of the listed bills are legalization, licensing or tax vehicles (AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, HI HB 1308/SB1569, IL HB2878) and do not regulate advertising in this detail. The closest analogues are CT HB05229, which restricts gaming advertising on college campuses, and CT HB05038 and IL HB5059 (ORACLE Act), which bar prediction-market advertising to or use by under-21s; SB 714 instead targets licensed sportsbooks and is broader. Its unusual features are universal board pre-approval of ads, a 30% under-21 audience cap (somewhat looser than the industry code benchmark of a 73.6% adult audience), express municipal authority over betting signage, and a mandatory-sounding license revocation on a third violation.","watch":"Watch the third-strike revocation in Sec. 9a(9)(c), because the bill does not say whether each ad, impression or day counts as a separate violation; also watch the operational burden of board pre-approval for every ad, the evidentiary standard for the 30% audience test, and the risk of a patchwork of municipal signage ordinances.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":3318,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:57:22.119Z"},"MN-HF4240":{"summary":"HF4240 (Laws 2026, ch. 102) is mainly an election-administration bill covering absentee and early voting, canvass timelines, and county ballot questions. Section 12 adds new Minn. Stat. 211B.25, which makes it a petty misdemeanor for a candidate to place a wager with a prediction market on the outcome of an election in which the candidate is running. Despite the title, the operative text reaches only candidates, not sitting elected officials who are not on the ballot, and it places no obligations on prediction-market operators.","mechanics":["New 211B.25, subd. 1 defines \"prediction market\" broadly as \"a system that allows consumers to place a wager on the future outcome of a federal, state, or local election.\" \"Wager\" is defined as a contract under which parties agree to a gain or loss of money, property, or benefit by one to the other.","Under 211B.25, subd. 2, a candidate who wagers with a prediction market on the outcome of their own election is guilty of a petty misdemeanor. There is no operator liability, no reporting duty, and no ban on betting on other races.","Sec. 13 amends 211B.32, subd. 1 to add 211B.25 to the exceptions from the requirement to exhaust administrative complaints, so a violation can be enforced directly rather than first going through the Office of Administrative Hearings.","Sec. 12 states no specific effective date, so Minnesota's default effective-date rule applies. Other sections run on their own schedules: some take effect the day after enactment, Sec. 3 on September 1, 2027, and the early-voting changes in Secs. 4-5 are tied to the revisor's receipt of the early voting certification.","The rest of the bill is non-gaming: canvass windows extended to the 3rd-14th day after municipal and school elections, the absentee administration election between 46 and 18 days, repeal of Minn. Stat. 5.31, and county special-election timing. No appropriation appears in the engrossed text despite the title."],"issue_tags":["prediction","integrity"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"No other states' bills are listed for comparison. What stands out is that Minnesota treats election prediction markets purely as a candidate-ethics problem in its campaign-practices chapter (211B). It does not regulate or prohibit the markets themselves, and it does not address CFTC-regulated event contracts or sports wagering. The sanction is light, a petty misdemeanor, and the conduct covered is narrow: only a candidate's bets on their own race.","watch":"Watch the broad \"prediction market\" definition, which would cover any platform offering election contracts, including CFTC-registered exchanges, because it could become a template for later Minnesota bills that impose know-your-customer, candidate-exclusion, or market-ban obligations directly on operators rather than on individual candidates.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Engrossed","text_chars":27561,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:57:31.099Z"},"MN-HF4410":{"summary":"HF4410 creates new Minn. Stat. § 325F.756 in the consumer-protection chapter, banning any person from operating, conducting, or promoting a dual-currency online sweepstakes game that simulates casino-style or other gambling. It extends the ban to a broad list of ancillary actors, including payment processors, geolocation providers, content suppliers, platform providers, and media affiliates. It also directs the commissioner of public safety and the attorney general to bar from operating anyone who, directly or through an affiliate, knowingly accepts revenue derived from such games.","mechanics":["Definitions (subd. 1): \"dual-currency\" is a payment system letting a person play a simulated gambling program for direct or indirect consideration (including consideration tied to a related product or service) with eligibility for a prize, cash, or cash equivalent. Free-entry contests with no consideration are excluded. \"Prize\" cross-references § 325F.755, subd. 1.","\"Online sweepstakes game\" (subd. 1(c)) has three elements: it is offered via the Internet, a mobile device, or a terminal; it uses a dual-currency system exchangeable for a prize, cash, or cash equivalent; and it simulates casino-style or another form of gambling.","Subd. 2(a) prohibits any person or entity from operating, conducting, or promoting an online sweepstakes game in Minnesota.","Subd. 2(b) bars an applicant, licensed entity, financial institution, payment processor, geolocation provider, gaming content supplier, platform provider, or media affiliate from supporting, conducting, or promoting such games.","Subd. 2(c) requires the commissioner of public safety and the attorney general to deny commencing or continuing operations to any person whose own operations or an affiliate's knowingly accept revenue directly or indirectly derived from online sweepstakes, and to enforce penalties.","Subd. 3 imports the violations in § 325F.755, subd. 7. The text sets no specific penalty amounts and no effective date."],"issue_tags":["sweeps","advertising","licensing","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike CA AB 831, which works within California's existing unfair-practices framework for contests and sweepstakes, HF4410 imposes an outright ban and expressly reaches third-party vendors and payment and geolocation providers. That makes it closer to the supplier-liability approach some states pair with sweepstakes bans. The bill does nothing to authorize or tax sports wagering, in contrast to AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, or HI HB 1308. Its most unusual feature is the subd. 2(c) \"revenue from illegal markets\" clause, which makes a regulator-level bar to operating turn on an affiliate's acceptance of sweepstakes-derived revenue.","watch":"Watch subd. 2(b)–(c): an operator, its licensed entity, or its suppliers could face a bar on commencing or continuing operations in Minnesota if any affiliate knowingly accepts sweepstakes-derived revenue anywhere, so operators should audit corporate affiliates, vendors, and media partners for sweepstakes exposure.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":2894,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:57:33.090Z"},"MN-HF4949":{"summary":"HF4949 creates a Minnesota sports and events reimbursement program, modeled on events trust funds, under new section 116J.8753. After a listed major sporting or entertainment event is held in Minnesota, the University of Minnesota estimates the incremental state tax revenue attributable to it, and that amount is diverted to a special revenue account and paid to the local organizing committee. The bill does not mention sports wagering and does not tax, authorize or regulate betting.","mechanics":["Eligible events are a closed list of 29 categories in 116J.8753 subd. 1(c), including the Super Bowl, NFL Draft, NCAA Final Four and Frozen Four, the MLB, NBA, NHL and WNBA All-Star Games, World Cup soccer, UFC and WWE premium events. To qualify, a site selection organization must have chosen Minnesota over at least one out-of-state site, as the sole site or sole regional site, and the event must be held no more than once a year (subd. 3).","Under new 270C.45 subd. 2, the University of Minnesota must begin estimating incremental revenue within 10 days after the event. The estimate covers a one-year window starting two months before the event and includes the state sales tax, rental vehicle taxes, the liquor gross receipts tax (295.75), the 295.81 gross receipts tax, the retail delivery fee (168E.03), and individual and corporate income taxes.","The commissioner must disburse the incremental amount to the requesting local organizing committee within 30 days of the determination (270C.45 subd. 3). The bill sets no dollar cap and no state matching requirement.","Allowable uses under 116J.8753 subd. 5 are event preparation and conduct costs, including facility improvements, renovations, acquisition or construction.","Local organizing committees must apply to the commissioner of revenue and must report audited financials, attendance including non-resident estimates, and economic impact data. The commissioner of revenue has rulemaking authority.","Conforming deposit amendments touch 168E.09, 290.62, 295.75, 295.81 and 297A.94 (new para. (m)). All effective dates are left blank. There are apparent drafting errors: 270C.45 subd. 1(d) cross-references 116J.7853, and subd. 3 refers to the commissioner of employment and economic development."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. The bill is unusual for its uncapped, post-event tax-increment reimbursement covering both state income taxes and gross receipts taxes. It also hands the revenue estimate to the University of Minnesota rather than the revenue department or comptroller, which resembles major-events trust fund structures elsewhere.","watch":"Watch whether any future Minnesota sports-wagering tax gets added to the list of captured taxes in 270C.45 subd. 2(c), since that would divert betting handle or revenue from marquee events away from whatever dedicated fund a legalization bill creates.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":26478,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:57:37.966Z"},"MN-HF4437":{"summary":"HF4437 makes it a felony in Minnesota to operate or allow a business that takes wagers, trades, contracts or financial positions on future events tied to sports, contests or casino-style games, people, politics, catastrophes or death. This squarely targets CFTC-registered prediction markets. It also criminalizes certain advertising of those products and payment processing after an attorney general cease-and-desist letter. A conviction bars the person from any gaming-related license for ten years. The analysis is based on the introduced text; a 1st engrossment adopted in committee on April 7, 2026 may differ.","mechanics":["Sec. 1 amends Minn. Stat. 609.75, subd. 3, clause (2). Futures contracts on securities or commodities are no longer automatically 'not bets' to the extent covered by new 609.76, subd. 9, removing the commodities-contract safe harbor for these event contracts.","New 609.76, subd. 9(b) creates a felony for allowing, maintaining or operating an entity that lets participants place wagers, trades, contracts or financial positions dependent on future events. Six categories are covered: (1) sports, including in-game events; (2) contests or casino-style gaming based on skill, merit, performance or chance, regardless of entry fee; (3) people; (4) federal, state or county politics and government conduct; (5) catastrophes; and (6) death.","Subd. 9(c) makes advertising that promotes the prohibited transactions a felony if any of the following applies: it airs between 8:00 a.m. and 10:00 p.m. or during a live sporting event; 10% or more of the audience is reasonably expected to be under 21; it claims legality although the conduct is not authorized gaming; the operator certified legality to place the ad; or it appears on public property or within 500 feet of a school or playground.","Subd. 9(d) makes it a felony for financial institutions, payment processors, geolocation providers, gaming content suppliers, platform providers, media affiliates and others to knowingly keep processing payments for unauthorized gaming after receiving an attorney general cease-and-desist letter.","Subd. 9(e) bars anyone convicted under the subdivision from receiving any gaming-related license until ten years after conviction. Subd. 9(f) carves pari-mutuel horse racing under chapter 240 and private social transactions out of the sports prong only.","Effective August 1, 2026, for crimes committed on or after that date."],"issue_tags":["prediction","advertising","licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Other states' prediction-market bills largely regulate rather than ban. Connecticut HB05038 and Illinois HB5059 (the ORACLE Act) impose a 21+ age floor and advertising limits on prediction market platforms. Illinois HB5142 folds sports prediction markets into the regulated definition of sports wagering, and California AB1840 addresses public officials' use of prediction contracts under the Political Reform Act. Minnesota's bill instead imposes outright felony prohibitions across six event categories. It is unusual in extending criminal liability to payment processors, geolocation providers and media affiliates after a cease-and-desist letter, and in attaching a ten-year licensing bar.","watch":"Watch for two risks: the subd. 9(e) ten-year bar on 'any gaming-related license' could exclude operators running or partnering on prediction-market products (sports event contracts) from any future Minnesota sports-betting market, and the broad 'contests' prong and daytime advertising felony could sweep in DFS and adjacent products; both prongs also invite a federal preemption challenge under the Commodity Exchange Act.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduction","text_chars":13025,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:57:41.189Z"},"MN-SF3432":{"summary":"SF3432, signed into law, is mainly a public-safety and security appropriations bill covering judicial, legislative, and constitutional-officer security, a new State Patrol legislative services unit for threat assessment, and related Capitol security policy. Its title also says it prohibits prediction-market-related activities and provides criminal penalties, and it amends Minn. Stat. 299L.03, subd. 12 (Gambling Enforcement Division) and 609.75, subd. 3 (the gambling-definitions provision). The supplied text is cut off in Article 3, so the operative prediction-market language is not in the excerpt.","mechanics":["Article 1 appropriates FY2027 security money: $6,062,000 to the Supreme Court (including a $4,000,000 courthouse security grant program with a 50% nonstate match), $60,000 to the Court of Appeals, $843,000 to district courts, $1,012,000 to the BCA for threat assessment, and $100,000 each to the AG, SOS, and State Auditor.","Article 2, sec. 10 creates new Minn. Stat. 299E.10, a legislative services unit within Capitol Security that identifies, assesses, and mitigates threats to legislators. The House and Senate must reimburse personal-protection costs.","Article 2, sec. 2 creates new 299A.96, requiring elected officials to submit emergency contact data annually by January 31 unless they opt out. The data is classified as private.","Article 2, sec. 11 establishes a Security Services Task Force. Its report is due February 1, 2027, and it expires June 30, 2027.","According to the title, the bill amends 299L.03, subd. 12 and 609.75, subd. 3, adds new chapter 609 coding with criminal penalties, and prohibits prediction-market-related activities. The specific text is not in the supplied excerpt.","The bill repeals 2026 S.F. 4760, article 3, section 1 and article 8, sections 1, 2, and 3, if enacted. The supplied text does not show what those sections contain."],"issue_tags":["prediction"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"No other states' bills are listed for comparison. The unusual feature is that a prediction-market prohibition with criminal penalties appears in an omnibus security appropriations bill rather than in standalone gaming legislation. That placement suggests late-session conference committee drafting; the conference committee report deleted everything on May 16, 2026. Because it amends 609.75, subd. 3 and the Gambling Enforcement Division's powers in 299L.03, Minnesota appears to be treating prediction-market activity through its existing criminal gambling framework rather than a licensing regime.","watch":"Obtain the full enrolled text to confirm how the new chapter 609 offense and the 609.75 and 299L.03 amendments define prohibited prediction-market activity, including whether they reach sports event contracts offered on CFTC-registered exchanges, advertising or facilitation, or Minnesota-located users; the bill also raises a likely federal preemption fight.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Engrossed","text_chars":90824,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:57:47.997Z"},"MN-SF2374":{"summary":"SF2374 is Minnesota's 2025 Senate omnibus tax bill, covering income and corporate franchise, sales and use, property, mining, local government aid, tax increment financing and other miscellaneous taxes, and it repeals the chapter 297D tax on illegal cannabis and controlled substances. The text provided covers only the start of Article 1 and contains no sports betting, lawful gambling or wagering provisions. Chapter 297E (lawful gambling taxes) and chapter 349 do not appear in the list of amended or repealed statutes.","mechanics":["Raises the net investment income tax under section 290.033 from 1 percent to 1.5 percent on net investment income above $1,000,000 for individuals, estates and trusts, effective for taxable years beginning after December 31, 2024.","Ties the elective pass-through entity tax (section 289A.08, subd. 7a) to expire at the same time and on the same terms as the federal SALT cap in IRC section 164(b)(6)(B), for taxable years beginning after December 31, 2027.","Revises the beginning farmer credit (section 41B.0391): replaces 'emerging farmer' with 'limited land access farmer' for the 12 percent sale-credit rate and keeps the $4,000,000 annual allocation cap.","Moves political contribution refunds to electronic receipts with unique validation numbers and daily validation reports from the Campaign Finance Board to the commissioner of revenue, effective for contributions made after December 31, 2026.","Adds new income subtractions for coerced-debt discharges, consumer enforcement compensation payments, critical access dental clinic student loan payments and foreign service pensions, and expands the National Guard subtraction to neighboring states.","Repeals sections 297D.01 to 297D.13 (illegal cannabis and controlled substances tax) and amends section 609.902, subd. 4, the racketeering 'criminal act' definition. The amendment to 609.902 is not visible in the excerpt and may be a conforming change."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"Kansas SB303 (House Substitute) shows a general tax package carrying a gaming measure, a 2 percent excise on all sports wagers, but nothing comparable appears in the visible SF2374 text. The same is true of Iowa SF 605 and SSB 1152 (state withholding on sports wagering winnings) and Illinois HB3159 (an added 2 percent tax on sports-wagering adjusted gross receipts). SF2374 is closer in character to Kentucky HB 757, North Carolina H554 and New York's budget bills: a broad revenue vehicle whose visible provisions do not touch gaming. That is unremarkable in itself, since Minnesota has not legalized sports betting.","watch":"Because this is a must-pass omnibus vehicle heading to the floor and likely conference, watch later engrossments and the conference report for any amendments to lawful gambling taxes (chapter 297E), sports wagering authorization, or the section 609.902 racketeering definitions, as well as the NIIT increase's effect on high-income owners.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Engrossed","text_chars":267173,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:57:50.037Z"},"MN-SF4223":{"summary":"As introduced, SF4223 is a campaign finance and ethics bill amending Minnesota Statutes chapter 10A. It narrows the definition of metropolitan governmental unit, extends earmarking, coordination and party-expenditure rules to local candidates, revises special-election reporting deadlines and periods, and raises certain itemization thresholds for ballot question committees. It does not regulate sports betting or gaming; the only gaming-adjacent text is an unchanged clause requiring Minnesota Racing Commission personnel to file statements of economic interest.","mechanics":["Sec. 1 redefines 'metropolitan governmental unit' (10A.01, subd. 24) as a metro-area county, a regional railroad authority, the Metropolitan Council, the Metropolitan Parks and Open Space Commission, the Metropolitan Airports Commission, or the Minnesota Sports Facilities Authority. Secs. 2 to 4 separately carry forward conflict-of-interest and economic-interest filing duties for metro counties and cities over 50,000.","Sec. 4 retains existing 10A.09, subd. 1(5): Racing Commission members, the director, chief of security, medical officer, inspector of pari-mutuels, and stewards (including contractors) must file a statement of economic interest within 60 days of assuming duties. This text is unchanged.","Sec. 5 requires the secretary of state, county auditors, and appointing officials to give the Campaign Finance Board each filer's mailing address, phone number and email.","Secs. 12 and 15 change special-election reporting. If a special primary or general election falls on regular election dates, the regular 15-day and 10-day deadlines apply. A new report is due seven days after the filing period closes, with defined coverage periods.","Sec. 14 sets contributor, loan and receipt itemization at $200, or $500 for ballot question political committees and funds. Sec. 16 adds a $100-per-day late fee (capped at $1,000) for failing to give the 24-hour notice of exceeding spending-limit thresholds. Sec. 17 extends the 10A.27, subd. 17 penalties to ballot question committees.","Sec. 8 extends the earmarking ban (civil penalty up to $3,000; gross misdemeanor) to local candidates. Sec. 18 extends party-unit expenditure exceptions to local candidates and to section 211A.12."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. What stands out for gaming clients is only that the bill re-enacts, without change, Minnesota's economic-interest filing requirement for Racing Commission officials and pari-mutuel inspectors and stewards. The bill also adds the Minnesota Sports Facilities Authority to the metropolitan governmental unit definition, a stadium governance body rather than a wagering regulator. Note that the text reviewed is the introduction; the bill passed third reading as amended under an 'omnibus elections' title, so the 2nd engrossment may contain different provisions.","watch":"Review the 2nd engrossment (posted 04/24/2026) for any added provisions touching gaming, such as political contribution limits or disclosure rules for gaming licensees or lobbyists, since the introduced text addresses wagering only through the unchanged Racing Commission disclosure clause.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduction","text_chars":44741,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:57:55.002Z"},"MN-SF4139":{"summary":"SF4139 would legalize mobile sports betting in Minnesota under the Department of Public Safety (new sections in chapter 299L). Per its title, it would also license operators, platform providers and suppliers, preempt local restrictions, create a sports betting tax in new chapter 297J, and touch charitable gambling tax rates, pari-mutuel racing and amateur sports grants. The excerpt provided covers only Article 1 (Responsible Gaming), which imposes unusually strict player-protection and advertising rules, and the bill stalled on 2026-03-23 when a motion to adopt the committee report failed.","mechanics":["Regulator and rules (§299L.15): the commissioner regulates mobile sports betting and may use expedited rulemaking for rules noticed before Jan. 1, 2027. Account funding by credit card is excluded. Sports governing bodies may request bet-type or event restrictions; operators get notice and a chance to respond, and the commissioner must decide before the event or within 7 days.","Advertising (§299L.37): every ad must carry 1-800-GAMBLER and an addiction warning. Ads are barred in media where 30% or more of the audience is reasonably expected to be under 21. Promotions may not be called \"risk-free\" if the player risks their own money. College partnerships may not promote betting except alumni-only or responsible-gambling content. NIL deals with in-state amateur athletes are banned, as is advertising on K-12 property, on college campuses (unless general-audience) and in college-owned media.","Personal limits (§299L.405): operators must offer deposit, loss/wager, time and cooling-off limits. Default settings are $500 per 24 hours in losses or wagers, $3,000 per 30 days, $500 daily deposit and a 72-hour cooling-off period. Loosening a limit takes effect only after 7 days, and players must review limits at account setup and at least quarterly. Exclusions triggered by limits must be reported to the commissioner and shared across all operators.","Push notifications (§299L.41): banned while the app is inactive, except for fraud, responsible-gaming or identity-verification messages.","Exclusion list (§299L.45): self-exclusion for 1, 3 or 5 years or lifetime, plus entries submitted by guardians, licensees and sports governing bodies. Insiders such as athletes, coaches, referees, owners and holders of nonpublic information are barred from betting. Knowingly accepting a wager from an excluded person is a license violation. Marketing contact is barred during a restriction period and afterward until the person bets again.","Studies (§§299L.75–.751): a baseline gambling-prevalence study before launch, updated every three years, plus a study of sports betting's impact on gambling disorders, suicide and youth. Tax rates, license fees and license counts are not in the excerpt provided."],"issue_tags":["legalize","licensing","tax","advertising","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Like AK HB145/SB194, HI HB 1308/HB 2570/SB1569 and GA HB 686, SF4139 is a full legalization-and-licensing bill, but its Article 1 player protections go well beyond those models. Its preset default loss and deposit limits, push-notification ban and statewide sharing of limit-based exclusions are features not reflected in the listed bills' summaries. Its campus-advertising restrictions parallel CT HB05229, though Minnesota adds a 30% under-21 audience threshold and an amateur-athlete NIL ban. Its stated preemption of local restrictions echoes IL HB4171's denial of home-rule power to regulate or tax sports wagering.","watch":"Watch the §299L.405 defaults ($500 daily and $3,000 monthly loss/wager caps, $500 daily deposit cap) and the post-exclusion marketing blackout, which would directly cap high-value player activity, and get the full text to see the chapter 297J tax rate and license terms if the bill is revived after its failed committee-report vote.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduction","text_chars":195963,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:57:58.837Z"},"MN-SF4474":{"summary":"SF4474 adds new section 325F.756 to Minnesota's consumer protection chapter, banning any person or entity from operating, conducting, or promoting an online sweepstakes game in Minnesota, defined as an internet- or mobile-accessible game using a dual-currency system redeemable for prizes or cash that simulates casino-style or other gambling. It also bars a broad set of ancillary parties, including financial institutions, payment processors, geolocation providers, content suppliers, platform providers, and media affiliates, from supporting or promoting such games. Enforcement runs through the existing penalties and remedies of section 325F.755, subdivision 7, with investigative and civil-fine authority given to the commissioner of public safety.","mechanics":["Definition (subd. 1(c)): an online sweepstakes game must (1) be available online or on a mobile or similar device, (2) use a dual-currency system exchangeable for a prize, cash, cash equivalent, or chance to win, and (3) simulate casino-style or another form of gambling.","Dual-currency (subd. 1(b)) covers direct or indirect consideration, including consideration tied to a related product, service, or activity; true no-consideration contests are excluded.","Subd. 2(a) prohibits operating, conducting, or promoting an online sweepstakes game in Minnesota.","Subd. 2(b) extends liability to applicants, licensed entities, financial institutions, payment processors, geolocation providers, gaming content suppliers, platform providers, and media affiliates that support, conduct, or promote such games.","Subd. 3 applies the penalties and remedies of 325F.755, subd. 7, and authorizes the commissioner of public safety to investigate, issue notices of violation, impose civil fines, and bring enforcement actions.","No effective date or specific fine amounts appear in the text; 'prize' is borrowed from 325F.755, subd. 1."],"issue_tags":["sweeps","advertising","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike the authorization-and-tax bills on the list (AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, HI HB 1308), SF4474 is purely prohibitory and targets the sweepstakes casino model rather than licensed wagering. It is closest to CA AB 831, which also addresses sweepstakes operations, and to IL HB3733/SB 1705 on gambling-device definitions. What stands out is the express secondary-liability reach to payment processors, geolocation vendors, content suppliers, platforms, and media affiliates, a supply-chain approach not described in the listed bills. It also sits in the consumer-protection chapter (325F) rather than a gaming code.","watch":"Clients that are licensed entities, applicants, content suppliers, or media and affiliate partners should audit any relationships with sweepstakes platforms, because subd. 2(b) explicitly reaches licensed entities and applicants that 'support' or 'promote' these games, which could create collateral licensing exposure.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Engrossed","text_chars":2945,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:58:00.449Z"},"MN-SF4365":{"summary":"SF4365 is Minnesota's 2026 omnibus commerce policy bill covering insurance, the premium security reinsurance plan, prescription drug affordability, consumer protection, telecommunications, securities, financial products and unclaimed property. Its only gaming content, per the title, is a modification of charitable gambling law through an amendment to Minn. Stat. section 349.211, subdivision 2b (lawful gambling prize limits), but that section is not in the excerpt provided. The Senate file was indefinitely postponed on 2026-04-28 after the House companion, HF4188, was substituted on General Orders, so HF4188 is now the live vehicle.","mechanics":["Article 1 removes the Prescription Drug Affordability Advisory Council from the board's conflict-of-interest and drug-identification provisions and repeals sections 62J.86, subd. 2 and 62J.88.","Article 2 restructures the one-time 2028 assessment on group health carriers for the premium security plan. The commissioner must approve each carrier's amount, payments go to the commissioner by August 1, 2028, and the association must check the assessment's accuracy by March 15, 2029. A premium tax credit for the assessment applies for taxable years after December 31, 2028.","Article 3 requires health insurers, nonprofit health service plans and HMOs with at least 25,000 enrollees to notify the commissioner by April 15 if enrollment grows more than 35 percent, or exceeds a commissioner-set threshold for a product line. The commissioner may then demand financial projections and issue corrective orders (new sections 60A.071 and 62D.085).","New section 60A.593 bars a domestic health organization at or below its company action level RBC from raising officer or director pay, or making preferential payments, without the commissioner's approval.","The title lists an amendment to section 349.211, subd. 2b (charitable gambling prizes), but the operative text is not in the provided excerpt."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. What is unusual is that the only gambling content is a charitable gambling prize-limit tweak buried in a sprawling commerce omnibus. Nothing in the visible text touches sports betting, online gaming, taxation of wagering or operator licensing.","watch":"Watch HF4188, the substituted vehicle, for the final text of the section 349.211, subd. 2b charitable gambling amendment and for any sports-betting or gaming language added in conference, since omnibus commerce bills can carry late gaming amendments.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Engrossed","text_chars":264159,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:58:05.986Z"},"MN-SF4511":{"summary":"SF4511 (introduction version) makes it a felony under Minn. Stat. 609.76, new subd. 9, to operate or allow a platform where participants place wagers, trades, contracts or financial positions, including securities or commodities, that depend on future events in sports, contests or casino-style gaming, people, politics, catastrophe, or death. It carves these event contracts out of the existing 609.75, subd. 3(2) exclusion for futures and commodities contracts. It also criminalizes certain advertising and payment facilitation, and bars anyone convicted under the subdivision from any gaming-related license for ten years.","mechanics":["Sec. 1 amends 609.75, subd. 3(2). Futures and commodities contracts are no longer 'not bets' to the extent covered by new 609.76, subd. 9.","Sec. 2, subd. 9(b) makes it a felony to allow or operate an entity offering wagers, trades or contracts on future events in six categories: sports (including in-game events), contests/casino-style gaming, people, politics (federal, state or county elections and government conduct), catastrophe, and death.","Subd. 9(c) makes advertising that promotes prohibited transactions a felony if any of the following apply: it airs between 8 a.m. and 10 p.m. or during a live sports broadcast; 10% or more of the audience is reasonably expected to be under 21; it claims legality; the operator certified legality to place the ad; or it appears on public property or within 500 feet of a school or playground.","Subd. 9(d) makes it a felony for payment processors, financial institutions, geolocation providers, platform providers, content suppliers and media affiliates to keep knowingly processing payments for unauthorized gaming after receiving an attorney general cease-and-desist letter.","Subd. 9(e) bars anyone convicted under subd. 9 from any gaming-related license for 10 years from conviction. Subd. 9(f) exempts pari-mutuel horse racing under ch. 240 and private social bets from the sports clause only.","Effective August 1, 2026, for crimes committed on or after that date."],"issue_tags":["prediction","advertising","licensing","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"SF4511 is far more aggressive than the other prediction-market bills listed. IL HB5059 (ORACLE Act) and CT HB05038 regulate prediction markets through age-21 limits and advertising restrictions, and IL HB5142 folds sports prediction markets into the regulated sports-wagering definition. SF4511 instead criminalizes event contracts outright as a felony across six broad categories. CA AB1840 addresses prediction markets only through Political Reform Act ethics rules. The cease-and-desist trigger for felony liability of payment processors and geolocation vendors, and the 10-year licensing bar, have no analogue among the listed bills.","watch":"Watch whether later engrossments keep the broad paragraph (b) 'contests or casino-style gaming' and sports language and the 10-year licensing bar in paragraph (e). Because Minnesota has not legalized sports betting, any future licensed sportsbook, its vendors (geolocation, payments, media), and its affiliates could face felony exposure or license disqualification, and the bill invites a federal CEA preemption fight with CFTC-registered exchanges.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduction","text_chars":13119,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:58:09.899Z"},"MN-SF4760":{"summary":"The introduced text of SF4760 amends Minnesota's Criminal Offenders Rehabilitation Act (Minn. Stat. ch. 364) to change how public employers and licensing authorities weigh a criminal conviction that directly relates to the job or licensed occupation sought. It requires the applicant to show both rehabilitation and present fitness, and it turns the previously mandatory forms of evidence into factors the authority may consider. The enacted omnibus public safety version, reflected in the 3rd engrossment and the 5/11/2026 conference report, is not in the text provided, so any gaming content it may contain cannot be confirmed here.","mechanics":["Sec. 1 amends 364.03, subd. 3(a): an applicant with a directly related conviction is not disqualified if they show 'both' competent evidence of sufficient rehabilitation and present fitness to perform the duties.","Sec. 1, new subd. 3(b): the licensing or hiring authority 'may consider' a list of evidence. Previously, rehabilitation could be established by producing specified documents, and the authority 'shall consider' the applicant's other evidence.","The listed evidence includes a DD-214 showing honorable discharge for service after conviction; a release order plus at least one year since release without a new conviction and compliance with probation or parole; a DOC discharge order; and the nature, circumstances, age at offense and time elapsed for the crime, along with any other competent evidence such as reference letters.","Subd. 3(c) is retained: a DD-214 stops counting as rehabilitation evidence if the person is later convicted of a gross misdemeanor or felony after discharge.","Sec. 2 amends 364.05: a written denial notice must state the earliest date the person may reapply with a hiring or licensing authority, and that all rehabilitation evidence presented on reapplication will be considered.","No effective date, gambling, wagering or gaming-specific provisions appear in the introduced text."],"issue_tags":["licensing"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No other states' bills are listed for comparison. What is unusual is the mismatch between this bill and its title. The introduced text is a narrow two-section amendment to the general occupational-licensing rehabilitation statute, while the signed law is an omnibus public safety bill that was substantially rewritten through three engrossments and a conference committee.","watch":"Pull the conference committee report and the final session law to confirm whether the enacted omnibus added any gambling-related provisions, and check whether ch. 364's discretionary rehabilitation standard reaches Minnesota gaming-related license or employee background determinations for operators' staff.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduction","text_chars":11613,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:58:12.198Z"},"MN-SF5052":{"summary":"SF5052 is Minnesota's 2026 Senate omnibus tax bill. It updates federal conformity, modifies income, corporate, property, sales, excise and mineral taxes and local government aids, and creates new levies including a social media tax, a Hennepin County health care tax and a tax on amounts acquired by fraud. Nothing in the text provided addresses sports betting, lawful gambling, pari-mutuel or casino taxation, and the long list of amended statutes in the title does not include Minnesota's gambling tax or regulatory chapters (297E, 349, 240).","mechanics":["Art. 1 keeps the general Internal Revenue Code reference date at May 1, 2023. New section 290.0112 selectively adopts only six provisions of the One Big Beautiful Bill Act (P.L. 119-21, sections 70301, 70307, 70404, 70405, 70434 and 70603), as enacted through March 1, 2026.","Art. 2 extends the sustainable aviation fuel credit ($1.50 per gallon) through 2035 and raises total certificate authority from $11.6 million to $36.9 million (section 41A.30).","Art. 2 raises the film production credit from 25% to 40%, plus a 5% bonus up to 45% for Minnesota key creative hires, filming outside the metro area, or a majority-Minnesota crew. The project spending threshold drops from $1,000,000 to $400,000, effective for tax years beginning after December 31, 2026 (section 116U.27).","Art. 2 extends the historic rehabilitation credit placed-in-service window from three to six years, retroactive to allocation certificates issued after June 30, 2021 (section 290.0681).","The title also announces a new social media tax, a Hennepin County health care tax, seasonal tax base replacement aid and a tax on amounts acquired by fraud. Those articles fall outside the excerpt provided."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"Unlike KS SB303, a tax omnibus that carries a 2% excise on all sports wagers, the visible text of SF5052 contains no wagering tax. Unlike IA SF 605, it does not touch withholding on sports-wagering winnings. In this respect it resembles general revenue packages such as IL HB2755 and KY HB 757 more than the gaming-specific bills from AK, GA, HI, MS and NE. Its selective OBBBA conformity list does not appear to include the federal wagering-loss limitation, which bears on the state tax treatment of bettors' losses.","watch":"Minnesota has not legalized sports betting, so operators should watch for floor or conference amendments that graft gaming, lawful-gambling tax or wagering-loss conformity provisions onto this vehicle, and should check whether the new social media tax reaches digital advertising spend.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Engrossed","text_chars":282223,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:58:18.980Z"},"MN-SF5101":{"summary":"SF5101 creates a Minnesota sports and events reimbursement program, similar to an events trust fund, under new section 116J.8753. After a listed major sporting or entertainment event is held in Minnesota, the incremental increase in specified state tax revenues attributable to it is estimated by the University of Minnesota and paid to the local organizing committee. The bill does not address wagering at all; its only gaming link is that many of the eligible events are heavily bet-on.","mechanics":["Eligible events are a closed list of 29 categories in 116J.8753 subd. 1(c). Examples include the Super Bowl, NFL Draft, NCAA Final Four and Frozen Four, MLB/NBA/NHL/WNBA/MLS all-star events, World Cup and Gold Cup soccer, Formula 1 US Grand Prix, UFC, WWE premium live events, X Games, and PGA of America championships.","Under subd. 3, a site selection organization must have considered at least one out-of-state site. Minnesota must be the sole site, or the sole site in a multistate region, and the event may be held no more than once a year.","Under new 270C.45 subd. 2, the University of Minnesota must start an estimate within 10 days after the event. The estimate covers a one-year window beginning two months before the event.","Taxes counted toward the increment are: state sales tax (297A.62 subd. 1), the 297A.64 vehicle rental taxes, the 295.75 liquor gross receipts tax, the 295.81 cannabis gross receipts tax, the 168E.03 retail delivery fee, and income/franchise taxes under 290.02 and 290.03. No gaming or wagering tax is included.","Revenues are diverted to a new special revenue fund account through amendments to 168E.09, 290.62, 295.75, 295.81 and 297A.94. Disbursement is due within 30 days of the determination and may only go to the requesting local organizing committee. The money can fund event preparation and conduct, including facility renovation or new construction (subd. 5).","Local organizing committees must submit an application and site-selection documentation, audited financial statements, and attendance figures including non-resident estimates. Effective dates are left blank, and the commissioner of revenue has rulemaking authority."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. The bill is an events-trust-fund style tax-increment rebate for event organizers rather than a gaming measure. Notably, it captures incremental cannabis and liquor gross receipts taxes alongside sales and income taxes, but no wagering revenue. It also contains apparent drafting errors: 270C.45 subd. 1(d) cross-references 116J.7853 instead of 116J.8753, and subd. 3 directs the commissioner (of revenue) to disburse while barring the commissioner of employment and economic development from paying other entities.","watch":"The bill has no direct effect on operators, but watch whether any amendment adds a sports-wagering or other gaming tax to the 270C.45 subd. 2(c) list of incremental revenues, which would divert betting tax receipts from tentpole events like the Super Bowl or Final Four to event organizers.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":26557,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:58:25.848Z"},"MO-HB2002":{"summary":"HB 2002 is Missouri's FY2027 (July 1, 2026 to June 30, 2027) operating appropriation for the State Board of Education and the Department of Elementary and Secondary Education. It funds the school foundation formula, transportation, early childhood, special education, career and technical education and federal pass-through programs. Its only gaming connection is that it draws on gaming-derived state funds, including the Sports Wagering for Education Fund, the Lottery Proceeds Fund and the Bingo Proceeds for Education Fund.","mechanics":["Section 2.015 appropriates $6,805,078 from the Sports Wagering for Education Fund (1244) to the Foundation Formula program distribution, part of a $4,282,736,000 formula line.","Lottery Proceeds Fund (1291) money is spread across many lines in the introduced text, including $132,479,987 for the foundation formula and $73,873,102 for transportation (both Sec. 2.015) and $37,467,000 for Career Ladder (Sec. 2.040).","Other Lottery Proceeds lines include $19,590,000 for special education excess costs (Sec. 2.275), $16,548,507 for early childhood special education (Sec. 2.285) and smaller amounts in Secs. 2.110, 2.140, 2.235 and 2.250.","Section 2.025 appropriates $1,876,355 from the Bingo Proceeds for Education Fund (1289) for expense and equipment of State Board-operated school programs.","The bill contains no provisions on wagering tax rates, licensing, permitted bet types, advertising by operators, or regulatory enforcement; the 'no advertising' condition in Sec. 2.160 restricts how career and technical education funds are spent and is unrelated to gaming.","The text reviewed is the introduced version and is truncated, so figures in the enacted version (signed and delivered to the Secretary of State 2026-06-30) may differ."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No other states' bills are listed for comparison. What stands out on its own is the relatively small Sports Wagering for Education Fund draw of about $6.8 million. It sits against roughly $2.58 billion in general revenue in the same formula line, and against far larger lottery contributions across the budget. This indicates sports wagering revenue earmarked for education is a marginal funding source in Missouri's FY2027 education budget.","watch":"Compare the enacted Sec. 2.015 Sports Wagering for Education Fund figure with actual wagering tax receipts, since the gap between projected and appropriated revenue could fuel future arguments to raise Missouri's sports wagering tax rate or narrow its deductions to deliver more education funding.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":65136,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:58:33.360Z"},"MN-SF757":{"summary":"SF757 would legalize statewide mobile sports betting for people 21 and older, regulated by the commissioner of public safety under new Minn. Stat. 299L.10 to 299L.80. Operator licenses go only to Minnesota's 11 federally recognized Tribes that run class III casinos, and commercial sportsbooks may take part only as licensed platform providers partnered with a Tribe. The title also covers fantasy contests, new tax chapters 297J and 297K, a ban on local restrictions, amateur sports grants, charitable gambling tax changes and pari-mutuel racing, but the supplied text cuts off in Article 1 (sec. 299L.40) before those provisions.","mechanics":["Licenses (sec. 299L.20, 299L.28): up to 11 mobile operator licenses, limited to entities wholly owned by an Indian Tribe conducting class III gaming, one per Tribe, valid 20 years, annual fee $2,125; each operator may contract with no more than one platform provider, whose app must display the Tribe's brand (sec. 299L.35).","Platform providers (sec. 299L.29): up to 11 one-year licenses, with a $16,500 application fee, a $250,000 license fee and $83,000 renewal; Iowa-licensed providers as of Feb. 1, 2024 get provisional licenses for a $250,000 registration fee, must partner with a Tribe, and the provisional license expires by Dec. 31, 2025.","Suppliers (sec. 299L.30): one-year licenses with a $16,500 application fee, a $75,000 license fee and $33,000 renewal; Iowa-licensed suppliers can get a provisional license for $75,000; sports governing bodies supplying raw statistical data are excluded from the supplier definition.","Wagering limits (sec. 299L.40): no bets on player injuries, penalties, disciplinary rulings or replay reviews; no proposition bets on college sports; deposits cannot be wagered until three hours after deposit; rules may not allow credit-card funding (sec. 299L.15, subd. 2(7)); peer-to-peer exchange wagers are excluded from regulation (sec. 299L.10, subd. 21(f)).","Advertising (sec. 299L.37): ads must carry 1-800-GAMBLER and an addiction warning; ads are barred in media where 30 percent or more of the audience is reasonably expected to be under 21, on K-12 property, in college-owned news outlets, and on campuses unless aimed at a general audience; ads may not target excluded persons.","Integrity and responsible gaming (sec. 299L.15): sports governing bodies may ask the commissioner to restrict wagers on a good-cause showing, with operators given notice and a chance to respond and a response due within seven days; the commissioner must run a 24/7 helpline, and may make rules on wager-frequency limits, waiting periods and behavioral monitoring including account suspension."],"issue_tags":["legalize","licensing","advertising","integrity","tax"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike AK HB145/SB194 and GA HB 686, which create commercially accessible mobile licensing and taxation, SF757 gives Tribes exclusive operator status and leaves national sportsbooks as subordinate platform vendors, one per Tribe. Its Iowa-reciprocity provisional licensing is unusual among the listed bills. Its campus and college-media advertising limits track CT HB05229, and its carve-out of peer-to-peer exchange wagers contrasts with IL HB5142, which would pull sports prediction markets into the definition of sports wagering. HI HB1308, HB 2570 and SB1569 likewise assign regulation to an executive department but are not Tribe-exclusive.","watch":"Operator clients should watch the Tribe-exclusive structure, which caps the market at 11 platform partnerships and requires carrying Tribal branding, along with the operationally heavy consumer rules: the three-hour deposit hold, the bans on college props and injury, penalty and replay markets, discretionary wager-frequency limits, and the 30 percent under-21 audience test. The tax rates in the proposed chapters 297J and 297K are not shown in the excerpt and still need to be reviewed.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":222521,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:58:34.977Z"},"MN-SF978":{"summary":"SF978 would legalize statewide mobile sports betting for adults 21 and over, run exclusively by Minnesota's 11 federally recognized Tribes as licensed operators, each partnering with at most one licensed platform provider, under regulation by the commissioner of public safety in new chapter 299L. It sets license tiers and fees, detailed problem-gambling and advertising restrictions, and a process for sports governing bodies to seek wager restrictions. The title also references a new tax chapter 297J, a ban on local restrictions, penalties and appropriations, but the supplied text stops at section 299L.40, so the tax rate and later articles cannot be confirmed.","mechanics":["Licensing (299L.20, .28–.30): up to 11 operator licenses, Tribe-only (one per Tribe, must conduct class III casino gaming), valid 20 years, $2,125 annual fee. Up to 11 platform provider licenses, valid 3 years, with a $6,000 application fee, $38,250 license fee and $25,500 renewal. Supplier licenses carry the same fees with no cap. Licenses are non-transferable except platform licenses with the commissioner's approval.","Each operator may contract with no more than one platform provider, and the platform's app must display the Tribe's brand (299L.35). Applicants filing within 30 days of the application window opening share a common launch date (299L.15 subd. 8).","Wagering scope (299L.10, .40): participants must be 21 or older. Exchange wagering and prop, parlay and futures bets are allowed if the commissioner approves the wager type. Excluded are school, college and youth events as organized events, fantasy contests, horse racing, and individual-athlete performance in a single collegiate game. Esports require approval by both the commissioner and the publisher.","Rulemaking (299L.15 subd. 2): no minimum hold requirement may be set. Account funding by credit card or 'electronic wallets' is barred. Operators must monitor for problem gambling, with the power to pause accounts. Expedited rulemaking is available for rules noticed before 1/1/2026. The state must run a 24/7 helpline and audit compliance with the exclusion list.","Advertising (299L.37): ads are banned where 10% or more of the audience is reasonably expected to be under 21, on public property, within 500 feet of a school or playground, and on college campuses or college-owned news outlets. College partnerships may not promote betting except with alumni networks or responsible-gambling content. NIL deals with in-state amateur athletes are banned, as are 'risk-free' or 'free play' claims. Ads must carry a helpline number and warning, and licensees must file an annual ad-placement report by Feb. 1.","Integrity (299L.15 subd. 4): sports governing bodies may request bans on events or wager types. Operators get notice and a chance to respond, and good cause is the standard. The commissioner must rule before the event or within 7 days and may grant provisional relief."],"issue_tags":["legalize","licensing","advertising","integrity","prop","tax"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike AK HB145/SB194 and HI HB1308/HB2570/SB1569, which create state-agency licensing open to commercial operators, SF978 restricts operator licenses to Tribes and lets commercial books in only as one-per-Tribe platform providers. GA HB 686 is a comparable full authorization statute but depends on the constitutional amendments in GA HR450/SR131, a step Minnesota's bill does not require. SF978's college-campus advertising ban parallels CT HB05229, though its NIL ban, 500-foot school buffer and e-wallet funding ban go further. Its express inclusion of 'exchange wagering' contrasts with IL HB5142's move to pull prediction markets into the sports-wagering definition.","watch":"Commercial operators should watch the tethered, one-platform-per-Tribe model capped at 11 partnerships, the unusually broad credit and e-wallet funding ban, the strict advertising rules (10% under-21 audience, public property and campus bans), and the 297J tax rate, which does not appear in the excerpt provided.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":135653,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:58:39.497Z"},"MO-HB2008":{"summary":"HB2008 is Missouri's FY2027 (July 1, 2026 to June 30, 2027) operating appropriations bill for the Department of Public Safety and the Department of National Guard. It funds the Office of the Director's grant programs, the State Highway Patrol, Capitol Police, Alcohol and Tobacco Control, Fire Safety and the Veterans' Commission. It touches gaming only through several line items drawn from the Gaming Commission Fund (1286) to support Highway Patrol operations.","mechanics":["Gaming Commission Fund (1286) appropriations to the State Highway Patrol: $79,567 for administration (s. 8.135), $550,585 for fringe benefits (s. 8.140), $537,786 for enforcement expense and equipment (s. 8.145), $69,440 for the Law Enforcement Academy (s. 8.165) and $233,721 for technical services (s. 8.180).","Section 8.155 appropriates $1,730,468 from the Gaming Commission Fund toward a $30,865,952 line for vehicles, aircraft and watercraft for the Highway Patrol and the Gaming Commission under s. 43.265 RSMo.","Gaming Commission Fund draws in the portion of the text provided total roughly $3.2 million.","Most line items carry 3% flexibility to Section 8.335, so funds can shift between lines within limits.","Unrelated World Cup items: $59,522,190 in federal funds for the FIFA World Cup Grant Program (s. 8.030) and $12,000,000 in General Revenue for 2026 World Cup security (s. 8.060).","The text supplied is truncated at s. 8.250, so any direct Missouri Gaming Commission operating appropriation appearing later in the bill is not visible here."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No other states' bills are listed for comparison. What stands out is that gaming money appears only as Gaming Commission Fund line items paying for Highway Patrol personnel, benefits, enforcement, training, vehicles and technology. That reflects Missouri's model, in which Patrol officers perform gaming enforcement funded by gaming revenue. The bill contains no substantive wagering policy, tax rates or licensing provisions.","watch":"Track the total Gaming Commission Fund draws for Highway Patrol enforcement and vehicles, and the Gaming Commission's own appropriation in the untruncated text, since these fee-funded amounts signal regulatory and enforcement capacity now that Missouri sports wagering has launched and can affect licensee fee assessments.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":71183,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:58:40.022Z"},"MO-HB3154":{"summary":"HB3154 adds three new sections to RSMo chapters 311 and 313 authorizing state and federal (FBI) fingerprint-based criminal history background checks through the Missouri State Highway Patrol for alcohol licensees (new 311.661), fantasy sports contest operator license applicants (new 313.912), and retail and mobile sports wagering license applicants (new 313.1000). The checks are discretionary ('may require') for the regulator, but once required they must run through the Highway Patrol's central repository and FBI under section 43.540. Results are made fully available to the Division of Alcohol and Tobacco Control or the Missouri Gaming Commission notwithstanding the closed-record provisions of section 610.120.","mechanics":["Section 311.661: the Division of Alcohol and Tobacco Control may require fingerprints from any 'owner' (an individual holding 10% or greater financial or voting interest) or applicant for six license types: carrier/transport, manufacturer-solicitor/domestic winery/microbrewery, primary retail, solicitor, wholesaler, and wine direct shipper.","Section 313.912: the Gaming Commission may require fingerprint submissions from employees, officers, or directors of a fantasy sports contest operator (as defined by reference to section 313.905) as part of the operator license application.","Section 313.1000: the Gaming Commission may require fingerprints as part of an application for a retail sports wagering license (in person at excursion gambling boats or within a 'sports district') or a mobile sports wagering license (online platform for persons physically in Missouri).","'Sports district' is defined as premises of a facility with capacity of 11,500 or more where one or more professional teams play home games, plus the area within 400 yards; retail locations within the district must be approved by the applicable team.","Procedure in all three sections: fingerprints and fees go to the Highway Patrol central repository, are searched against state records and forwarded to the FBI under section 43.540, and the Patrol reports any record or lack of record to the regulator; section 610.120 closed-record limits are overridden.","No effective date, fee amounts, or disqualifying offenses are specified in the text."],"issue_tags":["licensing","integrity"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"Unlike AK HB145 and SB194 or GA HB 686 and HI HB 1308, HB2570 and SB1569, which fold criminal record checks into comprehensive bills authorizing and taxing sports wagering, MO HB3154 is a narrow technical bill that layers FBI fingerprint authority onto Missouri's already-authorized sports wagering and fantasy sports regimes without touching tax, advertising, or market structure. It is closer in spirit to CO SB 163 and FL S1580, which adjust licensing administration and personnel eligibility, though HB3154 is purely about background-check mechanics. Its pairing of alcohol and gaming licensing in a single bill is unusual among the listed bills; IL HB5452 touches liquor regulation but not gaming background checks.","watch":"Watch whether the Commission exercises its discretionary fingerprint authority under sections 313.912 and 313.1000 through rulemaking that extends checks broadly to operator employees, officers, and directors (the fantasy section expressly reaches all three, while the sports wagering section says only 'applicants'), adding onboarding delays and costs. The override of section 610.120 also means closed or expunged-type records would be visible to the regulator in suitability reviews.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":7991,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:58:47.296Z"},"NC-H1171":{"summary":"H1171 amends North Carolina's general gambling statute, G.S. 14-292, so that its Class 2 misdemeanor prohibition on operating, playing at, or betting on games of chance expressly reaches online markets and platforms offered to North Carolina residents, including prediction markets. It defines a prediction market broadly as a market or platform where a person can bet on the outcome of a future event. It also appropriates $10,000 to the State Lottery Commission for enforcement.","mechanics":["Sec. 1(a) adds new G.S. 14-292(b): the gambling prohibition applies to any physical location in NC or any online market or platform offered to NC residents, including a prediction market.","Definition: a \"prediction market\" is a market or platform where a person can bet on the outcome of a future event. The definition does not limit it to sports or event contracts, and it has no CFTC or federal carve-out.","Penalty is unchanged: a Class 2 misdemeanor for operators and for bettors. The existing exceptions remain for Chapter 18C (the lottery and the sports wagering regime), Parts 2 and 4 of the Article, and lawful lottery play in any state.","Sec. 1(b): criminal provisions take effect December 1, 2026, for offenses committed on or after that date.","Sec. 2: $10,000 in nonrecurring 2026-27 General Fund money goes to the State Lottery Commission for enforcement, effective July 1, 2026."],"issue_tags":["prediction","legalize"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Like MN HF 4437 and SF 4511, H1171 takes a criminal-prohibition approach, but it does so in one sentence by extending the existing general gambling misdemeanor rather than creating a stand-alone offense. The Minnesota bills also add licensing disqualifications. H1171 contrasts sharply with the regulatory ORACLE models in IL HB5059 and NY S09414, which tolerate prediction markets subject to age-21 and conduct rules, and with the CT HB05038 age restrictions and the NJ A5336/S4447 surtax, all of which presuppose that the markets operate legally. Unlike IL HB5142, which folds sports-event prediction markets into the licensed sports wagering definition, H1171 bans all event betting outside Chapter 18C. Its definition is not limited to sports.","watch":"Operators should watch whether the Chapter 18C exception is read to protect licensed NC sports wagering platforms, and whether the open-ended \"bet on the outcome of a future event\" definition and the residents-targeting reach draw a federal preemption challenge under the Commodity Exchange Act from CFTC-registered platforms.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":1933,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:58:52.829Z"},"MO-HB3533":{"summary":"HCS HB 3533 raises the per-patron admission fee that Missouri excursion gambling boat (casino) licensees pay, from $2 per person embarking to $5.50 per person for every two hours spent in the gambling area, with annual CPI indexing. It also creates a 1.5% of handle \"remote wagering access fee\" for licensees in place of the admission fee, adds a new 13% tax on adjusted gross receipts on top of the existing tax in section 313.822, and redirects new money mainly to historic preservation, veterans, and developmental disability funds.","mechanics":["Section 313.820.1: the admission fee rises from $2 to $5.50 per person present, charged for every two hours in the gambling area. It is split $0.50 to the DNR historic preservation revolving fund (Capitol bicentennial through FY2030, then a riverboat heritage artifact facility), $2 to the veterans capital improvement trust fund, $1 to DMH developmental disabilities community services, $1 to the gaming commission fund and $1 to the home dock city or county. Up to one cent of the commission-fund share may go to the compulsive gamblers fund.","Section 313.820.1(2) and 313.805(19): the commission must index the fee each year to the Midwest CPI-U, with the new fee taking effect at the start of each fiscal year, initially for the rest of FY2027 and the next ten fiscal years. The fee still applies to complimentary passes.","Section 313.820.2: in \"all present and future forms of remote wagering,\" a boat licensee pays a remote wagering access fee of 1.5% of total monthly amounts wagered, in lieu of the admission fee. The first $35 million goes to the historic preservation fund and the rest to the gaming commission fund under commission rules. \"Remote wagering\" is not defined.","New section 313.823: adds a 13% tax on adjusted gross receipts from gambling games under sections 313.800 to 313.850, on top of the section 313.822 tax. It expressly is not subject to section 313.822(1), the home-dock local-share provision, so the full 13% goes to the state through the Director of Revenue.","Section 313.835.2(2)(d): inserts a fourth tier of $15 million from gaming commission fund net proceeds to the historic preservation fund for artifact collections. However, the residual clause in paragraph (e) still refers only to paragraphs (a) to (c), which is a drafting inconsistency.","Section 313.805(3) keeps the bar on commission rules limiting wager, loss, or buy-in amounts, and the 80% minimum payout stays in subdivision (12)."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"No other states' bills are listed for comparison. On its own the bill is unusual because it stacks three new costs on Missouri casino licensees at once: a time-based per-patron head tax more than doubled and indexed to inflation, an additional 13% gross gaming revenue tax, and a turnover-based levy of 1.5% of handle on undefined remote wagering. Taxing handle rather than revenue is rare for U.S. gaming, and at typical hold rates 1.5% of handle could equal a large share of gross gaming revenue.","watch":"Watch whether the undefined \"all present and future forms of remote wagering\" fee in section 313.820.2 could be read to reach mobile or online sports wagering offered by excursion boat licensees, because a 1.5% handle levy would stack on Missouri's separately enacted sports wagering tax and raise conflict and preemption questions under that constitutional and statutory framework.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Comm Sub","text_chars":18393,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:58:57.544Z"},"MS-SB2369":{"summary":"SB2369 requires Mississippi gaming licensees to check winners against a daily-updated Department of Human Services (MDHS) database. Where the winner owes child support arrearages, the licensee must deduct the arrearage from W-2G-reportable cash winnings, including sports betting payouts, and from progressive slot annuities before paying out. The Gaming Commission and MDHS must jointly adopt implementing rules, and licensees get civil and criminal immunity for good-faith withholding and disclosure plus the right to charge a capped administrative fee.","mechanics":["Scope (new §43-19-63(1)): covers only 'cash gaming winnings' and 'progressive slot machine annuities' for which the licensee must file IRS Form W-2G or an equivalent. §43-19-31(q) expressly names sports betting among the covered winnings, and 'gaming licensee' means any entity licensed or permitted to operate gaming under current law.","Withholding duty (§43-19-63(4)): the licensee deducts the full arrearage from the payout, remits it to MDHS within 7 days, and pays the winner any remainder, or nothing if the arrearage equals or exceeds the win. MDHS releases the funds to the obligee after 30 days unless the obligor requests a hearing, which is limited to mistaken identity or mistaken amount (§43-19-63(5)-(6)).","Licensee protections and fees: licensees and their officers, employees, attorneys, accountants and agents are immune from civil and criminal liability for disclosure, for encumbering assets, and for withholding or failing to withhold based on MDHS information (§43-19-63(3)). Licensees may deduct an administrative fee of up to $35 per payment, starting with the second annuity payment for annuities (§43-19-63(7)).","Operational rules: once a winner clears the database, the licensee need not re-check that winner for 24 hours (§43-19-63(8)). The Commission must require licensees to adopt procedures that stop employees from willfully failing to withhold (§43-19-63(9)).","Timing: the act takes effect July 1, 2026, and the Commission and MDHS must have all policies, procedures and processes in place by January 1, 2027 (§43-19-63(10))."],"issue_tags":["licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"None of the listed bills addresses intercepting gaming winnings for child support, so there is no direct counterpart. The closest is CT HB05229, which regulates withdrawals from gaming accounts, but as a consumer-protection measure rather than a debt-collection mechanism. The authorization and tax bills (AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, HI HB 1308) and the Illinois licensing and tax bills (e.g. IL HB2878, HB3159) do not impose payout-intercept duties. What is unusual here is that Mississippi expressly folds sports betting payouts into a W-2G-triggered intercept regime, with a daily database check, a 7-day remittance deadline, statutory immunity, and a licensee-retained fee of up to $35.","watch":"Watch the joint Commission/MDHS rulemaking due by January 1, 2027, particularly how the W-2G trigger is applied to sports wagers, the database-check and employee-control procedures, and any disciplinary exposure for failures to withhold, which the statutory immunity bars only as to civil and criminal claims by customers and other persons.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":16700,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:58:59.054Z"},"NC-H384":{"summary":"NC H384 (second edition, committee substitute of 4/8/25) creates a state income tax deduction for federal basic pay received by enlisted Army or Air National Guard members ranked E-1 through E-5. It also rewrites the distribution of remaining sports wagering tax proceeds under G.S. 105-113.128(5). The share going to the Major Events, Games, and Attractions Fund drops from 30% to 29%, and the General Fund's fixed 50% becomes a residual of all proceeds not otherwise credited, which in practice is 51%. The bill does not change the sports wagering tax rate, licensing, or operator obligations.","mechanics":["Section 1 adds G.S. 105-153.5(b)(17): a deduction for the total federal basic pay under Title 37 U.S.C. received by enlisted Army or Air National Guard members holding rank E-1 to E-5. It is effective for taxable years beginning on or after January 1, 2025.","Section 2 amends G.S. 105-113.128(5)b: the Major Events, Games, and Attractions Fund (G.S. 143B-437.112) share of remaining sports wagering proceeds is cut from 30% to 29%.","Section 2 amends G.S. 105-113.128(5)c: the General Fund receives 'proceeds not otherwise credited under this section' instead of a fixed 50%, an effective shift of 1 percentage point to the General Fund.","The 20% share for athletic departments at 13 listed UNC system institutions in sub-subdivision a is unchanged.","Section 2 is effective July 1, 2025 and applies to net proceeds credited on or after that date. The remainder of the act is effective when it becomes law.","Status: introduced; the committee substitute was reported favorably and re-referred to the House Finance Committee on 2025-04-08."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"Several listed bills earmark sports wagering revenue for new purposes by raising operator taxes. IL HB3159 imposes an additional 2% of adjusted gross receipts for fire and EMS, and KS SB303 adds a 2% excise on amounts wagered for property tax relief. NC H384 instead reallocates existing net proceeds without touching the rate or the base. That makes it closest to NE LB421/LB438, which change how sports wagering taxes are distributed, though those bills also expand online wagering. The pairing of an unrelated income tax break with a sports wagering revenue shift is unusual, and appears designed to partially offset the deduction's cost through the General Fund.","watch":"The bill imposes no new operator burden as drafted, but because it now sits in House Finance and treats sports wagering proceeds as a General Fund offset, operators should watch for amendments raising the North Carolina sports wagering tax rate or narrowing deductions to fund this or similar tax cuts.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":2452,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:59:00.315Z"},"NC-H828":{"summary":"H828 amends North Carolina's sports wagering article (G.S. Chapter 18C, Article 9) to define \"proposition wager\" and add it to the list of wagers the Article does not authorize when placed on amateur or college sports. It also bars sports facilities from being open to registered players for wagering during the eight hours before, and for the duration of, any college sports event held at or adjacent to the facility.","mechanics":["Section 1(a) adds G.S. 18C-901(13g), defining a proposition wager as a wager on an individual action, statistic, occurrence, or nonoccurrence determined during a sporting event, including those that do not directly affect the final outcome. The definition is broad and reaches both player and game props.","Section 1(b) adds G.S. 18C-902(d)(5), listing \"the placing of a proposition wager on amateur sports or college sports\" among activities the Article does not authorize. It sits alongside the existing exclusions for youth sports, injuries, penalties, disciplinary outcomes and replay reviews.","Section 2 adds G.S. 18C-926(h): notwithstanding any other provision of the section, no sports facility may be open to registered players for placing sports wagers during the eight hours before or during any college sports event at or adjacent to the facility.","No new penalties are created; enforcement would run through the Lottery Commission's existing authority over unauthorized wagers and facility licensees.","Section 3 sets an effective date of July 1, 2025, with no transition period for existing offerings."],"issue_tags":["prop","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"LA SB354 goes further by stripping sports-related proposition bets from permitted wagers entirely and also banning micro-bets, whereas NC H828 limits its ban to college and amateur sports and leaves professional props intact. NY S02616 moves in the opposite direction, expanding authorized season-long props and award futures with limits on in-game props, and NY A 10538 only creates a task force to study prop betting. MD HB518 addresses college athlete protection through problem-gambling regulation rather than market restrictions. H828's eight-hour, event-adjacent shutdown of in-person sportsbooks at college venues has no counterpart among the listed bills.","watch":"Watch the breadth of the 18C-901(13g) definition, since \"any occurrence or nonoccurrence determined during a sporting event\" could be read to sweep in college in-game markets beyond player props (such as halftime or quarter lines), and watch the undefined \"adjacent to\" trigger in 18C-926(h), which could force closure of retail sportsbooks near campus venues on college game days.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":2117,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:59:06.793Z"},"NC-H937":{"summary":"H937 amends G.S. 105-113.128, the distribution statute for North Carolina's sports wagering tax, to insert a new priority item (3a) earmarking $11 million a year to the Department of Public Instruction for salary supplements to public school athletic coaches. The money tops up each eligible coach's annual coaching supplement to at least $3,000 in combined State and non-State funds. Any unspent balance at fiscal year end goes to the North Carolina Alliance of YMCAs for youth sports. The bill does not change the tax rate, licensing, or wagering rules; it only reallocates proceeds.","mechanics":["New subdivision (3a) in G.S. 105-113.128 credits $11,000,000 annually from net sports wagering tax proceeds to DPI. It sits in the priority order after the existing items (1) through (3), which are elided in the text.","An eligible athletic coach is a full-time public school unit employee who coaches and receives a non-State coaching supplement of $3,000 or less per school year.","An eligible public school unit must report its prior-year non-State coaching supplement spending to DPI by August 15 each year.","Maintenance of effort applies: a unit must keep non-State supplements at least at the prior-year level, and a unit that supplants funds loses State supplement funds the following school year.","Unexpended funds at fiscal year end go to the North Carolina Alliance of YMCAs, Inc. for youth sports programming.","The act takes effect July 1, 2025, beginning with the 2025-2026 school year; the existing DOR administrative cap of $500,000 and Lottery Commission reimbursement are unchanged."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. What is unusual is that this is purely an earmarking bill: it carves a fixed-dollar, education-related line out of the sports wagering tax waterfall without touching the rate, the base, or operator obligations. Tying betting revenue to compensation for public school coaches is a notable policy link, since those coaches oversee student athletes who are a frequent focus of integrity concerns.","watch":"Operators should watch whether adding a fixed $11 million claim to the G.S. 105-113.128 waterfall creates pressure to raise the wagering tax rate or broaden the taxable base so that lower-priority recipients are still funded, especially if handle or gross wagering revenue underperforms.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":3615,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:59:08.923Z"},"NC-H554":{"summary":"H554 is an omnibus North Carolina Department of Revenue technical-corrections bill. It adopts a partnership-level audit regime conforming to the federal Bipartisan Budget Act of 2015, updates the IRC conformity date to January 1, 2025, and makes sales, excise, motor fuel and administrative changes. Its only gaming provision, Section 4.10, rewrites the definition of \"gross wagering revenue\" in G.S. 18C-901(7). That definition controls the base on which interactive sports wagering operators are taxed.","mechanics":["Sec. 4.10 defines gross wagering revenue as amounts received from authorized sports wagers less amounts paid as winnings \"before any deductions for expenses, fees, or taxes.\" Bonuses or promotional credits remain included at cash value when returned to the operator as a deposit or wager. The text as reproduced does not show strike/underline, so the precise added language should be confirmed against the official edition.","Sec. 4.11 makes Part IV, including the GGR definition change, effective when the act becomes law. No retroactive application is stated.","Part II (new G.S. 105-154.2) requires a partnership to report a final federal partnership adjustment within 90 days and notify its direct partners. Direct partners must file within six months. Alternatively, the partnership may make an irrevocable election to pay at the entity level within six months, with tiered-partner rules. Part II is effective for taxable years beginning on or after January 1, 2025.","Sec. 5.1 updates the IRC reference date in G.S. 105-228.90 from January 1, 2023 to January 1, 2025.","Sec. 5.2 raises the frivolous-return penalty from $500 to $2,000.","Sec. 3.1 adjusts remote-seller and marketplace-facilitator nexus timing to the first calendar month at least 60 days after the $100,000 threshold is exceeded.","The title's \"P2P tax parity\" item (extending the alternate highway use tax to peer-to-peer car rentals) is not visible in the truncated text provided."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"Several listed bills change rates or add new levies: KS SB303 adds a 2% excise on the amount wagered, IL HB3159 adds a 2% surcharge on adjusted gross receipts, and IL HB5143 sunsets Illinois's per-wager tax. H554 instead leaves NC's rate untouched and adjusts only the tax base definition inside a technical bill. It is closest in spirit to IA SF 605, a narrow administrative tax fix (winnings withholding) rather than a policy overhaul. Unlike AK HB145, GA HB 686, MS HB 4074 or NE LB421, it does not touch authorization, licensing or channel rules.","watch":"Confirm whether the \"before any deductions for expenses, fees, or taxes\" language is new, and whether it forecloses netting federal excise tax, payment or platform fees, or other costs from GGR. Combined with the continued inclusion of promotional credits, this would widen the taxable base from the date of enactment without any rate change.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":76742,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:59:13.105Z"},"NC-S257":{"summary":"S257 is North Carolina's FY 2026-27 base budget (S.L. 2026-41), appropriating about $34.37 billion in net General Fund spending plus Highway Fund and Highway Trust Fund budgets, and funding several reserves. Its only gaming-related content is a reallocation of Education Lottery Fund and Indian Gaming Education Revenue Fund money. Nothing in the text provided touches sports wagering licensing, tax rates, or operator obligations under Chapter 18C, though the supplied text is truncated partway through Part V.","mechanics":["Sec. 4.2(a) amends S.L. 2025-89, Sec. 2A.1(a), to raise total FY 2026-27 Education Lottery Fund allocations from $1,087,272,612 to $1,143,500,000. School Transportation rises from $186,033,702 to $257,311,891, and the Scholarship Reserve Fund for Public Colleges and Universities drops from $17,748,769 to $2,697,968.","Sec. 4.2(b), effective June 30, 2026, overrides G.S. 18C-164(b3) to send $56,567,388 of FY 2025-26 net lottery revenues, after the (b1) appropriation and (b2) transfer, to school transportation for FY 2026-27. Any remainder goes to the Needs-Based Public School Capital Fund.","Sec. 4.4 amends S.L. 2025-89, Sec. 2A.2(a), to raise FY 2026-27 Indian Gaming Education Revenue Fund allocations from $13.5 million to $19 million. The Classroom Materials Allotment goes from $3.5 million to $9 million, and 'Textbooks and Digital Resources' is renamed the 'Instructional Materials Allotment' (still $10 million).","Sec. 5.5(b)-(c) strips the 'interest-bearing' status from the lottery-funded Needs-Based Public School Capital Fund and repeals G.S. 147-69.2(a)(17m), redirecting investment income to strengthen the General Fund.","Sec. 4.3(b) directs that FY 2026-27 Needs-Based Fund grants prioritize Career and Technical Education Centers serving an entire public school unit."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. On its own, the bill is a standard appropriations act. Its gaming content is limited to reallocating existing lottery and tribal-gaming revenue streams, notably overriding the statutory lottery revenue waterfall in G.S. 18C-164(b3) for one year. It leaves the sports wagering framework untouched in the text shown.","watch":"Watch whether the legislature's reliance on one-time overrides of G.S. 18C-164 and increased gaming-derived appropriations signals pressure to raise gaming revenue, including sports wagering tax rates, in future budget or revenue bills, and check the truncated remainder of S.L. 2026-41 for any Chapter 18C amendments.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":326655,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:59:17.213Z"},"ND-HCR3002":{"summary":"HCR 3002 proposes amending Article XI, Section 25 of the North Dakota Constitution to permit, but not require, the Legislative Assembly to authorize sports betting on professional and college sports, conducted in the state and licensed and regulated by the state. The amendment would have gone to voters at the 2026 general election, and all sports-betting tax revenue would have been earmarked for K-12 public schools. The resolution failed on second reading on 2025-01-22, 24 yeas to 70 nays.","mechanics":["Restructures Art. XI, § 25 into numbered subsections and keeps the existing multi-state lottery mandate (subsection 1) and charitable gaming authority (subsection 2).","New subsection 3: the Legislative Assembly 'may authorize sports betting on professional and college sports to be conducted in the state and licensed and regulated by the state.'","All tax revenue from sports betting must be appropriated for the benefit of kindergarten through grade twelve public schools.","The text does not address tax rates, licensing structure, mobile versus retail betting, number of operators, or the regulator; all of this is left to later legislation.","Would have been submitted to qualified electors at the 2026 general election under Art. IV, § 16; it failed in the House 24-70, so it will not appear on the ballot."],"issue_tags":["legalize","tax"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Like GA HR450 and GA SR131, HCR 3002 is a constitutional amendment that only enables the legislature to act later; SR131 also covers casino gambling. By contrast, AK HB145/SB194, HI HB 1308/SB1569 and GA HB 686 are full statutory frameworks with licensing, tax rates and regulators. Unusually, the ND text expressly limits the authorization to professional and college sports and hard-wires a K-12 education earmark for all tax revenue into the constitution, which no listed bill does at the constitutional level.","watch":"The measure is effectively dead after the 24-70 vote, but any revival should be checked for the 'professional and college sports' limitation, which could constitutionally exclude other markets (e.g., esports, awards shows, prediction-style contracts), and for the constitutional revenue earmark, which would constrain future tax and fee design.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":2264,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:59:19.946Z"},"NE-LB1046":{"summary":"LB1046 would have required the Nebraska State Board of Education to adopt, by January 1, 2027, a model policy governing commercial name, image, and likeness (NIL) activity by high school athletes and activity participants, and required member school boards to adopt consistent policies starting in school year 2027-28. Among other restrictions, the model policy had to bar student-participants from NIL deals promoting gambling, lotteries, or sports betting. The bill was indefinitely postponed on April 17, 2026 and is dead.","mechanics":["Section 1(1)(d) covers 'student-participants': high school students in interscholastic athletics or activities at schools belonging to an athletics or activities association.","Section 1(2): the State Board of Education must adopt a model NIL policy by January 1, 2027. The policy must allow NIL activity, including through agents or attorneys, while students keep their eligibility.","Section 1(2)(b)(iv)(C): the policy must prohibit NIL activity that promotes 'gambling, lotteries, or sports betting'. The same list bars alcohol, tobacco, vaping, controlled substances, adult entertainment, weapons, and illegal products.","Section 1(2)(b)(i)-(iii): NIL activity is also barred if it uses school names, logos, uniforms, or facilities, takes place during school hours or school events, or is used to recruit students or induce transfers.","Section 1(2)(c)-(f): students must disclose NIL activity within five business days, with an appeal process, and the school must notify its association. Penalties may not be monetary but may include temporary ineligibility.","Section 1(3)-(4): school boards must adopt conforming policies beginning in school year 2027-28. Students may not be penalized for NIL activity that complies with the policy."],"issue_tags":["advertising"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. On its own terms, the bill is an education and NIL measure, and its only gaming touchpoint is a mandatory ban on high school athletes endorsing gambling, lotteries, or sports betting. That ban appears as one item in a standard list of prohibited 'vice' categories rather than in gaming regulation.","watch":"The bill is dead, but operators and affiliates should watch for revived high school NIL bills, which could ban sports-betting endorsements by minors or student-athletes through school-board policy rather than gaming law, broadening restrictions on athlete-based marketing.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":5186,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:59:23.179Z"},"NC-S595":{"summary":"S595 (S.L. 2026-31) is North Carolina's omnibus revenue-laws technical bill. It amends personal income tax, withholding, sales tax, motor fuel and other excise provisions, adopts a state regime for federal BBA partnership audit adjustments, and, per its title, also covers vapor directory enforcement, tax foreclosure, credit unions, the IRC reference date, savings banks, penny-rounding and PART board pay. Nothing in the text provided addresses sports wagering, gaming, or gambling taxation.","mechanics":["Section 1.4 moves semiweekly withholding filers (those withholding an average of $2,000+ a month) to quarterly returns due by the last day of the month after the quarter. It replaces federal-linked deposit timing with fixed Wednesday/Friday payment deadlines based on payday.","Section 1.11 replaces the federal section 6654(e) safe harbor for estimated-tax interest with a flat $1,000 threshold. Section 1.2 restricts the NOL statute (G.S. 105-153.5A) to individuals, estates and trusts, retroactive to tax years beginning on or after January 1, 2022.","Part II adds new G.S. 105-154.2, conforming to the federal centralized partnership audit regime. Partnerships must report final federal partnership adjustments within 90 days and direct partners within six months, or an audited partnership may irrevocably elect to pay at the entity level. It applies to adjustments final on or after January 1, 2026, with matching limitation periods in G.S. 105-241.6 and 105-241.8.","Section 3.1 changes the remote-seller and marketplace-facilitator nexus start date: a retailer exceeding the $100,000 threshold becomes engaged in business on the first day of the first month at least 60 days after crossing it. Section 3.5 lets the Secretary reduce the 65% sales tax prepayment for $20,000/month filers where collected tax is held in trust for another person.","Part IV makes excise changes. It adds renewable diesel to the definition of diesel fuel, treats owners of tax-paid fuel entering the terminal transfer system at non-approved terminals as suppliers subject to a $2,000,000 bond, and adds nonresident spirituous liquor vendors to the alcohol excise registration requirement."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. On its own, this is a routine North Carolina Department of Revenue technical-corrections package. Its most notable features are the new state partnership audit regime (G.S. 105-154.2) and the shift of large withholding filers off federal deposit schedules. The text supplied is truncated mid-Part IV, and the portion shown contains no wagering provisions.","watch":"Operators should check the truncated remainder, especially the IRC conformity update and any later Part, for changes touching Chapter 18C sports wagering tax administration or for flow-through of federal 2025–26 changes such as the section 165(d) gambling-loss limit. They should also note that the new quarterly withholding schedule in G.S. 105-163.6(d) will apply to any operator withholding $2,000 or more a month.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":202988,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:59:27.965Z"},"NE-LB280":{"summary":"LB280 would have required every authorized gaming operator licensed under the Nebraska Racetrack Gaming Act to obtain an annual independent CPA audit of its Nebraska financial statements and internal controls, and to file it with both the Auditor of Public Accounts and the State Racing and Gaming Commission. It would also have given the Auditor power to review those reports and to examine operators' books on its own initiative, focusing on how gross gaming revenue and the gaming tax are calculated. The bill was indefinitely postponed on 2026-04-17 and is dead.","mechanics":["Sec. 7(1): each authorized gaming operator must pay for an annual audit of its financial statements, including a report on internal controls. The audit covers all Nebraska financial transactions for the prior fiscal year and must be done by a CPA authorized to practice in Nebraska.","Sec. 7(2): the audit report is due electronically to the Auditor of Public Accounts and the gaming commission within six months after fiscal year-end, unless the Auditor grants a written extension. The Auditor reviews it for math errors, improper accounting or other deficiencies, especially those affecting gross gaming revenue or the gaming tax, and reports to the Governor, Legislature and commission.","Sec. 7(3): the Auditor may, at its discretion, examine an operator's books, records, funds and accounts relating to the gaming tax under section 9-1203, under GAO Government Auditing Standards. This includes whether the tax is properly calculated, collected and credited to the Property Tax Credit Cash Fund.","Secs. 2 and 7: the Auditor's review and examination costs are paid from the Racing and Gaming Commission's Racetrack Gaming Fund, which section 9-1107 is amended to allow, rather than billed to operators.","Secs. 4-6: existing enforcement provisions are extended to the new section 7. Payment is due by the 15th of each month, late tax carries a 10% penalty plus interest, and willful failure to make a required report or knowingly making a false statement is a Class IV felony (sec. 9-1207).","Sec. 3 keeps the definition of game of chance, which includes sports wagering, so sports wagering revenue at racetrack casinos falls within the audited gross gaming revenue."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"None of the listed bills is a post-licensure financial audit mandate. AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686 and HI HB 1308/SB1569 authorize and tax new sports wagering markets. CO SB 163 reallocates licensing duties between the Colorado commission and division. IA SSB 1157 and IL HB3159/HB5143 change tax rates or how revenue is used. LB280 is unusual because it brings in the state's elected financial auditor, not the gaming regulator, as a second check on tax computation, including the promotional-credit deduction in the gross gaming revenue definition.","watch":"If the concept returns, operators should watch the combination of mandatory internal-control audits, discretionary Auditor examinations of gaming-tax calculations (including promotional credit deductions on sports wagering), and the extension of Class IV felony exposure under section 9-1207 to reporting failures under the new audit section.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":10602,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:59:33.766Z"},"NE-LB177":{"summary":"LB177 amends Nebraska's Mechanical Amusement Device Tax Act to convert the operator, distributor and cash-device manufacturer licenses issued by the Tax Commissioner from annual to biennial terms. It appears to double the associated fees to match the two-year term, and it narrows the definition of distributor to exclude retail establishments. The bill leaves intact the fingerprint-based FBI background checks, the nine enumerated grounds for denial, and the gambling-winnings debt-setoff duties for cash-device operators and distributors.","mechanics":["Secs. 2–4 (77-3002, 77-3003, 77-3003.03): operator, distributor and cash-device manufacturer licenses become biennial rather than annual.","Sec. 3 (77-3003(3)): the distributor cash-device fee reads as $200 per device, capped at $10,000, per biennial license, replacing $100 per device capped at $5,000 annually; the chaptered text shows both figures, so confirm against the official redline.","Sec. 4 (77-3003.03(1)): the cash-device manufacturer license fee reads as $10,000 per biennial term, replacing $5,000 annually.","Sec. 1 (77-3001(3)): 'distributor' now covers any person 'other than a retail establishment' who places and controls or manages a mechanical amusement device in a retail establishment; 'cash device' still includes devices awarding cash, gift cards, credit or other currency-denominated value.","Retained: a one-time FBI fingerprint background check (liquor licensees exempt for operator and distributor licenses), personal history reports, and nine for-cause denial grounds including gambling, fraud or theft convictions and liquor-license discipline for illegal gambling.","Retained: Commissioner suspension and revocation powers, and the duty of operators and distributors to check the Gambling Winnings Setoff for Outstanding Debt Act collection system before paying cash-device winnings."],"issue_tags":["licensing","tax"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike the sports-wagering authorization bills listed (AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, HI HB 1308/SB1569), LB177 does not touch sports betting; it is administrative housekeeping for Nebraska's licensed skill-based 'cash device' market. Its closest analogues are Illinois' gray-machine bills. IL HB3733 would broaden the criminal 'gambling device' definition to capture credit-awarding machines, and IL HB3274/HB5048 would shield prize and gift kiosks from Gaming Board discipline. Nebraska instead keeps legal cash devices regulated as taxed amusement devices under the Department of Revenue rather than a gaming commission.","watch":"Watch whether the move to biennial licensing and the doubled fees signal that Nebraska is entrenching revenue-department oversight of skill-based cash devices, which compete with racetrack casino gaming and sports wagering for the same retail customers.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":19736,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:59:36.032Z"},"NE-LB342":{"summary":"LB342 would have created the Fantasy Sports Consumer Protection Act, requiring paid-entry fantasy contest operators to be licensed by the Nebraska Department of Revenue, imposing a 10% tax on adjusted gross fantasy contest receipts, and setting age, geolocation, integrity and responsible-gaming requirements. It would also have amended the criminal gambling statutes (28-1101, 28-1105, 28-1113) to exempt fantasy contests conducted under the Act. The bill was indefinitely postponed on April 17, 2026 and is dead.","mechanics":["Licensing (Sec. 4, 6): Department of Revenue license required, with a $5,000 nonrefundable application fee, fingerprint background checks for officers, directors and 10%+ owners, and a bar on applicants convicted of any felony or gambling offense. Licenses run four years, with a renewal fee of 1% of the prior four years' adjusted gross receipts.","Initial license fee (Sec. 6): existing operators pay the lesser of 10% of prior-year adjusted gross receipts or $5,000. New entrants pay $5,000 plus a supplemental fee equal to 10% of first-year receipts minus $5,000. Existing operators could keep operating pending licensure (Sec. 4(5)).","Tax (Sec. 9): from July 1, 2026, 10% of adjusted gross fantasy contest receipts, defined as entry fees minus prizes multiplied by the in-state location percentage. The tax is filed monthly on an accrual basis and split 85% to the General Fund and 15% to the Compulsive Gamblers Assistance Fund.","Definition (Sec. 2(7)): covers online skill contests built on statistical performance of individual athletes, including single-participant contests against an operator-set target score (pick'em style). It excludes free contests and single-sport, season-long peer contests whose administrative fee is 50% or less of entry fees. Sec. 3(4) bars the department from restricting contest types by labelling them sports wagering under 9-1103.","Consumer protection (Sec. 5, 12): minimum age 18, in-state geolocation required, problem-gambling messaging on platforms and all advertising, self-exclusion folded into the department's existing program, and forfeited prizes of prohibited participants donated to problem-gaming programs.","Enforcement and other duties (Sec. 7, 10, 13): civil penalty of up to $1,000 per knowing violation, capped at $10,000 per transaction or occurrence. Licensees must file a CPA audit and an annual compliance report, deferred two years for new licensees. Large operators (adjusted gross receipts of $1 million or more) must report on diverse and small-business procurement."],"issue_tags":["licensing","tax","legalize","integrity","prop"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. On its own, the bill is unusual in housing fantasy regulation in the Department of Revenue rather than the Nebraska Racing and Gaming Commission, although Sec. 4(5) mistakenly refers to 'the commission.' It also expressly protects operator-vs-player target-score contests and forbids regulators from reclassifying any contest type as sports wagering. Pairing a 10% tax with a percentage-based license fee and renewal fee is also distinctive.","watch":"Although LB342 is dead, operators should watch for a reintroduced version that keeps the Sec. 2(7)(b) inclusion of single-player target-score (pick'em) contests and the Sec. 3(4) bar on reclassifying contests as sports wagering, since those provisions decide whether prop-style DFS products can be offered in Nebraska outside the sports wagering regime.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":36010,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:59:39.276Z"},"NE-LB357":{"summary":"LB357 is a General Affairs Committee cleanup of Nebraska's racing and racetrack casino statutes. It creates a separate racetrack enclosure license distinct from the license to conduct race meets, and moves the definition of gross gaming revenue into the Nebraska Racetrack Gaming Act's definitions section. That definition includes a deduction for redeemed promotional gaming credits. The bill also lengthens race meet licenses, merges the commission's racing and gaming cash funds into a renamed Racetrack Gaming Fund, and tightens fingerprinting rules for racing licensees and racetrack management.","mechanics":["Sec. 6 creates a racetrack enclosure license. Eligible applicants are the State Fair Board, county fair boards, county agricultural societies, and civic or livestock-exposition nonprofits. The license costs $10,000 to apply, is valid for three years, and costs $10,000 to renew. It is expressly separate from the race meet license under §2-1204.","Sec. 14 (§9-1103) defines gross gaming revenue as dollars collected from all games of chance minus (a) federal non-income taxes on those games and (b) promotional gaming credits, but only to the extent players redeem them. It also adds definitions of 'promotional gaming credit' and 'designated sports wagering area' (a commission-approved area where sports wagering is conducted). The 20% annual gaming tax on gross gaming revenue in §9-1203 continues to apply. §9-1202 is outright repealed.","Sec. 8 (§2-1205) raises the maximum race meet license term from three to five years. The commission must meet at least eight times a year rather than six (§2-1202).","Sec. 5 (§2-1203.02) requires FBI fingerprint checks through the Nebraska State Patrol for licensees aged 16 to 75 and for anyone in racetrack administration or management, including the governing body. Applicants fingerprinted in Nebraska within the past five years are exempt. Non-restricted concession staff, admissions and parking staff, and medical personnel are excluded.","Sec. 16 (§9-1107) renames the fund as the Racetrack Gaming Fund and adds horseracing license fees and gross tax receipts to it, but not §2-1208 taxes. The fund now also covers horseracing administration. The Racing Cash Fund's balance is transferred into it, and the cash fund is eliminated.","Existing operator terms in §9-1106 are carried forward unchanged in the text: the $5 million operator license fee (payable over five years), terms of at least 20 years with a $50,000 annual review fee, fines of up to $50,000 or three times the highest daily gross receipts, and adjudication subcommittee fines of up to $15,000. The provided text is the introduced version and is truncated at Sec. 19."],"issue_tags":["tax","licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"No other states' bills are listed for comparison. On its own, the bill is unusual in two ways. First, it splits the real-property 'racetrack enclosure' license from the race meet license and restricts enclosure licensees to nonprofit or civic entities. Second, it writes into statute a promotional-credit deduction from the 20% gaming tax base that is limited to credits actually redeemed. That is narrower than in states that allow broader free-play deductions.","watch":"Operators should check whether the promotional-credit deduction in the enrolled version is limited to credits redeemed on games the operator itself runs, and how the commission will define 'designated sports wagering area' and implement the new enclosure license, since the operator license is tied to a licensed racetrack enclosure.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":60473,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:59:44.699Z"},"NE-LB370":{"summary":"LB370 would have rewritten Nebraska's Student-Athlete Name, Image, or Likeness Rights Act to let institutions, NIL collectives and third parties pay student-athletes directly, bar collegiate athletic associations from investigating or penalizing activity the Act protects, and regulate athlete-agent agency agreements. Its only gambling touchpoint is an athlete-agent disqualification for certain convictions, including misdemeanors involving gambling or sports wagering. The bill was indefinitely postponed on 2026-04-17 and is dead.","mechanics":["Sec. 2 adds definitions of agency agreement (with an agent registered under the Nebraska Uniform Athlete Agents Act), name, image, or likeness collective, parties and third party; Sec. 3 lets student-athletes be paid directly by institutions, athletic program supporters, collectives or third parties, and lets them rescind an agency agreement within 15 calendar days without penalty.","Sec. 8 requires a collective, institution or third party that expects to pay a student-athlete at least $5,000 in a calendar year to first sign a written contract covering payor identity, total compensation, payment timing, obligations, term, revision or termination options, and any non-payment terms.","Sec. 9(2) bars an athlete agent from entering an agency agreement if the agent has a pending civil case (excluding divorce) or a conviction for a felony; a misdemeanor involving physical contact, deception, or gambling or sports wagering; multiple alcohol or drug violations; or multiple misdemeanor sentences. Agents also may not pay inducements to the athlete or a relative, and noncompliant agreements are void under Sec. 9(6).","Sec. 7 bars collegiate athletic associations from accepting complaints, investigating, penalizing, or requiring reporting about NIL activity the Act protects; Sec. 6 bars institutions from complying with conflicting association rules or investigations.","Sec. 10 voids any contract offering an upfront payment in exchange for future NIL rights or professional sports earnings.","Sec. 11 expands civil-action standing to collectives, supporters and third parties, with actual damages, equitable relief and attorney's fees available, and a one-year limitations period for suits against public institutions."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"LB370 belongs with the NIL bills listed, including DE HB314, GA HB383, HI HB 2384 and SB3263, LA HB 513, NH HB312, TX HB126 and WI AB1034. It is unusually aggressive in using state law to shield institutions, collectives and third parties from collegiate athletic association enforcement (Sec. 7), and in regulating agency agreements, including the $5,000 written-contract threshold. Unlike MD HB518, which pairs problem-gambling rules with college athlete protections, or NC H828, which would prohibit prop bets on college and amateur sports, LB370 imposes no obligations on wagering operators. Its only gambling hook is the agent disqualification for gambling or sports-wagering misdemeanors.","watch":"The bill is dead, so operators face no direct exposure, but if it is reintroduced they should watch whether the gambling and sports-wagering conviction bar for athlete agents grows into broader athlete-integrity or prop-bet restrictions of the kind pending in NC H828 and MD HB518.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":27991,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:59:49.925Z"},"NE-LB415":{"summary":"As introduced, LB415 amends the voter-approved Nebraska Healthy Families and Workplaces Act (Initiative Law 2024, No. 436) to narrow who counts as an employee, delay and condition sick-time accrual, and give employers more flexibility in meeting the law. According to its enacted title and last action, the final law also amends the Conveyance Safety Act and, via AM545 importing LB698, lets unemployment-benefit overpayment debt be offset against gambling winnings under the Gambling Winnings Setoff for Outstanding Debt Act. None of that gambling language appears in the introduced text supplied here.","mechanics":["Sec. 1 redefines employee to exclude individual owner-operators, independent contractors, people working in Nebraska fewer than 80 hours a year, and railroad workers covered by the federal Railroad Unemployment Insurance Act. Employers exclude the United States, the State of Nebraska, and its agencies and political subdivisions.","Sec. 2 says accrual begins only after 80 hours of consecutive employment, at one hour per 30 hours worked. Annual earning and use caps are 40 hours for small businesses (fewer than 20 employees) and 56 hours for other employers.","Accrual starts at the later of commencement of employment or October 1, 2025. Paid sick time provided between January 1 and October 1, 2025 counts toward the employer's obligations.","Employers may front-load a year's sick time, pay out unused time at year end instead of carrying it over, or rely on an equivalent existing PTO policy without extra accrual or carryover. Payout at separation is not required.","Sec. 3 deletes the 'in compliance with the Act' qualifier on oral requests and allows documentation after more than three consecutive work days.","The gambling-winnings setoff for unemployment overpayment debt was added by AM545 from LB698. Its rates, thresholds and operator duties are not in the text provided and must be checked against the enrolled version."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No other states' bills are listed for comparison. What is unusual is that this is a labor bill, a rollback of a 2024 paid-sick-leave ballot initiative, that became a vehicle for a gambling-winnings intercept for unemployment overpayment debt. That intercept piece arrived only by late amendment (AM545, from LB698) and is not in the introduced text.","watch":"Pull the enrolled LB415 text for the LB698/AM545 language, because extending Nebraska's Gambling Winnings Setoff for Outstanding Debt Act to unemployment overpayment debt likely adds a new debt category that licensed gaming operators must screen for and intercept before paying out qualifying winnings.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":13098,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:59:54.038Z"},"NE-LB402":{"summary":"LB402 would have added the Nebraska Department of Labor as a claimant under the Gambling Winnings Setoff for Outstanding Debt Act. Operators would then intercept unemployment-benefit overpayment debts from reportable casino, parimutuel, sports wagering and cash device winnings, on top of the child support and state tax debts already covered. It also amended section 48-665 to list the gambling setoff as a recovery method. The bill was indefinitely postponed on 2026-04-17 and is dead.","mechanics":["Sections 1–2 add a new purpose (9-1302(3)), a new claimant (9-1303(4)(d)) and a new debt category (9-1303(6)(b)) for overpayments of benefits under section 48-663.01 to which the obligor was not entitled.","Covered sports wagering winnings are unchanged: winnings that must be reported on IRS Form W-2G, have tax withheld by the operator, and come from sports wagers authorized by the State Racing and Gaming Commission.","Under section 3 (9-1304(4)), the Department of Labor may submit certified debts of $25 or more, unless validity is legitimately disputed, on a continuous, fluctuating basis. The collection system shows operators only a total amount owed, without identifying its source.","Operators still deduct the amount shown in the collection system and remit it to the Department of Revenue. Under section 4 (9-1306(3)), Revenue credits remittances to HHS child support debt first, then Department of Labor debt, then state tax liability.","Under sections 5–6, the Department of Labor must notify the obligor within 20 days of a remittance; the obligor has 30 days to request a hearing, with appeals under the Administrative Procedure Act. Section 7 gives the Department of Labor rulemaking authority alongside HHS, Revenue and the Racing and Gaming Commission.","Section 8 adds offset against winnings payments as new clause (d) of 48-665(1). Despite the bill title, the introduced text shows no visible change to any limitations period. No separate effective date is stated; operator obligations follow the Tax Commissioner's implementation date under 9-1312."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"Most of the listed bills create or restructure sports wagering, iGaming, fantasy or prediction-market regimes, such as HI HB 1308, HI SB1569, IL HB3080 and IL HB5059. None of them, like LB402, simply expands the categories of state debt operators must intercept from winnings. The closest analogue is MS HB 1581, whose title suggests extending mobile sports betting provisions to child support. LB402 stands out for adding a third claimant to an existing intercept system and setting a statutory priority order among competing state claimants.","watch":"Although dead, the concept could return in a future session; if it does, operators would face a broader intercept obligation on W-2G winnings without seeing the source of the debt, so the operational points to watch are the Tax Commissioner's implementation date and any rules adopted by the Department of Labor or the Racing and Gaming Commission.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":21022,"generated":"2026-11-02T18:59:54.499Z"},"NE-LB438":{"summary":"LB438 would have amended the Nebraska Racetrack Gaming Act to let licensed racetrack casino operators (authorized gaming operators) offer statewide online sports wagering to persons located in Nebraska, either directly or through a single contracted platform provider, subject to State Racing and Gaming Commission approval of platform controls. It also would have carved sports wagering tax revenue out of the existing gaming-tax split and redirected it, principally to a new Education Future Fund allocation, with no local share. The bill was indefinitely postponed on April 17, 2026.","mechanics":["Adds definitions in sec. 9-1103: 'online sports wagering platform' (hardware/software/apps offering sports wagering over the Internet to persons located in Nebraska) and 'platform provider' (a sports wagering vendor contracting with an authorized gaming operator).","Amends sec. 9-1110(1) so wagers may be placed in person or at kiosks in the racetrack's designated area, or via an online platform offered by or in partnership with an authorized gaming operator to an individual located in Nebraska.","Tethered model with a one-skin cap: each authorized gaming operator may run its own platform or contract with up to one platform provider. Any vendor without such a contract is barred from conducting sports wagering in Nebraska.","Operators and contracted vendors must submit platform controls for Commission approval and may not launch online wagering until those controls are approved. Online wagering must meet all existing Act and rule requirements, including the existing in-state collegiate and player-prop exclusions and the prohibited-participant rules.","Amends sec. 9-1204(2) so the sports wagering tax under sec. 9-1203 goes entirely to the State Treasurer: 2.5% Compulsive Gamblers Assistance Fund, 5% Racetrack Gaming Fund, 27.5% Property Tax Credit Cash Fund, 20% General Fund, 45% Education Future Fund. Non-sports gaming revenue keeps the existing 75% state / 25% county-city split.","Does not change the tax rate in sec. 9-1203 or the 21+ age restriction for the physical wagering area. It sets no separate online licensing fee or age-verification and geolocation standards beyond Commission-approved controls."],"issue_tags":["legalize","tax","licensing","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Like AK HB145/SB194 and HI HB 1308/HB 2570/SB1569, LB438 would authorize mobile sports wagering, but it does so by extending an existing racetrack-casino framework rather than creating a standalone licensing regime with new operator and supplier licenses. GA HB 686 and the GA resolutions (HR450, SR131) start from scratch and, in Georgia's case, require constitutional change. LB438 also differs from Illinois bills such as HB2878/HB5034, which adjust license fees and caps for standalone online operators, by tying online access strictly to racetrack licensees with a one-provider limit. Unusually, it leaves the tax rate untouched and only reallocates proceeds, eliminating the host county and city share for sports wagering revenue.","watch":"Watch whether any revived version keeps the tethered 'up to one platform provider' cap per racetrack licensee and the bar on untethered vendors, since that dictates market-access deals, and whether a revival raises the sec. 9-1203 tax rate alongside the reallocation away from local governments.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":13209,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:00:03.681Z"},"NE-LB704":{"summary":"LB704 amends Nebraska's Title IV-D child support review statutes (sections 43-512.12 and 43-512.13) so that the Department of Health and Human Services must automatically start a review of a noncustodial parent's order within fifteen business days of learning the parent will be incarcerated more than 180 days, and must finish it within ninety days. Where the department finds no decrease is warranted, the incarcerated parent gets a hearing, in person or virtual, before a final determination. The only gaming connection is pre-existing language listing lottery, casino, parimutuel, sports wagering and cash device winnings as possible substantial changes in circumstances; the bill does not change that language.","mechanics":["Sec. 1, 43-512.12(3)(a): within 15 business days of learning a noncustodial parent will be incarcerated over 180 calendar days, DHHS must initiate a review rather than just notify of the right to request one.","Notice goes by first-class mail to both parents, who must submit financial information within 30 days; notice to the inmate goes to the facility address and to the facility administrator (as defined in 47-1003).","Sec. 1, 43-512.12(3)(b): DHHS must complete the review and notify the parties within 90 calendar days of learning of the incarceration.","Sec. 2, 43-512.13(2): if no decrease is warranted, DHHS must hold a hearing within 30 days, in person or virtually. Parties may submit evidence 10 business days before the hearing, and the inmate may waive it 5 business days before.","DHHS must issue a final determination within 30 days after the hearing; that determination is not a contested case under the Administrative Procedure Act.","Unchanged 43-512.12(2) keeps 'sports wagering winnings', 'casino winnings' and similar winnings as examples of an ongoing income source that can justify a review within three years."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. The bill is unusual for this tracker because its only gaming reference is existing statutory text treating sports wagering and casino winnings as income that can trigger a child support review. The substantive changes concern incarcerated parents only.","watch":"Nothing operational here, but note that Nebraska already treats sports wagering winnings as a basis for child support modification, which could tie into any future child-support intercept or winnings-reporting mandates on operators.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":8876,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:00:05.661Z"},"NE-LB421":{"summary":"LB421 would have let each Nebraska racetrack casino licensee (authorized gaming operator) offer statewide online sports wagering through one online platform, either directly or with one contracted platform provider, under the existing Nebraska Racetrack Gaming Act. It also would have redirected sports wagering tax revenue away from the current split, which includes a 25% county/city share, into a sports-specific distribution that sends 90% to property tax relief. The bill was indefinitely postponed on April 17, 2026 and is dead.","mechanics":["Online wagering is tethered to the tracks (Sec. 3, amending 9-1110(1)(b)). Each authorized gaming operator may run one online sports wagering platform and contract with one platform provider. Anyone without such a contract may not conduct sports wagering in Nebraska. Bettors must be physically located in the state, and servers may sit out of state except where federal law requires in-state servers.","Launch sequencing: the commission must begin accepting platform controls within 30 days of adopting online rules and has 30 days to approve or deny them. Operators or providers that file within the first 30-day window and are approved launch on the same day, no later than 60 days after the commission first accepts controls.","New definitions of 'online sports wagering platform' and 'platform provider' (Sec. 1, 9-1103(12)-(13)). The sports wagering definition appears to add exclusions for wagers on individual athletes under 18 in professional or international events and on individual sporting events excluded by the commission. The strike-and-insert formatting in the reproduced text ('includes, but is not limited to, does not include') is garbled, so the final operative language should be confirmed against the official bill.","Tax distribution (Sec. 4, 9-1204(2)): tax under 9-1203 on gross gaming revenue from sports wagering would go 3% to the Compulsive Gamblers Assistance Fund, 3.5% to the Racetrack Gaming Fund, 3.5% to the Racing Cash Fund, and 90% to the Property Tax Credit Cash Fund. There is no county or city share for sports wagering. The tax rate itself is unchanged.","The commission is directed to examine other states' online sports wagering rules and, if practicable, adopt a similar framework (new 9-1106(31)). No new license fee for online operation or platform providers is created.","Operative date September 1, 2025, with an emergency clause and a severability clause."],"issue_tags":["legalize","tax","licensing","prop","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Alaska's HB145 and SB194 and Georgia's HB 686 build standalone mobile sports wagering regimes with their own licensing and tax rates. Hawaii's HB 1308, HB 2570 and SB1569 create new operator and supplier licensing under a designated agency. LB421 instead bolts online wagering onto Nebraska's existing racetrack casino licenses: one skin per track, no new license class or fee for platform providers, and no rate change, only a redistribution of existing tax. It also differs from the Illinois license-fee bills (HB2878 and HB5034, which set a $15 million master license fee) by leaving fees to the commission's general authority, and its simultaneous-launch window is an unusual market-access feature.","watch":"If this approach returns, operators should focus on the one-platform-per-track tether: each national brand would need to win one of a handful of racetrack partnerships before the first 30-day controls window closes. They should also watch whether lost county and city revenue under the new 90% property-tax distribution draws local opposition or prompts a tax-rate change.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":28495,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:00:08.844Z"},"NE-LB63":{"summary":"LB63 would have amended the definitions section of the Nebraska Racetrack Gaming Act (section 9-1103) to narrow what counts as permissible sports wagering. Its clearest substantive change is a new carve-out removing from the definition of sports wagering any wager on the performance or nonperformance of an individual athlete under eighteen who is participating in a professional or international sporting event. The bill was indefinitely postponed on 2026-04-17 and is dead.","mechanics":["Amends only section 9-1103, the definitions section, and repeals the original section; no new licensing, tax or enforcement provisions.","Adds new subdivision (14)(c): sports wagering does not include wagers on the performance or nonperformance of any individual athlete under 18 participating in a professional or international sporting event. The existing commission-excluded individual-event carve-out is relettered from (c) to (d).","Restates the existing exclusions for collegiate games involving an in-state team: no individual-athlete wagers in a single game or match, and no in-game wagers.","Restates subdivision (3)(b), which excludes from 'authorized sporting event' in-state collegiate events with an in-state team, minor league and high-school-level events, parimutuel wagers, fantasy contests, and any event excluded by the commission.","Restates the 'prohibited participant' definition in (12), covering proxies, athletes, coaches, referees, people with influence or exclusive information, and people on lists supplied by sports governing bodies.","The introduced text does not show strike-through formatting, so beyond the under-18 carve-out and the relettering, the exact amendments to existing language cannot be confirmed from this copy."],"issue_tags":["prop","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. On its own terms, LB63 is unusual in targeting minors who compete in professional and international events, such as teenage tennis players, gymnasts or Olympic athletes. It does this through the statutory definition of sports wagering rather than leaving it to commission rulemaking, so the restriction would bind regardless of commission action.","watch":"The bill is dead, but operators should watch for the under-18 player-prop carve-out to return in a later session or through State Racing and Gaming Commission rulemaking, since it would require age-verification of athletes across professional and international markets (notably tennis, Olympic sports and esports) and the pulling of affected props.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":5927,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:00:12.027Z"},"NE-LR20CA":{"summary":"LR20CA would have placed on the November 2026 general election ballot an amendment to Nebraska Constitution Article III, section 24(5) letting authorized gaming operators that conduct sports wagering within a licensed racetrack enclosure accept sports wagers through a mobile or electronic platform, whether offered directly or in partnership with another party, from anyone physically located in Nebraska. The current constitutional authorization limits games of chance to licensed racetrack enclosures. The resolution was indefinitely postponed on 2026-04-17 and is dead.","mechanics":["Amends Art. III, sec. 24(5), which currently authorizes laws licensing and taxing games of chance conducted by authorized gaming operators within a licensed racetrack enclosure.","Adds a sentence allowing racetrack-casino operators conducting sports wagering to accept wagers via a mobile or electronic platform 'offered by or in partnership with' the operator.","Sole geographic condition: the bettor must be located inside Nebraska when the wager is placed, which implies a geolocation requirement. The text sets no age limit, tax rate, or license count.","Mobile access is tethered to the existing racetrack licensees. The text does not authorize standalone online sportsbook licenses.","Sec. 1 would have submitted the measure to voters at the November 2026 general election. Sec. 2 sets the ballot language, with For/Against options.","The rest of sec. 24, covering the lottery allocation (including the Compulsive Gamblers Assistance Fund transfers), parimutuel racing and bingo, is restated without substantive change."],"issue_tags":["legalize","licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Like GA HR450 and GA SR131, LR20CA is a constitutional amendment rather than a statute. Unlike them, it does not authorize sports betting from scratch. It only extends Nebraska's existing in-person racetrack sports wagering to mobile. AK HB145/SB194 and HI HB 1308/SB1569 would create full mobile frameworks with taxes and licensing. LR20CA instead ties mobile betting to existing racetrack licensees and leaves tax, licensing and consumer-protection details to later legislation, closer to a tethered model than Illinois's standalone online licenses (IL HB2878, HB5034).","watch":"Because the measure is dead for the 2026 ballot, watch for reintroduction, and note that the 'offered by or in partnership with' language would force national online operators to partner with Nebraska racetrack licensees (a tethered skin model) rather than obtain independent licenses.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":5586,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:00:15.830Z"},"NH-HB1194":{"summary":"HB1194, now Chapter 322 of 2026, is mainly an insurance tax bill. It stretches the premium tax credit for Life and Health Insurance Guaranty Association assessments from 20 percent a year over 5 years to 10 percent a year over 10 years, for assessments made after the effective date. Section 2 rewrites the sports wagering carve-out in the criminal gambling statute, RSA 647:2, V(f), so that it exempts only the conduct of a sports book, or participation in sports wagering, that is authorized by RSA 287-I.","mechanics":["Sec. 2 amends RSA 647:2, V(f). The old exemption covered \"sports wagering as defined by RSA 287-I:1, XV\". The new exemption covers \"the conduct of a sports book, or participation in sports wagering, authorized by RSA 287-I.\"","The amended exemption names both sides of the bet: the operator (conduct of a sports book) and the bettor (participation in sports wagering).","Sec. 2 took effect on passage, 7/15/2026, under Sec. 3, II.","Sec. 1 adds RSA 408-F:13, I-a. For assessments made after its effective date, including additional assessments on insurers already declared impaired or insolvent, a member insurer may offset 10 percent of the assessment against its RSA 400-A premium tax in each of the 10 following calendar years.","Under new I-b, an insurer that ceases doing business may credit all uncredited assessments in that year. Sec. 1 takes effect 9/13/2026, 60 days after passage.","The LBA fiscal note calls Sec. 2 a \"housekeeping measure\" with no fiscal impact."],"issue_tags":["legalize","licensing"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states were listed. What is unusual is that a one-line change to the criminal gambling carve-out rides on an insurance guaranty-fund tax credit bill. Rather than resting the exemption on the statutory definition of sports wagering, it expressly conditions it on authorization under RSA 287-I.","watch":"Because the criminal exemption now turns on wagering being \"authorized by RSA 287-I\" rather than merely meeting the statutory definition, operators and bettors outside the state's licensed sports-book framework (for example, unlicensed or event-contract offerings) may have less room to argue they fall outside the gambling offenses, so clients should confirm their New Hampshire activity fits squarely within 287-I authorization.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":6355,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:00:23.278Z"},"NJ-A3258":{"summary":"A3258 amends New Jersey's sports wagering law (P.L.2018, c.33) to bar sports wagering licensees from offering or accepting any \"micro bet,\" defined as a live, in-play proposition bet on the outcome of the next play or action in an event. Violations are disorderly persons offenses, fined per wager. The text provided is the introduced version, which bans micro bets in all channels; the current bill title, after the June 1, 2026 committee amendments, refers to micro bets \"online,\" so the amended version likely narrows the ban to online sports pools, but that amended text is not before us.","mechanics":["Section 2 adds two definitions to C.5:12A-10. A \"micro bet\" is a proposition bet wagered live while an event is ongoing that concerns the outcome of the next play or action. A \"proposition bet\" is a side wager on part of an event that does not concern its final outcome.","Section 3 adds new subsection n.(1) to C.5:12A-11: a sports wagering licensee shall not offer or accept any wager on a micro bet.","Under subsection n.(2), any licensee or person who violates the ban commits a disorderly persons offense, fined $500 to $1,000 per offense, and each wager offered or accepted is a separate offense.","The existing \"sports pool\" definition still lists in-game wagering, in-play bets and proposition bets, so the carve-out turns entirely on the \"next play or action\" language.","Section 4 makes the act effective immediately, with no transition period or rulemaking directive to the Division of Gaming Enforcement.","Status: reported out of Assembly committee with amendments on June 1, 2026 (title now says \"online\") and referred to Assembly Appropriations."],"issue_tags":["integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"None of the listed bills bans a specific bet type the way A3258 does. The closest analogue is CT HB05229, which bars using artificial intelligence to target certain bets at customers; it regulates how bets are marketed rather than prohibiting the bet itself. IL HB5142 and IL HB5059 (the ORACLE Act) concern bringing prediction markets within sports wagering regulation. The AK, GA and HI bills are about authorizing and taxing sports betting, and the IL HB2878/HB5034 bills about license fees. A3258 is also unusual in enforcing a product restriction through per-wager criminal (disorderly persons) liability rather than administrative discipline by the Division.","watch":"Watch how the committee amendments define the scope (online only versus retail and kiosks), and whether \"next play or action\" is tightened. As drafted, it could sweep in common in-play markets such as next pitch, next drive result, next point or next goal scorer, and the per-wager fines could add up to very large exposure across an operator's high-volume live-betting products, with no phase-in.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":30734,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:00:26.388Z"},"NH-HB312":{"summary":"HB312, the \"New Hampshire Fair Play Act,\" would create RSA 356-D, barring accredited four-year colleges from enforcing rules that stop student-athletes from earning name, image, and likeness (NIL) compensation or hiring agents and lawyers. It also flatly prohibits student-athletes from taking NIL compensation from any person or entity related to or associated with gambling, including casinos, sports betting, and the lottery. A violation costs the athlete their institutional financial aid eligibility. The bill was referred for interim study on January 7, 2026, which in New Hampshire usually means it is effectively shelved for the session.","mechanics":["356-D:1 covers accredited four-year institutions that support intercollegiate athletics, and 356-D:4 applies the chapter to all full-time students eligible for intercollegiate competition.","356-D:2 I bars institutions from blocking NIL earnings or professional representation and protects scholarships from revocation over NIL income. Institutions may not themselves pay prospective student-athletes for NIL.","356-D:2 IV(a) prohibits NIL compensation from anyone related to or associated with the development, production, distribution, wholesaling, or retailing of casinos and gambling, expressly including sports betting, the lottery, and betting on video, online, and mobile games. The same ban covers adult entertainment, alcohol, tobacco and vapes, prescription drugs, controlled substances, and weapons.","356-D:2 IV(b) makes revocation of institutional financial aid eligibility the penalty for taking NIL compensation from a prohibited category. 356-D:3 II(b) bars athletes from signing NIL contracts that conflict with the category ban.","356-D:3 requires athletes to disclose NIL contracts to a designated institutional official. An institution asserting a category conflict must disclose the conflicting provisions to the athlete and the athlete's representative.","Agents must comply with the federal Sports Agent Responsibility and Trust Act (15 U.S.C. 7801 et seq.). The stated effective date of September 1, 2025 has already passed and would need amending if the bill is revived."],"issue_tags":["advertising"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"Unlike Connecticut HB05229, which restricts gaming advertising on college campuses by regulating licensees, NH HB312 places the burden and penalty on the student-athlete. Under HB312 the athlete loses aid eligibility for any NIL tie to a gambling-associated entity, and no licensee obligation is imposed. Maryland HB518 pairs college athlete protections with problem-gambling regulation by the gaming commission, whereas HB312 sits entirely outside gaming law in a consumer-protection chapter. As described, the NIL bills in Hawaii (HB 2384, SB3263), Louisiana (HB 513), Wisconsin (AB1034), Texas (HB126), and Georgia (HB383) focus on affirming NIL rights and institutional policy. None of those descriptions indicates an express statutory gambling-sponsor ban with a financial-aid forfeiture penalty like HB312's.","watch":"If the bill is revived from interim study, watch the breadth of \"related to or associated with\" gambling in 356-D:2 IV(a), which could sweep in operator affiliates, media partners, and lottery vendors and expose any operator NIL or influencer program involving New Hampshire college athletes to athlete aid forfeiture and reputational risk.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":5873,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:00:26.408Z"},"NJ-A4002":{"summary":"A4002 supplements New Jersey's sports wagering law (P.L.2018, c.33) to require sports wagering licensees and their contracted operators to adopt Division of Gaming Enforcement-approved rules governing limitations placed on wagering accounts, and to publish those rules. Operators must give patrons written notice and an explanation whenever an account is limited, periodically review limited accounts under DGE standards, and report annually to DGE on the frequency and kind of limitations imposed.","mechanics":["Section 1a: licensees and contracted operators must adopt rules on account limitations, which may include play-time limits, maximum wager amounts, and deposit and withdrawal restrictions. The rules must be submitted to DGE for approval.","Section 1a: approved rules must be conspicuously displayed in the sports wagering lounge, posted on the operator's website, included in account terms and conditions, and made readily available to patrons.","Section 1b: any limitation triggers written notice to the patron with an explanation. Limited accounts must be reviewed at periodic intervals under DGE-set standards to decide whether the limits stay or are removed.","Section 1c: annual reporting to DGE on the frequency and kind of limitations imposed, and DGE may request additional information to assess compliance.","Section 1d: DGE has rulemaking authority under the Administrative Procedure Act. Section 2 makes the act effective immediately.","The bill sets no specific penalties, so enforcement rests on DGE's existing authority."],"issue_tags":["integrity","licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Most listed bills address authorization, taxation, or licensing, such as AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, HI HB 1308, and IL HB2878. A4002 instead targets the industry practice of limiting, or 'stake-limiting,' sharp or winning bettors. The closest analogue is CT HB05229, which regulates withdrawals from gaming accounts and customer service obligations, but CT does not mandate published limitation policies, per-patron written explanations, or periodic review of limits. Unusually, A4002 applies to contracted operators directly and creates a recurring transparency reporting duty on account limitations, which no other listed bill does.","watch":"Watch how DGE defines the periodic-review standards and the required content of the written 'explanation,' since disclosing risk-management or sharp-bettor criteria could expose proprietary trading practices and invite patron disputes over limits.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":3651,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:00:27.213Z"},"NJ-A4003":{"summary":"A4003 bars New Jersey sports wagering licensees and their contracted operators from offering promotional credits, incentives, bonuses, complimentaries, or similar inducements to wager to any person currently using a responsible gaming program, including self-exclusion, time or wager limits, and deposit restrictions. It applies \"notwithstanding any law, rule, or regulation to the contrary,\" sets a minimum fine of $500 per offense, and directs the Division of Gaming Enforcement, in consultation with the Casino Control Commission, to adopt implementing rules.","mechanics":["Section 1a: covers sports wagering licensees and contracted operators; prohibits offering promotional credits, incentives, bonuses, complimentaries, or similar benefits designed to induce wagering.","Protected class: any person currently utilizing a responsible gaming program, whether operator-offered or required by the Casino Control Act or DGE/CCC regulation. Listed examples are self-exclusion, time or wager limits, and deposit restrictions, and the list is expressly non-exhaustive.","Section 1b: fine of not less than $500 per offense, with no stated maximum and no definition of what counts as an offense (for example, per communication or per patron).","Section 1c: the Division, in consultation with the Commission, must promulgate rules under the Administrative Procedure Act.","Section 2: effective immediately on enactment, with no transition period. The bill supplements P.L.2018, c.33, the sports wagering act."],"issue_tags":["advertising","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Among the listed bills, the closest analogue is CT HB05229. That bill addresses responsible gaming and targeting through limits on campus advertising, withdrawal rules, customer service lines, and a bar on AI-targeted bets, while NJ A4003 is a single-purpose ban on promotional inducements to patrons using responsible gaming tools. Most of the other listed bills, such as AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, and HI HB 1308/HB 2570/SB1569, are authorization, licensing, and tax frameworks rather than promotional-conduct rules, and the IL HB1451/HB2580/HB5368 bills focus on public education about gambling disorders. What is unusual in A4003 is how broadly it defines the protected class: it reaches any patron using any limit tool, including a voluntary deposit or time limit, not just self-excluded persons.","watch":"Operators should watch whether the final text or DGE rules narrow \"utilizing responsible gaming programs\" and define \"per offense,\" because as drafted, any patron who sets a routine deposit limit would have to be suppressed from all bonus and CRM marketing immediately on enactment, with uncapped per-offense fines.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":2594,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:00:35.358Z"},"NJ-AR150":{"summary":"AR150 is a non-binding single-house Assembly resolution urging New Jersey sports wagering licensees, casino licensees, their contracted operators and Internet gaming affiliates to ensure their VIP programs do not target patrons known to be gambling addicts, or who have shown signs of problem gambling, with personalized marketing, including content featuring professional athletes or celebrities. It also urges MLB, the MLBPA and all professional leagues and players associations to refuse participation in such targeted VIP marketing. It creates no legal obligation, penalty or rulemaking mandate.","mechanics":["Section 1: urges (does not require) sports wagering licensees, casino licensees, contracted operators and Internet gaming affiliates to keep VIP programs from targeting known gambling addicts or patrons exhibiting signs of problem gambling with personalized marketing intended to induce continued gambling.","Section 1 specifically flags marketing that uses professional athletes or celebrities, and urges MLB, the MLBPA and all leagues and players associations to reject participation when directed at vulnerable patrons.","Section 2: the Clerk of the General Assembly must transmit copies to all NJ sports wagering and casino licensees, contracted operators and Internet gaming affiliates, the MLB Commissioner, the MLBPA Executive Director and NJ's congressional delegation.","Whereas clauses name FanDuel and describe a 2024 personalized athlete video sent via a VIP manager to a customer reported to have lost over $1.5 million.","No definitions of 'signs of problem gambling', no enforcement mechanism, no DGE directive and no effective date beyond adoption; status shows filed with the Secretary of State on 2026-09-24."],"issue_tags":["advertising","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike CT HB05229, which would impose binding restrictions such as limits on campus gaming advertising and a bar on using artificial intelligence to target certain bets to customers, AR150 is purely hortatory and carries no statutory force. It also differs from MD HB518 and MD HB 195, which direct the Maryland commission to adopt problem-gambling regulations or study gambling-prevention technology, and from study requests like LA SR 178, because AR150 targets VIP marketing practices and athlete endorsements specifically and names an operator. Its appeal to professional leagues and players associations is unusual among the listed bills.","watch":"Although non-binding, the resolution signals legislative and possibly Division of Gaming Enforcement appetite for binding rules on VIP host programs, personalized athlete or celebrity content, and duties to identify problem-gambling markers, so operators should audit VIP targeting and responsible-gaming flag integration now and track any follow-on bill or regulation.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":8614,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:00:41.221Z"},"NJ-A5327":{"summary":"A5327 is New Jersey's FY2027 general appropriations act (P.L.2026, c.27), signed with line-item vetoes on June 30, 2026, appropriating about $60.7 billion in State funds and $30.5 billion in federal funds. It does not change gaming law, tax rates, or licensing. Its relevance to gaming is limited to the anticipated-revenue schedules, which book projected receipts from sports betting, internet gaming, casino taxes, casino license fees, and fantasy sports fees.","mechanics":["The Casino Revenue Fund is projected at $1,004,359,000. Internet Gaming accounts for $670,249,000, Gross Revenue Tax for $181,465,000, Sports Betting for $126,986,000, and Other Casino Taxes and Fees for $13,641,000. Investment Earnings ($11,942,000) and the Casino Simulcasting Fund ($76,000) make up the rest.","A separate Treasury 'Sports Betting' line of $102,886,000 flows to the General Fund. Combined anticipated sports-betting receipts across both funds are therefore about $229.9 million.","Casino Control Fund license fees are anticipated at $81,325,000. These fees fund the regulatory apparatus.","The Department of Law and Public Safety's revenue lines include a Fantasy Sports Operations Fee of $3,231,000, Casino Fines of $1,103,000, and Legalized Games of Chance Control of $469,000.","Section 1 makes appropriations available for FY ending June 30, 2027, plus a one-month tail, after which unexpended balances lapse. The excerpt is truncated before the Law and Public Safety and Treasury spending sections, so any Division of Gaming Enforcement or Casino Revenue Fund language provisions are not visible here."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. The bill is unusual only in that it shows how heavily New Jersey's casino-dedicated revenue relies on iGaming: projected internet gaming receipts ($670.2M) are more than three and a half times the land-based gross revenue tax ($181.5M). Sports betting revenue is split between the Casino Revenue Fund and the General Fund rather than fully dedicated.","watch":"Watch whether the sports-betting and iGaming revenue projections exceed what current rates would yield, which would signal pressure for companion tax or fee legislation, and review the untruncated Division of Gaming Enforcement and Casino Revenue Fund language provisions for any budget-language changes to fee or receipt handling.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":978584,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:00:41.721Z"},"NJ-S2027":{"summary":"S2027 is New Jersey's fiscal year 2027 general appropriations act. It appropriates $60.74 billion in State funds and $30.50 billion in federal funds, and lists anticipated resources that include casino, internet gaming, sports betting and fantasy sports revenue. It was substituted by A5327 on June 30, 2026, and in the text provided it does not change gaming tax rates, licensing rules or wagering law.","mechanics":["Casino Revenue Fund anticipated resources total $1,004,359,000. That figure includes Internet Gaming at $670,249,000, Gross Revenue Tax at $181,465,000, Sports Betting at $126,986,000, Other Casino Taxes and Fees at $13,641,000, Investment Earnings at $11,942,000 and the Casino Simulcasting Fund at $76,000.","A separate Sports Betting line of $102,886,000 is booked to the General Fund under Department of the Treasury miscellaneous revenues. This is in addition to the Casino Revenue Fund sports betting figure.","Casino Control Fund license fees are anticipated at $81,325,000.","Three gaming items appear under Department of Law and Public Safety revenues: Fantasy Sports Operations Fee at $3,231,000, Casino Fines at $1,103,000 and Legalized Games of Chance Control at $469,000.","Section 1 makes appropriations available for FY ending June 30, 2027, plus one month. Unexpended balances lapse unless encumbered or pre-encumbered as of June 30, 2027.","The text supplied is truncated after the Department of Agriculture. Any Division of Gaming Enforcement, Casino Control Commission or Casino Revenue Fund spending language is not visible."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. What stands out is how much New Jersey's gaming revenue now leans on iGaming. Projected internet gaming receipts of $670.2 million are roughly 3.7 times the land-based casino gross revenue tax of $181.5 million. Sports betting revenue is also split between the Casino Revenue Fund and the General Fund, for a combined total of about $229.9 million.","watch":"Operators' counsel should check the full A5327 text and any companion revenue legislation for language provisions (for example, redirecting Casino Revenue Fund or sports betting receipts, or new DGE funding conditions), since the revenue projections may assume rate or fee changes enacted elsewhere.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":978331,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:00:49.072Z"},"NJ-S2356":{"summary":"S2356 directs the Division of Gaming Enforcement to commission a study, due within 12 months of enactment and published online, on which responsible-gambling words, phrases, placements and fonts work best in casino and sports pool advertising. The DGE must then adopt between three and ten key phrases by regulation, at least one of which must appear in ads alongside the existing 1-800-GAMBLER message. It also amends N.J.S.A. 5:12-70 to require regulations keeping gaming ads and promotions, to the extent practicable, away from locations such as schools and college campuses and off media predominantly targeting underage or self-excluded persons.","mechanics":["Section 1: DGE must arrange a study evaluating six factors, including resonance of phrases, effect on responsible betting, placement, font size and emphasis (bold, underline), effectiveness for problem gamblers, self-excluded and underage persons, and message fatigue from repetition. It must be completed within 12 months and published on the DGE website.","Section 1(d): after the study, DGE develops 3 to 10 key words or phrases, or takes other action informed by the study. It may revise them periodically and must adopt APA regulations requiring at least one phrase in casino and sports pool ads at a frequency that optimizes effectiveness.","Section 2 amends C.5:12-70(a)(16)(a). It deletes the mandatory 'Bet with your head, not over it' on-site casino signage language, keeps 1-800-GAMBLER, and adds a DGE-selected phrase to all print, billboard and sign ads, with clear communication in radio, TV, Internet and other media. Coverage expressly extends to contracted operators, including online gaming and online sports wagering operators.","Section 2 also adds new C.5:12-70(a)(16)(b): regulations must ensure ads and promotions are kept, to the extent practicable, out of or away from schools, college campuses, and media predominantly targeting underage or self-excluded persons.","Section 3 amends C.5:12A-13(a)(11) to require the DGE phrase, in addition to 1-800-GAMBLER, in all print, billboard, sign, online and broadcast sports pool and online sports pool ads and in every sports wagering lounge.","Effective immediately (Section 4). Status: introduced, referred to Senate Budget and Appropriations on 2026-05-21."],"issue_tags":["advertising"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"CT HB05229 also restricts gaming advertising at college and university campuses, but it pairs that with withdrawal, customer-service and AI-targeting rules; NJ S2356 is narrower and defers most of its substance to DGE rulemaking after a study. MI SB0714 and NY A 382 regulate operator advertising directly, and NY S 10092 targets platforms that advertise gambling to minors, whereas S2356's distinctive feature is an evidence-based, rotating mandatory-message regime. PA HR60 and VA SB819 are study measures too, but S2356 converts its study directly into binding content requirements on every ad.","watch":"Watch the DGE rulemaking under Section 1(d) and new C.5:12-70(a)(16)(b). The 'frequency that optimizes effectiveness' standard, the open-ended 'other action' authority, the undefined 'in or near' schools and campuses buffer, and the 'predominantly target' media test could impose costly creative-rotation and media-buy restrictions on online operators.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":18093,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:00:57.464Z"},"NJ-A5336":{"summary":"Despite its surtax title, A5336 as introduced is a comprehensive prediction-market regulation bill. It bans death, catastrophic-event and political markets, and imposes settlement-source disclosure and anti-manipulation duties on all platforms. It lets sports and horse-racing event contracts (\"athletic event markets\") operate only through a New Jersey sports wagering licensee or a newly licensed athletic event market operator partnered with one, taxed at the 19.75% online sports rate plus a 10% surcharge, and adds a 10% surcharge on all other prediction-market revenue from New Jersey residents. The bill was reported as a committee substitute on 2026-06-28, and the text analysed here is the introduced version, so the substitute may differ.","mechanics":["Tax (secs. 8 and 14): a 10% surcharge applies to sums an operator receives from non-athletic speculative positions opened by New Jersey residents, paid quarterly to the Division of Taxation under the State Uniform Tax Procedure Law. Athletic event market receipts bear the 19.75% tax under C.5:12A-16 plus a further 10% surcharge, administered by the Division of Gaming Enforcement (DGE). \"Sums received\" is not defined.","Licensing (secs. 9-11): athletic event markets may be offered only by a sports wagering licensee, or by an athletic event market operator licensed by DGE that has a DGE-approved agreement with such a licensee. The initial operator license costs $5 million with annual renewal fees set by DGE, and qualifiers must meet casino key employee standards and section 86 disqualification criteria. Each licensee is capped at three branded sites or apps, and servers must sit at the racetrack or in Atlantic City.","Prohibitions and enforcement (secs. 3-7): death, catastrophic-event and political markets are banned. All platforms must publish settlement sources, may not settle on proprietary information, and must report suspected manipulation or insider trading to the Attorney General. The Attorney General may seek an injunction for a \"persistent course of conduct\" in violation, and continued operation after an order carries a $1 million per day civil penalty. Section 3 disclaims any conflict with the Commodity Exchange Act.","Consumer protection (secs. 12-13): DGE must write rules mirroring online sports pool rules, covering age verification, position limits, promotional credits, deposit and time limits, self-exclusion and 1-800-GAMBLER messaging in advertisements. Excluded participants include anyone under 21, self-excluded persons, operator and settlement-source personnel, and anyone with insider information. Offering athletic event markets without DGE approval is a fourth-degree crime, with fines up to $25,000 for individuals and $100,000 for entities.","Integrity and ethics (secs. 17-20): State and local officials and their immediate families may not trade political markets or markets tied to their official duties, a fourth-degree crime for the official and a disorderly persons offense for family members. Candidates and campaign staff may not trade political markets during a campaign. A five-year revolving-door bar applies to work for athletic event market licensees or applicants.","Effective date: the act takes effect 30 days after enactment, with anticipatory administrative action authorized. DGE may adopt emergency regulations effective for 180 days under sec. 15."],"issue_tags":["prediction","tax","licensing","integrity","advertising"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike the outright bans in MN HF 4437/SF 4511, NC H1171 and VT H0913, NJ A5336 takes a permit-and-tax approach. It channels sports event contracts into the existing sports wagering licensing system and adds revenue surcharges no other listed bill imposes. IL HB5142 reaches a similar result by folding sports prediction markets into the statutory definition of sports wagering, whereas NJ creates a parallel \"athletic event market\" category with its own $5 million license. The bill overlaps with the age-21 and consumer rules in IL HB5059 and NY S09414 (the ORACLE Acts) and CT HB05038, and its official-trading restrictions parallel NY S09825 and CA AB1840, but it pairs those with the heaviest tax load in the group.","watch":"Operators should watch whether the committee substitute keeps the stacked 29.75% rate and 10% surcharge on athletic event markets offered by existing sportsbook licensees, how undefined \"sums received\" is interpreted against gross revenue, and whether new athletic event market skins count against the three-skin cap or sit alongside existing online sports pool skins.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":44161,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:00:57.498Z"},"NJ-S2160":{"summary":"S2160 would bar New Jersey sports wagering licensees from offering or accepting micro bets, defined as live proposition bets on the outcome of the next play or action in an ongoing sports event. It amends the definitions section of the Sports Wagering Act (C.5:12A-10) and adds a new subsection n to C.5:12A-11. Violations would be a disorderly persons offense, with each wager offered or accepted counted as a separate offense.","mechanics":["New definition (C.5:12A-10): \"micro bet\" means a proposition bet wagered live, while a sport or athletic event is ongoing, that concerns the outcome of the next play or action in the event (the findings give a next-pitch strike bet as an example).","New definition of \"proposition bet\": a side wager on a part of a sport or athletic event that does not concern its final outcome. The existing \"sports pool\" definition still lists in-game wagering, in-play bets and proposition bets as permitted.","New C.5:12A-11(n)(1): a sports wagering licensee shall not offer or accept any wager on a micro bet. The ban applies to retail lounges, kiosks and online sports pools alike, with no carve-out by channel.","Penalty, C.5:12A-11(n)(2): any licensee or person who violates the ban commits a disorderly persons offense, fined $500 to $1,000 per offense, with each wager offered or accepted a separate offense.","Section 4: effective immediately. There is no phase-in period and no direction to the Division of Gaming Enforcement to adopt implementing rules.","Status: introduced and referred to the Senate Budget and Appropriations Committee on 2026-03-23. Sponsors are Senators Moriarty and Diegnan."],"issue_tags":["prop","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"None of the listed bills bans a specific in-play bet type. CT HB05229 comes closest on consumer protection, since it bars using artificial intelligence to target certain bets to customers, but it regulates targeting rather than prohibiting a product. The Illinois bills IL HB5142 and IL HB5059 address prediction markets, and the Alaska, Georgia and Hawaii bills (AK HB145, GA HB 686, HI HB 1308) authorize and tax sports wagering, so S2160 stands out as a product-level prohibition in a mature market. It relies on both problem-gambling and match-fixing rationales.","watch":"Watch the breadth of \"next play or action,\" which could reach next-drive, next-at-bat or next-point markets, together with the uncapped per-wager penalty exposure, the immediate effective date with no wind-down, and the penalty clause reaching \"any licensee or person,\" which could expose Internet sports pool operators and platform suppliers, not just licensees.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":29790,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:01:00.723Z"},"NJ-S3419":{"summary":"S3419 supplements New Jersey's sports wagering law (P.L.2018, c.33) to require each sports wagering licensee and its contracted operators to adopt Division-approved rules on when and how wagering accounts may be limited, and to publish those rules. Operators must give patrons written notice and an explanation whenever an account is limited, periodically review limited accounts under Division standards, and report annually on the frequency and type of limitations imposed.","mechanics":["Section 1a: licensees and contracted operators must adopt rules on account limitations. Examples given are limits on play times, maximum wager amounts, and deposit and withdrawal restrictions. The rules must be submitted to the Division of Gaming Enforcement for approval.","Section 1a: once approved, the rules must be displayed conspicuously in the sports wagering lounge, posted on the operator's website, included in the account terms and conditions, and made readily available to patrons.","Section 1b: any limitation triggers a duty to give the patron written notice and an explanation. Limited accounts must be reviewed at periodic intervals, under Division-set standards, to decide whether the limits stay or are lifted.","Section 1c: annual reporting to the Division on the frequency and kind of limitations imposed. The Division may request additional information to assess compliance.","Section 1d: the Division may adopt implementing regulations under the Administrative Procedure Act. Section 2 makes the act effective immediately.","The First Reprint, reported by the Senate State Government, Wagering, Tourism & Historic Preservation Committee on May 21, 2026, only corrected a typo ('pstron' to 'patron'). The bill is now referred to Senate Budget and Appropriations."],"issue_tags":["integrity","licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"None of the listed bills directly regulates operator-imposed account limiting, the practice known as 'limiting sharps.' The closest is CT HB05229, which addresses withdrawal of funds from gaming accounts, customer service phone lines, and AI-targeted bets, but it does not reach bet-size restrictions or require notice of limits. Most of the other bills, such as AK HB145 and SB194, GA HB 686, HI HB 1308 and SB1569, and IL HB2878 and HB5034, concern authorization, licensing fees, or tax rather than operator conduct toward individual patrons. That makes S3419 an unusual patron-transparency measure aimed at a commercial risk-management practice.","watch":"Watch the Division standards for periodic review and how specific the required 'explanation' must be, because they could force disclosure of proprietary risk-management and trader-profiling criteria, make limiting decisions easier for patrons to dispute, and turn the annual reports into a public record of how often operators limit winning bettors.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":2518,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:01:01.598Z"},"NJ-S4447":{"summary":"The committee substitute imposes a 9 percent surtax on prediction market operators, layered on top of New Jersey's existing taxes. Corporations pay it on allocated taxable net income under the Corporation Business Tax. Individuals and pass-through owners pay it on gross income derived from operating a prediction market under the Gross Income Tax. Nearly all credits are barred against the surtax, and the corporate piece applies retroactively to privilege periods beginning on or after July 1, 2025.","mechanics":["Section 1 (CBT): a 9% surtax on allocated taxable net income, as defined in C.54:10A-4(w), for privilege periods beginning on or after July 1, 2025. It is administered under the CBT, and the only credits allowed are installment payments, extension payments and prior overpayments.","Section 2 (GIT): a 9% surtax on gross income, not net, derived from operating a prediction market operator, for taxable years beginning on or after January 1, 2026. For S corporations and partnerships it applies to each shareholder's pro rata share or partner's distributive share.","\"Prediction market operator\" is defined broadly. It covers a board of trade or other person and its affiliates, and expressly reaches anyone who owns, operates or controls a digital distribution service, platform, online portal or application store where a prediction market may be accessed.","\"Future events\" covers elections, pop-culture events, athletic events or games of skill (including portions and individual player statistics), card, dice and device games, and legal proceedings. \"Prediction market\" covers bid-ask systems \"and any other form,\" regardless of mechanism.","Section 3: the act takes effect immediately, with the retroactive CBT start date described above. The Senate Budget and Appropriations Committee reported the substitute on June 28, 2026."],"issue_tags":["tax","prediction"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"The other listed bills regulate, restrict or ban prediction markets rather than tax them. Examples include the age-21 and consumer-protection rules in IL HB5059, NY S09414 (the ORACLE Acts) and CT HB05038; the outright prohibitions in MN HF 4437/SF 4511, NC H1171 and VT H0913; IL HB5142, which folds sports prediction markets into the Sports Wagering Act; and the ethics and insider-use limits in CA AB1840 and NY S09825. New Jersey's S4447 is the only bill listed that uses the tax code to target prediction-market income. Its premise is to treat these platforms as taxable businesses operating in the state, not to license or prohibit them.","watch":"Watch three features: the operator definition sweeps in affiliates and app stores, which could pull in sportsbook parents with prediction-market affiliates and distribution platforms; the GIT surtax falls on gross rather than net income; and the CBT surtax reaches back to July 1, 2025.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Comm Sub","text_chars":6976,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:01:08.062Z"},"NY-A10003":{"summary":"A10003-D is New York's FY2026-27 Aid to Localities budget bill, which appropriates and reappropriates state, federal and special revenue funds to local governments and grantees from April 1, 2026. The text provided covers only the general provisions in Section 1 and the Office for the Aging schedule, and it contains no sports-betting or gaming provisions. The Assembly bill was substituted by its Senate twin, S9003-D, on 2026-05-27.","mechanics":["Section 1(e) lets the budget director withhold all or part of any appropriation in the bill if a cumulative general fund imbalance of $2,000,000,000 or more is projected for FY2026-27. The director must first deplete the $2B transaction risk reserve. The Legislature has 10 business days to adopt its own withhold plan by concurrent resolution.","Withholds may not be applied to public assistance and SSI-related payments, reductions that would violate federal law, debt service, or court-ordered payments.","Section 1(h) repeals appropriations and reappropriations in chapter 53 of the laws of 2025 that would otherwise lapse on March 31, 2027.","The Office for the Aging receives $430,736,841 in new appropriations and $671,058,513 in reappropriations.","Office for the Aging rate-setting is capped at a 2.7 percent targeted inflationary increase for April 1, 2026 through March 31, 2027.","No expenditure may be made until the director of the budget issues a certificate of approval (Section 1(d))."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No other states' bills are listed for comparison. The bill is a general appropriations vehicle rather than gaming legislation. In the excerpt provided, nothing addresses wagering, licensing, tax rates or advertising. Any gaming-related lines, such as local aid from gaming revenue or problem-gambling program funding, would sit in agency schedules not included in the text supplied, and their presence or amounts cannot be confirmed from this excerpt.","watch":"Check the full enacted S9003-D text for any appropriations tied to gaming revenue (for example host-community aid or problem-gambling services funded by mobile sports wagering) and whether they could be reached by the Section 1(e) withhold authority if a $2B general fund imbalance arises.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"A10003","text_chars":4708148,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:01:14.052Z"},"NY-A10008":{"summary":"A10008-C (same as S9008-C), signed as Chapter 58 of 2026, is New York's FY2026-27 transportation, economic development and environmental conservation (TED) Article VII budget bill, made up of Parts A through JJJ covering motor vehicle fees, speed enforcement, utility ratemaking, SEQRA reform, insurance, public pensions, bonding authority and other matters. Neither the summary nor the excerpted text authorizes, taxes or regulates sports wagering, casino gaming, lottery or prediction markets. The only provision of possible tangential interest to online operators is Part Y, the 'Safe by Design Act,' which lets the Attorney General adopt rules on age-assurance methods for 'covered platforms.'","mechanics":["Part D adds VTL §1642-b, letting New York City require intelligent speed assistance devices on vehicles whose owners are found liable on 16 school-zone camera speeding notices within 12 months. Installation runs 12 months for a first order, 24 months for a second within 10 years, 36 months for a third within 15 years, and indefinitely after that.","Part D also makes owners pay device costs, with a presumptive waiver at or below 200% of the federal poverty level. It bars disclosure of device data and requires the data to be destroyed when the installation term ends.","Parts A and B extend DMV transaction-fee provisions and the accident prevention course internet pilot from April 1, 2026 to April 1, 2028, retroactive to April 1, 2026.","Part Y amends the General Business Law to enact the 'Safe by Design Act.' The AG may promulgate rules on reasonable and technically feasible age assurance, considering platform size, resources, technical capability, cost and effectiveness of age-determination techniques, audience and industry practice. The definition of 'covered platform' is not in the excerpted text.","Part GGG covers administration of budget funds and accounts plus bonding authorizations. The excerpt does not show any gaming-revenue fund among them."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"Unlike the gaming-specific bills listed, this bill does not legalize, license or tax wagering. Examples include the mobile sports wagering bills (AK HB145 and SB194, GA HB 686, HI HB 2570), the tax-rate changes in IL HB3159 and IA SSB 1157, and the operator-conduct rules in CT HB05229. The closest thematic overlap is CT HB05038, which bars prediction-market platforms from serving or advertising to people under 21. New York's Part Y instead sets up general AG rulemaking on age assurance for 'covered platforms,' and the excerpt does not show it targeting gambling. What stands out is that New York's age-assurance framework was enacted inside a transportation and economic development budget vehicle rather than standalone consumer-protection or gaming legislation.","watch":"Confirm from the full Part Y text whether the 'covered platform' definition could reach online sportsbook, DFS or prediction-market apps, and track the Attorney General's age-assurance rulemaking for compliance obligations layered on top of existing NY Gaming Commission age-verification rules.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"A10008","text_chars":703865,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:01:16.115Z"},"NY-A10329":{"summary":"A10329 amends Racing, Pari-Mutuel Wagering and Breeding Law §1367-a(4)(a) by adding subparagraph (xvi), which requires every mobile sports wagering operator to give each authorized sports bettor a monthly account activity statement. The statement must be pushed electronically, kept continuously accessible in the bettor's account, and list deposits, amounts wagered, winnings and losses, net result, number of wagers, time logged in, promotional credits used, responsible gaming resources, and access to lifetime wagering history. The Gaming Commission must set standardized formatting and clarity rules by regulation.","mechanics":["New §1367-a(4)(a)(xvi): the statement must be delivered by push notification and stay continuously accessible through the bettor's account.","Minimum contents (A)-(I): total deposits, total wagered, total winnings and losses, net gain or loss, number of wagers, total time logged into the platform, an itemized list of promotional credits, bonuses and free wagers used, a prominent disclosure of the self-exclusion program and problem gambling services, and access to lifetime wagering history.","Deadline: no later than 15 days after the end of each calendar month.","The Gaming Commission must issue regulations on standardized formatting, clarity requirements, and any additional disclosures.","Effective the January 1 after enactment. Same as S09415. Sponsor Kassay, cosponsors Woerner and Griffin."],"issue_tags":["integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"CT HB05229 also adds consumer-facing account obligations for licensees (withdrawal rules, customer service phone lines, limits on AI-targeted bets), but A10329 is narrower and focuses only on periodic disclosure of account activity. The authorization bills listed (AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, HI HB 1308/HB 2570/SB1569) and the tax and licensing bills in Illinois (HB2878, HB3159, HB5034) deal with market structure, not post-launch consumer reporting. Requiring operators to disclose session time and lifetime history in a mandatory monthly push statement is unusual among the bills listed and goes beyond typical responsible-gaming page requirements.","watch":"Watch the Commission's implementing regulations, because the session-time tracking, the push-notification delivery mandate, the 15-day deadline, and the lifetime history access requirement may impose meaningful engineering and data-retention costs, and the regulations could add further disclosures before the January 1 effective date.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"A10329","text_chars":5075,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:01:17.568Z"},"NY-A10005":{"summary":"This is New York's FY2026-27 public protection and general government budget bill (S.9005-C/A.10005-C), an omnibus covering Parts A through OO. It extends the National Guard tuition program and the 911 surcharge suspension, creates new 3D-printed gun and convertible pistol crimes, requires blocking technology on 3D printers, and creates a drone misuse offense. Later Parts cover insurance fraud, liquor licensing, ethics and lobbying training and fees, procurement thresholds, auto insurance rating, and immigration enforcement limits. Nothing in the text supplied (which is truncated partway through Part F) addresses sports betting, gaming, or wagering, and none of the Part titles suggest that it does.","mechanics":["Part C, Subpart A: from May 31, 2027, a dealer or gunsmith who sells or transfers a 'convertible pistol' commits a class D felony (Penal Law 265.10(10)). Distributing digital firearm manufacturing code to anyone who does not hold both a state gunsmith license and a federal firearms license is a class A misdemeanor (265.10(11)).","Part C, Subpart B: GBL 396-eeee bars selling 3D printers in New York unless they have blocking technology. The ban starts one year after DCJS rules, which follow a working-group feasibility review. Gun industry members face a $5,000 civil penalty per product, and a private right of action is created.","Part D: new Penal Law article 280 makes 'unlawful use of a drone' a class A misdemeanor. It covers operating a drone in a 'nefarious manner' or within 500 feet of 'prohibited space' without approval. Prohibited space expressly includes large public gatherings of 500 or more people, such as sporting events.","Part D: Executive Law 236 lets police and peace officers use drone mitigation, kinetic only on probable cause. It also creates a 'New York state blue list' of approved vendors for public purchases of drones and counter-drone technology. Part D takes effect 90 days after enactment.","Parts U and Z, described in the bill title but not in the supplied text: more frequent ethics and lobbying training, fees for late completion, and changes to lobbyist and client registration fees.","Part B takes effect April 1, 2026 (retroactive if enacted later). Part A extends the National Guard program to September 1, 2031."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills is comparable, because this bill contains no gaming provisions. Examples include AK HB145/SB194 and GA HB 686 (authorizing sports wagering), IL HB3159 (sports wagering tax), CT HB05038 (prediction markets) and CT HB05229 (gaming advertising). Unlike those, A10005 is a general government budget vehicle. It touches the industry only indirectly, through lobbying registration and training rules and drone restrictions around large sporting events.","watch":"Operators' government-affairs teams should check the Part Z lobbyist and client registration fee changes and the Part U training-frequency rules and late fees. Operators with stadium or event partnerships should note that Part D's 500-foot drone 'prohibited space' covers sporting events of 500 or more people.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":275069,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:01:19.147Z"},"NY-A10538":{"summary":"A10538 creates a temporary, independent four-member proposition betting task force to study prop bets in New York, focusing in particular on \"under bets\" (wagers that an athlete will fall below a stated statistical threshold). The task force must report findings and legislative or administrative recommendations by December 31, 2026, and the act sunsets 90 days after the report is submitted. It does not itself restrict or prohibit any wager type.","mechanics":["Section 1(2) defines \"proposition bet\" as a wager on the occurrence or nonoccurrence of an act, statistic or event during a sports event that does not depend on the final outcome, and \"under bet\" as an individual-athlete wager won if the athlete achieves less than a stated measure such as points, rebounds, yards, strikeouts or minutes played.","Section 1(3) sets four unpaid members: two appointed by the governor, one by the temporary president of the senate and one by the speaker. All appointments are due by July 1, 2026.","Section 1(4) lists the study topics: types, prevalence, wagering volume and revenue of under bets; integrity risks; economic impact on operators and the market; harassment of and threats to athletes; consumer protection and problem gambling; integrity monitoring and the extent of reporting; conflicts of interest; and potential solutions.","Section 1(5)(b) requires the State Gaming Commission and mobile sports wagering licensees to cooperate and to provide aggregated, anonymized data to the extent permitted by law. The task force must consult operators, regulators, researchers, responsible-gaming advocates, leagues and collegiate associations, and may hold public hearings.","Section 1(6) requires the report by December 31, 2026, delivered to the governor and legislative leaders and posted on the Gaming Commission website. Section 2 makes the act effective immediately and repeals it 90 days after the report is submitted."],"issue_tags":["prop","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike LA SB354, which removes sports-related prop bets from permitted wagers and bans prop and micro-bets outright effective 8/1/26, and NC H828, which prohibits prop bets on college and amateur sports, A10538 is a study-only measure. It singles out athlete \"under\" bets as a distinct category, a focus none of the listed bills shares. It is closer in form to MD HB 195's study and workgroup approach, though narrower in subject. CT HB05229 and MD HB518 legislate operational and problem-gambling rules directly rather than commissioning a report.","watch":"Operators should prepare for the mandatory data-sharing duty under Section 1(5)(b) and engage early as consulted stakeholders, because the December 31, 2026 report is likely to be the vehicle for recommending restrictions or bans on player under props, and potentially on props generally.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"A10538","text_chars":7589,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:01:26.515Z"},"NY-A10513":{"summary":"A10513 amends Economic Development Law §353(5) to add mobile sports wagering operators, as defined in Racing, Pari-Mutuel Wagering and Breeding Law §1367, to the list of entities ineligible for the Excelsior Jobs Program tax credit. It also extends ineligibility to any parent company or subsidiary of every excluded entity. It takes effect immediately and is the same as S10259.","mechanics":["Restructures §353(5) into a lettered list of ineligible entities: (a) not-for-profits; (b) primarily service or utility providers; (c) predominantly retail or entertainment businesses, excluding defined entertainment companies and music production; (d) electricity, natural gas and steam generators or distributors; and new (e) mobile sports wagering operators.","Ties the definition of 'mobile sports wagering operator' to RPWBL §1367, so it reaches the state-licensed mobile platform providers.","New lead-in language makes 'any parent company or subsidiary thereof' ineligible for the credit 'or any component thereof'. Because the language sits in the lead-in, it applies to all five categories, not only sports wagering operators.","Effective immediately on enactment, with no grandfathering or transition provision for entities already admitted to the program.","Sponsored by Steck with cosponsors K. Brown, Hyndman, Kassay and McDonald. Introduced March 6, 2026 in Economic Development and referred to Ways and Means on April 21, 2026."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"None of the listed bills touch economic-development incentive eligibility. AK HB145/SB194 and IL HB3159 (an added 2% of adjusted gross receipts for fire and EMS) work through new or higher wagering taxes, and IL HB5143 would end Illinois's per-wager tax. A10513 instead raises operators' effective tax burden indirectly, by cutting off access to a state jobs credit. The parent and subsidiary exclusion is unusual because it reaches corporate affiliates outside the wagering business itself.","watch":"Watch the breadth of the parent-and-subsidiary exclusion, which could disqualify affiliated media, technology, casino or hospitality entities in a corporate group from Excelsior credits, and the immediate effective date with no grandfathering, which leaves it unclear whether already-certified participants lose remaining credit components.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"A10513","text_chars":3015,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:01:28.924Z"},"NY-A3003":{"summary":"A03003-D is New York's FY2025-26 Aid to Localities appropriations bill, submitted by the Governor under Article VII, which appropriates and reappropriates state, federal and other funds to localities and grantees for the fiscal year beginning April 1, 2025. The text provided covers only the general provisions in Section 1 and the Office for the Aging schedule, and contains no sports betting, gaming or wagering provisions. On May 8, 2025 it was substituted by its Senate same-as, S3003-D.","mechanics":["Section 1(d): no appropriated money is payable until the Director of the Budget issues a certificate of approval and files it with the Comptroller and the fiscal committee chairs.","Section 1(e): the Budget Director may withhold appropriations if a cumulative general fund imbalance of $2,000,000,000 or more arises in FY2025-26. The Director must first deplete the $2B transaction risk reserve and notify legislative leaders within 10 business days. The Legislature then has 10 business days to adopt its own withhold plan by concurrent resolution.","Section 1(e) also exempts certain payments from withholding: public assistance and SSI-related payments, reductions that would violate federal law, debt service, and court-ordered payments.","Section 1(g): appropriations and reappropriations in chapter 53 of the laws of 2024 that would otherwise lapse on March 31, 2026 are repealed on enactment.","The Office for the Aging receives $417,304,100 in all-funds appropriations ($244,080,100 General Fund) and $636,450,011 in reappropriations. A recurring rider limits rate adjustments to a 2.6 percent targeted inflationary increase."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No other states' bills are listed for comparison. The bill is a general Aid to Localities appropriations measure, not gaming legislation. Its main notable feature is the broad mid-year withholding authority given to the Budget Director, triggered by a $2B general fund imbalance.","watch":"The excerpt supplied stops in the Office for the Aging schedule, so review the full enacted S3003-D for any Gaming Commission local-aid, problem-gambling or sports-wagering revenue lines, and for whether the $2B-imbalance withholding authority in Section 1(e) could reach them.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"A3003","text_chars":4477757,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:01:30.269Z"},"NY-A3005":{"summary":"A03005-C (chapter 55 of 2025) is New York's Public Protection and General Government Article VII budget bill for FY 2025-26. It extends dozens of expiring criminal justice provisions to September 1, 2027 (Part A), changes police and correction officer eligibility and age rules (Parts C and ZZ), and makes a wide range of changes on victim services, public campaign finance, pensions, courts, prison closures and AI-generated child sexual abuse material. Nothing in the text provided, or in the Part-by-Part summary, authorizes, taxes or regulates sports wagering or other gaming.","mechanics":["Part A extends sunsets from September 1, 2025 to September 1, 2027 for items including prisoner furloughs, work release, the earned eligibility reporting under chapter 261 of 1987, the VTL §1809 mandatory surcharge and the ignition interlock program. NYC pre-criminal proceeding settlements are extended to March 31, 2027.","Part C waives state residency under Public Officers Law §3(9-a) for correction officers and trainees at state facilities. It raises the State Police mandatory retirement age from 60 to 63 (RSSL §381-b(e)). It also raises the maximum entry age for State Police (Executive Law §215(3)) and municipal police (Civil Service Law §58) to 43.","Part ZZ lowers the minimum hiring age for correction officers to 18. Part BBB authorizes DOCCS to close up to three correctional facilities in FY 2025-26.","Part J requires certain employers to adopt a model gender-based violence and the workplace policy, amending the State Finance Law and the Executive Law. Part L criminalizes AI-generated child sexual abuse material under the Penal Law.","Part RR addresses civil enforcement actions brought by the Attorney General. Part OO revises public campaign financing thresholds and procedures. Part Q extends alcoholic beverage liquidator's and temporary retail permits.","Each Part has its own effective date in its last section. Part A takes effect immediately (§24)."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"Every other listed bill is a gaming measure, so none is directly comparable. CT HB05038 restricts prediction market access and advertising for under-21s, IL HB3159 adds a 2% sports wagering tax, GA HB 686 would legalize sports betting, and AK HB145 and SB194 would authorize mobile wagering. NY A03005 has no wagering, gaming tax, advertising or licensing provisions at all. New York's gaming-related budget changes, where enacted, sit in other Article VII bills or the Racing, Pari-Mutuel Wagering and Breeding Law, not this one.","watch":"There is little here for sports-betting operators, but counsel should review the full text of Part RR, since any expansion of the Attorney General's civil enforcement authority could reach licensed operators. They should also check Part J's model workplace policy requirement in case it applies to operators as state contractors or New York employers.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":336806,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:01:38.528Z"},"NY-A382":{"summary":"A382-A amends Racing, Pari-Mutuel Wagering and Breeding Law § 1363 so that the existing advertising rules for casino gaming facility licensees and applicants also apply expressly to mobile sports wagering licensees as defined in § 1367. It also adds a requirement that, in video ads, the problem gambling hotline number stay on screen for the entire ad.","mechanics":["§ 1363(1)(a) and new (1)(c): 'advertisement' now covers any notice, communication, or promotional activity about the gaming-related business of a mobile sports wagering licensee (per § 1367), including electronic dissemination.","§ 1363(2): mobile sports wagering licensee ads must be factual and not false, deceptive, or misleading. They may not obscure material facts, omit material conditions or limiting factors, or depict anyone under 21 gaming. The existing (2)(d) duty to name the facility and its location is worded around gaming facilities.","§ 1363(3): every ad must clearly and conspicuously state a problem gambling hotline number. New language requires the number to be visible for the full duration of any video ad.","§ 1363(4): direct ads must offer at least two opt-out methods (telephone, U.S. mail, or email), and licensees must block opted-out individuals in their databases within 15 days.","§ 1363(5)-(6): licensees must file complete copies of all ads with the Gaming Commission within 5 business days of dissemination, stop an ad on Commission notice, and keep ad records for at least 2 years.","Effective on the 90th day after enactment. Same as S09436."],"issue_tags":["advertising"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike CT HB05229, which targets particular advertising settings (college campuses) and AI-driven bet targeting, A382-A does not create new substantive bans. It instead brings mobile operators under New York's existing casino advertising regime and adds a video hotline-duration rule. CT HB05038 and IL HB5059 (ORACLE Act) aim advertising and age limits at prediction markets rather than licensed sportsbooks. Most of the other listed bills (AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, HI HB 1308) are authorization and tax frameworks, not advertising-specific measures.","watch":"Watch the full-duration video hotline requirement, the 5-business-day ad filing and Commission takedown power applied to high-volume digital and promotional campaigns, and how § 1363(2)(d)'s facility name-and-location rule will be read for online-only mobile operators.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"A382","text_chars":5576,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:01:41.026Z"},"NY-A4279":{"summary":"A04279-A, the \"regulating addictive notifications act,\" adds a new paragraph (k) to Racing, Pari-Mutuel Wagering and Breeding Law §1367-a(4) barring New York mobile sports wagering operators and platforms from initiating or sending push notifications or text messages, including alerts, that solicit sports wagers or deposits. It defines \"push notification\" as an automatic electronic message shown on an account holder's device while the operator's app is not open or visible, prompting the user to check or engage with the platform. It takes effect immediately.","mechanics":["Amends RWB Law §1367-a(4) by adding paragraph (k); companion bill is S05537.","Covers both mobile sports wagering operators and mobile sports wagering platforms.","Bans push notifications and text message notifications, including alerts, relating to solicitation of sports wagers or deposits.","\"Push notification\" means an automatic electronic message displayed while the operator's user interface is not actively open or visible, prompting the account holder to check or engage with the platform.","The text contains no opt-in exception and no separate penalty provision, so enforcement would rely on existing §1367-a licensing and Gaming Commission authority.","Effective immediately on enactment; the bill was ordered to third reading (cal. 123) on 2026-01-07."],"issue_tags":["advertising","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Most of the listed bills authorize or tax sports wagering, such as AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, HI HB 1308 and IL HB3159. CT HB05229 is the closest analogue, because it also limits operator marketing conduct: it restricts campus advertising, regulates withdrawals and bars AI-targeted bets. New York's bill is narrower but more absolute, imposing a flat ban on one marketing channel (push notifications and texts soliciting wagers or deposits) rather than age- or location-based limits like those in CT HB05038 or IL HB5059 for prediction markets. Unusually, it contains no consent or opt-in safe harbor, so even customers who have opted in could not receive such messages.","watch":"The undefined scope of \"relating to the solicitation of sports wagers or deposits\" could sweep in odds boosts, promo alerts, game-start alerts and possibly transactional or responsible-gaming messages, and with immediate effect, no opt-in carve-out and the bill on the third-reading calendar, operators should be ready to reconfigure CRM and notification systems quickly.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"A4279","text_chars":4632,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:01:46.791Z"},"NY-A3006":{"summary":"A03006-C is New York's 2025-26 Education, Labor, Housing and Family Assistance (ELFA) Article VII budget bill, a 30-plus-part omnibus covering school aid, free school meals, student device policies, scholarships, housing, social services, labor law penalties, unemployment insurance, discovery reform and fund administration. Nothing in the title, the Part-by-Part summary or the portion of text provided authorizes, regulates or taxes sports wagering or other gaming. The Assembly version was substituted by the Senate companion S3006-C on May 8, 2025, so this bill number is not the operative vehicle.","mechanics":["Part A, §10: 2025-26 foundation aid equals the greater of total foundation aid or 1.02 times the foundation aid base (a 2% minimum increase).","Part A, §§3-7: the 'poverty count' now uses an economically disadvantaged student count and three-year average Census SAIPE rates, each weighted at 65%, replacing free and reduced-price lunch and Census 2000 data. Part A, §4-a raises the English language learner weight from 0.50 to 0.53 starting in 2025-26.","Part A, §9: the state sharing ratio cap for foundation aid rises to 0.93 for 2025-26 and later. Part A, §10-a sets a 1.351 regional cost index for Westchester County.","Part A, §10-b: the approved salary ceiling for BOCES aid rises in steps from $30,000 to $60,000 by 2028-29. Part A, §10-c raises career education aid to $4,100 per pupil after 2024-25.","Other Parts: universal free school meals (Part B), limits on internet-enabled devices in schools (Part C), higher child labor and wage-payment civil penalties (Parts V-W), a higher maximum unemployment insurance benefit (Part KK), discovery reform (Part LL), and fund transfers and bonding authority (Part MM).","Effective dates are set Part by Part in each Part's last section, as provided in §1."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills is comparable. AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686 and HI SB1569 would authorize and tax sports wagering, while IL HB3159 and HB4171 amend an existing sports wagering act, and this bill does neither. The closest structural analogue is IL HB2755, a multi-subject revenue omnibus, but HB2755 is a revenue measure and the provided text of A03006 contains no gaming tax or wagering provision. Any New York gaming-related budget changes would more likely appear in other Article VII bills, such as the revenue bill, not in ELFA.","watch":"Because the supplied text is truncated, counsel should review Part MM's fund-transfer and sweep provisions in the enacted S3006-C to confirm that no lottery, commercial gaming or mobile sports wagering education accounts are redirected.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"A3006","text_chars":669242,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:01:47.520Z"},"NY-A3009":{"summary":"New York's FY2025-26 revenue budget bill (Chapter 59 of 2025, signed May 9, 2025) is mostly a broad tax package covering income tax rate cuts, the high-income surcharge extension, the inflation refund credit, business credits and MTA financing. It also has a cluster of gaming Parts. These address pari-mutuel tax simplification and simulcasting extenders (Part FF), tax rates on gaming revenues in certain regions (Part GG), OTB capital funds (Part HH), vendor-track fees (Part MM), NYRA franchise board members (Part NN), and a larger problem-gambling carve-out from mobile sports wagering tax revenue (Part OO). The supplied text is truncated partway through Part B, so the gaming Parts can be described only from the bill summary and title.","mechanics":["Part OO: mobile sports wagering tax revenue set aside for problem gambling education and treatment is fixed at $6 million per fiscal year through FY2026 and $12 million per fiscal year after that, spendable only under a plan approved by the Director of the Budget.","Part GG: sets rates for the tax on gaming revenues in certain regions under the Racing, Pari-Mutuel Wagering and Breeding Law, and amends the effectiveness of Part OOO of Chapter 59 of the Laws of 2021. Repeal provisions apply on expiration.","Part FF: Subpart A simplifies the pari-mutuel tax rate system and repeals RPWBL §908. Subpart B extends the simulcast licensing, out-of-state thoroughbred and harness simulcast, and wager-distribution provisions (chapters 281/1994 and 346/1990). Subpart C sets amounts of market origin credits and fees and the state's use of the funds collected.","Part MM: amends the Tax Law on vendor fees paid to certain VLT vendor tracks, with a sunset and repeal on expiration.","Part NN: changes the terms and licensing requirements of members of the franchised corporation appointed by NYRA. Part HH addresses use of Capital OTB capital acquisition fund money.","Part II funds research on thoroughbred health and safety. Under §1 of the act, each Part's effective date is set in that Part's last section; for example, Part A (the inflation refund credit) takes effect immediately."],"issue_tags":["tax","licensing"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"Unlike the authorization bills listed (AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, HI HB 1308/SB1569), NY A3009 assumes an existing mobile market and adjusts how its revenue is used. On the face of the summary, it does not change the mobile sports wagering tax rate itself. Its doubled problem-gambling earmark is closest to IL HB3159, which would dedicate 2% of sports wagering AGR to fire and EMS. It also resembles the problem-gambling program bills IL HB1451 and HB2580. Those bills, however, build out program content, whereas NY simply fixes a dollar amount gated by Budget Director approval. Part GG's regional casino rate setting has no direct counterpart among the listed bills, though IA SSB 1157 similarly bundles tax rates and revenue use across gambling verticals.","watch":"Operators should track how the jump from $6 million to $12 million under Part OO, and the Budget Director's spending plan, feed into future pushes to raise New York's mobile tax or responsible-gaming obligations. They should also obtain the full text of Part GG, which was not included in the supplied excerpt, to confirm which regional gaming revenue rates were reset and when they sunset.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"A3009","text_chars":601354,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:01:48.095Z"},"NY-A6745":{"summary":"A06745-A adds a new section 912 to New York's Racing, Pari-Mutuel Wagering and Breeding Law that makes it unlawful to operate, conduct or promote online sweepstakes games in New York. It also bars applicants, licensees, payment processors, geolocation providers, content suppliers, platform providers and media affiliates from supporting such games. Violators face fines of $10,000 to $100,000 per violation and loss of, or ineligibility for, a gaming license, enforced by the Gaming Commission, State Police or Attorney General.","mechanics":["Definition (§912(1)(a)): an \"online sweepstakes game\" is any internet or mobile game, contest or promotion that uses a dual-currency system exchangeable for cash prizes or cash equivalents and simulates casino-style gaming (slots, video poker, table games, lottery games, bingo or sports wagering); the Gaming Commission decides what counts as a dual-currency system.","Direct prohibition (§912(2)(a)): it is unlawful for any applicant, licensed entity, gaming or key employee, individual investor or investment firm, board member, or any other person to operate, conduct or promote online sweepstakes games in New York.","Support prohibition (§912(2)(b)): it is unlawful for applicants, licensed entities, financial institutions, payment processors, geolocation providers, gaming content suppliers, platform providers or media affiliates to support the operation, conduct or promotion of such games.","Penalties (§912(3)): a fine of $10,000 to $100,000 per violation, plus possible loss of a gaming license and/or ineligibility for one; fines are deposited in the commercial gaming revenue fund (State Finance Law §97-nnnn) for problem gambling education and treatment.","Enforcement (§912(4)): the Gaming Commission, State Police or Attorney General may investigate, hold hearings, issue cease-and-desist letters and issue subpoenas.","Effective immediately; the Assembly bill was substituted by S5935-A on 2025-06-13."],"issue_tags":["sweeps","licensing","advertising"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike the authorization-and-tax bills listed (AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, HI HB 1308/SB1569), this is a pure prohibition aimed at dual-currency sweepstakes casinos and the vendors that support them. CA AB 831 also targets sweepstakes, but it works through California's unfair-practices framework for contests and sweepstakes, whereas the NY bill ties violations to gaming-license consequences and reaches payment processors, geolocation providers and media affiliates. It runs opposite to IL HB3274/HB5048, which would shield prize and gift kiosk operators from Gaming Board discipline. It is closer in spirit to FL H0189 and S1580, which tighten illegal-gambling enforcement.","watch":"Operators and their suppliers face exposure under the §912(2)(b) \"support\" and \"promote\" language, which could put a licensee's New York license at risk because of a vendor, affiliate or media partner's sweepstakes ties. Note that the legislative purpose's \"knowingly accepts revenue\" from sweepstakes standard does not appear in the operative text, and the Commission has undefined discretion to define \"dual-currency system.\"","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"A6745","text_chars":5936,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:01:54.925Z"},"NY-A7903":{"summary":"A7903-C amends Racing, Pari-Mutuel Wagering and Breeding Law § 1367(1)(r) to add a new class of 'prohibited sports bettor': individuals convicted of (or found in a final civil protection order to have committed) enumerated Penal Law offenses against athletes, coaches, officials, jockeys and other sports or horse-race participants, or sports bribery/tampering and pari-mutuel integrity offenses, where the conduct arose from or related to a wager or the event. The Gaming Commission must set a tiered schedule of fixed ban periods by regulation, notify designated individuals, hear limited written appeals, and distribute a consolidated list to casinos, mobile operators and horse-race wagering entities, which must block those individuals from wagering or holding accounts.","mechanics":["New § 1367(1)(r)(xi) covers convictions on or after the effective date for listed offenses (assault, menacing, stalking, harassment, coercion, burglary, arson, robbery, intimidation, false bomb in a stadium, sports bribing §§ 180.40-180.53, etc.) or substantially similar out-of-state offenses, plus final orders of protection, where the victim is a sports or horse-race participant and the conduct relates to a wager or the event; disorderly conduct and harassment 2nd degree (clause C) qualify only if the Commission determines designation is warranted.","New § 1367(17) requires a Commission-set schedule with fixed tiers: Tier I (class B misdemeanors/violations) 1-3 years, Tier II (specified class A misdemeanors) 3-5 years, Tier III (felonies, specified class A misdemeanors and all sports bribery/tampering offenses) 5-10 years; consecutive periods for multiple incidents and the tier maximum for offenses committed during an existing ban; no ban may be indefinite or exceed 10 years for a single offense; report to the Governor and legislative leaders every five years.","Individuals receive notice and may file a written appeal limited to identity errors, vacated convictions/orders, expired periods, lack of a wager/event nexus, or (for clause C) misapplication of regulations; designation cannot rest solely on constitutionally protected fan criticism or commentary.","New § 1367(18) requires the Commission, with OCA and DCJS, to adopt information-sharing rules respecting sealing and confidentiality laws, and to distribute at least annually (with interim updates) a consolidated list to casinos, mobile sports wagering licensees, racing associations, OTBs, simulcast facilities, account wagering licensees and multi-jurisdictional ADW providers.","Licensees must use the list to prevent sports wagers, account opening or maintenance, and horse-race wagers; § 1367(17)(c), § 1367(18)(d) and new § 1012(19) let operators rely on the list in good faith and require no independent criminal-history investigation beyond identity verification.","Takes effect 180 days after becoming law; rulemaking by the Commission, the Chief Administrator of the Courts and DCJS is authorized immediately."],"issue_tags":["integrity","licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"None of the listed bills creates a conviction-based bettor exclusion list tied to abuse of athletes. Bills such as AK HB145/SB194, HI HB 1308/HB 2570/SB1569 and GA HB 686 address criminal history only for operator licensure, and CT HB05229 regulates operator conduct (withdrawals, advertising, AI targeting) rather than individual bettor eligibility. NY A7903-C instead pairs the existing prohibited-bettor framework with a statutory tiered penalty schedule and a court/DCJS data pipeline, an approach aimed at bettor harassment of athletes that has no analogue in the listed bills.","watch":"Operators should watch the Commission's implementing regulations, which will govern list format and update frequency, identity-matching standards for account screening, and how far the good-faith reliance safe harbor protects licensees against enforcement for missed matches or for blocking the wrong person.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"A7903","text_chars":25132,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:02:00.975Z"},"NY-A9113":{"summary":"A9113-B amends Racing, Pari-Mutuel Wagering and Breeding Law §1344(2) to require the Gaming Commission's self-exclusion regulations to include procedures allowing a person to submit a self-exclusion request (placement or removal under §1344(1)) electronically. The procedures must ensure parity between electronic and paper requests for record retention and for transmission of requests to licensed gaming facilities and mobile sports wagering platforms. The existing obligations on facilities to strip self-excluded persons from marketing and deny them credit, comps, check cashing and club benefits are left unchanged.","mechanics":["Amends RWB Law §1344(2), added by ch. 174 of 2013, so that Gaming Commission regulations must provide for electronic submission of self-exclusion requests under §1344(1).","Requires parity between electronic and non-electronic requests in record retention and in transmission to licensed gaming facilities and mobile sports wagering platforms.","Expressly names mobile sports wagering platforms as recipients of self-exclusion transmissions alongside licensed gaming facilities.","Retains the existing requirement that licensed gaming facilities remove self-excluded persons from targeted mailings and promotions and deny them credit, complimentaries, check cashing, club programs and similar benefits.","Takes effect immediately on enactment, though implementation depends on Commission rulemaking.","The bill is the B-print and is the same as S8636-B. It passed the Assembly (Cal. No. 240) and was returned to the Assembly on 2026-06-04."],"issue_tags":["integrity","licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Most of the listed bills address authorization, licensing or tax rather than self-exclusion administration. Examples include AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, HI HB 1308/HB 2570/SB1569, and Illinois' license-fee bills HB2878/HB5034. The closest analogue is CT HB05229, which layers consumer-protection duties on licensees, such as withdrawal rules, customer service lines and AI targeting limits. NY A9113 is narrower: a procedural change aimed at the regulator's intake process rather than new operator conduct rules. Illinois HB1451/HB2580 likewise address problem gambling, but through DHS public education rather than exclusion-list mechanics.","watch":"Watch the Gaming Commission's implementing regulations, since electronic intake will likely increase the volume and speed of list updates that mobile operators must ingest, and parity in transmission may bring tighter timelines for honoring exclusions and suppressing marketing.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"A9113","text_chars":3606,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:02:01.738Z"},"NY-A8167":{"summary":"A08167-B amends Racing, Pari-Mutuel Wagering and Breeding Law \\u00a71367(8)(c) to rework the existing carve-out of New York mobile sports wagering tax revenue for a statewide youth sports grant program administered by OCFS. It fixes the annual amount at $5 million through FY2026 and $23 million each year after, splits it between capital and programming, and routes the money through county youth bureaus, NYC's Department of Youth and Community Development, and a qualifying sports-for-youth nonprofit. It does not change the mobile sports wagering tax rate or impose any new obligation on operators.","mechanics":["\\u00a71367(8)(c): starting in FY2022, the commission pays one percent of the state mobile sports wagering tax to the general fund for an OCFS youth sports activities and education grant program for underserved youth under 18. The amount is fixed at $5M a year through FY2026 and $23M a year after that. The bill's title and summary describe 'up to two percent' capped at $23M, but the operative text says one percent.","Split: 45% for capital expenditures and 'the remaining fifty percent' for programming. These shares add to 95%, which looks like a drafting error.","Distribution: county youth bureaus distribute all capital funding and the programming funding outside NYC. In NYC, 75% of programming funds go through DYCD and 25% through a not-for-profit with an established history of sports-based youth development grantmaking in underserved communities.","Reporting: each distributing entity must report to the commissioner on recipients, use of funds and impact within six months after the end of each grant period.","Unchanged surrounding provisions: under \\u00a71367(8)(a), mobile tax revenue otherwise goes to the lottery fund for education. Under (8)(b), problem gambling funding is $6M a year through FY2026 and $12M after. Under (8)(d), platform providers must make at least monthly deposits and file a reconciliation by the 10th of each month.","Effective immediately."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Illinois HB3159 also dedicates a slice of sports wagering money to a local public purpose: 2% of adjusted gross receipts for fire and EMS departments from July 1, 2026. That bill is framed as the State imposing and collecting the 2%, so it may add to operator cost. NY A08167-B only reallocates revenue the state already collects under the existing mobile tax. Iowa SSB 1157 likewise channels gambling and sports wagering revenue into sports tourism and infrastructure funds, but it does so alongside tax-rate and license-fee changes that this NY bill does not attempt. Unlike the Alaska, Georgia and Hawaii authorization bills, A08167-B assumes a mature market and deals purely with downstream appropriation.","watch":"There is no direct compliance or tax burden on operators, but watch whether a growing set of fixed-dollar earmarks from mobile tax revenue ($23M youth sports, $12M problem gambling) builds constituencies that make future reductions in New York's mobile tax rate harder, and whether the 1%-versus-2% and 45/50 drafting inconsistencies get fixed in a further amendment.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":5606,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:02:04.118Z"},"NY-A9146":{"summary":"A09146 amends Racing, Pari-Mutuel Wagering and Breeding Law §1367-a(4)(a) to require that, whenever a New York mobile sports wagering operator provides information on compulsive play, every link and referral for counseling, therapy or treatment points account holders only to the problem gambling services webpage maintained by the Office of Addiction Services and Supports (OASAS). It also bars operators from advertising or promoting any other organization that provides such services, while expressly preserving operators' ability to offer responsible gaming tools.","mechanics":["Inserts a new subparagraph (xv) in RWB Law §1367-a(4)(a): all links and referrals for counseling, therapy or treatment for compulsive play must direct account holders to the OASAS problem gambling services webpage.","Prohibits mobile sports wagering operators from advertising or promoting any other organization or entity that provides counseling, therapy or treatment services for compulsive play.","Carve-out: operators may still provide responsible gaming tools, including deposit limits, wager limits, time limits and voluntary self-exclusion.","Existing responsible-play page requirements in (xiv) are unchanged, and the annual problem gaming plan requirement, approved by the Gaming Commission in consultation with OASAS, is renumbered from (xv) to (xvi) without substantive change.","Applies to licensed mobile sports wagering operators; takes effect immediately on enactment, with no transition period.","Same-as bill: S10154. Passed one chamber and was referred to Racing, Gaming and Wagering on 2026-05-14."],"issue_tags":["advertising"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Most of the listed bills address authorization, licensing or taxes, such as AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686 and HI HB 1308/HB 2570/SB1569; A09146 does none of these and regulates only operator-facing problem gambling referrals. Illinois HB1451 and HB2580 also deal with gambling disorders, but they direct the Department of Human Services' public education program rather than restricting what operators may link to. Connecticut HB05229 is the closest in spirit, imposing operator conduct rules on advertising, withdrawals, customer service and AI-targeted bets, but it does not channel treatment referrals to a single state provider. A09146 is unusual in giving a state agency's webpage an exclusive role in treatment referrals and banning operators from promoting third-party treatment providers.","watch":"Operators should audit existing responsible-gaming partnerships and in-app content, such as links to national helplines, private treatment providers or sponsored RG nonprofits, because the bar on advertising or promoting any other treatment organization takes effect immediately and the bill does not define 'referrals' or 'promote'.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"A9146","text_chars":4368,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:02:08.845Z"},"NY-J493":{"summary":"Senate Resolution 493, sponsored by Senator Fernandez with a long bipartisan co-sponsor list, asks Governor Hochul to proclaim March 2025 as Problem Gambling Awareness Month in New York, alongside National Problem Gambling Awareness Month. It is a non-binding memorializing resolution, adopted 2025-03-18. Despite the 'signed into law' status, it creates no legal obligations, appropriations or regulatory changes.","mechanics":["Resolving clause only memorializes the Governor to proclaim March 2025 as Problem Gambling Awareness Month and urges citizens to raise awareness of prevention, treatment and recovery services.","Recitals cite an OASAS survey finding 5% of adults (668,000 people) showed problem gambling behaviors in the past year.","Recitals cite a survey finding 10% of 7th–12th graders (140,000 students) showed signs of problem gambling, with 10% of those needing treatment.","Recitals tie the concern directly to mobile sports betting growth and note the State has collected more than $1.55 billion in taxes on mobile bets.","No operator duties, funding, enforcement or effective-date provisions; the only operative step is transmitting an engrossed copy to the Governor."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Like NJ AR150 and LA SR 178, this is a non-binding resolution rather than a statute. It is weaker than both: AR150 urges a specific ban on targeted ads to people showing signs of problem gambling, and SR 178 asks for a health-department study, while J493 only asks for a proclamation. It has none of the operative responsible-gaming mandates in MD HB518 (self-exclusion list), MD HB1409/SB862 (mobile gambling prevalence studies and fund revenue) or NJ A 4003 (no incentives to patrons using responsible-gaming tools).","watch":"It has no direct compliance impact, but the recitals explicitly link New York's $1.55 billion in mobile betting tax receipts to an obligation to fund prevention and treatment, so watch for this framing being used to support binding bills raising problem-gambling contributions or responsible-gaming mandates on New York mobile operators.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"J493","text_chars":4126,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:02:14.946Z"},"NY-J1707":{"summary":"Senate Resolution J1707 is a non-binding memorializing resolution, adopted March 10, 2026, asking Governor Hochul to proclaim March 2026 as Problem Gambling Awareness Month in New York, in line with National Problem Gambling Awareness Month. It urges citizens to raise awareness of problem gambling and of prevention, treatment and recovery services. Although the status line says 'signed into law,' it is a one-house resolution that creates no legal obligations, regulatory changes or funding.","mechanics":["Form: Senate resolution sponsored by Senator Fernandez with roughly 60 co-sponsors; adopted 2026-03-10; transmitted to the Governor; no statutory amendments.","Operative clause: memorializes the Governor to proclaim March 2026 as Problem Gambling Awareness Month and urges public awareness of prevention, treatment and recovery services.","Findings cite an OASAS survey showing 5% of adults (668,000 people) had problem gambling behaviors in the past year, and 10% of grade 7-12 students (140,000) showed signs of problem gambling.","Expressly ties concern to the growth of mobile sports betting and notes the State has collected more than $1.55 billion in taxes on mobile bets.","Imposes no requirements on operators, the Gaming Commission or OASAS, and has no effective date or enforcement provisions."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Like LA SR 178 and NJ AR150, J1707 is a non-binding resolution, but it is narrower than both. LA SR 178 asks the Louisiana Department of Health to study and recommend modernized problem gambling programs, and NJ AR150 urges a substantive policy, a ban on targeted ads to people showing signs of problem gambling. J1707 only asks for an awareness proclamation. By contrast, Maryland's HB1409/SB862 (prevalence studies and fund revenue) and HB518 (voluntary exclusion list and problem gambling regulations), and New Jersey's A 4003 (no incentive-based wagering offers to patrons using responsible gaming tools), would impose actual duties affecting operators.","watch":"The resolution has no direct legal effect, but its explicit framing of mobile sports betting growth and the $1.55 billion tax take as reasons for more problem-gambling resources signals bipartisan Senate appetite for future responsible-gaming mandates or funding demands on New York mobile operators.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"J1707","text_chars":4078,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:02:16.999Z"},"NY-A9584":{"summary":"A09584-A adds a new Racing, Pari-Mutuel Wagering and Breeding Law § 1367-b imposing detailed know-your-customer, multi-factor authentication, account-sharing/proxy-betting monitoring, responsible gaming, risk-assessment, recordkeeping and consumer-data obligations on New York mobile sports wagering licensees. It requires licensees to tier bettors as at-risk, elevated-risk or high-risk under commission-set thresholds, lets bettors who knowingly share accounts or proxy bet be designated prohibited sports bettors, and redirects penalties for problem gaming and responsible gaming violations to a three-way split. It takes effect 180 days after enactment.","mechanics":["§ 1367-b(2): before account activation, licensees must collect full legal name, residential address, DOB, last four SSN digits (or alternative ID), email, phone, a photo of government ID and a live photograph. All data must be entered directly by the bettor with no pre-population or auto-fill. Race, gender and income must be requested on a voluntary basis. Bettors must attest to being 21+, having only one account per skin, no account sharing or proxy betting, and no bots. Bettors may close accounts at any time, and funds must be returned without unreasonable delay.","§ 1367-b(3)-(4): written identity verification procedures using independent data sources, with enhanced verification for red flags. Licensees remain liable when they use third-party vendors. Multi-factor authentication is mandatory, with a re-authentication window of no more than one hour on a verified device. Accounts flagged for material risk of sharing, proxy betting or fraud must be promptly suspended. Confirmed cases must be reported to the commission and stay suspended until corrective measures are taken. Knowing participants may be designated prohibited bettors.","§ 1367-b(5)-(7): written responsible gaming procedures subject to commission approval, a senior 'responsible gaming lead' who reports to management at least annually, and employee training. Licensees must monitor risk factors such as wager or deposit escalation, late-night sessions, limit changes, withdrawal reversals and failed payments. Risk assessments are triggered by commission-set evidence-based thresholds, and bettors receive at-risk, elevated-risk or high-risk designations.","§ 1367-b(8)-(9): records kept at least 5 years, an at-risk bettor list provided to the commission on request, and no obligation to verify income or credit. Licensees may not sell or share KYC data except where strictly necessary. Bettors have a data access right. On account deletion, a bettor may request deletion of KYC data within 30 days, and that deletion right overrides the § 1367-b retention rules, subject to exceptions for federal law, court orders and investigations.","§ 1367-b(10): commission rulemaking, examination, deficiency findings, corrective action plans and practice-suspension orders. Violations are subject to civil penalties, license conditions, suspension or revocation. There is no private right of action.","§ 1367(8) as amended: penalties for violations of § 1367-a(4) problem gaming plans and § 1367-b are split one-third to the lottery education fund, one-third to problem gambling education and treatment via the commercial gaming revenue fund, and one-third retained by the commission for enforcement. § 1367-a(4)(a)(xv) is amended so that annual problem gaming plans must include the § 1367-b procedures."],"issue_tags":["integrity","licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike the listed bills that authorize or tax sports wagering (AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, HI HB 1308/HB 2570/SB1569) or adjust licensing fees and taxes (IL HB2878, HB5034, HB3159), NY A09584 is a pure operator-conduct bill aimed at an existing mobile market. Its closest analogue is CT HB05229, which also regulates gaming-account practices such as withdrawals, customer service and AI-targeted bets. NY goes much further, mandating codified KYC data fields, MFA, a statutory tiered risk-designation scheme for bettors, and consumer data deletion rights. Earmarking penalty revenue to fund the commission's own enforcement is also unusual among the listed bills.","watch":"Watch the commission's implementing regulations on the 'objective, evidence-based thresholds' for risk assessments and tier designations, the mandatory-suspension trigger for suspected account sharing, and the tension between the 30-day data deletion right and five-year retention and AML obligations, since these will drive compliance cost and enforcement exposure, which is heightened by the commission retaining a third of penalties.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"A9584","text_chars":35629,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:02:19.703Z"},"NY-K1093":{"summary":"Assembly Resolution K1093 is a non-binding resolution that asks Governor Hochul to proclaim March 2026 as Problem Gambling Awareness Month in New York, alongside National Problem Gambling Awareness Month, and urges residents to raise awareness of prevention, treatment and recovery services. It creates no legal obligations, and despite the 'signed into law' status, it was simply adopted by the Assembly on 2026-03-25. Its recitals tie the need for awareness directly to the growth of mobile sports betting.","mechanics":["Memorializing resolution only: no statutory amendments, mandates, appropriations, or enforcement provisions.","Recitals cite an OASAS survey finding 5% of adults (668,000 people) showed problem gambling behaviors in the past year.","Recitals cite a survey finding 10% of 7th–12th graders (140,000 students) showed signs of problem gambling, with 10% of those needing treatment.","Recitals point to record mobile betting revenue and more than $1.55 billion in state taxes collected on mobile bets.","Resolved clauses ask the Governor to make the proclamation, urge public awareness, and direct that an engrossed copy be sent to the Governor.","Sponsored by Woerner, with 22 multi-sponsors."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Like LA SR 178, which asks the Louisiana Department of Health to study modernizing problem gambling prevention, K1093 is a non-binding resolution, but it does not even request a study or report. It is also softer than NJ AR150, which urges a specific ban on targeted gambling ads to at-risk individuals. By contrast, Maryland's HB518 (voluntary exclusion list and problem gambling regulations), HB1409/SB862 (prevalence studies and fund revenue) and HB758 (problem gambling fund uses), along with NJ A 4002/A 4003, would impose real obligations or funding changes on operators and agencies.","watch":"The resolution has no direct legal effect, but its explicit link between mobile sports betting growth and problem gambling, including youth gambling figures, signals legislative appetite for future responsible-gaming mandates, advertising limits, or problem-gambling funding carve-outs from mobile wagering tax revenue in New York.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"K1093","text_chars":3548,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:02:20.328Z"},"NY-S10092":{"summary":"S10092 adds a new General Business Law §396-kkk making it unlawful for operators of covered platforms (profile-based websites and apps, including social gaming platforms) and social media platforms to show New York users paid ads, sponsored content, influencer or affiliate marketing, algorithmic promotion, targeted amplification or cross-platform placements for services offering online gaming-related gambling, prediction market wagering, online sweepstakes gaming, sports-related gambling or traditional online gambling, unless the operator has reasonably determined that the user is not a minor (under 18) under attorney general age-assurance regulations. Violations are treated as false advertising under GBL §350-a and can be enforced by the attorney general or the State Gaming Commission.","mechanics":["Duty falls on platform operators, not gambling advertisers. Ads covered by §396-kkk(2) cannot be shown to any NY user unless the operator has reasonably determined the user is 18 or older, using age-assurance rules the AG must promulgate under subdivision 6.","Broad product definitions: 'sports-related gambling' includes bracket and fantasy contests, DFS, esports and in-game betting, and sports wagering as defined in RPWBL §1301. 'Predictive market wagering' covers wagers, trades or financial positions on future events. 'Online sweepstakes gaming' covers dual-currency games that simulate gambling. Loot boxes, skins and pay-to-win microtransactions are also covered.","Data minimization under subdivision 3: age-verification data may be used only for age determination and must be deleted immediately after the attempt, unless state or federal law requires otherwise.","Enforcement under subdivision 4: a violation is false advertising under GBL §350-a, enforceable by the AG or the Gaming Commission. The AG must also run a complaint website, and the Gaming Commission must maintain industry guidance on online advertising to people under 18.","Takes effect 180 days after enactment, with rulemaking authorized immediately. Includes a severability clause. Same-as bill: A10712."],"issue_tags":["advertising","prediction","sweeps"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"CT HB05038 and IL HB5059 (ORACLE Act) apply age limits to prediction markets at age 21, regulating the platforms' own users and marketing. S10092 instead sets the line at 18 and puts the obligation on the social media and gaming platforms that carry the ads. CT HB05229 restricts gaming advertising on college campuses by placement, while S10092 relies on age assurance across all online ad formats. Its single advertising rule covering sports betting, prediction markets, sweepstakes, loot boxes and skins is broader in product scope than any listed bill.","watch":"Watch how strict the AG's age-assurance regulations turn out to be, because platforms may respond by over-blocking or demanding contractual indemnities. That could sharply cut licensed sportsbooks' social, influencer and affiliate reach in New York even though the operators themselves are not the regulated party.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"S10092","text_chars":12217,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:02:29.688Z"},"NY-S2616":{"summary":"S2616 amends the definition of \"sports wagering\" in Racing, Pari-Mutuel Wagering and Breeding Law § 1367(1)(x) to expressly include wagers on annual awards or honors for individual athlete or team performance during or throughout a season, and adds season-long proposition bets to the list of authorized wager types. It conditions in-game and season-long prop bets on a Gaming Commission determination that they are not at risk of undue interference, insider trading or other integrity problems. It also permits coin toss bets that affect gameplay at all sporting events unless the Commission prohibits them for integrity reasons.","mechanics":["Expands § 1367(1)(x) to cover wagering on \"annual awards or honors for an individual athlete or team performance during or throughout a season\" (e.g., MVP and other futures award markets).","Adds \"in-game and season-long proposition bets\" to the non-exhaustive list of authorized sports wagers, alongside single-game, teaser, parlay, over-under, money line, pools, in-game, in-play and straight bets.","Limits in-game and season-long prop bets to wagers the Commission determines are not at risk of undue interference, insider trading of information, or other issues compromising integrity and fairness, making Commission approval the gatekeeper.","Permits coin toss bets that affect gameplay at all sporting events by default, subject to Commission prohibition for integrity and fairness reasons.","Effective immediately upon enactment; no tax, licensing or fee changes."],"issue_tags":["prop","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"None of the listed bills deals specifically with authorized bet types such as season-long props or award futures. Most of them concern authorization and tax (AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, HI HB 1308/SB1569), license fees (IL HB2878/HB5034), consumer protection (CT HB05229), or prediction markets (IL HB5059, IL HB5142). S2616 is unusual in widening the wagering menu rather than restricting it, which runs against the recent trend of states curbing player props. It pairs that expansion with a Commission integrity screen, which in practice gives regulators discretion to approve or deny individual markets.","watch":"Watch how the Commission would implement the \"not at risk of undue interference, insider trading\" standard, since that discretion could produce market-by-market approvals or restrictions on award futures and season-long props (including possible college or individual-athlete carve-outs), and track whether the bill moves out of the Racing, Gaming and Wagering committee given the national push to restrict props.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":2378,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:02:30.150Z"},"NY-S3003":{"summary":"S3003-D/A3003-D is New York's FY2025-26 Aid to Localities appropriations bill, enacted with line-item vetoes on 2025-05-20, which funds local assistance programs for the fiscal year beginning April 1, 2025. The excerpt provided covers only the general provisions in Section 1 and the start of the Office for the Aging schedule. Nothing in that excerpt addresses sports wagering, gaming taxation, licensing or problem gambling.","mechanics":["Section 1(e) lets the Budget Director withhold appropriated aid if a general fund imbalance of $2,000,000,000 or more is projected for FY2025-26. The Director must first deplete the $2 billion transaction risk reserve, and the Legislature gets 10 business days to adopt its own withhold plan by concurrent resolution.","Section 1(g) repeals any appropriations and reappropriations in chapter 53 of the laws of 2024 that would otherwise lapse on March 31, 2026.","The Office for the Aging receives $417,304,100 in all-funds appropriations: $244,080,100 General Fund, $172,244,000 federal and $980,000 other special revenue. It also receives $636,450,011 in reappropriations.","Aging program lines limit rate increases to the 2.6 percent targeted inflationary increase authorized by a separate 2025 chapter. They also earmark up to $53,000,000 and $15,000,000 for unmet needs of the elderly under Elder Law section 214.","Many line items require a Director of the Budget–approved spending plan before any expenditure, and some Senate-designated grants require a Senate resolution adopted by majority roll-call vote."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. On its own, this is a general appropriations vehicle rather than gaming policy legislation. The excerpt provided contains no wagering-related appropriations. Any gaming touchpoints would sit in agency schedules not reproduced here, such as Gaming Commission or OASAS lines.","watch":"Review the full enacted schedules, not just this Office for the Aging excerpt, for any Gaming Commission, problem-gambling or gaming-revenue local aid lines. Also note that the Section 1(e) withholding authority could reduce payments funded from gaming receipts if a $2 billion general fund imbalance arises.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"S3003","text_chars":4477757,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:02:31.549Z"},"NY-S3005":{"summary":"S3005-C is New York's FY2025-26 public protection and general government budget bill, a Governor's program bill split into Parts A through BBB covering criminal justice extenders, police and correction officer hiring, victim services, public employee retirement, campaign finance, court operations and prison closures. Nothing in the supplied text authorizes, regulates, taxes or otherwise addresses sports betting, gaming, prediction markets or sweepstakes. The Senate version was substituted by A3005C on 2025-05-07.","mechanics":["Part A pushes dozens of criminal-justice sunsets, including work release, furloughs, the ignition interlock program and the VTL §1809 mandatory surcharge, from September 1, 2025 to September 1, 2027; NYC pre-criminal proceeding settlements move from March 31, 2025 to March 31, 2027.","Part C §1 adds Public Officers Law §3(9-a), waiving state residency for correction officer trainees and correction officers at state facilities.","Part C §§2-3 raise mandatory State Police retirement from 60 to 63 (Retirement and Social Security Law §381-b(e)) and widen the trooper appointment age range to 21-43 (Executive Law §215(3)), replacing the prior 29, extendable to 35.","Part C §4 raises the maximum age for municipal and DEC police officers under Civil Service Law §58 from 35 to 43, and drops the bar on localities setting a stricter maximum age.","Other Parts, as summarized in the title, cover an AI-generated child sexual abuse material offense (Part L), an office of gun violence prevention (Part MM), public campaign finance changes (Part OO), Attorney General civil enforcement actions (Part RR), and authority to close up to three correctional facilities in FY2025-26 (Part BBB)."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills is comparable. Georgia's HB 686, Hawaii's HB 1308 and Alaska's HB145 authorize and tax sports or mobile wagering, and Illinois HB2878 and HB3159 change license fees and taxes under the Sports Wagering Act. S3005-C contains no gaming provisions in the supplied text. New York's FY2026 gaming and revenue measures sit in other budget bills, not this public protection and general government bill.","watch":"The only provision with plausible spillover for operators is Part RR on Attorney General civil enforcement actions, which is not in the supplied excerpt, so counsel should review the enacted A3005C text to see whether it broadens the Attorney General's consumer-protection or Executive Law §63(12)-style powers that could reach gaming companies.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":336806,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:02:35.217Z"},"NY-S3006":{"summary":"S3006-C (Chapter 56 of 2025) is New York's FY2025-26 education, labor, housing and family assistance budget implementation bill, split into Parts A through MM covering school aid, free school meals, scholarships, housing programs, labor penalties, unemployment insurance, criminal procedure and state fund administration. Nothing in the text provided addresses sports wagering, casino gaming, lottery or other gambling, so its relevance to an operator practice is at most incidental.","mechanics":["Part A rewrites foundation aid. The ELL weight rises from 0.50 to 0.53 for 2025-26. The state sharing ratio cap rises to 0.93. 2025-26 foundation aid equals the greater of total foundation aid or 1.02 times the foundation aid base. Westchester gets a regional cost index of 1.351.","Part A replaces free and reduced-price lunch and census 2000 poverty measures with a three-year average economically disadvantaged rate and a three-year average Census SAIPE rate in the poverty count (Education Law §3602(1)(q)).","Part A phases up the BOCES aidable salary cap from $30,000 to $60,000 by 2028-29 and raises career education aid per pupil from $3,900 to $4,100.","Part B creates universal free school meals. Part C restricts student use of internet-enabled devices during the school day.","Labor Parts limit liquidated damages for certain frequency-of-pay violations (Part U), raise wage payment and child labor civil penalties (Parts V and W), and increase the maximum unemployment insurance benefit rate (Part KK).","Part MM governs administration of state funds and accounts, bond authorizations and inter-fund transfers. The text supplied is truncated within Part A, so Part MM's contents were not reviewed."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills is comparable. AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, HI HB 1308 and FL H0189 all authorize or overhaul gaming or sports wagering. IA SF 605 and IL HB3159 adjust wagering taxes or withholding. The nearest structural analogue is IL HB2755, a multi-subject revenue omnibus, but unlike that bill S3006 contains no visible gaming or revenue-raising provisions; New York's gaming-related budget items typically sit in the separate revenue budget bill rather than this education, labor and housing vehicle.","watch":"Review the full text of Part MM, which was not in the excerpt, for any transfers or sweeps touching lottery or commercial gaming revenue accounts that fund education aid, since the visible text contains no gaming provisions.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"S3006","text_chars":669242,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:02:46.553Z"},"NY-S3632":{"summary":"S3632 adds a new section 25.04 to the Mental Hygiene Law creating a 13-member Problem Gambling Advisory Council to make findings and recommendations to the governor and legislature on preventing and treating problem gambling in New York. The council has no regulatory power over operators. It must study the impact of mobile sports betting on problem gambling services, recommend how certain casino machine and table fees are allocated, and file an annual report.","mechanics":["Composition (§25.04(b)): 13 members. These are the OASAS commissioner, the Gaming Commission chair, four appointees each from the Senate temporary president and the Assembly speaker (each must include at least two community-based behavioral health providers), one each from the two minority leaders, and one from the governor.","Duties (§25.04(c)): meet at least twice a year under the Open Meetings Law and develop access, prevention and public-awareness strategies. The council must also recommend allocation of machine and table fees collected under Racing, Pari-Mutuel Wagering and Breeding Law §1348 and distributed under State Finance Law §97-nnnn.","Mobile sports betting review (§25.04(c)(5)): the council must examine mobile sports betting's effect on problem gambling services. This covers hopeline call volume, outreach to resource centers, staffing needs, and growth in self-exclusion list enrollment at gaming facilities.","Reporting: an annual report is due to the governor and legislature by October 1 each year, with the first due the October 1 after the effective date.","Terms and information (§25.04(d)-(e)): appointees serve unpaid four-year terms with expense reimbursement and may vote by written designee. The council may require information from state and local agencies, excluding personally identifying or otherwise legally protected information.","Effective date: 180 days after enactment. Companion bill is A02151; the bill was last reported and committed to the Senate Finance Committee on 2026-01-29."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike NJ A 4002 and A 4003, which impose direct obligations on sports wagering licensees (account-limit rules, and no incentives for patrons using responsible gaming tools), S3632 creates only an advisory body with no operator mandates. It is closest to MD HB1409/SB862 (mobile gambling prevalence studies and fund revenue), MD HB 195 (a gambling prevention technology workgroup) and LA SR 178 (a study of problem gambling prevention). It differs from those by setting up a standing council with an annual reporting cycle and a recommending role over specific casino fee revenue. Maryland HB758 alters authorized uses of problem gambling funds directly, whereas S3632 only lets the council recommend allocations of existing §1348 fees.","watch":"Operators should watch the council's annual reports and its mandated review of mobile sports betting's impact on hopeline calls and self-exclusion, since these findings could seed future legislation on responsible gaming mandates, advertising limits or additional problem gambling funding from mobile sports wagering revenue.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"S3632","text_chars":6293,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:02:46.562Z"},"NY-S3009":{"summary":"S3009-C is New York's FY2025-26 revenue budget bill (Parts A through XX), mostly income tax, credit and MTA financing changes, with a cluster of racing and gaming Parts (FF, GG, HH, II, MM, NN, OO). The gaming Parts simplify the pari-mutuel tax rate system, extend simulcasting authority, set tax rates on gaming revenues in certain regions, adjust VLT vendor-track fees, address NYRA franchise board licensing, and raise the problem-gambling earmark from mobile sports wagering tax revenue. The Senate version was substituted by A3009-C on 2025-05-08, so the Assembly bill is the operative vehicle.","mechanics":["Part OO: mobile sports wagering tax revenue dedicated to problem gambling education and treatment is fixed at $6 million per fiscal year through FY2026 and $12 million per fiscal year after that, spendable only under a plan approved by the Director of the Budget. The underlying mobile tax rate is not changed in the text provided.","Part GG: sets tax rates on gaming revenues in certain regions and extends part OOO of chapter 59 of the laws of 2021, with provisions repealed when they expire. The specific rates are not in the excerpt provided.","Part FF: Subpart A simplifies the pari-mutuel tax rate system and repeals RPWBL §908. Subpart B extends simulcast facility licensing, out-of-state thoroughbred and harness simulcasting, and wager distribution provisions under chapter 281 of 1994 and chapter 346 of 1990. Subpart C sets market origin credits and fees and how the state uses those funds.","Parts MM and NN: Part MM sets vendor fees paid to certain vendor tracks under the Tax Law, sunsetting on expiration. Part NN amends the terms and licensing requirements of members of the franchised corporation appointed by NYRA.","Parts HH and II: Part HH governs use of the Capital OTB capital acquisition fund. Part II funds research on thoroughbred health and safety, with a sunset.","The supplied text runs only through Part B, which covers the inflation refund credit and the PIT rate cuts and surcharge extension to 2032. The gaming details above come from the bill summary and title, not the operative text."],"issue_tags":["tax","licensing"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"Unlike the legalization bills listed, such as AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686 and HI SB1569, New York's bill assumes an existing mobile market and only adjusts revenue plumbing, racing taxes and casino gaming tax rates inside an omnibus budget. IL HB2755 is the closest analog as a multi-subject revenue vehicle. IL HB3159, which would earmark 2% of sports wagering AGR for fire and EMS, parallels Part OO's earmarking of mobile tax proceeds. IL HB1451 and HB2580 address problem gambling through program mandates rather than a fixed dollar carve-out like New York's $6M to $12M step-up.","watch":"Watch the operative A3009-C text of Part GG for the actual casino gaming revenue tax rates and sunset dates, and Part OO's budget-director plan requirement, since future budgets could use the same vehicle to change the 51% mobile tax or redirect sports wagering revenue.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"S3009","text_chars":601354,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:02:49.970Z"},"NY-S5640":{"summary":"S5640-D amends Racing, Pari-Mutuel Wagering and Breeding Law section 1367(8)(c) to raise the fixed annual carve-out of New York's mobile sports wagering tax revenue for the OCFS statewide youth sports grant program. The carve-out stays at $5 million per fiscal year through FY2026 and rises to $23 million per fiscal year after that. The bill also prescribes how the money is split between capital and programming, and who distributes it.","mechanics":["Section 1367(8)(c): the youth sports allocation, nominally described as one percent of the state mobile sports wagering tax, is fixed at $5 million per fiscal year through FY2026 and $23 million per fiscal year thereafter. Note that the bill title and summary say 'up to two percent,' but the operative text still says one percent.","Split: 45% goes to capital expenditures and 'the remaining fifty percent' to programming. The shares add up to only 95%, a drafting defect worth flagging.","Capital money is distributed by county youth bureaus. Programming money is also distributed by county youth bureaus, except in New York City, where 75% goes through the Department of Youth and Community Development and 25% through a not-for-profit with an established history of sports-for-youth-development grantmaking.","Each distributing entity must report to the commissioner on recipients, use of funds and impact within six months after each grant period ends.","Unchanged in the reprinted text: 1367(8)(b) keeps the problem gambling allocation at $6 million per year through FY2026 and $12 million thereafter, subject to a Division of the Budget-approved plan. The remaining mobile tax revenue still goes to the lottery fund for education, and platform providers still make monthly deposits and file reconciliation reports by the 10th of each month. The tax rate itself is not changed.","Takes effect immediately. Status: reported from committee and committed to Senate Finance on March 17, 2026."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Like IL HB3159, which dedicates 2% of sports wagering adjusted gross receipts to fire and EMS departments starting July 1, 2026, NY S5640-D earmarks sports betting tax revenue for a local public purpose. The difference is that the NY bill only re-allocates revenue already collected under the existing mobile tax and does not change the rate or the base on which it is calculated. It is narrower than IA SSB 1157, which restructures tax rates, license fees and revenue uses together. It is also narrower than the Hawaii bills (HB 1308, HB 2570, SB1569) and AK HB145/SB194, which build new licensing and tax regimes from scratch. Unusually, it names a role for an unnamed qualifying not-for-profit in distributing state gaming tax revenue.","watch":"Operator costs do not change because the 51% mobile rate and the deposit mechanics are untouched, but counsel should watch whether Finance amendments resolve the 'one percent' versus 'two percent' and 45/50 split inconsistencies in a way that turns the earmark into an add-on levy rather than a carve-out from existing revenue.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":5711,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:02:50.945Z"},"NY-S5935":{"summary":"S5935-A, signed as Chapter 605 of 2025, adds a new section 912 to New York's Racing, Pari-Mutuel Wagering and Breeding Law. It bans operating, conducting or promoting dual-currency online sweepstakes games that simulate casino-style gaming, including sports wagering, and it bans payment processors, geolocation providers, content suppliers, platform providers and media affiliates from supporting them. Violators face fines of $10,000 to $100,000 per violation and loss of, or ineligibility for, a gaming license, enforced by the Gaming Commission, State Police or Attorney General.","mechanics":["Definition (§912(1)(a)): an 'online sweepstakes game' is any internet or mobile game, contest or promotion that uses a dual-currency system exchangeable for cash prizes or cash equivalents, or for chances to win them, and that simulates casino-style gaming such as slots, video poker, table games, lottery games, bingo or sports wagering. The Gaming Commission decides what counts as a dual-currency system.","Direct prohibition (§912(2)(a)): unlawful for any applicant, licensed entity, gaming employee, key employee, individual investor or investment firm, board member, or any other person or entity to operate, conduct or promote online sweepstakes games in New York.","Supplier and ancillary prohibition (§912(2)(b)): unlawful for applicants, licensed entities, financial institutions, payment processors, geolocation providers, gaming content suppliers, platform providers or media affiliates to support the operation, conduct or promotion of such games in New York.","Penalties (§912(3)): $10,000 to $100,000 per violation, plus loss of a gaming license and/or ineligibility for one. Fines go to the commercial gaming revenue fund (State Finance Law §97-nnnn) for problem gambling education and treatment.","Enforcement (§912(4)): the Gaming Commission, State Police and Attorney General may investigate, hold hearings, issue cease-and-desist letters and issue subpoenas.","Effective immediately on signing (December 5, 2025). The purpose clause in §1 also refers to denying operations to anyone whose affiliate knowingly accepts revenue derived from sweepstakes, but the operative text of §912 contains no separate revenue-acceptance provision."],"issue_tags":["sweeps","licensing","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Like CA AB 831, which tightens California's sweepstakes and contest rules, NY S5935 targets dual-currency sweepstakes casinos. New York's law differs by attaching gaming-license consequences and by reaching ancillary vendors such as payment processors, geolocation providers and media affiliates, not just operators. Unlike the authorization bills (AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, HI HB 1308), it legalizes nothing and only polices the unlicensed market. It sits closer to the illegal-gaming enforcement focus of FL S1580 and FL H0189, and to the gambling-device definitional fights in IL HB3733 and IL HB 2879.","watch":"Licensed New York operators and their suppliers, affiliates and media partners should audit any business ties to sweepstakes or dual-currency platforms, since 'support' and 'promote' are undefined, the Gaming Commission has open-ended power to define 'dual-currency system,' and the penalty includes losing or being barred from a New York gaming license.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":4781,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:02:58.259Z"},"NY-S8636":{"summary":"S8636-B amends Racing, Pari-Mutuel Wagering and Breeding Law § 1344(2) to require the Gaming Commission's self-exclusion regulations to include procedures letting a person submit placement or removal requests electronically. Those procedures must ensure parity between electronic and non-electronic requests in record retention and in how requests are transmitted to licensed gaming facilities and mobile sports wagering platforms. The existing duties on facilities, such as removing self-excluded persons from marketing and denying credit, comps and club benefits, are unchanged.","mechanics":["Amends RPWBL § 1344(2), added by ch. 174 of 2013, which governs the commission's list of self-excluded persons.","Commission regulations must provide procedures for electronic submission of self-exclusion requests made under § 1344(1), covering both placements on and removals from the list.","Electronic and paper requests must be treated with parity for record retention and for transmission to licensed gaming facilities and mobile sports wagering platforms.","Mobile sports wagering platforms are now expressly named as recipients of transmitted self-exclusion requests.","Existing requirements that facilities remove self-excluded persons from targeted mailings and promotions and deny them credit, complimentaries, check cashing and club programs are retained.","Takes effect immediately. The bill was substituted by Assembly companion A9113B on 2026-06-04."],"issue_tags":["integrity","advertising"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike the comprehensive authorization and tax bills listed (AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, HI HB 1308/SB1569), S8636 is a narrow responsible-gaming amendment to an existing self-exclusion regime. It is closest in spirit to CT HB05229's consumer-protection measures and IL HB1451/HB2580's gambling-disorder provisions, but it focuses only on the procedural channel for self-exclusion requests. Its distinctive feature is the parity mandate, which forces regulators to treat online requests the same as in-person ones in how they are recorded and transmitted to operators.","watch":"Watch the commission's implementing regulations, which take effect immediately with no lead time, for how quickly and in what format electronically submitted exclusions (and removals) must be transmitted to and honored by mobile sports wagering platforms, and the compliance exposure if they are not.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":2211,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:02:59.358Z"},"NY-S7908":{"summary":"S7908-B amends Racing, Pari-Mutuel Wagering and Breeding Law sections 1367 and 1367-a to require mobile sports wagering operators to conduct age assurance at account registration using commission-specified \"commercially reasonable and technically feasible\" methods, expressly barring bare self-reported age. It also directs the Gaming Commission to create a separate exclusion list of \"identifying information\" (including IP addresses) that adults, or parents and guardians on behalf of minors, can register to block account creation. Operators must refuse registrations and wagers tied to that excluded data.","mechanics":["New definitions in §1367(1)(dd)-(ee): \"identifying information\" is any data that identifies or could reasonably be linked, directly or indirectly, to a specific person or device, and may include an IP address; \"Internet protocol address\" is also defined.","New §1367(12)(k): the commission must maintain an identifying-information exclusion list, separate from the §1344 self-exclusion list, with electronic registration by individuals or by parents and guardians for minors. Operators may not permit account registration using listed data once the commission has given them access. Under new §1367(12)(i)(iii), operators may not accept wagers through accounts created or maintained with such data.","Removal from the list: registrants may rescind at any time. Parent-submitted minor data is automatically removed and deleted on the date the minor turns 21, which must be supplied at submission. In both cases the commission must notify operators and delete the data. The commission must also adopt data-security regulations covering encryption, firewalls, and access limits.","§1367-a(4)(a)(iii)(A): age assurance is required only at the point of account registration, not at each login or wager. Commission rules must set acceptable methods and accuracy levels, taking into account operator size, resources, and capabilities, cost and effectiveness, audience, and industry practice. Self-reported age with no supporting evidence is not acceptable. Age-assurance data must be deleted immediately after the check unless retention is required by state or federal law.","§1367-a(4)(a)(iii)(B): an operator must treat a person as a minor if, at registration, the person's device signals minor status through a privacy setting, device setting, or other mechanism.","Effective 180 days after enactment, with rulemaking authorized immediately. The bill includes a severability clause."],"issue_tags":["integrity","licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Most of the listed bills are authorization, tax, or licensing vehicles: AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, HI HB 1308/SB1569, and the IL license-fee bills HB2878/HB5034. They do not focus on age verification. The closest analogue is IL HB5059 (ORACLE Act), which bars under-21 registration on prediction markets, though it targets prediction-market platforms rather than licensed sportsbooks. CT HB05229 also adds consumer-protection duties for gaming licensees but addresses advertising, withdrawals, and AI targeting rather than age assurance. Unusual features of the NY bill are its parent-initiated, device- and IP-level exclusion registry and its obligation to honor device-level minor signals, both borrowed from children's online privacy frameworks.","watch":"Operators should watch the commission's rulemaking on age-assurance methods and accuracy thresholds, and should also note several features of the statute itself: the broad \"identifying information\" definition (including shared or dynamic IP addresses) could block legitimate adult registrations, the device-signal mandate in §1367-a(4)(a)(iii)(B) is open-ended, and the immediate-deletion rule may clash with KYC and AML record-retention obligations.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":11340,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:03:03.868Z"},"NY-S7482":{"summary":"S7482-C adds a new category to New York's definition of \"prohibited sports bettor\" in Racing Law § 1367(1)(r)(xi): individuals convicted of, or found in a final civil protection order to have committed, enumerated Penal Law offenses (assault, stalking, harassment, threats, sports bribing and tampering, and others) against athletes, coaches, officials, jockeys or other sports or racing participants, or involving corruption of sports contests or pari-mutuel systems, where the conduct arose from or related to a wager or the event. The Gaming Commission must set tiered fixed prohibition periods by regulation, notify designated individuals, allow limited written appeals, and distribute a consolidated list at least annually. Casinos, mobile sports wagering licensees, account wagering licensees and horse-race wagering entities must use the list to block wagers and accounts, and may rely on it in good faith.","mechanics":["Coverage applies only to convictions or final protection orders on or after the effective date, in New York or for substantially similar out-of-state offenses. The conduct must arise from or relate to a sports wager, pari-mutuel betting, or the sporting event or horse race. Disorderly conduct (PL 240.20) and harassment 2nd (PL 240.26) qualify only if the Commission determines designation is warranted under its regulations (clause C).","New § 1367(17) requires fixed, non-discretionary tiers. Tier I (class B misdemeanors and violations, e.g., menacing 3rd, harassment 1st) carries 1–3 years. Tier II (specified class A misdemeanors, e.g., stalking 3rd, aggravated harassment 2nd) carries 3–5 years. Tier III (felonies, plus sports bribing and tampering under PL 180.40–180.53) carries 5–10 years.","Repeat offenses from separate incidents run consecutively. A new offense committed during an existing ban triggers the maximum period for the higher tier, starting when the existing ban ends. No single-offense ban may be indefinite or exceed 10 years, and the Commission must report on the schedule every 5 years.","The Commission must notify each designated individual. Written appeals are limited to these grounds: identity errors, a vacated or reversed conviction or order, an expired ban, lack of a wagering or event nexus, or, for clause C, failure to apply the regulations. Designation may not be based solely on constitutionally protected fan expression.","Under § 1367(18) the Commission, with OCA and DCJS, sets information-sharing rules subject to sealing and confidentiality laws, and distributes the list at least annually with interim updates. Operators need not make legal-sufficiency determinations beyond identity verification and may rely on the list in good faith. New § 1012(19) bars account wagering licensees from opening or maintaining accounts for, or taking horse-race wagers from, designated individuals.","The act takes effect 180 days after enactment, and rulemaking by the Commission, the Chief Administrator of the Courts and DCJS is authorized immediately."],"issue_tags":["integrity","licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"None of the listed bills creates a statutory, conviction-based exclusion list for bettors who abuse or threaten athletes. The closest analogues are only adjacent. DE HB314 addresses student-athlete protection, and CT HB05229 imposes account and consumer-protection duties on gaming licensees. Bills such as GA HB 686, HI HB 1308 and AK HB145 contain conventional prohibited-bettor definitions (insiders, minors) within new authorization frameworks. New York's bill is unusual in tying bettor exclusion to criminal and civil protection-order records, with fixed tiered ban lengths and an operator safe harbor.","watch":"Watch the Commission's implementing regulations on list format, update frequency and matching standards, because operators must block existing accounts and wagers from listed individuals. The statutory safe harbor covers only good-faith reliance on the list, so exposure for list-lag or identity-matching failures between annual or interim updates remains uncertain.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":23526,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:03:05.837Z"},"NY-S9003":{"summary":"S09003-D/A10003-D is New York's FY2026-27 Aid to Localities appropriations bill, signed with line-item vetoes, which appropriates and reappropriates state, federal and special revenue funds to local governments and grantees. The excerpt provided covers only the general provisions in section 1 and the Office for the Aging schedule. It contains no sports betting or gaming provisions.","mechanics":["Section 1(e) lets the budget director withhold appropriated aid if a cumulative general fund imbalance of $2 billion or more is projected for FY2026-27. The director must first deplete the $2 billion transaction risk reserve. The Legislature then has 10 business days to adopt its own withhold plan by concurrent resolution.","Withholding exemptions: public assistance and SSI-related payments, reductions that would violate federal law, debt service, and court-ordered payments.","Section 1(h) repeals 2025 (chapter 53) appropriations and reappropriations that would otherwise lapse on March 31, 2027.","Office for the Aging receives $430,736,841 in all-funds appropriations and $671,058,513 in reappropriations. Several program lines bar any inflationary or cost-of-living increase except a 2.7 percent targeted inflationary increase authorized by a separate 2026 chapter.","Many grants require a plan approved by the director of the budget before any spending. Some legislative member items, such as Project Guardianship, require a Senate resolution adopted by roll-call vote."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No other states' bills are listed for comparison. On its own, this is a general appropriations vehicle rather than gaming policy legislation. In the portion provided, the only feature with possible indirect relevance to gaming is the broad mid-year withholding authority in section 1(e). That authority could reach any gaming-revenue-funded local aid lines elsewhere in the bill, though none appear in the excerpt.","watch":"Review the agency schedules not included in this excerpt for any gaming-funded or problem-gambling aid lines and for line-item vetoes affecting them, and note that section 1(e) lets the budget director withhold such aid if a $2 billion general fund imbalance arises.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":4707234,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:03:11.194Z"},"NY-S9008":{"summary":"S09008-C/A10008-C is New York's FY2026-27 transportation, economic development and environmental conservation budget bill, an omnibus of Parts A through JJJ covering motor vehicle fees, traffic enforcement, utility ratemaking, SEQRA reform, insurance, pensions and bond financing. It was substituted by A10008-C on 2026-05-26. None of the text provided authorizes, taxes or regulates sports wagering or other gaming.","mechanics":["Part A extends certain DMV transaction fee increases and the related state finance provisions from April 1, 2026 to April 1, 2028, retroactive to April 1, 2026.","Part D adds VTL §1642-b. It lets New York City require an intelligent speed assistance device on a vehicle after final liability on 16 school-zone speed camera notices within 12 months.","Part D installation periods escalate from 12 to 24 to 36 months, and then run indefinitely until the administering agency approves removal. Tampering, or failing to install, carries fines of $1,500 to $2,500 and registration suspension.","Part D costs are waived for owners at or below 200% of the federal poverty level, and device data is shielded from disclosure and discovery.","Part G expands automated work zone speed enforcement to additional New York roadways.","Part Y enacts the 'Safe by Design Act' in the General Business Law, authorizing the Attorney General to adopt age assurance rules for covered platforms. The excerpt provided does not define 'covered platforms', so it is unclear whether online wagering apps fall within scope."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills is a true analogue. AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, HI HB 1308 and IL HB2878 directly authorize, license or tax sports wagering, and CT HB05038 and HB05229 target prediction-market and gaming advertising to minors. This New York bill contains no wagering authorization, tax or advertising rule in the text provided. Its only conceivable overlap is Part Y's age assurance mandate, which addresses a theme similar to CT HB05038's under-21 restrictions but through a general online-platform framework rather than gaming-specific rules.","watch":"Watch whether the Attorney General's Part Y 'Safe by Design Act' age assurance rules define 'covered platforms' broadly enough to reach online sportsbook or prediction-market apps, which would layer AG-imposed age verification standards on top of Gaming Commission requirements.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":694111,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:03:18.468Z"},"NY-S9414":{"summary":"S09414 adds a new Article 48 to the General Business Law (the ORACLE Act) that regulates prediction market platforms offered to New York residents. It imposes a 21+ age floor, bans catastrophic, political, death, security and athletic event markets, sets responsible-trading, advertising, settlement-source and anti-manipulation duties, and gives the Attorney General enforcement and rulemaking power. A new Racing Law §913 separately bars any entity that is licensed, would need a license, or is prohibited under the Racing, Pari-Mutuel Wagering and Breeding Law from offering prediction markets in New York.","mechanics":["Covers platforms offering bid-ask speculative positions on future events to NY residents, but excludes from 'prediction market platform' any entity licensed, required to be licensed, or prohibited under the Racing Law. Those entities are instead barred outright by new §913, with fines of $10,000–$100,000 per violation, loss of or ineligibility for a gaming license, and enforcement by the Gaming Commission, State Police or AG.","§1804 prohibits NY users from opening positions on catastrophic event, political, death, security (public company price) and athletic event markets. Athletic event markets expressly include horse racing and prop betting.","§1802 sets a 21+ age requirement. §1803 requires exclusion of self-excluded persons, platform and settlement-source insiders, and persons with insider information. §1806 mandates self-exclusion, deposit/spend limits (14-day wait to raise), session reminders, HOPE NY hotline only, and blocking of at-risk traders.","§1807 advertising rules: no 'risk-free' promotions, no push notifications about bonuses or markets the user has no position in, no targeting of under-21s, and hotline display for the full duration of video ads. Operators are liable for affiliate compliance. §1808 bans gift certificates and credit card funding.","§1809 bars markets in which any market maker or liquidity provider, or any affiliate, knowingly engages in gaming in the ordinary course of business anywhere, or in which such a person holds a contract or revenue share. §1805 requires disclosed, non-proprietary settlement sources.","Penalties under §1812: up to $10,000 per violation, or $50,000 for a persistent course of conduct. Violations of §§1803/1809 carry the greater of 2x profits or $50,000. A mandatory court-ordered cessation of NY operations applies for persistent violations, with $1 million per day for operating after such an order. The act takes effect one year after enactment."],"issue_tags":["prediction","advertising","prop","integrity","licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"The bill is nearly identical to IL HB5059, the Illinois ORACLE Act, sharing the 21+ age floor and platform-regulation framework. It goes well beyond CT HB05038, which addresses only age and advertising. Unlike MN HF 4437/SF 4511 and NC H1171, which prohibit prediction-market wagering outright with criminal penalties, it permits regulated prediction markets but carves out athletic, political and other categories. Unlike IL HB5142, which folds sports prediction markets into the Sports Wagering Act, and NJ A5336/S4447, which tax prediction market income, its distinctive feature is the §913 and §1809 firewall that bars licensed gaming operators and their affiliates from offering prediction markets or acting as market makers or revenue partners.","watch":"Watch the operator-facing provisions: new Racing Law §913 and GBL §1809 would bar any NY-licensed sportsbook or casino (and its affiliates, parents and JV partners) from offering prediction markets, acting as a market maker, or sharing revenue with a platform anywhere, on pain of loss of the NY gaming license. Watch also for federal CFTC preemption challenges to the §1804 athletic-event ban, which could reshape how the statute applies in practice.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":21886,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:03:20.278Z"},"NY-S9005":{"summary":"S9005-C/A10005-C is New York's FY2026-27 public protection and general government budget implementation bill, signed as Chapter 55 of 2026. It covers firearms, drones, insurance fraud, liquor licensing, ethics and lobbying, procurement, auto insurance rating, and immigration-enforcement limits. Nothing in the text provided authorizes, taxes, or regulates sports betting, gaming, or prediction markets.","mechanics":["Part C, Subpart A adds 3D-printed and ghost-gun offenses to Penal Law 265.10. Selling or sharing digital firearm manufacturing code to anyone who lacks both a state gunsmith license and a federal firearms license is a class A misdemeanor. Dealers who sell convertible pistols on or after May 31, 2027 commit a class D felony.","Part C, Subpart B adds GBL 396-eeee, which bars selling 3D printers in New York unless they have blocking technology. The ban takes effect one year after DCJS promulgates performance standards and is subject to a working-group finding that the technology is feasible. Gun industry members face a $5,000 penalty per product.","Part D creates Penal Law article 280, under which unlawful use of a drone is a class A misdemeanor. 'Prohibited space' includes the area within 500 feet of any 'large public gathering' of 500 or more people, expressly including sporting events. Part D also authorizes police drone mitigation and creates a state 'blue list' of approved drone vendors. It takes effect 90 days after enactment.","Parts U and Z change ethics and lobbying training frequency, add fees for late training, and amend lobbyist and client registration fees under the Legislative Law. These changes apply to any registered lobbyist, including those retained by gaming companies.","Part Y raises certain state purchasing and advertising thresholds to $150,000. These are procurement-notice thresholds, not rules on gambling advertising.","Each Part carries its own effective date in its last section; the act as a whole was signed May 27, 2026 as Chapter 55."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills is comparable. AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, HI HB 1308 and the Illinois Sports Wagering Act amendments (e.g., IL HB2878, HB3159) all directly authorize, license, or tax wagering. S9005 contains no wagering provisions in the text provided. CT HB05038 is also a governor's budget implementer, but it targets prediction-market access and advertising to people under 21, and S9005 has no analogous gaming component. New York's gaming changes, if any were enacted this cycle, would sit in the separate revenue budget bill rather than this public protection bill.","watch":"Watch Parts U and Z, which change lobbyist registration fees and ethics and lobbying training deadlines for gaming companies' New York lobbying programs, and the Part D drone restrictions around sporting events with 500 or more attendees, which may affect stadium-based sportsbooks and event operations; note that the bill text supplied was truncated after Part F, so later Parts should be checked against the chaptered text.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":275069,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:03:23.961Z"},"NY-S9415":{"summary":"S09415 adds a new subparagraph (xvi) to Racing, Pari-Mutuel Wagering and Breeding Law § 1367-a(4)(a). It requires New York mobile sports wagering operators to give every authorized sports bettor a monthly account activity statement, pushed electronically and kept continuously available in the bettor's account. The statement must itemize deposits, wagers, wins and losses, time on platform, promotional credits and responsible-gaming resources, and the Gaming Commission must set standardized formatting by regulation.","mechanics":["Statement contents under new § 1367-a(4)(a)(xvi)(A)-(I): total deposits; total amount wagered; total winnings and losses; net gain or loss; number of wagers placed; total time logged into the platform; an itemized accounting of promotional credits, bonuses or free wagers used; a prominent disclosure of responsible gaming resources, including the state voluntary self-exclusion program and problem gambling services; and access to the bettor's lifetime wagering history.","Delivery: the statement must be sent electronically by push notification and remain continuously accessible through the bettor's account.","Timing: the statement is due no later than 15 days after the end of each calendar month.","Regulation: the Gaming Commission must promulgate rules on standardized formatting, clarity requirements and any additional disclosures needed so statements are readily understandable.","Covered parties: all mobile sports wagering operators. The obligation sits alongside the existing responsible-play page requirement in (xiv) and the annual problem gaming plan in (xv).","Effective date: January 1 following enactment. Status: on 2026-06-02 it was substituted by Assembly companion A10329, which is now the operative vehicle."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Among the listed bills, CT HB05229 is the closest analogue: it also imposes player-facing consumer protection duties on licensees, but through withdrawal-of-funds rules, customer service phone lines and a bar on AI-targeted betting rather than periodic activity statements. IL HB1451 and HB2580 address problem gambling through a Department of Human Services public education program, not operator-delivered disclosures. Most other listed bills (AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, HI HB 1308/HB 2570/SB1569, IL HB2878) concern legalization, licensing fees or tax. Mandatory itemized monthly statements that include time-on-platform and lifetime wagering history are unusual among them.","watch":"Watch the Assembly vehicle A10329 and the Commission's implementing rules, which will drive the real compliance burden: push-notification delivery, time-on-platform tracking, itemized bonus accounting and indefinite retention and display of lifetime wagering history, all on a 15-day monthly deadline.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":4118,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:03:25.458Z"},"NY-S9825":{"summary":"S9825 amends the existing code of ethics in Public Officers Law §74(3)(c), which already bars state agency officers and employees, legislators and legislative employees from disclosing or using confidential information acquired in their official duties to further personal interests. It adds that this prohibited personal use includes using that information to participate in prediction markets and/or mobile sports wagering platforms, and gives a non-exhaustive list of named platforms. It also makes gender-neutral wording changes and takes effect 90 days after enactment.","mechanics":["Amends Public Officers Law §74(3)(c). It covers officers or employees of a state agency, members of the legislature and legislative employees.","Treats using information acquired in the course of official duties to participate in prediction markets and/or mobile sports wagering platforms as a form of furthering personal interests.","Lists eight platforms as 'include, but are not limited to': Polymarket, Kalshi, PredictIt, Iowa Electronic Market, 'Auger' (likely meant to be Augur), Robinhood, FanDuel and DraftKings.","Imposes no direct obligations on operators. The restriction runs only to the public officer. §74(3) is framed as a 'should' standard of conduct, and the bill adds no new penalty provision.","Effective on the 90th day after it becomes law. Referred to Senate Ethics and Internal Governance on 2026-04-07."],"issue_tags":["prediction","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"The closest analogue is CA AB1840, which also uses a government-ethics statute (the Political Reform Act) to address public officials trading prediction market contracts. NY S9825 is narrower: it adds an insider-information clause to an existing ethics standard rather than creating new disclosure or prohibition sections. Unlike CT HB05038 and IL HB5059 (ORACLE Act), which regulate prediction market platforms directly through age-21 limits and consumer protections, S9825 places no duties on platforms. It is also unusual in naming specific licensed sportsbooks (FanDuel, DraftKings) alongside CFTC-regulated event-contract venues, treating both as functionally equivalent for insider-use purposes.","watch":"Watch whether amendments move the obligation from officials to platforms, for example through identity screening, reporting or account-blocking duties for New York public employees, since naming FanDuel and DraftKings in statute could become the hook for such operator-facing compliance requirements.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":1773,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:03:31.018Z"},"OH-HB209":{"summary":"HB209 amends R.C. 718.01, 5747.01, and 5748.01 to exempt tips from Ohio state, municipal, and school district income taxes. In the municipal income tax definitions, it adds \"Tips\" as a new category of exempt income at R.C. 718.01(C)(21), renumbering the existing constitutional/federal-law exemption to (C)(22). It does not change the existing treatment of lottery, sweepstakes, gambling, and sports winnings, which remain municipal \"income\" under 718.01(B)(4).","mechanics":["New R.C. 718.01(C)(21) lists \"Tips\" as exempt income for municipal income tax purposes.","\"Tips\" is not defined in the visible text, so under 718.01's preamble it takes its federal income tax meaning.","Existing 718.01(R)(1)(e) deducts exempt income from \"qualifying wages,\" so tips would also fall out of the municipal wage base used for employer withholding.","Parallel amendments to 5747.01 (state income tax) and 5748.01 (school district income tax) are referenced in the title, but those sections are cut off in the text provided.","No rate change, threshold, cap on the exemption, or effective date appears in the visible text.","Status: introduced and referred to House Ways and Means on 2025-04-02."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills addresses tip income. The closest tax-administration analogues are Iowa SF 605, SSB 1152, and HSB 91, but those concern state withholding on sports-wagering winnings rather than wages or gratuities. The many bills in Alaska, Georgia, Hawaii, Illinois, Kansas, Nebraska, and Mississippi impose or redistribute taxes on operators' wagering revenue, which HB209 does not touch. On its own, HB209 is a state- and local-level version of the federal \"no tax on tips\" concept, and it is notable for reaching Ohio's municipal and school district income taxes as well as the state tax.","watch":"Casino and racino operators that employ tipped dealers and service staff should watch whether the final 5747.01 and 5748.01 language and any definition of \"tips\" clearly cover dealer toke pools, and how withholding and reporting obligations change; they should also confirm the exemption is not later broadened to touch the 718.01(B)(4) treatment of gambling and sports winnings.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":127739,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:03:37.086Z"},"OH-HB116":{"summary":"Sub. H.B. 116, the Ohio Blockchain Basics Act, deals with digital assets (cryptocurrency), not gambling. It bars charter counties, limited home rule townships and municipalities from imposing taxes or fees on digital assets used to pay for goods and services, and it carves capital gains on small digital-asset payments out of grandfathered municipal taxation of intangible income. It also enacts new R.C. 1352.01 to 1352.04 and amends R.C. 1315.01 and 5747.01 to address mining, taxation and regulation of digital assets, but the supplied text is truncated before those sections.","mechanics":["Local tax preemption: amended R.C. 301.30(B) (charter counties), 504.04(B)(9) (limited home rule townships) and 715.013(B)(2) (municipalities) prohibit any fee, tax, assessment or other charge on digital assets used as a method of payment. The ban covers charges based on that use, on the sale, use or consumption of the assets, or on receipts from their sale.","Parity exception: each of these preemption provisions still allows a charge that would apply if the same transaction had been made in U.S. legal tender.","Municipal income tax: R.C. 718.01(C)(2)(b) stops municipalities that were grandfathered in 1988 to tax intangible income from taxing capital gains on the sale of a digital asset used to pay for goods or services. The exclusion applies only where the payment does not exceed the deduction threshold set in R.C. 5747.01(A)(44), which is not reproduced in the excerpt.","Gambling content: the only wagering language is existing, unamended text in R.C. 718.01(B)(4), which treats lottery, sweepstakes, gambling and sports winnings as municipal 'income' and lets professional gamblers deduct wagering losses. Related existing text in 718.01(S) excludes gambling winnings from 'intangible income.'","Definitions: 'digital asset' is defined in new R.C. 1352.01, but that section and R.C. 1352.02 to 1352.04 are not included in the supplied text.","Status: passed the House and referred on 2025-06-25 to the Senate Financial Institutions, Insurance and Technology committee."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills addresses digital assets or blockchain. The gaming tax bills on the list work through different mechanisms. KS SB303 imposes a 2% excise tax on the amount wagered, IL HB3159 adds 2% of sports wagering adjusted gross receipts for fire and EMS, and IA SF 605 changes state withholding on sports wagering winnings. OH HB116 touches gambling only because it amends R.C. 718.01, whose existing treatment of gambling and sports winnings as municipal taxable income it leaves unchanged. What is unusual is its use of local tax preemption, with a legal-tender parity exception, to protect payments made in cryptocurrency.","watch":"Operators should confirm that the R.C. 718.01(B)(4) gambling-winnings provision stays unchanged in the Senate, and should review the not-yet-visible R.C. 1352 definitions and the R.C. 1315.01 money-transmitter amendment for any effect on accepting digital assets for sportsbook deposits or withdrawals.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Engrossed","text_chars":146736,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:03:38.415Z"},"OH-HB298":{"summary":"HB 298 would legalize and regulate internet gambling in Ohio through a new Revised Code Chapter 3771 (sections 3771.01 to 3771.14 and 3771.99), levy a tax on businesses that provide internet gambling (new sections 5753.022 and 5753.032, plus amendments to the casino tax chapter 5753), and prohibit online sweepstakes games. The excerpt provided covers only the amendments to 109.572 (criminal records checks) and part of 2915.01 (gambling definitions). The new chapter's licensing structure, tax rate and sweepstakes prohibition language are not in the text supplied.","mechanics":["New R.C. 109.572(A)(20): applicants under new section 3771.03 must undergo a BCI fingerprint check plus an FBI check for any 'disqualifying offense' as defined in the existing casino statute, 3772.07.","Amended R.C. 2915.01(C): a slot machine authorized under new Chapter 3771 or existing Chapter 3772 is excluded from the criminal definition of 'scheme of chance', carving internet gambling out of the criminal gambling statutes.","Existing 2915.01(C) electronic-device sweepstakes tests are retained, and 'conduct' in 2915.01(P) expressly covers sweepstakes. These include the 50% redemption thresholds and the rule that prize payouts above 20% of gross revenue at a location indicate valuable consideration. The bill's title states it will prohibit online sweepstakes games.","Tax: the bill enacts 5753.022 and 5753.032 and amends 5753.01 through 5753.12 to tax internet gambling providers, with conforming changes to income tax (5747) and commercial activity tax (5751) provisions. The rate and base are not in the excerpt.","Conforming amendments reach the Casino Control Commission statutes (3772.01 to 3772.062), horse racing (3769.08) and child support (3123.90), with penalties in new 3771.99. Their details are not in the excerpt."],"issue_tags":["legalize","tax","licensing","sweeps"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Like IL HB3080 and HB4797 (Internet Gaming Act), OH HB298 creates a standalone internet casino framework rather than a sports wagering regime. Ohio already has legal sports betting under Chapter 3775, so this bill extends online offerings beyond sports. Unlike the Illinois bills, it pairs iGaming legalization with an express ban on online sweepstakes games, a theme otherwise seen in CA AB 831's sweepstakes restrictions and FL H0189's broad internet gambling overhaul. It also ties licensee suitability to the existing casino disqualifying-offense standard in 3772.07, rather than creating a new background-check regime as AK HB145 and SB194 do for mobile sports wagering.","watch":"Obtain the full text of Chapter 3771 and new sections 5753.022 and 5753.032 to see who is eligible for licenses (for example, whether licenses are tethered to existing casinos and racinos), the tax rate and deductions, and how broadly 'online sweepstakes game' is defined, since that definition could reach social casino or promotional products offered by sportsbook operators.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":181648,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:03:42.151Z"},"OH-HB476":{"summary":"Sub. H.B. 476 amends Ohio's Charitable Gaming Law (R.C. Chapter 2915) to authorize charitable organizations to conduct online raffles, selling electronic tickets with unique platform-assigned identifiers and having the platform randomly select winners. It folds online raffle platforms into the bingo-supplies regime, carves them out of the slot-machine and scheme-of-chance definitions, and creates new registration and licensing provisions in enacted sections 2915.17 to 2915.20. The bill carries an emergency clause.","mechanics":["R.C. 2915.01(CC) splits raffles into a 'traditional raffle', which uses physical tickets drawn from a receptacle, and an 'online raffle', which is sold through an 'online raffle platform', defined as an internet website, application or electronic service, with each ticket carrying a unique identifier and the winner chosen by random platform selection.","Online raffle platforms are expressly excluded from the definitions of 'slot machine' (2915.01(QQ)(2)) and of 'electronic device' in the scheme-of-chance definition (2915.01(C)). A person operating a platform is excluded from 'bingo game operator' (2915.01(Q)).","Online raffle platforms become 'bingo supplies' under 2915.01(Z), while physical traditional-raffle tickets are removed from that definition. This appears to bring platform providers under the existing distributor and manufacturer framework in 2915.081 and 2915.082.","New sections 2915.17 and 2915.18 impose registration and license fees collected by the Attorney General. Under R.C. 109.32, those fees go to the charitable law fund for enforcement of Chapter 2915. The fees also count as deductible 'expenses' under 2915.01(GG)(2).","R.C. 2915.07, the illegal-bingo felony, is revised to except activity permitted by renumbered 2915.16 (formerly 2915.092, the raffle section). The prior blanket carve-out for charitable raffles is deleted.","'Net profit from the proceeds of the sale of instant bingo, electronic instant bingo, or online raffles' (2915.01(RR)) now covers online raffles, including the property-tax deduction for veteran's, fraternal and sporting organizations. Details in 2915.19 and 2915.20 are not visible in the truncated text."],"issue_tags":["legalize","licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Most listed bills authorize or regulate sports wagering, such as AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686 and HI HB 1308. By contrast, OH HB476 is a narrow digitization of an existing charitable game. Its closest analogue is IN HB1078, which moves state lottery draw and eInstant games online, though Ohio's bill is limited to charitable raffles regulated by the Attorney General. Its technique of carving a defined device out of the 'slot machine' definition resembles the safe-harbor approach in IL HB3274 and HB5048 for prize and gift kiosks under the Video Gaming Act.","watch":"Watch the not-yet-visible terms of new sections 2915.17 to 2915.20, which are the registration and licensing rules for platforms and raffle conductors, and the precise scope of the slot-machine and scheme-of-chance exclusions for 'online raffle platforms', since they determine whether gaming technology vendors can supply charitable online raffles and whether the carve-out could be stretched to cover other app-based prize products.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Engrossed","text_chars":150164,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:03:58.977Z"},"OH-HB344":{"summary":"HB 344 would let electronic instant bingo be offered in more locations and in more ways, and would create a new category of retailer video lottery terminals under new R.C. 3770.31 to 3770.41. It would also levy a new tax on electronic instant bingo through new R.C. 5753.022 and 5753.032. Retailer VLT operators would be brought into the municipal income tax withholding regime that already applies to casinos, racino video lottery sales agents and type B sports gaming proprietors.","mechanics":["R.C. 718.031 adds retailer video lottery terminal operators as mandatory municipal income tax withholders. They must withhold on prize awards that are IRS-reportable under IRC 6041, including prizes from lottery sports gaming (as defined in R.C. 3770.23) offered at a retailer video lottery terminal.","Withholding rules for retailer VLT operators: monthly electronic returns are due by the 10th, annual returns and player information returns by January 31, and the operator is personally liable for amounts withheld but not remitted. A successor buyer must hold back purchase money until a tax clearance receipt or certificate is produced.","Penalties under 718.031(H): 50% of tax withheld for late remittance or non-remittance, and $500 per monthly or annual return filed late or not filed, plus interest at the R.C. 5703.47 rate.","R.C. 2915.01 is amended to accommodate expanded electronic instant bingo. An electronic instant bingo system is excluded from the scheme-of-chance 'electronic device' definition and from the slot machine definition. A new 'ideal gross profit of a deal' definition is added, apparently as a measure for the new tax. 'Bingo game operator' now excludes people servicing e-bingo systems and an 'instant bingo host's' staff whose duties are nongaming only.","The tax rate, base and effective date for the new electronic instant bingo tax (5753.022 and 5753.032), and the licensing terms for retailer VLTs (3770.31 to 3770.41), are not in the excerpt provided and need review in the full text."],"issue_tags":["tax","licensing","legalize"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"The closest analogue on the list is IL HB3276, which creates a lottery-run sports wagering pilot using sports lottery terminals. HB 344 similarly pushes lottery-controlled terminals, potentially including lottery sports gaming, into retail locations, but it pairs this with expanded charitable electronic instant bingo. Its withholding provisions parallel IA SF 605, HSB 91 and SSB 1152 on withholding from sports wagering winnings, but HB 344 operates at the municipal income tax level. Unlike the Illinois video gaming kiosk bills (HB3274, HB3473, HB5048, HB5139), which carve out devices from Gaming Board discipline, HB 344 legalizes and taxes a new terminal class outright.","watch":"Watch whether the full text of R.C. 3770.31 to 3770.41 lets retailer VLTs offer lottery sports gaming in bars and other retail sites, which could compete with or dilute Type C kiosk and mobile sports gaming markets, and check the e-bingo tax rate against Ohio's sports gaming tax.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":179658,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:04:00.040Z"},"OH-HB617":{"summary":"HB 617 would exempt capital gains from both Ohio state income tax and municipal income tax by amending R.C. 718.01 and 5747.01 and repealing R.C. 5747.79. It is a general income-tax bill. Its only connection to gaming is that the restated municipal definitions keep existing law, which taxes gambling and sports winnings as income and excludes them from tax-exempt 'intangible income'.","mechanics":["R.C. 718.01(A)(1)(b)(i) and (c) define municipal taxable income for resident and nonresident individuals as income reduced by exempt income 'and any net capital gain that is not exempt income to the extent otherwise included in income'. This is the apparent new municipal capital-gains carve-out.","Under R.C. 718.01(C)(2) and (S), capital gains remain within exempt 'intangible income' for municipal purposes. Division (S) still provides that intangible income does not include lottery, gambling, or similar game-of-chance winnings.","R.C. 718.01(B)(4) is unchanged. It continues to treat lottery, sweepstakes, gambling and sports winnings as municipal 'income', and professional gamblers may deduct wagering losses to the extent the Internal Revenue Code allows.","R.C. 718.01(X) is unchanged. It continues to list casino operators and video lottery terminal sales agents as 'other payers' for withholding purposes.","The bill repeals R.C. 5747.79 and amends R.C. 5747.01 to remove capital gains from state taxation. The supplied text is truncated before the 5747.01 amendments, so the state-level mechanics and any effective date cannot be confirmed.","Status: introduced and referred to House Ways and Means on 2026-02-04."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"Unlike IA SF 605, IA SSB 1152 and IA HSB 91, which change state income tax withholding specifically on sports-wagering winnings, HB 617 leaves the tax treatment of winnings alone and targets only capital gains. It also imposes no wagering excise or handle tax like KS SB303's 2% tax on amounts wagered, and it does not alter operator tax rates as IL HB3159 and IL HB5143 would. Its closest analogues are general tax vehicles such as MN SF2374 and KY HB 757, but even those are broader revenue packages, whereas this bill is a single-purpose capital-gains repeal.","watch":"Confirm in the full text of the R.C. 5747.01 amendments that the new capital-gains exclusion cannot be read to reach gambling or sports-wagering winnings, and that it does not disturb the 718.01(B)(4) and (S) treatment of winnings or casino-operator withholding obligations, particularly if the bill becomes a vehicle for broader tax amendments in committee.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":126305,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:04:00.290Z"},"OH-HB642":{"summary":"HB 642 amends Ohio's uniform municipal income tax law (R.C. 718.01, 718.81 and 718.84) to remove the five-year cap on carrying forward net operating losses, so post-2017 NOLs can be deducted against municipal net profit in later years until fully used. It is a general business tax measure, not a gaming bill; wagering appears only in existing, apparently unchanged definitions in R.C. 718.01.","mechanics":["R.C. 718.01(D)(3)(a): NOLs incurred in taxable years beginning on or after January 1, 2017 may be deducted to reduce municipal taxable income to zero, with the unused portion carried to the next succeeding taxable year or years until fully used, replacing the 'not more than five consecutive taxable years' limit.","R.C. 718.01(B)(1)(b)(i): a resident's own NOL and distributive share of pass-through NOLs is likewise carried forward until fully used rather than for 'the following five taxable years.' Under (B)(1)(d), cumulative deductions still cannot exceed the original loss.","Existing limits stay in place: NOLs cannot offset qualifying wages under (D)(3)(b), and the 2018–2022 50% limitation and its carryover rules in (D)(3)(c) and (e) remain.","The redrafted text appears to drop the separate 'pre-2017 net operating loss carryforward' definition in former division (SS) and its references. This cannot be confirmed because the supplied text shows no strike-through formatting and is truncated before the 718.81 and 718.84 amendments.","Existing gaming references appear unchanged in the supplied text: gambling and sports winnings are taxable 'income' under (B)(4), with professional gamblers allowed to deduct wagering losses, and casino operators and video lottery terminal sales agents are 'other payers' under (X).","No effective date or applicable-tax-year provision appears in the supplied text. The bill was referred to House Ways and Means on 2026-02-04."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills is comparable. The gaming tax bills (IL HB3159's 2% AGR levy, IL HB5143 ending the per-wager tax, KS SB303's 2% handle excise, the AK HB145/SB194 and HI HB1308/HB2570/SB1569 authorization-and-tax bills) target wagering revenue directly. IA SF 605 and its companion bills deal with withholding on winnings. HB 642 instead changes a generally applicable municipal net profit tax base, closer in kind to IL HB5318's combined-reporting change or KY HB757's revenue package. Its relevance to gaming comes only from operators being municipal net profit taxpayers.","watch":"Ohio casino, racino and sportsbook entities with accumulated losses filing municipal net profit returns should check whether the unlimited carryforward applies to losses already older than five years, and how the redrafted pre-2017 carryforward language and the truncated 718.81/718.84 changes affect them, since no applicability date appears in the supplied text.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":65720,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:04:05.727Z"},"OH-SB197":{"summary":"Ohio SB 197 is an omnibus gaming bill that would legalize internet gambling and tax the businesses that offer it, authorize internet lottery gaming and online wagering on horse racing, and restructure other parts of Ohio's Gambling Law, including moving charitable gaming from R.C. Chapter 2915 into a new Chapter 3768. It also amends the existing sports gaming chapter (3775) and the casino control chapter (3772) and makes an appropriation. The supplied text is truncated after the early conforming sections, so the operative internet gambling provisions, including the tax rate, are not visible.","mechanics":["Enacts a new R.C. Chapter 3771 (3771.01–3771.13 and penalty section 3771.99), adds many new 3772 sections (3772.311–3772.317, 3772.38–3772.41), and adds new tax sections 5753.022 and 5753.032 in the casino-tax chapter, apparently to carry the internet gambling licensing and tax framework; the rate and license fees are not in the excerpt provided.","Authorizes internet lottery gaming through new R.C. 3770.211–3770.214 and online horse-race wagering through amendments to Chapter 3769, including new 3769.01, 3769.081, 3769.11 and 3769.99.","Amends R.C. 109.572 by adding division (A)(19), which requires fingerprint-based BCI and FBI criminal records checks for applicants under R.C. 3771.03 or 3775.03, screened against the 'disqualifying offense' definition in R.C. 3772.07; 3771.03 and 3772.132 are also added to the list of requests that may access sealed records.","Amends multiple sports gaming sections (3775.01, .02, .03, .06, .09, .10, .11, .13, .99) and repeals R.C. 3775.16, 3772.25, 3770.23, 3769.02, 3769.021 and 5747.064 (the last relating to income tax).","Recodifies charitable gaming from 2915.08–2915.15 into new Chapter 3768 and creates a charitable gaming fund (3768.23) whose moneys may be transferred to the attorney general's charitable law fund (amended 109.32); moves 2915.06/2915.061 into new Chapter 3777.","Status: introduced and referred to the Senate Select Committee on Gaming on 2025-05-14; no effective dates are visible in the excerpt."],"issue_tags":["legalize","tax","licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike the sports-wagering legalization bills in AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686 and HI HB 1308/SB1569, Ohio already has legal sports gaming under Chapter 3775, so SB 197 is an expansion into iGaming, iLottery and online pari-mutuel wagering. It is closest to IL HB3080 and HB4797, the Illinois Internet Gaming Act bills, but it goes further by packaging iGaming with iLottery, horse racing and a charitable gaming recodification. In that respect it resembles the breadth of FL H0189's omnibus rewrite.","watch":"Watch the Chapter 3771 and 5753.022/5753.032 provisions for the internet gambling tax rate, for whether iGaming licenses are tethered to existing casino or sports gaming licensees, and for how the amendments to Chapter 3775 and the repeal of R.C. 3775.16 change current sports gaming licensees' obligations.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":179344,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:04:20.135Z"},"OK-HB1101":{"summary":"HB1101 is a legislative referendum that, if approved by voters at the next General Election, would offer Oklahoma's compacted tribes a model Gaming Compact Supplement adding sports betting as a covered game under their existing state-tribal compacts, in exchange for a 10% exclusivity fee on adjusted sports-betting revenue. Racetrack organization licensees are expressly excluded, so the market would be tribal-only. Under Section 1, the referendum is not sent to voters if Enrolled HB1047 (60th Legislature, 1st Session) becomes law.","mechanics":["Section 4(A) defines \"sports betting\" broadly as any wagering on the outcome of sporting events \"or other events,\" other than horse or animal races. Operation is conditioned on federal law permitting it, and each supplement takes effect only on Interior Secretary approval and Federal Register publication, with no further state action required (Part 3).","Part 2(B) sets the fee at 10% of monthly \"adjusted transaction total.\" That base is gross handle less winnings paid, voided wagers, free play and promotional credits, \"generally accepted associated operating costs,\" and federal excise tax. The fee is due by the 20th of the following month to the State Treasurer and is deemed an exclusivity payment under Part 11(A)(2) of the compact.","Section 4(E) also lets the tribe \"keep an amount equal to state payments\" from sports betting, again deemed an exclusivity payment. The text does not explain how this works.","Section 3 routes sports-betting supplement fees through the existing 3A O.S. §280 split: 12% to the General Revenue Fund and 88% to the Education Reform Revolving Fund. A monthly carve-out goes first to the Department of Mental Health and Substance Abuse Services for problem gambling. The amended text shows that carve-out at $20,833.33, but the Section 5 ballot title says it rises to $25,000.","Section 2 amends §262(H) and Section 4(F) bars racetrack organization licensees from any new gaming and bars any additional electronic or machine gaming. Section 4(G) carves sports betting out of the criminal gambling provisions in 21 O.S. §§941-988 once a supplement is effective."],"issue_tags":["legalize","tax","prop"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. On its own, the bill is unusual in several ways. It uses a self-executing offer of a compact supplement rather than a licensing regime, and it gives commercial operators and racetracks no role at all. Its fee base allows tribes to deduct \"operating costs\" as well as promotional credits, which is far more generous than typical GGR-based taxes. It is also conditioned on the companion bill HB1047 failing to become law.","watch":"Watch three things: the fate of HB1047, which would moot this referendum; the absence of any commercial or mobile licensing pathway, meaning operators could participate only as tribal vendors or partners; and the drafting defects (the unexplained Section 4(E) \"keep an amount equal to state payments\" clause, the unused \"net win\" definition, and the $20,833.33 versus $25,000 mismatch with the ballot title), which could draw legal challenges or be fixed by amendment.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":24170,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:04:20.210Z"},"OK-HB1047":{"summary":"HB1047 would offer Oklahoma's compacting tribes a model 'Gaming Compact Sports Betting Supplement' that adds sports betting as a covered game under their existing State-Tribal Gaming Compacts, in exchange for a 10% fee on a net revenue measure. The supplement is deemed approved by the state once executed and approved by the Interior Secretary, and it expressly bars racetrack organization licensees from offering sports betting or any new machine gaming. The bill passed one chamber, but its last action on 2026-04-28 was 'Motion expired.'","mechanics":["Section 3 (new 3A O.S. 280.2) defines 'sports betting' as any wagering on the outcome of sporting events or other events, other than horse or other animal races. The offer is conditioned on federal law permitting it, and the tribe certifies it will not offer sports betting until that is legal under federal law.","The fee is 10% of the monthly 'adjusted transaction total', due by the 20th of the following month and paid to the State Treasurer. That total is gross sports betting transactions less winnings, voided wagers, free play and promotional credits, 'generally accepted associated operating costs,' and federal excise taxes. The payment is deemed an exclusivity and fee payment under Part 11(A)(2) of the compact.","Section 2 routes supplement fees through the existing split: 12% to the General Revenue Fund and 88% to the Education Reform Revolving Fund, after the first $20,833.33 each month goes to the Department of Mental Health and Substance Abuse Services for compulsive gambling treatment and education.","Section 1 amends 3A O.S. 262(H) to keep the ban on sports wagering by racetrack organization licensees, carving out only Section 3. Section 3(F) states the offer does not authorize any additional gaming by organization licensees or any additional electronic or machine gaming.","No state licensing or regulatory regime is created. Sports betting is governed by the existing compact's enforcement and exclusivity terms, and the supplement takes effect on Interior approval and Federal Register publication without further state action. A Section 21 O.S. 941-988 criminal carve-out applies. The stated effective date is November 1, 2025."],"issue_tags":["legalize","tax"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike the commercial frameworks in AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686 and HI HB 1308/HB 2570/SB1569, HB1047 creates no state regulator, operator or supplier licensing, or commercial tax. It works entirely through a tribal compact supplement with a 10% revenue-share fee, and it excludes racetracks and any non-tribal operators. The Illinois bills (e.g., HB2878/HB5034's $15 million master license fee) adjust an existing commercial licensing market, which is a different posture altogether. The fee base is unusually generous to operators because it deducts promotional credits and 'generally accepted associated operating costs.' The bill is also silent on mobile or online wagering, so off-reservation online betting would depend on federal law under the 'federal laws permit' contingency.","watch":"Watch whether the bill is revived given the expired motion, how the 'federal laws permit' contingency and the silence on mobile wagering are read to limit tribal online or off-reservation betting, and whether commercial operators can participate only as tribal vendors given the express exclusion of racetracks and the exclusivity framing.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":21840,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:04:20.316Z"},"OH-HB96":{"summary":"Ohio HB 96 is the FY 2026-27 biennial operating budget, signed with appropriations effective June 30, 2025 and most other provisions effective September 30, 2025. It amends hundreds of Revised Code sections across state government, and only a small subset touches gambling. The excerpt supplied ends early in Section 101.01, so none of the gaming-related amended text actually appears in it.","mechanics":["Effective dates: operating appropriations took effect June 30, 2025; other provisions are generally effective September 30, 2025, with some special effective dates.","The amendment list includes sports gaming law section 3775.16, Casino Control Commission section 3772.02, and casino tax sections 5753.031 and 5753.07; the substance of these changes is not shown in the excerpt.","Section 2915.01, the gambling definitions section in the criminal code, is listed as amended; the change is not visible but could matter for skill-game or sweepstakes treatment.","Lottery provisions 3770.071-3770.073, 3770.10, 3770.12, 3770.121, 3770.13 and 3770.25 are amended, new sections 3770.074 and 3770.075 are enacted, and horse racing section 3769.088 is amended.","The bill also levies and changes numerous non-gaming taxes (income, sales, commercial activity, cigarette and tobacco) and enacts unrelated policy provisions such as the sex-definitions section 9.05."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"Unlike the standalone legalization bills (AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, HI HB 1308/SB1569), HB 96 assumes Ohio's existing sports gaming framework under Chapter 3775 and only adjusts it inside an omnibus budget. Illinois bills on the list target specific operator economics: IL HB3159 adds a 2% AGR earmark, IL HB2878/HB5034 raise license fees to $15 million, and IL HB5142 brings prediction markets within the definition of sports wagering. Nothing in the visible HB 96 text matches those changes; its gaming touches appear limited to fund, tax-administration and definitional sections. Any increase to Ohio's sports gaming tax rate would sit in section 5753.021, which is not on the amendment list.","watch":"Pull the enrolled text of sections 3775.16, 5753.031, 5753.07, 3772.02 and 2915.01 to check whether the budget redirects sports gaming or casino tax proceeds, changes Commission duties, or alters gambling definitions in ways that affect sweepstakes, skill games or competing products.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":330955,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:04:22.078Z"},"PA-HR60":{"summary":"HR60 is a simple House resolution, not a statute, directing Pennsylvania's Joint State Government Commission to study sports betting and related interactive (online) gambling and to recommend further regulation aimed at reducing problem gambling, gambling debt and children's exposure to gambling advertising. It creates no binding obligations on licensees but sets up a formal record that could support future legislation or Gaming Control Board action. It was adopted by the House 189-14 on May 14, 2025; despite the listed status, a House resolution is not signed into law.","mechanics":["Study scope (amended resolved clauses (1)-(4)): licensee marketing strategies including promotions, in-game advertising, ads during sports broadcasts and webcasts, and use of celebrities and animated spokespersons; loan, bonus, incentive and VIP programs offered in Pennsylvania; recommendations, 'if needed,' to further regulate in order to reduce problem gambling and gambling debt; and possible employee-training protocols addressing major losses and gambling addiction.","The Commission must also collect information on how sports betting affects the mental health and well-being of Pennsylvania college student athletes, including harassment, coercion or retaliation by bettors.","The Commission must consult qualified legal counsel so that its recommendations do not conflict with the U.S. or Pennsylvania Constitutions, expressly including First Amendment free speech, which signals awareness of commercial-speech limits on ad restrictions.","A report of findings and recommendations is due to the General Assembly within 12 months of adoption, roughly by May 14, 2026.","The recitals cite PGCB data that, as of January 2025, 95% of Pennsylvania sports wagers and 60% of slot wagers were placed online, and that more than $300 million was spent on sports-betting TV advertising in 2022."],"issue_tags":["advertising","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike CT HB05229, which would directly restrict gaming advertising on college campuses, impose withdrawal requirements and bar AI-driven bet targeting, PA HR60 only commissions a study and imposes no operator obligations. It also does not reach prediction markets, unlike CT HB05038 and IL HB5059 (ORACLE Act), which bar under-21 use of and advertising by prediction market platforms. Its problem-gambling focus is closer to IL HB1451 and HB2580, which expand DHS public education on gambling disorders, but HR60 aims at industry practices such as VIP programs, bonuses and celebrity endorsements rather than public awareness.","watch":"Watch the Joint State Government Commission report due around May 2026, especially any recommendations to restrict VIP, bonus and loan-type programs, celebrity or animated-spokesperson advertising and in-broadcast marketing, or to mandate employee training, because these are likely templates for follow-on Pennsylvania legislation or PGCB rulemaking.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":7895,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:04:36.537Z"},"RI-S3118":{"summary":"S3118 Sub A moves Rhode Island's state-run sports betting from a single vendor to a multi-vendor market. The Lottery Division must solicit and award contracts until 4 to 6 sports-wagering vendors are operating, beginning no later than January 1, 2027. The bill also rewrites the revenue split: the state keeps 51% until it recoups its FY2025 take and then drops to 12%. Vendors get more operational control over marketing and promotions under a Division-approved written marketing plan.","mechanics":["Revenue split (§42-61.2-5(a)): the state takes 51% and vendors 40.5% until the state has received the amount sports wagering generated in FY2025. After that, the state gets 12% and vendors 79.5%. Host facilities drop from 17% to 8.5%, with a $4.5 million floor. Lincoln and Tiverton each keep their $200,000 annual host fee.","Deficit guarantee (§42-61.2-5(a)(1)(i)): if combined online sports betting revenue to the state in any fiscal year falls below FY2025 online revenue, each vendor must make an equal additional payment to close the gap.","Market opening (§42-61.2-3.3(c)): by January 1, 2027 the Division must issue an open invitation and award contracts until 4 to 6 vendors are operating. The RFP is to prioritize: integrity and responsible-gaming capability, compliance history, out-of-state operating history, and a commitment to maximizing revenue. Rules are due within 90 days, and vendors bear their own server costs (§3.3(d)).","Marketing deduction (§42-61.2-1(28)): for vendor contracts entered on or after the effective date, online sports-wagering revenue is calculated without a marketing-expense deduction. Pre-existing contracts, including renewals and successor agreements, keep the deduction.","Marketing control (§42-61.2-1(56); §2.4(e),(g),(h)): adds a high-level annual 'written marketing plan' that cannot require campaign-level detail or spend commitments. Approval may be withheld only if the plan violates the chapter, regulations, or responsible-gaming standards. Vendors may make marketing, advertising and promotion decisions, use player data for approved marketing, and honor promotions regardless of the jurisdiction where they were awarded.","Other: the Division may not make host facilities pay expenses of hosting vendors (§2.4(f)). Settlement rules now cover 'the process by which wagers are settled.' The act takes effect upon passage."],"issue_tags":["tax","licensing","advertising"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"No other states' bills are listed. Rhode Island is a state-operated lottery model, not a licensing regime, so this bill reshapes the market through contract counts and a negotiated revenue split rather than a tax rate. Several features are unusual. The split is tiered on a FY2025 revenue hurdle, after which the state's share falls sharply from 51% to 12%. Vendors must jointly backstop any shortfall against FY2025 online revenue in equal shares. And the marketing-expense deduction is grandfathered for the incumbent's contract but denied to new entrants.","watch":"Watch three points in House Finance: the equal-share deficit make-up obligation (it is unrelated to each vendor's market share, and its drafting is ambiguous, comparing 'revenue to the state' with 'total' FY2025 revenue); how the FY2025 hurdle is measured; and the grandfathered marketing deduction that favors the incumbent over new vendors.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Comm Sub","text_chars":44769,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:04:39.228Z"},"RI-H8186":{"summary":"H8186 would move Rhode Island's state-run sports wagering from a single vendor to a multi-vendor model, requiring the Lottery Division to award four to six sports-wagering vendor contracts through an open invitation by January 1, 2027. It rewrites the revenue split in § 42-61.2-5 so the state takes 51% only until it recoups its FY2025 sports-wagering revenue and 12% after that, with vendors rising to 79.5% and host facilities cut from 17% to 8.5%. It also gives vendors operational control over marketing, advertising and promotions, and changes how online sports-wagering revenue is calculated for new contracts.","mechanics":["§ 42-61.2-5(a): state share is 51% until it has received the amount generated in FY2025, then 12%. Vendors get 40.5% (up from 32%), then 79.5% once the threshold is met. Host facilities (Lincoln and Tiverton) drop from 17% to 8.5%, with an unclear proviso tied to the hosts having received $4,500,000. The Lincoln and Tiverton $200,000 annual flat fees are retained.","§ 42-61.2-3.3(c): by January 1, 2027 the Division must issue an open invitation and award four to six vendor contracts. The RFP prioritizes platform integrity and responsible gaming, regulatory compliance history, operating history in other states, and revenue maximization. Implementing rules are due within 90 days of the effective date.","§ 42-61.2-1(28): for vendor contracts entered on or after the effective date, online sports-wagering revenue excludes the marketing-expense deduction, so only payouts and federal excise tax are deducted. Pre-existing contracts, including renewals and successor agreements, keep the marketing deduction.","§ 42-61.2-2.4(f)-(g): vendors may make operational decisions on marketing, advertising and promotions, subject to Division rules. Vendors may accept wagers using promotions, bonuses or cash equivalents regardless of the jurisdiction where they were first awarded.","§ 42-61.2-2.4(e): player data remains Division property, but marketing-use rights shift from the hosting facilities to the sports-wagering vendor.","Effective upon passage. On 2026-04-29 the House Finance Committee recommended the bill be held for further study."],"issue_tags":["tax","licensing","advertising"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. The bill is unusual because it would dismantle Rhode Island's state-operated, single-vendor model and replace it with a competitive four-to-six vendor market while keeping the state-as-operator, revenue-share structure rather than adopting a license-and-tax regime. The split is also unusual: the state's share collapses to 12% once FY2025 revenue is recouped, which pushes economics heavily toward vendors.","watch":"Watch the drafting ambiguities: the host facilities' $4.5 million proviso, whether the FY2025 threshold resets each fiscal year, and the stray reference to a 'commission' in § 42-61.2-3.3(c). Also watch the two-tier revenue definition, which denies new vendors the marketing deduction that incumbent contracts and their renewals keep.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":42870,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:04:41.924Z"},"SC-H3625":{"summary":"H3625 would legalize mobile and online-only sports wagering in South Carolina by adding Chapter 55 to Title 39 and creating a nine-member South Carolina Sports Wagering Commission to license and regulate it. It caps operators at eight, imposes a 12.5% privilege tax on adjusted gross sports wagering receipts, and sets bettor-eligibility, KYC, geolocation and self-exclusion rules. The title also references a new Section 16-19-135 exempting lawful bettors from gambling prohibitions, but the text provided ends at Section 39-55-270.","mechanics":["Tax (39-55-140): 12.5% privilege tax on adjusted gross sports wagering receipts. Promotional credits, free bets, federal excise tax, voids and uncollectibles (capped at 2%) are deductible, and negative months carry forward. Revenue splits 82% general fund, 10% per-capita local infrastructure, 5% Department of Mental Health problem gambling, 3% commission. No other state or local tax applies.","Operator licensing (39-55-150): no more than eight operators, each paying a $100,000 nonrefundable application fee and a $1,000,000 license fee (refundable if denied). Licenses run five years and renew at the same fees. The commission must decide within 60 days.","Operator qualifications: applicants need proof of active operation in at least five states, plus character and financial suitability shown by clear and convincing evidence. Each must name a South Carolina 'resident representative' entity owned by at least two state residents with public-service records. Applicants licensed in five jurisdictions can get a temporary license on paying fees.","Suppliers (39-55-160): license of $20,000 plus processing costs, valid five years, with temporary licenses available. 'Supplier' is defined narrowly as geolocation and KYC providers.","Wagering rules: bettors must be 18 or older and physically in the state. Credit and youth-sports wagers are barred, collegiate events are permitted, and official league data is not required (39-55-200). Bettors get one account each, SSN-based KYC, and in-state servers with cloud computing allowed (39-55-260(H)). If a bettor intentionally avoids identification, the operator must retain winnings and refund deposits.","Integrity and responsible gaming: there is an extensive list of prohibited bettors (39-55-190), with misdemeanor fines of $1,000 to $10,000. Self-exclusion periods are 2 years, 5 years or lifetime, with no early removal (39-55-220). Disputes go to a 48-hour protest, a 7-business-day operator decision, and appeal to the commission."],"issue_tags":["legalize","tax","licensing","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Like AK HB145/SB194 and HI HB 1308/SB1569, H3625 is a mobile-only legalization bill with operator and supplier licensing, but it adds an eight-license cap and an unusual mandatory South Carolina 'resident representative' entity. Its $1 million five-year license fee is modest next to the $15 million master license fee proposed in IL HB2878/HB5034. Its bar on additional state or local taxation (39-55-140(E)) parallels IL HB4171's preemption of home-rule taxation. Unlike GA, where HR450/SR131 seek a constitutional amendment alongside the HB 686 statute, the SC bill proceeds by statute alone.","watch":"Watch the eight-license cap and five-state experience requirement, the politically connected 'resident representative' mandate and in-state server requirement, and whether the 12.5% rate and the free-bet deduction in the AGR definition survive amendment.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":65196,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:04:42.780Z"},"SC-H5281":{"summary":"H5281 creates a new South Carolina retail alcoholic beverage caterer license (new Article 15, Chapter 6, Title 61), issued by the Department of Revenue, letting for-profit caterers with a restaurant or commercial kitchen serve beer, wine and liquor with prepared meals at private off-site events. It also authorizes 24-hour special event liquor permits, allows event-host contracts at licensed premises, raises the number of permittee-sponsored free-drink functions under Section 61-4-160 from two to twelve per year, and repeals Section 61-6-2000 on nonprofit temporary permits. The bill contains no gambling, wagering or gaming provisions.","mechanics":["Section 61-6-4800 defines a caterer as a for-profit business with a restaurant or permitted commercial kitchen, primarily engaged in preparing and serving meals. A private event is an off-site, non-public, temporary event, and food trucks, residential kitchens and shared kitchens are excluded.","Section 61-6-4810(J) sets a $300 nonrefundable filing fee and a $2,300 biennial license fee. Liquor sales are barred from 2 a.m. to 10 a.m., Sunday service requires a favorable local referendum, and SLED must get 24 hours' notice before each event.","Liquor liability insurance is required for events after 5 p.m. (Section 61-6-4810(G)-(I)). Inclusive per-guest bar packages may be taxed on actual consumption or a per-guest drink-allocation method, with three-year record retention (Section 61-6-4810(L)).","Section 61-6-4820 creates special event liquor permits. These cost $35 per event or up to 15 days at $50 per day, require a SLED criminal records check within 90 days of application, and allow up to 25 permits per application in a 12-month period.","Section 61-4-160 is amended to raise permittee-sponsored functions from two to twelve per year. The act takes effect on the Governor's approval."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills is comparable. Alaska HB145/SB194, Georgia HB 686, Hawaii HB 1308 and the many Illinois Sports Wagering Act amendments all address authorization, taxation or regulation of sports wagering, while H5281 is purely an alcohol beverage licensing bill. It does not mention wagering at all and was likely surfaced by keyword overlap on licensing.","watch":"There is nothing in H5281 that directly affects sports-betting operators, so monitoring is warranted only if a future amendment attaches gaming language to this Title 61 vehicle while it sits in the Judiciary Committee.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":17146,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:04:54.081Z"},"SC-S1001":{"summary":"S. 1001 is a South Carolina alcoholic-beverage bill. It creates a biennial retail alcoholic beverage caterer license (new Sections 61-2-400 to 61-2-420) that lets bona fide caterers serve beer, wine and liquor at off-site private events and lets event hosts charge entry fees at licensed premises. It also loosens free-drink and special-event permit rules in Sections 61-4-160, 61-6-2000 and 61-4-550, and does not address sports betting, wagering or gaming in any way.","mechanics":["Section 61-2-410(J): the caterer license carries a $300 nonrefundable filing fee plus $2,300 biennially. Licensees may buy beer and wine from wholesalers and liquor from retail liquor stores holding a federal wholesaler's permit (no 1.75L bottles). Wholesalers may credit unused, unopened stock if it is not held at the caterer's premises more than 72 hours after the event.","Service limits: no liquor between 2 a.m. and 10 a.m. Sunday service is allowed only in counties or municipalities with a favorable Sunday referendum under Section 61-6-2010. Liquor liability insurance is required for events after 5 p.m. The caterer must give the division 24 hours' notice of each event. Off-premises retail sales are barred.","Section 61-2-410(L): caterers may remit liquor-by-the-drink excise tax and sales tax on fixed per-guest inclusive bar packages, using actual consumption or a per-guest drink-allocation method. A drink is defined as 12 oz beer, 5 oz wine or 1.5 oz spirits, and records must be kept for 3 years.","Section 61-2-420: caterer licensees and business liquor-by-the-drink licensees may contract with event hosts, who may charge entry fees for charitable purposes and costs without a special event permit.","Section 61-4-160: the number of sponsored free-beverage functions rises from two to twelve per year. Section 61-6-2000 extends special event liquor permits to caterers and food establishments as well as nonprofits, adds 15-day permits at $50 per day, and requires 24-hour notice.","Section 8: the act takes effect on the Governor's approval."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills is comparable. Bills such as AK HB145, GA HB 686, HI HB 1308, IL HB5059 and IL HB5142 deal with sports wagering, prediction markets or gaming regulation, while S. 1001 is purely an alcohol licensing and tax bill. Despite the 'licensing' overlap in subject matter, it contains no wagering, gaming or betting provisions at all. It is most likely in this set only by keyword or topic-matching error.","watch":"There is nothing sports-betting-specific to watch; the only tangential point is for operators running hospitality or watch-party events in South Carolina, who should note the Section 61-2-420 entry-fee and event-host framework and the Section 61-4-160 expansion of sponsored free-drink functions.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Comm Sub","text_chars":23425,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:04:56.882Z"},"SC-H4176":{"summary":"H. 4176, the \"I-95 Economic and Education Stimulus Act,\" would add Chapter 36 to Title 1 to create a South Carolina Gaming Commission and authorize a single, site-specific casino license in an I-95 county classified as Tier IV, issued only at the request of that county's council under a development agreement. Casino gaming is defined as all Class III gaming under 25 C.F.R. 502.4 except lottery, and the licensee would pay a 15% monthly privilege tax on adjusted gross gaming revenue. The April 30, 2025 Ways and Means committee amendment would expand the commission from three to five members, delete the \"host county\" definition, and send 97% of the tax to the general fund and 3% split equally among eligible counties, with an appropriated portion going to compulsive gambling programs; the bill was recommitted to Ways and Means on 2026-01-28.","mechanics":["Eligibility and exclusivity (1-36-10(9), 1-36-60(A)): the county must contain part of I-95, be a Tier IV county under 12-6-3360(B) per DOR Information Letter #23-18, and pass a requesting ordinance. After the initial license issues, no other license may issue for 10 years. The initial term is 15 years, renewable in 5-year increments.","Fees (1-36-60(C), (K), (L)): $500,000 nonrefundable application fee and $2,000,000 license fee (refunded if denied). An annual fee equal to the application fee is due each year of operation, and the same fees apply on renewal. The commission must approve or deny within 30 days of receipt.","Applicant requirements (1-36-60(D)): at least 10 years of casino gaming experience and at least 2 years of ownership or control of the site before the development agreement. Also required: an economic impact study assuming at least $200M in real property and infrastructure investment, national background checks on each 5%+ owner, no elected office in the prior 5 years, and no felony or illegal-gambling convictions.","Tax (1-36-70): 15% privilege tax on adjusted GGR, filed monthly by the 15th, with negative GGR carried forward. GGR is exempt from other state or local taxes, including local business license taxes, except state income tax. Ancillary sales remain subject to sales and use tax. The committee amendment splits proceeds 97% general fund and 3% to eligible counties.","Commission (1-36-30 as amended): five members appointed by the Governor, Senate President, Senate Finance Chair, Speaker, and Ways and Means Chair, with staggered initial terms of 2 to 4 years and a quorum of three. Members may not hold a direct pecuniary interest in the sports wagering or gaming industry. The commission must be self-funded from fees, with no general fund money (1-36-90), and is subject to a Legislative Audit Council performance audit after year one and every three years after that.","Integrity and participation (1-36-60(H)): suitability licenses are required for 5%+ owners, audit and compliance committee members, and persons with significant influence. Players must be 21 or older. Commission staff, licensee and vendor principals, and employees directly involved in gaming are barred from playing. Application materials are confidential and FOIA-exempt. The act takes effect on the Governor's approval."],"issue_tags":["legalize","licensing","tax","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike the sports-wagering authorization bills listed, such as AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, and HI HB 1308/HB 2570/SB1569, which build multi-operator mobile wagering regimes, SC H4176 authorizes a single land-based casino and never expressly addresses mobile or online wagering. It is closer to FL H0189 and CO SB 163 in that it deals with gaming-commission structure. However, it builds a regulator from scratch around one license tied to a county ordinance and development agreement, rather than reallocating powers within an existing agency. Its 10-year exclusivity window and its 10-year experience and 2-year site-control requirements are unusually restrictive gatekeeping features.","watch":"Because 25 C.F.R. 502.4(c) treats sports betting as Class III gaming, the bill's casino-gaming definition could let the sole licensee offer retail sports wagering for at least 10 years under a 15% GGR tax, so watch for amendments that clarify, exclude, or extend this to mobile wagering, and for how the commission defines \"adjusted\" GGR.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Comm Sub","text_chars":36441,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:04:57.830Z"},"SC-S344":{"summary":"S. 344 creates a South Carolina Equine Commission, chaired by the Director of Revenue, that licenses advance deposit wagering (ADW) operators to take pari-mutuel bets on live or simulcast horse races, and makes ADW the only lawful channel for pari-mutuel wagering. Fee revenue goes to an Equine Industry Development Fund that makes grants to the horse industry. The March 18, 2026 Senate Finance committee amendment limits ADW wagering to bettors physically present within the confines of the horse racing event, shifts background-check costs to applicants, and adds a detailed advertising code (new Section 11-61-175).","mechanics":["Licensing (Section 11-61-140): ADW licenses run one year, renewable for an additional year, with a $5,000 initial and $1,000 renewal fee. Applicants face SLED and FBI fingerprint checks, and the commission must decide within 30 days of receiving the results. The committee amendment makes the applicant, not the commission, bear the check costs.","Fees: the introduced text set a monthly 1% of in-state handle paid to the commission plus an annual 5% of handle paid to the Equine Industry Development Fund (Section 11-61-140(D)). The committee amendment appears to strike that subsection and the fee-collection power in Section 11-61-130(9), yet Section 11-61-170(A) still funds the Fund from 'fees collected pursuant to Section 11-61-140(D)', so the funding mechanism is unclear.","Physical presence and scope: per the committee amendment, ADW and pari-mutuel wagering require the bettor to be physically present within the confines of the horse racing event. Section 11-61-120(D) bars the commission from expanding gambling or approving historical horse racing, slot machines, or video games of chance.","Penalties (Section 11-61-160): offering an ADW app to residents without a license is a misdemeanor punishable by up to a $5,000 fine and/or one year, with each wager a separate offense and a permanent licensing bar after conviction. Pari-mutuel wagering outside a licensed ADW app is a misdemeanor punishable by up to a $2,000 fine and/or one year.","Advertising (new Section 11-61-175): licensees must retain materials for the commission and include the National Problem Gambling Helpline number. The section also bars targeting minors, appeals to or depictions of persons under 21, saturation advertising, success or guaranteed-win claims, and marketing to self-excluded individuals, and requires an unsubscribe option. The text repeatedly refers to 'sports betting.'","Age and timing: the wagering age is 18 (Section 11-61-190), while the advertising rules use a 21 standard. The commission must form within 60 days of approval and adopt grant regulations within 12 months. Section 5's April 1, 2026 licensing deadline has already passed. The act is effective on the Governor's approval."],"issue_tags":["licensing","legalize","advertising","tax"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike the sports-wagering authorization bills such as AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686 and HI HB 1308, S. 344 authorizes only pari-mutuel horse racing ADW and does not legalize sports betting. Its handle-based fee (if it survives) contrasts with AK's tax on mobile sports wagering revenue. Its advertising code resembles CT HB05229's gaming-advertising restrictions, but it is written for sports betting and grafted onto a horse-racing bill. The explicit ban on historical horse racing and on any commission-driven gambling expansion is more restrictive than FL H0189's broad revision of pari-mutuel permitholder rules.","watch":"Watch whether the 'sports betting' advertising template, the under-21 marketing standard and the anti-expansion clause in Section 11-61-120(D) become South Carolina's baseline for any future sports-wagering bill, and whether the on-site physical-presence requirement and the conflicting fee cross-reference are resolved on the floor.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Comm Sub","text_chars":24457,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:05:07.262Z"},"SC-S444":{"summary":"S0444 would enact the South Carolina Sports Wagering Act, adding Chapter 55 to Title 39 to legalize mobile and online-only sports wagering by up to eight licensed operators, regulated by a new nine-member South Carolina Sports Wagering Commission. It imposes a 12.5% privilege tax on adjusted gross sports wagering receipts, sets operator and supplier licensing and bettor-eligibility rules, and adds Section 16-19-135 to exempt lawful sports wagering from the state's gambling prohibitions. The supplied text is truncated partway through Section 39-55-270, which covers the commission's rulemaking.","mechanics":["Tax (39-55-140): 12.5% privilege tax on adjusted gross sports wagering receipts. AGR deducts winnings paid, promotional credits and free bets, federal excise tax, voided wagers, and uncollectibles capped at 2%. Negative months carry forward. The tax is split 82% to the general fund, 10% to local governments per capita for infrastructure, 5% to the Department of Mental Health for problem gambling, and 3% to the commission. Licensees are not subject to any other state or local tax.","Operator licensing (39-55-150): no more than eight operators. Each pays a $100,000 nonrefundable application fee and a $1,000,000 license fee, refundable if denied, and the same amounts again at renewal. Licenses run five years. Applicants need proof of active operation in at least five states. The commission must decide within 60 days. Temporary licenses are available to applicants licensed in five or more jurisdictions.","Resident representative (39-55-150(D)): each applicant must name an SC-organized company wholly owned by at least two SC residents with a demonstrated record of public service, such as legislatively elected or Senate-confirmed board service. Supplier licenses (39-55-160) cost $20,000 plus processing costs, run five years, and are available on a temporary basis; covered critical services are defined as geolocation and KYC.","Bettors (39-55-180 to 39-55-210, 39-55-260): the minimum age is 18 and bettors must be physically in SC. Credit, youth sports wagers, and wagers from self-excluded persons are prohibited. Registration requires an SSN, physical address and email, with one account per bettor. Licensees must use geolocation and keep their wagering servers in SC, though cloud computing is permitted.","Integrity (39-55-190, 39-55-200): lists prohibited bettor categories, including athletes, team owners of 10% or more, officials, insiders holding material nonpublic information, and resident representatives. Violations are misdemeanors with fines of up to $1,000, $5,000 and $10,000 for first, second and later offenses. Official league data is expressly not required. Operators must run a dispute process: bettors protest within 48 hours, operators decide within 7 business days, and appeals go to the commission within 7 business days.","Responsible gaming (39-55-220): voluntary exclusion periods of 2 years, 5 years or lifetime, with no early removal. The exclusion list is confidential and exempt from FOIA. Licensees must make reasonable efforts to stop direct marketing to excluded persons. DMH runs grant programs and must report annually."],"issue_tags":["legalize","tax","licensing","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Like AK HB145 and SB194, S0444 is a mobile-only legalization bill pairing a revenue tax with criminal background checks for licensure. Unlike Georgia's approach, which pairs the statutory GA HB 686 with the constitutional amendments GA HR450 and GA SR131, S0444 proceeds by statute alone. Its $1M license fee is far below the $15M master license fee proposed in IL HB2878. Its express bar on additional state or local taxes (39-55-140(E)) parallels IL HB4171's denial of home-rule taxing power. The mandatory SC resident-representative entity owned by public-service-connected residents is unusual and has no analogue among the listed bills.","watch":"Watch the resident-representative mandate and the eight-license cap, which together could create costly local-partner gatekeeping. Also watch whether the promotional-credit deduction, the 12.5% rate, the in-state server requirement, the 18 age floor and the no-official-league-data provision survive committee amendment.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":65092,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:05:13.553Z"},"TN-HB2079":{"summary":"Tennessee Public Chapter 1109 (enacted as SB1992, substituted for HB2079) creates a new theft-chapter felony for anyone who, to obtain a pecuniary benefit, acts to influence whether an event occurs while they or someone acting in active concert with them hold a prediction-market contract that will pay out based on that event. It is in effect a manipulation and insider-integrity offense aimed specifically at prediction-market contracts, not at licensed sportsbook wagers.","mechanics":["New section added to Tenn. Code Ann. Title 39, Chapter 14, Part 1 (theft and property offenses).","Offense elements: purpose of deriving a pecuniary benefit, conduct intended to influence the occurrence or nonoccurrence of an event, and the person or another acting in active concert being a party to a prediction-market contract that settles on that event and pays the person directly.","Penalty: Class E felony (§1(b)).","\"Prediction-market\" is defined broadly as any marketplace or exchange on which individuals trade contracts based on the occurrence or nonoccurrence of specified future events (§1(c)). There is no carve-out for CFTC-registered exchanges and no limit to sports events.","Effective July 1, 2026 (§2)."],"issue_tags":["prediction","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. On its own terms, the bill is unusual because it targets prediction-market event contracts by name rather than sports wagering. It also places the offense in the property-crime title instead of the Sports Gaming Act's integrity provisions. It does not regulate or authorize prediction markets; it only criminalizes manipulation tied to holding a contract.","watch":"Operators should watch how prosecutors and courts read the broad \"prediction-market\" definition and the \"active concert\" language, including possible federal preemption challenges by CFTC-regulated exchanges. They should also watch whether this signals Tennessee treating sports event contracts as within state criminal reach, which would bear on competition between licensed sportsbooks and prediction-market platforms.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":1514,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:05:14.821Z"},"TN-HB1409":{"summary":"Tennessee's general appropriations act (Public Chapter 530) funds state government for FY2025-26 and amends FY2024-25 appropriations. It is a budget bill and makes no substantive change to sports wagering law. Its only gaming connections in the text provided are funding lines for the sports wagering regulator and lottery-funded education accounts.","mechanics":["Title III-2, Item 2.8 appropriates $6,835,500 for FY2025-26 to the 'Sports Wagering Advisory Council', listed among the executive-branch Commissions (total $157,714,300).","Title III-10, Item 1.7 appropriates $504,800,000 to the Lottery for Education Account (Lottery-Funded) under Higher Education administration.","Title III-9, Item 2.1(k) funds the After-School Programs Special Account (Lottery-Funded). By line order, the amount appears to be $19,332,600, though the scanned layout makes this mapping less certain.","Effective dates are 05/21/2025 and 07/01/2025. The bill substituted for SB 1431.","The supplied text is truncated partway through Title III-22 (Miscellaneous Appropriations), so any later provisos, earmarks or fund transfers involving sports wagering revenue could not be reviewed."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"The listed bills are substantive gaming measures, including CO SB 163 (commission delegation of licensing), HI HB 1308, HB 2570 and SB1569 (legalizing and licensing sports wagering), and the MD bills on promotional-play deductions (HB291/SB102) and problem-gambling funding (HB1409/SB862). TN HB1409 changes none of those rules. Its relevance is limited to setting the operating budget of Tennessee's sports wagering regulator and appropriating lottery proceeds. It is closest in spirit to the Maryland problem-gambling fund bills only insofar as it touches how gaming-related money is allocated, but it does so purely as a line-item appropriation.","watch":"Watch whether the regulator's $6.8 million appropriation, and any provisos in the untruncated sections, signal expanded enforcement capacity or fee and privilege-tax-funded spending that could lead to higher licensee assessments.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":409242,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:05:17.071Z"},"TN-HB2281":{"summary":"HB2281 (companion SB2330) gives Tennessee's fiscal review committee staff broad access, including direct electronic access, to all records and data systems of executive branch agencies, confidential or otherwise, where directly related to the committee's statutory duties. The definition of \"agency\" expressly includes the Sports Wagering Council (§ 4-49-105) and the Tennessee Education Lottery Corporation. Section 2 separately requires the commissioner of finance and administration to give the office of legislative budget analysis full access to the BEARS, Sherpa and Office of Evidence and Impact budget data.","mechanics":["New T.C.A. § 3-7-115 defines \"agency\" to include the AOC, all executive branch instrumentalities, and expressly the Sports Wagering Council, Education Lottery Corporation, ABC, Wildlife Resources Agency and THEC.","Access covers inspecting, querying and retrieving contracts, transaction-level financial data, and program, performance and outcome data. It is delivered by direct secure electronic access, data extracts, or on-site access (§ 3-7-115(b)-(c)).","Access is excluded only where disclosure to fiscal review staff is expressly prohibited by state or federal law, a federal grant agreement with the force of law, or a court order. An agency claiming a bar must give a written explanation within 10 business days and work toward redacted, de-identified or aggregated alternatives (subsections (d)-(e)).","Confidential records keep their confidential status, are not public records under Title 10, Chapter 7, and may be disclosed only in aggregate, de-identified or summary form to the General Assembly or as otherwise authorized by law (subsection (f)).","Agencies must respond within 15 business days unless a longer schedule is mutually agreed. They must also list all data systems and data sets within 30 days of enactment and every January 1 after that (subsections (g), (j)).","The act takes effect upon becoming law."],"issue_tags":["integrity"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. The bill is unusual because it is a general legislative-oversight data-access measure that names the Sports Wagering Council as a covered agency, which would bring the regulator's licensing, financial and operator-submitted data within legislative staff's reach. It does not change wagering rules, taxes or licensing.","watch":"Operators should watch whether confidential licensee filings, wagering transaction data or investigative records held by the Sports Wagering Council become accessible to fiscal review staff, and whether the § 3-7-115(f) confidentiality safeguards and the de-identified-reporting limit are enough to protect proprietary and customer information.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Draft","text_chars":8001,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:05:23.598Z"},"TN-HB1885":{"summary":"Tennessee Public Chapter 1117 (SB 2136, substituted for HB1885) adds a new part 59 to the Consumer Protection Act, Title 47, Chapter 18. It declares online sweepstakes casinos, other online or app-based gambling, and gambling devices unlawful, and makes offering, promoting, or facilitating them an unfair or deceptive practice enforceable by the attorney general. It also gives the attorney general a general civil investigative demand power, removes the six-month limitation period for gaming prosecutions, and expands enforcement under Title 4, Chapter 49, Part 2 (the Fantasy Sports Act) to reach non-licensees.","mechanics":["Definition (§47-18-5901(5)): an \"online sweepstakes game\" is internet or mobile gambling that uses a virtual, dual, or multi-currency system. The currency may be purchased, given as a bonus, or received free with another purchase, and is redeemable for cash, prizes, or the chance to win them. The game must simulate gambling, including slots, video poker, table games, lottery, bingo, or \"unlicensed sports wagering.\"","Prohibited acts (§47-18-5902(b)): offering wagers or entry fees to, or accepting them from, persons located in Tennessee; operating, conducting, or commercially promoting online sweepstakes or app-based gambling; selling or distributing gambling devices; permitting devices on premises one controls; and supporting, facilitating, or assisting sweepstakes or other gambling.","Carve-out (§47-18-5902(c)): lawful games under the Tennessee Sports Gaming Act and Fantasy Sports Act (Title 4, Ch. 49), nonprofit gaming (Title 3, Ch. 17), and the Tennessee Education Lottery are excluded.","Enforcement: violations are TCPA violations and deemed unfair or deceptive acts carrying TCPA penalties and remedies (§47-18-5903; §47-18-104(b)), with no costs taxed against the state. AG records are confidential (§47-18-5904). New §8-6-409 gives the AG general civil investigative demand power, with a $1,000-per-document civil penalty for destroying or concealing records.","Section 3 deletes the six-month limitation period for gaming offenses in §40-2-102(a). Sections 5–9 amend the Fantasy Sports Act: the sports wagering council may investigate and sanction \"other persons,\" not just licensees (§4-49-208(b)); the AG may investigate §4-49-205 violations under the TCPA with venue in Williamson County (§4-49-206(b)); and the body is renamed the \"sports wagering council\" throughout Part 2.","Effective upon becoming law (approved May 2026); the act includes a severability clause."],"issue_tags":["sweeps","licensing","legalize"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Like CA AB 831, this targets dual-currency sweepstakes casinos, but Tennessee enforces mainly through the Consumer Protection Act and the attorney general rather than criminal-code amendments. It also expressly imposes liability on anyone who \"supports, facilitates, or assists\" these operations. FL S1580 and IL HB3733 address illegal gaming and gambling-device definitions through the gaming and criminal codes, whereas Tennessee pairs a civil UDAP regime with a new statewide AG investigative demand power. Unlike the authorization bills listed (AK HB145, GA HB 686, HI HB 1308), it leaves legal sports wagering untouched and carves it out.","watch":"Watch the breadth of the \"supports, facilitates, or assists\" and \"commercially promotes\" prongs in §47-18-5902(b), which could expose suppliers, payment processors, affiliates, and media partners that also serve sweepstakes or unlicensed operators, and the council's new power under §4-49-208 to sanction non-licensees in the fantasy market.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":17032,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:05:26.409Z"},"TN-HB2080":{"summary":"Public Chapter 1084 (SB 1723, substituted for HB 2080) vacates the Tennessee Education Lottery Corporation's board of directors on July 1, 2026 and replaces it with a nine-member board. The governor, the speaker of the senate and the speaker of the house each appoint three members, expressly modeled on the Tennessee Sports Wagering Council's appointment structure. It also tightens the TELC's public-records and open-meetings obligations, broadens distribution of audits, and preserves the corporation's status as an independent instrumentality.","mechanics":["Section 1 rewrites TCA 4-51-103. The current board is vacated July 1, 2026, and a new nine-member board appointed 3/3/3 by the governor, senate speaker and house speaker immediately assumes full authority, with all existing contracts and obligations remaining in force.","Initial terms are staggered at four, three and two years per appointing authority, calculated from July 1, 2026. Later terms are four years with holdover until replaced. Directors serve at the pleasure of the appointing authority, may be removed with or without cause, and are unpaid apart from per diem and travel.","Conflict-of-interest rule in new 4-51-103(l). Neither a director nor an immediate family member may have held a conflicting interest, including in a major procurement contract or a participating retailer, at appointment or in the prior two years. The only exception is an incidental holding of up to 1% of a participating retailer's stock. Directors are also barred from contributing to campaigns for the general assembly or governor.","Section 2 amends TCA 4-51-124 by deleting subdivision (a)(9). It makes the CEO's or president's hiring, compensation, evaluation and contract terms public records under title 10, chapter 7, with personally identifying information redacted under 10-7-503(a)(5). It also requires the TELC to comply with all state-agency public records and open meetings laws (title 10, ch. 7; title 8, ch. 44), except as provided in subsection (a).","Section 3 requires comptroller and independent CPA audits to go to the governor, both speakers, specified committee chairs, the state treasurer and, if applicable, the comptroller. Section 4 confirms the TELC is not subject to supervision by any department except as provided by law.","Effective on becoming law for administrative purposes and July 1, 2026 for all other purposes. Passed April 21, 2026 and signed by Governor Lee."],"issue_tags":["integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. The bill is unusual because it is a pure governance overhaul of a state lottery corporation. It wholesale vacates a sitting board and gives the legislative leadership two-thirds of the appointments. Its findings expressly cite the Tennessee Sports Wagering Council's appointment model as the template, rather than changing any wagering, tax or licensing rules.","watch":"Operators should watch how the reconstituted, leadership-appointed TELC board, now subject to full open-records and open-meetings rules, approaches procurement and its relationship with the Sports Wagering Council. The findings signal the legislature is applying the SWC governance model across Tennessee gaming and may pursue further structural changes.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":8361,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:05:27.635Z"},"TN-HJR162":{"summary":"HJR 162 is a joint resolution of the Tennessee General Assembly confirming Governor Bill Lee's appointment of Hanes Torbett to the Fish and Wildlife Commission under Tenn. Code Ann. § 70-1-201(b)(1), for a term expiring February 28, 2030. The only gaming connection is a recital noting that Mr. Torbett currently serves on the Tennessee Sports Wagering Council. It makes no change to sports wagering law or to the Council's composition or powers.","mechanics":["Confirms Hanes Torbett's appointment to the nine-member Fish and Wildlife Commission created by Tenn. Code Ann. § 70-1-201(a).","Term expires February 28, 2030.","Confirmation is by joint resolution, as required by § 70-1-201(b)(1), after review by the relevant House committee and the Senate Energy, Agricultural and Natural Resources Committee.","A recital notes Torbett currently sits on the Tennessee Sports Wagering Council and formerly served on the Tennessee Historical Commission.","Signed by the Governor on 2025-03-19."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. This is a routine Tennessee confirmation resolution for a wildlife commission seat, and its only link to gaming is the biographical reference to the appointee's current Sports Wagering Council membership. It contains no substantive gaming provisions.","watch":"The only point of interest is whether Torbett's new Fish and Wildlife Commission role leads him to leave, or creates a vacancy or change in attention on, the Tennessee Sports Wagering Council, which licenses and regulates operators; the resolution itself does not address this.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Draft","text_chars":2611,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:05:30.671Z"},"TN-HJR903":{"summary":"House Joint Resolution 903 confirms Governor Bill Lee's appointment of Mike Keeney to the state university board of the University of Memphis for a term expiring June 30, 2031, as required by Tenn. Code Ann. § 49-8-201. The only gaming connection is a recital noting that Keeney is a member of the Tennessee Sports Wagering Commission. The resolution makes no change to sports wagering law or to the Commission.","mechanics":["Confirms the gubernatorial appointment of Mike Keeney to the University of Memphis state university board under Tenn. Code Ann. §§ 49-8-101 and 49-8-201.","Sets the term to expire June 30, 2031.","Recitals note that § 49-8-201 requires the governor to appoint six members, at least three of them alumni, and requires confirmation by both chambers.","Recitals list Keeney's other roles, including membership on the Tennessee Sports Wagering Commission and chairing the Memphis-Shelby County Airport Authority.","The resolution was signed by the Governor on 2026-03-18 and has no regulatory, tax or enforcement provisions."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. On its own, this is a routine confirmation resolution for a university board seat. Its only tie to wagering is a biographical recital that the appointee also sits on the Tennessee Sports Wagering Commission.","watch":"Nothing substantive to watch for operators, beyond noting that a sitting Sports Wagering Commission member now also holds a University of Memphis board seat, which could matter for any collegiate-wagering or integrity question involving that institution.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Draft","text_chars":2384,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:05:33.734Z"},"TN-HB2631":{"summary":"This is Tennessee's general appropriations act for fiscal year 2026-2027 (enacted as SB 2690, Pub. Ch. 1142, with HB 2631 substituted), funding the legislative, judicial and executive branches. It does not change sports wagering law. Its only gaming touchpoint in the text provided is a line-item appropriation for the Sports Wagering Advisory Council, plus lottery-funded education accounts.","mechanics":["Section 1, Title III-2, Item 2.7 appropriates $6,955,200 for FY 2026-2027 to the Sports Wagering Advisory Council, within a $126,903,600 total for Commissions.","Title III-10, Item 1.7 appropriates $382,916,100 to the Lottery for Education Account (Lottery-Funded), and Title III-9, Item 2.1k appropriates $19,346,300 to the After-School Programs Special Account (Lottery-Funded). These are lottery items, not sports wagering items.","The text provided contains no sports wagering tax rate changes, licensing fees, advertising rules or problem-gambling earmarks.","The chaptered text supplied is truncated at Title III-22, Item 10 (Amendment and Legislation), so any later general provisions, restrictions or earmarks cannot be reviewed here.","Effective for the fiscal year beginning July 1, 2026; the act also addresses FY 2025-2026 appropriations and provides provisional continuing appropriations."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"Unlike HI HB 1308, HI HB 2570 and HI SB1569, which would build a sports wagering licensing and tax framework from scratch, and IL HB5443, which amends substantive sports wagering law, TN HB2631 only funds Tennessee's existing regulator through a budget line. It also differs from MD HB1409 and SB862, which redirect gaming revenue to problem gambling programs, and from KY HB 757, a revenue bill: on the text provided, HB2631 neither moves wagering revenue nor adds operator obligations.","watch":"Watch whether the $6,955,200 Sports Wagering Advisory Council appropriation signals expanded enforcement or staffing, and review the truncated remainder of the act for any general provisions that earmark or redirect sports wagering privilege tax proceeds.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":429708,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:05:34.602Z"},"TN-SB1431":{"summary":"This is Tennessee's general appropriations act for state government (enacted as Public Chapter 530, House Bill 1409, which was substituted for SB1431). It funds the legislative, judicial and executive branches, primarily for the fiscal year beginning July 1, 2025. Its only gaming content is a budget line funding the Sports Wagering Advisory Council and appropriations of lottery-funded education accounts; it makes no change to sports-wagering law, tax rates or licensing.","mechanics":["Section 1 appropriates funds for FY 2025-26 by title and line item across all three branches.","Title III-2, Item 2.8 (Commissions) funds the Sports Wagering Advisory Council. Read in sequence, the Commissions column gives the Council $6,835,500, within a Commissions total of $157,714,300.","Lottery-funded lines include the Lottery for Education Account at $504,800,000 (Title III-10, Item 1.7). They also include the After-School Programs Special Account, which appears to be $19,332,600 (Title III-9, Item 2.1.k).","The text provided is truncated. No visible provision amends the sports-wagering privilege tax, licensing fees or operator obligations.","Some appropriations may float with fund receipts. Examples are the Criminal Injuries Compensation Fund and the Wildlife Resources Fund, each subject to Tenn. Code Ann. Title 4, Ch. 3, Part 10 and Title 9, Ch. 4, Part 51."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills is a general budget act. Kentucky's HB 757 is a revenue measure, but it changes tax law rather than just appropriating funds. The substantive sports-wagering bills listed (Hawaii HB 1308 and SB1569, Illinois HB5443, Maryland HB291 and SB102, New Jersey A 4002 and A 4003) set licensing, tax, promotional-play or consumer-protection rules. By contrast, TN SB1431 only sets the operating budget of Tennessee's sports-wagering regulator. What is distinctive is that Tennessee funds a standalone advisory council for sports wagering as its own line item in the general budget.","watch":"Track the Sports Wagering Advisory Council's appropriation, about $6.8 million for FY 2025-26, and any changes in later budgets, because it signals the regulator's enforcement and rulemaking capacity, and its costs are ultimately backed by licensee fees and privilege-tax revenue.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":409242,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:05:43.734Z"},"TN-SB1662":{"summary":"SB1662 would add a new section to Tennessee's sports gaming statute (Title 4, Chapter 49) requiring a vendor to pay the council a fee equal to 25% of all compensation and payments it receives from a licensee under a revenue sharing agreement. The money would go into a new \"public institution athletic program fund\" administered by the Tennessee Higher Education Commission. The commission would make grants to smaller public four-year universities for athletic recruiting expenses. The bill was deferred to summer study on March 4, 2026.","mechanics":["Section 1(a): a vendor owes the council 25% of all compensation and payments received from a licensee under a revenue sharing agreement, due within 30 days of receipt and deposited into the fund created by new § 49-7-3202.","Section 1(b): both vendors and licensees must give the council all information needed to support the fee; this information is confidential and exempt from the Public Records Act (Title 10, Chapter 7).","New §§ 49-7-3202 and 49-7-3203: the fund is a non-reverting special account that may also take gifts and grants, with interest credited to the fund and commission administrative costs capped at 5% of any grant.","Grants may be used only for \"eligible recruiting expenses\" such as lodging, meals, travel, visits and recruiting materials, and expressly not for athlete compensation under § 49-7-2802(a).","Grants are distributed by an inverse full-time-equivalent enrollment formula, with priority for the six locally governed state universities; board of regents institutions are excluded, and recipients must compete in at least three intercollegiate sports.","Rulemaking authority is effective on enactment, all other provisions on July 1, 2026, and annual reports to legislative leadership are due starting December 31, 2026."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"Most listed bills that earmark wagering money tax the operator's own receipts: IL HB3159 takes 2% of adjusted gross sports wagering receipts for fire and EMS, and IA SSB 1157 adjusts tax rates to fund sports tourism programs. TN SB1662 is unusual because it does not tax licensees directly; instead it levies 25% on the revenue-share payments that third-party vendors receive from licensees. It is also distinct from the college-athlete bills listed (HI HB2384 and SB3263, GA HB383 and DE HB314), which address name, image and likeness rights or athlete protection without any gaming-sourced funding.","watch":"Operators should watch whether the 25% levy on vendor revenue-share receipts gets passed back through renegotiated platform, data or marketing contracts, how broadly \"revenue sharing agreement\" will be read, and what new disclosure duties to the council Section 1(b) places on licensees, particularly during the summer study.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Draft","text_chars":6650,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:05:46.699Z"},"TN-SB1723":{"summary":"Public Chapter 1084 dissolves the Tennessee Education Lottery Corporation's board of directors on July 1, 2026 and replaces it with a nine-member board appointed three each by the governor, the speaker of the Senate, and the speaker of the House. The whereas clauses say this copies the Tennessee Sports Wagering Council's appointment model. The act also subjects the TELC to state-agency public records and open meetings laws, makes the CEO's employment and compensation terms public, and widens the list of officials who receive TELC audits.","mechanics":["Section 1 (rewriting § 4-51-103): The existing board is vacated on July 1, 2026, and the new nine-member board takes over without interruption. All existing contracts and obligations stay in force.","Terms are counted from July 1, 2026. Each appointing authority staggers its initial picks at 4, 3 and 2 years; later terms are 4 years. Directors serve at the pleasure of whoever appointed them and can be removed with or without cause.","Section 1(l) covers conflicts and contributions. A director or immediate family member cannot hold, at appointment or in the prior two years, any conflicting interest, including in a major procurement contract or a participating retailer. The one exception is an incidental stake of up to 1% of a participating retailer's stock. Directors are also barred from contributing to campaigns for governor or the General Assembly.","Section 2 (amending § 4-51-124): Deletes subdivision (a)(9). Makes records on the CEO or president's hiring, retention, performance evaluation, compensation and contract terms public, with personally identifying information redacted. Requires the corporation to comply with public records (Title 10, Ch. 7) and open meetings (Title 8, Ch. 44) laws as a state agency would, except as provided in subsection (a).","Section 3 sends comptroller and independent CPA audits to the governor, both speakers, the relevant government and finance committee chairs, the state treasurer and, if applicable, the comptroller. Section 4 confirms TELC remains an independent instrumentality.","Effective upon becoming law for administrative purposes (05/22/2026) and July 1, 2026 for all other purposes."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"core","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. The bill is unusual because it removes a state lottery's board wholesale and splits appointment power between the governor and both legislative speakers. Each director can be removed at will by the official who appointed them. This mirrors Tennessee's own Sports Wagering Council model rather than any multistate template, and pairs that political-control shift with full open-records and open-meetings coverage.","watch":"The act itself contains no sports-wagering provisions, but operators should watch whether the reconstituted, legislatively appointed TELC board, now under open-meetings and records rules, becomes a vehicle for future proposals to bring sports wagering or other gaming back within the lottery's orbit, or a precedent for further restructuring of the Sports Wagering Council.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":8361,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:05:47.715Z"},"TN-SB1831":{"summary":"SB1831 requires Tennessee interactive sports wagering licensees to geofence out wagering on the campuses of public colleges and universities that field sanctioned athletics. That includes off-campus venues used for sanctioned collegiate sporting events, which must be blocked for the entire calendar day of the event. It separately requires those public institutions to block access to interactive sports wagering through any server or network they operate on campus.","mechanics":["New TCA 4-49-134(a): a licensee must prohibit access to interactive sports wagering on the campus of a covered institution of higher education.","\"Campus\" (4-49-134(c)(1)(A)) means the primary contiguous campus, plus any non-contiguous parcel or satellite facility within one mile of its boundary used for student residential or communal purposes. Parcels used solely for classrooms, maintenance, administration or storage are excluded.","\"Campus\" also includes (c)(1)(B) every in-state sports venue used by the institution for a sanctioned collegiate sporting event governed by a sports governing body, whether or not the institution owns it. Under (b), blocking at these venues applies for the entire calendar day of the event, and \"sports venue\" extends to contiguous or ancillary parking and event-related property controlled by the venue (c)(3).","Covered institutions are limited to public institutions of higher education that sanction at least one collegiate sport governed by a sports governing body; private colleges are not covered.","New TCA 49-7-194: covered public institutions providing internet access must block interactive sports wagering, as defined in 4-49-102, on their campus servers and wired or wireless networks.","The act takes effect July 1, 2026. It contains no specific penalty provision, so enforcement would fall to the existing Sports Wagering Council authority under Title 4, Chapter 49."],"issue_tags":["integrity","licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Connecticut HB05229 also targets college campuses, but it restricts gaming advertising there rather than mandating geolocation blocking of wagering itself. SB1831 goes further, imposing an affirmative geofencing duty on operators and a network-blocking duty on the universities themselves. Unusually, it treats off-campus venues hosting collegiate games, such as NFL stadiums or arenas, as campus for the whole event day. None of the listed Illinois, Hawaii, Alaska or Georgia bills contains a comparable campus wagering ban.","watch":"Watch the geofencing compliance burden. Operators would have to map one-mile satellite residential parcels and set full-day blocks at every in-state venue hosting a sanctioned college event, including non-university stadiums and their parking areas, and the absent penalty and safe-harbor language could leave operators exposed to Council discipline for geolocation edge cases.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Draft","text_chars":3680,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:05:48.433Z"},"TN-SB1992":{"summary":"Tennessee Public Chapter 1109 creates a new criminal offense in Title 39, Chapter 14, Part 1 for anyone who, to obtain a pecuniary benefit, engages in conduct intended to influence whether an event occurs while that person, or someone acting in active concert with them, holds a prediction-market contract that will pay out based on that event. A violation is a Class E felony. The law takes effect July 1, 2026.","mechanics":["Offense (Section 1(a)): conduct intended to influence the occurrence or nonoccurrence of an event, done to derive a pecuniary benefit, while the actor or someone in active concert with the actor is a party to a prediction-market contract that will pay the actor directly on settlement based on that event.","Penalty (Section 1(b)): Class E felony, the lowest felony class in Tennessee.","Definition (Section 1(c)): a 'prediction-market' is a marketplace or exchange on which individuals trade contracts based on the occurrence or nonoccurrence of specified future events. The definition is not limited to sports or to CFTC-registered exchanges.","Covered persons: any person, including athletes, officials, insiders and their co-conspirators ('active concert'). It does not regulate the operators or exchanges themselves.","Effective date (Section 2): July 1, 2026. The bill was signed after passing the Senate on April 21, 2026, and was substituted for HB 2079."],"issue_tags":["prediction","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. On its own terms, the law is unusual because it criminalizes event manipulation specifically tied to prediction-market contracts, rather than to traditional sportsbook wagers. It does so in Tennessee's property-offense chapter, not in the Sports Gaming Act. The law does not license, prohibit or tax prediction markets. It reaches only the manipulator who stands to profit from settlement, and it uses a broad event definition that covers non-sports events such as elections or corporate outcomes.","watch":"Operators should note that Tennessee is treating prediction-market contracts as a distinct product in its criminal code, which could foreshadow regulation or a ban, and should watch how prosecutors read 'active concert' and 'directly from the settlement,' since those terms decide whether tipsters, syndicates, or counterparties on licensed sportsbooks fall outside this statute and under existing Sports Gaming Act integrity provisions instead.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":1514,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:05:55.973Z"},"TN-SB2330":{"summary":"SB2330 adds a new TCA § 3-7-115 giving the staff of the legislature's Fiscal Review Committee access to all agency information and records, confidential or otherwise, including direct electronic access to agency data systems, and expressly lists the Sports Wagering Council and the Tennessee Education Lottery Corporation as covered agencies. It also amends § 3-14-202(h) to give the Office of Legislative Budget Analysis full access to the BEARS and Sherpa budget systems and to data held by the Office of Evidence and Impact. It does not regulate wagering itself; the gaming link is that the Sports Wagering Council's records, potentially including licensee data, become reachable by legislative staff.","mechanics":["Covered 'agency' (§ 3-7-115(a)(1)) includes the Administrative Office of the Courts, executive branch instrumentalities, and expressly the Sports Wagering Council (§ 4-49-105) and the Tennessee Education Lottery Corporation (§ 4-51-101).","Access under subsection (c) covers contracts, transaction-level financial data and performance data. Agencies must provide it through direct secure electronic access, data extracts, or on-site access, within 15 business days (subsection (g)) unless both sides agree to an alternative schedule.","Under subsection (d), access may be withheld only where disclosure to committee staff is expressly prohibited by state or federal law, a federal grant agreement with the force of law, or court order. Under subsection (e), an agency claiming a bar must explain the legal basis in writing within 10 business days and work toward redacted, de-identified, aggregated or limited-access alternatives.","Under subsection (f), confidential records keep their confidential status, are not public records under Title 10, Chapter 7, may be used only for committee duties, and may be disclosed only in aggregate or de-identified form to the General Assembly or as otherwise authorized by law.","Under subsection (j), every agency must give the committee an inventory of all its data systems and data sets within 30 days of the effective date, and annually by January 1 after that.","The act takes effect upon becoming law."],"issue_tags":["integrity"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. What is unusual is that a general legislative-oversight data-access bill singles out the Sports Wagering Council by name as a covered agency, potentially opening its regulatory files and transaction-level data to Fiscal Review Committee staff. That access would apply notwithstanding contrary law, except where disclosure to committee staff is expressly prohibited.","watch":"Watch whether the Sports Wagering Council's existing confidentiality provisions for licensee financial, wagering-transaction and integrity-monitoring data under Title 4, Chapter 49 count as an express prohibition under subsection (d); if they do not, operators' proprietary data submitted to the council could flow to legislative staff, protected only by the subsection (f) confidentiality safeguards.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Draft","text_chars":7997,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:05:58.510Z"},"TN-SJR631":{"summary":"Senate Joint Resolution 631 confirms Governor Bill Lee's appointment of Mike Keeney to the state university board of the University of Memphis for a term expiring June 30, 2031, as required by Tenn. Code Ann. 49-8-201. Its only connection to gaming is a recital noting that Keeney currently serves as a member of the Tennessee Sports Wagering Commission.","mechanics":["Confirms the gubernatorial appointment of Mike Keeney to the University of Memphis state university board under Tenn. Code Ann. 49-8-101 and 49-8-201.","Term of the appointment expires June 30, 2031.","Joint resolution requires Senate adoption with House concurrence, per the statutory confirmation requirement in 49-8-201.","A recital lists Keeney's membership on the Tennessee Sports Wagering Commission among his civic roles, but the resolution makes no change to that commission or to any wagering law."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. The resolution is a routine confirmation of a university board appointment. It is notable only because it records that a sitting Tennessee Sports Wagering Commission member also holds several other public posts, including chair of the Memphis-Shelby County Airport Authority.","watch":"Nothing in the resolution affects wagering regulation, though operators may want to note that Commissioner Keeney's additional board role adds to his outside commitments, which could matter for potential recusal or conflict questions at the Sports Wagering Commission.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Draft","text_chars":2385,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:06:02.197Z"},"TN-SJR99":{"summary":"Senate Joint Resolution 99 confirms Governor Bill Lee's appointment of Hanes Torbett to the Tennessee Fish and Wildlife Commission for a term expiring February 28, 2030, as required by Tenn. Code Ann. § 70-1-201(b)(1). Its only connection to sports betting is a recital noting that Torbett currently serves on the Tennessee Sports Wagering Council. The resolution makes no change to wagering law or regulation.","mechanics":["Confirms Hanes Torbett's appointment to the Fish and Wildlife Commission under Tenn. Code Ann. § 70-1-201.","The term expires February 28, 2030.","Confirmation requires approval by the relevant House committee, the Senate Energy, Agriculture and Natural Resources Committee, and a joint resolution of the General Assembly (§ 70-1-201(b)(1)).","A recital states that Torbett currently serves on the Tennessee Sports Wagering Council. Nothing in the text says he must leave that seat.","On 2025-03-10 the companion bill, HJR 162, was substituted for this SJR."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. The resolution is a routine confirmation of a wildlife commissioner. It is notable only because it shows a sitting Sports Wagering Council member taking on a second state appointment.","watch":"Nothing here affects operators directly, but counsel may want to note whether Torbett keeps his Sports Wagering Council seat alongside the new commission role, since that bears on council composition and any future vacancy.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Draft","text_chars":2609,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:06:03.958Z"},"TN-SB2690":{"summary":"Tennessee's general appropriations act (Public Chapter 1142) for fiscal year 2026-27, which funds the legislative, judicial and executive branches and sets conditions on spending. Sports wagering comes up only as one budget line: an appropriation to the Sports Wagering Advisory Council, the state's sports betting regulatory body, among the executive commissions in Title III-2.","mechanics":["Section 1, Title III-2, Item 2.7 appropriates $6,955,200 for FY 2026-27 to the Sports Wagering Advisory Council, out of $126,903,600 for all executive commissions.","Lottery-funded education lines are separate from the sports wagering line: Title III-9, Item 2.1k After-School Programs Special Account at $19,346,300, and Title III-10, Item 1.7 Lottery for Education Account at $382,916,100.","Item 2.12 appropriates $4,860,400 to the Alcoholic Beverage Commission, which sits alongside the Council in the commissions list.","The act was signed into law with effective dates of May 22, 2026 and July 1, 2026; the appropriations schedule covers the fiscal year beginning July 1, 2026.","The supplied text is truncated partway through Title III-22 (Miscellaneous Appropriations, Item 10, Amendment and Legislation), so any later sections or earmarks touching gaming revenue could not be reviewed."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills is a general appropriations act. KY HB 757 is the nearest analogue as an omnibus revenue measure, but it changes tax law, whereas this bill only funds agencies. Bills such as MD HB1409/SB862 and MD HB758 redirect gaming fund revenue or uses, while TN SB2690, in the portion reviewed, only sets the Council's operating budget. Unlike HI HB 1308, HB 2570 and SB1569, it makes no change to licensing, tax rates or regulatory structure.","watch":"Track whether the Council's $6,955,200 appropriation supports or limits its enforcement, licensing and rulemaking capacity, and review the untruncated remainder of the act for any earmark or diversion of sports wagering privilege tax revenue.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":429708,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:06:06.270Z"},"TN-SB2136":{"summary":"Tennessee Public Chapter 1117 creates a new civil enforcement part in the Consumer Protection Act (Tenn. Code 47-18-5901 to -5904) declaring online sweepstakes casinos, other online or app-based gambling and gambling devices unlawful, and makes violations unfair or deceptive acts enforceable by the attorney general. It also gives the attorney general a general civil investigative demand power (new 8-6-409), removes the six-month statute of limitations for gaming prosecutions, and expands the Sports Wagering Council's and the AG's enforcement reach under the Sports Gaming Act to unlicensed 'other persons.'","mechanics":["'Online sweepstakes game' (47-18-5901(5)) means internet gambling using a virtual, dual or multi-currency system where coins are purchased, given as a bonus, or received free with another purchase, can be exchanged for cash or prizes, and simulate casino games, lottery, bingo or 'unlicensed sports wagering.'","Covered conduct (47-18-5902(b)) includes offering or accepting wagers from persons in Tennessee, operating or 'commercially promoting' online sweepstakes or app-based gambling, selling or distributing gambling devices, permitting devices on controlled premises, and anyone who 'supports, facilitates, or assists' such operations. Lawful activity under the Sports Gaming Act, Fantasy Sports Act, nonprofit gaming laws and the education lottery is carved out (47-18-5902(c)).","Violations are per se TCPA violations and are added to the 47-18-104(b) list (Section 2), with TCPA penalties and remedies. No costs may be taxed against the state, and AG investigative records are exempt from the Public Records Act even when shared with other agencies.","Section 3 deletes the six-month limitations period for gaming offenses in 40-2-102(a). Section 4 (8-6-409) authorizes AG civil investigative demands, sworn examinations and court orders, including ex parte preservation orders against persons leaving the state, with a civil penalty of up to $1,000 per document for spoliation.","Sections 5-9 amend Title 4, Chapter 49. The council may investigate and sanction 'a licensee or other person' (4-49-208(b)), and the AG may investigate violations of 4-49-205 under the TCPA with venue in Williamson County (4-49-206(b)). The 'sports wagering advisory council' is renamed the 'sports wagering council.'","The act takes effect on becoming law, recorded as effective 05/22/2026, and includes a severability clause."],"issue_tags":["sweeps","licensing","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Like CA AB 831, which targets contests and sweepstakes using dual-currency mechanics, TN SB2136 bans online sweepstakes casinos. Tennessee relies on Consumer Protection Act civil enforcement by the attorney general rather than a gambling-code prohibition. It also reaches anyone who 'supports, facilitates, or assists' an operator. Unlike FL S1580 and FL H0189, which revise criminal gambling penalties and regulator authority, Tennessee pairs the ban with a broad new AG investigative-demand statute and removes the gaming limitations period. It does not expressly address prediction markets, unlike IL HB5142.","watch":"Operators and their B2B suppliers, payment processors, affiliates and marketers should watch how broadly the AG and the Sports Wagering Council read 'supports, facilitates, or assists' and 'commercially promotes,' since that language, the council's new jurisdiction over 'other persons,' and the reference to 'unlicensed sports wagering' could reach vendors and offshore or prediction-market-style products. They should also note that confidential AG investigative demands, backed by $1,000-per-document penalties, are now available.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":17032,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:06:09.864Z"},"TX-SB844":{"summary":"SB 844 rewrites the Texas municipal zoning protest procedure in Local Government Code Chapter 211, exempting \"proposed comprehensive zoning changes\" that increase residential density from the landowner protest/supermajority mechanism and setting new protest thresholds for all other zoning changes. Its only gaming-relevant provision keeps a low 20 percent neighbor-protest trigger, and with it a three-fourths council supermajority, for any zoning change that would allow a building to be used for any form of gambling or sports wagering. The bill also adds website notice for zoning hearings and a 60-day conclusive presumption of validity for density-increasing changes.","mechanics":["New Sec. 211.0061(b): a written protest triggers heightened voting if signed by owners of at least 20% of the land covered by the change, or at least 60% of land within 200 feet of it, or only 20% of the adjoining 200-foot area if the change would allow use of a building or structure for the operation of any form of gambling or sports wagering.","Sec. 211.0061(d): a protest by 20% of covered owners or a gambling/sports-wagering-related protest requires approval by three-fourths of all members of the governing body; a general 60% adjoining-owner protest requires only a majority of all members.","Sec. 211.0011 defines \"proposed comprehensive zoning change\" (density-increasing uniform district changes, citywide new codes or maps, and density-increasing overlays along major roadway, highway or transit corridors), which are excluded from the protest procedure and receive notice-only treatment under Sec. 211.0063.","Sec. 211.006(a) amended to require hearing notice, before the 15th day before the hearing, in a newspaper and on the municipal website if one exists; land area for protests is calculated in aggregate including streets and alleys (Sec. 211.0061(c)).","Sec. 211.0065: density-increasing zoning changes are conclusively presumed valid unless challenged before the 60th day after their effective date; former Secs. 211.006(d) and (f) are repealed.","Applies to proposals made on or after the September 1, 2025 effective date."],"issue_tags":["licensing"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No other states' bills are listed for comparison. What is unusual is that a housing-density zoning reform singles out gambling and sports wagering facilities for preserved local veto leverage: those projects keep the 20% neighbor-protest trigger and three-fourths supermajority, while other non-comprehensive changes need a 60% neighbor protest and only a simple majority. Texas does not currently authorize casinos or sports wagering, so the provision functions as a pre-emptive land-use barrier should legalization occur.","watch":"If Texas later legalizes casinos or sports betting, Sec. 211.0061(b)(3) would let owners of just 20% of land within 200 feet of a proposed site force a three-fourths council supermajority for any rezoning that would permit a retail sportsbook, casino or other gambling facility, so operators should track the broad undefined phrase \"any form of gambling or sports wagering\" and its interaction with any future enabling legislation.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Comm Sub","text_chars":8726,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:06:14.739Z"},"TX-HB126":{"summary":"HB126 amends Texas Education Code Section 51.9246 so Texas institutions can pay student athletes directly for name, image and likeness (NIL) and can offer NIL compensation to recruits. It also lets institutions, athletes and others take any action authorized by an athletic association, a conference, or a final court order (aimed at the House v. NCAA settlement framework), subject to those bodies' enforcement rules. The existing ban on NIL contracts endorsing sports betting and casino gambling is kept and renumbered.","mechanics":["Section 51.9246(c) deletes the old bar on institutions providing or soliciting NIL compensation to prospective student athletes. (g)(2)(B) deletes the bars on compensation paid by the institution, for accepting an admission offer, or for acts during official team activities. Compensation in exchange for athletic performance is still prohibited.","Section 51.9246(g)(2)(B)(ii) retains the prohibition on a student athlete entering an NIL contract that pays for endorsing alcohol, tobacco, e-cigarettes or other nicotine devices, anabolic steroids, sports betting, casino gambling, firearms the athlete cannot legally buy, or sexually oriented businesses.","New (c-2) and (c-3) apply where state law conflicts with association, conference or court-order requirements. Institutions, athletes and other parties may act as those bodies authorize or a final court order requires, but they remain subject to those bodies' rules and enforcement provisions.","New (k-1) bars any individual, company or institution from entering an NIL arrangement with a prospective student athlete under 17, or with that athlete's relatives by blood or marriage, unless the athlete is enrolled at an institution of higher education.","Section 2 repeals Subsections (g-1) and (j); their content is not reproduced in the text.","The changes apply only to NIL compensation paid on or after the effective date. The bill passed with two-thirds votes in both houses and took immediate effect, recorded as of 2025-06-05."],"issue_tags":["advertising"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"Like HI HB 2384, HI SB3263, WI AB1034, NE LB370 and LA HB 513, HB126 is mainly an NIL statute aligning state law with the post-settlement model of direct institutional payments. Its only gaming content is the carried-over ban on sports-betting and casino endorsements. By contrast, MD HB518 and CT HB05229 regulate operators directly, through college-athlete protections and problem-gambling rules in Maryland and campus advertising limits in Connecticut. NC H828 and LA SB354 target prop bets on college and amateur athletes. HB126 creates no operator obligations or wagering-market restrictions and does not touch Texas's lack of a legal sports betting regime.","watch":"Operators and their affiliates still cannot use NIL deals with student athletes at covered Texas institutions to promote sports betting or casino gambling. Watch whether conference or settlement rules adopted under the new (c-2) carve-out, or future amendments, add further gambling-related restrictions on athletes or institutional sponsorships.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":11884,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:06:19.311Z"},"UT-HB243":{"summary":"HB 243 amends Utah's criminal gambling definitions in Utah Code 76-9-1401 to add a new defined term, \"proposition bet,\" and to state expressly that a proposition bet is included within \"gambling.\" Because Utah prohibits essentially all gambling, the practical effect is to remove any argument that wagers on individual actions or statistics fall outside the prohibition. The listed status of \"Failed / Dead\" conflicts with the last action, which records the Governor's signature filed with the Lieutenant Governor on 2026-03-25, so the bill appears to have been enacted and the status should be verified.","mechanics":["New Subsection 76-9-1401(17) defines \"proposition bet\" as \"a gambling bet on an individual action, statistic, occurrence, or non-occurrence.\"","Subsection (8)(b) now provides that \"gambling\" includes (i) a lottery and (ii) a proposition bet.","The core definition of gambling in Subsection (8)(a) is unchanged: risking anything of value on an outcome based on an element of chance under an agreement that someone receives value on a certain outcome, regardless of preview or pre-reveal features.","\"Gambling bet\" in Subsection (9) already covers money, checks, credit, or any other representation of value. The existing \"online gambling\" definition in Subsection (14) reaches internet and mobile-device play, so online prop wagering is captured.","The remaining changes are renumbering of Subsections (17) through (21) plus stylistic edits, such as \"internet\" and the Utah Constitution Article VI, Section 27 citation format.","The bill takes effect May 6, 2026, with no appropriation and no new penalties. Enforcement runs through the existing criminal provisions of Title 76, Chapter 9, Part 14."],"issue_tags":["prop","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. Most state prop-bet legislation operates inside a legal sports-betting framework, banning or restricting particular prop markets such as college player props for integrity reasons. Utah's bill is unusual because it works through a total criminal prohibition: it codifies that any proposition bet is gambling. The definition is broad enough to reach player-stat contests and potentially event-style contracts on any \"occurrence or non-occurrence,\" though the text does not mention daily fantasy, pick'em, or prediction markets by name.","watch":"Watch whether Utah prosecutors or the Attorney General use the broad \"individual action, statistic, occurrence, or non-occurrence\" language against pick'em-style fantasy products and CFTC-regulated event contracts offered to Utah residents online, which would raise skill-versus-chance and federal preemption disputes.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":11577,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:06:24.149Z"},"VA-HB1001":{"summary":"House Education Committee substitute for HB1001 that would create a Virginia College Access and Affordability Scholarship Fund under the State Council of Higher Education for Virginia, in new Article 5 (§§ 23.1-235 through 23.1-237) of Chapter 2 of Title 23.1. It would also amend the Commonwealth Savers Plan statutes (§§ 23.1-700, -701, -702, -704, -706) and the closed-meeting provisions of § 2.2-3711, and set up a work group and a report. The supplied text is cut off inside § 2.2-3711, so the fund's actual terms (funding source, eligibility, award amounts) cannot be confirmed here; the bill was left in House Appropriations on February 18, 2026.","mechanics":["Adds new §§ 23.1-235 to 23.1-237 creating the Scholarship Fund under SCHEV, with a work group and report per the title; the substantive text of these sections is not in the supplied excerpt.","Amends §§ 23.1-700, 23.1-701, 23.1-702, 23.1-704 and 23.1-706 governing the Commonwealth Savers Plan, its Board and its Investment Advisory Committee.","Amends § 2.2-3711 in both its current version and the version effective July 1, 2026. The visible text tracks existing closed-meeting authority, including Commonwealth Savers Plan Board items in subdivisions 20, 25 and 38.","Subdivision 54 of § 2.2-3711 is reproduced without visible change. It covers Virginia Lottery Board closed deliberations on appeals over sports betting licenses or permits under § 58.1-4007; subdivision 53 does the same for casino gaming.","Procedural posture: House Education substitute of February 9, 2026 (original patron Delegate Tran); left in Committee on Appropriations on February 18, 2026, effectively ending it for the session."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"Unlike TN SB1662, which expressly funds public-institution athletic programs through a fee on vendors registered with the Tennessee sports wagering council, nothing in the visible HB1001 text ties the scholarship fund to sports betting revenue or operator fees. It also has nothing in common with the college-gaming and integrity bills listed, such as MD HB518, CT HB05229, NC H828 and LA SB354, or with the NIL bills (e.g., HI HB 2384, TX HB126). Its only wagering contact is the reproduced Lottery Board closed-session authority for sports betting license appeals in § 2.2-3711(A)(54).","watch":"Confirm from the full text of §§ 23.1-235 to 23.1-237 that the fund is not capitalized by a sports betting tax diversion or operator assessment, and that the § 2.2-3711 reenactment leaves the Lottery Board's closed-session authority for sports betting license appeals in subdivision 54 unchanged, if the bill is revived.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Comm Sub","text_chars":86945,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:06:25.689Z"},"VA-HB1272":{"summary":"HB1272, as rewritten by the Second Joint Conference Committee substitute of March 14, 2026, would create a regulatory regime for electronic skill gaming devices under the Virginia Lottery Board. It adds a new Chapter 42 to Title 58.1 (§§ 58.1-4200 through 58.1-4226, Articles 1-5), amends the criminal gambling statutes, and amends existing Lottery and casino provisions. The conference report failed on March 14, 2026, so the bill is dead for the session.","mechanics":["Adds new Title 58.1, Chapter 42 (§§ 58.1-4200 to 58.1-4226) on 'regulation of gaming commerce and development'; its substantive text (fees, tax rate, device caps, locations) is not in the excerpt provided.","Adds § 2.2-3711(A)(56), letting the Virginia Lottery Board close meetings for § 58.1-4007 appeals on denial, revocation, suspension or non-renewal of licenses for electronic skill gaming devices, mirroring existing subdivision 54 for sports betting permits.","Amends criminal gambling provisions §§ 18.2-325, 18.2-331.1, 18.2-334 and 18.2-339, and adds § 18.2-334.7 and § 19.2-386.30:1 (a forfeiture-chapter section), signalling new penalties and seizure of unlicensed devices.","Amends core Lottery Board powers and licensing sections (§§ 58.1-4002, -4003, -4006, -4007, -4015.1), § 58.1-4048 and casino section § 58.1-4103, plus problem-gambling statute § 37.2-314.2.","The short title references a sunset, but the sunset clause and effective-date enactments are not in the supplied text."],"issue_tags":["licensing","legalize"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike the listed mobile sports wagering authorization bills (AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, HI HB 1308), HB1272 targets skill-game and electronic gaming machines rather than sports wagering. Its closest analogues are Illinois device bills. IL HB3733 and HB 2879 expand the criminal 'gambling device' definition. IL HB3274 and HB5048 address prize and gift kiosks under the Video Gaming Act. Virginia's approach is distinctive because it would build a full licensing chapter under the same Lottery Board that regulates sports betting and casinos, rather than only tightening criminal definitions.","watch":"Although the bill failed in conference, operators should watch for reintroduction and for how the conforming amendments to §§ 58.1-4007, 58.1-4048 and 58.1-4103 would put skill-game licensees into the same Lottery Board appeal, enforcement and problem-gambling framework as sports-betting and casino permit holders; the full Chapter 42 text was not supplied and should be reviewed.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Comm Sub","text_chars":208812,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:06:30.755Z"},"VA-HB1403":{"summary":"HB1403 (Chapter 842, approved April 13, 2026) creates a Severe Maternal Morbidity Surveillance and Review Program in a new Article 25, § 32.1-73.33, of Title 32.1 (Health). It also amends Virginia FOIA and the Administrative Process Act to protect the Program's work. The bill has nothing to do with gaming; sports betting appears only because the reenacted closed-meetings statute, § 2.2-3711, carries existing, unchanged Virginia Lottery Board provisions.","mechanics":["Adds new § 32.1-73.33, which establishes the Severe Maternal Morbidity Surveillance and Review Program and defines 'severe maternal morbidity'. The full text of that section is truncated in the excerpt provided.","Amends § 2.2-3705.5(7) to add clause (vi). It excludes from mandatory FOIA disclosure information acquired during the Program's reviews, to the extent § 32.1-73.33 makes it confidential. The Developmental Disabilities Mortality Review Committee clause is relettered to (vii).","Amends § 2.2-3711(A)(21), in both the version effective until July 1, 2026 and the version effective July 1, 2026, to let the Program discuss severe maternal morbidity cases in closed meeting.","Section 2.2-4002 (Administrative Process Act exemptions) is also amended, but the excerpt does not show the specific change.","Reenacted § 2.2-3711(A)(17), (53) and (54) keep, without visible change, the existing closed-meeting authority for Virginia Lottery Board deliberations on lottery, casino and sports betting license denials, revocations and appeals under §§ 58.1-4007 and 58.1-4105."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. This is a maternal health surveillance bill. Its only gaming connection is that a full reenactment of § 2.2-3711 restates the existing Lottery Board closed-session authority for sports betting and casino licensing appeals in subdivisions 53 and 54, without substantive change in the text shown.","watch":"Nothing operator-specific; the only point to confirm is that the reenactment of § 2.2-3711 (both versions) leaves subdivisions 53 and 54 on Lottery Board closed deliberations over casino and sports betting license actions unchanged.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":78741,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:06:36.176Z"},"VA-HB1398":{"summary":"HB1398 would create a Fetal and Infant Mortality Review Team in a new Code of Virginia § 32.1-283.9. It also makes conforming changes so that information the Team gathers is excluded from mandatory FOIA disclosure and the Team can discuss individual fetal and infant death cases in closed meetings. The bill has nothing to do with gaming; wagering appears only because § 2.2-3711, which the bill amends, already contains existing Virginia Lottery Board closed-meeting provisions that are reprinted unchanged.","mechanics":["Adds new § 32.1-283.9 establishing the Fetal and Infant Mortality Review Team; the title references definitions, a penalty and a report, but the supplied text is truncated before that section, so its substantive terms cannot be confirmed.","Amends § 2.2-3705.5(7)(v) to exclude information the Team acquires during death reviews from mandatory FOIA disclosure, to the extent § 32.1-283.9 makes it confidential.","Amends § 2.2-3711(A)(21), in both the version effective until July 1, 2026 and the version effective July 1, 2026, to allow closed meetings for the portions in which the Team discusses individual fetal and infant death cases.","Also amends § 2.2-4002, an Administrative Process Act section; the amending language is not in the supplied text.","House Appropriations substitute dated February 11, 2026, which folds in HB 336 (Delegates Franklin and Anthony). Senate Finance and Appropriations continued the bill to the next session on March 9, 2026, by a 14-0 vote."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed, and the bill is not comparable to gaming legislation. The only gaming-related text is existing language in § 2.2-3711(A)(17), (53) and (54). Those subdivisions authorize closed Virginia Lottery Board deliberations on lottery, casino and sports betting license denials, revocations, suspensions and appeals under §§ 58.1-4007 and 58.1-4105. They appear only because the whole section is reprinted, and HB1398 leaves them untouched.","watch":"No action is needed beyond confirming that any later substitute does not alter the existing § 2.2-3711(A)(54) closed-meeting authority for Lottery Board sports betting license appeals; the bill is in any case carried over to the 2027 session.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Comm Sub","text_chars":83469,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:06:36.206Z"},"VA-HB1476":{"summary":"HB1476 adds a new closed-meeting exemption to Virginia's FOIA, § 2.2-3711(A)(56), so that law-enforcement civilian oversight bodies can privately discuss criminal investigative files, audit findings, and deliberations on police operations tied to specific complaints involving sexual offenses or confidential juvenile matters. The chapter also amends §§ 16.1-301 (juvenile law-enforcement records) and 19.2-11.2 on disclosure of law-enforcement records, but those amendments fall outside the excerpt provided. Nothing in it concerns sports betting or gaming.","mechanics":["New § 2.2-3711(A)(56), added to both the current version and the version effective July 1, 2026, covers civilian oversight bodies established under § 9.1-601. It also covers bodies a local governing body set up before July 1, 2020 that operate consistently with § 9.1-601.","Closed discussion is allowed only for criminal investigative files, audit findings, and deliberations regarding police operations related to a specific complaint before the body.","The complaint must involve an actual or attempted offense under Article 7 of Chapter 4 of Title 18.2 (criminal sexual assault) or under § 18.2-361, 18.2-370, or 18.2-370.1. Alternatively, it must involve a juvenile whose records are not open to public inspection under § 16.1-301 or 16.1-305.","The existing § 2.2-3711(B) requirement still applies: any action taken must be voted on in a reconvened open meeting before it is effective.","The chapter was approved April 13, 2026 as Chapter 861. The text of the §§ 16.1-301 and 19.2-11.2 amendments is not in the excerpt provided."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No other states' bills are listed. Wagering appears only because the reenacted § 2.2-3711 reproduces existing, unamended exemptions. These include subdivision 17 (Lottery Board lottery sales agent appeals), subdivision 53 (casino license denials and revocations) and subdivision 54 (Lottery Board appeals on sports betting licenses or permits under § 58.1-4007, plus related investigations excluded under § 2.2-3705.3(1)). The bill's substance is police oversight transparency, not gaming.","watch":"Nothing here affects operators; confirm only that the existing Lottery Board closed-meeting authority for sports-betting license appeals in § 2.2-3711(A)(54) carries over unchanged into the version effective July 1, 2026.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":63522,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:06:48.684Z"},"VA-HB145":{"summary":"HB145 (Chapter 565) repeals Virginia's Fantasy Contests Act (Chapter 51 of Title 59.1) and re-creates fantasy contest regulation as new Chapter 42 of Title 58.1 (§§ 58.1-4200 through 58.1-4214), placing permitting and oversight under the Virginia Lottery Board and Department alongside sports betting and casino gaming. It sets a permit regime with a $50,000 application fee, consumer-protection and integrity conditions, Board investigative and injunctive powers, and a revenue base for taxation. Conforming amendments fold fantasy contests into the State Police gaming enforcement definitions, the voluntary exclusion programs, FOIA exclusions, and the Problem Gambling Treatment and Support Fund.","mechanics":["Definition (§ 58.1-4200): a fantasy contest must be online with an entry fee, have two or more players competing against each other, and have prizes fixed and disclosed in advance. Outcomes must reflect skill via accumulated statistics and may not rest on any single team's score or point spread, or solely on a single athlete's single performance. No player may compete against the operator, which excludes house-banked formats.","Permit required before offering contests in Virginia (§ 58.1-4202). Permits are valid three years, require a nonrefundable $50,000 initial application fee deposited to the Problem Gambling Treatment and Support Fund, and a lapsed permit means filing a new initial application.","Permit conditions (§ 58.1-4202 D) include 21+ age verification, bars on operator and employee play, confidential-information controls, and restrictions on athletes entering contests based on their own statistics. Operators must also offer self-exclusion, disclose and enforce entry limits, and segregate player funds with a reserve sufficient to cover all prizes.","Taxable base: 'fantasy contest revenue' means entry fees less prizes paid, multiplied by a Virginia 'location percentage.' § 58.1-4208 also directs moneys to the Problem Gambling Fund, but the tax rate itself is in sections beyond the excerpt provided.","Enforcement: the Board may investigate, issue subpoenas (hearings held in Richmond), and seek circuit court injunctions (§ 58.1-4201). Knowingly falsifying a permit application is a Class 1 misdemeanor (§ 58.1-4202 G), and the civil penalty referenced in the title appears in sections not shown.","Conforming changes: new § 11-16.3 exempts lawful fantasy contests from Title 11 gaming-contract rules, and the 15% principal stockholder threshold applies. Fantasy contests are added to the lottery and casino voluntary exclusion lists (§§ 58.1-4015.1, 58.1-4103), the FOIA investigative exclusion (§ 2.2-3705.3), and the gaming laws definition (§ 52-53)."],"issue_tags":["licensing","tax","integrity","prop"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"The closest analogue on the list is IL HB2342 (Fantasy Sports Consumer Protection Act), which would likewise hand fantasy oversight to the state gaming regulator with fees, civil penalties and a tax on adjusted gross fantasy receipts; Virginia's version, unlike that proposal, is enacted. FL H0189 addresses fantasy sports only as one piece of an omnibus gambling rewrite. Most other listed bills, such as GA HB 686, HI HB 1308 and AK HB145/SB194, authorize sports wagering rather than restructuring fantasy regulation. Virginia's move is distinctive in consolidating fantasy under the same lottery agency that already runs sports betting and casinos, with a single statewide self-exclusion list.","watch":"Watch how the Department applies the § 58.1-4200 definition, particularly the bans on player-versus-operator play and on outcomes based solely on a single athlete's single performance, since it could exclude pick'em and prop-style products; also confirm the tax rate, civil penalty amounts and effective/transition dates in §§ 58.1-4204 to 58.1-4214, which fall outside the excerpt.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":61658,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:06:48.760Z"},"VA-HB161":{"summary":"The Senate Finance substitute for HB161 would authorize internet casino gaming (iGaming) in Virginia, licensed by the Virginia Lottery Board and tethered to the casino operators in the four eligible host cities under § 58.1-4107 A 1-4. Each operator could run up to three skins (a fourth if run with a 'qualified entertainment company'). Revenue would be taxed at 20% plus a 6% economic development fee that funds a Casino Gaming Hold Harmless Fund. The bill also declares unlicensed paid-entry sweepstakes to be illegal internet gaming. It would not take effect unless reenacted by the 2027 Session, and it ultimately failed in conference on 2026-03-14.","mechanics":["Licensing (§§ 58.1-4143, 58.1-4144): the operator license goes only to the casino licensees in host cities A1-4, issued as an amendment to the casino license, for a $500,000 initial fee and $250,000 renewal over a 5-year term. The platform provider license costs a $2 million application fee and a $1 million renewal per platform. Qualified internet gaming entities licensed in two or more U.S. states can get a one-year temporary license for $10,000. Applicants filing in the first 30 days get a simultaneous launch.","Platforms (§ 58.1-4143 D-E): up to three platforms per operator, each under a single brand plus an optional second brand for poker. No casino co-branding is required. A fourth platform is allowed only if the platform provider contracts with a qualified entertainment company, which must meet a size threshold, conduct community outreach and file a DEI plan.","Tax and fees (§§ 58.1-4152 to 58.1-4157): 20% of adjusted gross internet gaming revenue, allocated 3% to the Gaming Regulatory Fund, 2% to problem gambling and 95% to a new Modern Public Education Fund, and in lieu of other taxes. A separate 6% fee funds equal monthly hold-harmless payments to the casinos until July 1, 2032. After that date, casinos must show a revenue loss against FY ending June 30, 2027. Recipients must put at least 25% of payments toward promotional spending.","Operator conditions: a job-loss mitigation plan, published by the Director. At least 25% of promotional free play must be redeemable online or at the associated land-based casino. Accounts may be shared with sports betting (§ 58.1-4146 E). Players must be 21+, located in-state and geolocated. Dormant account balances are split 50/50 between the operator and the general fund. No internet gaming terminals are allowed in places of public accommodation other than the operator's own casino.","Responsible gaming (§ 58.1-4147): Board regulations covering advertising guidelines, deposit limits and time-outs. Operators must use player data and automated triggers to flag at-risk players and apply a three-phase intervention: communications, video tutorial, then access to a responsible gaming professional. Operators also need pop-up break-in-play messages and 12-month account statements.","Enforcement (§§ 58.1-4102(8), 58.1-4149 to 58.1-4154): the Board gets cease-and-desist and injunction power against unlicensed iGaming, sports betting or sweepstakes. Unlicensed iGaming is a Class 6 felony, with fines up to $25,000 for individuals and $1 million for entities. Tampering is a Class 5 felony, with fines up to $50,000 or $200,000. Unlicensed paid-entry sweepstakes carry civil penalties of $100,000 for the first offense and $250,000 for each later offense, with each day a separate violation. Regulations are due by January 1, 2028, and the act requires 2027 reenactment."],"issue_tags":["legalize","licensing","tax","sweeps","integrity","advertising"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike the listed sports-wagering legalization bills (AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, HI HB 1308/SB1569), HB161 legalizes iGaming on top of an existing Virginia sports betting regime. Its closest analogues are Illinois's Internet Gaming Act bills, HB3080 and HB4797, which also authorize operators and management or platform providers. The Virginia substitute differs in several ways: it tethers licenses to the four host-city casinos, uses a 6% hold-harmless fee that pays casinos directly, requires a job-loss mitigation plan, and mandates a cross-channel free-play allocation. Its sweepstakes ban and three-phase responsible gaming intervention go further than the advertising and account-withdrawal limits in CT HB05229.","watch":"Even though the bill failed in conference, watch how a 2026 or 2027 version handles several points: the market-access constraints (casino tethering, three skins, a fourth only through the DEI-linked qualified entertainment company); the combined 26% effective burden and the $2 million platform fee; the mandated player-data intervention protocols; and the Board's new cease-and-desist authority over unlicensed sports betting and sweepstakes, which could reach sweepstakes-adjacent products and affiliates.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Comm Sub","text_chars":44277,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:06:59.198Z"},"VA-HB271":{"summary":"HB271, as substituted by House General Laws on February 12, 2026, creates a Virginia Gaming Commission and recodifies Virginia gambling law into a new Title 29.5. The new title (§§ 29.5-100 through 29.5-913) replaces the current statutes on illegal gambling, charitable gaming, the Lottery, sports betting, horse racing and casino gaming, which the bill repeals. The excerpt provided consists mostly of conforming amendments, so the substantive regulatory terms in Title 29.5 cannot be reviewed from this text.","mechanics":["Repeals the current gaming chapters and folds them into new Title 29.5: Va. Code § 18.2-325 et seq. (illegal gambling), § 18.2-340.15 et seq. (charitable gaming), Ch. 40 of Title 58.1 (Lottery), Ch. 41 of Title 58.1 (§§ 58.1-4100 to -4141, sports betting), Ch. 29 and 29.1 of Title 59.1 (racing), Ch. 51 of Title 59.1 (casino gaming), and §§ 2.2-2455 and -2456.","Title 29.5 has three subtitles. Subtitle I is Chapters 1 to 6 (§§ 29.5-100 to -650), with charitable gaming at Chapter 2 (§ 29.5-200 et seq.). Subtitle II is Chapters 7 and 8 (§§ 29.5-700 to -814), where lottery crimes now sit at §§ 29.5-713 and 29.5-803 to -807. Subtitle III is Chapter 9 (§§ 29.5-900 to -913).","Revenue now earmarked for the Virginia Indigenous People's Trust Fund (§ 2.2-401.01(D)) is cross-referenced to § 29.5-327 instead of § 58.1-4125, the current sports betting tax allocation section. This suggests the sports betting tax provisions move to Chapter 3 of Title 29.5, but the rate and distribution are not shown in the excerpt.","Charitable gaming oversight moves from the Department of Agriculture and Consumer Services to the Gaming Commission and a 'Virginia Gaming Commission Board', including approval of supplier equipment (§ 2.2-3705.6(14), § 29.5-225).","The Attorney General may assist in prosecuting violations of Title 29.5 with the concurrence of the local Commonwealth's attorney (§ 2.2-511(A)(xvii)).","Gaming Commission licensing investigations are added to the FOIA exclusion in § 2.2-3705.3(1), and the Virginia Racing Commission is placed under the Secretary of Agriculture and Forestry (§ 2.2-203.3)."],"issue_tags":["licensing","tax","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. The bill is unusual for its scope: it does not adjust one gaming vertical but dismantles Virginia's fragmented structure of Lottery, sports betting, casino, charitable gaming, racing and criminal gambling statutes. It replaces that structure with a single recodified title under a new consolidated Gaming Commission.","watch":"Because the provided text cuts off before the operative Title 29.5 provisions, operators should obtain the full substitute and check whether the recodification preserves current sports betting permits, regulations, tax rate and § 29.5-327 revenue allocation unchanged, and how the transition from the Virginia Lottery to the new Commission and Board is handled when the bill is taken up in the 2027 session.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Comm Sub","text_chars":910738,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:07:00.739Z"},"VA-HB281":{"summary":"VA HB281 (Chapter 1041, approved April 22, 2026) amends Va. Code § 55.1-1241 of the Virginia Residential Landlord and Tenant Act. It governs when a tenant may defend an action for possession or rent for nonpayment by showing that the landlord failed to remedy dangerous conditions or material noncompliance, and it sets the remedies the court may order. The bill has no connection to sports betting or gaming.","mechanics":["Subsection A: the defense covers conditions that are a fire hazard or a serious threat to life, health or safety, including lack of heat, running water, light, electricity or sewage disposal, rodent infestation, and material noncompliance by the landlord with the rental agreement or law.","Precondition: before suit, the landlord must have received the tenant's written notice or a state or local violation or condemnation notice and refused, or unreasonably failed, to fix the condition; there is a rebuttable presumption that more than 30 days after the landlord receives notice is unreasonable.","A tenant in possession must pay into court the rent found due, held pending the court's order under subsection C.","Subsection B: the landlord defeats the defense by showing the condition does not exist, has been remedied, was caused by the tenant or the tenant's household or guests, or that the tenant unreasonably refused entry.","Subsection C remedies: equitable rent reduction, termination or surrender at the tenant's request, referral to an agency with a continuance and rent escrow (funds usable for mortgage, lien or repairs), or an order that the landlord remedy the condition.","Fee-shifting: costs and attorney fees may be imposed on a bad-faith tenant (D) or, at the court's discretion, on the landlord when the tenant prevails (E); subsection F preserves the landlord's right to amend the amount sought at trial to include ongoing rent."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills is comparable. They cover gaming and wagering matters such as Hawaii's sports wagering bills (HB 1308, HB 2570, SB1569), Illinois' internet gaming and prediction-market bills (HB3080, HB5059), and Maryland's promotional-play and problem-gambling bills (HB291, HB1409), while HB281 is purely a residential landlord-tenant habitability and eviction-defense measure. Its likely appearance on a gaming tracker is a keyword or classification error; the text never mentions wagering, gaming or gambling.","watch":"Nothing for sports-betting operators; this bill can be dropped from the gaming tracker unless the client has residential rental property exposure in Virginia.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":4895,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:07:01.645Z"},"VA-HB30":{"summary":"HB30 is Virginia's 2026 Appropriation Act, appropriating about $206.9 billion across the 2026-28 biennium (FY2027-FY2028) and amending numerous Code sections, many in Title 4.1 (alcohol and cannabis) and Title 58.1 (tax). In the portion of the text provided, gaming appears only incidentally: Item 1 funds a legislative joint subcommittee studying a Virginia Gaming Commission, and § 3 lists Lottery Proceeds Fund revenues. The text shows the bill was chaptered as Chapter 1 of the 2026 Special Session I, approved June 29, 2026, even though the listed status says it passed only one chamber.","mechanics":["§ 7 allocates total appropriations of $206,907,394,349 for the 2026-28 biennium: $75.17 billion general fund and $131.74 billion nongeneral fund.","§ 3 lists Lottery Proceeds Fund revenues of $887,725,168 in FY2027 and $877,725,168 in FY2028, totaling $1,765,450,336, as part of nongeneral fund resources.","Item 1, paragraph P.1 appropriates $15,000 in each year from the general fund to the Joint Subcommittee to Study the Feasibility of Establishing the Virginia Gaming Commission, authorized by HJR 10 (2024).","The title lists amended tax provisions (§§ 58.1-322.03, 58.1-339.8, 58.1-602, 58.1-605, 58.1-605.1, 58.1-606.1, 58.1-301) and consumer-protection provisions (§ 59.1-200); none is identified in the provided text as gaming-related.","The provided text is truncated partway through Item 1, so any later items on the Virginia Lottery, sports betting tax distributions, or problem-gambling funding were not reviewed."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states were listed. What stands out is that Virginia keeps funding, through the budget, a legislative study of whether to consolidate gaming oversight into a standalone Virginia Gaming Commission, rather than leaving it with the Virginia Lottery. The bill itself makes no change to that structure in the portion provided.","watch":"Watch the Gaming Commission feasibility study funded in Item 1.P, and review the untruncated agency items (Virginia Lottery and Department of Taxation) for any changes to sports betting tax revenue distribution, problem-gambling fund transfers, or Lottery staffing that the excerpt does not show.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":378569,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:07:04.146Z"},"VA-HB336":{"summary":"HB336, as substituted by House Rules on February 6, 2026, creates a Fetal and Infant Health Data and Quality Improvement Review Team in new Code § 32.1-283.9 to review fetal and infant deaths. It also amends the Virginia Freedom of Information Act so that information the Team gathers is excluded from mandatory disclosure (§ 2.2-3705.5(7)) and so that the Team may discuss individual fetal and infant death cases in closed meetings (§ 2.2-3711(A)(21)), with a conforming change to § 2.2-4002. The bill has no substantive gaming content and was incorporated into HB1398 by House Appropriations on February 11, 2026.","mechanics":["Adds new § 32.1-283.9 establishing the Fetal and Infant Health Data and Quality Improvement Review Team, with duties, confidentiality rules, a penalty and a reporting requirement; the text of that section is truncated in the excerpt provided.","Amends § 2.2-3705.5(7)(v) to exclude information acquired by the Team from mandatory FOIA disclosure to the extent § 32.1-283.9 makes it confidential.","Amends § 2.2-3711(A)(21), in both the version effective until July 1, 2026 and the version effective July 1, 2026, to authorize closed meetings for discussion of individual fetal and infant death cases.","Amends § 2.2-4002, apparently a conforming change.","The only references to wagering are the unchanged existing text of § 2.2-3711(A)(53)-(54), which already lets the Virginia Lottery Board hold closed deliberations on casino gaming and sports betting license denials, suspensions and revocations; the bill does not alter those subdivisions.","Status: incorporated into HB1398 (Franklin) by House Appropriations on a voice vote, February 11, 2026."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. The bill is a public-health fatality-review measure modeled on Virginia's existing Maternal Mortality Review Team (§ 32.1-283.8) and other fatality review teams. Its only connection to gaming is that it reprints the full closed-meetings statute, which already contains Lottery Board provisions for casino and sports betting licensing appeals.","watch":"Nothing operator-specific is at stake here, but confirm that the vehicle bill HB1398 does not alter the Virginia Lottery Board's existing closed-meeting authority under § 2.2-3711(A)(53)-(54) for casino and sports betting license actions.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Comm Sub","text_chars":83979,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:07:12.103Z"},"VA-HB32":{"summary":"Virginia HB32 (Chapter 907, approved April 13, 2026) amends Code § 37.2-304(14) to add two members to the Problem Gambling Treatment and Support Advisory Committee, which the Commissioner of Behavioral Health and Developmental Services must maintain so that treatment providers and legal gaming operators can work together to reduce problem gambling harms. The new seats go to a representative of the Virginia Council on Problem Gambling and a representative of the Virginia State Police Gaming Enforcement Unit. The bill creates no new obligations, fees or restrictions for operators.","mechanics":["Amends Va. Code § 37.2-304(14), part of the DBHDS Commissioner's statutory duties to establish and maintain the Advisory Committee.","Adds a new clause (iii), a representative from the Virginia Council on Problem Gambling, and renumbers the existing clauses (iii) through (viii) as (iv) through (ix).","Adds a new clause (x), a representative from the Virginia State Police Gaming Enforcement Unit.","Existing membership is unchanged. It still includes one representative from each gaming regulator (lottery, casino, sports betting, pari-mutuel racing, historical horse racing, charitable gaming), one casino operator, one sports betting permit holder, one HHR operator and one charitable gaming permit holder.","The DBHDS Problem Gambling Prevention Coordinator remains chair, and a vice-chair is elected from the members.","The text contains no special effective-date clause, no funding change, and no enforcement provisions."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"core","comparison":"VA HB32 is a narrow governance change that only alters who sits on an advisory body. By contrast, Maryland's HB1409/SB862 mandate prevalence studies of mobile gambling and address fund revenue, and MD HB758 changes how Problem Gambling Fund money may be spent. Other bills impose direct operator duties, such as NJ A 4003, which bars incentive-based wagering offers to patrons using responsible-gaming tools, and MD HB518, which requires a voluntary exclusion list. HB32 resembles study- and process-oriented measures like LA SR 178 and MD HB 195, but it is even more limited because it creates no new study, mandate or funding.","watch":"Adding the State Police Gaming Enforcement Unit and an advocacy group (the Virginia Council on Problem Gambling) alongside the single sports betting permit holder seat could shift the committee's recommendations toward enforcement and harm reduction, which may later feed into operator-facing rules or legislation, so operators should keep active engagement through that seat.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":7359,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:07:14.108Z"},"VA-HB513":{"summary":"HB513 (Acts 2026, Chapter 158, approved April 6, 2026) authorizes local and regional suicide fatality review teams in a new Code of Virginia section, 32.1-283.9, which sets out membership and a penalty. It also amends Virginia's FOIA so that team information is shielded from disclosure and team discussions of individual deaths can happen in closed meetings. It is a public-health and open-government bill with no sports-betting or gaming provisions of its own.","mechanics":["Adds new §32.1-283.9 establishing local and regional suicide fatality review teams, including membership and a penalty per the title. The operative text of that section is not in the excerpt provided, so its specifics (for example what the penalty applies to) cannot be confirmed here.","Amends §2.2-3705.5(7) by adding clause (vii), which excludes from mandatory FOIA disclosure information acquired during a death review by a local or regional suicide fatality review team, to the extent §32.1-283.9 makes it confidential.","Amends §2.2-3711(A)(21), in both the version effective until July 1, 2026 and the version effective on that date, to allow closed meetings for the portions in which individual death cases are discussed by these teams.","The reprinted §2.2-3711 includes existing subdivisions 17, 53 and 54, which cover closed Virginia Lottery Board deliberations on lottery, casino and sports-betting license appeals and investigations. These are carried over unchanged and are not amended by HB513."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills is comparable. AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686 and the HI bills authorize and tax sports wagering, while the IL bills address licensing, prediction markets and internet gaming. The closest in theme are IL HB1451, HB2580 and HB5368, which direct the Department of Human Services to run gambling-disorder education. HB513 is different in kind: it creates suicide death-review bodies and FOIA exemptions without mentioning gambling, and its only gaming references are pre-existing Lottery Board closed-meeting provisions reprinted in §2.2-3711.","watch":"There is little here for operators beyond confirming that the full text of §32.1-283.9 does not let suicide review teams demand player or account records from licensed sports-betting operators, and noting that the existing closed-meeting protection for Lottery Board sports-betting license appeals (§2.2-3711(A)(54)) is untouched.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":68363,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:07:15.679Z"},"VA-HB515":{"summary":"HB515, signed as Chapter 926 of the 2026 Acts of Assembly, bars credit cards as a funding method for Virginia sports betting accounts. It directs the Lottery Director not to approve credit cards as a deposit method under § 58.1-4031(D), and requires permit holders under § 58.1-4034(A)(7) to take reasonable measures to prohibit accepting credit cards to fund accounts on their platforms.","mechanics":["§ 58.1-4031(D): the Director still approves account-funding methods (ACH, debit cards, wire transfers and other Board-approved methods) but now 'shall not approve the use of credit cards' to fund sports betting accounts.","§ 58.1-4034(A)(7): permit holders 'shall take reasonable measures to prohibit the acceptance of credit cards' to fund sports betting accounts on their platforms. This is a reasonable-measures standard, not strict liability.","The chaptered text as reproduced still lists 'credit cards' among the enumerated methods in both sections; the amendments' strike-throughs are not visible, so the operative effect is carried by the new prohibitory sentences.","Technical change: 'clearing house' becomes 'clearinghouse' in both sections.","No special effective date or enactment clause is included, so the default Virginia effective date for regular-session acts (July 1) would ordinarily apply.","Enforcement runs through existing permit tools, including the Director's power to suspend, revoke or refuse to renew permits under § 58.1-4031(B)(2) and Board regulations."],"issue_tags":["licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"The closest listed analogue is CT HB05229, which also targets account-level consumer protections, but it addresses withdrawals from gaming accounts, customer-service lines, campus advertising and AI-targeted bets rather than deposit methods. The AK (HB145, SB194), GA (HB 686) and HI (HB 1308, HB 2570, SB1569) bills are full authorization and licensing frameworks. Illinois's many bills deal with license fees, taxes, prediction markets and iGaming. Virginia's bill is unusual as a narrow, single-issue payments restriction layered onto an existing mature market.","watch":"Watch how the Board and Director define 'reasonable measures' in regulations, especially for indirect credit funding through e-wallets (e.g., PayPal balances funded by credit card), prepaid cards loaded by credit, and card-type detection via BIN screening, since compliance gaps could expose permits to action under § 58.1-4031(B)(2).","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":8830,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:07:19.275Z"},"VA-HB642":{"summary":"HB642 is an omnibus cannabis bill. It would amend dozens of Virginia Code sections and add new chapters to Title 4.1 to create a regulated adult-use retail marijuana market under the Virginia Cannabis Control Authority, with related criminal-penalty changes and a reporting requirement. The version provided is a Governor's substitute dated April 13, 2026, and the Governor ultimately vetoed the bill on May 19, 2026; it does not address sports betting or gaming.","mechanics":["Adds new Title 4.1 provisions: §§ 4.1-629 and 4.1-630, Chapter 7 (§§ 4.1-700 and 4.1-701), Chapter 10 (§§ 4.1-1000 through 4.1-1007), Chapter 12 (§§ 4.1-1200 through 4.1-1206), new Chapter 13 sections, and Chapter 17 (§§ 4.1-1700 through 4.1-1704), together building out the retail cannabis licensing and regulatory framework.","Repeals Article 4 of Chapter 41.1 of Title 3.2 and §§ 3.2-5145.4:1, 4.1-611, 4.1-1101.1, 4.1-1105.1, 18.2-248.1 and 18.2-251.1, and amends many Title 18.2 and Title 19.2 criminal-law sections on possession, distribution, search and seizure, forfeiture and records.","Brings the Virginia Cannabis Control Authority in line with the ABC Authority on personnel matters: its employees are added to the state employee health plan (§ 2.2-2818 D), and its CEO, agents, officers and employees are exempted from the Virginia Personnel Act (§ 2.2-2905, new subdivision 29).","Adds members of the Cannabis Control Authority Board of Directors to the statement-of-economic-interests filing requirement in § 2.2-3114 A, alongside the Virginia Lottery Board and the ABC Board.","Amends § 58.1-301 (tax conformity) and adds § 6.2-108 (banking) and § 54.1-4426. The specific tax rates and banking terms are not visible in the truncated text provided."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills were listed. The bill's only contact with gaming is incidental: existing statutory lists that already name the Virginia Lottery and Lottery Board (§§ 2.2-2905 and 2.2-3114) are amended to add the Cannabis Control Authority alongside them. What is unusual is the procedural posture: the Governor rewrote the bill through a substitute and then vetoed it anyway.","watch":"Because the bill was vetoed and nothing in the provided text touches wagering, there is little to monitor beyond the general precedent of Virginia housing a new vice-industry regulator outside the Personnel Act and subjecting its board to the same disclosure regime as the Lottery Board.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Comm Sub","text_chars":691287,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:07:27.357Z"},"VA-HB65":{"summary":"Chapter 23 (HB 65) repeals the Virginia State Apple Board and the Apple Fund by repealing Chapter 12 of Title 3.2 (§§ 3.2-1200 through 3.2-1217), with the repeal taking effect on a delayed date of July 1, 2028. It also makes conforming amendments elsewhere in the Code, including Virginia's FOIA exclusions and closed-meeting provisions. The bill has nothing to do with sports betting or gaming regulation.","mechanics":["Repeals Chapter 12 of Title 3.2 (§§ 3.2-1200 through 3.2-1217), which establishes the Virginia State Apple Board and the Apple Fund; per the title, the repeal is effective July 1, 2028.","Deletes the FOIA exclusion in § 2.2-3705.6(15) for apple producer sales information provided to the Apple Board under § 3.2-1215. The excerpt renumbers the following subdivisions, which suggests the obsolete E-911 CMRS trade-secret exclusion is also removed.","Updates cross-references in the closed-meeting statute, § 2.2-3711 (both the current and future versions), to match the renumbered § 2.2-3705.6 subdivisions. For example, the Commonwealth Health Research Board reference changes from subdivision 17 to 16.","Makes conforming amendments to §§ 3.2-1100, 3.2-1104, 3.2-1105, 3.2-1106, 10.1-1458, and 15.2-2103.1.","The bill title references a report requirement, but its details are not in the excerpt provided.","Approved March 31, 2026, as Acts of Assembly Chapter 23."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills is comparable. Bills such as AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, HI HB 1308/2570, MS HB 4074, and IL HB5443 authorize, tax, or regulate wagering, while VA HB65 is an agricultural-board sunset. Its only contact with gaming is that it reprints, without substantive change, the existing FOIA exclusion for charitable gaming supplier information (§ 2.2-3705.6(14)) and the Virginia Lottery Board closed-meeting authority (§ 2.2-3711(A)(17)).","watch":"Nothing substantive for sports-betting operators; at most, confirm that the § 2.2-3705.6 renumbering does not disturb any FOIA or closed-meeting cross-references relied on for confidential Virginia Lottery or gaming submissions.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":89693,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:07:27.742Z"},"VA-HB972":{"summary":"HB972 (Chapter 1120, approved May 14, 2026) is a Virginia election-administration law that restructures voter registration list maintenance, cancellation procedures, required record matches, identification information, and data standards in Title 24.2. It has nothing substantive to do with gaming. Sports betting appears only because the bill reenacts the whole closed-meetings statute, § 2.2-3711, to update a single cross-reference.","mechanics":["Amends § 2.2-3711(A)(34), in both the version effective until July 1, 2026 and the version effective on that date, so that the State Board of Elections' and local electoral boards' closed-meeting authority for voting security matters cites new § 24.2-407.2 instead of § 24.2-410.2, which is repealed.","Adds new § 24.2-407.2 and a new Article 2.1 in Chapter 4 of Title 24.2 containing §§ 24.2-407.3 and 24.2-410.3 through 24.2-410.6, plus new § 24.2-426.1. The substantive text of these sections is not included in the excerpt provided.","Repeals §§ 24.2-404.3, 24.2-404.4, 24.2-410.2, 24.2-428, 24.2-428.1 and 24.2-428.2, and amends §§ 24.2-401, -404, -408, -409, -410, -410.1, -427, -435, -444, -610, -611, -703.1 and -709.","The reenacted § 2.2-3711(A)(54) carries forward, apparently unchanged, the Virginia Lottery Board's authority to deliberate in closed session on appeals under § 58.1-4007 involving denial, revocation, suspension or non-renewal of sports betting licenses or permits, and to discuss related investigations excluded from disclosure under § 2.2-3705.3(1). Subdivisions 17 (lottery) and 53 (casino) are likewise carried forward."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills is comparable, because HB972 is an election-law bill rather than a gaming bill. Bills such as AK HB145 and SB194, GA HB 686 and HI HB 1308 and SB1569 authorize and tax sports wagering, and IL HB5059 and HB5142 address prediction markets. HB972's only connection to wagering is that it republishes Virginia's closed-meeting exemptions, including the existing Lottery Board exemption for sports betting license appeals in § 2.2-3711(A)(54).","watch":"Nothing in this bill changes sports betting regulation, so the only thing to confirm is that the reenacted § 2.2-3711(A)(54) closed-session protection for Lottery Board sports betting license appeals survives unaltered in both effective-date versions; the excerpt provided is truncated before that subdivision appears.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":108842,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:07:28.507Z"},"VA-SB118":{"summary":"The House General Laws substitute for SB118 would authorize internet casino gaming (iGaming) in Virginia under a new Article 12 of Chapter 41, Title 58.1 (§§ 58.1-4142 through 58.1-4159). Licenses would be available only to casino operators in the host cities in § 58.1-4107(A)(1)-(4), with up to three skins each and a fourth tied to a 'qualified entertainment company.' The bill taxes adjusted gross internet gaming revenue at 20% plus a 6% economic development fee, bans unlicensed sweepstakes, and does not take effect unless reenacted by the 2027 Session; it ultimately failed in conference on 2026-03-14.","mechanics":["Licensing (§§ 58.1-4143 to -4145): The operator license is an amendment to an existing casino license, with a $500,000 initial fee and $250,000 renewal. Each platform costs $2M, prorated, plus $1M per renewal. Platform providers pay a $2M application fee. An entity licensed in two or more US jurisdictions can get a temporary one-year platform license for $10,000. Terms run five years.","Platforms: Each operator may offer up to three platforms, each under one brand plus an optional separate poker brand, with no co-branding required. A fourth platform is allowed only if run with a 'qualified entertainment company' that meets DEI or minority-investment plan criteria. Applicants must submit a job-loss mitigation plan and signed labor peace agreements covering the casino and any live game studio.","Tax and fees (§§ 58.1-4153, -4158): A 20% tax on adjusted gross internet gaming revenue is in lieu of other taxes. The uncollectible-receivables deduction is capped at 2% unless the Board sets otherwise. A separate 6% fee goes to a Casino Gaming Hold Harmless Fund, which pays equal monthly amounts to eligible casinos until July 1, 2032, and then requires proof of revenue loss against 2025-26 or year 3-4 baselines.","Tax distribution (§ 58.1-4154): 5% goes to problem gambling. Until January 1, 2037, 6% goes to an Internet Lottery Hold Harmless Fund and 89% to the general fund; after that date, 95% goes to the general fund.","Penalties and enforcement: Offering iGaming without a license is a Class 6 felony, with fines up to $25,000, or $1M for entities. Tampering is a Class 5 felony, with fines up to $50,000, or $200,000 for entities, and suspension of the casino license. Unlicensed paid-entry sweepstakes are deemed illegal internet gaming, with civil penalties up to $100,000 for a first offense and $250,000 thereafter, each day counted separately. New § 58.1-4102(8) lets the Board seek cease-and-desist orders and injunctions against unlicensed iGaming, sports betting or sweepstakes.","Player protections and timing: Players must be 21 or older and geolocated; the bill also requires debit and online payment options, a single wallet shared with sports betting accounts, responsible-gaming triggers in three intervention phases, the 1-800-MY-RESET helpline, and dormant account balances split 50/50 between operator and state. Regulations are due January 1, 2028 (exempt from the Administrative Process Act), notices of intent open July 1, 2027, and reenactment in 2027 is required."],"issue_tags":["legalize","licensing","tax","sweeps","integrity","advertising"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Like IL HB3080 and HB4797 (Internet Gaming Act), SB118 creates a full iGaming licensing regime. SB118 differs by tethering licenses strictly to existing casino operators with capped skins and by stacking a 6% casino hold-harmless fee and a lottery hold-harmless carve-out on top of a 20% tax. Its explicit civil ban on dual-currency sweepstakes, with per-day penalties, is a more direct sweepstakes attack than the general internet gambling penalty revisions described in FL H0189 and FL S1580. Its responsible-gaming mandates overlap with CT HB05229's consumer-protection focus, though SB118 does not address AI targeting or campus advertising.","watch":"Watch whether the 2027 Session revives or reenacts this framework, particularly the 26% effective rate, the three-skin cap with the qualified-entertainment-company fourth skin, labor peace preconditions, the conflict between the 60-day-from-enactment launch window in enactment clause 4 and the July 1, 2027 notice-of-intent start, and the sweepstakes and unlicensed sports-betting enforcement powers that could be split off into standalone legislation.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Comm Sub","text_chars":49065,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:07:41.151Z"},"VA-SB30":{"summary":"SB30 is the Governor's introduced biennial budget bill for Virginia's fiscal years ending June 30, 2027 and June 30, 2028, appropriating general fund and nongeneral fund revenues across state government. It also amends several Code of Virginia tax provisions, mostly income tax and sales tax sections, and repeals the third enactments of Chapters 256 and 257 of the 2022 Acts of Assembly for taxable years beginning on or after January 1, 2027. The excerpt provided contains only the enacting clause and the opening appropriation sections, with no gaming or wagering provisions.","mechanics":["Appropriates for the biennium ending June 30, 2028, pursuant to § 2.2-1509, including unexpended prior balances and all taxes and nontax moneys (fees, licenses, grants) coming into the treasury.","Defines 'moneys' to include projected revenues from proposed legislation contingent on General Assembly passage (§ 1.B).","Amends § 58.1-416 and §§ 58.1-322.02, 58.1-322.03, 58.1-332, 58.1-339.8, 58.1-390.3, 58.1-422.4, 58.1-422.5 and 58.1-609.3, which are income tax, pass-through, withholding and sales tax exemption provisions, not gaming tax sections.","Repeals the third enactments of 2022 Acts Chapters 256 and 257, effective for taxable years beginning on and after January 1, 2027.","Status: left in Senate Finance and Appropriations on March 14, 2026, so this vehicle is effectively dead for the session."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No other states' bills are listed for comparison. The bill is a general appropriations act, and the supplied text contains no wagering provisions. Any gaming relevance would sit in budget items not reproduced here, such as Virginia Lottery appropriations or allocations of sports betting tax revenue.","watch":"Because the excerpt is truncated, review the full item-level text and the enacted budget vehicle for any Virginia Lottery appropriations, changes to sports betting tax revenue distributions (for example to the Problem Gambling Treatment and Support Fund), or skill-game and gaming language that could be added through budget amendments rather than standalone legislation.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":2299,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:07:41.924Z"},"VA-SB195":{"summary":"SB195 would consolidate Virginia's gaming regulation into a new Title 29.5 creating a Virginia Gaming Commission, recodifying and repealing the existing lottery (Ch. 40, Title 58.1), sports betting (Ch. 41, §§ 58.1-4100 through 58.1-4141), horse racing (Ch. 29, Title 59.1), casino (Ch. 51, §§ 59.1-556 through 59.1-570), charitable gaming (Art. 1.1:1, Ch. 8, Title 18.2), and illegal gambling (Art. 1, Ch. 8, Title 18.2) statutes. The supplied text is truncated after the conforming amendments, so the substantive Title 29.5 provisions on licensing, taxes, and penalties are not visible and cannot be summarized here.","mechanics":["Adds Title 29.5 with Subtitle I (Chs. 1-6, §§ 29.5-100 to 29.5-650), Subtitle II (Chs. 7-8, §§ 29.5-700 to 29.5-814), and Subtitle III (Ch. 9, §§ 29.5-900 to 29.5-913), and repeals §§ 2.2-2455 and 2.2-2456 along with the existing gaming chapters.","The sports betting tax revenue reference that feeds the Virginia Indigenous People's Trust Fund moves from § 58.1-4125 to § 29.5-327 (§ 2.2-401.01 D). This suggests sports betting tax distribution is recodified in Chapter 3 of the new title.","Amends § 2.2-511 to give the Attorney General authority, with the local Commonwealth's attorney's concurrence, to assist in prosecuting violations of Title 29.5 (new clause xvii).","FOIA amendments (§ 2.2-3705.3(1)) shield Virginia Gaming Commission investigations of license applicants and licensees, replacing references to the Lottery's Chapter 40/41 role. Lottery crime references are remapped to §§ 29.5-713 and 29.5-803 through 29.5-807.","Charitable gaming oversight shifts from the Department of Agriculture and Consumer Services to the Gaming Commission and its Board, including supplier proprietary information under § 29.5-225 (§ 2.2-3705.6(14)).","Adds the Virginia Racing Commission to the agencies under the Secretary of Agriculture and Forestry (§ 2.2-203.3)."],"issue_tags":["licensing","tax","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"No other states' bills are listed for comparison. What stands out is the scope: rather than amending sports betting rules piecemeal, SB195 folds lottery, sports betting, casinos, racing, charitable gaming, and the criminal gambling statutes into one omnibus title under a single new regulator. Few states attempt a recodification that broad in a single bill.","watch":"Obtain the full Title 29.5 text to check whether the transfer from the Virginia Lottery to the new Commission alters existing sports betting permit terms, tax rates or deductions under the recodified § 29.5-327, transition and grandfathering of current permits, and the penalty and AG enforcement provisions.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":909935,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:07:44.409Z"},"VA-SB129":{"summary":"SB129 repeals Virginia's Fantasy Contests Act (Chapter 51 of Title 59.1) and recreates fantasy contest regulation as new Chapter 42 of Title 58.1 (§§ 58.1-4200 through 58.1-4214). This places permitting, investigation and enforcement under the Virginia Lottery Board and Department, the same body that regulates sports betting and casinos. It adds a $50,000 application fee, a 21+ age floor, operator integrity and player-fund controls, and, per the title and cross-references, a tax and civil penalties whose details fall outside the excerpt provided.","mechanics":["Definition (§ 58.1-4200): a fantasy contest must be online and charge an entry fee. Prizes must be fixed and disclosed in advance, and outcomes must rest on accumulated statistical results of individuals. No outcome may be based on a single team's score or point spread, or solely on a single athlete's performance in a single event, and no player may compete against the operator.","Permit (§§ 58.1-4202, 58.1-4203): no operator may offer contests without a Department permit, valid for three years. A nonrefundable $50,000 initial application fee goes to the Problem Gambling Treatment and Support Fund. A lapsed permit requires a fresh application unless an extension is granted. Knowingly falsifying an application is a Class 1 misdemeanor.","Permit conditions (§ 58.1-4202(D)): operators must verify players are 21 or older and bar themselves, employees and household relatives from cash-prize contests. They must block the sharing of confidential information, keep athletes out of contests based on their own team's statistics, and offer self-restriction. They must also disclose entry limits, segregate player funds, and hold a reserve sufficient to pay all prizes.","Revenue base: fantasy contest revenue means entry fees minus prizes paid, multiplied by a location percentage (the share of entry fees from players in Virginia). The tax rate in § 58.1-4208 is not in the excerpt, but § 37.2-314.2 shows at least some § 58.1-4208 moneys go to the Problem Gambling Fund. A principal stockholder is anyone holding 15% or more.","Board powers (§ 58.1-4201): the Board can investigate, take sworn statements, issue subpoenas (hearings held in Richmond), seek injunctions in circuit court, and pursue contempt in Richmond Circuit Court.","Conforming changes: FOIA exemption for licensing investigations under § 2.2-3705.3 (current and July 1, 2026 versions). New § 11-16.3 exempts lawful contests from the gaming-contracts chapter. State Police gaming-law definition (§ 52-53), the voluntary exclusion programs (§§ 58.1-4015.1, 58.1-4103) and the sports betting definitions (§ 58.1-4030) are updated to point to § 58.1-4200."],"issue_tags":["licensing","tax","integrity","prop"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"SB129 is the closest analogue to IL HB2342 (Fantasy Sports Consumer Protection Act), which likewise gives an existing gaming regulator, the Illinois Gaming Board, power over fantasy operators, with fees, civil penalties and a tax on adjusted gross fantasy receipts. Unlike the Illinois proposal, Virginia's bill is enacted and folds fantasy into the same agency, exclusion list and State Police enforcement as sports betting and casinos. FL H0189 also touches fantasy sports, but as one piece of an omnibus gambling rewrite. The remaining listed bills (e.g., GA HB 686, HI HB 1308, AK HB145/SB194) authorize sports wagering rather than regulating fantasy.","watch":"Watch how the Lottery Board applies the § 58.1-4200 bars on player-versus-operator contests and outcomes based solely on a single athlete's single-event performance to pick'em and prop-style products. Also confirm, against the full chaptered text, the § 58.1-4208 tax rate and the civil penalty amounts that were not in the excerpt.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":61658,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:07:51.877Z"},"VA-SB375":{"summary":"SB375, as substituted by Senate Finance and Appropriations on February 12, 2026, would establish a Virginia College Opportunity Fund tied to the Commonwealth Savers Plan, Virginia's 529 college savings program, and adds reporting requirements. The visible text consists almost entirely of Virginia's open-meetings statute (§ 2.2-3711). The only wagering references are existing closed-meeting exemptions for Virginia Lottery Board casino and sports betting licensing deliberations (subdivisions A 53 and A 54), which the bill carries forward unchanged. The bill was tabled in House Appropriations 21-1 on March 4, 2026, so it is effectively dead for the session.","mechanics":["Amends § 2.2-3711 (both the version effective until July 1, 2026 and the version effective July 1, 2026) and §§ 23.1-700, 23.1-701, 23.1-704 and 23.1-706.","Adds a new Article 5 (§§ 23.1-235 and 23.1-236) to Chapter 2 of Title 23.1, plus a new § 23.1-707.2, to establish the Virginia College Opportunity Fund and its report.","Closed-meeting authority in § 2.2-3711 A 20 and A 38 covers the Commonwealth Savers Plan Board's discussion of confidential analyses of non-exchange-traded investments.","Existing subdivisions A 53 and A 54 are reproduced without visible change; they let the Virginia Lottery Board deliberate in closed session on casino gaming licenses and on sports betting license denials, revocations, suspensions and non-renewals under § 58.1-4007.","The supplied text is truncated before the new Title 23.1 sections, so the Fund's funding source, eligibility and reporting terms cannot be confirmed from it."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"Unlike Kansas SB303, which imposes a 2% excise tax on all sports wagers, and Illinois HB3159, which earmarks 2% of sports wagering adjusted gross receipts for fire and EMS departments, nothing in the visible SB375 text taxes or earmarks gaming revenue. It is a higher-education savings vehicle bill. Its only gaming touchpoint is boilerplate FOIA language on Lottery Board sports betting licensing appeals. It is also unrelated to the college-athlete and campus-advertising measures in Connecticut HB05229 and Maryland HB518.","watch":"Confirm from the full text of new §§ 23.1-235, 23.1-236 and 23.1-707.2 whether the Fund draws on sports betting or casino tax revenue; if the concept returns in a later session, also check whether any change is made to the Lottery Board closed-session authority in § 2.2-3711 A 54.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Comm Sub","text_chars":80514,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:07:57.456Z"},"VA-SB390":{"summary":"Chapter 24 (SB 390), signed March 31, 2026, abolishes the Virginia State Apple Board by repealing Chapter 12 of Title 3.2 (§§ 3.2-1200 through 3.2-1217) and makes conforming amendments elsewhere in the Code. Per the bill title, the repeal of the Board and the Apple Fund takes effect July 1, 2028 and a report is required; those enactment clauses are not in the excerpt provided. It has nothing to do with sports betting; its only contact with gaming is that two Virginia FOIA sections are reenacted with their existing gaming provisions unchanged.","mechanics":["Repeals Chapter 12 of Title 3.2 (§§ 3.2-1200 through 3.2-1217), which establishes the Virginia State Apple Board and its funding structure.","Deletes the FOIA exclusion in § 2.2-3705.6 for apple producer sales information provided to the Apple Board under § 3.2-1215, and renumbers the later subdivisions (for example, the Commonwealth Health Research Board exclusion moves from 17 to 16).","Updates cross-references in the closed-meeting statute, § 2.2-3711, to match the renumbering (for example, subdivisions 30, 32 and 33 now point to the new subdivision numbers).","Amends §§ 3.2-1100, 3.2-1104 through 3.2-1106, 10.1-1458 and 15.2-2103.1 as conforming changes; those amendments are not visible in the excerpt provided.","Per the title, the effective date is delayed to July 1, 2028 and a report is required; the excerpt does not include that text."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills is comparable. They all address gaming policy directly, such as mobile sports wagering authorization (AK HB145, MS HB 4074), sports wagering tax changes (IL HB3159, KS SB303) and fantasy contest regulation (IL HB2342, KY HB 904); SB 390 is an agricultural commodity board repeal. Its only overlap with gaming is incidental: the reenacted § 2.2-3705.6 keeps, unchanged, the subdivision 14 FOIA exclusion for proprietary information that charitable gaming suppliers give the Department of Agriculture and Consumer Services, and § 2.2-3711(A)(17) keeps, unchanged, closed-meeting authority for Virginia Lottery Board licensing appeals and proprietary lottery matters.","watch":"Little to watch for operators beyond confirming that the renumbering of § 2.2-3705.6 leaves the charitable gaming supplier confidentiality exclusion and the Lottery Board closed-meeting authority in § 2.2-3711 substantively intact.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":89694,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:07:58.493Z"},"VA-SB542":{"summary":"SB542 would have created a licensed retail marijuana market in Virginia under the Virginia Cannabis Control Authority, rewriting dozens of Title 4.1 cannabis provisions and related criminal, court, tax, and consumer-protection statutes. The version provided is a Governor's amendment in the nature of a substitute dated April 13, 2026, but the Governor vetoed the bill on May 19, 2026. Nothing in the excerpt regulates sports betting or gaming.","mechanics":["Adds new Title 4.1 chapters 7, 10, 12, and 17, plus new sections in chapters 6, 11, and 13, to build the retail cannabis framework. The substantive text of these chapters is not in the excerpt provided.","Repeals Article 4 of Chapter 41.1 of Title 3.2, and §§ 3.2-5145.4:1, 4.1-611, 4.1-1101.1, 4.1-1105.1, 18.2-248.1, and 18.2-251.1, which are existing cannabis-related provisions.","Amends § 2.2-2905 to add the CEO, agents, officers, and employees of the Virginia Cannabis Control Authority to the list of positions exempt from the state personnel chapter (new item 29). That list already exempts Virginia Lottery employees (item 16).","Amends § 2.2-3114 to require members of the Cannabis Control Authority Board of Directors to file annual financial disclosure statements by February 1, alongside the Virginia Lottery Board and ABC Authority board members.","Amends § 2.2-2818 so that Cannabis Control Authority employees are covered by the state employee health plan.","Also amends § 58.1-301 (tax) and § 59.1-200 (consumer protection), and adds new § 6.2-108 (financial institutions). The content of these changes is not shown in the excerpt."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. What is unusual is the procedural history: the Governor proposed a full substitute merging the Aird, Rouse (SB 671), and Lucas (SB 826) bills, yet the bill was ultimately vetoed. The only gaming touchpoint is that the new Cannabis Control Authority is structured in parallel with the Virginia Lottery and ABC Authority for personnel exemptions and ethics disclosures.","watch":"The bill is dead after the May 19, 2026 veto and has no direct sports-betting impact, so the main thing to watch is whether a 2027 reintroduction carries over advertising, tax, or consumer-protection (§ 59.1-200) language that could later be borrowed as a template for gaming regulation in Virginia.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Comm Sub","text_chars":691324,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:08:00.791Z"},"VA-SB579":{"summary":"The Senate Courts of Justice substitute for SB579 drops the illegal-gambling and civil-penalty approach suggested by the title and replaces it with an uncodified directive (§ 1) for the Virginia Lottery Board to convene a stakeholder work group to study interactive online sweepstakes games. The work group must report findings and recommendations by December 1, 2026. The bill was continued to the next session in Courts of Justice on a 14-1 vote, so even the study is not yet in motion.","mechanics":["The Virginia Lottery Board must convene a work group of relevant stakeholders, expressly including representatives of the online sweepstakes and social games industry.","Scope covers the legal, economic, technological and public policy implications of interactive online sweepstakes games.","Clause (i) requires review of how sweepstakes are used as marketing and promotional tools by online and retail businesses, including online social games and licensed casino operators.","Clause (ii) requires the group to identify differences between online sweepstakes games and real-money gambling or internet gaming.","Clause (iii) requires the group to develop a definition of \"online sweepstakes games\"; clause (iv) allows other relevant research.","The report is due December 1, 2026 to the Senate General Laws and Technology and Courts of Justice Committees and the House General Laws and Courts of Justice Committees. The substitute contains no penalties, prohibitions or enforcement provisions."],"issue_tags":["sweeps"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Among the listed bills, CA AB 831 is the closest analogue: it amends California's existing contest and sweepstakes prohibitions on simulated gambling devices and acts directly. Virginia's substitute instead defers to a study and asks the work group to draft a definition. FL S1580 and FL H0189 also address illegal gaming and internet gambling through penalty and regulatory changes rather than study. Unlike those, VA SB579 as substituted creates no new offenses and gives the sweepstakes industry a seat at the table.","watch":"If the study proceeds, the work group's definition of \"online sweepstakes games\" and its analysis of how licensed casino operators use sweepstakes promotions could shape a later ban or civil-penalty bill, which the original title suggests was the intent, and could reach operators' own promotional sweepstakes, so operators should seek participation in the work group.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Comm Sub","text_chars":1496,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:08:09.082Z"},"VA-SB661":{"summary":"SB661 would create a new Chapter 42 in Title 58.1 (§§ 58.1-4200 through 58.1-4226, in five articles) to license, regulate and tax electronic skill gaming devices (\"skill games\") under the Virginia Lottery Board, with conforming changes to Virginia's criminal gambling statutes and the Lottery Board's general powers. The Governor vetoed the enrolled bill, and on 2026-04-22 it was passed by for the day at the reconvened session, so it is not law unless a veto override succeeds.","mechanics":["Adds new Chapter 42 of Title 58.1 (§§ 58.1-4200 to 58.1-4226) to regulate and tax skill game machines; the rates, license classes, device caps and fees are in sections not included in the excerpt provided.","Amends the Lottery Board's general statutes (§§ 58.1-4002, -4003, -4006, -4007, -4015.1, -4048) and the casino chapter (§ 58.1-4103), which places skill-game oversight with the existing lottery, sports-betting and casino regulator.","Adds subdivision A 56 to § 2.2-3711, letting the Lottery Board deliberate in closed session on appeals under § 58.1-4007 over the denial, revocation, suspension or nonrenewal of electronic skill gaming device licenses, and on related investigative records.","Amends criminal gambling provisions (§§ 18.2-325, -331.1, -334, -339), adds a new § 18.2-334.7, and adds a new forfeiture-related § 19.2-386.30:1, giving licensed devices a carve-out while setting penalties for unlicensed ones.","Also touches § 37.2-314.2 (problem gambling), § 19.2-389 (criminal history record access) and adds § 11-16.3 in Title 11."],"issue_tags":["legalize","licensing","tax"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike the listed sports-wagering bills (AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, HI HB 1308), SB661 creates a licensed, taxed category for retail skill game terminals rather than for sports betting. It runs in the opposite direction from IL HB3733, which would widen Illinois's \"gambling device\" definition to capture these kinds of machines, and from CA AB 831, which targets sweepstakes devices that simulate gaming. It is closer in spirit to IL HB3274 and HB3473, which would shield video gaming licensees operating devices not considered gambling, but it goes further by building a full Lottery Board licensing and tax regime.","watch":"Watch whether a veto override or a re-filed version succeeds, and how the new Chapter 42 tax rates, device caps and changes to the Lottery Board's § 58.1-4007 licensing and appeals process interact with existing sports-betting and casino permit holders and with the problem-gambling fund.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":208674,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:08:14.946Z"},"VA-SB558":{"summary":"SB558 would create a single Virginia Gaming Commission and move Virginia's gaming statutes into a new Title 29.5 (§§ 29.5-100 through 29.5-814). It would repeal the existing lottery and sports betting chapter (Ch. 40, Title 58.1), casino gaming (Ch. 41, Title 58.1), horse racing and pari-mutuel wagering (Ch. 29, Title 59.1), fantasy contests (Ch. 51, Title 59.1) and charitable gaming (Art. 1.1:1, Ch. 8, Title 18.2), with conforming amendments across the Code. On January 28, 2026, General Laws and Technology voted 15-0 to incorporate the bill into SB195 (Aird), so it will not move on its own.","mechanics":["Adds a new Title 29.5 in two parts: Subtitle I, Chapters 1-6 (§§ 29.5-100 to 29.5-648), and Subtitle II, Chapters 7-8 (§§ 29.5-700 to 29.5-814). The provided text is cut off before the substantive Title 29.5 sections, so their licensing, tax and enforcement terms cannot be confirmed here.","Repeals §§ 2.2-2455 and 2.2-2456, Ch. 40 (§§ 58.1-4000 to 4048), Ch. 41 (§§ 58.1-4100 to 4141), Ch. 29 (§§ 59.1-364 to 405), Ch. 51 (§§ 59.1-556 to 570) and Art. 1.1:1 (§§ 18.2-340.15 to 340.37), folding these programs into the new title.","Amending § 2.2-203.3 removes the Virginia Racing Commission from the Secretary of Agriculture and Forestry. The FOIA investigative-records exclusion in § 2.2-3705.3(1) is redrafted to cover the Virginia Gaming Commission in place of the Lottery, Racing Commission and VDACS charitable gaming references. Lottery crimes are recodified at §§ 29.5-713 and 29.5-803 to 29.5-807.","Charitable gaming supplier and manufacturer proprietary information (FOIA § 2.2-3705.6(14)) would go to the Gaming Commission rather than VDACS, and equipment approval rules would be set by a 'Virginia Gaming Commission Board.' Former § 18.2-340.34 is recodified as § 29.5-225.","The tax revenue that funds the Virginia Indigenous People's Trust Fund (§ 2.2-401.01(D)) is re-pointed from § 58.1-4125 to § 29.5-327, which suggests the casino tax distribution carries over into the new title.","Also amends the general gambling offense provisions (§§ 18.2-325, 18.2-326, 18.2-334.2 to 334.5), local taxing provisions (§§ 58.1-3510, 58.1-3732.1) and procurement and APA exemption sections (§§ 2.2-4002, 2.2-4346)."],"issue_tags":["licensing","tax","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"No other states' bills are listed for comparison. What sets SB558 apart is its scope: it does not adjust one product but replaces the regulators for lottery, sports betting, casinos, racing, fantasy contests and charitable gaming with one commission and recodifies all of them in a new title. Its committee-approved incorporation into SB195 (Aird) means SB195 is now the vehicle to track.","watch":"Track SB195, which absorbed this bill, for whether sports betting permits, tax rates (formerly in Ch. 40, Title 58.1), regulations and confidentiality protections move into Title 29.5 unchanged, and how existing operator permits transition to the new Gaming Commission and its Board.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":857255,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:08:15.701Z"},"VA-SB609":{"summary":"The House General Laws substitute for SB609 would repeal Virginia's scattered gaming statutes and recodify them in a new Title 29.5 under a single regulator, the Virginia Gaming Commission. The repealed law covers illegal gambling and charitable gaming in Title 18.2, the lottery and casino chapters in Title 58.1 (Chapters 40 and 41), and Title 59.1 Chapters 29, 29.1 and 51. The bill failed to pass from conference on March 14, 2026, so the current structure stays in place. The text provided is truncated and shows only the conforming amendments, not the substantive Title 29.5 provisions.","mechanics":["Creates new Title 29.5 in three subtitles: chapters 1–6 (§§ 29.5-100 to 29.5-650), chapters 7–8 (§§ 29.5-700 to 29.5-814) and chapter 9 (§§ 29.5-900 to 29.5-913).","Repeals: Title 18.2 Article 1 (§§ 18.2-325 to -340) and Article 1.1:1 (charitable gaming, §§ 18.2-340.15 to -340.37); Title 58.1 Chapters 40 and 41; Title 59.1 Chapters 29, 29.1 and 51; and §§ 2.2-2455 and 2.2-2456.","Amends § 2.2-511 so the Attorney General may assist in prosecuting violations of Title 29.5, with the local Commonwealth's Attorney's concurrence.","Renumbers lottery crimes to §§ 29.5-713 and 29.5-803 through 29.5-807 for FOIA purposes, and lists the Virginia Gaming Commission among the licensing bodies whose applicant and licensee investigation records are excluded from mandatory FOIA disclosure (§ 2.2-3705.3).","Transfers charitable gaming oversight from the Department of Agriculture and Consumer Services to the Commission and a Commission Board, including protection of supplier proprietary information (§ 29.5-225) and approval of electronic and mechanical equipment (§ 2.2-3705.6(14)).","Redirects the tax revenue feeding the Virginia Indigenous People's Trust Fund from § 58.1-4125 to new § 29.5-327. The bill also places the Virginia Racing Commission under the Secretary of Agriculture and Forestry (§ 2.2-203.3)."],"issue_tags":["licensing","integrity","tax"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. This is unusual on its own because it is a wholesale recodification and regulator consolidation rather than a change to rates or activities. It folds lottery, casino, charitable gaming, racing and other gaming chapters into one title under one commission. The caption still refers to a 'Virginia Lottery and Gaming Authority' while the substitute text uses 'Virginia Gaming Commission', which signals the drafting shifted during the session.","watch":"Because this vehicle died in conference, watch for it to return in 2027 and get the full Title 29.5 text to check that the recodified licensing, tax and enforcement rules carry over without substantive change. The provided excerpt does not show the sports-betting tax rate or licensing terms.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Comm Sub","text_chars":910816,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:08:22.616Z"},"VA-SB819":{"summary":"Senate substitute directing the Virginia Lottery to study whether to regulate advertising for lottery, casino gaming and sports betting, specifically mail and SMS marketing, targeted digital ads using personal data, and use of individual player data, plus mandatory age/addiction warnings and helpline numbers on ads. It also directs the Lottery Board and Director to develop a phased plan to require age verification software on all lottery terminals, with both reports due October 1, 2026. The bill passed the Senate but was left in House General Laws on March 10, 2026, so it is effectively dead for the session.","mechanics":["§ 1 study covers regulations restricting (i) mail and SMS advertising, (ii) targeted digital advertising using personal data such as geolocation, consumer profiles or purchase history, and (iii) use of individual player data for marketing, across lottery, casino gaming and sports betting.","§ 1 also studies requiring all advertisements to carry a warning that the lottery is for persons 18 or older and can be addictive, plus the Virginia Problem Gambling Helpline toll-free number.","§ 2 requires an implementation plan for age verification software (18+) on all lottery terminals, defined as devices interacting with the Department's gaming system to issue tickets and process game transactions. The plan must include a phased schedule, a completion date, cost estimates, alternative schedules, and recommended statutory or appropriation changes, and it may track the normal terminal replacement cycle.","Both reports are due by October 1, 2026, to the General Laws chairs; the § 2 plan also goes to the Governor and the appropriations and finance chairs.","Implementation costs must be built into the Lottery Board's revenue estimates, and work may proceed only after a general appropriation act adopts a forecast that includes those costs.","The bill is study-only: it creates no binding rules, penalties or operator obligations."],"issue_tags":["advertising","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike CT HB05229, which directly restricts gaming advertising on campuses and bars AI-driven bet targeting, VA SB819 only commissions a study of advertising limits, though its scope (SMS, geolocation and player-data targeting) overlaps heavily. It resembles study vehicles such as MD HB 195 (emerging technology and gambling prevention workgroup), MD HB1409 (prevalence studies) and LA SR 178, but is distinctive in targeting the use of operator-held player data for marketing. Its age-verification piece concerns retail lottery terminals at 18+, not the 21+ prediction-market age limits in CT HB05038 and IL HB5059.","watch":"Although SB819 stalled, watch whether the Virginia Lottery takes up the study administratively or the idea returns in 2027, because restrictions on SMS, mail and player-data-driven targeted marketing would directly hit sportsbook CRM and promotional practices.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Comm Sub","text_chars":3474,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:08:28.363Z"},"VA-SB756":{"summary":"SB756 would add a sixth eligible casino host locality in Virginia: a large urban-county-executive county, which in practice means Fairfax County. It does this by replacing \"city\" with \"locality\" throughout the casino chapter and confining any Fairfax casino to a narrowly drawn Silver Line, Tysons-area site. It also broadens the definition of \"casino gaming establishment\" to cover the entire property, such as a hotel, conference center, entertainment district or large-scale concert venue. The Governor vetoed it, and on 2026-04-22 it was passed by for the day, so it is not law.","mechanics":["New § 58.1-4107(A)(6) adds as an eligible host locality any county with (i) a 2020 population over 1.15 million, (ii) at least 6% of assessed real estate tax-exempt (FY2021 Tax Department report) and (iii) the urban county executive form of government.","New § 58.1-4107(E) limits that county's casino site to one (i) within a quarter mile of an existing Metro Silver Line station, (ii) within a coordinated mixed-use development of at least 1.5 million sq ft, (iii) within two miles of a regional enclosed mall of at least 1.5 million sq ft gross building area, and (iv) outside the I-495 Beltway.","§ 58.1-4100 expands \"casino gaming establishment\" to the entire property at the licensed address, including a conference center, hotel, entertainment district or large-scale concert venue. This also widens the footprint for on-premises mobile casino gaming and sports betting facilities.","§ 58.1-4032(D)(6) gives sports betting permit applicants that invest at least $300M in a casino and are named by an \"eligible host locality\" substantial and preferred consideration. Under (D)(2), those permits do not count toward the 4–12 permit cap.","The existing process otherwise applies to the new county: local referendum (§ 58.1-4123), Department preliminary review within 45 days, one operator license per locality, the $300M capital investment requirement, $50,000-per-principal application fee, and the 10-year license term.","Gaming Proceeds Fund distributions (6/7/8% tiered local share of AGR under § 58.1-4125) are unchanged in substance; they are simply redirected to the \"locality\" rather than the \"city.\""],"issue_tags":["legalize","licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"None of the listed bills deal with siting land-based casinos. The Alaska (HB145, SB194), Hawaii (HB 1308, HB 2570, SB1569) and Georgia (HB 686) bills would authorize new sports wagering regimes, while the Illinois bills (e.g., HB2878, HB5034, HB2761) adjust sports wagering licensing and fees. The closest parallel is Illinois HB2761, which, like SB756's link to § 58.1-4032(D)(6), ties sports wagering licensure to a physical venue. SB756 is unusual in using census, tax-exemption and transit criteria so specific that they fit a single county and a single site.","watch":"If the bill is revived, a Fairfax casino licensee would gain a preferred-consideration sports betting permit outside the 12-permit cap and an expanded \"entire property\" footprint for retail and on-premises mobile wagering in the Northern Virginia market, so operators should track it closely.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":46977,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:08:29.621Z"},"VA-SB826":{"summary":"SB826 would create a regulated adult-use retail cannabis market in Virginia, amending dozens of Code sections across Titles 2.2, 4.1, 18.2, 19.2 and others. It would add new Chapters 7 through 10 and 12 to Title 4.1 under the Virginia Alcoholic Beverage and Cannabis Control Authority, and it is titled as relating to taxation, revenues, and cannabis. On February 12, 2026, Senate Finance and Appropriations voted 15-0 to incorporate it into SB542 (Aird), so it no longer moves as a standalone bill.","mechanics":["Adds §§ 4.1-700 through 4.1-1008 (new Chapters 7–10 of Title 4.1) and §§ 4.1-1200 through 4.1-1206 (new Chapter 12), plus new sections in Chapters 6, 11, 13 and 14 of Title 4.1, building out the retail cannabis framework; the substantive licensing and tax provisions are not in the excerpt provided.","Repeals §§ 4.1-1101.1, 4.1-1105.1, 18.2-248.1 and 18.2-251.1 and amends many Title 18.2 and 19.2 criminal and procedure sections, including marijuana distribution, possession and record provisions.","Amends § 58.1-301 (tax) and adds § 6.2-108 (Title 6.2, financial institutions) and § 54.1-4426, suggesting tax-conformity and banking-access provisions for cannabis businesses; specifics are not in the excerpt.","Keeps the Virginia Alcoholic Beverage and Cannabis Control Authority under the Secretary of Public Safety and Homeland Security (§ 2.2-221), including oversight of its law-enforcement operations (§ 2.2-221(B)(19)).","Reproduces § 2.2-2696 (Virginia Addiction Recovery Council), which covers problem gambling as defined in § 37.2-314.2 and includes two problem-gambling recovery members; the excerpt shows no substantive gambling change."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills is a cannabis bill. The AK (HB145, SB194), GA (HB 686), HI (HB 1308, HB 2570), MS (HB 4074) and NE (LB421, LB438) bills authorize or regulate sports wagering, while IA SF 605, KS SB303 and IL HB3159 adjust wagering taxes. The closest analog is IL HB5452, which places hemp products under a liquor-control commission, much as SB826 places cannabis under Virginia's alcohol regulator. SB826's only gaming touchpoint is the existing problem-gambling mandate of the Virginia Addiction Recovery Council in § 2.2-2696.","watch":"Track SB542 (Aird), the vehicle SB826 was folded into, for any expansion of the Cannabis Control Authority's powers, any shared problem-gambling or addiction funding, or any advertising rules that could later serve as a model for gaming, though nothing in the provided text directly affects sports-betting operators.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":910842,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:08:33.563Z"},"VA-SB91":{"summary":"SB91 (Chapter 482, approved April 8, 2026) is a conforming name-change bill that renames the Eastern Virginia Health Sciences Center at Old Dominion University as Virginia Health Sciences at ODU across a dozen Code of Virginia sections covering ethics, FOIA, open meetings, education, health and insurance law. It does not regulate sports betting or gaming. Wagering appears only because § 2.2-3711, the closed-meetings statute, is reenacted in full, and that statute already contains the Virginia Lottery Board's closed-session authority for casino and sports-betting licensing matters.","mechanics":["Amends §§ 2.2-3106, 2.2-3705.4, 2.2-3711 (both the version effective until July 1, 2026 and the version effective July 1, 2026), 22.1-209.2, 23.1-2002, 23.1-2006, 23.1-2007, 32.1-69.3, 32.1-71.5, 32.1-71.6, 38.2-5008 and 54.1-2961 to substitute the new institutional name.","§ 2.2-3106 D: the medical center's federally compliant conflict-of-interest policies continue to satisfy subdivisions C 8 and C 9, subject to annual notice to the Secretary of the Commonwealth by January 31.","§ 2.2-3705.4 A 5 and § 2.2-3711 A 22: the existing FOIA exclusion and closed-meeting authority for proprietary health-care business information of the UVA Medical Center and the ODU health sciences entity carry over under the new name.","§ 2.2-3711 A 53 and A 54: the existing closed-session authority for Virginia Lottery Board deliberations on casino-gaming license actions (under § 58.1-4105) and sports-betting license or permit appeals (under § 58.1-4007) is reenacted. The text shown indicates no substantive change.","The supplied text is truncated and does not display the strike/insert markup, so the exact name-change edits cannot be confirmed line by line."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. What is unusual is only that a higher-education renaming bill reenacts Virginia's entire closed-meetings statute. As a result, the Lottery Board's sports-betting and casino deliberation exemptions in § 2.2-3711 A 53 and A 54 appear in the chaptered text even though the bill does nothing to them.","watch":"Nothing here affects operators substantively, but confirm against the chaptered markup that § 2.2-3711 A 53 and A 54, which let the Lottery Board deliberate in closed session on sports-betting and casino license denials, suspensions and revocations, were carried forward unchanged in both the pre- and post-July 1, 2026 versions.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":98370,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:08:34.092Z"},"VT-H133":{"summary":"H.133 is a Vermont short-form bill that proposes to repeal 31 V.S.A. chapter 14 (State Lottery) and chapter 25 (sports wagering) in their entirety. It would also ensure that anyone offering a lottery or sports wagering is subject to the criminal gambling and lottery provisions of 13 V.S.A. chapter 51. Because it is a short-form bill, only the statement of purpose is available and the operative text is omitted.","mechanics":["Repeals 31 V.S.A. chapter 14, which authorizes the Vermont State Lottery.","Repeals 31 V.S.A. chapter 25, which authorizes and regulates sports wagering, including the existing mobile operator framework.","Brings any person offering a lottery or sports wagering within the criminal provisions of 13 V.S.A. chapter 51 (gambling and lotteries).","Short-form bill: no effective date, transition or wind-down terms, or treatment of existing operator contracts and licenses appears in the text.","Introduced by Reps. Stevens, Headrick and Mrowicki; referred 2025-02-04 to House Government Operations and Military Affairs."],"issue_tags":["legalize","licensing","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Nearly every listed bill moves toward expansion or regulation of legal wagering. AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686, and HI HB 1308/SB1569 would authorize sports betting, and IL HB3080/HB4797 would authorize internet gaming. CT HB05229 and IL HB5142 tighten rules within an existing legal market, and FL S1580 adjusts illegal-gambling penalties. H.133 is unusual in proposing outright repeal of an operating sports wagering market and a state lottery, and in re-criminalizing the activity under 13 V.S.A. chapter 51.","watch":"Watch whether the bill gets committee action and a full-text draft, since any version would need to address termination of existing Vermont mobile operator agreements and licenses, wind-down of customer accounts and funds, and the timing of criminal exposure under 13 V.S.A. chapter 51.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":829,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:08:36.529Z"},"VT-H141":{"summary":"H.141 is Vermont's fiscal year 2025 budget adjustment act, amending the FY2025 Big Bill (2024 Acts and Resolves No. 113) and related 2024 acts to revise appropriations, fund transfers, reversions, reserves and position authorizations across state government. Its only gaming-related provision is in Sec. 52, which lowers the FY2025 transfer from the Sports Wagering Enterprise Fund (#50250) to the General Fund from $7,000,000 to $6,139,162. The bill was vetoed, and the last recorded action recommitted it to House Appropriations.","mechanics":["Sec. 52 amends Sec. D.101(b)(1)(C): the transfer from the Sports Wagering Enterprise Fund (#50250) to the General Fund is cut from $7,000,000 to $6,139,162 for FY2025.","Same section revises other General Fund sweeps, including the Cannabis Regulation Fund ($12,000,000 to $15,417,084.32), the Liquor Control Fund ($21,100,000 to $9,543,353) and the Unclaimed Property Fund ($6,500,000 to $10,995,595).","Secs. 1–49 adjust line-item appropriations, with large increases in human services: total human services rises from $5.40 billion to $5.66 billion (Sec. 38).","Sec. 58 delays repeal of the telephone personal property tax to July 1, 2026 and of the alternative telephone gross revenues tax to January 1, 2027.","Sec. 54 sets reserve actions, including $15,168,660.85 to the Budget Stabilization Reserve and $5,480,000 to the 27/53 reserve."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. What sets this bill apart for gaming clients is that it touches sports wagering only through a single General Fund sweep figure from the Sports Wagering Enterprise Fund. It does not change tax rates, licensing or operator obligations.","watch":"Watch whether the reworked budget adjustment, or later appropriations bills, change the size of sweeps from the Sports Wagering Enterprise Fund, since the $6,139,162 figure signals revenue expectations that could feed pressure to revisit Vermont's operator revenue-share terms.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":108717,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:08:40.957Z"},"VT-H489":{"summary":"H.489 is Vermont's fiscal year 2025 budget adjustment act, amending the FY2025 Big Bill (2024 Acts and Resolves No. 113) and several other 2024 acts to revise line-item appropriations, fund transfers, reversions and reserves across state government. Its only gaming-related provision is a downward revision of the FY2025 transfer from the Sports Wagering Enterprise Fund to the General Fund. The bill was vetoed and recommitted to House Appropriations on April 9, 2025.","mechanics":["Sec. 52 amends Sec. D.101(b)(1)(C) to reduce the FY2025 transfer from the Sports Wagering Enterprise Fund (#50250) to the General Fund from $7,000,000 to $6,139,162.","The same section revises other fund sweeps, including the Cannabis Regulation Fund (raised from $12,000,000 to $15,417,084.32) and the Liquor Control Fund (cut from $21,100,000 to $9,543,353).","Large human services increases, including total human services rising from about $5.40 billion to $5.66 billion (Sec. 38), plus $10 million in Provider Stabilization Grants and $11 million for Brattleboro Retreat (Sec. 50).","Sec. 54 allows up to $133.5 million of General Fund surplus otherwise subject to 32 V.S.A. § 308c(a) to be reserved for housing, property tax relief and other uses.","Secs. 59–60 delay the repeal of the telephone personal property tax and the alternative telephone gross revenues tax by one year, to 2026 and 2027."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. The bill is notable only because it shows Vermont treating sports wagering enterprise revenue as a General Fund source, and because the downward revision to $6,139,162 suggests FY2025 sports-wagering proceeds came in below the original $7 million estimate. It makes no change to Vermont's sports wagering statute, tax rate or licensing terms.","watch":"Track the reworked budget adjustment bill that follows the veto to see whether the Sports Wagering Enterprise Fund transfer figure changes, since lower-than-projected transfers can prompt legislators to revisit operator revenue-share terms or market structure.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":86081,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:08:42.719Z"},"VT-H288":{"summary":"H.288 expands Vermont's veterans' motor vehicle registration fee exemption (23 V.S.A. § 378) to give resident veterans discharged under other than dishonorable conditions a 50 percent reduction on registration fees for a range of vehicles and boats, plus one 100 percent reduction per calendar year. It funds the foregone registration revenue by diverting sports wagering revenues and fees under 31 V.S.A. chapter 25 from the Sports Wagering Enterprise Fund to the Transportation Fund.","mechanics":["Sec. 1, 23 V.S.A. § 378(b): 50 percent reduction of registration fees for pleasure cars, motorcycles, motor-driven cycles, all-surface vehicles, exhibition vehicles, diesel pleasure cars, snowmobiles, antique snowmobiles, motorboats, ATVs and the Clean Air Fund fee under 3 V.S.A. § 2822(m), on submission of an approved DD Form 214; the veteran must be listed as owner or co-owner.","Sec. 1, § 378(c): one 100 percent reduction per calendar year for the same fees, unavailable to veterans already receiving the VA-assisted vehicle exemption under subsection (a).","Sec. 1, § 378(a): the existing full exemption for VA-financed vehicles is extended from 'honorably discharged' veterans to those discharged under other than dishonorable conditions.","Sec. 2, 31 V.S.A. § 1304: sports wagering revenues and fees still go to the Sports Wagering Enterprise Fund, except an amount needed to offset foregone § 378 registration fees, which goes to the Transportation Fund.","The bill does not cap or quantify the diverted amount and does not change operator tax rates.","Sec. 3: effective January 1, 2026."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills uses existing sports wagering revenue to backfill an unrelated tax expenditure. Bills such as AK HB145/SB194, GA HB 686 and HI HB 1308 authorize and tax wagering, and IA SSB 1157 and IL HB5143 change rates or revenue use. H.288 leaves Vermont's wagering framework and rates untouched and only reallocates the proceeds by amending the § 1304 allocation provision, with no dollar cap.","watch":"Watch whether an uncapped diversion of sports wagering receipts to the Transportation Fund creates fiscal pressure on the Sports Wagering Enterprise Fund, which could later prompt efforts to raise Vermont's operator revenue share or fees.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":4288,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:08:46.053Z"},"VT-H493":{"summary":"H.493 (Act No. 27), the \"BIG BILL – Fiscal Year 2026 Appropriations Act,\" is Vermont's general operating budget for the fiscal year ending June 30, 2026. It appropriates funds for all state agencies by function, and it sets general rules on federal funds, new positions and interim adjustments. In the portion of the text provided, the only gaming-related item is a single appropriation line for the Liquor and Lottery Commission, which administers Vermont's sports wagering program.","mechanics":["Sec. B.236.1 appropriates $18,282,300 to the Liquor and Lottery Commission: $10,866,788 personal services and $7,415,512 operating expenses.","That appropriation is funded mainly from enterprise funds ($17,836,721), with $125,000 in special funds, $250,579 from the Tobacco Fund and $70,000 in interdepartmental transfers.","Sec. A.102(c): narrative portions apply only to FY2026 unless codified. Sec. A.104: the act does not impair existing laws except as specifically provided.","Sec. A.107 freezes total authorized state positions in FY2026, except positions authorized in the 2025 session and limited-service positions under 32 V.S.A. § 5.","Signed by the Governor on May 21, 2025.","The text supplied is truncated at Sec. B.514 (about page 69 of 277), so the D sections (fund transfers and reversions), E sections (program language) and G section (effective dates) were not reviewed."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states were listed. What stands out here is that sports wagering appears only implicitly, through the funding line for the Liquor and Lottery Commission's operations. Nothing in the reviewed text changes wagering tax rates, licensing or the operator revenue-share structure.","watch":"Review the untruncated D and E sections for any transfers, reversions or program language involving sports wagering revenue or the Commission's enterprise fund, since those would be where an appropriations act could quietly redirect wagering proceeds or alter Commission operations.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":75887,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:08:48.684Z"},"VT-H913":{"summary":"H.913 is a Vermont short-form bill whose statement of purpose would amend the criminal gambling statute (13 V.S.A. chapter 51) so that offering prediction market securities or commodities contingent on sports, contests, natural persons, politics and campaigns, disasters, war, all-hazards, or death counts as prohibited wagering. It would also amend 9 V.S.A. chapter 101 to void agreements for such contracts and allow civil actions to recover lost money. It would add a $0.50 per-wager fee on all wagers placed through Vermont's licensed sports wagering operators. Because it is a short-form bill, no operative text exists yet and the details are unknown.","mechanics":["Short-form bill: the statutory text is omitted; only the statement of purpose is available, so definitions, penalties and effective dates are not yet drafted.","Criminal: amends 13 V.S.A. ch. 51 so the wagering prohibition covers offering prediction market securities or commodities on sports, contests, natural persons, politics and campaigns, disasters, war, all-hazards, or death.","Civil: amends 9 V.S.A. ch. 101 (wagering contracts) so these contracts are void and losses are recoverable by civil action.","Fiscal: adds a $0.50 per-wager fee on all wagers made through the State's sports wagering operators. Who bears the fee and how it is remitted is not specified.","Referred 2026-02-25 to House Government Operations and Military Affairs; it has 13 bipartisan-area sponsors led by Rep. Stevens of Waterbury."],"issue_tags":["prediction","tax","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Like MN HF 4437/SF 4511 and NC H1171, H.913 takes a prohibition approach, folding prediction markets into the criminal gambling ban rather than regulating them. That contrasts with the ORACLE Act model in IL HB5059 and NY S09414, which licenses or conditions platforms with age-21 limits, and with CT HB05038's age and advertising rules. Its event categories go well beyond IL HB5142, which reaches only sports-event contracts by redefining sports wagering, to include politics, war, disasters and death. Its civil voiding and loss-recovery remedy is distinctive. Unusually, it pairs the ban with a $0.50 per-wager fee on licensed sportsbooks, whereas NJ A5336/S4447 tax prediction market operators themselves.","watch":"Watch the full text when it replaces the short form, particularly how the $0.50 per-wager fee is imposed on licensed operators (absorbed or passed to bettors, and its effect on low-stakes and parlay wagers) and whether the definitions of prediction market securities could sweep in licensed sportsbook products or exchange-style offerings.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":1753,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:08:52.331Z"},"VT-H790":{"summary":"H.790 (Act No. 74) is Vermont's fiscal year 2026 budget adjustment act, amending line-item appropriations, fund transfers, reversions and reserves in the FY2026 appropriations act (2025 Acts and Resolves No. 27). It also makes unrelated statutory changes, including a new Child Care Contribution Reserve (32 V.S.A. § 308f) and conforming references to a new Office of Legislative Operations. Its only gaming content is a downward revision of the estimated FY2026 transfer from the Sports Wagering Enterprise Fund to the General Fund.","mechanics":["Sec. 53 amends Sec. D.101(c)(1) to cut the estimated transfer from the Sports Wagering Enterprise Fund (#50250) to the General Fund from $7,600,000 to $6,678,828, a reduction of about $921,000.","Under D.101(c), enterprise-fund transfers may not exceed actual fiscal year-end profits, net of any amount needed to cure a fund deficit, and the stated figures are estimates for budget development only.","For comparison, the Liquor Control Fund transfer estimate rises from $14,849,754 to $16,802,502. The Cannabis Regulation Fund retention threshold rises to $4,665,150, with the estimated 70/30 split to the General Fund and the Substance Misuse Prevention Fund revised to $9,182,689 and $3,935,438.","Sec. 58 adds 32 V.S.A. § 308f, requiring an annual consensus estimate of fourth-quarter Child Care Contribution receipts and a General Fund reserve of that amount during year-end closeout.","The act was approved by the Governor on March 5, 2026. The text provided is truncated at Sec. 74 of a 60-page act, so later sections were not reviewed."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. On its own, the bill is a routine mid-year budget true-up. It does not change Vermont's sports wagering statute, tax or revenue-share terms, licensing or consumer protection rules; it only re-estimates the year-end profit transfer from the Sports Wagering Enterprise Fund. The roughly 12 percent downward revision is notable as a signal that the State's FY2026 sports wagering receipts are running below the original budget assumption.","watch":"Watch whether the downward revision of the Sports Wagering Enterprise Fund transfer to $6,678,828 prompts FY2027 budget proposals to adjust Vermont's operator revenue-share terms or enterprise-fund cost allocations to close the gap, and confirm that the untruncated remainder of the act contains no wagering provisions.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":68662,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:08:53.030Z"},"VT-H951":{"summary":"H.951 (Act No. 144) is Vermont's FY2027 general appropriations act (the \"BIG BILL\"), funding state operations and non-bonded capital spending for the fiscal year ending June 30, 2027. In the portion of the text provided, its only gaming-related content is the operating appropriation for the Department of Liquor and Lottery, the agency that regulates Vermont's sports wagering market. No substantive sports-betting policy appears in the reviewed text.","mechanics":["Sec. B.236.1 appropriates $17,210,042 to the Liquor and Lottery Commission: $11,295,690 personal services and $5,914,352 operating expenses.","That appropriation is funded mainly by enterprise funds ($16,762,179), plus $252,863 from the tobacco fund, $125,000 from special funds and $70,000 from interdepartmental transfers.","Sec. A.107 freezes the total number of authorized state positions for FY2027, apart from positions the 2026 session authorized and limited-service positions, which constrains regulator headcount.","Sec. A.102(c) limits narrative provisions to FY2027 unless codified, and Sec. A.104 preserves existing law except as specifically provided.","The text supplied is truncated at Sec. B.504. The D sections (fund transfers and reservations) and E sections (program language), where any routing of sports wagering revenue would likely appear, were not reviewed."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. On its own, this is a routine omnibus budget: wagering appears only as a line-item appropriation for the Liquor and Lottery Commission, with no change to sports wagering tax rates, licensing or consumer-protection rules in the reviewed text.","watch":"Check the untruncated D and E sections for any transfer, reservation or earmark of sports wagering revenue, or any change to Department of Liquor and Lottery staffing or regulatory funding, that could affect operator obligations or revenue-share arrangements.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":82337,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:08:57.525Z"},"WA-HB2205":{"summary":"SHB 2205 amends Washington's tribal-only sports wagering statute to allow betting on in-state college teams (while barring proposition bets on named athletes enrolled at Washington institutions), ban wagering on minor league sports, and let tribes accept on-premises wagers at any tribe's gaming facility rather than only their own. It also creates a new class C felony for threatening sports officials, coaches, or participants in connection with a wagerable event and bars violators from placing sports wagers. It does not authorize statewide mobile or commercial (non-tribal) sports betting.","mechanics":["Sec. 2 deletes the exclusion of Washington-located colleges from the 'collegiate sport or athletic event' definition, so wagering on in-state college teams becomes permissible, but bets on the performance or nonperformance of any specifically named participant enrolled at a Washington institution are prohibited.","Sec. 2 excludes minor league sports from 'professional sport or athletic event' and expressly prohibits sports wagering on any minor league sport.","Sec. 3 changes compact amendment authority from sports wagering on 'its' Indian lands to 'Indian lands' generally, and Sec. 4 changes the physical-presence requirement from 'that' tribe's gaming facility to 'a' tribe's gaming facility, opening the door to cross-tribal or hosted wagering arrangements while keeping on-premises-only acceptance.","Sec. 5 adds RCW 9.46.037(5): threatening a sports official, coach, or participant about a wagerable event (threat of bodily injury or property damage, communicated directly or indirectly) is a class C felony under RCW 9A.20.021.","Sec. 5 adds RCW 9.46.037(6): any person who violates the threat provision may not place sports wagers, with no stated mechanism for exclusion lists or operator obligations.","Sec. 7 names the act the 'sports wagering integrity act'; Sec. 6 is a severability clause. No effective date beyond the default applies, and as of 2026-02-19 the bill sits in the House Rules 'X' file, signalling it is unlikely to advance this session."],"issue_tags":["integrity","legalize","prop"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. On its own the bill is unusual because it expands the betting menu (in-state college games) while narrowing it elsewhere (minor leagues, in-state college player props). It pairs this with a criminal threat-to-participants offense that carries a wagering ban, similar to bettor-harassment measures seen in some other states, all while preserving Washington's tribal-exclusive, on-premises-only model.","watch":"Operators and tribal partners should watch how the 'a tribe's gaming facility' and 'Indian lands' changes would be implemented through compact amendments, since they could enable shared or off-reservation tribal wagering, and how the threat-based wagering ban would be enforced without a defined exclusion list or operator duty.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Comm Sub","text_chars":7357,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:09:05.204Z"},"WI-AB1034":{"summary":"AB1034 creates Wis. Stat. 36.11(12), authorizing the UW Board of Regents to pay student athletes for name, image, and likeness, barring UW policies that block NIL use outside official team activities or block professional representation, and prohibiting student athletes from entering NIL deals that pay for athletic performance or for endorsing gambling, including sports betting and casino gambling. It also shifts debt service for UW–Madison athletic facility maintenance from program revenue (the 40 percent allocation in s. 20.285(1)(gj)) to $14.6 million annual GPR, and adds $200,000 annually each for the UW–Milwaukee Klotsche Center and the UW–Green Bay soccer complex. The status shows the bill was signed with partial vetoes and that a veto override failed, so the enacted text may differ from this introduced version.","mechanics":["New s. 36.11(12)(h)2.b. bars a student athlete from any NIL agreement compensating endorsement of 'gambling, including sports betting and casino gambling,' alongside tobacco, vaping, alcohol, banned substances, illegal activity, and any other activity the board proscribes.","Student athletes must disclose each third-party NIL agreement before signing, in the manner the board or institution prescribes (s. 36.11(12)(g)); agreements conflicting with board, institution, or athletic program policies or agreements are barred (h)1.","The board may enter NIL compensation agreements and affiliation agreements to facilitate deals and manage program revenue (b), (k), but may not use GPR to enable NIL opportunities (d); NIL pay is not a grant under sub. (6) and does not make the athlete a system employee (e), (f).","Third-party payors that are separate legal entities not owned or controlled by the board are not treated as acting on the board's behalf (j).","Open-records exemption under s. 19.35 for NIL agreement terms and for athletic program revenue records kept confidential for competitive reasons (i).","Appropriations: $14,600,000 GPR annually under new s. 20.285(1)(cj) for UW–Madison athletic facilities debt service, replacing the 40 percent program-revenue allocation in (gj); $200,000 each under (ck) Klotsche Center and (cL) Green Bay soccer complex, for 2025-26 and 2026-27."],"issue_tags":["advertising","integrity"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"Unlike MD HB518 and CT HB05229, which regulate operators directly through college-athlete protections and campus advertising limits, WI AB1034 reaches gambling only on the athlete side, through a flat ban on NIL endorsement deals for sports betting and casino gambling. It is closer to the NIL frameworks in HI HB 2384/SB3263, TX HB126, NE LB370, and LA HB 513, which set institutional NIL authority and disclosure rules. TN SB1662 instead funds athletic programs from sportsbook vendor fees, whereas Wisconsin funds facility debt service from general purpose revenue.","watch":"Watch whether the gambling endorsement ban in s. 36.11(12)(h)2.b. survived the partial veto, and how broadly the Regents use their power to proscribe 'any other activity,' because together they effectively close off UW student-athlete NIL partnerships for sportsbooks and casino operators and may extend to adjacent products such as fantasy or prediction platforms.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":11961,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:09:05.647Z"},"WA-SB6137":{"summary":"SB 6137 (Chapter 246, Laws of 2026, the 'sports wagering integrity act') amends Washington's tribal-only sports wagering statutes. It deletes the bar on wagering on games involving in-state colleges and replaces it with narrower bans on in-state player props, coach decisions and officiating decisions. It also loosens the 'its Indian lands' and 'that facility' wording in the compact and internet-transmission provisions, and creates a new gross misdemeanor for wager-related threats against officials, coaches and participants.","mechanics":["Sec. 2 (RCW 9.46.038): deletes the clause excluding Washington-located institutions from 'collegiate sport or athletic event,' so wagering on in-state college games becomes permissible. It still prohibits wagers on (i) the performance or nonperformance of any named individual participant enrolled at a Washington institution, (ii) in-game decisions (substitutions, timeouts, replay challenges, appeals) by coaches employed by Washington institutions, and (iii) officiating decisions in games involving Washington institutions.","Sec. 2 also defines 'officiating decision' broadly to include officiating assignments and penalty, foul, violation or replay calls. The definition of sports wagering covers esports and portions of events, excludes horse racing under ch. 67.16 RCW, and prohibits wagering on any minor league sport. The chaptered text does not show underlining, so which of these definitions are new is not clear from the copy provided.","Sec. 3 (RCW 9.46.0364): tribal compacts may be amended to authorize sports wagering on 'Indian lands' rather than 'its Indian lands.' Sec. 4 (RCW 9.46.0368): internet wagers may be placed while the customer is physically present at 'a tribe's gaming facility' rather than 'that' facility. Together these appear to open the door to cross-tribe or shared-premises arrangements, but still require on-premises placement.","Sec. 5 (RCW 9.46.037): existing match-fixing and insider-information offenses remain class C felonies. New subsection (2) makes it a gross misdemeanor to knowingly threaten bodily injury or property damage to a sports official, coach or participant where the threat relates to a wager on that event, including threats sent by electronic communication. The offense does not apply where the target can see the person lacks the ability to carry out the threat.","Anyone convicted under new RCW 9.46.037(2) may not place any sports wagers.","Severability clause (Sec. 6). Effective June 11, 2026. The exclusive tribal model is unchanged (Sec. 1 intent statement), with no commercial or statewide mobile licensing."],"issue_tags":["prop","integrity","legalize"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. The bill is unusual in pairing a market expansion (lifting Washington's in-state collegiate ban) with targeted in-state college player-prop, coach-decision and officiating-decision bans. It also criminalizes bettor threats against athletes and officials and imposes a statutory wagering ban on those convicted, rather than relying on operator self-exclusion. It does all of this while keeping sports wagering confined to tribal compacts and on-premises placement.","watch":"Watch how the Gambling Commission and tribes implement the new in-state college market restrictions, since any compact amendments and the reach of the 'officiating decision' ban affect permissible markets. Also watch how the Commission and tribes read the 'Indian lands' and 'a tribe's gaming facility' changes, which may enable cross-tribe or shared-platform arrangements. Finally, watch how operators will be expected to identify and block bettors convicted under new RCW 9.46.037(2).","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":9951,"generated":"2026-11-02T19:09:16.406Z"},"CT-HB5035":{"summary":"Connecticut HB 5035 (substitute) requires public school students to keep personal wireless communication devices powered off and stored during the school day unless access is authorized for instructional purposes, and bars students from accessing social media through school-provided Internet except for instruction. Local and regional boards of education must adopt implementing policies, including emergency communication and discipline procedures, and notify parents annually. It has nothing to do with sports betting, iGaming, or collectibles; the only gaming reference is to 'gaming device' and 'gaming' as device functions.","mechanics":["Sec. 1(a)(1): 'wireless communication device' covers any portable device capable of voice, messaging or data communication, including cell phones, tablets, computers, gaming devices and smart watches, but excludes district-provided instructional technology.","Sec. 1(b)(1): no student may access such a device on school property during the school day (scheduled start to end time, grades K-12) unless authorized for instructional purposes; devices must be powered off and stored as the board prescribes, e.g. lockers.","Sec. 1(b)(2): exceptions for use required by an IEP under IDEA or a Section 504 accommodations plan, or deemed necessary for health by a licensed physician, PA or APRN.","Sec. 1(c): bars student access to social media platforms via school-provided Internet during the school day except for instructional purposes.","Sec. 1(d)-(e): boards must adopt policies covering emergency parent communication and discipline, notify parents annually, and include the policies in student handbooks.","Effective July 1, 2027; passed the House, and on 2026-04-29 was moved to the foot of the Senate calendar."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills is comparable in subject. The iGaming authorization bills (IL HB3080/HB4797, MA H332/S235, MD HB1343/SB885, ME LD1164), sports wagering bills (MS HB 4074, NJ A 3258) and advertising bills (MI SB0713/SB0714, NJ AR150) all regulate gambling directly. The closest overlap is with the youth and social-media bills (MI HB4388, NC H462, CO HB1255), but HB 5035 regulates school device use, not platform operators. Its only gaming link is that 'gaming' appears as a device function in the definition of 'access' in Sec. 1(a)(3).","watch":"Nothing here imposes obligations on betting, iGaming or card-break operators; at most, note that it restricts minors' in-school access to gaming and social-media functions, so monitor whether later amendments add youth-gambling or app-specific provisions.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Comm Sub","text_chars":4003,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:15:23.289Z"},"DC-B26-0659":{"summary":"This enacted act adopts the local portion of the District of Columbia's Fiscal Year 2027 budget, with total operating appropriations capped at $21,876,166,228, allocated agency by agency. Its only gaming-related content is a carryover clause for the Office of the Chief Financial Officer. That clause makes all money deposited in the Internet Gaming Revenue Fund, regardless of fiscal year, available for spending through September 30, 2027, and does the same for FY2027 deposits into the DC Lottery Reimbursement Fund. It does not create, amend or regulate any gaming, wagering or lottery program.","mechanics":["Sec. 2 caps FY2027 operating appropriations at the lesser of total District revenues or $21,876,166,228. Of that, $12,242,076,569 is local funds and $782,454,817 is dedicated taxes.","OCFO appropriation (Governmental Direction and Support, para. (21)) is $221,181,516. All funds deposited into the Internet Gaming Revenue Fund, without regard to fiscal year, are authorized for expenditure and remain available until September 30, 2027.","Under the same paragraph, funds deposited during FY2027 into the DC Lottery Reimbursement Fund are authorized for expenditure through September 30, 2027.","General provisos let local and other funds be reprogrammed through November 15, 2027 (proviso (8)). Unspent balances in non-lapsing funds roll into FY2027 (proviso (14)).","The act became law as L26-0179, effective September 15, 2026. The supplied text is truncated partway through the Public Education Systems section, so later agency lines were not reviewed."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"Like the omnibus budgets in MI HB5619 and NJ A5327/S2027, this is a general appropriations act in which gaming appears only as a fund line. MI HB6127 and MI HB4346 are different: they substantively change how internet gaming fund money is spent or allocated, whereas DC B26-0659 merely carries forward spending authority for an already existing Internet Gaming Revenue Fund. Unlike the iGaming authorization bills (IL HB3080/HB4797, MD HB1343/SB885, MA H332/S235), it contains no licensing, tax-rate or regulatory provisions.","watch":"Watch the companion FY2027 Budget Support Act and the underlying statute governing the Internet Gaming Revenue Fund, since any change to DC gaming revenue use, rates or regulation would appear there rather than in this appropriations act.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":81923,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:15:27.583Z"},"CT-SB240":{"summary":"SB 240 directs the Commissioner of Mental Health and Addiction Services to develop a comprehensive public health plan to identify, prevent and reduce the risks of Internet gaming disorder, and to identify any link between that disorder and gambling behaviors. 'Internet gaming disorder' is not defined; the term usually refers to compulsive video or online game play rather than licensed online casino wagering. The bill creates no operator obligations and is a planning and reporting mandate only.","mechanics":["Section 1 assigns the Commissioner of Mental Health and Addiction Services (DMHAS) to develop the plan; it is a new, uncodified section.","The plan must cover data monitoring, public education, early screening, health care provider training and evidence-based treatment pathways.","The plan must also coordinate with existing gambling harm prevention efforts, with particular attention to youth and other vulnerable populations.","The plan must identify any link between Internet gaming disorder and gambling behaviors.","The report is due to the Public Health Committee by January 1, 2027, under CGS 11-4a.","The act takes effect from passage; it sets no enforcement, penalties, fees or appropriation, and does not define 'Internet gaming disorder.'"],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"Unlike the iGaming authorization bills in IL (HB3080, HB4797), MD (HB1343, SB885, SB884) and MA (H332, S235), SB 240 neither authorizes nor regulates any form of gaming. It also imposes no direct operator restrictions, unlike ME LD 2080 (credit card ban) or NJ A 3258 (micro-bet ban). It is closest in spirit to NJ AR150's harm-reduction focus, but it is a public-health study aimed at video-game-style 'gaming disorder' and its possible crossover into gambling. That framing is unusual among the listed bills.","watch":"Watch whether the DMHAS plan, due January 1, 2027, treats gambling-like mechanics such as loot boxes, mystery packs, live card breaks or social and sweepstakes casinos as a bridge between gaming disorder and gambling, and whether it recommends youth-focused screening, advertising or product restrictions that later legislation could impose on operators and collectibles companies.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":1916,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:15:28.965Z"},"DC-B26-0660":{"summary":"This is the District of Columbia's emergency act adopting the local portion of the FY2027 budget (fiscal year ending September 30, 2027), setting agency-by-agency appropriations and fund-availability rules. Its only gaming content in the text provided is a proviso under the Office of the Chief Financial Officer keeping the Internet Gaming Revenue Fund and the DC Lottery Reimbursement Fund available for expenditure through September 30, 2027. It creates no new wagering rules, tax rates, or licensing requirements.","mechanics":["Caps total FY2027 operating appropriations at the lesser of total District revenues or $21,876,166,228, of which $12,242,076,569 is local funds and $782,454,817 is dedicated taxes (Sec. 2, proviso (1)).","Office of the Chief Financial Officer receives $221,181,516. All funds deposited in the Internet Gaming Revenue Fund, regardless of fiscal year, are authorized for expenditure and remain available until September 30, 2027.","All funds deposited during FY2027 into the DC Lottery Reimbursement Fund, also under the CFO, are authorized for expenditure and remain available until September 30, 2027.","Unspent balances in non-lapsing funds at the end of FY2026 carry forward and are appropriated for FY2027 (proviso (14)). Reprogramming and transfers are allowed through November 15, 2027 (proviso (8)).","Emergency act: published as A26-0378 at 73 DCR 011097 and expires October 27, 2026, so a permanent or temporary companion measure is needed for continuity.","The text supplied is truncated partway through the Public Education Systems heading (at Special Education Transportation), so any lottery or gaming-agency appropriations appearing later in the act cannot be confirmed."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"Like the omnibus appropriations bills listed, including MI HB5619, MI SB0878, NJ A5327 and NJ S2027, this act touches gaming only as a budget line. Unlike MI HB4346 and MI HB6127, it does not change how internet gaming revenue is allocated or how the gaming fund may be spent. It only preserves expenditure availability of the existing Internet Gaming Revenue Fund. It contains nothing comparable to the substantive iGaming authorization bills (IL HB3080, MD HB1343, MA S235), the advertising limits (MI SB0713), or the prediction-market measures (CT HB05038, NJ A5336).","watch":"Operators should check whether the untruncated text or the companion Budget Support Act redirects Internet Gaming Revenue Fund or lottery-related balances, or changes sports-wagering fees or taxes, since this appropriations act only carries those funds forward.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":81898,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:15:37.395Z"},"CO-HB1255":{"summary":"HB26-1255 would have required large social media platforms to keep a 24/7 law-enforcement contact process and staffed hotline, and to comply with search warrants for user-account information within 24 hours. It also required operators to report to the user's local police or sheriff, within 24 hours, any human-reviewed account restriction, suspension, or termination imposed for publicly posted content violating the platform's policies on specific or imminent threats. The Governor vetoed it on 2026-05-28, so none of it takes effect.","mechanics":["Coverage (new 16-3-314(1)(b)): a public or semipublic site or app with at least 1,000,000 discrete monthly users, user profiles, and user-generated content viewable by others. Services whose predominant function is interactive or virtual gaming, as described in 6-1-1601(4)(b)(VII), are expressly included. Excluded are internal enterprise tools, pure e-commerce marketplaces, health or reproductive-care services, review-only comment features, and predominantly provider-selected news, sports, entertainment, or e-commerce content whose chat features relate to that content.","Warrants (16-3-314(2)-(4)): operators must run an always-available process and staffed hotline, acknowledge a warrant within 8 hours, give status updates, and comply within 24 hours. This is subject to the Colorado Privacy Act, the Reproductive Health Equity Act, and the state constitution, and a court may extend the deadline on a written good-cause finding with no 'adverse result' under 18 U.S.C. 2705(a)(2).","Enforcement of the warrant section: the AG or a district attorney may seek an injunction, restitution, disgorgement, damages, and civil penalties of up to $250,000 per violation.","Adverse-action reporting (new 6-1-1602): operators must report to the user's municipal police or county sheriff within 24 hours, including the content at issue. Reporting is barred where the operator believes in good faith that the content is legally protected health-care activity. A violation is a deceptive trade practice under 6-1-105(1)(vvvv), and the AG has rulemaking authority.","Amendments to other law: Section 5 would have amended 16-3-313, as added by SB26-011, cutting that bill's warrant deadline for covered platforms from 72 to 24 hours and raising its penalty from $5,000 to $250,000 per violation, contingent on SB26-011 becoming law. Section 4 also amends the active-user threshold in 6-1-1601(4)(a)(I); the scanned strike-through is garbled, so the new figure is unclear.","Effective date would have been the 90-day post-adjournment date (August 12, 2026), subject to referendum, but the bill was vetoed."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills is a close analogue. AK HB47 targets criminal liability for generated sexual imagery and CSAM rather than platform warrant or reporting duties. AL HB351 is a general consumer data-privacy bill. AL HJR51 only creates a children's internet safety study commission. What stands out about CO HB1255 is the combination of a 24-hour warrant-compliance mandate, compulsory proactive reporting of threat-based moderation decisions to local police, and $250,000-per-violation penalties, along with its express inclusion of interactive or virtual gaming services in the social media definition.","watch":"If this is reintroduced after the veto, check whether a sportsbook, iGaming, or card-break app with user profiles, community chat or live-stream comments, and 1 million or more monthly users falls outside the provider-selected sports or entertainment content and e-commerce exclusions, or inside the interactive-gaming inclusion, because that would trigger 24-hour warrant compliance, threat-report duties, and $250,000-per-violation exposure.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":14535,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:15:39.320Z"},"DC-B26-0661":{"summary":"DC B26-0661 is the District's omnibus Fiscal Year 2027 Budget Support Act, enacted as D.C. Law L26-0189 effective October 2, 2026. It amends dozens of unrelated laws covering telework, lobbying fees, the Attorney General's litigation fund, economic development incentives, education funding, tax conformity, TIF districts and special fund transfers. The table of contents lists a Title II Subtitle DD titled \"Internet Gaming Revenue Fund\" (p. 59), but the text supplied is cut off in Title I, so the substance of that subtitle cannot be confirmed here.","mechanics":["Title II, Subtitle DD, \"Internet Gaming Revenue Fund\" (p. 59 of the enrolled text) is the only gaming-related heading in the act; its operative text is not in the excerpt provided.","Title VII includes Subtitle M (Special Fund Transfers) and Subtitle N (Special Fund Sweep Reversals), which could move money into or out of dedicated funds; whether any gaming fund is affected cannot be confirmed from the excerpt.","Sec. 1032 raises the lobbyist registration fee from $350 to $500, and the fee for nonprofit lobbyists from $100 to $250, extending the nonprofit rate to 501(c)(4) organizations. It also raises the late-filing penalty to $200 per day for up to 60 days.","Sec. 1072 redirects most Office of the Attorney General litigation recoveries into the Litigation Support Fund, with carve-outs for listed funds. Where an agency referred the matter or gave significant support, recoveries are split 50/50 with the agency's special fund or the General Fund. Outside contingency-counsel amounts are excluded from the $23.5 million cap.","Effective October 2, 2026, published in the DC Register Vol. 73, p. 013120."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"Like the other omnibus budget measures listed, AK HB53, AL HB238, and CA AB101 and AB109, DC B26-0661 is primarily a fiscal vehicle; none of those listings indicate gaming provisions. What sets DC's bill apart is a dedicated \"Internet Gaming Revenue Fund\" subtitle. That points to the District earmarking or restructuring proceeds from online gaming through the budget process rather than through standalone gaming legislation.","watch":"Obtain the full text of Subtitle DD and the Title VII fund transfer and sweep subtitles to confirm whether internet gaming or sports-wagering revenue is being re-earmarked, swept to the General Fund, or tied to new operator obligations or a broader iGaming authorization.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":246898,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:15:43.211Z"},"MD-HB1255":{"summary":"HB1255 places a question on the November 2026 general election ballot asking Maryland voters whether to expand commercial gaming to authorize Internet gaming (iGaming), as required by Article XIX, § 1(e) of the Maryland Constitution. If voters approve, the General Assembly may by later legislation authorize the State Lottery and Gaming Control Commission to issue iGaming licenses, limited to video lottery operation (casino) licensees and entities under contract with them. The bill itself sets no tax rate, license fee, or operating rules; it only states legislative intent that the State's share of revenue go to dedicated purposes including public education.","mechanics":["Section 1(a): the General Assembly may authorize, by law, the State Lottery and Gaming Control Commission to license Internet gaming; the bill does not itself authorize operations.","Section 1(b)(2): licenses may issue only to holders of a video lottery operation license (as defined in State Government § 9–1A–01) and, subject to further eligibility criteria set by law, entities under contract with such holders. This effectively ties iGaming to Maryland's six casinos and their platform partners.","Section 1(b)(1): implementing legislation must set criteria for eligible applicants and specify the permissible forms and means of conducting Internet gaming.","Section 2: if the referendum passes, the State's share of iGaming revenue is to be used for dedicated purposes including public education. No tax rate or fee is specified.","Section 4: ballot question at the November 2026 general election. If a majority votes 'For', the Act takes effect 30 days after the official canvass. If a majority votes 'Against', the Act is null and void with no further legislative action required.","Section 5: the Act takes effect July 1, 2026, solely to enable the referendum. Cross-filed with SB 761; assigned to Ways and Means, with a hearing set for 3/05."],"issue_tags":["legalize","licensing","tax"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike IL HB3080/HB4797 and OH SB197, which are full statutory iGaming frameworks setting licensing, taxes and operating rules, MD HB1255 is only an enabling referendum that defers every operational and fiscal term to future legislation. It most resembles the MA bills (H332, H4431, S235) in aim, but Maryland's constitutional requirement for voter approval of gaming expansion makes a ballot question a precondition. Its land-based tether, limiting licenses to casino licensees and their contracted partners, contrasts with ME LD1164, which would channel iGaming to the Wabanaki Nations.","watch":"Watch whether the cross-filed SB 761 or the implementing legislation that follows a successful November 2026 vote limits standalone online operators to contracted 'skin' status under casino licensees, and what tax rate, skin caps, and eligibility criteria it sets under Section 1(b).","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":4328,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:15:48.585Z"},"MD-SB761":{"summary":"SB761 would place a question on the November 2026 Maryland general election ballot, as required by Article XIX, § 1(e) of the Maryland Constitution for any expansion of commercial gaming, asking voters whether to authorize Internet gaming. If voters approve, the General Assembly may by later law authorize the State Lottery and Gaming Control Commission to issue Internet gaming licenses, limited to video lottery operation licensees and entities under contract with them. The bill itself contains no tax rate, licensing fee or operating rules, and it was withdrawn by its sponsor on March 13, 2026.","mechanics":["Section 1(a): the General Assembly may by law authorize the State Lottery and Gaming Control Commission to license Internet gaming in Maryland.","Section 1(b)(2): licenses are available only to holders of a video lottery operation license (the casino licensees, as defined in State Government Article § 9–1A–01) and, subject to further eligibility criteria set by law, entities under contract with those holders.","Section 1(b)(1): implementing legislation must set the eligibility criteria for applicants and specify the permissible forms and means of conducting Internet gaming.","Section 2: the State's share of Internet gaming revenue is to go to dedicated purposes, including public education funding; no rate or amount is specified.","Sections 3 and 4: the ballot question for November 2026 reads 'Do you favor the expansion of commercial gaming in the State of Maryland to authorize Internet gaming for dedicated purposes including the funding of public education?' If voters approve, the Act takes effect 30 days after the canvass; if they reject it, the Act is null and void.","Section 5: the Act takes effect July 1, 2026, solely to allow the referendum to be held."],"issue_tags":["legalize","licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike IL HB3080 and HB4797 and OH SB197, which are full statutory iGaming frameworks with licensing and tax provisions, MD SB761 is only an enabling referendum that defers every operational and tax detail to later legislation. It is narrower than ME LD1164, which steers iGaming opportunity to the Wabanaki Nations, because it confines eligibility to Maryland's existing video lottery (casino) licensees and their contracted partners. It does not address advertising or revenue allocation mechanics, which are the focus of MI SB0713/SB0714 and MI HB4346/HB6127.","watch":"Although this bill was withdrawn, watch whether the cross-filed companion (6lr3217) or a later session revives the referendum, because Section 1(b)(2) would effectively shut out standalone online operators except as contracted partners of Maryland casino licensees, and all tax and licensing terms would be set in later implementing legislation.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":4336,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:15:49.087Z"},"DC-B26-0724":{"summary":"This is the District of Columbia's emergency Fiscal Year 2027 Budget Support Act, an omnibus measure that enacts or amends dozens of unrelated provisions across government operations, economic development, public safety, education, human services, infrastructure, and finance and revenue. Its only gaming-related element visible in the provided text is a table-of-contents entry for Title II, Subtitle DD, \"Internet Gaming Revenue Fund\"; the operative text of that subtitle is not included in the excerpt, which cuts off in Title II, Subtitle B. As an emergency act it is temporary and, per the status line, expires November 11, 2026.","mechanics":["Omnibus structure: nine titles covering government direction (telework, ANC funding, lobbying fees), economic development (downtown conversions, tax abatements, TIFs), public safety, education, human services, operations and infrastructure, finance and revenue, technical corrections, and applicability.","Title II, Subtitle DD is captioned \"Internet Gaming Revenue Fund,\" but its text is not in the provided excerpt, so the fund's revenue source, deposit rules and permitted uses cannot be confirmed.","Title VII includes Subtitles M (Special Fund Transfers) and N (Special Fund Sweep Reversals), which could affect gaming-related special funds; their text is also not in the excerpt.","Sample non-gaming changes in the text: lobbyist registration fee raised from $350 to $500 (Sec. 1032); a $300,000 cap on mayoral transition payments (Sec. 1044); and the Central Washington activation abatement extended to 2027 with lowered annual caps (Sec. 2002).","Emergency act published as A26-0416 at 73 DCR 011509, expiring November 11, 2026, so any gaming-fund provision lapses unless carried in temporary or permanent companion legislation."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"The comparable listed bills are general budget vehicles: AK HB53 (operating budget), AL HB238 (Education Trust Fund appropriations), and CA AB101, AB102, AB109 and AB111 (Budget Acts of 2025 and 2026). Like those, DC B26-0724 touches gaming only as one line item within a broad fiscal package. It is unusual in carrying a dedicated subtitle captioned \"Internet Gaming Revenue Fund,\" a fund keyed to online gaming revenue, whereas none of the listed state budget bills shows a comparable gaming-specific fund in its title or description.","watch":"Obtain the full text of Subtitle DD and the special fund transfer and sweep subtitles to see whether the District is earmarking or diverting internet gaming or sports-wagering revenue, which could signal iGaming expansion or changes to the treatment of operator payments, and track the permanent FY2027 Budget Support Act that will replace this emergency act after November 11, 2026.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":197009,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:15:50.772Z"},"MD-SB932":{"summary":"SB932 adds new Commercial Law § 14-1330, requiring operators of social media platforms with Maryland users to show those users the general geographical location (state, province, or comparable subdivision plus country, derived from IP address) of each user whose account is visible to them. Platforms may not display the location of known minors or of participants in Maryland's Address Confidentiality Program. Violations are unfair, abusive, or deceptive trade practices under the Consumer Protection Act, effective October 1, 2026.","mechanics":["Under § 14-1330(a)(2), 'general geographical location' means the state, province, or comparable subdivision and the country, derived at the time of access from the IP address of the user's network connection. It expressly excludes precise geolocation data as defined in §§ 14-4701 and 14-4801. The engrossed text appears to strike 'city.'","Under § 14-1330(a)(3), a 'social media platform' is a website, app, or AR/VR application with a Maryland user that meets four conditions: it allows accounts or profiles for creating, sharing, and viewing user-generated content; it lets users view each other's content; it primarily serves as a medium for social interaction; and it populates a list of users with whom a user shares a social connection.","The definition excludes services that qualify only because they offer email or direct messaging, services composed primarily of preselected provider content, and services 'for interactive gaming.' 'Interactive gaming' is not defined in the section.","Under § 14-1330(b)(1), operators must display to a Maryland user the general location of each user whose account is visible to that user.","Under § 14-1330(b)(2)–(3), operators may not display the location of a user the operator knows or has reason to know is a minor, or of a user who certifies participation in the Address Confidentiality Program (State Government Art. Title 7, Subtitle 3). Operators must build a process for users to make that certification.","Under § 14-1330(c) and § 13-301(14)(xlix), a violation is a UDAP practice enforceable under Title 13, except §§ 13-407, 13-408, and 13-411. The effective date is October 1, 2026."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"This bill sits alongside the social-media consumer protection bills on the list rather than the gaming bills. CO HB1255 compels platforms to report adverse actions to law enforcement. MI HB4388 and OK HB1275 regulate minors' access and parental consent, and NC H462 pairs data privacy with social media safety. None of those, as described, mandates public display of every user's IP-derived location, which makes SB932 unusual: it imposes an affirmative transparency obligation rather than an access or privacy restriction. Its only gaming touchpoint is the carve-out for services 'for interactive gaming.'","watch":"Because 'interactive gaming' is undefined and sports wagering and collectibles marketplaces are not separately excluded, operators should assess whether any social features could bring their service within the four-part platform definition and trigger location-display duties and UDAP exposure from October 1, 2026; examples include sportsbook bet-sharing feeds, live card-break streaming apps with follower lists and user profiles, and community features.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Engrossed","text_chars":6050,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:15:59.595Z"},"MI-HB4388":{"summary":"HB4388 creates the Social Media Regulation Act, requiring social media companies with at least 5,000,000 account holders worldwide to verify the age of Michigan account applicants and existing account holders and to obtain express parental or guardian consent before a minor (under 18, unemancipated and unmarried) may hold an account. For minor accounts it imposes design restrictions, parental access rights, a default 10:30 p.m. to 6:30 a.m. curfew, and limits on data collection. It is enforced by the Attorney General after a 30-day notice-and-cure period and by a private right of action.","mechanics":["Coverage (Sec. 3(l)-(m)): applies to interactive computer services running a social media platform with at least 5,000,000 account holders worldwide. A long list of exclusions includes email, direct messaging, e-commerce where interaction is generally limited to reviews and listings, and 'interactive gaming, virtual gaming' services where communication relates to the content (Sec. 3(m)(i)(F)).","Age verification and consent (Sec. 5): starting 180 days after the effective date, age verification and express parental consent are required at application. Existing account holders must comply within 14 days of their first access attempt or be locked out. The AG writes rules on verification methods, which may not be limited to government ID, and verification data may be used only for compliance.","Minor-account restrictions (Sec. 7): minor accounts may not appear in search results except through friend links. Targeted or suggested groups, services, products, posts, accounts or users are banned in minor accounts. Personal information from a minor account's posts, messages or activity may not be collected or used unless needed to comply with state or federal law.","Parental access and curfew (Secs. 9, 11): parents get a password or other means to view all of the minor's posts and message responses. Access is blocked from 10:30 p.m. to 6:30 a.m. based on IP address, and parents may change or remove the curfew or set daily hour limits.","AG enforcement (Secs. 13, 15): the AG must investigate consumer complaints. Civil fines are up to $2,500 per violation, plus actual damages, injunctive relief, fees and costs. A 30-day written notice and cure period applies, and a cure with a written assurance bars suit.","Private action and anti-waiver (Secs. 17, 21): consumers may recover the greater of actual damages or $2,500 per violation. Contract terms entered into on or after the effective date that waive the act's protections, the complaint right or the private action are void."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"The closest analogues are OK HB1275 and OK HB4356, which also tie minors' social media access to age verification and parental permission. Michigan goes further, adding a statutory curfew, full parental viewing access to posts and messages, a ban on targeted or suggested content and products in minor accounts, and a $2,500-per-violation private right of action. NC H462 pairs social media safety with broader data privacy, whereas HB4388 limits data use only for minor accounts and verification data. CO HB1255 (law-enforcement reporting) and MD SB932 (user location display) address different platform duties, and none of the listed gaming bills, such as CT HB05038's under-21 prediction-market ban, regulate wagering through social media rules.","watch":"Sportsbook and iGaming operators fall outside the 5,000,000-holder social media definition and benefit from the interactive-gaming exclusion in Sec. 3(m)(i)(F), but collectibles clients running live card breaks with chat should check whether a platform's interactive features exceed the narrow e-commerce exclusion in Sec. 3(m)(i)(E). Separately, Sec. 7(1)(b)'s ban on targeted or suggested products in minor accounts, combined with the $2,500-per-violation private action, could push large platforms to tighten ad-delivery controls in ways that affect all operators' Michigan social media marketing.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":15680,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:16:07.700Z"},"MI-HB5834":{"summary":"HB 5834 rewrites section 19 of Michigan's Lawful Internet Gaming Act (MCL 432.319) to specify which individuals the Gaming Control Board must fingerprint and run through state police and FBI criminal history record checks. It replaces the general reference to applicants, licensees, and \"other persons\" with defined lists of individuals tied to internet gaming supplier and occupational licenses. Fingerprints are kept for ongoing arrest notifications (rap back).","mechanics":["Sec. 19(2) and (4): Anyone applying for or holding an internet gaming supplier license or occupational license must consent to inspections, criminal history record checks, searches and seizures, and to providing handwriting exemplars, fingerprints, and photographs; the Board may run checks on these individuals and on Board employees and prospective employees. The prior reference to Board agents and contractors and the separate photo-plus-two-fingerprint-sets requirement in former subsection (1) appear to be struck.","Sec. 19(6): Occupational licensees subject to checks include live internet game dealers, infrastructure technology personnel who can directly affect gameplay, their supervisors, and managers of an internet gaming operation, including the heads of fraud and risk, accounting and finance, and operations, and whoever remits daily payment to the state.","Sec. 19(7): Supplier-side individuals include directors, and principal executive, operations, or accounting officers who are not occupationally licensed. It also covers directors and principal officers of any \"qualifying business,\" meaning an entity that controls the applicant, holds more than a 5% direct or indirect ownership interest, or shares common ownership and provides a material aspect of the applicant's goods or services in Michigan.","Sec. 19(5): Fingerprints go to the Michigan State Police and the FBI. State Police must perform every check the Board requests and may charge the Board a fee. The Board may not share check results with any private entity.","Sec. 19(6)-(7) (renumbered retention provisions): State Police keep fingerprints in an automated identification system that flags new arrests and must notify the Board immediately. The FBI may also retain prints in its automatic notification system if State Police participates.","No effective date, penalties, or fee amounts are stated in the text."],"issue_tags":["licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Most listed iGaming bills would build new regimes from scratch, including IL HB3080/HB4797, MD HB1343/SB885/SB884, MA H332/S235/H4431, and ME LD1164. HB 5834 instead is a narrow technical amendment to an existing, operating Michigan iGaming market. It focuses solely on the statutory authority for fingerprint-based background checks. The closest analogue is OH SB197, which amends Ohio's criminal-records-check statute (R.C. 109.572) as part of legalizing internet gambling. Michigan's bill is unusual in listing specific job functions and parent-company officers by role rather than relying on general \"applicant/licensee\" language.","watch":"Watch the expanded reach to directors and principal officers of parent and affiliate entities holding more than 5% or sharing common ownership, which creates continuing FBI rap-back monitoring of their arrests, and watch for a parallel amendment to the Lawful Sports Betting Act (2019 PA 149) that would extend the same regime to sportsbook personnel.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":6874,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:16:09.828Z"},"MI-HB4347":{"summary":"HB 4347 rewrites how Michigan's agriculture and equine industry development fund under the Horse Racing Law of 1995 is distributed. It replaces open-ended sums and caps with fixed percentages of the fund's prior fiscal-year-end balance, calculated after set carve-outs, including a backfill to the school aid fund if internet gaming deposits fall below FY2024 levels. It also tightens section 26 so that pari-mutuel wagering, including through third-party facilitators, may be offered only by a race meeting licensee and only at the racetrack named in its license.","mechanics":["Sec. 20(18) sets the distribution base as the prior fiscal-year-end fund balance after these deductions: $2.5M to the Michigan Gaming Control Board; MDARD's administrative costs; testing costs under 20(13); the compulsive gaming prevention deposit of 1/15 of 1% of gross wagers under 20(15); and, if current-year Lawful Internet Gaming Act school aid deposits (MCL 432.316) fall below the FY ending 9/30/2024, the shortfall paid to the state school aid fund.","Standardbred and fair shares under 20(5): 16% purses; 6% split equally among county fairs; 25% special 2- and 3-year-old purses; 12% breeders' awards, with the cap raised from 10% to 20% of gross purse; 2% fair training and stabling; 8% fair operations (judges, photo finish, vet, drug and alcohol testing); 5% 4-year-old races; 15% standardbred sire stakes.","Thoroughbred shares under 20(6): 3% owner awards; 3% breeders' awards, with the cap raised from 10% to 30%; 1% 4-year-old-and-older races; 3% sire stakes. If no thoroughbred meet runs, top-5 earner award pools pay out 50/25/12/8/5. Research and development grants under 20(11) receive 0.5% each.","New 'eligible recipient' definition (resident of Michigan) in sec. 2(h) is used to restrict owner and breeder awards and stallion ownership. Owners must file eligibility forms by January 15, and MDARD must certify to Treasury by February 15.","New 20(19) sends any fund allocation above $8,000,000 in a fiscal year to the pari-mutuel horse racing disbursement account under sec. 19. Third-party facilitator wager tax money stays excluded from the fund under 20(2).","Sec. 26(2): wagering on live or simulcast races, in person or via a third-party facilitator, may be offered only by a race meeting licensee holding all required approvals, and only at the licensed racetrack identified in the license; fair racetracks need a current track license. Enacting section 1 makes the bill contingent on enactment of HB 4346 or the Senate companion (request S01228'25)."],"issue_tags":["licensing","tax"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"None of the listed bills addresses horse racing or pari-mutuel fund allocation. The internet gaming authorization bills (IL HB3080 and HB4797, MD HB1343 and SB885, OH SB197, MA H332 and S235) create new iGaming markets and tax structures. MI HB4347 instead treats existing Michigan iGaming revenue as a benchmark, using equine fund money to make the school aid fund whole if Lawful Internet Gaming Act deposits drop below FY2024. That cross-subsidy, paired with a tie-bar to HB 4346, is unusual and suggests the package anticipates changes to iGaming revenue flows, though HB 4346's text is not provided here.","watch":"Operators should track tie-barred HB 4346 and the 20(18)(e) school-aid backfill keyed to FY2024 iGaming deposits, which signals possible changes to iGaming revenue allocation. Online horse-wagering partners should also note that sec. 26(2) restricts third-party facilitator wagering to race meeting licensees tied to a specific licensed racetrack.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Engrossed","text_chars":31810,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:16:12.182Z"},"MI-HB6127":{"summary":"Public Act 93 of 2026 amends sections 14 and 16 of Michigan's Lawful Internet Gaming Act (MCL 432.314 and 432.316), mainly to change how money in the internet gaming fund is spent. The chaptered text restates the operator tax in section 14 and sets out a revised spending order (waterfall) in section 16 that now ends with a $1,000,000 annual allocation for tribal governments before the remainder goes to the state school aid fund. It took immediate effect on September 21, 2026.","mechanics":["Sec. 14(1) restates the graduated tax on non-tribal operators' annual adjusted gross receipts: 20% under $4M, 22% from $4M to under $8M, 24% from $8M to under $10M, 26% from $10M to under $12M, and 28% at $12M or more. Tribal operators instead make payments under section 7(1)(f).","Sec. 14(3) requires monthly payment, due on the 10th of the following month. Sec. 14(5) keeps the 1.25% city municipal services fee on the adjusted gross receipts of operators that hold a casino license in a city imposing that fee on casinos.","Sec. 14(4) bars any other tax, payment or fee imposed only on internet gaming operators, except as provided in the act and in section 12(17) of the Michigan Gaming Control and Revenue Act (MCL 432.212). It preserves existing city development agreements.","Sec. 16(4) waterfall, in order: (a) the board's internet gaming regulatory costs; (b) costs of administering millionaire party activity under the bingo act; (c) $3M a year to the compulsive gaming prevention fund; (d) up to $7M a year to the Christopher R. Slezak first responder presumed coverage fund, to cover 50% of claims and administration costs.","Sec. 16(4)(e)-(f): $1M a year goes to the Michigan strategic fund for distribution to federally recognized tribal governments for essential government services, under an allocation plan recommended by the Inter-Tribal Council of Michigan. All remaining money goes to the state school aid fund.","Immediate effect: approved and effective September 21, 2026."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Ohio SB197, Illinois HB3080/HB4797, Maryland HB1343/SB885 and Massachusetts H332/S235 would create or legalize and tax iGaming from scratch, whereas Michigan HB6127 adjusts revenue distribution inside an established iGaming regime without changing operator obligations in substance. New Jersey A5336/S4447 add a new prediction-market surtax, while Michigan's bill leaves the operator rate schedule as restated. Its $3M problem-gambling allocation is a funding counterpart to Connecticut SB00240, which addresses internet gaming disorder through a public health plan rather than earmarked operator tax revenue.","watch":"Because the chaptered text does not show strikeouts, operators should confirm against the prior version that the section 14 rates and the new cross-reference to MGCRA section 12(17) do not add any payment obligation, and should watch whether diverting more fund money to non-gaming uses (the first-responder fund and tribal grants) signals future pressure to raise the 20–28% iGaming rates.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":6138,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:16:15.897Z"},"MI-SB713":{"summary":"SB 713 adds section 9a to Michigan's Lawful Internet Gaming Act (2019 PA 152) to create a statutory advertising and promotions regime for internet gaming. It requires Gaming Control Board pre-approval of all internet gaming advertisements, bars deceptive ads and marketing to people under 21, and sets a 30% under-21 audience cap and mandatory responsible-gaming disclaimers. Violations carry escalating civil fines and, on a third violation, revocation of the operator's license, with Attorney General enforcement.","mechanics":["Sec. 9a(1): every internet gaming advertisement must be approved by the board. Sec. 9a(3) lets municipalities adopt ordinances placing reasonable restrictions on public signs related to internet gaming, provided they are not unreasonably impracticable and do not conflict with the act or rules.","Sec. 9a(5): no advertising or marketing unless the advertiser has reliable evidence that not more than 30% of the TV, radio, website or print audience is reasonably expected to be under 21. Sec. 9a(7) bans marketing and sponsorships targeting under-21s, but allows named sponsorship of arenas, stadiums and other public places and of television channels.","Sec. 9a(4) bans deceptive, false or misleading ads and statements. Sec. 9a(6) requires every ad or promotion to carry a problem-gambling disclaimer and the toll-free compulsive gaming helpline number.","Sec. 9a(8) exempts ads that appear on live TV or sports broadcasts only because of their physical placement at the venue. Sec. 9a(11) defines \"advertisement\" broadly as paid marketing likely to induce internet wagering, including search, display, native, video, app ads, billboards, leaflets and sponsorships. The definition excludes TV or internet programs that discuss gaming and name a specific operator, supplier or platform provider.","Sec. 9a(9)-(10) sets penalties: a civil fine of up to $1,000 for a first violation, up to $5,000 for a second, and license revocation for a third or subsequent violation. The Attorney General may bring enforcement actions. No separate effective date is stated."],"issue_tags":["advertising"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike the authorization bills listed, which create full iGaming licensing frameworks with advertising as one component, SB 713 is a standalone advertising statute for an already-legal iGaming market. These include IL HB3080/HB4797, MD HB1343/SB885, OH SB197 and MA H332/S235. NJ AR150 is only a non-binding resolution urging limits on targeted ads to problem gamblers. CT HB05038 applies an under-21 advertising ban to prediction markets rather than licensed casinos. SB 713 is unusual for combining universal board pre-approval, local sign ordinances and mandatory license revocation on a third violation, which is far harsher than the modest fine amounts suggest.","watch":"Operators should watch the automatic license revocation on a third violation, which could be triggered by low-level ad infractions given universal pre-approval and helpline disclaimer requirements, along with how the board and Attorney General will define \"reliable evidence\" for the 30% under-21 audience test and whether affiliate or influencer content falls within the broad \"advertisement\" definition.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":3280,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:16:24.997Z"},"NC-H198":{"summary":"H198 is North Carolina's 2026 alcoholic beverage control omnibus. It authorizes the ABC Commission to borrow up to $310 million for a new automated warehouse, makes technical fixes across Chapter 18B, and creates new permit types, including a service business permit and a special multiple event permit for nonprofits and political organizations. Its only gaming-adjacent content is letting nonprofit and political organizations offer sealed alcoholic beverages as raffle prizes or auction items at fund-raising events.","mechanics":["Sec. 1: one-time ABC Commission loan of up to $310,000,000, repaid only from permit fees and with no pledge of State credit or real property; effective July 1, 2026.","Sec. 6: permit year runs to May 31, a safe harbor bars revocation for unpaid renewal or registration fees until June 1, and a 25% late fee applies after April 30; the Commission gains discretion to renew despite tax noncompliance, and late-fee refund requests are due by May 31, 2027.","Sec. 11 (new G.S. 18B-1001(26)): a service business permit lets occupational-licensed service businesses serve complimentary beer and wine, capped at two servings per customer per day (16 oz beer, 8 oz wine); the application fee and the annual registration fee are each $50; effective September 1, 2026.","Sec. 16 (G.S. 18B-1002(a)(5)): special one-time permits for fund-raisers are no longer limited to ticketed events, and nonprofits may offer alcoholic beverages in the manufacturer's original closed container as raffle prizes or sell them at auction.","Sec. 17 (new G.S. 18B-1002.2): a special multiple event permit covers sales, brown-bagging, raffle prizes and auctions at multiple fund-raising events, with at most two permit holders per organization and 30 days' written notice to the Commission and local government; the provided text is truncated at this point.","Sec. 15: local boards must give mixed beverage permittees 30 days' notice of any lottery or random drawing for apportioned liquor; this is an allocation mechanism, not gambling; effective October 1, 2026."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"Unlike OH HB344, which restructures electronic instant bingo and lottery terminals and levies a tax, H198 does not regulate any wagering product; its raffle references concern only alcohol as a prize at nonprofit fund-raisers. It also differs from NY S 10092, which restricts gambling advertising to minors across sports betting, prediction markets and sweepstakes. Structurally it resembles the single-subject regulatory omnibuses and budget vehicles listed (IL HB2949, MI HB5619, OH HB96), but it is confined to Chapter 18B, with no tax or appropriation provisions touching gaming.","watch":"Because the bill is now in conference and its title covers raffles, watch whether conferees add raffle-law or other gaming provisions beyond the alcohol-as-prize language, and review the truncated remainder of Section 17 and later sections for any changes to North Carolina's raffle statutes.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":129597,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:16:28.306Z"},"NC-H425":{"summary":"H425 creates a new Chapter 18D requiring anyone selling tobacco, vapor, or alternative nicotine products to North Carolina consumers to hold a permit from the ABC Commission, enforced with the ALE Division, and sets 21 as the purchase age. It builds an ABC-style licensing regime covering suitability review, fingerprint background checks, inspections, administrative penalties, and seizure and forfeiture. Gambling appears only incidentally, as prohibited conduct on permitted premises and as a disqualifier in the change-of-ownership rules.","mechanics":["Three permit types, each with a nonrefundable $400 application fee and the same renewal fee (§ 18D-302(d)): retail seller (face-to-face sales on fixed premises), delivery seller, and remote seller. Permits run May 1 to April 30, with a 25% late renewal fee (§ 18D-303).","Owners, officers, managers, and holders of 25% or more must be at least 21, generally NC residents, and free of a felony within three years and of a tobacco or misdemeanor drug conviction within two years; they must also be current on state taxes (§ 18D-300).","Retail sellers must check ID for anyone appearing under 30 (§ 18D-400(d)). Delivery and remote sellers must use independent third-party age verification and require an adult (21+) signature on delivery (§ 18D-400(e)). Vending-machine sales are banned (§ 18D-402(b)(3)).","Penalties (§ 18D-103): suspension of up to three years, revocation, or fines of $500, $750, and $1,000 for first through third violations within three years; offers in compromise of up to $5,000; and a Class 2 misdemeanor for obstructing an inspection (§ 18D-102).","§ 18D-401(a)(3) makes it unlawful for a permittee to knowingly allow any violation of the gambling statutes, among other unlawful acts, on the premises. A gambling violation within the prior 12 months also bars successor-operation rights on a change of ownership (§ 18D-303(d)(2)d).","The supplied text is truncated at § 18D-402(b)(4), so effective dates and any remaining provisions are not shown."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No listed bill is a standalone tobacco retail-licensing measure. IL HB2755 touches tobacco products only as one item in a revenue omnibus. The closest structural analogues are the Virginia cannabis bills (VA HB642, SB542, SB671, SB826), which likewise build a new licensing authority with suitability, background-check, and penalty frameworks for an age-restricted product. NY S10092 shares the youth-protection theme but targets gambling advertising to minors rather than retail sales.","watch":"Watch § 18D-401(a)(3), which would turn any gambling-statute violation on a tobacco or vape retailer's premises into a permit violation and give ALE agents broad warrantless inspection authority, raising the risk for sweepstakes terminals or card-break and mystery-pack activity hosted at convenience and vape stores.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":80431,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:16:28.863Z"},"NC-H430":{"summary":"H430 creates a new Chapter 18D requiring anyone selling tobacco, nicotine, or vapor products to North Carolina consumers to hold a tobacco retail sales permit from the ABC Commission, enforced with the ALE Division. It raises the legal purchase age to 21 and sets age-verification, signage, and product restrictions. The bill is a tobacco measure and touches gambling only in two places that tie permit status to gambling violations on the premises.","mechanics":["Under § 18D-200 it is unlawful to sell a tobacco product to a consumer without a Commission permit. There are three permit types (retail seller, delivery seller, remote seller), each with a $400 non-refundable application fee and an equal annual renewal fee. Permits run May 1 to April 30, with a 25% late fee (§§ 18D-302, 18D-303).","Under § 18D-103 the Commission may suspend a permit for up to 3 years, revoke it, or impose fines of up to $500, $750, and $1,000 for first, second, and third violations within 3 years. It may instead accept an offer in compromise of up to $5,000.","Applicants must be 21 and are fingerprinted, with SBI/FBI background checks. Disqualifiers include a felony within 3 years and a tobacco-sales or misdemeanor drug conviction within 2 years. Owners of 25% or more and managers must also qualify, and a change of 25% or more in ownership terminates the permit (§§ 18D-300, 18D-302, 18D-303(d)).","Retail clerks must check ID for anyone who appears under 30 (§ 18D-400(d)). Delivery and remote sellers must use third-party age verification and require an adult (21+) signature at delivery (§ 18D-400(e)).","Under § 18D-401(a)(3) it is unlawful for a permittee to knowingly allow violations of the gambling statutes on licensed premises. Under § 18D-303(d)(2)d, the successor-operation grace period is denied to establishments found responsible for a gambling violation within the prior 12 months.","Section 18D-402 bans vending-machine sales, sales of products not authorized by the FDA, sales outside original sealed packaging, and self-service displays except in 21+ establishments. The provided text is truncated after § 18D-402."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills is a tobacco retail licensing measure. IL HB2755 touches tobacco products only within an omnibus revenue bill. The closest structural analogues are the Virginia cannabis bills (HB642, SB542, SB671, SB826), which likewise build a new permit-based retail regime with background checks and age limits. NY S10092 shares the youth-protection theme but targets advertising of gambling to minors, whereas H430's only gambling hook is a premises-conduct rule tying tobacco permit status to gambling violations.","watch":"Convenience stores and other tobacco permittees that host sweepstakes terminals, skill games, or other contested gaming devices could lose their tobacco permits under § 18D-401(a)(3) and § 18D-303(d)(2)d if those devices are found to violate North Carolina gambling statutes, which creates indirect enforcement pressure on venue partners.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":80227,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:16:33.191Z"},"NC-H462":{"summary":"Part I of NC H462 (committee substitute, 4/29/25) enacts a comprehensive consumer data privacy law, new Chapter 75F, modeled on the Connecticut/Virginia template. It gives North Carolina residents rights to access, correct, delete and port their data and to opt out of targeted advertising, data sales and significant automated profiling, and it imposes controller and processor duties. The bill's title also references a Social Media Safety Act, but the supplied text is truncated partway through G.S. 75F-110, so neither that Part nor the enforcement provisions can be reviewed.","mechanics":["Applicability (G.S. 75F-103): covers persons doing business in NC or targeting NC residents that, in the prior year, either processed personal data of at least 35,000 consumers (excluding payment-only data), or processed data of at least 10,000 consumers and derived more than 20% of gross revenue from data sales. State bodies and GLBA-covered financial institutions are exempt at the entity level.","Consumer rights (G.S. 75F-104): access, correction, deletion, portability, and a list of specific third-party recipients. Consumers may opt out of targeted advertising, sale, and profiling for legal or similarly significant decisions. Controllers must respond within 45 days, extendable by 45, and must offer an appeal process answered within 60 days.","Universal opt-out (G.S. 75F-105, 75F-106(e)): controllers must honor authorized agents and browser or global-device opt-out preference signals. An opt-out signal overrides conflicting controller-specific settings or loyalty enrolment, though the controller may ask the consumer to confirm.","Sensitive data and minors (G.S. 75F-102(27), 75F-106(a)(4) and (7)): sensitive data, which includes precise geolocation within a 1,750-foot radius and biometrics, requires opt-in consent. Targeted advertising to, or sale of data of, consumers aged 13 to 17 requires consent where the controller has actual knowledge or wilfully disregards their age. Dark-pattern consent is invalid.","Data protection assessments (G.S. 75F-108): required for controllers processing data of at least 100,000 consumers, covering targeted advertising, sales, risky profiling and sensitive data. The Attorney General can demand them in an investigation; they are confidential but usable in enforcement.","Effective January 1, 2026 (Section 1.2). Exclusions in G.S. 75F-110 include compliance with law and regulatory inquiries, and fraud and security prevention. Penalty and enforcement terms are not in the supplied excerpt."],"issue_tags":["advertising"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"Unlike the gaming-specific advertising bills on the list, NC H462 regulates advertising only through general privacy rules. Michigan SB0713/SB0714 add internet gaming advertising and promotion rules, and Connecticut HB05038 bars prediction market use by or advertising to under-21s; H462 instead imposes targeted-ad opt-outs and minor-consent rules that apply to every covered business. Its social media component sits alongside Colorado HB1255 (platform reporting to law enforcement), Maryland SB932 (display of user location) and Michigan HB4388 (minors' social media use), but the visible text is a horizontal privacy statute. That statute is notable for a low 35,000-consumer threshold and a mandatory universal opt-out signal.","watch":"Operators should check whether geolocation compliance checks (precise geolocation is sensitive data needing consent), cross-site targeted and affiliate marketing, player-behaviour profiling, and loyalty or bonus programs fit within the G.S. 75F-110 compliance-with-law and fraud exclusions and the G.S. 75F-106(b) loyalty carve-out, and should obtain the missing enforcement and Social Media Safety Act text before the January 1, 2026 effective date.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":65891,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:16:45.504Z"},"NM-HB2":{"summary":"HB2 is New Mexico's General Appropriation Act of 2026 (signed as Chapter 67 on March 11, 2026). It makes fiscal year 2027 operating appropriations to state agencies, with amounts stated in thousands of dollars, sets reversion rules for unexpended balances, and attaches performance measures to agency programs. The portion of the text provided covers the legislative branch, the judiciary, district attorneys, the public defender, and general control agencies through part of the Department of Finance and Administration, and contains no gaming, wagering, sweepstakes, or collectibles provisions.","mechanics":["Section 3(C) appropriates Section 4 amounts for expenditure in FY2027. Section 3(D)-(E) revert unexpended FY2026 and FY2027 balances to the general fund by October 1, 2026 and October 1, 2027 respectively, unless otherwise provided.","Section 3(F) directs the state budget division to cut the operating budget of any agency whose non-general-fund revenue falls short of projections, with notice to the legislative finance committee.","Section 3(H) requires DFA to present a deficit plan to the legislative finance committee if FY2027 revenues are not expected to meet appropriations.","DFA may transfer up to $6,000,000 in aggregate from the general fund operating reserve to the board of finance emergency fund on certification of a critical emergency.","The Attorney General's legal services program draws $23,057,300 from the consumer settlement fund, including $1,500,000 reserved for the office of the child advocate.","Taxation and Revenue Department tax administration carries targets of collecting 23% of prior-year outstanding collectible balances and 50% of prior-year audit assessments."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"HB2 is a general operating budget, comparable in form to AK HB53 (operating budget), CA AB109 (Budget Act of 2026), and AL HB238 (Education Trust Fund appropriations), rather than to any substantive gaming bill on the list. None of the listed bills addresses sports betting, iGaming, sweepstakes, or card breaks either. Unlike California's practice of amending prior budget acts by item (e.g., CA AB111, AB112), New Mexico uses a single annual act with program purpose statements and embedded performance targets. The excerpt ends mid-DFA, so any appropriation to the New Mexico Gaming Control Board or Racing Commission is not in the provided text and cannot be assessed.","watch":"Check the later, unexcerpted sections of HB2 for the Gaming Control Board and Racing Commission appropriations, any staffing or enforcement language, and any performance measures that could signal regulatory priorities, along with any contingent appropriations tied to gaming legislation passed in the same session.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":159138,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:16:47.627Z"},"NC-H680":{"summary":"H680 creates a new Chapter 18D that brings hemp-derived consumable products (finished goods for ingestion or inhalation with no more than 0.3% delta-9 THC on a dry weight basis, but possibly higher levels of other cannabinoids such as delta-8, delta-10 and THCA) under a permit regime run by the ABC Commission, with enforcement by the ALE Division. It requires a permit for any sale to a North Carolina consumer, whether in store or by delivery. It also sets permit eligibility rules, age-verification duties, inspection powers, administrative penalties, and seizure and forfeiture procedures. Gambling appears only once, as a disqualifier in the change-of-ownership provision.","mechanics":["Under § 18D-200 it is unlawful to sell hemp-derived consumable products to a consumer in NC without a Commission permit. There are two permit types: retail seller (in-person sales on fixed premises only) and delivery seller (in-state or out-of-state, covering online, phone and mail orders). Delivery services carrying goods for a permittee are exempt.","Under §§ 18D-302 and 18D-303 the application fee is $2,000 per location or per delivery website, capped at $50,000 for entities with more than 25 locations or websites. Fees are non-refundable and go to the General Fund. Permits run May 1 to April 30, renewal costs the same as the application fee, and late renewal carries a 25% surcharge.","Under § 18D-300 owners, officers and holders of 25% or more must be at least 21 and must meet residency requirements (or appoint a resident attorney-in-fact). They must have no felony conviction within 3 years, no hemp-law or misdemeanor drug conviction within 5 years, and no permit revocation within 5 years, and must be current on state taxes. Applicants submit fingerprints for SBI/FBI background checks.","Under § 18D-400 retail sellers must demand proof of age from anyone who reasonably appears to be under 30. Delivery sellers must use an independent third-party age verification service that checks against public records. The text is truncated at this point; the preamble targets under-21s.","Under § 18D-103 the Commission may suspend a permit for up to 3 years, revoke it, or impose fines of up to $500, $750 and $1,000 for first, second and third violations within 3 years. It may instead accept an offer in compromise of up to $5,000. Under § 18D-102, obstructing an inspection is a Class 2 misdemeanor.","Under § 18D-303(d)(2)d, a successor owner may keep operating under the prior permit during a change of ownership only if the establishment has not been found responsible for a gambling, assault, disorderly conduct, prostitution or controlled substance violation in the prior 12 months. Under § 18D-304(c), no permits are issued on public school, college or university property."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"The closest listed bills are the cannabis bills: OH SB56 and VA HB642, SB542, SB671 and SB826. Those build full adult-use marijuana markets with dedicated licensing authorities, and in Virginia's SB826 a 12.875% marijuana tax plus sales and local taxes. NC H680 is narrower: it regulates only intoxicating hemp-derived products under the federal 0.3% delta-9 threshold. It bolts that regulation onto the existing ABC Commission and ALE structure, and the excerpt contains no excise tax. Its only gaming touchpoint is the gambling-violation bar on successor operation, which is far more tangential than gaming-focused bills like NY S 10092 (youth gambling ad ban) or OH HB344 (electronic instant bingo).","watch":"The bill is essentially irrelevant to wagering, but operator counsel should note two things: the § 18D-303(d)(2)d gambling-violation disqualifier could affect convenience-store or bar licensees that also host gaming, sweepstakes or lottery activity, and the delivery-seller third-party age-verification and permit-number-in-advertising requirements could become a template for future NC online gaming or sweepstakes rules.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":73395,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:16:48.283Z"},"NC-H999":{"summary":"H999 creates a new Article 11 in Chapter 18C authorizing video gaming terminals (VGTs), classified as a lottery game, regulated by the NC Lottery Commission and placed with ABC-permitted retailers through licensed operators and manufacturers. It also creates Article 2F of Chapter 105, a $10,000-per-machine excise stamp tax on unauthorized gaming machines, including illegal video sweepstakes devices. Several of its general Chapter 18C amendments, on records, gifts and dispute resolution, reach existing sports wagering licensees under Articles 9 and 10.","mechanics":["Placement (18C-1102): up to 3 VGTs per location, plus up to 3 more based on a Commission net machine revenue benchmark. Terminals must be at ABC-permit merchants, more than 1,000 feet from churches and schools, on no government property, and connected at all times to a central monitoring system.","Revenue split of net machine revenue (18C-1103): 32% to the NC Video Gaming Fund; 8% for administration and enforcement, including at least $3M a year to ALE, $1M in local anti-illegal-gaming grants and $225K for a prosecutor; 30% to operators; 30% to merchants.","Licensing (18C-1114, 18C-1120): initial fees are $150K for manufacturers, $250K plus $150 per terminal for operators, and $1,500 per location for merchants; renewals are $100K, $100K plus $150 per terminal, and $1,000. Operators need 2 years of uninterrupted in-state amusement operation and residency or organization, and no foreign change of control within 2 years. Strict tier separation (18C-1122).","Play rules: 21+ only; maximum cash award of $1,199 per wager; odds posted on or near each terminal; a voluntary exclusion program; and a total advertising ban, including websites, social media and broadcast (18C-1115), apart from a standard Commission entry sign.","Criminal penalties (18C-1142): possessing an unauthorized VGT or tampering is a Class 1 misdemeanor, escalating to a Class G felony on a third offense; manipulation or false revenue reporting is a Class G felony, then Class F.","Unauthorized machine tax (G.S. 105-113.152): $10,000 excise per unauthorized gaming machine, payable within 48 hours of placement and evidenced by a stamp. It covers unpermitted VGTs, illegal slots and devices under G.S. 14-306, 14-306.1A, 14-306.3(b) and 14-306.4 (electronic sweepstakes). Paying the tax gives no immunity from prosecution."],"issue_tags":["legalize","licensing","tax","advertising","integrity","sweeps"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"The closest listed analog is OH HB344, which likewise expands lottery-run terminal gaming (electronic instant bingo and lottery terminals) with a tax. H999 instead builds a full private three-tier VGT market under the Lottery Commission with a fixed 30/30 operator-merchant revenue share. H999's flat ban on all VGT advertising is far broader than NY S10092, which only bars gambling ads directed at minors on covered platforms. The NE LB402 and LB 415 winnings-setoff bills have no counterpart here beyond H999's statement that winnings are subject to state income tax, and the remaining listed bills (budgets, cannabis, revisory) are not comparable.","watch":"Sports wagering licensees should watch three spillover provisions in the Chapter 18C amendments: new 18C-114(a)(17), which lets the Commission impose mandatory dispute-resolution policies on Article 9 and 10 licensees that must be exhausted before suing; the new 18C-117 gift restrictions applying to all Chapter 18C licensees (drafted oddly as 'not exceeding $100'); and the unexplained repeals of G.S. 18C-143(e) and 18C-151(f).","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":68041,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:16:54.504Z"},"NY-S8578":{"summary":"S08578 is the City of Staten Island Act. It incorporates Staten Island (Richmond County) as a city separate from New York City, enacts a full city charter, and sets a staged transition. It also makes conforming amendments across the education, judiciary, housing, public authorities, county, election, finance and tax laws, including provisions letting the new city continue taxes currently applicable there. The portion of the text provided contains no provision on sports wagering, casino gaming, lotteries or collectibles.","mechanics":["Timeline (§1-003, §1-004): incorporation on January 1 after enactment; the charter takes effect the following November 1; full municipal powers devolve on July 1 of the second year after enactment; a judiciary transition period runs through December 31 of the fifth year after incorporation.","During the transition period New York City, the 'preceding municipality', keeps full municipal powers over the territory except as the act provides.","Charter §2-01 grants a broad general power of local self-government, stating that enumerated powers are not exclusive. The executive is a mayor elected to a four-year term (§3-02); the legislature is a 15-member common council split into 'A' and 'B' districts (§4-02).","The mayor has finance-board powers under the local finance law (§3-08(c)), and an elected comptroller has audit authority (§5-02).","The title and table of contents reference §13-001 (taxes and tax collection authority) and amendments to the general city law, chapter 772 of the laws of 1966 (city business tax) and the tax law, so the new city can continue presently applicable taxes. The substance of those sections is not in the excerpt provided."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"Nothing listed is truly comparable. The nearest analogues are large structural or omnibus vehicles, such as IL SB3731 (general revisory), IL HB2949 and OH HB96 (budget implementation) and MI HB5619 (omnibus appropriations), where gaming can be touched only through cross-references or tax continuations. That contrasts with bills squarely on gaming like NC H999 (video gaming terminals) and OH HB344 (electronic instant bingo and lottery terminals). What is unusual here is the creation of an entirely new city of roughly half a million residents, carved out of New York City, with a broad home-rule grant and wholesale re-mapping of state statutes that currently refer to 'the city of New York'.","watch":"Review the untruncated §13-001 tax-continuation provisions and the conforming statutory amendments to see whether any New York City-specific wagering-related taxes, local gaming or licensing powers, or statutory references to 'the city of New York' (for example, in casino siting or local-share provisions) are reallocated to or newly exercisable by the city of Staten Island under its broad charter §2-01 home-rule grant.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":5009470,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:17:03.342Z"},"OK-HB1275":{"summary":"HB1275 bars social media companies from letting Oklahoma users under 16 hold accounts on covered platforms and requires express parental consent for 16- and 17-year-olds, backed by mandatory age verification. It also imposes minor-specific data limits on platforms accessed by minors, including no profiling without safeguards, no sale or sharing of minors' data, and limits on geolocation and dark patterns. Enforcement is by the Attorney General plus a private right of action. Gambling appears only in the legislative-intent analogy and in a carve-out for interactive gaming services.","mechanics":["Sec. 3: no accounts for Oklahoma minors under 16. Ages 16–17 need express parent or guardian consent, confirmed when the account is opened. Age verification can use a digital ID, government ID, any commercially reasonable method, or app-store age confirmation, and may be done through a third-party vendor.","Sec. 2(B)(11): a covered 'social media platform' must have Oklahoma users, use algorithmic content selection, feature infinite scrolling, and have social interaction as a substantial function. Exclusions cover e-commerce where user interaction is generally limited to reviews and lists, preselected sports or entertainment content with incidental chat, and businesses with under $100 million in annual gross revenue from US users.","Sec. 2(B)(10)(b)(2): companies that exclusively offer interactive or virtual gaming, or user content created for gaming or entertainment, are outside the 'social media company' definition.","Sec. 4: platforms accessed by minors may not process minors' data where they know, or willfully disregard, that it risks substantial harm. They also may not profile minors without safeguards, collect precise geolocation unless strictly necessary and visibly signalled, use dark patterns, or sell or share minors' personal information or geolocation. Violations go to the AG, who must give notice, followed by a 45-day cure period.","Sec. 5: knowing violators are liable for failing to verify age or to comply with Sec. 4. The AG may bring enforcement actions. Individuals may recover $2,500 per violation, or damages for a minor's access without consent, plus costs and attorney fees. There is a safe harbor where a minor gets in by fraud or fake ID after reasonable verification.","Age-verification data retention conflicts within the bill: Sec. 5(A)(2) bars keeping identifying information after access is granted, while Sec. 6 allows retention for audit and testing for up to 30 days. Sec. 6 also creates a private damages action for knowing retention. No effective date appears in the text."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"This bill sits alongside the minors-and-social-media bills on the list, NC H462 and MI HB4388. It goes further than a parental-consent model by imposing an outright account ban under 16, layering in a Sec. 4 data-minimization regime, and adding a $2,500-per-violation private right of action. It is unrelated to the platform-reporting focus of CO HB1255 and the location-display mandate of MD SB932. Unlike CT HB05038, which restricts under-21 access to prediction markets, it does not regulate wagering platforms; gambling is invoked only as a policy analogy in Sec. 1.","watch":"Watch whether the exclusive-interactive-gaming and e-commerce carve-outs are narrowed. Live card-break and collectibles streaming marketplaces with algorithmic feeds, infinite scroll and live chat that goes beyond reviews and lists could fall within the platform definition if they exceed the $100 million revenue threshold, triggering under-16 bans, age verification and private suits.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":22491,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:17:09.170Z"},"OH-SB56":{"summary":"Ohio SB 56, now signed, overhauls the state's medical and adult-use marijuana laws. It repeals the voter-initiated adult-use chapter (R.C. 3780) and folds that program into the Division of Cannabis Control's Chapter 3796. It also revises hemp law (R.C. 928), enacts new provisions in 3779.21 to 3779.30, amends the commercial driver's license law, and makes an appropriation. It does not regulate wagering or gaming.","mechanics":["Repeals R.C. 3780.01 to 3780.99 and renumbers 3780.37 as 3796.34, consolidating adult-use cannabis regulation under Chapter 3796 and the Department of Commerce's Division of Cannabis Control.","Amends R.C. 9.79(I)(10) to exempt Division of Cannabis Control licenses from the general rules limiting how licensing authorities may use criminal convictions. Disqualifying offenses for those licenses are instead set by rules under 9.79 and 3796.03 (see 109.572(A)(13) and (A)(14)).","Requires criminal records checks under 109.572(A)(13) and (A)(14) for owners, officers, board members, administrators and employees of Chapter 3796 licensees.","Enacts R.C. 3779.21 to 3779.30 and 3779.99, and separately repeals those same newly enacted versions effective December 31, 2026. It also enacts new hemp provisions at 928.01, 928.031 and 928.08.","Amends R.C. 4506.01 (commercial driver's licenses) effective December 31, 2026, and makes an appropriation; the appropriations provisions took effect December 19, 2025.","The excerpt provided is truncated, so the marijuana tax rates referenced in the title cannot be confirmed from the text supplied."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"The closest listed analogues are the Virginia retail cannabis bills (VA HB642, SB542, SB671 and SB826), which build a new retail market from scratch. SB826 sets a 12.875% marijuana tax, a 1.125% sales tax and a 3% local tax. Ohio SB 56 instead restructures an existing voter-approved adult-use market and moves it into the medical marijuana chapter. NC H680 addresses youth cannabis protection, and none of the listed bills, including NC H999 on video gaming terminals or NY S 10092 on gambling ads, overlaps with SB 56 on gaming. The only gaming touchpoints are existing cross-references in R.C. 109.572 to casino and sports-gaming background checks (3772.07, 3775.03) and charitable gaming (2915.081, 2915.082), which appear to carry over unchanged.","watch":"Operators should confirm that the cross-references in R.C. 109.572(A)(11), (A)(18) and (A)(19) to Casino Control Commission and sports-gaming background checks were left unchanged, and should watch whether Ohio extends SB 56's approach of carving a regulated vice industry out of the general 9.79 criminal-record limits to gaming licensing.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":310187,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:17:09.964Z"},"OK-HB1388":{"summary":"HB1388 creates the Protecting Oklahoma's Children on Social Media Act of 2025, which imposes school-technology duties on Oklahoma public school districts and charter schools. Each must adopt an internet acceptable-use policy and a social media policy that bars students from reaching social media platforms on school equipment or networks, subject to State Department of Education and State Board of Education review. It does not regulate gambling, gaming operators, or commercial platforms directly.","mechanics":["Acceptable-use policy due by October 1, 2025 (Sec. 4). It must block obscene material, child pornography, and material harmful to minors on school equipment. Policies go to the State Board of Education each October 15, and districts get a 30-day cure period after a notice of noncompliance.","Social media policy due by April 1, 2026, and submitted by April 15, 2026 (Sec. 6). Student access is allowed only when directed and supervised by staff, for age-appropriate educational resources, during a school activity. Districts must use blocking and monitoring software or servers, and parents may opt their child out of specific platforms.","The 'social media platform' definition (Sec. 2(10)) excludes 22 categories. These include (f) interactive gaming and virtual gaming, (e) e-commerce where interaction is limited to reviews and lists of goods for sale, and (b) services that bar minors and use commercially reasonable age assurance.","The Department of Education must set annual technology-protection-measure standards by April 1, 2026 (Sec. 5). It must also publish a list of compliant vendors, require providers to verify compliance each year, and issue staff training guidelines by December 1, 2025.","Sec. 7 sets an effective date of July 1, 2025, and Sec. 8 adds an emergency clause. The Senate floor version is a committee substitute, placed on General Order on April 22, 2025."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"The closest analogues on the list are CT HB05035, which bans cellphones in classrooms, and MI HB4388 and NC H462, which regulate minors' social media use. Unlike the Michigan and North Carolina bills, HB1388 places no duties on platform operators; every obligation falls on school boards and state education agencies. CO HB1255 and MD SB932 likewise impose operator-facing duties that this bill lacks. Its only gaming touchpoint is the Sec. 2(10)(f) carve-out of interactive and virtual gaming from the 'social media platform' definition, which differs from the iGaming bills listed (IL HB3080, MD HB1343) that actually authorize or regulate wagering.","watch":"Operators should watch whether the 'interactive gaming' and 'age assurance' carve-outs in Sec. 2(10)(b) and (f) are carried into later Oklahoma operator-facing social media bills, since those exemptions could shield adult-only betting and iGaming apps. Collectibles clients should note that the e-commerce exclusion in (e) is narrow, so streamed card breaks on social platforms would likely fall within school blocking requirements.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":29434,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:17:12.187Z"},"OK-HB4130":{"summary":"HB4130 amends Oklahoma's core gambling-game crime, 21 O.S. Section 941, to expressly cover online casino games and to extend criminal liability beyond owners and dealers to geolocation providers, gaming suppliers, platform providers, promoters and media affiliates who provide support for an illegal game. It defines 'representatives of value' by reference to dual-currency systems, which squarely targets sweepstakes-model online casinos. The House Judiciary and Public Safety Oversight committee reported it Do Pass as amended on 02/26/2026.","mechanics":["Section 941(A) makes it a Class C2 felony to open, conduct or carry on poker, roulette, craps, banking or percentage games, or any gambling game played with dice, cards or any device, 'including, but not limited to, online casino games'; the penalty is a $500 to $2,000 fine plus imprisonment under subsections B through F of 21 O.S. Section 20M.","Covered persons expand from 'owner or employee' to include any geolocation provider, gaming supplier, platform provider, promoter or media affiliate who, whether for hire or not, 'deals or provides support for' those engaged in the game.","Section 941(B)(1) defines 'online casino games' as any gambling game accessed over the Internet by computer, phone, smartwatch, tablet or similar device that, upon risking representatives of value, simulates a Section 941 game or any other gambling, including slot machines (21 O.S. 964), lotteries (21 O.S. 1051), bingo and U-PIK-EM bingo (3A O.S. 402), and prohibited games under 3A O.S. 262(H).","Section 941(B)(2) defines 'representatives of value' as any currency used in a dual-currency payment system that can be exchanged for a prize, award, cash or cash equivalent, or a chance to win one.","The existing carve-outs remain for the Oklahoma Charity Games Act and for tribal gaming conducted under the Indian Gaming Regulatory Act.","The act takes effect November 1, 2026."],"issue_tags":["sweeps","advertising"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Like MD HB295 and SB112, which prohibit operating or promoting 'interactive games' and require licensees to report ties to illegal-market actors, HB4130 is a prohibition bill aimed at sweepstakes and offshore online casinos rather than a legalization framework. It contrasts sharply with IL HB3080 and HB4797, MD HB1343 and SB885, MA H332 and S235, and ME LD1164, all of which would authorize and license iGaming. HB4130 is unusual in using the existing criminal gambling statute, rather than a regulatory act, to reach B2B vendors and marketing affiliates. Those vendors include geolocation providers, platform providers and media affiliates, and they face direct felony exposure.","watch":"Watch the felony exposure for geolocation providers, platform providers, gaming suppliers, promoters and media affiliates who 'provide support' to dual-currency or online casino games, which could reach vendors and affiliates that serve licensed operators in other states. Also watch whether the broad 'simulates any gambling game' and dual-currency definitions could be read to sweep in prize-redeemable digital collectibles, mystery packs or card-break platforms that use coin or credit systems.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":3867,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:17:20.753Z"},"OK-SB1589":{"summary":"SB1589 amends Oklahoma's core gambling-house offense (21 O.S. Section 941) to expressly cover online casino games, including sweepstakes-style casinos that use a dual-currency system. It also extends felony liability beyond owners and dealers to geolocation providers, gaming suppliers, platform providers, promoters and media affiliates that deal or provide support for such games. It was signed and filed with the Secretary of State and takes effect November 1, 2026.","mechanics":["Section 941(A)(1) defines \"online casino games\" as any internet-accessed game on a computer, phone, smartwatch, tablet or similar device that, upon risking any representative of value, simulates a gambling game or any other form of gambling. The listed examples are slot machines (21 O.S. 964), lotteries (21 O.S. 1051(A)), bingo and U-PIK-EM bingo (3A O.S. 402), and prohibited games under 3A O.S. 262(H).","Section 941(A)(2) defines \"representative of value\" to include any currency in a dual-currency system that can be exchanged for any prize, award, cash or cash equivalent, or for any chance to win one. This targets the sweeps-coin model directly.","Section 941(B) makes it a crime to open, conduct or carry on poker, roulette, craps, banking or percentage games, or any game played with dice, cards or any device, including online casino games, for money, checks, credits or representatives of value. Liability reaches anyone acting as owner, employee, geolocation provider, gaming supplier, platform provider, promoter or media affiliate who deals or provides support, whether for hire or not.","The penalty is a Class C2 felony with a fine of $500 to $2,000 plus imprisonment under subsections B through E of 21 O.S. Section 20M.","Activities under the Oklahoma Charity Games Act and tribal gaming conducted on Indian lands in compliance with IGRA are carved out. There is no exemption for sweepstakes promotions, and none of the new supporting-party roles (such as \"media affiliate\" or \"platform provider\") is defined.","Effective November 1, 2026."],"issue_tags":["sweeps","advertising"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"The closest analogs are Maryland HB295 and SB112, which also prohibit operating, conducting or promoting unlicensed \"interactive games.\" Those bills work through the gaming regulator, requiring licensees to report business relationships with illegal-market actors. Oklahoma's bill instead works through the criminal code, imposing felony exposure on third-party vendors such as geolocation, platform, supplier and media/affiliate businesses. It sits at the opposite end from the iGaming legalization bills in Illinois (HB3080, HB4797), Massachusetts (H332, S235), Maryland (HB1343, SB885) and Maine (LD1164), offering no licensing pathway at all.","watch":"Watch the undefined supporting-party categories, especially \"media affiliate,\" \"promoter\" and \"platform provider,\" and the broad dual-currency and \"simulates any other form of gambling\" language. These could expose affiliates, payment and geolocation vendors, and potentially online card-break or digital mystery-pack sellers using redeemable currencies to felony liability starting November 1, 2026, because the text contains no express knowledge requirement and no sweepstakes safe harbor.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":3977,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:17:26.967Z"},"OK-HB4356":{"summary":"HB4356 would bar social media companies from letting Oklahoma users under 16 hold accounts on covered platforms and would require age verification of every account holder. It also restricts how platforms accessed by minors (under 18) may process their data, including profiling, precise geolocation and dark patterns. Enforcement is by the Attorney General, with a private right of action. The bill does not address wagering, and its exclusion for exclusively interactive-gaming services means it only touches gaming or collectibles businesses at the definitional margins.","mechanics":["Sec. 2: no Oklahoma user under 16 may be an account holder, and all account holders must be age-verified. Permitted methods include a digitized ID or driver license, government ID, any 'commercially reasonable' method, or app-store confirmation, and third-party vendors may be used.","Sec. 1(B)(11): a covered 'social media platform' must have Oklahoma users, use algorithmic content selection, feature infinite scrolling and have social interaction as a substantial function. Entities under $100M in annual gross revenue from U.S. users are carved out, as are e-commerce services whose user interaction is limited to reviews, lists of goods or wish lists, and services that exclusively offer interactive gaming (Sec. 1(B)(10)(b)(2)).","Sec. 3: platforms accessed by minors (defined as under 18) may not process personal information that risks substantial harm, profile minors without safeguards and necessity, collect unnecessary data, or collect or sell precise geolocation unless strictly necessary and visibly signalled. They also may not use dark patterns. The AG gives notice of violations and there is a 45-day cure period.","Sec. 4: knowing violators are liable, the AG may bring enforcement actions, and individuals may recover $2,500 per violation or damages for a minor's access without parental consent, plus costs and attorney fees. There is a safe harbor where a minor gets access by fraud or fake ID after reasonable verification.","Sec. 4(A)(2) and Sec. 5: identifying information may not be retained after access is granted, with an audit and testing exception of up to 30 days in Sec. 5. Knowing retention creates a private damages claim.","Effective November 1, 2026."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"MI HB4388 and NC H462 are the closest comparators: both regulate minors' social media use and data, although their texts are not provided here for a detailed comparison. MD SB932 instead mandates displaying adult users' locations, and CO HB1255 concerns platform reporting to law enforcement. Neither imposes an account ban. The age-gating approach loosely parallels CT HB05038's under-21 bar on prediction markets, but HB4356 has no gambling nexus. Unusually, it sets a hard under-16 account ban while defining 'minor' as under 18 for its data-practice rules. It also pairs a 45-day AG cure period with an uncured private right of action at $2,500 per violation.","watch":"Clients running livestreamed card breaks, collector communities, or social or community features in betting and casino apps should check whether their feeds meet the algorithm, infinite-scroll and social-interaction tests. The e-commerce carve-out is limited to reviews and lists, and the interactive-gaming exclusion requires that gaming be the 'exclusive' offering, so neither may protect them.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":20039,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:17:30.653Z"},"PA-HB257":{"summary":"Although framed as a transportation omnibus, HB257 (Senate-amended, PN 2211) contains one gaming provision. Section 1 amends 4 Pa.C.S. § 13B52(d)(2)(ii) to divert money from the restricted receipt account funded by 10% of the interactive gaming tax imposed under § 13B52(a)(3) to transit and secondary-road funding. It does not change any interactive gaming tax rate or operator obligation; the rest of the bill covers transit funding, fare indexing, a mass-transit special prosecutor, new transit crimes and driver licensing.","mechanics":["One-time FY2025-26 transfers within 30 days of effectiveness from the § 13B52(d)(2)(ii) restricted receipt account: $87.5M to the Public Transportation Trust Fund and $87.5M to the new Supplemental Funding for Three and Four Digit State Routes Account in the Motor License Fund (new clause (C.1)).","Recurring transfers beginning FY2025-26: $43M each year to the Public Transportation Trust Fund and $43M each year to the Three and Four Digit State Routes Account, paid at least quarterly (new clause (C.2)).","Floor in new clause (D.1): the account balance may not fall below $20M, except as needed for county distributions under existing clauses (B) or (C). The year-end 60-day distribution deadline in clause (D) no longer applies to the new transfers.","Within the Public Transportation Trust Fund, iGaming-sourced deposits (new 74 Pa.C.S. § 1506(c)(3.5)) are split 86.76% to the operating program (§ 1513) and 13.24% to programs of statewide significance (§ 1516).","No change to tax rates, licensing, advertising or operator conduct; the gaming section only reallocates existing receipts. The available text is truncated, so the effective date for Section 1 is not shown."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"Like Michigan HB4346 (removing the cap on internet gaming revenue for the equine industry fund) and MI HB6127 (modifying internet gaming fund expenditures), PA HB257 reallocates existing iGaming receipts rather than changing the rate or regulatory framework. It differs from the authorization bills, IL HB3080/HB4797, MD HB1343/SB885 and MA H332/S235, which would create iGaming markets and set taxes from scratch. Unusually, it ties fixed dollar amounts ($175M one-time, $86M a year) from a county-oriented iGaming account to statewide transit and road funding, and attaches them to unrelated transportation and criminal provisions.","watch":"Watch whether committing fixed annual sums ($86M plus a $20M floor) from the iGaming restricted receipt account to transit and roads creates pressure to raise the § 13B52 interactive gaming tax or expand the base if receipts fall short, and how the bill fares in House Rules after Senate amendment.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":58670,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:17:33.574Z"},"PA-HB416":{"summary":"Act 45 of 2025 is Pennsylvania's omnibus Fiscal Code amendment implementing the 2025-2026 budget. Among many unrelated provisions, it creates a child care staff bonus program, extends the 911 surcharge, decouples the corporate net income tax from several One Big Beautiful Bill Act changes, restructures the Joint Underwriting Association and takes $100 million from it, and creates a Rural Health Transformation Program. Its only gaming connection is a title reference to amending distributions from the Pennsylvania Race Horse Development Fund, which falls in a part of the text not provided.","mechanics":["Child Care Staff Recruitment and Retention Program (Art. I-H.1): DHS pays qualified subsidized providers an individual retention bonus of at least $450 per direct-care staff member, with remaining funds allocated by provider type and licensed capacity. Applications are due within 45 days of the form being posted, and annual reports start September 15, 2026.","The 911 surcharge under 35 Pa.C.S. § 5306.2(a) stays at $1.95 until February 1, 2029, extended from 2026 (§ 102-K).","CNIT decoupling (§§ 216-217): R&E deductions under IRC §§ 174, 174A and 59(e), § 481 adjustments, and § 168(n) qualified production property depreciation are added back and recovered at 20% per year. For tax years after December 31, 2024, interest expense under § 163(j) is computed as the federal rule stood on December 31, 2024. DOR must report on the impact by December 31, 2026.","Joint Underwriting Association (§§ 208-D to 216-D): the 2017 sunset is repealed and $100 million must be transferred to the General Fund within 30 days for Medicaid capitation. From June 1, 2026, the Commissioner certifies amounts available for transfer when risk-based capital exceeds 1,000%. A new seven-member board is created, and the association is treated as an independent agency for Right-to-Know and procurement purposes.","Treasury (§ 313): the Treasurer may waive interest on 2025-26 budget-impasse loans to counties, domestic violence, rape crisis, Head Start and Pre-K Counts entities. From FY 2026-27, such loans replacing anticipated appropriations are barred. Separately, the Governor's disaster transfer cap under § 1508 rises from $20 million to $40 million.","The title lists amendments to distributions from the Pennsylvania Race Horse Development Fund, but the provided text is truncated before that section, so its terms cannot be confirmed."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills addresses gaming, and this act is closest in form to the omnibus budget vehicles on the list, such as CA AB101 (Budget Act of 2025) and AK HB53 (operating budget). Unlike those pure appropriation acts, PA HB416 is a substantive Fiscal Code trailer. It makes permanent tax-base changes, notably decoupling the CNIT from federal § 174A, § 168(n) and § 163(j) changes, and restructures a state insurance instrumentality. Its only gaming touchpoint is the Race Horse Development Fund distribution amendment named in the title.","watch":"Operators should obtain and review the truncated Race Horse Development Fund distribution amendments, since that fund is fed by slot gaming revenue, and should model the impact of the § 217 interest-expense and § 216 R&E/depreciation decoupling on their Pennsylvania CNIT liability for tax years beginning after December 31, 2024.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":165454,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:17:45.162Z"},"PA-SB146":{"summary":"Act 21 of 2026 is Pennsylvania's omnibus Fiscal Code amendment implementing the 2026-2027 budget. The excerpt provided covers COVID-19 fund reallocation, child care retention bonuses, cigarette stamping agent sourcing limits, Joint Underwriting Association transfers, a new food processing residuals regulatory article, and a Veterans' Trust Fund Board. The title also lists amendments on distributions from the Pennsylvania Race Horse Development Fund, the State Gaming Fund, and an amusement tax clarification, but those sections are not in the text supplied, so their content cannot be confirmed.","mechanics":["New Section 197-C: PHFA must transfer $11,789,348 of ARPA housing relief funds to the COVID-19 Response Restricted Account by September 10, 2026, for the Department of Corrections. Funds unspent by December 11, 2026 are swept by December 18, and all such money must be spent by December 31, 2026.","Section 103-H.1: the child care retention bonus rises from at least $450 to at least $540 per qualified staff member, and the application decision window extends from 45 to 60 days.","New Section 204.1-A: licensed cigarette stamping agents may buy only directly from a tobacco product manufacturer or from a newly defined 'related entity supplier' (majority common ownership), and may not sell unstamped cigarettes to other dealers.","Section 209-D: $14,300,000 is transferred from the Joint Underwriting Association to the General Fund for FY beginning July 1, 2026, earmarked for Medical Assistance capitation payments.","New Article XIII (food processing residuals) requires land application systems and three-year notifications to the State Conservation Commission, with civil penalties up to $500 for the first day and $100 per day after (Section 1314). It also creates a Department of Agriculture hauler/broker certification program with penalties up to $1,000 for the first day and $500 per day after (Section 1323).","The Veterans' Trust Fund Board is created as an advisory board with a quorum of four, and the Auditor General must deliver a performance audit by July 31, 2028 and every three years thereafter."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills address gaming. The closest analogues are other omnibus budget vehicles such as AK HB53, AL HB238, and California's budget and trailer bills (CA AB101, AB109, AB111). Unlike those appropriation acts, PA SB146 is a substantive Fiscal Code rewrite that carries unrelated regulatory programs, such as food processing residuals certification and cigarette stamping agent sourcing rules, alongside budget transfers. Its title also flags gaming-fund items (Race Horse Development Fund distributions and the State Gaming Fund) that the other states' bills do not touch.","watch":"Obtain the full enacted text to review the Race Horse Development Fund distribution, State Gaming Fund, and amusement tax clarification sections, which are listed in the title but absent from this excerpt and could redirect gaming tax revenue or affect local amusement tax exposure for gaming or entertainment operators.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":165230,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:17:46.095Z"},"RI-H7953":{"summary":"H7953 would create a new chapter 6-63 requiring social media companies with at least 5 million account holders worldwide to bar Rhode Island residents under 18 from holding accounts and to verify the age of all new and existing Rhode Island account holders starting January 1, 2027. It is enforced by the Department of Business Regulation and the Attorney General, subject to a 30-day cure period, and it also creates a private right of action. Wagering appears only in an exclusion: platforms providing interactive or virtual gaming are carved out of the definition of social media platform.","mechanics":["Coverage (6-63-1(8)-(9)): an interactive computer service with at least 5,000,000 account holders worldwide that lets users create profiles, upload posts, view others' posts and interact. There are 21 exclusions, including interactive or virtual gaming services (9)(ii)(E) and e-commerce where interaction is 'generally limited' to reviews, comments and lists of goods (9)(ii)(D).","Outright ban and age verification (6-63-2): from January 1, 2027, no minor (under 18) Rhode Island resident may be an account holder, and there is no parental-consent path despite the section heading. Age must be verified when an account is opened, or within 14 days of an existing user's attempt to access the account. Accounts that are not verified must be denied access.","DBR rulemaking: rules must cover verification methods, specifically valid government-issued ID. Verification data must be used only for compliance and securely disposed of, and any verification agent must have its principal place of business in the U.S.","Agency enforcement (6-63-4): DBR may impose administrative fines up to $2,500 per violation. A 30-day notice-and-cure period applies before any action. Repeat violators of an order face civil penalties up to $5,000. The AG may seek injunctions, disgorgement and damages, and the AG can act without a referral.","Private right of action (6-63-6): suit is filed in superior court and the plaintiff recovers the greater of $2,500 per incident or actual damages, plus attorneys' fees. Waivers, arbitration limits and choice-of-law clauses are void (6-63-7).","Status: held for further study in committee on April 8, 2026. The effective date would be January 1, 2027."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"This is closest to MI HB4388 (regulation of social media use by minors) and NC H462 (personal data privacy/social media safety). Unlike a parental-consent model, H7953 imposes a flat under-18 ban with mandatory government-ID-based verification and a $2,500 statutory-damages private right of action. CO HB1255 (operator reporting to law enforcement) and MD SB932 (display of user location) regulate platforms on different axes. The gaming-focused bills on the list, such as CT HB05038 (under-21 bar on prediction-market use and advertising) and the IL, MD and MA iGaming bills, regulate wagering directly, whereas H7953 expressly excludes interactive gaming services from its scope.","watch":"Operators should confirm their products fall within the interactive-gaming exclusion in 6-63-1(9)(ii)(E). Collectibles clients running live card breaks or community features on e-commerce platforms should check whether live chat and social interaction push them beyond the narrow 'generally limited' e-commerce carve-out in (9)(ii)(D) and the 5-million-account threshold, which would expose them to the under-18 ban and the private right of action if the bill revives.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":16405,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:17:46.406Z"},"RI-S2968":{"summary":"S2968 would create a Rhode Island Social Media Regulation Act (new chapter 6-63) requiring social media companies with at least 5 million account holders worldwide to verify the age of every Rhode Island account holder and bar minors under 18 from holding accounts without express parental or guardian consent. Minor accounts would be subject to an advertising ban, data-collection limits, direct-messaging and search restrictions, parental access, and a default 10:30 p.m. to 6:30 a.m. curfew. The Department of Business Regulation would enforce the Act, and there is a private right of action. The bill is not a gaming bill, and Senate Judiciary held it for further study on 2026-04-07.","mechanics":["Coverage (6-63-1(8)-(9)): the Act applies to interactive computer services running a social media platform with 5,000,000 or more account holders worldwide. Twenty-one carve-outs are listed, including interactive or virtual gaming services (9)(ii)(E), licensed-media streaming (B), preselected news and sports content (C), and e-commerce where user interaction is generally limited to reviews, product lists and wish lists (D).","Age verification and consent (6-63-2): starting January 1, 2027, companies must verify age at account opening, or within 14 days of an existing user's attempted access, and deny access until verification and parental consent are complete. DBR must write rules on acceptable ID methods, consent confirmation, and data retention and disposal, and any verification agent must have its principal place of business in the U.S.","Restrictions on minor accounts (6-63-3): no advertising may be displayed, no personal data may be collected beyond what compliance requires, no direct messages or search visibility for unlinked (non-friend) users, and no targeted or suggested content, groups, products or accounts.","Parental access and curfew (6-63-4, 6-63-5): consenting parents must get full access to the minor's posts and messages. A default 10:30 p.m. to 6:30 a.m. lockout applies, measured by the minor's IP address, and parents can change it and set daily hour limits.","Agency enforcement (6-63-7): DBR may impose administrative fines of up to $2,500 per violation, sue for injunction, disgorgement and damages, and recover attorney fees. Repeat violators of an order face up to $5,000. A mandatory 30-day notice-and-cure period applies before DBR can act.","Private action and waivers (6-63-9, 6-63-10): subsection (a) authorizes private suits only for 6-63-2 violations, but subsection (c) awards relief for violations of 6-63-2, 6-63-3 or 6-63-4, which is internally inconsistent. Relief is the greater of $2,500 per incident or actual damages, plus attorney fees. Contractual waivers and choice-of-law limits on these protections are void."],"issue_tags":["advertising"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"This is a minor-access social media bill most comparable to MI HB4388 (regulation of social media use by minors) and NC H462 (personal data privacy and social media safety). It differs from MD SB932, which requires location display, and CO HB1255, which covers law-enforcement reporting and warrants. Its only gaming touchpoint is the express exclusion of interactive gaming services from the definition of social media platform. That makes it unlike CT HB05038, which age-gates prediction markets to 21 and restricts advertising to under-21s directly, and unlike the iGaming authorization bills listed (for example IL HB4797 and MD HB1343).","watch":"Operators should note that the 6-63-1(9)(ii)(E) gaming exclusion and the narrow e-commerce exclusion in (D) may not shelter live-streamed card breaks or mystery-pack platforms that offer open chat, and that the ban on displaying advertising in minor accounts would affect any operator or affiliate social media marketing that reaches Rhode Island minors if the bill is revived after being held for study.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":19905,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:17:53.089Z"},"SD-SB111":{"summary":"SB111 amends South Dakota's electronic transactions chapter (ch. 53-12) to require very large social media services, those with more than 100 million monthly active users whose primary focus is not charity or religion, to give South Dakota users their personal data in a portable format. It also requires those services to maintain a transparent, third-party-accessible interoperability interface, including one-time or continuous export of a user's social graph. It does not regulate gambling, and its only connection to gaming is that it excludes 'interactive gaming' services from the definition of social media service.","mechanics":["Coverage (Sections 3 to 6): applies only to a social media service or company with more than 100 million active monthly users whose primary focus is not charity or religion. Section 7 exempts entities owned or operated by a religious organization that are property-tax exempt.","Definitions (§53-12-1(19)): a 'social media service' excludes services identifiable as email, cloud storage, interactive gaming, or document sharing, and excludes services consisting primarily of e-commerce where chat or comments are incidental. A 'user' is a South Dakota resident acting in an individual or household context.","Data access (Section 3): on request, a covered service must provide the user's personal data in a format that is portable, readily usable, and transmittable to another social media service without impediment.","Social graph export (Section 5): users may export all or a subset of their data (by type, by date, or since the last transfer). Exports must be machine-readable and use a royalty-free, patent-free public standard. Users may choose a single export or continuous exports at least every 24 hours. Reasonable privacy and security terms are permitted if they are non-discriminatory and do not self-preference.","Security and safe harbors: Section 6 requires securing personal data obtained through the interface. Section 5 preserves platforms' ability to act on security, fraud, harassment, or illegal activity, and Section 8 imposes no obligation to import data.","Effective July 1, 2027 (Section 9). The text contains no express enforcement mechanism or penalty provision."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"Several listed bills also regulate social media platforms but take different approaches. CO HB1255 requires reporting adverse user actions to law enforcement, MD SB932 requires displaying users' general location, MI HB4388 targets minors' use, and NC H462 pairs data privacy with social media safety. SD SB111 is distinct as a data-portability and interoperability mandate limited to platforms above 100 million monthly active users. None of the listed gaming bills, such as the IL HB3080/HB4797 or MD HB1343 iGaming authorizations, overlap substantively, because SB111 expressly carves interactive gaming out of its scope.","watch":"Operators should confirm that the 'interactive gaming' and primarily-e-commerce exclusions in §53-12-1(19)(e)-(f) keep betting, iGaming, sweepstakes, and live card-break marketplaces out of scope, though the 100 million monthly active user threshold makes coverage unlikely in practice; they should also note that player data they receive via social-graph imports from major platforms after July 1, 2027 may trigger the Section 6 security obligations only if they themselves are a covered service.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":12417,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:18:03.979Z"},"SC-H3431":{"summary":"The text provided (House amendment 1A of January 14, 2026) adds a new Chapter 80 to Title 39, an 'Age-Appropriate Code Design' law that imposes a duty of reasonable care on large online services reasonably likely to be accessed by minors under 18. It requires default-on safety and privacy settings, data minimization, bans on targeted advertising and profiling of known minors, parental tools, and annual independent third-party audit reports. It also prohibits facilitating ads for gambling and other age-restricted products to known minors, and is enforced by the Attorney General with treble damages and possible personal liability for officers. The bill's long title still describes a different social-media account bill (Article 9, Chapter 5), so the enacted Act No. 96 should be checked against this amendment text.","mechanics":["Coverage (39-80-10(4), (17)): applies to entities doing business in SC that meet one of three tests: over $25M annual gross revenue (CPI-adjusted), data on 50,000+ consumers, or 50%+ of revenue from selling or sharing personal data. The service must also be 'reasonably likely to be accessed by minors', meaning it has actual knowledge, including inferred or marketing-attributed age, or is directed to children under COPPA.","Duty of care (39-80-20): reasonable care to prevent compulsive usage, severe psychological harm, financial injury and other listed harms, limited to harms actionable notwithstanding 47 U.S.C. 230. 'Covered design features' include gamification (streaks, badges, rewards), in-game purchases of digital items or tokens, notifications, infinite scroll and autoplay.","User tools and defaults (39-80-30, -40): known minors get default-on safeguards, including opt-outs from design features, time limits, user-set caps on purchase value, and opt-out of personalized recommendations. Other rules: no targeted advertising to minors, no profiling, data minimization, deletion of age-verification data, and a notification-blocking option for 10 p.m.–6 a.m. daily and 8 a.m.–3 p.m. on weekdays from August to May.","Advertising and dark patterns (39-80-60(B)-(C)): bars facilitating ads directed to known minors for products prohibited to minors, expressly including gambling, tobacco, alcohol and narcotics. Dark patterns are an unfair trade practice under SCUTPA (39-5-20).","Parental tools and audit (39-80-50, -70): parental controls over settings, purchases and time-of-day use are on by default. An annual public report by an independent auditor is due by July 1 to the Attorney General and must cover design features, data use, age-verification methods and algorithms.","Enforcement (39-80-80, Sections 2–4): the Attorney General enforces, with treble financial damages. Officers and employees face personal liability for wilful and wanton violations. Where laws conflict, the most protective law controls. The act is severable and effective on the Governor's approval."],"issue_tags":["advertising"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills is a comparable age-appropriate design code. AL HB351 is a general consumer data-privacy bill, AL HJR51 only creates a study commission on AI and children's internet safety, CA AB1159 addresses student personal information, and AK HB47 targets AI-generated sexual depictions. Unusual features of SC H3431 on its own are: personal officer and employee liability for wilful violations; treble damages; a mandatory third-party audit posted by the Attorney General; a statutory school-hours notification blackout; and an express bar on gambling ads to known minors embedded in a platform-design law.","watch":"Operators should watch whether ad-delivery platforms over-filter gambling and iGaming ads to avoid 'facilitating' them to users with inferred minor age under 39-80-60(B). Collectibles companies selling digital or mystery packs or running gamified breaks on covered platforms should watch whether 'in-game purchases' and 'gamification' design features pull them into the duty-of-care and purchase-limit requirements, especially given treble damages and personal officer liability.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":26133,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:18:04.088Z"},"SC-S268":{"summary":"S. 268 adds Chapter 80 to Title 39, an Age-Appropriate Code Design act imposing a duty of reasonable care on large online services reasonably likely to be accessed by minors (under 18) to prevent listed harms, including compulsive use and material financial injury. It requires default-on safety and privacy settings, parental tools, data minimization, a ban on targeted advertising to minors, and annual independent third-party audits filed with the Attorney General. Gambling appears only in 39-80-60(B), which bars covered services from facilitating ads for gambling and other age-restricted products to users they know are minors.","mechanics":["Covered online service (39-80-10(4)): an entity doing business in SC, reasonably likely to be accessed by minors, that determines the purposes and means of processing personal data, and that either has over $25M in annual gross revenue (CPI-adjusted), handles personal data of 50,000 or more consumers, households or devices, or derives 50% or more of revenue from selling data. Exempt are COPPA-compliant interactive gaming platforms, educational and career services, telecom, broadband and video programming services, and the sale of physical products.","Actual knowledge of minor status includes any age the service has inferred or attributed for marketing, advertising or product development (39-80-10(7)). Covered design features include infinite scroll, rewards for visits or engagement, push notifications, in-game purchases, and appearance filters.","Required tools default to the highest protection (39-80-30, 39-80-40(F)): minors must be able to opt out of covered design features, limit or block in-game purchases and other transactions, and control geolocation sharing. Parents must be able to restrict purchases and financial transactions, set time limits, and block use during school hours and at night (39-80-50).","Prohibitions: no targeted advertising to minors (39-80-40(B)); no notifications to known minors from 10 p.m. to 6 a.m. daily, or from 8 a.m. to 3 p.m. on weekdays August through May (39-80-40(D)); no facilitating ads for narcotics, tobacco, gambling or alcohol to known minors (39-80-60(B)); no dark patterns that undermine safeguards (39-80-60(C)).","Annual public report due July 1, prepared by an independent third-party auditor and posted by the Attorney General (39-80-70). It must cover user counts by age band, time spent, sensitive data use, age verification methods, and algorithms.","Enforcement (39-80-80): the Attorney General enforces; services are liable for treble financial damages; officers and employees face personal liability for willful and wanton violations. Harms are limited to those actionable under 47 U.S.C. 230 (39-80-20(B)). Where laws conflict, the most protective law controls. The act is effective on the Governor's approval."],"issue_tags":["advertising"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills is a comparable age-appropriate design code. AL HB351 is a general consumer data privacy bill, AL HJR51 only creates a study commission on AI and children's internet safety, CA AB1159 addresses student personal information, and AK HB47 targets generated sexual imagery. S. 268 is unusual for combining a statutory duty of care, school-hours and overnight notification curfews, mandatory third-party audits published by the Attorney General, treble damages, and personal liability for officers and employees. It also expressly names gambling in an ad-facilitation ban tied to actual or inferred knowledge of a user's age.","watch":"Watch 39-80-60(B) and the broad inferred-age definition of 'know to be a minor': platforms may respond by restricting or over-filtering sportsbook and iGaming ad buys. Collectibles clients running card-break or mystery-pack apps or streams that minors use could themselves be covered services, triggering purchase-limit tools, reward-feature opt-outs, notification curfews, audits, and treble or personal liability, so the drafting errors in the definitions (duplicate numbering, the stray 'subsection (5)' cross-reference) are worth tracking in House Judiciary.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Comm Sub","text_chars":24841,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:18:05.433Z"},"TN-SB1799":{"summary":"SB1799 adds a new part to Tennessee's consumer protection chapter (Title 47, Chapter 18, new §§ 47-18-3501 to -3503) requiring a social media platform to delete all personal information tied to an account when the account holder deletes that account. Account holders who request deletion of personal information without deleting the account fall back on the Tennessee Information Protection Act, including § 47-18-3304. Violations are enforceable through a private right of action carrying actual and punitive damages, injunctive relief, and attorney fees.","mechanics":["Deletion duty (§ 47-18-3502(a)): when an account holder deletes an account, the platform must delete all personal information about that account, or collected through it, by the account holder or another user.","Definitions (§ 47-18-3501): 'social media platform' means a site or app that allows account creation and lets account holders communicate through posts. It excludes email, broadband, cloud services, career sites, preselected-content services, online shopping, and peer-to-peer payment platforms where user interaction is limited. 'Content' excludes interactive gaming and educational entertainment.","'Personal information' excludes publicly available information, de-identified data, and aggregate consumer information.","TIPA preserved (§ 47-18-3502(b)): requests to delete data from an account that remains open are governed by TIPA, part 33, to the extent TIPA applies.","Private right of action (§ 47-18-3503): any person who believes a platform violated the deletion duty may sue for an injunction, actual damages, punitive damages, and reasonable costs and attorney fees.","Effective January 1, 2027, applying to conduct on or after that date. The bill passed the Senate and is held on the House desk as of 2026-03-12."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"This is a data-deletion privacy bill and shares no gaming subject matter with the listed gambling bills, such as IL HB3080/HB4797, MD HB1343/SB885, NJ A 3258, or MI SB0713/SB0714. Its closest peers are the social media bills: MI HB4388 regulates minors' use of social media, NC H462 combines data privacy with social media safety, MD SB932 requires platforms to display user location, and CO HB1255 requires platforms to report adverse actions to law enforcement. SB1799 is unusual among these because it focuses narrowly on post-deletion data purging and backs that duty with a private right of action that includes punitive damages, rather than relying on attorney general enforcement.","watch":"Operators should check whether community features in their betting, casino, or card-break apps (chat, social feeds, user posts) could make them a 'social media platform', since the gaming carve-out in § 47-18-3501(2) applies only to the 'content' definition and not to the platform definition, and blanket deletion of account data could conflict with gaming-regulator record-retention, KYC, and AML obligations while exposing the operator to private suits for punitive damages.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Draft","text_chars":5169,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:18:07.261Z"},"TX-HB499":{"summary":"HB 499 adds Subchapter Z to Chapter 161 of the Texas Health and Safety Code. It requires covered social media platforms to show a mental-health warning label on their landing page every time a user opens the platform, and to block use until the user confirms they understand the warning. The Health and Human Services Commission executive commissioner must adopt evidence-based rules on the label's form and content. Wagering appears only through an exemption for sites or apps used primarily for interactive gaming.","mechanics":["Sec. 161.801 borrows the definitions of 'social media platform' and 'user' from Bus. & Com. Code Sec. 120.001, and Sec. 161.8015(a) limits coverage to platforms described in Sec. 120.002(b).","Sec. 161.8015(b) exempts any website or application 'used primarily for the purpose of interactive gaming.' That term is not defined in the bill.","Sec. 161.802(a) requires the label on every open. It must include mental-health resources, including the 988 Suicide & Crisis Lifeline phone number or website link, and a statement on the platform's use of personal data and the addictive nature of social media.","Sec. 161.802(b) requires a gate: the user cannot operate the platform until they verify they understand the warning.","HHSC rules are due by January 1, 2026, compliance is not required before April 1, 2026, and the Act takes effect September 1, 2025.","The bill text contains no penalty or enforcement provision."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"This is a youth social-media health bill, closest to MI HB4388 (regulation of minors' social media use), NC H462 (data privacy and social media safety), MD SB932 (location display) and CO HB1255 (platform reporting duties), none of which use a per-session warning gate. Unlike the gambling-focused youth measures listed, such as CT HB05038 (no prediction-market use or advertising to under-21s) and CT SB00240 (internet gaming disorder and its link to gambling), HB 499 does not regulate wagering at all. Its one gaming-related feature is a carve-out for interactive gaming platforms. The mandatory click-through acknowledgment on every open, coupled with agency-written label content, is unusual.","watch":"Watch whether the undefined 'interactive gaming' exemption in Sec. 161.8015(b) is read to cover gambling-adjacent apps with social or chat features (and, for the collectibles clients, live card-break streaming communities), and whether the warning-label-plus-acknowledgment model is later borrowed for responsible-gaming or prediction-market messaging.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Engrossed","text_chars":3778,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:18:16.869Z"},"UT-HB505":{"summary":"Utah 1st Sub. H.B. 505 reclassifies Utah's vehicle registration charges into service fees, regulatory fees, and vehicle taxes, and reorganizes the related imposition and revenue-distribution provisions in Title 41, Chapter 1a, Part 12. It also creates a new Motor Vehicle Division Restricted Account and eliminates the six-month registration option. The bill has no sports-betting, iGaming, sweepstakes, or collectibles content, and it died when it was filed in the House file for bills not passed on March 6, 2026.","mechanics":["Most provisions take effect January 1, 2027, under a special effective date. Section 41-1a-1201 is repealed and reenacted, along with sections 41-1a-1206, -1211 and -1218, to restructure vehicle taxes and fees and how they are distributed.","New section 41-1a-124 creates the Motor Vehicle Division Restricted Account within the General Fund, funded from deposits described in 41-1a-1201. Appropriations from the account to the Tax Commission are nonlapsing.","The six-month registration under 41-1a-215.5(1) is repealed. The 24-month registration remains available for trailers, electric motor vehicles, off-highway vehicles and street-legal ATVs at double the 12-month tax or fee, but apportioned interstate vehicles are excluded.","Fleet registration-period adjustments under 41-1a-215(3)(c) are charged at 25%, 50%, 75% or 100% of the regular vehicle tax or fees, depending on adjustment length in three-month bands.","The bill appropriates $9,883,900 in General Fund capital project funds for FY2027. It also makes conforming changes that replace 'registration fee' with 'vehicle tax or fee' across insurance, health, education, tax and transportation code sections."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills is substantively comparable. The closest analogue is IL HB2755, an Illinois revenue omnibus that touches Road Fund deposits, but HB 505 is a narrow vehicle-fee recodification with no gaming component. It shares nothing with the gaming-focused bills on the list: NC H999 (video gaming terminals), OH HB344 (electronic instant bingo tax), NY S 10092 (gambling ad restrictions for minors) and NE LB402/LB415 (gambling winnings setoff). The provided text is truncated, but nothing in the visible portion references wagering.","watch":"Nothing in this bill affects sports-betting, iGaming or card-break clients, and because it is dead no action is needed beyond confirming that any 2027 reintroduction does not add unrelated gaming or excise provisions.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Substitute","text_chars":232989,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:18:18.309Z"},"TX-HB186":{"summary":"HB 186 would bar anyone under 18 from using a social media platform in Texas and require platforms to verify that a new account holder is 18 or older, using a commercially reasonable method based on public or private transactional data. It amends the Business & Commerce Code definition of social media platform (Sec. 120.001(1)) to add an exclusion for services used primarily for interactive gaming. Violations are enforceable by the Attorney General as deceptive trade practices.","mechanics":["New Sec. 120.112 says a child (under 18) may not use a social media platform, to the extent permitted by federal law including COPPA. Sec. 120.113 requires platforms to bar children from contracting for accounts and to verify age before accepting an account holder.","Age verification under Sec. 120.113(b) must use a commercially reasonable method relying on public or private transactional data. Under Sec. 120.113(c), the data may be used only for verification, may not be retained or transferred, and must be deleted immediately once verification is complete.","Sec. 120.114 requires a company to delete a child's account on all of its platforms within 10 days of a verified request from a parent or guardian, and to provide an accessible, verifiable way to make that request.","The amended definition in Sec. 120.001(1)(D) excludes any online service, application, or website used primarily for interactive gaming. 'Interactive gaming' is not defined. The existing exclusion for provider-preselected news, sports, or entertainment content with only incidental chat is retained.","Under Sec. 120.115, a violation requires a knowing failure to verify, knowingly allowing a child to use the platform, misuse of verification data, or failure to remove an account. Violations are DTPA deceptive trade practices enforceable by the AG consumer protection division under Secs. 17.47, 17.58, 17.60 and 17.61.","The Act takes effect September 1, 2025 and applies to platform access on or after January 1, 2026. The bill passed the House and was reported favorably 8-3 by Senate State Affairs on May 23, 2025."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"Among the listed bills, MI HB4388, which regulates minors' use of social media, and NC H462 (Personal Data Privacy/Social Media Safety) are the closest analogues. Texas's version is unusually blunt: it is an outright under-18 ban with mandatory transactional-data age verification, not a consent or design-code model. MD SB932 (displaying user location) and CO HB1255 (law-enforcement reporting and warrants) address other social media duties and impose no age ban. CT HB05038, which bars prediction-market use by and advertising to under-21s, is the nearest gaming-side parallel, but it targets wagering platforms directly, whereas TX HB186 expressly carves 'interactive gaming' services out of its scope.","watch":"Track how 'used primarily for the purpose of interactive gaming' is read, since the term is undefined and could shield gaming apps, along with whether live-stream commerce or card-break platforms with user chat fall within the social media definition. Also watch the downstream effect on operator and collectibles marketing that reaches teen audiences through mainstream platforms, which would have to purge under-18 users.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Comm Sub","text_chars":8132,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:18:21.159Z"},"VA-SB201":{"summary":"SB201 would have amended Virginia's Consumer Data Protection Act by adding § 59.1-577.2, which requires covered controllers and processors to obtain verifiable parental consent before registering anyone under 18 or before collecting, using, or disclosing that minor's personal data. This would raise the effective age for parental-consent obligations from the Act's general under-13 'child' definition to under 18. The bill died in Senate General Laws and Technology on February 4, 2026, failing to report on a 7-7-1 vote.","mechanics":["New § 59.1-577.2(A) defines 'child' as under 18 for that section only; the general § 59.1-575 definition stays at under 13, so 'sensitive data' from a 'known child' is still tied to under-13s.","Under § 59.1-577.2(B), verifiable parental consent is required before registering a child or collecting, using, or disclosing the child's personal data, and parents must be offered the option to consent to collection and use without consenting to disclosure to third parties.","Under § 59.1-577.2(C), operators must make 'reasonable efforts,' considering available technology, to verify the consenting adult is the parent or guardian, via (1) a signed consent form, (2) a credit card, debit card, or online payment transaction that notifies the primary account holder, or (3) any valid government-issued ID.","Under § 59.1-576(A), the Act applies only to entities that control or process personal data of at least 100,000 Virginia consumers a year, or at least 25,000 consumers while deriving over 50% of gross revenue from data sales; § 59.1-576(B) exempts state bodies, GLBA financial institutions, HIPAA entities, nonprofits, and higher-ed institutions.","Under § 59.1-576(D), compliance with COPPA's verifiable parental consent requirements is deemed compliance with the chapter's parental consent obligations, although COPPA itself covers only under-13s.","No new penalty or enforcement mechanism is added, so the Act's existing enforcement would apply. Status: defeated in committee on February 4, 2026."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"Unlike CT HB05038, which bars prediction-market platforms from serving or advertising to under-21s, SB201 is a general privacy measure and does not target wagering. Its only gaming reference is the existing exclusion of 'interactive gaming' services from the 'social media platform' definition. It is closest to NC H462 (personal data privacy and social media safety) and MI HB4388 (regulation of minors' social media use), but it is not limited to social media: it applies to any covered controller's 'product or service' for all users under 18. The listed iGaming and sports-wagering bills (e.g., IL HB3080, MD HB1343, NJ A 3258) do not address data privacy for minors.","watch":"Although the bill is dead for 2026, a reintroduction would push teen-facing collectibles, card-break, and mystery-pack platforms that meet the 100,000-consumer threshold toward verifiable parental consent for every 13–17-year-old user, and because § 59.1-577.2(B) has no actual-knowledge qualifier, it would effectively require age-screening all users.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":18014,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:18:25.207Z"},"VA-SB245":{"summary":"Virginia SB245 (Chapter 338, approved April 6, 2026) adds Va. Code § 22.1-79.3:2, requiring each school board to bar public K-12 schools, school board employees, and school volunteers from using a social media platform as the sole means of communicating with students to facilitate school-related extracurricular activities. Division-approved communication or messaging apps remain permitted, and superintendents may grant revocable written exceptions. The bill has nothing to do with sports betting, iGaming, or collectibles.","mechanics":["Definitions cross-reference existing law: \"extracurricular activities\" as defined in § 22.1-370 and \"social media platform\" as defined in § 59.1-575 (subsection A).","Covered actors are public elementary and secondary schools, school board employees, and school volunteers (subsection B).","The prohibition is narrow. It bars social media only as the sole means of communication with students for school-related extracurricular activities, and it expressly preserves school division-approved communication or messaging applications (subsection B).","A division superintendent or designee may grant a written exception where social media use is required to meet an objective that cannot reasonably be achieved otherwise, must give clear written instructions, and may revoke the exception at any time (subsection C).","The text specifies no penalty or enforcement mechanism and no special effective date, so Virginia's default July 1 effective date would presumably apply."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills is a close analogue. The nearest overlap is with child online-safety and student-data measures such as AL HJR51 (AI and Children's Internet Safety Study Commission), CA AB1159 (student personal information), and AK HB47 (generated child sexual abuse material and teaching certificates). Those target platforms, data processors, or criminal conduct, whereas SB245 regulates only how school personnel communicate with students. What is unusual is its modest design: it does not ban social media outright, but forbids it as the exclusive channel for extracurricular communications, with a superintendent exception process.","watch":"There is nothing for sports-betting, iGaming, or trading-card clients here beyond a general signal of Virginia's interest in minors' online exposure, which could resurface in age-verification or youth-marketing proposals affecting social-media advertising or live card-break streams.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":1639,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:18:27.242Z"},"VA-SB615":{"summary":"SB615 adds a new § 59.1-577.2 to Virginia's Consumer Data Protection Act that bars controllers and processors from setting a price offered to a consumer through the consumer's online device based on the device's hardware or hardware state, the presence or absence of software on the device, or precise geolocation data used to infer things about the consumer. It also adds supporting definitions to § 59.1-575 and carves out device repair and trade-in pricing, real-time local demand pricing, legitimate location-based cost and tax differences, and generally available coupons and discounts.","mechanics":["Prohibited inputs (§ 59.1-577.2(A)): in connection with a 'consumer transaction' (as defined in § 59.1-198), a price may not be generated 'in whole or in part' from (1) device hardware or hardware state, (2) presence or absence of any software, or (3) precise geolocation data (within 1,750 feet) when used to infer things about the consumer.","New definitions in § 59.1-575: 'hardware state' (battery life, number of wireless connections detected, performance characteristics, device age, and volatile and persistent data); 'online device' (laptop, tablet, phone, or other smart hardware); 'coupon'; 'discount'; and 'consumer transaction' by cross-reference to the Virginia Consumer Protection Act.","Exceptions (§ 59.1-577.2(B)): hardware-based pricing for device repair, maintenance, or trade-in value; geolocation pricing based on real-time demand nearby for goods or services provided immediately on request, or on legitimate location cost differences including state or local taxes, duties, and fees.","Promotions safe harbor: coupons, discounts, rebates, sales, or promotions are allowed only if they are separate from the price, offered to the general public on the same terms, and do not use any of the prohibited inputs.","Applies to controllers and processors through the existing CDPA framework; the bill text adds no separate penalty or effective-date clause.","Status: continued to the 2027 session by Senate General Laws and Technology, 15-0."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills addresses algorithmic or device-based price discrimination. The closest are general privacy and platform bills such as NC H462 (personal data privacy/social media safety), MD SB932 (displaying social media users' locations), and MI HB4388 (minors' social media use), none of which regulates pricing inputs. The gaming bills listed, such as MI SB0713/SB0714 on iGaming advertising and promotions and NJ A 3258 on micro-bets, regulate wagering products directly rather than through consumer data law. What stands out in SB615 is that it targets 'surveillance pricing' based on device fingerprinting and inferred location, not data collection or disclosure.","watch":"Operators should watch whether odds, deposit bonuses, or personalized promotions could be treated as a 'price' in a 'consumer transaction', since the promotions safe harbor covers only offers made to the general public on the same terms without device or geolocation inputs, which could reach tailored, device-segmented, or geolocation-informed offers and personalized pack or card-break pricing.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":14440,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:18:37.502Z"},"VA-SB232":{"summary":"SB232 amends Virginia's Consumer Data Protection Act to add a general \"minor\" definition (under 18) and new duties for controllers of any \"online service, product, or feature\" toward known minors. It bars such controllers from entering terms of service, user, or account-creation agreements with a known minor unless a parent gives verified consent, and requires parental controls at that point, including the ability to block the minor's purchases. It also imposes advertising and algorithm disclosure duties and a duty of reasonable care to prevent specified harms to minors, including exposure to ads for gambling and other products unlawful for minors.","mechanics":["Scope is unchanged under § 59.1-576 A: businesses that control or process personal data of at least 100,000 Virginia consumers a year, or at least 25,000 consumers if over 50% of gross revenue comes from data sales. Nonprofits, state agencies, GLBA financial institutions, HIPAA entities and higher education remain exempt.","New § 59.1-577.2 bars agreements (terms of service, user, or account creation) with known minors under 18 absent verified parental consent. Permitted consent methods include a signed form, toll-free call, videoconference, government ID check with deletion afterward, email plus additional verification, or any other commercially reasonable method.","At consent, the parent must be offered controls under § 59.1-577.2 C. These include the highest privacy setting, data minimization, no sale of the minor's data, no precise geolocation, no targeted ads, and the ability to prevent the minor from making purchases or financial transactions.","§ 59.1-577 B gives parents an expanded access right to all data held on the minor. The response must cover third-party recipients, sources, retention period, and any index or score assigned to the minor and how it is used.","Under § 59.1-577.3 A–B, controllers that let advertisers reach known minors must disclose at display time the brand, subject matter, why and how the ad was targeted, and whether the content is an ad. Controllers using recommendation algorithms on known minors must describe them in the terms of service.","§ 59.1-577.3 C imposes a duty of reasonable care to prevent listed harms to known minors, including addiction-like patterns of use and ads for products unlawful for minors (expressly including gambling), plus predatory, unfair, or deceptive marketing. Effective January 1, 2027; enforcement runs through the existing Act's Attorney General mechanism."],"issue_tags":["advertising"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"Unlike CT HB05038, which directly bars prediction-market platforms from serving or advertising to under-21s, SB232 reaches gambling only through a general duty of care to prevent gambling ads to known minors under 18 on any covered online service. It is closer to NC H462 and MI HB4388, which address minors' data and social media. However, SB232 goes beyond social media to cover any online service, product, or feature, and adds a parental-consent gate on minors' account agreements. Its existing social-media definition expressly excludes services \"for interactive gaming,\" but the new §§ 59.1-577.2 and 59.1-577.3 duties are not limited to social media.","watch":"Watch the § 59.1-577.3 C(5) duty to prevent gambling ads to known minors and the \"addiction\" and \"predatory marketing\" prongs, which could be invoked against operator advertising on third-party platforms. For card-break and mystery-pack sellers, the § 59.1-577.2 parental-consent requirement for minors' accounts and the parental purchase-blocking control apply to any business meeting the 100,000-consumer threshold.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":29873,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:18:37.956Z"},"VA-SB85":{"summary":"SB85, as substituted by the House Communications, Technology and Innovation Committee, amends Virginia's Consumer Data Protection Act to extend deletion and portability rights to \"social graph data\" held by social media platforms and \"contextual data\" held by deployers of general-purpose generative AI models. It requires both to build open-protocol, third-party-accessible interoperability interfaces so users can share that data in real time with competing platforms or AI models. The House committee tabled the bill 18-3 on March 9, 2026, so it is effectively dead for the session.","mechanics":["New definitions in § 59.1-575 cover \"social graph data\" (user content, followers and follows, engagements, public profile, metadata and relational references, but not other users' content), \"contextual data\" (prompts, conversation histories, files, preferences and metadata across sessions, excluding model trade secrets), \"deployer\", \"general-purpose generative AI model\" and \"open protocol\".","§ 59.1-577(A)(3)-(4) adds social graph and contextual data to the existing deletion and portability rights; the portability right expressly does not require a controller to reveal trade secrets.","New § 59.1-577.2(A)-(C) requires platforms and deployers to offer interoperability interfaces with continuous, real-time sharing on an open protocol and on non-discriminatory terms. Covered entities must also provide consent mechanisms and disclosures, and may set reasonable request thresholds, including a reasonable access fee.","§ 59.1-577.2(D) exempts internally derived inferences, proprietary algorithms and ranking systems, and certain proprietary-format data, and does not require platforms to import data. Subsection (E) limits use of received data to security, the user-requested service and maintaining interoperability.","The existing \"social media platform\" definition excludes services that are primarily news, sports, entertainment or ecommerce with only incidental interactive features, as well as services \"for interactive gaming\".","The effective date is July 1, 2027, with enforcement through the existing CDPA Attorney General framework."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"AL HB351 is a general consumer data privacy bill that regulates processing and consumer actions, but on its description it does not impose the social-graph and AI-context interoperability mandate found in SB85. The AI bills listed, such as CA AB1018 (automated decision systems), CA AB1609 (chatbots) and AZ SB1786 (content verification), address disclosure, accuracy or verification rather than data portability to competitors. SB85 is unusual in bringing a competition-style, real-time interoperability obligation into a state privacy statute and applying it to generative AI conversation histories.","watch":"The bill touches wagering only through the existing \"interactive gaming\" and \"sports\" carve-outs in the social media platform definition, so operators should watch whether any revived version narrows those exclusions in a way that pulls in betting apps with social or community features, or AI chat features that would count as a deployer.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Comm Sub","text_chars":23591,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:18:38.425Z"},"VA-SB671":{"summary":"SB671 would create a licensed adult-use retail cannabis market in Virginia under the Virginia Cannabis Control Authority. It adds new licensing chapters (4.1-700 through 4.1-1008), rewrites the social-equity program as a micro business program, and revises criminal penalties, including repealing §§ 18.2-248.1 and 18.2-251.1. Nothing in the provided text concerns sports betting, iGaming, sweepstakes, or collectibles.","mechanics":["§ 4.1-606 B 7 raises the edible THC caps from 5 mg to 10 mg per serving and from 50 mg to 100 mg per package.","§ 4.1-606 B 13 replaces social-equity applicants with micro business applicants. These need 12 months' Virginia residency and at least 66% ownership and control by persons meeting criteria such as a prior marijuana misdemeanor (or a family member with one), residence or schooling in a historically economically disadvantaged community, Pell Grant receipt, or veteran status. Licensing priority rises with the number of criteria met.","The license categories are restructured: 'manufacturing' becomes 'processing' and 'wholesaler' becomes 'transporter', with each type defined in new §§ 4.1-800 to 4.1-804. 'Canopy' is defined for cultivation limits.","§ 4.1-603 D 3 requires the Cannabis Public Health Advisory Council to approve or deny Board public-health regulations within 30 days; if it does not act in time, the Board may adopt them without approval.","§ 4.1-604 gives the Board inspection access to any business selling cannabinoid-labeled consumables, quarterly reporting duties, and authority to retain its own counsel. The Cannabis Equity Reinvestment Fund (§ 2.2-2499.8) would also fund the Indigent Defense Commission and the Cannabis Equity Business Loan Fund.","§ 4.1-606 B 16 directs time, place, and manner limits on outdoor cannabis advertising. The provided text is truncated, so tax provisions such as the § 58.1-301 amendment are not visible."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"The closest listed analog is OH SB56, which also revises adult-use marijuana law and levies marijuana taxes, and NC H680 (Protect Children from Cannabis Act). VA SB671 differs in building the retail market from scratch around a micro business preference system and expanded equity funds. None of the listed gaming bills, such as NC H999 (video gaming terminals), OH HB344 (electronic instant bingo) or NY S 10092 (gambling ads to minors), overlaps in substance.","watch":"There is no gaming or collectibles exposure in the visible text, but the truncated remainder should be checked for any amendments to § 59.1-200 (consumer protection) or other general provisions that could reach promotional or chance-based sales practices.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":473629,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:18:45.570Z"},"VA-SJR133":{"summary":"Virginia SJR 133 is a commemorative joint resolution noting the death of Winchester resident Evan Morgan Jewell on February 2, 2026, and directing the Clerk of the Senate to give a copy to his family. It creates no legal obligations and does not touch gaming, wagering, or collectibles regulation. The only arguably relevant reference is a biographical note that he was an avid Magic: The Gathering trading card game player.","mechanics":["Agreed to by the Senate on March 5, 2026 and by the House of Delegates on March 9, 2026; enrolled as SJ133ER.","Operative clauses only record the General Assembly's sadness at his death and direct the Clerk of the Senate to prepare a copy for his family.","Recitals mention his swimming, 4-H, Masonic lodge membership, conservation club work, Kenpo Karate black belt, and Magic: The Gathering play.","No definitions, rates, licensing, enforcement, or effective-date provisions."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"Nothing in the listed bills is comparable. Those include Illinois revenue and budget vehicles (IL HB2755, HB2949), Michigan omnibus appropriations (MI HB4706, HB5619, SB0878), NC H999 on video gaming terminals, OH HB344 on electronic instant bingo, and NY S 10092 restricting gambling ads to minors. All of them carry substantive policy or fiscal provisions. SJR 133 is a purely ceremonial memorial resolution, and its passing mention of a trading card game has no regulatory significance.","watch":"Nothing to watch: this is a ceremonial memorial resolution with no regulatory effect on sports betting, iGaming, or trading-card and card-break businesses, and it can be removed from the tracking list.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":2200,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:18:47.975Z"},"WA-HB1203":{"summary":"HB 1203 would ban retailers from selling, offering, displaying, marketing, or advertising any flavored tobacco or nicotine product (including menthol and cooling-sensation products) or any \"entertainment vapor product\" in Washington starting January 1, 2026. Enforcement runs through Liquor and Cannabis Board licensing sanctions and the Consumer Protection Act. The bill's references to \"video games\" and \"interactive gaming\" describe vape devices with built-in screens or games and have nothing to do with wagering.","mechanics":["Sec. 2 defines \"entertainment vapor product\" as any vapor product with interactive gaming or entertainment features, such as playing music, displaying photos or video, playing virtual games, or showing animations. \"Flavored tobacco or nicotine product\" covers any non-tobacco taste or smell, including menthol and mint, or any cooling or numbing sensation. FDA-authorized products and cannabis are excluded.","Sec. 3 bars retail sale, display, marketing, or advertising of covered products from January 1, 2026. A rebuttable presumption of flavoring arises from manufacturer or retailer communications using terms such as \"cool,\" \"chill,\" \"ice,\" \"fresh,\" \"arctic,\" or \"frost.\"","Sec. 6 makes a violation a per se unfair or deceptive practice under RCW 19.86.020 (the Consumer Protection Act).","Sec. 9 adds violations of Sec. 3 to the higher RCW 70.155.100(3)(b) penalty tier: $1,000, then $2,500, then $5,000 plus a 6-month suspension, then $10,000 plus a 12-month suspension, then 5-year revocation, all within a three-year period. Sec. 11 adds Sec. 3 violations to the vapor-license sanctions in RCW 70.345.180.","Secs. 8 and 10 require new prohibition signage. Sec. 4 directs a Department of Health awareness campaign. Sec. 7 authorizes tribal compact consultations. Secs. 12 and 13 fold the new product definitions into the vapor and tobacco tax chapters."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills is comparable. They address iGaming authorization (e.g., IL HB3080, MD HB1343, MA H332), gaming advertising (MI SB0713/SB0714), prediction markets (CT HB05038), and social media, while HB 1203 is a tobacco and nicotine flavor ban. Its only overlap is linguistic: the Sec. 2 definition of \"entertainment vapor product\" uses the phrase \"interactive gaming\" for vape devices with built-in games, which likely triggered the keyword match. The one structurally notable feature is its marketing-language presumption combined with per se Consumer Protection Act liability, a model sometimes borrowed for other restricted-product advertising regimes.","watch":"Nothing in HB 1203 regulates wagering, iGaming, or collectibles, so operators need only note that its \"interactive gaming\" language refers to vape devices; the advertising-terms presumption and per se Consumer Protection Act enforcement model is worth tracking only as a potential template for future restricted-product marketing bills in Washington.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":38203,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:18:53.386Z"},"WA-HB1834":{"summary":"Third Substitute HB 1834, requested by the Attorney General, creates a new chapter in Title 19 RCW that bars operators of \"addictive internet-based services or applications\" from serving algorithmically personalized \"addictive feeds\" to Washington minors and restricts push notifications to minors during overnight and school hours without verifiable parental consent. It also requires all users to be offered feed time limits, hidden like counts, a non-personalized default feed, and a private-account mode. Violations are enforceable as unfair or deceptive acts under the Consumer Protection Act, chapter 19.86 RCW. The bill does not address wagering or gaming at all.","mechanics":["Sec. 1 defines \"addictive feed\" as user-generated or user-shared media recommended, selected, or prioritized using information tied to the user or device. Carve-outs include non-persistent data, search terms, express user requests, private messages, and next-in-sequence content with no autoplay. Sites whose user interactions are limited to commercial transactions or consumer reviews, and cloud-storage feeds, are excluded.","Sec. 2 sets a two-stage age standard for providing addictive feeds. Before July 1, 2027, an operator is liable only with actual knowledge that the user is a minor (under 18 and a Washington resident). From July 1, 2027, the operator must have \"reasonably determined\" that the user is not a minor.","Sec. 3 bans push notifications to minors from 12–6 a.m., and from 8 a.m.–3 p.m. on weekdays September through May, absent verifiable parental consent. The same actual-knowledge and reasonable-determination standards apply before and after July 1, 2027. Software update, security, and account-integrity notices are exempt.","Sec. 4 requires tools available to all users, not only minors: daily time limits on the addictive feed, hiding likes and other feedback, a non-personalized default feed, and a private account mode.","Sec. 6 provides that compliance is not a defense to mental-health harm claims by minors. Sec. 7 limits use and retention of personal data collected for age determination.","Sec. 8 makes violations per se unfair or deceptive acts under RCW 19.86. Sec. 11 voids the act unless specific funding is provided in the omnibus appropriations act by June 30, 2026."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"The closest listed analogues are the minor social media bills: MI HB4388 (regulating social media use by minors) and NC H462 (personal data privacy and social media safety). WA HB1834 targets feed design and notification timing rather than account access or data privacy generally. It differs from CO HB1255 (law-enforcement reporting of adverse actions) and MD SB932 (displaying user location), which regulate platform disclosures rather than algorithmic feeds. Unlike CT HB05038, which bars prediction-market platforms from serving or advertising to under-21s, HB1834 contains no gambling-specific age gate or advertising rule.","watch":"The bill is in the House Rules \"X\" file and dies without funding under Sec. 11, but operators should check whether any app or live card-break streaming platform with personalized feeds of user-shared video, chat, or social content falls outside the Sec. 1(2)(b)(i) commercial-transaction exclusion and would trigger the post-July 1, 2027 age-determination and push-notification limits.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Comm Sub","text_chars":9711,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:18:57.394Z"},"WA-HB2439":{"summary":"Substitute HB 2439 is a tobacco and vapor product bill. It creates a voluntary responsible vendor program for tobacco and vapor retailers and tightens licensed-distributor sourcing rules. It also requires nicotine vapor manufacturers to be certified by the Liquor and Cannabis Board from January 1, 2027, mandates digital ID verification for all tobacco and vapor sales, and from 2027 bans 'entertainment vapor products' and vapes that imitate products marketed to minors. It does not regulate wagering, gaming, or collectibles.","mechanics":["Secs. 2-4: the Liquor and Cannabis Board must create a free, voluntary responsible vendor program. Participating licensees get a 50 percent reduction of the monetary penalty for a single violation in any 12-month period.","Secs. 5-10: retailers may buy tobacco, vapor products, and cigarettes only from licensed distributors or wholesalers. Product bought from unlicensed sources, including directly from a manufacturer, is contraband subject to seizure unless the retailer also holds a distributor license. Operating without a vapor license is a class C felony (Sec. 7).","Secs. 11-13: from January 1, 2027, nicotine vapor manufacturers must be certified, at $1,000 per product type or model initially and $500 per model on each annual renewal. Nonresident manufacturers not registered in Washington must post a $25,000 surety bond and appoint an in-state agent for service of process.","Secs. 21-22: retailers must verify every tobacco or vapor purchaser's ID with a digital device or application. The defense to prosecution under RCW 26.28.080 is tied to a digital verification showing the purchaser is 21 or over.","Secs. 23-24: from January 1, 2027, the bill bans selling or advertising 'entertainment vapor products', meaning devices with interactive gaming, music, video, or animation features. It also bans vapes that imitate candy, school supplies, or characters, celebrities, or mythical creatures known to appeal to minors.","Sec. 26 declares these practices matters affecting the public interest for the Consumer Protection Act, chapter 19.86 RCW (the text is truncated there). Sec. 19 orders an Ecology study of vape extended producer responsibility, with a report due May 1, 2028."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills is comparable; they concern internet gaming, sports wagering, prediction markets, social media, and budgets. The nearest thematic overlaps are age-gating and youth-appeal rules. CT HB05038 bars prediction markets from use by or advertising to under-21s, and MI SB0713/SB0714 regulate iGaming advertising and promotions. CT SB00240 likewise treats 'internet gaming' as a public-health issue. Unusually, the only 'gaming' reference in HB 2439 (Sec. 23) is to video-game features built into vape devices, not wagering.","watch":"There is no direct operator exposure, but Sec. 24's ban on imitating characters, celebrities, or mythical creatures known to appeal to minors, backed by the Consumer Protection Act, and the mandatory digital ID check in Secs. 21-22 are templates that Washington could carry over to gambling advertising or to youth-appealing collectibles and mystery-pack products.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Comm Sub","text_chars":80170,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:19:04.724Z"},"WA-HB2068":{"summary":"HB 2068 would ban the retail sale, display, marketing, and advertising of all flavored tobacco and nicotine products, including menthol, and of 'entertainment vapor products' (vapes with built-in games, music, video, or animations) starting July 1, 2027. It also raises retailer penalties and license fees, bans nicotine analogues, adds a $2 per pack cigarette surtax, and revises vapor product taxation. It is a tobacco and nicotine control bill and does not regulate gambling.","mechanics":["Sec. 4 bars retailers from selling, displaying, marketing, or advertising flavored tobacco or nicotine products or entertainment vapor products from July 1, 2027. Marketing terms like 'cool,' 'ice,' or 'arctic' create a rebuttable presumption that a product is flavored. Flavored shisha sold in 21+ premises is exempt.","Sec. 3(1) defines 'entertainment vapor product' as any vapor product with interactive gaming or entertainment features, such as playing music, showing photos or video, playing virtual games, or displaying animations.","Enforcement sits with the Liquor and Cannabis Board (Secs. 6, 12, 19). Penalties for underage-sale and flavor-ban violations rise to $2,000, $4,000, $8,000, and $12,000 across successive violations, and to $20,000 plus five-year license revocation for a fifth violation within three years. Under Sec. 7, a violation is also a per se Consumer Protection Act violation.","Sec. 21 adds a $2 per pack cigarette tax, CPI-adjusted every three years from December 2028. The first $5M goes to the youth tobacco and vapor prevention account.","Most retailer, wholesaler, distributor, and delivery-seller license fees rise to $1,000 (Secs. 14-16, 22-23). The combined tobacco and vapor license fee discount is repealed.","Sec. 20 bans selling or furnishing any product containing a nicotine analogue. The provided text cuts off partway through the vapor product tax rewrite in Sec. 25."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills is comparable. They cover internet gaming authorization (IL HB3080, MD HB1343, MA H332), sports wagering consumer protection (CT SB 296, ME LD 2080, NJ A 3258), and prediction-market age limits (CT HB05038), while HB 2068 is a tobacco control bill. Its only gaming-adjacent element is the Sec. 3 'entertainment vapor product' category, which targets devices with embedded 'virtual games.' That concerns device features, not wagering, and is unrelated to the gaming-disorder focus of CT SB00240. It is notable mainly as an example of a youth-protection ban framed around game features and advertising restrictions.","watch":"There is no direct exposure for betting, iGaming, or collectibles clients, but the bill's model of banning 'display, market, or advertise' combined with a per se Consumer Protection Act violation and steep escalating penalties is a youth-protection template worth tracking in case Washington legislators apply it to gaming or mystery-pack products.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":81736,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:19:04.743Z"},"WA-SB5183":{"summary":"SB 5183 is a tobacco-control bill, not a gaming bill. Starting January 1, 2026, it bars retailers from selling, offering, displaying, marketing or advertising any flavored tobacco or nicotine product (including menthol and cooling agents) or any \"entertainment vapor product\" in Washington. It is enforced through Liquor and Cannabis Board license penalties and makes any violation a per se Consumer Protection Act violation. The only \"gaming\" reference is to vapes with built-in video games, which the findings illustrate with pac-man and tetris.","mechanics":["Sec. 2(1) defines \"entertainment vapor product\" as any vapor product with \"interactive gaming or entertainment features,\" such as playing music, showing photos or video, playing virtual games or displaying animations on the device.","Sec. 2(2) defines \"flavored tobacco or nicotine product\" as any product imparting a non-tobacco taste or smell (including mint, menthol and wintergreen) or a cooling or numbing sensation.","Sec. 3(2) creates a rebuttable presumption that a product is flavored if manufacturer or retailer communications use terms like \"cool,\" \"ice\" or \"arctic.\"","Sec. 3 imposes a sale, display, marketing and advertising ban on retailers effective January 1, 2026. That date has now passed, since the bill was reintroduced in 2026 and the date would need amendment.","Sec. 9 sets Liquor and Cannabis Board penalties for licensed tobacco retailers: $1,000, $2,500, $5,000 plus a 6-month suspension, $10,000 plus a 12-month suspension, then 5-year revocation, all within a 3-year window.","Sec. 11 applies a parallel $200 to $3,000 penalty scale with suspensions and revocation to vapor licensees. Sec. 6 makes violations a per se unfair or deceptive practice under RCW 19.86. Sec. 7 authorizes tribal compact consultations, and Sec. 8 and Sec. 10 add point-of-sale signage requirements."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills is comparable. SB 5183 appears to have been flagged only because Sec. 2(1) uses the phrase \"interactive gaming\" to describe video-game features built into vape devices. It shares nothing substantive with the iGaming authorization bills (IL HB3080/HB4797, MD HB1343/SB885, MA H332/S235), the advertising bills (MI SB0713/SB0714), or CT SB00240 on Internet gaming disorder. Its only structural point of interest for gaming counsel is the enforcement model: a product ban backed by a per se state consumer protection act violation and a rebuttable presumption triggered by marketing vocabulary. Washington could reuse that model in other consumer-product contexts.","watch":"There is no direct exposure for sports-betting, iGaming or collectibles clients, but the \"interactive gaming or entertainment features\" definition and the marketing-language presumption are drafting templates worth noting if Washington later targets gamified consumer products.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":38228,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:19:08.824Z"},"CO-SB107":{"summary":"SB 25-107 is a FY 2024-25 supplemental appropriation to the Colorado Department of Revenue, amending Part XX of the 2024 Long Bill (HB 24-1430). It adjusts line items across the department, including the Limited Gaming Division, and extends the availability of a $1.6 million appropriation from the 2023 act (HB 23-1017) through the close of FY 2024-25. It makes no substantive change to gaming or sports-betting law.","mechanics":["Section 1 restates Part XX of HB 24-1430, with revised totals of $524,783,777 (down from $525,369,300). The only visible line-item changes are Payments to OIT, which falls from $22,016,901 to $21,431,378, and the matching cash-fund and subtotal reductions of $585,523.","Limited Gaming Division (Part XX(4)(B)) totals $44,980,060, including $9,604,145 for Personal Services (106.0 FTE) and $26,035,153 for distributions to gaming cities and counties. Most of this is (I)-noted, Limited Gaming Fund money that the constitution (Art. XVIII, Sec. 9(5)(b)(I)) and C.R.S. 44-30-701(1)(b)(I) make independent of legislative appropriation.","Footnote a to the Limited Gaming Division shows $1,755,188 from the Sports Betting Fund (C.R.S. 44-30-1509(1)(a)). It is continuously appropriated under 44-30-1509(1)(b) and listed for informational purposes only.","The Responsible Gaming Grant Program is listed at $3,200,000 from the Responsible Gaming Grant Program Cash Fund (C.R.S. 44-30-1702(8)(a)). The Division of Racing Events ($3,442,042) and the State Lottery Division ($150,024,177) are also listed under their own cash funds.","Section 2 adds subsection (2) to section 2 of HB 23-1017 (2023 Session Laws, ch. 365). It provides that $1,600,000 of the money appropriated in subsection (1)(c) and not spent before July 1, 2023 stays available until the close of the 2024-25 fiscal year \"for the same purpose.\" The text does not say what that purpose is.","Section 3 is a safety clause, so the act takes effect on signing. The Governor signed it on 2025-02-27."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. This is a routine supplemental appropriation. Its only gaming content is informational line items for the Limited Gaming Division, the Sports Betting Fund, and the Responsible Gaming Grant Program, and it neither changes nor creates any tax rate, license, or operating rule.","watch":"Nothing in this bill changes operator obligations, but the informational figures for the Sports Betting Fund ($1,755,188 for administration) and the $3.2 million Responsible Gaming Grant Program line are useful baselines for regulator funding and responsible-gaming spending.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":21047,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:41:29.286Z"},"AR-HB1861":{"summary":"HB1861 expands Arkansas's keeping-a-gambling-house offense (Ark. Code § 5-66-103(a)) to reach online operators and sweepstakes-style dual-currency games that simulate casino games, lottery games or sports wagering and pay out cash or cash equivalents. It also creates an Interactive Gaming Act (new Subchapter 2 of Title 23, Ch. 117) that lets Arkansas Racing Commission casino licensees offer online casino gaming and name, image, or likeness (NIL) drawing games, with unlicensed operation made a Class D felony. It takes effect immediately under an emergency clause. The House Judiciary Committee recommended it for interim study on 2025-04-07.","mechanics":["Section 1 adds § 5-66-103(a)(4): it is an offense to make available or take part in any internet- or mobile-accessible game or promotion using a dual-currency payment system that lets a player exchange currency for a prize, cash or cash equivalent (or a chance at one) and simulates casino games, lottery games or sports wagering. Sweepstakes games that do not award cash or a cash equivalent are carved out in (a)(4)(B), and casinos licensed by the Racing Commission are exempt in (a)(4)(C). A new (a)(5) covers any online game the Racing Commission or DFA determines by rule to be sports wagering, again exempting licensed casinos. Section 1 also extends (a)(1) to gambling places 'whether a physical location or online'.","§ 23-117-202 defines 'interactive gaming' as wagers placed through a US-situated server-based system, via mobile app or browser, by a casino licensee for account holders. The system's core components must be hosted in the US, and participation must be confined to Arkansas. Interactive gaming also includes NIL drawing games run through an electronic device to benefit an NIL collective under the Arkansas Student-Athlete Publicity Rights Act, § 4-75-1301 et seq.","§ 23-117-203: only casino licensees (Amendment 100) may operate an interactive gaming system, and only if the Racing Commission has approved it. Each licensee is limited to one branded system. Operation is prohibited where a majority of net casino gaming receipts goes to a third-party vendor. Systems must meet GLI-19 (as revised July 17, 2020) or an equivalent certification. Amendment 84 activity is excluded.","§ 23-117-204 requires the Commission to approve game titles or themes and to accept GLI or other approved-lab validation from other US jurisdictions. Live dealers and similar personnel must be licensed in the jurisdiction where they physically operate, and licensees must send the Commission a list of that personnel at least monthly. § 23-117-205 bars accepting wagers other than as set by Commission rule.","§ 23-117-206 treats an account as dormant after 16 months without patron activity. Dormant accounts may be suspended and reactivated only after ID reverification, and licensees must attempt contact at least once a year before closing. Balances still inactive two years after the account becomes dormant go to a Commission cash fund.","§ 23-117-207 prohibits operating an interactive gaming system without a casino license and makes a violation a Class D felony, consistent with § 5-66-103. § 23-117-208 gives the Commission general rulemaking authority. The bill states no tax rate and contains no mobile sports betting licensing scheme. The NIL drawing game is defined as splitting net proceeds between an NIL collective or affiliated organization and the winning participant, and the emergency clause describes the split as equal."],"issue_tags":["sweeps","legalize","licensing","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike the licensing-and-tax sports betting bills in AK HB145/SB194, DE HB365, HI HB1308/HB2570/SB1569 and the Georgia bills (e.g., GA HB380, SB57), HB1861 sets no tax rate and runs no open licensing process. It limits online casino play to existing Amendment 100 casino licensees, one branded system each. It is closest to IL HB2239 (Internet Gaming Act) and CT SB01464 in regulating online casino gaming, but its distinctive features are the felony targeting of sweepstakes-style dual-currency games (§ 5-66-103(a)(4)) and the NIL drawing game benefiting collegiate collectives. Of the listed bills, only FL H0189 and S1580 also stiffen illegal-gambling penalties.","watch":"Operators and sweepstakes or promotional-game providers should focus on the broad dual-currency definition in new § 5-66-103(a)(4), which exposes anyone who 'makes available or participates in' a cash-redeemable simulated casino, lottery or sports game to felony liability. The Commission and DFA can also expand the offense by rule under (a)(5), and only Arkansas casino licensees, limited to one branded system and barred from majority revenue-share arrangements with vendors, may offer interactive gaming.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Draft","text_chars":12434,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:41:33.857Z"},"CT-HB5271":{"summary":"CT HB 5271 amends the advertising standards in Conn. Gen. Stat. § 12-863(e) that apply to gaming entity licensees. It adds a new subdivision (8) barring licensee advertising, marketing and promotional materials from being published, aired, displayed or disseminated on the campus of any constituent unit of the state system of higher education, or on any website, online service, mobile app or social media platform established or maintained by or on behalf of such a unit. The rest of the subsection is renumbered, and the act takes effect from passage.","mechanics":["New § 12-863(e)(8)(A) bars gaming-entity-licensee advertising, marketing and other promotional materials from the campus of any constituent unit of the state system of higher education.","New § 12-863(e)(8)(B) extends the bar to any website, online service, mobile app or social media platform established or maintained by or on behalf of any constituent unit.","Coverage runs through the existing § 12-863(e) text, which reaches materials published or disseminated 'by or on behalf of' a gaming entity licensee. This reaches affiliates and agents, but the bill does not define 'gaming entity licensee' or 'campus'.","Existing subdivisions (8) through (13) are renumbered (9) through (14), with no substantive change to them.","The bill adds no new penalty or enforcement mechanism and relies on the existing § 12-863 regime. It takes effect from passage.","The text covers constituent units of the state system of higher education, which appear to mean public institutions. Private colleges are not addressed, and there is no carve-out for sponsorships, athletics venues or media rights."],"issue_tags":["advertising"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"The comparable bills are mostly broader or different in kind. NY S 10092 (the 'no gambling ads for kids act') targets advertising to minors on covered platforms and social media, and NY A 382 extends gaming-facility advertising restrictions to mobile sports wagering licensees. NJ A5562, S2356 and S4366 restrict advertisements and promotions in certain locations and add language requirements, which is the closest parallel to CT's location-based ban. MI SB0714 regulates internet gaming ads and promotions generally. HB 5271 is narrower than all of these because it adds one venue-based prohibition (public higher-education campuses and their digital properties) to an existing licensee advertising code, with no warning, hotline or notification provisions.","watch":"An operator's counsel should check how far 'on behalf of' and 'disseminated' reach, since digital ads, geofenced mobile ads, and university athletic sponsorships or media-rights deals could create compliance exposure for the licensee even where a third party placed the ad.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Comm Sub","text_chars":4837,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:41:36.844Z"},"CT-HB5269":{"summary":"CT HB 5269 amends Conn. Gen. Stat. 12-863(c) to (e), which sets responsible-gaming account controls for master wagering licensees, online gaming operators, online gaming service providers and sports wagering retailers. It also sets advertising standards for gaming entity licensees. The change that is visible in the text is a new advertising rule in subsection (e)(7) barring any financial incentive offered to induce people to engage in gaming, with the later paragraphs renumbered. The title also promises changes to permissible payment mechanisms, but the supplied text has no underlining or strike-through, so I cannot tell which wording in subsection (c)(2) is new.","mechanics":["Effective October 1, 2025. Amends only 12-863(c) to (e); the bill is one section and creates no new enforcement or penalty provisions.","New (e)(7): advertising, marketing and promotional materials published by or for a gaming entity licensee must not offer any financial incentive to induce individuals to engage in gaming. The old (7) to (13) become (8) to (14).","Subsection (c)(2), payment mechanics: limits an account to one debit card or one credit card, requires consent of all holders of a jointly held card, and requires a monetary limit on credit card use over a period. Single-use stored value instruments bought with cash or debit (gift cards, lottery terminal vouchers) are allowed under 12-853(d)(3). I cannot tell from the text what is newly changed here.","Subsection (c) also requires operators to allow deposit and daily spend limits and to run a voluntary self-exclusion process. Websites and apps must show a time-spent display, a break-in-play tool, the account balance, and responsible gambling links and a toll-free number.","Subsection (d): each master wagering licensee must undergo an independent responsible-play review at least every five years, by a department-approved third party and at the licensee's cost.","Subsection (e), other advertising rules: no depiction of persons who are or appear to be under 21 (with a professional or collegiate athlete exception), no targeting of self-excluded individuals, no misleading claims, and age statements required (18 for keno, online lottery and fantasy only). Direct or targeted messages need a one-click unsubscribe link."],"issue_tags":["advertising","licensing","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"None of the listed bills deals directly with advertising or payment-method rules. AK HB145 and SB194, GA SB57 and SB386, HI HB1308 and IL HB2239 are mainly authorization, licensing or tax bills for states building a market. CT HB5269 instead amends an existing regime. Its notable feature is the broad ban in (e)(7) on financial incentives in advertising, which is not paralleled in any listed bill.","watch":"Watch how the Department of Consumer Protection reads new 12-863(e)(7): if \"financial incentive\" is read broadly, it could reach sign-up bonuses, deposit matches, free bets and odds boosts in any marketing, and it applies to all gaming entity licensees, including the lottery.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Comm Sub","text_chars":7311,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:41:37.520Z"},"CT-HB7259":{"summary":"Connecticut Public Act 25-29 is an omnibus criminal-justice clean-up covering DNA data bank use, sexual assault evidence kits, failure to appear, immigration detainer limits, presentence credit, use-of-force definitions, and liquor-control penalties. The only gaming-related provision is Sec. 11, a new standalone offense for knowingly allowing an underage person to open or use an online gaming account or to wager on Internet games or with a sports wagering retailer, a class C misdemeanor, effective October 1, 2025.","mechanics":["Sec. 11(a): no person may knowingly allow a person who is not of legal age for online gaming and retail sports wagering to (1) open, maintain or use an account with an online gaming operator, or (2) make or attempt to make a wager on Internet games or with a sports wagering retailer.","Sec. 11(b): \"online gaming operator,\" \"Internet games\" and \"sports wagering retailer\" take their meanings from C.G.S. 12-850; the bill does not state the legal age itself.","Sec. 11(c): a violation is a class C misdemeanor, effective October 1, 2025 (like the rest of the act).","The offense turns on the \"knowingly allow\" standard and reaches any \"person,\" not only licensees. Its text does not exempt operators or retailers, though a knowledge element is required and no operator-specific duty is created.","Sec. 10 amends C.G.S. 30-113 (liquor control chapter): where no penalty is specified, a first violation is a class C misdemeanor and later violations are class B misdemeanors, replacing the cross-reference to the section 30-55 penalties. This is not gaming-related.","Remaining sections (1-9) concern DNA data bank disclosures, sexual assault kits, failure to appear (first offense reduced to class D misdemeanor), 54-192h immigration limits and a new municipal injunctive-relief action (Sec. 6), presentence credit including extradition confinement, and peace-officer force definitions."],"issue_tags":["integrity","licensing"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"This is an unrelated criminal-justice omnibus with one gaming section, so it is not truly comparable to the listed bills. Arkansas HB1861 also creates criminal liability in the iGaming space, but it makes operating an illegal online casino or sports betting a felony and builds a full Interactive Gaming Act, whereas CT HB07259 Sec. 11 only adds a class C misdemeanor for knowingly allowing a minor to use an account or wager. Unlike the licensing and tax frameworks in AK HB145, DE HB365 or GA SB57, it creates no regulatory structure and relies on existing definitions in C.G.S. 12-850.","watch":"Watch how the Sec. 11 \"knowingly allow\" standard could be read against operators, retailers, or third parties such as account holders and parents, since it applies to any \"person\" and sits outside the licensing regime and its existing age-verification rules.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":27193,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:41:42.964Z"},"CT-HB5272":{"summary":"This bill amends Conn. Gen. Stat. § 12-863(e), the advertising, marketing and promotional standards for gaming entity licensees, effective from passage. Judging by the renumbering brackets (the text does not show underlining), its one substantive addition is new subdivision (7), which bars any advertising, marketing or promotional material from offering or providing a bonus, credit or other inducement to engage in or continue gaming. The remaining changes renumber former (7)-(13) as (8)-(14).","mechanics":["New § 12-863(e)(7): advertising, marketing and other promotional materials published, aired, displayed or disseminated by or on behalf of a gaming entity licensee must not offer or provide any bonus, credit or other inducement to engage in or continue gaming.","The new subdivision has no carve-out, such as for existing customers, loyalty programs or particular products, and no cap or threshold. It covers both starting and 'continuing' to gamble.","It reaches materials 'by or on behalf of' the licensee, so affiliates, marketers and media partners acting for the licensee are within the clause. The section does not define 'gaming entity licensee' in the text provided.","Existing subdivisions appear unchanged but renumbered (former (7)-(13) become (8)-(14)). These cover under-21 appeal and audience limits, no implied better odds, no guaranteed success claims, no obscuring of material facts, and a one-click unsubscribe link on direct promotions.","Effective from passage, with no delayed date, grace period or transition for live promotions.","The bill text adds no new penalty or enforcement mechanism; it relies on whatever enforcement attaches to § 12-863 generally."],"issue_tags":["advertising"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"CT HB05272 is a short, single-clause amendment that bans promotional inducements outright in advertising. MD HB291 and SB102 take a softer approach, letting the commission cap how much free promotional play may be excluded from sports wagering proceeds, without banning promotions. NY A 382 extends existing advertising restrictions to mobile sports wagering licensees, NY A1118 and S01550 require warnings and hotline numbers, and NJ S2356 and MI SB0714 focus on language and content or internet gaming promotions. None of the listed bills combines a flat bonus and credit ban with an advertising-standards statute in this way.","watch":"Watch whether the unqualified ban on any 'bonus, credit or other inducement' in marketing is read to reach sign-up offers, free bets, deposit matches, odds boosts and loyalty rewards across channels, given the text's lack of exceptions, definitions or transition period.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Comm Sub","text_chars":4415,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:41:43.142Z"},"CT-HB7287":{"summary":"Connecticut's biennial budget act (Public Act 25-168) appropriates money from the General Fund and eleven other funds for FY2026 and FY2027 and sets transfer, lapse and allotment-reduction authority. The portion of the text supplied (Sections 1-20 of a 745-page act) is almost entirely agency line items and contains no substantive sports-betting, iGaming, sweepstakes or collectibles provisions. The revenue and implementer sections that would carry any gaming-law changes are not in the text provided.","mechanics":["Sec. 1 appropriates a General Fund total of $24,130,611,244 for FY2026 and $25,455,622,254 for FY2027, or net of lapses and targeted savings, $24,036,381,982 and $25,361,911,684.","Sec. 3 appropriates $52,541,796 in each year from the Mashantucket Pequot and Mohegan Fund for OPM Grants to Towns. This is a fund tied to tribal casino revenue, but the appropriation is a grant line and the text supplied does not change any gaming compact or rate.","Sec. 1 funds the Department of Consumer Protection at $16,816,119 (FY2026) and $17,565,215 (FY2027), and the Department of Revenue Services at $59,219,374 and $59,318,342. These are plain agency lines with no gaming-specific earmark visible.","Sec. 13 lets the OPM Secretary reduce executive-branch allotments to achieve General Fund savings of $63,710,570 (FY2026) and $73,710,570 (FY2027), plus targeted savings of $25,518,692 and $15,000,000. Judicial-branch savings are $5,000,000 per year.","Secs. 14-19 give transfer and adjustment authority: collective-bargaining transfers to the Reserve for Salary Adjustments, agency-to-agency transfers to capture federal matching funds with Finance Advisory Committee approval, and UConn Health Center transfers to the Medicaid account. Most sections are effective July 1, 2025; Sec. 15 is effective from passage.","The supplied text cuts off mid-Sec. 20, so any tax rates, licensing changes or enforcement provisions in the remaining pages are not visible."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. On its own, this is a general budget act, and in the portion supplied the only link to gaming is the Mashantucket Pequot and Mohegan Fund grant appropriation in Sec. 3, which is a funding line rather than regulation.","watch":"Review the unsupplied implementer and revenue sections of the 745-page act for any changes to sports-wagering or iGaming taxes, Department of Consumer Protection or Lottery authority, or tribal-compact revenue, since none appear in the text provided.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":168935,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:41:48.225Z"},"CT-SB1237":{"summary":"Connecticut SB 1237 (Substitute) is mainly a Connecticut Lottery Corporation (CLC) housekeeping bill. It restructures delinquency assessments on lottery sales agents, exempts ticket distribution vendors from vendor licensing, creates a CLC-employee licensing endorsement for Internet games and retail sports wagering, and relaxes one advertising restriction on online keno and lottery draw games. It also requires CLC to set a redemption period of at least 365 days for winning tickets.","mechanics":["Sec. 1 (amending 12-569): defines \"delinquency assessment\" and has the commissioner impose a penalty of 10% of the unremitted principal or $10, whichever is greater, effective on notice to the agent, plus simple interest of 1.5% per month. New hardship waiver for agents delinquent on or after July 1, 2025: apply to the CLC president, pay principal and penalty up front or on a payment plan of up to one year, and get interest reduced. No refunds or credits. Effective July 1, 2025.","Sec. 2 (12-815a(b)): a business providing ticket distribution services for CLC is not required to hold a vendor license. The \"primary contract\" thresholds ($75,000 or 25% of gross annual sales) are unchanged, and the $250 vendor application fee is retained. Effective from passage.","Sec. 3 (12-815a(e)-(n)), effective Jan. 1, 2026: Class III and IV CLC employees must disclose which gaming forms they will work on. The commissioner may add an endorsement to operate Internet games or retail sports wagering under chapter 229b, in lieu of a license under 12-858 or 12-859. Licensees must report criminal convictions within two business days and report changes in job scope immediately. Application fees stay at $20 (Class I/III) and $100 (Class II/IV).","Secs. 4-5 (12-858(a), 12-859(b)), effective Jan. 1, 2026: a CLC employee with an active Class III or IV license plus the Internet games or retail sports wagering endorsement does not need an occupational employee license or a key employee license. Sec. 6 conforms 12-859c, so third-party background checks can be accepted for lottery sales agents under the renumbered subsection (h).","Sec. 7 (12-863(e)), effective from passage: the advertising rules for gaming entity licensees are preserved, but the carve-out in (e)(10) for implying greater chances of winning by wagering more is rewritten. It now covers online keno and online lottery draw games with department-approved features that increase winning chances, and drops the prior limits (approved before Jan. 1, 2024, available as of June 6, 2024, and not exclusively sold by sales agents).","Sec. 8 (new), effective Jan. 1, 2026: by July 1, 2026, CLC must adopt procedures giving at least 365 days to redeem a winning lottery ticket."],"issue_tags":["licensing","advertising","integrity"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"The comparison set is mostly legalization and tax bills, such as AK HB145 and SB194, GA SB57 and HB380, and HI HB1308, so little is directly comparable. The closest is IL HB2754, which also revises occupational licensing definitions under the Illinois Gambling Act. Unlike the legalization bills, CT SB 1237 does not create or tax a market. It adjusts the existing CLC-run framework, which already has a master wagering licensee and online casino operators. The CLC-specific licensing carve-outs and the advertising tweak limited to lottery products are unusual.","watch":"Operators should watch the Sec. 7 revision of the 12-863(e)(10) \"greater chances of winning\" exception, since it widens what the CLC's online keno and draw games may advertise while private licensees remain bound by the rest of the advertising rules, along with the Sec. 3-5 licensing relief given to CLC employees but not to private licensee employees.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Comm Sub","text_chars":28941,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:41:53.190Z"},"CT-SB1235":{"summary":"Public Act 25-112 makes Department of Consumer Protection-requested changes to Connecticut's lottery and gaming statutes. The visible sections cover lottery sales agent delinquency penalties, lottery licensing and enforcement, amended definitions in the online casino, sports wagering and fantasy contest chapter (12-850), and new rules on voiding and modifying sports wagers. The text provided cuts off mid-sentence in Sec. 6(c)(1)(F), about 25 of 40 pages in, so later sections are not reflected here.","mechanics":["Sec. 1 (eff. 7/1/2025): for delinquent lottery sales agents, the commissioner imposes a penalty of 10% of the unremitted principal or $10, whichever is greater, effective on delivery of notice, plus simple interest of 1.5% per month. Agents delinquent on or after 7/1/2025 can apply to the Connecticut Lottery Corporation president for a hardship waiver of interest, with payment plans of up to one year.","Sec. 3 (eff. 10/1/2025): lottery advertising must state average chances of winning in type no smaller than 10% of the largest font in the ad. The ad rules cover the Lottery Corporation's games under 12-850 to 12-872, and online draw-game and keno audio ads under 30 seconds are exempt. The Corporation may not publish the name or address of anyone paid a winning online or retail sports wager or a fantasy contest prize, or the photograph of winners, without written consent. The prior 'web site' limit is removed, and FOIA disclosure is preserved.","Sec. 4 (eff. 10/1/2025): vendor licenses are required for primary contractors, defined as those receiving over $75,000 or 25% of gross annual sales from the Corporation, with a $250 application fee. Class III and IV Corporation employees must disclose which gaming forms they will work on, and the commissioner may issue endorsements for Internet games or retail sports wagering. Vendors must file an incident report within 24 hours of a lottery gaming system incident and a root-cause report within 5 business days. Provisional authorizations run up to six months.","Sec. 4 enforcement: after a chapter 54 hearing, the commissioner may suspend, revoke, refuse to renew, condition or deny a license, or impose a civil penalty of up to $2,500 per violation. Lottery sales agents found with unauthorized gambling devices, illegal bookmaking equipment or professional gambling face a fine of up to $4,000 per violation and summary suspension, with a 15-day window to request a hearing.","Sec. 5 (eff. from passage) amends 12-850 definitions. 'Wager' now expressly includes free play, loyalty points and other redeemable betting credits, and excludes entry fees. 'Online casino gaming' covers slots, table games, poker, bingo, live dealer and peer-to-peer games. New definitions cover 'consumables,' 'handling consumables' and 'live game employee.' 'Sporting event' is restructured to carve out Connecticut intercollegiate team events other than permitted intercollegiate tournaments of four or more teams. Retail sports wagering is capped at up to fifteen facility locations.","Sec. 6 (eff. 7/1/2025): an online gaming operator may void accepted sports wagers without department approval if the event is cancelled, delayed more than 24 hours or moved to another venue, if the player does not participate, if a specified act does not occur, if a post-season field is reduced, or if the event format changes. Operators must void or modify a wager on patron request before the event where the operator's platform or a retailer employee made an error. Operators must keep a change log with the patron name, reason, market, event and dates, and must credit voided stakes promptly. Retailers must post an 8.5x11 inch, 20-point font notice about voids and refunds. Other voids require a written department request."],"issue_tags":["licensing","integrity","advertising"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"This is a regulatory clean-up of an existing, already-legal market under chapter 229b and the Lottery Corporation, not a legalization or tax bill like AK HB145/SB194, DE HB365 or the Georgia sports betting bills (e.g., GA SB57, HB 686). Its closest analogs are administrative: CO SB 163 on delegating licensing duties, and AR HB1861 and IL HB2239 on frameworks for online casino operators. Unlike DE HB187, which diverts winnings to child support, CT's Sec. 3(d) goes the other way and restricts publication of winner identities. The voiding rules and the 'wager' definition covering free play and loyalty credits are detailed operator-facing provisions that most of the listed bills do not appear to address.","watch":"Operators contracting with Connecticut's master wagering licensees should focus on the Sec. 6 void and modify triggers and change-log duties and on the expanded 'wager' definition covering free play and loyalty credits, and should confirm what the truncated remainder of the Act adds, since the text provided ends mid-section.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":79640,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:41:57.158Z"},"CT-SB1295":{"summary":"Public Act 25-113 is an omnibus consumer-technology act. Its gaming content in the text provided is Sec. 2, which amends the Connecticut Lottery Corporation licensing statute (CGS 12-815a), and Sec. 3, which sets new rules for voiding and modifying sports wagers. The rest of the provided text covers a broadband affordability program, social media safety centers, and amendments to the Connecticut data privacy law. The text supplied stops partway through Sec. 5 (page 25 of 80), so any later gaming provisions are not reviewed.","mechanics":["Sec. 3(b)(1), effective July 1, 2025: an online gaming operator may void accepted sports wagers without Department approval if the event is cancelled, delayed more than 24 hours or moved to another venue; a player does not take part; a specified act does not occur; the number of post-season qualifying teams is reduced; or the event format or participants change. Voided funds must be credited promptly to patron accounts.","Sec. 3(b)(2): the operator must modify or void a wager, without Department approval, if the patron asks before the event and the operator or its platform misstated the wager's type, amount or parameters, or a retailer employee made an entry error. Sec. 3(b)(3): the operator must keep a change log of every void or modification, with patron name (except for retail wagers), reason, market, event and dates.","Sec. 3(c): for any void not allowed under subsection (b), the operator must submit a written request to the Department with the reason, affected patrons, event, wager type, total amount and a patron-contact plan. It may not void until it receives written approval. Sec. 3(b)(1)(C): each sports wagering retailer must post a notice of at least 8.5 x 11 inches, in 20-point font or larger, at every wager-placement location, explaining how to check for a void and get a refund.","Sec. 3(d): by Sept. 1, 2025, each online gaming operator must file internal controls on voiding wagers and allocating patron funds. The Department must approve or disapprove by Dec. 1, 2025. The controls must provide patron notice within 24 hours of an approved void and prompt return of funds. Approved controls must be built into the house rules and displayed clearly on the wagering platform.","Sec. 2, effective Oct. 1, 2025, amends the lottery licensing statute. It adds a Class III/IV occupational license endorsement for Lottery Corporation employees who work on Internet games or retail sports wagering under chapter 229b; these employees need no separate license under 12-858 or 12-859. It also adds a 24-hour incident report, followed by a root-cause report within 5 business days, for vendors of the lottery gaming system, and the Department may require recertification by a gaming laboratory.","Sec. 2 also expands discipline. After a hearing, the Commissioner may suspend, revoke, refuse to renew, condition or deny a license, or impose civil penalties of up to $2,500 per violation, for enumerated causes such as fraud, false information, insolvency or a failure to comply. A lottery sales agent found with unauthorized gambling devices, illegitimate tickets, illegal bookmaking equipment or professional gambling faces a fine of up to $4,000 per violation and summary suspension. The provision allowing rejection of an application 'for good cause' is deleted and replaced by the broader discipline provisions."],"issue_tags":["licensing","integrity"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"None of the listed bills appears to deal with voiding or modifying accepted wagers, so Sec. 3 has no direct comparator. Most listed bills create or expand markets or taxes, such as DE HB365, AK HB145 and SB194, and IL HB2239, or they are Georgia constitutional and enabling measures. The closest in kind are the Illinois Sports Wagering Act licensing amendments (IL HB2761 and HB2878), though those address master license structure and fees rather than operational rules. This Connecticut act is unusual because it places mandatory void grounds, patron-requested error corrections and operator internal-controls filings in an omnibus consumer-technology act.","watch":"Operators should watch the Sept. 1, 2025 internal-controls filing deadline and the requirement that approved void procedures be built into house rules, as well as the change-log and Department-approval rules for voids outside the listed grounds, and should confirm the unreviewed pages (after Sec. 5) contain no further gaming or consumer-data provisions that apply to them.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":162740,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:41:59.048Z"},"CT-SB1463":{"summary":"Connecticut SB 1463 (Substitute) restructures Connecticut Lottery Corporation (CLC) finances and disclosures. It creates a separate Online Lottery Ticket Sales Fund with transfers to the Early Childhood Care and Education Fund, and it redirects 100% of gross gaming revenue from CLC-licensed online sports wagering under 12-853 to that same fund. It also raises the minimum lottery agent commission, shields winner identities, and changes the odds-disclosure and self-exclusion records provisions.","mechanics":["Sec. 5 rewrites 12-867(a): master wagering licensees under 12-852 (online or retail) and retail licensees under 12-853 still pay 13.75% of gross gaming revenue to the General Fund. For months starting on or after July 1, 2025, a licensee operating online sports wagering under 12-853 (the CLC skin) must pay the gross gaming revenue from it to the Early Childhood Care and Education Fund, with the first payment due July 15, 2025 and later payments by the 15th of each month.","Sec. 5(a)(2) directs the commissioner to deposit 2% of the amounts the state receives under 12-867 into the youth sports grant account (4-68aaa) for months starting on or after July 1, 2025. Sec. 6 conforms the OPM biennial reporting cross-reference, with the first report due by January 1, 2029.","Sec. 5(b) leaves the gross gaming revenue definition intact, including the promotional credit cap of 25% of GGR in year one, 20% in year two and 15% thereafter, with excess credits added back to GGR.","Secs. 1, 2 and 4 create the Online Lottery Ticket Sales Fund, effective July 1, 2025. Online draw-game sales revenue, prizes and expenses are separated from the lottery and gaming fund. Weekly certified excess amounts are transferred to the Early Childhood Care and Education Fund starting in FY2026.","Sec. 3 raises the floor on average lottery sales agent commission from 4% to 5.5% of an agent's lottery sales. It also adds CLC purposes to pay the Teachers' Retirement Fund reserve and to transfer to the early childhood fund, and it retains the 15-facility retail cap and the single online skin.","Secs. 7 to 9 (effective Oct. 1, 2025) exempt from FOIA the name and address of anyone who redeems a winning lottery ticket, claims a winning online or retail sports wager, or is paid a fantasy contest prize. They bar CLC from publishing names, addresses or photographs without written consent and repeal the opt-out removal procedure. They also limit disclosure of self-exclusion data to licensees and the department, and they require odds-of-winning statements in lottery advertising, with a minimum font of 10% of the largest font."],"issue_tags":["tax","advertising","licensing","integrity"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"This bill is about how a state lottery corporation accounts for and transfers its gaming revenue, which is different from the full legalization or licensing frameworks in AK HB145 and SB194, FL H0189, HI HB1308 or IL HB2239. It is closer to the lottery-run models in DE HB365, GA SB57 and HB380, and IL HB3276, but it does not set up a market or license operators. Unusually, it sends 100% of gross gaming revenue from the CLC's online sports wagering skin to an early childhood fund, while the 13.75% rate stays for other master wagering licensees. No listed bill pairs a lottery-fund restructuring with winner-anonymity rules for sports wagering and fantasy contest winners.","watch":"Operators partnered with other Connecticut licensees should confirm that the 13.75% rate and promotional-credit caps under 12-867 are unchanged for them, and should note that the CLC skin's full revenue goes to the early childhood fund, along with the new confidentiality and self-exclusion data-sharing rules.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Comm Sub","text_chars":35957,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:41:59.666Z"},"CT-SB1464":{"summary":"CT SB 1464 (Comm Sub) amends Connecticut's existing 2021 online gaming framework (Conn. Gen. Stat. 12-850 et seq.) in four sections. It adds definitions (including \"wager\" and \"peer-to-peer casino game\"), lets the Governor enter multijurisdictional agreements for online peer-to-peer casino games such as poker, adds new player-protection duties for licensees including mandatory refunds on odds errors, and directs the Commissioner of Consumer Protection to set maximum online sports wagers by regulation. It does not change tax rates, license counts or the master-licensee structure.","mechanics":["Sec. 1 (amending 12-850): adds a \"wager\" definition (new subdiv. 37) covering money, credit, deposits, free play, loyalty points and other redeemable betting credit, and excluding entry fees; adds \"peer-to-peer casino game\" (patrons compete against each other and the licensee takes a fee without wagering against patrons); and expands \"online casino gaming\" to include poker, video poker, bingo, live dealer and other peer-to-peer games. Effective July 1, 2025.","Sec. 2 (new section): the Governor may enter agreements with other states, territories or federally recognized tribes running Indian gaming to authorize online gaming operators to conduct multijurisdictional Internet gaming for peer-to-peer casino games, provided it is consistent with state and federal law and conducted exclusively within the United States.","Sec. 3 (amending 12-863(c)): adds a new subdiv. (8) requiring a licensee that finds an error in the odds on a sporting event to stop accepting wagers on that event until it is corrected and fully refund every patron who bet before the error was discovered.","Sec. 3 also adds a requirement that licensees establish a toll-free number for account and platform problem resolution, and requires each applicable website or app to display that number and the maximum online sports wagers set by regulation.","Sec. 4 (amending 12-865): adds to the mandatory regulatory topics the establishment of maximum sports wagers for online sports wagering (new subdiv. 13). Existing authority for emergency regulations with the Governor's approval is retained.","Structure unchanged: master wagering licensees remain the two tribes and the Connecticut Lottery Corporation, and operators and service providers contract with them. The text sets no bet-limit amounts and no penalty for the refund rule beyond existing enforcement."],"issue_tags":["integrity","licensing","advertising"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike the bills that would create or open new markets (AK HB145 and SB194, DE HB365, GA SB57, HI HB 1308, IL HB2239), CT SB 1464 is a technical amendment to an existing tribal and lottery-based structure. Its multijurisdictional peer-to-peer compact authority (Sec. 2) has no counterpart in the listed bills, and it does not touch taxes, unlike IL HB2878's license fees or AK's revenue tax. The mandatory odds-error refund and regulator-set maximum online wagers are also not seen in the listed summaries, though IL HB2239 and AR HB1861 deal with internet gaming regulation more broadly.","watch":"Watch the regulator-set maximum online sports wager and its display requirement, and the odds-error refund rule in Sec. 3(c)(8), which as drafted has no carve-out for obvious errors, a materiality threshold or settled bets, and could create open-ended liability for operators.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Comm Sub","text_chars":22805,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:42:02.424Z"},"DC-B25-0202":{"summary":"The Fiscal Year 2024 Budget Support Act of 2023 is a broad omnibus budget bill that amends District law across government operations, housing, economic development, public safety, education, health, transportation and finance. The text supplied is truncated partway through Title II, Subtitle N, and none of the provisions I could read mention sports betting, iGaming, lotteries, sweepstakes, collectibles or trading cards. I could not review Title VII (Finance and Revenue), Title VIII (Technical Amendments) or Title IX, which appear in the table of contents but not in the text.","mechanics":["Tourism Recovery Tax (Subtitle J, Sec. 2092): amends D.C. Code §§ 47-2002.03a and 47-2202.03 to set the tax rate at 1.3% from April 1, 2023 through March 31, 2027, with Destination DC required to consult stakeholders and enter an MOU with the Mayor on spending the revenue.","Downtown housing abatement (Subtitle G): lowers an affordability threshold from 15% to 10% and adds an alternative of 18% of units at 80% MFI or below for 20 years. It exempts abated projects from First Source Agreements for construction and from TOPA on the first sale within 10 years, and sets abatement caps of up to $41 million for FY2028 and 104% of the prior year's cap after that.","Retail Recovery Grant Program (Subtitle F, new § 1-328.04(hh)): FY2024 grants for businesses opening or expanding in space vacant at least 6 months in the Downtown or Golden Triangle BIDs, with a 20% non-competitive set-aside for resident-owned small businesses that are woman-owned or disadvantaged.","Housing Authority accountability (Subtitle N): monthly reporting on reserves, vacancies and spending, with a waiver for 12 months if HUD designates the Authority a Standard or High Performer, plus mandatory commissioner training with automatic suspension for non-compliance.","Office of the Attorney General funds (Subtitle A): raises dollar caps in the Attorney General's fund provisions (e.g., $19 million to $23.5 million in § 106b(d)(3)(A)) and revises treatment of excess restitution funds.","The law was signed as L25-0050 and took effect September 6, 2023."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. This is an omnibus budget support act with no gaming-specific content in the portion supplied, so it is not comparable to a dedicated sports-betting or iGaming measure. Its only tax change in the readable text is the hospitality-sector Tourism Recovery Tax rate of 1.3% through March 31, 2027.","watch":"Because the text is cut off before Title VII (dedicated revenue adjustments, designated fund transfers) and Title VIII, check the full enrolled act for any lottery, gaming or Office of Lottery and Gaming fund transfers or technical amendments, since none appear in the portion reviewed.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":231947,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:42:10.710Z"},"DC-B25-0784":{"summary":"This is the District's omnibus Fiscal Year 2025 budget support act, signed as Law L25-0217 and effective Sep 18, 2024. The text supplied ends in Title II, Subtitle B (Vitality Fund), before any gaming provision. The table of contents lists Title VII, Subtitle R (Sports Wagering, pp. 137-143) and Title V, Subtitle K (Problem Gambling Program Establishment Act, p. 92), but their operative language is not in the excerpt, so their substance cannot be summarized.","mechanics":["Status: signed into law as L25-0217, effective Sep 18, 2024, and published in DC Register Vol. 71, p. 011542.","The table of contents lists Title VII, Subtitle R, Sports Wagering, at pp. 137-143, about six pages. The text supplied does not reach it, so no rates, definitions, licensing terms or effective dates can be stated.","The table of contents lists Title V, Subtitle K, Problem Gambling Program Establishment Act, at p. 92. Its funding source and operator obligations, if any, are not visible in the excerpt.","The visible text covers only non-gaming provisions in Title I and the start of Title II, such as the OIG, grant termination procedures, DGS work order dashboards and the Vitality Fund. None of these touches wagering.","Title IX (Applicability; Fiscal Impact; Effective Date) and Subtitle CC (Subject to Appropriation Provisions) are listed but not included in the excerpt, so any gaming-specific applicability dates cannot be confirmed."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. On its own, this is a general budget vehicle in which gaming appears only as discrete subtitles, a sports wagering subtitle and a problem gambling subtitle per the table of contents. The excerpt does not allow any comparison of their content.","watch":"Pull the full enrolled text of Subtitle R (pp. 137-143) and Subtitle K (p. 92) and check them for any change to the District's sports wagering framework or any new fee, assessment or compliance obligation on operators, since the excerpt does not show either.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":242040,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:42:11.814Z"},"DC-B25-0320":{"summary":"This is the District's omnibus Fiscal Year 2024 budget support act, enacted on an emergency basis as Act A25-0172. It amends a wide range of District laws on government operations, housing, economic development grants, education, health and transportation. In the portion of the text provided, nothing addresses sports betting, iGaming, lotteries, sweepstakes or collectibles.","mechanics":["Sec. 2092 amends D.C. Code §§ 47-2002.03a and 47-2202.03 so that the Tourism Recovery Tax applies at a 1.3% rate from April 1, 2023 through March 31, 2027. Destination DC must consult stakeholders and enter into an MOU with the Mayor on how the revenue is spent.","Sec. 2062 amends the downtown housing tax abatement law (D.C. Code § 47-860.02 and § 47-860.03(b)). It cuts one affordability set-aside from 15% to 10% or adds an alternative of 18% of units at 80% AMI for 20 years. It also adds a $41 million abatement cap for FY2028 and a 104% annual escalator after that. Sec. 47-860.02a adds exemptions from First Source and, for a first sale, from TOPA.","Sec. 2052 adds a Retail Recovery Grant Program (new subsection (hh)) for businesses in the Downtown and Golden Triangle BIDs. Eligible businesses are those opening or expanding in space vacant for at least 6 months. At least 20% of funds may be distributed non-competitively. It also adds a $3 million grant to Ford's Theatre and a $2 million grant to the Douglass Community Land Trust.","Sec. 1002 raises Office of the Attorney General fund caps, for example from $19 million to $23.5 million in § 106b(d)(3)(A), and revises how excess restitution funds are treated.","Sec. 2132 requires monthly DC Housing Authority reporting on reserves, vacancies and expenses, and mandatory board training with automatic suspension for non-compliance.","The act is an emergency act and expires October 12, 2023. The table of contents lists Title VII (Finance and Revenue) subtitles on dedicated revenue, designated fund transfers and subject-to-appropriation provisions, but the text provided cuts off before them."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. This is an omnibus municipal budget support act, and the visible text contains no wagering, gaming, lottery or collectibles provisions. The nearest revenue measure is the temporary 1.3% Tourism Recovery Tax rate in Sec. 2092, which is not a gaming tax.","watch":"Check the truncated Title VII provisions (dedicated revenue adjustments, designated fund transfers, subject-to-appropriation items) for any lottery, gaming or sports-wagering fund transfers or revenue changes, since none appear in the text provided.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":233171,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:42:12.348Z"},"DC-B25-0875":{"summary":"B25-0875 is the District of Columbia's omnibus FY2025 budget support act, passed as emergency legislation and enacted as Act A25-0506. It has nine titles covering government operations, economic development, public safety, education, human services, infrastructure, and finance and revenue. The table of contents lists two gaming-related subtitles: Title V, Subtitle K (\"Problem Gambling Program Establishment Act\", p. 94) and Title VII, Subtitle R (\"Sports Wagering\", pp. 139-145). The text supplied cuts off in Title II, Subtitle B, so I cannot describe what either gaming subtitle actually provides.","mechanics":["The text supplied ends partway through Title II, Subtitle B (Vitality Fund). It does not include Title V, Subtitle K (problem gambling) or Title VII, Subtitle R (sports wagering), so no gaming provisions can be summarized.","The table of contents places Subtitle R (Sports Wagering) at pp. 139-145, about six pages inside Title VII (Finance and Revenue). Title VII also has a Sales and Use Tax subtitle (Subtitle F) and a Subject to Appropriation Provisions subtitle (Subtitle CC), but their content is not in the supplied text.","The table of contents places the Problem Gambling Program Establishment Act at p. 94 within Title V (Human Support Services). It is a short subtitle of roughly one page, and its funding source and administering agency cannot be confirmed from the supplied text.","The Act is an emergency measure. The status line shows it was published in DC Register Vol. 71, p. 008406, and expires on Oct 13, 2024, so any gaming provisions in it are temporary unless re-enacted by permanent legislation.","In the visible text, Sec. 1082 amends the Attorney General litigation support fund. It allows up to $9.7 million per year for public safety initiatives, reverts amounts above $23.5 million to the General Fund, and allocates JUUL settlement proceeds 50/50 between the fund and a Tobacco Use Cessation Fund. None of this concerns gaming."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. On its own, this is an emergency-enacted, roughly 165-page omnibus budget act in which sports wagering and problem gambling are two small subtitles among dozens. The table of contents suggests these provisions were folded into the budget vehicle rather than moved as a standalone gaming bill. The supplied text does not reach them, so no substantive comparison can be made.","watch":"Get the full enrolled text of Title VII, Subtitle R (Sports Wagering, pp. 139-145) and Title V, Subtitle K (problem gambling) to check for changes to operator licensing, fees or taxes, and problem-gambling funding obligations. Also confirm whether permanent legislation replaces them before the Oct 13, 2024 emergency expiration.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":238641,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:42:16.641Z"},"DC-B25-1004":{"summary":"B25-1004 approves the District's development and financing agreement, amended and restated lease, and purchase and sale agreement with DC Arena L.P. for the downtown arena (Verizon Center), and authorizes related easements and airspace leases. It also continues property tax, deed transfer and recordation tax exemptions for the arena and loosens the rulemaking process for additional arena signage. The text contains no sports wagering, gaming, or sportsbook provisions.","mechanics":["Sec. 2(a): Council approves three agreements submitted by the Mayor on Oct. 18, 2024 (Development and Financing Agreement, Amended and Restated Lease, Purchase and Sale Agreement, improvements only) with DC Arena L.P., notwithstanding the Procurement Practices Reform Act, the Public-Private Partnership Act and the Verizon Center Sales Tax Revenue Bond Approval Act.","Sec. 2(c): First Source Act (sections 2, 4(a)-(c), (e), 4a) and apprenticeship law (section 5) apply to the financing agreement regardless of the dollar value of government assistance.","Sec. 3 and 4: The Mayor may grant easements over Lot 47 (Square 455) and Lot 884 (Square 454), and may lease adjacent airspace to DC Arena L.P. or its designee at no other rent or fee for as long as the Amended and Restated Lease remains in effect, with listed Public Space Utilization Act provisions waived.","Sec. 5(a)-(d): Adds new exemptions from real property and possessory interest tax (D.C. Code 47-1002(34)-(35), 47-1005.01(c-2)) while the ground lease or new lease is in effect, and new exemptions from deed transfer tax (47-902(29)) and deed recordation tax (42-1102(37)) for transfers between the District and DC Arena L.P. or its designee.","Sec. 5(e): Repeals section 3 of the Arena Tax Amendment Act of 1994, applicable from the effective date of the emergency act (Dec. 17, 2024).","Sec. 6: Amends 12-A DCMR N101.19.16 so the signage rules may authorize additional outdoor signs, digital displays and static canvas displays on the Verizon Center, with a 45-day Council review and deemed approval if the Council does not act. Sec. 8: takes effect after Mayor approval and a 30-day congressional review period."],"issue_tags":["tax","advertising"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. This is a site-specific real estate, financing and tax-abatement approval act for a single arena. It does not touch wagering, licensing or gaming taxes, so it is not comparable to sports betting legislation.","watch":"Nothing in the text regulates wagering, but the Sec. 6 signage rulemaking, which allows more digital displays and visuals on the arena with deemed approval after 45 days, could matter to operators with sponsorship or advertising ties to the venue, so check any later rules.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":14229,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:42:19.528Z"},"DC-B25-0937":{"summary":"This is an emergency omnibus budget support act for DC Fiscal Year 2025, enacted because of the congressional review period. Most of it has nothing to do with gaming. The bill text I was given cuts off in Title II, Subtitle B, so I could not read the gaming subtitles. The table of contents lists Title VII, Subtitle R (Sports Wagering, pp. 137-143) and Title V, Subtitle K (Problem Gambling Program Establishment Act, p. 92). I can say nothing about what those subtitles actually do.","mechanics":["Per the status line, this is Act A25-0554, an emergency act published at 71 DCR 012143 that expires January 6, 2025. It was passed on a congressional-review emergency basis, so any gaming changes are temporary unless carried in other legislation.","The table of contents lists Title VII, Subtitle R, Sports Wagering, at pp. 137-143. That is roughly six pages, but the text supplied stops before it, so I cannot state any rates, definitions, covered operators or dates.","The table of contents lists Title V, Subtitle K, Problem Gambling Program Establishment Act, at p. 92. Its funding source and structure are not in the supplied text.","Title VII, Subtitle F (Sales and Use Tax, p. 126) and Title IX (Applicability; Fiscal Impact; Effective Date, p. 162) may bear on gaming revenue or timing. I cannot confirm that from the supplied text.","The portions I could read (Titles I and II, Subtitles A and B) cover unrelated subjects such as OIG authority, grant termination procedure, ANC funding and the Vitality Fund. None mention wagering."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. What is unusual here is that sports wagering and problem gambling are two small subtitles inside a roughly 160-page omnibus budget act. The act is an emergency measure with a short life, so any sports-wagering changes in Subtitle R may be temporary or later replaced by permanent budget legislation.","watch":"Get the full text of Title VII, Subtitle R (pp. 137-143) and Title V, Subtitle K (p. 92) to check for changes to the DC sports wagering framework, licensing or operator terms, revenue allocations and any problem-gambling funding assessments, and note that the emergency act lapses January 6, 2025.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":243659,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:42:23.443Z"},"DC-B25-1064":{"summary":"This DC emergency act approves Modification Nos. 3 and 5 to Contract No. DCAM-22-CS-SS-0007 with Hartman-Cox Architects, LLP for architectural and engineering services for a new archival facility. It authorizes payment of up to $7,098,088 for services received and to be received. It has nothing to do with sports betting, iGaming, or collectibles.","mechanics":["Sec. 2: Council approves Modification Nos. 3 and 5 to Contract No. DCAM-22-CS-SS-0007 under section 451 of the Home Rule Act (D.C. Official Code § 1-204.51).","Sec. 2: Applies notwithstanding the Council-approval requirements of section 202 of the Procurement Practices Reform Act of 2010 (D.C. Official Code § 2-352.02).","Sec. 2: Payment authorization is capped at a not-to-exceed amount of $7,098,088, covering services already received and those still to be received.","Sec. 3: Council adopts the CFO's fiscal statement as the fiscal impact statement under D.C. Official Code § 1-301.47a.","Sec. 4: Takes effect on Mayoral approval (or veto override) and lasts no more than 90 days as an emergency act under Home Rule Act section 412(a); the status line shows it expiring Mar 24, 2025."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"Nothing comparable is listed. None of the other states' bills (for example AK HB145, GA SB57, IL HB2239, CT HB05229) touch government procurement or architectural contracts. This bill is a one-off DC emergency contract approval with no gaming, wagering, tax, or collectibles content, so the overlap is only nominal.","watch":"Nothing for sports-betting operators to act on; the bill contains no wagering, licensing, tax, or advertising provisions.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":2208,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:42:23.926Z"},"DC-PR25-0637":{"summary":"PR25-0637 is a proposed Council resolution confirming Rosemary Suggs-Evans as Director of the DC Department of Small and Local Business Development (DSLBD), to serve at the pleasure of the Mayor. It makes no change to gambling law. Wagering appears only in the nominee's biographical material, which notes her role in overseeing development of DC's sports wagering legislation with local business participation requirements.","mechanics":["Sec. 2 confirms Suggs-Evans as DSLBD Director under section 2311 of the Small and Certified Business Enterprise Development and Assistance Act of 2005 (D.C. Official Code § 2-218.11); she serves at the pleasure of the Mayor.","The nomination was made under section 2 of the Confirmation Act of 1978 (D.C. Official Code § 1-523.01) and section 2312 of the 2005 Act (§ 2-218.12).","Sec. 3 requires the Council to transmit the resolution to the nominee and the Mayor's office; Sec. 4 makes it effective immediately.","The nominee's résumé says she supervised development of the nation's first sports wagering legislation mandating local business participation, and created opportunities for small businesses in sports wagering, cannabis and energy sustainability.","The résumé also credits her with leading Certified Business Enterprise (CBE) law reform to close loopholes and with the Equity Impact Enterprise grant program, and notes a $21M agency budget.","Status per the record: introduced, with a committee report filed on 2024-05-20."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. This is a gubernatorial-style confirmation resolution with no regulatory content. The only link to sports betting is biographical, in the nominee's résumé.","watch":"Little to act on in the resolution itself, but DSLBD under this Director will likely administer CBE and local-business participation requirements tied to DC sports wagering licensing, so operators should watch how the agency enforces them.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":16965,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:42:25.371Z"},"DC-B25-1062":{"summary":"Emergency act approving the Development and Financing Agreement, the Amended and Restated Lease and the Purchase and Sale Agreement between the District and DC Arena L.P. for the downtown arena (Verizon Center). It also authorizes easements and an airspace lease, continues and extends property tax and deed transfer/recordation tax exemptions for the arena parcels, and loosens the Council-approval process for arena signage rules. It contains no provisions on wagering, sports betting or gaming.","mechanics":["Sec. 2(a)-(b): Council approves, notwithstanding the Procurement Practices Reform Act, the Public-Private Partnership Act and other laws, the three agreements submitted October 18, 2024. The Mayor may take actions to implement them. Sec. 2(c) still applies the First Source Act and apprenticeship requirements to the Development and Financing Agreement.","Sec. 3: The Mayor may grant easements over Lot 47 in Square 455 and Lot 884 in Square 454 to adjacent owners, WMATA, the lessee of Lot 47, or others the Mayor deems appropriate.","Sec. 4: The Mayor may lease airspace adjacent to those lots to DC Arena L.P. or its designee at no other rent or fee, for as long as the Amended and Restated Lease remains in effect. Sec. 4(b) adds a new paragraph (3) to D.C. Code § 10-1121.07 for this lease.","Sec. 5(a)-(d): Adds new D.C. Code § 47-1002(34)-(35) (real property and possessory interest tax exemptions), § 47-1005.01(c-2), § 47-902(29) (deed transfer tax exemption) and § 42-1102(37) (deed recordation tax exemption). These cover transfers between the District and DC Arena L.P. or its designee, and last while the relevant lease remains in effect.","Sec. 5(e): Repeals section 3 of the Arena Tax Amendment Act of 1994, effective as of the effective date of this act.","Sec. 6: Amends 12-A DCMR § N101.19.16 to remove the requirement of Council active approval. Rules may authorize additional outdoor signs, digital displays and static canvas displays on the Verizon Center. Proposed rules go to a 45-day Council review and are deemed approved if the Council does not act.","Sec. 8: Emergency act effective on Mayor's approval and lasting no more than 90 days under Home Rule Act § 412(a). The register entry shows it expires March 17, 2025."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. This is a real-estate, financing and tax-abatement emergency act for a single arena, with no gaming content at all. Its only commercial-adjacent feature is the Sec. 6 signage authority. The enrolled text also uses inconsistent short titles ('Downtown Sports Arena Revitalization Emergency Act' and 'Downtown Sports Arena Modernization and Downtown Revitalization Emergency Act') when cross-referencing itself, which is a drafting oddity.","watch":"Nothing in the text touches wagering, but an operator with a sportsbook, sponsorship or signage arrangement tied to the arena should check the underlying Amended and Restated Lease (not reproduced here) and any rules issued under the Sec. 6 signage authority, and note that this emergency act expires in 90 days unless followed by further legislation.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":14619,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:42:25.599Z"},"DE-HR6":{"summary":"Delaware House Resolution 6 creates a four-member House working group to study whether and how Delaware should authorize an internet sports lottery. It is non-binding and legalizes nothing. Its stated aim is to inform the choice of model before the Lottery Office awards a contract of up to 10 years under its pending RFP (FIN23001-IWSSS) for an internet wagering system and services vendor.","mechanics":["Establishes an Internet Sports Lottery Legislative Working Group with four members: two House Majority Caucus members appointed by the Speaker, who serve as co-chairs, and two House Minority Caucus members appointed by the Minority Leader.","Directs the group to evaluate Delaware's current sports lottery framework and the legal online sports wagering markets in other states, focusing on Maryland, Pennsylvania and New Jersey.","The evaluation must cover tax and licensing structure, number of licensees, revenue history and projections, and the estimated size of Delaware's internet sports lottery market.","Recommendations are requested for the House on or before June 30, 2023.","The recitals cite the Lottery Office RFP seeking a single primary vendor, with an optional add-on for internet sports betting, a 5-year initial term and up to five 1-year extensions. They also say that multi-operator markets show higher gross gaming revenue per adult than one- or two-vendor markets.","The House's stated criteria for any internet sports lottery are competitiveness, a legal regulated market, drawing consumers from neighboring states and offshore, consumer protections, and the greatest income for the State. The resolution contains no tax rates, licensing rules or enforcement provisions."],"issue_tags":["legalize","licensing","tax"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike the substantive authorization or tax bills listed, such as AK HB145/SB194 (mobile sports wagering with a tax), GA SB57 and HB380 (Georgia Lottery-run betting) or IL HB2239 (Internet Gaming Act), DE HR6 enacts nothing and sets no rates or licensing rules. It is closest in spirit to the Georgia lottery-model bills because Delaware's sports lottery is state-run, but it only studies options. Its recitals favor a competitive multi-operator market even though the Lottery Office RFP contemplates a single primary vendor, which is unusual.","watch":"Watch whether the working group's recommendations, due by June 30, 2023, push Delaware toward a multi-operator licensed market or toward a single-vendor lottery contract under RFP FIN23001-IWSSS, since that determines operator access and the timing of any follow-on legislation.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Draft","text_chars":4739,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:42:32.968Z"},"DE-HB187":{"summary":"Delaware HB187 expands the Video Lottery Intercept Program in Title 29, ch. 48 so that delinquent child support can be offset against sports lottery and table game prizes, not just video lottery prizes. Operators must check winners against child support obligations before paying and remit the intercepted amount to the Division of Child Support Services. It was signed by the Governor on 2023-10-18.","mechanics":["Section 1 amends the § 4803(v) definition of 'qualified video lottery prize' to cover any video lottery, sports lottery, table game, or charitable gaming organization prize paid by cash or check for which a W-2G is issued, at or above $1,200 (less income tax withholding).","Section 2 amends § 4838(a): before awarding a qualified prize, the operator must determine whether the winner owes a qualified child support obligation. The prize is reduced by 100% if it is at or below the obligation, or by the amount of the obligation if the prize exceeds it, and any remainder goes to the winner.","Under § 4838(b), the cooperative agreement between the Division of Child Support Services, the Director and each operator must provide for monthly information updates from the Division and for automated data exchanges to the maximum extent feasible. It must also set the remittance procedure and require written notice to the winner of the amount withheld, with instructions for contesting the intercept directly with the Division.","Under § 4838(c), the operator has no liability under state law for disclosing information to the Division, for relying on the Division's records as presumptive of payment or nonpayment under 13 Del. C. § 513(c)(4), or for other good-faith actions taken to substantially comply.","Under § 4838(d), the Director must promulgate the rules needed to implement the section. The text gives no separate effective date and no penalties for noncompliance.","The text strikes the word 'video' in several places, but § 4838 still refers to operators of a 'video lottery facility' and 'video lottery or charitable gaming organization facility'. It does not expressly name sports wagering operators or table game licensees as parties to the cooperative agreements."],"issue_tags":["integrity"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"None of the listed bills (AK HB145/SB194, CT HB05229, GA SB57, IL HB2239 and the others) addresses child support intercepts, so nothing in the list is directly comparable. They are mostly licensing, tax, advertising or authorization measures. HB187 is a narrow payout-side compliance mandate that adds sports lottery and table game winnings to an existing intercept program and gives operators a good-faith liability shield, which none of the listed bills does.","watch":"Confirm whether the amended definition and the 'video lottery facility' language actually reach sports lottery and online or retail sports betting operators, and what the cooperative agreements will require for pre-payout database checks at the $1,200 W-2G threshold, winner notice, remittance timing and the scope of the liability protection.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Draft","text_chars":4858,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:42:34.845Z"},"DE-HB365":{"summary":"HB365, the Delaware Internet Sports Lottery Authorization Act, directs the Lottery Director to launch a competitive online sports lottery through new 29 Del. C. § 4826A. Operators are licensed through a Request for Applications and must hold a market access agreement with one of Delaware's licensed video lottery agents. The bill also broadens the definition of sports lottery and adds a new category of licensed service companies, and it carves out the incumbent vendor under the January 2023 Internet Wagering System RFP.","mechanics":["Licensing and market structure (§ 4826A(b)): operators need a market access agreement with a licensed video lottery agent. Each agent may partner with no more than 2 operators, and each operator may partner with only 1 agent. Applicants must submit a detailed package, including a responsible gaming plan, geolocation technology, integrity monitoring, a player acquisition and marketing plan, and a plan to maximize long-term State revenue. The Director may disqualify applicants and is not required to license unqualified ones.","Fees: an initial licence costs $500,000 for 5 years, and a negotiated renewal costs $250,000 for 5 years (§ 4826A(c)(2)). Fees go to the General Fund.","Tax and distributions (§ 4826A(e)): 18% of monthly adjusted gross sports lottery receipts is returned to the State, plus 1.5% to horse racing purses. Operators also owe a pro rata share of any purse deficit measured against the 12 months before launch. Remittance is due by the 15th of the following month. Negative AGR carries forward but never back, and no refunds are allowed. After Lottery Office costs, the greater of $400,000 or 3.5% of State proceeds funds problem gambling programs, and the balance goes to the State Lottery Fund.","Promotional deduction: free bets and promotional credits may be excluded from AGR up to 2.5% of total monthly bets until July 1, 2025, and up to 2.0% from July 1, 2025. The Director may allow a higher percentage by rule. AGR is also reduced by federal excise tax payments.","Scope and player rules: wagering is open to persons aged 21 or older who are physically in Delaware. 'Sports lottery' now covers amateur, collegiate, e-sports and player-statistic (prop) events, but excludes 'excluded events', meaning Delaware college or university events other than futures or sanctioned tournaments. Accounts must be held by natural persons and be non-transferable, and may be funded by cards, transfers, digital payments, cash and bonuses or promotions. Operators may not contract with Delaware colleges to promote wagering and must display 1-800-GAMBLER in all advertisements.","Timeline and vendors: the Director must issue the Request for Applications within 75 days of enactment and award licences within 150 days, and may grant conditional or probationary licences. Emergency regulations are permitted, and final rules are due within 1 year. Section 4 adds geolocation, payment processing, lab testing and KYC vendors to the 'service company' licensing category under § 4829. Section 7 exempts the vendor under RFP FIN23001-IWSSS, and any renewal or successor contract, from the Act."],"issue_tags":["legalize","licensing","tax","advertising","prop","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Delaware's bill is a Lottery-run, enabling-statute model that ties licensure to market access agreements with the three existing video lottery agents. This differs from open competitive licensing in AK HB145/SB194, HI HB 1308/HB 2570/SB1569, and the Georgia bills (such as GA SB57, GA HB380 and GA SB386), which create standalone licensing regimes or run through the Georgia Lottery. Its 18% rate with a separate 1.5% purse contribution and its low $500,000 five-year fee are specific to Delaware. Like CT HB05229 and CT HB05269, it restricts college-related marketing, but only for Delaware institutions and without the account-withdrawal or payment-method rules proposed there. Section 7's carve-out for the incumbent RFP vendor has no obvious counterpart in the listed bills.","watch":"Watch the market access agreement requirement (each of the three video lottery agents may partner with at most 2 operators, and each operator with only 1 agent) and the § 7 carve-out for the incumbent platform vendor, since together they cap the number of entrants and may disadvantage operators who cannot secure a partner or who rely on a different platform.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Draft","text_chars":30505,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:42:37.008Z"},"GA-HB1276":{"summary":"Georgia HB1276 creates a framework allowing public and private high school athletes to earn NIL compensation, subject to restrictions on school involvement, recruiting inducements, and categories of endorsements. It also amends Title 39 so that minors receiving NIL compensation fall under Commissioner of Labor consent, with conservator and trust-account requirements and mandatory training and third-party registration. NIL contracts automatically expire shortly after graduation or loss of enrollment.","mechanics":["New Code Section 20-2-2105.3(e)(7) bars a student athlete from receiving NIL compensation for any activity that reasonably can be perceived as endorsing or promoting casinos and gambling, expressly including sports betting, the lottery, and betting tied to video games, online games, and mobile devices. Alcohol, tobacco, vapor, cannabis, weapons, adult content and any product restricted to under-18s are also barred (paras. (1)-(10)).","Section 20-2-2105.5 voids NIL contracts at the earlier of 10 days after graduation or 30 days after enrollment ends for any reason. Any non-conforming contract provision is void and unenforceable. Under Section 5, the Act applies to contracts entered into after the effective date.","Section 20-2-2105.3(b)-(d) bars NIL pay as an inducement to attend a particular school. It also bars schools and booster-type entities from providing NIL compensation, and bars use of school marks or references to the school or athletic association. Athletes may not promote a NIL payer's goods during interscholastic activity, except at tryouts, practices and training sessions.","Amended 39-2-18 makes Commissioner of Labor consent a precondition, with the minor protections in the chapter otherwise disapplied. The Commissioner must find that the minor's name, image or likeness will not be used for any purpose prohibited by 20-2-2105.3(e). For a minor with more than $15,000 in NIL compensation, a probate-court conservator must be appointed under Chapter 3 of Title 29.","New 39-2-18.1 requires a trust account with the minor as beneficiary and at least 50% of gross after-tax compensation deposited, held by a trust company or other authorized institution, with funds available at age 18 or emancipation. If account information is not supplied within 30 days, the funds go to the Commissioner of Revenue as unclaimed property. Once the funds are deposited, the payer (defined to include any business responsible for paying the minor) has no further duty to monitor them.","39-2-18.1(e)-(f) requires training for all parties to NIL agreements (students, families and contracted third parties), at a fee of up to $50 per person, with a certificate on completion. Any third party contracting with minors on NIL must register with the state. The Commissioner of Labor has rulemaking authority and sets up the training and registration. Athletic associations may not penalize athletes or schools for NIL pay, and public schools receiving state funding must withdraw from associations whose governing documents do not comply (20-2-2105.6)."],"issue_tags":["advertising","prop","integrity","licensing"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"Only the NIL bills in the list are comparable: DE HB314, HI HB 2384 and SB3263, and IL HB0307. The Hawaii bills affirm student-athletes' NIL rights through University of Hawaii policy, and IL HB0307 amends the Student-Athlete Endorsement Rights Act. Those are college-focused, whereas Georgia HB1276 targets high school and minor athletes and adds a conservator and trust-account regime, contract-expiry rules, third-party registration, and a flat ban on gambling endorsements. CT HB05229 restricts gaming advertising on college campuses, but that is a licensee-side advertising rule, while Georgia restricts what the athlete may be paid to promote and does not regulate operators directly.","watch":"Operators and any other gambling-adjacent brands (including sweepstakes, prediction-market and card-break businesses that could be read as 'betting') should note that 20-2-2105.3(e)(7) bars high school athletes from receiving NIL pay for anything that could reasonably be perceived as promoting casinos or gambling, and that the Labor Commissioner consent and registration regime could make third-party deals with minors void or unlawful.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":16887,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:42:38.477Z"},"GA-HB1329":{"summary":"The committee substitute to GA HB1329 creates the Georgia Fantasy Contest Consumer Protection Act as new Article 4 of the Georgia Lottery for Education Act (O.C.G.A. 50-27-120 et seq.). It makes the Georgia Lottery Corporation the licensor and regulator of paid online fantasy contests, imposes a 20% privilege tax on adjusted gross fantasy contest receipts, and carves licensed fantasy play out of the criminal definition of \"bet,\" the 'void in Georgia' advertising rule, and the gambling-information offense. It also changes the Lottery for Education shortfall reserve formula in 50-27-13(b)(3).","mechanics":["Tax (50-27-150): annual privilege tax of 20% of adjusted gross fantasy contest receipts (entry fees less federal excise tax and prizes paid, multiplied by a Georgia location percentage), paid monthly to the Corporation on an accrual basis, with negative months carried forward. Proceeds go to the lottery-for-education purposes in Ga. Const. Art. I, Sec. II, Para. VIII(c).","Licensing (50-27-130): any person offering fantasy contests must hold a Corporation license, valid five years. Large operators (adjusted gross receipts of $5M or more in the prior 12 months) pay a $100,000 application fee and a $1,000,000 annual fee; all other operators pay a $5,000 annual fee. The Corporation must act on applications within 60 days and may waive review (but not fees) for applicants licensed in other U.S. states or as Georgia sports wagering operators.","Transition and effective date (50-27-130(d), Sec. 5-1): operators already offering contests before Oct. 1, 2024 may keep operating pending a decision if they file a complete application within 60 days after the Corporation publishes the application. The Act is effective on signing for rulemaking and on Oct. 1, 2024 for all other purposes. Initial rules must be adopted after public comment before Oct. 1, 2024 and are exempt from the Georgia APA.","Contest definition (50-27-122(4)): online contests of skill with an entry fee, prizes set in advance, and outcomes based on athlete statistical performance. It excludes outcomes based on a single team's score or spread, or on a single athlete's performance in a single event. Both pick-em contests against a house-set target score and head-to-head contests are covered. Sporting events include college, esports and Olympic events, but not events where most participants are under 18.","Participant and operator controls: participants must be 19 or older and geolocated in Georgia. Operators must verify identity and age, limit each person to one account, and offer self-exclusion and limit tools. Rules must cover geolocation, AML, reserves and advertising. The Corporation cannot set minimum or maximum prize payouts (50-27-123(b)). Large operators need an annual independent CPA audit (50-27-132). Principal owners need a Department of Revenue certificate of fitness with fingerprint background check (50-27-180).","Enforcement: administrative fines up to $25,000 per violation and $50,000 per occurrence, plus suspension, revocation or non-renewal. Knowingly allowing prohibited participants carries fixed fines of $1,000, $2,000 and $5,000 for first, second and third offenses. Civil penalties are up to $5,000 per violation, capped at $50,000 per occurrence. Prohibited individuals who pay entry fees face escalating misdemeanor penalties. Appeals go to Fulton County Superior Court under a deferential standard (50-27-195).","Related amendments: the sales tax exemption in 48-8-3(55) is extended to entry fees; 16-12-20(1) excludes consideration paid for licensed fantasy contests from \"bet\"; 16-12-27(c) and 16-12-28(c) exempt licensees from the 'void in Georgia' advertising rule and the communicating-gambling-information offense."],"issue_tags":["tax","licensing","legalize","advertising","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"This is a fantasy-contest-only licensing and tax bill, whereas most listed bills (AK HB145/SB194, HI HB1308, DE HB365) authorize sports wagering. Closest comparables are IL HB2342, the Fantasy Sports Consumer Protection Act with Gaming Board regulation, fees and a tax on adjusted gross fantasy receipts, and the Iowa fantasy-and-sports-wagering tax bills (IA HF673, HSB122). Unusual features are the Lottery Corporation as regulator (not a gaming commission), the 20% tax paid to the Corporation, a $1M annual fee tier for large operators, an age floor of 19, and rules exempt from the Georgia APA. The bill also carves licensed fantasy play out of Georgia's criminal gambling code and includes a stray reserve-formula change.","watch":"An operator should watch whether the definition in 50-27-122(4) captures pick-em and player-prop style products (the single-athlete or single-event exclusion in (F)), and whether the Corporation's APA-exempt rulemaking, due before Oct. 1, 2024, shapes fees, tax base and affiliate fitness requirements without conventional review.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Comm Sub","text_chars":46923,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:42:46.934Z"},"GA-HB380":{"summary":"The Committee Substitute to HB 380 would make online sports betting a Georgia Lottery game under a new Article 4 of the Lottery for Education Act (the 'Georgia Lottery Game of Sports Betting Act'), relying on the lottery clause of the Georgia Constitution rather than a constitutional amendment. The Georgia Lottery Corporation would be the regulator and master licensee, and would license up to 16 Type 1 operators, each tied to a qualifying sports entity or a procured services provider. Operators would pay a 25% privilege tax on adjusted gross income. The bill also changes the lottery shortfall reserve formula and amends the gambling and tax codes to carve out sports betting.","mechanics":["Licensing structure (50-27-133): at most 16 Type 1 licenses. Five are reserved for professional sports teams, or their designees, that meet the attendance test. One each goes to a qualifying golf governing body, an invitational golf tournament owner, and a NASCAR-track owner. The remaining seven are awarded to online sports betting services providers by public procurement. The Lottery Corporation is itself a Type 1 eligible entity and master licensee, and operates through a designated provider.","Tax (50-27-150): annual privilege tax of 25% of adjusted gross income, paid monthly to the corporation by the services provider, or by the Type 1 licensee if it has no provider. The master licensee is excluded. Adjusted gross income is bets received minus federal excise tax minus winnings paid, and a negative month can be carried over (the text cuts off here).","Fees (50-27-130(c)(10)): Type 1 license has a $100,000 application fee and $1.5M annual fee, or $750,000 for the licenses reserved under 133(b)(3), paid by the provider. Services provider license is $10,000 and $100,000. Supplier license is $2,000 and $20,000. Licenses run five years (131(g)). A minimum $500,000 reserve, or outstanding wager liabilities if greater, is needed to be presumed financially stable.","Scope of wagers (50-27-122(42)): fixed-odds, futures, tier 1 and tier 2, money line, over/under, parlay, proposition and spread bets. Collegiate events and esports are covered. Events where most participants are under 18 are excluded. Bettors must be 21 or older and physically in Georgia. Proposition bets may only be offered by the master licensee, its provider, or a Type 1 licensee (50-27-135). Official league data is not mandated, but the definition ties tier 2 settlement to it.","Timing and process: the corporation may not issue licenses before Sept. 1, 2023 (123(c)). Initial rules are due within 90 days of the effective date and are exempt from the Georgia APA. Eligible entities must give notice of intent within 60 days or be permanently barred (133(d)). Applicants that apply within 30 days get an equal chance of a same-day launch, no later than Jan. 31, 2024 (133(f)). Applications must be decided within 90 days (130(d)).","Other terms: the corporation cannot set payout or hold limits (123(b)). Sports governing bodies may enter revenue-share agreements with no corporate approval (131(d)). Institutional investors are exempt from qualification and disclosure (134(d)). Licensees from other jurisdictions can request a temporary license of up to one year (131(e)-(f)). The corporation sets advertising rules and problem-gambling programs and may eject people from the platform. Disqualified persons include players, coaches, and league employees, though 131(b) allows minority ownership below 25% without corporate approval."],"issue_tags":["legalize","licensing","tax","prop","advertising","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Most comparable bills are not close structurally. Alaska HB145 and SB194 and Delaware HB365 also authorize mobile sports wagering with a tax, but Georgia is unusual in housing it inside the state lottery under the existing constitutional lottery authority, with a hard cap of 16 licenses reserved mostly for in-state sports franchises, golf, and racing entities. Delaware HB365 also uses lottery-run oversight. Hawaii HB 1308, HB 2570 and SB1569 use a general excise tax and a department regulator. Georgia's 25% rate is a flat privilege tax on adjusted gross income, and the bill is mobile-only with no retail sportsbooks. Illinois HB1405 (exchange trade wagering) and Illinois HB2239 (iGaming) have no counterpart here, since Georgia covers only sports betting.","watch":"Operators should focus on the 16-license cap and reserved slots (only seven open via procurement), the 60-day notice and permanent-disqualification trap for eligible entities, the 25% tax on adjusted gross income, and the ability of the Lottery Corporation to write initial rules outside the APA and set advertising and prop-bet rules.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Comm Sub","text_chars":89443,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:42:50.399Z"},"GA-HB237":{"summary":"The bill text provided is a Senate committee substitute that replaces the soap box derby designation in the title with a framework to legalize online-only sports betting in Georgia as a lottery game run under the Georgia Lottery Corporation. The Corporation is the master licensee and regulator. It would license up to 16 Type 1 licensees, plus operators and suppliers, and impose a 22% privilege tax on adjusted gross income. The bill also changes the Lottery for Education shortfall reserve formula and adds responsible-gaming mandates. The supplied text cuts off at 50-27-151(c), so later provisions on bettor restrictions, penalties, fines and the effective date are not visible.","mechanics":["Tax: annual privilege tax of 22% of adjusted gross income from online sports betting (50-27-150), paid monthly by the online sports betting services provider (or by a Type 1 licensee operating without one). Negative AGI carries forward. The tax is in lieu of other state and local sales and income taxes, and proceeds go to the lottery education purposes in Ga. Const. Art. I, Sec. II, Para. VIII(c). Adjusted gross income deducts federal excise tax and winning payouts, including the cash value of merchandise.","Licence structure: up to 16 Type 1 licences (50-27-133). Five are reserved for professional sports teams, one each for a PGA-type governing body, a 30-year invitational golf facility owner, a NASCAR-track owner and the Lottery Corporation, and seven are awarded to operators through public procurement. A Type 1 eligible entity must give notice of intent within 60 days of the effective date or be permanently barred. The services provider is treated as the licensee for regulatory purposes, and a provider may contract with only one Type 1 licensee.","Fees and reserves: Type 1 licence costs $100,000 to apply and $1M annually, or $750,000 annually for the procured licences. Services provider licence costs $10,000 and $100,000. Supplier licence costs $2,000 and $20,000. Licences run five years. Applicants must keep a reserve of at least $500,000 or outstanding wager liabilities, whichever is greater (50-27-130(c)(7)(A)). The Corporation must decide on applications within 90 days.","Scope of wagering: online only (50-27-122(33)), with a bettor required to be 21 or older and physically in Georgia. Covered events include professional, collegiate, Olympic and esports events, but not non-Olympic or nonprofessional events where most participants are under 18. Prop bets may be offered only by the master licensee, its services provider, or a Type 1 licensee (50-27-135). Fantasy entry fees and pari-mutuel bets are excluded from 'wager'.","Regulatory design: the Corporation's rules, including the initial rules due within 90 days, are exempt from the Georgia APA. It may not set minimum or maximum payout or hold percentages (50-27-123(b)). It must set advertising rules and problem-gambling measures: ads not targeted to minors, self-exclusion, bet limits for at-risk bettors, and posting of helpline numbers. It may not issue licences before September 1, 2023, and the first Type 1 launch is set for no later than January 31, 2024.","Licence eligibility and integrity: persons barred from licensure include Corporation staff, team employees, coaches and players, governing-body staff, and higher-education trustees (50-27-132). Other persons can hold ownership interests, but a stake of 25% or more needs Corporation approval. Institutional investors are exempt from qualification and disclosure (50-27-134(d)). Sports governing bodies may make revenue-share deals without Corporation approval (50-27-131(d)). Out-of-state licensees may get a temporary licence of up to one year at the CEO's discretion (50-27-131(e)-(f))."],"issue_tags":["legalize","tax","licensing","advertising","prop","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"The title (soap box derby designation) does not match the committee substitute text, which is a full sports betting legalization act, so the listed bills offer little direct comparison. Iowa SSB 1157 is the closest, since it also covers licence fees, tax rates and sports wagering enforcement. Georgia's 22% rate and tiered fee schedule would be the point of comparison. Unlike the Connecticut advertising bills (HB05229, HB05269, HB05272) and the New York and New Jersey ad-restriction bills (NY A1118, S1550; NJ A5562), Georgia sets no specific ad restrictions and only delegates advertising rules to the Corporation (50-27-123(a)(12)(G) and (a)(14)). Maryland HB291/SB102 on promotional play is likewise not matched, because Georgia defines AGI without any promo-credit deduction and does not address free play. Georgia's structure is also unusual: the state lottery is the master licensee, licences are reserved for sports teams and venues, and the whole scheme rests on lottery authority in the existing Constitution rather than a constitutional amendment.","watch":"An operator's counsel should watch whether the bill is revived after the Senate recommitted it on 2024-01-08, and in particular the lottery-based constitutional theory, the limited Type 1 licence pool tied to teams and venues, the 60-day notice forfeiture, the 22% tax with no promotional-credit deduction, and the Corporation's APA-exempt rulemaking over ads, prop bets and bet limits.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Comm Sub","text_chars":86386,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:42:55.662Z"},"GA-SB172":{"summary":"This committee substitute creates the Georgia Sports Betting Integrity Act (new O.C.G.A. Chapter 28 of Title 50). It sets up a public corporation, the Georgia Gaming Corporation, governed by a seven-member Georgia Sports Betting Commission, to license and regulate online and retail (machine-based) sports betting for bettors aged 21 or older. It also lets the Georgia Lottery Corporation act as a Type 1 licensee and offer online betting through a single procured services provider. The supplied text is cut off in Part 2 of Article 2, so the privilege tax rate and the licensing sections are not visible.","mechanics":["Licence structure: a Type 1 licensee offers online betting directly or indirectly. Retail betting uses Type 2 machines (no live betting) owned by Type 2 distributor licensees, hosted at Type 2 retail licensees, and run through Type 2 platform licensees. Suppliers and online sports betting services providers are also licensed (50-28-3).","Lottery role (50-27-121): the Georgia Lottery Corporation may offer online betting as a Type 1 licensee only through one online sports betting services provider, chosen by a public procurement process under board-adopted procedures.","Commission (50-28-5, 50-28-7, 50-28-9): seven members, three appointed by the Governor, two by the Senate President and two by the House Speaker, with four-year terms. Four members make a quorum, and at least three votes are needed to adopt a rule. It cannot set a licensee's minimum or maximum payout or hold percentage (50-28-9(b)). It cannot issue any licence before September 1, 2025 (50-28-9(c)). Initial rules are due within 90 days of the effective date and are exempt from the Georgia APA (50-28-9(a)(5)).","Wager definition (50-28-3(51)): limited to fixed-odds bets, futures, live bets, money line, over-under, parlay, proposition and spread bets. It excludes pari-mutuel wagers and fantasy or simulated contest entry fees. 'Sporting event' covers professional, collegiate, Olympic and sanctioned events, but excludes non-professional events where most participants are under 18. 'Adjusted gross income' is bets received minus federal excise taxes and winnings paid out.","Privilege tax and funding: the title and 50-28-8(d) refer to a privilege tax under 50-28-70, whose rate is not in the supplied text. The executive director's pay is fixed at 1% to 5% of privilege taxes collected. Net proceeds go to the purposes in Art. I, Sec. II, Para. VIII of the Georgia Constitution.","Integrity and responsible gaming: a certificate of fitness is required for commissioners, the executive director and certain licence-related persons, with fingerprint checks run through the Department of Revenue, GCIC and the FBI. Commissioners and employees face gift, wagering and one-year revolving-door limits. The commission must run self-exclusion, limit-setting and problem-gambling programs and adopt advertising rules (50-28-9(a)(13), (16)). 'Material nonpublic information' is defined (50-28-3(18))."],"issue_tags":["legalize","licensing","tax","integrity","advertising"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Georgia is a lottery-linked, regulator-heavy model. The Georgia Lottery Corporation (via 50-27-121) can run online betting through one procured provider, and a seven-member commission regulates. That is closest to Delaware HB365, which uses the Lottery Director to build a competitive mobile market, but Georgia is more restrictive on the lottery's own offering. It also adds a retail layer (Type 2 machines, with no live betting) that the supplied Alaska bills (HB145, SB194) and Hawaii bills (HB1308, HB2570, SB1569) do not describe. Unlike Illinois HB2878, which specifies a $250,000 application fee and a $15,000,000 licence fee, the licence fees and tax rate are not visible in the supplied Georgia text.","watch":"Because the text is truncated, check the privilege tax rate (50-28-70), the Type 1 licensing provisions (50-28-43) and the licence fee and number-of-licences provisions to see whether private operators can hold Type 1 licences or must operate under the lottery's single-provider contract, and note that the bill was tabled in the Senate on 2024-01-12.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Comm Sub","text_chars":114801,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:42:57.144Z"},"GA-SR140":{"summary":"GA SR140 (Senate committee substitute) is a proposed constitutional amendment that would revise Article I, Section II, Paragraph VIII of the Georgia Constitution to let the General Assembly legalize and regulate sports betting by or on behalf of the state. It creates no betting rules itself and would require implementing legislation, a new gaming commission, and voter ratification. The status shows the resolution was lost in the Senate on 2023-03-06.","mechanics":["Subparagraph (a) keeps the constitutional ban on lotteries, sports betting, pari-mutuel betting and casino gambling, except as Paragraph VIII specifically allows. Sports betting becomes a listed exception only through new subparagraph (d).","New subparagraph (d) authorizes the General Assembly to provide by law for sports betting activities 'by or on behalf of the state.' That wording points toward a state-run or state-affiliated model rather than an open commercial market, though the text does not define it.","Subparagraph (d) requires the General Assembly to create a gaming commission with the powers needed to regulate and tax sports betting. Tax proceeds go to the (e)(1) purposes, and other regulatory proceeds fund commission operating expenses. The Governor must show 'Sports Betting Proceeds' as a separate budget category.","Subparagraph (e)(1) allocates tax proceeds: 50% to need-based scholarships, grants or loans; 25% to health care, mental health, economic development and poverty reduction in high-poverty areas; 15% to problem-gambling prevention and treatment; 5% to advancing sports and hosting sporting events; and 5% to innovational education. Subparagraph (e)(2) authorizes dedicated funds that are exempt from lapsing and general-fund rules.","Subparagraph (f) authorizes the Georgia Lottery Corporation to participate in the legislature's sports betting framework. Its net proceeds go to lottery education purposes under (c), while taxes on its participation that the commission receives follow (e)(1). No tax rate, license structure, or online/retail distinction is specified.","Section 2 sends the amendment to voters under Article X, Section I, Paragraph II, with a ballot question on need-based education funding, high-poverty-area services, addiction services, sports promotion and innovational education. It takes effect only if ratified."],"issue_tags":["legalize","tax","licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike the other states' bills listed, which are statutory (for example AK HB145 and SB194, HI HB1308 and SB1569, DE HB365, IL HB2878), GA SR140 is only a constitutional enabling measure with no license fees, tax rate, operator categories or mobile provisions. Those bills set rates and licensing details, while SR140 fixes only the allocation of tax proceeds (50/25/15/5/5) and requires a commission. Its 'by or on behalf of the state' wording and the Georgia Lottery Corporation role in subparagraph (f) resemble the lottery-run approach in DE HB365 and IL HB3276 more than the open competitive licensing in AK HB145 or HI HB1308.","watch":"Because SR140 authorizes sports betting only 'by or on behalf of the state' and gives the Georgia Lottery Corporation a role, an operator should watch whether any implementing legislation reserves the market to the state or lottery rather than allowing private licensed operators, and how taxes are set on the lottery's participation.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Comm Sub","text_chars":11473,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:43:04.362Z"},"GA-SR538":{"summary":"Senate committee substitute to SR 538 proposes a constitutional amendment to Art. I, Sec. II, Para. VIII of the Georgia Constitution. If voters ratify it, the General Assembly must legislate by July 2, 2025 to authorize and regulate sports betting and limited casino gambling, create a five-member gaming commission, and license five casino facilities. Casino revenue would be taxed at 13 percent, with proceeds earmarked to five dedicated funds. The resolution is not self-executing and leaves sports betting structure and tax rate to implementing legislation.","mechanics":["Amends Para. VIII(a) to keep the general prohibition on lotteries, sports betting, pari-mutuel betting and casino gambling except as provided, and adds new subparagraphs (e) and (f). Under (e)(1), the General Assembly must enact enabling law by July 2, 2025.","Gaming commission under (e)(2): five members (three Governor appointees, one by the President of the Senate, one by the Speaker), staggered terms, to be established by Dec. 31, 2025. The commission must adopt rules by June 30, 2026 and issue initial sports betting and casino licenses by Aug. 31, 2026 (e)(6).","Casino gambling under (e)(3)-(5): five casino gaming licenses, each for a single on-site facility; no off-site casino gambling; open every day and all hours; alcohol sales and complimentary service permitted on premises; one license per person or entity and no ownership interest in more than one licensee; counties may bar new casino facilities by ordinance or resolution (e)(4), with existing facilities grandfathered.","Tax under (e)(7): casino gambling taxed at 13 percent of gross income less operating expenses, winnings paid and permitted reserves. No rate is set for sports betting, which is left to the General Assembly. Casino and sports betting licensees are subject to other taxes but exempt from state and local sales and use tax. Non-tax regulatory proceeds fund commission operations without appropriation, with any net amount going to the general fund under a 'Gaming Commission Proceeds' budget category.","Allocation under (f)(1) of tax proceeds: 50% Freight and Logistics Fund, 10% Mental Health and Gambling Addiction Fund, 10% Rural Health Care Fund, 20% Child Care Fund, 10% HBCU Fund; funds do not lapse (f)(2).","Section 2 provides for submission to voters under Art. X, Sec. I, Para. II, with a ballot question asking whether the General Assembly must provide for sports betting and casino gambling by July 2, 2025. The text sets out no mobile or online sports betting provisions, and no operator count or license fees for sports betting."],"issue_tags":["legalize","tax","licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike the statutory sports wagering bills listed, such as AK HB145/SB194, HI HB1308/HB2570/SB1569, DE HB365 and IL HB2878, GA SR538 is a constitutional amendment that sets no operator licensing terms, fees or sports betting tax rate. It instead requires the legislature to act and fixes a 13 percent casino tax, a five-license cap and the earmarks. Those are features not found in the listed bills, which deal with mobile sports wagering, online gaming or incremental changes to existing regimes (AR HB1861, IL HB2239/HB3080, CT SB01464).","watch":"Watch whether the implementing legislation permits mobile or online sports betting, how many sports betting licenses it creates, and what tax rate and fees it sets, since the amendment leaves all of these open while requiring action by July 2, 2025 and licensing by Aug. 31, 2026.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Comm Sub","text_chars":11983,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:43:06.805Z"},"GA-SB57":{"summary":"This Senate committee substitute adds Article 4 (the 'Georgia Sports Betting Integrity Act') to the Georgia Lottery for Education Act. It authorizes sports betting, declares it a lottery game and not casino gambling or pari-mutuel betting, and creates a Sports Betting Division inside the Georgia Lottery Corporation to license and regulate it. Online betting runs only through a small set of 'Type 1' licensees, with third-party services providers operating under those licenses, and retail betting runs through licensed kiosks. The bill also adjusts the lottery shortfall reserve and exempts sports-betting wagers from Title 48. The introduced text shows the Senate lost the bill on 2023-03-02, and the text supplied cuts off in the middle of Code Section 50-27-143(c).","mechanics":["Online sports betting is limited to Type 1 eligible entities: in-state professional teams (baseball, football, men's and women's basketball, soccer, ranked by attendance), certain professional golf tournament hosts, a 30-year invitational golf facility owner, certain NASCAR and international motor sports track owners, and the Lottery Corporation itself. Sec. 50-27-143(c) provides for five Type 1 licenses, and the text cuts off before paragraph (3).","Each Type 1 licensee must designate a licensed online sports betting services provider to operate the platform. The provider is treated as the applicant for licensure and pays the Type 1 fee (50-27-140(c)(11)(A)).","Fees (50-27-140(c)(11)): Type 1 costs $100,000 to apply and $1 million annually. Kiosk distributor, retail platform, services provider and supplier licenses each cost $10,000 to apply and $100,000 annually. A manufacturer license costs $5,000 and $50,000. A standard retail location license costs $500 and $500. A retail license for equestrian track locations costs $100,000 and $1 million. Licenses run 20 years and are renewable (50-27-141(e)). The division must decide within 90 days of a complete application.","Applicants are presumed financially stable if they hold a reserve of at least $500,000, or the amount needed to cover outstanding wager liabilities if greater. The reserve may be a bond, letter of credit, payment processor reserves, or segregated cash (50-27-140(c)(7)(A)). Applicants must also show good character by clear and convincing evidence over a ten-year lookback and provide a letter of reference from any other sports betting regulator.","A bettor must be at least 21 and physically present in Georgia. Wagers are limited to fixed-odds, futures, live, money line, over/under, parlay, proposition and spread bets. Fantasy or simulated contests and pari-mutuel bets are excluded. Covered events include pro, collegiate, Olympic and esports events, but not non-professional events where most participants are under 18. 'Tier 2' in-play proposition wagers are defined by reference to official league or event data.","Each individual required to be licensed, including principal owners of 10% or more, needs a certificate of fitness from the Department of Revenue after an FBI fingerprint check. Any felony, or a misdemeanor directly related to gambling, dishonesty, theft or fraud, disqualifies the applicant (50-27-128). Corporation board members and employees face a one-year revolving-door ban and a $100 annual gift cap, and several categories of people (athletes, coaches, league employees, university trustees) cannot hold licenses (50-27-142). The division may not set minimum or maximum payout or hold percentages (50-27-124(b))."],"issue_tags":["legalize","licensing","integrity","tax"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Georgia's model is run through the state lottery. The Sports Betting Division sits inside the Georgia Lottery Corporation, which is also eligible for a Type 1 license. The closest listed analogue is DE HB365, which sets up a competitive mobile sports wagering market under the Lottery Director. By contrast, HI HB1308 and HB2570 and AK HB145 and SB194 are listed as using a general excise tax or a mobile wagering revenue tax, and the Georgia text supplied never reaches its privilege tax rate. Georgia is unusual in limiting online access to a closed list of sports franchises, golf and motorsports venue owners and the Lottery, which must partner with licensed services providers, and in pairing that with licensed retail kiosks and 20-year license terms. No listed bill (for example IL HB1405 on exchange trade wagering or IL HB2239 on Internet gaming) shows a comparable structure.","watch":"Operators would have no direct license path and would need a commercial deal with one of the five Type 1 licensees as a designated services provider, while the privilege tax rate, the truncated remainder of 50-27-143 and the advertising and problem-gambling provisions are not in the supplied text, and the bill was lost in the Senate.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Comm Sub","text_chars":93583,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:43:08.838Z"},"GA-SB386":{"summary":"SB 386 (House Higher Education committee substitute) creates the Georgia Sports Betting Act as a new Article 4 of Chapter 27 of Title 50. It legalizes online-only sports betting for bettors 21 or older who are physically in Georgia, with the Georgia Lottery Corporation acting as regulator and as the master sports betting licensee. Operators are capped at 16 Type 1 licenses, five of them reserved for professional sports teams, and online sports betting services providers pay a 25 percent privilege tax on adjusted gross income. The bill also amends the Lottery for Education shortfall reserve in 50-27-13(b)(3). Its title also refers to a contingent effective date, automatic repeal, increased Pre-K funding, and conforming changes to Titles 16 and 48, but the supplied text cuts off partway through 50-27-150, so those provisions could not be reviewed.","mechanics":["Licensing structure (50-27-133): no more than 16 Type 1 licenses. Five are reserved for professional sports teams (a team can be a Type 1 eligible entity only if it plays baseball, football, men's basketball, soccer or women's basketball and has the highest in-state attendance in its sport over five years). One license each is reserved for a PGA-type governing body with 30 years of Georgia tournaments, the owner of a facility with a 30-year invitational golf tournament, and the owner of a NASCAR-type track. The remaining seven go to online sports betting services providers through public procurement. The Lottery Corporation is itself a Type 1 licensee and does not apply. An eligible entity that misses the 60-day written notice of intent after the effective date is permanently barred. Each eligible entity may use only one services provider, and each provider may contract with only one Type 1 licensee.","Fees and terms (50-27-130(c)(9), 50-27-131(g)): Type 1 licenses carry a $100,000 nonrefundable application fee and a $1 million annual fee. Services providers pay $10,000 and $100,000, and suppliers pay $2,000 and $20,000. The application fee is credited against the annual fee. Licenses run five years. Applicants must hold a reserve of at least $500,000 or outstanding wager liabilities, whichever is greater, which may be a bond, letter of credit, segregated cash or similar. The corporation must act on an application within 90 days. Operators licensed in another jurisdiction may request a temporary license of up to one year or until the final determination, whichever is later (50-27-131(e)-(f)).","Tax (50-27-150): the annual privilege tax is 25 percent of adjusted gross income, paid monthly to the corporation by the online sports betting services provider, or by a Type 1 licensee that has no provider. The master licensee is excluded. Adjusted gross income is wagers minus federal excise tax minus payouts, including the cash value of merchandise won, and negative months carry forward. The tax is in lieu of other state and local sales, use and income taxes and fees on betting operations, apart from the application and licensing fees. Proceeds go to the purposes in Article I, Section II, Paragraph VIII(c) of the Constitution.","Launch and rulemaking (50-27-123(a)(4), 50-27-133(f)-(h)): initial rules must be adopted within 90 days of the effective date, and all rules are exempt from the Georgia APA. A Type 1 licensee may not launch until at least one procurement-awarded provider is licensed. Applicants who file within 30 days of the opening of applications get an equal opportunity to launch on the same day, and in any event not later than January 31, 2025.","Product and operating limits: the wager definition covers tier 1 and tier 2 wagers, parlays, in-play wagers, exchange wagering and propositions, and sporting events include collegiate and esports events. Proposition wagers may be offered only by the master licensee, its provider, or a Type 1 licensee (50-27-135). The corporation may not set payout or hold percentages (50-27-123(b)). Official league data is referenced for tier 2 outcomes, and professional sports governing bodies and Type 1 eligible entities may enter revenue-sharing agreements without corporation approval (50-27-131(d)).","Integrity and responsible gaming: 50-27-132 bars categories such as corporation employees, players and coaches, and governing-body personnel from holding licenses, though ownership interests of 25 percent or more need corporation approval. Institutional investors are exempt from qualification and disclosure (50-27-134(d)). The corporation must adopt self-exclusion rules requiring name, address, date of birth and SSN, bet limits for bettors showing problem-gambling signs, 1-800-GAMBLER postings, and advertising rules aimed at keeping marketing away from minors."],"issue_tags":["legalize","licensing","tax","advertising","prop","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Among the listed bills, Delaware HB365 is the closest analogue, since it also sets up a competitive mobile sports wagering market under the Lottery Director. Georgia goes further by making the Lottery Corporation both regulator and master licensee, capping licenses at 16, and reserving most of them (nine of 16, counting the five team licenses and four venue or governing-body licenses) for sports teams and venue or tour entities. Alaska HB145/SB194 and Hawaii HB 1308, HB 2570 and SB1569 also authorize and tax sports wagering, but the listed summaries show regulation by other agencies and no comparable reserved-license design. Illinois HB1405 addresses exchange trade wagering, which Georgia only folds into its definition of wager, and the supplied text shows no separate exchange framework.","watch":"An operator should focus on the reserved and capped license structure, including the rule that a provider can contract with only one Type 1 licensee, the 25 percent tax on adjusted gross income with no deduction for promotional credits, the rulemaking exemption from the APA, and the contingent effective date the title refers to, while confirming the rest of 50-27-150 and the later sections in the full text because the supplied text is cut off.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Comm Sub","text_chars":88678,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:43:13.210Z"},"IA-HF673":{"summary":"Iowa HF 673 does not change the sports wagering or fantasy tax rates. It redirects where the receipts go: the sports wagering receipts fund (Code 8.57(6)) is repealed effective June 30, 2024, and fantasy sports and sports wagering taxes are routed to a fixed waterfall and the county endowment fund (Code 15E.311). It also raises the Endow Iowa tax credit cap and appropriates $7 million for the credit. The bill was withdrawn on 2023-04-13 (see HF 710).","mechanics":["Sec. 3 (Code 99E.6(2)): taxes on internet fantasy sports contests, paid by the contest service provider to the treasurer of state, go to the county endowment fund (15E.311) rather than the sports wagering receipts fund.","Sec. 4 (Code 99F.11(5)(b)): sports wagering taxes paid by licensed operators are distributed as follows: (1) $1.75M each fiscal year to the health and human services gambling treatment program (135.150); (2) $1M to the general fund for the Endow Iowa credit (15E.305); (3) 85% of the remainder to the county endowment fund; (4) the rest to the economic development authority, split equally among qualified sponsoring organizations licensed under chapter 99F.","Sec. 1: for the fiscal year starting July 1, 2023, unobligated sports wagering receipts fund balances move to the county endowment fund, and subsection 6 is repealed June 30, 2024. Sec. 2 removes the fund reference from 15E.311(3)(a).","Div. II (Secs. 5-6): $7M from the sports wagering receipts fund to the general fund for FY2022-23 for the 15E.305 Endow Iowa credit, effective on enactment.","Div. III (Secs. 7-9): Endow Iowa credit aggregate cap is $13M for tax year 2023 (up from $6M), retroactive to January 1, 2023. Div. IV (Secs. 10-11): the cap is $7M annually for tax years beginning on or after January 1, 2024.","Div. V (Secs. 12-15): the 2022 Iowa Acts ch. 1002 changes to the credit do not apply to endowment gifts made before January 1, 2023, retroactive to January 1, 2023."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"Unlike the listed bills from AK (HB145, SB194), HI (HB 1308, HB 2570, SB1569), GA, and DE (HB365), which authorize sports wagering and set tax rates, licensing and regulation, HF 673 only changes where existing tax revenue is directed. It contains no licensing, rate, or operator-conduct provisions. The closest listed analogue is IL HB2342, which addresses fantasy contest taxes, and IL HB2755, a general revenue bill. The unusual feature here is the funding waterfall, including earmarks for gambling treatment and a county endowment fund, and retroactive tax credit changes.","watch":"Operators would see no change to tax rates or compliance duties, but they should note that the bill diverts wagering and fantasy tax revenue away from the sports wagering receipts fund to earmarked uses, which could affect future budget and rate debates.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":14588,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:43:15.383Z"},"IA-HJR2004":{"summary":"Iowa HJR 2004 would nullify amendments to 491 Iowa Administrative Code chapters 13 and 14 adopted by the Iowa Racing and Gaming Commission and published as ARC 7634C in the Iowa Administrative Bulletin (vol. XLVI, no. 17, Feb. 21, 2024, pp. 7268-7272). Those amendments cover sports wagering and fantasy sports contests. If enacted, the resolution takes effect immediately and the amended rules would not take effect.","mechanics":["Section 1 nullifies only the amendments to 491 IAC chapters 13 and 14 shown in ARC 7634C. It does not touch the underlying statute or any earlier rule text.","The non-binding explanation says the rules add procedures and requirements for wagering and entry restrictions, account registration, advance deposit wagering, system testing, and service provider operating requirements.","Section 2 declares the resolution of immediate importance and makes it effective on enactment.","The resolution imposes no rates, penalties or new obligations. Its only effect is to block the specific rule amendments, so the rules in force before ARC 7634C stay in place.","Status: it passed one chamber, and the last action on 2024-03-11 was assignment to a subcommittee in the other chamber (Bousselot, Bisignano, Brown; S.J. 508)."],"issue_tags":["licensing","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. The bill is unusual because it is a legislative nullification of agency rules rather than a substantive regulatory or tax measure. It uses Iowa's joint-resolution procedure for overriding administrative rules and sets no policy of its own.","watch":"A lawyer representing operators should check what ARC 7634C changed in chapters 13 and 14, especially account registration, system testing and service provider requirements, and whether the pre-amendment rules leave compliance gaps or uncertainty if the nullification is enacted.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":1825,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:43:17.766Z"},"GA-SR579":{"summary":"SR 579 (House Higher Education Committee substitute) is a proposed constitutional amendment, not a regulatory statute. It would amend Art. I, Sec. II, Para. VIII of the Georgia Constitution to let the General Assembly provide by general law for the operation and regulation of sports betting, and would allow the Georgia Lottery Corporation to be authorized to participate. It sets a constitutional allocation of sports betting tax proceeds between responsible gaming and education, and it goes to voters for ratification.","mechanics":["Subpara. (a) is revised so that all forms of sports betting, along with lotteries, pari-mutuel betting and casino gambling, remain prohibited and enforced by penal laws except as the Paragraph provides. Pari-mutuel betting and casino gambling are not authorized.","New subpara. (d) lets the General Assembly provide by law for the operation and regulation of sports betting. Non-tax regulatory proceeds pay operating expenses and any legally provided operating reserves without appropriation, and the net goes to the general fund. The Governor's budget must show it as a separate category, 'Sports Betting Proceeds.'","New subpara. (e)(1) allocates sports betting tax proceeds each fiscal year: 15% of the first $150 million goes to the Responsible Gaming Fund, and 85% of the first $150 million plus all proceeds above $150 million go to the Educational Opportunity Fund. The Educational Opportunity Fund supports voluntary pre-K and the other education purposes in subpara. (c).","Subpara. (e)(2) authorizes the General Assembly to create and appropriate to both funds, and the money in them is exempt from the lapsing rule in Art. III, Sec. IX, Para. IV(c).","New subpara. (f) allows the Georgia Lottery Corporation to be authorized by law to participate in sports betting. Its proceeds are used under subpara. (c), meaning lottery operating expenses and education, and any tax on its participation is allocated under subpara. (e).","Section 2 submits the amendment under Art. X, Sec. I, Para. II with the ballot question 'Shall the Constitution of Georgia be amended so as to authorize sports betting in this state to provide additional funding for pre-kindergarten and HOPE scholarships?' The text contains no tax rate, license categories, mobile or retail distinction, operator criteria, or effective date for betting."],"issue_tags":["legalize","tax"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"The comparable bills listed are all statutory, so SR 579 differs in kind because it only creates constitutional authority and leaves implementation to later legislation. AK HB145 and SB194 (mobile sports wagering tax and criminal-history licensing checks), HI HB1308, HB2570 and SB1569 (licensing, excise tax), and DE HB365 (enabling framework under the Lottery Director) set up operating regimes directly. SR 579 sets only a fixed earmark of tax proceeds, with 15% of the first $150 million to responsible gaming. IL HB3159 (2% of adjusted gross receipts to fire and EMS) is the closest earmark comparison. Its Lottery Corporation participation clause echoes the lottery-run models in DE HB365 and IL HB3276.","watch":"Watch the enabling legislation that would follow ratification (rate, mobile versus retail, license count, and the Georgia Lottery Corporation's role or exclusivity), and note that the bill's status line is internally inconsistent: it says 'Signed into law,' but the last action shown is only a House committee substitute report, and a constitutional amendment still needs a vote of the electorate.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Comm Sub","text_chars":10720,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:43:22.350Z"},"IA-HSB228":{"summary":"Iowa HSB228 adds suitability screening to licensing for advance deposit sports wagering under Iowa Code chapter 99F. Before granting a license, the racing and gaming commission must investigate whether the applicant or any affiliate, including entities under common control, takes revenue from OFAC-sanctioned jurisdictions or takes online wagers from countries where online gambling is illegal. A finding of either activity bars licensure, and for an existing licensee it requires immediate revocation.","mechanics":["Sec. 1 adds new 99F.6(3A): the commission must conduct a comprehensive investigation of each applicant for an advance deposit sports wagering license before granting it.","Scope of review reaches the applicant and any affiliates, including entities under common control, so conduct by related companies can be attributed to the applicant.","Trigger (a): knowingly accepting revenue, directly or indirectly, derived from any jurisdiction sanctioned by the Treasury's Office of Foreign Assets Control. The knowledge qualifier applies only here.","Trigger (b): accepting or assisting, directly or indirectly, in accepting online wagers or related consideration from any country where such online gambling is prohibited or illegal. This has no express knowledge qualifier and covers assisting.","Sec. 2 adds new 99F.7(8)(h) making a determination that the applicant is doing either activity a ground for denial. The explanatory note says denial applies where the commission has substantial evidence, but the operative text in the bill does not state that standard.","Sec. 3 amends 99F.7(16): on a determination that an advance deposit sports wagering licensee is doing either activity, the commission shall immediately revoke the license. Revocation is mandatory, with no discretion, cure period or stated hearing process in the bill. No effective date or penalties are specified."],"issue_tags":["licensing","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"This bill is a narrow licensing-integrity amendment, unlike the broad enabling or tax bills listed, such as AK HB145/SB194, DE HB365, HI HB1308 and the Georgia sports betting bills. It sets no tax rate, advertising rule or market structure. It is closest in subject to the licensing-focused bills like IL HB2878 (license fees) and CT SB01235 (disciplinary authority and books and records), but none of those listed bills imposes an OFAC or foreign-market screen on applicants and their affiliates. The mandatory immediate revocation is also harsher than the discretionary discipline framing in the CT bill.","watch":"Watch the affiliate-wide, no-knowledge-required trigger in new 99F.6(3A)(b), together with mandatory immediate revocation, which could expose an Iowa licensee to loss of its license over a parent's or sister company's offshore or grey-market activity, and the lack of any defined standard of proof or cure process.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":4269,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:43:24.009Z"},"IA-HSB122":{"summary":"Iowa HSB 122 does not change the rates or licensing rules for sports wagering or internet fantasy sports. It redirects where the tax receipts go by abolishing the sports wagering receipts fund (repealed June 30, 2024) and sending the money to a gambling treatment appropriation, the Endow Iowa tax credit, a county endowment fund, and licensed qualified sponsoring organizations. It also raises the annual cap on Endow Iowa tax credits. The bill was renumbered as HF 673.","mechanics":["Sec. 4 (Code 99F.11(5)(b)) replaces the credit to the sports wagering receipts fund with a waterfall of sports wagering taxes: (1) $1.75M each fiscal year to the department of health and human services for the gambling treatment program under 135.150; (2) $1M to the general fund for the Endow Iowa credit under 15E.305; (3) 85% of the remainder to the county endowment fund under 15E.311; (4) the final remainder to the economic development authority for equal distribution to each qualified sponsoring organization licensed under chapter 99F.","Sec. 3 (Code 99E.6(2)) sends all internet fantasy sports contest taxes, paid by the service provider to the treasurer of state, to the county endowment fund under 15E.311 instead of the sports wagering receipts fund. The tax rate is untouched.","Sec. 1 appropriates any unobligated and unencumbered balance in the sports wagering receipts fund at the start of FY beginning July 1, 2023 to the county endowment fund, and repeals the fund's subsection (8.57(6)) on June 30, 2024. Sec. 2 strikes the 15E.311 references to that fund.","Division II, Sec. 5 appropriates $7M from the sports wagering receipts fund to the general fund for FY2023 (July 1, 2022 to June 30, 2023) for Endow Iowa credits, effective on enactment (Sec. 6).","Division III, Sec. 7 raises the Endow Iowa credit cap to $13M for tax year 2023, effective on enactment and retroactive to January 1, 2023 (Secs. 8-9). Division IV, Sec. 10 sets the annual cap at $7M (up from $6M) for tax years beginning on or after January 1, 2024 (Sec. 11).","The bill does not touch operator licensing, tax rates, definitions or compliance. Operators' tax liability is unchanged, and only the destination of the funds changes."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"Unlike most of the listed bills, such as AK HB145/SB194 (new mobile sports wagering tax), GA HB380/SB172 (new regulatory and taxation frameworks) or DE HB365 (enabling mobile wagering legislation), this bill legalizes nothing and sets no rate. It only reallocates revenue from Iowa's existing 99F and 99E taxes. The closest parallels are tax-focused bills like HI HB1308 and IL HB2342 (fantasy sports tax), but those create or set taxes, while this one only directs where the proceeds go. Its distinctive features are earmarking for county endowments, gambling treatment and racetrack-linked sponsoring organizations.","watch":"Operators face no change in tax liability, but the earmarks (a fixed $1.75M for gambling treatment, with most of the remainder going to county endowments) and the repeal of the receipts fund show where future pressure for rate or allocation changes could come from, and the bill moved on as HF 673.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":12766,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:43:25.343Z"},"IA-HSB723":{"summary":"This is a Department of Revenue omnibus housekeeping bill (renumbered HF 2710). Its sports-wagering content is narrow: it confirms that sports wagering winnings are Iowa earned income subject to state and federal income tax, and it sets a $5,000 state income tax withholding threshold for sports wagering winnings under chapter 99F. The remaining divisions cover record confidentiality in tax appeals, lottery couriers, mandatory electronic filing for cigarette and tobacco permits and returns, and alcohol regulator conflict-of-interest rules.","mechanics":["Sec. 3 amends Iowa Code 99F.18 by adding subsection 2: sports wagering winnings are Iowa earned income subject to state and federal income tax, and any amount withheld under 422.16(2) is remitted to the Department of Revenue on the winner's behalf.","Sec. 4 amends 422.16(2)(d) to require state income tax withholding on sports wagering winnings over $5,000. The existing thresholds are unchanged: $600 for chapters 99B and 99G, $1,000 for 99D, and $1,200 for 99F slot machines.","Sec. 5-9 (lottery) define 'lottery courier' as a person who procures tickets or shares from a retailer for another person without a 'bona fide social relationship'. Sec. 7 bars acting as a courier or doing business with one and bars retailers from selling to couriers or their employees or agents. Sec. 6 adds a ground to deny a lottery applicant if the structure or activities of its business are likely to violate chapter 99G. Sec. 8 broadens the ban on purchases and prizes to board members, department officers and employees, certain state staff with access to confidential information, and household family members. Sec. 9 makes ticket order history, inventory, and security-sensitive records confidential.","Division I (Sec. 1) lets the department submit the entire record under seal for judicial review under 17A.19, notwithstanding 422.20 and 422.72, while the record held by the department stays open to public inspection to the extent existing law allows. It takes effect on enactment.","Division IV (Sec. 10-41) requires cigarette and tobacco permit applications, bonds, fees, returns, remittances, and reports to be filed electronically, with a director-approved alternative method (new 453A.41 and 453A.52). It takes effect January 1, 2025, for documents due on or after that date.","Division V (Sec. 42-45) adds conflict-of-interest and political-activity prohibitions for alcoholic beverages commission members, the director, and department employees, and requires changes in ownership of retail alcohol licensees to be reported within 30 days."],"issue_tags":["tax","integrity","licensing"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"Unlike the dedicated sports wagering bills listed, such as AK HB145/SB194, GA HB380 and SB57, HI HB1308 and HB2570, or IL HB3159 and HB5143, this bill creates no licensing regime and imposes no operator tax or rate change. Its only sports-betting provision concerns player-level income tax withholding, which is closest in subject to the Illinois revenue bill IL HB2755 and the Kansas tax bill KS SB303 (which imposes a 2% excise tax on wagers). Its lottery courier ban is the most notable gaming-adjacent item, and no listed bill from another state contains a comparable provision.","watch":"Operators should confirm how the new $5,000 sports wagering withholding threshold in 422.16(2)(d) fits their payout, W-2G, and remittance processes in Iowa, and should note that the lottery courier ban in 99G does not reach sports wagering under chapter 99F.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":57261,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:43:28.100Z"},"IL-HB1405":{"summary":"IL HB1405 would add new Section 25-61 to the Sports Wagering Act, creating a capped exchange trade wagering license for peer-style trading of betting contracts on sports events. The Illinois Gaming Board could issue at most two licenses, taxed at the same rate as master sports wagering licensees under Section 25-90. The bill died at sine die on 2025-01-07 without action beyond introduction.","mechanics":["Definition (25-61(a)): 'exchange trade wagering' is the buying and selling of betting contracts at any time before an event concludes, priced on a describable 0 to 100 probability scale, using recognized US financial-market surveillance technology able to flag problem gambling, money laundering and integrity threats.","Cap (25-61(b)): the Board may issue up to 2 exchange trade wagering licenses.","Eligibility (25-61(b)): applicant must be at least 21, have 'no criminal background', and show the skill required to operate sports wagering; all plans submitted to the Board become conditions of licensure.","Fees and term (25-61(c)): $500,000 initial fee, 4-year term, renewal for 4 years at $100,000.","Tax (25-61(e)): same taxes as a master sports wagering licensee under Section 25-90; no separate rate is stated.","Scope and status (25-61(d),(f); Sec. 99): license is not a property right and is limited to exchange trade wagering, with no other gambling permitted; effective on becoming law, but the bill is dead."],"issue_tags":["licensing","tax","integrity","legalize"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"None of the listed bills creates an exchange or contract-trading license. AK HB145/SB194, DE HB365, HI HB1308/HB2570/SB1569 and the Georgia bills (e.g., GA SB57, HB380) set up conventional sportsbook licensing under a regulator or lottery, whereas IL HB1405 is a narrow add-on to an existing licensed market with a two-license cap. Its tax-parity approach (cross-reference to Section 25-90) and its mandated financial-market surveillance technology are unusual, as is the lack of any detail on how exchange contracts are settled or how revenue is measured.","watch":"Operators should note that the bill never defines how taxable adjusted gross receipts are computed for an exchange model (where the licensee may earn fees rather than bet outcomes) and that the vague 'no criminal background' and 'skill' standards and the two-license cap would leave wide Board discretion if the bill were reintroduced.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":3548,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:43:33.097Z"},"IA-SSB3179":{"summary":"Iowa Department of Revenue omnibus study bill (later renumbered SF 2427) covering records confidentiality, lottery, tobacco and alcohol administration. Its sports-wagering content is narrow: it states that sports wagering winnings under chapter 99F are Iowa earned income and sets a state income tax withholding threshold for them. It also adds lottery courier prohibitions and mandatory electronic filing for tobacco permits and returns.","mechanics":["Div. II, Sec. 3 (amending 99F.18): new subsection 2 says all winnings from sports wagering authorized under chapter 99F are Iowa earned income subject to state and federal income tax, and any amount withheld under 422.16(2) is remitted to the Department of Revenue on the winner's behalf.","Div. II, Sec. 4 (amending 422.16(2)(d)): state income tax must be withheld on sports wagering winnings over $5,000. The existing thresholds are $600 (chapters 99B and 99G), $1,000 (99D) and $1,200 (slot machines under 99F).","Div. III, Secs. 5-7: defines 'lottery courier' (a person who procures lottery tickets or shares from a retailer for another person without a 'bona fide social relationship'). It bars acting as or doing business with a courier, and bars retailers from selling to couriers or their employees or agents.","Div. III, Sec. 6 adds a ground for denying a lottery retailer application: the department finds, from available information, that the applicant's structure or activities are likely to violate chapter 99G or division rules. Sec. 9 makes ticket order history, ticket inventory and records whose disclosure could impair lottery security or integrity confidential.","Div. III, Sec. 8 replaces 99G.31(3)(g) to bar ticket purchases and prize payments to board members, department officers and employees, certain state personnel with access to confidential information, and their same-household spouses, children, siblings and parents.","Div. IV, Secs. 10-41: requires electronic filing of chapter 453A cigarette and tobacco permit applications, bonds, fees, reports and returns, with an alternative method on director approval. Effective January 1, 2025. Div. I (sealed record for judicial review) takes effect on enactment.","Div. V, Secs. 42-45: conflict-of-interest and prohibited-activity rules for alcohol commission members, the director and department employees, and a 30-day reporting requirement for changes in ownership of retail alcohol licensees."],"issue_tags":["tax","integrity"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"Nothing comparable is listed. The other states' bills (e.g., AK HB145/SB194, GA SB57, IL HB3159, KS SB303) authorize or tax sports wagering, set wagering tax rates or legalize new products, whereas Iowa's SSB3179 only sets a withholding threshold on bettor winnings in an existing market. Iowa's $5,000 sports wagering withholding trigger sits above its $1,200 slot threshold. The lottery courier ban is an unusual add-on that resembles courier restrictions seen in other lottery-focused measures, such as IN HB1432's lottery provisions, though that bill takes a different approach.","watch":"Confirm how operators must implement the new $5,000 sports wagering withholding and remittance obligation under 422.16(2)(d), and whether SF 2427 as renumbered changes the threshold or timing.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":57263,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:43:34.458Z"},"IL-HB1566":{"summary":"Public Act 103-0129 amends Section 6z-27 of the Illinois State Finance Act, which governs the Audit Expense Fund. The bill refreshes the schedule of dollar amounts that the Comptroller and Treasurer must transfer from dozens of State funds to pay for Auditor General audits under the State Auditing Act. It does not regulate gaming, but the schedules include small line items for the Sports Wagering Fund, State Gaming Fund and State Lottery Fund.","mechanics":["Within 30 days after July 1, 2022 (or as soon as practical), the Comptroller must order and the Treasurer must make transfers from the listed funds into the Audit Expense Fund. The amounts are specified per fund.","Gaming-related line items in the first schedule: Sports Wagering $4,450; State Gaming $94,435; State Lottery $340,323. The second schedule lists Sports Wagering Fund $8,572; State Gaming Fund $204,660; State Lottery Fund $129,814; Horse Racing Fund $169,632; Illinois Gaming Law Enforcement Fund $1,869.","The provisions do not apply to federal or State trust funds, except that the Auditor General notifies trustees of the estimated audit cost and the trustees direct the transfer, unless the trust agreement prohibits it.","If a listed fund cannot lawfully transfer its amount, the shortfall is covered by transfer from the General Revenue Fund.","The Auditor General may bill entities outside the transfer provisions, including private entities, related organizations and locally-held funds, for audit, study and investigation costs, with revenue deposited into the Audit Expense Fund.","Any excess transferred over actual audit costs (other than from General Revenue) must be returned on or about September 30. The Act takes effect on becoming law (Section 99)."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. This is a routine fiscal housekeeping measure that updates the Audit Expense Fund transfer schedules. Sports Wagering Fund, State Gaming Fund and State Lottery Fund appear only as line items among well over a hundred funds, and the bill has no sports-wagering policy content.","watch":"Little to act on for operators: the only point of note is that the Sports Wagering Fund and State Gaming Fund bear small audit-cost transfers ($4,450/$8,572 and $94,435/$204,660), with no change to tax rates, licensing or operator obligations.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":22504,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:43:34.585Z"},"IA-SF2427":{"summary":"Omnibus Department of Revenue bill. For sports betting, it makes sports wagering winnings Iowa earned income subject to state and federal income tax, and requires state withholding wherever federal withholding applies. It also bans lottery couriers, adds lottery integrity and confidentiality rules, moves cigarette and tobacco permit and report filings online, and tightens ethics rules for alcohol regulators.","mechanics":["Sec. 1 adds subsection 2 to Iowa Code 99F.18: winnings from sports wagering authorized under chapter 99F are Iowa earned income, subject to state and federal income tax, and amounts withheld under 422.16(2) are remitted to the Department of Revenue on the winner's behalf.","Sec. 2 amends 422.16(2)(d): state income tax is withheld on chapter 99F sports wagering winnings whenever federal income tax must be withheld from the same winnings under the Internal Revenue Code. This avoids a separate dollar threshold. The existing thresholds stay at $600 (99B/99G), $1,000 (99D) and $1,200 (99F slots).","Secs. 3 and 5 define 'lottery courier' (someone who procures lottery tickets or shares from a retailer for another person without a 'bona fide social relationship'). They bar anyone from acting as or doing business with a courier, and bar retailers from knowingly selling to a courier or its employees or agents.","Sec. 4 adds a ground to deny a lottery retailer applicant: the department finds, from available information, that the structure or activities of the applicant's business are likely to violate chapter 99G or division rules, policies or procedures. Sec. 6 bars ticket purchases by, and prize payments to, board members, department officers and employees, certain state staff with confidential access, and their resident household family. Sec. 7 makes ticket order history, inventory and security-sensitive records confidential.","Secs. 8-39 require cigarette and tobacco permit applications, bonds, fees, returns, reports and remittances to be filed electronically, with an alternative method on director approval. This division takes effect January 1, 2025 (Sec. 39). Secs. 40 onward amend alcohol commission and director conflict-of-interest and political-activity rules (Secs. 41-43) and canned cocktail definitions; the text provided is cut off partway through Division IV.","The visible text of Secs. 1-2 contains no separate effective date. Only Division III has one (Sec. 39)."],"issue_tags":["tax","integrity","licensing"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"The bill does not legalize sports betting, set an operator tax rate or change licensing, unlike AK HB145/SB194, the GA bills (HB380, HB686, SB57, SB172, SB386) and HI HB1308/HB2570. It is closer to the omnibus revenue bills IL HB2755, KY HB757 and IN SB0394, and its only sports wagering content is the player-level withholding. By contrast, KS SB303 (2% excise on all sports wagers) and IL HB5143 (ending the per-wager tax) change operator-level taxation, which SF 2427 does not. The lottery courier ban is unusual; IN HB1432, which authorizes the lottery commission to sell draw games and eInstant games over the Internet, is the nearest listed bill on lottery matters.","watch":"Confirm how the Sec. 2 withholding trigger, tied to federal withholding under the Internal Revenue Code, will be implemented and enforced against licensed operators, and whether the broad 'lottery courier' definition and the 'likely to violate' denial ground in Sec. 4 could reach operator-affiliated ticket or share-purchasing services.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":52763,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:43:39.677Z"},"IL-HB2204":{"summary":"Despite its title (DHS-Gambling Disorders), the enacted text of Public Act 103-0268 is the Hydrogen Fuel Replacement Tax Credit Act. It creates an Illinois income tax credit for the in-state use of 'qualifying hydrogen' (hydrogen that earns 100% of the federal 26 U.S.C. 45V credit and meets the Act's attestation rules) and adds Section 240 to the Illinois Income Tax Act. Nothing in the text addresses gambling, gaming, sports wagering, or gambling disorders.","mechanics":["Credit of $1 per kg of eligible qualifying hydrogen used in the preceding calendar year, for tax years ending on or after Dec. 31, 2027 and beginning before Jan. 1, 2029 (Sec. 15(a)); an added $0.15 per kg applies for uses impacting equity investment eligible communities if workforce-equity and 40% community-benefit tests are met (Sec. 15(b)).","Statewide cap of $10,000,000 in credits per calendar year, plus unallocated amounts from the prior year (Sec. 20(a)); no more than 10% of credits may go to hydrogen used for direct-gas-combustion electricity generation (Sec. 25(c)); allocations cannot be rolled forward (Sec. 25(e)).","Excluded uses: combustion-engine vehicles and GVWR classes 1-6 vehicles, residential and commercial heating and cooking, and direct-combustion electricity generation unless needed for EPA State Implementation Plan compliance (Sec. 10).","Producer attestation under penalty of perjury is required, including that the hydrogen was not produced during a PJM performance assessment interval or MISO maximum generation event, together with hourly power-source and emissions-intensity documentation (Sec. 27).","Labor conditions: project labor agreements for conversion and installation work and labor peace agreements with bona fide labor organizations (Sec. 15(e), (f)). Priority of allocation goes up to 90% to DOE Hydrogen Hub participants and their suppliers or customers (Sec. 30).","Unused credits carry forward 5 years and may be transferred within one year of award (Sec. 35; 35 ILCS 5/240). The Illinois EPA must publish an emissions and economic impact report by April 1, 2028 (Sec. 36). The Act is effective on becoming law (Sec. 999)."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"This enacted bill has no gambling content, so it is not comparable to the listed sports-wagering tax and licensing bills such as AK HB145, GA SB57, HI HB 1308, KS SB303 (2% excise tax on wagers) or LA HB 639 (online sports wagering tax). The only overlap is that it is a tax-credit statute. The mismatch between the title, 'DHS-Gambling Disorders', and the chaptered text appears to be a shell-bill artifact, in which a bill was amended to carry unrelated subject matter.","watch":"Nothing in this Act affects sports-betting or iGaming operators, but confirm that the bill tracker's title and status reflect the hydrogen credit and not a problem-gambling measure, and do not rely on this Public Act for any Illinois gaming compliance purpose.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":27120,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:43:42.834Z"},"IL-HB1603":{"summary":"IL HB1603 would expand the Criminal Code's definition of \"gambling device\" in 720 ILCS 5/28-2(a) with a new clause (iii). Clause (iii) covers any vending or other electronic machine that offers entry into a chance-based contest, sweepstakes or similar selection process for a prize when the offer is incidental to or results from a purchase or from receipt of a coupon or credit. This targets \"no-purchase-necessary\" sweepstakes and skill-style terminals. The bill also makes gambling offenses involving such devices Class 4 felonies and removes a game-device exception from the Video Gaming Act. It died at sine die on 2025-01-07.","mechanics":["New 720 ILCS 5/28-2(a)(iii): a \"gambling device\" includes any vending or other electronic machine or device, including one that awards credits and has a circuit, meter or switch able to remove and record removal of credits, that offers entry into a chance-based contest, competition, sweepstakes, scheme, plan or other selection process for a gift, award or item or service of value.","Trigger: the offer must be incidental to or result from (A) the purchase of an item or service of value, or (B) the purchase or gratuitous receipt of a coupon, voucher, certificate or similar credit redeemable for or applicable toward an item or service of value from the machine or elsewhere. The gratuitous-receipt language reaches free-entry or alternate-method-of-entry models.","New 28-2(d): states the intent that (a)(iii) bars any mechanism that tries to avoid gambling-device status through any \"subterfuge or pretense whatsoever.\"","28-1(b)(13): the no-payment-required games-of-skill-or-chance exemption no longer applies where participation is through a device prohibited by 28-2(a)(iii). New 28-1(c)(2): a gambling offense involving an (a)(iii) device is a Class 4 felony, notwithstanding the general Class A misdemeanor rule for a first offense.","Video Gaming Act, 230 ILCS 40/35(a): the bill strikes the sentence permitting use of a game device in an activity that is not gambling under 28-1(b). Existing penalties remain: Class 4 felony, public-nuisance declaration, and seizure and destruction under 28-5.","Effective immediately on becoming law (Section 99). Existing exclusions in 28-2(a)(1)-(5) (amusement devices, crane games, redemption machines, licensed video gaming terminals) are unchanged, and the bill does not exempt sports wagering or iGaming platforms."],"issue_tags":["sweeps","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Closest in subject is CA AB 831, which also addresses contests and sweepstakes operated through electronic video monitors, but CA works through the unfair-practices and contest and sweepstakes statute. IL HB1603 instead uses criminal law, widening the gambling-device definition and imposing a Class 4 felony. The other listed bills (e.g., AK HB145, GA HB380, HI HB 1308, CT SB01464) concern licensing, taxation or advertising of legal sports wagering and igaming and have no comparable device-based sweepstakes ban. HB1603 does not touch Illinois's Sports Wagering Act, which 28-1(b)(15) already exempts.","watch":"Operators and collectibles or trading-card companies running sweepstakes, promotional free-entry games or mystery-pack and break offers through any electronic machine or device should check whether the broad \"vending or other electronic machine or device\" language, the gratuitous-coupon trigger and the anti-subterfuge clause, backed by Class 4 felony exposure, could reach digital or kiosk-based promotions.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":21160,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:43:43.773Z"},"IL-HB2239":{"summary":"HB2239 would create the Illinois Internet Gaming Act, legalizing online casino games (slots, poker, table games, live-dealer) under the Illinois Gaming Board. Only existing owners licensees and organization (racetrack) licensees could hold an Internet gaming license, each with up to 3 skins and up to 3 contracted management service providers. Operators would pay a 15% privilege tax on adjusted gross gaming revenue, with Gaming Board emergency rulemaking and an express carve-out for sports wagering and fantasy contests. The bill died at sine die on 2025-01-07.","mechanics":["Section 20(b): Internet gaming is offered only by an owners or organization licensee holding an Internet gaming license, or by an Internet management service provider (IMSP) contracted to one. Each licensee may offer no more than 3 individually branded skins and contract with up to 3 IMSPs.","Section 45: 15% privilege tax on adjusted gross gaming revenue, which excludes voided wagers, free play, promotional credits and non-cashable vouchers. It is the exclusive tax, local taxes including home rule are barred, and monthly negative AGR carries forward up to 12 months. Revenue goes to the State Gaming Fund and is distributed under Section 13 of the Illinois Gambling Act.","Section 35: Fees are $250,000 for an Internet gaming license ($100,000 renewal), $100,000 for an IMSP license ($50,000 if already licensed elsewhere in Illinois; $50,000 renewal), and $75,000 for a supplier license ($50,000 for existing Illinois licensees). Occupational licenses are $150. Licenses are issued on payment of the fee plus proof of an equivalent Illinois sports wagering license or a comparable license from another U.S. state. Temporary one-year licenses are issued within 30 days to such applicants.","Section 35(i): The Board may not issue or renew an Internet gaming license for a licensee that has cut its workforce by 25% or more (since Feb. 28, 2020 for issuance, or since the last license issuance or renewal for renewal).","Section 20(c): Primary servers may be anywhere in the U.S. for one year after the effective date, then must be located in Illinois. Section 25: 21+ age and geolocation checks are required. Section 40: a statewide self-exclusion database and daily, weekly and monthly deposit and spend limits are required. Section 30: one account per operator, a single account may be shared with sports wagering, and deposits may be made by cards, ACH, cash, prepaid cards, and digital, crypto and virtual currencies.","Sections 15, 60 and 95: The Board must adopt emergency rules within 90 days and use existing Gambling Act rules and other states' internet gaming rules where possible. Out-of-state wagers and multijurisdictional poker compacts are permitted. Section 50 imposes annual supplier diversity reporting, and the act takes effect on becoming law (Section 99)."],"issue_tags":["legalize","tax","licensing","advertising"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Of the other states' bills listed, none legalizes online casino games the way HB2239 does. The nearest is AR HB1861 (Interactive Gaming Act), which pairs regulation of internet gaming with felony penalties for illegal online casinos and sports betting. CT SB01464 also addresses multijurisdictional internet gaming for peer-to-peer casino games, comparable to HB2239 Section 60's reciprocal agreements. The remaining bills (AK HB145/SB194, GA HB380, HI HB1308, DE HB365) cover sports wagering. HB2239 differs by restricting licensure to incumbent casino and racetrack owners, with a 15% tax, a 3-skin cap, a workforce-reduction bar, and a requirement that servers be moved in-state after one year.","watch":"Watch the incumbent-only licensing (market access for non-casino operators only as IMSPs under up to 3 contracts per licensee), the 3-skin cap, the in-state server mandate after year one, and the permissive payment methods, including crypto and virtual currency, against the light-touch advertising rules (no preapproval, 2-year sample retention).","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":37051,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:43:46.182Z"},"IL-HB2479":{"summary":"HB2479 amends the Illinois Lottery Law to require the Department of the Lottery to offer a special instant scratch-off game benefiting the Juvenile Diabetes Research Foundation, Illinois Chapter. Net revenue goes into a new Juvenile Diabetes Research Foundation Fund, which the Department of Public Health administers and passes through entirely as grants to the Foundation. The bill is a charitable-lottery earmark and does not touch sports wagering, iGaming, or other gaming. The bill died at sine die on 2025-01-07.","mechanics":["New 20 ILCS 1605/21.15(a): the Department 'shall offer' a special instant scratch-off game, to commence January 1, 2024 or as soon after as the Director finds reasonably practical; Section 21.15 controls over any inconsistent provision of the Lottery Law.","Section 21.15(b): net revenue is deposited into the Juvenile Diabetes Research Foundation Fund. 'Net revenue' is total ticket sales less prizes paid and the Department's actual administrative expenses solely related to this game. Gifts, grants, and awards from public or private entities are also deposited into the Fund.","Section 21.15(c): while the game is on sale, the Department must not unreasonably diminish marketing efforts for other instant scratch-off games.","Section 21.15(d): the Department may adopt rules to implement the Section.","New 20 ILCS 2305/8.5: the Department of Public Health administers the Fund and passes all deposited moneys as grants to the Foundation for (i) research, treatment, and awareness of diabetes and (ii) improved diabetes self-management and training, as defined in Section 356w(b) of the Illinois Insurance Code.","Conforming changes: Sections 9.3 and 20 of the Lottery Law carve Section 21.15 out of the standard proceeds waterfall, and new State Finance Act Section 5.990 creates the Fund. The carve-out from Section 9.3 sits alongside the existing Section 25-70 Sports Wagering Act carve-out."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. On its own, the bill is a routine charitable-cause scratch-off earmark, one of a series of Illinois Lottery Law Sections (21.5 to 21.13) with the same structure. It imposes no tax, licensing, or advertising rules on sports-betting operators. The only sports wagering connection is that the amended Sections 9.3 and 20 already cross-reference Section 25-70 of the Sports Wagering Act.","watch":"Nothing substantive for sports-betting operators, though the bill is dead; the only point to track is the amended Section 9.3 and 20 waterfall language, which sits next to the Section 25-70 sports wagering proceeds carve-out.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":9883,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:43:48.592Z"},"IL-HB2754":{"summary":"IL HB2754 makes a single change to the Illinois Gambling Act's definition of \"occupational license\" in 230 ILCS 10/4. It replaces the current scope (riverboat gambling, casino gambling, or gaming under an organization gaming license) with \"the administration, control, and conduct of gambling games and sports wagering.\" The effect is to tie occupational licensing expressly to sports wagering. The bill is dead, having failed on sine die adjournment on 2025-01-07.","mechanics":["Sole amendment is to the \"occupational license\" definition in Section 4 of the Illinois Gambling Act (230 ILCS 10/4); no other section is amended.","An occupational license remains a Board-issued license to a person or entity to perform an occupation the Board has identified as requiring a license.","New scope language: \"administration, control, and conduct of gambling games and sports wagering,\" replacing the references to riverboat gambling, casino gambling, and organization gaming licenses.","The bill sets no fees, tax rates, thresholds, or penalties, and has no stated effective date or enforcement provisions.","The bill does not amend the definitions of \"gambling game,\" \"gambling operation,\" or \"gross receipts,\" and does not define \"sports wagering,\" so the new wording depends on definitions elsewhere in Illinois law.","Status: failed; session sine die on 2025-01-07."],"issue_tags":["licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"This is a narrow definitional fix, unlike the broader licensing or regulatory bills listed. HI HB 1308, HI HB 2570 and HI SB1569 create full licensing regimes for sports wagering operators and suppliers, and AK HB145 and AK SB194 add criminal history checks for mobile sports wagering licensure. CO SB 163 addresses delegation of licensing duties to the division of gaming, and CT SB01237 touches occupational licenses, but both operate on specific licensing procedures. IL HB2754 does not create new license categories, fees or procedures. It only widens the statutory definition so that occupational licensing clearly covers sports wagering personnel.","watch":"Watch for whether the Board reads the broader definition as authorizing new occupational licensing requirements for sports wagering staff, and whether any successor bill pairs it with fees or background-check mandates, though this bill is dead.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":6527,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:43:50.690Z"},"IL-HB2497":{"summary":"HB2497 amends Section 5-45 of the Illinois State Officials and Employees Ethics Act (revolving door). Per the synopsis, it lets officers, members and State employees covered by the strict subsection (h) bar ask the appropriate Inspector General whether a non-State job they are considering or have been offered is barred. The Inspector General must answer in writing within 10 calendar days, or the person is deemed eligible. The bill died at sine die adjournment on 2025-01-07.","mechanics":["New subsection (h-5): an officer, member or State employee covered by subsection (h) who is considering or offered non-State employment, during State service or within one year after, may ask the appropriate Inspector General for a determination under subsection (h).","The Inspector General must decide within 10 calendar days of the request. The determination must be written, signed, dated and delivered to the subject within that period, or the person is deemed eligible for the job.","Subsection (h) bars covered persons for one year after leaving from taking jobs with, or fees from, a party to State contracts of $25,000 or more with their agency, or the subject of a regulatory or licensing decision by their agency. It applies regardless of personal and substantial participation. Covered persons include Illinois Racing Board employees (h)(7) and Illinois Gaming Board employees (h)(8).","The text as provided carries no strike/underline markup, so the exact new words cannot be isolated. Subsection (f) already has the same 10-day, deemed-eligible mechanism for employees outside (h), and subsections (a) through (g) and (i) through (j) appear unchanged.","Existing subsection (a-5), restated but apparently not changed, bars officers, members and Gaming Board or Racing Board employees, and their immediate family members living with them, from holding non-passive ownership in licenses under the Illinois Gambling Act, Video Gaming Act, Horse Racing Act or Sports Wagering Act during service and for 2 years after.","No new penalties, rates or effective date are stated beyond the ordinary session effective date."],"issue_tags":["integrity","licensing"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"No other states' bills are listed for comparison. HB2497 is an ethics-process bill, not a gaming bill, and its only gaming link is that the section it amends already covers Illinois Gaming Board and Racing Board staff (subsections (a-5) and (h)(7)-(8)). The proposed change is procedural: it adds an advance-clearance request with a 10-day deemed-eligible default and does not change substantive restrictions on gaming-license ownership or post-employment.","watch":"Although the bill is dead, a lawyer for sports-betting operators should note that Gaming Board employees are covered by the one-year bar in (h)(8) and that the 10-day silence-equals-eligibility default would affect operators hiring former regulators if it is reintroduced, so operators should insist on a written determination and keep records of it.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":16070,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:43:57.461Z"},"IL-HB307":{"summary":"Illinois HB0307 (P.A. 103-0724) amends the Student-Athlete Endorsement Rights Act, the state's college NIL law, to loosen restrictions on schools, conferences and boosters, and adds a FOIA exemption for school records on NIL deals. It keeps the existing bar on student-athletes endorsing gambling and sports betting. The bill is mainly an education/NIL measure; the gambling link is the retained endorsement ban in Sec. 20(i).","mechanics":["Sec. 20(i), unchanged by this bill, bars a student-athlete from entering a publicity rights agreement or taking compensation from a third party licensee for endorsing or promoting 'gambling, sports betting', along with controlled substances, cannabis, tobacco, alcohol, nicotine products, supplements and adult entertainment, or anything inconsistent with the school's values or reputation.","Sec. 15(e) is rewritten so that, if allowed by a court order, settlement agreement, athletic association or conference, or school policy, a school, association or conference may directly or indirectly enter into publicity rights agreements with, or pay NIL compensation to, prospective or current student-athletes. Previously this was flatly prohibited.","Sec. 20(e) (the ban on boosters and third party licensees giving inducements to enroll, and on conditioning NIL pay on athletic performance at a school) and Sec. 20(h) (no NIL deals before enrollment) are blanked out. Sec. 20(g) now says a school 'may' provide compensation or enter publicity rights agreements, and no school is required to facilitate deals.","Sec. 10(3), the express no-employee-status clause, is blanked and replaced by Sec. 10(4), which says the Act may not be interpreted to treat a student-athlete as an employee, agent or independent contractor of an association, conference or school.","New Sec. 40 exempts school-held information on the terms of existing or proposed student-athlete publicity rights agreements from FOIA, with a conforming new item (nnn) in 5 ILCS 140/7.5, set out in both versions of the section.","New Sec. 45 lets schools give intangible, de minimis or nonmonetary benefits, such as priority status, to those who fund an outside entity that mainly supports creating and facilitating student-athlete publicity rights agreements. The definition of 'Compensation' and the $500 written-contract threshold in Sec. 20(c) remain."],"issue_tags":["integrity"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"Unlike the other states' listed bills, which regulate wagering directly, this bill is a pure college NIL measure. The comparison set is split. The NIL bills are GA HB1276 and HB383, HI HB 2384 and SB3263, KY SB3, LA HB 513, MS SB2517, NE LB370, NH HB312, SC H4957 and S0993, MI HB4816 and DE HB314; Illinois sits with the deregulatory ones, widening school and booster involvement in NIL rather than restricting it. The wagering bills, such as NJ A4905 and S3080, NC H828 and H967, MD HB465 and HB518, NE LB168 and TN SB1662, restrict college prop bets, protect athletes or fund athletics from wagering revenue, and Illinois does none of that; its only gambling content is the existing endorsement ban in Sec. 20(i).","watch":"Operators should note that Sec. 20(i) still bars student-athlete endorsement deals for gambling and sports betting, so any college-athlete NIL or influencer marketing in Illinois remains off limits, and the new FOIA exemption in Sec. 40 will limit public visibility into school-held NIL agreements.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":41234,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:43:57.486Z"},"IL-HB3234":{"summary":"IL HB3234 amends the Illinois Lottery Law to create a single \"joint special instant scratch-off game\" (new Sec. 21.4) that funds all of the Lottery's cause-based scratch-off games, and adds two new causes, United Negro College Fund Illinois (Sec. 21.15) and Illinois DREAM (Sec. 21.16). It repeals Sec. 21.12 (school STEAM scratch-off) and makes conforming changes to the State Finance Act and the Higher Education Student Assistance Act. It is a state-run lottery measure with no effect on sports wagering, iGaming or private-operator licensing. The bill died at sine die on 2025-01-07.","mechanics":["New Sec. 21.4(a): the Department of the Lottery must offer one joint special instant scratch-off game benefiting the causes in Secs. 21.5-21.11, 21.13, 21.15 and 21.16, starting January 1, 2024 or as soon after as the Director finds reasonably practical; Sec. 21.4 controls over inconsistent provisions of the Act.","Sec. 21.4(c)-(d): new specialty tickets and causes must be funded through the joint game, and those added after February 1, 2024 must have a sunset date. The Department may support no more than 10 causes at once. Net revenue is split equally among the causes and transferred to each cause's special fund.","Sec. 21.4(d) defines \"net revenue\" as total ticket sales less prizes, retailer payments, and direct and estimated administrative expenses.","Sec. 21.15 creates the UNCF Scholarship Fund (a State treasury special fund, also added to the State Finance Act as Sec. 5.990). Money is appropriated solely to the Student Assistance Commission for UNCF Illinois scholarships, with no administrative costs for the United Negro College Fund, Inc.","Sec. 21.16 creates the Illinois DREAM scratch-off and the Illinois DREAM Fund (State Finance Act Sec. 5.991) for scholarships and the DREAM Fund Commission's duties. Sec. 67 of the Higher Education Student Assistance Act is amended so that the Fund is financed by private contributions, gifts, grants, awards and the Sec. 21.16 scratch-off proceeds.","Secs. 9.3 and 20 are amended so these funds sit outside the standard Common School Fund distribution waterfall. Sec. 21.12 is repealed. The bill takes effect on becoming law (Sec. 99)."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"This bill is a charitable-cause lottery bill and does not overlap with the listed sports betting, iGaming, tax or advertising bills. The nearest listed items are Connecticut lottery bills such as CT SB01235, CT SB01237 and CT SB01463, which deal with lottery administration, but none of them concerns earmarking scratch-off revenue to specific causes. Its only link to wagering is a cross-reference in Sec. 9.3 and Sec. 20 to Section 25-70 of the Illinois Sports Wagering Act, left unchanged. Unusually, it consolidates many cause-based games into one pooled game that is split equally and capped at 10 causes.","watch":"Little to watch: the bill is dead and does not alter the Sports Wagering Act, beyond preserving the existing Sec. 25-70 carve-out references in Secs. 9.3 and 20 of the Lottery Law.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":42596,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:43:58.381Z"},"IL-HB3817":{"summary":"Public Act 103-0008 is the FY2024 Budget Implementation Act. In the text provided it makes State-government housekeeping changes: it authorizes legislative participation in the Council of State Governments, sets community college retiree health contribution rates, funds the Illinois Higher Education Savings Program, and creates a Loan Guarantee Administrative Trust Fund. The text is cut off partway through Section 405-293 of the Department of Central Management Services Law (Section 5-20), and nothing in the portion provided deals with sports betting, iGaming, or collectibles.","mechanics":["Art. 3 (Council of State Governments Act): legislative leadership and designated committee members may attend CSG meetings and pay the annual membership fee.","Sec. 5-5 (5 ILCS 375/6.10): community college health insurance contribution rates for employees and employers rise from 0.50% to 0.75% of salary for July 1, 2023 to June 30, 2024. From July 1, 2024 to June 30, 2026 CMS sets the rate, capped at a 0.1 percentage point increase per year. After July 1, 2026 the cap is 105% of the prior year's rate.","Sec. 5-15 (15 ILCS 505/16.8): the Illinois Higher Education Savings Program gives each eligible child (born or adopted after 12/31/2022 to an Illinois-resident parent) a $50 seed deposit. Annual transfers of $2.5 million, or the appropriated amount if greater, go from the General Revenue Fund starting July 15, 2023. Seed funds are forfeited if unclaimed by the child's 10th birthday or unused by age 26.","Sec. 5-16 (15 ILCS 516/30-35, 30-36): the Treasurer may allocate up to $10 million of investment earnings per year to the Community Development Loan Guarantee Program, with no more than $50 million of loans guaranteed at any time. The bill creates the nonappropriated Loan Guarantee Administrative Trust Fund.","Sec. 5-17 (20 ILCS 301/5-10): amends the Substance Use Disorder Act to add a Maintenance of Effort calculation role with HFS, an Opioid Remediation Services Capital Investment Grant Program funded from the Illinois Opioid Remediation State Trust Fund, and an Office of Opioid Settlement Administration within DHS.","Sec. 5-20: amends 20 ILCS 405/405-293 (CMS Professional Services), but the text breaks off mid-section, so later articles and sections cannot be assessed."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. On its own, this is a general budget implementation act, and nothing in the text provided addresses wagering, gaming taxes, licensing, or collectibles. It is therefore not comparable to a dedicated sports-betting or iGaming bill.","watch":"Because the supplied text is truncated at Section 5-20, check the remaining articles of Public Act 103-0008 for any amendments to the Sports Wagering Act, the Illinois Gambling Act, or gaming fund transfers before treating this act as irrelevant to operators.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":1257086,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:44:02.009Z"},"IL-HB400":{"summary":"IL HB0400 is a shell bill that amends Section 25-1 (short title) of the Illinois Sports Wagering Act, 230 ILCS 45/25-1, with a purely technical change. It adds a stray \"the\" in the text and makes no substantive change to licensing, taxation, or operations. The bill died at sine die adjournment on 2025-01-07.","mechanics":["Amends only 230 ILCS 45/25-1, the short-title section of the Sports Wagering Act (Article 25 of 230 ILCS 45).","The change is a technical edit to the wording of the short title. The text still says the Article may be cited as the Sports Wagering Act and that references to \"this Act\" mean this Article.","No tax rates, license categories, thresholds, definitions, or enforcement provisions are created or changed.","No effective date is specified beyond Illinois defaults. The bill is dead, so nothing takes effect.","Introduced by Rep. Emanuel Chris Welch. Such shell bills are often kept as vehicles for later substantive amendments."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike the other states' bills listed, such as GA SB57 and HB380, HI HB1308, AK HB145, DE HB365, and AR HB1861, which set up or revise licensing, tax, or integrity frameworks, IL HB0400 contains no substantive policy. It is closest to a placeholder, and none of the listed bills is a comparable shell. The only unusual feature is that it targets an existing, mature regime, the Illinois Sports Wagering Act (P.A. 101-31), rather than creating a new one.","watch":"Nothing substantive to watch in this dead bill, but a lawyer should note that Illinois shell bills like this are often reused as vehicles for later gut-and-replace amendments to the Sports Wagering Act, for example on tax rates or player-prop rules.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":763,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:44:03.428Z"},"IL-HB403":{"summary":"IL HB0403 is a shell bill that amends Section 25-1 (short title) of the Illinois Sports Wagering Act, 230 ILCS 45/25-1, with a purely technical change to the wording of the citation clause. It adds no substantive regulatory, tax, licensing or consumer-protection provisions. It died at sine die on 2025-01-07 without further action.","mechanics":["Amends only 230 ILCS 45/25-1, the short title section of Article 25 of the Illinois Gambling Act framework, so that it reads \"This Article may be cited as the Sports Wagering Act.\"","The text keeps the sentence that references to \"this Act\" mean this Article; the change is a minor wording edit (a duplicated \"the\" appears in the redline).","No tax rate, license fee, definition, coverage, enforcement or effective-date provision is added or changed.","Status is Failed/Dead; the bill was introduced by Rep. Emanuel Chris Welch (LRB103 03970 AMQ 48976 b) and adjourned sine die 2025-01-07.","The bill is a placeholder vehicle that could have been amended later to carry substantive gaming changes, but none were added."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"Unlike the substantive bills listed, such as GA SB57 and HB380 (sports betting regulation and taxation), AK HB145 and SB194 (mobile sports wagering with a tax), HI HB1308 and HB2570 (licensing and a general excise tax), and CT HB05229 (advertising restrictions and withdrawal rules), IL HB0403 contains no operative policy. It is a technical-change shell bill that touches only the short title of the Sports Wagering Act. Its only significance is as a possible vehicle for later amendment, and it is now dead.","watch":"Nothing substantive to act on, but watch for similar Illinois shell bills being revived in the new General Assembly and amended with tax, advertising or prop-bet provisions affecting operators.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":763,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:44:04.023Z"},"IL-HB4959":{"summary":"HB4959 (Public Act 103-0588) is Illinois's Fiscal Year 2025 Budget Implementation Act, an omnibus vehicle that makes program changes needed to carry out the FY2025 budget. The text provided includes the Pretrial Success Act (Article 2) and Article 3 amendments to health benefits, child welfare, tourism grants and developmental disability wage rates. Nothing in the provided text addresses sports betting, iGaming, or collectibles. The text is truncated mid-Section 3-9, so later articles may contain other provisions.","mechanics":["Article 2 (Pretrial Success Act): the Department of Human Services issues grants, subject to appropriation, for voluntary community-based pretrial services. Grants must be awarded by January 1, 2025.","Sec. 2-25(b): grants are $100,000 to $300,000 per organization in FY2025 and $100,000 to $500,000 afterward. Each service area gets 1 to 3 grantees, or 1 to 10 where the population exceeds 2,000,000.","Sec. 2-30: service areas are judicial circuits or contiguous groups with at least 500,000 people, and the minimum annual grant total per service area is $300,000. Sec. 2-35 requires local advisory councils by July 1, 2025, at least 40% of whose members have been charged with a felony.","Sec. 3-3 amends the State Employees Group Insurance Act to add dental and vision benefits for TRS retirees from plan years beginning January 1, 2025. The added premium is capped at $7.11 a month per recipient and $28.43 a month per dependent.","Sec. 3-8 amends the local tourism grant matching requirement to 30% of the grant in FY2025 and 40% in FY2026, up from 25% in FY2021-2024. Sec. 3-6 directs DHS, subject to appropriation, to issue an RFP for a supplemental substance use disorder treatment locator.","Sec. 3-2 authorizes emergency rulemaking for DHS to implement Section 74 of the Mental Health and Developmental Disabilities Administrative Act (front-line wage and rate increases). That authority is repealed one year after it takes effect."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. This is a general budget implementation vehicle, and the text provided has no wagering, gaming or collectibles provisions. The only gaming-adjacent theme is the funding of social services, such as substance use treatment, through appropriations.","watch":"The text is truncated, so confirm the remaining articles of the enrolled Act for any gaming-related fund transfers, tax changes or appropriations, because nothing in the provided text affects sports-betting operators.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":573672,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:44:11.639Z"},"IL-HB3850":{"summary":"HB3850 creates an Illinois Gaming Oversight Officer within the Gaming Board, with a disparity study and minority-business mentorship and licensing pilot. It rewrites the Sports Wagering Act's lottery sports wagering pilot (Sec. 25-70) around Lottery-run sports lottery terminals and drops the January 1, 2024 repeal. It also adds Prizes and Gifts Act rules for prize and gift kiosks, which are sweepstakes-style devices. The bill died at sine die on 2025-01-07.","mechanics":["New 230 ILCS 10/5.5: Governor appoints the Oversight Officer on the Speaker's recommendation. The Officer commissions a biannual disparity and availability study (subsec. (d)), may direct the Board and DCEO to gather data, and runs a mentorship program pairing minority businesses with terminal operator and casino licensees. The pilot (subsec. (h)) would cut license fees for minority-owned terminal operator applicants, cap Board suitability determinations at 6 months, and provide subsidized or guaranteed equipment loans funded by sports lottery kiosk revenue.","Sec. 25-70(b) says lottery games are part of the private management and competitive bidding process under Sec. 9.1 of the Illinois Lottery Law. The text still shows the central system provider licensing procedure, including the $20,000,000 fee, in the struck/amended mix, so it is unclear whether the open-bid licence survives.","Sec. 25-70(c): every sports lottery terminal must conform to an approved model ready for play within 90 days of the amendatory Act's effective date, plus Department testing dates. Terminals are tested by accredited independent labs. Under subsec. (d), terminals go in up to 2,500 lottery retail locations in the first 360 days and an additional 2,500 in year two.","Subsec. (g) shifts wager types from parlay and fixed-odds parlay wagers only to wagers set by Department rule. The old 80% payout and 8%/12% revenue split appears to be replaced by Department rules on retailer retention. Retailers face a fine of up to $5,000 for allowing under-21 play (subsec. (f)). Under subsec. (k) the Department must facilitate purchase or lease of all terminals, with financing for minority retailers, and the January 1, 2024 repeal date is removed.","Prizes and Gifts Act: new Sec. 33 makes it unlawful to operate a prize and gift kiosk that fails the technical standard. It bars internet or network connection except a redemption vault, bars selling anything but a bona fide product, and requires a self-contained revenue-based fill system. New Sec. 45 requires registration with the Department of Revenue as a COAD 'other device' with an annual fee. Filing a non-kiosk device is treated as a false filing, with seizure as a penalty, and Revenue and the Liquor Control Commission have jurisdiction.","Effective immediately on becoming law (Sec. 99). Video Gaming Act Sec. 35 is also amended, but the text shows only a clarification that a game device operated lawfully outside Criminal Code Sec. 28-1(b) is not a violation."],"issue_tags":["sweeps","licensing","legalize","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"None of the listed bills is comparable. They concern online or mobile sports wagering, taxes, advertising, and operator licensing in AK HB145, DE HB365, HI HB1308 and GA SB57. HB3850 instead expands a retail, Lottery-operated sports betting terminal model, using existing video gaming and lottery retail footprints rather than licensed operators. Its minority-ownership and disparity-study structure and its regulation of sweepstakes-style prize and gift kiosks also have no counterpart in the listed bills.","watch":"Operators should note the Lottery-run, retail-terminal sports betting channel with an uncertain central system provider licence and the removal of the sunset, which could entrench a competing lottery product, and the technical and registration rules for prize and gift kiosks, which could reach sweepstakes-style products.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":29879,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:44:11.819Z"},"IL-HB5092":{"summary":"HB5092 would add new Section 25-47 to the Illinois Sports Wagering Act. If a public entity that owns a sports facility does not apply for a master sports wagering license, a professional team that plays most of its home games there and has the owner's written authorization could apply to the Illinois Gaming Board in the entity's place and be deemed a sports facility under the Act. The team must operate through a designee, and the bill sets a team-specific license fee. The bill died at sine die adjournment on 2025-01-07.","mechanics":["Eligibility (25-47(a)): applies only where the public entity that owns the sports facility does not apply; the team must (i) play the majority of its home contests at the facility and (ii) hold written authorization from the public entity.","Deemed status: the licensed team is deemed a sports facility for all purposes of the Act.","Designee requirement: the team must operate through a designee as defined in Section 25-40(a), and the designee is treated as the owner of the sports facility for all purposes under the Act and its rules.","Initial license fee: $1,000,000, adjusted 12 months after the team begins sportsbook operations based on 5% of its handle from the first 12 months of operations (per the synopsis; the text provided cuts off mid-subsection (b), so the exact adjustment mechanics are not visible).","Term: the master sports wagering license is valid for 4 years (per the synopsis; this text is not in the excerpt provided).","Rulemaking: the Gaming Board may adopt rules necessary to implement the Section (per the synopsis)."],"issue_tags":["licensing","tax"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"None of the listed bills from other states addresses the same issue. Those bills (e.g., HI HB 1308, GA HB380, DE HB365, AK HB145) create or amend general licensing and tax frameworks, while IL HB5092 is a narrow amendment to an existing regime. It lets a professional team step into a public entity's master license slot, with a bespoke fee tied to 5% of first-year handle, which is unusual compared with the flat or tiered license fees typical of other states.","watch":"Watch how the fee adjustment tied to 5% of first-year handle is finally defined (whether it can be an increase or a refund, and whether it is a one-time payment or recurring) and how the designee structure would let a team license a third-party operator to run its sportsbook.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":2610,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:44:17.733Z"},"IL-HB4951":{"summary":"The supplied text of HB4951 (Public Act 103-0592) is a general revenue omnibus. Article 5 creates a County Official Compensation Task Force within the Department of Revenue. Article 10 creates the Workforce Development through Charitable Loan Repayment Act, and Section 10-900 begins amending Section 203 of the Illinois Income Tax Act. The text is cut off mid-way through Section 203, and the visible portion contains no sports wagering, iGaming, or collectibles provisions.","mechanics":["Sec. 2505-815 (Art. 5): the Task Force is chaired by the Director of Revenue and reviews county-official pay. Members must be appointed by Sept. 1, 2024, a status report is due Feb. 1, 2025, and the final report is due May 1, 2025 (or Dec. 31, 2025 if appointments are late). The Task Force dissolves Jan. 1, 2026.","Art. 10: the Illinois Student Assistance Commission approves community foundations (Sec. 10-17), and the foundations make grants directly to student loan servicers for qualified workers. The workers must be full-time employees (35+ hours a week) of qualifying businesses at job sites in designated areas.","Qualifying employers are registered new business ventures, businesses in targeted growth industries (e.g., advanced manufacturing, energy, life sciences), minority-, women- or disability-owned businesses, and not-for-profits (Sec. 10-10).","Sec. 10-30 contemplates an income-tax deduction for loan repayment assistance under Income Tax Act Sec. 203(a), subparagraph (LL), with the Department of Revenue to write documentation rules. Sec. 10-35 requires annual reports to the Commission.","Sec. 10-900 amends the Sec. 203 base-income addition and subtraction modifications. The visible text only restates existing items such as bonus depreciation, 529 plans and related-party interest add-backs, and cuts off at subparagraph (HH), so any new items cannot be confirmed from what was supplied."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"Nothing in the visible text is comparable to the other states' bills. AK HB145 and SB194, HI HB1308, HB2570 and SB1569, GA SB57, and IA HF673 are dedicated sports-wagering licensing and tax bills. IL HB4951 is a general revenue omnibus on county compensation, student-loan philanthropy, and income tax base modifications. The \"Neighborhood Concert Tax\" short title does not match any visible content. If wagering provisions exist, they would be in the truncated remainder of the bill, which I cannot see.","watch":"Because the supplied text is truncated and the title is a placeholder, read the full enrolled bill for any amendments to the Illinois Sports Wagering Act or gaming tax provisions that appear after Section 203 of the Income Tax Act.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":1824333,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:44:18.431Z"},"IL-HB4582":{"summary":"Public Act 103-0591 (HB4582, the Bond Authorization Act of 2024) is a capital-financing bill with no gaming provisions. It amends the State Finance Act, the General Obligation Bond Act and the Build Illinois Bond Act to change bond authorization ceilings and category allocations, and to allow unequal principal amounts on certain bonds issued in fiscal year 2025. The supplied text is truncated partway through Build Illinois Bond Act Sec. 6(b), so Sec. 13 of that Act is not shown.","mechanics":["GO Bond Act Sec. 2: the total authorization appears as $81,789,839,969 and $79,440,839,969, a $2.349 billion difference. The plain text has no strike/underline markup, so the direction of the change is not visible. Sec. 3 capital-facilities categories show similar paired figures, e.g. correctional $2,590,506,300 vs $1,690,506,300 and State agencies $8,439,753,300 vs $7,568,753,300.","Build Illinois Bond Act Sec. 2: the limited-obligation bond total appears as $11,358,681,100 and $10,019,681,100, a $1.339 billion difference. Sec. 4 category figures are paired the same way, e.g. paragraph (b) $3,554,636,967 vs $2,474,636,967.","GO Bond Act Sec. 9(a) and Build Illinois Bond Act Sec. 6(a): the equal-principal requirement now has an exception allowing unequal principal or mandatory redemption amounts for Bonds issued during fiscal year 2025. This also covers information-technology Bonds under GO Sec. 9.","State Finance Act Sec. 6z-78: adds this amendatory Act to the bond authorizations whose debt service is paid from the Capital Projects Fund through monthly transfers to the General Obligation Bond Retirement and Interest Fund.","The existing 25-year maximum term, the 20% variable-rate exposure cap (GO Sec. 9(c)) and the Bond Sale Order process are left in place. The Governor, on recommendation of the Director of the Governor's Office of Management and Budget, directs issuance."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills is comparable. The listed bills (e.g. AK HB145, GA SB57, IA SF2427, CT HB05229) regulate or tax sports wagering, gaming advertising or lottery operations. This Illinois act is a bond authorization and fund-transfer measure that does not mention wagering in the supplied text. The DC contract-approval acts (B25-1064, B26-0034) are the only other listed bills of a similarly non-gaming, appropriations-type character.","watch":"Nothing in the supplied text affects sports-betting operators, and the text is truncated, so confirm that the full enrolled act has no gaming-revenue or fund-transfer provisions before treating it as irrelevant.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":222226,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:44:20.425Z"},"IL-HB4975":{"summary":"HB4975 amends the Illinois Substance Use Disorder Act (20 ILCS 301) to fold gambling disorders into the Department of Human Services' public health mandate. It adds definitions of \"gambling\" and \"gambling disorder\" and rewrites Sec. 5-20 to expand the public education program, including a stated aim of reducing gambling-related suicide attempts. It also keeps the existing requirement that licensed gambling establishments and master sports wagering licensees display a DHS-determined help statement. The bill died at sine die on 2025-01-07 and does not touch taxes, licensing of operators, or advertising.","mechanics":["Sec. 1-10 adds definitions: \"gambling\" (risking money or items of value in games of chance, including video gaming, sports betting and other games of chance) and \"gambling disorder\" (persistent, recurring maladaptive gambling behavior that disrupts personal, family or vocational pursuits). \"Gaming\" is separately defined as playing video games.","Sec. 5-20(a), subject to appropriation, requires the DHS program to promote public awareness to create a \"gambling informed State\" and to use screening, crisis intervention, treatment, prevention and training to decrease suicide attempts related to gambling disorder. It also replaces the \"800\" number with a toll-free hotline and website.","Sec. 5-20(c) says DHS \"shall determine a statement\" on obtaining help for a gambling disorder. Each licensed gambling establishment owner must post it, and each master sports wagering licensee must put it on its portal, website, or computer or mobile application. The text does not set wording, placement, size, or penalties for noncompliance. DHS supplies signs subject to appropriation, referencing Sports Wagering Act Sec. 25.95 and Illinois Gambling Act Sec. 13.1.","Sec. 5-20(d) and (e) give DHS discretionary (\"may\") powers: advise State and local officials on gambling disorders, support prevention and treatment projects, collaborate with community organizations and treatment providers, and award grants. Eligible grantees include local health departments, correctional institutions, hospitals, universities, community and faith-based organizations. DHS may also seek private and federal funding.","Sec. 5-10 and related sections extend DHS's existing duties on prevention, early intervention, treatment and recovery support to gambling disorders. They also encourage insurers to cover gambling disorder treatment and add \"gambling disorder\" to Advisory Council duties and membership. Sec. 15-5(a) extends the licensing requirement to anyone providing gambling disorder treatment.","No new operator fees, taxes, or funding mechanism are created. Funding stays \"subject to appropriation\" plus existing Video Gaming Act Sec. 50 and Illinois Gambling Act Sec. 13(c) grants referenced in Sec. 5-10(a)(11.5). No effective date is shown in the provided text, and the text is truncated partway through Sec. 15-5."],"issue_tags":["integrity"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"Unlike the licensing and tax bills in the list (AK HB145/SB194, DE HB365, GA SB57, HI HB1308), HB4975 creates no market, tax, or operator licensing. It is a public-health bill, closest to the consumer-protection provisions in CT HB05229 and CT SB01464, which impose operator-side duties such as toll-free customer service numbers and withdrawal rules. Those Connecticut bills impose more concrete operator obligations. HB4975 leaves the operator-facing help statement entirely to DHS to define later.","watch":"Watch the Sec. 5-20(c) mandate that master sports wagering licensees display a DHS-determined help statement on their portal, website and app, since the content, placement and enforcement are left to DHS discretion and could be revised without further legislation; the bill is dead, but the language may return.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":85550,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:44:22.791Z"},"IL-HB5230":{"summary":"HB5230 amends the Illinois Lottery Law (20 ILCS 1605/20, 20.1, 24 and 27) to shift lottery financial and prize-payment functions from the State Treasurer toward the Department of Lottery. It lets the Department pay prizes of any size from its separate account, and lets the Department itself contract with banks and investment firms for lottery financial services. It does not touch the Sports Wagering Act or any wagering-operator obligations beyond a pre-existing cross-reference to Section 25-70. The bill is dead after sine die adjournment on 2025-01-07.","mechanics":["Sec. 20: the State Lottery Fund receives sales revenue net of agent commissions, fees and prizes of $600 or less (replacing the garbled 'less than $600 or less') that were validly paid at the agent level. Sec. 20(k), the cross-reference to Sports Wagering Act Sec. 25-70, is left unchanged.","Sec. 20.1: the Department may pay any validated prize, not just prizes up to $25,000, from its account held separate from public State moneys. The provisions sending account moneys to the Public Treasurers' Investment Pool and barring use of the account for deferred prize winners are removed in the synopsis.","Sec. 27(a): the Department, rather than the State Treasurer with the Director's consent, may contract with any person or corporation, including a bank, trust company or investment banking firm, for financial functions in connection with lottery operation. The Treasurer may still act as the Department's agent with the Director's consent.","Sec. 27(b): the text removes the requirement that the Director prepare and send vouchers to the Comptroller for payments from the Deferred Lottery Prize Winners Trust Fund to deferred prize winners.","The bill text as provided does not conform Sec. 24 (Comptroller preaudit excluding prizes of $25,000 or less; Auditor General post-audit referencing $25,000 prizes) to the removal of the $25,000 cap in Sec. 20.1, and it has no stated effective date or penalty provisions."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"This bill is a state-lottery treasury and prize-payment housekeeping measure, so it is not comparable to the sports-wagering licensing and tax bills such as AK HB145/SB194, HI HB1308 or GA SB57. Its closest analogues are lottery administration bills such as CT SB01235 and SB01237 and IN SB0094, though those deal with courier services, licensing and advertising rather than prize-payment funds. The one wagering link is the unchanged cross-reference to Sports Wagering Act Sec. 25-70 in Sec. 20(k).","watch":"Nothing in the bill affects operators directly, and it is dead, so the only point to note is that Sec. 20(k) still routes Sec. 25-70 sports wagering moneys through the State Lottery Fund if the lottery financial structure is revisited.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":11124,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:44:26.213Z"},"IL-HB5229":{"summary":"IL HB5229 is an engrossed housekeeping bill amending nine sections of the Illinois Lottery Law (20 ILCS 1605/7.1, 9.1, 10.1, 10.6, 19, 20, 20.1, 24 and 27). It mostly updates stale language: it blanks the 2010 private-manager selection provisions, raises a procurement threshold, and adjusts prize-claim, account and audit provisions. It does not touch the Sports Wagering Act or the Illinois Gambling Act. The bill died at sine die on 2025-01-07.","mechanics":["Sec. 9.1(b) and (c) are marked (Blank), removing the 2010 deadlines and transition rules for selecting the initial private manager. The management agreement requirements (10-year maximum term, Department control, and so on) are left in place.","Sec. 9.1(d)(19) replaces the $50,000 competitive-bidding trigger with the small purchase limits under Section 20-20 of the Illinois Procurement Code. Sec. 9.1(d)(8-5) is changed so that the 20% minority-, women- and disability-owned business spend is 'encouraged' rather than mandated.","Sec. 10.1 licence ineligibility (felony, professional gambler, bookmaking, fraud, bad character) is restructured only in its punctuation and conjunctions. The disqualifier for a firm in which a disqualified person holds a 5% or greater interest is kept. The 10-year rehabilitation path and the summary revocation with a hearing within 30 days are kept.","Secs. 10.6 and 19 require the Department to publish odds of winning, and play instructions, on its public website. Sec. 10.6 still requires ticket agents to display odds placards. Sec. 19 limits the no-claim-for-lost-or-stolen-ticket rule to physical lottery tickets.","Secs. 20, 20.1, 24 and 27 are mostly technical. Sec. 27(a) is rewritten so the Department, as well as the State Treasurer, can contract for financial functions in connection with lottery operations. Sec. 20.1 keeps the $25,000 prize-payment account and Sec. 24 keeps the $25,000 preaudit carve-out. Sec. 20(k) already cross-references Section 25-70 of the Sports Wagering Act for fund distribution, and the bill leaves that unchanged."],"issue_tags":["licensing","integrity"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"IL HB5229 is a state-lottery administrative cleanup and has no real counterpart on sports-betting substance such as tax rates, mobile licensing or advertising. It is closest to CT SB01235 and CT SB01237, which also amend lottery and gaming licensing and administration, and to IN SB0094 and IN HB1432, which also deal with lottery operations and procurement. Unlike those bills, and unlike the Delaware, Indiana and Georgia sports-betting bills, it neither authorises nor restricts sports wagering, and it adds no advertising rules or consumer protections. The only sports-betting link is an unchanged cross-reference in Sec. 20(k).","watch":"An operator should note that the bill leaves the Sports Wagering Act alone, and that the only sports-betting touchpoint is the existing Sec. 20(k) cross-reference to Section 25-70 on revenue distribution. The bill is dead, so the practical concern is whether a later vehicle brings back the Sec. 10.1 licence-disqualifier edits or the Department-level contracting authority in Sec. 27.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Engrossed","text_chars":42794,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:44:27.612Z"},"IL-HB5367":{"summary":"IL HB5367 would amend Section 25 of the Video Gaming Act (230 ILCS 40/25) by adding a new subsection (h-5) that bars the Illinois Gaming Board from licensing a video gaming location in a mall if doing so would more likely than not let terminal operators or others run the terminals at 2 or more licensed locations as a single video gaming operation. The rest of Section 25 appears to be existing law carried forward. The bill is dead: it failed on sine die adjournment on 2025-01-07.","mechanics":["New subsection (h-5) directs the Board not to grant a video gaming location application if it determines the grant would more likely than not cause a terminal operator, alone or with others, to operate the terminals at 2 or more licensed locations as a single video gaming operation.","The Board must consider, among other factors, the physical aspects of the location, ownership, control or management, any written or unwritten arrangements on conducting video gaming or sharing costs and revenues, and how any terminal operator or related entity markets or describes the locations.","Rebuttable presumption: granting a license within a mall is presumed to create a single operation if the Board determines the license would create a local concentration of licensed video gaming locations.","Definitions: 'mall' means a building, or adjoining or connected buildings, with 4 or more separate locations; 'local concentration' means licensed video gaming locations exceed half of the separate locations in the mall; 'licensed video gaming location' covers licensed establishments, fraternal, veterans, truck stop and large truck stop establishments.","Existing provisions are retained: the 6-terminal cap per location (10 at large truck stops), the 50/50 split of after-tax profits, the Class 4 felony for terminal operator violations, the 1,000-foot and 100-foot location restrictions with waivers, the undue economic concentration rules in (i), and application of the Illinois Antitrust Act in (j).","The text contains no effective date, penalty, or rulemaking directive specific to (h-5)."],"issue_tags":["licensing"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"The bill concerns Illinois video gaming terminals in bars, truck stops and fraternal and veterans halls, which is not comparable to any listed bill. The listed bills from other states (for example AK HB145, DE HB365, GA SB57, HI HB 1308) deal with authorizing or taxing mobile sports wagering. Its closest analogue in subject is CO SB 163 on licensing delegation, but that is a different mechanism. The unusual feature here is a location-licensing rule aimed at preventing de facto aggregation of terminals in mall settings, backed by a rebuttable presumption.","watch":"Because the bill is dead and covers only land-based video gaming terminals, it has little direct effect on sports-betting or iGaming operators, but its concentration test and its focus on marketing and revenue-sharing arrangements may reappear in future Illinois bills.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Engrossed","text_chars":15552,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:44:31.607Z"},"IL-HB5307":{"summary":"IL HB5307 would add Section 13.3 to the Illinois Gambling Act (230 ILCS 10/13.3) creating a Problem Gambling Task Force to recommend to the General Assembly how the State should keep meeting its responsibility to people with a gambling problem or disorder. It is a study-and-recommend body only and creates no new licensing, tax, or conduct obligations for operators. The bill is dead: it died on sine die adjournment on 2025-01-07, and the engrossed text provided cuts off partway through the Task Force's duties.","mechanics":["Section 5 adds 230 ILCS 10/13.3 to the Illinois Gambling Act; Section 1 contains legislative findings that a task force is needed for a comprehensive review of gambling funding, services, programs, and policies.","Membership: four legislators (one each appointed by the Senate President, Senate Minority Leader, Speaker, and House Minority Leader, with the Senate and House President/Speaker appointees serving as cochairs), plus Governor appointees from health care, a problem-gambling nonprofit, a person affected by a gambling problem, and a licensee representative.","Industry seat: one Governor appointee must represent a business or organization licensed under the Illinois Horse Racing Act of 1975, the Illinois Gambling Act, the Video Gaming Act, or the Sports Wagering Act, so sports-betting operators would have at most one seat among all licensee types.","Agency seats: one representative each from the Department of Public Health, the Illinois Gaming Board, the Department of the Lottery, and the Illinois Racing Board; the Task Force may also specially appoint other agency or stakeholder representatives for specific topics or subcommittees.","Members are unpaid but reimbursed for expenses, and the Task Force must meet at least four times.","Duties in the available text: review existing in-state programs and treatment, review other states' and federal programs, and review the emerging nexus between gambling advertising and gambling addiction; the text is truncated after item (3), so any further duties, report deadline, or sunset are not visible."],"issue_tags":["advertising","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike the Maryland problem-gambling bills, which direct money or regulation (MD HB1409/HB700/SB706/SB862 mandate prevalence studies tied to fund revenue, MD HB465/HB518 require Commission regulations and a voluntary exclusion list, and MD SB86 mandates helpline links on mobile sportsbook landing pages), IL HB5307 only creates an advisory body with no operator mandates. It is closest to MD HB 195, which also creates a workgroup on gambling prevention technology, and to Louisiana SR 178, a non-binding request for a health department study. Its explicit directive to examine the link between gambling advertising and addiction is a notable feature that the listed Maryland, Massachusetts (H4037, H5258), and Hawaii bills do not clearly show in their summaries.","watch":"Although the bill is dead, a lawyer for sportsbook operators should note that its advertising-and-addiction review could seed later advertising restrictions and that only one industry seat on the Task Force would cover all licensee types, so any revived version should be checked for its report scope and operator representation.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Engrossed","text_chars":3803,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:44:32.405Z"},"IL-SB1062":{"summary":"IL SB1062 is a shell bill that amends Section 25-1 (short title) of the Illinois Sports Wagering Act, 230 ILCS 45/25-1, with a purely technical change. It does not alter any substantive sports wagering rule, tax, license, or enforcement provision. The bill died at sine die on 2025-01-07 with no action beyond introduction.","mechanics":["Sole operative provision: Section 5 amends 230 ILCS 45/25-1 (Short title) of the Sports Wagering Act.","The change is a technical edit to the short-title text. The Article may still be cited as the Sports Wagering Act, and references to 'this Act' mean this Article.","No tax rates, license fees, thresholds, definitions, or covered persons are changed.","No effective date or enforcement provisions beyond Illinois default rules; no penalties are created.","Status: introduced 2/2/2023 by Sen. John F. Curran (LRB103 05483 AMQ 50502 b); failed at sine die 2025-01-07."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"Unlike the other states' bills listed, such as AK HB145/SB194 (mobile sports wagering authorization and tax), GA SB57 and HB380 (regulation and taxation), and CT HB05229 and SB01464 (advertising, withdrawals, customer service), IL SB1062 contains no substantive policy. It is a placeholder shell bill that could later have been amended into a vehicle for Illinois sports wagering changes, but as introduced it has no regulatory content to compare. It touches the existing Illinois Sports Wagering Act only through the short-title section.","watch":"Because the bill is dead and has no substantive content, the only point to watch is whether similar shell bills in a later session are amended to carry Illinois sports wagering tax, licensing, or advertising changes.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":766,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:44:36.326Z"},"IL-HB5648":{"summary":"HB5648 creates the Fantasy Sports Consumer Protection Act, which would have made the Illinois Gaming Board the licensing and tax authority for paid online fantasy contest operators. It declares licensed fantasy contests not to be gambling, carves them out of the Sports Wagering Act definition of \"sports wagering\", and amends the Criminal Code (720 ILCS 5/28-1 and 28-3) to protect participants and premises. The bill is dead: the session ended sine die on 2025-01-07.","mechanics":["Licensing (Sec. 15): operators must be licensed by the Board, with fingerprint-based national criminal history checks for owners, officers and directors, disclosure of holders of 10% or more, and licenses valid for 4 years (Sec. 25(c)). Pre-existing operators may keep operating if they apply within 90 days after rules are adopted (Sec. 25(b)).","Fees (Sec. 25): an incumbent operator pays the lesser of 8% of prior-year adjusted gross fantasy contest receipts or $1,000,000. A new entrant pays $10,000, but must remit a further $990,000 within 10 days of notifying the Board if it has 10,000 or more active paid Illinois participants. Renewal fee is 1% of adjusted gross receipts for the preceding 4 years.","Tax (Sec. 40): starting July 1, 2025, a privilege tax of at least 10% and not more than 15% (rate set by Board rule) on adjusted gross fantasy contest receipts (entry fees less prizes, multiplied by an Illinois location percentage). Accrual accounting applies, monthly returns are due by month-end, and proceeds go to the Common School Fund (Sec. 10(c)(2)).","Contest definition and eligibility (Secs. 5, 20): contests need a paid entry fee, prizes known in advance, skill-based outcomes, and no outcome based solely on one team's score or a single athlete's performance in a single event. Participants must be at least 19, and entries are accepted only from persons physically in Illinois, verified by geolocation. The definition also covers esports.","Compliance (Secs. 10, 30, 45): requires AML compliance under the BSA and AMLA 2020, age and identity verification, anti-fraud and anti-collusion controls, an annual CPA audit and testing-lab report (first due 24 months after licensing), integrity-breach reporting to the Board, and a voluntary self-exclusion program folded into the Board's existing one. Self-excluded persons' prizes are forfeited and donated to problem-gaming programs. Sec. 50 adds supplier-diversity reporting.","Rulemaking and carve-outs: the Board must adopt rules within 180 days and may use emergency rules. Sec. 20(e) bars the Board from regulating the rules, statistical makeup or digital platform of an individual contest. Sec. 20(f) protects licenses from being terminated early by rule changes."],"issue_tags":["legalize","licensing","tax","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Fantasy-only regimes are rare in the listed bills. GA HB1329 is the closest, as it also authorizes, regulates and taxes fantasy contests, though it runs through the Georgia Lottery Corporation rather than a gaming board. IA HF673 and IA HSB122 also tax internet fantasy sports contests, but alongside sports wagering. Unlike the sports-betting bills (e.g., DE HB365, HI HB 1308, AK HB145), HB5648 expressly separates fantasy from sports wagering. It sets a 10-15% tax on net entry fees, a 19-year minimum age and an in-state-only entry requirement.","watch":"The fantasy carve-out from \"sports wagering\" and the Sec. 20(a) declaration that fantasy is not gambling would shape whether pick'em or player-prop style products fall under the fantasy exemption or the Sports Wagering Act, and the Sec. 5 contest criteria (no single-performance outcomes) are where that line is drawn.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":43165,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:44:39.075Z"},"IL-SB1508":{"summary":"Despite its title (\"Sports Gaming-Addiction Help\"), the enrolled Public Act 103-0381 does not touch sports wagering. It amends the Illinois Lottery Law to create a joint special instant scratch-off game (new Sec. 21.4) that funds multiple charitable causes, adds two new causes (UNCF Illinois and the Illinois DREAM Fund), repeals Sec. 21.12, and creates a task force to study the program.","mechanics":["New Sec. 21.4: the Lottery must offer a joint special instant scratch-off game starting January 1, 2024 (or as soon as practical, at the Director's discretion), discontinued January 1, 2027. Net revenue is split equally among the special causes in Secs. 21.5-21.11, 21.13, 21.15 and 21.16.","Cap and sunset rules: the Department may support no more than 10 causes at once, and new causes or specialty tickets after February 1, 2024 must carry a sunset date. 'Net revenue' is ticket sales less prizes, retailer payments, and direct and estimated administrative expenses.","New Sec. 21.15 (UNCF Illinois scholarships, UNCF Scholarship Fund) and Sec. 21.16 (Illinois DREAM scratch-off, Illinois DREAM Fund), both starting January 1, 2024. State Finance Act Secs. 5.990 and 5.991 create the two funds, and Sec. 67 of the Higher Education Student Assistance Act is amended to allow the DREAM Fund to receive scratch-off proceeds.","Existing causes are conformed: Secs. 9.3 and 20 cross-reference the new sections, and Sec. 21.12 is repealed. Sunset dates appear in some sections (Sec. 21.5 Dec. 31, 2026; Sec. 21.8 Dec. 31, 2025; Sec. 21.13 Jan. 1, 2025).","New Sec. 21.17 creates the Illinois Lottery Special Instant Scratch-off Task Force: Lottery Director plus 10 legislative and gubernatorial appointees, members named by September 1, 2023, at least 4 meetings, and a report due January 1, 2025. The task force is repealed January 1, 2027."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"Nothing comparable is listed. The listed bills (e.g., GA SB57, HI HB 1308, DE HB365, CT SB01464) deal with sports-wagering licensing, tax or responsible-gaming rules, whereas IL SB1508 deals only with charitable instant-ticket lottery funding. Its enrolled text has no sports wagering or problem-gambling provisions, so the title bears no relation to the content and is the unusual feature of the bill.","watch":"Nothing in the text directly affects sports-betting, iGaming or trading-card operators; the only point of note is the mismatch between the \"Sports Gaming-Addiction Help\" title and the content, so do not rely on the title when searching for Illinois responsible-gaming obligations.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":39946,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:44:41.619Z"},"IL-SB1839":{"summary":"SB1839 amends Section 6z-27 of the State Finance Act (30 ILCS 105/6z-27) to update the schedule of fund-by-fund transfers into the Audit Expense Fund, which pays for audits under the Illinois State Auditing Act. It is a routine appropriations-style housekeeping measure; the gaming content is limited to line items for gaming-related State funds in the transfer schedule. It died at sine die on 2025-01-07 and takes effect on becoming law.","mechanics":["Within 30 days after July 1, 2022 (or as soon as practical), the Comptroller and Treasurer must transfer specified dollar amounts from two long lists of State funds into the Audit Expense Fund. The text lists amounts in two sets, one with dollar signs and one without.","Gaming-related funds appear in both lists: Sports Wagering Fund ($4,450 in the first list; $8,572 in the second), State Gaming Fund ($94,435; $204,660), State Lottery Fund ($340,323; $129,814), Illinois Gaming Law Enforcement Fund ($1,869) and Horse Racing Fund ($169,632).","Federal and State trust funds are excluded from the transfer provisions. They may be tapped only as directed, and only where the trust agreement does not prohibit it. The Auditor General notifies trustees of the estimated audit cost, and the trustees direct the transfer.","The Auditor General may bill entities outside the transfer provisions, expressly including private entities, related organizations and locally held funds, for the cost of audits, studies and investigations done on their behalf. Receipts go into the Audit Expense Fund.","If a listed fund cannot lawfully fund its transfer, the shortfall is transferred from the General Revenue Fund. The Auditor General must notify the Governor's Office of Management and Budget by December 1 each year of estimated audit costs. Transfers exceeding actual costs (other than from General Revenue) are returned around September 30.","Section 99 provides that the Act takes effect upon becoming law."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. On its own, this is an unusual item for a gaming tracker: it is a general State Finance Act audit-funding schedule in which the Sports Wagering Fund, State Gaming Fund, State Lottery Fund and Horse Racing Fund appear only as transfer sources, with no change to wagering law, taxes or licensing.","watch":"The only point of note for operators is the Auditor General's authority to bill private entities for audit costs incurred on their behalf, and that the Sports Wagering Fund and State Gaming Fund are named as transfer sources. Neither affects operators directly, and the bill is dead.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":24449,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:44:44.752Z"},"IL-SB1504":{"summary":"IL SB1504 (engrossed; died at sine die on 2025-01-07) expands the Criminal Code's definition of \"gambling device\" in 720 ILCS 5/28-2(a) to cover any vending or other electronic machine that offers entry into a chance-based contest, sweepstakes, scheme or plan when the offer is incidental to or results from a purchase or from receipt of a coupon, voucher or credit. It targets sweepstakes-style \"no-purchase-necessary\" gaming terminals, removes the no-payment exemption for games played on such devices, and makes gambling offenses involving them a Class 4 felony. It also makes conforming edits to the Video Gaming Act.","mechanics":["New 28-2(a)(iii) defines a gambling device as any vending or other electronic machine, expressly including one that awards credits and has a circuit, meter or switch able to remove and record removal of credits, that offers entry into a contest, competition, sweepstakes, scheme, plan or other selection process dependent on chance for a gift, award or item of value, if the offer is incidental to or results from (A) a purchase or (B) purchase or gratuitous receipt of a coupon, voucher or certificate redeemable on or off the machine.","New 28-2(d) states the intent that (a)(iii) bars any mechanism that tries to avoid gambling-device status through any subterfuge or pretense whatsoever.","28-1(b)(13), the exemption for games with no payment or purchase required, no longer applies where participation is through a device prohibited by 28-2(a)(iii). This closes the free-alternate-method-of-entry defense for these machines.","New 28-1(c)(2): notwithstanding 28-1(c)(1), a gambling offense involving an (a)(iii) device is a Class 4 felony, with no first-offense misdemeanor tier. Otherwise gambling is a Class A misdemeanor, with a Class 4 felony on a second or later conviction under (a)(3) to (a)(12).","28-2(a)(2) is revised to say that, except as otherwise provided in subsection (a), a vending machine with full and adequate return and no element of chance is excluded. This keeps ordinary vending machines out unless they fall within (a)(iii).","Section 35 of the Video Gaming Act is amended to take effect on enactment (Section 99) and the Act has no delayed effective date or grandfathering. The changes it makes to Section 35 are formatting and cleanup, with no new substantive video-gaming provisions."],"issue_tags":["sweeps","integrity"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"This is a criminal-code bill aimed at sweepstakes and skill-style machines, so it is closest to CA AB 831, which also regulates contests and sweepstakes, in particular those using electronic video monitors that simulate gambling. Unlike CA AB 831, which works through the unfair-practices framework, IL SB1504 uses felony criminal penalties and broadens the gambling-device definition itself. It has little in common with the licensing, tax and advertising bills in AK, CT, GA, HI and IA, which build or amend regulated sports-wagering regimes, and it leaves the Sports Wagering Act exemption in 28-1(b)(15) untouched.","watch":"Counsel should check whether the broad (a)(iii) language, covering any electronic device and any chance-based \"scheme, plan or other selection process\" tied to a purchase or a coupon or credit, could reach promotional sweepstakes, mystery-pack or card-break mechanics, or iGaming-adjacent sweeps offerings, even though the bill is dead and may return in a later session.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Engrossed","text_chars":19507,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:44:45.740Z"},"IL-SB250":{"summary":"Illinois SB0250 is an omnibus FY2024 appropriations vehicle. It amends FY2023 appropriations in Public Act 102-698 by changing, adding and repealing line items, and makes FY2024 appropriations and reappropriations for specified purposes. The full text is unavailable, so the only source is the official abstract. The bill was vetoed in part (item/reduction veto), and the veto stood by a 103-0006 vote on 2023-11-08.","mechanics":["Amends Public Act 102-698 (FY2023 appropriations) by changing, adding and repealing various line items.","Makes FY2024 appropriations and reappropriations for unspecified purposes; the abstract gives no dollar figures or agency allocations.","Some provisions take effect immediately and others on July 1, 2023, per the abstract.","Status is an item/reduction veto, which the legislature let stand on 2023-11-08 by a vote of 103-0006.","The abstract does not mention sports betting, gaming, iGaming, collectibles or trading cards, and no such provisions can be confirmed without the full text."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states were listed. On its own, this is a general appropriations bill with no identifiable wagering, gaming or collectibles provisions in the available abstract, so it is not substantively comparable to licensing or tax bills.","watch":"Because the full text is unavailable, check the enacted line items for any appropriations to the Illinois Gaming Board, problem-gambling programs or gaming-tax-funded items, but nothing in the abstract indicates operator-facing changes.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Veto Message","text_chars":112,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:44:46.050Z"},"IL-SB1875":{"summary":"Public Act 103-0390 amends the Illinois Administrative Procedure Act (Sections 5-40, 5-45, 5-50, 5-60, 5-65) to require agencies to accept rulemaking comments by email or other public electronic means through their websites, and to publish the submission address in the Illinois Register notice. It also amends Section 18 of the Uniform Electronic Transactions Act to require the Department of Innovation and Technology and the Secretary of State to adopt electronic-records rules within 6 months. It is a general administrative-procedure bill with no gaming-specific substance; gaming appears only in existing emergency-rule subsections that the bill carries forward.","mechanics":["Sec. 5-40 (general rulemaking): agencies must accept written submissions, including by email or other publicly accessible electronic means through their website; oral submissions remain at agency discretion; the Illinois Register notice must state the email or website address for submissions. The 45-day first notice period and the 45-day second notice to JCAR are unchanged.","Sec. 5-45 (emergency rules): the same electronic-submission and notice-address requirement now applies to emergency rulemaking. The 150-day maximum effective period and the 24-month limit on repeat emergency rules are unchanged.","Sec. 5-50 (peremptory rulemaking): the same electronic-submission and notice-address requirement is added.","Sec. 5-60 (regulatory agenda): each agenda summary must now include the agency contact's email address. Sec. 5-65 (filing of rules): the notice of adopted rules must also include the contact's email address.","Uniform Electronic Transactions Act Sec. 18: the Department of Innovation and Technology and the Secretary of State must adopt rules within 6 months after the Act's effective date on the format, signatures, security and preservation of electronic records. Agencies are still not required to use electronic records (Sec. 18(c)).","Sec. 99: the Act takes effect on becoming law. The existing emergency-rule authority in Sec. 5-45(mm) and (nn) for the Department of the Lottery and the Illinois Gaming Board under the Sports Wagering Act, and in (oo) for the Gaming Board under the Video Gaming Act, is restated as part of the section but not altered."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. The bill is unusual for this tracker because it is a generic Illinois Administrative Procedure Act amendment on public comment channels, not a gaming bill. Its only gaming content is existing Sports Wagering Act and Video Gaming Act emergency-rule authority in Sec. 5-45(mm), (nn) and (oo), which it leaves unchanged.","watch":"Operators should note that comments on any Illinois Gaming Board or Lottery rulemaking, including emergency rules, can now be filed by email or through the agency website at the address stated in the Illinois Register notice, so comment-monitoring should track those notices.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":46905,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:44:46.767Z"},"IL-SB3478":{"summary":"SB3478 amends Section 6z-27 of the State Finance Act to update the annual schedule of transfers into the Audit Expense Fund, which pays for Auditor General audits under the Illinois State Auditing Act. The transfer date moves from 30 days after July 1, 2023 to 30 days after July 1, 2024, and the dollar amounts are specified fund by fund. It is a routine appropriations-housekeeping bill, effective immediately, and it did not advance; the session ended sine die.","mechanics":["Transfers are ordered by the Comptroller and made by the Treasurer within 30 days after July 1, 2024, or as soon as practical, from the listed funds into the Audit Expense Fund.","Two fund lists appear, one struck and one new. Gaming-related lines in the new list include the Sports Wagering Fund at $4,450, the State Gaming Fund at $94,435, the State Lottery Fund at $340,323, and the Horse Racing Fund at $233,685 (that last one appears in the struck list). The Illinois Gaming Law Enforcement Fund at $1,411 also appears in the struck list.","The largest single lines are $17,653,153 from the General Revenue Fund and $500,000 from the State Pensions Fund.","Federal and State trust funds are excluded from the fixed transfers. Transfers from them occur only when trustees direct them after the Auditor General notifies them of estimated audit costs, unless trust terms prohibit it.","The Auditor General may bill entities outside the transfer provisions, including private entities, related organizations and locally held funds, for audits, studies and investigations done on their behalf, with revenue deposited into the Audit Expense Fund.","If a listed fund cannot lawfully make its transfer, the General Revenue Fund covers the shortfall. Excess non-GRF transfers are returned on September 30, or as soon as practicable, after actual audit costs are determined."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. On its own, this is a pure State Finance Act housekeeping bill. Sports wagering appears only because the Sports Wagering Fund and State Gaming Fund are among the many funds assessed a small share of audit costs. It makes no change to wagering tax rates, licensing or operations.","watch":"Little to watch beyond the small Sports Wagering Fund assessment ($4,450) and the Auditor General's authority to bill private entities for audit costs, which in theory could reach licensees, although the bill is dead.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":27410,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:44:54.100Z"},"IL-SB323":{"summary":"SB0323 amends Section 25-50(d) of the Illinois Sports Wagering Act to cut the supplier license renewal fee. The text strikes $150,000 and inserts $50,000, so the fee falls by two-thirds for suppliers of sports wagering equipment, systems and related services to master sports wagering licensees. The bill is marked Failed/Dead after sine die adjournment on 2025-01-07, so it did not become law.","mechanics":["Amends 230 ILCS 45/25-50(d) only; no other section of the Sports Wagering Act is changed.","Reduces the fee for each additional 4-year renewal period from $150,000 to $50,000, applying to supplier licenses beginning 4 years after the initial license is issued.","Leaves the nonrefundable initial license and application fee at $150,000.","Leaves the initial 4-year license term and 4-year renewals unchanged, as is renewal for applicants that continue to comply with statutory and regulatory requirements.","Leaves untouched the Board's authority to accept other jurisdictions' licensing and equipment approvals, and the list and independent testing laboratory requirements in subsection (e).","The text has no separate effective date clause and no new enforcement provisions."],"issue_tags":["licensing","tax"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Nothing in the other states' list is directly comparable, because none of those bills deals with supplier license fees. The nearest overlap is on licensing and fee structure in the new-market bills, such as AK HB145/SB194, DE HB365 and HI HB1308/HB2570/SB1569, which set up operator and supplier licensing for the first time. By contrast, IL SB0323 is a narrow fee adjustment to an existing licensing scheme, and it touches neither operator licensing nor tax rates.","watch":"Although the bill is dead, supplier-fee relief could be reintroduced alone or in a larger gaming package, so suppliers and operators with vendor-cost exposure should watch for it, and for any changes to the $150,000 initial fee or the renewal structure.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Engrossed","text_chars":3033,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:44:55.142Z"},"IL-SB251":{"summary":"IL SB0251 (Public Act 103-0589) is a supplemental appropriations bill amending the FY2024 budget act, P.A. 103-0006. It adds, cuts and reappropriates amounts across many agencies and includes a long list of member-initiative grants. The text provided has no wagering, gaming, tax or licensing provisions for gaming, though it is cut off partway through Article 11.","mechanics":["Art. 1 and 1.5: cuts the Governor's Office appropriation from $850,000 to $500,000 and adds $5,000,000 from the General Revenue Fund to the Comptroller for the Capital Facility and Technology Modernization Fund.","Art. 3: appropriates $5,500,000 to CMS for the State Garage Revolving Fund and reduces the Group Insurance appropriation from $2,019,598,300 to $1,836,757,500.","Art. 9: raises Employment Security deposits into the Title III Social Security and Employment Fund (Sec. 10 changed from $25,000,000 to $30,000,000; new Sec. 60 adds $70,000,000) and appropriates $75,000,000 for return of federal funds to FEMA under 44 C.F.R. 206.120(f).","Art. 10: changes the Department of Financial and Professional Regulation (DFPR) appropriation for a new licensing system from $395,000 to $6,250,000, reappropriates $10,000,000, and adds $15,855,000 to the Professions Licensure Fund with a matching $15,855,000 appropriation for the licensing system. These sections concern professional licensing generally, not gaming licensing.","Art. 11: makes Department of Human Services changes, including reducing Sec. 1 from $916,207,200 to $815,207,200, cutting TANF from $245,000,000 to $185,000,000, and raising the Sec. 675 violence-prevention and youth-employment grant list to $64,024,500, which names more than 150 grantees.","The chaptered text is cut off mid-sentence in Sec. 1195 of Article 56, so any later articles or sections are not reviewed here."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states were listed. This is a general budget-adjustment appropriations act with no wagering provisions in the text provided, so it is not comparable to a gaming tax or regulatory bill. It is unusual for a gaming-focused review only because it contains nothing on sports betting, iGaming, or collectibles.","watch":"Nothing in the text provided affects operators directly, but because the text is truncated, check the remaining articles of the enrolled bill for any Gaming Board, lottery, or gaming-revenue appropriation lines, and note that the DFPR licensing-system funding in Art. 10 is for professional licensing.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":4381307,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:44:55.348Z"},"IL-SB3410":{"summary":"The portion of Public Act 103-1059 (SB3410) that was provided is an omnibus State-government measure. It mostly extends sunset dates and reporting deadlines in unrelated Illinois statutes, such as administrative rulemaking, aging, tax credits, task forces and the Vehicle Code. The text is cut off at Section 46 (Vehicle Code 6-308), and no gambling or gambling-disorder provision appears in what was supplied, even though the title refers to DHS and gambling disorders.","mechanics":["Section 5 amends IAPA Sections 5-45.56 and 5-45.57 to move the repeal of the emergency rulemaking authority (Public Aid Code changes and direct support personnel rate increases) to June 5, 2026.","Section 10 extends the Commission on LGBTQ Aging to May 16, 2026 (Act on the Aging Section 8.10) and moves the final report deadline to March 30, 2026.","Section 15 moves the repeal of the Student Career Development Liability Insurance Advisory Committee (Commerce and Economic Opportunity Law Section 605-1110) to January 1, 2026.","Section 20 changes the Zero Traffic Fatalities Task Force dates. It must convene by July 1, 2025, report by January 1, 2026, and the Section is repealed January 1, 2027.","Section 30 extends the apprenticeship education expense credit (Income Tax Act Section 231) to taxable years beginning on or before January 1, 2026. The credit stays capped at $3,500 per apprentice and $5,000,000 aggregate per calendar year.","Other visible sections extend sunsets for the Public Defender Quality Defense Task Force (to January 1, 2026), the Illinois Power Agency Act home rule preemption (to January 1, 2026), and the Rockford Park District Elliot Golf Course sale authority (to January 1, 2026). They also amend the Reimagine Public Safety Act, and the visible text ends at Vehicle Code Section 6-308."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills is comparable to the visible text of this Illinois act. The Hawaii sports wagering bills (HB 1308, HB 2570, SB1569) and the Indiana and Delaware child-support bills (IN HB1092, DE HB187) deal directly with wagering, licensing or winnings. This act, as supplied, is a general sunset-extension omnibus, and any gambling-disorder language would be in a truncated section that was not provided.","watch":"Review the full enrolled text, especially any DHS gambling-disorder section (for example a funding, program or reporting requirement) and whether it adds any assessment, fee or compliance obligation on licensed sports-betting or iGaming operators, because none is visible in the text supplied.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":140552,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:44:58.292Z"},"IL-SB590":{"summary":"IL SB0590 is a shell bill that makes a technical change to Section 25-1 (short title) of the Sports Wagering Act, 230 ILCS 45/25-1. It adds no substantive regulatory, tax, licensing or consumer-protection provisions. It died at sine die on 2025-01-07 with no further action.","mechanics":["Amends only 230 ILCS 45/25-1, the short-title section of Article 25 of the Illinois Sports Wagering Act.","The change is a wording or formatting tweak. The text still reads 'This Article may be cited as the Sports Wagering Act' and 'this Act' still means this Article.","No tax rates, licence categories, definitions, thresholds, effective dates or enforcement provisions are added or changed.","Status is Failed/Dead: introduced 2/2/2023 by Sen. Don Harmon and adjourned sine die 2025-01-07 without enactment."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"Nothing comparable on the list is a shell bill. Substantive bills such as GA SB57, HI HB 1308, AK HB145 and DE HB365 set up licensing, tax and regulatory frameworks, while IL SB0590 changes only the short-title section of an existing Act. Illinois already has a full regime under the Sports Wagering Act, so this bill is probably a placeholder vehicle for later amendment. The 'GAMING-TECH' label suggests nothing about the actual text.","watch":"Because the bill is dead, there is nothing to act on now, but a bill like this could have been used as a vehicle for later amendments to the Illinois Sports Wagering Act, so watch for similar shell bills being reused in future sessions.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":762,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:45:00.491Z"},"IL-SB591":{"summary":"IL SB0591 is a shell bill that amends Section 25-1 (short title) of the Illinois Sports Wagering Act (230 ILCS 45/25-1) with a purely technical change. It adds no substantive regulation, tax, licensing or enforcement provisions. It died at sine die on 2025-01-07 without further action.","mechanics":["Amends only 230 ILCS 45/25-1, the short-title section of the Sports Wagering Act.","The only textual change is a stylistic edit to the short-title sentence (the text shows a duplicated 'the' being fixed); the Act's name and the 'this Act' reference are unchanged.","No tax rates, license fees, definitions, thresholds or covered persons are altered.","No effective date or enforcement provisions are included; the bill was introduced 2/2/2023 by Sen. Don Harmon and failed at sine die on 2025-01-07.","As a placeholder bill, it could have been used as a vehicle for later substantive amendments to the Sports Wagering Act, but none were adopted."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike the substantive sports-wagering bills listed, such as AK HB145 and SB194 (mobile wagering authorization and tax), GA SB57 and HB380 (full regulatory frameworks), and HI HB 1308 (licensing and excise tax), IL SB0591 contains no operative provisions. It is closer to a placeholder vehicle than to the CT bills (HB05229, SB01464) that add advertising, withdrawal or customer-service rules. Nothing in the listed bills is comparable to a one-word technical change to a short title.","watch":"Because the bill is dead and substantively empty, the only point to watch is whether the Illinois Sports Wagering Act is later used as a shell vehicle for amendments, such as tax-rate changes or per-wager fees, in a future session.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":762,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:45:01.278Z"},"IL-SB593":{"summary":"IL SB0593 is a shell bill that amends Section 25-1 (short title) of the Illinois Sports Wagering Act (230 ILCS 45/25-1) to make a purely technical change to the citation language. It contains no substantive regulatory, tax, licensing or consumer-protection provisions. The bill died at sine die adjournment on 2025-01-07.","mechanics":["Sole operative provision: Section 5 amends 230 ILCS 45/25-1, the short-title section of the Sports Wagering Act (Article 25 of the Illinois Gambling Act framework).","The change is a technical edit to the short-title text (the introduced text shows a duplicated 'the' being struck); the 'this Act' reference language is retained.","No tax rate, license, fee, definition, wagering-limit, advertising or enforcement provision is added or changed.","No effective date clause is included, so the default Illinois effective date would apply had it passed.","Status: failed; last action was Session Sine Die on 2025-01-07, with no recorded movement beyond introduction on 2/2/2023 by Sen. Don Harmon."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"Nothing in the listed bills is comparable in substance. Unlike the full sports-wagering frameworks or tax measures in AK HB145/SB194, DE HB365, HI HB1308/HB2570/SB1569, GA SB57, or the Connecticut advertising and payment bills (CT HB05229, HB05269), IL SB0593 contains no operative content. It is a placeholder shell bill of the type Illinois leadership files to preserve a vehicle for later amendment, and it amends an already-existing Act rather than creating a new regime.","watch":"Because it is an empty shell bill amending the Sports Wagering Act, the only risk was that it could have been used as a vehicle for late substantive amendments (tax rate, player-prop or advertising changes), but it is now dead and requires no action beyond monitoring for similar shell bills in the new session.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":762,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:45:04.669Z"},"IN-HB1053":{"summary":"Indiana HEA 1053 is an omnibus bill on lottery, horse racing, casino occupational licensing and alcohol permits. For gaming, it defines \"lottery courier service\" and makes operating one without written Indiana Lottery Commission authorization a Class A misdemeanor. It also bars the lottery and gaming commissions from authorizing couriers absent specific legislation, adds a lottery records confidentiality provision, and broadens occupational licensing and fingerprint checks for casino and sports wagering personnel.","mechanics":["IC 4-30-2-4.3 defines \"lottery courier service\" as a for-profit service that buys or facilitates buying lottery tickets for others and delivers the tickets or electronic images of them for compensation; IC 4-30-3-7.5 makes operating one without the commission's written authorization a Class A misdemeanor and creates no duty or legal right to authorize one (effective July 1, 2025).","IC 4-30-3-20(b)(3) adds lottery courier services to the list of things the lottery commission and Indiana Gaming Commission may not operate or authorize, independently or through public-private partnership, unless a statute passed by the general assembly grants authority. The carve-out in subsection (a) for activities authorized under IC 4-38 (sports wagering) is unchanged.","IC 4-30-3-22 makes confidential under the public records law lottery ticket order history, ticket inventory, and records whose disclosure could impair lottery game security, integrity or fairness, or retailer security. IC 4-30-3-7 also adds bulk purchase of lottery products to the commission's rulemaking list.","New IC 4-33-8-1.5 requires an occupational license for individuals designated under 68 IAC 2-2, 2-3 or 27-2, key persons, substantial owners (both as the commission defines), and any employee, agent or affiliate of a gaming operation who holds or has applied for a license under IC 4-33-6, 4-33-7, IC 4-35 or IC 4-38 and whom the commission deems necessary for compliance. The IC 4-38 reference reaches sports wagering licensees' personnel.","IC 4-33-4-18 now requires applicants and licensees under IC 4-33-8-1.5 to submit fingerprints for state police and FBI criminal history checks; the state police may charge the commission a fee, paid from occupational license fees. IC 4-33-8-6 conforms the fingerprint fee language.","Horse racing changes: the Indiana Horse Racing Commission may incorporate the ARCI model rules and ARCI foreign-substance rules by reference, steward-imposed suspensions rise from one year to three years, the window to impose sanctions rises from 180 to 365 days, and from July 1, 2025 the governor appoints the director. The bill also changes alcohol rules, including artisan distiller caps (20,000 to 30,000 gallons) and wine dealer delivery. Section 27 declares an emergency, though most sections take effect July 1, 2025."],"issue_tags":["licensing","integrity"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"HEA 1053 is the only bill listed that regulates lottery courier services, and it does so with a criminal penalty for unauthorized operation. Connecticut SB01235 also prohibits lottery ticket resales and ticket courier services, and it sits in a Department of Consumer Protection lottery and gaming recommendations bill. The Indiana bill is not a sports betting expansion or tax measure like AK HB145/SB194, DE HB365 or the Georgia bills. Its sports wagering relevance is limited to the occupational licensing and fingerprinting expansion, which covers IC 4-38 personnel and is similar in kind to the criminal history check theme in AK HB145. The rest is horse racing and alcohol housekeeping.","watch":"Operators should check how the Gaming Commission uses the new IC 4-33-8-1.5(4) discretion to require occupational licenses of employees, agents and affiliates of IC 4-38 sports wagering licensees, and whether any ticket-courier or third-party purchasing model could fall within the new Class A misdemeanor and the IC 4-30-3-20(b)(3) bar.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":49476,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:45:13.747Z"},"IL-SB584":{"summary":"Public Act 103-0574 is an omnibus gaming bill amending the Illinois Lottery Law (Sec. 21.4) and the Illinois Gambling Act (Secs. 7 and 13). It sets the term and funding mechanics of the joint special instant scratch-off game, and it carries owners-licence and casino wagering-tax provisions, including a tax-relief mechanism for the East St. Louis casino tied to a new Collinsville organization gaming facility. The text supplied is truncated mid-Section 13(a-7) and shows no strike/underline markup, so anything after that point, including any sports wagering provisions, cannot be assessed.","mechanics":["Lottery Law Sec. 21.4: the joint special instant scratch-off game starts January 1, 2024 (or as soon as practical, at the Director's discretion) and ends January 1, 2027. It benefits the causes in Secs. 21.5 to 21.16, with net revenue split equally among causes. New causes after February 1, 2024 need a sunset date, and no more than 10 causes may be supported at once.","Gambling Act Sec. 13(a-5)(4): for 10 calendar years from the start of operations at an organization gaming facility in Collinsville (only if it opens within 3 years of the amendatory Act's effective date), the East St. Louis casino's privilege tax is reduced by the shortfall between 2022 adjusted gross receipts and the current year's.","The East St. Louis relief is conditioned on: at least 350 employees (waived during State-ordered closures), operations consistent with the Act and Board-approved viability, and Board-preapproved audited financial statements under FASB ASC nongovernmental GAAP.","Sec. 13(a-5) rate schedules are reproduced in the text: non-table games run from 15% to 50% of annual AGR in tiers. Table games are 15% up to $25M and 20% above. The Chicago casino pays split State/City tiers, e.g. 12% + 10.5% on the first $25M of slots, rising to 40% + 34.7% above $1B.","Sec. 7(l): temporary-facility gaming is allowed for up to 24 months. On a showing of good cause the Board shall extend by up to 30 months for the (e-5)(3) licensee (Waukegan) and by up to 12 months for all other licensees.","Sec. 7 licensing terms are reproduced: 4,000 gaming positions for Chicago, 2,000 for most others and 1,200 for Williamson County. Initial fees are at least $17,500 per position outside Cook County and $30,000 inside it. A $15M reconciliation fee applies on issuance, and a 75% of most-lucrative-year AGR reconciliation payment falls due after 3 years. Licences renewed after the 102nd GA amendment run 4 years."],"issue_tags":["tax","licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"None of the listed bills is comparable: they cover mobile sports wagering, fantasy sports, advertising and consumer protection (e.g. AK HB145/SB194, GA HB380, CT HB05229, HI HB1308), not land-based casino privilege tax or owners licences. The nearest overlap is DE HB187, which also touches video-lottery operators, but only for child-support intercepts. Unusual here is a carve-out that cuts one licensee's tax by reference to revenue lost to a competing facility (Collinsville), conditioned on employment and audit requirements.","watch":"An operator should check the unseen remainder of the Act for any sports wagering or online provisions, and note the new Collinsville/East St. Louis tax adjustment and Waukegan extension, which show the legislature will reallocate tax burden and licence timing licensee by licensee.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":72835,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:45:13.930Z"},"IL-SB89":{"summary":"Public Act 103-0004 (SB0089) is an omnibus regulatory measure. The portion visible here amends the Energy Efficient Building Act, the Public Building Commission Act, the University of Illinois Act, the Sports Wagering Act, the Liquor Control Act and the Clerks of Courts Act, mostly by extending sunset dates and deadlines. For sports betting, it amends 230 ILCS 45/25-25(d-5) to extend by one year the pilot allowing in-person wagers on Illinois collegiate teams. The supplied text is cut off mid-Clerks of Courts Act, so any later sections, including whatever the title's IIGF/cybersecurity reference covers, are not visible.","mechanics":["Sec. 9 amends 230 ILCS 45/25-25(d-5): the window for accepting wagers on events involving an Illinois collegiate team now runs from the 102nd General Assembly amendatory Act's effective date until July 1, 2024. The prior end date was July 1, 2023.","The exception stays narrow. The wager must be a tier 1 wager, must not relate to an individual athlete's performance, and must be made in person, not over the Internet or through a mobile application.","The general rule in 25-25(d) is unchanged: licensees may not accept wagers on Illinois collegiate team events outside the (d-5) exception. Unchanged provisions also keep the minimum wagering age at 21, the ban on minor league wagers, the ban on kindergarten-through-12th-grade events, the in-State physical presence requirement, and the tier 1 and tier 2 official league data framework.","The Sports Wagering Act change is only a date extension. It adds no new rates, license categories or enforcement provisions.","Other visible sections are unrelated to gaming. They extend the Public Building Commission design-build sunset to July 1, 2025, push the Illinois Stretch Energy Code deadlines into 2024, extend the water rates report deadlines, extend the Liquor Control Act mixed-drink delivery sunset to August 1, 2028, and amend circuit court clerk fees."],"issue_tags":["prop","integrity"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"Nothing listed is directly comparable. The listed bills are mostly full sports-wagering frameworks or taxes (AK HB145/SB194, GA SB57, DE HB365, HI HB 1308), advertising and consumer-protection bills (CT HB05229, CT HB05269), or student-athlete and NIL bills (DE HB314, GA HB1276, HI HB 2384). Illinois SB0089 does none of this. It is an omnibus act whose only gaming content is a one-year extension of the in-person, tier 1, no-player-prop collegiate wagering pilot. That is the closest overlap with the collegiate-focused advertising limits in CT HB05229 and the student-athlete protection theme of DE HB314, but it is not a new restriction.","watch":"Confirm that the in-person-only, tier 1, no-individual-athlete-performance limits on Illinois collegiate wagering still apply through July 1, 2024. Expect the question of making the pilot permanent or ending it to come back, and review the truncated remainder of the act for any other gaming provisions.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":66806,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:45:15.606Z"},"IN-HB1432":{"summary":"HB 1432 is an omnibus gaming bill. It authorizes the Indiana Lottery Commission to sell draw games and eInstant games online and to authorize courier services. It also creates a regulated iGaming regime (new IC 4-39) with an adjusted gross receipts tax of 22% to 30%, and raises the mobile sports wagering tax from 9.5% to 20%. It adds licensing-integrity, advertising, responsible-gaming and revenue-distribution provisions. The text supplied is cut off in IC 4-33-13-5, so the IC 4-39, sports-tax and advertising provisions are described here only from the digest.","mechanics":["Lottery: new IC 4-30-3-20(c) lets the commission operate or authorize online sales of draw games and eInstant games. The ban on online casino-style games, slot-simulating digital games and video lottery terminals stays unless a statute grants authority. Rules are required for online sales (IC 4-30-3-7(a)(13)) and for bulk purchases of lottery products (IC 4-30-3-7(a)(14)).","Couriers: IC 4-30-3-7(b) lets the commission authorize lottery courier services by rule (applications, certificates of authority, contract terms, fees and civil penalties). IC 4-30-3-7.5 makes operating a courier service without written commission authorization a Class A misdemeanor. The commission is not required to authorize any courier.","Digital lottery retailer fund: new IC 4-30-20 requires 2% of net quarterly internet digital lottery sales to be deposited into the fund within 30 days of quarter end. The commission must run a payment program compensating retailers that promote digital lottery games.","iGaming and sports tax (per digest, IC 4-39 not in the text provided): licensed owners, the historic-hotel-district operating agent, racetrack permit holders and qualifying Indian tribes may offer interactive gaming. Management vendors are licensed, and the adjusted gross receipts tax runs 22% to 30%. The sports wagering tax rises from 9.5% to 20% for mobile wagers and stays at 9.5% for in-facility wagers. Adjusted gross receipts under IC 4-33-2-2 now exclude iGaming receipts.","Licensing: new IC 4-33-4-5.5 gives the Indiana Gaming Commission sole licensing authority over IC 4-33, 4-32.3, 4-35, 4-38 and 4-39 activities and bars it from capping the number of qualified licensees. Grounds for denial include the applicant, a key person, a substantial owner or an affiliate being involved in, or financially benefiting from, illegal gambling in any U.S. jurisdiction (subsection (c)(4)-(5)). Subsection (d) lists mitigating factors such as pardon or restoration of rights, law-abiding conduct since, and severing ties with the person involved.","Other conduct rules (per digest unless stated): sports wagering certificate holders and vendors may not accept wagers on election outcomes or run false or misleading advertising. A new lottery voluntary exclusion program (IC 4-30-3-21) covers digital lottery and couriers, with forfeited winnings going to the responsible gaming fund. A new responsible gaming and problem gambling services program and fund is funded by licensee fees and a $500,000 annual lottery transfer. Surplus lottery revenue is redirected to counties without riverboats, with a $300 volunteer fire department contribution. The riverboat revenue-sharing set-aside rises from $33M to $66M. Most provisions are effective July 1, 2025, and some on passage."],"issue_tags":["legalize","tax","licensing","advertising","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Indiana pairs iGaming legalization with a sharp sports-tax increase and goes further than most listed bills. AR HB1861 also creates an Interactive Gaming Act but is built around criminalizing illegal online casinos, while Indiana uses licensing-denial criteria tied to illegal-gambling involvement. Indiana's 20% mobile sports tax is a rate change to an existing market, unlike AK HB145 and DE HB365, which would create new mobile markets. On lottery couriers, Indiana authorizes them by rule with a misdemeanor for unauthorized operation, the opposite of CT SB01235, which would prohibit ticket couriers and resales. Its advertising and responsible-gaming provisions overlap with CT HB05229 and CT SB01464, but Indiana's are far less detailed on advertising.","watch":"Operators should watch the 20% mobile sports tax, the 22% to 30% iGaming tax, the broad new license-denial grounds reaching affiliates' past involvement in illegal gambling in any U.S. jurisdiction, and the sports advertising ban, which may be read broadly, while noting the bill was recommitted to Ways and Means on 2025-01-29 and may change.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Comm Sub","text_chars":217701,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:45:23.682Z"},"IN-SB394":{"summary":"Indiana SB 394 is an omnibus tax bill. Most of it covers property tax relief, income tax credits and deductions, sales tax exemptions, and cigarette and alcohol excise increases. The gaming provisions, effective July 1, 2025, raise Indiana's wagering taxes: the riverboat supplemental wagering tax, the graduated riverboat and slot machine taxes, and the sports wagering tax on adjusted gross receipts (AGR), which goes from 9.5% to 11%. The bill also raises the share of sports wagering tax revenue sent to the addiction services fund, while including a floor that protects general fund receipts.","mechanics":["Sports wagering (IC 4-38-10-1): the tax on certificate holders' AGR rises from 9.5% to 11%. IC 4-38-10-3(b) raises the transfer to the addiction services fund from 3.33% to 10% of the tax collected. Subsection (d) provides that the general fund deposit may not fall below the amount that would result from a 9.5% rate and a 3.33% transfer.","Supplemental wagering tax (IC 4-33-12-1.5): the cap on the quotient rises from 3.5% to 5% after June 30, 2025. The Vigo County inland casino rate rises from 2.9% to 5%. Under IC 4-33-12-6(c), revenue above what the old rates (3.5% or 2.9%) would have produced stays in the general fund and is not shared with local governments, the state fair commission or the mental health and addiction division.","Riverboat graduated AGR tax (IC 4-33-13-1.5): for fiscal years beginning after June 30, 2025, the bracket rates rise for both riverboats with $75M or more of prior-year AGR and smaller riverboats. For the larger group the rates go 10 to 11%, 20 to 22%, 25 to 27.5%, 30 to 33%, 35 to 38.5% and 40 to 44%. For the smaller group the first two brackets become 2.75% and 11%, and the third becomes 22%. The $2.5M additional tax in subsection (c) is unchanged.","Slot machine wagering tax (IC 4-35-8-1): the first $100M of AGR is taxed at 27.5% (up from 25%) for periods after June 30, 2025. AGR above $100M is taxed at 33% (up from 30%). The bill does not change the 88% base percentage or the monthly remittance rules.","Reporting mechanics in the riverboat and slot sections are carried forward unchanged: remittance on the 24th of each month, daily gaming activity reports to the commission, and electronic filing.","The other provisions are outside gaming: a veterans' property tax exemption, a homestead credit, renter and first-time-buyer credits, a utility sales tax exemption, a cigarette tax increase of $1 per pack, and higher beer, liquor, wine and cider excise taxes. The provided text is cut off partway through the sales tax sections, and no Section 5 appears between Sections 4 and 6."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"Indiana is raising rates in an existing market through an omnibus revenue bill. Alaska HB145 and SB194 and the Georgia bills (HB380, HB686, SB57, SB172, SB386) would authorize and tax new markets. Illinois HB3159 adds a 2% AGR levy from July 1, 2026 for fire and EMS, and Illinois HB5143 would end the per-wager mobile tax on July 1, 2026. Those are narrower and differently structured, whereas Indiana moves a flat AGR rate from 9.5% to 11% and raises casino and slot taxes at the same time. Indiana also increases the addiction services share while guaranteeing the general fund a floor.","watch":"Watch the 9.5% to 11% sports wagering rate, the roughly 10% bracket increases on riverboat and slot AGR, and the 10% addiction-fund diversion with its general fund floor, since together they raise the cost of operating in Indiana, and check the full engrossed text for any amendments to the rates or the apparently missing Section 5.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":186736,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:45:32.446Z"},"KS-HB2001":{"summary":"HB2001 (Kansas special session 2024) lets the secretary of commerce make agreements with NFL or MLB franchises located in a state adjacent to Kansas to establish up to two STAR bond districts and projects for a major professional sports complex. Alternatively, the secretary can approve such projects proposed by a city or county. It amends the STAR bonds financing act to widen the revenue sources, raise the financing cap and give the secretary more discretion, and it lets the Kansas development finance authority (KDFA) issue the bonds. Per the title, it also moves money from the state gaming revenues fund to the attracting professional sports to Kansas fund. The sections amending K.S.A. 79-4108, 79-41a03 and 79-4801 and setting the transfer terms fall in the truncated part of the text provided.","mechanics":["Section 1 caps authority at two districts and projects in total. No district can be approved and no agreement executed after June 30, 2025, unless the legislative coordinating council (LCC) extends the authority through June 30, 2026. The extension can be approved retroactively at the next LCC meeting after July 1, 2025.","The secretary cannot finalize any agreement without a majority vote of the LCC. City or county-established projects also need LCC approval first. Review can be closed to the public, the proposed agreement is confidential and exempt from the open records act, and the vote must be in open session. The executed agreement and its supporting documents become public, and the confidentiality provision expires July 1, 2029 unless reenacted.","K.S.A. 12-17,162 defines a qualifying project as a stadium of at least 30,000 seats for NFL or MLB use, or a practice or training facility, with combined capital investment of at least $1,000,000,000. District parcels need not be contiguous, and they may overlap earlier STAR districts, with the earlier outstanding bonds having repayment priority.","Financing can reach 70% of total project costs, versus 50% for ordinary STAR projects (12-17,164(b)(2)). Bond maturity can run up to 30 years, versus 20 (12-17,164(g)(2)). Additional issuance authority also needs LCC approval, up to 70% of the cost of the expansion (12-17,168(a)(4)).","Repayment sources under 12-17,169(a)(2)(A) include sales tax increment from all retail sales in the district. This covers up to 100% of new state sales tax over a base the secretary sets in the secretary's discretion, and local sales tax over a base the city or county sets with the secretary's approval. They also include up to 100% of the alcoholic liquor tax increment, and, if the secretary approves, money from the attracting professional sports to Kansas fund. Local sales, use and transient guest taxes can be pledged only if the city or county approves, by ordinance or resolution within 60 days of LCC approval of the agreement.","Under 12-17,164(a)(2), the secretary may independently establish the district and project, skipping the city or county notice, hearing and feasibility procedures of 12-17,165 and 12-17,166. KDFA bonds are limited obligations payable only from the pledged revenues and are not a state debt. The relocation bar in 12-17,164(e) does not apply to these projects."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No listed bill is comparable. The AK (HB145, SB194), HI, GA, CT and IA (HF673, HSB122) bills legalize, regulate or tax sports wagering or fantasy contests, while KS HB2001 is a stadium-financing bill that only draws on the state gaming revenues fund as a funding source. The closest analogue is IA SSB 1157, which also steers gambling-related revenue into a tourism and major-events program and fund. Unusual features here are the LCC veto and confidentiality over the agreements, the secretary's discretion over tax base years, and the pledge of alcohol-tax increment.","watch":"Check the truncated sections amending K.S.A. 79-4801, 79-4108 and 79-41a03, and the transfer language itself, to see whether any sports wagering revenue is diverted from the state gaming revenues fund, in what amount, and for how long, because the enrolled title says each fiscal year after FY2025 while the supplied bill description says FY2026 only if the LCC approves.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":82008,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:45:33.434Z"},"IN-SB94":{"summary":"Engrossed SB 94 is mostly an alcohol-permit bill, but it also adds a lottery-courier regime to IC 4-30. It defines \"lottery courier service,\" bars the lottery commission and the Indiana gaming commission from operating or authorizing one unless a statute specifically allows it, and makes unauthorized operation a Class A misdemeanor. It also directs rulemaking on bulk purchases of lottery products and makes lottery ticket order history, inventory, and security-sensitive records confidential. The remaining sections cover wine delivery, additional three-way permits, artisan distiller limits, and permit revocation notice.","mechanics":["Sec. 1 (IC 4-30-2-4.3): a \"lottery courier service\" is a for-profit service that buys or facilitates buying lottery tickets for others and delivers or transmits the tickets, or electronic images of them, for compensation.","Sec. 3 (IC 4-30-3-7.5): operating a lottery courier service without the commission's written authorization is a Class A misdemeanor. The section does not require the commission to authorize one or create a legal right to operate. Sec. 13 adds a cross-reference in IC 35-52-4-7.7.","Sec. 4 (IC 4-30-3-20(b)(3)): adds \"a lottery courier service\" to the list of things the lottery commission and gaming commission may not operate or authorize, alone or through public-private partnership, absent a specific statute. The existing section 20(a) exclusions still apply, including activity authorized under IC 4-38 (sports wagering), IC 4-33, and IC 4-35. Its ban on digital sales of casino-style games, scratch-offs, and draw games is retained.","Sec. 2 (IC 4-30-3-7): the commission must adopt rules on \"the bulk purchase of lottery products,\" with no thresholds or standards stated. Sec. 5 (IC 4-30-3-22) makes lottery ticket order history, ticket inventory, and records whose disclosure could impair lottery game security, integrity, fairness, or retailer security confidential under IC 5-14-3.","Alcohol provisions: a wine dealer that is not a package liquor store may deliver wine to a customer's residence or office, with delivery limited to the permit holder or a permitted employee and a one-year delivery record. Sec. 8 adds three-way permits for Warsaw (up to 3) and Syracuse (up to 1) at $40,000 each. Sec. 10 raises the artisan distiller cap from 20,000 to 30,000 gallons. Sec. 11 permits contract manufacturing between artisan distillers. Sec. 9 cuts permit-revocation hearing notice from 10 to 3 business days. Sec. 12 bars municipalities from requiring refreshment-area permittees to buy containers from a specific vendor.","Effective July 1, 2025. The text is the April 4 reprint after House amendment. The April 1 House motion deleted specific page and line ranges that I cannot map to the reprint, so I have not assumed what it removed."],"issue_tags":["licensing","integrity"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"The closest comparison is CT SB01235, whose summary also prohibits lottery ticket resales and ticket courier services. Indiana instead defines a courier service, bans it by default, and attaches a misdemeanor to unauthorized operation. CT SB01237 and CT SB01463 touch lottery administration, but nothing listed from AK, AR, DE, FL, GA, HI, IA or IL is comparable to the courier ban, since those bills mainly address sports wagering, iGaming, or tax. This bill creates no new sports-betting or iGaming regime, and its alcohol sections have no counterpart in the listed bills.","watch":"Check whether the broad courier definition, which reaches anyone who \"facilitates\" a lottery ticket purchase for pay and delivers electronic images, could catch sports-wagering or iGaming affiliates or promotions, and rely on the IC 4-30-3-20(a)(1)(E) carve-out for IC 4-38 activity, noting the tension between the \"written authorization\" language in §7.5 and the §20(b) bar on authorizing couriers absent a statute.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Engrossed","text_chars":37299,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:45:34.377Z"},"KS-HB2058":{"summary":"Senate Sub. for HB 2058 amends K.S.A. 2022 Supp. 46-2305, which governs the Governor's duty to negotiate sports wagering compacts with federally recognized Kansas tribes. Per the enrolled title, the purpose is to let a sports wagering compact include provisions on wagering outside the boundaries of the tribe's Indian lands. The printed text appears to reach this by removing the existing subsection (b), which barred such provisions. The bill is short and was signed April 19, 2023.","mechanics":["Sec. 1(a): if a federally recognized tribe (as described in K.S.A. 74-9802(f)) requests negotiation of a sports wagering compact under K.S.A. 46-2302, the Governor or the Governor's designee must negotiate in good faith.","Sec. 1(b): the text provided reads 'No compact described in subsection (a) shall include sports wagering beyond the boundaries of the compacting tribe's Indian lands' (IGRA, 25 U.S.C. 2701 et seq.). The strike-through formatting was lost in the copy provided, but the enrolled title ('authorizing any compact... to include provisions governing sports wagering outside the boundaries of Indian lands') indicates this prohibition is the language being deleted. Verify against the official chaptered version.","Sec. 2 repeals the existing version of K.S.A. 2022 Supp. 46-2305.","Sec. 3: effective upon publication in the Kansas register.","The enrolled text provided contains no tax rates, licensing criteria, or mobile or online wagering mechanics. It also has no provision on historical horse race tax revenue, the horse breeding development fund, or the horse fair racing benefit fund, even though the captioned title mentions them. That language appears to belong to an earlier version."],"issue_tags":["legalize","licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"None of the listed bills (AK HB145 and SB194, DE HB365, HI HB 1308/HB 2570/SB1569, GA HB380/SB172/SB57, and the Iowa and Connecticut bills) addresses tribal compacting. They set up state-run licensing and tax regimes for operators, whereas KS HB2058 only changes the permissible scope of tribal compact negotiations. It has no operator licensing, tax rate, or advertising provisions. The tax-crediting language for historical horse racing in the Kansas title has no counterpart in the listed bills and does not appear in the enrolled text provided.","watch":"Watch whether the removal of the on-Indian-lands limit in K.S.A. 46-2305 is used to support compacts under which a tribe offers statewide mobile wagering, and how any non-tribal operators would have to partner or skin with tribes under such a compact, including the IGRA and federal law questions that raises.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":2376,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:45:37.296Z"},"KS-HB2273":{"summary":"Omnibus state appropriations bill for fiscal years 2024, 2025 and 2026. It sets expenditure limits for state agencies, authorizes fund transfers and capital projects, and amends a list of existing statutes. The supplied text stops partway through Sec. 19 (Board of Veterinary Examiners) and contains no sports-betting, iGaming, or collectibles provisions.","mechanics":["Sec. 1 makes appropriations for FY2024, FY2025 and FY2026, exempts the act from K.S.A. 75-6702(a), and exempts the appropriations from K.S.A. 46-155.","Secs. 2-19 (the portion supplied) set fee-fund expenditure caps for licensing boards and financial regulators, for example the bank commissioner fee fund at $12,809,736 for FY2024 and $12,720,158 for FY2025, with official-hospitality sub-caps.","Several special litigation reserve funds are 'No limit' but require Director of the Budget approval under a three-part test before any expenditure. Capped fee-fund-to-litigation-fund transfers are authorized, e.g. $20,000 per year for Accountancy and $50,000 per year for Pharmacy.","Sec. 4(b) sends fines and settlement money designated for consumer education under the Kansas mortgage business act and the uniform consumer credit code to the consumer education settlement fund, notwithstanding K.S.A. 9-2209, 9-2218, 16a-2-302 and 16a-6-104.","Sec. 14(b) transfers $93,000 from the optometry litigation fund to the optometry fee fund on July 1, 2023.","The title lists amendments to K.S.A. 2-223, 12-1775a, 12-5256 and several K.S.A. 2022 Supp. sections, including 79-4804. Those amending sections are not in the supplied text, so I cannot say what they change."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. This is a general state-agency appropriations vehicle, and nothing in the supplied portion deals with wagering. The only possible gaming link is the title's reference to amending K.S.A. 79-4804, which is not reproduced in the text provided.","watch":"Check the unseen later sections and the amended statutes, especially K.S.A. 79-4804 and the other 79-series sections cited in the title, for any change to gaming-revenue fund distributions or lottery and gaming agency funding, and note that the bill died in Senate committee on 2024-04-30.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":301864,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:45:45.833Z"},"KS-HB2541":{"summary":"Kansas HB2541 creates a state conservation fund and three grant-making sub-funds (working lands, wildlife, and Kansas outdoors) administered by the Kansas Department of Agriculture and the Department of Wildlife and Parks. It is funded by a share of sporting-goods sales tax and by redirecting Kansas Lottery sports wagering revenue. Section 5 amends K.S.A. 74-8711 to send the 80% sports wagering share, currently going to the attracting professional sports to Kansas fund, to the conservation fund instead, and adds a $32,000,000 annual transfer from the lottery operating fund. The bill died in committee.","mechanics":["Sec. 1(b): each July 1 starting 2024, the Secretary of Revenue certifies state general fund retail sales and use tax from sporting goods (NAICS 451110), and 50% of that amount moves from the state general fund to the state conservation fund.","Sec. 5 (amending K.S.A. 74-8711(h)): beginning July 1, 2024, 80% of the sports wagering revenue remaining in the lottery operating fund, after the first $750,000 goes to the white collar crime fund, is redirected from the attracting professional sports to Kansas fund to the state conservation fund. The 2% problem gambling transfer in subsection (g) is left intact.","Sec. 5 (new K.S.A. 74-8711(i)): in addition to the 80% transfer, $32,000,000 moves from the lottery operating fund to the state conservation fund each July 1 starting 2024.","Sec. 1(c)-(e): each July 15 the conservation fund balance is split 50% to the working lands fund, 25% to the wildlife conservation fund, and the remainder to the Kansas outdoors fund. Money must supplement and not supplant existing appropriations.","Secs. 2-4: each sub-fund runs a rules-based grant program with an external review panel or volunteer advisory board. Eligible recipients are public entities and nonprofits, and applications with matching funds are prioritized. Legislative intent is that money be obligated within 12 months, with annual reports to the governor and legislature by December 1.","Sec. 7: effective on publication in the statute book. No new licensing, tax rate, or operator obligations are imposed on sports wagering operators."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"Unlike the listed bills, such as AK HB145 and SB194, HI HB1308 and HB2570, or GA SB386 and HB380, which create or regulate sports wagering markets and set tax rates, this bill leaves the Kansas regulatory framework untouched and only changes where existing lottery-run sports wagering revenue goes. The closest parallels are earmarking measures like CT SB01463, which sends online lottery and sports wagering revenue to the Early Childhood Care and Education Fund, and GA SB386, which directs funding to Pre-K. HB2541 is unusual in redirecting an existing 80% sports wagering share away from the professional sports attraction fund toward conservation, and in adding a flat $32 million annual draw on the lottery operating fund.","watch":"A lawyer for operators should note that the bill does not change operator taxes or licensing but shows legislative appetite to redirect Kansas sports wagering proceeds, and the flat $32,000,000 annual transfer could create pressure for higher revenue or fees; the bill is dead, however, so it is useful mainly as a signal.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":24857,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:45:46.530Z"},"KS-HB2184":{"summary":"HB 2184 is Kansas's omnibus appropriations bill for FY2023 through FY2028. It funds state agencies, authorizes payment of small claims against the state, and amends a number of statutes, including K.S.A. 2022 Supp. 74-8711 and 79-4804 per the title. The supplied text is cut off in the Real Estate Appraisal Board section, so I cannot see any gaming, lottery or sports-wagering provisions.","mechanics":["Sec. 1 makes appropriations and authorizes capital projects for FY2023-FY2028, and exempts the act from K.S.A. 75-6702(a) and K.S.A. 46-155.","Secs. 2-7 direct payment of individual claims for lost property, property damage and late motor-fuel tax refunds, each for a named claimant in a specific dollar amount (e.g., $5,713.54 from the adjutant general in Sec. 4).","Sec. 8 requires a signed release of all claims against the state before payment, except for Sec. 7 fuel tax refunds and transfers between state agencies.","Secs. 9-27 set FY2024 and FY2025 expenditure limits for licensing boards' fee funds and raise or lower some FY2023 limits (e.g., the bank commissioner fee fund goes from $12,087,285 to $12,554,267 in Sec. 11, and the state board of pharmacy fee fund drops from $3,273,406 to $2,457,604 in Sec. 25).","Sec. 14(b) lets the barbering board charge an examination fee of up to $150, and Sec. 15(b) bars the behavioral sciences board from conditioning licensure on diversity, equity and inclusion (DEI) or similar training.","Per the Last action line, the line-item veto (No. 143) was overridden 28-11 in the Senate on 2023-04-27, but the supplied text does not show which item that was."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. This is a general appropriations act for agency budgets and claims, and the visible text contains no gaming-specific provisions. The only gaming-adjacent signal is the title's reference to amending K.S.A. 74-8711 and 79-4804, which I cannot confirm from the text provided.","watch":"Get the full enrolled text and check the amendments to K.S.A. 74-8711 and 79-4804, plus any Kansas Lottery, Racing and Gaming Commission or gaming-revenue fund lines, for changes to sports-wagering or iGaming funding, fund transfers or fees, and find out what the overridden line-item veto 143 covered.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":418873,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:45:47.468Z"},"KS-HB2422":{"summary":"The text supplied is the introduced version of HB 2422, which has nothing to do with gaming. It amends K.S.A. 44-918 on the state fire marshal's boiler-safety inspectors and, per its title, removes the statutory qualifications for the chief inspector. The Senate Substitute title says it would exempt certain gaming suppliers from certification under the Kansas Expanded Lottery Act if they do not contract with the state or the lottery gaming facility manager, but that language is not in the text provided, so I cannot analyse it. The bill died on the Senate calendar on 2024-04-30.","mechanics":["Sec. 1 amends K.S.A. 44-918, which covers the fire marshal's appointment of a chief inspector and deputy inspectors in the unclassified civil service, with compensation subject to the governor's approval.","Subsection (c)(1) currently requires the chief inspector to have at least five years of boiler or pressure-vessel experience and a National Board commission with in-service, 'A' and 'B' endorsements. The 'B' endorsement may be acquired within 18 months of appointment. The title says this qualification requirement is eliminated.","Subsection (c)(2) sets deputy inspector qualifications: two years of combined education, training and experience, or five years in HVAC or plumbing related to boilers. A deputy must also hold an in-service National Board commission, or be able to acquire one within 12 months.","Subsection (b) keeps the enforcement powers: maintaining boiler records, issuing, suspending or revoking inspection certificates under K.S.A. 44-924, and prosecuting violators.","Sec. 2 repeals the existing K.S.A. 44-918, and Sec. 3 makes the act effective on publication in the statute book.","The text provided has no gaming-supplier certification provisions, no definitions of 'gaming supplier', and no reference to the Kansas Expanded Lottery Act."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"The text provided has no gaming content, so it cannot be compared with the other states' bills. If the Senate Substitute does what its title says, it is a narrow carve-out from supplier certification, unlike the full supplier licensing regimes in HI HB1308, HI HB2570 and HI SB1569. It is also unlike the vendor licensing and books-and-records changes in CT SB01235 and the ticket-distribution vendor-license exemption in CT SB01237. The closest analogue is CT SB01237, but that concerns lottery ticket distribution rather than casino or gaming suppliers. None of the other listed bills turns on a supplier's contracting relationship with the state or a facility manager.","watch":"Obtain the actual Senate Substitute text before relying on this entry, and then check how it defines 'gaming supplier' and 'does not contract with' the state or the facility manager, since that wording would determine whether operator-facing or indirect suppliers fall outside the certification requirement.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":3501,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:45:48.133Z"},"KS-HR6026":{"summary":"HR 6026 is a one-paragraph House resolution approving an amendment to the gaming compact between the State of Kansas and the Prairie Band Potawatomi Nation. The amendment was submitted by the Governor to the Joint Committee on State-Tribal Relations on April 27, 2023. The resolution itself contains no sports wagering terms; its title says the amendment concerns sports wagering, but the substance sits in the compact amendment, which is not reproduced in the text.","mechanics":["Sole operative text: the amendment submitted by the Governor to the Joint Committee on State-Tribal Relations on April 27, 2023 'is hereby approved.'","Covers only the Prairie Band Potawatomi Nation compact; no other tribe is named in the text.","The resolution sets no tax rate, license fee, revenue share, geographic scope, or definition of sports wagering. Any such terms are in the separate compact amendment.","No effective date, sunset, or enforcement provision appears in the resolution.","The enrolled text shows House adoption only; the title and text do not describe how the amendment fits with the state's statutory sports wagering framework."],"issue_tags":["licensing","legalize"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. On its own, this is unusual because it is a bare approval resolution and not a regulatory statute: it incorporates a tribal-state compact amendment by reference and contains no substantive wagering provisions. The operative terms must be read in the compact amendment itself.","watch":"Obtain the actual Prairie Band Potawatomi compact amendment submitted April 27, 2023, since the resolution does not reproduce it, and check its terms on tribal exclusivity, mobile and online wagering, and any role for third-party operators or platforms.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":860,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:45:52.499Z"},"KS-HR6045":{"summary":"HR 6045 is a one-paragraph House resolution approving an amendment to the gaming compact between the State of Kansas and the Iowa Tribe of Kansas and Nebraska. The amendment was submitted by the Governor to the Joint Committee on State-Tribal Relations on April 1, 2024. The resolution text does not state what the amendment does; the title says it concerns sports wagering, but the operative terms are in the separate compact amendment, which is not reproduced here.","mechanics":["Sole operative provision: the amendment submitted by the Governor to the Joint Committee on State-Tribal Relations on April 1, 2024, is 'hereby approved.'","Parties to the compact: State of Kansas and the Iowa Tribe of Kansas and Nebraska.","The resolution text contains no tax rates, revenue shares, licensing terms, geographic or mobile-platform limits, or definitions; these would be in the compact amendment itself.","No effective date or enforcement provision appears in the resolution; the title alone indicates the amendment concerns sports wagering.","Procedural posture: originated in and adopted by the House; status is signed into law with last action 2024-04-05."],"issue_tags":["legalize","licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. On its own, this is unusual because it is a bare approval resolution rather than a statute: it incorporates the compact amendment by reference and gives no substantive terms. Any analysis of sports-wagering rules for the Iowa Tribe in Kansas depends on obtaining the amendment as submitted on April 1, 2024.","watch":"Obtain the underlying compact amendment submitted April 1, 2024, since the resolution is silent on key terms such as mobile or online wagering scope, revenue sharing, platform or vendor licensing, and the interaction with the Kansas Sports Wagering Act.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":891,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:45:53.476Z"},"KS-HB2667":{"summary":"HB 2667 amends K.S.A. 2023 Supp. 74-8711, the statute governing the Kansas lottery operating fund, to raise the annual cap on transfers of lottery ticket vending machine (LTVM) net profits to the community crisis stabilization centers fund and the clubhouse model program fund. The cap for FY2025 and later goes from $8,000,000 to $12,000,000. The rest of the section, including the sports wagering revenue transfers, is left as is. The bill died on the House calendar.","mechanics":["Sec. 1(e)(2): the aggregate cap on LTVM net-profit transfers in FY2025 and each later fiscal year rises from $8,000,000 to $12,000,000. The FY2023 and FY2024 cap stays at $9,000,000.","Sec. 1(e)(1) is unchanged. The lottery director certifies LTVM net profits monthly by the 10th, and the transfer is split 75% to the community crisis stabilization centers fund and 25% to the clubhouse model program fund, both at KDADS.","Sports wagering revenue waterfall is unchanged. Under (f), the first $750,000 each July 1 goes to the white collar crime fund. Under (g), 2% of the remainder goes to the problem gambling and addictions grant fund. Under (h), 80% of the remainder goes to the attracting professional sports to Kansas fund.","Sec. 1(d) is unchanged. Monthly transfers to the state gaming revenues fund are the greater of excess fund moneys or at least 30% of ticket and share sales (20% for pull-tabs).","Sec. 3: effective on publication in the statute book. Sec. 2 repeals the existing version of the section."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. The bill is unusual in that it is a narrow earmark adjustment. It touches no sports wagering rules and only restates the existing sports wagering revenue allocations in 74-8711(f)-(h). Its only substantive change is the higher LTVM transfer cap.","watch":"The bill does not change the sports wagering transfers, but operators should watch any later amendment to 74-8711(f)-(h) that alters the $750,000 white collar crime set-aside or the 2% and 80% allocations, and any competing demand on lottery operating fund money.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":7019,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:45:53.808Z"},"KS-HB2551":{"summary":"Kansas HB 2551 is the 2024 omnibus reconciliation appropriations bill, covering FY2024 through FY2028 across many state agencies, plus small claims payments. Gaming and sports wagering appear only in a few agency provisions: Kansas Lottery transfers (Secs. 27-28), Racing and Gaming Commission historical horse race machine funding (Sec. 29), and Commerce Department transfers funded from sports-wagering-linked funds (Sec. 31(e)). It does not change any wagering tax rate, licensing rule or consumer-protection requirement.","mechanics":["Sec. 27: raises the FY2024 transfer from the lottery operating fund to the state gaming revenues fund from $72,490,000 to $75,490,000.","Sec. 28: for FY2025 and FY2026, the budget director certifies lottery transfers above or below a $71,490,000 baseline. Any excess goes to the attracting professional sports to Kansas fund (300-00-2942), and no transfer is made if the total is below the baseline. Sports wagering revenues deposited in the lottery operating fund are excluded from the certified amount.","Sec. 29: for FY2025 and FY2026, the Racing and Gaming Commission may use K.S.A. 74-8823(a)(5) remittances credited to the state racing fund to cover its costs of enforcing and overseeing historical horse race machines. Once those costs are covered, the remainder is split 1/3 to the Kansas horse breeding development fund and 2/3 to the horse fair racing benefit fund.","Sec. 31(e): on July 1, 2024, $2,000,000 moves from the attracting professional sports to Kansas fund to the existing horse racing facility remodel fund, notwithstanding K.S.A. 2023 Supp. 74-8793.","Sec. 31(a),(f) and Sec. 32: $200,000 for sports hall of fame support in each of FY2025 and FY2026, and the sports hall of fame support fund is abolished from July 1, 2024.","The bill is signed into law, with line-item vetoes sustained on 2024-04-30. The text provided is truncated after Sec. 34, so later provisions are not reviewed."],"issue_tags":["tax","licensing"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"This bill is not comparable to the listed gaming bills. Unlike Georgia SB57, Hawaii HB 1308 and HB 2570, or Illinois HB5443, it creates no sports wagering framework, tax rate or licensing regime. Its closest overlap is with Illinois budget and finance bills such as IL HB4582 and the lottery financial-function bills such as IL HB5230, since it only directs how existing gaming and lottery revenue flows. Unusual here is that the lottery transfer baseline expressly excludes sports wagering revenues, and surplus lottery money funds a professional-sports attraction fund.","watch":"Watch the Sec. 28 exclusion of sports wagering revenue from the lottery transfer baseline and the use of the attracting professional sports to Kansas fund, since these signal how the state may earmark sports-betting-related revenue in future budget bills.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":139542,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:45:54.450Z"},"KS-SB42":{"summary":"Kansas HS for SB 42 exempts rural emergency hospitals licensed under the rural emergency hospital act from the hospital provider assessment in K.S.A. 65-6208, and changes eligibility rules for hospital board members under K.S.A. 80-2506. It has no connection to sports betting, iGaming, or collectibles; it is a health-care financing and hospital governance measure.","mechanics":["Sec. 1 amends K.S.A. 65-6209(a) to add rural emergency hospitals licensed under K.S.A. 2022 Supp. 65-481 et seq. to the list of providers exempt from the K.S.A. 65-6208 assessment, alongside state agencies, the authority, state educational institutions, and critical access hospitals.","Sec. 1(b) keeps the existing exemption for hospitals the department operates for mental health or developmental disabilities functions.","Sec. 2 amends K.S.A. 80-2506(a): board members must be qualified electors of the hospital's county or an adjacent county, and an adjacent-county elector must own real property in the hospital's taxing district.","Sec. 2(a) requires a majority of board members to be residents of the county where the hospital is located. Boards stay at three, five, seven, or nine members.","The remainder of 80-2506 (petition to change board size at 5% of electors, three-year terms, staggered terms, vacancies filled by the chair with board consent) is carried forward with only technical drafting changes.","Sec. 4 makes the act effective on publication in the Kansas register. The bill was signed by the Governor on April 20, 2023."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. On its own, this bill is unusual in that it contains no wagering, gaming, or collectibles provisions at all. It only amends Kansas hospital assessment and hospital district governance statutes.","watch":"Nothing in this bill affects sports-betting, iGaming, or trading-card operators, so it needs no compliance action beyond confirming it was tagged in error.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":6115,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:45:59.726Z"},"KS-SB322":{"summary":"SB 322 amends K.S.A. 46-2305, the Kansas statute on gaming compacts with federally recognized tribes for sports wagering. Per the title, it would let a compact cover sports wagering outside the tribe's Indian lands (for example, statewide mobile wagering run through the tribe). The introduced text as supplied lacks strike-through formatting, so the deletion of the existing limit in subsection (b) is inferred from the title and structure. The bill died in the Senate committee on 2024-04-30.","mechanics":["Section 1 amends K.S.A. 2022 Supp. 46-2305. Subsection (a), which is retained, requires the governor or designee to negotiate in good faith with a federally recognized tribe (as described in K.S.A. 74-9802(f)) that requests a sports wagering compact under K.S.A. 46-2302.","Subsection (b) currently bars any such compact from including sports wagering beyond the boundaries of the tribe's Indian lands within the meaning of IGRA (25 U.S.C. § 2701 et seq.). The title indicates this bar is struck, but the supplied text does not show the strike-through.","Sec. 2 repeals the existing version of 46-2305.","Sec. 3 makes the act effective on publication in the Kansas register.","The bill has no tax rate, license fee, operator licensing, consumer protection or enforcement provisions. It changes only the permitted scope of tribal compacts."],"issue_tags":["legalize"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"None of the listed bills is a close comparator, and none is from Kansas. AK HB145 and SB194, DE HB365 and the Georgia bills (e.g. GA SB57, HB380) create full mobile sports wagering regimes with operator licensing and, in several cases, taxes. SB 322 is a one-section amendment to the tribal compact statute that sets no tax or licensing rules, and it would route off-reservation wagering through tribal compacts rather than state-licensed operators.","watch":"If a bill like this is revived, watch whether off-Indian-lands wagering authority ends up concentrated in tribal compact partners, which would force operators to access the Kansas market through tribal skins or market-access deals, and whether the compact terms leave room for any state-licensed non-tribal operators.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":1390,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:46:03.492Z"},"KS-SB28":{"summary":"The supplied text (the Introduced version) does not match the title. It is an insurance bill, not an appropriations bill. It ends payments into the group-funded pools fee fund and the group-funded workers' compensation fee fund, moves their balances into a new group-funded pools refund fund, and refunds that money pro rata to contributing entities before abolishing the refund fund on July 1, 2024. It contains nothing on wagering, gaming, or collectibles. The title and the veto-override history point to a later vehicle bill for state appropriations, whose enrolled text was not provided.","mechanics":["New Sec. 1 creates the group-funded pools refund fund, to be used only to refund entities that paid into the fee funds under K.S.A. 12-2623 and 44-587 before their repeal.","Refunds are pro rata, based on premium taxes each entity paid in fiscal year 2022 (Sec. 1(b)(2)). Expenditures require appropriations acts and warrants on vouchers approved by the insurance commissioner.","On July 1, 2023, the balances and liabilities of both fee funds transfer to the refund fund and the fee funds are abolished. The refund fund itself is abolished on July 1, 2024.","Sec. 2 amends K.S.A. 44-588 to drop the reference to the K.S.A. 44-587 fees. Group-funded workers' compensation pools must still pay a 1% annual tax on Kansas gross premium within 90 days after fiscal year end, less returned premiums, dividends, and excess insurance purchases.","Sec. 3 repeals K.S.A. 12-2623, 44-587 and the existing 44-588. Sec. 4 makes the act effective on publication in the statute book."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. On its own, this bill is unusual because the text provided (insurance pool fee funds and a 1% premium tax on workers' compensation pools) has no connection to the appropriations title or to gaming. The mismatch suggests the bill became a vehicle for the final appropriations act.","watch":"Review the enrolled or enacted appropriations text, not this Introduced version, for any lines touching the Kansas Lottery, sports wagering revenue funds, or gaming regulators, and note that the line-item veto on items 112a and 116a was overridden 97-28.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":3504,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:46:03.838Z"},"KS-SB155":{"summary":"Substitute for SB 155 is an omnibus state appropriations bill covering fiscal years 2024, 2025 and 2026. The text provided runs only through Sec. 19 and consists of fee-fund appropriations for professional and financial licensing boards. None of the provided text addresses sports betting, iGaming, or collectibles and trading cards, and the bill died in House committee.","mechanics":["Sec. 1 authorizes appropriations, transfers, capital projects and fees for FY2024-FY2026, and exempts the act from K.S.A. 75-6702(a) and K.S.A. 46-155.","Secs. 2-19 appropriate from each agency's own fee fund, with expenditure caps. Examples are Board of Accountancy $482,372 for FY2024 and the State Bank Commissioner fee fund $12,809,736 for FY2024. Many boards also have 'no limit' litigation, gifts or federal funds.","Most boards have official hospitality caps of $300 to $2,000. Disciplinary hearing costs are placed outside the caps for the Behavioral Sciences Regulatory Board and the Board of Healing Arts.","Litigation reserve fund spending requires budget director approval on three findings: an unforeseeable need, no prior legislative rejection, and a valid relationship to agency functions. Fee-fund-to-litigation-fund transfers are capped at $20,000 per year for the accountancy, real estate appraisal and real estate commission boards, and at $50,000 for the Board of Pharmacy.","Sec. 4(b) routes fines and settlement money received under the Kansas mortgage business act and the uniform consumer credit code, if designated for consumer education, to the consumer education settlement fund.","The title shows amendments to K.S.A. 2-223, 12-1775a, 12-5256 and several 2022 Supp. sections (including 74-99b34, 79-4804 and 79-3425i). Those amendatory sections are not in the text provided, so I cannot say what they do."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. This is a general appropriations vehicle for fee-funded licensing agencies, so it is not a gaming policy bill. The only gaming-relevant content could be in the truncated later sections or the amended statutes cited in the title, which I cannot confirm from the text provided.","watch":"Check the full enrolled text, especially any sections beyond Sec. 19 and the amended statutes listed in the title, for appropriations or fund transfers tied to the Kansas Lottery, the Racing and Gaming Commission, or sports wagering revenue; the bill is dead, so any live provisions would need to move in another vehicle.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":301765,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:46:05.086Z"},"KS-SR1725":{"summary":"SR 1725 is a Senate resolution approving an amendment to the Kansas–Prairie Band Potawatomi Nation gaming compact, which the Governor submitted to the Joint Committee on State-Tribal Relations on April 27, 2023. The resolution does not reproduce the amendment, so the sports wagering terms are not in the text provided. Its title says the amendment concerns sports wagering. The Secretary of the Senate must send enrolled copies to the Governor, the Secretary of State and the Nation's chairperson.","mechanics":["Approves, by Senate resolution, the compact amendment the Governor submitted to the Joint Committee on State-Tribal Relations on April 27, 2023.","The text does not set out the amendment's terms. No tax rates, revenue share, platform or skin rules, geofencing, or tribal exclusivity terms appear in the resolution.","Directs the Secretary of the Senate to send an enrolled copy to the Governor, the Secretary of State and the chairperson of the Prairie Band Potawatomi Nation.","Sponsored by the Joint Committee on State-Tribal Relations. The resolution has no effective-date clause, enforcement provision or definitions.","Last action was Senate enrollment on April 28, 2023, one day after the amendment was submitted, which shows the approval was expedited."],"issue_tags":["licensing","legalize"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed, so there is no direct comparison. This is unusual because it is a bare approval resolution for a tribal compact amendment, not a statute. It incorporates the substantive terms by reference to a document outside the text. Operators need the amendment itself, and the Kansas sports wagering statute enacted in 2022, to see how mobile wagering and tribal participation are structured.","watch":"Obtain the actual compact amendment, since the resolution contains no terms, and check how it handles mobile and online wagering, tribal exclusivity or skin arrangements, and any limits on third-party operator access.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":1056,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:46:09.289Z"},"KS-SR1750":{"summary":"Kansas Senate Resolution 1750 approves an amendment to the state's gaming compact with the Iowa Tribe of Kansas and Nebraska. The amendment, which the Governor submitted to the Joint Committee on State-Tribal Relations on April 1, 2024, concerns sports wagering according to the title. The resolution itself contains no operative sports-wagering terms and only records legislative approval and directs transmittal of copies.","mechanics":["Approves the compact amendment submitted by the Governor to the Joint Committee on State-Tribal Relations on April 1, 2024.","The text of the amendment is not reproduced in the resolution, so rates, scope, platforms, and geographic limits are not stated here and must be taken from the compact amendment itself.","The Secretary of the Senate must send an enrolled copy to the Governor, the Secretary of State, and the chairperson of the Iowa Tribe of Kansas and Nebraska.","Sponsored by the Joint Committee on State-Tribal Relations; the resolution originated in and was adopted by the Senate only.","The resolution sets no effective date, tax rate, licensing standard, or enforcement mechanism of its own."],"issue_tags":["licensing","legalize"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed, so there is nothing to compare directly. What is unusual here is that this is a legislative approval resolution rather than a statute. It adds no substantive provisions and incorporates the terms of the underlying compact amendment by reference, so the operative content sits outside the bill text.","watch":"Obtain and review the actual compact amendment submitted April 1, 2024, since the resolution is silent on its terms, including how tribal sports wagering (mobile or on-premises) interacts with the state-licensed market and any exclusivity, fee, or revenue-sharing terms.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":1065,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:46:10.334Z"},"KS-SB491":{"summary":"SB 491 is a housekeeping bill that creates three new central statutes (New Secs. 1-3) standardizing fingerprint-based and name-based criminal history record checks run through the KBI and FBI, and then conforms roughly 100 existing licensing and employment statutes to them. It lists which agencies get which categories of record (adult convictions, non-convictions, diversions, expunged records, juvenile records). It defines the persons to be fingerprinted, including those who serve as licensee agents, owners, directors or officers. It does not change gaming policy, but it touches the Kansas lottery, Kansas racing and gaming commission and state gaming agency background-check provisions.","mechanics":["New Sec. 2(a) lets non-criminal-justice agencies require fingerprinting, with submission to KBI and FBI. The checks may cover employment, licensure, registration, certification or a permit, acting as a licensee's agent, owning a licensee, or serving as a licensee director or officer.","New Sec. 2(b)(2) gives the state lottery (K.S.A. 74-8702/8704) and the Kansas racing and gaming commission (K.S.A. 74-8802/8804, including simulcasting license applicants) adult convictions, non-convictions, diversions and expunged adult records plus expunged juvenile records. New Sec. 2(b)(4)(B) gives the state gaming agency (K.S.A. 74-9802/9805) adult convictions, non-convictions, diversions and adult expunged records for employees and licensees.","New Sec. 2(b)(7)(H) lets the state lottery receive adult convictions and non-convictions on a vendor in line for a major procurement contract (K.S.A. 74-8705).","New Sec. 3 authorizes name-based, state-only checks for specified positions. These include lottery employment (K.S.A. 74-8763, 74-8769), governor/senate review of racing and gaming commission appointees and executive directors (74-8803, 74-8805, 74-9804), and racing commission animal health officers (74-8806), mostly limited to adult convictions. The state lottery also gets adult non-convictions for major contracts (74-8705).","Confidentiality and enforcement: records are exempt from the Kansas open records act, with a sunset on July 1, 2029 unless reenacted. Misuse of information is a class A nonperson misdemeanor and grounds for removal from office (New Secs. 1(d), 2(f), 3(d) as to confidentiality). Boards must discuss records in closed executive session (New Sec. 2(d)). KBI may charge a reasonable fee (New Secs. 2(e), 3(c)), and state and local law enforcement must assist with taking prints (New Sec. 2(c)).","Secs. 4 onward amend the underlying statutes (the text provided stops partway through the hemp provisions) to cross-reference New Sec. 2 and define who is fingerprinted. Examples are the hemp employee, hemp destruction employee and applicant definitions in K.S.A. 2-3901, and the hemp producer and processor fingerprint provisions in 2-3902, 2-3906, 2-3907 and 2-3911. The supplied text does not include the amendments to the gaming statutes, such as 74-8704, 74-8802, 74-8804 and 74-9805."],"issue_tags":["licensing","integrity"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. On its own, this is an omnibus background-check standardization bill touching about 100 statutes across hemp, banking, healthcare, real estate, insurance and law enforcement. Gaming appears only as a handful of entries in the lists of agencies and records, namely the lottery, the racing and gaming commission and the state gaming agency.","watch":"Check that the gaming conforming amendments (K.S.A. 74-8704, 74-8804, 74-9805 and related sections) and the Sec. 2(a) language reaching owners, directors, officers and agents of licensees do not widen who must be fingerprinted, or which record types (especially expunged records) the Kansas racing and gaming commission and state gaming agency can see, for operators and their key persons.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":385603,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:46:11.231Z"},"KS-SB535":{"summary":"SB 535 amends K.S.A. 74-8751 of the Kansas expanded lottery act to add an exemption from the supplier certification requirement in subsection (a)(2). The exemption covers second-tier suppliers that sell goods or services to a certified vendor, have no contract with the state or a facility manager, and do no work on facility premises. The bill died in the Senate committee on 2024-04-30.","mechanics":["New subparagraph (a)(2)(B) takes a person out of the (a)(2) certification requirement only if all three conditions are met: (i) it manufactures or supplies goods or services to a person who must be certified under (a)(2)(A); (ii) it does not propose to contract with a lottery gaming facility manager, a racetrack gaming facility manager or the state for those goods or services; and (iii) it does no work and provides no services on the premises of a lottery or racetrack gaming facility.","The exemption is limited by its terms to 'this paragraph', meaning (a)(2). It does not amend (a)(3), the separate sports wagering certification for facility manager employees directly involved in managing sports wagering and for persons contracting with a lottery gaming facility manager for more than $250,000 per year in sports-wagering goods or services, including interactive sports wagering platforms under K.S.A. 74-8782. Those certifications remain valid for one year.","Existing (a)(2) certification remains for persons who propose to contract with a facility manager or the state, including electronic gaming machine manufacturers, technology providers and computer system providers. It still includes security, fitness and background review of officers, directors, key gaming employees and holders of 5% or more. The executive director may rely on another state's comparable certification.","The revocation, suspension and nonrenewal grounds in (a)(4)-(6) are carried forward as written. They include false information, felony, gambling-related or moral turpitude convictions, failure to report ownership changes, delinquency in remitting money to the lottery, and contract or act violations. Certifications remain non-assignable and non-transferable under (b).","Section 2 repeals the existing version of the statute. Section 3 makes the act effective on publication in the statute book. The bill has no fiscal or enforcement provisions beyond the existing certification framework."],"issue_tags":["licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"SB 535 is a narrow carve-out from supplier certification and has no close counterpart among the listed bills. CT SB01237 similarly provides that ticket distribution service providers need no vendor license, and CT SB01235 moves the other way by requiring vendor and affiliate licensees to produce books and records. The Hawaii bills (HB 1308, HB 2570, SB1569) create sports wagering supplier licensing from scratch, and IL HB2754 redefines occupational licensing. Unlike those, SB 535 reduces the reach of an existing licensing scheme by drawing a line at privity and on-premises work, without changing the tiers or fees.","watch":"Operators and their vendors should check whether sub-tier technology, data or platform suppliers would fall under the (a)(2)(B) exemption, and note that it leaves the (a)(3) sports wagering certification untouched, so any revived version could shift where the 'contract with the manager' and on-premises lines fall.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":6868,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:46:16.618Z"},"KY-SB202":{"summary":"SB 202 (Acts Ch. 82) puts the retail licensing and distribution of hemp-derived, low-dose \"cannabis-infused beverages\" under Kentucky's Department of Alcoholic Beverage Control (ABC), alongside alcohol. It adds new definitions, new license types, and age and shipping rules, and amends the direct shipper statutes to cover these beverages. It has no connection to sports betting, iGaming or collectibles; the only relevant overlap is the licensing and age-verification model.","mechanics":["Sec. 1 defines \"cannabis-infused beverage\" as a permitted adult-use cannabinoid liquid, with no more than 5 mg of intoxicating adult-use cannabinoids per 12 oz serving. It excludes medicinal cannabis under KRS Ch. 218B, hemp tinctures, and products with only nonintoxicating cannabinoids.","Sec. 6 gives ABC sole regulatory authority over distribution and retail sale. ABC must adopt the Department for Public Health's regulations until it promulgates its own, which is due on or before July 1, 2026. Local administrators have only the authority expressly granted in KRS Chs. 241-244.","Sec. 6(2) allows retail sale only by the package, only in wet territory, and only by a holder of both a quota retail package license and the new cannabis-infused beverage retail package license (Sec. 7), which is supplemental and off-premises consumption only.","Sec. 8 creates a cannabis-infused beverage distributor's license, issued as a primary license or as a supplemental license to a distributor or wholesaler. It must be held for each separate place of business. The holder must have a Kentucky location and cannot also hold a retail package license. Transport is limited to the distributor's own marked vehicles.","Sec. 6(3) sets a minimum purchase and consumption age of 21 and applies all alcohol minor restrictions and offenses to these beverages.","Secs. 10-11 extend the direct shipper license ($100 annual fee) to licensed cannabis-infused beverage manufacturers and caps shipments at 10 cases per customer per month. Shipments go by common carrier with ID checks and a signature from someone 21 or older. Labels must read \"CONTAINS CANNABIS-INFUSED BEVERAGES: SIGNATURE OF PERSON AGE 21 OR OLDER REQUIRED FOR DELIVERY.\" Sec. 6(4) allows approved manufacturers to self-distribute as distilled spirits makers do."],"issue_tags":["licensing","legalize"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. This bill is unusual for a gaming practice because it concerns alcohol and cannabis beverage licensing, not wagering. Its three-tier licensing structure, 21+ ID-verified delivery and direct-shipper regime are the closest parallels to gaming regulation.","watch":"Nothing in the bill directly affects sports-betting, iGaming or collectibles operators; the only point worth noting is the ABC age-verification and delivery-signature model, which could be a template for other regulated-product rules.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":113444,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:46:20.348Z"},"KY-HB775":{"summary":"HB 775 is a general fiscal and tax bill. In the portion supplied it amends Kentucky's tax increment financing statutes (KRS 65.490 to 65.499), phases out ad valorem tax on barreled distilled spirits with a school and fire district replacement tax, and sets a 3.5% individual income tax rate for taxable years beginning on or after January 1, 2026. The supplied text breaks off in KRS 141.020(3) and contains no provision on sports betting, iGaming, collectibles, trading cards or card breaks.","mechanics":["Sections 1-2 amend KRS 65.490 and 65.494 to remove the one-square-mile minimum for a TIF development area (the six-square-mile cap stays). They also let a 'new development area' be carved out of an existing one if at least 10% of increment is paid to the original agency and the state and other listed parties approve.","Section 6 (KRS 132.140) phases down the assessed value of barreled distilled spirits subject to state and local ad valorem tax: 96% for the January 1, 2026 assessment, falling to 8% for 2042 and exempt from January 1, 2043. Spirits in a 'revenue bond-financed warehouse' stay taxed at 100% of assessed value at the 2023 rate until the 2043 exemption.","Section 7 (KRS 138.208) imposes a replacement tax from 2026 on bonded-warehouse taxpayers. It is calculated per school district (2023 collections less current collections and less growth in SEEK funding over 2022-23) and per fire district or emergency services board (2025 baseline). It is assessed pro rata by barrel count, with a hardship reduction through the Finance Cabinet secretary and distribution to local jurisdictions within 60 days.","Section 9 (KRS 141.020) sets the individual income tax rate at 4% for taxable years from January 1, 2024 to before January 1, 2026, and 3.5% from January 1, 2026. It resets the process for later rate reductions: the General Assembly must act, and the size of any reduction may depend on budget reserve trust fund and general fund conditions. Steps of 0.25% or 0.5% apply for FY 2025-26 and FY 2026-27, and steps of 0.1% to 0.5% apply from FY 2027-28.","Section 3 (KRS 131.250) requires microbrewery licensees to file and pay electronically from July 1, 2026. Sections 4-5 remove the 2024-2025 limit on the pipeline and conduit language in the definition of real property in KRS 132.010 and 136.010.","The supplied text is truncated, so any provisions after KRS 141.020(3)(a)1. (including the references to Sections 20 and 24 on malt beverage taxes) could not be reviewed."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. This is an omnibus revenue bill whose supplied text is about TIF districts, distilled spirits property tax, malt beverage filing and the personal income tax rate. It has no gaming content, so it is not comparable to a wagering-tax or licensing bill.","watch":"The only likely relevance is indirect: the income tax cut to 3.5% from 2026 and the trigger-based reductions may affect the state's revenue appetite, and the truncated text should be checked for any gaming-related provision before concluding there is none.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":259176,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:46:23.690Z"},"KY-SB3":{"summary":"Kentucky SB 3 (Acts Ch. 10) overhauls the state's college NIL statute (KRS 164.6941 to 164.6951) to permit institutions and affiliated corporations to pay student-athletes directly, including revenue sharing, under written institutional agreements, and it replaces the 'prevailing market rate' standard with a 'prevailing range of compensation'. It keeps the ban on student-athlete NIL deals that endorse sports betting, and it adds a rule barring high school athletes from using school property or IP for NIL. It is an emergency measure effective on the Governor's signature (March 13, 2025).","mechanics":["Sec. 3(4)(a) keeps the bar on a student-athlete entering an NIL agreement with a third party to endorse or promote sports betting. The same subsection lists controlled substances, substances the athletic association forbids, adult entertainment and products illegal for the athlete to possess. No penalty is attached to the sports-betting ban beyond the institution's disciplinary power under Sec. 4(1)(f).","Sec. 1 and 3(2) add 'institutional agreements' and 'affiliated corporations' (entities designated under KRS 164A.610). Institutions may pay athletes for NIL, brand promotion or other rights, including revenue sharing permitted or required by a legal settlement or applicable law. This replaces the old bar on institutions giving or promising NIL compensation.","Sec. 1(15) and 3(1) require third-party NIL pay to fall within the 'prevailing range of compensation'. That means pay for a valid business purpose, commensurate with what similarly situated non-student-athletes receive. The old 'prevailing market rate' definition is struck. Sec. 2(1) lets institutions and associations require compliance with this range.","Sec. 3 strikes the old prohibitions on boosters or other persons offering NIL pay to recruit or induce athletes to enroll or transfer, and the bar on institutions negotiating NIL deals for prospects. Sec. 3(3) lets institutions designate a media rights holder or other third parties with whom athletes may contract.","Sec. 4(2) requires athletes to submit proposed third-party NIL agreements to a designated institutional official, who has up to three business days to flag conflicts with the statute or institutional restrictions in writing. Governing boards must adopt review and appeal procedures. Sec. 4(7) exempts these agreements from Open Records Act disclosure.","Sec. 7 adds KRS 156.070(2)(j): no member school may let a high school athlete use school property or IP (trademarks, uniforms, copyrights) for NIL, and no athlete may do so. The state board or its designated athletics body must promulgate enforcing regulations. Sec. 8 declares an emergency, effective on signing."],"issue_tags":["prop","integrity"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"Among the listed bills, DE HB314, HI HB 2384 and HI SB3263 are the closest comparators (student-athlete NIL or protection). The GA high school NIL bills (HB1276, HB383) also overlap on high school NIL, and AR HB1861 addresses NIL games and gaming. KY SB3 is not a gambling bill. Its only betting content is the retained Sec. 3(4)(a) ban on sports-betting endorsements, whereas the listed sports wagering bills (e.g., AK HB145, GA SB57, HI HB 1308) regulate operators and taxes. Unlike AR HB1861, it does not regulate NIL-based wagering markets.","watch":"Operators should note that Sec. 3(4)(a) still bars college athletes from sports-betting endorsements, with enforcement left to institutional discipline and the three-day review of proposed deals in Sec. 4(2), so any athlete-linked marketing in Kentucky must avoid college athletes and avoid use of school IP.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":35219,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:46:27.425Z"},"KY-HB566":{"summary":"Kentucky HB 566 (Acts Ch. 124) is a governance and housekeeping act for the Kentucky Horse Racing and Gaming Corporation (KHRGC), the exclusive regulator of sports wagering in the Commonwealth. In the text provided, it adds definitions, expands and reshuffles the board, shifts power from the Governor to the board, changes the audit schedule, and prepares KHRGC to absorb charitable gaming oversight on July 1, 2025. The visible text does not change sports wagering rates, licensing standards or operator obligations. The text cuts off in Section 6, and later sections it cross-references (Sections 10, 11, 14 and 42, on voucher funds, quarter horse purse funds and presumed-abandoned pari-mutuel tickets) are not visible.","mechanics":["Board of directors grows from 15 to 17 members, all appointed by the Governor. Charitable gaming representatives go from two to three, a quarter horse industry seat and an at-large member with no financial interest in the regulated industry are added, and there are still three gaming-industry experts (KRS 230.227(2)(b)). Current members serve until July 1, 2026, and regular appointments under the new scheme begin then.","The president is appointed by the Governor from up to three names supplied by the board, and is removable by the board rather than serving at the Governor's pleasure (KRS 230.230(1)). The president must have at least five years of management experience in racing or gaming and prepares an annual rather than biennial budget.","KRS 230.215 and 230.225 keep KHRGC's exclusive jurisdiction over sports wagering and add charitable gaming from July 1, 2025. The word 'plenary' is struck from the rulemaking power. KHRGC cannot prohibit or restrict any approved game or charitable gaming activity in use as of July 1, 2025 without General Assembly action, and cannot exercise jurisdiction over matters within exclusive national authority (230.225(13)(c)-(d)).","The Auditor of Public Accounts audits KHRGC every four years. A different qualified auditor performs an annual audit in the other years, and the first audit covers FY2026-27 (KRS 230.225(2)(a)). After transfer of FY2024-25 and FY2025-26 appropriations, KHRGC must be self-funded with no general fund money (230.225(10)(b)).","KRS 230.210 adds definitions for 'charitable gaming', 'geofence', 'Kentucky resident' (183-day test or Kentucky address on an account application), 'proof of wagers', 'unclaimed pari-mutuel winning ticket' and 'unredeemed pari-mutuel voucher'. It keeps 'sports wagering service provider' and 'sports wagering device' (which includes mobile devices), and it separates a Kentucky quarter horse purse fund from the paint, Appaloosa and Arabian fund.","KHRGC must still develop sports-integrity monitoring programs and a program to share wagering information with sports governing bodies (KRS 230.225(5)(g)-(h)). The board's role on regulations changes from deciding whether to 'promulgate' them to deciding whether to 'recommend' them, and KHRGC itself holds final authority to promulgate (KRS 230.232(3))."],"issue_tags":["licensing","integrity"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"Nothing listed is a close match, since HB 566 is a regulator-restructuring act and not a legalization, tax or advertising bill. The nearest comparisons are CO SB 163, which also deals with how a gaming regulator delegates its functions to a division, and FL S1580, which deals with Florida Gaming Control Commission employment eligibility waivers. CT SB01235 likewise adjusts the Department of Consumer Protection's disciplinary authority over gaming licensees. Kentucky is unusual in putting horse racing, pari-mutuel wagering, sports wagering and, from July 2025, charitable gaming under one corporation that is a self-funded municipal corporation and political subdivision.","watch":"A sports-betting operator's lawyer should watch the rulemaking power (the removal of 'plenary', the board's shift to recommending regulations, and the 230.225(13) limits on KHRGC's authority), the board's new composition, and the voucher and unclaimed-wager provisions in the later sections (10, 11, 14 and 42), which were cut off and need review in the full chaptered text.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":267903,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:46:27.604Z"},"LA-HB151":{"summary":"Louisiana HB151 amends R.S. 27:29.3 so that the non-gaming supplier permit and suitability regime expressly covers suppliers to sports wagering operators (as defined in R.S. 27:602), in addition to licensees under R.S. 27:44 or 353 and the casino gaming operator. It also changes the waiver provisions in Subsection B and repeals Subsection F in its entirety. The bill is signed and takes effect 08/01/2025.","mechanics":["Section 1, R.S. 27:29.3(A)(1): the division must issue non-gaming supplier permits to suitable persons who furnish goods or services for compensation to a R.S. 27:44 or 353 licensee, the casino gaming operator, or a sports wagering operator under R.S. 27:602.","The board must set by rule the threshold amount of goods and services that triggers the permit requirement, and may limit the calculation to goods and services related to Louisiana operations.","R.S. 27:29.3(A)(2): any person who directly or indirectly furnishes goods or services to those entities, regardless of dollar amount, or who has a business association with them, is subject to board and division jurisdiction and may be found unsuitable. The board or division may require a suitability finding or permit application, with reimbursement of reasonable costs capped at $250. Failure to supply required information may result in a finding of unsuitability or permit denial.","R.S. 27:29.3(B)(1)(b) is reworded: the division may waive a person or field of commerce if requiring suitability is not advisable or necessary to protect the public interest or accomplish the section's policies.","R.S. 27:29.3(B)(2): a supplier required to hold a permit, other than those listed in Subsection E, may request a waiver, which the division may grant on a showing of good cause. No gaming licensee may contract for goods or services with anyone in its employ who holds a key gaming employee permit under R.S. 27:3.","Section 2 repeals R.S. 27:29.3(F) in its entirety; the text provided does not show what that subsection contained. Effective 08/01/2025."],"issue_tags":["licensing","integrity"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"Among the listed bills, the closest in subject matter are those on supplier licensing: HI HB 1308 and HI HB 2570 (sports wagering supplier licensing as part of new markets), CT SB01235 (vendor and affiliate licensee books and records), and CO SB 163 (delegation of licensing duties to the division). Unlike the Hawaii bills, which create new licensing for suppliers in a new market, LA HB151 is a narrow amendment to an existing supplier suitability regime that brings sports wagering operators' vendors within it. Unlike CT SB01235, it does not address books-and-records duties. It also does not touch tax, advertising, or the legalization of any form of gaming.","watch":"Operators should watch that the suitability and permit reach now extends to any vendor regardless of dollar amount (A(2)), that the board's threshold rules and the repeal of Subsection F, whose content is not shown, could change vendor onboarding obligations, and that waivers under Subsection B are discretionary, so vendor contracts should account for possible permit delays or unsuitability findings.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":4585,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:46:29.138Z"},"LA-HB312":{"summary":"HB 312 is Louisiana's omnibus supplemental appropriations act for FY 2025-2026. It adds spending and makes means-of-finance swaps across state agencies, with large Medicaid reductions, corrections and hurricane closeout funding, retirement system payments and higher education allocations. It contains no substantive gaming, sports-betting or card-product regulation. The only gaming-related items are routine line items for the State Police and the Racing Commission.","mechanics":["Gaming Enforcement Program (State Police, 08-419): $1,189,240 from the Office of Motor Vehicles Driver's License Escrow Dedicated Fund Account for a cloud-based licensing system.","Riverboat Gaming Enforcement Fund (State Police, 08-419): $4,127,747 of Riverboat Gaming Enforcement Fund dedications is cut from the Traffic Enforcement Program and replaced with the same amount from the Driver's License Escrow account. Separately, $4,337,997 from the Riverboat Gaming Enforcement Fund is appropriated to Traffic Enforcement, offset by a $4,337,997 State General Fund (Direct) reduction.","Louisiana State Racing Commission (01-254): $2,198,716 from the Video Draw Poker Device Purse Supplement Subfund for horse racing activities.","Louisiana Stadium and Exposition District (01-124): $469,930 from the Sports Facility Assistance Fund for contractual obligations.","Retirement systems (Schedule 18): $144,268,468 of FY 2024-2025 surplus goes to LASERS. Distribution formula covers systems with a June 30, 1988 amortization base and a funded ratio below 80%.","Effective date: the act was signed and took effect June 18, 2026."],"issue_tags":["licensing"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. On its own, HB 312 is a general supplemental appropriations act. Its gaming-related items are the cloud licensing system for the State Police Gaming Enforcement Program, the Riverboat Gaming Enforcement Fund swaps, and the video draw poker purse supplement for racing. It creates no new tax, license category, or product rule.","watch":"The $1,189,240 cloud-based licensing system for State Police Gaming Enforcement and the Riverboat Gaming Enforcement Fund reshuffling are the only items touching operators, so watch for licensing or credentialing process changes and for pressure on that enforcement fund, but nothing here changes operator obligations.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":184695,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:46:31.757Z"},"LA-HB503":{"summary":"Louisiana HB 503 is mostly a horse-racing housekeeping bill. It adds a legislative finding to R.S. 4:141 about the economic contributions of live-racing pari-mutuel facilities, adjusts the Racing Commission's steward-appointment language in R.S. 4:147(A)(2)(d), and amends R.S. 27:607(H)(2) on pari-mutuel wagering in a sports wagering licensee's retail sports book lounge. The bill text as printed does not show which words are new or struck, so the exact changes have to be checked against the underlying statutes.","mechanics":["R.S. 4:141: new subsection A declares it state policy that pari-mutuel facilities offering live horse racing have historically contributed to economic development, particularly agriculture and horse breeding; the existing policy items are redesignated (the old B becomes C).","R.S. 4:147(A)(2)(d): the Racing Commission may appoint up to three other stewards, compensated by the commission. They may not serve in the stewards stand during a race meeting without the written consent of the association conducting it. The digest describes this as a cap of three stewards.","R.S. 27:607(H)(2): a sports wagering licensee that is an association under R.S. 4:143 and also holds a R.S. 27:353 license (Chapter 7) may conduct pari-mutuel wagering in its retail sports book lounge.","Condition on lounge pari-mutuel wagering: it must follow a plan of operation approved by both the Gaming Control Board and the Louisiana State Racing Commission, as required by R.S. 4:213(C).","The digest says the 27:607 change only 'adds clarification language'. The bill sets no rates, thresholds, effective date, or new enforcement provisions."],"issue_tags":["licensing"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"No other states' bills on overlapping issues were listed, so there is nothing to compare directly. On its own, the bill is unusual because it sits at the intersection of Louisiana's racing and sports wagering statutes. It addresses pari-mutuel wagering in a sports book lounge, but it makes no tax, advertising, or market-structure changes.","watch":"Check what the R.S. 27:607(H)(2) amendment actually changes, since the digest calls it only 'clarification', particularly the dual Gaming Control Board and Racing Commission plan-of-operation approval for racetrack-affiliated sports wagering licensees offering pari-mutuel wagering in retail lounges.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":6300,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:46:36.020Z"},"LA-HB460":{"summary":"HB 460 (Act 461, 2025 Regular Session) is the Fiscal Year 2024-2025 supplemental appropriations bill for Louisiana, effective 06/23/2025. It makes line-item appropriations and directed means-of-finance adjustments across state agencies. In the portion of the text provided (through page 22 of 95), the only gaming-related item is a Riverboat Gaming Enforcement Fund appropriation. The bill does not amend any gaming statute, tax rate, or license.","mechanics":["Schedule 08, 08-419 (Office of State Police): appropriates $2,993,804 from the State General Fund by Statutory Dedications out of the Riverboat Gaming Enforcement Fund to the Traffic Enforcement Program.","The same section directs the commissioner of administration to reduce the Traffic Enforcement Program's State General Fund (Direct) appropriation by ($2,993,804). On its face this is a means-of-finance swap from general fund to the riverboat gaming dedicated fund, not new net spending.","The bill does not state a purpose for the Riverboat Gaming Enforcement Fund money beyond the program name, Traffic Enforcement.","No sports wagering, iGaming, fantasy sports, sweepstakes, or trading-card provisions appear in the text provided. The text cuts off at page 22 of 95, so Schedules 19 onward (the rest of higher education, and any later schedules) were not reviewed.","Effective date per the status line is 06/23/2025. Section 1 appropriates for Fiscal Year 2024-2025, and the text provided shows no separate enforcement or penalty provisions."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. This is a general supplemental appropriations act, and its only gaming link in the text provided is a fund-source line for the Riverboat Gaming Enforcement Fund in the State Police budget (08-419). It is not a gaming policy bill.","watch":"Review the unreviewed pages (after page 22 of 95) for any appropriations or reductions touching gaming-related dedicated funds or agencies, such as gaming enforcement or sports wagering revenue, and note that the Riverboat Gaming Enforcement Fund is being used as a State Police means of finance.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":222163,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:46:37.635Z"},"LA-HB404":{"summary":"Louisiana HB404 (Act 498) is a general Department of Revenue tax-administration bill. It changes refund and interest rules, adds drop-shipment and abstract-of-title sourcing rules, and makes technical edits to the Office of Debt Recovery statute, including its power to intercept casino and lottery winnings. Gaming is touched only through the debt-recovery provisions in R.S. 47:1676(D)(4), which now cover Chapter 10 of Title 27 licensees and permittees.","mechanics":["R.S. 47:1676(D)(4)(a)-(c): the Office of Debt Recovery 'may' (formerly 'shall be authorized to') withhold, offset, levy, garnish or seize progressive slot annuities and cash gaming winnings (per R.S. 27:24) and lottery prizes (per R.S. 47:9026). This is not done until a single-point inquiry debt database is available to entities licensed or permitted under Chapters 1, 4, 5, 7 or 10 of Title 27.","Chapter 10 of Title 27 is added to the licensee and permittee references in 1676(D)(4)(a)(i), (b) and (c). Those entities may deduct one administrative fee per payment (R.S. 27:24(A)(5)(d)), and they and the Gaming Control Board are immune from civil and criminal liability for disclosure or for withholding or failing to withhold at the office's request. The bill text does not say what Chapter 10 covers, so whether it reaches sports wagering or other newer licensees is unconfirmed.","R.S. 47:1676(E): the office may charge debtors a fee of up to 25% of total final debt, set by rule and applied uniformly. R.S. 47:1676(C)(4) requires the secretary to seek the Attorney General's help first before contracting with a third-party counsel.","Refunds and interest: new R.S. 47:1621(K) and 1624(A)(1)(c) bar refunds and interest on overpayments based on tax shelters, sham or evasion schemes, or transactions lacking business purpose or failing economic substance, as determined by a final court decision. The 1624(A)(1)(b) text is restructured, and 1624(A)(2) is repealed effective July 1, 2025. New R.S. 47:303.1(D) delays interest for 180 days (rather than 90) on sales and use tax overpayments on exempt purchases by direct payment (DP) number holders, effective July 1, 2025.","Sourcing, R.S. 47:301.4: drop shipment sales are sourced to the place of transfer of title or possession, whichever occurs first. Abstracts of title are sourced to the creator's principal place of business in Louisiana. Section 8 applies the sourcing changes both prospectively and retroactively.","Other changes: R.S. 47:31(6) taxes capital construction funds under 26 U.S.C. 7518 (effective January 1, 2026); R.S. 47:296.1 is repealed; the alcohol shipment notice in R.S. 26:364(C) is amended. Most sections take effect on the governor's signature, Sections 2 and 7 on July 1, 2025."],"issue_tags":["tax","integrity"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"This bill does not set a wagering tax rate, authorize any form of betting or license anyone, which sets it apart from the listed sports-betting bills such as AK HB145/SB194, GA HB380 and SB57, and IL HB3159. Its closest relatives are Iowa's Department of Revenue omnibus bills (IA SF2427, HSB723 and SSB3179), which also bundle sports wagering and lottery administration with general tax-administration changes, and IA SF605 on winnings withholding. Unlike those Iowa bills, the Louisiana bill leaves gaming-specific tax and withholding rules alone and amends only the debt-recovery offset against casino and lottery winnings.","watch":"Check what Chapter 10 of Title 27 covers and whether operators licensed there must support the debt-offset and single-point inquiry system, and note that the withholding obligations apply only once that system is available, while the bill also grants a liability shield and a one-administrative-fee limit.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":15975,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:46:38.505Z"},"LA-HB547":{"summary":"Louisiana Act 291 (HB 547) authorizes licensed sports wagering operators and platforms to accept fixed-odds wagers on live or simulcast horse races as a category of sports wager, carving the activity out of the existing rule that non-pari-mutuel horse race wagering is illegal. It creates the Fixed Odds Horse Wagering Purse Supplement Fund, funded by 5% of net gaming proceeds from fixed-odds horse wagering, and directs the Gaming Control Board to write guidelines. The Act is effective on signature (06/11/2025).","mechanics":["Definition (R.S. 27:612, 27:602(19)(b), 27:44(22)(b), 27:205(30)(b)): fixed odds wagering is acceptance of sports wagers on a live or simulcast horse race with predetermined odds. It may occur only in strict compliance with 27:612 and may be combined with other sports wagers. Covered wagers include single-race, teaser, moneyline, in-race, proposition, straight bets and parlays. R.S. 27:602(22)(b) adds racing as defined in R.S. 4:143 to the definition of sports event.","Funding (R.S. 27:625(G)(4)(a)(ii)): 5% of net gaming proceeds from fixed-odds horse wagering is credited to the new Fixed Odds Horse Wagering Purse Supplement Fund (R.S. 4:200). Under 27:612(B)(1), non-parlay proceeds go to this fund. Under 27:612(B)(2), horse-race legs in a parlay with other, non-horse sports events are allocated under 27:625(G)(4)(a)(i) and credited to the existing Sports Wagering Purse Supplement Fund (R.S. 4:199), which receives 2.5% of tax monies collected.","Fund use (R.S. 4:200): the fund pays out only on legislative appropriation to the Racing Commission. The allocation is 55% to thoroughbred purses (by thoroughbred race days), 24% to quarter horse purses (by quarter horse race days), 14% to Louisiana Thoroughbred Breeders Association breeder awards and 7% to Louisiana Quarter Horse Breeders Association breeder awards.","Market access (27:612(C)): a licensee or its platform may take fixed-odds wagers on out-of-state races in accordance with the Interstate Horseracing Act of 1978. For Louisiana races it needs the consent of the host racetrack and the Horsemen's Benevolent and Protective Association.","Integrity (27:608(B)(3)): an operator may not knowingly accept a wager from a jockey, horseman (including employees) or racing official on a race they are participating in.","Regulation: the Gaming Control Board must establish guidelines for accepting fixed-odds horse wagers under the APA (27:603(A)(2)(a)(xvii)). R.S. 4:149(A) is amended to except 27:612 from the general illegality of non-pari-mutuel horse wagering. Permit provisions (27:86(E), 27:249.1(E)) restrict fixed-odds acceptance to 27:612."],"issue_tags":["legalize","licensing","integrity","tax"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"None of the listed bills is comparable. They are mostly general mobile or online sports wagering authorizations or tax bills (AK HB145/SB194, GA HB380, HI HB1308, DE HB365, IA SSB 1157) or CT/IL regulatory and advertising measures, and none targets horse racing. HB 547 instead works inside Louisiana's existing sports wagering framework, adding a horse-racing product with a dedicated 5% purse-supplement earmark and a track and HBPA consent condition for in-state races. The closest analogue is IL HB1405 on exchange trade wagering, which creates a new wagering product within a sports wagering act, but that bill has nothing to do with racing or purse funds.","watch":"Watch the Gaming Control Board's forthcoming guidelines under 27:603(A)(2)(a)(xvii), the host-track and HBPA consent requirement for Louisiana races, and how the 5% of net gaming proceeds is calculated and split between the two funds for horse-race parlay legs, since all of these affect cost and product scope.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":14508,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:46:45.808Z"},"LA-HR217":{"summary":"Louisiana HR 217 is a non-binding House resolution asking the House Committee on Administration of Criminal Justice, or a subcommittee, to study the feasibility and revenue potential of selling Louisiana Lottery Corporation tickets over the internet. It asks the committee to review how other states do this and to report before the 2026 Regular Session. It does not authorize, regulate or tax anything.","mechanics":["Directs a study by the House Committee on Administration of Criminal Justice (or a subcommittee) of the feasibility and revenue-generating potential of internet lottery ticket sales.","Asks the study to analyze other states' processes and implementation; the resolution recites that nineteen other states allow online lottery ticket sales.","Asks the study to consider using existing contracts rather than procuring a new vendor, and to assess which option maximizes revenue and best benefits Louisiana.","Report due to the Legislature before the convening of the 2026 Regular Session.","One print and one electronic copy of any report must go to the David R. Poynter Legislative Research Library under R.S. 24:772.","Recitals note Louisiana Lottery Corporation FY2024 revenue of over $642 million and that other gaming, such as sports wagering, is already available online. The resolution creates no license, tax, or penalty and has no effective-date or enforcement provisions."],"issue_tags":["legalize"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"This is a study request, unlike the substantive enabling bills listed: AK HB145 and SB194 (mobile sports wagering with a tax), GA HB380 and SB172 (sports betting run through the Georgia Lottery Corporation), and AR HB1861 (Interactive Gaming Act). The closest analogue is DE HR6, which also creates a working group on an internet sports lottery. Unlike DE HR6, HR 217 covers only internet lottery tickets, not sports wagering or iGaming, and creates no working group. CT SB01235, SB01237 and SB01463 deal with lottery operations directly. HR 217 sets no rates, licensing rules or restrictions.","watch":"Operators should track the committee's study and any 2026-session bill it prompts, especially whether it expands from internet lottery sales to iGaming or sports wagering, and whether the preference for existing lottery contracts over new procurement would favor incumbent lottery vendors.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":2469,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:46:45.970Z"},"LA-HB650":{"summary":"HB 650 raises Louisiana's tax on net gaming proceeds from online and mobile sports wagering from 15% to 17% under R.S. 27:625(B). It dedicates 2% of the monies collected under Subsection B to a new Overdetention Compensation Fund, which pays people held in custody past their scheduled release date. The remainder continues to flow through the existing R.S. 27:625(G) waterfall to state general funds.","mechanics":["Amends R.S. 27:625(B): the tax on net gaming proceeds from sports wagering offered electronically through a website or mobile application rises from 15% to 17%. The existing carve-out for sports wagering conducted through a sports wagering mechanism (retail kiosk/terminal) is unchanged, so retail wagering is not reached.","Amends R.S. 27:625(G)(8): 'two percent of the monies collected pursuant to Subsection B' is credited to the Overdetention Compensation Fund, after the Subsection D allocations are made. The text is ambiguous: it is unclear whether the 2% is measured against total tax collections or against the 2-point increase, and a new (G)(9) restates the 'remaining funds' general-fund language, which duplicates (G)(8).","Creates R.S. 15:572.10, a special treasury fund administered by the Louisiana Commission on Law Enforcement and Administration of Criminal Justice. Fund money is subject to appropriation, interest stays in the fund, and unexpended balances carry over.","Fund proceeds may be used only to pay persons incarcerated one or more days beyond their scheduled release date, at $70 per day, paid directly to the individual. Receipt does not bar civil remedies, and no state agency, political subdivision, constitutional office or employee is liable for payments under the section.","The Commission must adopt implementing rules under the Administrative Procedure Act. The bill states no effective date, so Louisiana's default effective date for acts would apply, and it has no operator-specific compliance, licensing or reporting changes."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"HB 650 is a narrow rate increase on an existing, already-launched online sports wagering market. That sets it apart from the Alaska bills (HB145, SB194) and the Georgia bills (HB380, HB 686, SB57, SB172, SB386), which would create new regimes and set initial taxes. It is closer to the Illinois bills that adjust the tax or its earmarks, such as IL HB3159 (2% of adjusted gross receipts to fire and EMS) and IL HB5143 (sunsetting the per-wager tax). Its earmark is unusual: a 2% share dedicated to compensating wrongful overdetention, which has no obvious connection to gaming, whereas the Illinois and Georgia analogues direct money to fire/EMS or Pre-K.","watch":"An operator's lawyer should watch the 2-point rate increase on online net gaming proceeds, which cuts into margins on the existing 15% base, and the ambiguity in how the 2% dedication is calculated, since the earmark may attract amendments or become a template for further add-on rates.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":5339,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:46:46.775Z"},"LA-HB587":{"summary":"HB 587 replaces Louisiana's flat sports wagering taxes, 15% on online and 10% on retail, with a single five-bracket progressive rate schedule of 20% to 40% on net gaming proceeds. The schedule applies to onsite and mobile/web wagering alike. It amends both R.S. 27:625 (licensed sports wagering establishments, collected by the gaming division) and R.S. 47:9105 (the Louisiana Lottery Corporation's retail sports wagering). It also raises the share of 27:625 collections credited to the Louisiana Early Childhood Education Fund from 25% to 40% and raises the cap from $20 million to $30 million. The fiscal note shows a general fund revenue increase of about $36.2 million.","mechanics":["Rate schedule in new 27:625(A)(1)-(5) and 47:9105(A)(1)-(5): 20% of net gaming proceeds up to $30M; 25% from $30M to $50M; 30% from $50M to $100M; 35% from $100M to $200M; 40% above $200M. The bracket wording reads as marginal.","The existing 15% electronic tax in 27:625(B) and 47:9105(B) is deleted, so mobile and web wagering no longer have a separate rate. The old rule treating on-premises app wagering as electronic wagering taxed under Subsection B is also removed. The plain text does not show strikethrough, so confirm the final enrolled language, because the retained 'ten percent' sentence in Subsection A may be a drafting artifact.","The bill does not say whether the brackets apply per operator or in aggregate, or over what period (monthly, fiscal or calendar year). Section 47:9105 refers to proceeds 'of an operator,' while 27:625 does not. Returns and collection remain monthly, due within 20 days after month-end.","Early childhood earmark in 27:625(F)(2) (formerly G): 40% of monies collected under the section, up to $30M, is credited to the Louisiana Early Childhood Education Fund (R.S. 17:407.30), up from 25% and a $20M cap. The earmark applies after the Bond Security and Redemption Fund credit.","Lottery Corporation collections under 47:9105 still go to the Disability Services Fund (R.S. 28:826) and then the treasury. The negative net proceeds carryover is unchanged, limited to 12 months, and 27:625 still does not apply to Lottery-offered sports wagering.","No effective date, transition rule, or new enforcement provision appears in the text, and no changes to licensing or operations are made."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. On its own, the bill is unusual in that it abandons the retail/online rate split and adopts a graduated schedule topping out at 40%, which is a high ceiling for sports wagering taxation. It also earmarks a rising share of revenue, up to $30M, for early childhood education.","watch":"Watch how the brackets are measured (per operator or statewide, monthly or annual) and the effect of the 20% to 40% marginal rates on online operators, which currently pay a flat 15%, together with the missing effective date.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":8276,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:46:51.110Z"},"MA-H3548":{"summary":"This is a further amendment to the FY2023 supplemental appropriations bill (House No. 58). It is mostly general appropriations, notary and real estate closing reforms, and health-insurance technical fixes. Sections 8 to 13 amend the Massachusetts sports wagering statute (chapter 23N) on occupational licensing, operator control persons, license renewal, fund appropriation treatment and the Youth Development and Achievement Fund.","mechanics":["Section 8 redefines \"Occupational license\" in G.L. c. 23N, s. 3. A license is required only for operator employees who perform duties directly related to sports wagering in 15 listed roles or their equivalents, such as general manager, chief compliance officer, chief technology officer, and shift supervisors in in-person sports wagering, surveillance, cage or player development.","Section 9 rewrites c. 23N, s. 5(b) on who \"has control\" of an operator applicant and must qualify. This covers 10%+ owners of a corporate applicant, holders of a 10%+ beneficial or proprietary interest, listed corporate officers and inside directors, each LLC member, manager and transferee, and each partner. Banks and licensed lenders holding ordinary-course liens are excluded.","Sections 10 and 11 extend the s. 5(c)(1) background checks to occupational license applicants. They require the commission to obtain CORI and exchange fingerprint data with state police and the FBI. New s. 8(e) sets a nonrefundable $100 renewal fee due by March 1 of the third calendar year after issuance or renewal, and an employer may pay it.","Section 12 adds to c. 23N, s. 18(a) that expenditures from the fund shall not be subject to appropriation. The text does not name the fund in the amendment, and the bill's own text is cut off before the end. Section 13 replaces the duplicate s. 18 with a new s. 19 creating the Youth Development and Achievement Fund.","The new Youth Development and Achievement Fund is credited with transfers from the Sports Wagering Fund under s. 17. It is subject to appropriation and is spent equally on three purposes: higher-education financial aid, after-school and out-of-school programs, and matching grants for youth sports and school groups to attend events.","Sections 6 and 7 amend c. 23J, s. 9A(a) by striking \"separate and apart from its other funds\" and the word \"bond\". The summary of the bill's remainder (the Notary and attorney-closing provisions in ss. 22 to 28) is unrelated to wagering: new c. 221, s. 46E limits real estate closings to Massachusetts attorneys, with attorney general enforcement."],"issue_tags":["licensing","tax"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. This is unusual because it is a further amendment to a supplemental budget bill rather than a standalone gaming bill, and it carries sports wagering technical corrections to c. 23N (enacted in 2022) alongside unrelated appropriations, notary reform and closing-attorney requirements.","watch":"Watch the Section 8 occupational license definition (which roles and \"equivalent\" roles must be licensed), the Section 9 control-person list that sweeps in LLC transferees and 10% holders, and the new $100 renewal and fingerprint background-check obligations for operator staff.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":91079,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:46:56.664Z"},"MA-H361":{"summary":"MA H361 extends the sunset dates for Massachusetts horse racing and simulcasting authorizations from July 31, 2023 to July 31, 2028 by amending a string of session laws (ch. 494 of 1978, ch. 277 of 1986, ch. 114 of 1991, and later acts through 2015). It also lets the Suffolk County running horse racing licensee keep its license and simulcast authority without live racing, and it protects category 2 sports wagering licensees under chapter 23N from losing their licenses if racing or simulcasting authority lapses.","mechanics":["Sections 1-15 replace 'July 31, 2023' with 'July 31, 2028' in sections of ch. 494 of 1978 (12A, 13, 15), ch. 277 of 1986 (s. 9), ch. 114 of 1991 (ss. 3-5), ch. 139 of 2001 (s. 45), ch. 449 of 2006 (s. 20), ch. 194 of 2011 (ss. 92, 112) and ch. 10 of 2015 (s. 74). Section 16 repeals s. 17 of ch. 128 of 2022.","Section 17: the Suffolk County running horse meeting licensee licensed in 2023 stays licensed and authorized to simulcast under ch. 128C with no live racing requirement until the later of July 31, 2028 or expiry of its category 2 license term (including renewals). Jan 1, 2023 to Dec 31, 2028 are 'dark days' and live racing is barred unless it obtains a supplemental live racing license under ch. 128A.","Section 17 bars simulcasting or accepting wagers on greyhound racing on or after August 1, 2023. All simulcasts must comply with the Interstate Horse Racing Act, 15 U.S.C. 3001 et seq.","Section 17 requires New England Horsemen's Benevolent & Protective Association approval for simulcasts from states whose racing associations do not need approval under 15 U.S.C. 3004(a)(1)(A). If the association approves for one racing meeting licensee, it must approve for all otherwise eligible licensees.","Section 18 amends G.L. c. 128A, s. 5(g) to extend the language 'for the conduct of racing' to cover 'simulcasting under chapter 128C or sports wagering under chapter 23N'.","Section 19: expiration or repeal of ch. 128A, ch. 128C or related racing laws, or a resulting end to simulcasting by a category 2 sports wagering licensee, is not grounds to suspend, terminate or otherwise adversely affect the license. The licensee is deemed authorized by law to simulcast for its license term and renewals. No tax rates or fees are changed."],"issue_tags":["licensing","integrity"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"Nothing comparable is listed. The other bills (e.g., AK HB145, GA SB57, HI HB 1308, DE HB365) create or tax sports wagering markets, or address advertising and consumer protection (CT HB05229, CT SB01464). H361 is a racing and simulcasting sunset extension for an existing market. Its sports-betting content is limited to sections 18 and 19, which tie the MA category 2 (racetrack-linked) sports wagering license under ch. 23N to the racing and simulcast framework and insulate it from lapse of that framework.","watch":"Watch sections 18 and 19, which protect ch. 23N category 2 licenses from racing or simulcast lapses and could affect racetrack-tied skin or partner arrangements, along with the Suffolk County licensee's no-live-racing status through 2028; the bill was superseded by a new draft (S2409), so check the successor text for changes.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":8398,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:46:57.002Z"},"MA-H362":{"summary":"MA H362 repeals Massachusetts' existing horse and greyhound racing and simulcasting statutes (chapters 128A and 128C) and replaces them with a new chapter 128D that places racing, simulcasting and pari-mutuel wagering under the Massachusetts Gaming Commission. It authorizes advance deposit wagering (ADW) through racing and simulcasting licensees and their service providers, sets takeout caps and a revenue waterfall, grandfathers the 2022 simulcasters for ten years without a live-racing requirement, and bans live dog racing and greyhound simulcasting. It also amends the 'illegal gaming' definition in c.4 s.7 to carve out chapter 23N sports wagering and chapter 128D pari-mutuel wagering. The bill has been superseded by a new draft (S2409).","mechanics":["Takeout is capped at 19% on straight wagers and 26% on exotic wagers (s.11(a)-(c)). Live-race pari-mutuel tax is 0.75%; guest-track simulcast tax is 0.375%. Breaks are retained under the existing scheme, and the remaining takeout is split among breeders' programs, purses, a Tufts vet school share, and the host track (11% on in-state simulcasts).","Simulcasting licenses last 5 years and may go only to racing licensees, c.23K gaming licensees, or entities that held a 2022 c.128A license with c.128C simulcasting authority. Those 2022 entities are deemed licensed through Dec 31 of the tenth anniversary year of the Act, with no live-racing requirement (except the harness licensee's existing obligations) and with assignability subject to a commission background check (s.3(a)(3)).","Section 5 makes ADW by Massachusetts residents lawful on in-state and out-of-state races while a racing or simulcasting licensee exists. Only licensees may run ADW, directly or through a service provider with a written agreement, and the commission regulates accounts, rebates and rewards (s.4(a)(3)(ii)-(iii)). 'Rebate' is broadly defined to include bonuses, free wagers and other benefits.","The commission gets full rulemaking, emergency rulemaking, inspection and audit powers, plus authority to act as host and off-track racing commission under the Interstate Horseracing Act (s.3(a)(5)). Trade secrets are protectable by NDA or a c.66 s.10 exemption (ss.8-9). Application fees cover background investigation costs (s.10).","Revenue waterfall (s.11(d)): 0.35% local aid, $35,000 per licensee to DPH for problem gambling, $80,000 backstretch services, $20,000 hardship assistance, up to $1,080,976 for commission racing operations, up to $4.5M in purses, and the remainder to the General Fund.","Prohibitions and penalties: live dog racing is banned, with a civil penalty of at least $20,000, and simulcasting or wagering on greyhound racing is barred (s.7, amending s.14). Unauthorized acceptance of horse or dog race wagers carries up to $2,000 and one year for a first offense, and up to $10,000 and two years for later offenses (s.15). Sections 9-10 pay out the legacy capital and promotional trust funds to the 2022 licensees.","Sections 2 and 7 take effect Aug 1, 2023. Section 11 keeps existing 2023-24 authorizations in place until the commission adopts regulations."],"issue_tags":["licensing","legalize","integrity","tax"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"This bill is about pari-mutuel horse racing and simulcasting, not sports betting, so none of the listed bills is a close match. Delaware HB365, Alaska HB145/SB194 and the Georgia bills create or tax mobile sports wagering, which this bill does not do, since it only cross-references the existing c.23N sports wagering chapter in the illegal-gaming carve-out. Illinois HB2880 touches the Illinois Horse Racing Act, but only on employee conflicts at the racing board. The ADW framework here, under which only racing or simulcasting licensees may operate and tied to Interstate Horseracing Act status, is distinctive.","watch":"Operators should check whether the ADW exclusivity in s.5 and the broad 'rebate' and 'rewards' rulemaking power could constrain horse-wagering offered by sports-betting or iGaming licensees, and whether the c.4 s.7 carve-out for c.23N is the only protection for sportsbooks.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":40712,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:47:05.324Z"},"MA-H3994":{"summary":"This is a fiscal 2023 supplemental appropriations bill drawing on the Transitional Escrow Fund. It covers hospital aid, a pension transfer, school relief and collective bargaining costs, among other items. Gaming provisions are confined to horse racing: simulcasting is extended at Suffolk, Bristol and Norfolk county locations, and the Suffolk County racetrack licensee is kept in simulcast operation until the later of July 31, 2028 or the end of its category 2 sports wagering license term. The bill also extends a series of racing-related special-act sunset dates to July 31, 2028.","mechanics":["Sections 3-7 amend G.L. c.128A s.5 and c.128C s.2 so that simulcasting may occur at any location approved by the commission in Suffolk (twice), Bristol and Norfolk counties.","Sections 8-22 replace the July 31, 2023 sunset with July 31, 2028 in a string of special acts (c.494 of 1978, c.277 of 1986, c.114 of 1991, c.139 of 2001, c.449 of 2006, c.194 of 2011, c.10 of 2015). These acts are not described in the bill text itself, though they are tied to the racing and simulcast framework.","Section 27 lets the Suffolk County running horse racing licensee keep its license and its simulcast authority until the later of July 31, 2028 or the expiry of any category 2 sports wagering license it holds under c.23N (or a renewal). This applies notwithstanding c.128A s.2 and c.128C ss.1, 2, 2A and 4.","Section 27 also designates January 1, 2023 through December 31, 2028 as dark days, barring live racing unless a supplemental live racing license is granted under c.128A. It bars greyhound simulcasting and wagering on or after August 10, 2023 under c.128C s.9.","Section 27 requires simulcasts to comply with the Interstate Horse Racing Act, 15 U.S.C. 3001 et seq. Simulcasts from states whose racing associations need no approval under 15 U.S.C. 3004(a)(1)(A) require approval from the New England Horsemen's Benevolent & Protective Association. If approved for one racing meeting licensee, that approval must extend to all otherwise eligible licensees.","Section 21A repeals sections 46, 48, 61, 63 and 124A of c.287 of the acts of 2014. Section 24 repeals sections A1, 17 and 18 of c.128 of the acts of 2022. The bill text does not say what these sections contain."],"issue_tags":["licensing","integrity"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. This is unusual because it is a general supplemental budget, and its gaming content is limited to pari-mutuel simulcasting and licensing. It does not touch sports wagering tax rates or the c.23N operator framework, and refers to the category 2 sports wagering license only to set the end date for the racetrack licensee's simulcast authority.","watch":"A category 2 sports wagering licensee (the Suffolk County racetrack licensee) is given a simulcast and racing license lifeline tied to its c.23N license term in section 27, so check how that linkage, the dark-day bar on live racing and the horsemen's approval condition affect licensing and market-access arrangements.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":27844,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:47:05.410Z"},"MA-H3982":{"summary":"H3982 is a fiscal 2023 supplemental appropriations bill ($692.95 million total) that carries a handful of racing and simulcast provisions. Sections 3-22 extend a series of horse-racing and simulcasting sunset dates from July 31, 2023 to July 31, 2028 and add county-specific simulcast language to c. 128C. Section 27 keeps the Suffolk County running horse race meeting licensee licensed and authorized to simulcast through the later of July 31, 2028 or the end of its category 2 sports wagering license term under c. 23N, while barring live racing and greyhound simulcasting.","mechanics":["Sections 8-22 replace the July 31, 2023 sunset with July 31, 2028 in the 1978, 1986, 1991, 2001, 2006, 2011 and 2015 session-law provisions that previously carried the racing and simulcasting authorizations forward under c. 128 of the acts of 2022.","Sections 3-7 amend c. 128A s.5 and c. 128C s.2 to cover simulcasting and to allow simulcast at any location approved by the commission in Suffolk, Bristol and Norfolk counties.","Section 27 lets the Suffolk County running horse racing licensee stay licensed and simulcast until the later of July 31, 2028 or expiry of its c. 23N category 2 sports wagering license (or any renewal), tying racing and simulcast status to sports wagering licensure.","Section 27 treats January 1, 2023 through December 31, 2028 as dark days with no live racing unless a supplemental live racing license is granted under c. 128A, and bars simulcasting or accepting greyhound wagers on or after August 10, 2023 (c. 128C s.9).","Section 27 requires simulcasts to comply with the Interstate Horse Racing Act, 15 U.S.C. 3001 et seq.; for states whose associations do not need approval under 3004(a)(1)(A), the New England Horsemen's Benevolent & Protective Association must approve, and approval for one licensee must extend to all otherwise eligible licensees.","Section 24 repeals sections A1, 17 and 18 of c. 128 of the acts of 2022. The remaining sections are unrelated, covering hospital funding, collective bargaining, school nurse certification and utility cost recovery."],"issue_tags":["licensing","integrity"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. On its own, the bill is unusual because a supplemental budget vehicle carries the racing and simulcast extensions, and Section 27 links a racetrack's continued racing and simulcast licensure to its category 2 sports wagering license under c. 23N. It contains no tax rate, sports betting rule or iGaming provision.","watch":"A lawyer for operators should check whether the Section 27 link between the Suffolk racetrack's simulcast status and its c. 23N category 2 sports wagering license affects that license's term, renewal or the licensee's market position, and whether the licensee's dark-day and greyhound restrictions interact with its sports wagering operations.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":23046,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:47:05.902Z"},"MA-H3947":{"summary":"The bill creates a Bilingual Education Trust Fund (G.L. c.29, s.2BBBBBB) to pay for bilingual education programs and a new bilingual educator stipend program, and funds it partly by diverting existing sports wagering and marijuana excise revenue. It amends G.L. c.23N s.14(a) to impose 15% on in-person sports wagering, 20% on mobile/digital sports wagering and 15% on fantasy contests, with 5 points of each rate going to the new fund. The rest of the bill covers bilingual educator endorsements, licensure reciprocity, a task force and stipends in chapter 71A.","mechanics":["Section 2 rewrites c.23N s.14(a): 15% of adjusted gross receipts from in-person sports wagering (5 points to the Bilingual Education Trust Fund, 10 to the Sports Wagering Fund under s.17); 20% on mobile/digital sports wagering (5 to the new fund, 15 to the Sports Wagering Fund); and 15% on adjusted gross fantasy wagering receipts from fantasy contests defined in s.11M1/2 (5 to the new fund, 10 to the Sports Wagering Fund).","The text states these rates as the new operative rates without referring to the existing ones, so the bill sets the total rates as well as the earmark. It strikes only the first sentence of s.14(a); the rest of the subsection is left as is.","Section 3 restates the c.64N s.2 marijuana retail excise at 10.75%, with 2 points to the new fund and 8.75 points deposited under s.5. Section 4 makes a conforming amendment to c.64N s.5.","Fund (c.29 s.2BBBBBB): administered by the commissioner of elementary and secondary education; credited with appropriations, gifts and grants, the sports wagering and marijuana excise shares, and interest. It is not subject to further appropriation, balances do not revert to the General Fund, the commissioner reports annually by October 1, and DESE writes the regulations.","Spending (c.71A s.14): stipends of $10,000 a year for educators in approved bilingual programs, plus $5,000 for staff deemed bilingual, $1,000 for the state seal of biliteracy, and $1,000 a year for 5 years for graduates of Massachusetts public schools. Stipends count as compensation for retirement purposes, and $30,000 in loan repayment is available after 10 years of service in the same district.","Section 6 sets a bilingual endorsement with a language test, subject-matter knowledge and 75 hours of field experience, and requires reciprocity agreements. Section 7 adds licensure reciprocity, a task force and a 2035 goal for certified bilingual educators. The act takes effect January 1, 2025 (Section 8)."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed, so there is nothing to compare against. On its own, the bill is unusual in that it is an education bill that raises sports wagering taxes to pay for a new program. It also reaches fantasy contests under the same excise provision and puts a 5-point earmark on each of the three rates.","watch":"Operators should note that Section 2 rewrites the c.23N s.14(a) rates (15% retail, 20% mobile, 15% fantasy) in a way that may change the total tax burden, and that it diverts 5 points of each rate away from the Sports Wagering Fund to an education fund, which sets a precedent for further earmarking.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":17406,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:47:06.686Z"},"MA-H406":{"summary":"MA H406 amends G.L. c. 23N, s. 17 (the sports wagering revenue distribution section added by St. 2022, c. 173) to cut one allocation from 45 to 35 and to add a new clause (6) sending 10 percent to the Behavioral Health Outreach, Access and Support Trust Fund under c. 29, s. 2GGGGG. It does not change the tax rate or any operator obligation. It reshuffles where state sports wagering revenue goes. The bill was introduced and, on 2024-05-15, was accompanied by a study order (see H4593).","mechanics":["Section 1 strikes the figure \"45\" in c. 23N, s. 17 and inserts \"35\". The bill text does not say which existing clause carries the 45 percent figure, so the affected allocation must be identified from the underlying statute.","Section 2 adds clause (6) to s. 17, directing 10 percent to the Behavioral Health Outreach, Access and Support Trust Fund established in c. 29, s. 2GGGGG.","The 10-point increase offsets the 10-point reduction, so the total distribution stays the same on its face, assuming the 45 figure is a percentage share.","The bill sets no tax rate, license fee, or threshold, and has no new operator-facing compliance, reporting, or enforcement provisions.","No effective date is stated, so the default Massachusetts effective date for acts would apply. The bill was not enacted and was sent to a study order."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states were listed, so there is nothing to compare directly. It is narrow: it changes only the allocation of existing sports wagering revenue under c. 23N, s. 17 toward behavioral health, and leaves the tax rate and operator obligations untouched.","watch":"Operators face little direct exposure since the bill leaves rates and obligations unchanged, but its move to a study order and any later bills redirecting c. 23N, s. 17 revenue are worth tracking as signs of pressure to raise taxes or fees or add responsible-gaming mandates.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":1726,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:47:13.286Z"},"MA-H4171":{"summary":"This is a FY2023 supplemental appropriations bill that is mostly unrelated to gaming, covering migrant shelter funding, MassHealth, reserves and a consumer-directed care workforce council. Sections 5, 6 and 8-9 amend the income tax and withholding statutes (chapters 62 and 62B) to cover sports wagering under chapter 23N, mainly by extending gambling-loss deductions and withholding to licensed sports wagering operators. Sections 7 and 10 delay by 6 months a date in chapters 62 and 63. The text provided is truncated at Section 33 of 64 pages, so any further gaming provisions cannot be assessed.","mechanics":["Section 5 amends G.L. c. 62, s. 3: gambling losses become deductible for sports wagers placed through a chapter 23N licensed operator, alongside losses at chapter 23K gaming establishments and racing or simulcasting licensees. Deductible losses may not exceed gains from sports wagers and wagering transactions.","Section 5 replaces the existing wording, which limited deductions to gains from losses 'at a gaming establishment' or racing or simulcasting licensee. The new cap is aggregate: losses cannot exceed combined gains from sports wagers and other wagering transactions.","Section 6 amends G.L. c. 62, s. 5A, to add sports wagering winnings acquired through a chapter 23N operator to the provision that currently refers to chapter 23K winnings. The excerpt does not show what s. 5A does with them, which appears to be sourcing winnings to the commonwealth for nonresidents.","Sections 8 and 9 amend G.L. c. 62B, s. 2 (withholding) to add sports wagering under chapter 23N alongside gaming establishment winnings, and to add 'sports wagering operator' alongside the licensee reference. This extends withholding obligations to sports wagering operators.","Sections 7 and 10 replace 'the day' with '6 months after' in G.L. c. 62, s. 6(aa), and G.L. c. 63, s. 38KK. The excerpt does not show what date is being pushed back, and these appear unrelated to gaming.","Section 25 amends G.L. c. 128A, s. 5 to add simulcasting under chapter 128C after 'racing'. The excerpt does not show the context. Section 2CI also re-appropriates $207,500 to the State Lottery Commission (0640-0000), which is a budget line with no policy content."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed, so there is nothing to compare directly. The notable feature is that this is an omnibus supplemental budget carrying a narrow tax conforming change for sports wagering. It integrates chapter 23N operators into the existing chapter 62 and 62B treatment of casino and racing gambling, rather than imposing a new operator tax rate or excise.","watch":"Operators should check how the Section 8-9 withholding amendments to c. 62B s. 2 define the withholding and reporting obligations, including thresholds and timing, on chapter 23N operators, and confirm the rest of the 64-page text for any further gaming provisions.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":105848,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:47:15.274Z"},"MA-H4033":{"summary":"This FY2023 supplemental appropriations act (enacted as Chapter 26 of the Acts of 2023) is mostly an extender for horse-racing and simulcasting provisions. Sections 1-15 push a series of July 31, 2023 sunset dates in older racing acts out to July 31, 2028. Section 17 lets the Suffolk County running horse racing licensee keep its racing license and simulcast authority through at least 2028, even though it will not conduct live racing, and ties that authority to its category 2 sports wagering license under chapter 23N.","mechanics":["Sections 1-15 amend provisions of chapter 494 of the acts of 1978, chapter 277 of the acts of 1986, chapter 114 of the acts of 1991, chapter 139 of the acts of 2001, chapter 449 of the acts of 2006, chapter 194 of the acts of 2011 and chapter 10 of the acts of 2015, replacing July 31, 2023 with July 31, 2028.","Section 16 repeals sections A1, 17 and 18 of chapter 128 of the acts of 2022.","Section 17 keeps the Suffolk County running race horse meeting licensee licensed and authorized to simulcast under chapter 128C until the later of July 31, 2028 or expiry of the term (or any renewal) of its category 2 sports wagering license under chapter 23N. It applies notwithstanding G.L. c. 128A s. 2 and c. 128C ss. 1, 2, 2A, 4 and 9.","Section 17 makes January 1, 2023 through December 31, 2028 dark days and bars live racing during that period unless the licensee applies for and is granted a supplemental live racing license under chapter 128A.","Section 17 bars simulcasting or accepting wagers on greyhound racing on or after August 10, 2023, and requires all simulcasts to comply with the Interstate Horse Racing Act, 15 U.S.C. 3001 et seq.","For simulcasts from states whose racing associations do not need approval under 15 U.S.C. 3004(a)(1)(A), the New England Horsemen's Benevolent & Protective Association must approve first. If it approves for one racing meeting licensee, it must approve for all otherwise eligible licensees.","The act is declared an emergency law and takes effect immediately."],"issue_tags":["licensing","integrity"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. On its own, the bill is unusual because it is a budget supplemental that links a racetrack's continued racing and simulcast license to the term of a chapter 23N category 2 sports wagering license. It also ends greyhound simulcast wagering as of August 10, 2023, and gives a horsemen's association a veto-like approval right over simulcasts from certain states.","watch":"Watch the link in Section 17 between the Suffolk County track's simulcast and racing license status and its category 2 sports wagering license, along with the horsemen's association approval requirement, since both could affect the licensee's retail sports wagering position and any operator partnership or market-access arrangements with it.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":7601,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:47:16.324Z"},"MA-H4167":{"summary":"H4167 is a $2.7 billion fiscal 2023 supplemental appropriations bill. Buried among general-law changes are conforming amendments that bring chapter 23N sports wagering into the Massachusetts income tax and withholding statutes. Sections 5 and 6 add sports wagering to the gambling-loss deduction and winnings provisions, and sections 8 and 9 add it to the chapter 62B withholding provisions. The text provided cuts off at section 35, so any later gaming provisions are not covered here.","mechanics":["Section 5 amends G.L. c. 62, § 3 so losses from sports wagers placed through a c. 23N licensed operator can be deducted, alongside losses at c. 23K gaming establishments and racing or simulcasting licensees. Total deducted losses may not exceed the gains from those sports wagers and wagering transactions.","Section 6 amends G.L. c. 62, § 5A to add 'sports wagering winnings acquired through a sports wagering operator licensed under chapter 23N' after the existing reference to c. 23K. The excerpt does not show the surrounding text of § 5A.","Sections 8 and 9 amend G.L. c. 62B, § 2 (withholding) to add 'from sports wagering under chapter 23N' and 'or sports wagering operator'. The excerpt gives only the insertion points, so the withholding rate, threshold and operator duties are not visible.","Sections 7 and 10 replace 'the day' with '6 months after' in G.L. c. 62, § 6(aa) and G.L. c. 63, § 38KK. The excerpt does not show what these provisions cover, so any link to gaming cannot be confirmed.","Section 25 amends G.L. c. 128A, § 5 to add 'or simulcasting pursuant to chapter 128C' after 'racing'. This is a racing and simulcasting conforming change.","Section 2C.I re-appropriates up to $207,500 for the State Lottery Commission (item 0640-0000). The bill is declared an emergency law, so it takes effect on passage."],"issue_tags":["tax","licensing"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. H4167 is unusual because it is a general supplemental appropriations vehicle that carries sports wagering tax-conformity amendments (c. 62, c. 62B) as riders, rather than a standalone gaming bill. Its status is 'Published as amended, see H4171', so any enacted version would be in that bill.","watch":"Check the c. 62B § 2 amendments in sections 8 and 9, and the full statutory text, for any new withholding or reporting duties on chapter 23N operators, and compare the introduced language with the version published as amended in H4171.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":87511,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:47:19.477Z"},"MA-H4790":{"summary":"H4790 is the FY2024 supplemental appropriations bill ($558,065,809 total) and mostly funds unrelated agency lines, land transactions and collective bargaining agreements. Sections 8 and 9 amend G.L. c. 23N, s. 14 to set the sports wagering excise at 15% of in-person and 20% of mobile adjusted gross receipts, and to extend an excise at 15% to fantasy contests, with the excise in lieu of other state and local taxes. The bill was later published as amended in H4799, so the text reviewed is the introduced version.","mechanics":["Section 8 rewrites c. 23N, s. 14(a): 15% excise on adjusted gross sports wagering receipts from in-person wagering and 20% on receipts from mobile apps and other digital platforms approved by the commission.","Section 8 also imposes a 15% excise on adjusted gross fantasy wagering receipts of any person or entity offering fantasy contests, as defined in c. 12, s. 11M½. The bill text does not state how those receipts are defined.","Section 8 requires the accrual method of accounting to calculate the tax, with payment to the commission when the return is filed under subsection (b).","Section 9 rewrites s. 14(c): the excise on adjusted gross sports wagering receipts and adjusted gross fantasy wagering receipts is in lieu of all other state and local taxes and fees on the operation or proceeds of sports wagering or fantasy contests.","The bill has an emergency preamble, so the changes would take effect on enactment. It states no separate effective date for the tax provisions and no new enforcement mechanism.","The rest of the bill is unrelated to gaming: ice rink leases, a Monson land conveyance, rideshare data rules, and bargaining agreements. Section 41(5) is cut off in the text and appears to involve the State Lottery Commission."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed, so there is nothing to compare directly. What is unusual is that the gaming tax change appears in a general supplemental appropriations bill. It also brings fantasy contest operators into the c. 23N excise at 15%, and keeps the 15% in-person and 20% mobile split for sports wagering.","watch":"A lawyer for operators should check the new fantasy excise at 15% and its undefined receipts base, confirm that the in-lieu-of-other-taxes language in s. 14(c) now covers fantasy, and track whether the H4799 amended version keeps, changes or drops these provisions.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":50423,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:47:22.890Z"},"MA-H4799":{"summary":"This is the fiscal 2024 supplemental appropriations act (Chapter 206 of the Acts of 2024). It is mostly unrelated to gaming, but Sections 8 and 9 amend G.L. c. 23N, s. 14 to restructure the excise on sports wagering and fantasy contests and to confirm that the excise is in lieu of all other state and local taxes and fees. The rest of the bill covers appropriations, ice rink leases, a land conveyance, rideshare data and emissions rules, and collective bargaining agreements.","mechanics":["Section 8 replaces c. 23N, s. 14(a). It imposes an excise at 15% of adjusted gross sports wagering receipts from in-person wagering and 20% of adjusted gross sports wagering receipts from mobile apps and other digital platforms approved by the commission.","Section 8 also taxes a person or entity that offers fantasy contests (as defined in c. 12, s. 11M½) at 15% of adjusted gross fantasy wagering receipts. The text does not state the prior rates.","Section 8 requires the accrual method of accounting to calculate the tax. The excise is payable to the Gaming Commission when the return is filed under subsection (b).","Section 9 replaces s. 14(c). The excise on adjusted gross sports wagering and fantasy wagering receipts is in lieu of all other state and local taxes and fees on the operation of, or proceeds from, sports wagering or fantasy contests.","The act carries an emergency preamble, so it takes effect immediately on signing. Sections 8 and 9 contain no separate effective date or delayed operative date.","Section 2 includes a $232,499 supplemental line for the Alcoholic Beverages Control Commission (0610-0050). Section 40(3) approves a collective bargaining agreement for ABCC investigators. Neither involves gaming."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. What is unusual here is that the gaming tax change is a rider in a supplemental budget bill, not a standalone gaming bill. The rider also extends the excise to fantasy contest operators, beyond the sports wagering operators who were already covered, and it sets different rates for retail and mobile wagering.","watch":"Confirm how the new 20% mobile and 15% fantasy rates interact with each operator's existing license terms and promotional deductions, and check how \"adjusted gross\" is defined in the rest of c. 23N and in Gaming Commission regulations, since the in-lieu clause in Section 9 now bars other state and local taxes and fees on these activities.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":51462,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:47:23.965Z"},"MA-H4204":{"summary":"This is the fiscal 2023 supplemental appropriations act (Chapter 77 of the Acts of 2023, an emergency law). It is mostly general-government spending, but it also contains conforming income tax amendments for sports wagering under G.L. c. 23N. These let sports wagering losses count toward gambling-loss deductions and bring sports wagering winnings and operators into the existing withholding and winnings provisions that now cover casino and racing activity. The text provided cuts off at Section 17, so any later gaming provisions are not reflected here.","mechanics":["Section 11 amends G.L. c. 62, § 3. Losses from sports wagers placed through a c. 23N licensed operator are added to losses from c. 23K gaming establishments and racing or simulcasting licensees. Total deducted losses may not exceed gains from sports wagers and wagering transactions.","Section 12 amends G.L. c. 62, § 5A. After the reference to c. 23K it adds sports wagering winnings acquired through a c. 23N licensed operator. The text shown does not reproduce the substance of § 5A.","Sections 14 and 15 amend G.L. c. 62B, § 2, the withholding provision. They add sports wagering under c. 23N and sports wagering operators alongside the existing references to gaming establishments and licensees. The shown text does not state the withholding threshold or rate.","Sections 13 and 16 change a trigger date in c. 62, § 6(aa) and c. 63, § 38KK from \"the day\" to \"6 months after\". The text shown does not say what event the date is tied to, so a gaming link cannot be confirmed.","The preamble declares an emergency law, so the act took effect on signing. The only gaming-adjacent appropriation line visible is a $207,500 reauthorization for the State Lottery Commission (0640-0000), which is incidental."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. This bill is unusual because the sports-wagering provisions are small conforming tax amendments inside a roughly $3.1 billion supplemental budget. The visible text shows no change to the sports wagering tax rate, licensing, or advertising rules.","watch":"A lawyer for operators should check how the Section 14 and 15 additions to c. 62B § 2 affect operator withholding and reporting duties for sports wagering winnings, and how the Section 11 cap on loss deductions affects bettor tax treatment and customer disclosures.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":149170,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:47:26.318Z"},"MA-H57":{"summary":"MA H57 is the Governor's FY2023 supplemental appropriations bill, reported by House Ways and Means, with $353.2 million in appropriations and $585.3 million in bond authority. It is mostly a general budget vehicle (shelter, school meals, SNAP, MassWorks, clean water, and similar items). Sections 8 to 13 amend the sports wagering statute, G.L. c. 23N, on occupational licensing, control persons, and fund mechanics. The remainder covers notary, remote notarization, and real estate closing reforms.","mechanics":["Section 8 replaces the c. 23N, s. 3 definition of 'Occupational license' so that it covers employees of an operator who perform duties directly related to sports wagering in 15 listed or equivalent roles, among them general manager, chief compliance officer, chief information officer, chief technology officer, chief surveillance officer, and shift supervisors of in-person sports wagering, surveillance, cage, or player development departments.","Section 9 rewrites c. 23N, s. 5(b). The Gaming Commission may not grant an operator license until each person with control of the applicant is qualified. Control includes holders of 10% or more of a corporate applicant, holders of a 10% or greater beneficial or proprietary interest, listed corporate officers, inside directors and the board chair, all LLC members, managers and transferees, and all partners. A bank or licensed lender holding a lien acquired in the ordinary course is excluded.","Sections 10 and 11 extend criminal-history and fingerprint checks (s. 5(c)(1)) to occupational license applicants and require the Commission to investigate them (new s. 8(d)). Section 11 also adds s. 8(e): a nonrefundable $100 renewal fee, with a renewal application due by March 1 of the third calendar year after issuance or renewal. The employer may pay the fee.","Section 12 adds a sentence to c. 23N, s. 18(a) providing that expenditures from that fund are not subject to appropriation. Section 14 does the same for the fund under c. 94G, s. 14A(a), which was inserted by St. 2022, c. 180, s. 18 (the c. 94G references appear to concern the cannabis regime, not sports wagering).","Section 13 replaces the duplicate 'section 18' of c. 23N with a new s. 19 creating the Youth Development and Achievement Fund. The fund is credited with transfers from the Sports Wagering Fund under s. 17 and with other sources. Spending is subject to appropriation and is split equally among higher-education aid, after-school and out-of-school programs, and matching grants for youth sports and school groups.","Sections 6 and 7 amend c. 23J, s. 9A(a) by deleting the words 'separate and apart from its other funds' and the word 'bond'. The text shown does not say what s. 9A covers. Section 2 and Section 2A contain the general appropriations, and the bill is declared an emergency law."],"issue_tags":["licensing","integrity","tax"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"No other states' bills are listed for comparison. What stands out here is that sports wagering is handled as a technical cleanup of Massachusetts' 2022 law (St. 2022, c. 173) inside an unrelated supplemental budget bill. It does not change tax rates, legalization, or advertising rules. The bill was later published as amended in H58.","watch":"Operators should check whether their personnel fall within the new 15-role 'occupational license' definition, which carries fingerprinting, background checks, and a $100 renewal fee. They should also review the broadened control-person definition under s. 5(b), including the 10% ownership trigger and LLC transferees, and confirm how the Youth Development and Achievement Fund is funded from sports wagering revenue.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":75644,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:47:31.918Z"},"MA-S2573":{"summary":"The Massachusetts Gaming Commission files proposed 205 CMR 15.00 under G.L. c. 128A, § 9B, setting the procedure for applying for, evaluating and awarding racing meeting licenses (live horse racing). It covers application contents, administrative sufficiency review, the substantive evaluation factors, durable and temporary suitability findings for applicants and qualifiers, ongoing disclosure duties, and withdrawal of applications. Advance deposit wagering, occupational licensing and financial payments are reserved for later rulemaking.","mechanics":["Application (15.02): due by the October 1 deadline of the year before the license year. Requires a $300 licensing fee (G.L. c. 128A, § 4), a $125,000 bond, and the statutory license fee. Non-licensees must also file a scoping survey and BED, MJPHD and Mass. Supp. forms for each qualifier. Proof of municipal approval (c. 128A, § 13A) and an affirmative county vote (§ 14) is required, and the Commission cannot grant leave to cure those items after the deadline (15.03(2)(a)(ii)(d)).","New-track applicants (15.02(3)(b)) must disclose financing, feasibility and economic studies, any purse agreement with a horsemen's organization, any Project Labor Agreement plans, the pari-mutuel plan covering live racing, simulcasting and advance account deposit wagering, responsible gaming plans, and whether they will seek a Category 2 sports wagering license. Non-licensees must sign attestations acknowledging that forms not administratively sufficient by May 1 (scoping survey) or August 1 (BED/MJPHD/Mass. Supp.) may lead to denial.","Evaluation (15.03(5)-(6)): the Commission weighs the c. 128A, § 3(i) factors in no set order or weight, plus responsible wagering and advertising plans, DEI commitments, undue concentration of ownership, and impact on racing integrity. Deliberation is by adjudicatory proceeding, and at least one public meeting must be held in the host municipality. A license cannot issue without a durable suitability finding within the current or prior four calendar years, or a temporary suitability finding within the last calendar year. Licenses carry conditions, including that the licensee and its qualifiers not be found unsuitable.","Suitability (15.04): durable suitability must be shown by clear and convincing evidence after a Bureau investigation and adjudicatory hearing. Temporary suitability is discretionary, based on a perjury-backed certification and a limited Bureau review, and lasts to the end of the racing year. Mandatory unsuitability grounds include false statements, revocation of a racing, gaming or sports wagering license elsewhere, specified felonies, and inadequate financial responsibility. Qualifiers include officers, inside directors, and holders of 10% or more. Institutional investors holding up to 15% may seek waivers. Persons previously qualified under c. 23K or 205 CMR 116 or 215 may be deemed suitable.","Ongoing duties (15.08): responses to information requests are due within 10 days. Licensees and qualifiers must report within 10 days a long list of events, including license denials or discipline in any jurisdiction, investigations that could lead to a fine of $50,000 or more, arrests, and significant financial events such as an SEC penalty of $250,000 or more, debt covenant defaults, adverse audit opinions, and delisting notices. Knowing non-disclosure or non-cooperation can lead to ineligibility, suspension or revocation.","Transfers and withdrawal: a transferee of any interest in a license must be found suitable as if it were a new applicant (15.04(12), c. 128A, § 11C). Licensees must keep Commission-approved divestiture mechanisms for unsuitable holders (15.04(11)). Withdrawal is allowed before final action, but once a hearing is requested or directed it needs Commission approval for good cause and may carry a re-application bar (15.09)."],"issue_tags":["licensing","integrity","advertising"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"This is an agency regulation filing, not a bill, and it addresses horse-racing licensing rather than new legalization or tax rates, so none of the listed bills are close analogues. The nearest are the Illinois licensing bills (IL HB2754 on occupational licenses and IL HB2880 on Racing Board and Gaming Board employees) and the Iowa advance deposit wagering bill (IA HSB228). Unlike those, this filing reserves advance deposit wagering (15.05) rather than regulating it. Its unusual features are the qualifier and suitability regime borrowed from c. 23K casino licensing, the requirement to disclose plans for a Category 2 sports wagering license, and the responsible wagering and DEI evaluation factors.","watch":"Operators with racetrack or pari-mutuel ties should note that the Category 2 sports wagering plan disclosure (15.02(3)(b)(ix)), the cross-jurisdiction suitability inquiry into sports wagering history, the 10-day event reporting duties, and the reserved advance deposit wagering section (15.05) signal where future licensing rules and disclosure burdens are likely to land.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":47611,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:47:39.166Z"},"MA-H58":{"summary":"This is the FY2023 supplemental appropriations act (Chapter 2 of the Acts of 2023), and it is mostly spending items and unrelated technical amendments. Sections 8 to 13 make cleanup amendments to the 2022 sports wagering statute, G.L. c. 23N. They redefine \"occupational license\", set who counts as having \"control\" of an operator applicant, add fingerprint and background checks and a renewal fee for occupational licensees, and re-section the Youth Development and Achievement Fund. Sections 6 and 7 also make small edits to G.L. c. 23J, s. 9A, but the text does not show what that section governs.","mechanics":["Section 8 redefines \"Occupational license\" in c. 23N, s. 3. A license is required for an operator's employees who perform duties directly related to sports wagering in 15 listed or equivalent roles, including general manager, chief compliance officer, chief security officer, chief technology officer, software development manager, and shift supervisors of in-person sports wagering, surveillance, cage or player development departments.","Section 9 replaces c. 23N, s. 5(b). The commission cannot grant an operator license until each person with \"control\" of the applicant qualifies. Control covers 10% or greater owners and holders of a 10% or greater beneficial or proprietary interest, with an exception for banks and licensed lenders holding liens acquired in the ordinary course. It also covers named corporate officers, each inside director and the board chair, each LLC member, manager and transferee of a member's interest, and each partner.","Sections 10 and 11 extend the s. 5(c)(1) background investigation to occupational license applicants. This includes criminal offender record information and fingerprint and criminal history exchange with state police and the FBI. The new s. 8(d) requires the commission to investigate each occupational license applicant.","New s. 8(e) requires a nonrefundable $100 renewal fee and a renewal application by March 1 of the third calendar year after issuance or renewal. The employer may pay the fee on the employee's behalf.","Section 12 adds to c. 23N, s. 18(a) that expenditures from the fund \"shall not be subject to appropriation\". The amending text does not name the fund. Section 13 strikes the duplicate second s. 18 and inserts s. 19, the Youth Development and Achievement Fund. That fund is credited with transfers from the Sports Wagering Fund under s. 17, is subject to appropriation, and is spent equally on (1) higher-education financial aid, (2) after-school and out-of-school programs, and (3) matching grants for youth sports and school groups to attend events.","The act is declared an emergency law that takes effect immediately, and the Governor signed it on 2023-03-29. The bill text provided is cut off partway through the notary provisions, so I have not seen the rest of the act."],"issue_tags":["licensing","integrity"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"No other states' bills are listed for comparison. This bill is unusual because the sports wagering changes are corrective amendments to Massachusetts' 2022 enabling statute (c. 173 of the Acts of 2022). They sit inside an omnibus supplemental budget with housing, notary and other unrelated provisions, and they set no tax rates or new operator obligations.","watch":"Check whether your client's employees fall within the 15 listed or \"equivalent\" roles that now need an occupational license, whether your client's owners and officers fall within the s. 5(b) \"control\" definition (10% interests, named officers, inside directors), and which fund s. 18(a)'s non-appropriation language covers.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":78448,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:47:39.177Z"},"MA-S172":{"summary":"This bill moves Massachusetts horse-racing and simulcasting regulation into the Gaming Commission framework. It creates a three-member State Racing Board within the commission and recodifies the racing statutes as new chapters 23K¼ (racing meetings) and 23K½ (simulcasting, which is not in the text provided). It also restructures the Race Horse Development Fund and the thoroughbred and standardbred capital-improvement trust funds, and it amends the c.4 s.7 definition of \"illegal gaming\" to carve out lawful games, including sports wagering and fantasy contests. The supplied text cuts off at about page 26 of 74, partway through c.23K¼ s.9, so the rest of that chapter and all of 23K½ could not be reviewed.","mechanics":["Section 1 redefines \"illegal gaming\" (c.4 s.7, clause Tenth) to exclude state lottery games, c.23K games, \"sports wagering conducted under chapter 10\", pari-mutuel racing under c.23K¼ and 23K½, bingo, charitable gaming and fantasy contests under s.11M½.","Section 5 (new c.23K s.7A) creates a State Racing Board of three governor-appointed members, no more than two from one party, with 5-year terms and a 15-year cap. The chair needs gaming or racing regulatory experience and one member must be an equine-experienced veterinarian. The board administers c.23K¼ and 23K½ and acts as host racing commission and off-track betting commission under 15 U.S.C. 3001. Under new s.7 the Gaming Commission hears appeals of board pari-mutuel and simulcasting decisions and may grant a simulcasting license to a gaming establishment, considering the impact on racetracks.","Section 11 (new s.60) has the board administer the Race Horse Development Fund and recommend the thoroughbred/standardbred split, with changes capped at 10 percentage points a year. Distributions are 80% to purses, 16% to breeding programs and 4% to horsemen's health and pension benefits.","Section 12 (new ss.60A and 60B) sets up the Thoroughbred and Standardbred Capital Improvements Trust Funds, funded by \"breaks\". The thoroughbred fund pays $100,000 of breaks to the Massachusetts council on compulsive gambling, and the standardbred fund also receives 2% of exotic-wager handle. Unspent balances at December 31 roll into the Race Horse Development Fund.","New c.23K¼ licensing: applications are due October 1 and decided by November 15. The bond is $1,250,000 ($250,000 for fairs). Racing days are capped at 200 a year for running horses and 200 for harness. There is one licensee per county outside fairs, and 85% of staff must be Massachusetts citizens of at least 2 years. The license fee under s.4 is the greater of $300 or 0.075% of prior-year average daily handle per racing day, and fairs pay no more than $100 a day.","New c.23K¼ s.9 limits takeout to 19% on single-horse wagers and 26% on exotics. It requires 0.75% of daily handle to go to the board, 1% to the Massachusetts Thoroughbred Breeders Association, 8.5% or 9.5% of the withhold to purses and 0.25% plus breaks to the thoroughbred trust fund. Wagering is limited to the track except for simulcast and account wagering under c.23K½."],"issue_tags":["licensing"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"None of the listed bills is a close analogue, because they mostly authorize or amend mobile sports wagering or online gaming (for example AK HB145, GA HB380 and HI HB1308). This bill is a pari-mutuel and simulcasting restructuring that touches sports wagering only through the definition of illegal gaming. The nearest overlaps are FL H0189, which also revises pari-mutuel permits and permitholders, and IL HB2880, which deals with Racing Board employee rules, but neither creates a standalone racing board inside a gaming commission as this bill does. No listed bill covers purse and capital-trust fund allocations like Sections 11 and 12 here.","watch":"Section 1 excludes \"sports wagering conducted under chapter 10\" from illegal gaming rather than citing the sports-wagering chapter, so confirm that licensed operators are actually covered, and review the unseen c.23K½ simulcast and account-wagering provisions for any overlap with online wagering.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":144790,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:47:42.788Z"},"MD-HB1134":{"summary":"HB1134 amends Md. Code, State Gov't § 9-1E-09(d) to address cross-licensee service arrangements among Maryland sports wagering facility licensees. When a Class A licensee provides sports wagering services on behalf of a Class B licensee, the Class A licensee may use its own branding and loyalty rewards programs at the Class B facility, but may not let a patron use their Class A account wallet to bet with the Class B licensee. The Class B licensee, subject to Class A approval, may use and market the Class A licensee's branding and loyalty programs in the same manner as Class A.","mechanics":["New § 9-1E-09(d)(1)(II)1: a Class A facility licensee providing services on behalf of a Class B facility licensee may use the Class A branding and loyalty rewards programs at the Class B facility.","New § 9-1E-09(d)(1)(II)2: the Class A licensee may not allow an individual to use the account wallet maintained with Class A when placing wagers with the Class B licensee, so wallets stay separate.","New § 9-1E-09(d)(1)(III): a Class B licensee receiving services from a Class A licensee may use and market Class A's branding and loyalty programs in the same manner as Class A, but only with Class A's approval.","The existing authority in (d)(1)(I) for a licensee to contract with another facility licensee is retained as (I) and made subject to paragraph (2), which is unchanged.","Existing (d)(2) is unchanged: a stadium licensee contracting with a video lottery operator (or its affiliate or subsidiary) may only use one operating a video lottery facility in the same county as the stadium.","Effective July 1, 2025. The bill sets no new tax, fee, penalty or enforcement mechanism."],"issue_tags":["licensing","advertising"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"The bill is narrow and none of the listed bills from other states is comparable. The listed bills (AK HB145 and SB194, GA SB57, HI HB1308, DE HB365) mainly authorize or regulate sports wagering markets, set taxes, or deal with licensing frameworks. HB1134 instead deals with a skin-style service arrangement between existing brick-and-mortar licensees, specifically brand and loyalty program sharing, with a ban on sharing account wallets. Nothing listed from CT, IA or IL addresses the same issue.","watch":"Watch for the wallet-separation rule in new (d)(1)(II)2, and for the unstated limits on what loyalty rewards and co-branded marketing are allowed, since these affect whether a Class A operator's online or app-based account can be linked to a Class B facility's wagering and how promotions are structured.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":3331,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:47:46.807Z"},"MD-HB1048":{"summary":"HB 1048 adds \"a historical horse race\" to Maryland's definition of \"sporting event\" in State Government § 9-1E-01(i). It lets certain in-person sports wagering facility licensees take pari-mutuel wagers on historical horse races (HHR) on Commission-approved terminals, under a new § 9-1E-09.1. Mobile licensees and other facility licensees are expressly barred from HHR wagering. HHR proceeds appear to flow through the existing sports-wagering revenue split in § 9-1E-12, which the bill reenacts without change.","mechanics":["Eligible HHR venues under new § 9-1E-09(f)(1): a Class A-2 licensee at a thoroughbred or standardbred race course; a State Racing Commission § 11-524 licensee (or subsidiary) at the Maryland State Fairgrounds in Timonium; and the owner or lessee of a satellite simulcast facility described in § 9-1E-06(a)(2). Under § 9-1E-09(f)(2), any other facility licensee and all mobile licensees may not accept HHR wagers.","Under new § 9-1E-09.1(c), HHR wagering must be conducted as pari-mutuel betting, on races of any breed. Under § 9-1E-09.1(h)(2)-(3), payouts come from bettors' money and cannot be wagers against the licensee. The licensee's retention cannot depend on the outcome of any race or wager.","Under § 9-1E-09.1(g)(1), wagering is allowed only on Commission-approved terminals, and the Commission cannot authorize more than 4,000 terminals. Under § 9-1E-09.1(d)-(f), HHR wagering is limited to a Commission-approved designated area, on Commission-approved days and hours, and only after written approval of all wager types. Pool explanations must also be posted after Commission approval.","Under § 9-1E-09.1(g)(4)-(6), terminals must hide the race's identity (location, date, horse and jockey names) until after the wager is placed. They must show true past-performance data, then the bettor's selections, official results and a replay.","Under § 9-1E-09.1(i), a Commission-approved player-funded pool may be used to cover minus pools, and the licensee may assign a percentage of each wager to it. Under § 9-1E-09.1(j), the minimum wager is 10 cents and the minimum payout may not be less than the amount wagered.","The bill takes effect July 1, 2025. It is cross-filed as SB 982 and was assigned to Ways and Means. It sets no new tax rate or fee, and § 9-1E-12 is reenacted without amendment."],"issue_tags":["legalize","licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"None of the other states' bills listed address historical horse racing, so there is no direct comparator. FL H0189 is the closest in subject because it revises gaming machines and pari-mutuel permitholders, but it is a broad gambling overhaul. The Alaska, Delaware (HB365), Hawaii and Georgia bills are about authorizing or taxing mobile or retail sports wagering and do not create a racetrack-based, pari-mutuel terminal product. HB 1048 is unusual in folding HHR into the sports wagering statute and the existing 85/15 proceeds split, rather than creating a separate pari-mutuel or gaming-machine regime.","watch":"A lawyer for sports-betting operators should watch the exclusion of mobile licensees and non-listed facility licensees under § 9-1E-09(f)(2), and the unamended § 9-1E-12(b)(1)(ii), which refers to locations in § 9-1E-09(a) and so leaves HHR proceeds and the 85% retention unclear for the new § 9-1E-09(f) locations.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":18702,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:47:48.893Z"},"MD-HB1347":{"summary":"Maryland HB1347 (Ch. 702) amends the Business Occupations and Professions Article so that video lottery operators, sports wagering facility licensees and health care facilities are carved out of the definition of \"security guard employer\" in § 19-101(m). It also exempts cannabis agents who work as security guards from the State and national criminal history records check otherwise required for cannabis agent registration, provided they are already authorized under Title 19, Subtitle 4. The gaming-related change is a narrow definitional carve-out within the private security guard licensing regime, not a gaming regulatory measure.","mechanics":["New § 19-101(m)(2)(II)-(III) excludes a video lottery operator (State Government § 9-1A-01) and a sports wagering facility licensee (State Government § 9-1E-01) from the definition of \"security guard employer.\"","New § 19-101(m)(2)(IV) also excludes a health care facility as defined in Health-General § 19-114.","Under the existing § 19-401(c), a security guard employer may employ a security guard only if certified by the Secretary; the carve-out takes these gaming licensees outside that in-house employer category.","Alcoholic Beverages and Cannabis § 36-501(c)(2) exempts a cannabis agent employed as a security guard, or by a security guard agency, for a cannabis licensee or registrant from the State and national criminal history records check under § 36-505, if authorized under Title 19, Subtitle 4.","The bill makes no change to the video lottery employee licensing in § 9-1A-14 or to the incorporation of § 9-1A provisions for sports wagering licensees in § 9-1E-03(a)(1); those sections are reenacted without amendment.","Effective October 1, 2025; signed by the Governor May 20, 2025."],"issue_tags":["licensing"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills is comparable. They deal with sports wagering legalization, taxation, advertising, or gaming regulation, such as AK HB145, GA SB57, CT HB05229 and DE HB365. MD HB1347 is a private-security licensing bill that touches gaming only through a definitional exclusion for video lottery and sports wagering facility licensees. The cannabis-agent background-check exemption is also unrelated to the listed gaming bills.","watch":"A Maryland sports wagering facility licensee should confirm how its in-house security staff are now treated, since it is no longer a \"security guard employer\" and may need to use licensed security guard agencies or rely on gaming-specific licensing and employee requirements, because the bill itself does not say which regime applies.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":7272,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:47:50.128Z"},"MD-HB1543":{"summary":"Maryland HB1543 (Ch. 333, 2025) adds subsection (j) to State Government § 9-1E-07, letting a Class B sports wagering facility licensee ask the State Lottery and Gaming Control Commission to relocate its facility. The Commission may grant a request only if the licensee was originally issued its Class B license on or before September 30, 2023, and the new site is in the same county, meets the § 9-1E-06 location requirements, and is approved by the Sports Wagering Application Review Commission (SWARC). It takes effect July 1, 2025.","mechanics":["New § 9-1E-07(j)(1): a licensee issued a license under § 9-1E-06(a)(2)(ii) may submit a written request to the Commission to relocate a Class B sports wagering facility.","Eligibility cutoff, § 9-1E-07(j)(2)(i): the licensee must have been originally issued a Class B license on or before September 30, 2023, which excludes later-issued Class B licenses.","Same-county limit, (j)(2)(ii)1: the new location must be within the same county as the existing facility.","Location compliance, (j)(2)(ii)2: the new site must comply with the location requirements of § 9-1E-06.","Approval, (j)(2)(ii)3: the new location must be approved by the Sports Wagering Application Review Commission, subject to § 9-1E-15(q).","Grant of a request is discretionary (\"may\") for the Commission. Effective July 1, 2025 (Section 2). The text adds no new fees, penalties or enforcement provisions."],"issue_tags":["licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"This is a narrow licensing-administration bill and has no close counterpart in the listed bills. Most of the listed state bills deal with legalization, tax rates or advertising: AK HB145/SB194 (mobile sports wagering and tax), DE HB365 (mobile framework), HI HB 1308/HB 2570/SB1569 (new regimes), and CT HB05229 (advertising limits). The closest in subject is CO SB 163, which concerns licensing and regulatory delegation, but it does not address relocating retail venues. MD HB1543 only changes where an existing retail Class B licensee may operate.","watch":"A Class B licensee or prospective purchaser should check the September 30, 2023 original-issuance cutoff, the same-county limit, the § 9-1E-06 location rules, and the SWARC approval step, and note that the bill sets no timeline or standard for the Commission's discretionary decision.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":1684,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:47:54.131Z"},"MD-HB1140":{"summary":"HB 1140 adds a new Criminal Law § 12-115 that bars anyone from operating, conducting or promoting an \"online sweepstakes game\" in Maryland, a defined term covering dual-currency games that simulate casino games, lottery games or sports wagering and pay cash or cash equivalents. It also adds a licensing overlay to State Government Title 9. Applicants and licensees must disclose revenue from illegal interactive gaming markets, and the Commission may not license or allow operations by a person whose affiliates knowingly take revenue from certain blacklisted or prohibited jurisdictions.","mechanics":["Definition (CR § 12-115(a)(3)): an online sweepstakes game is available online or on a mobile device, uses a dual-currency payment system exchangeable for prizes, cash or cash equivalents, and simulates casino-style games, lottery games or sports wagering. Games that do not award cash prizes or cash equivalents are excluded, and \"cash equivalents\" is not defined.","Prohibition (§ 12-115(b)): no person may operate, conduct or promote an online sweepstakes game. Applicants, licensees, and financial institutions, payment processors, geolocation providers, gaming content suppliers, platform providers and media affiliates of a licensee may not support one.","Penalty (§ 12-115(c)): misdemeanor, up to 3 years' imprisonment and/or a fine of $10,000 to $100,000. If a person is \"found in violation,\" the Agency shall deny the license application or revoke the license, so mandatory denial or revocation does not obviously depend on a conviction.","Disclosure (SG § 9-1A-07(g)): on application for a license under § 9-1A-06(a)(2) or (3) and annually after issuance, the applicant or licensee must list the jurisdictions where it or any affiliate directly or indirectly took revenue from supplying interactive game content in an \"illegal interactive gaming market,\" meaning any jurisdiction that prohibits interactive gaming. A material misrepresentation or omission may lead to denial, suspension or revocation, and to penalties for officers or board members. The existing false-statement offense is relettered (h).","Tainted-revenue bar (new § 9-1A-08.1): the Commission may not license, or allow to commence or continue operations, a person (including affiliates and entities under common control) that knowingly accepts revenue directly or indirectly derived from FATF blacklist jurisdictions, US-designated state sponsors of terrorism, or jurisdictions that prohibit online casino gaming where the revenue is from online casino gaming there. Revocation is discretionary after notice and a hearing if it would further the public interest.","Conforming and effective date: § 9-1E-03(a)(1) is amended to apply § 9-1A-08.1 to sports wagering licensees and their employees and contractors. The Act takes effect July 1, 2025."],"issue_tags":["sweeps","licensing","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Of the listed bills, CA AB 831 is the closest on sweepstakes, as it addresses contests and sweepstakes that simulate casino-style play. AR HB1861 also targets illegal online casino and sports betting operators, but makes operation a felony and pairs it with an Interactive Gaming Act, whereas MD HB1140 uses a misdemeanor with fines of $10,000 to $100,000 and up to 3 years. The listed bill summaries show nothing like MD's licensing-linked disclosure and tainted-revenue bar (§§ 9-1A-07(g), 9-1A-08.1), which reaches affiliates and extends to sports wagering licensees through § 9-1E-03; this appears to be the unusual feature.","watch":"Watch the affiliate-wide, \"directly or indirectly\" tainted-revenue test in § 9-1A-08.1 and the annual illegal-market disclosure, because revenue from any affiliate in a jurisdiction that prohibits online casino or interactive gaming could block licensure or force cessation of operations, and the undefined \"knowingly\" and \"cash equivalents\" terms and the vendor-support prohibition could also reach suppliers and payment partners.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":8114,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:47:59.811Z"},"MD-HB218":{"summary":"Maryland HB 218 is a Department of Human Services child support omnibus that mainly amends enforcement and guideline provisions. Its gaming-relevant piece is an amendment to Family Law § 10-113.1, which extends the existing lottery and video lottery prize-intercept regime for child support arrears to sports wagering licensees and fantasy competition operators. Other changes include longer contempt limitation periods, a lien on personal injury awards, and independent contractor new-hire reporting.","mechanics":["Family Law § 10-113.1(e)-(g), (i): when a certified obligor (arrears of $150 or more per § 10-113.1(b)) wins a sports wagering or fantasy competition prize that requires IRS Form W-2G or an equivalent, the sports wagering licensee or fantasy competition operator must give the obligor notice, withhold the prize up to the arrearage, and transfer it to the Child Support Administration, paying any excess to the obligor.","Covered operators: 'sports wagering licensee' is defined to include mobile sports wagering licensees, online sports wagering operators, and sports wagering facility licensees (as defined in State Government § 9-1E-01). Fantasy competition operator and fantasy competition take their State Government § 9-1D-01 meanings.","Process and priority: the obligor has 15 days to appeal to the Administration. Interception requests are honored in this order: this section, then Criminal Procedure § 11-618, then State Finance and Procurement § 3-307. Good-faith substantial compliance is shielded from liability under § 10-113.1(i). The text does not add an operator fee, penalty, or reporting deadline.","Other enforcement: contempt limitations in CJP § 5-111 and FL § 10-102 go from 3 to 7 years running from when each installment originally became due. New FL § 10-119.4 creates a lien by operation of law on the net recovery of personal injury awards (not for recipients under 12), enforced through paying agents. New § 10-139 requires employers to report independent contractors (1099 payees) within 20 days, with a warning for the first violation, then $20 per month, or $500 for conspiracy.","Driver's license and guidelines: FL § 10-119 adds a 250% of federal poverty ($37,650) income ground to contest license suspension, and the commercial-license threshold is 120 days versus 60 for noncommercial. Guideline changes in § 12-201 and § 12-204 add a 92-overnight deduction for other children in the home, multiplied by 0.75. New § 12-104.2 terminates foster-care support orders.","Effective October 1, 2026 (Section 2)."],"issue_tags":["integrity"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"Nothing in the other states' list is comparable. The listed bills (for example GA SB 57, IL HB 2239, CT SB 1464, AK HB 145) are licensing, tax, or consumer-protection measures. HB 218 does not regulate wagering. It adds sports wagering and fantasy operators to Maryland's existing lottery and video lottery child-support prize-intercept mechanism, which is unusual because it makes private licensees withholding agents for state arrears collection. Among the listed bills, CT SB 1463 only touches winner-name disclosure and revenue transfers, not prize interception.","watch":"Operators should watch the new withholding, notice and remittance duties in § 10-113.1(e)-(g), including the unaddressed question of how they will get the certified obligor list from the State Lottery and Gaming Control Agency, how the W-2G trigger and interception priority interact with other offsets, and how the good-faith immunity in § 10-113.1(i) applies, because the bill adds no operator fee, penalty, or implementation timeline.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":47186,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:48:00.754Z"},"MD-HB350":{"summary":"This is Maryland's fiscal 2026 operating budget (Ch. 602), which appropriates funds by agency for the year starting July 1, 2025. It is a general appropriations act and contains no substantive gaming, sports-betting or iGaming regulation in the portion supplied. The only gaming-adjacent items visible are Maryland Stadium Authority racing-related fund lines. The supplied text is truncated, ending at the Maryland Thoroughbred Racetrack Operating Authority heading, so I cannot confirm what appears in later sections such as the Lottery and Gaming Control Agency.","mechanics":["Maryland Stadium Authority, D28A03.69 Racing and Community Development Financing Fund: special fund appropriation. The extracted text shows several figures (17,000,000; 0; 1,000,000; 13,400,000) that look like original and amended amounts. Which is the final enacted figure is unclear from the extraction. The text does not state the source of these special funds.","Maryland Stadium Authority, D28A03.02 Maryland Stadium Facilities Fund: special fund appropriation of 59,505,777, shown as reduced to 59,255,777.","Maryland Thoroughbred Racetrack Operating Authority: the heading and a special fund line beginning 1,500 appear before the text cuts off, so the amount cannot be read.","Maryland Cannabis Administration (D23A01): a $100,000 restriction on administrative funds pending a report due September 1, 2025 on community engagement for the cannabis incubator project. D23A01.03 also makes $5,000,000 of the Office of Social Equity appropriation contingent on legislation allowing the Cannabis Regulation and Enforcement Fund to fund the Social Equity Partnership Grants. This is adjacent to, but not part of, the gaming or collectibles sectors.","The text contains no wagering tax rates, licensing provisions, definitions, advertising rules, or sweepstakes or trading-card provisions.","Status: approved by the Governor with a line-item veto (Chapter 602). The extracted text shows struck and revised figures but does not identify which were vetoed or reduced."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. This is a general appropriations act rather than a gaming policy bill. Its only gaming-adjacent content is the Stadium Authority racing and facilities fund lines, and nothing in the supplied text sets or changes sports-betting or iGaming policy.","watch":"Check the unreproduced portions of the budget (especially the Lottery and Gaming Control Agency and any Comptroller or fund-transfer language) and the Stadium Authority racing fund lines for gaming-derived revenue allocations or contingent reductions tied to separate legislation, because the supplied text is truncated and the racing fund amounts are ambiguous.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":167847,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:48:05.730Z"},"MD-HB465":{"summary":"Maryland HB 465 amends the State Government Article to add problem-gambling safeguards across the State's lottery, video lottery, fantasy competition and sports wagering regimes. It also bans player-specific proposition bets on collegiate participants and requires sports wagering licensees to give anonymised transactional data to three Maryland universities for research. The bill takes effect July 1, 2025.","mechanics":["Voluntary exclusion lists: new §9-109(d) (lottery and games), amended §9-1A-24(e) (video lottery), new §9-1D-05(a) (fantasy) and new §9-1E-04(b)(7)-(8) (sports wagering) require the Commission to adopt regulations creating lists and a simple mechanism for a sober, informed person to self-exclude for a specified period. The Commission must pass the person's contact information to the Maryland Center for Excellence on Problem Gambling unless the person affirmatively declines.","Fantasy competitions: new §9-1D-02(b) bars anyone under 21 from participating, and bars operators from offering fantasy competitions, or related services, with an entry fee to anyone under 21.","College props: new §9-1E-09(f) defines 'proposition bet' as a wager on an individual action, statistic or occurrence, whether player- or team-related, not directly connected to the final outcome or score. It bars licensees from offering or accepting, and individuals from placing, a player-specific proposition bet on a participant in a collegiate sports or athletic event.","Mobile licensee duties under §9-1E-11(b): age and identity verification at account opening with periodic reverification; a 1-800-GAMBLER message at log-on and log-off; mandatory account-holder deposit limits with a lockout period; a temporary suspension tool; a ban on credit cards for sports wagering transactions; a problem gambling disclosure at login, with certification before account creation and monthly thereafter.","Suspension and limit rules, new §9-1E-11(f)-(h): no sports-wagering marketing email during a suspension of at least 72 hours; a deposit-limit increase takes effect only after at least 24 hours or the end of the prior limit period, whichever is later; controls cannot be changed during a suspension; the account holder keeps access and may withdraw funds.","Data sharing, new §9-1E-18: by September 1 each year, licensees must give Morgan State University, Bowie State University and the University of Maryland School of Medicine quarterly transactional data and metrics. These cover bans, complaints, demographics, geolocation, platform use, balances, bet amounts, bonus usage, deposits and withdrawals, plus counts of participants showing erratic or increased gambling behaviour and the number and length of resulting bans or suspensions. The data must exclude personally identifiable information and is exempt from the Public Information Act. The universities may disclose it only for research, prevalence studies or peer-reviewed reports."],"issue_tags":["integrity","prop","licensing","advertising"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Maryland's ban on player-specific collegiate prop bets (§9-1E-09(f)) targets college athlete protection through the wagering side. The Delaware (HB314) and Hawaii (HB 2384, SB3263) bills in the list are student-athlete bills, and the Georgia NIL bills (HB1276, HB383) address NIL compensation, not betting markets. Connecticut HB05229 also deals with college campuses, but through advertising limits, withdrawal rules and a ban on AI targeting of bets, none of which appear here. Maryland's mandatory data-sharing with three named universities and its self-exclusion requirements across lottery, video lottery, fantasy and sports betting are unusual compared with the listed bills. Illinois HB5307 (problem gambling task force) and HB5059 (prediction markets, age 21) are the nearest problem-gambling and age-limit comparisons.","watch":"Operators should focus on the §9-1E-18 data-sharing mandate, which covers granular financial and geolocation data and requires internally flagging 'erratic or increased gambling behavior' without a defined standard, as well as the credit card ban, the mandatory deposit limits and the college player-prop prohibition, which would remove a significant market.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":16736,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:48:11.906Z"},"MD-HB17":{"summary":"HB 17 creates a new Subtitle 1F of Title 9 of the State Government Article authorizing online casino-style gaming (\"Internet gaming\") in Maryland under the State Lottery and Gaming Control Commission. Licenses go first to existing video lottery operators and sports wagering facility licensees, with limited competitive rounds and social-equity ownership requirements. The bill sets a state-controlled revenue split, responsible-gaming rules, labor-peace requirements and multijurisdictional-agreement authority, and it is subject to a statewide referendum. The supplied text cuts off partway through § 9-1F-11(D)(6), so §§ 9-1F-12 and 9-1F-13 are not visible. The title and the conforming amendments indicate that § 9-1F-13 creates the Video Lottery Facility Employee Displacement Fund.","mechanics":["Licensing (§ 9-1F-03): the Commission may license (1) video lottery operators, (2) holders of sports wagering facility licenses under § 9-1E-06(a)(2)(i)2 or 3, and (3) Maryland-headquartered applicants (10 years in the State, at least 250 full-time-equivalent employees on 12/31/2024, a prior qualified 5%+ interest in a video lottery operator, a Maryland-based brand, and a $5M live studio commitment). A video lottery operator that partners with social equity applicants holding at least 33% of a joint venture may get an additional license. The Commission may then run two competitive rounds, including five extra licenses, with the first round limited to applicants at least 33% owned by social equity applicants. Otherwise an applicant needs at least 5% social equity ownership or a profit-sharing agreement with qualifying non-management employees. Each licensee may contract with only one Internet gaming operator (§ 9-1F-02(a)(2)).","Fees and term: $1,000,000 initial fee, payable in installments, and a 5-year term. Renewal costs 1% of average annual retained proceeds over the prior 3 years. Ownership or control may not be transferred for 3 years, with exceptions for death, incapacity, bankruptcy or receivership, and court order. The Commission may waive requirements for an applicant holding a gaming license in Maryland or at least three other states.","Proceeds and split (§§ 9-1F-01(I), 9-1F-05): proceeds are money bet less winnings and prizes. During a licensee's first 5 years they are also reduced by a share of redeemed free play and promotional credits, from 35% (proceeds up to $4M) down to 20% (proceeds above $12M). All proceeds go daily to the State Lottery Fund, and the licensee retains 80% of live-dealer proceeds and 45% of all other Internet gaming proceeds. The remainder is distributed monthly in fixed dollar amounts for FY2027-2031 to local jurisdictions with video lottery facilities, the Purse Dedication Account, the Racetrack Facility Renewal Account, and the Small, Minority, and Women-Owned Businesses Account. Up to $10M goes to the employee displacement fund in the first 12 months. Percentage shares are 1% each to the Agency, the Problem Gambling Fund and county education funding, with the rest to the Blueprint for Maryland's Future Fund.","Live dealer and games (§ 9-1F-10): authorized interactive games are Commission-approved online versions of casino games, including tournaments. A live gaming studio must be in Maryland, and in the same county as the facility if the licensee is a video lottery operator. Live studio dealers must be licensed.","Operating and integrity rules: licensees must be 21+, verify age and identity, keep data 5 years, and may not use false or deceptive advertising or target at-risk or prohibited persons. Credit cards are banned. Account holders must set deposit limits, and a limit increase takes effect only after 24 hours. There are temporary self-suspensions, a voluntary exclusion list, and monthly responsible-gambling certifications. The Commission may fine up to $1,000,000 for tampering and suspend licenses for at least 30 days. Under § 9-1F-02(e), applicants and affiliates may not knowingly take revenue from FATF blacklist jurisdictions, state sponsors of terrorism, or jurisdictions where online casino gaming is prohibited, and must file annual disclosures about illegal interactive gaming markets. Licensees must submit a diversity plan within 30 days and comply with the Minority Business Enterprise program.","Labor and political conditions: applicants, licensees and live studio operators must show by clear and convincing evidence either an existing collective bargaining agreement or a labor peace agreement. The agreement must bar picketing, work stoppages and boycotts for the first 5 years, and the applicant must stay neutral on unionization (§ 9-1F-04(c)). The Governor may enter multijurisdictional agreements on Commission recommendation under § 9-1F-11. The Act is submitted to a referendum of qualified voters, and its operative effect depends on voter approval."],"issue_tags":["legalize","licensing","tax","advertising","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Among the listed bills, HB 17 is the only one authorizing full online casino gaming with a license pool limited largely to incumbents and social equity applicants. AR HB1861 also creates an Interactive Gaming Act, but it pairs that with felony penalties for illegal online casinos and sports betting, and it includes name, image, or likeness games. CT SB01464 and HB 17 § 9-1F-11 both authorize multijurisdictional peer-to-peer or interactive gaming agreements. FL H0189 touches internet gambling, but through broader gambling revisions rather than a standalone framework. The other listed bills (AK HB145/SB194, DE HB365, HI bills, GA bills) concern sports wagering, so HB 17 is distinctive for its operator-retention split (80% live dealer, 45% other) in place of a conventional tax rate, its mandatory labor-peace and neutrality conditions, its fixed-dollar legacy-industry set-asides, and its referendum requirement.","watch":"An operator should focus on whether it can qualify at all given the incumbent-first license pool, social equity ownership or profit-sharing requirement, labor-peace/neutrality showing by clear and convincing evidence, and tainted-market disclosure, and on the economics of the roughly 55% state share on non-live games, which the truncated text does not let us confirm past § 9-1F-11(D)(6).","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":54946,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:48:12.838Z"},"MD-HB352":{"summary":"Maryland's FY2026 budget reconciliation omnibus (Ch. 604) is a financing bill that touches dozens of articles. Its gaming-relevant piece, per the title and the amended-section list, is an increase in the tax rate on mobile sports wagering, which reaches State Government Art. § 9-20B-05(e), (f) and (i). The supplied text cuts off in the Economic Development Article, before the State Government sections, so the new rate, the effective date and any earmarks are not visible in what I was given.","mechanics":["The title states the bill is \"increasing the tax rate imposed on mobile sports wagering\". The amended-section list includes State Government § 9-20B-05(e), (f) and (i), but the operative text is not in the supplied excerpt, so the rate and effective date cannot be confirmed here.","Econ. Dev. § 5-1501(k) (as far as it is visible) directs set amounts for FY2026 through FY2028 to the Small, Minority, and Women-Owned Businesses Account from current allocations under State Gov't § 9-1A-27. The text breaks off mid-sentence, so the amounts are not shown. This indicates gaming-derived revenue is being redirected.","Bus. Reg. § 11-518(e) raises the cap on the share of open purses that the owners' and trainers' organization may deduct and keep from 2% to 3% (floor stays at 1%). This is a racing-side change.","Alc. Bev. & Cannabis § 1-323(f) and § 36-206 fund the $5,000,000 annual Social Equity Partnership Grant Program from the Cannabis Regulation and Enforcement Fund. The Fund may now also support that program.","The bill also changes many non-gaming taxes and fees, per the title: income tax rates and brackets, a new capital-gains rate, a pass-through entity tax, sales tax on certain services, custom software and bullion, and combined reporting. These may affect operators' broader tax exposure, but the provisions are not in the supplied text."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"Unlike AK HB145 and SB194, which would create a mobile sports wagering regime and a new tax, Maryland's bill raises the rate in an existing, already-legal market, and does so inside a general budget vehicle rather than a gaming bill. It is closest in form to Iowa's department-of-revenue omnibus bills (IA SF2427, HSB723, SSB3179), which also fold sports-wagering tax items into broader revenue legislation. It is also unlike the standalone tax bills IA HF673 and HSB122, and the new-market bills in GA, HI and DE (HB365). None of the listed bills is a comparable budget-reconciliation measure.","watch":"Pull the actual text of State Gov't § 9-20B-05 as amended to confirm the new mobile sports wagering rate, its effective date and whether it applies to the first reporting period after enactment, and check for any related change to promotional or free-play deductions.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":201155,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:48:14.999Z"},"MD-HB696":{"summary":"HB 696 is a general governance bill that strips U.S. citizenship requirements from the membership qualifications of dozens of Maryland boards and commissions, generally leaving a state-residency requirement. It replaces \"citizen\" with \"resident\" or \"private individual\" in many board names and member descriptions, and adds new State Government § 10–1702, which says that, to the extent practicable, the membership of any board, committee, commission, task force, or workgroup created by State law shall reflect the full diversity of the State. Gaming is touched only through one amendment to the Sports Wagering Application Review Commission, State Government § 9–1E–15(c).","mechanics":["State Government § 9–1E–15(c) is amended to delete the requirement that a Sports Wagering Application Review Commission member be a U.S. citizen (item (2)). Members must still be state residents and at least 21 years old, have 10 years of fiscal or financial experience, and have no gaming-related convictions, financial interests, or official relationships to applicants or licensees. The remaining items are renumbered.","New State Government § 10–1702(b) provides that, \"to the extent practicable,\" the membership of any board, committee, commission, task force, or workgroup created by State law shall reflect the full diversity of the State. Subsection (a) exempts standing committees under § 2–101 and committees established under Title 2 of the State Government Article. The bill defines no diversity criteria and sets no enforcement mechanism.","Citizenship requirements are removed from numerous licensing boards, including Public Accountancy, Architects, Electricians, Professional Engineers, Real Estate Commission, Nursing, Psychologists, the Workers' Compensation Commission (which also loses the qualified-voter requirement), the Home Improvement Commission, and the HVACR Board. Most keep residency requirements. Long-Term Care Administrators and Residential Child Care Professionals keep a 1-year residency requirement.","Many advisory bodies are renamed or reworded from \"citizens\" to \"residents\" (for example, Correctional Services § 4–207 and Family Law § 5–535), or from \"citizen\" to \"private individual\" (for example, Criminal Law § 12–301.1(f)(3) and Transportation § 2–606). These are stylistic changes.","Section 2 sets the effective date as October 1, 2025."],"issue_tags":["licensing","integrity"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. The bill is unusual because it is a cross-cutting governance bill that never mentions wagering; its only gaming effect is dropping the citizenship requirement for Maryland's Sports Wagering Application Review Commission, and the broad diversity provision in § 10–1702 would apply to that commission too.","watch":"A lawyer for sports-betting operators should note that the Sports Wagering Application Review Commission, which reviews license applications, would have non-citizen members eligible and be subject to the new \"full diversity\" membership directive, which could affect future appointments, though the bill leaves all conflict-of-interest and gaming-related disqualifications intact.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":40564,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:48:20.900Z"},"MD-HB797":{"summary":"HB797 amends two sections of Maryland's sports wagering subtitle (State Government Art. 9-1E). It directs the Sports Wagering Application Review Commission to accept and consider sports wagering license applications submitted before January 1, 2027, subject to the license-number limits in § 9-1E-06. It also pushes the sunset of the Minority Business Enterprise (MBE) requirements for sports wagering licensees from July 1, 2024 to July 1, 2026. The Act takes effect July 1, 2025.","mechanics":["§ 9-1E-15(h)(4)(iii) (new): the Commission must, subject to the limitations in § 9-1E-06, accept and consider any sports wagering license application submitted before January 1, 2027. The text does not say which license types are covered, although the purpose paragraph refers to sports wagering facility licenses and mobile sports wagering licenses.","§ 9-1E-07(b)(4): the MBE sunset moves from July 1, 2024 to July 1, 2026. After that date, subsection (b) and its implementing regulations have no effect and may not be enforced. Because the current sunset has already passed, the bill reads as a revival or extension of the MBE obligations.","The MBE obligations that would continue: licensees must comply with the State MBE Program 'to the extent practicable and authorized by the United States Constitution' (§ 9-1E-07(b)(2)). Within 6 months of license issuance, the Governor's Office of Small, Minority, and Women Business Affairs, consulting with the Attorney General and the licensee, must set MBE participation goals and procedures for procurement of construction, equipment and ongoing services (§ 9-1E-07(b)(3)).","Existing Commission duties in § 9-1E-15(h)(4)(i)-(ii) are retained: seek racial, ethnic and gender diversity in awards to the extent federal and State law permit, and encourage MBE and small, minority or women-owned applicants. The bill adds the new item (iii) after these.","The bill does not change tax rates, the number of licenses in § 9-1E-06, or fees. The effect of the application window depends on how many licenses remain under the § 9-1E-06 caps."],"issue_tags":["licensing","legalize"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"This is a narrow licensing-process and MBE bill. It does not set a tax rate, a framework or a new market, unlike AK HB145/SB194, DE HB365 and HI HB1308/HB2570/SB1569, which build full sports wagering regimes, or the Georgia bills (e.g. GA SB57, HB380). None of the listed bills deals with an MBE sunset or a fixed application window for an already-operating market. The nearest listed bill is CO SB 163, which concerns delegation of licensing duties, but it does not address application deadlines or diversity requirements.","watch":"Check which license categories and how many slots remain under § 9-1E-06 when the January 1, 2027 application window opens, and whether the revived MBE procurement plans under § 9-1E-07(b)(3) would impose goals on new licensees that run until July 1, 2026.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":3682,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:48:23.148Z"},"MD-HB922":{"summary":"HB 922 amends Md. Code, State Gov't § 9-1E-17(d) to change one word, \"may\" to \"shall.\" A sports wagering licensee or sports wagering operator that advertises in Maryland would have to contract with an independent evaluator licensed under § 9-1E-17(b). The evaluator would rate the licensee's sports wagering content, experts, influencers, and content partners. Under current law this contract is optional.","mechanics":["Section 9-1E-17(d) is amended so that \"may\" becomes \"shall\": covered advertisers must contract with an independent evaluator.","Covered parties are any sports wagering licensee or sports wagering operator that advertises in the State. The trigger is advertising in Maryland, not holding a license alone, so it could reach operators that are not themselves licensees.","The evaluator must be licensed under subsection (b) of § 9-1E-17. The bill does not change the licensing criteria or define the evaluation standards.","The evaluation scope is the licensee's sports wagering content, sports wagering experts, sports wagering influencers, and content partners.","The bill adds no new penalty, fee, rating threshold, or enforcement mechanism. Any enforcement would rely on existing licensee compliance and discipline powers, which the bill does not address.","The effective date is July 1, 2025 (Section 2). There is no phase-in or grace period."],"issue_tags":["advertising","integrity","licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"The other states' bills listed do not address a mandatory independent evaluation of advertising content, influencers, or content partners, so HB 922 has no direct analogue among them. CT HB05229, HB05269, HB05271, and HB05272 also regulate gaming advertising, but they do so through content and placement restrictions: bans at college campuses, bans on bonus inducements, and payment rules. HB 922 instead creates a mandatory third-party rating process for advertising, which is unusual. It is also a one-word amendment to an existing Maryland statute rather than a broader regulatory package.","watch":"Watch for how the Maryland Lottery and Gaming Control Commission implements the mandate, including evaluator licensing availability, rating standards, whether out-of-state operators that merely advertise in Maryland are covered, and what consequences follow a poor rating, since the bill itself is silent on all of these.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":1545,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:48:27.715Z"},"MD-HB700":{"summary":"HB 700 requires the Maryland Department of Health to run a separate prevalence study, with replications, on problem and pathological mobile gambling, with the first due by July 1, 2030. It also cuts the share of proceeds that fantasy competition operators and sports wagering licensees retain from 85% to 84% and routes 6.25% of the State's remainder to the Problem Gambling Fund. Together these changes work out to a one-percentage-point increase in the State's take, which funds problem gambling programs.","mechanics":["Health-General §19-804(b), (d): the Secretary must conduct in-person and mobile gambling prevalence studies separately. The initial mobile study is due by July 1, 2030, and the existing in-person deadline of July 1, 2009 is retained. Under unchanged §19-804(e), replication studies run at least every 5 years.","State Government §9-1D-04(a): a fantasy competition operator retains 84% of proceeds, down from 85%, and pays the remainder to the Commission.","State Government §9-1E-12(b)(1)(ii)-(iv): Class A-1/A-2 and B-1/B-2 facility licensees and mobile sports wagering licensees each retain 84% of proceeds, down from 85%.","Split of the State's share, in §9-1D-04(b) and §9-1E-12(b)(2): 93.75% goes to the Blueprint for Maryland's Future Fund and 6.25% to the Problem Gambling Fund. On the 16% remainder that is 15% of proceeds to Blueprint, unchanged, and 1% of proceeds to the Problem Gambling Fund, which is new.","State Government §9-1A-33(b)(7): starting November 1, 2026 and every November 1 after, the Secretary of Health must report to the General Assembly on how Problem Gambling Fund money was spent on treatment and prevention.","Section 2: the Act takes effect July 1, 2025. The bill text contains no new penalties or licensing changes."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"The comparable bills listed are legalization or tax-structure bills, whereas HB 700 leaves Maryland's regime in place and adjusts the revenue split by 1 point, to fund problem gambling. Hawaii's HB 1308 and HB 2570 pair a general excise tax on operator licenses with a problem gambling provision, and Alaska's HB145 and SB194 impose a new mobile wagering tax. Illinois HB1451 also addresses gambling disorders, but it amends the Substance Use Disorder Act's public education program and does not change tax or revenue distribution. Unusually, HB 700 splits in-person and mobile prevalence studies and applies the same one-point change to fantasy competitions and to all sports wagering licensee classes.","watch":"Watch that the drop in operator retention from 85% to 84% is an effective one-point tax increase, from 15% to 16% of proceeds, on mobile, retail and fantasy operators alike, effective July 1, 2025.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":6691,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:48:28.573Z"},"MD-HB484":{"summary":"HB 484 amends Maryland's fantasy competition subtitle (State Gov't 9-1D) to widen the definition of \"fantasy competition\" so that a single participant competing against an operator-set statistical measurement qualifies. It declares that a compliant fantasy competition is not betting, wagering or gambling for any purpose, and carves compliant fantasy competitions out of the sports wagering definitions in Subtitle 1E. It also adds responsible-gaming duties for fantasy operators, including a Commission-run voluntary exclusion list with prize forfeiture. Effective October 1, 2025.","mechanics":["Section 9-1D-01(d): adds a new item (3) to the definition. A fantasy competition can now be one where a participant competes against other participants OR, \"subject to the provisions of this subtitle,\" a single participant competes against a statistical measurement established by the operator. Teams may now be \"an imaginary team or teams.\" The existing bars remain: no winning outcome may rest solely on one athlete's performance, or on the score, spread or performance of any real-world team(s). Prizes must be fixed and known in advance.","Section 9-1D-02(a): replaces the current exemption from the Criminal Law Title 12 gambling prohibitions with a statement that a fantasy competition conducted in compliance with the subtitle \"does not constitute betting, wagering, or gambling for any purpose.\" The ban on kiosks and machines in 9-1D-02(b) is unchanged.","New 9-1D-05(a): every operator must maintain a program to mitigate and curtail compulsive play. It must also put a statement about help with real-money gaming problems on its portal, website or app and on all marketing materials and advertisements.","New 9-1D-05(b)-(e): the Commission must set up a voluntary exclusion list with a simple mechanism for a sober, informed individual to self-exclude for a specified period, and fold it into any other self-exclusion program it runs. Operators may not let listed individuals open accounts or play. Existing account holders may access the account only to view and download their transaction history. If an excluded person plays, the operator must report the name, date, amount of any money or prizes forfeited, and any other action taken. Operators may not pay prizes to listed individuals. Any prize won is forfeited and must be sent quarterly to the Problem Gambling Fund (§ 9-1A-33).","New 9-1D-05(f): the Commission \"may impose sanctions\" on an operator that knowingly fails to exclude a listed individual. The current 9-1D-05 is renumbered 9-1D-06, and its optional authority for regulations on a voluntary exclusion list is deleted.","Sections 9-1E-01(i) and (j): \"sporting event\" item (vii) (any portion of an event, including individual athlete statistics) now applies \"except as provided in Subtitle 1D,\" and new (j)(2) says \"sports wagering\" does not include a fantasy competition conducted in compliance with Subtitle 1D."],"issue_tags":["advertising","prop"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike GA HB1329 (regulation and taxation of fantasy contests) and IL HB2342 (Fantasy Sports Consumer Protection Act, with fees and a tax on adjusted gross fantasy contest receipts), MD HB484 adds no tax, license fee or licensing scheme. It works through the definition of fantasy competition, a gambling-law carve-out, and self-exclusion duties. FL H0189 also touches fantasy sports contests, but within a broad gambling overhaul. The distinctive feature here is the single-participant-versus-operator-set-statistic format, which comes close to over/under player-prop products, paired with an express exclusion from the sports wagering definition.","watch":"Watch how the new single-participant format interacts with the retained bars on outcomes based solely on one athlete's performance or on real-world team results, since the Commission's reading will decide whether pick'em or over/under player-stat products fall outside \"sports wagering\" and unlicensed operators can offer them, and note the drafting loose ends (the purpose paragraph refers to a \"mandatory\" exclusion list, the text uses \"voluntary\" and \"self-exclusion\" interchangeably, and 9-1D-05(b) refers to competitions \"licensed under this subtitle\" although the subtitle shown has no licensing provision).","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":10053,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:48:29.030Z"},"MD-SB1000":{"summary":"Maryland SB1000 (Ch. 332, 2025) lets certain Class B sports wagering facility licensees ask the State Lottery and Gaming Control Commission to relocate their facility. It also sets rules for branding and loyalty programs when a Class A licensee provides sports wagering services on behalf of a Class B licensee. It takes effect July 1, 2025.","mechanics":["New § 9-1E-07(j): a licensee issued a license under § 9-1E-06(a)(2)(ii) may submit a written request to the Commission to relocate a Class B sports wagering facility.","The Commission may grant a relocation only if the licensee was originally issued a Class B license on or before September 30, 2023.","The new location must be in the same county, comply with the § 9-1E-06 location requirements, and, subject to § 9-1E-15(q), be approved by the Sports Wagering Application Review Commission.","Amended § 9-1E-09(d)(1)(ii): a Class A licensee providing services on behalf of a Class B licensee may use the Class A branding and loyalty rewards programs at the Class B facility.","The same Class A licensee may not let an individual use the account wallet he or she holds with the Class A licensee to place wagers with the Class B licensee.","Amended § 9-1E-09(d)(1)(iii): subject to Class A approval, a Class B licensee receiving services may use and market the Class A branding and loyalty programs in the same manner as the Class A licensee. The existing stadium/video lottery operator same-county rule in (d)(2) is unchanged. Effective July 1, 2025."],"issue_tags":["licensing","advertising"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"None of the listed bills is comparable. They mostly concern launching or taxing mobile sports wagering (AK HB145/SB194, DE HB365, HI HB1308/HB2570/SB1569, GA bills), advertising and payment limits (CT HB05229, HB05269), or illegal gaming (AR HB1861, FL S1580). MD SB1000 is a narrow amendment to an existing licensed retail structure. It covers same-county relocation of Class B facilities and the sharing of brands and loyalty programs between Class A and Class B licensees, with no tax, advertising-restriction or legalization provisions.","watch":"Watch the account-wallet ban in § 9-1E-09(d)(1)(ii)2, which prevents Class A licensees from using their existing player wallets for Class B wagers, along with the Class A approval condition on Class B loyalty marketing and the dual Commission and Application Review Commission approvals for any relocation.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":4269,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:48:38.008Z"},"MD-HB991":{"summary":"Maryland HB991 (Chapter 620) extends the sunset of the Minority Business Enterprise (MBE) Program statutes from July 1, 2025 to July 1, 2026, and moves back the due dates for several related studies, reports and guidelines by about a year. The extension covers the general MBE provisions (SFP §§ 14-301 to 14-305), offshore wind (PUA § 7-704.1(j)(4)), and the MBE provisions for video lottery (SG § 9-1A-10(c)) and sports wagering licensees (SG § 9-1E-07(b)(4)). It also adds SFP § 10A-404, which applies the MBE Program to public-private partnerships. The bill takes effect July 1, 2025.","mechanics":["SG § 9-1E-07(b)(4) is amended so the sunset of that subsection and its regulations moves from July 1, 2024 to July 1, 2026. The purpose paragraph describes this as covering sports wagering licensees. The bill text does not reproduce the substantive MBE requirement in subsection (b).","SG § 9-1A-10(c) is amended so that subsections (a)(1) and (2) and (b), and the regulations under them, lapse on July 1, 2026 instead of July 1, 2025. The purpose paragraph links this section to video lottery terminals.","SFP § 14-309 is amended so §§ 14-301 through 14-305 and their regulations are of no effect after July 1, 2026 instead of July 1, 2025. PUA § 7-704.1(j)(4) gets the same one-year extension for offshore wind.","New SFP § 10A-404 incorporates the General Assembly's findings supporting the MBE Program. It applies the MBE Program to public-private partnerships to the extent practicable and constitutionally permitted. It bars Board of Public Works approval of a P3 agreement under § 10A-203 until MBE participation goals and procedures are set, based on Title 14, Subtitle 3 where practicable.","The disparity study and constitutional compliance analyses (2020 Ch. 553 §§ 4-5, 2012 Ch. 154 § 2) are now due September 30, 2025 instead of 2024. Other reporting dates move from 2025 to 2026: the capital MBE subgoal guidelines (July 1) and report (December 31) under 2021 Ch. 728, and the brokerage and investment management study findings and race- and gender-neutral approaches report (December 31) under 2022 Ch. 641 § 2(b) and (d).","Enforcement is by sunset only. After the new dates the provisions and implementing regulations are 'of no effect and may not be enforced'. The bill adds no new penalties or rates."],"issue_tags":["licensing"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"None of the listed bills deal with minority business participation or procurement-style diversity requirements, so there is nothing directly comparable. The closest are licensing bills such as IL HB2878 (master sports wagering license and application fees) and IL HB2761 (licensee eligibility), which regulate who can hold a license and on what terms. Maryland HB991 is unusual because it is a general MBE sunset extension that touches sports wagering and video lottery only through the sunset dates in SG §§ 9-1E-07(b)(4) and 9-1A-10(c). It does not change gaming taxes, licensing or conduct rules.","watch":"Because the SG § 9-1E-07(b)(4) sunset had already passed on July 1, 2024 under the prior text and is now set to July 1, 2026, check what the underlying subsection requires of sports wagering licensees, whether it applies to current licensees or renewals, and whether it lapses again or is extended once the disparity study and reports come in.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":10695,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:48:39.374Z"},"MD-SB299":{"summary":"Maryland SB299 (Ch. 703) amends the security guard regulatory framework in the Business Occupations and Professions Article so that video lottery operators, sports wagering facility licensees, and health care facilities are no longer treated as \"security guard employers.\" It also relieves cannabis agents who work as security guards, or for security guard agencies, of the State and national criminal history records check for cannabis agent registration. The bill is mainly a security-staffing and licensing measure; its sports-wagering effect is limited to the carve-out for facility licensees.","mechanics":["BOP § 19-101(m)(2) is amended so that \"security guard employer\" excludes a video lottery operator (as defined in State Gov't § 9-1A-01), a sports wagering facility licensee (as defined in § 9-1E-01), and a health care facility (as defined in Health-General § 19-114), in addition to the existing exclusion for security guard agencies.","Under the unchanged § 19-401(c), a security guard employer may employ a security guard only if the Secretary certifies the individual. Because the excluded entities are no longer security guard employers, this in-house certification requirement no longer applies to them by its terms. The text does not say what regime replaces it.","Alc. Bev. & Cannabis § 36-501(c)(2) is new: a cannabis agent employed as a security guard for a cannabis licensee or registrant, or by a security guard agency, need not obtain a State or national criminal history records check under § 36-505, provided the agent is authorized to provide security guard services under BOP Title 19, Subtitle 4.","The remaining cannabis agent rules are unchanged: registration is valid for 2 years, the minimum age is 21, and the Administration may disqualify applicants for crimes of moral turpitude.","State Gov't §§ 9-1A-14(a)-(b) and 9-1E-03(a)(1) are reenacted without amendment. Video lottery employee licensing still applies to sports wagering licensees and their employees and contractors through the cross-reference.","Effective October 1, 2025. The text contains no new penalties, fees, or enforcement provisions."],"issue_tags":["licensing"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills is comparable. The AK, GA, HI, and DE bills and the CT, CO, and FL gaming bills create or revise sports-wagering regulatory frameworks, tax rates, advertising limits, or licensing regimes. MD SB299 does none of this and only removes sports wagering facility licensees from the definition of security guard employer in the general security guard statute. The closest overlap is Colorado SB 163 and the Connecticut licensing bills (CT SB01235, CT SB01237) on licensing mechanics, but those regulate gaming licensees directly, whereas this bill adjusts a separate occupational-licensing statute.","watch":"Confirm whether Maryland's sports wagering facility licensees' in-house security staff are now outside the Secretary's guard certification under BOP Title 19 and instead governed only by the Sports Wagering Commission's licensing and employee rules incorporated through § 9-1E-03, since the bill does not say what replaces the certification requirement.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":7273,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:48:41.109Z"},"MD-SB195":{"summary":"Maryland SB 195 is a Department of Human Services child support package that mostly changes family-law enforcement and guidelines. One part matters to gaming operators: it extends the existing child support arrearage prize-interception regime in Family Law § 10-113.1, which now covers lottery and video lottery prizes, to prizes from sports wagering and fantasy competitions. Sports wagering licensees (mobile, online operators, facility licensees) and fantasy competition operators would have to notice, withhold and remit prizes that trigger a W-2G to the Child Support Administration. The effective date is October 1, 2026.","mechanics":["Amended § 10-113.1(a) adds definitions of fantasy competition, fantasy competition operator, sports wagering and sports wagering licensee, all cross-referenced to State Government Art. §§ 9-1D-01 and 9-1E-01. 'Sports wagering licensee' expressly includes mobile sports wagering licensees, online sports wagering operators and sports wagering facility licensees.","Amended § 10-113.1(e): when a certified obligor wins a prize from sports wagering or in a fantasy competition that requires an IRS W-2G or equivalent, the licensee or operator must give the obligor notice of the certified arrearage, the withholding duty, a 15-day right to appeal to the Administration, and the transfer of the prize. The Administration may certify obligors owing $150 or more (§ 10-113.1(b)).","Amended § 10-113.1(f) and (g): the licensee or operator must withhold and remit up to the arrearage amount and pay any excess to the obligor. Interception requests are honored in order: this section first, then Crim. Proc. § 11-618, then State Fin. & Proc. § 3-307. If no appeal is filed within 15 days, the Administration keeps the funds. If the obligor appeals, the Administration decides after a hearing.","Amended § 10-113.1(i): a video lottery operation licensee, sports wagering licensee or fantasy competition operator is not liable for good-faith acts or omissions taken to comply substantially with the section. The Secretary of Human Services and the Agency Director may jointly adopt implementing regulations (§ 10-113.1(h)).","Other parts of the bill extend the contempt limitations period for unpaid support from 3 to 7 years (CJP § 5-111; FL § 10-102). They also create a lien on the net recovery of personal injury awards (new FL § 10-119.4) and require employers to report independent contractors within 20 days (new § 10-139), with civil penalties of $20 per month or $500 for conspiracy. The bill also changes the guidelines to add a deduction for other children in the home (92-overnight threshold, 0.75 multiplier), adds a driver's license suspension income screen of 250% of the 2024 federal poverty guidelines ($37,650), and adds a foster-care support order termination provision.","The whole Act takes effect October 1, 2026 (Section 2)."],"issue_tags":["integrity"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills is comparable. The listed Alaska, Arkansas, Connecticut, Delaware, Florida, Georgia, Hawaii, Iowa and Illinois bills (e.g., AK HB145, GA SB57, IL HB2239, CT SB01464) create or revise licensing, tax, advertising and consumer-protection regimes for sports wagering and iGaming. MD SB195 is a child support enforcement bill with no licensing, tax or advertising provisions. Its only gaming link is that it adds sports wagering licensees and fantasy competition operators to Maryland's existing prize-interception mechanism, a compliance duty not seen in the listed bills.","watch":"Operators should check the operational burden of the new § 10-113.1 duty to notify, withhold and remit W-2G-level prizes to the Administration, including how they will receive arrearage certifications from the Agency and Administration, how they handle the 15-day appeal window, and whether the good-faith immunity in subsection (i) is adequate.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":47229,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:48:41.302Z"},"MD-SB310":{"summary":"Maryland SB310 (Ch. 246) amends the Youth Suicide Prevention School Program in the Education Article to add gambling to the problems the program addresses. It does not regulate operators, impose taxes, or change advertising rules; it only adds gambling to the legislative findings and to the permitted classroom instruction topics. It takes effect July 1, 2025.","mechanics":["Sec. 7-501(2): legislative findings now say youth suicide often coexists with drug abuse, alcohol use, and gambling addiction.","Sec. 7-501(3) (new): finding that a youth suicide prevention program should promote recognizing student behavioral health issues, recognizing students experiencing trauma or violence out of school, and referring students to behavioral health services.","Sec. 7-504(b)(1)(ii): optional classroom instruction objective now covers increasing pupils' awareness of the relationship between drug and alcohol use, gambling, and youth suicide.","The instruction is permissive: Sec. 7-504(b) says programs 'may include' the listed items, so there is no mandate to teach gambling content and no new funding, penalty, or enforcement mechanism.","Preamble recitals cite Maryland youth gambling survey data (17.5% of high school students reported gambling in the last year, up from 15.3%), youth focus-group feedback on advertising, and the 2022 launch of online and mobile sports betting. These are non-operative.","Effective July 1, 2025 (Sec. 2)."],"issue_tags":["advertising"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. This bill is unusual in that it is a pure education-code measure with no operator-facing obligations, tax, licensing, or advertising restrictions; its link to the gambling industry is limited to findings in the preamble and a permissive curriculum topic.","watch":"Nothing in the operative text binds operators, but the preamble's reference to youth exposure to gambling advertising and rising youth gambling rates could be cited later to support advertising restrictions or responsible-gaming funding measures in Maryland.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":7371,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:48:47.301Z"},"MD-SB490":{"summary":"SB 490 is an omnibus bill that removes U.S. citizenship requirements for members of many Maryland boards and commissions, mostly leaving a State residency requirement. It also replaces \"citizen\" with \"resident\" or \"private individual\" in the names and membership descriptions of various advisory bodies. New State Government Article § 10-1702 would require every board, committee, commission, task force or workgroup created by State law to reflect the full diversity of the State \"to the extent practicable.\" Only one gaming-related body is touched: the Sports Wagering Application Review Commission.","mechanics":["State Government § 9-1E-15(c) strikes the requirement that a member of the Sports Wagering Application Review Commission be a U.S. citizen. The 21-year age minimum and State residency requirements stay, and the remaining paragraphs are renumbered.","The other § 9-1E-15(c) qualifications are unchanged: 10 years of fiscal or finance experience, no conviction or probation before judgment for a serious crime or a crime involving gambling or moral turpitude, and no official relationship to an applicant or licensee. They also include no financial interest in gaming, no beneficial interest in gaming contracts, no elected office, and a financial disclosure filing with the State Ethics Commission.","New State Government § 10-1702(b) says that, to the extent practicable, the membership of any board, committee, commission, task force or workgroup created by State law shall reflect the full diversity of the State. Standing committees and Title 2 legislative committees are exempt under subsection (a). The bill sets no definition of diversity, no metrics and no enforcement mechanism.","Citizenship is struck from licensing boards in the Business Occupations and Professions, Business Regulation and Health Occupations Articles, including accountancy, architects, cosmetologists, electricians, engineers, real estate, nursing, dietetics and tax preparers. The Workers' Compensation Commission also loses its \"citizen and qualified voter\" requirement. Residency requirements are generally retained, for example at least 3 years for Foresters, 5 years for the Home Improvement Commission and 1 year for the Long-Term Care Administrators and Residential Child Care boards.","Many conforming wording changes replace \"citizen\" with \"resident\" or \"private individual\" in advisory board names and member categories, such as the Citizens Advisory Board becoming the Residents Advisory Board, the Citizens Review Board for Children and the Traumatic Brain Injury Advisory Board. The Critical Area Commission, which already requires members to reflect State diversity, is changed only by swapping \"citizen\" for \"individual.\"","The Act takes effect October 1, 2025."],"issue_tags":["integrity","licensing"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. This is a cross-cutting bill on board composition across dozens of Maryland statutes. Its only gaming-specific change is the removal of the citizenship requirement for the Sports Wagering Application Review Commission in § 9-1E-15(c), and it adds no wagering rules, taxes or licensing standards.","watch":"An operator's counsel should note that sports wagering license applications are reviewed by a commission whose members would no longer need to be U.S. citizens, and that the new diversity mandate in § 10-1702 could apply to that commission's membership, though both changes affect only who sits on the commission and not operators' own obligations.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":40541,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:48:56.205Z"},"MD-SB321":{"summary":"SB 321 is the Administration's omnibus Budget Reconciliation and Financing Act of 2025 (cross-file HB 352). It changes fees, appropriation mandates, fund compositions and State and local tax provisions across dozens of articles of the Maryland Code. The supplied text breaks off partway through the Education Article, so none of the sports-wagering or gaming provisions are visible.","mechanics":["The enacting clause amends State Government §§ 9-1A-27(d), 9-1E-12(b), 9-20B-05(e) and (f), 9-3209(b), 21-205(c) and 21-206(f). The supplied text ends before those sections, so I cannot confirm what they change. The title paragraph does not mention wagering.","The title paragraph and enacting clause list the headline revenue measures: altered State income tax rates and brackets, an additional rate on individuals' net capital gains, a pass-through entity tax, combined reporting for corporate groups, changes to the Maryland estate tax limit, and a new Retail Delivery Fee (Transportation Title 18.8).","Several mandated appropriations are cut or converted to discretionary: Education § 7-447.1(p)(9) falls from $110M and $130M to $40M a year; Criminal Procedure § 11-934(f)(2) becomes a $35M general fund appropriation; Agriculture § 10-407(d) changes 'shall' to 'may'.","Fees and penalties change: the weight-and-measure fee schedule in Agriculture § 11-204.7 is replaced with Secretary-set 'reasonable fees', and the nutrient-management reporting penalty in § 8-801.1(c) rises to a single $1,000 to $2,000 penalty.","The county share of nonpublic special-education placement costs rises in Education § 8-415(d)(3): 40% for FY 2026 and 50% for FY 2027 onward, up from 30%.","The bill repeals the inheritance tax subtitle (Tax-General §§ 7-201 through 7-234), the enterprise zone income tax credit, and the Low Intensity Support Services Program."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"The other states' bills listed are mostly dedicated sports-wagering, iGaming or lottery measures, such as AK HB145, CT HB05229, GA SB57 and HI HB 2570. SB 321 is an omnibus budget vehicle with no stated gaming purpose. Its closest analogues are Iowa's department-of-revenue omnibus bills (IA SF2427, IA HSB723), which fold sports wagering tax and administration into broader revenue legislation. The listed bills are not directly comparable on rates or licensing, and I cannot compare Maryland's wagering terms because the supplied text does not reach them.","watch":"Read the State Government Article amendments (§§ 9-1A-27(d) and 9-1E-12(b)) in the full text and any amendments adopted at the 2/28 hearing, since those are the likeliest places for a sports-wagering tax or fund change to be added to this omnibus bill.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":203814,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:48:56.310Z"},"MD-SB340":{"summary":"SB 340 would authorize the State Lottery and Gaming Control Commission to license online casino-style gaming (\"Internet gaming\") in Maryland under new Subtitle 1F of the State Government Article. Licenses go first to existing video lottery operators and certain sports wagering facility licensees, then to Maryland-headquartered applicants and competitive rounds, all with social-equity ownership conditions. Revenue is split between licensees and the State through a daily sweep into the State Lottery Fund. The Act would take effect only if approved by voters in a referendum, and it includes a displaced-worker fund for video lottery employees.","mechanics":["Eligibility (§ 9-1F-03): video lottery operators and certain sports wagering facility licensees (§ 9-1E-06(a)(2)(i)2 or 3) are eligible, plus applicants with 10 years of Maryland headquarters, 250 Maryland employees at 12/31/2024, a prior 5%+ video lottery equity filing, a Maryland-associated brand (waivable after 1 year), and a $5M live studio or film-studio commitment. Five more licenses may be issued in two competitive rounds, the first limited to applicants at least 33% owned by social equity applicants. Every licensee needs at least 5% social-equity ownership or a profit-sharing agreement with qualifying nonmanagement employees.","Fees and term: the initial license fee is $1,000,000, payable in installments if the Commission allows. The term is 5 years. Renewal costs 1% of average annual retained proceeds over the prior 3 years. Ownership or control cannot be transferred for 3 years, with exceptions for death, disability, bankruptcy or receivership under a lending agreement, and court order. Each licensee may contract with at most one Internet gaming operator (§ 9-1F-02(a)(2)).","Revenue split (§ 9-1F-05): all proceeds are swept daily into the State Lottery Fund. The licensee retains 80% of live dealer proceeds and 45% of all other Internet gaming proceeds. The State's distributions include fixed FY2027-2031 amounts to video lottery host jurisdictions, the Purse Dedication Account, the Racetrack Facility Renewal Account, and the Small, Minority, and Women-Owned Businesses Account, plus 1% each to the regulatory agency, the Problem Gambling Fund, and county education funding. The remainder goes to the Blueprint for Maryland's Future Fund. Up to $10M goes to the new Video Lottery Facility Employee Displacement Fund in the first 12 months of operation.","'Proceeds' (§ 9-1F-01(i)) is money bet less payouts and prizes. For the first 5 years of a licensee's operation, a sliding share of redeemed free play and promotional credits is also deducted: 35% when prior-year proceeds are under $4M, falling through 31.25%, 27.5% and 23.75% to 20% above $12M.","Operator conduct: players must be 21+, with age and identity verification and periodic reverification. Licensees must keep data for 5 years, may not target advertising at excluded or at-risk individuals, and may not engage in false or deceptive advertising. Credit cards are banned, and licensees must offer deposit limits, temporary suspension, and a voluntary exclusion list. They must also report yearly on erratic-play identification and bans. Applicants must show by clear and convincing evidence a valid labor peace agreement and union neutrality, unless a collective bargaining agreement already exists (§ 9-1F-04(c)).","Integrity and penalties: the Commission may not license, and may revoke the license of, a supplier or operator that knowingly takes revenue from FATF-blacklisted jurisdictions, state sponsors of terrorism, or jurisdictions that prohibit online casino gaming. Annual disclosure of revenue from illegal interactive gaming markets is required (§ 9-1F-02(e)). Tampering with games to alter odds or payouts draws a penalty of up to $1M and a suspension of at least 30 days (§ 9-1F-07). Live dealer games must be run from a studio in Maryland, or in the host county for a video lottery operator (§ 9-1F-10). The Governor may enter multijurisdictional agreements on the Commission's recommendation (§ 9-1F-11), though the supplied text cuts off mid-subsection (D)(6)."],"issue_tags":["legalize","licensing","tax","advertising","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"None of the listed bills is a comparable full online-casino authorization, because nearly all concern sports wagering or lottery matters. The closest are AR HB1861, which creates an Interactive Gaming Act while making illegal online casinos and sports betting a felony, and CT SB01464, which lets the Governor enter multijurisdictional peer-to-peer casino agreements, a feature SB 340 also has in § 9-1F-11. FL H0189 touches internet gambling more broadly. SB 340 is unusual for its closed, incumbent-first licensing tied to social-equity ownership, mandatory labor peace agreements, and an 80%/45% retention structure under which the State takes roughly 20% of live dealer and 55% of other proceeds. It also has a referendum condition and a displaced-worker fund.","watch":"A sports-betting operator seeking an iGaming license should check the closed eligibility tiers (video lottery operators and Maryland-headquartered applicants first), the 5% social-equity ownership requirement, the clear-and-convincing labor peace showing, the effective 55% State share on non-live games, the referendum contingency, and the unseen §§ 9-1F-12 and 9-1F-13 and effective-date text missing from the excerpt.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":54963,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:49:00.707Z"},"MD-SB470":{"summary":"Senate Bill 470 amends Maryland's fantasy competition subtitle (State Government Art. 9-1D) to cover contests in which a single participant competes against a statistical measurement set by the operator, as an alternative to competing against other participants. It states that a compliant fantasy competition is not betting, wagering or gambling \"for any purpose,\" and it carves compliant fantasy competitions out of the sports wagering definitions in Subtitle 1E. It also adds a new 9-1D-05 with responsible-gaming duties, including a Commission-run voluntary exclusion list, prize forfeiture, and sanctions for operators that knowingly fail to exclude listed individuals.","mechanics":["Section 9-1D-01(d): new item (3)(ii) lets a single participant compete against a statistical measurement established by the operator, \"subject to the provisions of this subtitle.\" Existing limits remain, renumbered as (5): no winning outcome may be based solely on one athlete's performance, or on the score, spread or performance of any real-world team. The \"imaginary team or teams\" requirement is also kept.","Section 9-1D-02(a): replaces the old \"notwithstanding Title 12 of the Criminal Law Article\" exemption with a statement that a compliant fantasy competition does not constitute betting, wagering or gambling \"for any purpose.\" The kiosk and machine ban in 9-1D-02(b) is unchanged.","New 9-1D-05(a): each operator must maintain a program to mitigate and curtail compulsive play. It must also display a statement on obtaining help with real-money gaming problems on its portal, website or app and on all marketing materials and advertisements.","New 9-1D-05(b)-(e): the Commission must set up a voluntary exclusion list, with a simple mechanism for a sober and informed individual to join it for a specified period, and fold it into any other self-exclusion program the Commission runs. Operators may not let listed individuals open accounts or play. Existing account holders may only view and download transaction history. Operators may not pay prizes to listed individuals. Forfeited prizes go to the Problem Gambling Fund (§ 9-1A-33) quarterly, as the Commission specifies. If an excluded person plays, the operator must report their name, the date, any amounts forfeited and any other action taken.","New 9-1D-05(f): the Commission may impose sanctions on an operator that knowingly fails to exclude a listed individual. Old 9-1D-05 is renumbered 9-1D-06, and its permissive language on a voluntary exclusion list in regulations is deleted.","Section 9-1E-01(i)(1)(vii) and (j)(2): the sporting event definition (which includes individual athlete performance statistics) now applies \"except as provided in Subtitle 1D,\" and \"sports wagering\" expressly excludes a fantasy competition conducted in compliance with Subtitle 1D. The Act takes effect October 1, 2025."],"issue_tags":["prop","advertising","legalize"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Of the listed bills, the closest comparables are GA HB1329 (regulation and taxation of fantasy contests through the Georgia Lottery Corporation), IL HB2342 (Fantasy Sports Consumer Protection Act, with Gaming Board fees and a levy on adjusted gross fantasy contest receipts) and FL H0189 (a broad gambling bill that also touches fantasy sports contests). Unlike GA HB1329 and IL HB2342, MD SB470 adds no tax, fee or licensing regime. Its main change is to widen the product definition to operator-set statistical benchmarks and to wall that product off from sports wagering, while adding self-exclusion duties. The sports-betting bills listed from AK, HI, GA and others legalize or regulate sports wagering and do not address this fantasy and sports wagering boundary.","watch":"Watch how the new single-participant-versus-operator-benchmark language, together with the \"not gambling for any purpose\" clause and the sports wagering carve-out, would let untaxed, unlicensed pick'em or player-prop style contests operate outside Maryland's sports wagering regime. The retained bar on outcomes based solely on one athlete's performance, and the bill's drafting inconsistencies (its purpose paragraph says \"mandatory\" exclusion list, the text says \"voluntary,\" and it refers to contests \"licensed under this subtitle\" although no licensing provision appears), could be sorted out in Commission regulations.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":10075,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:49:02.775Z"},"MD-SB708":{"summary":"SB 708 amends State Government Article § 9-1E-17(d) to make it mandatory, not optional, for a Maryland sports wagering licensee or sports wagering operator that advertises in the State to contract with an independent evaluator licensed under § 9-1E-17(b). The evaluator must evaluate and rate the licensee's sports wagering content, sports wagering experts, sports wagering influencers and content partners. The engrossed text makes the mandate contingent on the State Lottery and Gaming Control Commission having licensed at least three independent evaluators.","mechanics":["Section 1 changes 'may' to 'SHALL' in § 9-1E-17(d). This is the only substantive amendment to the statute.","Covered parties are any sports wagering licensee or sports wagering operator that advertises in the State. The text does not define 'advertises' or set any minimum level of advertising.","The evaluator must be licensed under § 9-1E-17(b). The evaluation covers four categories: sports wagering content, sports wagering experts, sports wagering influencers and content partners.","Section 2 makes Section 1 contingent on the Commission issuing licenses under § 9-1E-17 to at least three independent evaluators. The text does not say who determines that the condition is met or how it is publicly confirmed.","The Act takes effect July 1, 2025 (renumbered Section 3). The bill adds no new penalties, rating standards, reporting requirements or cost allocation."],"issue_tags":["advertising","licensing","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"None of the listed bills creates a comparable mandatory third-party evaluation of advertising content, influencers and partners. The Connecticut bills (HB05229, HB05269, HB05271, HB05272) regulate advertising directly, through campus restrictions, rules on payment methods and bans on inducements. Maryland instead requires operators to buy a private rating service from a state-licensed evaluator. The Georgia bills (e.g., SB57) are broader sports betting authorization and integrity measures. SB 708 is a narrow one-subsection amendment that turns an existing optional program into a mandate.","watch":"A lawyer for operators should watch whether the contingency in Section 2 is met, since it determines when the mandate bites. The text also leaves undefined what 'advertises in the State' means, what evaluators must assess, how the ratings affect compliance, and who bears the cost, so Commission regulations will probably fill those gaps.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Engrossed","text_chars":2516,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:49:07.420Z"},"MD-SB706":{"summary":"SB 706 directs the Maryland Department of Health to run separate prevalence studies of problem and pathological mobile gambling alongside the existing in-person studies. It also cuts the share that fantasy competition operators and sports wagering licensees retain from 85% to 84%, and sends part of the resulting state share to the Problem Gambling Fund. It adds an annual reporting requirement on how that Fund is spent.","mechanics":["Health-General 19-804(b) and (d): the Secretary must conduct a mobile gambling prevalence study and replication studies. The initial mobile study is due by July 1, 2030. The existing 5-year replication cycle in 19-804(e) would apply, and the study uses an independent researcher and current diagnostic criteria under 19-804(c).","State Government 9-1E-12(b)(1)(ii)-(iv): Class A-1/A-2 and B-1/B-2 facility licensees and mobile sports wagering licensees would retain 84% of sports wagering proceeds instead of 85%. The state share rises from 15% to 16%, and the text does not phase this in.","State Government 9-1E-12(b)(2): monthly distributions from the State Lottery Fund of sports wagering proceeds would be split 93.75% to the Blueprint for Maryland's Future Fund and 6.25% to the Problem Gambling Fund (9-1A-33). On a 16% state share, 6.25% is about 1% of proceeds.","State Government 9-1D-04: a fantasy competition operator would retain 84% instead of 85% and pay the remainder to the Commission. The Commission would then split that remainder 93.75% to the Blueprint Fund and 6.25% to the Problem Gambling Fund.","State Government 9-1A-33(b)(7): the Secretary of Health must report to the General Assembly by November 1, 2026, and each November 1 after, on how Problem Gambling Fund money was spent on treatment and prevention.","Section 2: the Act takes effect July 1, 2025."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"The listed bills mostly authorize or tax new markets, such as AK HB145/SB194, IA HF673/HSB122 and GA HB380/SB57. SB 706 amends an existing regime and does not authorize anything new. It raises the state take by one point and earmarks part of it for problem gambling. HI HB 1308 and HB 2570 pair a general excise tax with a problem gambling program, but as part of full legalization. IL HB1451 focuses on a DHS gambling-disorder education program, not a revenue split. A prevalence study specific to mobile gambling is unusual among the listed bills.","watch":"Watch the across-the-board drop in operator retention from 85% to 84% for mobile, retail and fantasy licensees, effective July 1, 2025 with no phase-in, and the mobile-specific prevalence study due by 2030, which could later support tighter mobile regulation.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Engrossed","text_chars":7019,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:49:12.629Z"},"MD-SB86":{"summary":"SB 86 amends Md. Code, State Gov't § 9-1E-11(b) to add a problem-gambling disclosure duty for mobile sports wagering licensees. Each licensee must prominently publish a set statement on the homepage landing page of its website and mobile app. The statement is \"If you or someone you know has a gambling problem, click here or call 1-800-GAMBLER for help\", or comparable language the Commission approves. The \"click here\" text must link to the website of the Maryland Center of Excellence on Problem Gambling. The bill is narrow and changes nothing else in the licensee's obligations.","mechanics":["Covered parties: mobile sports wagering licensees, under the existing online sports wagering duties in § 9-1E-11(b). The bill restructures (b) into (b)(1)(I)-(IV) and adds a new (b)(2).","New (b)(1)(III) requires the statement to be published \"prominently\" on the homepage landing page of both the online website and the mobile application. The bill does not define \"prominently\".","The mandated wording is \"If you or someone you know has a gambling problem, click here or call 1-800-GAMBLER for help\". The Commission may approve other comparable language.","New (b)(2) requires the words \"click here\" to contain a link to the website of the Maryland Center of Excellence on Problem Gambling.","The existing duties are kept but renumbered: age and geolocation controls, a description of repercussions for underage or out-of-state bettors, and procedures to bar prohibited individuals.","Effective October 1, 2025. The text has no new penalty, rulemaking, or phase-in provisions, so enforcement would presumably run through the Commission's existing licensing authority."],"issue_tags":["licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"This is a narrow, single-section amendment to an existing Maryland licensee-conduct provision, not an enabling or tax bill like AK HB145/SB194, DE HB365, or the Georgia sports betting bills. It is closest in subject to CT HB05229 and CT SB01464, which impose customer-service or toll-free-number requirements on Connecticut gaming licensees, and to the problem-gambling funding in HI HB1308. Unlike those bills, SB 86 prescribes exact homepage wording and requires a link to a named state entity, the Maryland Center of Excellence on Problem Gambling. It adds no new funding, hotline operation, or reporting duties.","watch":"Confirm that the homepage landing page placement and \"prominently\" standard can be met on both the web and app interfaces by October 1, 2025, and watch for any Commission guidance on approved alternative language.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Engrossed","text_chars":3374,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:49:14.394Z"},"MD-SB829":{"summary":"Maryland SB829 (Ch. 621) extends the sunset on the Minority Business Enterprise (MBE) program by one year, to July 1, 2026, in State Finance and Procurement § 14-309. It does the same for related MBE provisions on public-private partnerships, offshore wind, and the video lottery and sports wagering licensee provisions in State Government §§ 9-1A-10(c) and 9-1E-07(b)(4). It also pushes back the due dates for several MBE disparity-study analyses and reports, and adds a new § 10A-404 applying MBE goals to public-private partnerships.","mechanics":["State Finance and Procurement § 14-309: MBE provisions in §§ 14-301 through 14-305 and their regulations are now of no effect after July 1, 2026, replacing July 1, 2025.","State Government § 9-1A-10(c): the sunset moves from July 1, 2025 to July 1, 2026 for the MBE provisions in subsections (a)(1), (a)(2) and (b). The purpose clause ties this to video lottery terminals.","State Government § 9-1E-07(b)(4): the sunset moves from July 1, 2024 to July 1, 2026. The purpose clause ties this to sports wagering licensees. The old date had already passed, and the text has no retroactivity or savings language.","New State Finance and Procurement § 10A-404 applies the MBE program to public-private partnerships to the extent practicable and permitted by the U.S. Constitution. The Board of Public Works may not approve a partnership agreement until MBE participation goals and procedures are set, based on Title 14, Subtitle 3.","Public Utilities § 7-704.1(j)(4), the offshore wind MBE provision, moves its sunset to July 1, 2026. The Chapter 553 (2020) analysis and report deadlines move from Sept. 30, 2024 to Sept. 30, 2025. The Chapter 154 (2012) Croson-compliance study report is due Sept. 30, 2025, ahead of the 2026 Session.","The Chapter 728 (2021) guidelines and the capital-project subgoal report move to July 1, 2026 and Dec. 31, 2026. The Chapter 641 (2022) brokerage and investment management study findings and race- and gender-neutral report move to Dec. 31, 2026. The Act takes effect July 1, 2025."],"issue_tags":["licensing"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"None of the listed bills deals with minority business enterprise requirements. The nearest in subject are licensing bills such as IL HB2878 (master sports wagering license and application fees), IL HB2761 (licensing of sports facility owners and teams) and AK HB145/SB194 (mobile sports wagering licensing and criminal history checks). Those bills create or change licensing frameworks, while MD SB829 only extends an existing sunset on MBE conditions attached to sports wagering and video lottery licensees as part of a general procurement extension.","watch":"Watch whether the MBE participation provisions in State Government § 9-1E-07 that apply to sports wagering licensees are actually in force after the 2024 sunset lapsed and before this Act's July 1, 2025 effective date, and whether the 2025 disparity-study reports prompt legislative changes before the July 1, 2026 sunset.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":10697,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:49:14.403Z"},"MD-SB860":{"summary":"SB 860 adds Criminal Law § 12-115, which bans operating, conducting or promoting an \"online sweepstakes game\" in Maryland. The definition covers dual-currency games that simulate casino games, lottery games or sports wagering and can pay cash or cash equivalents. It also adds licensing rules in the State Government Article: licensees must disclose revenue from illegal interactive gaming markets, and the Commission may not license or keep operating anyone whose affiliates knowingly take revenue from certain blacklisted or prohibitive jurisdictions.","mechanics":["§ 12-115(a)(3) defines \"online sweepstakes game\" as an internet or mobile game, contest or promotion that (1) uses a dual-currency payment system exchangeable for any prize, cash, cash equivalents or a chance to win them, and (2) simulates casino-style gaming, lottery games or sports wagering. Games that do not award cash prizes or cash equivalents are excluded.","§ 12-115(b)(1) bars any person from operating, conducting or promoting such a game in the State. Under (b)(2), license applicants and holders, and any financial institution, payment processor, geolocation provider, gaming content supplier, platform provider or media affiliate of a licensee, may not support the operation, conducting or promotion of one. \"Promote\" and \"support\" are not defined.","§ 12-115(c): a violation is a misdemeanor punishable by up to 3 years' imprisonment, a fine of $10,000 to $100,000, or both. If a person is found in violation, the Agency \"shall\" deny the person's license application or revoke a license the person holds.","§ 9-1A-07(g): applicants under § 9-1A-06(a)(2) or (3), and licensees annually, must disclose the jurisdictions in which they or any affiliate directly or indirectly accepted revenue from supplying interactive game content in an \"illegal interactive gaming market\" (a jurisdiction that prohibits interactive gaming) in the prior 12 months. A material misrepresentation or omission may, at the Commission's discretion, lead to denial or discipline, including suspension or revocation and penalties for officers or board members. Existing subsection (g) is relettered (h).","§ 9-1A-08.1(a)-(b): the Commission may not license, or allow to start or continue operations, a § 9-1A-06(a)(2) or (3) licensee if it or an affiliate (including entities under common control) knowingly accepts revenue directly or indirectly derived from (1) an FATF-blacklisted jurisdiction, (2) a U.S.-designated state sponsor of terrorism, or (3) a jurisdiction where online casino gaming is prohibited, if the revenue comes from online casino gaming there. Revocation is discretionary and requires notice, a hearing and a public-interest finding.","§ 9-1E-03(a)(1) is amended to apply § 9-1A-08.1 to sports wagering licensees and their employees and contractors. The Act takes effect July 1, 2025."],"issue_tags":["sweeps","licensing","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Of the listed bills, CA AB 831 is the closest, since it deals with sweepstakes and contests simulating casino games, though it appears to work through the existing contest and sweepstakes unfair-practices law rather than a new criminal ban. AR HB1861 also targets illegal online casino and sports betting, but it makes operation a felony and creates an Interactive Gaming Act. MD SB860 uses a misdemeanor with up to 3 years and a $10,000 to $100,000 fine, and it reaches payment processors, geolocation providers, content suppliers and media affiliates of licensees. Its \"tainted revenue\" licensing test (§ 9-1A-08.1 and the § 9-1A-07(g) disclosure) has no counterpart among the listed bills, which are mostly legalization, tax or advertising measures such as AK HB145 and GA SB57.","watch":"Watch the combination of the broad, undefined \"promote\" and \"support\" language, the mandatory license denial or revocation under § 12-115(c)(2), and the \"affiliate\" and \"indirect revenue\" tests in § 9-1A-08.1, which could pull licensed operators and their vendors into liability through sweepstakes-adjacent affiliates or revenue from other states where online casino gaming is not authorized.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Engrossed","text_chars":8753,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:49:27.167Z"},"ME-LD2117":{"summary":"LD 2117 (P.L. 2024, c. 577, approved March 28, 2024) amends 8 MRSA §§1206-1209 to cut sports wagering license fees sharply and shorten license terms from four years to one year. The changes apply to licenses issued, granted or renewed on or after September 1, 2024. The bill covers four license types: facility, mobile, supplier and management services. It changes no tax rate, market structure or operating rule.","mechanics":["Initial and renewal fees for licenses issued on or after Sept. 1, 2024 fall as follows: facility $4,000 to $1,000 (§1206(4)), mobile $200,000 to $50,000 (§1207(4)), supplier $40,000 to $10,000 (§1208(4)), and management services $40,000 to $10,000 (§1209(4)). Licenses issued before that date keep the old fees.","Licenses granted or renewed before Sept. 1, 2024 stay valid for 4 years, but those granted or renewed on or after that date are valid for only one year, unless sooner revoked (§§1206(5), 1207(5), 1208(5), 1209(5)). Licensees therefore move to annual renewal.","A temporary license is now valid for the length of the license term under subsection 5, or until a final determination is made, whichever is sooner, instead of a flat one year (§§1206(6), 1207(6), 1208(6), 1209(6)). An initial license issued after a temporary license runs 4 years from the temporary license date for applications filed before Sept. 1, 2024, and one year for applications filed on or after that date.","The chaptered text of the temporary-license subsections still states the old initial fee ($4,000, $200,000, $40,000, $40,000) as the amount to submit with a temporary license request. Strike-through formatting is not visible, so it is unclear whether these figures were left unchanged or deleted.","The director's discretionary processing fee, set at projected cost with true-up or refund, is unchanged in all four sections. The fee is still retained by the director for administering the chapter.","A facility license is still voided if the licensee's underlying off-track betting license lapses (§1206(5)). Section 9 also corrects an internal cross-reference in §1208(6), changing 'subsection 2' to 'subsection 1'."],"issue_tags":["licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"The comparable bills mostly go the other way or address different issues. IL HB2878 sets a $250,000 application fee and a $15,000,000 master license fee, whereas Maine cuts its mobile license fee from $200,000 to $50,000 and its supplier fee to $10,000. HI HB 1308, HB 2570 and SB1569, AK HB145 and SB194, and GA HB380, HB 686, SB386 and SB57 would create new licensing regimes and, in HI and AK, add a tax. LD 2117 only adjusts fees and terms in an existing, already-live regime. It is unusual in pairing much lower fees with a four-year-to-one-year term cut, so licensees pay less per filing but renew four times as often.","watch":"Confirm which fee must accompany a temporary license request after Sept. 1, 2024, since the chaptered text still shows the old amounts, and budget for annual renewals and background-investigation processing fees under the new one-year terms.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":12218,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:49:28.901Z"},"MD-SB982":{"summary":"SB 982 amends Maryland's sports wagering subtitle (State Government Art. § 9-1E-01) so that a \"historical horse race\" is a \"sporting event\". It lets certain in-person sports wagering facility licensees take pari-mutuel wagers on historical races at Commission-approved terminals, which are in effect historical horse racing machines. It adds a new § 9-1E-09.1 with operating rules and excludes mobile licensees and other facility licensees from the product. Effective July 1, 2025; cross-filed as HB 1048.","mechanics":["Definition: new § 9-1E-01(i)(1)(VII) adds \"a historical horse race\" to \"sporting event\", with no definition of the term in the text provided. Because \"proceeds\" and the § 9-1E-12 revenue split are reenacted unchanged, HHR wagers appear to fall under the existing 85% licensee retention and 15% to the State Lottery Fund, then the Blueprint for Maryland's Future Fund. The bill does not say how those provisions apply to HHR pari-mutuel pools.","Who may offer: under new § 9-1E-09(f), only Class A-2 facility licensees at a thoroughbred or standardbred race course, State Racing Commission § 11-524 licensees (or subsidiaries) at the Timonium fairgrounds, and satellite simulcast facility owners or lessees under § 9-1E-06(a)(2). Wagers must be made by an individual physically present. Mobile licensees and all other facility licensees are barred (§ 9-1E-09(f)(2)).","Pari-mutuel structure: § 9-1E-09.1(c) and (h) require HHR wagering to be conducted as pari-mutuel betting, on races of any breed. Payouts come from bettors' pooled money, not as a wager against the licensee. The licensee's retention may not depend on any race outcome or wager. Licensees may run player-funded pools to cover minus pools (§ 9-1E-09.1(i)).","Terminals and location: wagering is only on Commission-approved terminals in a Commission-approved designated area, on Commission-approved days and hours. The Commission sets terminal counts per facility and may not authorize more than 4,000 terminals in total (§ 9-1E-09.1(g)(1)(iii)). Wager types need prior written Commission approval (§ 9-1E-09.1(f)).","Player-facing rules: before wagering, the terminal may not reveal the race's location, date, horse names or jockey/driver names. It must show true past-performance data current as of the race date. After selections are final it must display selections, official results and a replay, and the race's identity is revealed only after the wager is placed (§ 9-1E-09.1(g)(4)-(6)). Approximate odds or payouts must be viewable on each terminal. Facilities must post Commission-approved pari-mutuel explanations.","Wager limits: the minimum wager is 10 cents, and the minimum payout on any wager may not be less than the amount wagered (§ 9-1E-09.1(j)). The bill adds no new tax rate, penalties or enforcement provisions beyond Commission approval powers."],"issue_tags":["legalize","licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"None of the other states' bills listed address historical horse racing. The closest structural parallel is IL HB1405, which also amends an existing Sports Wagering Act definition to bring in a new wagering product, in that case exchange trade wagering. Other listed bills, such as GA HB380 and SB172, DE HB365, HI HB1308 and AK HB145, create or expand general sports betting or mobile frameworks. SB 982 does the reverse: it uses the existing sports wagering license structure to authorize a retail-only, pari-mutuel, terminal-based product and expressly shuts out mobile licensees.","watch":"Watch whether HHR terminal revenue is taxed and shared as sports wagering \"proceeds\" under the unamended § 9-1E-12, given the pari-mutuel structure and the 4,000-terminal cap, and the carve-out that bars mobile and non-racing facility licensees from the product.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":18695,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:49:31.353Z"},"MI-HB4292":{"summary":"Michigan PA 321 of 2023 (HB 4292) is a general-government supplemental appropriations act. It adds about $275.8M in FY2023-24 and about $339.8M in FY2022-23 across state departments, including capital outlay, drinking water and PFAS grants, health programs and school debt relief. It contains no sports betting, iGaming, or collectibles provisions in the text provided.","mechanics":["Sec. 101 supplements FY2023-24 appropriations by a gross $275,759,900, of which $103,689,900 is general fund and $117,070,000 is other state restricted revenue. Sec. 150 supplements FY2022-23 by a gross $339,808,300, mostly federal funds.","Sec. 110 appropriates $114,070,000 from the school aid fund for school district emergency loan debt relief, and $10,000,000 for late-qualifying eligible manufacturing personal property tax reimbursement.","Sec. 206 sets conditions for single-recipient grants: a legislator or department sponsor, a grant agreement with claw-back, a 50% initial disbursement, 7-year record retention, projects completed by Sept. 30, 2028, and public reporting by Sept. 30, 2024.","Secs. 302-304 raise capital project costs: the public health lab by $66M to $326M, the psychiatric hospital by $51M to $376M, and the MSU greenhouse and dairy project by $57M to $110M.","Secs. 401-404 and 451-453 direct earmarked funds, identified by population brackets, to water authorities, Muskegon County PFAS remediation, and a medical debt relief pilot of $4.5M. Unexpended amounts are work project appropriations under MCL 18.1451a.","Sec. 201 reports total state spending from state sources of $220,759,900 under Part 1 for FY2023-24, of which $148,970,600 goes to local units of government. The act took effect Feb. 13, 2024."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. This is a general omnibus supplemental appropriation with no wagering content in the text provided. It has no gaming tax, licensing, or sports-betting provisions, and the text provided cuts off partway through Sec. 453.","watch":"Nothing in the provided text affects sports-betting, iGaming, or collectibles operators, but confirm the truncated remainder contains no appropriations from the Michigan lottery, casino, or internet gaming funds.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":95776,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:49:35.547Z"},"ME-LD424":{"summary":"LD 424 is Maine's biennial general fund budget bill for FY2023-24 and FY2024-25, and the text provided covers only the Department of Administrative and Financial Services appropriations in Part A. Sports wagering appears only as a one-time $66,000 General Fund line in the Bureau of Revenue Services (program 0002) for tax-return programming tied to P.L. 2021, ch. 681. The bill makes no substantive change to gaming or wagering law in the text provided.","mechanics":["Bureau of Revenue Services (0002): one-time General Fund All Other of $66,000 in 2023-24 and $0 in 2024-25 to add lines to the individual, fiduciary and corporate income tax returns because of P.L. 2021, ch. 681.","P.L. 2021, ch. 681 is the act that authorizes casinos, off-track betting facilities, federally recognized tribes and certain commercial tracks to conduct sports wagering and revises tribal tax law. The bill only cites it by title and does not set a tax rate, license term or operator obligation.","Lottery Operations (0023) baseline: State Lottery Fund of $4,559,449 in 2023-24 and $4,609,447 in 2024-25, with 22 positions. This is continuing lottery administration, not a new gaming initiative.","Cannabis funds (Z263, Z264, Z265) carry baseline funding, plus $150,000 one-time in 2023-24 for the Office of Cannabis Policy trace-and-track upgrade under P.L. 2021, ch. 628. This is not gaming-related.","The text provided is chaptered as P.L. 2023, ch. 17, approved March 31, 2023, and cuts off mid-table in the Department of Administrative and Financial Services totals. I saw no gaming or wagering substantive provisions, effective dates or enforcement language in it."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. On its own, this bill is a general appropriations act, and its only link to sports betting is an administrative funding line for income tax form changes tied to Maine's 2021 sports wagering and tribal-state law (ch. 681). It sets no wagering tax rate, licensing rule or operator requirement.","watch":"Little to act on here beyond confirming that the $66,000 is only for income tax form programming, but watch for whether the revised tax return lines add reporting or withholding burdens that affect operators or tribal partners under ch. 681.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":152482,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:49:36.595Z"},"ME-LD1777":{"summary":"LD 1777 would legalize Internet gaming in Maine under a new 8 MRSA chapter 37, run by the Gambling Control Unit in the Department of Public Safety. Only federally recognized Indian nations, tribes or bands in Maine could hold an operator license (the bill's \"Internet gaming license\"). Each could hold one, and a licensee could use at most one licensed management services provider. Sections 1-13 also amend the 2021 sports wagering chapter (8 MRSA ch. 35) by repealing several provisions and conforming its definitions. The bill died in committee files on 2024-04-17. The text supplied is cut off partway through §1315, so any tax rate and later provisions are not visible.","mechanics":["Eligibility and fees (§1306): only a federally recognized tribe, nation or band in Maine may hold an Internet gaming license, one per tribe. The license can be transferred only to a Maine-based entity wholly owned by the tribe. The fee is $200,000 for initial and renewed licenses, the term is 4 years, and a one-year temporary license is available on payment of the $200,000 fee.","Vendor tiers (§§1307-1309): supplier license $40,000 and management services license $40,000, each with a 4-year term and temporary license option. Occupational license application fee $250, renewal $25 for one year or $50 for 3 years, waived for active casino-employee licensees under §1015. The director may also charge processing and background-check costs.","Management contracts (§1303(2)(F)): the director must approve the contract before play begins. The management fee is capped at 30% of the operator's adjusted gross Internet gaming receipts, or up to 40% if the director finds the fee commercially reasonable given the licensee's capital investment and projected receipts. Contracts with multiple operators require separate accounting for each.","Scope and base (§1302): \"Internet gaming\" is any game of skill or chance offered over the Internet for money or something of value, excluding pari-mutuel pools and fantasy contests. Adjusted gross receipts are gross receipts less winnings paid (including the cash value of prizes) and less federal excise tax. The director is to collect \"tax on adjusted gross Internet gaming receipts imposed by this chapter,\" but the rate is not in the supplied text.","Operations (§§1303, 1310, 1312-1314): wagers are accepted only from people physically in Maine and aged 21 or older. Operators must verify age, identity and location. Excluded bettors include licensee insiders and their household relatives, interested parties, self-excluded or listed persons, third-party agents and regulatory staff. House rules need director approval. Operators must report abnormal wagering, keep wager records for 3 years, and share anonymized account-level data with integrity monitors. Advertising rules must require disclosure of tribal ownership and restrict ads likely to reach or appeal to under-21s.","Enforcement (§1305): grounds for denial or sanction include good moral character, felony or fraud history, and financial responsibility. Sanctions are reprimand, probation, suspension, revocation, or a civil penalty of up to $25,000 per violation. The director can also sue for injunctions. Appeals go to Superior Court. Under §1315, licensees must intercept winnings to pay liquidated child support debt, checking a registry kept by DHHS."],"issue_tags":["legalize","licensing","tax","advertising","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Maine's bill is tribal-exclusive: only Maine's federally recognized tribes, nations or bands can hold the operator license, one each, with outside operators limited to supplier or management-services roles and capped at 30-40% of adjusted gross receipts. IL HB2239 (Internet Gaming Act) and AR HB1861 (Interactive Gaming Act) also create iGaming frameworks, but from their titles and summaries they do not appear to limit licensing to tribes. CT SB01464 (multijurisdictional peer-to-peer) and DE HB365 and HR6 (mobile sports wagering and internet sports lottery) deal with different or narrower products. Unusually for an iGaming bill, LD 1777 also amends Maine's existing sports wagering chapter to remove several license categories, and its Internet gaming definition covers any game of skill or chance.","watch":"Watch the tribal-only operator eligibility, the 30%/40% management-fee cap that limits what outside operators and platforms can earn, the sports wagering license repeals in Sections 1-12 (whose effect cannot be fully assessed from the supplied text), and the tax rate and any remaining provisions, which are missing from the text provided.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":59711,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:49:36.611Z"},"MI-HB5516":{"summary":"Engrossed Senate substitute for MI HB5516 is a placeholder general government appropriations bill for fiscal year ending September 30, 2025. It appropriates a nominal $100 gross, all from the state general fund/general purpose, across the legislature, executive, attorney general, state, treasury, DTMB, civil rights and related purposes. The text contains no gambling, gaming, or collectibles provisions.","mechanics":["Part 1, Sec. 101: total general government gross appropriation of $100, all state general fund/general purpose, for FY ending Sept. 30, 2025.","Sec. 201: under Article IX, section 30 of the Michigan Constitution, total state spending from state sources is $100.00, with $0.00 paid to local units of government.","Sec. 202: appropriations are subject to the Management and Budget Act, 1984 PA 431, MCL 18.1101 to 18.1594.","The title refers to expenditure provisions, disposition of agency fees and income, and declaring the act's effect, but the engrossed text contains only the line-item total and these general sections.","No line items are listed for the Department of Treasury or any other named agency, and no gaming-specific lines, tax rates, or regulatory directives appear."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. On its own, this bill is unusual in being a $100 placeholder appropriation with no line-item detail, and it does not mention wagering, the lottery, or gaming regulation at all, even though the departments named (Treasury, Attorney General) have roles in Michigan gaming oversight and revenue.","watch":"Nothing substantive for operators now; watch only for a later substitute or conference version that fills in the placeholder with Treasury, Attorney General, or gaming-related line items or boilerplate affecting the Michigan Gaming Control Board or iGaming and sports betting tax revenue.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Engrossed","text_chars":1726,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:49:41.759Z"},"MI-HB4437":{"summary":"Enrolled HB 4437 (Public Act 119 of 2023) is Michigan's omnibus appropriations act. It makes FY2023-24 appropriations across state departments, the judiciary, the legislature and capital outlay, and also makes FY2022-23 supplemental appropriations. The text provided is only Article 1 (Agriculture and Rural Development) and the veto notice, and the text cuts off mid-sentence in Sec. 802. The only gaming-adjacent content is the horse-racing and equine fund provisions, which touch the Michigan gaming control board. Nothing here addresses sports betting, iGaming, sweepstakes or collectibles.","mechanics":["Sec. 101 appropriates a gross $168,612,700 to the Department of Agriculture and Rural Development for FY ending Sept. 30, 2024, of which $92,781,800 is general fund/general purpose.","Sec. 110 appropriates $4,294,200 for fairs and expositions: $3,794,200 from the Agriculture Equine Industry Development Fund and $500,000 general fund. Line items include the horse racing advisory commission ($125,000) and purses and supplements for licensed tracks.","Sec. 801 requires all appropriations from the equine industry development fund to be spent on equine-related purposes, and bars non-equine spending without prior legislative approval.","Sec. 802 sets a priority order for equine fund revenue, and the first priority (a) is administrative, contractual and regulatory costs of the department and the Michigan gaming control board. The rest of the section is cut off in the text provided.","The act is effective August 1, 2023. The Governor item-vetoed Art. 9 Sec. 1003(26) (Dept. of Labor and Economic Opportunity) and Art. 16 Sec. 511(3)(h) (Dept. of Environment, Great Lakes, and Energy). The text provided does not show what those vetoed provisions said.","Sec. 211 adds contingency authorizations that are unavailable until transferred under MCL 18.1393(2): federal up to $3M, state restricted up to $2M, local up to $100K and private up to $100K."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. This is a general omnibus appropriations act, and the only wagering-related content in the text provided is the equine fund and gaming control board cost-recovery language in Secs. 110 and 801-802. It does not create or change any sports-betting, iGaming or card/collectibles rules, tax rates or licensing.","watch":"Check the truncated remainder of Sec. 802 and the unseen articles (including the vetoed Art. 9 Sec. 1003(26) and Art. 16 Sec. 511(3)(h)) for any funding that touches the Michigan gaming control board, since the text provided does not show their content.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":1611623,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:49:42.053Z"},"MI-HB4816":{"summary":"The engrossed substitute for MI HB4816 is titled 'Compensation of High School Student Athletes Act'. Despite the bill's caption, it does not ban high school NIL compensation. It permits it, subject to category bans, contract restrictions, parental consent and MHSAA pre-review. It bars schools, athletic associations and booster-type groups from penalizing athletes for earning NIL income or retaining agents or lawyers, and bars them from paying athletes or facilitating NIL deals.","mechanics":["Sec. 3(1) allows high school student athletes to earn NIL compensation from activities such as endorsements, personal appearances, autographs, merchandise and social media influencing. Sec. 3(2) bars compensation from activities that involve, display or endorse adult products, alcohol, tobacco/vaping, controlled substances, 'any form of gambling, including, but not limited to, sports betting and horse racing', weapons, or nutritional supplements.","Sec. 5 bars written NIL contracts if the counterparty is an entity formed to receive or pool funds for NIL opportunities (a collective), if payment depends on athletic performance or achievement, or if the contract requires missing school or instruction time. It also bars apparel contracts that require displaying sponsor apparel or advertising during an official team activity.","Sec. 7 requires consent from at least one parent or guardian before an NIL contract is signed, unless the athlete is 18 or older or is an emancipated minor. Sec. 9 requires disclosure of any verbal or written NIL deal to the Michigan High School Athletic Association (MHSAA) at least 7 days before commitment. MHSAA reviews it, flags conflicts, and must approve any revision. Contracts entered into, modified or renewed on or before the effective date are exempt (Sec. 9(4)).","Secs. 11, 15 and 19 bar schools, athletic associations, conferences and other groups from limiting athletic participation or penalizing a school because a student earns NIL income or retains an athlete agent, financial advisor or attorney. Representatives must be licensed in Michigan (Sec. 19(3)).","Sec. 13 bars school staff from identifying or assisting with NIL opportunities, acting as agent, taking compensation for facilitating deals, attending contract negotiations, or steering athletes. Parents and guardians are exempt. Sec. 17 bars schools, associations and groups (including booster clubs, dad's clubs and athletic foundations) from providing NIL 'compensation' to current or prospective athletes. Compensation is defined broadly and includes food, shelter, medical expenses, insurance, cash, barter, free products and discounts.","Sec. 23 requires nonprofit high school trade associations to report to the Department of Education on federal NIL legislation (by Dec. 31, 2024 and Dec. 31, 2025) and on member schools' implementation readiness (by June 30, 2026). The text contains no penalty or enforcement provisions and no express effective date."],"issue_tags":["prop","integrity"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"This bill is a student-protective, permissive NIL framework, like GA HB383 and HB1276 (Georgia High School NIL Protection Act) and DE HB314. Unlike the college-focused NIL bills (KY SB3, MS SB2517, NH HB312, NE LB370, HI HB2384), it covers high school athletes and restricts third-party deals as well as school conduct. It has a categorical bar on gambling and sports-betting endorsements (Sec. 3(2)(e)). The listed betting bills (MD HB465/HB518, NJ A4905/S3080, NC H828/H967) regulate betting products or operators directly, while this one only restricts what a minor athlete may endorse.","watch":"Operators should make sure no marketing, sponsorship or influencer arrangement involves a Michigan high school athlete, because Sec. 3(2)(e) bars gambling and sports-betting endorsements and the text does not say who bears liability if a deal is made anyway.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Engrossed","text_chars":12381,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:49:45.997Z"},"MI-SB189":{"summary":"Substitute for SB 189 is the general government appropriations act for FY 2023-24 (year ending Sept. 30, 2024), with supplemental FY 2022-23 appropriations in Part 1A. Part 1 totals $5,003,503,300 gross and $1,185,472,000 GF/GP. For gaming, it funds the Department of Treasury's gaming regulators and the lottery from dedicated gaming revenue funds, and it funds Attorney General operations partly from the state casino gaming fund and the state lottery fund. It does not change substantive gaming law.","mechanics":["Sec. 108(9) Casino gaming: $48,738,600 gross, 215.0 FTEs, GF/GP $0. Lines: casino gaming control operations $38,085,500 (185.0 FTEs); gaming IT services and projects $5,322,700; horse racing $2,121,100 (10.0 FTEs); Michigan gaming control board $50,000; millionaire party regulation $3,159,300 (20.0 FTEs).","Sec. 108(9) funding sources: state services fee fund $27,771,600; internet gaming fund $13,924,300; internet sports betting fund $2,836,500; equine development fund $2,240,700; casino gambling agreements $1,007,500; fantasy contest fund $958,000.","Sec. 108(15) one-time: $2,000,000 for gaming control IT services and projects, funded by the internet gaming fund ($1,000,000), internet sports betting fund ($200,000) and state services fee fund ($800,000).","Sec. 108(8) Bureau of State Lottery: $35,114,600 and 212.0 FTEs, funded entirely by the state lottery fund. Sec. 108(2) also draws $315,500 from the state lottery fund for Treasury administration.","Sec. 102(2) Attorney General operations draw $1,922,500 from the state casino gaming fund and $377,400 from the state lottery fund.","Sec. 202 makes the appropriations subject to the Management and Budget Act, 1984 PA 431. The provided text cuts off mid-sentence at Sec. 206, so any later boilerplate or gaming-related reporting requirements are not visible."],"issue_tags":["licensing"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states were listed. This is an omnibus general government budget, and gaming appears only as line items: the gaming regulators' operating budget in Sec. 108(9) and a few funding sources elsewhere. It is not a gaming policy bill.","watch":"Watch the internet sports betting fund ($2,836,500) and internet gaming fund ($13,924,300) allocations, plus any boilerplate in the omitted portion of Part 2, because they signal regulator staffing and funding levels and any later fee or assessment changes affecting operators.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Engrossed","text_chars":238327,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:49:48.141Z"},"MI-SB413":{"summary":"SB 413 amends section 15 of Michigan's Lawful Sports Betting Act (2019 PA 149) to change how the section 14(1) sports betting tax is allocated. The introduced text appears to show the existing allocation, with the proviso capping the equine fund's share at $3,000,000 per fiscal year still in place, so the cap-removal effect cannot be confirmed from the text as printed. The bill leaves the 30/65/5 split among the host city, the state fund and the equine fund unchanged.","mechanics":["Section 15(a): 30% of the section 14(1) tax goes to the city where the sports betting operator's casino is located, for eight listed uses, including street patrol officers, neighborhood development, public safety, anti-gang and youth programs, taxpayer relief, capital improvements and road repairs.","Section 15(b): 65% goes to the state, deposited into the fund (the fund is not named in this section; section 16 is referenced separately).","Section 15(c): 5% goes to the Michigan agriculture equine industry development fund under section 20 of the horse racing law of 1995 (1995 PA 279, MCL 431.320).","Cap proviso in 15(c): any amount above $3,000,000 in a fiscal year is allocated to the fund created under section 16. The title says the cap is removed, but the text shown still contains the proviso, so the intended deletion is not visible in this version.","No change to the tax rate in section 14(1), operator obligations, licensing or enforcement, and no effective date is stated in the text."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. On its own, the bill is a narrow revenue-allocation amendment that directs a share of the sports betting tax to an equine industry fund and does not touch the tax rate or operator conduct. The mismatch between the title (remove the cap) and the printed text (cap language still present) is unusual and suggests the text may be an unmarked or incomplete rendering of the amendment.","watch":"Because the bill only redistributes tax proceeds and does not change the rate, operator liability falls on the tax itself, so check the final enrolled text to see whether the $3,000,000 cap is actually struck and whether the amendment is paired with any change to the section 14 rate.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":1697,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:49:50.419Z"},"MI-SB412":{"summary":"SB 412 amends section 15 of Michigan's Lawful Internet Gaming Act (2019 PA 152, MCL 432.315), which allocates the internet gaming tax imposed under section 14. According to its title, the bill removes the $3,000,000 per-fiscal-year cap on the 5% share going to the Michigan agriculture equine industry development fund. The text shown does not mark the deletion, but the cap language appears in subsection (1)(c), and that is the apparent target. The bill does not change the tax rate or any operator obligation.","mechanics":["Sec. 15(1) split of the section 14 tax: 30% to the city hosting the licensee's casino (subdiv. (a)), 65% to the state fund (subdiv. (b)), and 5% to the Michigan agriculture equine industry development fund under sec. 20 of the horse racing law of 1995, 1995 PA 279 (subdiv. (c)).","Current cap in (1)(c): any equine-fund share above $3,000,000 in a fiscal year is redirected to the fund created under section 16. Per the title, this is the sentence the bill removes, so the full 5% would flow to the equine fund.","Sec. 15(2) is unchanged on its face: each September 30 the board tops up the host city from the fund if its combined receipts fall below $183,000,000, counting the internet gaming allocation, Michigan Gaming Control and Revenue Act wagering tax, sports betting act wagering tax, and development-agreement payments.","The sec. 15(2) top-up is itself limited so the city's total under (1)(a) and subsection (2) cannot exceed 55% of the section 14 tax received in the state fiscal year.","The text shows no new tax rate, license requirement, enforcement mechanism or effective date. Status is introduced and referred to the Senate Natural Resources and Agriculture committee on 2023-06-27."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Nothing in the other states' list is a close match, because SB 412 is a narrow revenue-allocation amendment to an existing iGaming statute rather than a legalization or rate bill. The nearest comparisons are tax and revenue-routing bills such as AK HB145/SB194 (a tax on mobile sports wagering revenue), IA HF673/HSB122 (tax receipts and appropriations) and CT SB01463 (transfer of online lottery and sports wagering revenue to the Early Childhood Care and Education Fund). Unlike those, SB 412 earmarks revenue for the horse racing industry and leaves the underlying tax untouched.","watch":"Because this only reallocates revenue already collected under section 14, operators face little direct exposure, but watch for amendments that touch the section 14 rate, the section 16 fund diversion, or the city top-up formula, since reduced flow to the section 16 fund could prompt later revenue-raising proposals.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":3640,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:49:54.451Z"},"MI-SB747":{"summary":"Michigan SB 747 (Public Act 121 of 2024) is the omnibus appropriations act for FY2024-25, with supplemental appropriations for FY2023-24, covering state departments, the judicial and legislative branches, and capital outlay. The text supplied is only Article 1 (Department of Agriculture and Rural Development) and is cut off mid-Article. It contains no sports-betting, iGaming, or collectibles provisions. The only gaming-adjacent items are horse-racing lines inside the agriculture budget.","mechanics":["Act approved and filed July 24, 2024, effective the same day. The Governor item-vetoed Article 9 (Dept. of Labor and Economic Opportunity): Sec. 1019(8) and (14), Sec. 1050b(18), Sec. 1050d(14), and all of Sec. 1050e. The text provided does not include Article 9, so the content of those vetoed provisions cannot be assessed here.","Article 1, Sec. 101: gross appropriation of $156,938,900 for agriculture, with $89,056,400 from general fund/general purpose. Sec. 201 states total state-source spending of $136,227,300.","Sec. 108 (Fairs and Expositions) appropriates a gross $5,014,200. Lines include Horse racing advisory commission ($125,000), Purses and supplements for fairs and licensed tracks ($2,073,600), Standardbred purses and supplements for licensed tracks ($991,100), Standardbred breeders awards ($345,900), and Standardbred sire stakes ($720,000). $4,514,200 comes from the Agriculture equine industry development fund and $500,000 from the general fund.","Sec. 104 draws $349,900 from the Marihuana regulatory fund and $350,000 from the Marihuana regulation fund for food safety and animal health. These are funding sources only, not substantive cannabis regulation.","Sec. 250 sets conditions for single-recipient grants. They require a legislative or departmental sponsor, a 12-month existence requirement for recipients, a claw-back provision, a 7-year records retention period, and a spend-by date of Sept. 30, 2029.","Standard boilerplate follows: contingency authorizations in Sec. 210 (up to $3M federal and $2M state restricted), reporting duties, and work-project designations."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. On its own, SB 747 is a general omnibus appropriations act, and the supplied text is limited to agriculture. Its only link to wagering is the horse-racing purse and equine-fund lines in Sec. 108, with no betting regulation, tax rate, or licensing change.","watch":"Check the omitted articles, especially Article 9 (LEO, including the vetoed Sec. 1050e) and any Michigan Gaming Control Board or Treasury lines, for iGaming or sports-betting fund appropriations or reporting conditions, since the supplied text does not cover them.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":1666083,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:49:57.820Z"},"MI-SB931":{"summary":"Michigan SB 931 (2024 PA 140) amends section 3 of 1997 PA 70 to restructure the sources and uses of the compulsive gaming prevention fund, which sits in the Department of Treasury. It adds appropriations from the internet gaming fund (2019 PA 152) and the internet sports betting fund (2019 PA 149) as funding sources and permits up to $1,040,500 a year to go to the domestic and sexual violence prevention and treatment board. It imposes no new tax or operator obligation on its face; the funding comes through appropriations from existing fund balances.","mechanics":["Fund created within Treasury (sec. 3(1)); unspent money carries over and does not lapse to the general fund (sec. 3(5)).","Deposits under sec. 3(2): appropriations from the state services fee fund (casino); lottery fund money equal to at least 10% of annual lottery advertising budget, capped at $2,000,000; 1/15 of 1% of gross wagers at licensed racetracks via the equine industry development fund; and appropriations from the internet gaming fund and the internet sports betting fund.","The internet gaming and sports betting deposits are 'money appropriated' from those funds under sec. 16 of the respective 2019 acts, with no fixed percentage or dollar amount in the text, so the amounts are set through the appropriations process.","Up to $1,040,500 annually may be distributed to the domestic and sexual violence prevention and treatment board (1978 PA 389, MCL 400.1502) under sec. 3(3).","The rest goes as the DHHS director determines, exclusively for treatment, prevention, education, training, research and evaluation for pathological gamblers and their families, and to fund the toll-free helpline (sec. 3(3)).","The state treasurer directs investment and credits earnings to the fund (sec. 3(4)); DHHS may set fees for pathological gambling treatment (sec. 3(6)). Effective on the 91st day after sine die adjournment of the 2024 regular session."],"issue_tags":["integrity"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. On its own, the bill is unusual in using appropriations from the online casino and online sports betting funds, rather than a fixed percentage of revenue or a per-operator assessment, to finance problem gambling programs. It also allows a portion of the money to go to a domestic and sexual violence board, which is outside the usual scope of problem gambling funding.","watch":"Operators should watch the annual appropriations from the internet gaming and internet sports betting funds, since the bill sets no cap or formula and the amounts could grow, and should check whether any of that money ultimately reduces funds otherwise available for other purposes such as local or state aid.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":3505,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:49:57.825Z"},"MI-SB760":{"summary":"The engrossed text is not an appropriations act despite the title. It is the \"Leading Ethical AI Development for Kids Act,\" which regulates operators that make generative-AI \"advanced chatbots\" available to Michigan minors (under 18). It bars companionship-simulating features, harmful outputs and training on minors' inputs without parental consent unless the operator has actual knowledge the user is an adult, and it limits use of personal data for age determination. The attorney general can sue for civil fines and other relief, and minors have a private right of action.","mechanics":["Sec. 3 defines \"advanced chatbot\" as a generative AI system with a natural-language interface giving ongoing, adaptive responses. It excludes systems used solely for customer service or product information, research or technical assistance, internal or employee-productivity use, clinical use under health-professional supervision, and narrowly tailored educational tools. \"Chatbot operator\" is any person making an advanced chatbot available to a user.","Sec. 5(1) bars features that simulate companionship (human or relational claims, first-person pronouns, personal opinions, flattery over safety, unsolicited emotional prompts, reuse of personal health data older than 12 hours or from earlier sessions, sexually explicit interaction). It also bars outputs promoting self-harm, disordered eating or unlawful drug or alcohol use, outputs encouraging secrecy or isolation, engagement optimization that overrides safety, and sexually explicit content or CSAM. These bars apply unless the operator has actual knowledge the user is not a minor.","Sec. 5(2)-(3) sets the actual-knowledge standard: operators must weigh age signals, age-verification results and any age attributed for marketing, advertising or product development. If marketing or advertising classification conflicts with an age signal or verification, the user's self-identified age is disregarded. Self-identification as an adult is never enough alone.","Sec. 4 allows personal data to be used only to determine whether a user is a minor, with no sharing, sale or storage and immediate deletion. For minors, data collection is limited to the minimum necessary and must have a stated purpose. Sec. 5(4) bars training the underlying model on a minor's inputs without written parental or guardian consent.","Sec. 7 gives the AG a civil action with a $25,000 fine per violation, each day a separate violation, plus injunctive relief, restitution, disgorgement (including destruction of unlawfully obtained data and trained algorithms), damages and fees. A minor or parent may sue for actual and punitive damages, fees and equitable relief. There is a rebuttable presumption of causation where the bot encouraged the harmful conduct. Joint and several liability applies to affiliates that structured themselves to avoid liability.","Secs. 4(4) and 5(5) delay those sections until the effective date of the AG's initial rules under Sec. 19. Sec. 11 voids waivers and liability-shifting contracts. Sec. 13 requires an AG complaint website. Sec. 15 covers conduct in whole or in part in Michigan but excludes users physically outside the state."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. The notable feature is the mismatch between the title (general government appropriations for FY 2024-25) and the engrossed text, which is a standalone AI chatbot child-safety act with no wagering, gaming or collectibles provisions. The record should be checked for the correct bill version, since the conference-committee status may attach to the appropriations vehicle.","watch":"If this text is the operative version, operators that deploy generative-AI features such as customer-support or promotional chatbots should check whether they fall outside the Sec. 3(b) carve-outs, and should note the age-signal rules that treat marketing-based age classifications as relevant to actual knowledge, the $25,000-per-day exposure and the private right of action.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Engrossed","text_chars":12857,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:50:01.787Z"},"MI-SB933":{"summary":"Michigan SB 933 (2024 PA 141) amends section 16 of the Lawful Sports Betting Act to set the order in which the Internet Sports Betting Fund is spent. It sets fixed annual payments of $1,000,000 to the compulsive gaming prevention fund and $2,000,000 to the Christopher R. Slezak first responder presumed coverage fund, both after the Board's regulatory costs, with the remainder going to the state school aid fund. It does not change tax rates, licensing, or operator obligations.","mechanics":["Sec. 16(4)(a): the Michigan Gaming Control Board's costs of regulating and enforcing internet sports betting are paid first, on appropriation.","Sec. 16(4)(b): after the Board's costs, $1,000,000 per year goes to the compulsive gaming prevention fund (MCL 432.253, Compulsive Gaming Prevention Act, 1997 PA 70).","Sec. 16(4)(c): after (a) and (b), $2,000,000 per year goes to the Christopher R. Slezak first responder presumed coverage fund (MCL 418.405, Workers' Disability Compensation Act).","Sec. 16(4)(d): all remaining money goes to the state school aid fund under Const 1963, art IX, sec 11.","Sec. 16(1)-(3): the fund stays in the state treasury, the treasurer directs investment and credits earnings, and the Board administers it for auditing purposes.","Effective on sine die, the 91st day after final adjournment of the 2024 regular session; signed Oct. 8, 2024."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. On its own, this bill is narrow: it only reorders and fixes the dollar amounts of appropriations from Michigan's internet sports betting fund. It creates a flat $1M problem-gambling earmark and a $2M first-responder workers' compensation earmark, with school aid taking the residual, rather than a percentage-based allocation.","watch":"Because the bill changes only fund distribution and not the tax rate or operator obligations, the main thing to watch is whether the fixed $1M and $2M earmarks, which are funded from the same pool as the Board's regulatory costs, prompt later pressure to raise taxes, fees, or responsible-gaming contributions on licensees.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":2523,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:50:02.227Z"},"MI-SB952":{"summary":"SB 952 creates the Hospital Price Transparency Act, which bars Michigan-licensed hospitals from initiating or pursuing collection actions (debt-collector referral, suit or arbitration enforcement, credit reporting) on debts for services provided on dates when the hospital was not in material compliance with federal price transparency law (42 USC 300gg-18(e) and its HHS regulations). Patients can sue to determine noncompliance, and a finding triggers refund, a penalty equal to the debt, dismissal with prejudice, fees and costs, and removal of credit reports. The bill has no connection to gaming apart from a carve-out in the 'collection agency' definition.","mechanics":["Sec. 3(1): after the effective date, a hospital not in material compliance with hospital price transparency laws on the date of service may not initiate or pursue a 'collection action' against the patient or guarantor for that debt.","Sec. 2(b): 'collection action' covers referral to a debt collector or collection agency, suing or enforcing an arbitration or mediation clause, and directly or indirectly causing a report to a consumer reporting agency.","Sec. 3(2): critical access hospitals (42 CFR 485 subpart F) are covered only 6 months after the effective date.","Sec. 3(3): the patient or guarantor may file a civil action to determine material noncompliance on the date of service and its relation to the services; collection is stayed while the action is pending.","Sec. 3(4): if a judge or jury finds material noncompliance (considering CMS compliance standards), the hospital must refund amounts paid, pay a penalty equal to the total debt, dismiss with prejudice any court action on the debt and pay attorney fees and costs, and remove credit reports.","Sec. 2(d)(viii): licensees under the Lawful Sports Betting Act (2019 PA 149), Lawful Internet Gaming Act (2019 PA 152), Fantasy Contests Consumer Protection Act (2019 PA 157), Michigan Gaming Control and Revenue Act, and the horse racing, lottery and bingo acts are excluded from the definition of 'collection agency'."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. The bill is unusual for a gaming-related feed because it is a hospital billing and debt-collection measure. The only gaming link is the licensee carve-out from 'collection agency' in Sec. 2(d)(viii), which lists Michigan's sports betting, internet gaming and fantasy contest acts. It is tie-barred with SB 1179 of 2024.","watch":"Operators should note that licensees under the Lawful Sports Betting Act and Lawful Internet Gaming Act are excluded from the 'collection agency' definition, which is a drafting convention only, and the bill otherwise does not affect their operations.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":8882,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:50:04.900Z"},"MO-HB2":{"summary":"Missouri HB 2 is the FY2026 (July 1, 2025 to June 30, 2026) appropriations act for the State Board of Education and DESE. It funds the School Foundation Program, transportation, pre-K, teacher salary grants, career and technical education, literacy and many earmarked grants. Sports wagering appears only as a funding source: the Sports Wagering for Education Fund (1244) supplies two line items.","mechanics":["Section 2.015 draws $1,084,066 from the Sports Wagering for Education Fund (1244) toward the $4,282,736,000 Foundation Formula, within $4,689,311,449 of total school distributions. The State Adequacy Target is capped at $7,145.","Section 2.163 appropriates $1,200,000 (one-time) from the Sports Wagering for Education Fund for nationally recognized career readiness assessments for eleventh and twelfth graders.","Section 2.000 provides that Part 2 clarifications are not severable from Part 1, and that any amount labeled one-time is not added to the ongoing core appropriation after June 30, 2026.","Section 2.015 bars use of funds to share individually identifiable student data for non-educational purposes, marketing or advertising. Sections 2.135 and 2.150 bar sharing student data with the federal government, with limited exceptions.","The act creates no tax, licensing, or operator obligations. It covers only the period July 1, 2025 to June 30, 2026, and carries no new gaming rules."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. The bill is unusual for a gaming-related filing because it is a general education appropriations act. Sports wagering appears only as a dedicated funding source (Fund 1244) in Sections 2.015 and 2.163, and the bill sets no betting policy.","watch":"Watch the size of the Sports Wagering for Education Fund appropriations ($1,084,066 in Section 2.015 and $1,200,000 in Section 2.163) as an indicator of how much sports wagering tax revenue the state expects and how it is allocated, since this bill changes no operator obligations.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":98632,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:50:06.298Z"},"MO-HB8":{"summary":"HB 8 is the fiscal year 2026 (July 1, 2025 to June 30, 2026) appropriations act for the Missouri Department of Public Safety and the National Guard. It funds the Director's office, grants, victim services, the Capitol Police, and the Highway Patrol, among others. It is not a gaming or betting regulation bill. Gaming appears only as Gaming Commission Fund (1286) line items for Highway Patrol personnel, vehicles, and fuel assigned to Gaming Commission work.","mechanics":["Gaming Commission Fund (1286) lines in the text provided: $79,567 for Highway Patrol administration (8.140), $505,727 for fringe benefits (8.145), $859,918 for enforcement expense and equipment, including $264,001 one-time (8.150), $843,161 for the trooper and commercial vehicle officer wage adjustments (8.156), $1,029,089 for gasoline (8.160), and $549,074 for vehicles, aircraft and watercraft (8.165).","Section 8.156 gives uniformed Highway Patrol job classes a 6.8% wage adjustment and commercial vehicle officer classes 3.0%, partly funded from the Gaming Commission Fund at $843,161.","Section 8.145 also draws $400,085 from the Veterans, Health, and Community Reinvestment Fund (1608) for Highway Patrol fringe benefits. The text does not say what revenue feeds that fund.","Section 8.8.000 says Part 2 clarifications of purpose cannot be severed from Part 1. If a Part 2 clarification is held invalid, every appropriation it attaches to is invalidated. Amounts marked one-time are not part of the ongoing core budget after June 30, 2026.","Most sections carry 3% flexibility into Section 8.335. Section 8.060 appropriates $20,000,000 from General Revenue, including $10,000,000 one-time, for security and other services for the 2026 World Cup.","The text supplied ends partway through Section 8.170 (Crime Labs). Later sections, including Section 8.335 and Part 2 and Part 3, are not visible, so I cannot confirm whether any of them touch gaming."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. On its own, this is a general public-safety budget bill whose only gaming content is funding for Highway Patrol work tied to the Gaming Commission, paid from the Gaming Commission Fund. It has no betting, iGaming, tax, licensing, or collectibles provisions in the text provided.","watch":"Watch only for Gaming Commission Fund (1286) appropriations and any Highway Patrol gaming-enforcement staffing in the remaining sections, since the text provided contains no sports-betting, iGaming, or collectibles regulatory provisions.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":94383,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:50:13.626Z"},"NC-H259":{"summary":"H259 is North Carolina's 2023-25 base budget (S.L. 2023-134), which sets General Fund, Highway Fund and Highway Trust Fund appropriations and reserves. The only gaming-related item in the text provided is a revenue-availability line in Section 2.2(a) that counts projected receipts from S.L. 2023-42 (Sport Wagering/Horse Racing Wagering). The bill does not create or amend any wagering rules, and the supplied text is cut off partway through Part IV.","mechanics":["Section 2.2(a) adds revenue from S.L. 2023-42, Sport Wagering/Horse Racing Wagering, to General Fund availability: $8,500,000 in FY 2023-24 and $36,100,000 in FY 2024-25. This is a revenue-estimate line only, and the provided text does not state the tax rate or the underlying provisions.","Section 2.1 appropriates $29.71 billion (FY 2023-24) and $30.82 billion (FY 2024-25) in net General Fund money. Section 2.2 sets availability and lists the statutory and discretionary reserves, such as $1.05 billion in additional SCIF transfers in FY 2023-24.","Section 4.3(a) allocates Education Lottery Fund money ($931,000,000 in FY 2023-24 and $935,000,000 in FY 2024-25). Section 4.3(b) rewrites the Needs-Based Public School Capital Fund grant rules, including maximum awards of $42M, $52M and $62M. This concerns the state lottery, not sports betting or iGaming.","Section 2.2(u) says funds reserved under that section are not an 'appropriation made by law' under Art. V, Sec. 7(1) of the NC Constitution.","The supplied text ends mid-way through G.S. 115C-546.14, so any later wagering, gaming, collectibles or card-break provisions in the remaining parts of the act cannot be assessed."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. This is an omnibus budget, and the only wagering reference in the provided text is a one-line revenue projection tied to a separate enabling statute, S.L. 2023-42. It does not itself regulate, tax or license anything.","watch":"Watch the revenue-estimate line ($8.5M in FY 2023-24 and $36.1M in FY 2024-25) as a signal of the assumed launch timing and tax yield under S.L. 2023-42, and check the unseen remainder of the act for any gaming-related provisions.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":347057,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:50:17.944Z"},"NC-H263":{"summary":"This is North Carolina's omnibus 2024 budget bill, which rewrites the 2023-25 appropriations in S.L. 2023-134 for FY 2024-25 across the General Fund, Highway Fund and Highway Trust Fund. Gaming content is limited to Section 4.6, \"Sports Wagering Changes,\" which amends the G.S. 18C-901(13d) definition of \"professional sports team,\" plus lottery and Indian gaming fund allocations. The supplied text cuts off mid-definition at item \"f\" in Section 4.6(a), so the rest of Section 4.6 is not visible and is not characterized here.","mechanics":["Section 4.6(a) rewrites G.S. 18C-901(13d), defining a professional sports team as a team in North Carolina competing at the highest level of a listed sport. The visible list is baseball, men's soccer, basketball, football and ice hockey, followed by a truncated item \"f\". The text does not show which entries are new.","Section 4.2 amends Section 4.3(a) of S.L. 2023-134 on Education Lottery Fund allocations. The FY 2024-25 total allocation rises from $935,000,000 to $1,006,000,000, with Noninstructional Support Personnel at $406,914,455 and Public School Repair & Renovation at $100,000,000.","Section 4.4 amends the Indian Gaming Education Revenue Fund allocations for FY 2024-25. Textbook and Digital Resources falls from $10,000,000 to $0, Classroom Materials is $1,000,000, Instructional Materials Allotment is $19,000,000, and the total is $20,000,000, up from $11,000,000.","Section 4.1(a) says that, notwithstanding G.S. 18C-164(b3), net revenues remaining after the (b1) appropriation and (b2) transfer go to the NC State Education Assistance Authority for the Opportunity Scholarship program in FY 2024-25. The visible text does not say whether any of these revenues come from sports wagering. Section 4.1(d) makes this section effective June 30, 2024.","Section 4.3 rewrites the Needs-Based Public School Capital Fund (G.S. 115C-546.10 to 546.14). It is not gaming-related and is relevant only as an unrelated use of the budget vehicle."],"issue_tags":["licensing"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. This bill is unusual because it is a general appropriations act with a short, embedded sports-wagering amendment to the definitions in G.S. 18C-901, not a standalone gaming bill. The only visible change concerns which sports count toward a professional sports team, and the rest of Section 4.6 is cut off.","watch":"Obtain the complete Section 4.6 text beyond the truncated G.S. 18C-901(13d) amendment, and check whether the changed definition of professional sports team affects the team-affiliated operator licensing and whether any rate, fee or revenue-allocation changes in the lottery and gaming funds follow.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":352875,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:50:20.349Z"},"MS-SB2517":{"summary":"SB 2517 amends Miss. Code 37-97-103, -105, -107 and -109 so that postsecondary institutions, directly or through third parties, may share athletics-related revenue with student-athletes and pay them for publicity rights (name, image and likeness). It adds definitions, keeps institutional control over marks and conflicting deals, bars NCAA-type bodies from penalizing institutions for activity the act authorizes, and adds treble-damages liability for inducing enrolled or signed athletes to move. Despite the title's reference to the Uniform Athletes Agent Act, the enrolled text amends only the four NIL sections. Section 37-97-107(7) bars student-athletes from endorsement deals involving gambling or sports betting.","mechanics":["Section 1 defines 'athletics-related revenue' by reference to categories 1, 7, 11, 12, 13, 13A, 15 and 19 of the 2024 NCAA Membership Financial Reporting System Revenue Categories (ticket sales, media rights, NCAA and conference distributions, licensing, sponsorships, bowl games). It also defines 'revenue sharing agreement', 'athletics financial aid agreement' and 'publicity rights'.","Section 2 (37-97-105) lets an institution, directly or via a third party, share a portion of athletics-related revenue or pay for publicity rights, to the extent consistent with legally enforceable association or conference rules. Institution staff may facilitate NIL opportunities but may not take a fee, steer the athlete's choice of representation, or act in bad faith to reduce competing third-party opportunities. Athlete agreements and related documents are exempt from the Public Records Act and cannot be compelled to be disclosed to athletics governing bodies.","Section 3 (37-97-107)(7) bars an enrolled student-athlete from entering a publicity rights agreement or taking third-party compensation to endorse brands, products or services involving 'gambling, sports betting,' controlled substances, marijuana, tobacco or nicotine products, alcohol or adult entertainment. It also covers anything the institution in its 'sole judgment' finds inconsistent with its values or reputation.","Section 3 also lets third-party deals formed after the athlete signs an athletics financial aid agreement or gives notice of transfer be terminated when the athlete's participation ends. Under (6), a third party may not offer a deal that conflicts with institutional contracts or rules unless the institution approves in writing. Section 3 (9) bars use of State General Fund appropriations for revenue sharing or compensation, and (8) states that athletes are not employees.","Section 4 (37-97-109) bars NCAA-type organizations from penalizing institutions or athletes for activity the chapter authorizes. It makes any person or entity, regardless of residence, liable to the athlete's institution for three times the amount given or promised if it offers revenue or compensation to induce an enrolled or signed athlete to go to another school, unless the athlete has given notice of intent to transfer under association or conference rules. The institution sues in a Mississippi state court, and the offeror consents to personal jurisdiction and waives immunity.","Section 5 makes the act effective July 1, 2025; it was approved by the Governor on 2025-04-23."],"issue_tags":["advertising"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"This is a general NIL and revenue-sharing statute, like DE HB314, KY SB3, NH HB312, NE LB370, HI HB 2384 and LA HB 513, with one gambling-related restriction. Mississippi's 37-97-107(7) works on the athlete side by barring endorsement deals for gambling and sports betting. CT HB05229 instead restricts gaming advertising on college campuses, which falls on operators and licensees. Unlike MD HB465/HB518, NJ A4905/S3080, NC H828/H967 and LA SB354, it does not regulate college prop bets or adopt any operator-facing rule.","watch":"A sports-betting operator should watch the 37-97-107(7) bar on athletes taking gambling or sports-betting endorsement money, which cuts off college NIL sponsorship, and the 'sole judgment' catch-all and the 37-97-107(6) rule voiding deals that conflict with institutional contracts, as well as the 37-97-109(3) treble-damages exposure for compensation offers that could be read as inducing transfers.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":15095,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:50:21.603Z"},"NC-H38":{"summary":"NC H38 is an education omnibus. Only Part VI touches wagering: it adds G.S. 116-30.3(g) so that sports-wagering tax proceeds appropriated to UNC constituent institutions under G.S. 105-113.128 for collegiate athletic departments do not revert at fiscal year-end and stay available until expended. The rest covers cash admissions at high school athletic events, teacher supplement supplanting rules, UNC construction and procurement flexibility, tuition, Teaching Fellows, and scholarship administration funds.","mechanics":["Section 6(a) adds G.S. 116-30.3(g): funds appropriated to UNC constituent institutions under G.S. 105-113.128 to support collegiate athletic departments do not revert at fiscal year-end and remain available until expended.","Section 6(b) makes the carryforward effective June 30, 2024. The bill does not change the tax rate, the tax base, or the amount appropriated under G.S. 105-113.128.","Section 1 amends G.S. 115C-407.55(10) to require the State Board of Education's rules to mandate acceptance of cash for admission at high school interscholastic athletic events that charge admission, starting with the 2024-2025 school year.","Section 3 revises the supplant factor and nonsupplant enforcement for teacher supplement funds in S.L. 2023-134 §7A.4. For 2024-2025 an offending unit loses funds only if it cannot show it has remedied the funding deficiency.","Section 10 lets the Opportunity Scholarships administrative set-aside (2.5%) cover Personal Education Student Accounts administration, and raises the cap in G.S. 115C-597(d) from $2 million to $3 million.","Section 8.1 amends G.S. 143-64.03 to let UNC enter vendor trade-in or buyback agreements for technological equipment, with recordkeeping and reporting duties."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. The wagering content is unusual because it is a single non-reversion provision for sports-wagering tax revenue earmarked for UNC athletic departments, buried in an education omnibus. It does not alter licensing, tax rates, or operator obligations.","watch":"Watch for any amendment to the G.S. 105-113.128 earmark or to the wagering-tax distribution, since this bill only changes how the athletic-department funds are held and does not affect operators directly.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":15357,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:50:24.426Z"},"NC-H347":{"summary":"H347 (S.L. 2023-42) adds Article 9 to Chapter 18C, legalizing sports wagering on professional, college, amateur and esports events through interactive accounts or at places of public accommodation, under a North Carolina Commission licensing regime. The Commission may license up to 12 interactive operators, plus service providers and suppliers, with 21+ eligibility, geofencing, and exclusions for youth sports and Indian lands. The title also covers horse racing wagering and live horse racing (Article 10), but the supplied text cuts off in G.S. 18C-914(d), so those provisions and the tax provisions in G.S. 105-113.128 are not visible.","mechanics":["Licensing: no more than 12 interactive sports wagering operators (G.S. 18C-904(a)). Fees are $1,000,000 for an operator, $50,000 for a service provider and $30,000 for a supplier. Licenses last five years and renewal fees equal the initial fees (18C-908). A denied applicant's fee is refunded minus 5%.","Selection and timing: if applicants exceed the cap, the Commission picks the best qualified using factors including projected gross wagering revenue and tax revenue, jobs and capital investment in NC, and plans for a place of public accommodation at a defined sports facility (18C-904(g)). Review takes 60 days, extendable by 30. Operators must hold a reserve of at least $500,000 or outstanding liabilities, whichever is greater (18C-904(m)).","Wager scope: includes parlays, in-play, proposition and exchange wagering. Tier one wagers (pre-game, final-outcome only) need no official league data, and tier two wagers need it only for events of non-US-headquartered bodies or where a governing body notifies the Commission (18C-914(c)-(d)). Prohibited wagers are youth sports, injuries, penalties, disciplinary outcomes, replay reviews and pari-mutuel wagers (18C-902(d)). The Commission may not set a minimum or maximum payout or hold percentage (18C-902(h)).","Player and account rules: players must be 21+, with one account per operator, funding by cash or cash equivalents (including crypto and cards), no VPNs, and no account transfers (18C-912). Geofencing must exclude players on Indian lands. Operators must support voluntary self-exclusion under 18C-922 and may suspend or terminate accounts at their sole discretion if not in violation of law (18C-912(e)).","Operator duties: they must keep wager records for 3 years and give the Commission a daily activity summary. On a governing body's request they must share anonymized account-level data at least every 72 hours, for integrity use only. All non-backup servers must be physically in NC. Advertising must not target under-21s, must identify the operator, must link to gambling-addiction resources and must not mislead (18C-910). Operators cannot require players to waive rights, must be governed by NC law, and are subject to exclusive NC state and federal venue (18C-910(i)).","Integrity and tribal carve-outs: a sports governing body may petition the Commission to restrict a wager type on a showing of good cause. The Commission must respond before the event or within 7 days, and operators may keep offering the wager during review unless provisionally restricted (18C-914(a)). The Article does not apply to wagering conducted exclusively on Indian lands under a compact (18C-902(b)). A tribal operator's technology and brand partners are covered by its authorization (18C-901(25))."],"issue_tags":["legalize","licensing","integrity","advertising","prop"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed, so there is no direct comparison. On its own, the bill is unusual in capping operators at 12 and scoring applicants on NC jobs, capital investment and sports-facility partnerships. It also bars any Commission-mandated hold or payout percentage, makes official league data optional for tier one wagers, and requires in-state servers and an exclusive NC forum for player disputes. It extends tribal authorization to tribal technology and brand partners and bans wagers on injuries, penalties, replay reviews and disciplinary outcomes.","watch":"A lawyer for operators should check the tax rate and procedures in G.S. 105-113.128, the horse racing provisions and the Commission's rulemaking, none of which appear in the supplied text, along with the 12-license cap, the sports-facility and jobs scoring factors, the in-state server requirement, and the governing-body data-sharing and restriction-request powers.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":112056,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:50:28.674Z"},"NC-H967":{"summary":"NC H967 amends the North Carolina sports wagering article (Chapter 18C, Article 9) to define \"proposition wager\" and to bar proposition wagers on amateur and college sports. It also adds a restriction under G.S. 18C-926 on in-person sports wagering at sports facilities hosting college sports events. The bill was introduced and referred to the House Rules Committee on May 6, 2024, with no further action shown.","mechanics":["New G.S. 18C-901(13g) defines a proposition wager as a wager on an individual action, statistic, occurrence, or nonoccurrence determined during a sporting event, expressly including those that do not directly affect the final outcome.","G.S. 18C-902(d) gets a new subdivision (5): nothing in the Article authorizes placing a proposition wager on amateur sports or college sports. This works as a carve-out from authorization rather than an express penalty provision.","New G.S. 18C-926(h): no sports facility may be open to registered players for placing sports wagers during the eight hours before or during any college sports event at the facility or adjacent to it.","The text does not address mobile or online wagering on college props separately, but the 18C-902(d)(5) carve-out is not limited by channel and so would reach online platforms; the new 18C-926(h) is facility-based.","The bill adds no new penalties, rulemaking, or enforcement provisions, so enforcement would rest on existing Chapter 18C licensing and discipline powers.","Effective July 1, 2024, with no stated phase-in or grandfathering of existing offerings."],"issue_tags":["prop","integrity","legalize"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"H967 is a flat statutory ban on all college and amateur props, paired with a facility-based wagering blackout. LA SB354 goes further by removing all sports-related proposition betting and micro-bets from permitted wagers, while NJ A4905 and S3080 are narrower, covering only player-specific props on college sports. MD HB465 and HB518 address college athlete protections within broader problem-gambling regulation, and NY A 10538 only creates a task force to study props. The eight-hour in-person blackout around college events at the venue is unusual and has no counterpart among the listed bills.","watch":"Watch the breadth of the \"proposition wager\" definition (any in-event statistic or occurrence, including non-outcome-affecting ones) and its interaction with the eight-hour in-person blackout around college events, since both lack any enforcement detail, safe harbor, or clarification for online wagering.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":2114,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:50:29.685Z"},"NC-H962":{"summary":"This is the Governor's budget bill (the \"Current Operations Appropriations Act of 2024\"). It repeals and replaces the FY 2024-25 General Fund, Highway Fund and Highway Trust Fund schedules in S.L. 2023-134 and adjusts reserves and other availability. The text provided has no sports-betting, iGaming or trading-card provisions. Gaming appears only as allocations from the Education Lottery Fund and the Indian Gaming Education Revenue Fund. The text is cut off partway through Section 5.3(d), so later parts are not reviewed.","mechanics":["Section 2.1(a) sets FY 2024-25 General Fund requirements at $72,635,987,590, receipts at $38,089,420,380 and net appropriations at $34,546,567,210. Section 2.2(a) shows a remaining unappropriated balance of $198,797,677.","Section 2.2(a) lists revenue adjustments, including Individual Income Tax Fairness (rate at 4.5% for income above $200K/$100K MFJ/single, $126.9M), maintaining the 2.5% corporate rate ($74.2M) and maintaining the 4% sales tax transfer to DOT ($236.9M). It lists no gaming or wagering revenue line in the text provided.","Section 4.1(a) amends the Education Lottery Fund allocations in S.L. 2023-134 s. 4.3(a). The FY 2024-25 total rises to $1,006,000,000 from $935,000,000. Noninstructional support personnel rises to $415,914,455, prekindergarten to $111,152,110, and school health personnel goes from 0 to $8,100,000.","Section 4.2(a) amends the Indian Gaming Education Revenue Fund allocations in S.L. 2023-134 s. 4.4(a). Classroom Materials for FY 2024-25 rises from $1,000,000 to $6,000,000, and the FY 2024-25 total rises from $11,000,000 to $16,000,000.","Sections 2.2(d)-(k) amend or repeal the reserve transfers under S.L. 2023-134, including Medicaid Contingency ($250M for 2024-25), Economic Development Project, Housing ($60M), NCInnovation ($250M) and SERDRF ($75M). Section 5.3 reallocates $131,887,882 in unexpended SFRF, and Section 5.2(a) raises the grant-spending threshold from $2.5M to $5M.","Section 2.1(b) and Section 4.4 refer to a Committee Report under Section 43.2 that is not in the provided text, so any allocations there cannot be confirmed."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. On its own, this is an omnibus appropriations bill and not gaming legislation. Its only gaming-adjacent content is the allocation of Education Lottery and tribal (Indian Gaming) education fund money, and it creates no wagering tax, licensing or advertising provisions in the portion provided.","watch":"Because the provided text stops mid-bill and refers to a Section 43.2 Committee Report, check the full bill and report for any sports-wagering tax revenue, regulatory funding, or lottery-commission or gaming-related policy changes before concluding it has no operator impact.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":320058,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:50:35.477Z"},"ND-HCR3032":{"summary":"HCR3032 is a non-binding concurrent resolution asking the North Dakota Legislative Management to consider studying whether racing, sports and gaming regulation should be consolidated into a single office, either as a division of the Attorney General's office or as a separate agency. It also asks whether a sports and gaming commission should be created to expand or replace the North Dakota Racing Commission. The study would report findings and any implementing legislation to the Sixty-ninth Legislative Assembly. The resolution legalizes and regulates nothing itself.","mechanics":["Directs Legislative Management only to 'consider studying' the feasibility and desirability of consolidation, so even the study is discretionary.","Two structural options are named: a single office as a division of the Attorney General's office, or a separate agency.","It also raises a sports and gaming commission that would expand or replace the North Dakota Racing Commission.","Proposed scope: mixed martial arts, boxing and live combative sports; charitable gaming including licensing and permits; sports betting and other permitted games of chance; the lottery; live and simulcast racing; and similar activities.","Recitals cite about $1.3 billion in electronic pull-tab spending in FY2021, about 138,000 residents betting a combined $300 million a year on sports, over $30 million in lottery sales in FY2021 (up 24.5 percent), and say sports betting legalization 'may be forthcoming'.","Legislative Management must report findings, recommendations and any needed legislation to the Sixty-ninth Legislative Assembly; no tax rates, licensing rules, penalties or effective dates are set."],"issue_tags":["licensing","legalize"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"This is a study resolution, unlike the substantive bills listed. It sets no tax, licensing or advertising rules, unlike AK HB145/SB194, GA HB380/SB57, HI HB1308 or IL HB2878. Its closest analogues are bills that allocate regulatory authority, such as CO SB 163 (delegation between the Limited Gaming Control Commission and the Division of Gaming), DE HB365 (lottery director as regulator) and HI HB1308 (Department of Law Enforcement). Unusually, it contemplates putting combat sports, charitable gaming, the lottery, racing and sports betting under one regulator, and it presumes sports betting is not yet legal in North Dakota.","watch":"Watch for any follow-on study and implementing legislation that picks the future regulator (Attorney General division versus a new commission) and sets the licensing and oversight structure that would govern sports betting if North Dakota legalizes it.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":3202,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:50:35.632Z"},"NC-S382":{"summary":"Session Law 2024-57 is North Carolina's third Hurricane Helene disaster-recovery act, combined with technical budget corrections to the 2023 Current Operations Appropriations Act. Part I moves money and extends or waives licensing, permitting and building-code deadlines in the federally declared affected area. Part II makes General Fund appropriations and technical changes, mostly for education. The supplied text is cut off partway through Part II (G.S. 116-40.5, campus law enforcement). In the portion provided, nothing concerns sports betting, iGaming, collectibles or trading cards.","mechanics":["Sec. 1A.5 transfers $227,000,000 from the Savings Reserve to the Hurricane Helene Disaster Recovery Fund. The money stays unspent until the General Assembly appropriates it. The \"affected area\" is the counties covered by the federal Helene major disaster declaration (Sec. 1A.4).","Secs. 1C.1, 1C.3, 1C.4 and 1C.5 extend certain license, registration and administrator-certification validity dates and renewal deadlines from December 31, 2024 to March 1, 2025. They cover MH/DD/SUS facilities, multiunit assisted housing and adult and family care homes in the affected area. Sec. 1E.1 does the same for concealed handgun permits expiring September 25 to December 31, 2024.","Sec. 1D.3 suspends the running of state and local development approvals and G.S. 160D-108 vested rights in the affected area from January 1, 2024 through December 31, 2027. State agencies must publish notice in the NC Register within 30 days.","Secs. 1D.5 to 1D.9 are temporary regulatory waivers. They allow unpermitted transient non-community water systems to run for up to 59 days. They allow 12-month pump-and-haul wastewater permits. They waive pre-approval of sedimentation plans (expires March 1, 2025). They bar local tree ordinances beyond 10 feet from the property line (expires March 1, 2025). They allow temporary housing hookups to existing septic systems (expires June 1, 2025).","Sec. 1D.10 amends the DEQ $100,000,000 water infrastructure bridge loan program. Loans are interest-free and mature at the earlier of receipt of disaster relief or June 30, 2030. Nonprofit water corporations become eligible, and emergency rulemaking is allowed. Sec. 1F.1 amends the $100,000,000 local government cashflow loan program, shifting administration to the Department of State Treasurer. Loans are interest-free and repayable within five years or by June 30, 2030.","Sec. 1F.3 delays the 2024 NC State Building Code to July 1, 2025. Sec. 1F.2 appropriates $50,000,000 for Rebuild NC and requires a State Auditor audit of the Office of Recovery and Resiliency by July 1, 2025. Part II appropriates General Fund money for the UNC system and the NC School for the Deaf (Secs. 2A.1 to 2A.5)."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. The bill is unusual in that it is a hurricane-recovery and budget-correction omnibus. The visible text contains no wagering, gaming or collectibles provisions, so it has nothing to compare with other states' sports-betting legislation.","watch":"The supplied text ends mid-Part II, so confirm that the omitted remainder of S.L. 2024-57 contains no gaming, wagering, tax or licensing provisions; nothing in the portion provided affects sports-betting operators.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":370029,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:50:36.684Z"},"ND-SB2358":{"summary":"SB 2358 would create a five-member sports betting task force under new section in N.D.C.C. chapter 53-06 to build a statewide implementation plan for online sports betting and draft implementing legislation. It does not itself legalize or tax sports betting; the report is triggered only if voters approve a constitutional amendment authorizing it. The bill failed on second reading in the Senate (12-34) and the Act would have expired December 31, 2024.","mechanics":["Membership (Sec. 1(1)): House majority leader, Senate majority leader, governor, attorney general, and the president of the United Tribes Gaming Association, each or a designee.","Governor or designee convenes the task force; members elect the chairman, and it meets at the chairman's call (Sec. 1(2)).","Mandate: develop a comprehensive statewide implementation plan for online sports betting and regulation, and prepare proposed legislation (Sec. 1(3)-(4)).","Factors to consider include consumer protection, best practices, tethered operations, multistate operations, revenue sharing, taxation options, gambling addiction services, youth and young adult addiction, and effects on charitable gaming, tribal gaming and collegiate sports (Sec. 1(4)(a)-(j)).","Sec. 1(5) bars the task force from spending state funds on lobbying or campaigning for or against a constitutional amendment authorizing sports betting.","Sec. 1(6): if voters approve a constitutional amendment, the task force reports findings, recommendations and proposed legislation to the 69th Legislative Assembly. Sec. 2: the Act is effective only through December 31, 2024."],"issue_tags":["legalize","tax","licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed, so there is nothing to compare directly. The bill is unusual in being a study vehicle rather than a legalization or tax bill. It gives a tribal gaming association president a seat, expressly contemplates tethered and multistate online models, and conditions its report on a voter-approved constitutional amendment.","watch":"Although the bill failed, a lawyer for operators should note that any future North Dakota online framework would likely follow a constitutional amendment and be shaped by tribal-gaming interests, with tethered or tribal-partnered operation models and revenue-sharing and taxation options under consideration.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":2832,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:50:36.850Z"},"NE-LB168":{"summary":"LB168 amends the Nebraska Racetrack Gaming Act to take sports wagering on instate collegiate events (those with a Nebraska college or university team as a participant) out of the definition of authorized sporting event, which would effectively bar those wagers. It also sets a dedicated tax stream: the 9-1203 tax on gross gaming revenue from such events goes to the Nebraska Opportunity Grant Fund. The bill was indefinitely postponed on 2024-04-18.","mechanics":["Sec. 1 (9-1103(3)): splits 'authorized sporting event' into (a) and (b); (b) excludes instate collegiate events with an instate team as a participant, plus parimutuel wagers, fantasy contests, minor league events, high school and below, and anything the commission excludes.","Sec. 1 (9-1103(14)): 'sports wagering' excludes individual-athlete prop wagers and in-game wagers on any collegiate game involving a Nebraska team, as well as prop wagers on athletes under 18 in professional or international events.","Sec. 2 (9-1110(1)): an authorized gaming operator must separately account for wagers on instate collegiate events with an instate team from other sports wagers for the 9-1203 tax. Wagers remain in person or at kiosks in the designated area at a licensed racetrack enclosure.","Sec. 3 (9-1204(1)): tax on gross gaming revenue from instate collegiate wagering goes to the State Treasurer for credit to the Nebraska Opportunity Grant Fund. Under 9-1204(2), the remaining tax keeps the existing split: 75% state (2.5% Compulsive Gamblers Assistance Fund, 2.5% General Fund, 70% Property Tax Credit Cash Fund) and 25% to the county and city or village.","Sec. 4 (85-1920): adds amounts credited under 9-1204(1) to the Nebraska Opportunity Grant Fund's funding sources. Sec. 5 repeals the original sections. No effective date or rate is stated in the bill; the rate stays in 9-1203, which is not amended.","The exclusion and the dedicated tax stream sit in tension: if instate collegiate events are not authorized sporting events, no taxable revenue should arise from them, so the provision's practical effect is unclear."],"issue_tags":["tax","prop","legalize"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"LB168 is a narrow amendment to Nebraska's existing racetrack-based, in-person and kiosk-only framework and does not create a new market. That differs from the mobile-launch bills such as AK HB145/SB194 and GA HB380/SB57/SB386, and from Illinois bills like IL HB3159 (2% of adjusted gross receipts for fire and EMS) and IL HB4171 (preempting local regulation and taxes), which adjust tax allocation or regulation in an existing market. Its limits on collegiate props and in-game wagering resemble integrity-driven restrictions generally, but none of the listed bills are directly comparable on instate-team exclusion; the Delaware, Georgia and Hawaii NIL bills address student athletes rather than wagering.","watch":"Although the bill is dead, operators should note the model: an instate-team exclusion plus a prohibition on collegiate props and in-game wagers, and the internal inconsistency of dedicating tax revenue from wagers the bill removes from the authorized-event definition, which could resurface in future Nebraska legislation.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":10543,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:50:45.676Z"},"NE-LB13":{"summary":"LB13 would amend the Nebraska Racetrack Gaming Act to let a licensed racetrack authorized gaming operator offer sports wagering through an online sports wagering platform to persons located in Nebraska, either directly or through one contracted platform provider. It also creates a separate distribution of sports wagering tax revenue, with 90% going to the Property Tax Credit Cash Fund, and changes the deadline for ballot explanatory statements on legislative constitutional proposals referred in a special session. The bill was introduced in the 2024 first special session and is dead (indefinitely postponed).","mechanics":["Sec. 1 adds definitions of 'online sports wagering platform' (an integrated system of hardware, software or apps through which an authorized gaming operator or its platform provider offers sports wagering over the Internet to persons located in Nebraska) and 'platform provider' (a sports wagering vendor contracting with an authorized gaming operator). It also renumbers the remaining definitions.","Sec. 2 amends 9-1110: wagers may be placed in person or at a kiosk in the designated area at the racetrack, or online through a platform offered by or in partnership with an authorized gaming operator, by a person located in Nebraska. An operator may contract with 'up to one' platform provider, and a vendor with no operator contract may not conduct sports wagering in Nebraska.","Sec. 2(1)(b)(ii) requires the operator or vendor to submit platform controls to the State Racing and Gaming Commission for approval. No online wagering may begin until the commission approves them.","Sec. 3 amends 9-1204 to split the distribution of revenue. Non-sports gross gaming revenue keeps the existing split (75% state, 25% local). The tax imposed by 9-1203 on sports wagering gross gaming revenue goes 3% to the Compulsive Gamblers Assistance Fund, 3.5% to the Racetrack Gaming Fund, 3.5% to the Racing Cash Fund and 90% to the Property Tax Credit Cash Fund. The local share to the county and city is eliminated for sports wagering revenue. The text sets no tax rate; it relies on the existing 9-1203 tax.","The existing definition of sports wagering still excludes wagers on individual athlete performance and in-game wagers on collegiate events involving an in-state team, and on individual athletes under 18. The definition of authorized sporting event still excludes in-state collegiate events involving a Nebraska team.","Sec. 4 amends 49-202.01: for a constitutional proposal referred in a special session, the Executive Board of the Legislative Council must submit the explanatory statement to the Secretary of State by September 1, rather than four months before the general election. Sec. 5 makes Secs. 1, 2, 3 and 7 operative December 16, 2024; the other sections are operative on the effective date. Sec. 9 is an emergency clause, and Sec. 6 is a severability clause.","Sec. 2(5) retains cross-references to sections 35, 38 and 44 of 'this act' and to the Gambling Winnings Setoff for Outstanding Debt Act. Those sections do not appear in LB13 as introduced, so the setoff provision does not match the bill's own text."],"issue_tags":["legalize","tax","licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"LB13 is a narrow amendment to an existing racetrack-casino framework, so online sports wagering is tied to racetrack licensees. Mobile access is also limited to one platform provider per operator. Alaska HB145 and SB194 and Delaware HB365 set up broader standalone mobile sports wagering regimes, the Delaware one under the Lottery Director, and Hawaii HB 1308, HB 2570 and SB1569 create licensing structures for operators and suppliers. Georgia's bills (e.g., HB380, SB57, SB386) use the Georgia Lottery Corporation as regulator, and Georgia's resolutions (e.g., HR450, SR140) go the constitutional route, while LB13 is statutory. Its distinguishing features are the 90% earmark to the Property Tax Credit Cash Fund, the platform-provider cap, and the special-session ballot deadline.","watch":"The one-platform-provider-per-operator cap, the dependence on racetrack licensure, and the revenue distribution (90% to property tax credits, no local share) would decide which operators could enter and what the economics look like, and the tax rate itself is not set here but in the existing section 9-1203.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":16457,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:50:46.737Z"},"NE-LB32":{"summary":"LB32 amends Neb. Rev. Stat. 9-1103, the definitions section of the Nebraska Racetrack Gaming Act, to narrow what counts as \"sports wagering.\" Wagers within the new exclusions fall outside the defined term, and so outside the sports wagering the Act authorizes. The bill was indefinitely postponed on 2024-08-12 and is dead.","mechanics":["Sec. 1(14): adds exclusion (a) for a wager on the performance or nonperformance of any individual athlete in a single game or match of a collegiate sporting event in which a Nebraska collegiate team is participating (an in-state college player prop).","Sec. 1(14): adds exclusion (b) for any in-game wager on a game or match of a collegiate sporting event in which a Nebraska collegiate team is participating.","Sec. 1(14): adds exclusion (c) for a wager on the performance or nonperformance of any individual athlete under 18 participating in a professional or international sporting event.","Sec. 1(14): the existing exclusion for wagers on athletes in an individual sporting event excluded by the commission is relabeled from (b) to (d). The text has no strike/insert markup, so I infer which language is new from this relabeling.","The rest of 9-1103 is reproduced unchanged as far as the text shows, including the definition of \"authorized sporting event\" in (3), which already excludes in-state collegiate events, and the \"prohibited participant\" definition in (12).","The text has no penalties, new licensing provisions, tax rates, or effective-date clause. Sec. 2 repeals the original section."],"issue_tags":["prop","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed, so there is nothing to compare against. The bill is unusual in being a pure definitional amendment. It carves wager types (in-state college player props, in-state college in-game wagers, and props on athletes under 18) out of the defined term \"sports wagering\" rather than creating a prohibition or penalty. It contains no tax, licensing, or enforcement changes.","watch":"Although the bill is dead, a lawyer for operators should note the likely overlap between new exclusions (a) and (b) and the existing 9-1103(3)(b) bar on in-state collegiate events, and that exclusion (c) would turn on whether an operator knows an athlete's age, a point the text does not address, should similar language return in a later session.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":5905,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:50:49.785Z"},"NE-LB1317":{"summary":"LB1317 is a tax and economic-development omnibus. It creates the Good Life District Economic Development Act, which lets cities with a good life district levy local sales and occupation taxes and issue bonds. It creates the Financial Institution Data Match Act for collecting delinquent tax debts. By its title it also creates a Gambling Winnings Setoff for Outstanding Debt Act, requiring action on parimutuel, lottery, gaming and gambling winnings. The text supplied cuts off in section 30, before that Act, so its operative terms cannot be confirmed here.","mechanics":["Good Life District Economic Development Act (secs. 1-23): a city may adopt a program only after a majority of voters approve the ballot question (sec. 4). The program lasts up to 30 years from the district's establishment (sec. 5(6)).","Local revenue sources (sec. 6): a local option sales and use tax of up to the greater of (i) the gap between the general state sales tax rate and the reduced rate in the district or (ii) 2.75%. This is in addition to any sec. 77-27,142 local option tax and is collected by the Tax Commissioner. A city may also use a general business occupation tax, or designate part of its existing local option tax.","Bonds (secs. 11-22): bonds are payable only from the dedicated local revenues and pledged community-development taxes, and are not general obligations. The Act authorizes refunding bonds, makes the bonds tax-exempt, and includes a state pledge not to impair bondholder rights. Sec. 9 requires an annual independent audit, and sec. 10 exempts the program from the Nebraska Budget Act.","Financial Institution Data Match Act (secs. 24-31): the Department of Revenue sends financial institutions a quarterly list of tax debtors. Institutions must match it against open accounts, and accounts closed in the prior quarter, within 30 days. They report matches within 5 working days, with name, address, SSN or FEIN, and account balance. Institutions need not disclose account numbers, may charge a fee not exceeding actual cost, and are bound by tax confidentiality penalties.","Data match funding (sec. 28): starting after fiscal year 2024-25, the State Treasurer transfers to the Department of Revenue Enforcement Fund the lesser of the program's costs or 2% of the taxes, penalties and interest collected through the vendor contracts.","Gambling-related amendments: the title lists amendments to sections 9-810, 9-1104 and 9-1110 and a requirement relating to parimutuel, lottery, gaming and gambling winnings. These provisions are not in the text provided, so thresholds, covered debts and payor duties are unverified."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"The closest comparison is DE HB187, which expands Delaware's Video Lottery Intercept Program to require sports betting and table game winnings to go to Child Support Services for delinquent child support. Nebraska's setoff Act is titled as covering outstanding debt generally, and its scope is not visible in the text provided. Iowa's HSB 91, SF 605 and SSB 1152 address income tax withholding on sports wagering winnings, which is a different mechanism. Unlike the sports-betting bills from AK, GA, HI and others, LB1317 does not legalize, license or tax sports wagering, and gambling is a small part of a large tax omnibus.","watch":"Obtain the full text of the Gambling Winnings Setoff for Outstanding Debt Act and the 9-810, 9-1104 and 9-1110 amendments to see which debts and winnings are covered, what checking, withholding and remittance duties fall on licensed operators, the operative date, and operator liability.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":304841,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:50:51.273Z"},"NE-LR23CA":{"summary":"LR23CA is a proposed amendment to Neb. Const. art. III, sec. 24 that would direct the Legislature to authorize and regulate all gambling activities, expressly including sports wagering, other wagering and online or mobile versions of any gambling activity, and would bar any gambling not authorized under the section. It earmarks tax proceeds from authorized gambling and limits online or mobile gambling to operators with a physical location in Nebraska. The resolution was introduced in the 2024 special session for the November 2024 ballot and was indefinitely postponed.","mechanics":["Sec. 1, new III-24(1): the Legislature 'shall authorize and regulate' gambling activities, defined non-exhaustively to include games of chance, lotteries, bingo, gift enterprises, sports wagering or other wagering, and parimutuel horserace wagering within a licensed racetrack enclosure, where consideration is paid or substantial effort or time is required. Online and mobile versions are expressly included, and nothing is authorized except as provided in the section.","Tax proceeds allocation, III-24(2): 80% to a dedicated property tax relief fund, 5% to education, 5% to the Compulsive Gamblers Assistance Fund and 10% to the General Fund. The text sets no tax rate, license structure or regulator; those are left to the Legislature.","Physical-presence rule, III-24(3): no online or mobile gambling may be conducted by an individual or entity that lacks a physical location in Nebraska where the gambling is conducted. The ballot language repeats this condition.","Existing text is retained: the general prohibition on games of chance and lotteries, the state lottery with its fixed proceeds split (first $500,000 to the Compulsive Gamblers Assistance Fund, then 44.5% to the Environmental Trust, 44.5% to education, 10% to the State Fair Board, 1% to the Compulsive Gamblers Assistance Fund), parimutuel racing, and nonprofit bingo. The text as drafted is unclear on how the retained provisions interact with the new subsection (1).","Subsection (5) is retained: the section does not apply to laws licensing, regulating or taxing all forms of games of chance conducted by authorized gaming operators within a licensed racetrack enclosure.","Sec. 2: submission at the November 2024 general election under Art. XVI, sec. 1, with ballot language describing a requirement to authorize, regulate and tax gambling, including online or mobile gambling by entities with a Nebraska physical location. Status: indefinitely postponed on 2024-08-08, so it is dead."],"issue_tags":["legalize","tax","licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike the statutory sports-betting bills listed, such as AK HB145/SB194, DE HB365, HI HB 1308 and IA SF2427, LR23CA sets no tax rate, license categories or regulator. It only fixes a constitutional framework and a percentage split of proceeds. It is closest to the Georgia constitutional resolutions (GA HR450, SR131, SR140, SR538, SR579), but those authorize the General Assembly to legalize betting, while LR23CA mandates that the Legislature authorize and regulate all gambling, online and mobile included. Its requirement of a physical Nebraska location for online or mobile operators has no counterpart in the listed bills, and it would effectively tie online play to Nebraska licensed venues.","watch":"An operator should watch the physical-location condition in III-24(3), since it could shut out online-only operators and force partnerships with Nebraska racetrack or venue licensees if a successor amendment revives this language.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":7197,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:50:56.499Z"},"NH-HB83":{"summary":"HB 83-FN raises New Hampshire's minimum age for sports betting from 18 to 21 by amending the definition of \"authorized sports bettor\" in RSA 287-I:1, II and the mobile age-verification requirement in RSA 287-I:7, I. It takes effect January 1, 2026. The House voted 215-140 on 02/06/2025 to indefinitely postpone it, which effectively kills the bill for the session.","mechanics":["Section 1 amends RSA 287-I:1, II so an \"authorized sports bettor\" must be 21 or older rather than 18 or older, while keeping the physical-presence-in-NH requirement and the exclusion of prohibited sports bettors.","Section 2 amends RSA 287-I:7, I so that the age-verification measures for mobile wagering must block access and wagers by persons under 21 rather than under 18.","Section 3 sets the effective date at January 1, 2026, which is halfway through FY 2026.","No new penalties, licensing changes, tax-rate changes or enforcement provisions are added. The bill only changes the two age figures.","The LBA fiscal note estimates lottery revenue, and the resulting transfer to the Education Trust Fund, would fall by about $320,000 in FY 2026 and $640,000 in each of FY 2027 and FY 2028. It bases this on FY 2024 state sports betting revenue of $33,654,911, of which about 1.9% came from bettors aged 18 to 20.","Status: the House adopted a motion to indefinitely postpone on 02/06/2025 by 215-140, so the bill is effectively dead."],"issue_tags":["legalize","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"No other states' bills on overlapping issues are listed, so there is no direct comparison. On its own, HB 83 is a narrow two-line age change with no tax, advertising or licensing content. It would have moved New Hampshire from 18 to 21, which is the minimum age in most other regulated U.S. markets.","watch":"Although the bill was indefinitely postponed, operators should expect the 18-to-21 question to return, and if it ever passes they would need to rebuild age-verification and onboarding for the RSA 287-I:7 mobile requirements and plan for the roughly 1.9% of revenue the Lottery Commission attributes to 18-to-20-year-olds.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":3152,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:50:57.521Z"},"NE-LR3CA":{"summary":"LR3CA would put a constitutional amendment on the November 2024 ballot to amend Neb. Const. art. III, sec. 24 by adding a new subsection (5). It would exempt from the general gambling prohibition any law licensing, authorizing, regulating or taxing all forms of games of chance conducted by authorized gaming operators within a licensed racetrack enclosure. It would also let those operators accept sports wagers through a mobile or electronic platform if the bettor is physically in Nebraska. The resolution died, indefinitely postponed under Rule 9, Sec. 8.","mechanics":["New art. III, sec. 24(5) exempts any law, enacted contemporaneously with or after adoption, that licenses, authorizes, regulates or taxes all forms of games of chance conducted by authorized gaming operators within a licensed racetrack enclosure, which reaches well beyond sports wagering.","Mobile and electronic sports wagering is permitted for an authorized gaming operator conducting sports wagering within a licensed racetrack enclosure, if the platform is offered by or in partnership with that operator.","Geofencing standard: the bettor must be located inside Nebraska when the wager is placed. There is no residency requirement and no minimum age is stated.","The amendment sets no tax rate, license fee, revenue allocation or regulator. All of that is left to implementing legislation, and existing subsections (3) and (4) on the state lottery, parimutuel horse racing and bingo are unchanged.","The ballot question is in Sec. 2, with a For/Against vote under Art. XVI, sec. 1, at the November 2024 general election. The text gives no separate effective date.","Status is failed. The resolution was introduced July 25, 2024 in the first special session and indefinitely postponed on 2024-08-20."],"issue_tags":["legalize","licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"LR3CA is a narrow, racetrack-centric constitutional authorization, similar in form to the Georgia constitutional amendments (HR450, SR140, SR579) that let the legislature provide for sports betting by law. Unlike those, which grant general legislative authority, LR3CA ties mobile wagering to operators at licensed racetrack enclosures and also exempts all games of chance at racetracks, so it is closer to a racetrack casino authorization. Statutory bills such as Alaska HB145/SB194, Delaware HB365 and Hawaii HB1308 set taxes, licensing and regulators directly, whereas LR3CA contains no operational or tax terms.","watch":"Although the bill is dead, a lawyer for operators should note that any revived version would limit mobile access to racetrack-licensed operators or their partners (so market access depends on a racetrack partnership) and that its broad 'all forms of games of chance' language could open racetrack casino gaming, with tax and regulatory terms left entirely to later legislation.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":5577,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:50:58.100Z"},"NE-LB775":{"summary":"LB775 is an omnibus Nebraska gambling clean-up bill. It sets minimum live-racing requirements and a one-time track relocation right, authorizes digital-on-premises keno tickets under the County and City Lottery Act, and permits bingo card monitoring devices. It also amends the Racetrack Gaming Act's definitions and Racing and Gaming Commission powers, including an adjudication subcommittee and kiosk-based sports wagering inside the racetrack.","mechanics":["Sec. 1 (2-1205): Racetracks operating as of April 20, 2022 must hold at least 5 live race days and 50 live races annually from 2026 through 2030, then 15 days and 120 races from January 1, 2031. Tracks opening later ramp up over eight years. Failure to comply exposes the licensee to discipline, including revocation. Sec. 1(1) also provides that an agreement with an authorized gaming operator on employees and acceptance of parimutuel or sports wagers does not breach the bar on turning over track operations.","Sec. 14 (9-1110): Sports wagers must be placed in person or at a wagering kiosk in the designated sports wagering area at the licensed racetrack enclosure. The area is restricted to those 21 and over, and the same employee may take sports and parimutuel wagers. The operator must submit controls for commission approval covering technology partners, deposit and wager limits, and a complaint process. The commission must also develop procedures to keep prohibited participants from placing sports or parimutuel wagers.","Sec. 12 (9-1103): Redefines licensed racetrack enclosure as premises where licensed live horseracing is conducted. Keno under the County and City Lottery Act is added to the definition of game of chance. Authorized sporting events exclude instate collegiate events involving an instate team, fantasy contests, minor league events, and high school or below. Sports wagering excludes player props on instate college athletes, in-game wagers on instate college games, and wagers on the performance of athletes under 18 in professional or international events.","Secs. 6, 8, 11 (keno): Creates the digital-on-premises keno ticket, which is bought in person on a mobile device verified to be on the premises of the lottery operator or an authorized sales outlet. This requires consent of the county, city, or village governing body, and purchasers must be 19 or older. The operator must have department-approved geolocation and geofencing controls, limit-setting tools, and a complaint process. The department must act on controls within 30 days and adopt rules by January 1, 2024.","Sec. 9 (9-646.01): Keno and lottery payment is now allowed by debit card, payment-app cash balance, or bank transfer, in addition to cash or a player account. Credit cards remain barred for keno. Debit card keno purchases are capped at $200 per player per day, and debit funding of play accounts is also capped at $200 per day.","Sec. 13 (9-1106): Commission must complete statewide horseracing market, casino gaming market, and socioeconomic-impact studies by January 1, 2025 (the first two repeat every five years). It must deny a racetrack or gaming operator application if it would harm the statewide market. The one-time operator license fee is $5 million, payable over five years. A new adjudication subcommittee may fine up to $15,000 per violation, with appeal to the commission. Sec. 2 allows bingo players to use card monitoring devices (Sec. 3 defines them).","Secs. 15-16 (81-3717, 81-3720): County Visitors Improvement Fund grants and proceeds may not be used to improve facilities where parimutuel wagering is conducted, with an exception for state or county fair sites, or to promote parimutuel wagering."],"issue_tags":["licensing","integrity","legalize","tax"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. On its own, the bill is unusual in being a chaptered omnibus that touches racing, bingo, local keno, and the racetrack casino and sports-wagering regime together. It limits sports wagering to in-person and on-site kiosk betting at racetracks and permits digital keno only through on-premises geofenced mobile tickets. It also excludes some instate college props and in-game bets.","watch":"A sports-betting operator should note that sports wagering stays confined to in-person or kiosk wagering inside the racetrack enclosure, so mobile wagering is not authorized, and that operator-partner and technology-vendor controls need commission approval under 9-1110(3), together with tight prohibited-participant, instate-college and under-18 prop exclusions and license-fee and fine exposure under 9-1106.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":41366,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:50:59.569Z"},"NJ-A4113":{"summary":"New Jersey P.L.2025, c.96 adds N.J.S.A. 18A:3B-89.1, which bars public institutions of higher education from entering sports wagering partnerships with sports wagering operators or intermediaries. A partnership is defined as an agreement, including with an athletic department or booster club, for advertising access in stadiums and facilities, digital and broadcast sports content, and other means. Carve-outs cover institutionally related foundations (with limits on advertising to students) and academic or experiential-learning partnerships with casino or sports wagering licensees. The act took effect immediately on July 8, 2025.","mechanics":["Definition (s.1a): \"sports wagering partnership\" is a partnership or contractual agreement between a sports wagering operator or intermediary and a public institution of higher education, expressly including its athletic department or booster club, for advertising access in stadiums and other facilities, digital and broadcast sports content, and through other means.","Prohibition (s.1b): a public institution of higher education shall not participate in a sports wagering partnership. The prohibition falls on the institution, not the operator, and the text sets no penalty or enforcement mechanism.","Foundation exception (s.1c): the ban does not apply to a foundation institutionally related to a public institution, but its partnership may not include direct advertising or marketing to enrolled students.","Direct marketing definition (s.1c): covers advertising at on-campus locations and off-campus locations associated with the institution, and electronic advertising explicitly directed to enrolled students.","Academic exception (s.1d): the section does not apply to partnerships with casino licensees, sports wagering licensees, or their contracted operators established for academic purposes or providing experiential learning opportunities to enrolled students.","Effective immediately on approval (s.2), July 8, 2025; no grandfathering or transition period for existing agreements."],"issue_tags":["advertising","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison, so there is nothing to contrast directly. On its own, the bill is unusual in regulating the institution rather than the operator, and it reaches booster clubs and athletic departments. It also allows foundation deals subject to a student-marketing limit, and it has no penalty provision or grandfathering for existing deals.","watch":"Check existing NIL, athletic-department, booster-club and foundation sponsorship agreements with New Jersey public institutions, since the act took effect immediately with no grandfathering, and confirm that any foundation or academic arrangements fit within the s.1c student-marketing limit or the s.1d exception.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":1956,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:51:04.525Z"},"NJ-A1715":{"summary":"A1715, as amended and signed as P.L.2025, c.197, requires each New Jersey public institution of higher education to invite and facilitate the presence of the Council on Compulsive Gambling of New Jersey on campus at least once per semester. The Council's visits must provide information on gambling risks, compulsive gambling counseling services, and self-exclusion options. The original version tied obligations to institutions with sports wagering partnerships, but the second reprint struck that language and the partnership definition, so the duty now applies to all public institutions.","mechanics":["Section 1 applies to every public institution of higher education; the amendments deleted the trigger tied to a sports wagering partnership with an operator or intermediary.","The institution must invite and facilitate the Council's presence on campus at least once per semester, and more often if the institution deems appropriate.","Required content is information and resources on compulsive gambling, including potential risks of gambling, compulsive gambling counseling services, and gambling self-exclusion program options; the list is non-exhaustive ('shall include, but shall not be limited to').","The original hotline-posting and gambling addiction prevention program requirements (sports facilities, dorms, libraries, student centers, website) and the definition of 'sports wagering partnership' were struck.","Section 2: effective immediately. The text contains no penalties, enforcement mechanism, funding, or obligations on operators."],"issue_tags":["integrity"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed, so there is nothing to compare directly. Unusually, the bill began as a rule on institutions with operator advertising partnerships and was narrowed in amendment to a simple campus-outreach mandate with no operator-facing obligations and no advertising restrictions.","watch":"Although the signed law imposes no direct duties on operators, note that the deletion of the sports wagering partnership provisions removes a possible hook for campus advertising or hotline-posting requirements, and that the Council's expanded campus presence (including self-exclusion information) may increase related outreach and self-exclusion enrollment.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":2496,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:51:05.717Z"},"NJ-A4700":{"summary":"A4700 is New Jersey's FY2025 general appropriations act (P.L.2024, c.22), appropriating about $56.6 billion in State funds and $27.5 billion in federal funds. It is not a gaming bill. The only gaming-related content in the text provided is the anticipated-revenue schedule, which projects receipts from sports betting, internet gaming, casino taxes and fantasy sports fees. The Governor line-item vetoed a $5,000,000 revenue item (Riverfront State Prison sale), which is unrelated to gaming.","mechanics":["Anticipated Treasury revenue lines: Sports Betting - Race Track $3,246,000 and Sports Betting - Race Track Internet $92,395,000.","Casino Revenue Fund projections: Internet Gaming $346,365,000; Gross Revenue Tax $181,363,000; Sports Betting - Casinos $693,000; Sports Betting - Casinos Internet $70,774,000; Other Casino Taxes and Fees $9,268,000; Casino Simulcasting Fund $140,000. Total Casino Revenue Fund resources are $618,603,000.","Casino Control Fund resources are projected at $77,430,000 in casino license fees. Law and Public Safety lists Casino Fines of $532,000, a Fantasy Sports Operations Fee of $1,500,000 and Legalized Games of Chance Control of $469,000.","These are revenue estimates only. The text provided contains no new tax rate, definition, licensing change or enforcement provision for gaming, and the appropriation sections shown (Legislature, Chief Executive, start of Agriculture) do not touch gaming.","Section 1 makes appropriations available through June 30, 2025 plus one month, after which unexpended balances lapse except for encumbrances. The line-item veto cut the Treasury subtotal to $1,880,913,000 and total State resources to $63,061,176,000."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. As a general appropriations act, A4700 changes no gaming law. Its only value for this practice area is the revenue baseline, notably the split between race track and casino sports betting and the large internet gaming line of $346.4 million.","watch":"Check the Law and Public Safety, Treasury and Casino Revenue Fund appropriation sections (not included in the text provided) for any language directing gaming revenue, such as the Division of Gaming Enforcement budget or problem-gambling funding, and use the sports betting and iGaming revenue lines as a baseline for any later tax-increase proposals.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":1006244,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:51:07.537Z"},"NJ-A2172":{"summary":"NJ A2172 amends the 2018 sports wagering act (P.L.2018, c.33) to define \"electronic sports event\" (video-game competitions held at a physical venue or exclusively online) and to authorize the Division of Gaming Enforcement to approve wagering on them through casino and racetrack sports pools, lounges and online sports pools. It carves esports out of the casino \"authorized game\" definition, lets licensees run up to two extra esports-only branded websites/apps, and permits competitors to bet on themselves or their own team under division conditions. It takes effect immediately.","mechanics":["Definition: \"electronic sports event\" means a competition involving teams, individuals or the operator using video games, held at a physical location or exclusively online. Esports and esports wagering are expressly not an \"authorized game\" under section 5 of P.L.1977, c.110 (the Casino Control Act).","Online skins: each sports wagering licensee keeps up to three branded websites (each with one same-brand app) that may offer traditional and approved esports wagering, plus up to two additional branded sites/apps offering wagering solely on esports.","New subsection n, para. (1): for esports events held at a casino, a licensed racetrack, or a division-approved Atlantic City facility and sponsored by licensees or their providers, the division may let eligible competitors wager on themselves or their team, may make the host or sponsor the exclusive wagering operator, and wagers may be accepted if a third party such as a game publisher certifies that the event meets division integrity requirements.","Para. (2) applies a similar competitor self-betting and publisher-certification regime to in-state physical esports events outside those venues. Para. (3): wagering on out-of-state physical events requires division approval and terms. Para. (4): purely online esports events may be offered with division approval and terms. Para. (5): operators may offer payouts to competitors on division-set terms.","Minors: the \"prohibited sports event\" definition now covers esports and competitive video games sponsored by or affiliated with a high school, and events where a majority of competitors are under 18. Events with only a minority of under-18 participants are not prohibited.","Existing framework is retained: $100,000 initial license fee, age 21 minimum, section 2(m) director approval of events with a $100 wager or $500 win cap absent an integrity-monitoring certification, and a fourth-degree crime with fines up to $25,000 (individuals) or $100,000 (entities) for unlicensed pools."],"issue_tags":["legalize","licensing","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Nothing in the other states' list is comparable: the Alaska, Georgia, Hawaii and Delaware bills (e.g., AK HB145, GA SB57, HI HB1308, DE HB365) establish or expand general sports wagering regimes, and Connecticut and Illinois bills such as CT SB01464 and IL HB1405 address operations, exchange wagering and consumer protections. A2172 is a narrow amendment to an existing mature market, which creates a dedicated esports vertical with its own skin allotment. Unusually, it lets competitors bet on themselves and relies on game-publisher certification for integrity.","watch":"Operators should watch the open-ended grant of discretion to the division (the \"terms and limitations\" in subsection n), the competitor self-wagering and exclusive-operator provisions, the publisher-certification integrity standard, and the minor-participation test in \"prohibited sports event\", which could make eligibility of mixed-age events uncertain.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":29038,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:51:09.421Z"},"NJ-A4905":{"summary":"NJ A4905 amends section 2 of P.L.2018, c.33 (C.5:12A-11) by adding new subsection n., which bars a sports wagering licensee from offering or accepting any wager on a player-specific proposition bet on any collegiate sport or athletic event. A proposition bet is defined as a side wager on a part of an event that does not concern the final outcome. The bill is a one-subsection addition to the existing licensing statute and takes effect immediately.","mechanics":["New subsection n. of C.5:12A-11: a sports wagering licensee 'shall not offer or accept any wager on a player-specific proposition bet on any collegiate sport or athletic event.'","Definition: a 'proposition bet' is 'a side wager on a part of a sport or athletic event that does not concern the final outcome'; the statement gives examples such as a player's statistical lines and which player scores first.","The text defines only 'proposition bet' and does not define 'player-specific', so the boundary with team-level props (e.g., first team to score, team totals) is left to interpretation or regulation.","Section 2: the act takes effect immediately, with no transition period for existing markets or in-flight wagers.","The bill creates no new penalty or enforcement mechanism; it relies on the existing licensing and Division of Gaming Enforcement framework. The only criminal provision in the section (fourth-degree crime) covers unlicensed operators in subsection c.","The statement cites the NCAA President's call for state action on harassment of student-athletes and on college sports integrity as the rationale."],"issue_tags":["prop","integrity","licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"No other listed bill takes this approach. The Connecticut bills (HB05229, HB05269) address college-campus gaming advertising and payment mechanisms rather than banning a bet type. The Georgia and Hawaii NIL bills (GA HB1276, GA HB383, HI HB 2384, HI SB3263) deal with athlete compensation, not wagering limits. Delaware HB314 is a student-athlete protection bill, but only its title is shown, so a comparison of content is not possible. The Florida (H0189), Iowa (SSB 1157) and Illinois bills are broad regulatory or tax bills and do not appear to contain a college player-prop ban. A4905 is a narrow product restriction placed directly in the licensing statute.","watch":"Watch for how 'player-specific' is interpreted, whether the Division of Gaming Enforcement issues rules on what falls within it (for example, same-game parlays or team-level props), and the immediate effective date, which gives operators no time to remove existing college player-prop offerings.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":20817,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:51:13.190Z"},"NJ-A5196":{"summary":"NJ A5196 would fold online sweepstakes casinos into New Jersey's existing internet gaming framework under the Casino Control Act. Operators would need a casino service industry enterprise (CSIE) license, a partnership with an Atlantic City casino licensee, and an internet gaming permit. They would then be taxed on internet gaming gross revenue like other iGaming operators. The bill was withdrawn from consideration on 2025-04-10 and is dead.","mechanics":["Section 4 defines \"online sweepstakes casino\" as a website or app offering games equivalent to, or with similar statistical odds as, authorized gambling games, played primarily with free currency, where participants receive wagerable currency or promotional gaming credits (at random, bundled with a free-currency purchase, or for completing tasks) that are redeemable for cash, prizes, or other things of value. Such platforms may also sell currency that is directly redeemable.","Section 2 defines \"free currency\" as physical or virtual tokens or coins that cannot be redeemed on their own, may be purchased, and have no intrinsic value apart from use in specified games or until combined with other currency or credits.","Amended section 92 (C.5:12-92) requires operators of online sweepstakes casinos to hold a CSIE license before doing business with a casino applicant, racetrack, or licensee. They must meet the key-employee qualification standards (except residency), undergo fingerprint-based criminal background checks at their own cost, and bear the full cost of licensure, including investigative costs.","New section 92(c)(5) requires an independent third-party audit, at the enterprise's expense, of operations and financial standing before licensure. It also requires updated audits submitted to the Division of Gaming Enforcement bi-annually as a condition of keeping the license.","Sections 3 and 5 amend the definitions of \"internet gaming gross revenue\" (C.5:12-28.2) and \"promotional gaming credit\" (C.5:12-38a). Sweepstakes casino revenue becomes taxable internet gaming gross revenue, and sweepstakes tokens or coins awarded at random, in bundles, or for tasks count as promotional gaming credits. Section 8 expands \"Internet gaming affiliate\" to cover an entity running an online sweepstakes casino for a licensed casino.","Amended section 28 (C.5:12-95.30) directs the Division to work with law enforcement and telecommunications providers and other commercial enterprises to limit access to unauthorized sweepstakes casinos. It keeps the existing penalties of $1,000 per player per day and $10,000 per advertising violation. The bill takes effect 30 days after enactment (Section 9)."],"issue_tags":["sweeps","licensing","legalize","tax","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Of the bills listed, only CA AB 831 targets sweepstakes casinos, and it addresses them through the contest and sweepstakes unfair-practices statute. NJ A5196 instead legalizes and regulates them as a form of iGaming, requiring licensure, a casino partner, an internet gaming permit, and taxation. AR HB1861 also addresses illegal online casinos, but it creates felony penalties and an Interactive Gaming Act rather than folding sweepstakes into an existing licensed framework. The other listed bills are mostly sports-wagering authorization or tax measures and are not comparable.","watch":"A sweepstakes-model operator or its platform and game suppliers would need to weigh the CSIE licensing and audit costs, the mandatory Atlantic City casino partner and permit, and the broad definition covering any free-currency platform whose credits are redeemable for value, though the bill is dead after withdrawal.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":21242,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:51:14.646Z"},"NJ-A5562":{"summary":"NJ A5562 directs the Division of Gaming Enforcement (DGE) to commission a study within 12 months on which warning words and phrases, placement and typography best promote responsible gambling in casino-game and sports-pool advertising. DGE must then select 3 to 10 key words or phrases and adopt regulations requiring at least one of them in advertising, in addition to the existing 1-800 GAMBLER language. The bill also amends the required-regulations list to direct DGE to prevent, to the extent practicable, gambling ads and promotions from being placed in or near schools, college campuses, or on media that predominantly target underage or self-excluded persons.","mechanics":["Section 1: DGE must arrange a study within 12 months of the effective date and publish the findings on its website. The study must evaluate public resonance, effect on responsible betting, placement, font size and emphasis, messaging for problem gamblers, self-excluded persons and minors, and wear-out from repeated use.","Section 1(d): After reviewing the study, DGE must develop at least 3 and no more than 10 key words or phrases, may revise them at intervals it chooses, and must adopt APA regulations requiring at least one in casino-game and sports-pool ads at a frequency that optimizes effectiveness.","Section 2 amends C.5:12-70(a)(16)(a): the casino ad regulations must require at least one DGE-approved phrase on all print, billboard and sign advertising, and clear communication in radio, TV, internet and other media. It also strikes the mandatory 'Bet with your head, not over it' on-site signage requirement, which is deleted from the text.","Section 2 adds C.5:12-70(a)(16)(b): ads and promotions must be prevented, to the extent practicable, from being placed in or near locations such as schools or college campuses, or on media that predominantly target underage or self-excluded persons. The standard is practicability, with no fixed buffer distance.","Section 2 expressly covers casino licensees, their employees and agents, and contracted operators including online gaming operators and online sports wagering operators.","Section 3 amends C.5:12A-13(a)(11): the 1-800 GAMBLER message and at least one DGE-selected phrase must appear in all print, billboard, sign, online or broadcast sports pool and online sports pool ads and in every sports wagering lounge. Section 4: effective immediately, though the new phrases depend on later rulemaking. No penalties are specified, so enforcement runs through existing DGE regulatory authority."],"issue_tags":["advertising","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"This is a narrower, rulemaking-driven approach than the other bills listed. NY A01118/S01550 would mandate harm and addiction warnings and hotline numbers directly in statute, whereas NJ delegates the content to DGE after a 12-month study. On location limits, NJ's 'to the extent practicable' standard for schools and campuses is softer than CT HB05271 (outright ban at public higher-education institutions) and CT HB05229 (campus restrictions). It also has no bonus or inducement ban like CT HB05272, no minors-on-platforms rule like NY S10092, and no age-21 prediction market rule like CT HB05038. The closest analogue is PA HR60, which also calls for a study, but NJ's study leads directly to binding ad-copy rules.","watch":"Operators should watch the DGE study and rulemaking, which will set the mandatory phrases, their placement, font and frequency across all media, and the undefined 'to the extent practicable' placement standard that could be used to restrict campus-adjacent or youth-skewing marketing and promotions, along with the removal of the 'Bet with your head, not over it' requirement.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":18043,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:51:19.889Z"},"NJ-A5800":{"summary":"A5800 is New Jersey's FY2026 general appropriations act, appropriating about $58.78 billion in State funds and $31.0 billion in federal funds for the year ending June 30, 2026. The text provided covers the anticipated-resources schedule and the opening Legislature, Chief Executive and Agriculture line items. Gaming appears only as revenue lines and fund resources, with no operative gaming policy. The bill was substituted by S2026 on 2025-06-30.","mechanics":["Section 1 appropriates amounts out of the General Fund or other specified sources for FY ending June 30, 2026. Appropriations stay available for one month after year-end, and unexpended balances then lapse except encumbrances and pre-encumbrances.","The anticipated-resources schedule lists Casino Revenue Fund resources of $1,033,408,000, including Internet Gaming at $551,643,000 and Gross Revenue Tax at $178,838,000.","Sports betting revenue is itemized in two places. Treasury lists Sports Betting - Race Track at $2,143,000 and Race Track Internet at $93,124,000. The Casino Revenue Fund lists Casinos at $603,000 and Casinos Internet at $133,414,000.","Casino Control Fund resources are $79,929,000 from casino license fees, and Law and Public Safety lists $461,000 in casino fines. It also lists a $2,733,000 Fantasy Sports Operations Fee and $469,000 for Legalized Games of Chance Control.","The bill text supplied contains no change to gaming tax rates, licensing or advertising rules, and no new definitions, enforcement or effective-date provisions for gaming.","The summary header states $58,782,119,000 in State funds and $31,007,261,743 in federal funds. The resource tables show total federal revenue of $31,010,648,743, a difference of $3,386,999 that is not explained in the text provided."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. On its own, this is a standard annual appropriations act. It matters to operators only as a revenue forecast, which assumes roughly $227 million of online and retail sports betting revenue across the Treasury and Casino Revenue Fund lines, and about $552 million from internet gaming.","watch":"Watch the Casino Revenue Fund and Treasury sports-betting and internet-gaming revenue assumptions, and any later appropriation language or companion revenue bills (including S2026, which substituted for this bill) that could raise gaming tax rates or redirect gaming receipts.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":1017263,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:51:23.854Z"},"NJ-A5447":{"summary":"P.L.2025, c.128 declares that a pay-to-play sweepstakes open to people in New Jersey, including dual-currency models, is unlawful gambling under Art. IV, §VII, ¶2 of the State Constitution unless it fits a statutory safe harbor (§§2-3). It gives the Division of Consumer Affairs civil enforcement powers over sweepstakes violations and gives the Division of Gaming Enforcement parallel powers over other unlawful gambling, including against aiders and abettors. It also amends the Criminal Code to cover online gambling, sports-event rigging and sports-wager cheating.","mechanics":["§2 and §1 definitions: a sweepstakes is any promotional event, contest or game, online or in person, that awards a prize or prize equivalent directly or through a dual-currency system. Prize equivalents include tokens or virtual items redeemable through third-party marketplaces or cash-out functions. Paid entry by a person present in NJ is unlawful gambling, with civil liability and possible criminal liability under 2C:37-2, 37-4 and 37-7.","§3 safe harbor, all of which must be met: a free method of entry; any paid entry ancillary to food, non-alcoholic drinks or merchandise worth no more than $20 (or another amount set by the Consumer Affairs Director), excluding coins, tokens or online credits that only permit entry or can be exchanged for money or merchandise; no winner determination based on sports results unless the sole entry method is free; disclosure of rules, odds and prizes; prizes taxable as NJ income; no claim of prizes over $1,000 by minors under 18 without parental consent; and identical odds for free and paid entries.","§§4-5, 7 and 12: Consumer Affairs may seek summary injunctions in Superior Court and assess civil penalties of up to $100,000 for a first offense and $250,000 for each later offense, with each day a separate offense. It may also issue cease-and-desist orders, with up to $25,000 per violation of an order. Excess collections go to compulsive-gambling programs.","§§6, 8-11 and 13: Consumer Affairs has investigative powers (reports, examination under oath, subpoenas, impoundment by court order). On non-compliance the court may suspend charters, certificates of authority, licenses or permits used in the practice. Restitution can be ordered, at double the amount if the victim is 62 or older. Penalties are collectible for five years and create a first lien on NJ real property.","§§14-23: for unlawful gambling other than sweepstakes, the Division of Gaming Enforcement gets parallel injunction, investigation, hearing, restitution and cease-and-desist powers. Civil penalties are up to $10,000 for a first offense and $25,000 thereafter, and §17 expressly reaches third-party and service providers that knowingly aid or abet. A court may not suspend or revoke a DGE-issued license in the §14 injunction action.","Criminal amendments: new 2C:37-10 (disorderly persons offense for risking $1,000 or more while agreeing to pay 10% or more of proceeds to another). 2C:37-4(c) makes operating an online gambling resort for NJ wagerers a third-degree crime with a $50,000-$100,000 fine unless State-regulated. 2C:37-2 now reaches online gambling activity. 2C:21-11 on rigging is expanded to cover margin-of-victory manipulation and tiered by benefit ($75,000 or more is second degree). §113 swindling and cheating now covers sports wagers placed on non-public information and use of another person's gaming account. The act took effect immediately on Aug. 15, 2025."],"issue_tags":["sweeps","integrity","licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"This enacted NJ law is much more detailed on sweepstakes than anything listed. CA AB 831 also targets contests and sweepstakes that simulate casino-style games, but NJ combines a defined safe harbor with a daily-accruing civil penalty of up to $100,000/$250,000 and a separate DGE enforcement track. AR HB1861 makes operating an illegal online casino or sports betting a felony and pairs that with a regulated interactive gaming framework, whereas NJ relies on civil enforcement plus amendments to existing third-degree crimes (2C:37-4(c)). The NJ bill's insider-information and account-misuse cheating provisions and its aider-and-abettor liability for service providers have no obvious counterpart in the listed bills.","watch":"Watch the breadth of the 'prize equivalent' and 'game' definitions (game expressly includes sports wagering and anything that simulates it) and the DGE's §17 power to penalize third-party and service providers that knowingly aid unlawful gambling, which could reach payment, platform and marketing vendors tied to sweepstakes-style or unlicensed offerings.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":44080,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:51:27.327Z"},"NJ-A5971":{"summary":"NJ A5971 amends the New Jersey sports wagering act (P.L.2018, c.33) to bar sports wagering licensees from offering or accepting any micro bet, defined as a live proposition bet on the outcome of the next play or action in an ongoing event. Violations are disorderly persons offenses carrying a fine of $500 to $1,000 per offense, with each wager a separate offense. The bill rests on legislative findings about problem gambling and sports integrity, and takes effect immediately.","mechanics":["New definition in section 1 of P.L.2018, c.33 (C.5:12A-10): \"micro bet\" means a proposition bet wagered live, while the event is ongoing, concerning the outcome of the next play or action. It builds on the existing definition of \"proposition bet\" (a side wager on part of an event not concerning the final outcome).","New subsection 2n.(1) of P.L.2018, c.33 (C.5:12A-11): a sports wagering licensee shall not offer or accept any wager on a micro bet.","Penalty in new 2n.(2): a licensee or person who violates the ban commits a disorderly persons offense, with a fine of $500 to $1,000 per offense, and each wager offered or accepted is a separate offense. No licensing sanction (suspension, revocation) is added, and the bill does not say who prosecutes.","Coverage is limited to 'sports wagering licensees' in the ban. The definition of 'sports pool' still lists in-game wagering, in-play bets and proposition bets, so other in-play and prop markets remain permitted.","Section 1 sets out legislative findings, including a 277 percent rise in calls to the Council on Compulsive Gambling of New Jersey helpline, an estimate of 57 percent of professional athletes wagering, and the claim that micro bets are easier to fix. Section 4: effective immediately, with no transition or grandfathering period."],"issue_tags":["prop","integrity","legalize"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"None of the listed bills from other states addresses micro bets; the closest are Connecticut's HB05229 and HB05269, which target advertising and payment mechanics, and SB01464, which covers toll-free helplines and multijurisdictional gaming. None of these bans a bet type. Illinois HB1405 (exchange trade wagering) goes the other way, expanding product types, and the Alaska, Delaware and Hawaii bills are enabling or tax frameworks. A5971 is unusual in that it is a product-level prohibition on a whole category of live markets, enforced through criminal penalties rather than administrative rules.","watch":"Watch for how the narrow 'next play or action' definition gets applied to in-play prop markets (for example, next-drive or next-pitch outcomes versus player stat props), and for the per-wager criminal exposure, which could add up quickly for licensees and their operators or platform providers.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":29671,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:51:28.508Z"},"NJ-A5803":{"summary":"P.L.2025, c.66 raises New Jersey's tax rates on online gambling to 19.75 percent across three product lines: Internet casino gaming gross revenue (C.5:12-95.19), Internet sports wagering at casinos and racetracks (C.5:12A-16), and the fantasy sports operations fee (C.5:20-2(g)). It also changes the investment alternative tax on Internet gaming revenue. It takes effect July 1, 2025.","mechanics":["Section 1 (C.5:12-95.19): annual tax on Internet gaming gross revenue of 19.75 percent, paid into the Casino Revenue Fund. The 8 percent casino gross revenue tax does not apply. The investment alternative tax on these revenues is 5 percent, and the investment alternative is 2.5 percent.","Section 2 (C.5:12A-16), casinos: Internet sports wagering receipts less winnings paid are taxed at 19.75 percent into the Casino Revenue Fund, plus an additional 1.25 percent remitted to the Casino Reinvestment Development Authority for Atlantic City marketing and promotion. Non-internet sports wagering stays at 8.5 percent.","Section 2, racetrack permit holders: Internet sports wagering receipts less winnings are taxed at 19.75 percent to the State General Fund, plus 1.25 percent for local economic development, split 0.75 percent to the host municipality and 0.5 percent to the host county. Meadowlands amounts go to the intermunicipal account (C.5:10A-53). Non-internet racetrack sports wagering stays at 8.5 percent.","Section 2: the only deduction from the tax base is winnings paid out. The cash equivalent value of merchandise or things of value in a jackpot or payout is excluded from the winnings deduction. The text does not provide a deduction for promotional credits.","Section 3 (C.5:20-2(g)): the quarterly fantasy sports operations fee is 19.75 percent of fantasy sports gross revenue, paid to the Division of Consumer Affairs for the General Fund. Gross revenue is entry fees less prizes, multiplied by the New Jersey location percentage. Casino-operated fantasy revenue is excluded from casino gross revenue and Internet gaming gross revenue, and no investment alternative tax applies.","Section 4: effective July 1, 2025, with no phase-in or transition language. Section 3 also restates the existing fantasy sports fines of up to $25,000 for an individual and $100,000 for an entity for unapproved operation or violations, and up to $50,000 and $200,000 for tampering. The chaptered text does not show strikethrough or insertion markings, so the prior rates cannot be read from it."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"This is a rate-change amendment to an established New Jersey regime, not a new framework. AK HB145 and SB194 would create a mobile sports wagering tax from scratch, and HI HB1308 and HB2570 would create a general excise tax on operator licenses. IA HF673 and HSB122 also address taxes on fantasy sports and sports wagering, and GA HB1329 addresses taxation of fantasy contests. NJ is unusual in setting one 19.75 percent rate across iGaming, online sports wagering, and fantasy sports, with layered earmarks (Casino Revenue Fund, CRDA, and racetrack local shares).","watch":"Check the July 1, 2025 effective date with no transition, the unchanged winnings-only deduction that gives no relief for promotional or bonus credits, and how the 19.75 percent iGaming tax interacts with the changed investment alternative tax and the CRDA and racetrack add-ons.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":19342,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:51:30.165Z"},"NJ-S2155":{"summary":"NJ S2155 (First Reprint) bars New Jersey public institutions of higher education from entering a \"sports wagering partnership,\" defined as a partnership or contract between a sports wagering operator or intermediary and the institution (including its athletic department or booster club) for advertising access in stadiums, facilities, digital and broadcast sports content, or other means. The Senate Higher Education Committee amendments add carve-outs for institutionally related foundations (which may not direct advertising at enrolled students) and for academic or experiential-learning partnerships with casino or sports wagering licensees. The act takes effect immediately.","mechanics":["Sec. 1(a) defines \"sports wagering partnership\" as a partnership or contract between a sports wagering operator or intermediary and a public institution of higher education, expressly including its athletic department or booster club, for advertising access in stadiums and facilities, digital and broadcast sports content, and other means.","Sec. 1(b) prohibits a public institution of higher education from participating in such a partnership; the text contains no penalty, enforcement mechanism, or named enforcing agency.","Sec. 1(c) exempts a foundation institutionally related to a public institution, provided its partnership includes no direct advertising or marketing to enrolled students.","Sec. 1(c) defines direct advertising to include on-campus and off-campus locations associated with the institution, and electronic advertising explicitly directed to enrolled students.","Sec. 1(d) excludes partnerships with casino licensees, sports wagering licensees, or their contracted operators established for academic purposes or providing experiential learning to enrolled students.","Sec. 2: effective immediately, with no stated treatment of existing contracts or a wind-down period."],"issue_tags":["advertising","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. On its own, the bill is unusual in targeting the institution as the regulated party rather than the operator, and in carving out affiliated foundations (subject to a student-targeting limit) and academic or experiential-learning partnerships. It imposes no penalties and does not regulate operator conduct directly.","watch":"Operators should check whether existing NJ college or booster-club sponsorships must be terminated given the immediate effective date and no grandfathering, and how the foundation and academic/experiential-learning carve-outs (including the undefined \"direct advertising\" reach to off-campus locations \"associated with\" the institution) would be applied.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":2294,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:51:34.782Z"},"NJ-S2025":{"summary":"This is the New Jersey FY2025 general appropriations act. It appropriates $56,635,803,000 in State funds and about $27.5 billion in federal funds for the year ending June 30, 2025. It has no substantive gaming or sports-betting provisions in the text provided. Gaming appears only in the anticipated-revenue schedules, which project receipts from sports betting, iGaming, casino and fantasy sports sources.","mechanics":["Sports betting revenue lines: Treasury General Fund shows Sports Betting - Race Track at $3,246,000 and Race Track Internet at $92,395,000. The Casino Revenue Fund shows Sports Betting - Casinos at $693,000 and Casinos Internet at $70,774,000.","Internet Gaming is projected at $346,365,000 in the Casino Revenue Fund. Gross Revenue Tax is $181,363,000, Other Casino Taxes and Fees $9,268,000, Casino Simulcasting Fund $140,000, and investment earnings $10,000,000. The Casino Revenue Fund total is $618,603,000.","The Casino Control Fund is projected at $77,430,000, all from Casino License Fees. Law and Public Safety receipts include Casino Fines of $532,000, a Fantasy Sports Operations Fee of $1,500,000 and Legalized Games of Chance Control of $469,000.","The enacting section (section 1) is a generic appropriation clause. Appropriations stay available for one month after the fiscal year ends, and unexpended balances then lapse except those held by encumbrances or pre-encumbrances. The Treasurer must transmit the Annual Financial Report by December 1, 2024.","The text provided stops at the start of the Department of Agriculture appropriations, so the Casino Revenue Fund and Casino Control Fund appropriations, any Division of Gaming Enforcement line items, and any language provisions on gaming are not visible here.","The header shows the Assembly version, A4700 (Pintor Marin/Wimberly), and the Senate version, S2025 (Sarlo/Greenstein). S2025 is shown as substituted by A4700 on 2024-06-28."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. On its own, this is a comprehensive budget act with no gaming policy changes. The only gaming content is the revenue projections. They show racetrack-linked internet sports betting ($92.4M) well above casino internet sports betting ($70.8M), and iGaming ($346.4M) as the largest line in the Casino Revenue Fund.","watch":"Watch the gaming revenue projections and any Casino Revenue Fund, Casino Control Fund or Division of Gaming Enforcement appropriations and language provisions in the portions not provided, since a budget act can carry tax-rate or fund-dedication changes in riders that would not show up in the revenue tables.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":1006570,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:51:36.411Z"},"NJ-S2026":{"summary":"S2026 is New Jersey's FY2026 general appropriations act (P.L.2025, c.74), signed June 30, 2025 with a line-item veto. It sets anticipated resources and appropriates State and federal funds across all agencies. It is not a gaming bill, but its revenue schedules give the State's projected FY2026 receipts from internet gaming, sports betting, casinos and fantasy sports.","mechanics":["Casino Revenue Fund resources are $1,033,408,000, including Internet Gaming $551,643,000, Gross Revenue Tax $178,838,000, Sports Betting - Casinos Internet $133,414,000, Other Casino Taxes and Fees $13,821,000, and Sports Betting - Casinos (in-person) $603,000.","General Fund miscellaneous revenue under the Treasury shows Sports Betting - Race Track Internet at $93,124,000 and Sports Betting - Race Track at $2,143,000.","Law and Public Safety revenue lines include Fantasy Sports Operations Fee $2,733,000, Casino Fines $461,000 and Legalized Games of Chance Control $469,000. The Casino Control Fund shows Casino License Fees of $79,929,000.","The line-item veto changed only revenue figures in the text provided: Tobacco Products Wholesale Sales went from $56,855,000 to $54,855,000, and the related General Fund, All State Funds and Grand Total resource lines each fell by $2,000,000. The bill's title ($58,782,119,000) and synopsis ($58,779,619,000) give different State appropriation totals.","Section 1 makes appropriations available through June 30, 2026 plus one month, with unexpended balances lapsing except encumbrances and pre-encumbrances. The text provided cuts off in the Department of Agriculture appropriations, so any gaming-related appropriation language or provisos in later sections (Law and Public Safety, Treasury, Casino Revenue Fund allocations) cannot be assessed."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. This is a full-state omnibus budget, so gaming appears only as revenue line items, such as internet gaming at $551,643,000 and online sports betting at $133,414,000 (casino) and $93,124,000 (racetrack). It contains no rate changes, licensing rules or other substantive gaming regulation in the portion provided.","watch":"Check the unreviewed appropriation sections and provisos for Casino Revenue Fund allocations and any Division of Gaming Enforcement or Treasury language that could signal changes to gaming tax rates or fees, and use the revenue lines as a benchmark for the State's expectations of online sports betting and iGaming.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":1020068,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:51:40.694Z"},"NJ-S3184":{"summary":"NJ S3184, as amended in the Senate Higher Education Committee (first reprint, May 22, 2025), requires each public institution of higher education to invite and facilitate a campus presence by the Council on Compulsive Gambling of New Jersey at least once per semester. The introduced version would have required a gambling addiction prevention program and hotline postings at institutions with sports wagering partnerships; the reprint strikes that language entirely. The bill's last action shows it substituted by A1715.","mechanics":["Section 1: a public institution of higher education must invite and facilitate the Council on Compulsive Gambling of New Jersey's presence on campus at least once per semester, and more often if the institution deems appropriate.","Required content: information on the risks associated with gambling, compulsive gambling counseling services, and options for gambling self-exclusion programs; the list is stated to be non-exhaustive.","Coverage: all public institutions of higher education, with no trigger tied to a sports wagering partnership, which the committee amendment removed.","Deleted from the introduced text: the gambling addiction prevention program, hotline posting requirements (website, sports facilities, dormitories, libraries, student centers), and the definition of 'sports wagering partnership'.","Section 2: effective immediately. The text contains no penalties, enforcement mechanism, reporting requirement, or obligations on operators."],"issue_tags":["integrity"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed, so there is nothing to compare directly. On its own, the bill is unusual in that the committee amendment decoupled it from sports wagering partnerships and operator advertising. It now imposes a campus outreach duty on public colleges rather than any duty on operators, and it names a specific nonprofit, the Council on Compulsive Gambling of New Jersey, as the invitee.","watch":"The bill imposes no direct obligations on operators, but the deletion of the sports wagering partnership and campus advertising language suggests that any future campus-advertising or partnership restrictions would need to be tracked in A1715 (the substitute bill) and in related measures.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":2407,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:51:42.359Z"},"NJ-S3064":{"summary":"The Senate Budget Committee substitute for NJ S3064 raises New Jersey's tax on online gaming and wagering to a uniform 19.75 percent. It applies to iGaming gross revenue, internet sports wagering revenue at casinos and racetracks, and fantasy sports gross revenue. It takes effect July 1, 2025, and was later substituted by A5803.","mechanics":["Section 1 (C.5:12-95.19): the tax on Internet gaming gross revenues rises from 15% to 19.75% and is paid into the Casino Revenue Fund. The 8% casino gross revenue tax still does not apply. The investment alternative tax stays at 5%, with the alternative at 2.5%; those figures are only retyped as '5 percent' and '2.5 percent'.","Section 2 (C.5:12A-16): the tax on casino internet sports wagering revenue (receipts less winnings paid) rises from 13% to 19.75%, paid to the Casino Revenue Fund. The additional 1.25% for the Casino Reinvestment Development Authority is kept, for a combined 21%. The 8.5% rate on in-person sports wagering is unchanged.","Section 2: the same rise from 13% to 19.75% applies to internet sports wagering revenue of horse racing permit holders, collected by the division and paid to the State General Fund. The additional 1.25% municipal/county economic development tax is kept (0.75% municipality, 0.5% county, with Meadowlands amounts going to the intermunicipal account), as are the cash-equivalent jackpot exclusion and the written agreements with horsemen's associations.","Section 3 (C.5:20-2(g)): the quarterly operations fee on fantasy sports gross revenue rises from 10.5% to 19.75%, paid to the Division of Consumer Affairs for the general fund. Gross revenue is entry fees less prizes, multiplied by the New Jersey location percentage. The rest of the fantasy sports permitting and penalty framework is reprinted without change.","Section 4: the act takes effect July 1, 2025. The text has no phase-in, no delayed effective date for any category, and no sunset or deduction for promotional credits."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Nothing in the listed bills is a direct match, since the other states' bills deal with authorizing or regulating markets rather than raising a rate on an existing one. AK HB145 and AK SB194 impose a tax on mobile sports wagering revenue as part of a new market, and HI HB1308 and HB2570 set a general excise tax in new licensing schemes. IA HF673 and HSB122 also address tax receipts on fantasy sports and sports wagering. S3064 is unusual in that it sets the same 19.75% rate across iGaming, online sports wagering and fantasy sports, so fantasy sports, previously taxed far lower, is lifted to match.","watch":"An operator should check the combined effective burden, since the stacked 1.25% add-on takes online sports wagering to 21% and the 19.75% rate hits fantasy sports operators for the first time, all effective July 1, 2025 with no transition, and note that the bill was substituted by A5803, so the final rates may differ.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Comm Sub","text_chars":19734,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:51:43.065Z"},"NJ-S3080":{"summary":"S3080 adds a new subsection n. to N.J.S.A. 5:12A-11 (section 2 of P.L.2018, c.33) barring any sports wagering licensee from offering or accepting a player-specific proposition bet on any collegiate sport or athletic event. It defines a proposition bet as a side wager on a part of an event that does not concern the final outcome. The bill is a one-paragraph addition to an otherwise reprinted section and takes effect immediately.","mechanics":["New N.J.S.A. 5:12A-11(n): a sports wagering licensee 'shall not offer or accept any wager on a player-specific proposition bet on any collegiate sport or athletic event.'","Definition: a 'proposition bet' is 'a side wager on a part of a sport or athletic event that does not concern the final outcome'. 'Player-specific' is not defined in the text; the Statement gives examples such as statistical performance lines and which player will score first.","The ban covers all collegiate events with no carve-out for in-state or out-of-state schools, and it reaches both retail and online pools because it applies to any 'sports wagering licensee'.","The bill creates no new penalty or enforcement mechanism, so enforcement would run through the existing licensing and regulatory authority of the Division of Gaming Enforcement and the racing commission.","Section 2: effective immediately, with no phase-in or grace period for existing offerings.","The rest of section 2 of P.L.2018, c.33 is reprinted unchanged, including the $100,000 license fee, the three-skin limit, the integrity-certification and wager-limit rule in (m), and the prohibited-bettor rules in (f). The Statement cites the NCAA President's call for state action against harassment of student-athletes and to protect integrity."],"issue_tags":["integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"None of the listed bills is a close analogue. The Connecticut bills (HB05229, HB05269) address college-campus advertising and payment mechanics, not college prop-bet bans, and Delaware HB314 (student athlete protection) and the Georgia and Hawaii NIL bills (GA HB1276, GA HB383, HI HB2384, HI SB3263) deal with student-athlete protection or compensation without restricting betting markets. S3080 is unusual in being a narrow, single-subsection market restriction on one product type, rather than a licensing, tax or advertising package like the Alaska, Georgia, Hawaii or Illinois sports wagering bills.","watch":"Watch the undefined term 'player-specific' (including whether player-linked parlays or same-game parlays, and team-level props, are caught) and the immediate effective date with no transition period, since both determine how quickly operators must pull existing college player-prop markets and whether the Division would clarify scope by rule.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":20824,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:51:43.785Z"},"NJ-S4494":{"summary":"NJ S4494 amends P.L.1993, c.159 so that the entire casino hotel parking fee in Atlantic City goes to a State Treasurer special fund for the Casino Reinvestment Development Authority (CRDA). It ends the existing $0.50 per day diversion to the Casino Revenue Fund and loosens the limits on how CRDA may spend the money. The bill does not touch sports betting or iGaming taxes, licensing, or operations.","mechanics":["Amends N.J.S.A. 5:12-173.4(a) to delete the split of the section 3 parking fee proceeds. The Treasurer would deposit the proceeds into the special fund for CRDA's exclusive use. The $0.50 per day share currently going to the Casino Revenue Fund is cut off.","Per the Statement, current law sets the fee at $3.00 per day per casino hotel parking space. $2.50 goes to the CRDA special fund and $0.50 to the Casino Revenue Fund. The bill does not change the fee amount.","Amended 5:12-173.4(b) replaces the restriction to 'corridor region' infrastructure, traffic and public safety projects. Money can now be used for projects anywhere within the City of Atlantic City, or for any other CRDA purpose under section 12 of P.L.1984, c.218 (C.5:12-160), as CRDA approves.","The State Treasurer may still require a financial plan showing need, schedule and use before allocation. Pending use, the money is invested and the income is credited to the special fund.","The Statement says the redirection starts in State fiscal year 2026. The act takes effect on the first day of the second month after enactment. Section 1 adds a legislative finding that the entire fee proceeds should be available to CRDA.","The Statement notes the Casino Revenue Fund is funded by taxes on casinos, internet gaming and sports betting, and is appropriated to property tax and utility relief for eligible seniors and people with disabilities. The bill removes the parking fee as a source for that fund."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills compares well. They cover sports wagering and iGaming authorization, taxes, licensing, advertising and consumer protection (for example AK HB145/SB194, DE HB365, IL HB2239, CT HB05229). S4494 is a narrow casino-district funding bill that reallocates a parking fee in Atlantic City. It is closest to the revenue-allocation parts of IA SSB 1157, but it creates no new tax or wagering rule.","watch":"Operators with Atlantic City casino hotels should confirm that the fee itself (the $3.00 charge and its collection) is unchanged, and note that shifting $0.50 away from the Casino Revenue Fund may feed into future pressure on gaming-related tax or fee levels to backfill the fund.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":8457,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:51:52.601Z"},"NJ-S4390":{"summary":"The text supplied does not match the title or status line (a higher-education sports wagering partnership ban). It is the First Reprint of a different bill, S4390 \"End Data Center Tax Credits Act,\" which amends section 98 of P.L.2020, c.156 (C.34:1B-362) to cut the Next New Jersey Program tax credit allocation. Nothing in the text addresses sports wagering, iGaming, collectibles or higher education, so the title and text should be checked against the official bill record.","mechanics":["Section 1 amends C.34:1B-362 subsection b.(1)(k): the amount available from Aspire/Emerge credits for Next New Jersey Program awards beginning in fiscal year 2025 falls from $500,000,000 to $250,000,000.","The reprint strikes the proposed cross-reference to the \"End Data Center Tax Credits Act\" from the list of programs counted toward the $11.5 billion nine-year overall cap in subsection a. The cap and the $2.5 billion reserve for Aspire transformative projects are otherwise unchanged.","The reprint deletes proposed subparagraph (n), which would have set aside up to $250,000,000 from fiscal year 2027, including up to $125,000,000 for energy storage tax credits.","The reprint also strikes original sections 2 through 7: amendments to P.L.2025, c.136 energy storage definitions and procurement, a new energy storage tax credit, tax credit transfer certificates, a $100 gross income tax credit for residential electric customers with income of $55,000 or less, and BPU and Treasurer rulemaking.","Section 2 provides that the act takes effect immediately. The text contains no penalties, licensing provisions or enforcement mechanisms."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No other states' bills are listed for comparison. The unusual feature is the mismatch: the title and status describe a New Jersey ban on higher-education sports wagering partnerships, but the text is an economic-development tax credit bill with no gaming content. The text also describes itself as reported by the Senate Budget and Appropriations Committee on June 24, 2026 and introduced June 1, 2026, which conflicts with the stated May 19, 2025 introduction, so the metadata may be unreliable.","watch":"Confirm which bill text is actually attached to S4390 and whether a separate college sports wagering partnership bill exists, because this text imposes no obligations on sports-betting operators.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":48416,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:51:54.798Z"},"NJ-S4366":{"summary":"NJ S4366 directs the Division of Gaming Enforcement (DGE) to arrange a study, due within 12 months of enactment, on which words and phrases best deter irresponsible gambling in casino-game and sports-pool advertising, and to publish the findings online. Afterward DGE must pick 3 to 10 key words or phrases and adopt APA regulations requiring at least one to appear in advertising at a frequency that optimizes effectiveness. It also amends N.J.S.A. 5:12-70 to require DGE regulations that keep advertising and promotions, to the extent practicable, out of or away from locations such as schools and college campuses and off media that predominantly target underage or self-excluded persons.","mechanics":["Section 1: DGE must arrange a study within 12 months of the effective date, publish the findings on its website, and evaluate six factors: public resonance, effect on responsible betting, placement, font size and emphasis, messaging for problem gamblers, self-excluded and underage persons, and wear-out from repeated use.","Section 1(d): After reviewing the study, DGE must develop at least 3 and no more than 10 key words or phrases, or take other action informed by the study. It may revise them at intervals it deems necessary, and must adopt APA regulations requiring at least one phrase in casino-game and sports-pool advertising at a frequency that optimizes effectiveness.","Section 2 (amending 5:12-70(a)(16)(a)): Deletes the mandatory 'Bet with your head, not over it' language for on-site casino advertising. Keeps the '1-800 GAMBLER' language, which must include 'gambling problem' and 'call 1-800 GAMBLER'. Adds at least one DGE-selected phrase, to appear legibly on all print, billboard and sign advertising and to be clearly communicated in radio, television, Internet and other media. Coverage extends to casino licensees, employees, agents and contracted operators, including online gaming and online sports wagering operators.","Section 2 (new 5:12-70(a)(16)(b)): DGE regulations must ensure that advertising and promotions are prevented, to the extent practicable, from being placed in or near locations such as schools or college campuses, or on media that would predominantly target underage or other prohibited persons, including those on the self-exclusion list. 'Promotions' is expressly covered, not only advertising.","Section 3 (amending 5:12A-13(a)(11)): Sports-wagering advertising rules must include the 1-800 GAMBLER language plus at least one DGE-selected phrase in all print, billboard, sign, online or broadcast advertisements of a sports pool or online sports pool, and in every sports wagering lounge.","Section 4: Takes effect immediately. The bill has no penalty provisions of its own and relies on DGE rulemaking and existing enforcement. Status: introduced 5/12/2025 and referred to Senate Budget and Appropriations on 5/29/2025."],"issue_tags":["advertising","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"S4366 is a study-then-rulemaking bill that delegates the content of the required messaging to DGE, whereas most comparable bills set the rules directly. NY A01118 and S01550 mandate harm and addiction warnings plus a hotline number in the statute itself, and NY A 382 and A 4279 restrict mobile sports wagering advertising and push notifications. CT HB05229 and HB05271 bar gaming advertising on campuses outright, CT HB05272 bans bonus and inducement advertising, and NY S 10092 bars gambling ads to minors on platforms; NJ's location restriction is only 'to the extent practicable' and requires regulations rather than imposing a flat ban. The closest analogue is VA SB819, which also directs a state agency to study responsible-gaming measures, and PA HR60, which orders a study without binding rules.","watch":"Watch for the open-ended DGE rulemaking: the as-yet-unknown 3 to 10 mandated phrases, the undefined 'frequency that optimizes effectiveness', and the 'to the extent practicable' placement standard for schools, college campuses and media that 'predominantly target' underage or self-excluded persons, since these will set operators' marketing compliance obligations (including promotions) and are vague enough to invite inconsistent enforcement.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":17968,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:51:57.271Z"},"NJ-S4282":{"summary":"NJ S4282 (First Reprint) declares that offering a paid-entry sweepstakes to anyone physically in New Jersey is unlawful gambling under Art. IV, Sec. VII, para. 2 of the State Constitution, unless it satisfies a safe harbor (free alternate method of entry and related conditions). It gives the Division of Consumer Affairs civil enforcement of the sweepstakes ban, and gives the Division of Gaming Enforcement parallel civil enforcement of N.J.S.A. 5:12-112 and other unlawful gambling. It also raises criminal grading for online gambling resorts, adds disorderly-persons offenses for gambling backers, and broadens the match-fixing and cheating statutes. The act takes effect immediately.","mechanics":["Sec. 1 defines \"game\" (casino-style, lottery and sports wagering games, plus anything that mimics them), \"prize or prize equivalent\" (including tokens or virtual items redeemable directly or indirectly via third-party marketplaces or cash-out functions), and \"sweepstakes\" (expressly including dual-currency systems). Sec. 2 makes paid-entry sweepstakes unlawful gambling, with civil liability for the operator and its officers, employees and agents, plus possible criminal liability under 2C:37-2, 2C:37-4 and 2C:37-7.","Sec. 3 safe harbor requires all of the following: a free method of entry; paid entry only ancillary to food, non-alcoholic beverages or merchandise of $20 or less (Director may adjust), excluding coins, tokens or online credits whose only value is sweepstakes entry or that are exchangeable; no winner determination based on sports results unless entry is solely free; disclosure of rules, odds and prizes; prize treated as NJ taxable income; no minor under 18 claiming a prize over $1,000 without parental consent; and identical odds for paid and free entries.","Sweepstakes penalties (Secs. 4-13, Consumer Affairs): civil penalty up to $100,000 for a first offense and $250,000 for each later offense, each day a separate offense; injunctions; subpoena and investigative powers; cease-and-desist orders with up to $25,000 per violation; restitution, doubled where the victim is 62 or older; contempt and charter or license suspension for non-compliance with subpoenas; a 5-year collection window; and first-priority liens on NJ real property. Penalties above costs go to compulsive-gambling programs.","Secs. 14-23 give the Division of Gaming Enforcement similar tools against violations of N.J.S.A. 5:12-112 and other unlawful gambling (excluding sweepstakes covered by Sec. 2), with civil penalties up to $10,000 for a first and $25,000 for each later offense. Under Sec. 17 the Director may penalize third-party and service providers found to have knowingly aided or abetted a violation. Sec. 14 bars the court from suspending or revoking any Division-issued license in an injunction proceeding.","Criminal changes: 2C:37-4 raises maintenance of a gambling resort from fourth to third degree with fines up to $50,000, and adds new subsection c making operation of an online gambling resort open to NJ persons a third-degree crime with a $50,000 to $100,000 fine unless State-regulated. 2C:37-2 now reaches online proceeds participation, and the player defense becomes an affirmative defense without the clear-and-convincing burden. 2C:37-1 broadens \"gambling\" to cover buying, selling or trading, and expressly includes sports events wagered on with licensees. Sec. 24 makes backing a $1,000+ bet for a 10%+ share of proceeds a disorderly persons offense.","Integrity changes: 2C:21-11 now covers rigging via point-shaving, throwing games and corrupt officiating, and lowers the grading threshold from $1,000 to $500. 5:12-113 swindling and cheating now covers Internet gaming and sports wagering, betting on non-public information, and use of another person's gaming account."],"issue_tags":["sweeps","integrity","legalize","advertising"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Among the listed bills, CA AB 831 is the closest, since it also targets contests and sweepstakes that simulate gambling, while AR HB1861 similarly makes illegal online casino and sports betting operation a felony. S4282 is unusual in that it pairs a conditional safe harbor for free-entry sweepstakes with a heavy civil penalty scheme: $100,000 and $250,000 per offense, accruing daily. It splits enforcement between Consumer Affairs and the Division of Gaming Enforcement and reaches aiding-and-abetting service providers. Most other listed bills (CT, GA, HI, IA, AK) concern licensing, taxation or consumer-protection rules for legal betting rather than prohibition.","watch":"Watch the breadth of the \"prize or prize equivalent\" and \"game\" definitions (which cover virtual items cashable through third-party marketplaces and anything mimicking casino or sports wagering), and the Sec. 17 aider-and-abettor exposure for service providers, because these could reach sweepstakes-style or dual-currency products as well as collectibles, card-break or mystery-pack offerings with secondary-market redemption.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":44431,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:51:57.347Z"},"NY-A8803":{"summary":"This is the New York 2024-25 Aid to Localities budget bill, which appropriates money for local assistance and reappropriates unspent 2023-24 balances for the fiscal year beginning April 1, 2024. The text provided covers only the general provisions in section 1 and the Office for the Aging schedule, and it contains no provisions on sports betting, iGaming, collectibles or trading cards. It is a spending bill with no substantive gaming regulation in the portion supplied.","mechanics":["Section 1(a) and (g) appropriate the listed amounts for aid to localities for the fiscal year beginning April 1, 2024, and section 1(c) reappropriates prior-year undisbursed balances for the same purposes.","Section 1(d) bars payment of any appropriation until the budget director issues a certificate of approval and files it with audit and control and the Senate Finance and Assembly Ways and Means chairs.","Section 1(f) repeals, on enactment, appropriations and reappropriations in chapter 53 of the laws of 2023 that would otherwise lapse on March 31, 2025.","Section 1(e) defines refunds, rebates, reimbursements, credits, repayments and disallowances for net-of-refund appropriations, and requires the comptroller to credit them back to the original appropriation.","The Office for the Aging schedule appropriates $392,410,824 (General Fund $219,186,824; federal $172,244,000; other $980,000) and reappropriates $528,702,513. It bars cost-of-living adjustments other than a 1.5 percent COLA authorized by a 2024 chapter."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. On its own, this is a general appropriations vehicle, and the portion supplied has no wagering, tax, licensing or gaming-integrity content. Any gaming relevance would have to come from schedules not included in the text provided, such as items for the gaming commission or problem-gambling services.","watch":"Check the remaining agency schedules, which were truncated here, for any appropriation or reappropriation touching the Gaming Commission, problem-gambling services or mobile sports wagering revenue, since the Office for the Aging text contains none.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":4052887,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:52:04.875Z"},"NY-A1118":{"summary":"NY A01118 amends the racing, pari-mutuel wagering and breeding law so that the State Gaming Commission must work with the commissioner of addiction services and supports to ensure all advertisements for gaming activity clearly and conspicuously state a problem gambling hotline number. It also amends the definition of \"advertisement\" in section 1363(1)(a) and directs the commission to publish advertising guidelines and technical support on its website. Although the title refers to warnings about harmful and addictive effects, the introduced text shown contains no operative warning-content mandate, only the hotline requirement.","mechanics":["Section 2 adds new Racing, Pari-Mutuel Wagering and Breeding Law section 111(1)(b): the commission shall cooperate with the commissioner of addiction services and supports to ensure that all advertisements for gaming activity clearly and conspicuously state a problem gambling hotline number. The mandate runs through the commission's cooperation, and the text sets no specific hotline number, font size, duration or format.","Section 1 amends the definition of \"advertisement\" in section 1363(1)(a) to cover any notice, communication or information about the gaming-related business of a gaming facility licensee or applicant or a mobile sports wagering licensee (as defined in section 1367), by any means including electronic dissemination. Promotional activities are treated as advertisements.","New section 111(1)(c) requires the commission to publish on its website information and technical support, including guidelines on advertising restrictions, for associations and corporations licensed or enfranchised under the chapter.","Existing section 111(1) is restructured as paragraph (a): commission cooperation with the addiction services commissioner on signs and internet listings for compulsive gamblers under Mental Hygiene Law 19.09(h), including information on how to join the self-excluded list. Signs must be posted at a reasonable distance from each entrance, exit and ATM in the facility.","The act takes effect on the 60th day after enactment, and implementing rules and regulations may be adopted and completed before that date.","The introduced text sets no penalties or enforcement mechanism of its own. The bill was signed as chapter 423 (2024-10-01), and the chaptered text may differ from this introduced version."],"issue_tags":["advertising"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"NY A01118 is a disclosure-only advertising measure. It requires a hotline number and leaves format and enforcement to the commission, and it does not restrict where, to whom or how gaming is advertised. Several Connecticut bills go further: HB05271 and HB05229 restrict gaming advertising at colleges and universities, HB05272 bars advertising that offers bonuses, credits or other inducements, and HB05269 also limits advertising. CT HB05229 also adds licensee customer-service phone line requirements and limits on AI-targeted bets. None of the listed bills mirrors New York's hotline mandate.","watch":"Confirm whether the chaptered version added specific warning language beyond the hotline requirement, and watch the commission's guidelines and rulemaking on what counts as clear and conspicuous placement and which promotional activities now count as advertisements, since the 60-day effective date leaves little time to revise creative and affiliate marketing.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":3213,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:52:05.152Z"},"NY-A8805":{"summary":"NY A8805-C is the omnibus public protection and general government budget bill for FY 2024-25, signed as Chapter 55 of 2024. It bundles about 30 unrelated Parts: new retail-worker assault and stolen-goods offenses, judicial security, cannabis enforcement, alcoholic beverage control extensions, pension measures and local-government items. It does not authorize, tax or regulate sports betting or iGaming.","mechanics":["Part A adds Penal Law 120.19, assault on a retail worker, a class E felony; it takes effect 180 days after enactment.","Part B adds Penal Law 165.66, fostering the sale of stolen goods, a class A misdemeanor. It covers anyone who, for financial gain, uses any website, app, online marketplace or physical venue to offer retail goods they knew or should have known were stolen. Effective the next November 1.","Part F creates the Judicial Security Act (Judiciary Law art. 22-C): covered judges can have personal information removed within 72 hours of notice. Remedies are injunctions, fees and damages of up to 3x actual damages with a $4,000 minimum. It also adds aggravated harassment of a judge as a class E felony. Effective 90 days after enactment.","Part G amends the Cannabis Law to remove the 'normal business hours' limit on OCM regulatory inspections and to give localities additional enforcement powers.","Part FF allows aggregation of the value of goods stolen under a common scheme for grand larceny and exempts grand larceny from the persistent felony offender definition.","Parts H-K, Y and CC amend or extend Alcoholic Beverage Control Law provisions on temporary permits, off-premises sales and delivery, and alcohol in movie theatres. Parts EE-KK address pension and retirement programs."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No other state bill listed is comparable. The AK, GA, HI, DE and CT bills regulate and tax sports betting, and the CT bills also address gaming advertising, which this bill does not. This NY bill is a general budget vehicle whose only loose overlap is the ABC and cannabis licensing and enforcement material (Parts G-K, Y, CC) and the new online-marketplace stolen-goods offense in Part B.","watch":"Little here touches sports betting directly; the closest points are Part B's 'fostering the sale of stolen goods' offense, which could reach online marketplaces, and Part FF's aggregation of stolen-goods value. Both matter for trading-card and collectibles resale rather than for operators of sportsbooks or iGaming.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":350887,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:52:05.698Z"},"NY-A8806":{"summary":"This is the New York 2024-25 Education, Labor and Housing (ELFA) budget implementer, covering school aid, housing and tenant measures (including good cause eviction), child care, transit and many other items. Its only gaming-adjacent provision is Part RR, which cuts the tax rate on certain authorized combative sports under Tax Law article 19 from 8.5% to 3%. Nothing in the bill, as described in its title and caption, addresses sports wagering, iGaming or trading cards. The text supplied stops partway through Part A, so Part RR is described from the caption and not from its operative language.","mechanics":["Part RR: reduces the Tax Law article 19 rate for certain authorized combative sports from 8.5% to 3%. The effective date, tax base and covered events are not visible in the supplied text.","The bill is an omnibus. Section 1 states that each Part is self-contained and carries its own effective date in its last section, so Part RR's effective date will be set within Part RR.","Part MM amends the Alcoholic Beverage Control Law to let licensees use contiguous and non-contiguous municipal public space. It is not gaming-related.","Part AAA creates a newspaper and broadcast media jobs program and tax credit. It has no stated link to wagering.","Status: A8806 was substituted by S8306C on 2024-04-19, so the Senate bill is the operative vehicle."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"The listed bills (AK HB145 and SB194, GA HB380, SB172 and SB57, HI HB1308 and HB2570, IA HF673 and HSB122) tax or regulate sports wagering revenue directly. NY A8806 has no wagering licensing, tax or advertising provisions. Its one tax change, in Part RR, is a rate cut on combative sports events under Tax Law article 19, which is a different tax base from wagering revenue. None of the listed bills compares to it, because it is a general budget implementer with a single incidental, adjacent provision.","watch":"Confirm in the final enacted version (S8306C) that Part RR is limited to the article 19 combative sports tax and that no wagering, fantasy contest or iGaming language was added, since combative sports event economics could still affect betting-market and sponsorship activity.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":771129,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:52:08.995Z"},"NY-A9630":{"summary":"A.9630 creates the New York state school safety and security act, adding Education Law Part II of Article 3 (§§150-152). It sets up an Office of School Safety and Security within the education department to run annual assessments of all public educational facilities, plus a 14-member advisory board to set best practices. Section 3 amends Racing, Pari-Mutuel Wagering and Breeding Law §1367(8) to divert one percent of the state tax imposed on mobile sports wagering to fund the office. It does not change the underlying mobile sports wagering tax rate.","mechanics":["Education Law §151: the office, headed by an executive director appointed by the commissioner, must assess every public educational facility annually and may enter facilities at reasonable times. It must issue written vulnerability reports and file an annual report by September 1.","Education Law §152: a 14-member advisory board (four gubernatorial appointees, state agency and law enforcement representatives, and four legislators) sets best practices in a searchable machine-readable repository and reports to the legislature and governor by February 1 each year.","RPWBL §1367(8) as amended: beginning fiscal year 2024-2025, the commission pays one percent of the state tax on mobile sports wagering to the general fund for expenses of the office, capped at the actual costs of the office's duties each fiscal year.","The existing earmarks remain: one percent for problem gambling (fixed at $6 million per year after the first fiscal year) and one percent for the youth sports grant program via OCFS (fixed at $5 million per year after the first fiscal year). The remainder continues to go to the state lottery fund for education aid.","Existing operator obligations are unchanged: at least monthly platform provider deposits under subdivision 7 and a monthly report and reconciliation statement due by the tenth day of each month.","Effective immediately. The text does not state how the new one percent allocation interacts with the stated tax rate or with the education aid share."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"None of the listed bills is comparable. AK HB145 and SB194, HI HB1308 and HB2570, and the Iowa tax bills such as HF673 and HSB122 address the sports wagering tax base, rate, or licensing. NY A09630 leaves the tax rate and licensing alone and only changes how existing mobile wagering tax revenue is allocated, to fund a school security office. It is unusual in tying gaming revenue to a school safety program, and in using a one percent allocation with a cost-based cap rather than a fixed dollar figure like the $6 million and $5 million earmarks in the same subdivision.","watch":"Operators should see that the bill does not raise their tax, but should watch whether the cost-based one percent allocation, with its uncapped percentage in early years, draws pressure on the education aid share or prompts later proposals to raise the rate.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":10495,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:52:13.728Z"},"NY-J1955":{"summary":"Senate Resolution 1955 is a non-binding legislative memorial urging Governor Hochul to proclaim March 2024 as Problem Gambling Awareness Month in New York, aligned with National Problem Gambling Awareness Month. It creates no regulatory, tax, licensing or operator obligations. Its recitals tie the request to the growth of mobile sports betting and the state's tax receipts from it.","mechanics":["Operative clause: the Senate memorializes the Governor to proclaim March 2024 as Problem Gambling Awareness Month and urges citizens to raise public awareness of problem gambling and of prevention, treatment and recovery services.","The resolution is hortatory only. It contains no statutory amendments, mandates, penalties, funding or enforcement provisions, and the proclamation itself is at the Governor's discretion.","Recital citing an OASAS survey: 5% of adults (668,000 people) showed problem gambling behaviors in the past year.","Recital on youth: 10% of grade 7-12 students (140,000) showed signs of problem gambling in the past 12 months, and 10% of those were in need of treatment.","Recital on mobile betting: the state has collected more than $1.55 billion in taxes on mobile bets, and growth in mobile betting requires education and resources to prevent and mitigate problem gambling.","A copy of the resolution is to be engrossed and transmitted to the Governor. Status shows it was adopted on 2024-03-19."],"issue_tags":["integrity"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"This is a symbolic awareness resolution, unlike the substantive regulatory bills listed from other states, such as GA SB57 (sports betting regulation and taxation), HI HB 1308, HB 2570 and SB1569 (sports wagering licensing and taxes), and IL HB2239 (Internet Gaming Act). It is closest in subject to IL HB5307 (Problem Gambling Task Force), IL SB 118 (DHS gambling disorders) and LA SR 178 (a Louisiana Senate resolution asking the Department of Health to study problem gambling prevention). Unlike the Hawaii bills, which set up a Problem Gambling Program, and the Illinois task force bill, it creates no program, body or funding. Its only distinctive content is the recital that New York has collected more than $1.55 billion in mobile betting taxes.","watch":"Nothing here binds operators, but the recitals signal legislative attention to problem gambling, youth exposure and mobile betting growth, which could support later responsible-gaming, advertising or funding mandates.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":2700,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:52:15.749Z"},"NY-A9082":{"summary":"The bill amends Racing, Pari-Mutuel Wagering and Breeding Law § 1367(8) to change how New York's mobile sports wagering tax revenue is earmarked for problem gambling. After the first fiscal year, the problem gambling set-aside is one percent of mobile sports tax revenue and no less than $6 million each fiscal year. It does not change the tax rate, licensing or operator conduct rules.","mechanics":["In the first fiscal year of mobile operations, the Gaming Commission pays one percent of the state mobile sports wagering tax into the commercial gaming fund for problem gambling education and treatment under State Finance Law § 97-nnnn(4)(a).","For each later fiscal year, the problem gambling amount is one percent of mobile sports tax revenue and no less than $6 million. The text supplied does not show which words are new, but the floor appears to be the substantive change.","A separate one percent of the tax goes in the first year to a general fund program run by the Office of Children and Family Services, a youth sports and education grant program for underserved youth under 18. After that it is $5 million per fiscal year.","Remaining mobile sports tax revenue, plus interest, penalties and forfeitures, continues to go to the state lottery fund for education aid. Mobile revenue is kept separate from retail sports wagering revenue notwithstanding § 1351.","Platform providers must make at least monthly deposits as the comptroller prescribes, and must file a monthly report and reconciliation with the commission by the tenth day of each month.","The act takes effect on April 1 next succeeding enactment."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike the Alaska mobile sports bills (HB145, SB194), which would authorize wagering and impose a new tax, this NY bill leaves an existing regime in place and only adjusts a revenue earmark. Its guaranteed dollar floor for problem gambling is a different approach from Hawaii HB 1308 and HB 2570, which pair licensing and a general excise tax with a dedicated problem gambling fund. Other listed bills, such as CT SB01463 and GA SB386, also direct sports wagering revenue to education purposes, but none uses a statutory minimum like NY's $6 million.","watch":"The $6 million floor and the fixed $5 million youth sports amount are state-side allocation commitments that could come under pressure if mobile tax revenue falls or if the legislature revisits the tax rate or deposit mechanics, so watch for any amendment that adds operator-funded assessments or changes the platform provider deposit and reporting duties.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":3613,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:52:18.802Z"},"NY-S4003":{"summary":"This is the New York FY2023-24 Aid to Localities budget bill (S4003-B), and the text provided covers only the general provisions and the Office for the Aging appropriations. It appropriates local assistance funds and reappropriates prior-year balances. Nothing in the text provided addresses sports betting, iGaming, collectibles or trading cards; it is a general appropriations bill with no gaming content.","mechanics":["Section 1(a) appropriates the listed amounts for aid to localities. Section 1(c) reappropriates undisbursed prior-year balances for FY beginning April 1, 2023, citing chapter 53, section 1 of the laws of 2022.","Section 1(d) bars payment of any appropriation until the budget director issues a certificate of approval and files it with audit and control and the Senate and Assembly finance committee chairs.","Section 1(g) repeals appropriations and reappropriations in chapter 53 of the laws of 2022 that would otherwise lapse on March 31, 2024.","Office for the Aging: $332,515,744 in new appropriations (General Fund $216,550,744; federal $114,985,000; other $980,000) and $452,482,513 in reappropriations.","The Office for the Aging items include a $15,000,000 Community Services for the Elderly (CSE) line and a $13,000,000 line for the human services cost-of-living adjustment tied to an 8.5 percent COLA authorized by separate 2023 legislation. A $1,900,000 line is allocated only under a plan approved by the Senate temporary president and the budget director and then adopted by a Senate resolution.","The text provided is truncated mid-reappropriation (Colonie Senior Service Center, item 10744), so other agencies' appropriations are not visible."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. This is a general budget appropriations bill, and the portion provided contains no gaming-related provisions at all. It is not comparable to substantive wagering legislation.","watch":"Nothing in the text provided touches wagering, so the only reason to track it would be if later portions, which are not shown, or related budget bills carry gaming tax, licensing or problem-gambling funding items; check those separately.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":3836725,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:52:21.337Z"},"NY-S1550":{"summary":"S1550 amends the New York racing, pari-mutuel wagering and breeding law to widen the definition of 'advertisement' in section 1363(1)(a) and to require the State Gaming Commission to work with the commissioner of addiction services and supports so that all gaming advertisements clearly and conspicuously state a problem gambling hotline number. Despite the title, the text provided contains no operative language prescribing warnings about harmful or addictive effects. The only advertising content mandate is the hotline number. The bill was substituted by A1118 on 2024-05-30.","mechanics":["Section 1 amends the definition of 'advertisement' in RPWBL section 1363(1)(a). It covers communications about the gaming-related business of a gaming facility licensee or applicant 'or a mobile sports wagering licensee as defined in section 1367', through any means including electronic dissemination. It states that promotional activities are advertisements. The italics marking new text were lost in the provided version, so which words are new is inferred.","Section 2 amends RPWBL section 111(1), designating the existing sign-posting and self-exclusion information duty as paragraph (a).","New paragraph 111(1)(b) directs the commission to cooperate with the commissioner of addiction services and supports to ensure all advertisements for gaming activity 'clearly and conspicuously state a problem gambling hotline number'. It sets no format, size, duration or placement standard and prescribes no hotline number.","New paragraph 111(1)(c) requires the commission to post on its website information and technical support, including guidelines for licensed or enfranchised associations and corporations on advertising restrictions.","The bill sets no penalties, safe harbor or private right of action. Enforcement would presumably run through the commission's general licensing authority.","Section 3 makes the act effective on the 60th day after enactment, and authorizes implementing rules to be adopted on or before that date."],"issue_tags":["advertising"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"This is a light-touch disclosure mandate (hotline number in every gaming ad), whereas the Connecticut advertising bills restrict content or placement. CT HB05272 bans ads offering bonuses or inducements, CT HB05229 and HB05271 restrict advertising on college campuses, and CT HB05269 addresses ad restrictions alongside joint-card payment rules. CT HB05038 and IL HB5059 (ORACLE Act) address prediction-market advertising and access for under-21s, which S1550 does not touch. Unusually, S1550 leaves the content, format and enforcement of the hotline requirement to commission and agency cooperation rather than statute.","watch":"Watch for how the commission defines 'clearly and conspicuously' and the scope of 'promotional activities' under the expanded advertisement definition, since the bill sets no standards or penalties and gives the commission broad rulemaking discretion; the bill was also substituted by A1118, so any live version of this language should be tracked there.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":3271,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:52:21.426Z"},"NY-S4005":{"summary":"This is the 2023-24 New York public protection and general government budget implementer (S4005-C/A3005-C). It extends dozens of expiring criminal-justice and correction sunsets from September 1, 2023 to September 1, 2025, and it also covers firearms, hazard mitigation loans, civil service, pension and retirement benefits, and an unmarked burial site act. The only gaming-related content in the text provided is a reference to gaming venues in the firearms 'sensitive location' definition in Part F, so the bill is not a gaming measure.","mechanics":["Part A (sections 1-23) moves sunset dates from September 1, 2023 to September 1, 2025 for programs including work release and furlough, community treatment facilities, the ignition interlock program, the mandatory surcharge and victim assistance fee (VTL 1809(8)), the interstate compact for adult offender supervision, and the sentencing reform act of 1995. The City of New York pre-criminal proceeding settlements provision is extended to March 31, 2025. Part A takes effect immediately (section 24).","Part F amends Penal Law 265.01-e, which makes possession of a firearm, rifle or shotgun in a sensitive location a class E felony. Subdivision 2(p) lists places used for performance, art, entertainment, gaming or sporting events, including theaters, stadiums, racetracks, museums, conference centers, and 'gaming facilities and video lottery terminal facilities as licensed by the gaming commission'. The Part adds exemptions, including for persons responsible for security at places of worship and for qualified law enforcement officers under 18 U.S.C. 926B and 926C.","Part F also amends the restricted-location offense (Penal Law 265.01-d) and the vehicle gun storage rule (265.45(2)). It exempts police, qualified law enforcement officers and military personnel acting in their official duties from the storage requirement, and it adds an exemption for MTA and NYC Transit revenue control and security personnel.","Part G creates a hazard mitigation revolving loan fund (State Finance Law 99-qq) and a loan authority (Executive Law 719) for local governments, funded in part by FEMA capitalization grants under the federal STORM Act. Part H lets volunteer fire companies pay training stipends to volunteer firefighters.","Parts B-E, I, K-N, P, U, X and CC are intentionally omitted. The text provided cuts off in Part H, so I could not review Parts I through OO, which cover civil service, retirement benefits, state procurement, the liquidator's permit extension in Part O, and the unmarked burial site act in Part OO. The summary of those Parts rests on the title only."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills is comparable. The Alaska, Georgia, Hawaii, Iowa, Connecticut, Delaware, Arkansas and Florida measures (for example AK HB145, GA SB57, HI HB 1308, CT SB01464, DE HB365) all legalize, regulate, tax or police wagering directly. NY S04005 is a multi-subject budget bill whose only link to gaming is that it names licensed gaming facilities and video lottery terminal facilities as firearm-restricted sensitive locations. It is also a procedural vehicle, having been substituted by A3005C.","watch":"An operator with licensed gaming or VLT facilities in New York should note that Part F keeps those premises on the Penal Law 265.01-e sensitive-location list, which affects security and signage policy but not betting or iGaming regulation.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":235244,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:52:29.729Z"},"NY-S8303":{"summary":"This is the Governor's Aid to Localities budget bill for FY 2024-25 (S8303-B), and the portion provided is limited to the Office for the Aging: general fund appropriations of $219,186,824 and federal and other special revenue funds, plus reappropriations of prior-year balances. Nothing in the supplied text concerns sports betting, iGaming, collectibles or trading cards. The bill was substituted by A8803D on 2024-04-19.","mechanics":["Section 1(a)-(g) are the general budget terms: appropriations are payable only after the budget director issues a certificate of approval (1(d)); prior-year balances are reappropriated for FY beginning April 1, 2024 (1(c)); and unspent chapter 53 (L. 2023) items that would otherwise lapse on March 31, 2025 are repealed (1(f)).","Office for the Aging 2024-25 totals: General Fund $219,186,824 and Special Revenue Federal $172,244,000. Other Special Revenue is $980,000, for $392,410,824 in all funds, plus $528,702,513 in reappropriations.","Major lines include CSE $38,696,508, EISEP $67,498,000, WIN nutrition $36,978,816 and the long-term care ombudsman program $3,690,000. Several lines carry a cost-of-living restriction: no COLA other than 1.5 percent authorized by a 2024 chapter.","Many appropriations bar spending until the budget director approves a plan from the Office for the Aging. One $5,000,000 line is allocated only under a plan approved by the temporary president of the Senate and the budget director.","Federal Older Americans Act lines include Title III-b $49,069,000, Title III-c $69,610,000, Title III-e $12,000,000 and Title V $9,000,000. The text also contains a $1,000,000 Elder Justice task force line tied to Article VII HMH Part W in S8307B.","The supplied text cuts off partway through the Office for the Aging reappropriations. Any gaming-related provisions elsewhere in the full budget, such as the Gaming Commission or problem-gambling funding, are not visible."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. On its own, the supplied text is a pure appropriations bill for senior services. It has no tax rates, licensing, or wagering or collectibles provisions, and any gaming items in the wider budget are outside the text provided.","watch":"Nothing relevant to operators appears in the supplied Office for the Aging text, but a lawyer should review the complete S8303-B and the companion Article VII bills for Gaming Commission, problem-gambling or mobile-wagering revenue provisions, which would appear in other agency sections or in the revenue legislation.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":4052887,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:52:29.935Z"},"NY-S6701":{"summary":"S06701 adds a new section 25.04 to the Mental Hygiene Law creating a 13-member Problem Gambling Advisory Council that advises the governor and legislature on preventing and treating problem gambling. It is purely advisory: it has no rulemaking, licensing, tax or enforcement powers, but it must recommend allocations of gaming-licensee machine and table fees and examine the effect of mobile sports betting on problem gambling services.","mechanics":["Composition (25.04(b)): 13 members, being the OASAS commissioner, the Gaming Commission chair, and 11 appointees: 4 by the Senate temporary president, 4 by the Assembly speaker, 1 each by the Senate and Assembly minority leaders, and 1 by the governor. The Senate and Assembly leaders must each appoint at least two community-based behavioral health providers.","Meetings (25.04(c)): at least twice a year, subject to the open meetings law. Members serve four-year terms without compensation but with expense reimbursement, and may designate a proxy in writing.","Fee recommendations (25.04(c)(4)): the council recommends allocations of machine and table fees collected from gaming licensees under Racing, Pari-Mutuel Wagering and Breeding Law section 1348 and distributed under State Finance Law section 97-nnnn. These fees come from commercial casino licensees, not mobile sports wagering operators.","Mobile betting study (25.04(c)(5)): the council examines the effect of mobile sports betting on the problem gambling hopeline call volume, outreach to local resource centers, staffing needs, and the number of people added to self-exclusion lists at gaming facilities.","Annual report (25.04(c)(6)): due by October 1 each year, with the first due on the October 1 following the effective date.","Information sharing (25.04(e)): the council may request information from state and local agencies, excluding personally identifying information and legally protected data. The act takes effect 180 days after enactment."],"issue_tags":["integrity"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"Illinois HB5307 (Problem Gambling Task Force) and Illinois SB 118 (DHS gambling disorders) are the closest comparables, as are Louisiana SR 178, which asks the Department of Health to study problem gambling prevention. The Hawaii sports wagering bills (HB 1308, HB 2570) fund problem gambling through a dedicated program inside a full licensing and tax structure. NY S06701 has no licensing, tax or operator obligations of its own. It creates only an advisory body and ties its funding recommendations to existing casino machine and table fees. The mandate to study mobile sports betting's effect on problem gambling services is the most operator-relevant feature.","watch":"Watch whether the council's annual recommendations, especially its mobile-betting impact findings and fee-allocation advice, become the basis for later legislation or regulation imposing new responsible-gaming, self-exclusion, or funding obligations on mobile sports wagering operators.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":5344,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:52:30.149Z"},"NY-S4006":{"summary":"S4006 is the omnibus 2023-24 New York Education, Labor and Housing Budget bill (ELFA). Parts A through VV cover school aid, SUNY/CUNY, minimum wage, child care, social services, energy and climate, cannabis tax enforcement and bail. Gaming appears only in two small Parts that amend the racing, pari-mutuel wagering and breeding law: Part JJ on the Western Regional OTB board, and Part OO on the use of Capital Region OTB capital acquisition funds. The bill was substituted by A3006C, so S4006 itself is no longer the operative vehicle.","mechanics":["Part JJ amends the racing, pari-mutuel wagering and breeding law on the membership and appointment of the Western Regional Off-Track Betting Corporation board of directors, with a repeal on expiration. The Part's operative text was not in the excerpt provided, so only the title description is available.","Part OO amends the same law on how funds in the Capital Region OTB corporations' capital acquisition funds may be used. The operative text was not in the excerpt provided.","Part A, section 2 adds Education Law section 3602(4)(k): 2023-24 foundation aid equals the foundation aid base plus the greater of the formula increase or 3% of the base.","Part A adds Education Law section 925: a state subsidy for community eligibility provision schools that tops paid-meal reimbursement up to the combined state and federal free-meal rate, starting in 2023-24.","Part A section 4 allows up to 22 previously surrendered, revoked or terminated charters to be reissued once, 14 of them in New York City, outside the existing numerical caps.","Part S raises the minimum wage and indexes it to inflation, and Part UU sets cannabis tax and unlicensed-retail enforcement measures. Neither is a gaming provision. Part S is described in the title only, and Part UU appears beyond the excerpt."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills is comparable. The AK HB145/SB194, DE HB365, GA SB57 and HI HB1308 bills are dedicated legalization, licensing and tax measures, and the CT bills are dedicated gaming-regulation measures. NY S4006 is an education and social-services budget bill whose only gaming content is governance and fund-use changes for the regional OTB corporations in Parts JJ and OO. It does not legalize, tax, license or regulate sports betting or iGaming, and it does not cover advertising or integrity matters.","watch":"A lawyer for sports-betting operators should check the full text of Parts JJ and OO for any change in OTB governance or fund use that affects OTB-linked partnerships or market access, since nothing else in the bill touches wagering.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":669675,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:52:30.248Z"},"NY-S8305":{"summary":"NY S8305-C is the omnibus public protection and general government budget bill for FY 2024-25, bundling Parts A through KK on criminal law, cannabis and alcohol enforcement, judicial security, pensions and local government. It has no sports betting, iGaming or card-product provisions. The nearest items for gaming counsel are the new retail-theft and fostering-stolen-goods crimes (Parts A, B, FF), the cannabis and ABC enforcement changes, and the judges' data-privacy takedown regime (Part F).","mechanics":["Part A adds Penal Law 120.19, a class E felony for causing physical injury to a retail worker to prevent them from performing job duties, where the actor knew or should have known the victim was a retail worker. It takes effect 180 days after enactment.","Part B adds Penal Law 165.66, a class A misdemeanor for fostering the sale of stolen goods. It covers anyone acting for financial gain who uses any website, app, online marketplace, digital service or physical venue to offer retail goods they knew or should have known were stolen or unlawfully obtained. Effective November 1 following enactment.","Part FF allows the value of goods stolen under a common scheme or plan to be aggregated for grand larceny thresholds and exempts grand larceny offenses from the persistent felony offender definition.","Part F creates the Judicial Security Act (Judiciary Law art. 22-C). On a written request from an eligible judge, the court employer must send certified-mail notice within 5 business days, and the recipient has 72 hours to cease making the judge's personal information public. Remedies are injunctive or declaratory relief, with fees; violating such an order carries a fine up to $1,000 for public agencies and up to treble damages (minimum $4,000) for private recipients. Exclusions cover news, FCRA, GLBA and HIPAA entities. Effective 90 days after enactment.","Part F also creates aggravated harassment of a judge (Penal Law 240.33, class E felony) and aggravated assault on a judge (120.09-a, class B felony).","Part G, as shown in the truncated text, removes the 'normal business hours' limit on Office of Cannabis Management inspections and adds enforcement powers for localities. Parts H through K, Y and CC adjust alcoholic beverage control law, covering temporary permits, a temporary wholesale permit, and extenders for off-premises sales and delivery."],"issue_tags":["integrity"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills (AK HB145, CT SB01464, GA SB57 and the others) are comparable, because they are sports-wagering, iGaming or lottery bills and this is a general budget bill. The only overlap is thematic. Part F's data-takedown regime and the stolen-goods and retail-theft crimes are unrelated to wagering licensing, tax or advertising. The bill was substituted by A8805C, so the Assembly version is the live vehicle.","watch":"Operators and collectibles or trading-card sellers should watch the Part B fostering-sale-of-stolen-goods offense, whose 'knew or should have known' standard and 'any platform' scope could reach marketplaces, live card breaks and mystery-pack sales involving stolen merchandise, as well as the Part F 72-hour takedown duty if they hold judges' personal data.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":350887,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:52:39.645Z"},"NY-S8439":{"summary":"S08439 amends Racing, Pari-Mutuel Wagering and Breeding Law section 1367(8) to set a floor on New York's mobile sports wagering dedication to problem gambling. In the first operating fiscal year the commission pays 1% of the mobile sports wagering state tax into the commercial gaming fund for problem gambling education and treatment, and for each later fiscal year the amount is 1% of mobile sports tax revenue and no less than $6 million. It leaves the existing $5 million annual youth sports grant program (OCFS) and the monthly deposit and reporting rules in place.","mechanics":["Problem gambling set-aside: 1% of the state tax on mobile sports wagering in the first operating fiscal year, then 1% of mobile sports tax revenue with a minimum of $6 million per fiscal year thereafter.","Funds are paid by the commission into the commercial gaming fund and distributed for problem gambling education and treatment under State Finance Law section 97-nnnn(4)(a).","The remaining mobile tax revenue, interest, penalties and forfeited funds still go to the state lottery fund for education aid, except as the subdivision provides otherwise.","Youth sports grant: 1% of the mobile tax goes to the general fund for an OCFS statewide youth sports grant program for under-18s in the first year, then a fixed $5 million per fiscal year.","The commission must require at least monthly deposits by platform providers under subdivision 7, to the credit of the state commercial gaming revenue fund, plus a monthly report and reconciliation due by the 10th of each month.","Effective April 1 following enactment. It adds no new tax rate and imposes no new obligation on licensees; it only changes how the state allocates revenue."],"issue_tags":["tax","legalize"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"None of the listed bills is a close analogue. Hawaii's HB 1308 and HB 2570 also address problem gambling funding, but as part of full sports wagering frameworks with licensing and a general excise tax. Iowa's HF673 and HSB122 deal with tax receipts and credits. S08439 is a narrow earmark amendment to an existing mobile regime, and its distinguishing feature is a dollar floor ($6 million) on top of a percentage.","watch":"The bill does not raise the tax paid by operators, but the $6 million floor could take a larger share of mobile tax revenue than 1% when revenue is low, which may feed future pressure on the rate or on other earmarks, such as the $5 million youth grant and education aid.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":3658,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:52:41.094Z"},"NY-S8306":{"summary":"S8306-C is the omnibus 2024-25 New York budget bill for education, labor, housing and family assistance (Parts A through BBB). It covers school aid, housing and tenant measures (including good cause eviction), tuition aid, child care rates and other items. The only gaming-adjacent item is Part RR, which, per the bill's title and summary, cuts the article 19 Tax Law rate on certain authorized combative sports from 8.5% to 3%. The text supplied ends in Part A, so Part RR's operative language is not reviewed here. Nothing in the supplied text or summary addresses sports betting, iGaming, sweepstakes or trading cards.","mechanics":["Part RR amends Tax Law article 19 to reduce the tax on certain authorized combative sports from eight and one-half percent to three percent, per the bill title and summary; the effective date and the definition of covered events or receipts are not in the supplied text.","Part A: foundation aid changes include deeming the 2024-25 CPI increase at 2.8% (0.028) and capping the state sharing ratio at 0.91 from 2024-25 onward; the Rockefeller Institute is directed to study the foundation aid formula.","Part HH enacts the good cause eviction law, with opt-in by local law for municipalities outside New York City.","Part Y sets differential child care payment rates at no less than 10% and no more than 15% above the actual cost of care.","Part BBB provides a supplemental Empire State child credit payment, and Part AAA creates a newspaper and broadcast media jobs tax credit.","Under section 1, each Part carries its own effective date in its last section, and section 3 sets the general effective date."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"This bill is not comparable to the listed wagering bills, which are standalone sports betting, iGaming, advertising or lottery measures such as AK HB145, GA HB380, HI HB 2570, DE HB365 and CT HB05229. The one overlap is that the Alaska and Georgia bills impose taxes on wagering revenue, whereas Part RR of NY S08306 cuts an existing tax on combative sports events, which is not a tax on betting. Within the supplied text it is an omnibus budget vehicle in which the sole gaming-adjacent item is one tax-rate reduction.","watch":"Confirm the actual text of Part RR to see whether the 3% rate reaches only event-level combative sports receipts or also touches broadcast, streaming or sponsorship revenue, since the bill contains no wagering provisions that would affect operators directly.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":771129,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:52:42.719Z"},"NY-S8309":{"summary":"S08309 (Chapter 59 of 2024) is the omnibus revenue Article VII budget bill for NY FY2024-25. It is overwhelmingly general tax and administrative measures, with a few gaming-adjacent pieces: extensions of off-track betting (OTB) and simulcast provisions in the racing, pari-mutuel wagering and breeding law (Parts O, P, R, S), and a rewrite of the adult-use cannabis tax (Part L). It does not authorize, tax or regulate sports betting, iGaming or collectibles.","mechanics":["Part O (RPW Law 509-a(2)): for April 1, 2024 to March 31, 2025, the Catskill OTB capital acquisition fund may release 23% of funds up to $2.5M and Capital OTB $1M, for expenditures necessary to accept wagers and past-due obligations to racing associations, breeding funds and the state. Use requires attestation that the section 532 surcharge monies are segregated and paid to localities, plus a commission-approved expenditure plan. The accounting report deadline moves to the last day of the calendar year for which funds are requested, and the commission may order an audit with reimbursement of unauthorized spending.","Part P (RPW Law 1003(1)(a)): extends in-home simulcasting experiment agreements from June 30, 2024 to June 30, 2025. Licensing terms are unchanged: $500 per simulcast facility, and $20,000 per year for account wagering licensees with no NY simulcast facility or racetrack. The text provided is cut off mid-sentence, so the other Part P simulcast extensions (out-of-state thoroughbred and harness simulcasting, wager distributions, chapters 281/1994 and 346/1990) and Parts Q to T are not shown here. Their content comes from the bill title only.","Part L (Tax Law 493, 496, 496-b): replaces the potency-based cannabis tax rates with a 9% tax on the amount charged by a distributor to a retailer. Microbusiness and registered-organization licensees that also sell at retail owe the tax at retail sale on 75% of the retail price. New 496(d) lets the Commissioner estimate tax from available information, using retail price if wholesale price evidence is absent, when books and records are missing or insufficient. Sections 1 and 2 apply to sales on or after June 1, 2024, and section 3 (Article 28 Part 4 cross-reference) to sales on or after December 1, 2024.","Part H (Tax Law 1136(d-1), 1145(a)(8)): permits amended sales tax returns that do not reduce or eliminate a past-due liability, treats reductions as refund claims, and sets a penalty of up to $1,000 per return for willfully filing or amending a return with false information to reduce a liability. The amended-return rule applies to periods starting on or after December 1, 2024.","Part E creates a commercial security tax credit of $3,000 per NY retail location for qualifying small retailers (50 or fewer employees). The credit applies for tax years 2024 and 2025, with a $5M annual cap, certificates issued by DCJS, and an application deadline of October 31.","Parts A, B, F, G, I, J extend sunsets: the $10M+ itemized deduction limit to 2030, the voluntary compliance initiative to July 1, 2029, e-filing and segregated sales tax account provisions to December 31, 2029, the related-person sales tax exemption to June 30, 2025, and the vending machine exemption to May 31, 2025."],"issue_tags":["tax","licensing"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"This bill has little in common with the sports-betting bills listed. AK HB145 and SB194, HI HB1308 and HB2570, and GA SB172 and HB380 create new sports wagering regimes with licensing and revenue taxes, and IA SF2427 and HSB723 address sports wagering tax administration. NY S08309 does none of that and leaves NY's existing mobile sports wagering framework untouched. Its gaming content is limited to housekeeping on racing, OTB and simulcast law, which sits closest to the pari-mutuel and racing material in FL H0189, plus a cannabis tax overhaul that has no counterpart in the listed bills.","watch":"Little here directly affects sports-betting operators, but account wagering licensees should note the unchanged $20,000 annual licence fee for non-NY-facility operators in RPW Law 1003 and the in-home simulcast extension to June 30, 2025, and should read the omitted Part P to T text for further simulcast or distribution changes.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":76462,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:52:43.392Z"},"NY-S8777":{"summary":"NY S8777-A amends the definition of \"sports wagering\" in Racing, Pari-Mutuel Wagering and Breeding Law section 1367(1)(x) to add wagers on annual awards or honors for an individual athlete or team performance during or throughout a season. It also expressly names season-long proposition bets alongside in-game proposition bets, and limits both to wagers the Gaming Commission determines are not at risk of undue interference, insider trading, or other integrity problems. The act takes effect immediately.","mechanics":["Section 1 amends the definition of \"sports wagering\" in RPWBL 1367(1)(x) to cover \"annual awards or honors for an individual athlete or team performance during or throughout a season,\" which brings future award-winner markets such as MVP into the definition.","The list of authorized wager types now includes \"in-game and season-long proposition bets,\" where the existing text covered only in-game props.","Both in-game and season-long props are limited to wagers the commission determines are not \"at risk of undue interference, insider trading of information, or other issues\" that could compromise the integrity and fairness of the sports wagering program, so the commission decides which markets are allowed.","The coin toss language is retained: coin toss bets that affect gameplay are permitted at all sporting events unless the commission prohibits them for integrity and fairness reasons.","Section 2 makes the act effective immediately. The bill sets no new tax rate, license requirement, or penalty, and it gives no standard or process for the commission's determinations."],"issue_tags":["prop","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Nothing comparable is listed. The other states' bills (e.g., AK HB145, DE HB365, GA SB57, HI HB 2570) are broad enabling, licensing, or tax bills, while NY S8777-A is a narrow amendment to an existing definition in a mature market. The bill is unusual because it writes integrity-based commission gatekeeping for prop markets into the statutory definition of sports wagering, not into the regulations or licensing provisions. It also authorizes season-long award markets directly, which the listed bills do not address.","watch":"Watch how the Gaming Commission uses its open-ended discretion to decide which season-long and in-game prop and award markets are permitted, because the bill sets no criteria, timeline, or approval process and operators may have to get market-by-market clearance.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":2539,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:52:46.353Z"},"OH-HB126":{"summary":"Ohio HB126 amends R.C. 718.01, the definitions section of the municipal income tax chapter. Its only substantive change is to division (C)(1) of the exempt-income definition: the exemption for military pay and allowances is widened from members of the 'armed forces' to members of the 'uniformed services' of the United States (and their reserve components, including the national guard), with 'uniformed services' defined by reference to 10 U.S.C. 101. The rest of the section is existing text restated. It is not a gaming bill.","mechanics":["Sec. 718.01(C)(1): replaces 'armed forces' with 'uniformed services' in the exemption for military pay or allowances, so the exemption can reach members of services beyond the armed forces, such as the commissioned corps of NOAA and the Public Health Service.","New sentence in (C)(1) defines 'uniformed services' as having the same meaning as in 10 U.S.C. 101.","The exemption continues to cover members of reserve components, including the national guard of any state.","No rate, threshold, effective date or enforcement provision is added; the bill leaves the municipal income tax structure otherwise unchanged.","Existing text that touches gaming is carried forward unchanged: (B)(4) includes lottery, sweepstakes, gambling and sports winnings in municipal 'income' and lets professional gamblers deduct wagering losses; (S) excludes gambling winnings from 'intangible income'; (X) treats casino operators and video lottery terminal sales agents as 'other payers'."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"Nothing in the comparison set is truly comparable, since HB126 is a municipal income tax definitions bill with a military-pay exemption change and does not regulate or tax wagering. The closest overlap is the tax angle: Iowa's SF 605 and HSB 91 address state income tax withholding on sports wagering winnings, and Illinois HB4171 would bar home rule units from taxing sports wagering. HB126 does none of that. It only reproduces existing Ohio language that already counts gambling and sports winnings as municipal taxable income.","watch":"Nothing here changes operator obligations, but note that the carried-forward text in 718.01(B)(4) and (X) keeps sports winnings taxable as municipal income and keeps casino operators within the 'other payer' definition, so any amendment in committee that touches those provisions would matter.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":53262,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:52:50.981Z"},"NY-S9236":{"summary":"S9236 amends Racing, Pari-Mutuel Wagering and Breeding Law section 1367(8) so that set percentages of the existing state tax on mobile sports wagering are carved out of the education lottery fund deposit. One percent goes to problem gambling education and treatment, with a floor of $6 million a year. One percent goes to a new youth sports activities and education grant program run by the Office of Children and Family Services, with a floor of $5 million a year. It does not change the tax rate or any operator licensing, conduct or product rules.","mechanics":["Sec. 1367(8)(a): mobile sports tax revenue stays separately maintained and goes to the state lottery fund for education aid 'except as otherwise provided in this subdivision'. Interest, penalties and forfeited funds or prizes also go to the education fund.","Sec. 1367(8)(b): the commission pays one percent of the mobile sports state tax into the commercial gaming fund for problem gambling education and treatment under State Finance Law 97-nnnn(4)(a). After the first fiscal year the amount is one percent of mobile sports tax revenue and no less than $6 million.","Sec. 1367(8)(c): one percent of the mobile tax goes to the general fund for an OCFS youth sports and education grant program, with a floor of $5 million per year after the first year. Grants go to sports teams and programs for underserved youth under 18, for programming, improvements and capital equipment.","Grant distribution under (c): county youth bureaus distribute funds, except in New York City, where Laureus Sport for Good Foundation USA does. Funding availability and award standards must be widely advertised. An annual report on recipients and impact is due to OCFS by December 1 after each grant concludes.","Sec. 1367(8)(d): platform providers must make at least monthly deposits under subdivision 7 as prescribed by the Comptroller, credited to the commercial gaming revenue fund. A monthly report and reconciliation is due by the 10th of each month.","Effective April 1 next succeeding enactment. The title refers to a third one percent for youth team sports, but the text has only two distinct set-asides, (b) and (c). Operators bear no new direct payment obligation; the carve-outs come from state tax proceeds."],"issue_tags":["tax","legalize"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"The comparable bills listed here are mostly new authorizations or broad regulatory packages. Examples are AK HB145/SB194 (mobile wagering and tax), HI HB1308/HB2570/SB1569 (which set up a Problem Gambling P... fund), GA SB386 (Pre-K funding), and CT SB01463 (revenue transfer to the Early Childhood Care and Education Fund). S9236 amends an existing mature tax earmark rather than creating a market. Its distinguishing features are fixed dollar floors ($6M and $5M) and the statutory naming of a private grant distributor, Laureus Sport for Good Foundation USA, for New York City. Unlike CT SB01463, it diverts a small share of revenue rather than redirecting the whole stream.","watch":"Operators should confirm that this is purely a reallocation of existing tax proceeds with no change to the tax rate or platform-provider payments, and watch the dollar floors, the unexplained 'third one percent' in the title, and any amendments in Finance that would raise the set-asides or add operator-funded obligations.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":4426,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:52:51.702Z"},"NY-S9044":{"summary":"S9044-A amends Article 14 of the NY Racing, Pari-Mutuel Wagering and Breeding Law to bring over/under \"target score\" contests (the pick-more-or-less format used in pick'em products) within the definition of interactive fantasy sports (IFS) and to declare that such contests are not sports wagering under PBL 1367(1)(x), provided they are offered by a registrant as peer-to-peer contests. It also raises the player age to 21, sets a $5 million initial registration fee and a five-year registration term, and limits the Gaming Commission's power to regulate or relabel individual contests.","mechanics":["Section 1401(2) redefines \"interactive fantasy sports contest\" as a game of skill in which two or more contestants compete against each other, expressly including contests where participants choose whether athletes will accumulate more or less than an operator-set target score. Section 1401(3) defines \"target score\" and states that its use does not make a contest sports wagering, if offered by a registrant as part of a peer-to-peer contest.","Section 1401(7) and (11) raise the minimum age for authorized players to 21, and \"minor\" now means anyone under 21 (previously 18). \"Prohibited player\" and \"prohibited sports event\" (collegiate, high school, horse racing) are retained. The gross revenue definition is moved to 1401(14) and the revenue formula is unchanged: entry fees less winnings, multiplied by the NY resident percentage.","Section 1402 sets an initial registration fee of $5,000,000 for IFS operators, extends registrations from three to five years, and sets renewal at 1% of IFS gross revenue for the prior registration period. It repeals the pre-November 10, 2015 grandfathering and temporary permit provisions. Registered contests remain outside Penal Law art. 225 gambling.","New PBL 1405(3) bars the commission from adopting rules that regulate the rules or administration of an individual contest, its statistical makeup, or the operator's digital platform, and from limiting contest types by labelling them sports wagering under PBL 1367(1)(x). It also directs liberal construction of the article.","New 1405(4)-(6) require the commission to verify that operators use identity and geolocation checks, which may involve a third party, and mechanisms to detect unauthorized accounts, fraud, money laundering and collusion. The commission must also review and revise 9 NYCRR 5600-5613.4 within 120 days of the effective date.","Section 1403(4) changes the commission's denial, suspension and revocation power from \"shall\" to \"may\". Section 1404(1)(o) and (q) are tweaked: winning outcomes must be determined predominantly by accumulated statistical results, and none may rest solely on a single performance in a single event. Annual reporting (1406) and regulatory cost assessments (1408) now apply to each \"operator\" rather than each \"registrant\". The act takes effect immediately."],"issue_tags":["prediction","licensing","legalize","tax","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"None of the listed bills from other states addresses over/under pick'em products by redefining them as fantasy contests. GA HB1329 (fantasy contest regulation and taxation) and IA HF673/HSB122 (tax receipts on internet fantasy sports and sports wagering) are the closest on fantasy regulation and tax, but neither carves target-score contests out of sports wagering. NY S9044-A is unusual in combining a statutory sports-wagering safe harbor for pick'em with a bar on commission rulemaking over contest design, which the other states' sports-wagering bills (e.g. AK HB145, HI HB1308, GA SB57) do not do.","watch":"Operators should watch whether the statutory declaration that target-score contests are not sports wagering, together with the bar on commission relabelling in 1405(3), survives scrutiny, since it would give pick'em operators a lighter-regulated route than licensed mobile sportsbooks, who face a much higher tax and fee burden.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":17176,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:52:52.492Z"},"OH-HB166":{"summary":"HB 166 amends Ohio Revised Code 718.01 and 718.03, the municipal income tax definitions and withholding provisions, so that foreign temporary agricultural workers become subject to municipal income tax and the withholding rules for them change. The text provided shows the change in 718.01(R)(2)(g), which adds back to \"qualifying wages\" any compensation paid to a foreign agricultural worker lawfully admitted on a temporary basis to perform agricultural labor as defined in IRC section 3121. The text is cut off partway through 718.01 and the amended 718.03 is not included, so the withholding changes cannot be verified. The bill does not concern wagering, though the amended section restates existing definitions that cover gambling winnings.","mechanics":["718.01(R)(2)(g) adds a new item to the amounts added to wages in computing \"qualifying wages\": compensation paid to a foreign agricultural worker lawfully admitted to the U.S. temporarily to perform agricultural labor, as defined in IRC section 3121.","The title says the bill also modifies withholding rules for these workers in 718.03, but that section is not in the text provided.","No rates, thresholds, effective date, or enforcement provisions appear in the text provided.","Existing 718.01(B)(4) already defines municipal \"income\" to include lottery, sweepstakes, gambling and sports winnings, with a loss and expense deduction for professional gamblers. The bill does not appear to change this.","Existing 718.01(X) defines \"other payer\" to include casino operators and video lottery terminal sales agents, and 718.01(S) excludes gambling winnings from \"intangible income\". Neither appears to be altered.","Status: introduced and referred to House Ways and Means on 2023-05-09."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"This bill is an agricultural-labor tax measure and is not comparable to the listed sports-wagering bills, such as the Alaska mobile wagering bills (AK HB145, AK SB194), the Georgia bills (GA HB380, GA SB172, GA SB57) or the Hawaii bills (HI HB 1308, HI HB 2570). The closest overlap is Illinois HB4171, which would bar home rule units from taxing sports wagering. Ohio's chapter 718 already treats gambling and sports winnings as municipal income, and HB166 does not change that. Iowa HSB 91 and SF 605 address state income tax withholding on sports wagering winnings, which is a different subject from HB166's withholding changes for agricultural workers.","watch":"Only the 718.01 definitions are shown, so confirm that the full amended 718.03 and any other changes do not touch the withholding duties of casino operators, video lottery terminal sales agents, or other payers on gambling winnings.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":63874,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:53:01.070Z"},"OH-HB33":{"summary":"Ohio's biennial operating budget (Am. Sub. HB 33, 135th G.A.) makes appropriations for July 1, 2023 to June 30, 2025, levies taxes, and amends or enacts hundreds of Revised Code sections across state government. The supplied text is cut off within the first amendatory section (101.01), so the substance of the gaming changes cannot be read. The title shows that the lottery, casino-control and sports-gaming chapters are among those amended.","mechanics":["Operating appropriations take effect July 4, 2023, for the biennium July 1, 2023 to June 30, 2025. Other provisions are generally effective October 3, 2023, and some have special effective dates.","The title lists Chapter 3775 (sports gaming) sections 3775.01 and 3775.07 among those amended. The supplied text does not show what changed.","The title also lists casino-control sections 3772.01, 3772.031, 3772.04, 3772.11, 3772.12, 3772.13 and 3772.131 as amended. The versions of 3772.13 and 3772.131 scheduled to take effect December 29, 2023 are amended to continue the changes on and after that date.","Lottery sections 3770.03, 3770.071 and 3770.99 are listed as amended. The substance is not visible in the supplied text.","The title lists many tax sections as amended, including income tax (5747), commercial activity tax (5751), sales and use tax (5739, 5741) and cigarette and other tobacco tax (5743). I cannot confirm whether any of these change wagering-related taxation."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. HB 33 is an omnibus biennial budget, so any sports-betting or iGaming changes sit among hundreds of unrelated amendments, and the supplied text does not show their substance.","watch":"Pull the enrolled text of the amendments to R.C. 3775.01 and 3775.07 and 3772.01 to 3772.131, and check for changes to licensing, definitions, enforcement or the December 29, 2023 continuation versions, because the title alone does not show whether they are substantive.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":249160,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:53:03.416Z"},"OH-HB406":{"summary":"Ohio HB 406, the Ohio Blockchain Basics Act, is a digital-asset bill, not a gaming bill. As shown in the text provided, it bars the General Assembly and local governments from imposing fees or taxes on digital assets used as a payment method, and it exempts certain capital gains on digital-asset payments from municipal income tax. The title also covers digital asset mining limits, state income tax exemptions and a retirement-system investment review, but those provisions (new R.C. 1352.01-1352.04, 1315.01 and 5747.01) fall outside the supplied excerpt, which cuts off in the middle of R.C. 718.01.","mechanics":["New R.C. 101.88 says the General Assembly shall not enact a bill that taxes or charges digital assets used as a payment method, based on that use, on their sale, use or consumption, or on receipts from their sale. This is a self-restraint on the legislature, and \"digital asset\" takes its meaning from R.C. 1352.01.","The same prohibition is added for charter counties (R.C. 301.30(B)), limited-home-rule townships (R.C. 504.04(B)(9)) and municipal corporations (R.C. 715.013(B)(2)).","Each of the four provisions has a safe harbor: a charge is still allowed if it would apply to the same transaction made in U.S. legal tender. Digital-asset payments are therefore taxed no more heavily than cash, but are not exempted from generally applicable taxes.","R.C. 718.01(C)(2)(b) is amended so that municipalities that taxed intangible income as of 1988 cannot tax capital gains from the sale of a digital asset used to pay for goods or services. The carve-out applies only when the payment does not exceed the deduction threshold in R.C. 5747.01(A)(44), which is not in the supplied text.","The existing definitions in R.C. 718.01 are left in place: \"income\" still includes gambling and sports winnings and prizes (division (B)(4)), and \"intangible income\" still excludes gambling winnings (division (S)). The excerpt shows no gaming-specific changes and no enforcement mechanism or effective date."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills is comparable, because they are all sports wagering, fantasy contest or internet gaming measures (for example AK HB145, GA HB380, IA SF2427, IL HB2239). The nearest structural parallel is IL HB4171, which denies home rule units the power to tax sports wagering, whereas OH HB406 preempts local charges on digital assets as a payment method. Gambling appears only as pre-existing definitional text in the R.C. 718.01 sections being amended.","watch":"A lawyer for operators should check whether the \"digital assets used as a method of payment for goods and services\" language could be read to reach crypto deposits or payouts, and note that the municipal income tax definitions still capture gambling winnings.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":146233,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:53:05.941Z"},"OH-SB74":{"summary":"Ohio Sub. S.B. 74 is a Treasurer of State housekeeping and modernization bill. It moves state treasury payment and record-keeping toward electronic processes, repeals a large block of obsolete Revised Code sections (sinking fund, chapters 129, 135 and 144 provisions), and makes conforming changes across many tax chapters. The portion of the text provided contains no sports wagering, iGaming, or collectibles provisions.","mechanics":["Sec. 113.12: the treasurer must provide the director of budget and management with electronic records of all warrants paid on a daily basis, replacing the monthly surrender of paid warrants and receipt practice.","Sec. 113.22 (new): creates the treasurer's information technology reserve fund, funded by unexpended amounts from the securities lending program fund (sec. 135.47) and the sec. 3366.05 account, and limited to hardware, software, and contract services for the treasurer's office.","Sec. 131.01: expands the definition of \"warrant\" to cover payment instruments such as paper warrants, stored value cards, direct deposit and electronic benefit transfer, and adds a definition of \"stored value card.\"","Sec. 113.40: the board of deposit's rules for accepting credit/debit and other electronic payments of state expenses (including taxes) are retained, but the notice of intent to request proposals moves from newspaper advertisement to electronic publication on a state agency website.","Sec. 113.41 is renumbered 125.903 and the state real-property database moves from the treasurer to the department of administrative services. Sec. 125.901 drops the treasurer from the geographic information council, reducing it from 15 to 14 members.","Repeals sections 129.02 to 129.76 (various), 135.101 to 135.106, 135.61 to 135.97 (various) and 144.01 to 144.07. It also amends many tax sections (e.g., 5727, 5733, 5735, 5739, 5743, 5745, 5747) that fall outside the portion of the text provided."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills is comparable. AK HB145 and SB194, GA SB57, HB380 and SB172, HI HB 1308 and HB 2570, IL HB3080 and HB3159, and IA SF 605 and HSB 91 all regulate, tax, or withhold on sports wagering or iGaming, while OH SB74 as provided imposes no gaming tax, license, or withholding rule. Its overlap with those bills is limited to general state tax-payment and treasury administration, which would apply to operators only as ordinary taxpayers.","watch":"Check the truncated tax-chapter amendments (especially chapters 5743 and 5747, and any electronic payment or remittance mandates) for provisions that could reach gaming or winnings-related tax payments, since nothing in the portion provided touches wagering.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Engrossed","text_chars":186527,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:53:09.128Z"},"PA-HB1300":{"summary":"HB1300 is the 2023-24 Pennsylvania budget-implementation bill, enacted as Act 34 of 2023. It amends The Fiscal Code across dozens of unrelated subjects, including housing repair, school funds, mental health, tenant protections and special-fund transfers. The enacting title also lists changes to the Pennsylvania Race Horse Development Fund, a repeal of the Enhanced Revenue Collection Account, and the Sports Tourism and Marketing Account. The bill text supplied cuts off at Section 101-F.1 (tenant protections), before any of those gaming-related provisions.","mechanics":["Section 135-C continues the Whole-Home Repairs Program: grants and forgivable loans of up to $50,000 per unit, no more than one applicant funded per county, and up to 4% each for administrative costs for the department and grantees.","Section 148-C: the Department of Education must notify local education agencies about declined or unclaimed ARPA and CAA 2021 allocations. The Budget Secretary then moves the aggregate amount, capped at $26,000,000, into a restricted account for grant performance monitoring.","Section 164-C allocates adult mental health money: $34,000,000 for workforce, $31,500,000 for criminal justice and public safety, and $34,500,000 for mental health services. Most grant programs carry administrative fees of no more than 2%.","Section 164-C(f) requires the Legislative Budget and Finance Committee to report on the funded initiatives by June 30, 2027.","Article I-F.1 (tenant protections) begins with Section 101-F.1, which makes it unlawful to use rebates as part of a lease or agreement. The supplied text ends mid-sentence in that section.","The title lists amendments to the Race Horse Development Fund distributions, repeal of the Enhanced Revenue Collection Account, and changes to the Sports Tourism and Marketing Account. The supplied text does not reach those sections, so their terms cannot be confirmed from it."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No other states' bills are listed for comparison. On its own, this is an omnibus budget code bill rather than a gaming bill. Any wagering-related content appears only in the title (Race Horse Development Fund, Sports Tourism and Marketing Account, Enhanced Revenue Collection Account), and the supplied text does not include those provisions.","watch":"Read the full enrolled text of the Fiscal Code sections on Race Horse Development Fund distributions, the Enhanced Revenue Collection Account repeal and the Sports Tourism and Marketing Account to see whether they change gaming-revenue allocations or any operator-facing obligations, since the supplied text omits them.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":163598,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:53:13.581Z"},"RI-H6048":{"summary":"RI H6048 amends R.I. Gen. Laws § 42-61.2-3.3 (sports wagering regulation) by adding new subsection (a)(9). After July 1, 2026, the lottery division may not renew any existing sports-wagering vendor contract, and before those contracts expire it must issue an open invitation to applicants and award at least five individual vendor contracts. It is aimed at breaking up Rhode Island's single-vendor sports wagering structure, in which the state operates the market through the lottery division and a hosting-facility model.","mechanics":["Adds new § 42-61.2-3.3(a)(9): after July 1, 2026 the division shall not renew any existing sports-wagering vendor contract.","Before any existing contract expires, the division must issue an open invitation to applicants for sports-wagering vendor contracts.","The division must then award at least five (5) individual sports-wagering vendor contracts, a minimum number with no stated maximum.","Savings language: the subsection does not direct the commission to award a contract to an unqualified vendor. The text says 'commission' while the section otherwise refers to the division or division director, an ambiguity.","The bill sets no tax rate, fee, application criteria, selection standard, or contract term. It is silent on online versus retail scope and on how the five contracts relate to the existing hosting-facility structure.","Section 2: the act takes effect upon passage, but the non-renewal obligation operates only after July 1, 2026. Last action 2025-05-01: held for further study in committee."],"issue_tags":["licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike the broad regulatory and tax bills elsewhere, such as AK HB145/SB194, HI HB1308/HB2570/SB1569 and GA HB380/SB57, H6048 creates no new market or tax and makes one narrow change to vendor procurement in an existing state-run monopoly. Its closest analogues are DE HB365, which sets up a competitive mobile market under the Lottery Director, and IL HB2878, which sets license fees and removes limits on master license numbers. Unlike those, H6048 sets no fees, criteria, or revenue share, and it mandates a floor of five vendors rather than a cap. IL HB3276 (lottery sports wagering pilot) is another lottery-run model, but it does not address vendor competition.","watch":"Operators should watch whether this narrow, unspecified procurement mandate gets amended to add selection criteria, fees, revenue-share terms, or a hosting-facility or online structure, and whether the incumbent's contract is effectively forced into open competition with at least five awards.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":8677,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:53:15.483Z"},"RI-H6241":{"summary":"This is a House condolence resolution on the death of Senate President Dominick J. Ruggerio. It creates no law and regulates no one. Its recitals mention his gaming record: advocacy for a Narragansett Indian Tribe casino in Providence, a study commission on state-run sports betting, championing sports betting legalization, and pushing the legislature to authorize remote iGaming in 2023.","mechanics":["Two operative RESOLVED clauses: the House expresses condolences to the family, and the Secretary of State transmits certified copies to Charles Ruggerio, Amanda Fallon and Lisa Aceto and their families.","The recitals credit Ruggerio with seeking a study commission on state-run sports betting and with championing the legalization of sports betting.","The recitals say he successfully pushed the legislature to authorize remote iGaming in 2023.","The text has no tax rates, licensing terms, definitions, enforcement provisions or effective dates, and it amends no statute.","The status shows the House read and passed it on April 22, 2025, the day it was introduced. Passage by one chamber of a simple resolution does not make it law."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. On its own, this is a ceremonial resolution. The only gaming content is a biographical recital on Ruggerio's role in Rhode Island's sports betting and 2023 iGaming authorization, with no operative provision.","watch":"Nothing in this resolution affects operators, but the loss of the Senate President who drove sports betting and iGaming authorization could change the Senate's leadership and gaming-policy dynamics, so watch who succeeds him.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":6338,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:53:19.396Z"},"RI-H6076":{"summary":"RI H6076 adds a new chapter 5-97 to the General Laws creating a licensing and tax regime for paid online fantasy contests, administered by the Department of Business Regulation (DBR). It declares that fantasy contests are not gambling (§ 5-97-5(a)), requires operators to be licensed, and imposes a 10% tax on adjusted gross fantasy contest receipts. It also sets consumer-protection, integrity, audit and self-exclusion requirements. The bill was held for further study in House Finance on 2025-05-13.","mechanics":["Definition of fantasy contest (§ 5-97-2(6)): entry fee, prizes known in advance, outcomes reflecting skill and based on statistical performance of multiple athletes, and no outcome based on a single team's score, point spread, or a single athlete's performance in a single event. This definition would exclude pick'em-style single-performance products.","Licensing (§§ 5-97-4, 5-97-6): DBR license required, with fingerprint-based national background checks and disclosure of 10%+ owners. Initial fee is the lesser of 15% of prior-year adjusted gross receipts or $10,000 for existing operators, and $5,000 plus a supplemental fee for new entrants. Licenses last 4 years, with renewal at 1% of adjusted gross receipts for the preceding four years. Existing operators may keep operating if they apply within 90 days after rules are adopted.","Tax (§ 5-97-9): 10% of adjusted gross fantasy contest receipts, which are entry fees less prizes, multiplied by the Rhode Island location percentage. Tax is calculated on an accrual basis, with monthly returns and payment due by the last day of each month, beginning with the fiscal year starting July 1, 2025.","Player protection and integrity (§§ 5-97-3, 5-97-5, 5-97-10): minimum age of 18, geolocation limiting entries to persons physically in Rhode Island, identity verification, and anti-fraud, anti-money-laundering and anti-collusion controls. Prohibited participants include self-excluded persons and athletes, coaches and similar staff of the same governing body. Operators must notify DBR of integrity breaches. Self-exclusion is integrated into DBR's existing program, and prizes won by self-excluded persons are forfeited and donated to problem gaming programs. Responsible gaming statements are required on all marketing.","Regulatory limits (§ 5-97-5(e)): DBR may not regulate the rules or administration of an individual contest, its statistical makeup, or the operator's digital platform, and may not restrict contest types by labelling them sports wagering. Licensees are protected from early license termination solely because of later rule amendments (§ 5-97-5(f)).","Audits and rulemaking (§§ 5-97-3, 5-97-7): DBR must adopt rules within 180 days of the effective date, and may use emergency rules. Applicants must obtain an independent CPA audit and an annual compliance verification. The first audit report is not due until 24 months after licensure. The act takes effect on passage."],"issue_tags":["licensing","tax","integrity","advertising","legalize"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. On its own, the bill is unusual in expressly barring DBR from regulating contest rules, statistical makeup or platforms, and from recasting contests as sports wagering (§ 5-97-5(e)). The 10% tax is paired with a license fee pegged to the lesser of 15% of prior-year receipts or $10,000, and a 1% four-year renewal fee. The bill covers only entry-fee fantasy contests and does not touch sports wagering or iGaming.","watch":"Operators of pick'em or prop-style products should check whether the § 5-97-2(6)(vii) exclusion of single-athlete and single-event outcomes, combined with the ban on DBR labelling contests as sports wagering in § 5-97-5(e), would leave those products outside the safe harbor and exposed under Rhode Island's existing gambling and sports wagering law.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":21640,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:53:22.707Z"},"PA-SB1150":{"summary":"Pennsylvania SB1150, enacted as Act 89 of 2024, is mainly a higher-education bill. It creates the Grow Pennsylvania Scholarship Grant Program ($5,000 per year, with a work-in-state requirement), a Grow Pennsylvania Merit Scholarship for nonresident students at State System universities, and a new Article XX-M on college athlete name, image and likeness (NIL) compensation. The only gambling content is a bar on athletes earning NIL compensation from casinos and gambling, including sports betting, the lottery and online or mobile betting. The bill does not otherwise regulate wagering or operators.","mechanics":["Sec. 2006-M(D)(3): a college student athlete may not earn NIL compensation in connection with a person, company or organization related to or associated with the development, production, distribution, wholesaling or retailing of 'casinos and gambling, including sports betting, the lottery and betting in connection with video games, online games and mobile devices.' The same subsection also bars adult entertainment, alcohol, tobacco and e-smoking products, prescription pharmaceuticals and controlled substances.","Sec. 2003-M(A)-(C): athletes may earn NIL compensation, and institutions, conferences and the NCAA may not block it or retaliate against institutions or athletes for permitted activity. Sec. 2006-M(E) lets an institution bar NIL activity that conflicts with existing institutional sponsorships or institutional values, and requires institutions to adopt NIL policies.","Sec. 2003-M(D): anyone producing a college team jersey, video game or trading cards for profit 'shall make a royalty payment' to each college student athlete whose name, image, likeness or other identifiable feature is used. The text shown does not set a rate, a payment mechanism or an enforcement method.","Sec. 2004-M: athletes may use professional representation, either an athlete agent under 5 Pa.C.S. Ch. 35 or a Pennsylvania-admitted attorney. Agent contracts must state that the athlete is not obligated to use the agent for services beyond NIL representation.","Sec. 2052-L: Grow Pennsylvania Scholarship Grant of $5,000 per academic year (or less where federal and state aid exceeds educational cost). Recipients must work in an in-demand occupation in Pennsylvania for 12 months per grant year, with prorated repayment. Sec. 2022-A: the merit scholarship for nonresidents (starting with the 2025-26 freshman class) waives the resident/nonresident tuition differential, requires a 2.5 GPA, and carries a similar work-or-repay agreement.","The text provided is cut off in the middle of Sec. 2006-M(F) (NIL contract disclosure). It does not show any penalty or enforcement provisions for Article XX-M, or the amended bill's effective date; the original printing said 60 days."],"issue_tags":["advertising"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"Unlike CT HB05229, which restricts gaming advertising on campuses, or MD HB465/HB518, which put college-athlete protections into the gaming statute, PA SB1150 regulates only the athlete's side. It bars NIL compensation from gambling-related companies and imposes no duties on licensed operators. It also does not address college prop bets, as NJ A4905/S3080, NC H828/H967 and LA SB354 do. It is one of several state NIL bills listed (KY SB3, HI HB2384/SB3263, MS SB2517, NE LB370, NH HB312, IL HB0307), but its gambling carve-out sits inside an education omnibus with scholarship programs. Its mandatory royalty to athletes for profit-making trading cards, jerseys and video games is also unusual among the listed bills.","watch":"Operators and their marketing partners should note that Sec. 2006-M(D)(3) makes any NIL deal with a sports-betting, casino, lottery or online/mobile betting business impermissible for Pennsylvania college athletes, so affiliate and sponsorship arrangements need screening, and the truncated text leaves open who enforces the bar and what the consequences are.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":53707,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:53:23.717Z"},"RI-H6388":{"summary":"RI H6388 adds R.I. Gen. Laws § 42-61.2-19, directing the Division (Lottery) to permit Twin River and Twin River-Tiverton to livestream live-dealer gaming activity (dice, cards, roulette wheels) from a Rhode Island hosting facility to other jurisdictions where external online table games are lawful. Players, wallets, servers, wagers and payouts all sit in the external jurisdiction, so the activity is a live-studio supply model, not Rhode Island-facing wagering. The Division screens each external jurisdiction, and the licensees bear its costs and indemnify the state.","mechanics":["Defines 'external game play activities,' requiring that outcomes come from livestreamed images, e-wallets be kept in the external jurisdiction, wagers be placed, accepted and processed on servers there, prizes be paid there, and external play not affect Rhode Island online table game activity (§ 42-61.2-19(a)(1)).","'External jurisdiction' means any non-Rhode Island jurisdiction that permits such play and is authorized by the Division, but excludes any jurisdiction that competes with the Division's revenue-generating operations, as determined in the Division's sole discretion (a)(2).","'Gaming-related activities' are Division-approved (sole discretion) livestreamed acts by a live person in a hosting facility, sent through a 'live studio RGS,' e.g. rolling dice, dealing cards, spinning roulette wheels (a)(4)-(5).","Subsection (b) overrides conflicting law, including chapter 19 of title 11, chapters 1-11 of title 41, and chapters 61, 61.2 and 61.3 of title 42, and directs the Division to permit livestreaming by Twin River, Twin River-Tiverton, or both.","The Division decides whether external play is permitted in the target jurisdiction and sets conditions; the licensees reimburse its costs and attorneys' fees (b)(1) and indemnify and hold the state harmless for claims relating directly or indirectly to the livestreaming (b)(2).","Permission cannot be assigned, resold, pledged, licensed or transferred except to Bally's Corporation or its affiliates, using a control-based affiliate definition (b)(3); effective on passage (Section 3). The bill sets no tax rate."],"issue_tags":["licensing","legalize"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"No listed bill is comparable. CT SB01464 (multijurisdictional peer-to-peer casino games via governor-signed agreements) and IL HB2239 / HB3080 (Internet Gaming Act) deal with online casino gaming, but they authorize or regulate play by in-state or compacted players. RI H6388 instead exports a live-studio feed to other jurisdictions with no in-state player activity. It is also unusual in naming the two Bally's-operated facilities and restricting transfer of the permission to Bally's and its affiliates, and in giving the Division sole discretion to exclude competing jurisdictions.","watch":"A lawyer for operators should watch that the permission is effectively limited to Twin River and Bally's affiliates, that the Division can exclude jurisdictions in its sole discretion, and that third-party operators or RGS suppliers in the target jurisdictions may have to contract through Bally's and bear the indemnity and cost-reimbursement terms.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":6897,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:53:24.216Z"},"RI-S1017":{"summary":"RI S1017 (Sub A), signed 2025-06-30, adds R.I. Gen. Laws § 42-61.2-19 and directs the Division (Lottery) to permit Twin River and Twin River-Tiverton to livestream live-dealer gaming-related activities (dice, cards, roulette wheels) from their Rhode Island facilities to external jurisdictions. Players in those jurisdictions play external online table games under local law, with wallets, wagers, servers and payouts all located outside Rhode Island. The act overrides conflicting gambling statutes and takes effect on passage.","mechanics":["§ 42-61.2-19(b): 'Notwithstanding' contrary law, including ch. 19 of title 11, chs. 1-11 of title 41 and chs. 61, 61.2 and 61.3 of title 42, the division 'is hereby directed to permit' Twin River, Twin River-Tiverton, or both to livestream gaming-related activities to external jurisdictions.","Definition of 'external game play activities' (a)(1): outcomes derive from livestreamed images; e-wallets, servers, wagering and prize payments are all in the external jurisdiction; external play must not affect Rhode Island online table game activity.","'External jurisdiction' (a)(2) means any non-RI jurisdiction that permits external game play and is authorized by the division, excluding any jurisdiction the division, in its sole discretion, determines competes with its revenue-generating operations.","'Gaming-related activities' (a)(4) are those the division approves in its sole discretion, conducted by a live person in a hosting facility and transmitted via a 'live studio RGS' (a)(5).","(b)(1): the division decides whether external play is permitted in each external jurisdiction and on what conditions; the licensees reimburse the division's costs and fees, including attorneys' fees.","(b)(2): licensees must indemnify and hold the state harmless for claims, penalties, fines and fees related directly or indirectly to the livestreaming.","(b)(3): the permission cannot be assigned, resold, pledged, licensed or transferred to anyone other than Bally's Corporation (a Delaware corporation) or its affiliates; 'affiliate' and 'control' are defined."],"issue_tags":["licensing","legalize"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"None of the listed bills is comparable. They deal with domestic mobile sports wagering (AK HB145/SB194, DE HB365, GA SB57), internet gaming (IL HB2239/HB3080), taxes, advertising or consumer protection (CT HB05229, HB05269), and none addresses exporting live-dealer studio streams to other jurisdictions. The closest conceptual link is CT SB01464, which would let the Governor enter multijurisdictional internet gaming agreements for peer-to-peer casino games, but that is about pooling players, not hosting a live studio. S1017 is unusual in being a statutory mandate to the regulator that names the incumbent licensee (Bally's) and limits transfer of the permission to Bally's and its affiliates.","watch":"The permission is non-transferable except to Bally's and its affiliates, and the division has sole discretion over approved activities and over excluding competing jurisdictions, so third-party live-dealer studio or platform operators can take part only through a Bally's arrangement and must expect cost-reimbursement and indemnity obligations.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Comm Sub","text_chars":6994,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:53:27.904Z"},"RI-S995":{"summary":"This is a Senate condolence resolution on the death of Senate President Dominick J. Ruggerio. It creates no law, regulation, tax or licensing requirement. Its recitals mention his gaming record, but the resolution does nothing to change gaming law.","mechanics":["Operative clause 1 expresses the Senate's sympathy and condolences to Ruggerio's family and friends.","Operative clause 2 directs the Secretary of State to send certified copies to Charles Ruggerio, Amanda Fallon and Lisa Aceto and their families.","The recitals say he led efforts to let the Narragansett Indian Tribe build a Providence casino, requested a study commission on state-run sports betting, championed sports betting legalization, and pushed the legislature to authorize remote iGaming in 2023.","The text contains no rates, definitions, covered entities, effective dates or enforcement provisions."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills are listed from other states. This is a ceremonial resolution with no substantive gaming provisions, so it has no regulatory counterpart. Its only link to the sector is the historical recital of Ruggerio's role in Rhode Island sports betting legalization and the 2023 remote iGaming authorization.","watch":"Nothing in this resolution affects operators, but the recitals confirm that Ruggerio, a key Senate backer of Rhode Island's sports betting and iGaming framework, is gone, which may shift future Senate leadership dynamics on gaming policy.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":6258,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:53:28.559Z"},"RI-S748":{"summary":"RI S0748 Sub A amends R.I. Gen. Laws § 42-61.2-3.3, the sports wagering regulation section, to end renewal of existing sports-wagering vendor contracts and force a competitive reprocurement. Before the current contracts expire, the lottery division must issue an open invitation to applicants and award multiple individual vendor contracts. The bill does not change tax rates, licensing standards or the existing rulemaking framework.","mechanics":["New § 42-61.2-3.3(a)(9): the division shall not renew any existing sports-wagering vendor contract, and before any existing contract expires it must issue an open invitation to applicants for sports-wagering vendor contracts.","Number of awards: the operative text says not less than three (3) but not more than five (5) individual vendor contracts; the Legislative Council explanation says not less than two (2) but not more than five (5). The text and explanation conflict.","Cutoff date is also inconsistent: the operative text says renewals stop 'after January 31, 2026', while the explanation (and the summary) says July 1, 2026.","Savings clause: the subsection is not to be read as directing the commission to award a contract to an unqualified vendor, so the division keeps qualification discretion. The text says 'commission' where the rest of the subsection refers to the 'division'.","Section 2: effective upon passage. Existing text of § 42-61.2-3.3 (rulemaking on bet types, online account controls, hosting facility surveillance, under-18 and problem gambling standards) is otherwise unchanged.","No provisions on tax or revenue share, application fees, selection criteria, contract term, protest or appeal rights, or transition of existing vendors."],"issue_tags":["licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Rhode Island's bill is narrow and procurement-focused, whereas most listed bills set up or rewrite whole regulatory regimes. The closest parallels are DE HB365, which creates a competitive mobile sports wagering market under the Lottery Director, and IL HB2878, which restructures master sports wagering license numbers and fees. IL HB3276 (lottery sports wagering pilot) and the Georgia lottery-run bills (GA HB380, GA SB57, GA SB386) also use a lottery model. Unlike those, RI S0748 only caps the number of vendor awards at the existing lottery-run structure and states no fees or tax.","watch":"Watch the unresolved conflicts between the operative text (three to five awards, January 31, 2026 cutoff) and the explanation (two to five awards, July 1, 2026), and whether the division's selection criteria could displace the incumbent vendor or vendors, since the bill gives no criteria or transition rules.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":8784,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:53:30.533Z"},"SD-HB1203":{"summary":"HB1203 amends the Deadwood gaming act (SDCL ch. 42-7B) to authorize pari-mutuel historical horse racing (HHR) machines in Deadwood retail licensed establishments. It carves HHR out of the existing 8% and 1% gaming taxes and imposes a separate 9% tax on HHR 'net winnings', with its own revenue split that sends part of the proceeds to racing funds. It does not touch sports wagering, but it amends shared definitions and the gaming device cap.","mechanics":["Sections 1, 2 and 4 add 'pari-mutuel historical horse race wagering' to the authorized games, to the definitions of 'gaming', 'gaming device', 'operator' and 'route operator', and to the games whose rules require commission approval and posting.","Section 5 (§ 42-7B-16) excludes HHR machines from the 30-device cap per retail license and sets a separate cap of four HHR machines per retail license. Section 6 ties the floor-plan limit in § 42-7B-18 to that section.","Sections 7 and 8 exempt HHR wagering from the 8% gaming tax (§ 42-7B-28) and the additional 1% tax (§ 42-7B-28.1).","Section 9 imposes a new 9% tax on 'net winnings' of HHR wagering. Net winnings are defined as 'the total payout from a pari-mutuel betting pool in a historical horse race', which reads oddly because it looks like a payout measure rather than retained revenue.","Section 12 distributes the 9% tax: 45% to the special racing revolving fund, 5% to the South Dakota-bred racing fund, and 50% under the § 42-7B-48.1 formula. Section 10 removes this tax from executive secretary disbursement authority, and § 42-7B-48.1 is made subject to section 12.","Section 3 gives the commission rulemaking authority over HHR machines. The bill has no emergency clause or stated effective date, and no technical standards, supplier licensing or pool/takeout rules for HHR."],"issue_tags":["tax","licensing","legalize"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"None of the listed bills is comparable. They deal with mobile sports wagering, iGaming, lottery or fantasy contests (e.g., AK HB145/SB194, GA SB57, HI HB 1308, IA HF673, CT HB05229). SD HB1203 is a land-based, Deadwood-only expansion into historical horse racing machines, a category none of them addresses. Its unusual features are a machine cap of four per retail license outside the general 30-device cap and a dedicated 9% tax with earmarks for racing funds.","watch":"Watch the vague 'net winnings' tax base (total pool payout rather than adjusted gross proceeds), the unspecified licensing and technical standards for HHR suppliers, and whether the amended definitions or the separate machine cap could affect operators and route operators already licensed in Deadwood, including sports wagering licensees.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":20866,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:53:39.580Z"},"SC-S993":{"summary":"S0993 rewrites South Carolina's intercollegiate athlete NIL chapter (Title 59, Chapter 158) so that institutions and their officers can themselves identify, solicit and facilitate NIL deals for enrolled athletes, directly or through third parties. It also limits institutional liability for routine athletic decisions, bars athletic associations and conferences from penalizing institutions for complying, and shields NIL contract records from public disclosure. Based on the bill caption, it appears to delete the current 59-158-40(B)(2) ban on athletes earning NIL compensation for endorsing gambling, including sports betting, but the supplied text lacks strike/underline markup, so that deletion is inferred.","mechanics":["Revised 59-158-20: an institution or its officer, trustee, director or employee may identify, create, solicit, facilitate and enable NIL opportunities for currently enrolled athletes, and may let athletes use its trademarks and facilities. The institution may not take compensation or fees from the athlete for facilitating NIL.","Revised 59-158-40: institutions and their employees, including coaches, are not liable for damages to an athlete's NIL earning ability from decisions routinely taken in intercollegiate athletics. Associations, conferences and similar bodies may not enforce rules that penalize institutions or athletes for activity the chapter authorizes, or block institutions from using third-party NIL agents. The current 59-158-40(B)(2) endorsement ban on tobacco, alcohol, illegal activities and gambling including sports betting appears to be removed, per the caption.","Revised 59-158-30: an NIL contract may not extend beyond the athlete's eligibility to participate in the institution's intercollegiate athletics program.","Revised 59-158-50: institutional records of NIL contract terms are not public records under 30-4-20(C) unless the requester is a party, and an institution cannot be compelled to disclose them to a collegiate association, conference or similar body.","Revised 59-158-60 and 59-102-20: if Chapter 158 conflicts with Chapter 102, Chapter 158 governs, and athlete agents must comply with the federal Sports Agent Responsibility and Trust Act (15 U.S.C. 7801-7807). The 'athlete agent' definition in 59-102-20 is amended to exclude an institution, its employees, or a family member acting under Chapter 158.","Section 8 amends 59-102-100(H), which currently caps agent compensation on NIL agency contracts at 10% of the NIL contract. The caption says the amendment removes a compensation provision, which implies the cap is deleted. Section 9 repeals 59-158-70 (disclosure and revocation-period rules) and 59-158-80 (governing law and federal compliance). The act takes effect on the Governor's approval (Section 10)."],"issue_tags":["advertising"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"This is an NIL bill, not a gambling bill, and the only gambling reference is the endorsement ban in the existing text that the bill appears to strike. The listed NIL measures are GA HB1276 and HB383 (high school NIL), HI HB 2384 and SB3263 (University of Hawaii NIL policies), and DE HB314 (student athlete protection). AR HB1861 is the only listed bill that links NIL to gaming, and it does so by regulating NIL games. SC S0993 is distinctive for authorizing institution-facilitated NIL statewide, shielding associations' enforcement, and apparently dropping the sports-betting endorsement bar. The listing does not show whether the other states' NIL bills contain a comparable bar.","watch":"If the caption is right that 59-158-40(B)(2) is deleted, South Carolina would no longer bar athletes from sports betting endorsement deals, so confirm the strike markup in the enrolled text and check whether operators' advertising rules or NCAA and league policies still restrict such deals.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":20003,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:53:45.617Z"},"SC-H3749":{"summary":"SC H3749 would legalize interactive (online and mobile) sports wagering in South Carolina under a new Chapter 23 of Title 52, regulated by a newly created South Carolina Equine and Sports Wagering Commission. It sets a 10% privilege tax on adjusted gross sports wagering receipts, caps the number of operator licenses, and adds bettor-eligibility, official-league-data, registration and responsible-gaming rules. The title also covers an equine industry grant program, pari-mutuel wagering and a new Section 16-19-135 carving sports wagering and fantasy contests out of existing gambling law, but the supplied text cuts off at Section 52-23-360(D), so those provisions could not be reviewed.","mechanics":["Tax (52-23-230): 10% privilege tax on adjusted gross sports wagering receipts. Promotional costs, voided wagers, federal excise tax and uncollectibles (capped at 2%) are deductible, and negative months carry forward. Returns are due by the 15th of the following month. Revenue is split 80% general fund, 15% to local governments per capita for infrastructure, and 5% to the Department of Mental Health for problem-gambling programs. Licensees are exempt from any other state or local tax. Section 1 appropriates $1 million to the Department of Revenue to administer the tax.","Licensing (52-23-240): the commission issues a license to each qualifying 'designated operator' (a major-league team, a PGA Tour tournament host or a NASCAR touring-race host, or their designees) plus up to six licenses to other operators. The license fee is $500,000, the term is three years, and the commission must decide within 60 days. An applicant must show proof of active operation in at least five states. An applicant already licensed in at least five other jurisdictions can request a temporary license on payment of the $500,000 fee. Principal owners holding 10% or more face national criminal background checks and 'clear and convincing' good-character and financial-stability showings.","Suppliers (52-23-250): a supplier license costs $10,000 and runs three years, with temporary licenses available. 'Sports wagering supplier' is defined narrowly as a provider of critical services, namely geolocation and KYC, that does not directly accept wagers.","Official league data (52-23-290): none is required for tier one wagers (final score or outcome, placed pre-game). For tier two wagers, operators must use only official league data within 60 days after a sports governing body notifies the commission, unless the data is not offered on commercially reasonable terms or no feed exists. Sports governing bodies need no license to share in handle through commercial agreements.","Operator conduct (52-23-300 to 350): licensees may not allow minors to bet, extend credit, advertise directly to minors, or offer wagers on youth sports or anything other than sporting events the commission approves. They must offer self-exclusion with time and amount limits and keep a commission-maintained registry. Registration and identity verification are required before any deposit or wager, with one account per bettor. Geolocation is required and servers used to take and pay out bets must be located in South Carolina, though cloud computing is permitted. Accounts may be funded by bank transfer, debit or credit card, or online payment systems. The commission must also prohibit wagering on injuries, penalties and similar forms contrary to public policy (52-23-360(D)).","Bettor restrictions and enforcement: Section 52-23-280 bars insiders, athletes, officials, licensee owners and employees, and people with nonpublic information from betting. Violations are misdemeanors escalating from a $100 fine or 30 days to a $1,000 fine or 90 days, tried in magistrates court. The commission must adopt rules within 180 days of the effective date, with temporary rules allowed, and is subject to a Legislative Audit Council performance audit beginning December 2023 and every three years after."],"issue_tags":["legalize","tax","licensing","integrity","advertising"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Among the listed bills, the closest comparators are the Georgia sports betting bills (GA HB380, HB 686, SB172, SB386 and SB57), the Alaska mobile wagering bills (AK HB145 and SB194) and the Hawaii bills (HI HB 1308, HB 2570 and SB1569). Georgia's bills, by their titles, run the market through the Georgia Lottery Corporation, and Georgia has also filed constitutional-amendment resolutions (GA HR450 and others). SC H3749 instead creates a standalone commission, mandates an affirmative 'designated operator' license for in-state pro teams, PGA Tour and NASCAR hosts, and caps other operators at six. It also ties eligibility to five-state operating experience, fixes a 10% tax on adjusted gross receipts with a 15% local-government share, and pairs sports wagering with an equine and pari-mutuel development program, which none of the listed bills appears to do.","watch":"An operator should focus on the license cap and the five-state operating-history requirement (up to six non-designated licenses, with automatic licenses for designated operators), the in-state server mandate in 52-23-350(H), the official-league-data mandate for tier two wagers, and the cross-reference mismatch between the 5% mental-health allocation in 52-23-230(D)(3) and the 'Section 52-23-230(E)(3)' cited in 52-23-310(C).","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Draft","text_chars":84174,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:53:48.184Z"},"SC-H4957":{"summary":"H4957 rewrites South Carolina's collegiate NIL statute (Chapter 158, Title 59) to let institutions and their officers and employees identify, solicit, facilitate and enable NIL deals for currently enrolled athletes, directly or through third-party agreements. It also shields institutions from damages claims arising from routine athletics decisions, bars athletic associations and conferences from penalizing institutions for complying, and shields NIL contract terms from public-records disclosure. It adjusts the Uniform Athlete Agents Act (Chapter 102) and repeals Sections 59-158-70 and 59-158-80. Signed as Act No. 207, effective on the Governor's approval.","mechanics":["Revised 59-158-20: an institution or any officer, trustee, director or employee may directly or through a third-party agreement identify, create, solicit, facilitate and enable NIL opportunities for a currently enrolled athlete, and may permit athletes to use its trademarks and facilities. The institution may not take fees from the athlete for facilitating NIL, and no appropriated funds may be used to pay athletes for NIL.","Revised 59-158-20(D): NIL compensation must be payment for NIL independent of athletic performance, unless permitted by a collegiate athletic association and institutional policy, a court order, or a settlement agreement. Revised 59-158-30: an NIL contract may not extend beyond the athlete's eligibility.","Revised 59-158-40: institutions and their personnel are not liable for damages to an athlete's NIL earning ability from decisions routinely taken in intercollegiate athletics, but common-law fraud and intentional misrepresentation claims survive. Associations, conferences and similar bodies may not enforce rules that penalize an institution or athletes for activity this chapter authorizes, and may not stop an institution from using third-party NIL agreements.","Revised 59-158-50: institutional copies or summaries of athletes' NIL contracts are not public records under Section 30-4-20(C) unless the requester is a party, and the institution cannot be compelled to disclose them to the NCAA, a conference or similar bodies.","Revised 59-158-60: if Chapter 158 conflicts with Chapter 102, Chapter 158 governs, and agents must comply with the federal SPARTA (15 U.S.C. 7801-7807). Section 59-102-20(2)(b) now excludes institutions and their employees and contractors from the 'athlete agent' definition.","Section 59-102-100(H) amends the cap on agent compensation under NIL agency contracts. The markup is lost in the text provided, but the cap appears to move from ten to twenty percent of the NIL contract. Section 9 repeals 59-158-70 and 59-158-80, which covered NIL contract disclosures, limitations, revocation periods, governing law and federal compliance. The prior bar on NIL endorsements of tobacco, alcohol, illegal substances and gambling including sports betting (old 59-158-40(B)(2)) appears to be deleted, because Section 4 replaces the section with the liability and association provisions."],"issue_tags":["advertising"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"Nothing listed is a direct counterpart; most listed bills legalize or tax sports betting. The closest NIL bills are GA HB1276 and HB383 (high school NIL), HI HB2384 and SB3263 (affirming student-athlete NIL rights and requiring University of Hawaii policies), and DE HB314 (student athlete protection). SC H4957 differs mainly in letting colleges themselves facilitate NIL deals, blocking association retaliation, and sealing NIL contracts from public-records requests. The apparent deletion of the gambling-endorsement bar runs opposite to CT HB05229, which restricts gaming advertising on campuses, and AR HB1861, which regulates NIL-linked games within an interactive gaming act.","watch":"The old statutory bar on athletes endorsing gambling and sports betting appears to be gone, so check the enrolled version for the strikethroughs; any remaining limit would come from institutional policy or NCAA rules, which matters for sportsbook and iGaming NIL deals.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":21150,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:53:52.126Z"},"TN-HB1767":{"summary":"Tennessee Public Chapter 598 (SB 1624, substituted for HB 1767) is a short conforming amendment to the Tennessee Sunset Law schedules. It removes the sports wagering council from the list of entities in T.C.A. § 4-29-245(a) and adds it to the list in § 4-29-249(a). The text does not state the purpose, but the change appears to move the council to a different sunset review date.","mechanics":["Section 1 amends T.C.A. § 4-29-245(a) by deleting subdivision (30). The text does not name the entity, but the title and Section 2 indicate it is the sports wagering council.","Section 2 amends T.C.A. § 4-29-249(a) by adding a new subdivision: 'Sports wagering council, created by § 4-49-105.' The subdivision number is left blank in the chaptered text.","The council is created by § 4-49-105 (Title 4, Chapter 49). The act does not amend that section or any substantive wagering provision.","Section 3: the act takes effect on becoming law, 'the public welfare requiring it.' Passed by the Senate and House on March 14, 2024, and approved by the Governor.","No rates, licensing, tax, advertising, consumer-protection, or enforcement provisions are changed."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. On its own, this is a purely technical sunset-schedule amendment that makes no change to Tennessee's sports wagering framework, so it differs in kind from substantive wagering legislation. The title says it amends Chapter 49, but the operative sections amend only Chapter 29, the Sunset Law provisions.","watch":"Little needs attention beyond the council's new sunset review date, which sets when its authority to regulate Tennessee sports wagering would terminate unless the legislature continues it. Confirm the date by reading the text of § 4-29-249 as amended against the prior § 4-29-245 listing.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":956,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:53:54.136Z"},"TN-HB1362":{"summary":"Public Chapter 450 (SB 475, substituted for HB 1362) amends Tennessee's Sports Gaming Act (T.C.A. Title 4, Ch. 49). It replaces the privilege tax on adjusted gross income with a 1.85% tax on gross handle, creates a three-year vendor registration regime with a $150,000 fee, and restructures licence fees, key-personnel disclosure, disqualification rules and council enforcement powers. It takes effect July 1, 2023 (Section 43).","mechanics":["Tax (Sections 35-37): the privilege tax is now 1.85% of gross handle, paid monthly on the prior month's handle. Licensees may deduct federal excise tax paid. They may not deduct winning payouts, promotional wagers or promotional payouts. The council must write rules for adjusting handle for cancelled or voided wagers and must repeal its rules on the adjusted-gross-income tax.","New definitions (Section 6): gross handle (gross wagers less cancelled or voided wagers), gross wager (includes cash, promotional and other media wagers), key personnel (owners of more than 5%, partners, voting directors, officers, and employees who oversee wagering, risk, platform or integrity), obvious error, cancelled, voided and resettled wagers, promotional wager and promotional payout. Section 3 redefines money line wager.","Vendor registration (Sections 5, 31): 'vendor' covers geolocation, platform software, account management, risk and odds-making, data feeds, KYC and payment processing providers. It also covers independent testing labs, vendors with direct interface with patron accounts or platforms, and revenue-share vendors. The council can add similar material service providers. Marketing affiliates not paid by revenue share are excluded. Registration lasts 3 years and requires key-personnel background checks and a jurisdiction list. The fee is $150,000 for the first period, paid $50,000 at registration and on each of the first two anniversaries. The council must decide within 90 days, and renewal fees are set by rule by July 1, 2023.","Licence fees (Section 42): a non-refundable $50,000 application fee is credited against a $750,000 initial licence fee. For year two or later, before June 30, 2025, the fee is $750,000 for licensees with $100 million or more in gross wagers in the prior 12 months and $375,000 for those below that. From July 1, 2025, fees are set by rule to cover costs and adjusted no more than biennially. Section 18 adds a requirement that new businesses show by clear and convincing evidence that they have arranged funding for operations.","Disqualification and personnel (Sections 20-21, 23): the council may not license or register an applicant that has a disqualified individual (for example, an employee, coach, player or governing-body member of a team, league or association on which wagers are permitted) as key personnel. Licensees must report key-personnel changes within 3 business days.","Enforcement (Sections 22, 25-30): the council may inspect licensee, registrant and third-party premises and may investigate on information and belief or on a credible complaint. It may suspend, revoke or refuse to renew a licence or registration and impose administrative fines of up to $25,000 per violation. Unspent fine and fee balances carry forward (Sections 19, 26). Council employees may place nominal wagers for audits, with winnings returned to the operator quarterly (Section 10)."],"issue_tags":["tax","licensing","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike the Alaska bills (AK HB145/SB194), which are described as taxing mobile sports wagering revenue, Tennessee moves to a tax on gross handle with no deduction for payouts or promotions. Hawaii's HB 1308, HB 2570 and SB1569 are described as using a general excise tax on operator licences, and Iowa HF673 and HSB122 address tax receipts, so none is listed as a handle-based levy. Tennessee amends an existing regime, whereas Georgia (HB 686, SB 57, SB 172 and others) and Delaware HB365 are establishing or authorising frameworks. The Tennessee bill also has no consumer-protection or advertising provisions of the kind in Connecticut HB05229 and SB01464, and instead centres on vendor registration, key personnel and the tax base.","watch":"Check the 1.85% handle tax, which allows no deduction for winning payouts or promotional wagers and so can erode margins on low-hold or promotion-heavy books, and check the vendor definition, whose council-determined catch-alls and revenue-share limb could pull in partners and affiliates that must pay the $150,000 registration fee.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":19735,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:53:59.278Z"},"TN-HB2946":{"summary":"HB2946 creates a dormant-account rule for Tennessee sports wagering licensees. It lets a licensee return an unused balance to the bettor after three years of dormancy, bars forfeiture of unused balances, and limits when balances go to the state treasurer under the Unclaimed Property Act. It amends T.C.A. Title 4, Ch. 49, Pt. 1 and § 66-29-105.","mechanics":["Section 1(a)(1): where an account has been dormant for three years or more and holds an unused balance, the licensee may refund it to the bettor, either by the original funding method or by certified-mail check to the address of record. Except as provided in (c), the licensee may not remit the balance to the state treasurer.","Section 1(a)(2): for any period of dormancy, a licensee may not confiscate, take possession of, forfeit, or assume an unused balance.","Section 1(b)(1): at least 90 days before refunding, the licensee must send notice of its intent to remit by email to the bettor's email address of record.","Section 1(b)(2): a licensee may not send notice that funds may be confiscated, forfeited or assumed, or that the account may be closed, because of inactivity.","Section 1(c): if the refund fails, the licensee must use reasonable due diligence for six months after the three-year dormancy period to locate the bettor. After that it may remit the balance to the state treasurer under the Unclaimed Property Act. Balances must be remitted account by account, not aggregated, together with the licensee's latest information on the bettor.","Section 2 adds § 66-29-105(d): a bettor's unused balance, with 'bettor' as defined in § 4-49-102, is deemed abandoned and unclaimed on remittance to the treasurer under Section 1. Section 3: effective on becoming law. The text has no penalty or enforcement provision."],"issue_tags":["integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"None of the listed bills deals with dormant accounts or unclaimed property. The nearest is DE HB187, which also ties sports betting funds to a state collection mechanism, but for child support intercepts on winnings, not abandoned balances. CT HB05229 and CT HB05269 regulate account withdrawals and payment mechanics, and CT HB05269 requires permission from joint card holders. HB2946 is a narrow consumer-protection and escheat bill with no licensing, tax or advertising provisions, and it overrides the state's general unclaimed property regime for betting accounts.","watch":"Operators should check that the ban on forfeiting dormant balances, the bar on inactivity-closure warnings, and the permissive 'may' refund conflict with their terms of service and current escheat practice. They should also check whether the 90-day email notice and six-month locate period are workable, and what happens if a balance is neither refunded nor remitted, since the bill is silent on that and on penalties.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Draft","text_chars":3340,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:54:03.344Z"},"TN-HB1545":{"summary":"This is Tennessee's general appropriations act for the fiscal years beginning July 1, 2022 and July 1, 2023. The schedule in the text provided is for FY 2023-24, and the text cuts off partway through the Miscellaneous Appropriations title. It touches gaming only through two line items: a Fantasy Sports line in the Secretary of State's budget and a Sports Wagering Advisory Council line among the executive commissions. It adds no substantive gaming, tax, licensing or consumer-protection rules.","mechanics":["Title III-1, Item 3.7 (Secretary of State) appropriates $228,700 for 'Fantasy Sports' for 2023-24. This is a funding line for the office that oversees fantasy sports; the bill does not change the underlying regulatory regime.","Title III-2, Item 2.9 (Commissions) funds the 'Sports Wagering Advisory Council'. The OCR of the amount column looks misaligned, so the dollar figure cannot be reliably assigned to this line.","The Sports Wagering Advisory Council line sits among executive-branch commissions, alongside the Public Utility Commission and the Arts Commission. The visible text contains no rate, threshold, definition or enforcement provision for sports wagering.","The Department of Revenue is funded in Title III-18, including Collection Services and the Audit Division. No gaming-specific appropriation or earmark appears there in the text provided.","The act lists the Tennessee Lottery-funded education accounts (Lottery for Education Account, $443.1 million, and After-School Programs). These are lottery proceeds, not sports-betting revenue.","The text provided ends before the full Miscellaneous Appropriations and any general provisions or later sections. Any other gaming-related provisions in those parts cannot be assessed."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"This is a pure budget bill, not a gaming bill, so it is not comparable to the substantive sports-betting or iGaming bills listed (GA SB57, HI HB1308, IL HB2239). The nearest comparisons are other appropriations or finance bills: KS HB2551 (omnibus appropriations) and IL HB4582 (fund transfers). TN's Secretary of State fantasy sports funding line parallels KY HB 904 and IL HB2342, which create regulatory frameworks for fantasy contests, but TN HB1545 only funds the existing office.","watch":"Watch the Sports Wagering Advisory Council and Fantasy Sports line items as an indicator of the resources behind Tennessee's oversight and enforcement of wagering and fantasy contests, and check whether the full enacted text or any later budget document adds earmarks or conditions.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":395238,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:54:03.554Z"},"TN-HB2184":{"summary":"The bill amends the Tennessee sports wagering privilege tax allocation in T.C.A. 4-49-104(e). It cuts the share in subdivision (1) from 80% to 75% and adds a new subdivision (4) sending 5% of the privilege tax to a new Tennessee Promise completion grant special account. The rest of the bill converts the Tennessee Promise completion grant program in T.C.A. 49-7-178 from a four-year pilot into a permanent program and sets rules for funding and awarding the grants.","mechanics":["Section 1 reduces the 4-49-104(e)(1) share from 80% to 75% and adds (4): 5% of the privilege tax collected is distributed by the council to the state treasurer for deposit in the new account. The tax rate and the operators' tax base are unchanged.","Section 2 creates the account in new 49-7-178(e), administered by the commission. It receives 5% of the privilege tax from sports wagering licensees and may be used only for completion grants. Unspent balances and interest carry forward and do not revert to the general fund, and the treasurer may invest the money under 9-4-602.","Section 2 also removes the \"pilot\" language, deletes subsections (c) and (h), and redesignates the remaining subsections.","Section 3 makes all completion grants subject to appropriation. It bars use of net state lottery proceeds and permits grants from the new account only from July 1, 2027, for the 2027-2028 academic year onward. Grants may also come from the unexpended balance of the qualified work-based learning grant fund (49-11-903).","Section 3 sets a $250,000 minimum July 1 balance from the work-based learning fund, with a $250,000 annual cap on grants awarded from that fund. From fiscal year 2027-2028, each Pell-eligible college coaching initiative student must be assigned a coach and have access to a completion grant of up to $1,000 per semester.","Section 4: the act takes effect upon becoming law, so the 5% diversion starts immediately even though grants from the account cannot be awarded until July 1, 2027."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"None of the listed bills is a close match, because they mostly authorize or regulate sports wagering and set new tax rates (for example AK HB145 and SB194, HI HB 1308, HB 2570 and SB1569, and Georgia's SB172, SB57 and HB380). HB2184 changes neither the rate nor operator obligations in an existing Tennessee market and only redirects a 5% slice of the existing privilege tax to a scholarship completion-grant account. The closest parallel is GA SB386, which ties sports betting legalization to more Pre-K funding. That is an earmark inside a new authorization bill, whereas this one amends the distribution formula for an already-operating tax.","watch":"Operators' tax liability is unchanged, but watch for this bill setting a precedent for further carve-outs of the privilege tax and for any later amendment that changes the rate or the distribution percentages in 4-49-104(e).","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Draft","text_chars":4857,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:54:07.273Z"},"TN-HB374":{"summary":"Tennessee Public Chapter 143 (SB 218, substituted for HB 374) moves regulation of fantasy sports from the Secretary of State to the Sports Wagering Advisory Council. It transfers the Fantasy Sports Act from Title 47, Ch. 18, Part 16 to Title 4, Ch. 49, Part 2, and conforms the related criminal-gambling exemption and tax definitions. It is largely a transfer-of-authority and conforming bill, not a change to substantive fantasy rules.","mechanics":["Sections 1 and 5: replaces \"secretary of state\" with \"sports wagering advisory council\" throughout the Fantasy Sports Act and transfers the Act to Title 4, Ch. 49, as new Part 2. Existing Ch. 49 becomes Part 1 (Sections 3-4).","Section 10: licenses and permits issued by the Secretary of State under § 47-18-1603 stay valid and are deemed Council licenses. Existing Secretary of State fantasy sports rules stay in force until the Council adopts emergency or permanent rules.","Section 12: the Secretary of State must help transfer its duties to the Council. Remaining administrative funds go to the Council on or after July 1, 2023, once the Council promulgates rules. Existing Secretary of State contracts are assigned to the Council upon rule promulgation and run to their original terms, with renewal at the parties' discretion.","Section 8 (§ 67-4-902): rewrites the tax definitions. \"Adjusted revenues\" is entry fees collected less winnings paid, multiplied by the resident percentage. \"Resident percentage\" is Tennessee-consumer entry fees divided by total entry fees, rounded to the nearest 0.1%. Other terms cross-reference § 4-49-202.","Section 9 (§ 67-4-905(3)): 10% of the revenue allocated under that section goes to the fantasy sports fund created by § 4-49-207.","Section 7 conforms the § 39-17-501 gambling exemption to the relocated Act. Section 13: effective on enactment for rulemaking and administration, and July 1, 2023 for all other purposes."],"issue_tags":["licensing","tax"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. On its own, the bill is unusual in being a purely administrative reallocation of fantasy sports oversight to the Sports Wagering Advisory Council, the body that regulates Tennessee sports wagering. The tax change also keeps the resident-percentage method of calculating adjusted revenues.","watch":"Watch for the Sports Wagering Advisory Council's emergency or permanent rulemaking, because it triggers the contract assignment and fund transfer and could change licensing, fees and operating requirements for fantasy operators, particularly those that also hold sports wagering licenses.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":5882,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:54:12.266Z"},"TN-HR36":{"summary":"HR0036 is a House resolution approving a list of annual charitable gaming events for 501(c)(3) and 501(c)(19) organizations under Article XI, Section 5 of the Tennessee Constitution and the Tennessee Nonprofit Gaming Law. It adopts the Secretary of State's omnibus list of qualifying annual event applicants for events held 7/1/2023 to 6/30/2024, organized by county. It was signed into law on 2023-03-14. It is a routine approval of nonprofit raffles, reverse raffles and bingo, and has no bearing on commercial sports betting or iGaming.","mechanics":["The recital says Art. XI, Sec. 5 and the Tennessee Nonprofit Gaming Law require each annual event for a 501(c)(3) or 501(c)(19) organization to be approved by a two-thirds vote of all members elected to each house.","Approval covers events dated 7/1/2023 through 6/30/2024, drawn from the Division of Charitable Solicitations, Fantasy Sports, and Gaming's Omnibus List of Qualifying Annual Event Applicants.","Each entry gives the organization, event address, date, event name and event type. The types are Raffle, Reverse Raffle and Bingo, and the list is grouped by county, with Davidson (24), Shelby (21) and Sevier (11) the largest.","Named events include a 'Charity Casino Night' (Kimberly Family Foundation, Rutherford County, 12/31/2023), an 'ST. JUDE DREAM HOME GIVEAWAY' listed in Davidson, Hamilton and Shelby counties, and several gun, vehicle and 50/50 raffles.","The text sets no rates, taxes, fees or new substantive rules, and it contains no enforcement provisions. It operates only as a legislative approval of the listed events."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills from other states is comparable. They deal with mobile sports wagering, iGaming, taxation, advertising and lottery regulation, while TN HR0036 is a ministerial approval of charitable raffle and bingo events. The closest overlap is AR HB1861 and similar bills that address illegal online gaming, but those regulate commercial operators. This resolution regulates nothing and only implements the Tennessee constitutional requirement for legislative approval of nonprofit gaming events.","watch":"There is little here for a sports-betting operator beyond noting that charitable raffles and the 'Charity Casino Night' entry sit in a separate nonprofit regime administered by the Secretary of State, apart from the Tennessee Sports Gaming Act.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Draft","text_chars":40637,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:54:15.008Z"},"TN-HB2973":{"summary":"Tennessee's general appropriations act (Public Chapter 966, HB2973) funds state government for the fiscal year beginning July 1, 2024. It is a line-item schedule with no substantive gaming, sports-wagering, iGaming or collectibles regulation. The only wagering-related items in the text provided are the Sports Wagering Advisory Council line in Title III-2 and the lottery-funded education lines.","mechanics":["Section 1 appropriates funds across the legislative, judicial and executive branches, state aid, capital outlay, debt service and emergency/contingency for the fiscal year beginning July 1, 2024. The caption also refers to the fiscal year beginning July 1, 2023.","Title III-2 (Executive Offices, Commissions) lists item 2.8, Sports Wagering Advisory Council, as a separate line. Because the extracted figures are misaligned with their labels, I cannot reliably read the dollar amount for that line.","Title III-9 (Education) includes a lottery-funded After-School Programs Special Account (item 2.1.k). Title III-10 (Higher Education) includes a Lottery for Education Account (item 1.7) at $461,600,000.","The Title III-2 Commissions block also includes the Tennessee Public Utility Commission and the Human Rights Commission. These are unrelated to gaming.","Status shows signed into law, with effective dates of 05/15/2024 and 07/01/2024. It was substituted for SB2942. The text supplied cuts off partway through Title III-22 (Miscellaneous Appropriations), so any later general provisions or limitations sections are not reviewed."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"This is a general appropriations bill and is not comparable to the substantive gaming bills listed, such as GA SB57, HI HB 1308 and IL HB2239. It is closest in form to KS HB2551, another omnibus appropriations bill. Unlike KS HB2001, which moves money out of the state gaming revenues fund, the Tennessee text provided contains no gaming-revenue transfer or tax rate. Its only gaming-adjacent content is a funded Sports Wagering Advisory Council line and lottery-funded education accounts.","watch":"Confirm the Sports Wagering Advisory Council appropriation amount and any conditions or limitations attached to it in the full chaptered text, since the supplied text is truncated and the figure is not reliably readable.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":408714,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:54:17.208Z"},"TN-HB785":{"summary":"HB0785 (companion SB750) mainly creates a 'promising futures' scholarship program run by the Department of Education for early care and learning. Section 1 rewrites the revenue split in T.C.A. 4-49-104(e)(1), which routes the 'privilege tax collected under this section' through 'the council'. That code section, in Title 4, Chapter 49, is the Tennessee sports gaming statute. The bill text never says 'sports', so that reading rests on the code citation. The rewrite shifts a growing share of the tax to the new scholarship account and phases out the lottery-for-education share.","mechanics":["Sec. 1 (4-49-104(e)(1)), FY2023-24: 60% of the privilege tax goes to the promising futures account and 20% to the lottery for education account (4-51-111).","Sec. 1, FY2024-25: 70% goes to promising futures and 10% to lottery for education. From FY2025-26 on, 80% goes to promising futures and nothing is directed to lottery for education. The bill does not say what happens to the remaining share in each year, and it deletes only subdivision (1).","The tax rate, the tax base and operator obligations are not changed. Only the allocation of proceeds changes.","Sec. 2 (new 49-1-1111): scholarships of up to the lesser of tuition and mandatory fees net of other aid, or $4,500 per child per year. Eligible children are at least 6 weeks old and not yet school age, in households at or below state median income (or foster/adoptive households), with working or studying parents, and are Tennessee citizens. A lottery process applies if applications exceed funding.","Sec. 2 sets a July 1, 2024 implementation date and a July 1, 2023 deadline for appointing an advisory group. The department may suspend or terminate participation for noncompliance and refer knowing fraud by providers for criminal prosecution. The promising futures account is a non-reverting treasury account.","Sec. 3 says the act is not an appropriation, so no funds may be obligated unless specifically appropriated. Sec. 5 makes the act effective on becoming law."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"The closest comparison is GA SB386, which also ties sports-betting authorization to increased Pre-K funding. Unlike SB386, TN HB0785 legalizes nothing and creates no tax. It only redirects an existing privilege tax toward early learning scholarships. Bills such as AK HB145 and AK SB194 or the Hawaii bills (HI HB 1308, HB 2570, SB1569) set up new taxes and licensing regimes, and nothing in this bill does that. The same goes for the Iowa and Delaware measures on withholding and intercepts.","watch":"Watch whether the earmark shift, the unspecified treatment of the remaining share, and the non-appropriation clause in Sec. 3 become a basis for later changes to the privilege tax rate or base, noting the bill is stalled in the Finance, Ways, and Means Subcommittee and its FY2023-24 and July 2023 dates have already passed.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Draft","text_chars":9285,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:54:17.289Z"},"TN-SB1783":{"summary":"TN SB1783 changes how Tennessee's sports wagering privilege tax is split. It cuts the education share from 80% to 75% and sends a new 5% slice to a Tennessee Promise completion grant special account run by the Tennessee Student Assistance Corporation's commission under § 49-7-178. The bill does not change the tax rate, licensing, or operator obligations; it only redirects existing revenue.","mechanics":["Section 1 amends T.C.A. § 4-49-104(e): the first-listed share of privilege tax falls from 80% to 75%, and new subdivision (4) directs 5% of the privilege tax collected to the state treasurer for the Tennessee Promise completion grant special account created under § 49-7-178.","Section 2 creates the special account in new § 49-7-178(e), administered by the commission, funded by 5% of the privilege tax collected from licensees offering sports wagering, and restricted to completion grants.","Account balances and interest do not revert to the general fund and carry forward. The state treasurer may invest the funds under § 9-4-602.","Section 3 rewrites § 49-7-178(f): subject to appropriation, the commission may not use net state lottery proceeds for completion grants. It may use the account (but not before July 1, 2027, for the 2027-2028 academic year) or the unexpended balance of the qualified work-based learning grant fund (§ 49-11-903).","Section 3 also sets a $250,000 minimum July 1 balance and a $250,000 annual cap on grants from the work-based learning fund. From 2027-2028, eligible Pell-eligible college coaching initiative students get a coach and up to $1,000 per semester.","Section 2 also removes the 'pilot' language from § 49-7-178, making the program permanent, and deletes subsections (c) and (h). Section 4 makes the act effective on becoming law."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"None of the listed bills is closely comparable, because they mainly authorize or regulate sports wagering or add advertising and consumer-protection rules. The nearest in subject matter are the tax-focused bills such as AK HB145 and AK SB194, which impose a tax on mobile sports wagering revenue, and GA SB386, which directs sports betting funds to Pre-K. TN SB1783 is unusual because it leaves the rate and regulatory structure untouched and only reallocates 5% of existing privilege tax revenue to college completion grants.","watch":"Confirm that the reallocation is revenue-neutral for operators, with no change to the privilege tax rate or base, and watch for later amendments that tie more of the tax to education earmarks or raise the rate.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Draft","text_chars":4857,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:54:24.421Z"},"TN-SB218":{"summary":"Tennessee SB0218 (Public Chapter 143) moves regulation of daily fantasy sports from the Secretary of State to the Sports Wagering Advisory Council. It does this by transferring the Fantasy Sports Act from Title 47, Ch. 18, Part 16 to Title 4, Ch. 49, Part 2. It also redefines \"adjusted revenues\" for the fantasy sports tax in Title 67 and conforms related cross-references. It is largely an administrative reassignment and does not change the substantive licensing or consumer-protection provisions of the Act.","mechanics":["Sec. 1 and 5: replace \"secretary of state\" with \"sports wagering advisory council\" throughout the Fantasy Sports Act and transfer the Act to Title 4, Ch. 49 as new Part 2; existing Ch. 49 becomes Part 1 (Secs. 3-4).","Sec. 8 (T.C.A. 67-4-902): \"adjusted revenues\" per contest = total entry fees minus winnings paid, multiplied by the \"resident percentage\". The resident percentage is Tennessee-consumer entry fees divided by all entry fees, rounded to the nearest 0.1%. Definitions of entry fees, fantasy sports contest, operator, player and Tennessee consumer now cross-refer to 4-49-202.","Sec. 9 (T.C.A. 67-4-905(3)): 10% of the relevant tax proceeds is allocated to the fantasy sports fund established by 4-49-207.","Sec. 7: the criminal gambling exclusion in 39-17-501 for fantasy sports contests now points to the Fantasy Sports Act at Title 4, Ch. 49, Part 2.","Sec. 10 and 12: licenses and permits issued by the Secretary of State stay valid and are deemed Council-issued; existing Secretary of State rules stay in force until the Council adopts emergency or permanent rules; remaining funds and contracts transfer to the Council once rules are promulgated.","Sec. 13: effective on becoming law (approved 04/13/2023) for rulemaking and administrative duties; July 1, 2023 for all other purposes."],"issue_tags":["licensing","tax"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. On its own, this bill is unusual because it is a pure regulatory-transfer measure. It puts daily fantasy sports under the same Sports Wagering Advisory Council that oversees sports wagering, rather than a general-purpose office such as the Secretary of State. It also revises the tax base so that only the Tennessee-resident share of entry fees is taxed.","watch":"Watch for the Council's emergency and permanent rules, because until they are adopted the old Secretary of State rules and licenses continue, and the resident-percentage calculation for \"adjusted revenues\" will drive each fantasy operator's tax liability and reporting.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":5882,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:54:24.695Z"},"TN-SB1624":{"summary":"The text supplied is Public Chapter 625 (HB1513, substituted for SB1624), the Transparency for Deepfakes in Political Advertising Act. It does not concern the sports wagering council named in the bill title and status line. It amends Tenn. Code Ann. § 2-19-120(a) to require disclaimers on political communications containing deepfakes of candidates, and it gives the depicted candidate a damages and equitable-relief remedy. Nothing in the text touches sports betting, iGaming, or collectibles.","mechanics":["Section 2 adds § 2-19-120(a)(6). Audio deepfakes (as defined in § 39-17-1902) that impersonate a candidate must state at the beginning and end: \"This communication contains fake content impersonating speech that did not occur.\"","Video deepfakes depicting a candidate in action or speech they did not engage in must display a statement in large letters for the whole video. The wording differs for video-only and for audio-plus-video content.","Under (a)(6)(C), where the depicted candidate is not an incumbent, the disclaimer rules apply only if the communication is distributed within 120 days of the start of voting in an election in which the candidate is on the ballot.","Under (a)(6)(D), hosts, third-party producers, ISPs, search engines and cloud providers are not liable solely for providing access or connection, and paid broadcasters, cable or satellite operators, and streaming services are exempt, in each case to the extent they did not create the deepfake.","Under (a)(6)(E), the depicted candidate may recover damages and equitable relief under § 39-17-1904, except § 39-17-1904(c).","Section 3 sets the effective date at July 1, 2026. The chaptered act was approved in March 2026, which conflicts with the status line showing a 03/27/2024 effective date."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No other states' bills are listed for comparison. The main unusual point is that the bill title and status (sports wagering council, effective 03/27/2024) do not match the chaptered text, which is a 2026 political-deepfake disclosure act. The data source probably mismatched HB1513 with SB1624, so the record should be checked before anyone relies on it.","watch":"Confirm the correct text and status of the actual TN SB1624 sports wagering council amendments to Title 4, Chapters 29 and 49, because this chaptered text contains nothing relevant to operators.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":3723,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:54:25.436Z"},"TN-SB1532":{"summary":"Tennessee's general appropriations act for FY2023-24 (Public Chapter 418, HB1545 substituted for SB1532) funds state government across all branches. The visible text has no gambling-specific substantive provisions. Two line items touch the sector: a Sports Wagering Advisory Council (Title III-2, Item 2.9) and a Fantasy Sports line under the Secretary of State (Title III-1, Item 3.7).","mechanics":["Section 1 appropriates funds for the fiscal year beginning July 1, 2023, using a schedule of titles (Legislative, Judicial, Executive and others) with 2023-2024 dollar figures.","Title III-1, Item 3.7 (Secretary of State, Fantasy Sports) appears to be $228,700, based on the flattened table: the seven Secretary of State line amounts sum to the $46,065,900 total.","Title III-2, Item 2.9 lists the Sports Wagering Advisory Council among the Commissions, which total $104,633,500. The extracted table does not reliably show which dollar figure belongs to this line, so its amount cannot be confirmed.","The visible text does not appropriate any sports-wagering regulatory budget line, such as a gaming division or a problem-gambling program. The Tennessee Education Lottery appears only as lottery-funded education accounts (Title III-9 Item 2.1k, Title III-10 Item 1.7, $443,100,000), which are outside sports betting.","The text is truncated partway through Title III-22 (Miscellaneous Appropriations, Grants). Any later provisions, such as special-purpose language, revenue-sharing rules, or other wagering-related items, could not be reviewed."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"This is a budget bill, unlike the substantive gambling bills listed. Its closest overlap is the Secretary of State fantasy sports funding line, which relates to fantasy contest regulation in KY HB 904 and IL HB2342/HB5648, but this bill sets no operator rules, fees, or taxes. KS HB2551 is the nearest comparable appropriations vehicle, but nothing in the listed material suggests either bill is gambling-focused. The unusual feature here is the Sports Wagering Advisory Council line sitting in the executive-office commissions.","watch":"Watch the Sports Wagering Advisory Council and Fantasy Sports funding lines, and any unseen special-purpose language in the truncated portion, as signals of regulatory staffing and enforcement capacity rather than any change in operator obligations.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":395238,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:54:28.237Z"},"TN-SB2875":{"summary":"TN SB2875 (companion HB2946) sets rules for dormant sports-wagering accounts. A licensee may refund an unused balance to the bettor after three years of dormancy, may never confiscate or forfeit it, and may send it to the state treasurer as unclaimed property only after a six-month due-diligence period fails to locate the bettor. It overrides the Tennessee Unclaimed Property Act, Title 66, ch. 29, pt. 1, to the extent of any conflict.","mechanics":["New section in Title 4, ch. 49, pt. 1, subsection (a)(1): after 3+ years of dormancy with an unused balance, the licensee may refund the bettor by the original funding method or by certified-mail check to the address of record. The licensee may not remit to the treasurer except as allowed by subsection (c).","Subsection (a)(2): for any period of dormancy, a licensee may not confiscate, take possession of, forfeit, or assume an unused balance.","Subsection (b)(1): at least 90 days before refunding, the licensee must email notice of its intent to the bettor's email address of record. (b)(2): a licensee may not send notices that funds may be confiscated, forfeited or assumed, or that the account may be closed, because of inactivity.","Subsection (c)(1): if the refund fails, the licensee must exercise reasonable due diligence for six months after the three-year dormancy period, and may then remit the balance to the state treasurer under the Unclaimed Property Act.","Subsection (c)(2): no aggregation of balances. Each account must be remitted individually with the most current bettor information the licensee holds.","Section 2 adds § 66-29-105(d): a bettor's unused balance, using the § 4-49-102 definition of bettor, is deemed abandoned and unclaimed upon remittance to the treasurer under the new section. Section 3: effective on becoming law. The text has no penalty or enforcement provision of its own."],"issue_tags":["integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"None of the listed bills deals with dormant-account balances or unclaimed property for sports-wagering accounts, so there is no direct comparator. The Connecticut bills on account withdrawals and customer service (CT HB05229, CT HB05269, CT SB01464) are the closest, since they also regulate how licensees handle customer accounts and funds. They do not address dormancy or escheat. Unusually, this bill is narrowly about customer-fund handling. It has no licensing, tax, or advertising provisions, and it displaces the general unclaimed-property escheat rule with a bettor-first refund regime.","watch":"Operators should check how the bill squares with existing Tennessee account-balance and unclaimed-property obligations. It sets a three-year trigger and 90-day email notice, bars any inactivity-based forfeiture or closure warnings, requires per-account remittance with bettor data, and leaves open how a refund stays optional ('may') while confiscation is banned.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Draft","text_chars":3340,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:54:34.012Z"},"TN-SB475":{"summary":"Tennessee SB0475 (Public Chapter 450) amends the Tennessee Sports Gaming Act (T.C.A. Title 4, Chapter 49). It replaces the 20% tax on adjusted gross income with a 1.85% privilege tax on gross handle. It also creates a three-year vendor registration regime with a $150,000 fee, adds 'key personnel' vetting, redefines several wager terms, resets license fees, and expands council investigation and fining powers. It takes effect July 1, 2023.","mechanics":["Tax (Secs. 35-37): privilege tax of 1.85% on gross handle, paid monthly. Licensees may deduct federal excise tax paid but not winning payouts or promotional wagers/payouts. Gross handle is gross wagers less cancelled or voided wagers. The council must write rules for adjusting handle for cancelled or voided wagers and repeal the rules on tax on adjusted gross income. 'Gross wager' includes cash and promotional wagers.","Vendor registration (Sec. 5, Sec. 31): 'vendor' is broadly redefined to cover geolocation, platform software, account management, risk/odds services, data feeds, KYC, payment processing, independent test labs, revenue-share recipients, and similar services the council deems material. Marketing affiliates paid other than by revenue share are excluded. Registration lasts 3 years, with a $150,000 first-period fee paid in three $50,000 installments. Later fees are set by rule, and the council must decide within 90 days of a complete application. Fee rules are due July 1, 2023.","License fees (Sec. 42): $50,000 nonrefundable application fee, credited against a $750,000 initial license fee. Before June 30, 2025, annual renewal is $750,000 for licensees with $100M or more in gross wagers in the prior 12 months and $375,000 for those under $100M. Council rules effective July 1, 2025 will set cost-based fees, adjustable no more than biennially. Start-up applicants must show by clear and convincing evidence that they have funded their operations (Sec. 18).","Key personnel and eligibility (Secs. 6, 13-17, 20-21, 23): 'key personnel' means owners above 5%, partners, voting directors and officers, and employees who principally oversee wagering, risk, platform, or integrity. Applicants must disclose key personnel, civil judgments, and jurisdictions of operation. Licensees must report key personnel changes within 3 business days. The council may not license an applicant with a disqualified person as key personnel, including employees, coaches, players, and governing-body members or employees of teams, leagues, or associations on which wagers are permitted.","Enforcement (Secs. 22, 25-30): the council may investigate on information and belief or on a credible complaint. It may suspend, revoke, or refuse to renew a license or registration, and impose fines of up to $25,000 per violation. Fine proceeds carry forward each fiscal year (Sec. 26). The council may inspect licensee, registrant, and third-party premises during business hours and run audits for wagers from minors or ineligible persons. Council employees may place nominal wagers in audits, with winnings returned to the operator quarterly.","Wager definitions and fee carry-forward (Secs. 3, 6, 19): new definitions of money line, cancelled, voided, and resettled wagers, obvious error, and promotional wager/payout. Ten percent of remaining license fees stay with the council and carry forward rather than being distributed under subsection (g). Sec. 39 repeals T.C.A. 4-49-116 and Sec. 4 deletes definition (21)."],"issue_tags":["tax","licensing","integrity","advertising"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Among the listed bills, none is a direct Tennessee analogue. AK HB145 and SB194 also address mobile sports wagering tax and criminal history checks for licensure, but they start a new market, whereas TN SB0475 amends an existing regime. The key difference is the tax base. Tennessee taxes gross handle at 1.85% with no deduction for payouts or promotions, whereas Alaska's bills impose a tax on 'mobile sports wagering revenue.' The mandatory three-year vendor registration at $150,000 and the broad vendor definition go further than most listed bills, which focus on licensing operators (e.g., HI HB 1308/HB 2570, GA SB57, DE HB365).","watch":"Operators should watch the shift to a 1.85% tax on gross handle with no deduction for payouts or promotional wagers, which can raise effective tax rates and squeeze margins on low-hold products, and also the broad vendor registration definition, which can reach revenue-share partners and sweep in third-party suppliers.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":19735,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:54:38.047Z"},"TN-SB2942":{"summary":"This is the Tennessee general appropriations act for fiscal years beginning July 1, 2023 and July 1, 2024. It is enacted as Public Chapter 966 (HB2973, substituted for SB2942) and funds all branches of state government, debt service and capital outlay. It does not regulate or tax gambling. The only gaming-related item in the text provided is a line for the Sports Wagering Advisory Council among the Executive Offices commissions (Title III-2, Item 2.8).","mechanics":["Section 1 appropriates funds for the fiscal year beginning July 1, 2024, through a schedule organized by Title (Legislative, Judicial, Executive and others).","Title III-2, Item 2.8 funds the Sports Wagering Advisory Council as a line item under Commissions. The extracted text separates dollar figures from line labels, so I cannot reliably tie a specific amount to this line. The Commissions total is $86,313,200.","The Department of Revenue (Title III-18, total $109,289,500) and the Department of Finance and Administration are funded through ordinary agency lines. No sports-betting tax, fee or licensing provisions appear in the text provided.","Lottery-funded education accounts appear in the Department of Education (After-School Programs Special Account) and Higher Education (Lottery for Education Account, $461,600,000). These are lottery items, not sports-wagering items.","The provided text is truncated in the Miscellaneous Appropriations section, so any later sections or limiting language, including any on gaming or sports-wagering revenue, cannot be reviewed."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"This is a general appropriations bill that mentions sports wagering only through one council line item. It is not comparable to the substantive regulatory bills such as GA SB57, the Hawaii sports wagering bills (HB 1308, HB 2570, SB1569) or IL HB5443. The closest structural analogues are the other budget or finance measures, KS HB2551 and IL HB4582, which are also not gaming-specific. KS HB2001 differs in that it directs transfers from the state gaming revenues fund. Tennessee's text instead funds the oversight body for its already-legal sports wagering market.","watch":"Check the full enrolled text and the Sports Wagering Advisory Council line, along with any sections or special language tied to sports-wagering revenue, because they could signal funding or oversight changes for operators, though nothing substantive appears in the portion provided.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":408714,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:54:38.720Z"},"TN-SB750":{"summary":"The bill is mainly an early-learning measure. Section 1 changes how Tennessee's privilege tax under T.C.A. § 4-49-104(e) is distributed, and Section 2 creates a \"promising futures\" scholarship program for pre-school-age children. The bill text refers only to the \"privilege tax collected under this section\" and the \"council\"; § 4-49-104 sits in Title 4, Chapter 49, which is the sports wagering chapter. The bill changes neither the tax rate nor any operator obligation, and it redirects the lottery-for-education share to the new account by FY2025-26.","mechanics":["Section 1 replaces § 4-49-104(e)(1) with a phased split. In FY2023-24, 60% goes to the promising futures account and 20% to the lottery for education account under § 4-51-111. In FY2024-25 the split is 70% and 10%. From FY2025-26 on, 80% goes to the promising futures account and nothing to lottery for education.","The text does not address the remaining 20% of collections in each year, so that portion is presumably governed by other provisions of § 4-49-104. The bill does not change the tax rate, the tax base, or who pays.","New § 49-1-1111 requires the Department of Education to implement the program by July 1, 2024. Scholarships go to children at least six weeks old who are not yet school-age, from households at or below state median income or foster/adoptive households, whose parent works more than 30 hours a week or is in approved training, and who are Tennessee citizens.","Scholarships are capped at the lesser of tuition and mandatory fees (net of other aid or co-pay) or $4,500 per child per year, and are paid directly to the program. A lottery process applies if applications exceed available funding, and the department may suspend or terminate participation for noncompliance (§ 49-1-1111(g)). Programs that knowingly misuse funds with intent to defraud can be referred for criminal prosecution.","Section 1 sets the dedicated funding stream: the promising futures account is created in the state treasury and does not revert to the general fund (§ 49-1-1111(j)). Section 3 states the act is not an appropriation, and funds cannot be spent unless the general appropriations act provides them.","Section 5 makes the act effective upon becoming law. It prescribes FY2023-24 percentages, and the bill was still in committee in April 2024, so the retroactivity of those figures is unclear."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"Unlike the Alaska mobile sports wagering bills (AK HB145, SB194) or the Hawaii bills (HI HB1308, HB2570, SB1569), this bill does not authorize or license wagering and sets no tax rate; it only earmarks revenue from an existing tax. The closest parallel is GA SB386, which pairs sports betting legalization with increased Pre-K funding, but Tennessee already has a legal market, so this bill is purely a revenue-allocation change. DE HB187 also diverts gaming proceeds to a social purpose (child support intercepts), but it targets individual winnings rather than the state's tax receipts.","watch":"Watch whether amendments or the appropriations process change the privilege tax rate or the percentage of collections taken from sports wagering revenue, since this bill gives the tax a strong, permanent public-policy constituency.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Draft","text_chars":9285,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:54:42.240Z"},"UT-HB21":{"summary":"HB 21 is an omnibus, mostly non-substantive recodification of Utah Criminal Code Title 76, Chapters 9 and 10. It redrafts offenses into a standardized format, splits embedded offenses into stand-alone statutes, renumbers sections, and updates cross-references across many other titles. The gambling offenses in Title 76, Chapter 10, Part 11 are moved and reorganized into a new Chapter 9, Part 14, with no substantive change stated in the title or highlighted provisions. The text provided is truncated before the substantive section text, so the actual gambling language is not visible.","mechanics":["The long title lists \"gambling and related offenses\" among the embedded offenses being enacted as stand-alone statutes.","Existing gambling sections 76-10-1101, -1102, -1103, -1104, -1104.5, -1105, -1109, -1110, -1112 and -1113 are renumbered to 76-9-1401, -1402, -1405, -1406, -1407, -1408, -1409, -1410, -1411 and -1412. The numbering is not strictly sequential.","New sections 76-9-1403 and 76-9-1404 are enacted, apparently to hold offenses previously embedded in the old gambling sections. Their content is not in the provided text.","Sections 76-10-1101.5, 76-10-1106 and 76-10-1108 are repealed, with no indication in the provided text of whether their content is relocated.","The bill amends many cross-references elsewhere in the code, including 32B-7-202 and 4-41a-102, and amends 76-1-301. It contains no appropriation and no stated new rates, fees or licensing regime.","The bill was signed by the Governor on 2025-03-25. The effective date and any coordination clauses are not visible in the truncated text."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. This bill is unusual because it is a statewide criminal-code housekeeping recodification of which gambling is only one of more than twenty offense groups. It does not legalize, tax or license wagering of any kind.","watch":"Compare the new stand-alone gambling sections (76-9-1401 et seq., especially the new 76-9-1403 and -1404) and the repeals of 76-10-1101.5, -1106 and -1108 against the old text, to confirm that the definitions, exemptions and penalties underlying Utah's gambling prohibition were not inadvertently changed.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":224036,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:54:48.732Z"},"VT-H494":{"summary":"Vermont's FY2024 \"BIG BILL\" (Act 78, H.494) is the omnibus appropriations act for State government for the fiscal year ending June 30, 2024. In the portion of text provided, which cuts off at Sec. B.510 (education), it contains no sports-betting, iGaming, prediction-market, sweepstakes or collectibles provisions. The only gaming-adjacent item is a line-item appropriation for the Liquor & Lottery Commission office.","mechanics":["Sec. A.102(b)-(c): sums are single-year appropriations unless stated otherwise, and narrative portions apply only to the fiscal year ending June 30, 2024 unless codified or otherwise specified.","Sec. B.236.1 (Liquor & Lottery Comm. Office): total $14,139,444, of which $13,795,601 comes from enterprise funds. The rest is $60,000 special funds, $213,843 tobacco fund and $70,000 interdepartmental transfers. The text shows no wagering-specific conditions on this line.","Sec. B.240 (Cannabis Control Board): $5,170,692, all from special funds. This is a comparable regulator line but not gaming.","Sec. B.111 (Tax - administration/collection): $30,810,745 total, including $22,406,475 general fund and $8,359,270 special funds. It is not tied to any gambling tax in the visible text.","Sec. A.104: the act does not negate or impair existing law except as specifically provided. Sec. A.107: authorized State positions cannot increase in FY2024 except for new positions authorized by the 2023 session.","Sec. A.108 and the legend: sections E.100-E.9999 (policy language), F (workforce and economic development) and H (effective dates) fall after the provided text, so I cannot confirm what they contain."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed as comparable, so there is nothing to compare against. On its own, this is a general appropriations act. In the visible text the only gaming-related content is the Liquor & Lottery Commission office line in Sec. B.236.1. It contains no tax rate, licensing framework or legalization language of the kind found in dedicated sports-betting bills.","watch":"Because the provided text stops at Sec. B.510, check the unseen E-section policy language, the D-section fund transfers and the H effective dates for any lottery, gaming-enforcement or wagering-related provisions, and confirm that the Sec. B.236.1 appropriation carries no new regulatory mandate.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":83978,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:54:49.271Z"},"VT-H546":{"summary":"Vermont H.546 (Act 144 of 2024) is an omnibus tax-administration bill covering property tax reappraisal funding, conformity to the federal Internal Revenue Code, the renter credit, sales tax treatment of recreational vehicles, the machinery and equipment credit, and local option taxes. It contains no provisions on sports wagering, iGaming, fantasy contests, or trading cards or collectibles.","mechanics":["Sec. 3-4 (32 V.S.A. §§ 5824, 7402(8)): update Vermont's conformity to the federal Internal Revenue Code from December 31, 2022 to December 31, 2023, excluding federal rates under 26 U.S.C. § 1; effective retroactively to January 1, 2024 and applies to taxable years beginning on or after January 1, 2023 (Sec. 21(b)).","Sec. 1 (32 V.S.A. § 4041a): sets the reappraisal payment to municipalities at $8.50 per grand list parcel per year from the Education General Fund; effective on passage.","Sec. 5: expands the renter credit by changing the 'very low-income limit' to 1.3 times the HUD limit, with a statewide average used for Franklin and Grand Isle counties; applies to claim years 2025 and after.","Sec. 6-7: repeal the $15 late-filing reduction on the homestead property tax credit under § 6066a(d) and restructure the § 6068 late-claim reductions ($15 for a late claim; $150 for claims filed after October 15 and by March 15); apply to claim years 2024 and after.","Sec. 16-17: extend the machinery and equipment tax credit carry-forward from December 31, 2026 to 2030, the reporting end date from 2027 to 2031, and the subchapter repeal from July 1, 2026 to July 1, 2030; effective July 1, 2024.","Sec. 10 and 20: the fuel tax sunset under 33 V.S.A. § 2503(d) moves from June 30, 2024 to 2029; local option tax certification under 24 V.S.A. § 138(h) is capped at five municipalities per calendar year, with Commissioner discretion to delay or reject further notices."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"This bill is not comparable to the listed sports-wagering bills, such as AK HB145, GA SB57, HI HB 1308, IA SF2427 or IL HB3159, which authorize, regulate, or tax wagering. It is a general tax-administration bill with no gaming content. The closest overlap is thematic. Iowa's HSB723 and SF2427 also bundle revenue-department housekeeping, but they include sports wagering provisions, and Illinois HB2755 is a broad revenue omnibus, whereas H.546 has none. Vermont has no legal retail or online sports wagering framework in this text.","watch":"Nothing in this bill touches wagering, but the Sec. 20 local option tax changes (a one percent sales, meals and alcohol, or rooms tax) and the federal income tax conformity update are the only items of even indirect relevance to operators or collectibles sellers, so little action is needed.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":21690,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:54:51.204Z"},"VT-H127":{"summary":"Vermont H.127 (Act 63) legalizes mobile-only sports wagering under a state-run contracting model: the Department of Liquor and Lottery selects two to six operators by competitive bid, and the Board of Liquor and Lottery sets operating procedures. It moves fantasy sports regulation out of 9 V.S.A. chapter 116 (repealed by Sec. 5) into a new subchapter 3 of 31 V.S.A. chapter 25. It adds responsible-gaming mandates, an enterprise fund and problem-gambling funding, and takes effect on passage.","mechanics":["Operator selection (§1302(b), §1320): minimum two, maximum six mobile-only operators chosen by competitive bid. If bidding yields too few qualified applicants, the Department may authorize one operator or none. Operators pay a $550,000 fee, charged no more than once per three-year period, plus a bid-determined revenue share of at least 20% of adjusted gross sports wagering revenue (§1320(c)-(d)). Adjusted gross revenue is receipts excluding voided bets, less winnings paid and federal excise tax (§1301(1)).","Eligibility and prohibited events (§1301(9)-(10), §1321): bettors must be 21+ and physically in Vermont. Insiders, athletes, officials and proxies are barred. Prohibited events are in-state collegiate and high school events, games involving Vermont college teams (except tournament games), and events with primarily under-18 participants. Sports events include horse racing and equestrian events.","Board procedures (§1303): adopted under 3 V.S.A. §835 with 30-day comment and two hearings, and binding with force of law. They must require GLI-33 compliance, house rules, internal controls, minimum cash reserves, and promotion rules. Promotions cannot be called free or risk-free if the player must risk their own money, and operators cannot restrict withdrawal of the player's own funds or winnings from them.","Responsible gaming (§1340, §1302(c)): operators must file annual responsible gaming plans, with an independent review at least every five years, possibly at operator cost. Platforms must bar multiple accounts and credit card funding, and must offer deposit and spend limits, a statewide self-exclusion program that also covers fantasy operators, play-time pop-ups, and break-in-play tools. Ads may not depict or target under-21s or vulnerable people. Campus-targeted advertising is barred, and postsecondary schools may not allow wagering ads on their property apart from generally available media.","Enforcement (§1324-1325): the Board may impose monetary penalties, suspension, or termination on operators. Operator fines are capped at $25,000, $75,000 and $150,000 for a first, second and third violation. Unlicensed entities face fines of up to $50,000, $150,000 and $300,000 plus imprisonment of up to six months, one year and two years for a first, second and third or later violation. The chapter exempts licensed activity from the 13 V.S.A. chapter 51 gambling prohibitions (§1306).","Fantasy sports (§§1330-1334): contests are defined with a single-game exclusion. Operators must register annually by October 15 with a $5,000 fee, verify age 18+, and obtain an annual independent audit filed with the Attorney General. Contests on college, high school or youth events are barred, and the Attorney General enforces violations as unfair and deceptive acts under 9 V.S.A. §2453. Sec. 2 requires a Department report by January 15, 2024.","Funding (§1304, §1341, §1341a, Sec. 2a): revenues go to a Sports Wagering Enterprise Fund. FY2024 appropriations are $250,000 for the Department of Mental Health's Problem Gambling Program, $550,000 for the Department of Liquor and Lottery, and $100,000 for the Agency of Digital Services to build the self-exclusion program. Sec. 4 amends 32 V.S.A. §5823 on nonresident income from in-state wagering, and Sec. 3 amends 31 V.S.A. §655 to cover criminal history checks for sports wagering operators."],"issue_tags":["licensing","legalize","tax","advertising","integrity","prop"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"No other states' bills are listed for comparison. On its own, the bill is unusual in using a state-contracting model in which the Department negotiates contracts with a small, capped number of mobile-only operators rather than issuing open licenses. The revenue share is bid-based with a 20% floor, and the operator fee is $550,000. It also folds the fantasy sports regime into the same chapter and repeals the prior fantasy statute, and it has no retail sportsbook component.","watch":"Watch the competitive bid terms and the Board's procedures, since the cap of six operators, the 20% revenue-share floor, the $550,000 fee, and the promotion, advertising and prop-bet approval rules will determine access and economics, and note that the bill covers operators only through contract and procedure rather than a licensing regime.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":40799,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:54:51.260Z"},"VT-H883":{"summary":"Vermont's FY2025 general appropriations act (Act 113, H.883) funds state government for the year ending June 30, 2025. The portion of text supplied is only the purpose and definitions sections and the B-section line items through B.506, about 64 of 238 pages. It contains no sports-betting, iGaming, sweepstakes or collectibles provisions, only routine agency funding lines.","mechanics":["Sec. A.102(b)-(c) and A.101: appropriations are single-year unless stated otherwise, and uncodified narrative provisions apply only to FY2025.","Sec. B.236.1 (Liquor & Lottery Comm. Office): $15,498,900 total, of which $15,053,321 is enterprise funds, with $125,000 special funds, $250,579 tobacco fund and $70,000 interdepartmental transfers. This is the only lottery-related line in the supplied text.","Sec. B.240 (Cannabis Control Board): $6,062,214, all special funds. Sec. B.111 (Tax administration/collection): $35,243,728, of which $23,248,019 is general fund and $11,880,709 special funds.","Sec. A.107: the number of authorized state positions may not be increased in FY2025 except for new positions authorized in the 2024 session, and limited-service positions are exempt.","Sec. A.108: the legend says the E sections hold language tied to specific appropriations, the D sections hold fund transfers, the F sections hold financial regulation fee adjustments, and the H section holds effective dates. None of these sections are in the supplied text."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. On its own this is a standard omnibus budget bill, and the only gaming-adjacent content in the supplied text is the funding line for the Liquor & Lottery Comm. Office (B.236.1) and the Cannabis Control Board line (B.240). It does not legalize, tax or regulate sports betting or iGaming in the text provided.","watch":"Because the supplied text stops at B.506, check the omitted E, D, F and H sections for any lottery, gaming-revenue, fee or enforcement language, and note that B.236.1 funds the lottery regulator at an enterprise-fund level with no sports-wagering authorization shown.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":83735,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:54:58.287Z"},"VT-H839":{"summary":"Vermont's fiscal year 2024 budget adjustment act (Act 87, H.839) amends the FY2024 appropriations in 2023 Acts and Resolves No. 78 across state government. It adjusts line items for public safety, human services, education, natural resources and commerce, revises one-time appropriations, and moves money between funds. The text supplied (through Sec. 55, truncated mid-section) contains no provisions on sports betting, iGaming, wagering taxes, licensing or trading cards.","mechanics":["Secs. 1-37 amend departmental appropriation tables in 2023 Acts and Resolves No. 78 (Secs. B.209 to B.813). Examples: Sec. 6 raises the Global Commitment grant line from $1,990,896,293 to $2,039,037,932, and Sec. 21 adds $5,000,000 in ARPA State Fiscal funds for Corrections.","Sec. 3 raises the Cannabis Control Board (B.240) from $5,170,692 to $5,681,362, all special funds. This is the only regulatory-agency line visible in the text, and it concerns cannabis, not gaming.","Secs. 38-39 cut debt service (B.1000) from $75,705,398 to $675,000. Sec. 55 transfers $71,202,993 from the General Fund to the General Obligation Bonds Debt Service Fund (35100).","Sec. 40 amends the one-time appropriations, including $30,000,000 in FEMA matching funds and $6,250,000 in local economic damage grants for the July 2023 flooding. It also adds $20,000,000 to replenish the Business Emergency Gap Assistance Program.","Sec. 46 amends 29 V.S.A. § 161 on prevailing wage and fringe benefits for state construction projects, and Sec. 51 repeals the C.120(b)(1) reserve-reversion language. Neither affects gaming. No effective date is visible in the supplied text, and the text ends mid-way through Sec. 55."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. On its own, H.839 is a general budget adjustment bill, and the text supplied has no sports-betting, iGaming or collectibles provisions. The only regulatory item of note is the Cannabis Control Board funding increase in Sec. 3.","watch":"Little here bears on sports-betting operators in the text supplied, but because the text is truncated after Sec. 55, check the remaining sections (fund transfers, revenue and tax-related sections) for any gaming-related fund transfers or revenue provisions.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":121771,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:54:59.051Z"},"VT-S105":{"summary":"S.105 would legalize mobile-only sports wagering in Vermont under a state-run, contract-based model. The Department of Liquor and Lottery would contract with two to six operators (or a single operator if too few qualified bidders respond) selected by competitive bid, and the Board of Liquor and Lottery would set operating standards by procedure. Operators would pay a bid-determined revenue share and a $275,000 annual license fee, and the bill sets out responsible gaming duties, a Responsible Gaming Special Fund, and criminal penalties for unauthorized operators.","mechanics":["Market structure (§1302(b), §1305): minimum two, maximum six mobile-only operators chosen by competitive bid; one operator allowed if too few qualified applicants. Bids must state the percentage of gross mobile sports wagering revenue offered to the State, the number of branded websites, a responsible gaming plan, jurisdictions licensed, a player acquisition and affiliate/marketing plan including converting customers from illegal channels, and integrity monitoring.","Economics (§1303(c), §1305(c), §1307, §1311): revenue share set through bidding (no statutory rate); $275,000 annual license fee; proceeds less Department administrative costs go to the General Fund; 2.5% of annual sports wagering revenue received by the Department, but not less than $250,000, is credited to the Responsible Gaming Special Fund for the Department of Mental Health. 'Gross sports wagering revenue' is receipts excluding voided bets, less winnings paid and federal excise tax.","Wager scope (§1301(11), §1306): prohibited events include in-state high school and collegiate events, any collegiate event involving a Vermont college team (except tournament games), and events with participants primarily under 18. Prop bets, parlays, in-play and exchange wagering are expressly within 'sports wagering' but the Department must pre-approve wager categories and types (§1302(e)-(f)). Sports governing bodies or colleges may request restrictions (§1302(d)).","Standards adopted by Board procedure under 3 V.S.A. §835, not full rulemaking, with 30-day comment and two hearings (§1303): systems must meet or exceed GLI-33; third-party security testing, house rules, accounting controls with annual audit, internal controls and minimum cash reserves. Procedures have the force of law.","Consumer protection and advertising (§1304): one account per person, credit cards barred for deposits and wagers, daily deposit and spend limits, self-exclusion, periodic time-spent pop-ups, break-in-play tool, and balance display. Ads may not depict under-18s (except professional or permitted collegiate athletes) or be aimed primarily at them. Responsible gaming plan reviewed independently at least every five years at operator expense; operators may be charged for the annual problem gambling report.","Enforcement (§1310, §1313): Board may impose monetary penalties, suspension or termination of operations; Department may sue for damages or injunctive relief. Unauthorized operators face fines of $10,000/$25,000/$50,000 and imprisonment of 6 months/1 year/2 years for first/second/third offenses. Records such as self-exclusion lists, trade secrets and security records are confidential (§1312). Effective on passage."],"issue_tags":["legalize","licensing","tax","advertising","prop","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. On its own, S.105 is unusual in using a state-run, contract-based model with a bid-determined revenue share rather than a statutory tax rate. It also has no retail option, has the Board set standards by procedure instead of rulemaking, and bars credit card funding.","watch":"Because the revenue share is set only through competitive bidding with no statutory rate or cap, and the Board can change binding operating procedures without full rulemaking, operators should watch how bid criteria, the contract terms, and the Department's wager-type approvals and event restrictions are applied, particularly the in-state collegiate and high school prohibitions and the bar on credit card funding.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":23478,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:55:03.447Z"},"WA-HB2014":{"summary":"Second Substitute HB 2014 (Ch. 146, Laws of 2024) aligns Washington's definition of \"veteran\" with eligibility for federal VA monetary benefits. It creates a uniform \"qualifying discharge\" standard in a new section in chapter 73.04 RCW and applies it across dozens of state benefit statutes. Its only gambling-related change is in RCW 9.46.070(20), the Gambling Commission's powers, where the license-renewal accommodation for people discharged from active service is no longer limited to honorable discharges.","mechanics":["Sec. 4 defines \"qualifying discharge\" to include honorable and general under honorable conditions discharges. It also covers other than honorable discharges where the applicant documents VA eligibility for or receipt of monetary benefits, and any characterization of service where the sole reason for discharge was sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, or related statements or conduct.","Sec. 4(2) requires official documentation of the characterization of service, plus the reason or narrative reason for separation for the sec. 4(1)(d) category. A DD-214, NGB form 22, or equivalent is acceptable, and copies suffice.","Sec. 9 amends RCW 9.46.070(20). The commission must renew the license of anyone who applies within six months of being discharged, removed, or released from active duty \"with a qualifying discharge as defined in section 4,\" on payment of the renewal fee and if there is no cause for denial, suspension, or revocation. The word \"honorably\" is struck.","Sec. 9 also reproduces the rest of RCW 9.46.070, including the sports-wagering provisions in subsections (7)(c) and (21). The visible text shows no change to them.","Secs. 2, 3, and 6-12 amend the veteran definitions in RCW 41.04.005 and 41.04.007, the bar admission statutes, the high school diploma provision, and the resident-tuition and tuition-waiver statutes. The title also lists amendments to the licensing, public employment, and retirement statutes, but the text supplied is cut off after sec. 12.","Sec. 5 directs the Department of Veterans Affairs to run an outreach program on the changes. Secs. 8 and the title repeal RCW 2.48.100 and 73.04.042. The act takes effect June 6, 2024, except secs. 25-27, which take effect April 1, 2025."],"issue_tags":["licensing"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. This is a general veterans-benefits bill, and the gambling content is a single conforming amendment to the Gambling Commission's license-renewal power for discharged service members in RCW 9.46.070(20). It contains no tax, wagering-market, advertising, or sweepstakes provisions.","watch":"Nothing in the visible text changes sports-wagering licensing, but the sec. 9 reference to the sec. 4 \"qualifying discharge\" definition could matter for how operators and licensed employees document veteran renewal eligibility with the Gambling Commission.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":122647,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:55:04.615Z"},"WV-HB2267":{"summary":"WV HB2267 is the annual omnibus bill authorizing Department of Revenue agencies to promulgate legislative rules under W. Va. Code §64-7-1 et seq. The only gaming-relevant item is §64-7-3, which authorizes the Lottery Commission's West Virginia Lottery Sports Wagering Rule (179 CSR 09) as modified to meet Legislative Rule-Making Review Committee objections. The remainder covers alcohol, insurance and tax rules and has no bearing on gaming. The bill is effective from passage and was signed by the Governor.","mechanics":["§64-7-3 authorizes the Lottery Commission's Sports Wagering Rule, 179 CSR 09, under rulemaking authority in §29-22D-4.","The rule was originally filed in the State Register on August 2, 2024, then modified to meet Legislative Rule-Making Review Committee objections and refiled on November 14, 2024. It is authorized as refiled, with no further legislative amendment.","The bill does not reproduce the rule's text, so its substantive terms (licensing, integrity, advertising, taxation) are not visible in this bill.","The Alcohol Beverage Control, Insurance Commissioner and Tax Division rules (including 110 CSR 10F electronic funds transfer, 110 CSR 10G alternative tax dispute resolution and 110 CSR 50C information exchange) are authorized in §§64-7-1, 64-7-2 and 64-7-4 and are not gaming-specific.","Effective from passage (April 12, 2025); the Governor approved it on April 28, 2025."],"issue_tags":["licensing","integrity"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"This is a rulemaking-authorization vehicle rather than a substantive gaming statute, so it is not comparable to the listed bills such as AK HB145/SB194, DE HB365, IL HB2239 or CT SB01464, which create or amend licensing, tax and consumer-protection regimes. Its one gaming relevance is ratifying an existing sports wagering rule (179 CSR 09) under West Virginia's rulemaking-review process. No listed bill performs a comparable rule-ratification function.","watch":"A lawyer for operators should review the text of 179 CSR 09 as refiled on November 14, 2024, because this bill gives it legislative authorization but contains none of its substantive requirements.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":14779,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:55:06.859Z"},"WA-SB5871":{"summary":"SSB 5871 aligns Washington's definition of \"veteran\" with eligibility for federal VA monetary benefits. It adds a new \"qualifying discharge\" definition (new section in chapter 73.04 RCW, Sec. 4) and conforms dozens of RCW provisions to it, including the Gambling Commission's powers statute, RCW 9.46.070. The only gambling-related change in the text provided is a conforming edit to RCW 9.46.070(20), the license-renewal grace period for people leaving military service.","mechanics":["Sec. 4(1) defines \"qualifying discharge\" as: (a) honorable; (b) general under honorable conditions; (c) other than honorable, if the applicant documents VA eligibility for or receipt of monetary benefits such as disability compensation or nonservice-connected pension; or (d) any characterization of service where the listed reason for discharge was sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, or related statements, consensual conduct or disclosure to military officials.","Sec. 4(2) puts the burden on the individual to give the administering agency official documentation of the characterization of service, plus the reason or narrative reason for separation for (1)(d) claims. A DD-214, NGB Form 22 or equivalent is acceptable, and copies suffice.","Sec. 9 amends RCW 9.46.070(20). The Gambling Commission must renew the license of anyone who applies within six months of being discharged, removed or released from active service \"with a qualifying discharge as defined in section 4,\" replacing \"honorably.\" Payment of the renewal fee is still required, and renewal is available only if there is no cause for denial, suspension or revocation.","Secs. 2 and 3 amend RCW 41.04.005 and 41.04.007 to replace the honorable-discharge requirement with a qualifying discharge. Other amended sections cover education (RCW 28A.230.120, 28B.15.012, 28B.15.621), the bar admission statutes (RCW 2.48.070 and 2.48.090), licensing and vehicle provisions, and others. The title also repeals RCW 2.48.100 and 73.04.042.","Sec. 5 directs the Department of Veterans Affairs (chapter 43.60A RCW) to run an outreach program explaining the changes in law. The title also references an effective date and an expiration date, but the text provided is truncated and does not show them."],"issue_tags":["licensing"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. On its own, this is a veterans-benefits definitional bill, and its only gaming touchpoint is a one-phrase conforming amendment to RCW 9.46.070(20). It does not touch sports wagering, iGaming, taxes, or collectibles and trading-card products.","watch":"The change to RCW 9.46.070(20) is minor, but sports-wagering licensees and applicants who are recent service members should note that the renewal grace period now depends on a \"qualifying discharge\" under the new Sec. 4, and that the Commission may need documentation rules to administer it.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Comm Sub","text_chars":119859,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:55:09.772Z"},"WV-HB2268":{"summary":"WV HB2268 is a short rule-authorization bill that amends and reenacts W. Va. Code §64-7-1 to authorize the Lottery Commission's West Virginia Lottery Sports Wagering Rule, 179 CSR 09. The rule was filed in the State Register on August 2, 2024, modified to meet objections of the Legislative Rule-Making Review Committee, and refiled on November 14, 2024. The bill contains no substantive wagering provisions of its own; it only gives legislative approval to the rule under the Commission's authority in §29-22D-4.","mechanics":["Amends and reenacts §64-7-1 (Article 7, authorization for Department of Revenue to promulgate legislative rules), covering the State Lottery Commission.","Authorizes the legislative rule 179 CSR 09 (West Virginia Lottery Sports Wagering Rule) as originally filed August 2, 2024 and refiled November 14, 2024.","Cites §29-22D-4 as the Commission's underlying rulemaking authority.","The refiled version was modified to meet objections of the Legislative Rule-Making Review Committee.","The bill text sets no rates, thresholds, license requirements, effective date, or penalties; those are in the rule itself, which is not reproduced in the bill.","Status: introduced and referred to House Finance on 2025-02-12."],"issue_tags":["licensing","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike the other listed bills, which create or amend substantive sports wagering law (e.g., AK HB145 and SB194 on mobile wagering and tax, DE HB365 on a Lottery-run mobile market, GA SB57 and HB380 on Lottery-run regulation and taxation, CT SB01464 on licensee obligations), WV HB2268 enacts no operative terms. It is a routine rule-authorization vehicle that approves an existing administrative rule, so its content depends entirely on 179 CSR 09. Nothing else listed is a comparable rule-approval bill.","watch":"Obtain and review the text of 179 CSR 09 as refiled November 14, 2024, since the bill ratifies it wholesale and any licensing, integrity, advertising, or reporting obligations on operators will take legal effect through this authorization.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":1214,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:55:09.900Z"},"WV-HB2491":{"summary":"WV HB2491 amends the Online Charitable Raffles article (W. Va. Code §47-21A-1 et seq.) to let NCAA Division I and II institutions of higher education, including affiliated nonprofits and NIL collectives, conduct online raffles on the same footing as charitable and public service organizations. It states that an annual-licence online raffle run by such an institution is not sports wagering under §29-22D or interactive wagering under §29-22E. It also removes the geo-location/geo-fencing and in-state server requirements and extends the tax exemption and net-proceeds rules to these institutions.","mechanics":["Definitions: 'institution of higher education' (cross-referencing §21A-1A-21) must have an NCAA Division I or II athletics department. A new 'athletics department activity or endeavor' definition covers broad financial support of the athletics department, including affiliated nonprofits, trusts and NIL collectives. 'Licensee' now includes institutions.","Unlicensed raffles (§47-21A-3): institutions in existence in the state for at least one year may raffle without a licence if no single prize exceeds $4,000 and cumulative gross proceeds stay at or below $15,000 per calendar year. They must keep separate accounting for three years, open to inspection by the Tax Commissioner.","Licensing (§§47-21A-4 to -6): institutions apply to the Tax Commissioner, with a 60-day filing period and deemed approval if no denial issues. An annual licence costs $500 (§47-21A-7); only one is allowed per institution and all its affiliates, and no joint occasions are allowed under it. Limited occasion licences cost $50, cover two occasions within six months, and are capped at three per year in aggregate.","Regulatory carve-out (§47-21A-5(b)-(c)): an online raffle occasion by an institution holding an annual licence is deemed not a sports wagering activity under §29-22D-1 et seq. and not an interactive wagering activity under §29-22E-1 et seq. The carve-out is not stated for limited occasion licences or the unlicensed tier.","Tax and proceeds (§§47-21A-7, -13): gross proceeds are exempt from state and local B&O, income, excise and special taxes. Expenses are capped at 40% of gross proceeds. Net proceeds, and interest on them, must be spent within one year after licence expiry on the purposes stated in the application. The Commissioner can approve alternative uses or extensions.","Application content (§47-21A-8): institutions must name two (limited) or three (annual) employees or contractors responsible for raffle operations, plus the highest elected officer or designee, with one virtually present at draws. They must supply platform-provider agreements with all costs and fees, a monthly schedule of occasions, and a list of athletics department personnel involved. Licensees must link to the Tax Commissioner's licensee list on each ticket. Effective 90 days from passage (July 9, 2025)."],"issue_tags":["licensing","legalize","integrity"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"None of the listed bills is comparable. They are mostly sports-wagering authorization, tax or advertising bills (AK HB145/SB194, GA HB380/SB57, HI HB1308, CT HB05229, AR HB1861), none of which deals with charitable raffles. The closest overlap is AR HB1861, which regulates name, image and likeness games, whereas WV HB2491 lets NIL collectives and athletics departments fund themselves through raffles and expressly takes those raffles outside the state's sports wagering and interactive wagering statutes. It is unusual in being a narrow raffle-law carve-out for college athletics rather than a gaming regime.","watch":"Operators should watch the §47-21A-5(b)-(c) deeming language and the removal of geo-fencing and in-state server requirements, since they create a lightly regulated online prize product tied to college athletics and NIL collectives that sits outside §29-22D and §29-22E, and which may compete with or blur the line between raffles and wagering, including for collectibles and mystery-pack style offerings run through platform providers.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":30376,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:55:15.245Z"},"WV-HB2576":{"summary":"West Virginia HB2576 creates a new Article 22 in Chapter 18B authorizing state institutions of higher education to pay student-athletes for name, image, or likeness (NIL), including through revenue sharing, and shields institutions and athletes from adverse action by athletic associations and conferences. Gambling is not its subject, but §18B-22-4(8) bars student-athletes from earning NIL compensation in connection with casinos or gambling, including sports betting. It took effect on passage.","mechanics":["§18B-22-2(a) lets institutions contract to pay athletes for NIL, share athletic-department revenue, set conditions (including bans on prohibited or conflicting endorsements), require disclosure of similar deals, and help athletes find NIL opportunities.","§18B-22-2(b) lets athletes retain a registered athlete agent (§30-39-5) or a WV-licensed attorney, says athletes are not institutional employees, and bars use of legislatively appropriated money to pay athletes for NIL, endorsement, or revenue sharing.","§18B-22-4(8) prohibits athletes from earning NIL compensation tied to 'casinos or gambling, including sports betting.' Other barred categories are alcohol, adult entertainment, cannabis, controlled substances, performance enhancers, drug paraphernalia, and tobacco and nicotine products.","§18B-22-3 bars institutions, associations, and conferences from investigating, penalizing, or declaring athletes ineligible, or cutting scholarships, for activities the article authorizes. It also bars associations from acting against institutions and their employees or agents.","§18B-22-5 gives aggrieved athletes a claim for injunctive relief and lets institutions sue for actual damages, attorney fees, and injunctive relief if an association threatens or takes adverse action. Institutions are not liable for damages from decisions routinely made in intercollegiate athletics.","Coverage is limited to 'institutions' (state institutions of higher education under §18B-1-2); private colleges are not covered. The text sets no penalty for an athlete who violates §18B-22-4 and names no enforcement body."],"issue_tags":["advertising"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"The gambling piece is a single line in a college NIL bill. The listed Hawaii NIL bills (HI HB 2384, HI SB3263) and Illinois HB0307 cover student-athlete compensation, but their summaries do not show any gambling endorsement restriction. The Georgia high-school NIL bills (GA HB1276, GA HB383) and Delaware HB314 are also NIL or athlete-protection measures. CT HB05229 restricts gaming advertising on campuses, which targets operators rather than athletes. WV HB2576 instead bars the athlete from taking sports-betting or casino NIL money, with no operator-side prohibition or penalty.","watch":"Operators and their marketing affiliates should treat West Virginia student-athletes as off-limits for NIL endorsements, since §18B-22-4(8) covers casinos and gambling including sports betting and the text sets no penalty, leaving open how it will be enforced or whether other laws could reach the sponsor.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":9681,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:55:15.538Z"},"WV-SB576":{"summary":"WV SB576 amends the West Virginia Lottery Sports Wagering Act (Art. 22D) to authorize fixed odds wagering on live horse and dog racing, folding it into the definition of sports wagering and limiting it to licensed operators, licensed gaming facilities and management services providers. It imposes a 10 percent charge on adjusted gross receipts from fixed odds racing wagers and splits it between purse supplement funds and racetracks. It also bars racing participants and officials from betting on races they are involved in. The act takes effect 90 days from passage (July 11, 2025).","mechanics":["Authorization (new §29-22D-15a): fixed odds racing wagering is permitted only through a licensed operator, licensed gaming facility, or management services provider. Out-of-state horse race content must comply with the Interstate Horseracing Act of 1978; West Virginia horse or dog race content requires consent of the host racetrack and, for horse races, the recognized horsemen's group.","Definitions (§29-22D-3): adds fixed odds wagering and wager definitions for horse and dog racing, covering single-race, teaser, moneyline, in-race, proposition, straight and parlay bets or others approved by the Lottery. Racing is added to the definition of 'sports event' and fixed odds racing to 'sports wagering'. Pari-mutuel wagering under §19-23-12a and 12d remains excluded.","Rate and split (§§29-22D-17a and 17b): 10 percent of adjusted gross wagering receipts, collected as under §29-22D-16. Of that, 25 percent goes to the relevant Purse Supplement Fund and 25 percent is distributed equally among licensed thoroughbred racetracks (horse) or licensed dog racetracks (dog). For parlays, only the racing legs count toward these shares. The text as drafted allocates only 50 percent of the 10 percent, and does not say where the other 50 percent goes.","Fund distribution: horse fund pays out within 30 days of fiscal year start, 50 percent to thoroughbred purse funds under §19-23-9(b)(1) (pro rata by prior-year handle per the Racing Commission) and 50 percent equally among thoroughbred tracks. The dog fund pays 50 percent to the Greyhound Breeding Development Fund (§19-23-10) and 50 percent equally among dog tracks. Lottery administrative costs come from the Sports Wagering Fund.","Use of track funds (§29-22D-17c): money may support operations under Chapters 19-23, 22A, 22C, 22D and 22E, or capital improvements on or contiguous to track premises.","Integrity (§29-22D-25): operators may not knowingly accept wagers from owners, trainers, jockeys and their employees, or racing officials (stewards, judges, starter, timer, veterinarians and others listed) participating in the race. Rulemaking under §29-22D-4(c)(1) is expanded to cover fixed odds racing acceptance."],"issue_tags":["legalize","tax","licensing","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike the listed bills, which mostly authorize or tax general mobile sports wagering (AK HB145 and SB194, DE HB365, HI HB1308, GA SB57), this is a narrow amendment to an existing framework that adds fixed odds racing as a sports wagering product. Its 10 percent charge is earmarked to racing purses and tracks rather than a general fund or problem gambling. The listed bills do not address a comparable fixed odds racing carve-out, and the integrity provision here is limited to racing participants, not athlete-based prohibitions like those in GA SB57.","watch":"Watch the 10 percent levy and its apparent 50 percent allocation gap in §§29-22D-17a and 17b, the Lottery's forthcoming rules on fixed odds racing, and the host track and horsemen's consent requirement for West Virginia content, which gives racing interests leverage over operator access.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":25234,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:55:20.463Z"},"WY-HB162":{"summary":"WY HB0162 would legalize online casino-style gaming (internet poker, blackjack, slots and similar games) in Wyoming under the Wyoming gaming commission, through new W.S. 9-24-201 to 9-24-207. Only 'qualified gaming entities' already live in at least three states can hold operator permits, revenue is taxed at 16% of interactive gaming revenue, and unlicensed operation draws civil penalties. The bill died in committee and was returned under HR 5-4.","mechanics":["Eligibility: an operator must be a 'qualified gaming entity' offering interactive gaming in at least 3 US states under a state regulatory structure (9-24-201(a)(xi)). The commission gets an abbreviated application for existing Wyoming online sports wagering permitholders and may deem them fully qualified without further investigation (9-24-203(b)). It must issue at least 5 operator permits if enough qualified applicants apply (9-24-203(k)).","Fees: operator application and renewal fee $2,500; initial operator permit $100,000 and renewal $50,000, each term 5 years; vendor permit $10,000 initial and $5,000 renewal, 5 years (9-24-203(b), (c), (e)). Permit fees go to the commission gaming account, with amounts over $500,000 swept quarterly to the general fund. Vendors, including geolocation, KYC, payment processor and game provider services, must be permitted (9-24-203(d)).","Tax: 16% of monthly interactive gaming revenue, remitted by the 15th (9-24-204). Revenue is wagers in cash or equivalents minus payouts and federal excise taxes, excluding free wagers and promotional play (9-24-201(a)(v)). The first $300,000 goes to the department of health for county problem-gambling programs. The remainder is split 40% counties by population, 50% school foundation program account and 10% commission gaming account.","Operations: patrons must be 21 or older (9-24-205) and physically in Wyoming, or in a 'permissible jurisdiction' under a reciprocal agreement (9-24-206), verified by geolocation. Live game simulcasts are allowed from studios anywhere but not in Wyoming. Gaming cannot be conducted from a physical location built for patrons to visit, and a wager is deemed to occur where the patron is located (9-24-202(d)). Excluded activities include fantasy sports, lotteries and tribal gaming conducted exclusively on Indian lands.","Enforcement: unpermitted operation draws a civil penalty of $50,000 for a first offense and $100,000 for later offenses. Allowing underage play or providing public-access devices without a permit draws $25,000 and then $50,000. Similar events within a month count as one offense (9-24-207). Denial grounds include criminal history, nondisclosure and insufficient financial responsibility, with a 90-day decision deadline, a 30-day cure window, and contested-case review under the Wyoming APA (9-24-203(h), (j), (o)).","Timing: the commission must adopt rules by December 31, 2025 (Sec. 3). Sections 1 and 2 are effective January 1, 2026. Sec. 2 carves interactive gaming out of the 'gambling' definition at 6-7-101(a)(iii)(P) and adds a duty to the commission at 11-25-104(o)(iii)."],"issue_tags":["legalize","licensing","tax"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Among the listed bills, the closest is AR HB1861, which also creates an 'Interactive Gaming Act' for online casino games but pairs it with felony penalties for illegal online casinos and sports betting. WY HB0162 relies on civil penalties of $50,000 to $100,000 and a carve-out from the gambling definition instead. Most other listed bills (AK HB145 and SB194, DE HB365, HI HB 1308) cover sports wagering only, and CT SB01464 touches multijurisdictional peer-to-peer casino games but does not authorize general iGaming. Unusual features of the Wyoming bill are the three-state incumbency requirement for operators, the 5-permit floor, the fast-track for existing sports wagering permitholders, and reciprocal agreements covering both inbound and outbound play.","watch":"An operator should check the three-state 'qualified gaming entity' threshold and the abbreviated route for Wyoming sports wagering permitholders, along with the 16% tax base that excludes free wagers and promotional play, though the bill is dead and would need reintroduction.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":23376,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:55:21.108Z"},"AR-HB1717":{"summary":"Act 952 creates the Arkansas Children and Teens' Online Privacy Protection Act (Ark. Code 4-88-1501 to -1505), a COPPA-style statute that extends privacy protections to children 12 and under and teens 13 to 16. It restricts operators' collection, retention and targeted-advertising use of minors' personal information, and requires notice, teen or parental consent, and access, deletion and correction rights. Enforcement lies exclusively with the Attorney General, with no private right of action, and the Act takes effect July 1, 2026. It does not address gambling or gaming.","mechanics":["Coverage (4-88-1502): 'child' is 12 or younger and 'teen' is 13 to under 17 in Arkansas. 'Operator' means a commercial operator of a website, online service, app or mobile app that collects personal information. It applies if the service is directed at children or teens, or the operator has actual knowledge it is collecting their data. Exclusions: nonprofits exempt under FTC Act s.5, state and local government and public schools, and 'interactive gaming platforms' that comply with COPPA.","Prohibitions (4-88-1503(a)): operators may not collect minors' data in violation of the notice and consent rules, use it for targeted advertising or let others do so, collect it beyond the context of the service or what is legally required, or retain it longer than reasonably necessary to fulfil the requested service.","Notice and consent (4-88-1503(b)(1)-(2)): operators must give clear and conspicuous notice of data collected, purposes, disclosure practices, and categories of data and third parties. Teen data requires consent from the parent or the teen, which can be given through terms of service or privacy policy acknowledgement. There are broad exceptions for providing the requested service, internal operations, security and fraud prevention, legal claims and legal compliance.","Rights and duties (4-88-1503(b)(3)-(6)): parents of children, and teens themselves, may request deletion, correction and access. Operators may not require a child to disclose more data than reasonably necessary to play a game, win a prize or take part in another activity, and must maintain reasonable security practices. Operators may terminate service if consent is refused, but not because of a deletion request where the service can run without the data (4-88-1503(d)).","Enforcement (4-88-1504, 4-88-1503(e)): the Attorney General has exclusive authority and may seek injunctions, damages, restitution and other relief. There is no private right of action. Violations of (b)(2) and (b)(3) are treated as unfair or deceptive acts under the Deceptive Trade Practices Act, subject to the cross-references in 4-88-1503(e).","Construction and effective date: nothing requires operators to collect age data they do not already collect or to implement age-gating or age verification (4-88-1505). The Act has a severability clause and takes effect July 1, 2026."],"issue_tags":["advertising"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"This is a general minors' online privacy statute, not gambling legislation, so none of the listed gaming bills (MD, IL, MA, ME, DE, CT, DC) are directly comparable. It is closest to the social media and minors bills: CO HB1287 and SB086, GA HB1296 and SB351, IN SB0011 and CT SB01295. Unlike the Georgia and Indiana bills, which centre on age verification and parental consent for social media access, Arkansas expressly declines to require age-gating or age verification (4-88-1505) and regulates data practices rather than access. It also confines enforcement to the Attorney General, with no private action.","watch":"Operators with Arkansas users under 17 should check whether they have 'actual knowledge' of minor users and whether their products are 'directed at' teens, since either triggers the targeted-advertising ban, the consent, deletion and access duties and AG enforcement from July 1, 2026, while the exemption for 'interactive gaming platforms' is tied to COPPA compliance and is not defined for wagering or card-break products.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":26438,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:55:26.414Z"},"WY-HB299":{"summary":"HB0299 amends Wyoming's existing online sports wagering chapter (W.S. 9-24-101 and 9-24-102). It adds the Northern Arapaho and Eastern Shoshone tribes of the Wind River Reservation to the definition of \"qualified gaming entity\", alongside operators already live in at least three U.S. jurisdictions. It also amends the geolocation rule for sports wagering operators. The last recorded action is a failed Committee of the Whole vote on 2025-02-27 (12-12-7).","mechanics":["Sec. 1 amends W.S. 9-24-101(a)(x): \"qualified gaming entity\" now has three alternatives: (A) an entity offering online sports wagering in not fewer than three U.S. jurisdictions under a state regulatory structure; (B) the Northern Arapaho tribe; (C) the Eastern Shoshone tribe.","The tribes qualify by name, so they are not subject to the three-jurisdiction operating-history test in (A).","Sec. 1 amends W.S. 9-24-102(b)(i)(F), which directs the commission to adopt rules requiring each operator to use a geolocation system. Sub-(I) requires a patron to be physically in Wyoming when wagering unless the commission authorizes otherwise.","Sub-(II) requires all transactions to be initiated and received by a patron physically in the state, excluding patrons on Indian lands as defined by 25 U.S.C. § 2703(4). The excerpt has no strike or underline marks, so the exact edit to this language cannot be identified from the text.","No tax rate, fee, or enforcement change appears in the text; the commission's existing rulemaking authority under 9-24-102(b) is unchanged.","Sec. 2: effective July 1, 2025."],"issue_tags":["licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike the full-framework bills in AK HB145 and SB194, DE HB365 and HI HB1308, which create licensing, tax and regulatory structures, WY HB0299 is a narrow amendment to an existing online wagering chapter and sets no tax or fee. None of the other listed bills deals with tribal eligibility. The closest analogue on licence eligibility is IL HB2761, which concerns who may hold master licences, but it addresses sports teams, not tribes. The unusual feature here is the named-tribe carve-in to the \"qualified gaming entity\" definition plus a geolocation change involving Indian lands.","watch":"Watch how naming the two tribes as qualified gaming entities, without the three-jurisdiction test, changes who can hold licences or operate in Wyoming, and how the Indian-lands geolocation language would be applied in commission rules, given that the bill failed in Committee of the Whole and could return in amended form.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Engrossed","text_chars":2273,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:55:29.354Z"},"AR-HB1726":{"summary":"HB1726 would add Subchapter 15 to Ark. Code Title 4, Chapter 88 (the Arkansas Kids Online Safety Act), imposing a duty of care, default-on safeguards, parental tools, disclosure rules and algorithmic transparency obligations on \"covered platforms\" targeted to minors (16 and under). Enforcement is by the Attorney General alone under the Deceptive Trade Practices Act, with a 90-day cure period. Gambling appears only as one of several harm categories: platforms may not facilitate gambling advertising to known minors. The bill died in Senate committee at sine die adjournment in 2025.","mechanics":["Coverage (§ 4-88-1502(4)): an entity operating an online platform, messaging app or video streaming service that connects to the internet and is \"targeted to minors.\" \"Minor\" means 16 or younger and \"child\" means 12 or younger. Exclusions include common carriers, broadband, email, schools, libraries, nonprofits, B2B software, and interactive gaming platforms that comply with COPPA.","Duty of care (§ 4-88-1503(a)): reasonable measures to avoid heightened risk of harm from features the platform knows minors use. Listed harms include addiction-like usage patterns, \"promotion and marketing of narcotic drugs, tobacco products, gambling, or alcohol,\" and predatory or deceptive marketing and financial harms.","Advertising ban (§ 4-88-1504(f)): a platform that knows an individual is a minor shall not facilitate advertising of narcotic drugs, tobacco, gambling or alcohol to that minor. Video streaming services get a parallel \"safeguard against\" standard in § 4-88-1509(d)(3)(G). Ad labelling and \"why am I seeing this\" disclosures are required under § 4-88-1505(c).","Safeguards and parental tools (§ 4-88-1504): most protective defaults for known minors, limits on autoplay and rewards for time spent, opt-out of personalized recommendations, account deletion, and time limits. Parents can restrict purchases and financial transactions and view time metrics. For known children (12 and under) the controls are on by default and verifiable parental consent is required. Reports must be answered within 10 days for platforms with more than 10 million monthly US users and within 21 days for smaller ones.","Filter-bubble rules (§ 4-88-1508): platforms using an \"opaque algorithm\" must give notice, disclose inputs and optimization targets every six months, and offer an input-transparent version without price discrimination. Upstream search syndication providers must supply the same input-transparent algorithm.","Enforcement and timing: violations are unfair and deceptive acts under § 4-88-101 et seq., enforceable solely by the AG (no private right of action), with written notice and a 90-day cure period (§ 4-88-1506). Knowledge standard is actual or fairly implied (§ 4-88-1502(8)), and no age verification is required (§ 4-88-1509(b)(2)). A nine-member Kids Online Safety Council sits in the Department of Commerce (§ 4-88-1507). Effective July 1, 2026 (Section 2)."],"issue_tags":["advertising","integrity"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"Of the listed bills, this is closest to the social media minor-protection measures: CO HB1287 and CO SB086, CT SB01295, GA HB1296 and SB351, and IN SB0011. GA HB1296 and IN SB0011 center on age verification and parental consent for access, whereas AR HB1726 expressly disclaims any age-gating or verification mandate (§ 4-88-1509(b)(2)) and instead uses a knowledge-based duty of care with default-on safeguards, modeled on the federal KOSA. Unlike the gaming bills listed (CT SB01464, MD HB1343, IL HB2239, CT HB05038 on prediction markets and under-21 advertising), it does not regulate gambling operators or licensing. Its only gambling hook is a minor-directed advertising bar applied to platforms, not to the advertisers.","watch":"Even though the bill is dead, an operator should note that its advertising ban and duty-of-care language (§§ 4-88-1503(a)(5), 4-88-1504(f)) would reach gambling advertising on minor-targeted platforms under a \"knows\" standard that includes fairly implied knowledge, so ad-targeting and affiliate placements near youth audiences could be exposed if a similar bill is reintroduced.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Draft","text_chars":40249,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:55:32.244Z"},"CO-HB1287":{"summary":"CO HB25-1287 adds new duties for social media companies in the Colorado Consumer Protection Act (C.R.S. title 6, art. 1, part 16) to protect users under 18. It requires an age assurance system, minor-facing and parental control tools on by default, privacy-protective default settings, and a ban on manipulative interfaces. It treats engagement-maximizing design as 'heightened risk of harm to minors' processing under the Colorado Privacy Act. Violations are deceptive trade practices. The bill has no gaming or wagering provisions and is relevant here only as an adjacent child-safety and design-regulation model.","mechanics":["Age assurance (6-1-1601(1), 6-1-1603): companies must use measures reasonably calculated to identify minors with at least 95% accuracy. They must offer an appeal process with documentary evidence and decide within 30 days. Age-assurance data must be segregated and used only for that purpose.","Minor tools (6-1-1604): minors must be able to disable 'covered features' (autoplay, infinite scroll, notifications, rewards for time spent, personalized recommendations, likes/comments, geolocation sharing), set daily time limits, schedule breaks, and report problems. Disabling personalized recommendations is on by default.","Parental tools (6-1-1605): the tools are tiered. Parents of users known to be under 13 can change settings, disable features, restrict purchases and transactions, and delete data. Parents of users 13-17 get view-only access plus time limits and purchase restrictions. All tools default on.","Privacy safeguards (6-1-1606): minor accounts default to the most protective settings, with visibility and DMs limited to connected accounts, no search indexing, and no recommendation to unconnected adults. Users known to be under 16 need verifiable parental consent to change defaults.","Dark patterns and engagement design (6-1-1607, 6-1-1608): platforms may not use interfaces that substantially subvert user autonomy to get minors or parents to give consent, disable safeguards, or share data. Engagement-extending designs count as heightened-risk processing under 6-1-1303(14.5), triggering the 6-1-1309.5 data protection assessment requirements.","Enforcement and timing (6-1-1609, 6-1-1612, Sec. 3-4): knowing or reckless violations, and aiding and abetting, are deceptive trade practices under new 6-1-105(1)(iiii). The attorney general may make rules. Remedies are cumulative. Section 6-1-1608 takes effect Oct. 1, 2025. The rest takes effect after the 90-day post-adjournment period, subject to referendum petition. The 'social media platform' definition excludes interactive or virtual gaming services (6-1-1601(13)(b)(XVI))."],"issue_tags":["advertising","integrity"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"The bill is a child-online-safety measure like GA HB1296 and SB351, IN SB0011, AR HB1717 and HB1726, and CT SB01295, and unlike the gaming bills on the list. Compared with the Georgia and Indiana bills, which center on age verification and parental consent to access, Colorado requires 95%-accurate age assurance and then tiered default-on tools, plus an engagement-design rule tied to the Colorado Privacy Act. None of the listed gaming bills (e.g., CT SB01464, MD HB1343, AR HB1861) is directly comparable. Its only gaming link is the exclusion of interactive and virtual gaming services from 'social media platform'.","watch":"An operator should confirm that any social or community features it runs (chat, leaderboards, shared feeds, live-break communities, user-generated content) fall under the 6-1-1601(13)(b)(XVI) gaming exclusion or the e-commerce exclusion, since otherwise the age assurance, default-on engagement limits and CPA data assessment duties could reach it.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":31934,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:55:32.253Z"},"CT-HB7215":{"summary":"Connecticut HB 7215 is a one-section bill directing the Commissioner of Mental Health and Addiction Services to set up a program for prevention, treatment and rehabilitation of individuals and families affected by \"Internet gaming disorder.\" It is a public-health services measure only: it adds no licensing, tax, advertising or operator-conduct requirements, and the disorder is defined by reference to videogames, not wagering.","mechanics":["Section 1 (new, effective October 1, 2025): the Commissioner of Mental Health and Addiction Services \"shall establish\" a program for treatment and rehabilitation of individuals and families affected by Internet gaming disorder.","The program must provide prevention, treatment and rehabilitation services.","The commissioner may contract with subregional planning and action councils and nonprofit organizations to deliver services.","The commissioner may charge a reasonable sliding-scale fee to participants who can afford to pay.","\"Internet gaming disorder\" is defined as chronic and progressive preoccupation with videogames and the urge to play, with behavior that compromises, disrupts or damages personal, family or vocational pursuits.","No funding source, appropriation, operator assessment, reporting duty or enforcement mechanism is specified, though the bill was referred to Appropriations after the Public Health committee's joint favorable report."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"This bill is not comparable to the listed internet gaming bills (IL HB2239/HB3080/HB4797, MD HB17/HB1343/SB340, MA H332/H4431/S235), which create licensing and tax regimes for online casino gaming, or to the sweepstakes and illegal-market bills (MD HB1140/SB860, AR HB1861). It also differs from the social-media minor-protection bills (e.g., AR HB1726, CO HB1287, GA HB1296), which impose duties on platforms. Its unusual feature is that it targets videogame addiction rather than gambling, and it funds and regulates nothing on the industry side.","watch":"Little direct exposure for operators, but watch for amendments that broaden the definition beyond videogames to cover online wagering, or that add a funding mechanism such as an assessment on licensees to pay for the program.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Comm Sub","text_chars":1450,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:55:36.560Z"},"CO-SB86":{"summary":"CO SB25-086 would add duties for social media companies to Colorado's consumer protection code (C.R.S. 6-1-1601 et seq.). Companies would publish policies on illicit drugs, unlawful firearm sales, minor sex trafficking and sexual exploitation material, remove violators on fixed timelines, file annual reports, and meet search-warrant deadlines. Violations would be deceptive trade practices. The Governor vetoed it and the Senate passed an override vote on 2025-04-25. It is not a gambling bill, and gaming-focused platforms are carved out of the minor-notification section.","mechanics":["Covered 'social media platform' (6-1-1601(16)) is an internet service with Colorado users whose substantial function is social interaction, with accounts and user-posted content. It excludes e-commerce with limited interaction, streaming of licensed media, provider-created content, news, education, career networking and others (16)(b)-(c).","Section 6-1-1602(1) requires that on or after Jan 1, 2026, platforms show minors a brain-and-health notification or a pop-up after one cumulative hour in 24 hours or between 10 p.m. and 6 a.m., repeating every 30 minutes. Under 6-1-1602(5), a service whose predominant or exclusive function is interactive or virtual gaming, or uploading content for such gaming, is not a social media platform for this section.","Section 6-1-1603 requires published policies by July 1, 2026 covering 'subject uses' (sale or advertising of illicit substances, unlawful firearm sales, minor sex trafficking, sexually exploitative material), enforcement, and law-enforcement referral. Material updates must be posted within 14 days.","Section 6-1-1607 sets a removal process on a report or detection of a subject use: a 48-hour false-report screen, account suspension during a 10-day determination, termination within 24 hours of a violation finding, and an appeal decided within 14 days. Licensed marijuana sales or advertising to adults 21 and over is permitted if compliant with the 44-10-203(3)(a) rules.","Sections 6-1-1604 to 1606 require annual reports to the Department of Law and public minor-usage reports, disaggregated by age category and gender, on enforcement, appeals, referrals, age verification, and safety tools. The first reports are due within 180 days of the effective date, then by Feb 1 each year. Companies must certify reasonable efforts at accuracy.","Section 6-1-1608 requires a staffed law-enforcement hotline and compliance with Colorado search warrants within 72 hours, extendable by a court on good cause. It does not apply to platforms with fewer than 1 million monthly users. Under 6-1-1609 and 6-1-105(1)(iiii), knowing or reckless violations are deceptive trade practices, and the AG may make rules (6-1-1612)."],"issue_tags":["integrity"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"Nothing listed is directly comparable on gambling terms. Among the social media bills, GA HB1296 and SB351 and IN SB0011 focus on age verification and parental consent for minors, while CO SB086 uses disclosure, takedown timelines and law-enforcement cooperation, with no parental-consent mandate. CT SB01295 and MD SB932 are the nearest in subject matter, and CT SB01295 adds online safety centers and cyberbullying policies. The listed AR, IL, MD and MA bills authorize or ban online gaming, which this bill does not address except for the gaming exclusion in 6-1-1602(5).","watch":"An operator with in-app social or community features should check whether it falls outside the 'social media platform' definition, since the interactive-gaming exclusion in 6-1-1602(5) covers only the minor-notification section and not the policy, reporting, removal and warrant duties, and the bill's status after the veto and override vote is unresolved.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":37262,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:55:40.944Z"},"GA-HB1296":{"summary":"Georgia HB1296 (House Judiciary substitute) adds a new Article 2 to Chapter 5 of Title 39 requiring social media companies to make commercially reasonable efforts to verify the age of Georgia account holders and to obtain express parent or guardian consent before a Georgia resident under 16 can hold an account. It also imposes restrictions on minors' accounts, requires parental supervision tools, and gives the Consumer Protection Division of the Department of Law exclusive enforcement authority with a 45-day cure period. It does not regulate gambling, and it expressly carves interactive and virtual gaming services out of the definition of social media platform.","mechanics":["Minor is an individual under 16 who is not emancipated or married, where the company reasonably believes or actually knows of the age (39-5-10(7)); the company must treat a person as a minor if it verifies that age.","39-5-11(a): the company must make commercially reasonable age verification efforts, with certainty appropriate to its information practices, or apply the minor protections to all account holders. 39-5-11(b) bars minor accounts without express parental consent, and lists methods such as a signed form, toll-free call, video call, parent government ID (deleted after confirmation), or email plus verification steps.","39-5-12: for minor accounts, bars adult direct messages unless already connected, advertising based on personal information other than age and location, and collection or use of personal information beyond what is adequate, relevant and reasonably necessary.","39-5-13: parents who consented must get supervision tools, including viewing privacy settings, setting daily time limits and scheduling breaks.","Enforcement (39-5-15): the Division has exclusive authority. The AG may impose an administrative fine of up to $2,500 per violation. A court may award injunctions, forfeiture of profits paid to the aggrieved person, civil penalties of up to $2,500 per violation, damages, and attorney fees and costs. A violation of an order carries up to $5,000 per violation. A written notice and 45-day cure period applies before an action. 39-5-17 voids contractual waivers and choice-of-law clauses.","The definition of social media platform (39-5-10(10)(B)) excludes 21 categories, including (vi) interactive gaming, virtual gaming or services for creating and uploading content for gaming or associated entertainment, (iv) preselected news, sports and entertainment content, and (ii) services that bar minors and use commercially reasonable age assurance. Effective July 1, 2025 (Section 3)."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"This is a general social media age-verification and parental-consent bill, so it is closest to IN SB0011 (verifiable parental consent for users under 16), CO SB086 and CO HB1287 (minor protections on social media), AR HB1726 and AR HB1717 (kids' online safety and privacy) and CT SB01295 (social media online safety center). It is not comparable to the gaming bills listed from AR, IL, MD, DE and others, since it contains no wagering licensing, tax or advertising rules. The notable feature for gaming is the exclusion of interactive and virtual gaming services from coverage, plus a 45-day cure right and an enforcement structure limited to the Division, with no private right of action.","watch":"Confirm that sportsbook, iGaming or card-break and community features with user profiles, chat or feeds fall within the interactive gaming exclusion in 39-5-10(10)(B)(vi); the exclusion covers services for the purpose of interactive gaming, educational entertainment, or associated entertainment, and a platform with broader social features could still be treated as a social media platform subject to the age-verification and consent duties.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Comm Sub","text_chars":15970,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:55:41.461Z"},"IA-HF662":{"summary":"HF 662 is Iowa's omnibus state-government reorganization bill. The opening Division I merges the Department of Public Health, the Department on Aging and parts of the Department of Human Rights into a single Department of Health and Human Services. The text supplied is truncated and covers only Sections 1 to 38 of a roughly 1,500-page bill. It touches gambling only in passing, through conforming edits and an unchanged carve-out for racing and gaming commission funds. The bill was withdrawn on 2023-03-15.","mechanics":["Sections 1-38 replace references to the Department of Human Services, the Iowa Department of Public Health and the Department on Aging with the new Department of Health and Human Services (DHHS), and rename 'substance abuse' as 'substance use disorder'.","Section 17 amends Code 12.10 (state officers must deposit 90 percent of collections with the treasurer within ten days). It keeps the exemption for funds the state racing and gaming commission receives under Code 99D.7 and 99D.14 and adds DHHS to the exempt list.","Section 33 amends Code 22.7(35), keeping confidential the records of the gambling treatment program, now attributed to DHHS (formerly the Department of Public Health), except as otherwise provided.","Sections 9-14 move to DHHS the debts the Department of Inspections, Appeals, and Licensing collects through the 8A.504 setoff procedure, plus the 10A.108 lien and distress-warrant powers for improperly obtained benefits. Section 11 sets the 8A.504 setoff priority order (child support first, then the clerk of court, the college student aid commission and the investigations division).","Section 15 amends Code 11.5B so the Auditor of State is reimbursed for audits of DHHS and no longer lists the Department of Public Health separately.","The title also refers to salaries of appointed state officers, penalties, appropriations, Code editor directives, transition provisions and effective dates, but that text falls outside the excerpt supplied."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"This bill is not comparable to the gambling-focused bills listed. The nearest structural parallels are the omnibus appropriation and reorganization vehicles: LA HB624 (moving programs from DCFS to the Workforce Commission), IL HB2949 (FY27 budget implementation) and MI HB4437 (omnibus appropriations). NE LB402 and LB 415 amend the Gambling Winnings Setoff for Outstanding Debt Act, but in the excerpt HF 662 does not touch gambling-winnings setoff and only reorganizes the general 8A.504 debt-setoff procedure. Unusually, it is a mega-bill that touches gaming only through conforming edits.","watch":"Little here affects operators, but confirm in the unseen portion that the reorganization leaves intact the racing and gaming commission's funds, Code chapters 99D, 99F and 99G, and the gambling treatment program funding, and that the 8A.504 setoff changes do not affect payouts to winners.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":4169680,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:55:46.990Z"},"GA-SB351":{"summary":"Georgia's omnibus child-online-safety act has three parts. It adds school-side mandates: social media bans on school networks, acceptable-use policies, technology protection measures and anti-cyberbullying rules. It creates a new Title 39, Chapter 6, requiring social media platforms to make commercially reasonable efforts to verify account holders' ages and obtain parental consent before letting under-16s hold accounts. According to the caption, it also adds a civil remedy and Attorney General fines against commercial entities that distribute material harmful to minors without age verification. The supplied text cuts off partway through proposed Code Section 39-6-2(c), so the later provisions are described here only from the caption.","mechanics":["Proposed Code Section 39-6-1 defines 'minor' as a Georgia resident actually known or reasonably believed by the platform to be under 16. 'Social media platform' means a forum for creating profiles, posting, forming mutual connections and interacting publicly and privately.","The 'social media platform' definition carves out 22 categories, including email, peer-to-peer payments (39-6-1(6)(U)), online shopping or ecommerce, and 'interactive gaming, virtual gaming' services and related content-upload services (39-6-1(6)(F)). It also excludes services that bar minors and use commercially reasonable age assurance.","Section 39-6-2(a)-(c): a platform must make commercially reasonable efforts to verify account holders' ages at a level of certainty appropriate to its risks, or else apply the minor conditions to all account holders. It must treat anyone it verifies as under 16 as a minor. It may not permit a minor to hold an account without express parental or guardian consent, and acceptable methods begin with a signed form returned to the platform. The text ends mid-list.","Per the caption only, since the text is truncated: platforms must give parents certain information on request, the Attorney General enforces, waivers are barred, and commercial entities distributing material harmful to minors face a civil damages remedy unless they use reasonable age verification. Age verification data may not be retained, and the AG may impose fines.","Schools (Sections 2-3 and 2-4): by Oct. 1, 2025, local governing bodies must adopt acceptable-use policies and use technology protection measures that meet Department of Education standards. By April 1, 2026, they must adopt a policy prohibiting student access to social media on school equipment or networks, with limited supervised educational exceptions, and submit it to the Department of Education for review. The State Board of Education may withhold state funds for noncompliance.","Section 2-5 revises the bullying statute (20-2-751.4) to cover cyberbullying, including via social media platforms. Local boards must adopt compliant policies by July 1, 2026 and evaluate cyberbullying-detection technology, and a noncompliant system is ineligible for state funding under Code Sections 20-2-161 and 20-2-260."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"This is a child-online-safety statute, not a gaming bill. Nothing comparable on wagering appears in the listed bills, and the Arkansas, Connecticut, Illinois, Maryland and Massachusetts gaming and iGaming bills do not overlap with it. Its closest peers are the social media and minors bills: IN SB0011 (verifiable parental consent for users under 16, with AG enforcement), CO HB1287 and CO SB086, AR HB1717 and HB1726, CT SB01295, and MD SB932. Georgia's distinguishing features are the 'commercially reasonable' age-verification standard, the option to apply minor protections to all users, an extensive K-12 school-policy and technology-filtering regime, and a pornography-style age-verification civil remedy.","watch":"An operator should confirm that its apps fall within the exclusion for interactive or virtual gaming and peer-to-peer payments in 39-6-1(6)(F) and (U), and that any social, chat or community features do not make it a covered 'social media platform' subject to age-verification and parental-consent duties.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":60505,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:55:49.101Z"},"IA-HSB126":{"summary":"This is a House State Government Committee study bill, renumbered as HF 662, that restructures Iowa's executive branch. Division I, the only part in the supplied text, folds the Department of Human Rights, the Department on Aging and the Iowa Department of Public Health into a consolidated Department of Health and Human Services and makes conforming Code changes. The text provided stops at section 34 of roughly 1,568 pages, so later divisions, appropriations and effective dates are not visible. Nothing in the visible text changes gaming or wagering law.","mechanics":["Division I (secs. 1-34) renames and consolidates agency references to the 'department of health and human services' throughout the Code, including 7E.5 (principal departments list), 8A.504 (debt setoff), 10A.108 (improper benefit liens) and 11.5B (audit reimbursement).","Sec. 5 amends 7E.5 to drop the separate listings for the Iowa department of public health, the department on aging and the department of human rights, and relettering the remaining departments.","Sec. 13 (10A.104) leaves in place the existing exemption of the administrator of the racing and gaming commission from appointment by the director of inspections and appeals, and adds an exemption for the child advocacy board administrator.","Sec. 18 (12.10) updates the exemption from the ten-day, ninety-percent deposit rule to cover the HHS director, and keeps the existing exemption for funds the racing and gaming commission receives under 99D.7 and 99D.14.","Secs. 10-11 (8A.504) make only HHS nomenclature changes to the state debt setoff procedure and claim priority (child support recovery services first, then the clerk of court, college student aid, DIA investigations, and other agencies); no gambling-winnings setoff is added.","Sec. 6 (8.39) leaves the interdepartmental transfer cap at one-tenth of one percent of the funding source, and the 50 percent cap for non-entitlement appropriations, and treats HHS foster care, state supplementary assistance, medical assistance and the family investment program as entitlement appropriations."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No other listed state bill is a direct comparison on gaming. The closest in form are omnibus and budget vehicles such as OH HB33 and HB96, NC H259, MI HB4437 and HB4706, and IL HB2949. LA HB624 is the nearest on substance, since it also moves human-services programs between agencies. Unlike NE LB402 and LB415, which add unemployment overpayments to the Gambling Winnings Setoff for Outstanding Debt Act, this bill's setoff amendments in 8A.504 are only HHS renaming and add no gambling-winnings setoff.","watch":"A lawyer for sports-betting operators should check the unseen divisions for any change to Iowa Code chapters 99D, 99F or 99G or the racing and gaming commission's structure, because the visible text only preserves the commission's existing exemptions in 10A.104 and 12.10.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":4248307,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:55:53.731Z"},"IA-SF514":{"summary":"Enrolled SF 514 reorganizes Iowa state government by creating a unified Department of Health and Human Services and conforming the Code to it. The portion supplied (Division I, Secs. 1-39, through p. 19) is almost entirely name-change and agency-transfer amendments, with transfers of functions from the economic development authority and the department of human rights. Gaming appears only in passing, in the Sec. 12.10 deposit exemption and the Sec. 22.7 confidentiality of gambling treatment records. The text provided is truncated, so the later divisions, appropriations and effective-date provisions are not visible.","mechanics":["Sec. 5 amends Code 7E.5(1) to list the department of health and human services (created in 217.1) as a state department. It also revises the descriptions of the public health, aging and human rights departments.","Sec. 12 rewrites 10A.108 (improper benefit or provider payment debts) so the debt and lien run to the department of health and human services. The lien lasts ten years from filing, with unlimited ten-year extensions, and collection is by distress warrant through the department of inspections, appeals, and licensing.","Sec. 16 amends 12.10 (ten-day, 90 percent deposit rule for state officers). It keeps the exemption for funds the state racing and gaming commission receives under 99D.7 and 99D.14 and adds the director of the department of health and human services to the exempted list.","Sec. 32 amends 22.7 (confidential public records), including subsection 35, which keeps the records of the department's gambling treatment program participants confidential. It only conforms the agency name to the department of public health and human services.","Secs. 19-25 move the Iowa commission on volunteer service and the community programs account from the economic development authority to the department of health and human services, and Sec. 24 revises the RefugeeRISE program provisions.","Secs. 8 and 10 and others make conforming changes to state debt-setoff and fuel-purchase provisions. No tax rates, license requirements or wagering rules are changed in the visible text, and the bill's contingent effective date and 07/01/2023 applicability date are not in the portion supplied."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills is a close comparator. The closest in kind are the omnibus appropriations and revisory bills (e.g., MI HB4437, OH HB33, IL HB4844) and LA HB624, which also shifts programs between agencies. Unlike NE LB402 and NE LB 415, which expressly add unemployment overpayment debt to the Gambling Winnings Setoff for Outstanding Debt Act, nothing in the visible text of IA SF514 adds a gambling-winnings setoff. The bill is a structural agency merger, and its gaming content is limited to conforming amendments.","watch":"Check the truncated remainder of the bill (later divisions, the appropriations, and the Code editor and transition provisions) for any change to the racing and gaming commission, the gambling treatment program funding or the Chapter 99D, 99F or 99G references, since the visible text only conforms agency names.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":3780780,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:55:55.621Z"},"IL-HB4844":{"summary":"Illinois HB4844 (P.A. 103-0605), the First 2024 General Revisory Act, is a non-substantive statutory clean-up that combines conflicting amendments, renumbers duplicate section numbers, fixes cross-references and deletes obsolete text. Section 1(b) states it makes no substantive change in the law. The only gaming-related content in the text provided is an emergency-rulemaking provision for the Illinois Gaming Board, renumbered within the Administrative Procedure Act.","mechanics":["Section 1(b): the Act expressly makes no substantive change in the law. It reconciles multiple amendments and makes technical corrections. Public Acts 102-1119 through 103-583 were considered.","Section 10 renumbers the Administrative Procedure Act emergency-rulemaking sections, which were previously duplicate Section 5-45.35 versions. Examples: 5-45.38 and 5-45.48 were formerly numbered 5-45.35 and carry the old number as a strike-through.","Gaming provision: Sec. 5-45.48 authorizes the Illinois Gaming Board to adopt emergency rules under Section 5-45 implementing P.A. 103-550's changes to Section 9 of the Illinois Gambling Act (occupational licenses). The section is repealed January 1, 2025, one year after P.A. 103-550 took effect on 1-1-24.","Section 5 amends Regulatory Sunset Act Sec. 4.39 to list Acts repealed January 1, 2029 and December 31, 2029. The listed Acts are professional licensing statutes, none gaming-related.","Section 15 conforms Freedom of Information Act Sections 7 and 7.5 (exemptions), fixing duplicate lettering and presenting Sec. 7.5 in versions before and after P.A. 103-472. The text provided cuts off partway through Sec. 7.5.","Status: signed, with last action July 1, 2024 recorded as the effective date."],"issue_tags":["licensing"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills is a close comparable. Most are appropriations or omnibus government bills (IA HF662/SF514, MI HB4292, OH HB33, NM HB2). HB4844 is a pure revisory act, unlike the substantive gaming bills in other states such as OH HB344 (electronic instant bingo and lottery terminals), NC H999 (video gaming terminals) and NY S 10092 (gambling ads to minors). Its only gaming content is a renumbered emergency-rulemaking authorization for the Illinois Gaming Board, which is a technical conforming change rather than new policy.","watch":"Little to watch: confirm that the renumbered Sec. 5-45.48 (Gaming Board emergency rules for Gambling Act Section 9 occupational licensing under P.A. 103-550) is unchanged, noting that it sunsets January 1, 2025, and that any substantive changes would be unexpected given the Act's no-substantive-change statement.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":3056146,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:55:56.970Z"},"IA-SSB1123":{"summary":"This is a Senate State Government committee bill (SSB 1123, later SF 514) restructuring Iowa executive-branch agencies. It chiefly merges the departments of human services and public health, plus other functions, into a new Department of Health and Human Services. It also adds conforming Code changes, salary and appointment provisions, appropriations, and transition provisions. The text supplied is cut off around Section 34 of a roughly 1,569-page bill, and nothing in the visible portion regulates sports betting, iGaming, sweepstakes or collectibles.","mechanics":["Section 5 amends Iowa Code 7E.5(1), the list of principal executive departments. It folds the department of public health, the department on aging and the department of human rights functions into the department of health and human services and re-letters the remaining departments.","Section 13 amends 10A.104(2). The inspections and appeals director's appointment power still excludes the administrator of the racing and gaming commission. The visible edit appears to drop the child advocacy board administrator from the exclusion, though the redline marks are not legible.","Section 18 amends 12.10 (deposits by state officers). The deposit rule still does not apply to funds the state racing and gaming commission receives under 99D.7 and 99D.14. The visible change appears to be a conforming edit to the HHS director reference.","Sections 9-11 amend the 8A.504 debt setoff procedure and priority order for claims, and Section 14 amends the 10A.108 lien and distress-warrant debt collection scheme, substituting the department of health and human services as claimant. Nothing visible mentions gambling winnings.","Many sections replace 'substance abuse' with 'substance use disorder' and rename agency references, such as Section 6 on interdepartmental transfers (limit of one-tenth of one percent of appropriations from the funding source) and Section 16 on audit reimbursement.","Effective dates, applicability, penalties, appropriations, salary schedules and transition provisions are not in the portion supplied."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"Within the visible text, this bill is a general government reorganization and has no real gaming overlap. It is comparable in kind to the omnibus appropriation and reorganization bills in OH HB33 and HB96, MI HB4292 and HB4437, NM HB2 and LA HB624, which moves programs to the Louisiana Workforce Commission. It is unlike NE LB402 and LB415, which expressly add unemployment-overpayment debt to the Gambling Winnings Setoff for Outstanding Debt Act. Iowa's visible setoff amendments in 8A.504 only conform agency names and do not reach gambling winnings. It also has nothing like NC H999 (video gaming terminals) or NY S 10092 (gambling advertising to minors).","watch":"Because the supplied text is truncated, check the full engrossed SF 514 for any changes to Iowa Code chapters 99D, 99F, 99G or 8A.504 (including debt setoff against gambling winnings), the racing and gaming commission, the Department of Inspections and Appeals, or the Department of Revenue's enforcement role.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":4251871,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:56:01.633Z"},"IN-SB11":{"summary":"Indiana SB 11 adds IC 24-4-24 requiring a social media operator to obtain verifiable parental consent before a user under 16 may view social media, and to identify accounts created by minors without consent. Enforcement is by the attorney general only, after a 90-day cure period, with injunctive relief and civil penalties up to $250,000 per action. It also folds the collected data into Indiana's data-breach definition of personal information and the deceptive-act list in the consumer sales statute. The bill is a child-online-safety measure and has no gaming or wagering regulatory content beyond an exclusion for interactive gaming sites.","mechanics":["Sec. 1: 'minor user' means an individual under 16. Sec. 5: 'verifiable parental consent' means consent obtained by a method reasonably designed to ensure the consenting person is the minor's parent or legal guardian.","Sec. 3: 'social media' is a website or app allowing account creation to interact with others and create, share, or view content. Exclusions include a site a user accesses 'for the sole purpose of interactive gaming', enterprise communication tools, email-only services, non-user-generated preselected content, and crowdsourced visual-aid tools. Sec. 4 covers any operator whose social media is accessible in Indiana and excludes device manufacturers.","Sec. 6: operators must not let a minor user view social media without verifiable parental consent, must identify accounts created by minors without consent and require consent before access, must give notice that consent is revocable at any time, and must provide a revocation procedure. Sec. 6(d) lets a device-manufacturer/app-developer license agreement incorporate the subsection (b) requirements.","Sec. 7: operators must use commercially reasonable security and encrypt all information collected and retained under the chapter.","Sec. 8: the AG may sue for a Sec. 6 violation, but must first send a notice of violation (if no prior action or notice) giving 90 days to cure with documentation. Remedies are an injunction, a civil penalty of up to $250,000, and investigation and litigation costs. Sec. 9 authorizes civil investigative demands under IC 4-6-3-3. Sec. 10 makes minor-user information in court files confidential.","Sections 2 and 3 amend IC 24-4.9-2-10 and IC 24-5-0.5-3(b)(44) so that information collected under the chapter is 'personal information' and a violation of the security provision (IC 24-4-24-7) is a deceptive act. All provisions are effective July 1, 2025."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"Among the listed bills, SB 11 is closest to the social media minor-protection bills: GA HB1296 (age verification and parental permission), GA SB351, CO HB1287 and CO SB086, AR HB1726 and HB1717, and CT SB01295. Its design is narrow, built on parental consent for under-16s with AG-only enforcement and a 90-day cure period, and it has no private right of action or design-feature duties. It ties into Indiana's existing adult-oriented website statute (IC 24-4-23) by cross-reference. None of the listed gaming bills, such as AR HB1861, CT HB05038 or MD HB1140, share its subject; the only gaming link is the Sec. 3(b)(1) exclusion for interactive-gaming-only sites.","watch":"Check whether an operator's sportsbook, iGaming, or card-break platform with social or community features (chat, profiles, shared content) could fall outside the narrow 'sole purpose of interactive gaming' exclusion in Sec. 3(b)(1) and so need parental-consent controls for under-16 users.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Engrossed","text_chars":27302,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:56:05.327Z"},"LA-HB624":{"summary":"Louisiana Act 478 (HB 624) restructures state social-services and workforce administration. It renames the Louisiana Workforce Commission as Louisiana Works, a department of the executive branch, and moves certain DCFS family-support programs to it (TANF, FITAP, Kinship Care) and to the Louisiana Department of Health (SNAP administration, the Fraud Detection Fund). It has nothing to do with sports betting or gaming and is mostly conforming amendments to Titles 11, 14, 15, 17, 23, 36 and 46.","mechanics":["Renames the Louisiana Workforce Commission as Louisiana Works throughout Title 23 and Title 36. R.S. 23:1 states its purpose and adds a definition of \"customer\" and \"department\", and R.S. 36:4(A)(6) lists it as one of the executive-branch departments.","Replaces DCFS with the Louisiana Department of Health in SNAP-related statutes: fraud reporting under R.S. 14:68.2.1, juvenile-release reporting under R.S. 15:933.1, student-data sharing for summer EBT under R.S. 17:3914(M), and food-stamp overissuance recoupment from unemployment benefits under R.S. 23:1693(I).","Replaces DCFS with Louisiana Works in criminal-neglect-of-family fine and support payments under R.S. 14:74(D)(2) (FITAP and related cases), in vocational rehabilitation references (R.S. 11:780), and in literacy and agency-coordination provisions (R.S. 17:14.1, 17:3047.6).","Requires integration of case management and service integration between social-service and workforce programs, a uniform statewide customer application and enrollment process (R.S. 23:17), and a statewide workforce plan aligned with the Workforce Innovation and Opportunity Act.","The title provides for transfer of monies, continuity of transferred programs and contracts, and transfer of the Fraud Detection Fund to LDH. It authorizes the Law Institute and the state register to make conforming changes. It repeals R.S. 23:18 and 34 and various Title 46 sections. Effective dates are staggered and set in the Act, and the portion of the text provided does not reach them.","Text provided is truncated at R.S. 36:301(C); the Title 46 amendments, transfer sections and effective-date provisions are not visible in this excerpt."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"This bill is unrelated to the gaming and wagering bills in the comparison set. The closest link is NE LB402 and LB415, which let unemployment overpayment debt be collected from gambling winnings. HB 624 touches unemployment overpayment only through SNAP overissuance recoupment from unemployment benefits (R.S. 23:1693(I)) and has no gambling-winnings setoff. It resembles the Iowa government-reorganization bills (IA HF662, SF514) and the Illinois revisory bills (IL HB4844, SB3731) as an agency-restructuring and conforming-changes measure.","watch":"Nothing in the provided text affects sports-betting, iGaming or card-break operators, so the only item to monitor is whether any later amendment ties gambling winnings to the new Louisiana Works debt-collection or overpayment-recovery authority.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":142115,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:56:06.417Z"},"IL-SB1963":{"summary":"Illinois Public Act 103-0009 (SB1963) is a revenue measure whose visible text amends the Use Tax Act and the Service Use Tax Act exemption lists (35 ILCS 105/3-5 and 110/3-5). Its Article 5 (Aircraft Engines) extends the exemption for aircraft modification and repair materials and brings aircraft engine and power plant work within it. It contains no sports-betting, iGaming, or collectibles provisions in the text provided, and the text is cut off partway through the Service Use Tax Act section.","mechanics":["Use Tax Act item (35): the aircraft materials, parts and consumable supplies exemption now runs through December 31, 2029, replacing December 31, 2024.","The prior exclusion of materials, parts and supplies used on aircraft engines or power plants is limited to the period before January 1, 2024, so engine and power plant work is covered from that date.","From January 1, 2024 through December 31, 2029, the exemption covers (A) FAA-certificated Part 145 repair stations with a Class IV Rating, and (B) persons who modify, replace, repair or maintain aircraft engines or power plants, whether or not they meet the item (A) qualifications.","The exemption still excludes aircraft operated by a commercial air carrier providing scheduled passenger service under Part 121 or Part 129, and the existing no-refund language for the January 1, 2015 to February 5, 2020 gap is retained.","Item (41) is renumbered as item (42) for the State Treasurer unclaimed-property sales exemption, which is a conforming change.","The Service Use Tax Act section 3-5 is also amended, but the provided text ends before the aircraft item, so its changes cannot be confirmed here. The act was signed with the last action on 2023-06-07."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills deals with aircraft tax exemptions, so there is no direct comparison. The listed bills are mostly appropriations or omnibus measures (IA HF662/SF514, MI HB4437, OH HB33, NC H259). The closest gambling-adjacent ones are NE LB402 and LB415 on gambling winnings setoff, OH HB344 on electronic instant bingo and lottery terminals, and NC H999 on video gaming terminals. This Illinois act has no gambling content in the text provided and is a narrow use-tax exemption extension.","watch":"Nothing in the visible text affects operators, but because the text is truncated, check the remaining articles of Public Act 103-0009 for any changes to coin-operated amusement or gaming-related tax provisions.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":804561,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:56:06.827Z"},"MI-SB934":{"summary":"Michigan SB 934 (2024 PA 142) amends section 16 of the Lawful Internet Gaming Act (MCL 432.316), which governs how the internet gaming fund is spent. It sets a fixed $3,000,000 annual allocation to the compulsive gaming prevention fund and adds a new $2,000,000 annual allocation to the Christopher R. Slezak first responder presumed coverage fund, with the remainder going to the state school aid fund. It changes where operator-paid money goes, not operator obligations.","mechanics":["Sec. 16(4) sets the expenditure waterfall from the internet gaming fund, on appropriation: (a) Board regulation and enforcement costs, (b) costs of administering millionaire party activity under the bingo act, 1972 PA 382, (c) $3,000,000 to the compulsive gaming prevention fund, (d) $2,000,000 to the Slezak first responder fund, and (e) all remaining money to the state school aid fund.","Subdivisions (c) and (d) are paid each year only after the earlier tiers are funded, so the problem gambling and first responder amounts are subordinate to the Board's regulatory costs and the bingo costs.","The $2,000,000 annual transfer to the Christopher R. Slezak first responder presumed coverage fund (section 405 of the workers disability compensation act of 1969, MCL 418.405) is the new earmark in sub (4)(d), and it reduces the school aid residual by the same amount.","Sec. 16(4) is made subject to section 15(2), which the text excerpted here does not describe.","The state treasurer invests the fund and credits interest and earnings to it; the Board administers the fund for auditing purposes (sec. 16(2)-(3)).","The act takes effect on sine die, the 91st day after final adjournment of the 2024 regular session. It was approved October 8, 2024."],"issue_tags":["legalize"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"This is a narrow fund-allocation amendment, and none of the listed bills is closely comparable. The listed iGaming bills (IL HB2239, HB3080, HB4797; MD HB1343, HB17, SB340; MA H332, H4431, S235) create or regulate internet gaming with licensing, tax and operational rules, whereas MI SB 934 changes only the downstream use of existing iGaming revenue. The closest in subject is CT HB07215 and CT SB00240, which address gaming-related treatment and public health planning, but MI SB 934 simply fixes a $3M problem gambling transfer and adds a $2M first responder workers' compensation earmark.","watch":"Operators should note that this bill changes no tax rate, fee or licensee obligation, but the new earmark and the subordinated waterfall show how iGaming revenue is being claimed by other priorities, which may feed into future pressure on rates or fund allocation.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":3059,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:56:09.080Z"},"NJ-A5362":{"summary":"A5362 amends N.J.S.A. 5:12-100 by adding a new paragraph k.(2), which makes it unlawful for a casino licensee, or anyone acting for it, to run a free-to-play 'non-wagering casino game' that rewards immediate or continued participation and serves to solicit future wagering. The First Reprint adds a carve-out for non-wagering rewards such as complimentary hotel stays or event tickets. The bill reaches both in-person and online social/free-play casino games and takes effect immediately.","mechanics":["New N.J.S.A. 5:12-100k.(2) makes it unlawful for a casino licensee, 'through its agents, employees, or any contracted operator, or any party with which the casino licensee has a relationship,' to conduct a non-wagering casino game that provides a reward for immediate or continued participation and that serves to solicit future participation in wagering. The reprint changed 'or' to 'and', so the game must both reward participation and serve to solicit.","Definition: 'non-wagering casino game' is any game, in person or via the Internet, offering an experience equivalent to, or with similar odds as, a game that ordinarily requires a casino license, except that it requires no initial monetary investment to play.","Carve-out added in the First Reprint: the paragraph does not limit a licensee's ability to offer rewards unrelated to wagering, such as complimentary stays or event tickets, based on participation in non-wagering games.","Coverage extends beyond the licensee itself to contracted operators and any party with which the licensee has a relationship, which could capture affiliates, marketing vendors, and platform partners.","The bill sets no new penalty, enforcement mechanism, or rulemaking directive; enforcement would presumably run through the Casino Control Act's existing disciplinary framework and the Division of Gaming Enforcement.","Effective immediately on enactment (section 2). The text shows no grandfathering or transition period for existing free-play promotions."],"issue_tags":["advertising","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"None of the listed bills from other states addresses this issue. The Arkansas, Colorado, Georgia and Indiana bills concern minors' social media use. The Maryland bills (HB1140/SB860, HB295/SB112), Arkansas HB1861 and the Connecticut bills are about sweepstakes or interactive-game bans, new iGaming authorization, or problem gaming, and none restricts licensed operators' own free-to-play games as a marketing tool. NJ A5362 is narrower: it regulates how licensed casinos use free-play games to funnel players into real-money wagering. It does not ban free-play games outright.","watch":"Watch how 'reward,' 'serves to solicit future participation,' and 'any party with which the casino licensee has a relationship' are read, because free-to-play games, loyalty-linked rewards, and affiliate or vendor-run social casino products could fall within a prohibition with no stated penalty or regulatory guidance.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":21900,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:56:17.543Z"},"NH-SB168":{"summary":"SB 168 would authorize online casino-style gaming (games of chance and electronic gaming devices) in New Hampshire under the Lottery Commission, through a new RSA chapter 287-J. Operators must be licensed and must contract with an existing licensed casino through a branded Internet gaming skin. The bill sets a 45 percent revenue share on gross online gaming revenue and creates a fund to reimburse municipalities for elderly, disabled, blind, and deaf property tax exemptions. It also amends RSA 647:2, V to add online gambling, and the Lottery Commission would license, supervise, and audit operators. Effective date is January 1, 2026.","mechanics":["Licensing cap (287-J:2): the Commission may license no fewer than 3 and no more than 6 online gaming wagering platforms. Each operator must contract with a licensed gaming facility (a casino licensed for historic horse racing machines or table games) via an Internet gaming skin (287-J:3). Contracts need Commission approval and must provide the state a percentage of revenue (287-J:4).","Tax and distribution (287-J:7): licensees collect 45% of gross online gaming revenue, paid to the Commission and split 25% to the RSA 284:21-j special fund, 25% to the new elderly, disabled, blind, and deaf exemption reimbursement fund (RSA 72:42-a), and 50% to the general fund. The title also mentions the education trust fund, which the text does not name.","Player protection (287-J:5): age verification for 18+, identity verification, geofencing to NH or a permissible reciprocal jurisdiction, daily/weekly/monthly wager limits, a voluntary self-exclusion program, and data security. Prohibited bettors (287-J:1, XI) include Commission staff and relatives, operator principals and employees, contractors, and proxies.","Operator compliance (287-J:6): annual Division of Online Gaming Wagering approval of security and internal control reports, computer system security reports with third-party testing, house rules, accounting controls with a segregated NH account, and responsible gaming plans. Operators must hold cash reserves set by the Commission, and permitted payment methods include PayPal, debit card, ACH, and promotional funds.","Out-of-state and illegal-market limits: wagers from persons outside NH are allowed only if the Commission finds them consistent with federal and NH law or made under a reciprocal agreement (287-J:9). Under 287-J:13 the Commission must investigate and deny or discipline up to revocation any operator or affiliate that knowingly takes revenue from OFAC-sanctioned jurisdictions or wagers from countries where online gaming is illegal.","Reimbursement mechanics (RSA 72:42-a): the Department of Revenue Administration reports exemptions by May 1, pays municipalities by July 31, pro-rates payments if the fund is short, and reports by October 1. The fund is nonlapsing and continually appropriated. The fiscal note is incomplete."],"issue_tags":["legalize","tax","licensing","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"SB 168 is a casino-style iGaming legalization bill, comparable to the Maryland internet gaming bills (HB17, HB1343, SB340), the Illinois Internet Gaming Act bills (HB2239, HB3080, HB4797), and the Massachusetts bills (H332, S235, H4431). Unlike the Maryland and Illinois bills, it runs through the Lottery Commission, caps licenses at 3 to 6, and requires every operator to partner with an existing casino via a skin. Its 45% flat revenue share, with a share earmarked for municipal property tax exemption reimbursement, is unusual. The OFAC and illegal-market provisions in 287-J:13 echo the 'illegal markets' theme in Maryland HB295 and SB112 but work as a licensing screen rather than a general prohibition. Unlike Arkansas HB1861, it does not create new felony penalties for illegal operators.","watch":"Watch the 45% gross revenue rate, the 3-to-6 platform cap tied to casino skin contracts (which gates market access to existing facilities), the OFAC and 'illegal country' affiliate screen in 287-J:13 (the 'knowingly' standard and the reach to affiliates under common control), the open-ended Commission discretion over contracts and rules, and the gap between the title's education trust fund reference and the text.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":19574,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:56:17.558Z"},"MO-SB147":{"summary":"SB 147 is a Missouri firearms bill with no gaming content. It expands self-defense protections (a presumption of reasonableness and pretrial immunity), bars employers from firing employees over a locked, concealed gun in a vehicle, and rewrites the weapons-offense statutes. It also makes businesses that ban guns by posting signs liable for the safety of permit holders on their premises. The text provided cuts off partway through section 571.101, so later sections are not analysed.","mechanics":["New s. 290.148: an employer may not fire an employee for having a firearm in the employee's vehicle on employer property, provided the vehicle is locked, the firearm is not visible, and the employee is not otherwise prohibited from possessing a firearm. The employer has no civil liability for injuries from firearms stored in compliance.","s. 563.031(5) replaces the defendant's burden of injecting justification with a statutory presumption that the defendant reasonably believed force was necessary. New s. 563.085 gives immunity from criminal prosecution and civil action for justified force, including a bar on arrest absent probable cause the force was unlawful. Once a prima facie claim is raised at a pretrial hearing, the party opposing immunity must overcome it by clear and convincing evidence.","New s. 571.069: a business that bars firearms by s. 571.107 signage or another authorised method takes on custodial responsibility for permit holders on its premises, including a duty to guard against third-party criminal acts and animals. 'Business' means one selling goods or services to the public with annual gross sales above $500,000. Required notice must be posted with the sign. Plaintiffs must prove their case by a preponderance of the evidence, can recover actual damages, attorney's fees, expert costs and court costs, and have two years to sue. Businesses that allow firearms are immune absent purposeful conduct.","s. 571.030 is restructured: s. 571.107's restricted-location list moves into new paragraphs (a) to (j) of subdivision (1), which include sports arenas or stadiums with 5,000 or more seats that are privately managed or leased. The separate bans on carrying into churches, polling places and government buildings are deleted. Carrying onto signed private property ceases to be a criminal act and becomes grounds for denial of access or removal, with citations of up to $100, $200 and $500 for repeat violations. New subsection 14 excludes a firearm kept in a vehicle on listed premises from the offense unless prohibited by federal law, so long as it is not removed or brandished.","s. 571.020 deletes knuckles from the list of prohibited items and removes the 'in violation of federal law' qualifier on machine guns, short-barreled rifles and shotguns, silencers and switchblades. s. 571.101(2) lets any sheriff in the state, not just the sheriff of the applicant's county, issue a concealed carry permit."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"Nothing comparable is listed. The IA, IL, MI, NC, NM, OH and UT bills are appropriations, revisory or omnibus acts, and the NE, NC, OH, NY and VA bills deal with gambling, cannabis or advertising. None of them addresses firearms or self-defense law. OK SB461 is the closest topically, as it amends criminal provisions on bullets and body armor, but it is not a gaming measure either. SB 147 is unusual on its own terms for creating a private right of action against businesses that post no-gun signs.","watch":"Check whether s. 571.069's custodial duty and fee-shifting cause of action would reach casinos, retail sportsbooks, card shops or live-break event venues with over $500,000 in annual sales that post no-gun signs, and how the 5,000-seat arena restriction in s. 571.030 interacts with any operator-sponsored venue or sponsorship activity.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":142935,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:56:21.216Z"},"NJ-A4937":{"summary":"This is a New Jersey cannabis bill with no sports-betting, iGaming or collectibles content. It amends the medical cannabis permitting statute (P.L.2009, c.307, s.7) to let alternative treatment centers and dispensary permit holders operate up to two satellite dispensaries with Cannabis Regulatory Commission approval. It also amends the commission's membership provisions (P.L.2019, c.153, s.31) and, per the title, post-employment rules for State employees under P.L.1981, c.142. The supplied text cuts off partway through s.31, so the post-employment section is not visible.","mechanics":["Satellite dispensaries (s.7(a)(2)(d)(i)): an alternative treatment center with a dispensing permit may keep up to two satellites. A dispensary seeking one needs commission approval under P.L.2009, c.307 and P.L.2021, c.16.","Existing alternative treatment centers are deemed to hold a Class 1 cultivator, Class 2 manufacturer, Class 5 retailer plus an additional Class 5 license for each satellite, and a Class 6 delivery license, or alternatively a Class 3 wholesaler license and possibly a Class 4 distributor license. They may operate only after written municipal approval and an initial commission license.","Social-equity investor carve-out (s.7(a)(2)(d)(ii)): an investor, group or fund giving significant financial or technical help to a certified minority, women's or disabled-veterans' dispensary applicant may own up to 35 percent of up to seven permitted dispensaries. Terms must be commercially reasonable, and repayment periods run from 5 to 10 years depending on value, with a $100,000 to $500,000 tiering. Controlling interest cannot revert to the investor on default.","Integrity and enforcement provisions: criminal history checks, with a clear-and-convincing rehabilitation exception. A significantly involved person must disclose within 30 days or face permit revocation and at least a two-year bar. Price-list deviation costs $1,000 per sale, and a missing price list costs $10,000 per week. The commission may fine, suspend or terminate permits in summary proceedings (s.7(m)).","Commission structure (s.31): five full-time members, one each appointed by the Senate President and Speaker and three by the Governor, including the chair. Terms are five years, salary caps are $141,000 for the chair and $125,000 for other members, and a quorum is a majority of the total authorized membership. A member may hold local elected office only if the State Ethics Commission and Local Finance Board find no conflict.","Conflict rule (s.7(l)): any State, county or local employee reviewing permit applications may not hold a direct or indirect financial interest in cannabis or take anything of value from an applicant. The post-employment restrictions in the title are in the truncated portion of the text."],"issue_tags":["licensing","integrity"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills are comparable. They concern iGaming and sports-wagering authorization (MD HB1343, IL HB2239, MA H4431), sweepstakes and illegal-market bans (MD HB1140, AR HB1861), and social media or minors' privacy (CO SB086, GA SB351). The nearest parallels are the commission-issued licensing and background-check framework in the Maryland internet gaming bills and the licensee disciplinary authority in CT SB01235. This bill is unusual only in being cannabis licensing, with satellite-location caps, social-equity investor limits and a restructured regulator.","watch":"Little here bears on sports-betting operators directly, but check the unseen post-employment section amending P.L.1981, c.142 if it applies to State employees generally, since it could limit hiring regulators or officials who previously dealt with licensees.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":83565,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:56:23.182Z"},"NJ-A6267":{"summary":"NJ A6267 (second reprint, Assembly Appropriations amendments of Jan. 8, 2026) restructures the five-member Cannabis Regulatory Commission and rewrites its ethics and conflict-of-interest rules. It does not touch sports betting, iGaming or collectibles; the only gaming-related text is existing casino and Internet gaming conflict provisions in the Conflicts of Interest Law (C.52:13D-17.2), which it amends to separate the cannabis and casino definitions. The text provided is truncated mid-section 3, so later sections on the marketplace changes are not visible.","mechanics":["Section 1 amends C.24:6I-24(b): the chair is chosen by a majority of the total authorized members rather than designated by the Governor. The Governor keeps three appointments, and the Senate President and Speaker each keep one. The Senate advice-and-consent requirement for appointments is deleted, and vacancies are filled by the original appointing authority for the unexpired term only.","Salaries are fixed by statute: $165,000 for the chair and $160,000 for the other members, replacing the Governor-set caps of $141,000 and $125,000. The executive director's salary cap rises from $141,000 to $175,000.","A commissioner may hold local elected office with State Ethics Commission and Local Finance Board approval. The member may campaign and fundraise for it, but may not take donations from anyone with an ownership interest in a cannabis business.","Section 2 amends C.24:6I-27: the ban on discussing pending matters outside commission premises is relaxed. Commissioners, the executive director and supervisory staff may meet with any person, except on matters where they are recused, at commission premises or another commission-designated location. Meetings must be logged, and Open Public Meetings Act coverage applies only when more than two members attend.","Section 2 also carves the local-office exception out of the full-time and political-activity bans. Commissioners and senior staff may attend non-commission events, including political events, in an official capacity after written notice to the chair.","Section 3 amends C.52:13D-17.2: cannabis is removed from the casino-activity definition of covered persons and given its own paragraph (a)(1)(c). Commission members are dropped from that definition. Family members of State officers may hold interests in cannabis permittees or licensees with ethics-body approval. Special State employees without cannabis responsibility may hold interests in cannabis businesses, including those in Education, Health and Human Services. The casino and Internet gaming restrictions in subsection b(1)-(2) appear unchanged in the visible text."],"issue_tags":["licensing","integrity"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"Nothing comparable is listed. The other states' bills (e.g., MD HB1343, IL HB2239, AR HB1861, DE HB365, CT SB01464) deal with iGaming, sports wagering, sweepstakes or social media, while this bill concerns the governance and ethics of a cannabis regulator. It is unusual in that it amends the shared State Conflicts of Interest Law, which also carries NJ's casino and Internet gaming post-employment and interest restrictions, so the statute is touched by a cannabis bill.","watch":"Confirm that the restructuring of C.52:13D-17.2 leaves the casino and Internet gaming conflict restrictions in subsection b(1)-(2) and the post-employment bar in subsection c(1) unchanged, and read the truncated remainder of section 3 for any changes to the casino-related provisions.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":206422,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:56:27.399Z"},"NJ-S3486":{"summary":"S3486 (Second Reprint) amends New Jersey's medical cannabis statute so that any medical cannabis dispensary permit holder can seek Cannabis Regulatory Commission approval to run up to two satellite dispensaries, and it removes the earlier ban on new satellites. It also restructures how the five-member Cannabis Regulatory Commission is appointed, giving the Senate President and Speaker direct appointments. The title and synopsis also promise post-employment restrictions on State employees (amending P.L.1981, c.142), but the supplied text cuts off before that section. It is not a sports betting or gaming bill. The bill was substituted by A4937 on 2025-06-30.","mechanics":["Section 1 amends N.J.S.A. 24:6I-7(a)(2)(d)(i) to delete the bar on any medical cannabis dispensary establishing a satellite location after the effective date of P.L.2019, c.153.","It also deletes the carve-outs limiting satellites to pre-2019 alternative treatment centers (ATCs). It deletes the one-satellite cap for the three ATCs permitted under section 11 of P.L.2019, c.153. In their place, an ATC that holds a permit to dispense medical cannabis may maintain up to two satellite dispensaries.","A dispensary seeking a satellite must still obtain commission approval under P.L.2009, c.307 and P.L.2021, c.16. Once approved, it may establish and maintain up to two satellite locations (floor amendment changed 'one' to 'two').","Section 2 amends section 31 of P.L.2019, c.153 (C.24:6I-24). The commission stays at five members, but the Senate President and the Speaker each directly appoint one member instead of the Governor appointing 'upon recommendation of' them. The Governor appoints three members, including the chair.","Initial appointments need no Senate advice and consent. Later gubernatorial appointments and reappointments require it, and later Senate President and Speaker appointments are made in the same manner as the original appointments.","Unchanged commission provisions in the text shown include full-time service, salary caps of $141,000 for the chair and $125,000 for other members, a majority-of-authorized-membership quorum, and the requirement that all five members be present to take over medical program oversight from the Department of Health or to adopt the initial personal-use rules.","The title and synopsis say the bill amends P.L.1981, c.142 to add post-employment restrictions on State employees, but that section is not in the text provided. Its terms cannot be summarized here."],"issue_tags":["licensing"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills is comparable. They cover internet gaming, sports wagering, sweepstakes, prediction markets and social media child safety, and S3486 concerns medical cannabis dispensary permits and commission structure. The nearest overlap is regulatory-agency structure and licensing, for example CT SB01235, which modifies the Commissioner's disciplinary authority over gaming licensees, but the subject matter differs. What is unusual about S3486 is that it combines a satellite-location expansion with a change in how legislative leaders appoint regulators and a revolving-door provision.","watch":"For a gaming-operator lawyer this is only a model for regulator appointment and post-employment rules, so watch the P.L.1981, c.142 revolving-door language in the final text and in the substitute A4937 for any approach that could be copied into gaming-regulator reform.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":85619,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:56:32.334Z"},"NV-AB58":{"summary":"Nevada AB 58 (Ch. 325, effective July 1, 2025) is a gaming-code cleanup that replaces the Board-recommendation/Commission-approval pathway for new games with administrative approval by the Gaming Control Board Chair under Commission regulations. It also repeals the \"cash access and wagering instrument service provider\" category and the global risk management statutes, folding risk-management services into the definition of \"information service,\" and makes smaller technical fixes to restricted-license and interactive gaming licensing provisions.","mechanics":["Sec. 6 (NRS 463.164): a licensee may not offer a game or gambling game unless it has received administrative approval from the Board Chair or approval from the Commission. The old Board recommendation, immediate play pending Commission action, and 60-day deemed-approval rule are struck, and the Commission must adopt regulations governing administrative approval. Sec. 2 conforms the definition of \"game\" in NRS 463.0152.","Sec. 4 (NRS 463.01642): \"information service\" now expressly includes selling and providing management, or consultation or instruction in the management, of risks associated with wagering pools for a race, a sporting event or any other event on which a wager may be accepted. NRS 463.160(1)(b) still requires a license to provide or maintain an information service, so risk-management vendors fall under that licensing requirement.","Sec. 11 repeals NRS 463.01395 (cash access and wagering instrument service provider), NRS 463.810 and NRS 463.820 (global risk management). Secs. 3, 5 and 7 remove the related references, including the gaming-employee category and the NRS 463.160(1)(e) licensing/registration trigger. Secs. 9 and 10 delete the global risk management carve-outs from NRS 465.090 (out-of-state dissemination of racing information) and NRS 465.094.","Sec. 7 (NRS 463.677): \"service provider\" is now simply a person who meets criteria the Commission sets by regulation. The statutory reference to cash access providers is removed, as is \"system-based and system-supported games\" from the list of games licensed establishments may expose. The interactive gaming service provider licensing structure is otherwise unchanged.","Sec. 8 (NRS 463.750(3)(b)(5)): a technical correction from \"sports book and race pool\" to \"race book and sports pool\" in the gaming-area criteria for interactive gaming licensure in mid-size counties (52,000 to under 700,000 population). Penalties for unlicensed interactive gaming are unchanged: category B felony, 1 to 10 years and/or a fine up to $50,000.","Sec. 5.5 (NRS 463.161): for restricted licenses (15 or fewer slots) in counties of 100,000 or more, the required 2,500 square feet of patron space must be indoor space. Sec. 12 sets the effective date as July 1, 2025."],"issue_tags":["licensing","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"AB 58 is a regulatory housekeeping bill for an established market and has little in common with the listed bills. It does not legalize or authorize new gaming as MD HB17/SB340, IL HB2239 and MA H4431 do. It does not take on illegal operators or sweepstakes as AR HB1861, MD HB1140/SB860 and MD HB295/SB112 do. It does not touch tax, advertising, or minors' protection as CT HB05038 and CT SB01464 do. The closest analogue is CT SB01235, which also deals with licensing and the regulator's authority, but AB 58's distinctive feature is moving game approval from Board recommendation plus Commission final action to Chair-level administrative approval.","watch":"Operators and vendors should watch the Commission's implementing regulations on Chair-level administrative approval, which will set the timing and process for getting new games to market. They should also check whether risk-management services now fall within the information service license requirement, and whether any cash access or wagering instrument arrangements previously covered by the repealed category need new licensing or registration treatment.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":36341,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:56:35.475Z"},"NJ-S4847":{"summary":"S4847 (signed as P.L.2025, c.325) restructures the Cannabis Regulatory Commission's governance and amends its ethics rules. It also amends the State conflicts-of-interest statute (section 4 of P.L.1981, c.142) as it applies to cannabis. It is a cannabis-ethics and governance bill; the fourth-reprint text provided does not legalize, tax or regulate sports betting or iGaming.","mechanics":["Section 1: the five-member commission picks its own chair by a majority of the total authorized members, instead of the Governor designating the chair. The Governor still appoints three members and the Senate President and Speaker one each. The Senate advice-and-consent language in paragraph b.(3) is deleted, and vacancies are filled by the appropriate appointing authority for the unexpired term only.","Section 1 sets fixed salaries of $165,000 for the chair and $160,000 for the other members, replacing the Governor-set salaries capped at $141,000 and $125,000. The executive director's salary cap rises from $141,000 to $175,000.","Section 1: a member may hold local elected office (subject to State Ethics Commission and Local Finance Board review) and may fundraise for that campaign. A cannabis business owner may not donate to the campaign of a commission member seeking local office, and the member may not accept such donations.","Section 2 amends the commission Code of Ethics. Members, the executive director and supervisory or policy-making staff may meet with any person outside commission premises, at any location the commission designates, except on matters where the member is recused. Meetings must be logged and open to inspection under OPRA. A meeting attended by more than two members falls under the Open Public Meetings Act.","Section 2 also permits supervisory staff and members to attend events and political events in an official capacity after written notice to the chair, and carves the local-office exception out of the full-time-employment and political-activity bars.","Section 3 amends P.L.1981, c.142, s.4. It restructures the definition of 'person' so casino activity and cannabis activity each have their own list, and it deletes the Cannabis Regulatory Commission members from the cannabis list. It loosens the cannabis ownership and employment limits for some family members and special State officers, with State Ethics Commission review in places. The earlier municipal-ordinance section amending P.L.2021, c.16, s.31 was struck in this reprint. The supplied text is cut off mid-section, and no effective date appears in it."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills is comparable. They cover internet gaming authorization (MD HB17, IL HB2239, MA H332), sweepstakes and illegal-market bans (MD HB1140, AR HB1861), prediction markets (CT HB05038) and social-media or minors' safety, not commission governance. NJ S4847 is unusual because it is a cannabis-regulator ethics and structure bill. Its only link to gaming is that it amends N.J.S.A. 52:13D-17.2, which also contains the existing casino and Internet gaming revolving-door and conflict restrictions. From the text shown, it does not change those restrictions.","watch":"Confirm that the reprinted N.J.S.A. 52:13D-17.2, including the rewritten 'person' definition for casino activity and the unchanged Internet gaming conflict provisions in subsection b.(2), does not alter the restrictions on State officers and their families dealing with casino or Internet gaming licensees, since the supplied text is truncated.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":214720,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:56:37.305Z"},"NV-SB203":{"summary":"Nevada SB 203 (Ch. 181, effective May 31, 2025) restructures the pari-mutuel wagering regime in NRS chapters 463 and 464. It abolishes the disseminator licensing scheme for live racing broadcasts and moves operators of off-track pari-mutuel systems from Commission licensing to Board registration as service providers. It also adds good-faith civil immunity for state gaming regulators and confirms that off-track systems and their operators need not be physically in Nevada if they are in a jurisdiction where pari-mutuel wagering is legal and regulated.","mechanics":["Sec. 2.9 repeals NRS 463.421-463.480 (the disseminator licensing and live-broadcast scheme, including 463.430 licensing and 463.450 fees) and NRS 464.015 (the license fee for off-track system operators). Sec. 1.7 rewrites NRS 463.426 so the Board may authorize a track or its agent to contract directly with a 'user' (a Nevada-licensed race book, sports pool or gambling game operator), and all users must pay the same rate or fee for the live broadcast.","Secs. 2.1, 1.8 and 2.2: new NRS 464.005(7) defines 'operator of a system' as a person providing an off-track pari-mutuel system and deems it a 'service provider' under NRS 463.677. NRS 464.010(2) now requires registration with the Board instead of a state gaming license, and the Commission may regulate service providers under NRS 463.677 once it finds them secure and reliable (subsec. 4).","Sec. 2.1 adds definitions of 'licensed book' (a race book or sports pool licensed for pari-mutuel wagering) and 'pari-mutuel system of wagering' (participants wager against each other, not the house, and the term includes off-track wagering). Gross revenue is now tied to the licensed book's commission, plus breakage and unpaid winning tickets.","Sec. 2.3 amends NRS 464.020(3): neither the off-track pari-mutuel system nor the operator of a system must be physically present in Nevada, provided each is located in a jurisdiction where off-track pari-mutuel wagering is legal and is licensed or approved by a regulator with jurisdiction. Licensed books may take wire wagers from Nevada, other states and foreign places where such wagering is legal, and NRS 464.060's illegality rule now yields to this subsection. The Commission's regulations may still require border-control technology to prevent wagers from places where they are illegal.","Sec. 2 gives the State, the Commission, the Board, the Off-Track Pari-Mutuel Wagering Committee and their members, employees, attorneys and personnel immunity from civil liability for decisions or actions taken in good faith and without malicious intent under chapter 464.","Secs. 2.2, 2.5 and 2.8 extend the licensing framework to findings of suitability, registrations and approvals, and Commission suspension or revocation powers now cover registrations as well as licenses. The existing 18 percent commission cap on non-off-track pari-mutuel wagering and the 3 percent quarterly tax in NRS 464.040 are unchanged. The Committee retains exclusive negotiating rights with out-of-state pools and with registered operators of a system."],"issue_tags":["licensing","integrity"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"This bill is a technical clean-up of Nevada's existing pari-mutuel statutes and does not create or expand a market. That sets it apart from the other states' listed bills, such as DE HB365 (a framework for competitive mobile sports wagering), the Illinois, Maryland and Massachusetts internet gaming bills (e.g., IL HB2239, MD HB17), and AR HB1861 (criminalizing illegal online casinos and sports betting). It is closer in spirit to CT SB01235, which also adjusts the regulator's licensing and disciplinary authority, but the Connecticut bill does not address pari-mutuel systems or racing broadcast distribution. Its unusual features are the move from licensing to registration for system operators and the out-of-state presence rule.","watch":"Operators of race books and sports pools should check how the Board and Commission implement registration of off-track system operators as service providers under NRS 463.677, including the suitability findings and the Commission's prior determination that such providers are secure and reliable, and how the new direct track-to-user broadcast contracts (equal-rate requirement) and the repeal of the disseminator fee and licensing regime affect their content costs and vendor relationships.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":36110,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:56:39.862Z"},"NY-A9454":{"summary":"A.9454 amends State Finance Law section 92-c(3), which governs the lottery fund, so that if interactive (internet or mobile) casino gaming is later authorized and approved by the gaming commission, at least $10 million or 1% of the revenues, whichever is greater, goes to school safety and security expenses as determined by the commissioner of education. The bill does not legalize or regulate mobile casino gaming. It is a conditional earmark that applies only if and when authorization occurs.","mechanics":["Amends only subdivision 3 of State Finance Law section 92-c, adding a proviso to clause (iii) (elementary and secondary education).","Trigger: 'upon the authorization and legalization of interactive gaming', meaning internet-based versions of table games, slot machines with spinning reels, poker tournaments, or other games under Racing, Pari-Mutuel Wagering and Breeding Law section 1301 subdivisions 4, 19, 38, 39 or 41, approved by the gaming commission and accessed by computer or mobile device over the internet.","Earmark: the greater of $10 million or 1% of 'such revenues' goes to school safety and security expenses as determined by the commissioner of education. The text does not say whether 'revenues' means gross gaming revenue, net revenue, tax receipts or lottery fund deposits.","The bill sets no tax rate, license fee, licensing framework, operator obligation or enforcement mechanism, and it does not change the sports wagering rules.","Effective immediately (section 2). Status: introduced March 14, 2024 and held for consideration in the Racing and Wagering Committee on 2024-05-14."],"issue_tags":["legalize","tax"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"Unlike the listed bills, which actually authorize or regulate internet gaming (IL HB2239/HB3080/HB4797, MD HB1343/HB17/SB340, MA H332/H4431/S235), A.9454 creates no licensing or tax structure and only earmarks future proceeds, contingent on separate legalization. It is closest in spirit to budget-allocation measures such as the DC FY2027 budget acts, but it targets a single use, school safety. Unlike the Maryland and Arkansas measures that prohibit illegal online casinos or sweepstakes (MD HB1140, AR HB1861), it contains no enforcement or anti-illegal-market provisions.","watch":"Watch how 'revenues' is defined and whether a school-safety set-aside of $10 million or 1% is folded into the eventual iGaming tax rate and licensing legislation, since that could add to operators' effective tax burden or create a floor that applies in a small market.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":3749,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:56:42.810Z"},"NV-SB63":{"summary":"NV SB63 as enrolled adds sections 24-35 to NRS chapter 603A, creating a children's data privacy regime for controllers of online services, products or features targeted to Nevada residents. It requires verifiable parental consent under COPPA before processing a known child's (under 13) personal data or collecting precise geolocation. It bans targeted advertising, sale and certain profiling of known children's data, requires data protection assessments, and imposes de-identified data obligations. Enforcement is by the Attorney General only, with a 30-day cure period. The bill failed with no further action taken.","mechanics":["Scope (secs. 25, 27, 28.5): a 'child' is under 13 and a 'known child' is one the controller knows to be a child. A 'controller' is anyone who operates an online service, product or feature targeted to persons in Nevada and determines the purposes and means of processing. Sec. 32 exempts controllers that process data of fewer than 25,000 Nevada residents, or fewer than 100,000 residents while deriving under 50% of gross revenue from data sales. It also exempts GLBA, HIPAA and FCRA-regulated entities and data, and Nevada consumer health data.","Sec. 33: verifiable parental consent under COPPA is required before processing a known child's personal data or collecting precise geolocation. Processing is barred for targeted advertising, sale, or profiling that produces legal or similarly significant effects. Processing must be reasonably necessary to provide the service and no longer than necessary, and further processing is limited to the disclosed or a compatible purpose.","Sec. 33(4)-(5): precise geolocation collection must be necessary and limited in duration, with a persistent on-screen indicator. 'Precise geolocation' means location within a 1,750-foot radius. 'Targeted advertising' covers cross-context behavioral ads but excludes first-party, contextual and requested ads and ad measurement.","Sec. 32.5: a controller holding de-identified data must take reasonable measures against association with a natural person, publicly commit not to re-identify, and contractually bind recipients to the same terms.","Sec. 34: controllers offering services directed at children must conduct and retain, for 3 years after the service ends, a data protection assessment for each processing activity. A single assessment for comparable activities, or one done under another state's or federal law of reasonably comparable scope, satisfies this. The AG may request assessments relevant to an investigation of a sec. 33 violation. They are confidential, and disclosure does not waive privilege. Per sec. 37, assessments are required only for processing on or after Jan. 1, 2026.","Enforcement (sec. 35): the AG has exclusive authority and must give written notice identifying the provision and facts. The controller has 30 days to remedy, and failure is a deceptive trade practice under NRS 598.0903-598.0999. There is no private right of action. Sec. 38: effective on passage for rulemaking and preparatory tasks, and Jan. 1, 2026 for all other purposes."],"issue_tags":["advertising"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"This is a general children's privacy bill with no wagering, gaming or collectibles provisions, so it is not comparable to the gaming bills listed (e.g., IL HB2239, MD HB17, CT SB01464, AR HB1861). It is closest to the child-protection bills: AR HB1717 and HB1726, CO HB1287 and SB086, GA HB1296 and SB351, IN SB0011 and CT SB01295. Unlike the social-media age-verification and parental-consent bills in GA and IN, which regulate platform access, it is a COPPA-style data-processing regime limited to under-13s. It relies on an AG notice-and-cure process with no private right of action.","watch":"The bill is dead, but if revived, operators should check whether their apps are 'targeted to' Nevada residents or 'directed at children', whether they fall under the 25,000/100,000-resident thresholds, and how the bans on targeted advertising and profiling of known under-13 users would apply to marketing and geolocation use.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":36686,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:56:44.235Z"},"OK-SB461":{"summary":"The text provided (Senate Floor Version, committee substitute) does not touch gambling at all, despite the introduced title about restricted bullets and body armor. It creates a new Title 22 section 39.1 requiring prosecuting agencies that keep Brady-Giglio lists of officers with credibility problems to adopt notice-and-reconsideration procedures for the officers listed. Effective November 1, 2025.","mechanics":["Section 1(A) defines Brady-Giglio list, officer (certified law enforcement, firefighters, EMTs, corrections, probation/parole and similar) and prosecuting individual (AG, district attorney, special prosecutor, city attorney or other authorized prosecutor).","Section 1(B): any prosecuting individual that maintains a list must adopt a policy covering placement criteria, advance written notice, officer input, notice of decision, and reconsideration rights and timelines.","Sections 1(C)-(D): written notice goes by USPS or email to the officer's current or last known employer, who must pass it to the officer if contact information is known; the post-decision notice must state that a reconsideration request is due within 30 days of the notice being sent to the employer.","Section 1(E): an approved request removes the officer from the list; denial, no request, or a noncompliant request leaves the name on the list.","Section 1(F): for officers already listed, the prosecutor must send notice within 90 days of the effective date, and officers then have 30 days to request reconsideration.","Sections 1(G)-(I): prosecutors' constitutional and procedural Brady-Giglio discovery duties are preserved, prosecutors may remove names at any time, and no private cause of action lies against a prosecutor or employee for inclusion on a list. Section 2 sets the effective date of November 1, 2025."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"Nothing comparable is listed. The other states' bills (IA HF662/HSB126/SF514/SSB1123 government reorganization, IL HB2755 revenue, MI appropriations bills, NC H999 video gaming terminals, NE LB402/LB415 gambling-winnings setoff, NY S10092 gambling ads, OH HB344 electronic instant bingo) concern revenue, budgets or gaming, whereas this bill is purely a law-enforcement personnel and prosecutorial-disclosure measure. It is also unusual that the introduced title (restricted bullets and body armor) does not match the substituted text on Brady-Giglio lists, and neither concerns gaming.","watch":"Nothing in this bill affects sports-betting, iGaming or collectibles operators, so there is nothing to track beyond confirming that the title and text mismatch is not later used as a vehicle for unrelated amendments.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":10010,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:56:44.578Z"},"OK-SB885":{"summary":"Oklahoma SB885 creates the Safe Screens for Kids Act, which bars social media platforms from letting Oklahoma-resident minors (under 18) hold accounts without verified written parental consent. It also gives parents full account access, bans collecting non-de-identified minor data, bans advertising to minors, bars algorithmic personalization of content for minors, and prohibits compulsive-use design features. Enforcement is by the Attorney General through civil action for injunctive relief or a civil penalty, and the Act takes effect November 1, 2025.","mechanics":["Sec. 2(B): platforms may not allow a resident minor to create or maintain an account without written parental or guardian consent, must verify the consenting person's identity and relationship to the minor, and must use reasonable age verification for every user.","Sec. 2(C): parents get full access to the minor's account, including posts, messages, comments, likes, shares and reactions, and the platform may not restrict their ability to monitor, modify or delete content or activity.","Sec. 2(D): no collection of minor-user data unless de-identified, no processing inconsistent with the minor's 'best interests' (defined in Sec. 1(3)), and no displaying, sending or targeting ads to minors or using minor data for advertising.","Sec. 2(E)-(F): no algorithm, AI or machine learning to select, recommend, rank or personalize content for a minor based on profile, behavior, location or other data; no features that encourage or reward excessive or compulsive use or exploit psychological vulnerabilities.","Sec. 1(8): 'social media platform' covers sites that primarily serve social interaction with user-generated content; it excludes COPPA-compliant interactive gaming platforms and career or professional networking platforms.","Sec. 2(G)-(H), Sec. 3: AG civil action for injunction or civil penalty (no penalty amount stated, no private right of action), AG rulemaking on age verification, parental consent and reasonable exceptions, effective November 1, 2025."],"issue_tags":["advertising","integrity"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"This is a child online-safety bill, not a gambling bill, so none of the listed gaming bills (e.g., AR HB1861, MD HB17, IL HB2239, CT SB01464) are direct comparables. Its closest analogues are the social media minor-protection bills: IN SB0011 (parental consent for users under 16), GA HB1296 and GA SB351, CO HB1287 and CO SB086, AR HB1726 and AR HB1717, and CT SB01295. Unusual features are the under-18 threshold, the near-total ban on personalized algorithmic feeds and on advertising to minors, mandatory parental access to private messages, and the express carve-out for COPPA-compliant interactive gaming platforms.","watch":"Operators with social or community features (e.g., in-app feeds, chat, or promotional targeting) should check whether they meet the 'social media platform' definition, since the ad-targeting and personalization bans would reach marketing to under-18 users, while the COPPA-compliant interactive gaming platform exclusion is the key carve-out to confirm.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":8205,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:56:51.143Z"},"RI-H5643":{"summary":"RI H5643 amends the casino gaming crimes statute (R.I. Gen. Laws § 42-61.3-2) to add a definition of \"iGaming\" and to set a separate minimum age of 21 for playing iGaming casino games, while keeping 18 for in-person casino games. It adds new offenses for underage iGaming play and for permitting or accepting wagers from under-21s on iGaming, with misdemeanor penalties. The rest of the section, the cheating, hacking and unlicensed-operation felonies, is largely left as is.","mechanics":["New § 42-61.3-2(a)(8) defines \"iGaming\" by reference to § 42-61.2-1, so scope depends on that existing definition.","Subsections (b)(20) and (21) are amended so the 18+ rule applies to casino games \"other than an iGaming casino game\"; an under-18 player is referred to family court, and permitting or accepting a wager from an under-18 is a misdemeanor (up to 1 year and/or $1,000).","New (b)(22): playing or attempting to play an iGaming casino game or wagering while under 21 is unlawful; a person aged 18 to under 21 faces a misdemeanor (up to 1 year and/or $1,000), and a person under 18 is referred to family court.","New (b)(23): permitting, or attempting to permit, a person under 21 to play an iGaming casino game, or accepting or attempting to accept their wager, is a misdemeanor (up to 1 year and/or $1,000), with no scienter or due-diligence defense stated.","Existing felony offenses (up to 10 years and/or $100,000) are unchanged, including (b)(16) hacking casino gaming-related networks and software, (b)(17) trading confidential information, and (b)(18) conducting a gaming operation without a license or authorization from the division of state lottery.","Effective upon passage (Section 2); the bill has no rulemaking, age-verification standard, or safe harbor."],"issue_tags":["legalize","integrity","licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"This is a narrow criminal-code conforming amendment, not a licensing or tax bill like the iGaming authorization bills in IL (HB2239, HB3080, HB4797), MD (HB17, HB1343, SB340, SB885) or MA (H332, H4431, S235). Its 21+ iGaming age floor sits alongside the 18+ in-person floor, a split that CT HB05038 echoes in a different context, since that bill bars prediction market platforms from serving under-21s. Unlike AR HB1861, which makes illegal online casino or sports betting operation a felony, this bill adds no new operator-side offenses beyond underage-play provisions.","watch":"Operators should note that (b)(23) imposes misdemeanor liability for permitting or accepting wagers from under-21s on iGaming with no stated mens rea or verification safe harbor, and that the 18/21 split means age-gating and geolocation or product-classification controls must separate iGaming from in-person play.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":15648,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:56:53.631Z"},"RI-S623":{"summary":"RI S0623 amends the casino gaming crimes statute, R.I. Gen. Laws § 42-61.3-2, so it fits Rhode Island's iGaming regime. It adds an \"iGaming\" definition that cross-references § 42-61.2-1. It carves iGaming casino games out of the existing under-18 play and permit offenses and creates parallel offenses that set the iGaming age at 21. The existing cheating, hacking and unlicensed-operation offenses are left in place.","mechanics":["New § 42-61.3-2(a)(8) defines \"iGaming\" by reference to § 42-61.2-1, with no new substantive definition in this bill.","Amended (b)(20) and (b)(21) now apply to casino games \"other than an iGaming casino game.\" Under-18 play or wagering is referred to family court. Permitting or accepting a wager from an under-18 person is a misdemeanor, up to 1 year and/or $1,000.","New (b)(22) bars anyone under 21 from playing an iGaming casino game or wagering, or attempting to. Ages 18 to under 21 face a misdemeanor, up to 1 year and/or $1,000. Under-18s are referred to family court.","New (b)(23) makes it a misdemeanor, up to 1 year and/or $1,000, to permit or attempt to permit an under-21 person to play an iGaming casino game, or to accept or attempt to accept a wager from one. This is the operator-facing provision, and it covers attempts.","The other offenses in (b)(1)-(19), including hacking under (b)(16), unlicensed gaming operation under (b)(18), and false statements to regulators under (b)(19), keep their felony penalties of up to 10 years and/or $100,000. The bill does not extend the \"gaming facility\" definition to online platforms.","Effective upon passage (Section 2). Passed the Senate on 2025-06-10 and has not yet passed the other chamber."],"issue_tags":["legalize","integrity","licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"This is a narrow criminal-code conforming bill for a state that already has iGaming. It differs from the iGaming authorization bills in IL (HB2239, HB3080, HB4797), MD (HB17, HB1343, SB340) and MA (H332, H4431, S235), which build licensing frameworks. It also differs from the prohibition bills in AR HB1861 and MD HB295/SB112, which target illegal online operators. Its notable feature is setting a 21 minimum for iGaming, higher than the 18 for casino games, with criminal penalties for both players and those who permit play. The listed bills do not set a comparable dual-age criminal structure.","watch":"Operators should check that the (b)(23) offense, which reaches anyone who permits or accepts a wager from an under-21 person and includes attempts, has no knowledge or reasonable-reliance defense, so age-verification failures could create misdemeanor exposure, and that the iGaming definition in § 42-61.2-1 covers all of their offerings.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":15628,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:56:53.847Z"},"RI-H5291":{"summary":"H5291 adds R.I. Gen. Laws chapter 6-61, requiring social media companies (platforms with at least 5 million account holders worldwide) to verify age and obtain express parental consent before a Rhode Island minor under 18 may hold an account, starting January 1, 2026. It also imposes default restrictions on minor accounts (no ads, no targeted content, limited messaging and search visibility, a 10:30 p.m.–6:30 a.m. curfew, parental access to all content). Enforcement is by the Department of Business Regulation with a cure period, plus a private right of action. It is not a gaming bill.","mechanics":["Coverage: 'social media company' is an interactive computer service whose platform has at least 5,000,000 account holders worldwide (§ 6-61-1(8)). 'Social media platform' excludes, among others, platforms whose predominant function is email or private direct messaging, and interactive gaming or virtual gaming services that allow content creation and related communication (§ 6-61-1(9)(ii)(E)), plus online shopping/e-commerce, preselected content and streaming.","Age verification and consent (§ 6-61-2): from 1/1/2026 the company must verify the age of new and existing Rhode Island account holders and confirm parental consent for minors. Existing accounts have 14 days from the attempted access to verify, after which access is denied until verification is complete. DBR must write rules on verification methods and acceptable ID, consent confirmation, and data retention and disposal. Any verification agent must have its principal place of business in the U.S.","Minor account defaults (§ 6-61-3): no direct messages from users not linked by friending; the account is hidden from search by non-friends; no advertising at all; no collection or use of personal information from posts, messages or usage beyond what is needed for legal compliance; no targeted or suggested groups, products, posts, accounts or users.","Parental access and curfew (§§ 6-61-4, 6-61-5): the consenting parent or guardian gets a password to view all posts, responses and messages. Access is blocked 10:30 p.m.–6:30 a.m., measured by the minor's IP address. Parents may change or remove the curfew and set daily time limits, and minors cannot bypass the restrictions.","Public enforcement (§ 6-61-7): DBR has exclusive administrative authority, apart from the private right of action. It may impose fines of up to $2,500 per violation and sue for declaratory relief, injunctions, disgorgement, restitution and damages. It must give 30 days' written notice, and no action may be brought if the violation is cured and a no-further-violation statement is provided. Courts must award fees, costs and investigative fees to DBR. Repeat violators of an order face civil penalties of up to $5,000 for a second or subsequent violation. DBR must also file an annual report (§ 6-61-8).","Private right of action (§ 6-61-9): an aggrieved person or account holder may sue under § 6-61-2 in superior court for the greater of $2,500 per incident or actual financial, physical and emotional harm, plus attorney fees and costs. Waivers of the chapter's protections are void (§ 6-61-10), including through contract or choice-of-law provisions. The act takes effect 1/1/2026 (§ 2)."],"issue_tags":["advertising","integrity"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"Closest in subject matter are the social media minor-access bills: GA HB1296 and GA SB351, IN SB0011, CO HB1287 and CO SB086, and CT SB01295. Unlike IN SB0011, which covers users under 16 and is enforced by the attorney general, RI H5291 covers all minors under 18, bans advertising and targeted content on minor accounts, imposes a curfew, and adds a private right of action with $2,500 minimum damages per incident. It has nothing in common with the gambling bills listed (AR HB1861, MD, IL, CT SB01464, etc.), and the only gaming link is the carve-out for interactive gaming services in § 6-61-1(9)(ii)(E).","watch":"Operators should check whether any social or community features of their apps (chat, profiles, leaderboards, user-generated content) could fall outside the gaming carve-out in § 6-61-1(9)(ii)(E) and bring them within the 5-million-account threshold, since that would trigger the age verification, ad ban and per-incident private damages.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":19972,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:56:53.881Z"},"RI-S929":{"summary":"RI S0929 adds a new chapter 6-61 to Title 6 that bars social media companies (platforms with at least 5 million account holders worldwide) from allowing Rhode Island minors under 18 to hold accounts without express parental consent, and requires age verification from January 1, 2026. For minor accounts it also imposes default restrictions: no advertising, no targeted or suggested content, limited messaging and search visibility, a 10:30 p.m.-6:30 a.m. access curfew, and parental access to all posts and messages. The Department of Business Regulation enforces the chapter with a 30-day cure period, and a private right of action exists for violations of the consent requirement.","mechanics":["Coverage (§ 6-61-1): 'social media company' means an interactive computer service whose platform has at least 5,000,000 account holders worldwide. The platform definition excludes, among others, platforms whose predominant function is email or private direct messaging, and any service that provides interactive gaming, virtual gaming, or content uploading for gaming, edutainment, or associated entertainment (§ 6-61-1(9)(ii)(E)). It also excludes e-commerce, preselected news, sports, or entertainment content, and educational tools.","Consent and verification (§ 6-61-2): from 1/1/2026, minors (under 18) need express parent or guardian consent. Age is verified at account opening, or within 14 days of an existing holder's access attempt, and access is denied until verification is complete. DBA rulemaking will set verification methods, acceptable ID (not limited to government ID), and data retention and disposal. Verification data may be used only for compliance, and any verification agent must have its principal place of business in the U.S.","Minor-account defaults (§§ 6-61-3, 6-61-5): no direct messages from non-friends, no appearance in search results for non-friends, no advertising, no targeted or suggested groups, products, posts, or accounts, and no collection or use of personal information from the account beyond what compliance requires. Access is blocked from 10:30 p.m. to 6:30 a.m. by IP-based local time, and parents may modify the curfew and set daily hour limits. Minors cannot bypass these controls.","Parental access (§ 6-61-4): the consenting parent or guardian must be given a password or other means to view all of the minor's posts, responses, and messages.","Enforcement (§ 6-61-7): DBA has exclusive administrative authority except for the private right of action. The director may impose fines up to $2,500 per violation, and the department may sue for declaratory relief, injunction, disgorgement, restitution, actual damages, and fees. DBA must give 30 days' written notice and may not sue if the violation is cured and a written no-further-violation statement is provided. A repeat violator of an order faces civil penalties up to $5,000.","Private right of action (§ 6-61-9): an aggrieved person or account holder may sue in superior court over non-compliance with § 6-61-2, starting 1/1/2026. Recovery is attorney fees and costs plus the greater of $2,500 per incident or actual financial, physical, and emotional harm. § 6-61-9(c) also refers to violations of §§ 6-61-3 and 6-61-4. Waivers are void (§ 6-61-10), and the act takes effect 1/1/2026."],"issue_tags":["advertising","integrity"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"This is a general minors' social media bill, not a gaming bill, so no gaming-specific bill is directly comparable. It is closest to the social media and minor-protection bills: GA HB1296 and GA SB351 (age verification and parental permission), IN SB0011 (verifiable parental consent for users under 16), CO HB1287 and CO SB086 (tools and protections for minor users), and AR HB1717 and HB1726 (kids' online privacy and safety). It stands out for its breadth: an under-18 consent rule, a nightly curfew, a total advertising ban, parental access to private messages, and a private right of action with $2,500 minimum damages. Its definition also carves out interactive gaming and virtual gaming services from 'social media platform'. The bill has been held for further study in committee.","watch":"Operators should check whether any social or community features, such as chat, profiles, or user-generated content, could fall outside the interactive gaming exclusion in § 6-61-1(9)(ii)(E) and meet the 5-million-account threshold, which would trigger the age verification, advertising ban, and curfew requirements.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":19887,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:57:02.786Z"},"TN-SB2097":{"summary":"Tennessee's Protecting Children from Social Media Act (new T.C.A. 47-18-5701 to -5706, Pub. Ch. 899, effective Jan. 1, 2025) requires social media companies to verify the age of anyone opening an account and to obtain verified express parental consent before a Tennessee minor (under 18, unemancipated) can become an account holder. It also requires parental supervision tools, bars retention of identifying data used for verification, and is enforced by the Attorney General under the Tennessee Consumer Protection Act. It contains no gambling, sports-betting or iGaming provisions.","mechanics":["Scope (47-18-5702): applies to a 'social media company' running a 'social media platform', meaning a website or app where users create accounts and communicate through posts. Exclusions include broadband, email, preselected-content services, online shopping, career sites, cloud storage, tech-support/review sites and peer-to-peer payment platforms. 'Content' excludes interactive gaming and educational entertainment.","Age and consent (47-18-5703(a)): the company must verify age when an individual tries to become an account holder. For a minor (known or reasonably believed to be under 18, unemancipated, Tennessee resident) it must verify express parental consent and bar the account without it. No re-verification is needed unless consent is revoked. Only accounts created on or after Jan. 1, 2025 are covered.","Revocation and data (47-18-5703(b)-(c)): parents may revoke consent to open or continue an account. The company or any third party may not retain personally identifying information used to verify age or parental consent.","Parental supervision (47-18-5704): the company must give the parent tools to view privacy settings, set daily time limits and schedule breaks during which the minor cannot access the account.","Enforcement (47-18-5705): the Attorney General may investigate under 47-18-106 and sue under 47-18-108 to recover the penalties and relief available there. Remedies are cumulative. There is no private right of action in the text.","Waiver (47-18-5706): any waiver or limitation of the Act's protections or of the right to cooperate with the AG is void, and courts or arbitrators may not enforce it despite contract or choice-of-law clauses. Effective Jan. 1, 2025 (Section 2)."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"This is a minor-protection social media law and has no gambling content, so the listed gaming bills (AR HB1861, IL HB2239, MD HB1343, CT SB01464 and similar) address a different subject. Its closest comparables are the other states' social media age-verification and parental-consent bills: GA HB1296 and SB351, IN SB0011 (which applies to users under 16), CO HB1287 and SB086, AR HB1726 and HB1717, and CT SB01295. Distinctive features of Tennessee's law are the under-18 threshold, mandatory verified parental consent for all minor accounts, a ban on retaining verification data, AG-only enforcement through the existing consumer protection act and an anti-waiver clause.","watch":"Operators should check whether their apps, social features, community or chat functions or affiliated promotional channels could fall within the 'social media platform' definition (accounts plus posts) and so trigger the age-verification, parental-consent and data-retention limits, even though the Act has no gambling-specific provisions.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":7404,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:57:02.830Z"},"TN-HB1891":{"summary":"Tennessee's Protecting Children from Social Media Act (new T.C.A. 47-18-5701 to 5706) requires social media companies to verify the age of anyone opening an account and to obtain verified express parental consent before a Tennessee-resident minor under 18 can become an account holder. It also requires parental supervision tools, bars retention of the identifying data used for verification, and is enforced by the Attorney General through the Consumer Protection Act. It has no gambling content and reaches operators only if they run a platform that meets the definition of a social media platform.","mechanics":["Scope (47-18-5702): applies to a 'social media company', meaning an interactive computer service that provides a 'social media platform'. A platform is a website or app that lets a person create an account and lets account holders communicate with other account holders and users through posts. 'Account holder' covers only accounts created on or after January 1, 2025.","Minor definition (5702(4)): a person known or reasonably believed by the platform to be under 18, not emancipated, and a Tennessee resident.","Exclusions (5702(9)(B)): broadband access, email, services consisting primarily of provider-preselected content with only incidental interactivity, online shopping with limited interaction, career-development services, cloud services, tech-support or product-review services, and peer-to-peer payment platforms. 'Content' excludes interactive gaming and educational entertainment.","Age and consent (5703): the company must verify age when an individual tries to open an account. For minors it must verify express parental consent and bar the account without it. It need not reverify unless consent is revoked, and it must let a parent revoke consent to become or continue as an account holder. The company or any third party may not retain personally identifying information used for verification.","Parental supervision (5704): the company must give parents tools to view privacy settings, set daily time limits, and schedule breaks during which the minor cannot access the account.","Enforcement (5705, 5706): the Attorney General may investigate under 47-18-106 and sue under 47-18-108 and recover the penalties and relief available there. There is no private right of action in the text. Remedies are cumulative, and waivers of the part's protections, including through contract or choice-of-law clauses, are void. The act takes effect January 1, 2025."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"This is a minor-protection social media statute, not a gambling bill. Its closest analogues in the list are other states' social media bills: GA HB1296 and GA SB351 (age verification and parental permission), IN SB0011 (verifiable parental consent for users under 16), CO HB1287 and CO SB086, and AR HB1726 and HB1717 (kids online safety and privacy). Tennessee sets the cutoff at 18, requires consent at account creation, and is enforced only by the Attorney General. None of the gaming bills listed, such as AR HB1861, MD HB17 or CT SB01464, overlap in substance. The only link is CT HB05038, which also restricts minors' use of a platform category, though there the category is prediction markets.","watch":"Operators should check whether any social or community features in their apps, such as chat, user posts or feeds, could bring them within the 'social media platform' definition. If so, they would need age and parental-consent verification with no data retention, with the gaming exclusion applying only to 'content' and not to the platform definition.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":7404,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:57:03.593Z"},"SC-H4700":{"summary":"H4700 adds Article 9 to Chapter 5, Title 39 of the S.C. Code. It requires large social media companies (at least five million account holders worldwide) to verify age and obtain parental consent before a South Carolina minor under 18 can hold an account. It also imposes default protections for minor accounts: limits on adult direct messaging, no ad targeting based on personal data other than age and location, content filtering, and parental supervision tools. The Attorney General has exclusive administrative enforcement authority, and the bill creates a private right of action with statutory damages. It is not a gaming bill, and the definition of 'social media platform' carves out interactive and virtual gaming services.","mechanics":["Coverage (39-5-810(6)-(7)): a 'social media company' is an interactive computer service whose platform has at least 5 million account holders worldwide. A 'minor' is anyone under 18 whom the company reasonably believes, actually knows, or verifies to be under 18. The 'social media platform' definition excludes, among others, services whose predominant function is interactive or virtual gaming (xxi-vi), preselected news, sports or entertainment content (iv), and e-commerce (v).","Age verification and consent (39-5-820), from March 1, 2025: companies must make commercially reasonable efforts to verify the age of SC account holders, or apply minor protections to all account holders. A minor may hold an account only with express parental or guardian consent, by one of six listed methods including signed form, toll-free call, video call, government ID of the parent, or email plus verification. Data collected for verification may be used only for compliance and must be securely disposed of afterward.","Minor account defaults (39-5-830): adults may not direct-message a minor unless already connected. No advertising based on the minor's personal information other than age and location. Data collection is limited to what is adequate, relevant and reasonably necessary. Companies must make commercially reasonable efforts to filter content on incitement, self-harm, school property destruction and sexual conduct. Companies must report violating content posted by minors to SLED, local law enforcement and school districts.","Parental supervision (39-5-840): a consenting parent or guardian must be given tools to view privacy settings and the minor's connections list, set daily time limits, and schedule breaks. Minors may opt in to parental notifications when they report a person or issue.","Enforcement (39-5-850, 860, 880, 870): the Attorney General receives and investigates complaints and has exclusive administrative enforcement authority except for the private right of action. Under 39-5-880, from March 1, 2025, any person may sue in the circuit court of their residence and recover attorneys' fees and costs plus the greater of $2,500 per incident or actual financial, physical and emotional damages. The AG must publish an annual report on fines and penalties.","Anti-waiver and effective date (39-5-900, Sec. 3): waivers or limitations of the Act's protections, complaint rights or private right of action are void notwithstanding any contract or choice-of-law clause. The Act takes effect on Governor approval, with substantive duties starting March 1, 2025. Section 2 is a severability clause."],"issue_tags":["advertising"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed gaming bills (AR HB1861, the CT, IL, MA and MD internet gaming and sweepstakes bills, DE HB365) is comparable. The closest are the other states' minors-and-social-media bills: GA HB1296 and SB351, IN SB0011, CO HB1287 and SB086, AR HB1717 and HB1726, and CT SB01295. Like GA HB1296 and IN SB0011, H4700 uses age verification plus parental consent. It differs by adding a private right of action with $2,500 per-incident statutory damages, a 5-million-account threshold, and an express carve-out for interactive and virtual gaming platforms.","watch":"Confirm that any operator or card-break or collectibles community feature with user profiles, connections and posted content fits the interactive/virtual gaming, e-commerce or preselected-content exclusions in 39-5-810(7)(b), and note the drafting defects: 39-5-860 and 900 cross-reference a private right of action 'in Section 39-5-890' (it is in 880) and refer to an undefined 'division', and the bill sets no civil penalty amounts despite the annual-report provision.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Draft","text_chars":18224,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:57:09.861Z"},"UT-SB1001":{"summary":"Utah SB1001 (2025 First Special Session) is a pure recodification of the state higher education code. It moves provisions on statewide administration of the higher education system from Title 53B into a new Title 53H, enacts new organizational sections, repeals some, and makes technical and conforming changes. The text shows no gaming, wagering, tax or licensing content, and no appropriation (\"Money Appropriated in this Bill: None\").","mechanics":["Renumbers and amends existing Title 53B sections into new Title 53H chapters, for example 53B-1-101.5 to 53H-1-101 and 53B-2-101 to 53H-3-102.","Enacts new organizational sections across Title 53H, such as 53H-1-301, 53H-2-101 and 53H-3-101, and repeals provisions for organizational purposes.","Sets staggered sunset dates in the renumbered text: 53H-1-604 and 53H-4-313 are repealed 07/01/27, and 53H-4-306.1 to 306.6 and 53H-10-401 to 403 are repealed 07/01/28.","Defines terms and makes technical and conforming changes.","Has a special effective date: the Utah Code Sections Affected list marks the sections effective upon governor's approval.","Signed by the Governor; last action 2025-10-14."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills is a close comparable. The Iowa government-reorganization bills (HF662, HSB126, SF514, SSB1123), the Illinois revisory bills (HB4844, SB3731) and the Michigan appropriations bills are structural or housekeeping measures like this one, and they are only comparable on that basis. By contrast, bills with real gaming content, such as NY S 10092 on gambling ads, OH HB344 on electronic instant bingo and NC H999 on video gaming terminals, do not share its subject. SB1001 is notable only as a large Title 53B to 53H renumbering with no gaming provisions.","watch":"Nothing in the text bears on sports betting or iGaming, so the only item to watch is whether the renumbering affects any cross-references or citations in Utah code that your clients' compliance materials rely on, which seems unlikely.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":229223,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:57:10.291Z"},"TN-SB2372":{"summary":"TN SB2372 adds a new Part 57 to Title 47, Chapter 18 (the Tennessee Consumer Protection Act) that requires social media companies to verify the age of account holders and get express parental consent for minors under 18. It also imposes a 10:30 p.m. to 6:30 a.m. default curfew for minors, requires parental supervision tools, and bars design features that the company knows or should know cause minor addiction. It is enforced by the Attorney General with a cure period, plus a private right of action for addiction-related harm. It has no gambling or gaming provisions apart from an exclusion in the definitions.","mechanics":["Age and consent (§ 47-18-5702): platforms must verify age at account creation for IP addresses in Tennessee, and for existing resident accounts within 14 days of an access attempt. Minors need express parental consent, and without it the account is blocked except for deletion. Parents can revoke consent (§ 47-18-5702(b)).","Curfew and parental controls (§§ 47-18-5702(c), 5703): minors are locked out from 10:30 p.m. to 6:30 a.m., measured by IP address, unless the parent modifies it. Platforms must give parents views of privacy settings, posts, and messages, plus time limits and access outside curfew, and must not let minors bypass these controls.","Anti-addiction duty (§ 47-18-5704): bars features the company knows or should know cause a minor account holder to develop an addiction (substantial preoccupation plus harm). Affirmative defense: quarterly audits and correction of any more-than-de-minimis risk within 30 days of each audit. Third-party and user-generated content is carved out.","Scope (§ 47-18-5701(11)): 'social media platform' excludes services whose predominant or exclusive function is, among other things, interactive gaming, virtual gaming, or content upload for interactive gaming and associated communication (subdivision (11)(B)(i)(f)). It also excludes email, direct messaging, preselected news/sports/entertainment content, and online shopping with limited interaction.","Enforcement (§ 47-18-5706): the AG investigates under § 47-18-106 and must give written notice with at least 30 days to cure. Failure to cure, or a repeat violation, allows an action seeking an injunction, civil penalties up to $2,500 per violation, actual damages, and punitive damages. Remedies are cumulative.","Private action and waivers (§§ 47-18-5707, 5708): a private action for harm from an addiction violation (§ 47-18-5704) recovers the greater of $2,500 per incident of harm or actual damages, with fees to the prevailing party. Waivers are void notwithstanding contract or choice-of-law clauses. The Department of Commerce and Insurance issues age-verification rules (§ 47-18-5705); rulemaking is effective on enactment and the rest on Jan. 1, 2025."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"This is a general social media minor-protection bill, so it is closest to the social media bills on the list: GA HB1296 (age verification and parental permission), GA SB351, IN SB0011 (verifiable parental consent for users under 16), CO HB1287 and CO SB086, and AR HB1726 and HB1717. Unlike IN SB0011, which covers users under 16, TN covers all minors under 18 and adds a default overnight curfew, full parental access to messages, and a private right of action with $2,500 statutory damages for addiction. None of the gaming bills listed (AR HB1861, IL HB2239, MD HB17, CT SB01464) are comparable, because TN SB2372 does not regulate wagering.","watch":"Operators should confirm that their sports betting or iGaming apps with social or community features fall within the interactive-gaming exclusion in § 47-18-5701(11)(B)(i)(f), since a social feed or chat function that is not tied to gaming content could bring them within the age-verification, curfew, and addiction-design duties.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Draft","text_chars":16281,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:57:13.886Z"},"UT-SB133":{"summary":"Utah SB 133 creates a standalone offense of metal or catalytic converter theft (new Section 76-6-415) and removes the catalytic converter and 25-pound suspect-metal felony triggers from the general theft, theft by deception, theft by extortion and retail theft statutes. It also clarifies the catalytic converter purchase database provision, updates the metal dealer definitions, and adds the new offense to Utah's pattern of unlawful activity (racketeering) list. The bill has no connection to gaming, wagering or collectibles.","mechanics":["New Section 76-6-415 makes it an offense to obtain or exercise unauthorized control over another's metal or catalytic converter with purpose to deprive; \"metal\" covers nonferrous, ferrous and regulated metal and suspect metal items, and excludes jewelry.","Grading: second degree felony if value is $5,000 or more or the metal is taken from the person of another; third degree felony if value is $1,500 to under $5,000, if the property is a catalytic converter, or if it is 25+ pounds of suspect metal items containing aluminum or copper (not lead batteries) worth under $5,000.","Third degree felony also applies for $500+ with two prior theft, fraud or robbery-type convictions within 10 years, for $500 to under $1,500 on property where the actor has stolen in the last five years and received written notice under 78B-3-108(4), or for a prior felony conviction of the listed offenses; lower tiers are class A misdemeanor ($500 to under $1,500) and class B misdemeanor (under $500).","Sections 76-6-404, -405, -406 and -602 are amended to delete the catalytic converter and suspect-metal felony provisions, with subsection cross-references renumbered; 78B-3-108 shoplifting civil penalty cross-references are conformed.","Section 76-6-1402 definitions are amended, including replacing the reference to the Institute of Scrap Recycling Industries circular with the Recycled Materials Association materials specifications circular for #1 and #2 copper; Section 13-32a-104.7(7) now says a dealer that makes a catalytic converter purchase must comply with the Regulation of Metal Dealers part.","Section 76-10-1602 adds metal or catalytic converter theft (76-6-415) as unlawful activity under the pattern of unlawful activity definition, with the remaining paragraphs relettered. The bill has a special effective date, and the enrolled text provided does not state it. Governor signed March 2025."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills from Iowa, Illinois, Michigan, North Carolina, Nebraska, New York, Ohio, Virginia and others is comparable. They are budget, appropriations, cannabis, revisory or gambling measures (for example NC H999 on video gaming terminals, NE LB402 on gambling winnings setoff, NY S10092 on gambling ads) and share no substantive subject with this metal theft bill. The listed overlap appears to be only a general-purpose or incidental match; SB 133 is unusual for the gaming bar mainly in that it has nothing to do with gaming.","watch":"Nothing in this bill affects sports-betting, iGaming or collectibles operators; the only point of note is that it adds an offense to the Utah pattern of unlawful activity list in 76-10-1602, which already includes gambling offenses.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":51303,"generated":"2026-11-03T22:57:13.968Z"},"AZ-HB4154":{"summary":"HB4154 is Arizona's FY2026-27 general appropriations act. In the text provided it amends the FY2025-26 Department of Corrections appropriation (Laws 2025, ch. 233, sec. 23) and sets FY2026-27 agency budgets running from the Board of Accountancy through the start of the Department of Child Safety. Nothing in the supplied text addresses sports betting, iGaming, collectibles or trading cards.","mechanics":["Sec. 1 amends the FY2025-26 Corrections appropriation: operating lump sum rises to $828,190,500, private prison per diem falls to $266,956,700, inmate health care contracted services falls to $371,499,400, and total Corrections appropriation is $1,600,697,200.","Sec. 2 provides that appropriations are available only from the listed funding sources, and that no other source may be used if those monies are unavailable.","Sec. 10 appropriates AHCCCS (Medicaid) a total of $22,960,437,700, including $2,919,510,700 from the state general fund. It sets aside $4,244,300,000 of expenditure authority for HEALTH initiative payments funded by hospital assessments under A.R.S. 36-2999.72.","Sec. 10 directs AHCCCS to transfer up to $1,200,000 to the attorney general for e-cigarette enforcement and tobacco settlement litigation, and $836,000 to the Department of Revenue for enforcement of the 2013 master settlement agreement.","Sec. 13 appropriates $106,604,400 to the Attorney General, including line items for tobacco enforcement ($877,000), internet crimes against children, and an organized retail theft task force ($1,645,200).","Sec. 7 funds Department of Revenue integrated tax system modernization at $3,656,800 and sets minimum specifications, including querying, reporting and an income tax model with taxpayer confidentiality protections."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. This is an omnibus budget bill, and in the portion provided it has no gaming-specific provisions: no gaming regulator appropriation, no wagering tax or revenue allocation, and no problem-gambling funding. Its closest touchpoints are general-fund-supported tax administration and attorney general enforcement lines, none tied to wagering.","watch":"The supplied text is truncated at the Department of Child Safety, so check the remaining sections, especially the Department of Gaming, Department of Revenue, Attorney General and any budget reconciliation or revenue bills, for sports-betting-related appropriations, fund transfers or enforcement funding.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Engrossed","text_chars":249767,"generated":"2026-11-03T23:00:35.508Z"},"AZ-HB2962":{"summary":"HB2962 is Arizona's FY2026 general appropriations bill, as engrossed in the House. It amends the FY2024-25 Department of Child Safety appropriation in Laws 2024, ch. 209, sec. 25, and then sets 2025-26 line-item appropriations for dozens of agencies, including AHCCCS, the Attorney General, Corrections and DCS. The portion of the text provided has no provisions on sports betting, gaming, iGaming or collectibles. The Governor vetoed the bill on 2025-06-25.","mechanics":["Sec. 1 amends the FY2024-25 DCS appropriation, with the total raised to $1,322,004,400 and the caseworkers line raised to $146,739,700.","Sec. 15 sets the FY2025-26 DCS total at $1,247,672,800, of which $441,589,500 is from the state general fund. It carries over the $300 monthly kinship stipend and benchmarks of 1,000 backlog cases, fewer than 8,000 open reports and 3,323 long-term cases.","Sec. 9 sets the AHCCCS total at $21,729,347,900, including $3,606,700,000 for hospital enhanced access leading to health improvements (HEALTH) initiative payments.","Sec. 9 directs AHCCCS to transfer up to $1,200,000 to the Attorney General for e-cigarette enforcement and tobacco settlement litigation, and $836,000 to the Department of Revenue for enforcement of the 2013 tobacco master settlement agreement. These are the only enforcement-related items in the text provided.","Sec. 11 appropriates $87,967,000 to the Attorney General, including $1,575,700 for an organized retail theft task force and $877,500 for tobacco enforcement.","Sec. 2 limits spending to the listed funding sources and bars substitution of other sources if those monies are unavailable. The text provided is cut off partway through the Department of Corrections section."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. On its own, this is a general budget bill with no wagering, gaming or collectibles content in the text provided. It was also vetoed, so none of its appropriations took effect.","watch":"Nothing in the text provided affects sports-betting, iGaming or collectibles operators, but the text is truncated after the Corrections section, so it would be worth checking the remaining agency sections and any budget reconciliation bills for gaming-related items such as Department of Gaming funding.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Engrossed","text_chars":193436,"generated":"2026-11-03T23:00:36.025Z"},"AZ-HB4155":{"summary":"Arizona HB4155 is largely a fund-housekeeping bill. It folds the separate fantasy sports contest fund (A.R.S. 5-1212) into the event wagering fund (5-1318), moves the unarmed combat subaccount into that fund, and adds a one-year 0.5 percent regulatory assessment on racing pari-mutuel handle. It leaves the 10 percent caps on the event wagering and fantasy sports fees unchanged and adds a $500,000 annual problem gambling allocation and grant authority.","mechanics":["Sec. 6 repeals A.R.S. 5-1212 and transfers all unexpended, unencumbered monies in the fantasy sports contest fund to the event wagering fund (5-1318) on the section's effective date.","Sec. 5 amends 5-1211: fantasy sports fees (cap of 10 percent of adjusted revenues, due the 25th of each month) and the late-payment penalties (5 percent per month, max 25 percent) now go to the event wagering fund. The fee cap itself is unchanged.","Sec. 7 amends 5-1318: the event wagering fee cap stays at 10 percent of adjusted gross event wagering receipts, with the cash or modified accrual election and the 25th-of-following-month due date. The fund now also receives 5-1211 fantasy sports monies. 90 percent of the prior month's deposits still transfers to the general fund by the 25th.","Sec. 7 (subsections D and E) keeps the 10 percent cap on department spending to regulate chapters 5-1301 et seq. and chapter 10, and designates up to $500,000 per fiscal year of that amount for problem gambling programs. The department may also use that money plus other problem gambling funds (private donations, grants, federal money) to disburse problem gambling grants.","Secs. 2-4 and 7(C) move the unarmed combat (boxing/MMA) subaccount from the racing regulation fund to a subaccount of the event wagering fund. License fees and boxing gross receipts tax proceeds (4 percent) are deposited there, and the department may transfer regulatory spending from the event wagering fund into it.","Sec. 8 requires the department of gaming, in FY2026-27, to collect a 0.5 percent regulatory assessment on amounts wagered on live and simulcast races (in-state and out-of-state), paid from pool deductions on top of the 5-111(B) takeout. Sec. 9 lets first-time starter horses race in 2026-2027 with gate approval and two timed workouts; the section is repealed after Dec. 31, 2027."],"issue_tags":["tax","licensing","integrity"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed, so there is nothing to compare against directly. On its own, the bill is unusual in using the sports betting event wagering fund as the single repository for fantasy sports fees and unarmed combat (boxing/MMA) licensing money. It also adds a dedicated $500,000 problem gambling carve-out and a temporary racing pool assessment, and it changes no tax rates.","watch":"Operators should check that the 10 percent event wagering fee cap and the 90 percent general fund transfer are unchanged, and watch how the department uses its 10 percent regulatory share, which now also funds problem gambling grants (up to $500,000 a year) and supports the unarmed combat subaccount, since that could affect future fee-setting and assessments.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":13223,"generated":"2026-11-03T23:00:38.424Z"},"AZ-SB1126":{"summary":"SB1126 creates a continuously appropriated equine economic improvement fund (new A.R.S. 5-113.02) and redirects part of Arizona's event wagering and fantasy sports contest revenue to horse-racing purposes. It does not change the event wagering fee authority in 5-1318(A), which stays capped at 10% of adjusted gross event wagering receipts. It changes where the money goes after collection: the general fund share of event wagering revenue falls and equine funds gain.","mechanics":["New 5-113.02 creates the equine economic improvement fund, administered by the department. It is funded by deposits under 5-1212 and 5-1318 or from any other source, and its monies are continuously appropriated and exempt from lapsing under 35-190.","Annual distribution by August 1 under 5-113.02(B): $25,000 to the retired racehorse adoption fund, then all remaining monies to a recognized nonprofit horsemen's organization that has represented horsemen in racing meetings since 1988.","Event wagering (5-1318(B)): by the 25th of each month the prior month's event wagering fund deposits are split 75% to the state general fund, 10% to the equine economic improvement fund and 5% to the Arizona breeders' award fund. The text shows 'ninety' replaced by 'seventy-five', so the general fund share is reduced.","Fantasy sports (5-1212(C), new): each year the department transfers 5% of annual fund revenue to each of the breeders' award fund, the stallion award fund and the equine economic improvement fund, 15% in total. 5-113(F) and (G) are conformed to match.","The 10% departmental spending cap on regulating and enforcing the event wagering and fantasy chapters is kept in 5-1318(C) and 5-1212(D). The excess-revenue sweep to the general fund in 5-1212 is also kept.","Technical edits to 5-113 are minor: 'chairperson', 'minus', and 'prescribed by' wording. The $25,000 annual transfers from the breeders' award and stallion award funds to the retired racehorse adoption fund remain."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed, so there is nothing to compare against. On its own it is unusual in earmarking event wagering and DFS revenue for the horse-racing industry. It routes the money to a private nonprofit horsemen's group through a continuously appropriated fund, bypassing the normal appropriation process.","watch":"The operator fee rate and cap are unchanged, but the allocation shifts general-fund money to equine funds, and the percentages as engrossed (75% + 10% + 5%) total only 90% with no stated destination for the other 10%. That gap should be clarified, and the diversion could shape future fee-setting and revenue-share debates.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Engrossed","text_chars":16677,"generated":"2026-11-03T23:00:43.239Z"},"AZ-SB1671":{"summary":"AZ SB1671 is a sunset-continuation bill that extends the Arizona Department of Gaming, the Arizona Racing Commission and the Arizona State Boxing and Mixed Martial Arts Commission to July 1, 2032 (repeal of the underlying statutes January 1, 2033). It adds annual conflict-of-interest disclosure requirements for department and commission personnel, creates a complaint intake and tracking system with an annual report to legislative leaders, and expands the event wagering annual report in A.R.S. 5-1312.","mechanics":["Sections 6-7 repeal old sunset provisions (41-3026.04, .13, .22) and add new termination dates: Racing Commission (41-3032.11), Boxing/MMA Commission (41-3032.15) and Department of Gaming (41-3032.20) all terminate July 1, 2032, with the related statutes repealed January 1, 2033. Section 11 makes these provisions retroactive to July 1, 2026.","Sections 1-3 (5-101.01, 5-223, 5-604) require the racing director, deputy director and employees, the boxing commissioners, executive director and employees, and the gaming director and employees to file a conflict-of-interest disclosure on appointment or hire and update it annually if information has changed. Under 5-101.01, failure to comply is grounds for dismissal.","New 5-606 requires the department to build a complaint submission process, posted on its website, and a tracking system covering complaints under title 5 chapters 6, 2, 10 and 11. Chapter 11 covers event wagering, so operator-related complaints are within scope.","5-606(B) requires an annual report by September 30 to the Senate president, House speaker and governor with aggregate complaint data on persons and entities licensed or regulated under title 5 and within the department's authority.","Section 5 amends 5-1312 to add to the existing September 30 event wagering report (licensee counts, aggregate gross and net revenue, investigations, financial impact) a new subsection C. It requires a statement of how many independent audit results under 5-1319 were received from licensed operators and management services providers, and any corrective action on privilege fee overpayments or underpayments.","Section 8 states the legislature continues the department to act as state gaming agency under tribal compacts and IGRA, ensure fantasy sports integrity, enforce event wagering laws, and regulate racing and boxing/MMA with their commissions."],"issue_tags":["integrity","licensing"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. On its own the bill is a routine sunset continuation with a six-year extension rather than a substantive change to wagering law. The event-wagering content is limited to the new 5-1312(C) audit and privilege-fee reporting and the complaint tracking in 5-606.","watch":"Watch the new 5-606 complaint tracking system and the expanded 5-1312(C) report on 5-1319 independent audits and privilege fee corrections, since they create a public record that could prompt enforcement scrutiny of operators and management services providers.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":11466,"generated":"2026-11-03T23:00:44.336Z"},"AZ-SB1735":{"summary":"SB1735 is Arizona's FY2025-26 general appropriations act, signed as Chapter 233. It amends three FY2024-25 line items in Laws 2024, ch. 209: Department of Child Safety (sec. 25), Department of Corrections (sec. 31), and the state highway fund transfer (sec. 134). It then appropriates FY2025-26 operating budgets agency by agency. The text supplied stops partway through the Attorney General's budget (Sec. 13) and contains no wagering, gaming, or collectibles provisions.","mechanics":["Sec. 1 raises the DCS operating lump sum from $147,799,100 to $151,250,000 and the DCS total from $1,262,616,900 to $1,322,004,400. It keeps the $300 monthly kinship stipend and a 2% operating withholding if required JLBC reports are more than 30 days late.","Sec. 2 cuts the Department of Corrections total from $1,584,593,700 to $1,566,154,400. Noncontract medication drops from $55,000,000 to $18,000,000, and $16,000,000 is added for injunction-related IT upgrades tied to Jensen v. Thornell.","Sec. 3 reduces the FY2024-25 state highway fund transfer to the general fund from $18,700,000 to $17,200,000.","Sec. 4 limits each appropriation to its listed fund sources and bars substitution of other sources if those monies are unavailable.","Sec. 11 appropriates $22,654,530,300 to AHCCCS, with $2,611,707,100 from the state general fund. It also moves up to $1,200,000 to the Attorney General for e-cigarette enforcement and tobacco settlement litigation, and $836,000 to the Department of Revenue for master settlement agreement enforcement.","Sec. 13 gives the Attorney General's department of law $98,506,100, including $877,500 for tobacco enforcement. The text provided ends partway through the fund sources."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No other states' bills are listed for comparison. This is an omnibus state budget, and the portion of text provided has no sports betting, iGaming, or trading-card provisions. The only nearby items are tobacco and e-cigarette enforcement funding (AHCCCS transfers and the AG tobacco enforcement line), which are not gaming-related.","watch":"The text is cut off after Sec. 13, so check the remaining sections for a Department of Gaming appropriation, any fund transfers from gaming or event wagering revenue, and budget reconciliation bill cross-references, none of which appear in the portion supplied.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":289658,"generated":"2026-11-03T23:00:49.507Z"},"AZ-SB1274":{"summary":"SB1274 (Ch. 182, signed 2025-05-13) is an omnibus technical-corrections bill amending A.R.S. 42-1008, 42-5001, 42-5061, 43-403, 43-405, 43-1014 and 43-1414, plus Laws 2023, ch. 147, sec. 3. The text provided is cut off partway through 42-5061 and shows no strike/insert markup for most sections, so only a few changes can be identified. The only gaming-adjacent item visible is the transaction privilege tax (TPT) retail-classification exemption for lottery tickets. It contains no sports wagering, iGaming or fantasy provisions.","mechanics":["Sec. 1 (42-1008): Extends the ban on preparing tax returns for compensation to agents and contractors of the Department of Revenue and their managers or supervisors, and makes a violation grounds for immediate discharge, dismissal or removal from the department duties.","Sec. 3 (42-5061(A)(20)): The lottery-ticket exclusion from the retail TPT base now cites title 5, chapter 5.1, article 2 instead of article 1. This looks like a cross-reference fix, not a change in what is exempt.","Sec. 2 (42-5001): Restates the TPT definitions, including marketplace, marketplace facilitator, marketplace seller, remote seller, and 'sale' (which includes auctions and facilitated transactions). The text provided does not show what changed.","Sec. 3 (42-5061(A)(21)): Restates the retail exclusion for cash equivalents (gift cards, stored-value cards, vouchers) and precious metal bullion. It keeps taxation on redemption of a cash equivalent for taxable goods or services. The text does not show what changed.","The text is truncated within 42-5061(B), and the income tax sections (43-403, 43-405, 43-1014, 43-1414) and the 2023 session-law amendment are not visible. Their effective dates and any other changes cannot be confirmed."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"None of the listed bills is truly comparable. The Alaska (HB145, SB194), Georgia, Hawaii (HB1308, HB2570) and Illinois (HB3159, HB5143) bills authorize or tax sports wagering, while AZ SB1274 imposes no wagering tax and creates no license or program. The closest in form are the Iowa Department of Revenue clean-up bills (HSB723, SF2427, SSB3179) and Illinois HB2755 (revenue omnibus). Those bills expressly include sports wagering provisions, and SB1274 does not.","watch":"Little here affects sports-betting operators; the only gaming-related item is the lottery-ticket TPT cross-reference. Collectibles and card clients should check the marketplace-facilitator, sale and cash-equivalent definitions against the enrolled bill, because the strike/insert markup is missing from this text.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":86600,"generated":"2026-11-03T23:00:52.094Z"},"AZ-SB1847":{"summary":"SB1847 is Arizona's FY2026-27 general appropriations act. It amends the FY2025-26 Department of Corrections appropriation (Laws 2025, ch. 233, sec. 23) and then funds state agencies for 2026-27, starting with the Board of Accountancy and running through the first lines of the Department of Child Safety. The text provided is truncated and contains no sports betting, iGaming, or collectibles provisions.","mechanics":["Sec. 1 amends the 2025-26 Department of Corrections appropriation, with changes to the operating lump sum ($828,190,500), private prison per diem ($266,956,700) and inmate health care contracted services ($371,499,400). The total is $1,600,697,200, of which $1,537,433,000 is from the general fund.","Sec. 2 limits spending to the listed fund sources: if monies from a listed source are unavailable, no other funding source may be used.","Sec. 5 appropriates $299,933,300 to the Department of Administration, including one-time county distributions: $10,000,000 for Gila County disaster relief and $7,000,000 for county sheriff support.","Sec. 7 funds the Department of Revenue integrated tax system modernization ($3,656,800) and sets minimum specifications, including querying and modeling of individual and corporate income tax data within 24 months after the end of a tax year.","Sec. 10 appropriates $22,960,437,700 to AHCCCS (Medicaid), including $2,919,510,700 from the general fund. It transfers up to $1,200,000 to the attorney general for e-cigarette enforcement and tobacco settlement litigation, and $836,000 to the Department of Revenue for master settlement agreement enforcement.","Sec. 13 appropriates $106,604,400 to the Attorney General, with the largest earmarks being coordinated reentry grants ($17,000,000) and the organized retail theft task force ($1,645,200)."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. This is a general appropriations act, and the provided text contains no wagering, gaming, or collectibles provisions. The nearest points of contact are generic: the Department of Revenue tax-system modernization specifications in Sec. 7 and the Attorney General's consumer protection and enforcement funding in Sec. 13.","watch":"Nothing in the provided text affects operators directly, but the text is truncated at the Department of Child Safety, so check the remaining agency sections, especially the Department of Gaming and Department of Revenue, for any gaming-related lines or footnotes.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":250279,"generated":"2026-11-03T23:00:55.104Z"},"AZ-SB1831":{"summary":"SB1831 is Arizona's general appropriations act for FY 2026-27, setting line-item spending, FTE counts and fund sources for state agencies. In the portion of the text provided (Secs. 1-21, ending mid-section in the Department of Corrections), there is no provision on sports betting, iGaming, sweepstakes, trading cards or any other gaming topic. Any appropriation for gaming regulators would sit in the later, truncated sections.","mechanics":["Sec. 1: appropriations are made only from the listed fund sources, and no other source may be used if the listed monies are unavailable.","Sec. 4: the Department of Administration receives a total of $297,824,500, including $20,435,300 from the general fund, with reporting duties to the Joint Legislative Budget Committee (JLBC).","Sec. 9: AHCCCS receives $22,798,839,000 in total, including $2,900,224,700 from the general fund. This includes $4,244,300,000 for hospital enhanced access leading to health improvements (HEALTH) initiative payments, funded partly by hospital assessments under A.R.S. 36-2999.72. It also directs transfers of up to $1,200,000 to the attorney general for e-cigarette enforcement and tobacco settlement litigation, and $836,000 to the Department of Revenue for master settlement agreement enforcement.","Sec. 6: $19,756,800 funds the Department of Revenue integrated tax system modernization, paid by a $12,538,400 general fund transfer and a $7,218,400 transfer from the integrated tax system project fund (A.R.S. 42-5041). It must meet minimum data and modeling specifications, including income tax data available for querying within 24 months of the tax year's end.","Sec. 11: the Attorney General receives a total of $89,604,400, including a $1,645,200 organized retail theft task force line and a $1,613,300 internet crimes against children line, with no gaming-specific enforcement line in the visible text.","Sec. 15 and Sec. 21: the Department of Child Safety receives $1,354,483,600 and the Department of Corrections $1,625,011,200, with benchmark reporting and expenditure-plan review requirements that do not touch gaming."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. This is an omnibus state budget, and the portion provided contains no wagering provisions at all, so it has nothing directly comparable to sports-betting, iGaming or trading-card legislation. Any gaming content would have to be found in the truncated later sections, such as a Department of Gaming appropriation.","watch":"Review the truncated remainder of the bill, especially any Department of Gaming, Department of Revenue or Attorney General lines, for event wagering oversight funding, fund transfers and footnotes, since none of that appears in Secs. 1-21.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Engrossed","text_chars":201883,"generated":"2026-11-03T23:00:55.792Z"},"CO-HB1311":{"summary":"HB25-1311 amends C.R.S. 44-30-1501(7)(c) to accelerate the phase-out of the free bets deduction used to compute Colorado's net sports betting proceeds. The 2% cap is cut short to end December 31, 2025, a 1.75% cap applies for January 1 through June 30, 2026, and from July 1, 2026 operators may not deduct any free bets. It also appropriates $17,135 from the sports betting fund to the Department of Revenue for implementation.","mechanics":["Existing deduction regime in 44-30-1501(7)(c)(I) is limited to January 1, 2023 through June 30, 2026; the deduction is capped as a percentage of total bets placed with the operator each month.","Amended (7)(c)(I)(C): the cap is no more than 2% of total monthly bets, now running July 1, 2025 through December 31, 2025 (previously through June 30, 2026).","Amended (7)(c)(I)(D): the cap is no more than 1.75% of total monthly bets, now running January 1, 2026 through June 30, 2026 (previously began July 1, 2026).","New (7)(c)(I.5): on and after July 1, 2026, a sports betting operator or internet sports betting operator shall not deduct any free bets, so the deduction is eliminated entirely.","Section 2 appropriates $17,135 for FY 2025-26 from the sports betting fund (44-30-1509(1)(a)): $13,906 for GenTax IT support, $1,024 for taxation business group personal services, and $2,205 for the executive director's office.","Section 3: the act takes effect 12:01 a.m. on the day after the 90-day period following final adjournment, and is subject to a referendum petition; if one is filed, the act does not take effect unless approved at the November 2026 general election."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. On its own, the bill is unusual in that it does not change the tax rate but narrows the tax base by ending the promotional free-bet deduction, and it does so by accelerating a step-down schedule that previously ran through mid-2026. It also carries a small appropriation and a referendum clause.","watch":"Operators should model the loss of the free-bet deduction from the January 1, 2026 cap step-down to the full elimination on July 1, 2026 on Colorado net sports betting proceeds and tax liability, and revisit promotional spend accordingly, including the risk that a referendum petition delays effectiveness.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":4332,"generated":"2026-11-03T23:00:58.686Z"},"CT-SB229":{"summary":"Substitute SB 229 adds a new, uncodified section requiring electronic wagering platforms that exclusively offer fantasy contests, or exclusively sell lottery draw game and keno tickets online, to use a geolocation system. It takes effect on passage and fills a gap for platforms outside the full sports-wagering and iGaming framework. Definitions are borrowed from Conn. Gen. Stat. 12-850 and 12-801.","mechanics":["Scope (subsec. (b)): applies to each electronic wagering platform that exclusively provides (1) fantasy contests or (2) internet, online or mobile-app sales of lottery draw game and keno tickets. A platform that also offers sports wagering or casino games is outside the text.","Geolocation must either detect device location despite a proxy server or VPN, or bar participation by patrons using a proxy or VPN ((b)(A)).","Location must be checked, using industry-standard technology approved by the Department of Consumer Protection, each time a patron submits a fantasy contest entry fee or buys a draw game or keno ticket. The transaction must be blocked if location cannot be determined ((b)(B)).","Patrons must receive a pop-up notification when they attempt an entry or purchase and their location cannot be determined ((b)(C)).","Both the online gaming operator and the patron must be notified when an account is accessed from geographically inconsistent locations, such as locations a patron could not travel between across transactions ((b)(D)).","Effective from passage. The bill contains no penalty or enforcement provision, and no rulemaking authority beyond DCP approval of technologies."],"issue_tags":["integrity","licensing"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed, so there is nothing to compare directly. Unusually, the bill is a narrow Connecticut Lottery Corporation housekeeping measure. It imposes technical geofencing standards only on fantasy-contest and lottery-only platforms, and it does not change taxes, licensing or the scope of sports wagering or iGaming.","watch":"Check whether an operator that offers fantasy contests alongside sports wagering or iGaming falls outside the word \"exclusively\", and whether DCP's approval of geolocation technologies later becomes a template for broader requirements.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Comm Sub","text_chars":2541,"generated":"2026-11-03T23:01:02.748Z"},"AZ-SB1848":{"summary":"AZ SB1848 is largely a fund-consolidation and housekeeping bill for the Arizona Department of Gaming. It repeals the separate fantasy sports contest fund (A.R.S. 5-1212), routes fantasy sports fees into the event wagering fund (5-1318), and moves unarmed combat (boxing/MMA) revenues into a new subaccount of that fund. It also imposes a one-year 0.5% regulatory assessment on commercial racing permittees and relaxes first-time-starter rules for horse racing in 2026 and 2027.","mechanics":["Sec. 7 (5-1318): the event wagering fee stays capped at 10% of adjusted gross event wagering receipts, due by the 25th of the following month. Operators may choose cash accrual or modified accrual accounting. The text shown makes no change to the rate or the cap.","Sec. 5 and Sec. 6 (5-1211, 5-1212): fantasy sports fees (cap 10% of adjusted revenues) and the 5%-per-month late penalty (max 25%) are now deposited in the event wagering fund. Section 5-1212 is repealed, and unexpended balances in the fantasy sports contest fund transfer to the event wagering fund on the section's effective date.","Sec. 7 (5-1318(B)): the event wagering fund now also receives fantasy sports monies under 5-1211. By the 25th of each month, 90% of the prior month's deposits go to the state general fund. The 5-1318(D) cap on department regulatory spending stays at 10% of monies, with up to $500,000 per fiscal year for problem gambling programs.","Sec. 7 (5-1318(E)): the department may use problem gambling monies, including private donations, grants and federal funds, to disburse grants for problem gambling purposes.","Secs. 2-4 (5-226, 5-230, 5-238): the unarmed combat subaccount moves from the racing regulation fund to a subaccount of the event wagering fund. License fees, the 4% boxing gross receipts payments and withheld purses are redirected there. The department may transfer regulatory cost monies from the event wagering fund into that subaccount.","Sec. 8: for FY2026-27 only, the department must collect a regulatory assessment of 0.5% of amounts wagered on live and simulcast races, in-state and out-of-state, from each commercial racing permittee. It is paid from pari-mutuel pool deductions and is in addition to the 5-111(B) deductions.","Sec. 9: for 2026 and 2027 race meetings, a first-time starter may race with gate approval and two timed workouts, one of which is out of the gate within 60 days of the race. This section is repealed after Dec. 31, 2027. Sec. 1 also changes the liquor licenses fund reversion to amounts above $700,000 at fiscal year end."],"issue_tags":["tax","licensing","integrity"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. On its own, the bill is unusual because it works as a fund-restructuring and housekeeping vehicle. It merges fantasy sports and unarmed combat revenue streams into the event wagering fund and bundles unrelated liquor fund and horse racing provisions, rather than changing sports betting tax rates or market access.","watch":"Event wagering operators should watch the consolidation of fantasy sports fees and unarmed combat funding into the event wagering fund, because it could affect how the 10% cap on fees and the 10% regulatory spending limit are applied and the department's future fee-setting discretion.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Engrossed","text_chars":13066,"generated":"2026-11-03T23:01:03.245Z"},"CO-SB146":{"summary":"Colorado SB 25-146 conforms a broad set of state and local fingerprint-based criminal history record check statutes to federal FBI requirements (34 U.S.C. 41101 and CJIS policy). It standardizes the process: fingerprints go through CBI to the FBI, results go to the licensing authority, and an arrest without a disposition triggers a name-based judicial record check. Most of the bill covers health, funeral, childcare, massage, auto, and marijuana licensing, with narrow touches on gaming: fantasy contest definitions (part 16 of article 30) and key and support occupational licenses under the pari-mutuel racing article (44-32).","mechanics":["Sec. 18 amends 44-32-503(4)(a): applicants for a key occupational license or support occupational license under the racing/pari-mutuel article must submit fingerprints to the commission, which must now 'submit' them to CBI for a state and national check. Applicants bear only the actual costs of the check, and the commission may still make further background inquiries.","Sec. 19 amends 44-32-102 to add definitions of 'applicant' (an individual applying for or renewing a license, permit, or registration), 'key occupational license' (an individual with significant influence over decisions of a business licensed under 44-32-504), 'license,' and 'support occupational license.' The breakage definition is renumbered as (1.5).","Sec. 13 amends 44-30-1603 (fantasy contests): adds definitions of 'applicant' (a person applying for an initial or renewal license or registration as a fantasy contest or small fantasy contest operator, responsible for financial and contractual obligations), 'general partner,' and 'managing officer,' and clarifies that 'director' means the gaming director or a designee. It adds no new fingerprint mandate for fantasy operators in this bill.","Sec. 2 creates 24-33.5-424.5: a county, municipality, or city and county that requires fingerprint checks by ordinance or resolution under 34 U.S.C. 41101 must route fingerprints through CBI to the FBI. Third-party livescan vendors may keep applicant data no more than 30 days, and arrests without disposition require a name-based judicial record check.","Sec. 14 amends 44-10-307(4) (regulated marijuana): fingerprint checks are extended to occupational licenses and controlling beneficial owners. CBI submits fingerprints to the FBI, and federal results go to the state licensing authority only.","Sec. 1 adds 12-20-202(5)(b)-(h): regulators may require fingerprint checks for 15 health and funeral license types, with the applicant paying costs. Secs. 6-12 and 15-17 make conforming changes for massage facilities (the October 1, 2025 deadline becomes July 1, 2026), EMS, assisted living, home care, host homes, and auto dealers. Sec. 20 contains a safety clause, so the act takes effect on signing."],"issue_tags":["licensing","integrity"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. This is a general conformity bill, unusual for sweeping dozens of licensing regimes into one federal-alignment fix. Gaming is touched only by the racing key and support occupational license fingerprint changes in 44-32-503 and the fantasy contest definitions in 44-30-1603. It does not touch sports betting (article 30 part 15) or iGaming.","watch":"Operators with Colorado racing or pari-mutuel operations, or fantasy contest affiliations, should confirm how their key and support occupational licensees are affected by the new 44-32 definitions and the CBI/FBI submission route, and note that sports betting licensing is not amended.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Enrolled","text_chars":38239,"generated":"2026-11-03T23:01:07.105Z"},"IL-HB4885":{"summary":"HB4885 amends the Illinois Loss Recovery Act, 720 ILCS 5/28-8, in three ways: it raises the minimum loss that supports a civil recovery action from $50 to $5,000, repeals the qui tam-style provision in subsection (b) that lets any third party sue the winner for treble damages if the loser does not sue within 6 months, and adds a new subsection (d) barring recovery of losses from fantasy contests against a fantasy contest operator, including single-player contests. Subsection (d) is described as declarative of existing law, and the Act takes effect on becoming law.","mechanics":["Sec. 28-8(a): the threshold for a loser to sue the winner in circuit court rises from $50 to $5,000, with recovery of the amount lost and paid or delivered, plus costs.","Sec. 28-8(b): the third-party action after 6 months, with a judgment for triple the determined loss, is replaced with '(Blank)', which eliminates the treble-damages bounty mechanism.","New Sec. 28-8(d): losses from participation in any fantasy contest against a fantasy contest operator, including single-player fantasy contests, are not recoverable under the section.","Sec. 28-8(d) states that the amendatory Act is 'declarative of existing law,' which signals intent to apply the fantasy bar to pending or past claims, though the Act itself has no express retroactivity clause.","Existing Sec. 28-8(c) is retained: losses on video gaming terminals licensed under the Video Gaming Act are not recoverable.","Sec. 99: the Act takes effect upon becoming law. The text does not define 'fantasy contest' or 'fantasy contest operator' and cites no cross-reference to another statute."],"issue_tags":["prediction"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states were listed, so there is nothing to compare directly. On its own, the bill is unusual in using a loss-recovery statute to protect fantasy contest operators, and it undoes the third-party treble-damages mechanism, which has been the vehicle for suits against daily fantasy sports operators. It adds no licensing, tax, or consumer-protection provisions.","watch":"Watch for the undefined terms 'fantasy contest' and 'fantasy contest operator' and the 'declarative of existing law' language, which together determine whether the exemption reaches pick'em-style or prediction products and whether it ends pending Loss Recovery Act suits, since the bill gives no safe harbor for sportsbook or online casino losses.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Engrossed","text_chars":2386,"generated":"2026-11-03T23:01:10.278Z"},"HI-HB1308~2025":{"summary":"HB1308 (H.D. 3, S.D. 2) legalizes mobile-only sports wagering in Hawaii under a new chapter administered by the department of law enforcement. It sets up operator and supplier licensing, with at least four operator licenses available only to \"qualified gaming entities\". It imposes a 10% tax on adjusted gross sports wagering receipts through an amendment to the general excise tax statute (HRS 237-13), and carves sports wagering and fantasy sports out of the gambling offense definitions in HRS 712-1220. The text provided is cut off in the middle of the HRS 846-2.7 criminal history check amendment.","mechanics":["Licensing (§ -5, -6): the department must issue at least four operator licenses. Only a \"qualified gaming entity\" may apply, meaning an entity offering mobile sports wagering in Hawaii or in at least three U.S. jurisdictions. The operator fee is $250,000 for initial and renewal, plus a processing fee for the cost of background investigations. Terms are five years. The supplier fee is $10,000. Temporary licenses last up to three years or until a final determination, whichever is sooner.","Market structure: wagering is mobile or digital only, through department-approved apps or platforms (§ -10(c)). Kiosks, terminals and any physical-location wagering are banned. The department must set a universal start date no later than 180 days after the Act takes effect, and no one may offer wagering before it. Bettors must be 21 or older and physically in Hawaii, unless the department enters a compact under § -9 to accept wagers from outside the state.","Tax (§ -11; HRS 237-13(9)): 10% of adjusted gross sports wagering receipts, levied on the licensee on an accrual basis and in lieu of all other taxes on sports wagering operations or proceeds. Of the taxes collected, 7% goes to a problem gambling prevention and treatment special fund run by the department of health, and 7% to education programs. Adjusted gross receipts are cash wagers less winnings paid, including the value of prizes subject to federal income tax. Promotional or bonus credits are not expressly deducted. Section 237-13(9) applies the tax to operators and suppliers, while § -11 levies it on operator licensees.","Operator conduct (§ -7, -8, -10): the department must pre-approve house rules. Operators must run a monitoring system for odds or volume irregularities and report suspicious activity, with a reporting threshold set by the operator and approved by the department. All advertising must display the 1-800-GAMBLER helpline. Accounts may be funded by credit or debit card, bank transfer, mobile payment or bonuses and promotions. Layoff wagers between operators are allowed with disclosure. The department must run a voluntary exclusion program, including a stop on direct marketing to excluded persons. Employees may not bet on their own employer's platform.","Wagering events (§ -1): professional, collegiate and amateur events, Olympic and international events, motor racing and esports are covered, plus any other event the department permits. Youth sports events are excluded. Collegiate events are expressly included, and prop bets and in-play bets are expressly permitted.","Enforcement (§ -13, -14): civil fines of up to $5,000 per violation, or $10,000 for all violations from one occurrence, not limited to licensees. Unlicensed operation is a misdemeanor with a fine of at least $10,000. A second offense is a class C felony with a fine of at least $50,000. A third or later offense is a class B felony with a fine of at least $100,000. Licensed activity is exempt from the gambling offenses in HRS ch. 712 part III (§ -15)."],"issue_tags":["legalize","licensing","tax","integrity","advertising"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Of the listed bills, HI HB1308 is closest to AK HB145 and AK SB194 (mobile-only wagering with a tax and background checks) and to DE HB365 (a competitive mobile framework). Hawaii's version is unusual in that it requires at least four licenses, limits applicants to \"qualified gaming entities\" with experience in three or more jurisdictions, and uses a flat 10% tax on adjusted gross receipts imposed through the general excise tax statute with a fixed 7%/7% earmark. Unlike AR HB1861, FL H0189 or the CT gaming bills, it contains no iGaming or casino provisions, no college advertising limits and no payment-method restrictions beyond the funding list in § -10(b). The only advertising rule is the helpline disclosure.","watch":"Watch the conference committee outcome on the 10% tax rate, the 180-day universal start date, the four-license minimum and \"qualified gaming entity\" eligibility, the lack of a promotional-credit deduction from adjusted gross receipts, and the mandatory pre-approval of house rules by a law enforcement agency acting as regulator.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":64840,"generated":"2026-11-03T23:01:15.487Z"},"HI-SB1569~2025":{"summary":"HI SB1569 (S.D. 1) would legalize online-only sports wagering in Hawaii under a new chapter administered by the Department of Business, Economic Development, and Tourism (DBEDT). It requires at least four mobile operator licenses, limited to \"qualified gaming entities\" already operating in three or more U.S. jurisdictions, and creates a supplier license. It imposes a 10% tax on adjusted gross receipts through a new GET paragraph, §237-13(9), and carves sports wagering and fantasy sports out of the Penal Code's gambling definitions. The bill has a placeholder effective date of July 1, 2050 and was deferred by the WAM committee on 2025-02-26.","mechanics":["Licensing (§-5, §-6): DBEDT must issue at least four operator licenses at a $250,000 initial/renewal fee, plus processing costs. Terms run five years. Applicants may seek a three-year temporary license by paying the fee up front. Supplier licenses cost $10,000 and are available only to providers of geolocation and age-verification services. A universal start date must fall within 180 days of enactment, and no one may offer wagering before it.","Eligibility (§-1, §-3): Only a \"qualified gaming entity\" (mobile sports wagering in at least three U.S. jurisdictions) can apply. Applicants must show the platform was certified by an independent lab in another U.S. jurisdiction within six months. Control persons include 15% owners, parent and subsidiary companies, and key decision-makers, who must submit fingerprints. Check results from the prior 12 months are reusable.","Tax (§-11, §237-13(9)): 10% of adjusted gross sports wagering receipts, levied on operators and suppliers, on an accrual basis and in lieu of all other taxes on the operation. Adjusted gross receipts are cash wagers minus winnings, including the value of merchandise prizes. There is no deduction for promotional credits or bonuses. Bonuses and promotions are listed as an allowed funding source in §-10(b)(2).","Player rules (§-10): Bettors must be 21 or older and physically in Hawaii. Wagers are accepted only through DBEDT-approved mobile apps or platforms, with no kiosks or terminals. Operators must support credit/debit, bank transfer, and mobile payments. Layoff wagers between operators are permitted. DBEDT runs a voluntary exclusion program, with names shared at least quarterly. Employees may not bet on their employer's platform.","Operator duties (§-8, §-7): Operators must run a monitoring system for odds and volume irregularities and report suspicious activity. House rules need DBEDT approval, and all advertising must display 1-800-GAMBLER. Wagering events include professional, collegiate, amateur, Olympic, motor-race, and esports events, but not events in which most participants are under 18. DBEDT may enter interstate wagering agreements (§-9).","Enforcement and funds (§-13 to -15, §-12, §4): Violations are civil, with fines up to $5,000 per violation and $10,000 per occurrence. Unlicensed wagering is a misdemeanor, then a class C felony, then a class B felony, with fine amounts left blank. A problem gambling special fund is created, with the appropriation amount blank. §712-1220 is amended to exclude licensed sports wagering and fantasy sports from \"gambling\" and \"contest of chance.\""],"issue_tags":["legalize","licensing","tax","integrity","advertising"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Hawaii's bill is a mobile-only, limited-license model, as are AK HB145/SB194, which also authorize mobile sports wagering with a tax and criminal history checks. Georgia's HB380, SB57, and SB172 route wagering through the Georgia Lottery Corporation, and DE HB365 does so through the Lottery Director, while Hawaii uses a DBEDT licensing regime with no lottery role. The 10% tax is low relative to typical state rates, and the license fee is modest at $250,000. The qualified-gaming-entity requirement (three or more U.S. jurisdictions) and the six-month out-of-state lab certification are unusual barriers to entry. Unlike CT HB05229 or HB05269, the bill has no advertising restrictions beyond the helpline notice, and the 2050 effective date and blank fine and appropriation amounts show it is a placeholder draft.","watch":"Operators should watch the qualified-gaming-entity threshold and the four-license floor (who actually qualifies), the treatment of promotional credits in the 10% adjusted gross receipts tax base, the blank penalty and appropriation amounts, and the placeholder 2050 effective date that would have to be amended before the bill is operative.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":48389,"generated":"2026-11-03T23:01:15.993Z"},"LA-HB1~2025":{"summary":"HB 1 (Act No. 1, 2025 Regular Session) is Louisiana's general appropriations act for FY 2025-26. It funds the ordinary operating expenses of the executive branch, schools, roads, pensions and state institutions, and sets the general rules for spending and transferring those funds. The text provided cuts off in Section 6, so none of the schedule-level dollar amounts are visible. Gaming-related items appear only as line items in the table of contents.","mechanics":["Section 1: state-revenue appropriations are payable from the specified sources and limited by La. Const. art. VII, §10(D).","Section 2.A: increases in federal, interagency, statutory-dedication or self-generated revenue can be spent only after the commissioner of administration and the Joint Legislative Committee on the Budget approve an increase in the appropriation. If those revenues fall short, the appropriation is reduced accordingly.","Section 2.B: the commissioner of administration must correct the means of financing and expenditures in Schedule 20-901 (Sales Tax Dedications) to reflect current law enacted in any session.","Section 3.A: the governor may transfer executive-branch functions by executive order, but each transfer needs prior approval of the commissioner of administration and the Joint Legislative Committee on the Budget.","Section 6.A: funds cannot move between departments or schedules unless this Act expressly allows it. Unencumbered savings may move within a department or schedule with commissioner and Joint Legislative Committee on the Budget approval and written justification.","The table of contents lists gaming-related budget units: Attorney General Gaming (04-141), State Police Gaming Enforcement Program (08-419), Louisiana Gaming Control Board (08-423), Office of Charitable Gaming (12-440), State Racing Commission (01-254), Video Draw Poker Local Government Aid (20-924) and Sports Wagering Local Allocation Fund (20-926). The amounts and conditions for these are not in the text provided."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. This is an omnibus appropriations act rather than a gaming statute. Its gaming content is limited to agency funding lines, including a Sports Wagering Local Allocation Fund (20-926), and it creates no wagering rules, rates or licensing provisions in the text provided.","watch":"Check the full text of Schedules 04-141, 08-419, 08-423, 20-924 and 20-926 for funding levels and any conditions on gaming regulators' budgets and on the local allocation of sports-wagering revenue, since the portion provided does not include them.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":268261,"generated":"2026-11-03T23:01:19.955Z"},"IL-SB3394":{"summary":"SB3394 amends the Illinois Criminal Code gambling-loss recovery statute (720 ILCS 5/28-8) in two ways. It limits the subsection (a) civil action to natural persons residing in Illinois, and it adds a new subsection (d) providing that losses from fantasy contests played against a fantasy contest operator, including single-player contests, are not recoverable. The effect is a statutory shield for fantasy operators against loss-recovery suits, including the 6-month, treble-damages action in subsection (b).","mechanics":["Sec. 28-8(a) is amended so that only a \"natural person with residency in the State of Illinois\" who loses $50 or more by gambling and pays or delivers it may sue the winner in circuit court. The existing board-of-trade and exchange-intermediary carve-out is unchanged.","New Sec. 28-8(d): losses from participation in any fantasy contests against a fantasy contest operator, expressly including single-player fantasy contests, are not recoverable under the Section.","Subsection (b) is not amended. If the loser does not sue within 6 months, any person may sue the winner, and the court enters judgment for triple the determined loss. Subsection (d) removes fantasy losses from this action as well.","Existing subsection (c), which bars recovery of losses on licensed video gaming terminals under the Video Gaming Act, is retained. Subsection (d) follows it as a parallel carve-out.","The bill does not define \"fantasy contest\" or \"fantasy contest operator\" and creates no licensing, tax or consumer-protection regime.","Sec. 99: the Act takes effect upon becoming law."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Most of the listed bills are regulatory or tax measures. GA HB1329 authorizes, regulates and taxes fantasy contests, and KY HB 904 gives the Kentucky Horse Racing and Gaming Corporation oversight of fantasy contest operators with geolocation and other player-protection requirements. IL SB3394 does neither. It is a narrow civil-liability change that exempts fantasy operators from loss-recovery suits and adds a residency limit on plaintiffs. It is closer in spirit to the consumer-facing changes in DE HB187 and IN HB1092, but those concern child-support intercepts of winnings and withdrawals, not private suits to recover losses.","watch":"Because the bill defines neither fantasy term, an operator's protection under new subsection (d) will depend on whether its products, especially single-player pick'em or over/under formats, are treated as \"fantasy contests\" against the operator, and the synopsis refers to \"illegal gambling\" while the statutory text says only \"by gambling\", so watch for amendment (Senate Committee Amendment No. 1 is noted in the status) and for whether subsection (b)'s treble-damages action by \"any person\" remains open once the residency limit applies to subsection (a).","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":3137,"generated":"2026-11-03T23:01:22.737Z"},"LA-HB639~2025":{"summary":"Louisiana HB 639 (Act 298) raises the state tax on online sports wagering from 15% to 21.5% of net gaming proceeds under R.S. 27:625(B). It also earmarks part of that revenue for a new special fund for university athletic departments, called the Supporting Programs, Opportunities, Resources, and Teams (SPORT) Fund, and for the Louisiana Postsecondary Inclusive Education Fund. The enrolled text does not touch video poker, even though the digest title mentions it.","mechanics":["R.S. 27:625(B): the tax rate on net gaming proceeds from sports wagering offered electronically through a website or mobile application rises from 15% to 21.5%. The carve-out is retained for electronic sports wagering conducted through a sports wagering mechanism.","R.S. 27:625(G)(8): after Subsection D is satisfied, 25% of the monies collected under Subsection B is credited to the SPORT Fund created by new R.S. 17:1792.","R.S. 27:625(G)(9): 3% of the monies collected under Subsection B is credited to the Louisiana Postsecondary Inclusive Education Fund (R.S. 17:3138.11). Under (G)(10), any remainder is available as state general funds.","R.S. 17:1792 creates the SPORT Fund in the state treasury, subject to the Bond Security and Redemption Fund requirement in La. Const. art. VII, §9(B). Interest stays in the fund, and unexpended balances carry over.","SPORT Fund monies are appropriated to the Board of Regents for equal distribution among athletic departments at Louisiana public universities in conferences competing at the NCAA FBS and FCS levels. They may be used only for scholarships, insurance, medical coverage, facility enhancements, litigation settlement fees and Alston awards, and may not displace or supplant other awards or scholarships.","Per the bill status, the effective date is 08/01/2025. The text contains no operator-facing licensing, compliance or enforcement changes."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"Unlike AK HB145 and SB194, which pair a new mobile sports wagering tax with authorization and licensing, or the Hawaii bills (HB1308, HB 2570, SB1569), which set up a full regime with a general excise tax, LA HB639 only raises the rate in an existing mobile market and earmarks the revenue. The Iowa bills (HF673, HSB122) also deal with tax receipts on sports wagering, but the Louisiana bill is unusual for directing a fixed percentage of the tax to university athletics, including Alston awards and litigation settlement fees. The other states' summaries as listed do not give rate figures, so no rate comparison can be drawn from them.","watch":"Operators should model the jump from 15% to 21.5% of net gaming proceeds effective 08/01/2025, and note that the bill leaves the definition of net gaming proceeds and the retail-kiosk carve-out in R.S. 27:625(B) unchanged.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":4429,"generated":"2026-11-03T23:01:28.383Z"},"ME-LD2048":{"summary":"LD 2048 amends Maine's fantasy contest licensing statute (8 MRSA §1103) to add control-person disclosure and fingerprint-based state and FBI criminal history record checks for fantasy contest operator applicants. It also reworks the fee provisions and adds an occupational license fee for fantasy contest operators. The bill was submitted by the Department of Public Safety and died in the legislative files on 2026-03-05.","mechanics":["New §1103, sub-§3-A defines persons with 'control': for corporations, each holding, parent or subsidiary company and each person owning 10% or more who can control activities or elect a majority of the board (bank lien holders excepted); for non-corporate applicants, any holder of a beneficial or proprietary interest or anyone the director finds can control; and key personnel (executives, employees, agents) with significant influence over relevant business decisions.","New sub-§3, ¶H-1 requires an application to disclose every person with control over the applicant. Sub-§3, ¶C is changed from consent to a 'criminal background check' to consent to a 'criminal history record check'.","New sub-§4-A requires the director to request a Maine CJIS and FBI criminal history record check, with fingerprints, for each initial applicant and each disclosed control person. For renewals the director 'may' require checks of the licensee, persons with control and persons identified under sub-§3-A. Fees are set by director rule, results are confidential and usable only for license screening, and the director must adopt implementing rules after consulting State Bureau of Identification.","Sub-§5 is recast as a 'processing fee' limited to projected cost of processing and background investigations. Any excess is applied to the license fee or reimbursed to an unlicensed applicant, and the director may charge an additional fee if actual cost exceeds projection.","Sub-§6 now ties the $2,500 fee to initial license applications and renewals for operators with gross fantasy contest revenue of $100,000 or more in the prior 12 months. Operators under $100,000 pay no license fee, and licenses renew annually.","New sub-§6-A sets the occupational license fee for fantasy contest operator personnel at $250 initial and $250 annual renewal. The operator may pay on the licensee's behalf, a renewal application is required each year, and the director retains the fees for administering the chapter."],"issue_tags":["licensing","integrity"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"This is a narrow fantasy-contest licensing and background-check bill, not a legalization or tax measure. GA HB1329 and IL HB2342 also regulate fantasy contests, but GA HB1329 also provides for taxation and IL HB2342 imposes fees and a tax on adjusted gross fantasy contest receipts. The fingerprint-based check is closer to AK HB145 and AK SB194, which add criminal history record checks for mobile sports wagering licensure, and IL HB2754, which deals with occupational licenses. LD 2048 imposes no tax, advertising rules or consumer-protection provisions, and keeps the existing $100,000 revenue threshold for the $2,500 fee.","watch":"Operators should watch the breadth of the 'control' definition, especially the reach to subsidiaries, parent companies and any 'key personnel' including employees and agents, which could sweep in many individuals for fingerprinting, along with the discretionary renewal checks and the new $250 occupational license fee, even though the bill is dead and could return in a later session.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Introduced","text_chars":9125,"generated":"2026-11-03T23:01:29.694Z"},"ME-LD2080~2013":{"summary":"LD 2080 (P.L. 2024, c. 635) extends Maine's existing universal exclusion list under 8 MRSA §1003(3)(I), previously covering slot facilities and casinos, to advance deposit wagering, fantasy contests and sports wagering. It requires the sports wagering, fantasy contest and ADW rules to route self-exclusion through that single list, and it requires licensees to intercept winnings of excluded persons above the W-2G threshold, with the money going to gambling addiction programs.","mechanics":["Sec. 6 amends §1003(3)(I) so the universal list covers slot machine facilities, casinos, advance deposit wagering, fantasy contests and sports wagering. Rules must be consistent across all forms of gambling to the extent possible.","Sec. 6 (new (I)(2)) requires a licensee, before paying winnings at or above the W-2G filing threshold and after child support or other legally required interceptions, to intercept money or anything of value an excluded person seeks to redeem from wagers placed after exclusion.","Intercepted winnings go to the board or its designee for deposit in an Other Special Revenue Funds account in the Office of Behavioral Health (DHHS) for gambling addiction. The excluded person is entitled to an administrative hearing with reasonable notice to contest the interception.","Sec. 9 amends §1203(2)(K) so the sports wagering rules must let individuals self-restrict by joining the universal list, and must define the standards for involuntary placement and removal. This replaces the separate sports-wagering unauthorized-persons list. Sec. 10 conforms §1213(5) to say a person on the universal list is restricted from making sports wagers.","Sec. 8 amends the fantasy contest operator duty in §1104(1)(J) to tie self-restriction to the universal list. Sec. 5 adds a new ADW rule, §1003(2)(U)(9), on self-exclusion and on the standards for involuntary placement and removal. Sec. 3 requires ADW deposit rules to prohibit use of the EBT system.","Sec. 7 extends the §1006(8) confidentiality of voluntary exclusion records to ADW, fantasy contest and sports wagering. Release is allowed only with the person's written consent or as needed to inform the licensee and enforce the exclusion. De-identified statistics are not confidential. Secs. 1 and 2 add cross-reference definitions of fantasy contest and sports wagering. The text gives no separate effective date, so the default Maine effective date for enacted acts presumably applies."],"issue_tags":["integrity","licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison, so this is judged on its own terms. The unusual features are a single exclusion list spanning casino, ADW, fantasy and sports wagering, and a mandatory interception of post-exclusion winnings at the W-2G threshold, with an administrative hearing right and diversion of the funds to addiction services.","watch":"Operators should watch for the board's implementing rules on involuntary placement and removal standards, the mechanics of checking the universal list and intercepting winnings at the W-2G threshold, and the hearing process, since the statute leaves these to rulemaking and is silent on who bears the compliance burden.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":6215,"generated":"2026-11-03T23:01:31.038Z"},"ME-LD33":{"summary":"LD 33 makes two narrow amendments to Maine's fantasy contest statute (8 MRSA ch. 31). It changes the definition of a fantasy contest so that a game needs two or more paying contestants rather than one, and it revises the revenue-share provision so the 10% General Fund payment applies only to licensed operators with gross fantasy contest revenues of $100,000 or more, and expressly counts revenue earned while operating under a pending application.","mechanics":["Sec. 1 amends 8 MRSA §1101, sub-§4, ¶A: the entry-fee element now requires \"Two or more\" fantasy contestants per game or contest (formerly \"One\"), none of whom may be the operator.","Sec. 2 amends 8 MRSA §1105, sub-§2: the director collects 10% of gross fantasy contest revenues for deposit to the General Fund.","The 10% collection applies only to licensed operators with gross fantasy contest revenues of $100,000 or greater.","Gross revenues expressly include revenues earned while operating pending approval of an application under §1103, sub-§9, so revenue earned during the pending-application period is captured.","Enforcement and licensing mechanics are not changed; the text sets no new penalties or effective-date language beyond the chaptered enactment (Public Law 2025, c. 95, approved May 23, 2025)."],"issue_tags":["tax","licensing"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"No other states' bills were listed for comparison. On its own, the bill is unusually narrow: it does not alter the 10% rate, license structure, or consumer protections, and it only tightens the contest definition to require at least two paying contestants and clarifies the base for the revenue payment, including the pending-application period.","watch":"Confirm how the $100,000 threshold and the inclusion of revenue earned while an application is pending will be measured and applied, and whether the two-contestant requirement affects any single-entrant or house-played product formats.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Chaptered","text_chars":1013,"generated":"2026-11-03T23:01:32.192Z"},"MI-SB760~2027":{"summary":"Michigan SB 760 is the placeholder general government appropriations bill for FY ending September 30, 2025. As engrossed, Part 1 appropriates a nominal $100 of general fund/general purpose money across the legislature, executive, attorney general, state, treasury, DTMB, and civil rights. It contains no gaming, wagering, or tax provisions and was sent to conference committee on 2024-06-11.","mechanics":["Sec. 101: total general government gross appropriation of $100, all from state general fund/general purpose, with no line-item detail.","Sec. 201: under Const 1963 art IX, sec 30, total state spending from state sources is $100.00, and $0.00 is paid to local units of government.","Sec. 202: appropriations are subject to the management and budget act, 1984 PA 431, MCL 18.1101 to 18.1594.","Covered entities: legislature, executive, department of the attorney general, department of state, department of treasury, DTMB, and department of civil rights.","The text contains no effective date other than the fiscal year ending September 30, 2025, and no enforcement or gaming-specific language."],"issue_tags":[],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. On its own, this bill is unusual only in being a $100 placeholder appropriation with no line items, so it says nothing about gaming regulation, the Michigan Gaming Control Board, or gaming tax revenue. Any substantive content would have to come from the conference committee.","watch":"Watch the conference committee version for added line items or boilerplate touching the Department of Treasury, the attorney general, or gaming enforcement and revenue, since the current text has none.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Engrossed","text_chars":1725,"generated":"2026-11-03T23:01:33.690Z"},"NC-H430~2023":{"summary":"H 430 is the Governor's omnibus base budget (Current Operations Appropriations Act of 2023) for the 2023-25 biennium, with General Fund net appropriations of about $32.95B in FY 2023-24 and $34.23B in FY 2024-25. Gaming content is minor. Section 4.3 allocates lottery, video lottery, e-instant games and sports wagering proceeds to education, and Section 4.4 appropriates Indian Gaming Education Revenue Fund money. The provided text does not amend any sports-betting, iGaming or collectibles regulatory provisions.","mechanics":["Sec. 4.3 appropriates for the 2023-25 biennium $1.866B from the Education Lottery Fund, $243M from the North Carolina Video Lottery Fund, $184M from E-Instant Games and $85M from Sports Wagering, allocated across education line items (e.g. Noninstructional Support Personnel, Prekindergarten, Public School Building Capital Fund).","Sec. 4.3 total appropriation is $1,037,000,000 for FY 2023-24 and $1,341,000,000 for FY 2024-25. Sports wagering is shown only as a funding source ($85M over the biennium, with no per-year split), and the bill sets no rate, license or definition.","Sec. 4.4 appropriates $11,000,000 per year from the Indian Gaming Education Revenue Fund to the Department of Public Instruction (Textbooks and Digital Resources Allotment), notwithstanding G.S. 143C-9-7.","Sec. 2.2(a) revenue tables list no separate sports-wagering or gaming tax line. The adjustments hold the individual income tax rate at 4.75% for income above $200K (MFJ) / $100K (single) and the corporate rate at 2.5%.","Sec. 5.1 lets agencies establish or increase fees authorized by the act without consulting the Joint Legislative Commission on Governmental Operations and by emergency rule under G.S. 150B-21.1A.","The Department of Revenue receives $131.6M (FY 2023-24) and the Integrated Tax Administration System is funded through the IT Project Reserve (Sec. 2.2(i)(2), Sec. 5.11); the visible text does not show whether this touches gaming tax administration."],"issue_tags":["tax"],"relevance":"incidental","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed. This is a general appropriations act in which sports wagering appears only as one of four lottery and gaming funding sources, with no regulatory changes. It does show that North Carolina already counts on sports-wagering revenue ($85M over the biennium) to fund education.","watch":"Watch for the sports-wagering revenue assumption of $85M over the biennium, which could be used to justify tax or fee changes in the budget process, and for any later budget text (the provided text is truncated after Sec. 5.7) that touches gaming or wagering.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Amended","text_chars":346684,"generated":"2026-11-03T23:01:38.549Z"},"ND-HB1416":{"summary":"HB1416 amends N.D.C.C. 53-06.1-03(3) to carve out an exception to the rule that only one licensed or permitted charitable organization may conduct games at an authorized site on a given day. As amended by the committee substitute, two organizations may conduct two different types of games at the same site on the same day, for up to twelve hours per day, in small cities. The bill also restructures the existing raffle and sports pool exception. The bill failed on second reading in the second chamber, 10 yeas to 36 nays.","mechanics":["New paragraph (2) of subdivision a: two organizations may 'consent to conduct two different types of games' on the same day at the same site, for a period not to exceed twelve hours per day.","Eligibility conditions: the site must be in a city with a population of 1,200 or less; each organization employs its own staff; and gaming funds are not commingled.","Existing exception (paragraph (1)) is retained: a raffle or sports pool under a local permit may be run for a special occasion by another organization if the area is physically separated, or if the attorney general suspends the regular organization's license or permit at its request with the alcoholic beverage establishment's approval.","Unchanged provisions in subsection 3 remain, including the 15-site cap (subd. b), a 21+ age limit for pull tabs, punchboards, twenty-one, calcuttas, sports pools, paddlewheels and poker (subd. d), and a cap of ten electronic pull tab devices per site (subd. e).","The text contains no effective date, new penalty, or new enforcement provision, and no definition of 'different types of games.'"],"issue_tags":["licensing"],"relevance":"partial","comparison":"No comparable bills from other states are listed, so there is nothing to compare directly. Unusual features of this bill are its narrow scope, limited to North Dakota's charitable gaming regime. It uses a population threshold of 1,200 or less to allow shared sites, and sports pools appear only as an existing, unchanged exception for local permit holders.","watch":"Because the bill failed on second reading (10-36), it is unlikely to become law, and it does not touch commercial sports betting or iGaming, so an operator's counsel should watch only for a revived version or for effects on charitable sports pools.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Comm Sub","text_chars":4261,"generated":"2026-11-03T23:01:38.732Z"},"ND-HCR3002~1848":{"summary":"ND HCR3002 is a concurrent resolution that would amend and reenact section 25 of article XI of the North Dakota Constitution to authorize sports betting. Because it is a constitutional amendment, it would go to voters if adopted by both chambers. It passed the House but failed to adopt in the Senate on second reading on 2023-04-10 (16 yeas, 30 nays), so it is effectively dead. Only the official abstract is available, not the full text.","mechanics":["Vehicle is a concurrent resolution amending N.D. Const. art. XI, § 25, so it would need legislative adoption and then voter approval rather than a governor's signature.","The abstract says only that the amendment relates to authorizing sports betting. The full text is unavailable, so I cannot say whether it covers mobile or retail wagering, licensing, tax rates, or revenue allocation.","The text is not available, so there is no information on who would be covered, on definitions, or on an effective date.","Status: passed one chamber. Last action on 2023-04-10 was a failed second-reading adoption vote of 16-30, which ends the measure for that session."],"issue_tags":["legalize"],"relevance":"core","comparison":"This is a bare constitutional authorization, unlike the statutory frameworks in AK HB145 and SB194, HI HB1308, DE HB365 and IA SSB 1157, which set out licensing, tax and enforcement mechanics. It is closest in form to the Georgia constitutional resolutions (GA HR450, SR131, SR140, SR538, SR579), which also would let the legislature legalize sports betting by general law. Unlike those, it failed on a recorded vote, and the abstract suggests it contains no operational detail.","watch":"Because the bill failed 16-30 in the Senate, a lawyer for operators should treat North Dakota as closed for now and watch only for a future constitutional-amendment effort, since any authorization there would require a ballot measure and then implementing legislation.","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","version":"Comm Sub","text_chars":303,"generated":"2026-11-03T23:01:44.499Z"}};
